  "text": {
    "root.text.Letter to R Joseph son of Judah.Poem": "<i>My thought will guide you on the path of truth, and smooth the way.<br>Come, walk along its path, O all you who wander in the field of religion!<br>The impure and the ignorant shall not pass over it; it shall be called the sacred way.</i>",
    "root.text.Letter to R Joseph son of Judah.0": "<b>In the name of the Eternal</b> <br>God of the Universe[[fn:0]]",
    "root.text.Letter to R Joseph son of Judah.1": "When[[fn:1]], my dear disciple Rabbi Joseph[[fn:2]], son of R. Yehuda [may he rest in paradise!],",
    "root.text.Letter to R Joseph son of Judah.2": "you presented yourself before me, having come from a distant land[[fn:3]] to attend my lectures, you were already held in high esteem by me on account of your great passion for study, and because I had recognised in your poems a pronounced love for speculative matters, and this since your treatises and your <i>Makâmât</i>[[fn:4]] had reached me from Alexandria, and before I could (personally) test your understanding. I said to myself (initially): Perhaps his desire is stronger than his comprehension; but when you had undertaken, under my guidance, your studies of astronomy and mathematical sciences which you had previously approached and which must necessarily serve as a preparation, I felt a far greater joy towards you on account of the excellence of your intellect and the promptitude of your understanding; and, seeing that you had a great love for mathematics, I allowed you the freedom to exercise yourself in them, knowing what your future would be[[fn:5]]. And when you had completed your studies of logic under me, my hope was attached to you and I judged you worthy of revealing to you the mysteries of the prophetic books, so that you might understand what perfect men ought to comprehend. I began, therefore, to give you glimpses of things and to provide you with certain indications; but I saw you asking for more, and you insisted that I explain to you subjects pertaining to metaphysics and that I make known to you, in this regard, what the <i>Motécallemîn</i>[[fn:6]] aimed at, and whether their methods were based on demonstration, or, if not, to what art they belonged. I saw that you had already studied this matter somewhat under other (masters); but you were agitated and troubled[[fn:7]], and your noble soul stimulated you <i>to find the objects of your desire</i>[[fn:8]]. I did not cease to dissuade you from this path and I advised you to take things in order; for my intention was (to ensure) that the truth would establish itself for you methodically and that certainty would not come to you by chance. As long as you were with me, I did not refuse, when a verse or one of the texts of the doctors was under discussion, to provide you with an explanation;",
    "root.text.Letter to R Joseph son of Judah.3": "but when God decreed the separation and you departed to another side, these conferences revived within me an (ancient) resolution which had already waned, and your absence prompted me to compose this treatise which I have made for you and for your like, however few they may be. I have made of it detached chapters, and all that shall have been committed to writing shall reach you successively wherever thou shalt be. Fare you well.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.0": "Show me the path that I shall follow, for it is unto thee that I have lifted up my soul (Ps. 143, <small>V</small>. 8).",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.1": "It is you, men, that I call, and my voice (is addressed) to the sons of Adam (Prov. 8, 4).",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.2": "Lend thine ear, and hearken unto the words of the wise, and apply thy heart unto my science (Prov. 22, 17).",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.3": "This treatise has as its primary objective to expound upon the meaning of certain names[[fn:9]] that appear in the prophetic books. Amongst these names, there are some that are homonymous, but which the ignorant take in one of the senses in which the homonym is employed; others are metaphorical, and are taken in the primitive sense from which the metaphor is derived; and others, finally, are amphibological, such that one sometimes believes they are employed as appellative names[[fn:10]] and at other times one imagines they are homonymous[[fn:11]]. The purpose of this treatise is not to render these names comprehensible to the common man or to those who are commencing their studies, nor to instruct one who studies only the science of the Law, that is to say, its traditional interpretation[[fn:12]];",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.4": "for the purpose of this entire treatise and all that is of the same kind[[fn:13]] is the science of the Law in its reality, or rather its purpose is to awaken the religious man in whom the truth of our Law is established in the soul and has become an object of belief, who is perfect in his religion and in his morals, who has studied the sciences of the philosophers and is acquainted with their various subjects, and whom human reason has attracted and guided to enter into its domain, but who is perplexed by the external (literal) sense of the Law and by what he has always understood or been made to understand of the meaning of these homonymous, or metaphorical, or amphibological names, such that he remains in a state of agitation and turmoil. Will he permit himself to be guided by his reason and reject what he has learned regarding these names? He will then believe that he has rejected the foundations of the Law. Or will he adhere to what he has understood[[fn:14]] without allowing himself to be swayed by his reason? He will thus have turned his back on reason and will have distanced himself from it, believing nonetheless that he has suffered damage and loss in his religion, and persisting in these imaginary opinions by which he will feel troubled and oppressed, such that he will ceaselessly experience suffering in his heart and violent turmoil.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.5": "This treatise has yet another purpose: it is to expound very obscure allegories found in the books of the prophets, which are not clearly recognisable as allegories, and are taken in their external sense by the ignorant and the foolish, without perceiving an esoteric meaning. Nevertheless, if a truly educated man examines them, he will also experience great turmoil when taking them in their external sense; but when we shall have expounded to him the meaning of the allegory, or warned him that it is an allegory, he will be set on the right path and saved from this turmoil. It is for this reason that I have entitled this treatise <i>Dalâlat al-'Hâyirîn</i> (the guide of those who are perplexed or erring).",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.6": "I do not say that this treatise will dispel, for one who has understood it, every kind of doubt, but I say that it will dispel most of the obscurities, and the most serious ones. The attentive man will not ask me, nor will he expect, when we have spoken of a certain subject, that we should exhaust it, or, when we have begun to expound the meaning of a certain allegory, that we should exhaust all that has been said on the subject of this allegory: this, an intelligent man could not do viva voce, in conversation[[fn:15]], and how could he commit it to writing in a book without exposing himself to be a target for every ignorant pretender to knowledge, who would launch at him the arrows of his ignorance.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.7": "We have already given, in our Talmudic works[[fn:16]], several summary explanations of this kind, and drawn attention to many subjects, and we have noted that the <i>Ma'asé beréschîth</i> (account of the creation) is the physical science and the <i>Ma'asé mercabâ</i> (account of the celestial chariot) is the metaphysical science[[fn:17]], and we have expounded this sentence[[fn:18]]: 'Neither (shall one expound) the <i>mercabâ</i>, even to a single (pupil), unless he be a wise man, understanding by his own intellect, (and in this case) one shall only impart to him the first principles.' You will therefore not ask me for anything else here but the first principles. These principles themselves are not found, in the present treatise, arranged in order and in a continuous manner, but, on the contrary, scattered and mixed with other subjects that one wished to expound; for my purpose is (to make it so) that the truths are glimpsed, and then they withdraw, in order not to be in opposition to the divine purpose, which, moreover, it would be impossible to oppose, and which has made the truths that have particularly for their object to make intellect comprehend God to be hidden from the common run of men, as has said (the psalmist): <i>The secret of the Lord is for those who fear Him</i> (Ps. 25, 14).",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.8": "With regard to the matters of physics, it is necessary to know that there are principles which cannot be clearly taught [[fn:19]] as they are in reality. You are aware of what the doctors [[fn:20]] say: 'Neither (shall one interpret) the <i>Ma'asé beréschîth</i> before two persons'; now, if someone were to explain all these things in a book, he would interpret them <i>to</i> thousands of people. This is why these subjects also present themselves in prophetic books through allegories, and the doctors have also spoken of them in enigmas and allegories, following the trace of the (sacred) books, because they are things which have an intimate relation to metaphysical science, and which also form part of the mysteries of metaphysics.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.9": "One must not believe that there is a single one amongst us who knows these grave mysteries in all their extent [[fn:21]]. It is not so; but, on the contrary, truth sometimes appears to us in a manner that seems (clear as) the day, sometimes it is hidden by material and mundane things [[fn:22]], so that we fall back into a deep night, almost as we were before, and we are then like the man who, being in a deeply obscure night, sometimes sees a flash of lightning shine.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.10": "There are amongst us those to whom the flash shines repeatedly [[fn:23]], so that, as it were, they are constantly and without interruption surrounded by light, and the night becomes for them like the day, and this is the degree of the greatest of prophets, to whom it was said: <i>And you, stay here with me</i> (Deuteronomy, 5:28), and of whom it has been said: <i>For the skin of his face shone</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Exodus, 34:29) [[fn:24]]. There are others to whom (the flash) shines only once in their entire night, and this is the degree of those of whom it has been said: <i>And they prophesied and did not continue</i> (Numbers, 11:25). For others, finally, there are intervals, more or less long, between each flash.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.11": "But there are also those who do not attain a degree (sufficiently elevated) for their darkness to be illuminated by a flash; (they are not,) on the contrary, except as by a polished body or something similar, such as gemstones, etc. which shine in the darkness of night. And even this small amount of light that shines for us[[fn:25]] is not continuous; but it appears and hides as if it were <i>the gleam of the sword that turns</i> (Genesis, 3, 24).",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.12": "It is therefore according to these circumstances that the degrees of perfect men vary. As for those who never see the light, but who err in the night, those of whom it has been said: <i>They know nothing and understand nothing, they walk in darkness</i> (Ps. 82, 5); those to whom truth is entirely hidden, however distinctly it may appear, as has been said of them: <i>And now they do not see the light that shines in the heavens</i> (Job, 37, 21), and this is the common lot of men; (as for those, I say,) there is no place to mention them here in this treatise.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.13": "One must know that, when one of the perfect men desires, according to the degree of his perfection, to express himself, either verbally or in writing, on something he has understood concerning these mysteries, it is not possible for him to expound even what he has grasped with perfect clarity and in order, as he would for the other sciences whose teaching is widespread. On the contrary, the same thing will happen to him in the teaching of others as happened to him in his own studies; I mean that the thing will appear and become visible, and then it will withdraw; for one would say that such is the nature of this thing, whether it be much or little.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.14": "It is for this reason that all learned metaphysicians and theologians[[fn:26]], friends of the truth, when they wished to teach something of this nature, spoke only through allegories and enigmas, and, multiplying the allegories, they employed different species and even different genres; they formed most of them in such a manner as to make the subject in question understood, either at the beginning of the allegory, or in the middle, or at the end, unless one could find an image that applied to the thing in question from beginning to end. (Sometimes) also the subject that one had as a goal to teach the student, although it was an essentially <i>one</i> subject, was divided, by putting it into several allegories distant from one another; but what is even more obscure, is when a single and same allegory is used for diverse subjects, in such a manner that the beginning of the allegory applies to one subject and the end to another. Sometimes also the entire allegory is used for two analogous subjects[[fn:27]] in this genre of science. Finally, when someone wished to teach without the use of allegories and enigmas, there was in their words an obscurity and brevity that took the place of the use of allegories and enigmas. It would seem that the learned and the doctors are guided in this regard by the divine will, just as their physical dispositions guide them (in other respects).",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.15": "Do you not see, moreover, that God the Most High, wishing to perfect and improve the state of our societies through His practical laws — [which can only be done after (the establishment of) certain rational dogmas, the first condition of which is to understand the divinity according to our faculty, which can only be done through the science of metaphysics, which in turn can only come after the science of physics; for the latter is adjacent[[fn:28]] to metaphysics and precedes it in teaching, which is clear to one who has made these studies] — (do you not see, I say,) that He has placed at the opening of His book the account of creation, which is the science of physics, as we have explained? But because of the gravity and importance of this thing, and because our faculty is insufficient to understand the most serious of subjects in all its reality[[fn:29]], one has chosen, to speak to us of the profound subjects that divine wisdom judged necessary to discuss with us, allegories, enigmas, and extremely obscure words, as the doctors have said: 'To expose the power of the work of creation to mortals is impossible; this is why Scripture has spoken to you in an obscure manner: <i>In the beginning, God created, etc.</i>.' They have thus warned you that the things mentioned are <i>obscure</i>. You are also familiar with these words of Solomon: <i>What exists is far</i> (<i>from our conception</i>); <i>this very profound thing, who can find it</i> (Ecclesiastes, 7, 25)? To speak of all these subjects, one has used homonymous words, so that common men could take them in a certain sense according to the measure of their intelligence and the weakness of their understanding, and so that the perfect man who has received instruction could take them in another sense.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.16": "We had promised in the commentary on the Mischnâ[[fn:30]] to expound certain difficult subjects in (two works entitled): the <i>Book of Prophecy</i> and the <i>Book of Harmony;</i> the latter being a book in which we had promised to expound the obscurities of all the <i>Deraschôth</i>[[fn:31]], whose external words are in great discord with truth and deviate from reason, and which are all allegories. But when, some years ago, we began these books and had written a part of them, the explanations to which we had committed ourselves, according to this method, did not please us; for it seemed to us that, proceeding ourselves by way of allegory and hiding what ought to remain hidden, we would not have deviated from the original purpose (of the <i>Deraschôth</i>), and we would have done, as it were, nothing but substitute one individual for another individual of the same species; but that, in expounding what needs expounding, this would not be suitable for the common man, and yet we desired nothing else but to expound to the common man the meaning of the <i>Deraschôth</i> and the external words of the prophecies. It also seemed to us that an ignorant man of the common sort of rabbis, in studying these <i>Deraschôth</i>, would find no difficulty in them: for he who is ignorant, indolent, and devoid of knowledge concerning the nature of Being, does not find even impossible things implausible; that if, on the contrary, it were a perfect and distinguished man who studied them, he would necessarily arrive at one of two things: either he would take them in their external sense, and then he would have a bad opinion of the author and take him for an ignorant man, but there would be nothing in this that could undermine the foundations of faith; or he would attribute to them an esoteric meaning, and then he would fare well and have a good opinion of the author, no matter whether the (true) inner meaning of the words was clear to him or not. As for the significance of Prophecy, the exposition of its various degrees, and the interpretation of the allegories of its books, all this will be explained in a different manner in the present treatise. For all these reasons, therefore, we have renounced composing these two works as they had been conceived; and we have limited ourselves, — in speaking of the foundations of Faith and general truths with brevity and in giving a glimpse of (things) in a manner that approaches clear exposition —, to what we have said in the great Talmudic work <i>Mischné Torâ</i>.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.17": "As for the present treatise, I address the word, as I have said, to one who has studied philosophy and acquired true sciences, but who, believing in religious matters, is troubled concerning their meaning, with regard to which obscure names and allegories[[fn:32]] leave uncertainty. We will also give in this treatise chapters in which no homonymous word will be mentioned. But one chapter will serve as preparation for another or will draw attention to one of the meanings of a homonymous word that I do not wish to speak of explicitly in this place; another chapter will expound some allegory or warn that a certain subject is an allegory; another will contain difficult subjects, concerning which one sometimes believes the contrary of what is true, either because of certain homonymies or because one confuses the image with the thing it is meant to represent or takes the represented thing for an image[[fn:33]].",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.18": "And since I have spoken of allegories, we shall enunciate a proposition, which is this: 'It is necessary to know that the key to understanding all that the prophets have said and to knowing it in all its reality, is to understand allegories and their meaning and to know how to interpret their words.' You know what the Most High has said: <i>And by the prophets I make similitudes</i> (Hos., XII, 11); and you also know this other passage: <i>Propose an enigma and make a parable</i> (Ezek., 17, 2). You also know that it is because of the frequent use of allegories by the prophets that the prophet said: They say of me: <i>Is he not a maker of allegories</i> (<i>Ib</i>., XXI, 5)? You know, finally, how Solomon began (his book): <i>To understand the allegory and the eloquent discourse, the words of the wise and their enigmas</i> (Prov., 1, 6). And in the Midrash[[fn:34]] it is said: 'What did the words of the Torah resemble before Solomon appeared? A well whose cold water was situated in the depth, so that no one could drink from it. What did a wise man do then? He tied ropes together and threads together, and then he drew and drank. Thus Solomon passed[[fn:35]] from one allegory to another and from one discourse to another until he profoundized the words of the Torah.' Such are the textual words. I do not think that among men endowed with good sense there is one who imagines that the <i>words of the Torah</i> to which allusion is made here and which one has sought to understand by the (preliminary) intelligence of the meaning of allegories are the precepts concerning the making of cabins, the branches of palm trees, and the law relating to the four guardians[[fn:36]]; on the contrary, one undoubtedly had in view here the understanding of profound things. It is said in the same place (of the Midrash): 'The rabbis say: If someone loses a shekel or a pearl in his house, he only has to light a wick (worth) an obol to find the pearl; similarly, the allegory in itself is nothing, but by means of the allegory you understand the words of the Torah.' Such are their textual words again. And pay close attention that they clearly say that the interior of the words of the Torah is the pearl and that the external sense of every allegory is nothing, and that they compare the hidden and represented sense in the external words of the allegory to a pearl that someone has lost in his house, which is dark and full of furniture. This pearl exists, but he does not see it and does not know where it is: it is as if it had gone out of his possession, since he has no means of deriving any benefit from it until he lights the lamp as has been said; and it is to this that the understanding of the sense of the allegory resembles.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.19": "The wise man has said: <i>Like apples of gold in nets</i> (Maskiyyôth) <i>of silver, such is a word spoken according to its different faces</i> (Prov., 21, II)[[fn:37]].",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.20": "Hear the explanation of the subject of which he speaks: Maskiyyôth are reticular engravings, that is to say, where there are openings (formed) of extremely fine meshes, like the works of goldsmiths; they are called thus because the gaze penetrates them, for the Chaldaic version of <span dir=\"rtl\">וַיַּשְׁקֵף</span> (he looked) is <i>we-istekhi</i>[[fn:38]]. He says, therefore: As an apple of gold in a silver net with very fine openings, such is the word spoken according to its two faces. And behold[[fn:39]] how this sentence applies wonderfully to the allegory in rule; for he says that the discourse which has two faces, that is to say, which has an exterior and interior (sense), must have its exterior beautiful like silver, but its interior must be still more beautiful than its exterior, so that its interior will be, in comparison to its exterior, like gold is to silver. It is also necessary that there be in its exterior something which can indicate to the one examining it what is in its interior, like this apple of gold which has been covered with a silver net with extremely fine meshes: for, if one sees it from afar or without examining it attentively, one believes it to be a silver apple; but if the man with the penetrating eye examines it well attentively, what is within is shown to him, and he recognises that it is gold. And it is the same with the allegories of the prophets: their exterior words (contain) a wisdom useful for many things, and among others for the improvement of the state of human societies, as appears in the exterior words of the Proverbs (of Solomon) and other similar discourses; but their interior (sense) is a wisdom useful for beliefs having for object the true in all its reality.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.21": "Know also that prophetic allegories are made in two ways: there are some in which each word of the allegory desires (that one finds) a particular sense; and there are others in which the entirety of the allegory reveals the entirety of the subject represented, but where it is also found that many words do not each add something to the subject represented, and which serve only to embellish the allegory and to the symmetry of the discourse, or to conceal with more care the subject represented, so that the discourse is constantly conceived as it must be according to the exterior sense of the allegory. It is necessary to understand this well.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.22": "An example of the first species of prophetic allegories is found in this passage: <i>And behold, a ladder was set upon the earth, etc</i>. (Genesis, 28, 12); for the word <i>ladder</i> indicates a certain subject, the words <i>was set upon the earth</i> indicate a second, the words <i>and its head reached the heaven</i> indicate a third, the words <i>and behold, the angels of God</i> indicate a fourth, the word <i>went up</i> indicates a fifth, the words <i>and came down</i> indicate a sixth, and the words <i>and behold, the Lord stood above</i> indicate a seventh, so that each word which presents itself in this allegory adds something to the entirety of the subject represented.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.23": "An example of the second species of prophetic allegories is found in the following passage (Prov., 7, 6 et seq.): <i>For through the window of my house, through my lattice I looked, and I saw among the simple, I discerned among the youths a child void of intellect, who, passing through the market near the corner, was advancing towards the house</i> (<i>where she dwelt</i>). <i>During the twilight, when the day was disappearing, in the darkness of the night and in the shadows. And behold, a woman comes forth to meet him in the attire of a courtesan and with a cunning heart. She is boisterous, unmanageable, etc. Now in the street, now in the squares, etc. She seizes him, etc. I had to offer peace sacrifices, etc. Therefore, I went forth to meet thee, etc. I covered my bed with tapestries, etc. I perfumed my couch, etc. Come, let us inebriate ourselves with love, etc. For the husband is not at home, etc. He has taken the purse containing the money, etc. She seduces him with the abundance of her words, she entices him with her fallacious lips</i>.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.24": "The entire discourse is intended to warn that one ought not follow the pleasures and passions of the body; it therefore compares the matter which is the cause of all these corporeal passions to a courtesan who is at the same time a married woman, and it is upon this allegory that he has built his entire book. In several chapters of this treatise, we shall demonstrate the wisdom of comparing matter to an unfaithful married woman, and we shall note how he concludes his book with the praise of the woman who is not unfaithful, but rather occupies herself with the good order of her house and the position of her husband. (It will also be seen that) all the obstacles which prevent man from arriving at his final perfection, every vice and every sin which attaches itself to man, arise solely from the side of matter, as we shall explain in this treatise. It is therefore this general doctrine which forms the content of this entire allegory, namely that man ought not follow his sole animal nature, that is to say, his matter; for the immediate matter of man is also the immediate matter of other animals[[fn:40]]. After having given you this explanation and having revealed to you the mystery of this allegory, you will no longer[[fn:41]] hope to be able to ask me what is hidden beneath the words: <i>I had to offer peace sacrifices, today I have accomplished my vows;</i> what sense do the words convey: <i>I covered my bed with tapestries</i>; or what the words add to the general sense: <i>For the husband is not at home</i>, as well as all that follows in this chapter. All of this is merely a series of phrases relating to the external sense of the allegory; for the details which he described are of the same species as those (which relate) to fornicators, and similarly these words and others like them are of the same species as those which adulterers address to one another.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.25": "It is essential to comprehend my statements, for they are founded upon a principle of paramount importance to the explications I propose to furnish. Thus, should you perceive me elucidating the sense of a particular allégorie in a chapter of this treatise, and I direct your attention to the entirety of the thing represented, you shall not request (an explication of) every particular subject encountered within this allégorie, nor shall you endeavour to discover its analogue in the thing represented; for to do so would lead you to one of two consequences, either to lose sight of the subject which the allégorie was intended to convey, or to impose upon yourself the task of interpreting words not susceptible to interpretation and not written to be <i>interpreted</i>. And in imposing this task upon yourself, you would fall prey to that great folly which afflicts the majority of the world's schools in our era, as evidenced by their writings[[fn:42]]; for each of these individuals seeks to discern a meaning in words in which the author intended none of the significance they themselves wish to discern. You must, on the contrary, always direct your attention to comprehending the entirety of the thing intended to be conveyed in the majority of allégories, and in certain instances, it shall suffice for you to understand from my words that a particular subject is an allégorie, even if I do not expound further; for once you have ascertained that it is an allégorie, you shall immediately comprehend its object, and in stating that it is an allégorie, I shall, as it were, have removed the obstacle interposed between the viewer and the object viewed.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.26": "<small>RECOMMENDATION REGARDING THIS TREATISE.</small> <br>If you wish to comprehend all that (this treatise) contains, in such a manner that nothing escapes you, it is necessary to combine its chapters with one another, and in reading a chapter, you must not only aim to understand the entirety of its subject, but also to grasp each word that presents itself in the sequence of the discourse, even if it does not pertain to the principal subject of the chapter. For in this treatise, I have never spoken fortuitously, but (all has been said) with great exactitude and with much precision, and with care to leave no explanation of any obscurity wanting; and if something has been said (here and there) that is not in its place, it has been only to explain something else in its true place. You must not approach it with your preconceived opinions, for you would do me an injustice without profiting yourself; but, on the contrary, you must first learn all that it is necessary to learn. You will make it the object of your continual studies, for it will explain to you the most grave obscurities of the Law, which are difficult even for every intelligent man. I implore, by God the Most High, all those who will have read the present treatise, not to comment on a single word of it, nor to explain anything of it to others, unless it be what is already clearly explained in the words of those who have preceded me among the celebrated doctors of our Law. But (the reader) who will have understood something of what others of our celebrated men have not said, must not explain it to others; nor must he hasten to refute me[[fn:43]]; for it may be that what he will have understood in my words was the contrary of what I intended to say, and then he would do me an injustice in return for what I intended to be useful to him, and <i>he would repay good with evil</i>. On the contrary, he to whom (this treatise) will have fallen into the hands, must study it with care, and if he finds in it something to satisfy his desire[[fn:44]], be it only on a single point of all that is obscure, he must thank God and be content with what he has understood. If he finds in it absolutely nothing that is useful to him, he must consider it as if it had never been composed; but if it seems to him to result in harm with regard to his opinions, he must interpret (my words) <i>in a manner that judges me favourably</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ידין לכף זכות</span>), even by the most recherché interpretation, as it has been prescribed to us with regard to our common people, and all the more so with regard to our scholars and the supporters of our Law, who strive to teach us the truth according to their faculty.",
    "root.text.Prefatory Remarks.27": "I am aware that all beginners who have not yet undertaken any speculative study will derive benefit from certain chapters of this treatise; but perfect men, steeped in the study of the Law and who are troubled, as I have said, will benefit from all its chapters, and how joyful they will be and with what pleasure they will listen to (the reading) ! But as for those people who are perplexed, whose intellect is troubled by opinions contrary to truth and by false methods, and who nonetheless believe these to be true sciences and pretend to be men of speculative study, whilst they know absolutely nothing that can in reality be called science, — as for those, (I say,) they will turn away from a great number of its chapters, and they will experience a great repugnance, because they will not grasp the sense, and also because the falsity of the bad coin they hold in their hands and which is their treasure and the fortune set aside for their distress[[fn:45]] will be recognised. God, the Most High, knows that I have always felt a very great fear of committing to writing the things that I wish to deposit in this treatise; for these are hidden things, and on which no book has ever been composed among our coreligionists in this time of captivity, of which we still possess the works[[fn:46]]. And how, then, can I create something new and commit it to writing ! Nevertheless, I have relied on two principles: first, on what (the rabbis) have said concerning analogous matters: <i>When it is time to act for God, etc</i>.[[fn:47]]; second, on what they have also said: <i>That all works be done in the name of heaven</i>. It is, therefore, on these two principles that I have relied for what I have written in certain chapters of this treatise. Finally, I am the man who, seeing himself confined in a narrow arena and not finding the means to teach a well-demonstrated truth, except in a manner that suits one distinguished man and displeases ten thousand ignorant men, prefers to speak for this single distinguished person, without paying attention to the blame of the great multitude, and claims to draw this single distinguished man out of the embarrassment in which he has fallen and to show him the way (to exit) from his error, in order to become perfect and to obtain rest.",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.0": "PRELIMINARY OBSERVATION. <br>The causes of the contradiction or opposition that one finds in a book or in any writing whatsoever are seven in number.",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.1": "FIRST CAUSE</small>: When the author has gathered the words of people of different opinions, omitting (to cite) the authorities and to attribute each word to its author. One then finds in his work contradictions or opposed assertions, because one of the two propositions is the opinion of one individual, and the other the opinion of another individual.",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.2": "S<small>ECOND CAUSE</small>: When the author of the book has initially professed a certain opinion from which he has subsequently recanted, and when both what he initially said and what he said afterwards have been recorded.",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.3": "T<small>HIRD CAUSE</small>: When the words (of the author) are not all (taken) in their external sense (literal); but some retain their literal sense and others are an image and have a figurative sense, or when the two contradictory propositions, according to their literal sense, are allegories, and when, taken in their literal sense, they appear contradictory or opposed to one another.",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.4": "F<small>OURTH CAUSE</small>: When there exists a certain condition[[fn:48]] which, for some reason, is not explicitly indicated in the very place, or when the two subjects are different[[fn:49]] and neither of the two is clearly designated in the very place, such that it appears there is a contradiction in the discourse without there actually being one.",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.5": "C<small>FOURTH CAUSE</small>: The necessity (to which one is sometimes reduced) for teaching and making understood (a certain thing); that is to say, when there is a certain obscure and difficult subject to conceive which one needs to mention or take as premises, in order to explain a subject easy to conceive and which in teaching should precede this first subject, because one always begins with the easiest [[fn:50]]. Then, the one who teaches must make himself comfortable to make this first subject understood, in any way, by examining it roughly without undertaking to expose exactly all its reality, and leaving it, on the contrary, to the reach of the listener's imagination, so that the latter may understand what one wants him to understand at this moment, reserving the right to expose more exactly this obscure subject which will manifest itself in its reality in the suitable place [[fn:51]].",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.6": "S<small>SIXTH CAUSE</small>: When the contradiction hides and only manifests itself after several premises. The more premises are needed to manifest it, the more it will be hidden, in such a way as to escape the author, who will believe that there is no contradiction between the two first propositions. However, by taking each of the two propositions separately and joining a true premise to it, in such a way that a necessary conclusion is drawn from it, and doing the same with each conclusion, (I mean) by joining a true premise to it in such a way as to draw a necessary conclusion from it, one will arrive, after a series of syllogisms, at finding a contradiction or opposition between the two last conclusions. This is what happens even to learned authors; but if the two first propositions were manifestly contradictory and the author had only forgotten the first one when writing the other in another part of his work, this would be a very great inferiority, and such a man could not be counted among those whose words deserve attention.",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.7": "S<small>SEVENTH CAUSE</small>: The necessity of discourse, when it comes to very obscure things whose details must be partly hidden and partly revealed. For sometimes one is forced, when emitting an opinion, to express oneself in such a way as to affirm a certain proposition, while in another place one is forced to express oneself in such a way as to affirm a proposition which is in contradiction with the first. The common people should in no way perceive the place where the contradiction exists, and the author sometimes seeks all sorts of expedients to hide it [[fn:52]].",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.8": "With regard to the contradictions found in the <i>Mischnâ</i> and in the <i>Baraïthôth</i>, they emanate from the first cause. Thus you will continually find it said (in the Talmud): 'The beginning (of the chapter) is in contradiction with the end,' and this response is added: 'The beginning emanates from such-and-such a doctor and the end from another doctor.' You will also find these words: 'Rabbi[[fn:53]] approved the words of such-and-such a doctor in one case and simply reproduced his opinion (without naming him), and in another case he approved the words of another doctor and simply reproduced his opinion.' Often, too, you will find this formula: 'To whom does this anonymous assertion belong? It belongs to such-and-such a doctor. — To whom does our (paragraph of the) Mischnâ belong? It belongs to such-and-such a doctor.' These examples are innumerable.",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.9": "With regard to the contradictions and divergences found in the Talmud (or the <i>Guemarâ</i>), they emanate from the first and second cause. Thus you will always find these words: 'On such a subject he adopted the opinion of such-and-such a doctor and on another subject the opinion of another doctor.' It is also said: 'He adopted the opinion of one person in one case and deviated from it in another case.' It is said, finally: 'These are two <i>Amoraïm</i>[[fn:54]] (who differ) on the opinion of such-and-such a doctor.' All examples of this kind conform to the first cause. With regard to the second cause, they (the Talmudists) explicitly say: <i>Râb recanted such an opinion</i>, or <i>Rabâ recanted such an opinion</i>[[fn:55]], and (in such a case) they discuss which of the two opinions is the later one. We may also quote the following words: 'According to the first redaction of Rabbi Asché[[fn:56]], he expressed himself in such a sense, and according to the second redaction he expressed himself in another sense.'",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.10": "As for the contradiction or opposition that appears in the external sense of certain passages of all the prophetic books, it emanates from the third and fourth cause, and it is especially this subject that was the purpose of this entire preliminary observation. You know that (the doctors) often repeat these words: 'One text expresses itself in such a manner and another text in another manner.' They first establish the apparent contradiction, then they explain that there is a missing condition (in the text), or that it concerns two different subjects. Thus, for example, they say: 'Solomon, is it not enough that your words contradict those of your father? Must they also contradict each other, etc.?[[fn:57]]' The doctors often speak in this sense; but most of the time they deal with prophetic discourses related to (religious) precepts or morality. As for us, our only goal was to draw attention to verses that, taken in their literal sense, contain contradictions regarding certain opinions and beliefs; a part of this will be explained in various chapters of this treatise; for this subject is also part of the <i>Mysteries of the Law</i>. As for the question of whether there are contradictions in the books of the prophets emanating from the seventh cause, this is something that must be examined and discussed, and not decided upon hastily[[fn:58]].",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.11": "With regard to the divergence found in the books of true philosophers, it emanates from the fifth cause.",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.12": "As for the contradictions found in most of the works of authors and commentators other than those we have mentioned, they emanate from the sixth cause. Likewise, in the <i>מִדְרָשוֹת</i> and in the <i>הַגָּדוֹת</i> there exist grave contradictions emanating from this same cause; this is why (the rabbis) say: 'One does not raise contradictions in the <i>הַגָּדוֹת</i>.' There are also found contradictions emanating from the seventh cause.",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.13": "Lastly, the divergences that may exist in this treatise emanate from the fifth and seventh cause[[fn:59]]. It is necessary that you know this, that you penetrate it, and that you recall it well, in order not to be troubled regarding several of these chapters.",
    "root.text.Part 1.Introduction.14": "After these preliminary remarks, I commence to speak of the names from which it is necessary to extract the true meaning that was intended in each place according to the subject (which is being treated), and this shall be a key to enter into places whose doors are closed. And when these doors shall have been opened and one shall have entered into these places, the souls shall find rest there, the eyes shall delight, and the bodies shall be relieved of their pain and fatigue.",
    "root.text.Part 1..0.0": "Open the gates, that the righteous nation which keeps the truth may enter in.",
    "root.text.Part 1..0.1": "<i>Célem</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צֶלֶם</span>) and <i>demouth</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דְּמוּת</span>). — There have been people who believed that <i>célem</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צלם</span>), in the Hebrew language, designated the form of a thing and its lineaments, and this led to pure corporification (of God), because it is said (in Scripture): <i>Let us make man in our image</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בצלמנו</span>) <i>according to our likeness</i> (Genesis, 1, 26). They believed, therefore, that God had the form of a man, that is to say, his figure and lineaments, and it resulted in pure corporification for them, which they admitted as a belief, thinking that, if they deviated from this belief, they would deny the text (of Scripture), or even that they would deny the existence of God if He were not (for them) a body having a face and hands similar to theirs in form and lineaments; only, they admitted that He was greater and more resplendent (than they), and that His matter also was not blood and flesh, and this was all they could conceive of as more sublime with regard to God[[fn:60]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..0.2": "As for that which must be stated to remove corporeality and establish true unity, — which has no reality except by the exclusion[[fn:61]] of corporeality, — you shall know the demonstration of all this by the present treatise; here, in this chapter we only wish to draw attention to the explanation of the meaning of <i>célem</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צלם</span>) and of <i>demouth</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דמות</span>).",
    "root.text.Part 1..0.3": "I say, therefore, that the <i>form</i> as it is generally known to the common people[[fn:62]] [I mean the figure of the thing and its lineaments] bears in the Hebrew language the particular name of <i>toar</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תאר</span>); one says, for example: <i>Beautiful in figure</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תאר</span>) <i>and beautiful in countenance</i> (Genesis, 39, 6); <i>what is his figure</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תארו</span>) (I Samuel, 28, 14)? <i>As the figure</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כתאר</span>) <i>of the king's sons</i> (Judges, 13, 18). It has been said (in speaking) of the <i>artificial form</i>: He figures<span dir=\"rtl\"> (</span>יתארהו<i>) </i>with the burin<i>… and </i>he figures<span dir=\"rtl\"> (</span>יתארהו<i>) </i>with the circle[[t:34]] (Isaiah, 44, 13). This is a denomination that is never applied to God the Most High;—far be it from us (such a thought)!",
    "root.text.Part 1..0.4": "As for <i>célem</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צלם</span>), it applies to the <i>natural form</i>, I mean to that which constitutes the substance of the thing, whereby it becomes what it is and which forms its reality, inasmuch as it is such a being (determined). In man, this something is that from which comes the <i>understanding</i> of man, and it is because of this intellectual <i>compréhension</i> that it has been said of him: <span dir=\"rtl\">He created him in the image</span> (<i>בצלם</i>) [[t:14]]of God[[t:15]] (Genesis, 1, 26).",
    "root.text.Part 1..0.5": "This is why it has been said (speaking of the impious): <i>You despise their image</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צלמם</span>) (Ps. 73, 20); for contempt reaches the soul which is the <i>form specific</i>[[fn:63]], and not the figures of the members and their lineaments. I say likewise that the reason why idols were called <i>celamîm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צלמים</span>), is that what was sought in them was something that was supposed in them[[fn:64]]; but it was in no way for their figure and lineaments. I shall say the same thing regarding the words: <i>the images</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צלמי</span>) <i>of your</i> <small>TE’HORÎM</small> (I Sam., 6, 5)[[fn:65]]; for what was sought in them was the means of warding off evil from the <i>te’horîm</i>, and it was in no way the figure of the <i>te’horîm.</i> If, however, it were absolutely necessary to admit that the name of <i>célem</i>, applied to the images of the <i>te’horîm</i> and to idols, referred to the figure and lineaments, this name would be either homonymous or amphibological[[fn:66]], and would apply not only to the <i>form specific</i>, but also to the <i>artificial form</i>, as well as to the analogous figures of physical bodies and their lineaments.",
    "root.text.Part 1..0.6": "By the words: <i>Let us make man in our image</i>, one would thus have wished to speak <i>of the specific form</i>, that is to say, of the intellectual understanding, and not of the figure and lineaments. — Thus we have explained to you the difference between <i>célem</i> (image) and <i>toar</i> (figure), and we have also explained the meaning of <i>célem.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..0.7": "As for <i>demouth</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דמות</span>), it is a name (derived) from <span dir=\"rtl\">דמה</span> (to resemble), and which also indicates a resemblance with respect to some idea; for the words (of the psalmist): <i>I resemble</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דמיתי</span>) <i>the pelican of the desert</i> (Ps. 102, 7), do not mean that it resembled it with respect to the wings and plumage, but that the sadness of the one resembled the sadness of the other. Similarly (in this passage): <i>No tree in the garden of God resembled</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דמה</span>) <i>in beauty</i> (Ezek., 31, 8), it is a resemblance with respect to the idea of beauty; (likewise in these other passages): <i>They have poison similar</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כדמות</span>) <i>to the poison of the serpent</i> (Ps. 58, 5); <i>He resembles</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דמיונו</span>) <i>a lion eager for prey</i> (Ps. 17, 12). All (these passages indicate) a resemblance with respect to a certain idea, and not with respect to the figure and lineaments. Similarly: <i>The resemblance</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דמות</span>) <i>of the throne</i> (Ezek., 1, 26) is a resemblance with respect to the idea of elevation and majesty, and not with respect to the square shape, the thickness, and the length of the feet, as the poor in spirit believe, and it is the same <i>with the resemblance</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דמות</span>) <i>of the animals</i> (Ezek., 1, 13).",
    "root.text.Part 1..0.8": "Now, as man is distinguished by something very remarkable that exists within him and which is not found in any of the beings below the sphere of the moon, namely by intellectual comprehension, for which one employs neither senses, nor hands, nor arms[[fn:67]], (this faculty) has been compared to divine comprehension, which is not accomplished by means of an instrument; although the resemblance does not exist in reality, but only at first glance. And for this thing, I mean on account of the divine intellect that unites with man[[fn:68]], it has been said of him that he was made <i>in the image of God and in His likeness</i>, (and this means) by no means that God the Most High is a body having any form whatsoever.",
    "root.text.Part 1..1.0": "A man of science posed to me, several years ago, a remarkable objection that merits consideration, as well as the response we provided to refute it.",
    "root.text.Part 1..1.1": "But before reporting the objection and the manner in which to refute it, here is what I have to say. Every Hebrew knows that the name of <i>Élohîm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אלהים</span>) is a homonym, applying to God, to angels, and to rulers governing states. Already Onkelos, the proselyte, has explained, — and his explanation is true, — that by the words: <i>And you shall be as </i>Élohîm <i>knowing good and evil</i> (Genesis, 3, 5), one intended the latter sense; for he says (in his Chaldaic translation): «And you shall be as the <i>great personages</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כרברביא</span>).»",
    "root.text.Part 1..1.2": "After this preliminary observation on the homonymy of this name, we shall report the objection.",
    "root.text.Part 1..1.3": "It would appear, from the literal sense of the text, said the author of the objection, that the primitive intention in (the creation of) man was that he should be like the rest of the animals, without intellect and without reflection, and without the ability to distinguish between good and evil; but that, having disobeyed, his disobedience merited him this great particular perfection of man, namely to possess this discernment which is in us, which is the most noble thing of our existence and which constitutes our substance. But it is a remarkable thing that his punishment for his disobedience was to give him a perfection he had not previously had, namely, intellect. It is absolutely like the assertion of those who have said that a certain man, after having disobeyed (God) and committed excesses of injustice, was transformed and placed as a star in the heaven[[fn:69]].—Such was the purpose and thought of the objection, although it was not (presented) in the same terms.",
    "root.text.Part 1..1.4.sub_0": "Hear now in what manner we responded. O you, we said, who examine (things) with a superficial and unreflecting spirit[[fn:70]], and who believe you understand a book, a guide for the ancients and the moderns, by perusing it in a few moments of leisure (stolen from the pleasures) of drinking and cohabitation, as one would peruse some historical book or poem! Stop and examine; for the thing is not as you believed it to be at first glance, but as it shall manifest itself when one has considered what I am about to say. The reason that God has caused to emanate upon man, and which constitutes his final perfection, is that which Adam possessed before his disobedience; it is for this that he was said to be (made) <i>in the image of God and in His likeness</i>, and it is on account of this that the word was addressed to him and he received commands, as it is said (in Scripture): <i>And the Lord God commanded</i>, etc. (Genesis, 2, 16), for one cannot give commands to animals or to one who has no reason. By reason one distinguishes between the <i>true</i> and the <i>false</i>, and this faculty he (Adam) possessed perfectly and completely; but the <i>ugly</i> and the <i>beautiful</i> exist in (matters of) <i>probable opinions</i>, and not in (things that are) <i>intelligible</i>[[fn:71]]; for one does not say that this proposition: <i>the heaven is spherical</i>, is beautiful, nor that this other: <i>the earth is flat</i>, is ugly; but one calls the one <i>true</i> and the other <i>false</i>. Thus in our language we employ (in speaking of) the <i>true</i> and the <i>false</i> (the words) <i>אמת</i> and <span dir=\"rtl\">שקר</span>, and for <i>beautiful</i> and <span dir=\"rtl\">ugly</span> (we say) <i>טוב</i> and <i>רע</i>. By reason, therefore, man distinguishes the <i>true</i> from the <span dir=\"rtl\">false</span>, and this takes place in all (things that are) intelligible. When, therefore, he (Adam) was still in his most perfect and complete state, having only his primitive nature and his intelligible notions, on account of which it was said of him: <i>And You have made him a little lower than the angels</i> (Psalm),",
    "root.text.Part 1..1.4.sub_1": "8, 6), there was in him no faculty that applied to the <i>probable opinions</i> in any way, and he did not even understand them; so that what is most manifestly base with regard to the <i>probable opinions</i>, that is to say, to discover the shameful parts, was not base to him, and he did not even understand its baseness. But when, disobeying, he inclined towards his desires coming from the imaginative and towards the bodily pleasures of his senses, as it is said (in Scripture): … <i>That the tree was good to eat and that it was a pleasure for the eyes</i> (Genesis, 3, 6), he was punished by the privation of this intellectual understanding; that is why he transgressed[[fn:72]] the order that had been given to him because of his reason, and, having obtained the knowledge of the <i>probable opinions</i>, he was absorbed by what he had to find base or beautiful, and he then knew what the thing was worth that had escaped him and of which he had been deprived, and in what state he had fallen. That is why it has been said: <i>And you will be like Élohîm knowing good and evil</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 3, 5), and it has not been said: knowing the <i>false</i> and the <i>true</i>, or: understanding the <i>false</i> and the <i>true;</i> whereas in the (domain of the) <i>necessary</i>[[fn:73]] there is not at all good or evil, but false and true. — Consider also these words: <i>And the eyes of both of them were opened and they</i> <small>RECOGNIZED</small> <i>that they were naked</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 3, 7). It is not said: And the eyes of both of them were opened and they <i>saw;</i> for what (man) had <i>seen</i> before, he also <i>saw</i> after. It is not that there was a veil on the eye that was then removed; but he underwent another state in which he found base what he had not found base before.",
    "root.text.Part 1..1.5": "Know that this word, that is to say <span dir=\"rtl\">פקח</span>, is used absolutely only in the sense of: <i>opening the moral view</i> (and not) of the rebirth of the sense of sight; for example: <i>And God opened his eyes</i> (Genesis, 21, 19); <i>Then the eyes of the blind will be opened</i> (Isaiah, 35, 5); <i>The ears opened they do not hear</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 42, 20), which resembles these words: <i>Those who have eyes to see and do not see</i> (Ezekiel, 12, 2).",
    "root.text.Part 1..1.6": "As for what is said of Adam: <i>When he changed his</i> face <i>you sent him away</i> (Job, 14, 20)[[fn:74]], it must be interpreted and commented thus: \"When he changed his <i>direction</i> he was expelled\"; for <span dir=\"rtl\">פנים</span> (face, visage) is a noun derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">פנה</span> (to turn), because man directs himself with his face towards the thing he wishes to attain. It is said, therefore: When he changed his direction and turned towards the thing towards which he had been previously forbidden to turn, he was expelled from paradise. And this was a punishment similar to his disobedience, <i>measure for measure</i>[[fn:75]]; for he had been permitted to eat pleasant things and to delight in rest and tranquillity; but having become avid, having followed his pleasures and his imagination, as we have said, and having eaten what had been forbidden to him, he was deprived of all and forced to eat the vilest of foods and that which had not previously served him as nourishment, (and this) with great toil and fatigue, as Scripture says: <i>It shall bring forth thorns and thistles, etc., by the sweat of thy brow, etc.</i> (Genesis, 3, 18, 19), and then it is clearly said: <i>And the Lord God sent him forth from the paradise to till the earth</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 23). And He made him similar to the animals in his food and in most circumstances, as Scripture says: <i>And thou shalt eat the herb of the field</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 18). And as if to explain this passage (the psalmist) has said: Man shall not abide in his dignity, and he was made similar to the dumb beasts (Ps. 49, 13).",
    "root.text.Part 1..1.7": "—Praise be to the Master of this will whose ultimate term and wisdom cannot be comprehended.",
    "root.text.Part 1..2.0": "One imagines that the meaning of <i>temounâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תמונה</span>) and of <i>tabnîth</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תבנית</span>) in the Hebrew language is the same; but such is not the case. <i>Tabnîth</i> is a noun derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">בנה</span> (to build) and signifies the <i>structure</i> of a thing and its form, i.e., its figure, such as, for example, a square, circular, triangular figure, etc. It is said, for example: <i>The figure</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תבנית</span>) <i>of the tabernacle and the figure</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תבנית</span>) <i>of all its vessels</i> (Exod., 25, 9), and it is said again: <i>According to their figure</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כתבניתם</span>) <i>which was shown thee on the mount</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 40); <i>The figure</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תבנית</span>) <i>of every bird</i> (Deut., 4, 17); <i>The figure</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תבנית</span>) <i>of a hand</i> (Ezek., 8, 3); <i>The figure</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תבנית</span>) <i>of the porch</i> (I Chron., 28, 11). All this is a <i>figure</i> (<i>visible</i>); this is why the Hebrew language does not employ this kind of expression in descriptions that relate to God,intellect and faculty being distinct from form and matter.",
    "root.text.Part 1..2.1": "As for <i>temounâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תמונה</span>), it is a term employed amphibologically[[fn:76]] in three distinct senses. Firstly, it refers to the form of an object perceived by the senses independently of the mind—that is to say, its shape and its lineaments; and this is the sense of the words: <i>….. and that ye make a graven image of the figure</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תמונת</span>) <i>of any thing whatsoever, etc.</i> (Deuteronomy, 4, 25); <i>For ye saw no manner of similitude</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תמונה</span>) (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>v.</small> 15). Secondly, it denotes the imaginary figure which an object, having withdrawn from the senses, leaves in the imagination, as in this passage: <i>In thoughts</i> (<i>nées</i>) <i>from the visions of the night, etc.</i> (Job, 4, 13 et seq.), which concludes with these words: <i>It stood still, but I could not discern the form thereof: a figure</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תמונה</span>) <i>was before mine eyes</i>; that is to say, there was a phantasm before mine eyes in slumber. Finally, thirdly, it signifies the true idea (of a thing) perceived by the intellect; and it is in this third sense that <i>temounâ</i> is employed when speaking of God; for example: <i>And he beholds the similitude</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תמונת</span>) <i>of the LORD</i> (Numbers, 12, 8), which must be explicated in this wise: <i>And he comprehends God in His reality</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..3.0": "Be aware that the three verbs <i>raâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראה</span>), <i>hibbît</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הביט</span>), and <i>’</i>hazâ<span dir=\"rtl\"> (</span>הזה</span>) are applied to the vision of the eye; however, they are all employed metaphorically for the perception of the intellect.",
    "root.text.Part 1..3.1": "With regard to <i>raâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראה</span>), this is well-known to everyone. It is said, for example: <i>And he saw</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וירא</span>), <i>and behold, there was a well in the field</i> (Genesis, 29, 2), where it concerns the vision of the eye; however, in these words: <i>And my heart saw</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראה</span>) <i>much wisdom and knowledge</i> (Ecclesiastes, 1, 16), it concerns an intellect perception.",
    "root.text.Part 1..3.2": "It is in this metaphorical sense that the verb <i>raâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראה</span>) must be understood whenever it is applied to God, as for example in these passages: <i>I</i> <small>saw</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראיתי</span>) <i>the Lord</i> (I Kings, 22, 19); <i>And the Lord appeared</i> <small>to him</small> (Genesis, 18, 1); <i>And God </i> <i>saw</i> <small>that it was good</small> (Genesis, 1, <i>passim</i>); <i>Show me</i> <i>thy glory</i> (Exodus, 33, 18); <small>And they </small> <i>saw</i> <i>the God of Israel</i> (<small>Ibid.</small>, 24, 10). In all these instances, it is a matter of intellectual perception, and not at all of the vision of the eye; for the eyes only perceive (what is) corporeal and (only) from a certain side[[fn:77]], and with this, some accidents of the body, such as its colours, its geometric figure, etc., and God, on His part, does not perceive by means of an instrument, as will be explained (later).",
    "root.text.Part 1..3.3": "Similarly, <i>hibbît</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הביט</span>) is used in the sense of: <i>looking</i> at a thing with the eye; for example: <i>Do not look</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">אל תבט</span> <i>behind thee</i> (Genesis, 19, 17); <i>And his wife looked</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">ותבט</span>, <i>being behind him</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 26); <i>And he shall look</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">ונבט</span> <i>toward the earth</i> (Isaiah, 5, 30). But it is used metaphorically for theintuition of the mind approaching the consideration of a thing in order to understand it, as in this passage: <i>No iniquity is seen</i> <i>לא הביט</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">in Jacob</span> (Numbers, 23, 21); for iniquity is not seen with the eye. It is the same with these words: <i>And they looked</i> <i>והביטו</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">after Moses</span> (Exodus, 33, 8); for, according to what the doctors say, they express the same idea, and they state that they (the Israelites) were watching his actions and words and examining them[[fn:78]]. The words: <i>Look</i> <i>הבט</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">toward the heaven</span> (Genesis, 15, 5), have the same meaning; for this took place in a prophetic vision. And (in general) the verb <i>הביט</i> (to look) has this metaphorical sense whenever it is applied to God; for example: … <span dir=\"rtl\">to look</span> <i>מהביט</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">toward God</span> (Exodus, 3, 6); <i>And he contemplates</i> <i>יביט</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">the figure of God</span> (Numbers, 12, 8); <i>And thou canst not look</i> <i>והביט</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">at iniquity</span> (Habakkuk, 1, 13).",
    "root.text.Part 1..3.4": "Similarly, ’<i>hazâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חזה</span>) applies to the vision of the eye; for example: <i>And our eyes shall see</i> <i>ותחז</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">the fall of Zion</span> (Micah, 4, 11); and it is used metaphorically for the perception of the heart, for example: … <i>he saw</i> <i>חזה</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">upon Judah and Jerusalem</span> (Isaiah, 1, 1); … <i>the word of the Lord to Abram</i> <i>במחזה</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">in a vision</span> (Genesis, 15, 1); and according to this metaphor it has been said: <i>And they saw</i> <i>ויחזו</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">God</span> (Exodus, 24, 11). You must thoroughly understand this.",
    "root.text.Part 1..4.0": "The prince of philosophers[[fn:79]], in undertaking the investigation and demonstration of a most profound matter, expresses himself, by way of apology, in terms whose sense is: that the reader of his writings ought not to tax him with impudence or accuse him of speaking temerariously and precipitously on matters of which he is ignorant; but rather, he ought to perceive in him only the passion and zeal with which he is animated to produce and impart true opinions, in so far as this lies within the power of man.",
    "root.text.Part 1..4.1": "We say likewise that man ought not to approach this grave and important subject with precipitance, without having first exercised himself in the sciences and knowledge, and without having corrected his manners with the greatest care and slain his desires and passions dependent on the imaginativeimaginative faculty. It is only after having acquired the knowledge of true and certain axioms[[fn:80]], after having learned the rules of syllogism and demonstration, as well as the manner of preserving oneself from the errors of the intellect, that he will be able to undertake the investigation of this subject. He ought not to decide anything according to a first opinion that may occur to him, nor allow his thoughts to proceed directly, directing them resolutely towards the knowledge of God; but he ought to approach it with modesty and reserve, and pause occasionally, in order to advance gradually.",
    "root.text.Part 1..4.2": "It is in this sense that it was said: <i>And Moses hid his face, for he was afraid to look upon God</i> (Exodus 3:6)—where regard must also be had[[fn:81]] for what the literal sense indicates, namely, that he was afraid to look at the resplendent light (of the burning bush), not that the eyes could perceive the Divinity [may He be exalted and raised far above all imperfection!]. Moses earned praise for this, and the Most High bestowed His goodness and favor upon him to such an extent that it could later be said of him: <i>And the figure of God he shall behold</i> (Numbers 12:8); for the sages say that this was a reward for having initially <i>hidden his face so as not to look upon God</i>[[fn:82]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..4.3": "But as regards <i>the elect among the sons of Israel</i> (Exodus, 24, 11), they acted with precipitation, giving free rein to their thoughts; they perceived (the divinity), but in an imperfect manner. This is why it is said of them: <i>And they saw the God of Israel, and under His feet, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 10), and one does not simply say: <i>And they saw the God of Israel;</i> for the entire phrase has no other purpose than to criticise their <i>vision</i>, and not to describe how they had seen. Thus, one has only criticised the form under which they had perceived (God) and which was tainted with corporeality, which was the necessary result of the precipitation they had shown before perfecting themselves. They had deserved destruction[[fn:83]]; but Moses having interceded for them, they were granted a reprieve until they were burned at Tab’érâ and Nadab and Abihou were burned in the <i>tent of meeting</i>, as reported by the true tradition[[fn:84]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..4.4": "If this was the case with them, all the more reason that inferior men like us and those below us should first strive to occupy themselves with the perfection of their preparatory knowledge and acquire preliminary principles that can purify the understanding of its stain, which consists in errors, and then they can advance[[fn:85]] to contemplate the holy divine majesty; for: <i>The priests also who approach the Lord shall sanctify themselves, lest the Lord break out among them</i> (Exodus, 19, 22). Already Solomon has recommended the greatest caution to the man who desires to attain the degree in question; and, using an image, he has given this warning: <i>Keep thy foot when thou goest to the house of God</i> (Ecclesiastes, 4, 17)..",
    "root.text.Part 1..4.5": "I now return to complete what I had begun to explain, and I say that, <i>the elect among the sons of Israel</i> having made mistakes in their perception, their actions also were troubled by it, and they inclined towards corporeal things, through the vice of their perception; this is why (the Scripture) says: <i>And they saw God, and they ate and drank</i> (Exodus, 24, 11)[[fn:86]].— As for the end of the verse (cited above)[[fn:87]], I mean the words: <i>And under His feet there was as it were a work of the splendour of the sapphire, etc.</i>, it will be explained in a few chapters of this treatise[[fn:88]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..4.6": "In short, we have as our aim to establish that each time the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ראה</span>, or <span dir=\"rtl\">חזה</span>, or <span dir=\"rtl\">הביט</span>, is employed in the sense in question, it pertains to an intellectual perception, and not to the vision of the eye; for the Most High is not a being that eyes can perceive.",
    "root.text.Part 1..4.7": "If, however, there be such a narrow-minded man who does not wish to attain to the degree to which we desire to ascend, and who admits that all these words, employed in the sense in question, indicate the sensible perception of certain created lights <i>créées</i>, whether angels or something else, there is no harm in this.",
    "root.text.Part 1..5.0": "<i>Isch</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">איש</span>) and <i>ischâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אשה</span>) are names originally employed to denoteman andwoman, and subsequently borrowed to signify the male and female of all other species of animals. It has been said, for example: <i>Of all the clean quadrupeds thou shalt take seven couples</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">איש ואשתו</span>, <i>themale and hisfemale </i> (Genesis, 7, 2); it is as if it had been said, the male and the female. Subsequently, the name of <i>ischâ</i> has been employed metaphorically for anything destined and ready to be joined to another thing; for example: <i>The five curtains shall be joined</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">אשה אל אחותה</span> <i>one to another</i> (Exodus, 26, 3)[[fn:89]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..5.1": "It is clear from this that <span dir=\"rtl\">אחות</span> (sister) and <span dir=\"rtl\">אח</span> (brother) also, with regard to the figurative sense[[fn:90]], are employed as homonyms, similar to <span dir=\"rtl\">איש</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">אשה</span>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..6.0": "<i>Yalad</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ילד</span>). — What is understood by this word is known; it signifies to beget; e.g.: <i>And they shall have begotten</i> (<i>וילדו</i>) <span dir=\"rtl\">sons</span> (Deuteronomy, 21:15). Subsequently, this word was employed figuratively for the production of natural things; e.g.: <i>Before the mountains were brought forth</i> (<i>ילדו</i>) (Psalm 90:2), and it was also used in the sense of to cause to sprout, (speaking of) what the earth causes to sprout, by comparison with begetting; e.g.: … <span dir=\"rtl\">that she has made it germinate</span> (<i>והולידה</i>) <i>and has caused it to sprout</i> (Isaiah, 55:10). It was also applied to the events of time, as if they were things that are born; e.g.: <span dir=\"rtl\">For thou knowest not what the day shall bring forth</span> (<i>ילד</i>) <i>the day</i> (Proverbs, 17:1). Finally, it was applied to what occurs in thoughts and to what they produce in terms of ideas and opinions, as it was said: <span dir=\"rtl\">And he shall bring forth</span> (<i>וילד</i>) <i>a lie</i> (Psalm 7:15), and it is in this sense that it was said: <span dir=\"rtl\">And they are content with the children</span> (<i>בילדי</i>) <i>of strangers</i> (Isaiah, 2:6), that is to say, they are content[[fn:91]] with their opinions, as Jonathan ben-Usiel said in the version of this passage: <span dir=\"rtl\">And they follow the laws of the nations.</span>",
    "root.text.Part 1..6.1": "It is in this same sense that one who has taught something to a person and has given him an idea can be considered as having begotten that person, being himself the author of that idea; and it is in this sense also that the disciples of the prophets were called <span dir=\"rtl\">בני הנביאים</span>, <i>sons of the prophets</i>, as we shall explain when speaking of the homonymy of the substantive <span dir=\"rtl\">בן</span>, <i>son</i>[[fn:92]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..6.2": "It is according to this metaphor that it has been said of Adam: <i>And Adam lived a hundred and thirty years, and begat a son in his own likeness, after his image</i> (Genesis, 5, 3); for it has already been stated previously[[fn:93]] what is meant by <i>the image</i> of Adam and <i>his likeness</i>. This is because all the children he had previously did not possess (what constitutes) the human form in reality, which is called <i>the image</i> of Adam and <i>his likeness</i>, and with regard to which it is said (that he was created) <i>in the image of God and after his likeness</i>. But with regard to <i>Seth</i>, (Adam) having instructed him and given him understanding, so that he arrived at human perfection, it has been said of him: <i>And he</i> (Adam) <i>begat a son in his own likeness, after his image</i>. You know that whoever has not obtained this <i>form</i> which we have explained is not a man, but an animal having the figure of a man and his lineaments; but he has the faculty that other animals do not possess, of doing all sorts of harm and producing evils. For the reflection and thought which, in him, were intended to enable him to obtain a perfection he has not obtained, he employs in all sorts of cunning producing evils and giving rise to harm; he is, therefore, as it were, something that resembles a man or counterfeits him. Such were the sons of Adam prior to <i>Seth;</i> this is why it has been said in the <i>Midrash</i>[[fn:94]]: 'Adam, during the hundred and thirty years he was reproved, begat <i>spirits</i>', that is to say, <i>demons;</i> but when he had obtained his grace, he begat his like, that is to say, <i>in his likeness, after his image</i>. This is what is expressed by these words: <i>And Adam lived a hundred and thirty years, and begat a son in his own likeness, after his image</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..7.0": "<i>Makôm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מקום</span>). — This name is applied primarily to a particular and common <i>place</i>[[fn:95]]; subsequently, the language extended its usage and made it a term designating the degree and rank of a person, I mean their perfection in any matter whatsoever, such that one says: So-and-so is in such a <i>place</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מקום</span>) in such a thing (that is, having reached such a degree). You are familiar with the frequent usage made of it in our language when saying: <i>To fill the place</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מקום</span>) <i>of his fathers</i>, e.g.: \"He filled the <i>place</i> of his fathers in science or in piety,\" and further saying: \"The discussion remains in the same <i>place</i>,\" that is, at the same degree. It is by this type of metaphor that it was said: <i>Blessed be the glory of the Eternal from His place</i> (Ezek. 3:12), that is to say, according to the elevated rank He occupies in the universe[[fn:96]]. Likewise, whenever the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מקום</span> (place) is used in reference to God, it signifies nothing other than the rank of His existence, which has no equal or likeness, as will be demonstrated.",
    "root.text.Part 1..7.1": "Know that whenever we explain, in this treatise, the homonymy of a certain name, we do not have as our sole purpose to draw attention to what we mention in the chapter itself, but we wish to open a door and draw your attention to the various meanings of the name in question, which are useful with regard to our purpose, and not with regard to the purpose of those who speak any common language[[fn:97]]. It is for you to examine the prophetic books and the other books composed by the wise, to consider all the names that are employed in them, and to take each homonymous name in one of the senses that may suit it with regard to the discourse (in which it is found). What we have just said is the key to this treatise and others (of our writings).",
    "root.text.Part 1..7.2": "Thus, e.g., (considering) the explanation we have given here of the meaning of <i>makôm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מקום</span>) in the passage: <i>Let the glory of the Eternal be praised in His place</i>, you will know that this same meaning is that of <i>makôm</i> in the passage: <i>Behold, a place</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מקום</span>) <i>beside me</i> (Exodus, 33, 21), that is to say, a degree of speculation, of penetration by means of the intellect, and not of penetration by means of the eye, having regard at the same time to <i>the spot</i> on the mountain to which allusion is made and where the isolation (of Moses) took place to attain perfection.",
    "root.text.Part 1..8.0": "<i>Kissé</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כסא</span>) is primitively employed in the language (Hebrew) as the name of the <i>throne;</i> and, as the throne is occupied only by people of distinction and greatness, such as kings, and, consequently, it is a visible thing[[fn:98]], indicating the greatness of him who has been deemed worthy of it, his distinction and lofty position, the sanctuary has been called <i>Kissé</i> (throne), because it indicates the greatness of Him who has manifested Himself therein and who has caused His light and glory to descend upon it. Thus (the prophet) said: <i>A throne of glory, exalted, from the beginning, etc.</i> (Jeremiah, 17, 12). It is also in the same sense that the heaven has been called <i>Kissé</i> (throne); for him who knows[[fn:99]] and contemplates it, it indicates the greatness of Him who has brought it into existence, who sets it in motion, and who, by the good that emanates from it, governs the lower world. One reads, e.g.: <i>Thus says the Eternal, the heaven is my throne</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כסאי</span>), <i>etc.</i> (Isaiah, 66, 1), that is to say, it indicates my existence, my greatness, and my power, just as the throne indicates the greatness of him who has been deemed worthy of it[[fn:100]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..8.1": "This is what those who seek the truth must believe, and not that there is a body upon which God rises; — how elevated (above such a thought) He is!—For it will be demonstrated to you that the Most High is incorporeal, and how could He take a place or rest upon a body? But the matter is as we have noted, namely, that every place which God has ennobled and distinguished by His light and radiance, such as the sanctuary or the heaven, is called <i>Kissé</i> (throne).",
    "root.text.Part 1..8.2": "If the language has extended the usage of this word by saying: <i>Car la main</i> (<i>est placée</i>) <i>sur le trône</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כם</span>) <i>de Dieu</i> (Exodus, 17, 16)[[fn:101]], it is still a qualification of His grandeur and majesty, a thing that must not be represented as being outside of His essence, nor as one of His creatures, so that God would exist at one time without the throne and at another time with the throne. This would, without a doubt, be an impious belief; for (the prophet) has said clearly: <i>Toi, Éternel, tu résides éternellement, ton trône</i> (<i>reste</i>) <i>de génération en génération</i> (Lamentations, 5, 19), which indicates that (the throne) is a thing inseparable from Him. Thus, in this passage and in all the others similar to it, one wants to designate by <span dir=\"rtl\">כםא</span> (throne) the majesty and grandeur of God, which are not something outside of His essence, as will be explained in a few chapters of this treatise[[fn:102]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..9.0": "We have already stated that every time we speak, in this treatise, of one of the homonymous names, our intention is not to mention all the senses in which this name is employed, — for this is not a treatise on language; — but we mention those of the various senses which we require for our purpose, nothing more.",
    "root.text.Part 1..9.1": "Among these words (homonyms) are <i>yarad</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ירד</span>) and <i>’alâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עלה</span>); for these two words are used in the Hebrew language in the sense ofdescendere andascendere. When a body is transported from one place to another lower, one says <i>ירד</i> (descendere), and when it is transported from one place to another higher than that one[[fn:103]], one says <i>עלה</i> (ascendere). Then these two words have been applied metaphorically to illustration and grandeur; so that, when a man's rank has been lowered, one says <span dir=\"rtl\">ירד</span> (descendere), and when his rank has been elevated in illustration, one says <span dir=\"rtl\">עלה</span> (ascendere). This is how the Most High has said: <span dir=\"rtl\">The stranger who is in your midst willascendere</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יעלה</span>) <i>higher and higher above you, and you willdescendere</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תרד</span>) <i>lower and lower</i> (Deuteronomy, 28, 43). It has also been said (using derivatives of <span dir=\"rtl\">עלה</span>): <i>And the Lord your God will place you above</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עליון</span>) <i>all the nations of the earth</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">Ibid.</span>, <i>V</i>. 1); and elsewhere: <i>And the Lord elevated Solomon very high</i> (<small>למעלה</small>) (I Chronicles, 29, 25). You also know that the doctors often use this expression: «One must makeascendere the sacred things, but not make themdescendere»[[fn:104]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..9.2": "One still expresses oneself in the same manner (when speaking of thought): when the reflection of man is lowered and his thought turns towards a very vile thing, one says that <i>he has descended</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ירד</span>), and likewise when his thought turns towards something elevated and sublime, one says: <i>he has ascended</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עלה</span>) <span dir=\"ltr\">[[fn:105]]</span>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..9.3": "Now, as we find ourselves, we men, in the most inferior place of creation and at the lowest degree in relation to the surrounding sphere[[fn:106]], whilst God is at the highest degree by the reality of existence, majesty, and grandeur, and not by an elevation of place, —the Most High, having wished to bring forth from Himself knowledge and to cause revelation to emanate upon some amongst us, employed, when speaking of the revelation descending upon the prophet and of the entrance of divine majesty into a place, the expression of <i>descending</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ירד</span>); and, when speaking of the cessation of this state of prophetic inspiration in an individual or of the divine majesty withdrawing from a place, He employed the expression of <i>ascending</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עלה</span>).",
    "root.text.Part 1..9.4": "Thus, whenever you find the expressions of <i>descending</i> and of <i>ascending</i> relating to the Creator, they can only be taken in the aforementioned signification.",
    "root.text.Part 1..9.5": "Similarly, when it comes to the arrival of a catastrophe in a nation or in a country, due to the eternal will of God,—where the prophetic books, before describing this calamity, first say that God, after having visited the actions of these people, sent down upon them the punishment,—the expressiondescendere to descend is also employed for this purpose;<i>descendre;</i> for man is too insignificant for his actions to be visited, so that he might suffer the penalty, were it not for the will (of God)[[fn:107]]. This has been clearly indicated in the prophetic books, where it is said: <i>What is man, that thou art mindful of him, and the son of man, that thou visitest him? etc.</i> (Ps. 8, 5), which is an allusion to the subject in question.",
    "root.text.Part 1..9.6": "Thus, (I say) the expressiondescendere to descend has been employed in this regard;<i>descendre;</i> e.g.: <i>Come, let us go down</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נרדה</span>) <i>and confound their language</i> (Genesis, 11, 7); <i>And God went down</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וירד</span>) <i>to see</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 5); <i>I will go down</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ארדה</span>) <i>and see</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 18, 21). The meaning of all this is the arrival of punishment upon the people of this world.",
    "root.text.Part 1..9.7": "As for the previous sense, I mean that of revelation (divine) and ennoblement, it occurs frequently; e.g.: <i>I will go down</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וירדתי</span>) <i>and speak to thee</i> (Numbers, 11, 17); <i>And the Lord went down</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וירד</span>) <i>upon Mount Sinai</i> (Exodus, 19, 20); <i>The Lord will go down</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ירד</span>) <i>before the eyes of all the people</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 11); <i>And God went up</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויעל</span>) <i>from him</i> (Genesis, 35, 13); <i>And God went up</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויעל</span>) <i>from Abraham</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 17, 22).",
    "root.text.Part 1..9.8": "As for these words: <i>And Moses went up</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עלה</span>) <i>towards God</i> (Exodus, 19, 3), they possess the third sense[[fn:108]], whilst simultaneously stating that he <i>went up to the summit of the mountain</i> upon which the created light descended[[fn:109]]; (but they do not signify) in any manner that God the Most High has a place whereupon one may ascend or descend. How elevated He is above such imaginings of the ignorant!",
    "root.text.Part 1..10.0": "<i>Yaschab</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ישב</span>). — The primitive acceptation of this word in our language is that of <i>being seated;</i> e.g.: <i>And Elijah the priest was seated</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ישב</span>) <i>upon the seat</i> (I Sam., 1, 9). And, as the person seated is found established in the most perfect state of rest and stability, this word has been applied metaphorically to any stable and fixed state which does not change. Thus, in promising to Jerusalem, at its highest degree (of prosperity), duration and stability, it is expressed: <i>And she shall be elevated and seated</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וישבה</span>) <i>in her place</i> (Zechariah, 14, 10). And elsewhere it is said: <i>He seats</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מושיבי</span>) <i>the barren woman in the house</i> (Ps. 113, 9), which signifies: He establishes her in a stable manner[[fn:110]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..10.1": "It is in this latter sense that it has been said of God: <i>Thou, O Lord, dost reside</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תשב</span>) <i>eternally</i> (Lament., 5, 19); <i>Thou who residest</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">היושבי</span>) <i>in heaven</i> (Ps. 123, 1); <i>He who resids</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יושב</span>) <i>in heaven</i> (Ps. 2, 4), that is to say, He who is perpetual and stable, and who is not subject to any species of change, neither change of essence, nor change with respect to any state which would be outside of His essence, nor, finally, change with respect to His relation with anything else; for there is no relation between Him and other things such that He could suffer a change in this relation, as shall be explained[[fn:111]]. And thereby it is perfectly established that He cannot change at all, in any manner whatsoever, as He has clearly stated: <i>For I, the Lord, do not change</i> (Malach., 3, 6), that is to say, by no change[[fn:112]]; and it is this idea which is expressed by the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ישב</span> (to be seated) when it is applied to God.",
    "root.text.Part 1..10.2": "But in the majority of passages, he is only associated with the heavens, because the heavens are a thing in which there is neither change nor variation, I mean to say that their individuals [[fn:113]] are not subject to change, as are the individuals in earthly things which are born and perish [[fn:114]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..10.3": "Similarly, when God is placed in this relation [expressed by homonymy[[fn:115]]] with the <i>species</i> of beings subject to birth and destruction, it is also said of Him that He <i>is seated</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יושב</span>); for these species are perpetual, well-ordered, and of a stable existence like that of the individuals of the heavens. Thus, for example, it has been said: <i>The One who is seated</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">היושב</span>) <i>above the circle of the earth</i> (Isaiah, 40, 22), which means the One who is perpetual and stable, above the circuit of the earth, or its <i>turn</i>, alluding to the things that are born <i>in turn</i>[[fn:116]];",
    "root.text.Part 1..10.4": "and it has been said again: <i>The Lord was seated</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ישב</span>) <i>at the flood</i> (Ps. 29, 10), that is to say, when the things of the earth changed and perished, there was no change in God's relation, but this relation that He has with the thing[[fn:117]], whether it is born or perishes, is a single stable and fixed relation; for it is a relation to the species of beings, and not to their individuals. Pay close attention that whenever you find the expression of <i>being seated</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ישב</span>) applied to God, it will be in the sense in question.",
    "root.text.Part 1..11.0": "<i>Koum</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">קום</span>) is a homonym, and one of its meanings[[fn:118]] is <i>to be upright</i>, opposed to <i>to be seated;</i> e.g.: <i>And he did not stand upright</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ולא קם</span>) <i>and did not stir before him</i> (Esther, 5, 9). It also contains the sense of <i>stability</i> and of <i>confirmation</i>, or <i>affirmation;</i> e.g.: <i>May the Lord confirm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יקם</span>) <i>His word</i> (I Sam., 1, 23); <i>And the field of Ephron remained acquired</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויקם</span>) (Genesis, 23, 17); <i>The house which is in the city shall remain acquired</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וקם</span>) (Leviticus, 25, 30); <i>And the kingdom of Israel shall remain</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וקמה</span>) <i>in thy hand</i> (I Sam., 24, 21). It is always in this sense that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קום</span> is spoken of God; e.g.: <i>Now I shall be upright</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אקום</span>), <i>says the Lord</i> (Ps. 12, 6; Isaiah, 33, 10), which means, now I shall confirm my order, my promise, and my threat; <i>Thou shalt be upright</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תקום</span>), <i>thou shalt have pity on Sion</i> (Ps. 114, 14), that is to say, thou shalt confirm the promise of commiseration which thou hast made to it.",
    "root.text.Part 1..11.1": "And, as he who is determined to do a thing is drawn towards action in <i>being upright</i>, it is said of anyone who feels excited to a thing that he <i>is upright;</i> e.g.: <i>For my son has excited</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הקים</span>, has made upright) <i>my servant against me</i> (I Sam., 22, 8). This latter meaning also applies metaphorically to the execution of the decree of destruction pronounced by God against people who have deserved punishment; e.g.: <i>And I shall be upright</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וקמתי</span>) <i>against the house of Jeroboam</i> (Amos, 7, 9); <i>And he shall be upright</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וקם</span>) <i>against the house of the wicked</i> (Isaiah, 21, 2). It may be that the words: «Now I shall be <i>upright</i>» (cited above) have this same meaning; likewise these words: «Thou <i>shalt be upright</i>, thou shalt have pity on Sion», that is to say, thou shalt rise against its enemies.",
    "root.text.Part 1..11.2": "It is this sense which is expressed in many passages, and it cannot be a question there[[fn:119]] of <i>being upright</i> or of <i>being seated</i>, which would be unworthy of the divinity[[fn:120]]. «Above, say the doctors[[fn:121]], there is no question of <i>being seated</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ישיבה</span>), nor of <i>being upright</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עמידה</span>)»; for <i>’amad</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עמד</span>) is used in the sense of <i>kâm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">קם</span>).",
    "root.text.Part 1..12.0": "<i>’Amad</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עמד</span>) is a homonym which has (firstly) the meaning ofstare, to stand; e.g.: When he stood </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בעמדו</span>) <i>before Pharaoh</i> (Genesis, 41, 46); When Moses and Samuel would stand </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יעמד</span>) <i>before me</i> (Jeremiah, 15, 1); And he stood </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עמד</span>) <i>beside them</i> (Genesis, 18, 8). It has (subsequently) the meaning of to abstain, to ceasecessare; e.g.: For they abstained </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עמדו</span>) <i>and did not answer again</i> (Job, 32, 16); And she ceased </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ותעמד</span>) <i>from bearing children</i> (Genesis, 29, 35). It also has the meaning of to be stable, to endureconservare, subsistere; e.g.: So that they may be preserved </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יעמדו</span>) <i>for a long time</i> (Jeremiah, 32, 14); You shall be able to endure </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עמוד</span>) (Exodus, 18, 23); Its flavor remained </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עמד</span>) <i>in it</i> (Jeremiah, 48, 11), it continued to endure and be preserved; And its righteousness endures </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עומדת</span>) <i>forever</i> (Psalm 111, 3), it is stable and permanent. Whenever the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">עמד</span> is applied to God, it is in this last sense; e.g.: And his feet will stand </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ועמדו</span>), <i>on that day, on the Mount of Olives</i> (Zechariah, 14, 4), his causes, I mean the (events) caused by him, will endure, will be confirmed </i>. This will be further explained when we discuss the homonymy of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">רגל</span> (foot)[[fn:122]]. It is in this sense that we must understand the words of God addressed to Moses[[fn:123]]: And you, stand </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עמד</span>) <i>here, beside me</i> (Deuteronomy, 5, 28), and (these words of Moses): I stood </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אנכי עמד</span>) <i>between the Lord and you</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 5)[[fn:124]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..13.0": "As for the homonymy of the word <i>Adam</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אדם</span>), it is primarily the name of the first man, a derived name[[fn:125]] which, according to the text (of Scripture), comes from <i>adamâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אדמה</span>, earth)[[fn:126]]; subsequently, it is the name of the species; e.g.: <i>My spirit shall not always strive with man</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">באדם</span>) (Genesis 6:3); <i>Who knows if the spirit of the sons of man</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בני האדם</span>), <i>etc</i>. (Ecclesiastes 3:21); <i>The advantage of man</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">האדם</span>) <i>over the beast is nothing</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 19). It is also a name for designating the multitude, I mean the common folk to the exclusion of distinguished men; e.g.: <i>Both low-born men</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בני אדם</span>) <i>and men of high degree</i> (Ps. 49:3).",
    "root.text.Part 1..13.1": "In this third sense (it is also found in the following verses): <i>And the sons of the Elohim</i>[[fn:127]] <i>saw the daughters of</i> <small>MAN</small> (Genesis, 6, 2); <i>Truly, you will die like</i> <small>MAN</small> <i>vulgar</i> (Psalm 82, 7).",
    "root.text.Part 1..14.0": "<i>Naçab</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נצב</span>) or <i>yaçab</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יצב</span>). — Although these two roots are different, they have, as you know, the same meaning in all their conjugational forms.",
    "root.text.Part 1..14.1": "It is a homonym which sometimes has the meaning of <i>standing upright, rising;</i> e.g.: <i>And his sister stood</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ותתצב</span>) <i>from afar</i> (Exodus, 2, 4); <i>The kings of the earth rise</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יתיצבו</span>) (Ps. II. 2); …. <i>went out, placing themselves upright</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נצבים</span>) (Numbers, 16, 27); sometimes that of <i>being stable, permanent;</i> e.g.: <i>Your word stands</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נצב</span>) <i>in the heaven</i> (Ps. 119, 89), meaning <i>stable</i> and <i>permanent</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..14.2": "Every time this word is used in relation to the Creator, it has the latter meaning; e.g.: <i>And behold the Lord standing</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נצב</span>) <i>above</i> (Genesis, 28, 13), being stable and permanent <i>above it</i>, that is to say above the ladder whose one end is in heaven and the other on earth, and where all those who ascend [[fn:128]] and climb, in order to perceive the one who is above necessarily [[fn:129]]; for He is stable and permanent at the head of the ladder.",
    "root.text.Part 1..14.3": "It is clear, furthermore, that, if I say here <i>above it</i>, it is with respect to the allegory that has been employed [[fn:130]]. The <i>messengers of God</i> are the prophets [[fn:131]], thus called clearly (in these passages): <i>And He sent a messenger</i> (Numbers, 20, 16); <i>And a messenger of the Lord went up from Gilgal to Bochim</i> (Judges, 2, 1). And how justly has it been expressed in saying <i>went up and came down</i>, (that is to say, by using) the verb <i>to go up</i> before the verb <i>to come down</i>, for (the prophet), after having gone up and attained certain degrees of the ladder, comes down afterwards with what he has learned to guide the inhabitants of the earth and instruct them, which is designated by the verb <i>to come down</i>, as we have explained [[fn:132]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..14.4": "I return to our subject, namely that (in the passage in question), <span dir=\"rtl\">נצב</span> means <i>stable, perpetual, permanent</i>, and not <i>to stand</i> like a body. In the same sense also (this passage must be explained): <i>And you shall stand</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ונצבת</span>) <i>upon the rock</i> (Exodus, 33, 21) [[fn:133]]; for you have already understood that <i>naçab</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נצב</span>) and <i>’amad</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עמד</span>) have the same meaning [[fn:134]] in this regard, and indeed God has said: <i>Behold, I will stand</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עומד</span>) <i>before you upon the rock, at Horeb</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 17, 6) [[fn:135]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..15.0": "<i>Çour</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צור</span>) is a homonym which first means <i>rock</i> [[fn:136]]; for example: <i>You shall strike the rock</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צור</span>) (Exodus, 17, 6), then (in general) <i>hard stone</i>, like a pebble; for example: <i>Knives of stone</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צורים</span>) (Joshua, 5, 2); finally, it is the name of the mine from which the ore is extracted; for example: <i>Look to the rock</i> (or the <i>mine</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">צור</span>) <i>from which you were hewn</i> (Isaiah, 51, 1). In the latter sense, this name has been used figuratively to (designate) the stock and principle of all things; that is why (the prophet), after having said: <i>Look to the rock from which you were hewn</i> adds: <i>Look to Abraham, your father</i>, etc. (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 2), as if he were explaining by saying: 'The rock from which you were hewn is Abraham, your father; you must therefore follow in his footsteps, embrace his religion, and adopt his customs; for the nature of the mine must be found in what has been extracted from it.'",
    "root.text.Part 1..15.1": "It is with respect to this latter sense that God has been called <span dir=\"rtl\">צור</span> (rock); for He is the principle and efficient cause of all that is outside of Him. It has been said, e.g.: <i>The rock!</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הצור</span>) <i>His work is perfect</i> (Deuter., 32, 4); <i>You forget the rock that begat you</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 18); <i>Their rock</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צורם</span>) <i>has sold them</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 30); <i>And there is no rock like our God</i> (I Sam., 2, 2); <i>The eternal rock</i> (Isaiah, 26, 4). (Similarly, the words): <i>And you shall stand upon the rock</i> (Exodus, 33, 21) (signify): 'Rest and insist upon this consideration, that God is the principle (of all things), for it is the entrance by which you shall arrive at Him', as we have explained with regard to the words[[fn:137]]: <i>Behold, a place is by me</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>).",
    "root.text.Part 1..16.0": "One must not believe that it is of metaphysical science alone that one has been niggardly[[fn:138]] towards the common people, for it has been the same with the greater part of physical science[[fn:139]], and we have already cited on several occasions these words: 'Nor (shall one expound) the <i>Ma’asé beréschîth</i> before two persons'[[fn:140]]. This (was done) not only among theologians[[fn:141]], but also among philosophers; and the pagan sages of antiquity expressed themselves on the principles of things in an obscure and enigmatic manner. Thus,Plato et alii Plato and others before him called matter the <i>feminine</i>, and form the <i>masculine</i>[[fn:142]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..16.1": "[You know that the principles of beings that are born and perish are three in number:materia, forma, et privatio matter, form, and particular privation <i>particular</i>[[fn:143]], which is always joined to matter; for, if the latter were not accompanied by privation, it would not be affected by form, and in this manner privation is one of the principles. When form arrives, this privation (particular), I mean the privation of this form that has arrived, ceases, and another privation is joined (to matter), and so on, as is explained in physics[[fn:144]].]",
    "root.text.Part 1..16.2": "If, therefore, those who had nothing to lose by explaining themselves clearly, made use, in their teaching, of figurative names and employed images, with greater reason must we, men of religion, avoid stating clearly things the intellect of which is difficult [[fn:145]] for the common people, or (with regard to which) they imagine the truth in a sense contrary to that which we have in view [[fn:146]]. One must also imbue oneself with this notion.",
    "root.text.Part 1..17.0": "<i>Karab</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">קרב</span>), <i>naga’</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נגע</span>) and <i>nagasch</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נגש</span>). — These three words sometimes have the meaning <i>of approaching</i> (<i>touching</i>), <i>drawing near</i> in space, and sometimes they express the union of knowledge with the thing known, (a union) which is compared, in some way, to one body <i>approaching</i> another body. — As for the primitive meaning of <i>karab</i>, which is that of <i>drawing near</i> in space (here are examples): <i>When he drew near</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">קרב</span>) <i>to the camp</i> (Exodus, 32, 19); <i>And Pharaoh drew near</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הקריב</span>) (<i>Ibid.</i>, 14, 10). <i>Naga</i>’ primitively expresses the bringing into contact of one body with another; for example: <i>She touched</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ותנע</span>) <i>his feet</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 4, 25); <i>He touched</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויגע</span>) <i>my mouth</i> (Isaiah, 6, 7). The primitive meaning of <i>nagasch</i> is <i>to advance</i> towards a person, <i>to move</i> towards him; for example: <i>And Judah advanced</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויגש</span>) <i>towards him</i> (Genesis, 44, 18).",
    "root.text.Part 1..17.1": "— The second meaning of these three words expresses a union through knowledge, a drawing near through perception, and not a local drawing near. <i>Naga’</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נגע</span>) was used in the sense of <i>union through knowledge</i> when it was said: <i>For his judgment has touched</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נגע</span>) <i>up to heaven</i>[[fn:147]] (Jeremiah, 51, 9). It was said, using <i>karab</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">קרב</span>): <i>And the cause which will be too difficult for you, you shall present</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תקריבון</span>) <i>to me</i>, that is to say, you will make me <i>know</i>; thus (this verb) was used in the sense of: to make <i>known</i> that which must be <i>known</i>. It was said, using <i>nagasch</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נגש</span>): <i>And Abraham advanced</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויגש</span>) <i>and said</i> (Genesis, 18, 23); for he was then in a state of prophetic vision and slumber, as will be explained[[fn:148]]. (Elsewhere it is said): <i>Since this people, in approaching</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נגש</span>), <i>has honored me with its mouth and lips</i> (Isaiah, 29, 13).",
    "root.text.Part 1..17.2": "Every time one encounters in prophetic books the expression of <i>karab</i> or of <i>nagasch</i> (applying to a relation) between God and any creature whatsoever, it is always in the latter sense; for God is not a body, as will be demonstrated in this treatise, and therefore the Most High does not approach anything, nor does He draw near to anything, and nothing draws near to Him nor does He approach anything; for, by excluding corporeality, one excludes space, and it is not a question of proximity, access, distance, union, separation, contact, or succession.",
    "root.text.Part 1..17.3": "I do not think thou hast any doubt (in this regard), and there shall be nothing obscure to thee in these passages: <i>The Lord is near to all who call upon Him</i> (Ps. 145, 18); <i>They desire to draw near to God</i> (Isaiah, 58, 2); <i>To draw near to God is my happiness</i> (Ps. 73, 28); for in all these passages it is a matter of drawing near through knowledge, that is to say, through scientific perception, and not through local approach. It is the same in these passages: (<i>God</i>) <i>near to Him</i> (Deuteronomy, 4, 7); <i>Draw near and hearken</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 5, 27); <i>And Moses shall approach alone unto the Lord, but they shall not approach</i> (Exodus, 24, 2).",
    "root.text.Part 1..17.4": "However, if thou desirest to understand by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ונגש</span>, <i>shall approach</i>, applied to Moses, that he could approach that place on the mountain where the light descended, that is to say, <i>the glory of the Lord</i>, thou art at liberty to do so; only thou must adhere to this principle, that no matter whether the individual be in the centre of the earth or at the summit of the ninth sphere[[fn:149]], — if this were possible, — he is not thereby farther from God, and there he is not nearer to Him; but one is near to God in perceiving Him, and he who ignores Him is far from Him. There is, in this regard, a great variety of gradations[[fn:150]] in approach and withdrawal; in one of the chapters of this treatise[[fn:151]] I shall explain what this relative superiority is in the perception (of the divinity).",
    "root.text.Part 1..17.5": "— As for these words: <i>Touche les montagnes, et qu’elles fument</i> (Ps. 144, 5), one intends to convey thereby; Cause thy command to reach them (which, in turn, is to be understood) metaphorically; likewise the words: <i>Et touche sa personne</i> (Job, 2, 5) signify: Cause thy affliction to descend upon him.",
    "root.text.Part 1..17.6": "Thus, in each passage, thou must consider the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">נגע</span> (to touch), as well as its derived forms, in accordance with the entirety: one expresses by this verb, at times, the contact of one body with another, and, at times, the union through the science and perception of something; for he who perceives the thing he had not previously perceived, as it were, approaches a thing that was distant from him. This must be well understood.",
    "root.text.Part 1..18.0": "<i>Malé</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מלא</span>). — This is a homonymous word that the people of the language (Hebrew) employ (in speaking) of one body entering into another body, so as toimplere fill it; e.g.: <i>And she filled</i> (<i>ותמלא</i>) <span dir=\"rtl\">her pitcher</span> (Genesis, 24, 16); <i>A full</i> (<i>מלא</i>) <span dir=\"rtl\">Omer</span> (Exodus, 16, 32, 33)[[fn:152]]; and this is frequent. It is also employed in the sense offinis end andconsummatio consummation of a determined time; e.g.: <i>And when her days were fulfilled</i> (<i>וימלאו</i>) (Genesis, 25, 24); <i>And when his forty days were fulfilled</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 50, 3). It is then used to denoteperfectio perfection and the highest degree in merit; e.g.: <span dir=\"rtl\">And filled</span> (<i>ומלאׁ</i>) <i>with the blessing of the Lord</i> (Deuteronomy, 33, 25); <i>He filled them</i> (<i>מלא</i>) <span dir=\"rtl\">with wisdom of heart</span> (Exodus, 35, 35); <i>And he was filled</i> (<i>וימלא</i>) <span dir=\"rtl\">with wisdom, understanding, and knowledge</span> (I Kings, 7, 14).",
    "root.text.Part 1..18.1": "— It is in this sense that it has been said: <i>The whole earth is full of His glory</i> (Isaiah, 6, 3), which signifies: The whole earth bears witness to His perfection, that is to say, it shows Him (everywhere). It is the same with the words: <i>And the glory of the Lord filled</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מלא</span>) <i>the tabernacle</i> (Exodus, 40, 34). Every time you find the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">מלא</span>, <i>to fill</i>, attributed to God, it is in this same sense, and one does not mean to say that there is a body <i>filling</i> a space. However, if you wish to admit that <i>the glory of the Lord</i> signifies the <i>created light</i>[[fn:153]], which everywhere is called <i>glory</i>, and that it is this which <i>filled the tabernacle</i>, there is no harm in this.",
    "root.text.Part 1..19.0": "<i>Râm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רם</span>) is a homonym to designate the <i>elevation</i> of the place, as well as the elevation of rank, I mean the majesty, nobility, and power; one reads, for example: <i>And the ark</i>[[fn:154]] <i>rose</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ותרם</span>) <i>above the earth</i> (Genesis, 7, 17), which is in the first sense; in the second sense, one reads, for example: <i>I have exalted</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הרימותי</span>) <i>the chosen one among the people</i> (Psalm 89, 20); <i>For I have exalted</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הרימותיך</span>) <i>from the dust</i> (1 Kings, 16, 2)[[fn:155]]; <i>For I have exalted</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הרימותיך</span>) <i>from the midst of the people</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 14, 7).",
    "root.text.Part 1..19.1": "Every time the verb <i>râm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רם</span>) is applied to God, it is taken in this second sense; for example: <i>Rise</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רומה</span>) <i>above the heavens, O God</i> (Psalm 57, 6).",
    "root.text.Part 1..19.2": "It is the same <i>nasâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נשׂא</span>) that conveys the sense of <i>elevation of place</i> and that of <i>elevation of rank</i> and enlargement in dignity[[fn:156]]; one reads, for example, in the first sense: <i>And they bore</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וישׂאו</span>) <i>their grain upon their asses</i> (Genesis, 42, 26), and there are many other passages (in which the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">נשׂא</span> is taken) in the sense of <i>bearing</i> and of <i>transporting</i>, because there is a <i>elevation</i> of place. In the second sense one reads: <i>And his kingdom shall be exalted</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ותנשׂא</span>) (Numbers, 24, 7); <i>And he has borne them and has exalted them</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וינשׂאם</span>) (Isaiah, 63, 9); <i>And why do you exalt yourselves</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תתנשׂאו</span>) (Numbers, 16, 3)?",
    "root.text.Part 1..19.3": "— In all instances where the verb <i>nasâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נשׂא</span>) is applied to God, it is taken in this latter sense; for example: <i>Élève-toi</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הנשׂא</span>), <i>ô juge de la terre</i> (Ps. 94, 2) ! <i>Ainsi a dit celui qui est haut et élevé</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ונשׂא</span>) (Isaïe, 57, 15), (in which it is a matter of) elevation, majesty[[fn:157]] and power, and not of local height.",
    "root.text.Part 1..19.4": "Perhaps thou shalt find a difficulty in what I say: <i>elevation of rank, majesty, and power</i>; how, thou wilt ask me, canst thou attach several ideas to one and the same sense[[fn:158]]? But thou shalt be explained (further on) that God, the Most High, for perfect men who comprehend (His being), cannot be described by multiple attributes, and that all these numerous attributes indicating glorification, power, authority, perfection, goodness, etc., all revert to one thing, and this thing is the divine essence, and not something which would be outside of this essence.",
    "root.text.Part 1..19.5": "You will have further chapters on the names and attributes (of God); the purpose of the present chapter is solely (to demonstrate) that the words <i>râm</i> ('ram' <span dir=\"rtl\">רם</span>) and <i>nissa</i> ('nasa' <span dir=\"rtl\">נשׂא</span>) (applied to God) must be understood in the sense, not of a <i>local elevation</i>, but of a <i>elevation of rank</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..20.0": "<i>’Abar</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עבר</span>) signifies primarily the same as the verb ’abara <span dir=\"rtl\">(عبر)</span> in Arabic, and is predicated of a body which <i>transports itself</i> in space. It denots first[[fn:159]] the movement of a living creature at a certain direct distance; e.g.: <i>And he passed over</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עבר</span>) <i>before them</i> (Genesis, 33, 3); <i>Pass</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עבֹר</span>) <i>before the people</i> (Exodus, 17, 5); and such is frequent. Thenceforth it has been employed figuratively to express the propagation of sounds through the air; e.g.: <i>They caused it to be proclaimed</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויעבירו קול</span>) <i>throughout the camp</i> (Exodus, 36, 6); (<i>The report</i>) <i>which I hear spreading</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מעבירים</span>) <i>among the people of the Lord</i> (I Sam., 2, 24).",
    "root.text.Part 1..20.1": "It has been further employed to designate the arrival of the light and the divine majesty which the prophets beheld in prophetic vision; e.g.: <i>And behold a smoking furnace, and a burning lamp that passed</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עבר</span>) <i>between those pieces</i> (Genesis, 15, 17), which occurred in a prophetic vision; for it is said at the beginning of the narrative (v. 12): <i>And a deep sleep fell upon Abram</i>, etc. It is in accordance with this metaphor that we must understand these words: <i>And I will pass</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ועברתי</span>) <i>through the land of Egypt</i> (Exodus, 12, 12), and all other similar passages.",
    "root.text.Part 1..20.2": "It is also used at times in speaking of one who, in performing any action, exceeds and overstepps the proper limit; e.g.: <i>And like a man whom</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עברו</span>) <i>wine has overcome</i>[[fn:160]] (Jeremiah, 23, 9).",
    "root.text.Part 1..20.3": "Sometimes also it is employed in speaking of one who passes[[fn:161]] beyond a mark which he had in view, and directs himself toward another goal and another term; e.g.: <i>And he shot the arrow to make it pass beyond</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">להעבירו</span>) (I Sam., 20, 36). It is according to this figurative sense that one must, in my opinion, understand these words: <i>And the Lord passed</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויעבר</span>) <i>before his face</i> (Exodus, 34, 6), the pronoun in <span dir=\"rtl\">פניו</span>, <i>his face</i>, referring to God. This is likewise what the Sages have admitted, to wit, that <span dir=\"rtl\">פניו</span> (his face) referrs to God[[fn:162]]. Albeit they say this in a collection of <i>haggadoth</i> (or allegorical explanations) which would not be in their place here, there is nevertheless therein something which corroborats our opinion. Thus, the pronoun in <span dir=\"rtl\">פניו</span> referring to God, the explanation of the passage in question is, as it seems to me, this: that Moses had requested a certain perception, namely, that which has been designated by the expression <i>to see the face</i>, in these words: <i>But my face shall not be seen</i> (Exodus, 34, 23), and that there was promised to him a perception inferior to that which he had requested, namely, that which has been designated by the expression <i>to see the back</i>, in these words: <i>And thou shalt see my back</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>). We have already called attention to this subject in the <i>Mishneh Torah</i>[[fn:163]]. It is intended here to be said[[fn:164]] that God veiled from him that perception designated by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פנים</span>, <i>face</i>, and caused him to pass toward another thing, I mean toward the knowledge of the actions attributed to God, which are taken for numerous attributes, as we shall explain[[fn:165]]. If I say: <i>He veiled from him</i>, I mean thereby that this perception is veiled and inaccessible by its very nature, and that every perfect man, whose intellect having attained that which it is in its nature to perceive, desirs thereafter another more profound perception[[fn:166]], ses his perception become blunted, or even lost[[fn:167]] [as shall be explained in one of the chapters of this treatise[[fn:168]]], unless he be assisted by divine aid, even as He has said: <i>And I will cover thee with my hand until I have passed</i> (Exodus, 33, 22).",
    "root.text.Part 1..20.4": "As for the Chaldaic paraphrase, it has here done what it habitually performs in such cases; for, whenever it encounters aught attributed to God which is tainted with corporality or with that which partakes of corporality, it presupposes the omission of <i>the annex</i>[[fn:169]], and ascribes the relation to something understood as being <i>the annex</i> (of the name) of God[[fn:170]]. Thus, for instance, the paraphrast renders the words: <i>And, behold, the LORD standing above it</i> (Genesis, 28, 15) by these: \"And, behold, <i>the glory of the LORD</i> standing ready above it;\" the words: <i>The LORD watch between me and thee</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 31, 49), he renders by these: \"Let <i>the Word of the LORD</i> watch.\" Such is his constant method in his exposition; and he has done likewise in these words: <i>And the LORD passed before his face</i> (translating): \"And the LORD <i>caused His Majesty to pass</i> before his face, and cried;\" so that the thing which <i>passed</i> was, doubtless, in his estimation, something <i>created</i>. The pronoun in <span dir=\"rtl\">פניו</span>, <i>his face</i>, is by him referred to Moses, our master, so that the words <span dir=\"rtl\">על פניו</span> (before his face) signify <i>in his presence</i> (or <i>before him</i>), as in that passage: <i>And the present passed over before him</i> (Genesis, 32, 22), which is likewise a sound and plausible interpretation. That which confirms the explanation of Onkelos, the proselyte, are these words of Scripture: <i>And it shall come to pass, while My glory passes by</i>, etc. (Exodus, 33, 22), where it is clearly stated that that which <i>passes by</i> is a thing <i>attributed</i> to God, and not <i>His essence</i> [glorified be His name!], and concerning this glory it might have been said by Him: <i>Until I be passed by</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>); <i>And the LORD passed before his face</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..20.5": "But should it be deemed absolutely necessary to suppose an <i>adjunct</i> understood—as is ever the wont of Onkelos, who admitts as understood now <i>the Glory</i>, now <i>the Shekhinâ</i> or <i>Majesty</i>, and anon <i>the Word</i> (divine), according to that which befitts each passage—we likewise should admit here, as the adjunct understood, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קול</span>, <i>voice</i>, so that there would be virtually in the said passage: “And <i>the voice</i> of the Lord passed before him and cried.” We have already explained above that the Hebrew tongue employs the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">עבר</span>, <i>to pass</i>, when speaking of the voice; as for example: They published (literally, <i>they caused a voice to pass</i>) <i>throughout the camp</i>. It would therefore be the <i>voice</i> which <i>cried;</i> nor shouldst thou find it improbable that the <i>cry</i> be attributed to the <i>voice</i>, for the selfsame expressions are employed in speaking of the word of God addressed to Moses; e.g.: <i>And he heard the voice speaking unto him</i> (Numbers vii. 89); as therefore the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">דבר</span>, <i>to speak</i>, has been attributed to the <i>voice</i>, even so the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קרא</span>, <i>to cry or call</i>, is here attributed to the <i>voice</i>. Something similar is expressly found in Scripture, I mean to say, that the verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר</span>, <i>to say</i>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">קרא</span>, <i>to cry</i>, are expressly attributed to the voice; e.g.: <small>A VOICE SAYS</small>: <i>Cry; and he said, What shall I cry</i> (Isaiah xl. 6)? According to this ellipsis, one must needs explain the passage in question thus: “And <i>a voice</i> from God passed before him and cried: The Lord! the Lord! etc.”; the repetition of the word <i>Lord</i> is to strengthen the vocative—God being He unto whom the call is addressed—even as we find elsewhere, Moses! Moses!—Abraham! Abraham! This is likewise a most excellent interpretation.",
    "root.text.Part 1..20.6": "Thou shalt not find it strange that a subject so profound and so difficult of apprehension should be susceptible of so many diverse interpretations, for this presents no inconvenience to our present purpose. Thou art therefore free to choose such opinion as thou wilt: thou mayest admit either that all this awful scene was indubitably a prophetic vision, and that all the efforts of Moses[[fn:171]] tended toward intellectual perceptions, so that what he sought, what was denied him, and what he perceived, were all alike intellectual, without the intervention of any sense, as we interpreted it at the beginning; or else, that there was simultaneously a perception by means of the sense of sight, but which had for its object a created thing, by the sight of which the perfection of the intellectual perception was obtained, as Onkelos has interpreted—provided that this perception by means of sight was not itself a prophetic vision[[fn:172]], like that which is read of Abraham: <i>And behold a smoking furnace, and a burning lamp that passed</i>, etc.; or finally, that there was also therewith a perception by means of the sense of hearing, and that what passed before him was <i>the voice</i>, which undoubtedly was also something <i>created</i>. Choose, therefore, whichever opinion thou pleasest; for my sole aim is that thou shouldst not believe that the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ויעבר</span>, <i>He passed</i>, has here the same sense as <span dir=\"rtl\">עבֹר</span> in the passage: <i>Pass on before the people</i> (Exodus xvii. 5). For God (blessed be He!) is not a body, and one cannot attribute movement unto Him; it cannot therefore be said that He <i>passed</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עבר</span>), according to the primary signification of that word in the language.",
    "root.text.Part 1..21.0": "The verb <i>bâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בא</span>), in the Hebrew tongue, signifies <i>to come</i>, being said of a living being moving toward any place or toward another individual; e.g.: <i>Thy brother came</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בא</span>) <i>with subtilty</i> (Genesis xxvii. 35). It is also applied to the <i>entering</i> of a living being into a place; e.g.: <i>And Joseph came</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויבֹא</span>) <i>into the house</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, xliii. 26); <i>When ye be come</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תבואו</span>) <i>to the land</i> (Exodus xii. 25). But this verb has also been employed metaphorically to denote <i>the arrival</i> of a thing which is not a body; e.g.: <i>That when </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יבוא</span>) <i>thy sayings come to pass, we may do thee honour</i> (Judges xiii. 17); <i>Things that shall come</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יבֹאו</span>) (Isaiah xlvii. 13); and men have gone so far as to employ it in speaking of certain <i>privations</i>[[fn:173]]; e.g.: <i>There came</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויבא</span>) <i>evil</i> (Job xxx. 26); <i>Darkness came</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>). And, according to this metaphor by which it has been applied to something that is not a body, it has also been employed in speaking of the Creator (blessed be He!), whether to denote the arrival of His word or the arrival (the manifestation) of His Majesty. Conformably to this metaphor, it is said: <i>Lo, I come</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בא</span>) <i>unto thee in a thick cloud</i> (Exodus xix. 9); <i>For the Lord, the God of Israel, enters</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בא</span>) <i>by it</i> (Ezek. xliv. 2); and in all similar passages is signified <i>the arrival of His Majesty</i>. In the passage: <i>And the Lord my God shall come</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ובא</span>), <i>and all the saints with thee</i> (Zechariah xiv. 5), is signified the arrival of His word, or[[fn:174]] the confirmation of the promises He has made through His prophets; and this is what he expresses by the words: <i>all the saints with thee</i>. It is as if he said: “Then shall come to pass the promise of the Lord my God, made through all the holy ones who are with thee,” addressing the words[[fn:175]] unto Israel.",
    "root.text.Part 1..22.0": "The verb <i>yaṣâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יצא</span>) is the opposite of the verb <i>bâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בא</span>). This verb has been employed in speaking of a body <i>going forth</i> from a place where it was established toward another place, whether that body be an animate or inanimate being; e.g.: <i>They were gone out</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יצאו</span>) <i>of the city</i> (Genesis xliv. 4); <i>If fire break out</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תצא</span>) (literally, <i>go forth</i>) (Exodus xxii. 6). But it has been employed metaphorically in speaking of the <i>appearance</i> of a thing which is not a body; e.g.: <i>As the word went out</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יצא</span>) <i>of the king's mouth</i> (Esther vii. 8); <i>For this deed of the queen shall come abroad</i> (literally, <span dir=\"rtl\">go forth</span>) (<i>Ibid.</i>, i. 17), that is to say, the matter shall be published[[fn:176]]; <i>For out of Zion shall go forth</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תצא</span>) <i>the Law</i> (Isaiah ii. 3). Likewise in this passage: <i>The sun was risen</i> (literally, <span dir=\"rtl\">had gone forth</span>) <i>upon the earth</i> (Genesis xix. 23), in which is meant the <i>appearance</i> of the light.",
    "root.text.Part 1..22.1": "It is in this metaphorical sense that the verb <i>yaṣâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יצא</span>) must be taken whenever it is attributed to God; e.g.: <i>For, behold, the Lord comes out</i> (literally, <span dir=\"rtl\">goes forth</span>) <i>out of his place</i> (Isaiah xxvi. 21), that is to say, His word, which is now hidden from us, is about <i>to be manifested</i>. Herein is meant the coming into being of things which have not yet existed; for everything that happens from Him is attributed to His word; e.g.: <i>By the word of the Lord were the heavens made; and all the host of them by the breath of his mouth</i> (Ps. xxxiii. 6), which is said by way of comparison with the acts emanating from kings, who, to transmit their will, employ speech as an instrument[[fn:177]]. But He, the Most High, has no need of an instrument for action; His action, on the contrary, is by His will alone, and there can be no question of literal speech[[fn:178]], as shall be explained[[fn:179]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..22.2": "— Now, as the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יצא</span>, <i>to go forth</i>, has been metaphorically employed to signify the manifestation of any act whatsoever proceeding from Him [as we have even now explained], and as it has been expressed: <i>Behold, the Lord goes forth out of His place</i>, one has likewise employed the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">שוב</span>, <i>to return</i>, to denote the cessation, according to the Divine will, of the act in question; and it is said: <i>I will go and return</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אשובה</span>) <i>to my place</i> (Hosea, v, 15), which signifies that the Divine Majesty, which was in the midst of us, shall withdraw from us, and, in consequence thereof[[fn:180]], Divine Providence shall fail us, as God has said in threatening us: <i>And I will hide my face from them, and they shall be devoured</i> (Deuter., xxxi, 17); for when Providence fails a man, he is abandoned to himself, and remains a mark for all that may occur by accident, so that his happiness and his misery depend upon chance. How terrible is this threat! It is that which has been expressed by these words: <i>I will go and return to my place</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..23.0": "The verb <i>halakh</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הלך</span>, <i>to go, to walk</i>) is likewise among those which are applied to certain particular movements of living beings; e.g.: <i>And Jacob went</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הלך</span>) <i>on his way</i> (Genesis, xxxii, 1); of which there are numerous examples. This word has been metaphorically employed to denote the expansion of bodies which are more subtle than the bodies of animals; e.g.: <i>And the waters decreased</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הלוך</span>) <i>continually</i> (Genesis, viii, 5); <i>And the fire ran along</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ותהלך</span>) <i>upon the ground</i> (Exodus, ix, 23). Subsequently, it was employed in a general sense to signify that a thing spreads and manifests itself, even though that thing be not a body; e.g.: <i>The voice thereof shall go</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ילך</span>) <i>like</i> (<i>the gliding of</i>) <i>a serpent</i> (Jeremiah, xlvi, 22). Likewise in these words: <i>The voice of the Lord God walking</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מתהלך</span>) <i>in the garden</i> (Genesis, iii, 8), it is to the voice that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מתהלך</span> (walking or spreading) is applied[[fn:181]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..23.1": "It is in this metaphorical sense that the verb <i>halakh</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הלך</span>) must be understood whenever it relats to God, — I mean to say, having regard to the fact that it is said metaphorically of that which is not a body, — whether it be applied to the diffusion of the Divine word[[fn:182]] or to the <i>withdrawal</i> of Providence, analogous to that which, in a living being, is called <i>turning away from a thing</i>, which the animal performs by the act of walking[[fn:183]]. As therefore the withdrawal of Providence has been denoted by the expression <i>hiding the face</i>, in these words: <i>And I will surely hide my face</i> (Deuter., xxxi, 18), so has it been denoted by <span dir=\"rtl\">הלך</span> (to walk, to go away), taken in the sense of <i>turning away from a thing</i>; e.g.: <i>I will go and return to my place</i> (Hosea, v, 15).",
    "root.text.Part 1..23.2": "As regards this passage: <i>And the wrath of the Eternal was kindled against them, and he</i> (or <i>she</i>) <i>went away</i> (Vayilach (Nombr., XII, 9)), it contains at once both senses[[fn:184]], I mean the sense of the <i>retreat of Providence</i>, designated by the expression of <i>turning away</i> (s'en aller <i>), and that of the <i>dispersion</i> of the divine word which spreads and manifests itself, I mean that it is <i>the wrath</i> which <i>went away</i> and extended itself towards them both[[fn:185]], wherefore she (Miriam) became <i>leprous</i>, (blanche <i>as white as snow</i> <i>like the snow</i> (Ibid. <i>, </i>V<small>. 10).",
    "root.text.Part 1..23.3": "In like manner is the verb <i>halakh</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הלך</span>) metaphorically used to signify <i>walking in the right path</i> (or <i>possessing a goodly conduct</i>), without there being any question of the movement of a body; e.g.: <i>And thou shalt walk</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">והלכת</span>) <i>in His ways</i> (Deuter., xxviii, 9); <i>Ye shall walk</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תלכו</span>) <i>after the Lord your God</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, xiii, 4); <i>Come ye, and let us walk</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ונלכה</span>) <i>in the light of the Lord</i> (Isaiah, ii, 5).",
    "root.text.Part 1..24.0": "<i>Schakhan</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שכן</span>). — It is well known that the signification of this verb is <i>to dwell;</i> e.g.: <i>And he dwelt</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שוכן</span>) <i>in the plain of Mamre</i> (Genesis, xiv, 13); <i>And it came to pass, when Israel dwelt</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בשכן</span>)…(<i>Ibid.</i>, xxxv, 22); and this is the generally accepted sense. <i>To dwell</i> signifies: to abide permanently in one and the same place[[fn:186]]; for, when a living creature prolongs its stay in a place, whether it be <i>common</i> or particular[[fn:187]], it is said of him that <i>he dwells</i> in that place, albeit, without doubt, he be there in motion.",
    "root.text.Part 1..24.1": "This verb is applied metaphorically to that which is inanimate or, to speak more precisely, to any thing which remains fixed and has attached itself to another thing; wherefore the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">שכן</span> (to dwell) is likewise employed in this case, even though the object to which the thing in question has attached itself be not <i>a place</i>, nor the thing an animate being; e.g.: <i>Let a cloud dwell</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תשכן</span>) <i>upon it</i> (Job, iii, 5); for the cloud, verily, is not an animate being, nor is the day a body, but a portion of time.",
    "root.text.Part 1..24.2": "It is in this metaphorical sense that (the verb in question) has been applied to God, I mean to the permanence of His <i>Shekhinâ</i> (Majesty)[[fn:188]], or of His Providence in any place whatsoever[[fn:189]], or to the Providence manifesting itself permanently[[fn:190]] in any thing. It has been said, e.g.: <i>And the glory of the Lord abode</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וישכן</span>) (Exodus, xxiv, 17); <i>And I will dwell</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ושכניתי</span>) <i>among the children of Israel</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, xxix, 45); <i>And the good will of Him that dwelt</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שכני</span>) <i>in the bush</i> (Deuter., xxxiii, 16)[[fn:191]]. And, whenever this verb is found attributed to God, it signifies the permanence, in some place, of His <i>Shekhinâ</i>, I mean of His <i>light</i> (which is a) <i>created</i> thing, or the permanence of His Providence (manifesting itself) in any thing whatsoever, according as it befitts each passage.",
    "root.text.Part 1..25.0": "Thou art already acquainted with their dictum[[fn:192]] concerning all manner of interpretation relating to this subject[[fn:193]], to wit: that <i>the Scripture has spoken according to the language of men</i>. This signifies that whatever men in general[[fn:194]] are able to comprehend and to conceive at first sight has been applied to God, who, for this reason, has been described by epithets indicating corporeality, in order to signify that God exists; for the generality of men cannot conceive of existence, save in a body particularly, and whatever is not a body nor is found in a body has for them no existence.",
    "root.text.Part 1..25.1": "Likewise, all that is perfection for us has been attributed to God to indicate that He possesses all kinds of perfection without any admixture of imperfection; and whatever is conceived by the vulgar as being an imperfection or a lack, is not attributed unto Him. Wherefore, neither eating, nor drinking, nor sleep, nor sickness, nor injustice, nor any other such thing, is attributed to Him. But all that the vulgar believe to be a perfection has been attributed to Him, although it be a perfection only in relation to ourselves; for as regards Him[[fn:195]] all these things which we believe to be perfections are an extreme imperfection. The vulgar, however, would deem themselves to attribute an imperfection to God, were they to imagine that such human perfection might be wanting in Him[[fn:196]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..25.2": "Thou knowest that motion forms part of the perfection of the animal and is necessary unto it that it may be perfect; for, even as it has need to eat and to drink to replace that which has been dissolved[[fn:197]], so too it has need of motion to direct itself towards that which is suitable unto it and to flee from that which is contrary. There is no difference between attributing to God eating and drinking and attributing unto Him motion; nevertheless, <i>according to the language of men</i>, that is to say, according to popular imagination, it would be to attribute an imperfection unto God to say that He eats and drinks, whereas motion would not denote an imperfection in Him[[fn:198]], albeit it is but necessity which compels to motion. It has been demonstrated that whatsoever moves is undoubtedly of a certain magnitude and divisible[[fn:199]]; now, it will be demonstrated that God possesses no magnitude, and consequently has no motion. Neither can repose be attributed unto Him; for repose can be attributed only unto him[[fn:200]] whose condition it is to move. Thus, therefore, all words indicating the different species of animal movements have been employed, in the aforesaid manner[[fn:201]], as attributes of God, even as <i>life;</i> for motion is an accident inherent in the animate being, and there is no doubt that by setting aside corporeality, one also sets aside all these notions of <i>descending</i>, of <i>ascending</i>, of <i>walking</i>, of <i>standing</i>, of <i>halting</i>, of <i>going about</i>, of <i>sitting</i>, of <i>dwelling</i>, of <i>going forth</i>, of <i>entering</i>, of <i>passing</i>, and others similar.",
    "root.text.Part 1..25.3": "It would be superfluous to dwell at length upon this subject, were it not for that which has become familiar to the minds of the vulgar[[fn:202]]; wherefore it is necessary to provide an explanation thereof to those who have set themselves the task (of acquiring) human perfection and of ridding themselves of those errors, preconceived since the years of infancy[[fn:203]], (and to speak thereof) with some detail, even as we have done.",
    "root.text.Part 1..26.0": "Onkelos the proselyte, who possessed a perfect mastery of the Hebrew and Syriac tongues[[fn:204]], used his utmost endeavors to remove all corporification (of God); so that, whenever Scripture employs (concerning God) an epithet which might lead to corporality, he interprets it according to its (true) sense. Whenever he finds one of those words which indicate any of the diverse species of motion, he takes motion in the sense of <i>manifestation</i>, of the <i>appearance of a created light</i>, I mean of <i>Divine Majesty</i>, or else (in that) of <i>Providence</i>[[fn:205]]. He therefore translats <span dir=\"rtl\">ירד י״י</span> (the Eternal <i>shall descend</i>, Exodus, 19, 11) by <span dir=\"rtl\">יתגלי י״י</span> (the Eternal <i>shall manifest Himself</i>), <span dir=\"rtl\">וירד י״י</span> (and the Eternal <i>descended, ibid.</i>, <small>v.</small> 20) by <span dir=\"rtl\">ואתגלי י״י</span> (and the Eternal <i>manifested Himself</i>), and he says not <span dir=\"rtl\">ונחת י״י</span> (and the Eternal <i>descended</i>); likewise <span dir=\"rtl\">ארדה נא ואראה</span> (I will now <i>go down</i> and see, Genesis, 18, 21) by <span dir=\"rtl\">אתגלי כען ואחזי</span> (I will now <i>manifest Myself</i> and see[[fn:206]]); and this he does continually in his paraphrase.",
    "root.text.Part 1..26.1": "Nevertheless, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אנכי ארד עמך מצרימה</span> (I <i>will go down</i> with thee into Egypt, Genesis, 46, 4), he translats (literally): <span dir=\"rtl\">אנא איחות עמך למצריס</span>, and this is a most remarkable thing which proves the perfect talent of this master, the excellence of his manner of interpretation, and how exactly he understood these matters; for by this translation (of the latter passage), he has likewise given us a glimpse[[fn:207]] of one of the principal points of <i>Prophecy</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..26.2": "This is how: In the beginning of this narrative (<i>ibid.</i>, <small>v.</small> 2 and 3), it is said: <i>And God spake unto Israel in the visions of the night, and said, Jacob, Jacob</i>, etc. <i>And He said, I am God</i>, etc. (and God said in conclusion): <i>I will go down with thee into Egypt</i>. Now, as it follows from the beginning of the discourse that this occurred <i>in the visions of the night</i>, Onkelos saw no harm in rendering literally the words which had been spoken in these nocturnal visions. And this with reason[[fn:208]]: for it is the relation of something that had been said, and not the relation of a fact that had occurred, as (in the passage): And <i>the Eternal descended upon mount Sinai</i> (Exodus, 19, 20), which is the relation of a fact that came to pass in the real world[[fn:209]]; wherefore he has substituted the idea of <i>manifestation</i> and removed that which might indicate the existence of a <i>motion</i>. But (concerning) the things of the imagination, I mean the relation of that which had been said (to Jacob), he has left it intact[[fn:210]]. This is a remarkable thing.",
    "root.text.Part 1..26.3": "Thou art, in sooth, admonished thereby that the matter is right different, according as there be used the words <i>in a dream</i> or <i>in the visions of the night</i>, or whether the words <i>in a vision</i> and <i>in an appearance</i> be employed, or whether it be simply said: <i>And the word of the Lord came unto me, saying</i>, or: <i>The Lord said unto me</i>[[fn:211]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..26.4": "It is also possible, in mine opinion, that Onkelos has interpreted here the word <i>Elohim</i> (God) in the sense <i>of angels</i>, and that for this cause he felt no repugnance to say (in his version): “I will <i>go down</i> with thee into Egypt.” One must not deem it improper that Onkelos should have perceived in <i>Elohim</i> an <i>angel</i>, albeit (the apparition) says unto him (to Jacob): <i>I am the</i> G<small>OD</small>, <i>the God of thy father;</i> for the same terms are also sometimes employed by an angel. Dost thou not perceive that elsewhere, after it is said: <i>And the angel of God spake unto me in a dream, saying, Jacob: And I said, Here am I</i> (Genesis, xxxi, 11), it is expressed at the conclusion of the speech addressed unto Jacob: <i>I am the God of Beth-el, where thou anointedst the pillar, and where thou vowedst a vow unto me</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 13)? Doubtless Jacob offered his vows unto God, and not unto the angel; but this occurs continually in the discourses of the prophets, I mean that in reporting the words which the angel spake unto them in the name of God, they express themselves as if it were God Himself who spake unto them. In all passages (of this kind) there is an ellipsis of a noun in the construct state[[fn:212]]; it is as if it were said: <i>I am</i> <small>THE MESSENGER</small> <i>of the God of thy father; I am</i> <small>THE MESSENGER</small> <i>of the God who appeared unto thee at Beth-el</i>, and so forth.",
    "root.text.Part 1..26.5": "Concerning prophecy and its divers degrees, as also concerning the angels, there shall yet be spoken more at large, in accordance with the design of this treatise.",
    "root.text.Part 1..27.0": "<i>Reghel</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רגל</span>) is an homonymous term. It is (primarily) the name of the <i>foot;</i> e.g., <span dir=\"rtl\">רגל תחת רגל</span>, <i>foot for foot</i> (Exodus, xxi, 24); but it is also found in the sense of <i>following;</i> e.g., <i>Get thee out</i>, <i>and all the people that follow thee</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ברגליך</span>) (<i>Ibid.</i>, xi, 8), that is to say: who <i>follow</i> thee. It is likewise met with in a sense of <i>causality;</i> e.g., <i>And the Lord has blessed thee since my coming</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לרגלי</span>) (Genesis, xxx, 30), that is to say, <i>by my cause</i> or <i>for my sake</i>, for that which is done for the sake of a certain thing has the latter for its <i>cause</i>. It is often thus employed; e.g., <i>According to the pace</i> or <i>because</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לרגל</span>) <i>of the cattle that goes before me and because</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לרגל</span>) <i>of the children</i> (Genesis, xxxiii, 14).",
    "root.text.Part 1..27.1": "Thus, by these words: <i>And His feet</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רגליו</span>) <i>shall stand in that day upon the mount of Olives</i> (Zechariah, xiv, 4), it is meant that <i>His causes</i> shall subsist, to wit, the miracles which shall then be manifested in that place, and of which God is <i>the cause</i>, I mean the author[[fn:213]]. It is this interpretation which Jonathan ben Uziel had in view when he said (in his Chaldee paraphrase): <i>He shall manifest Himself in His power, in that day, upon the mount of Olives;</i> and in like manner he translats by <span dir=\"rtl\">גבורתיה</span>, <i>His power</i>, all (the words designating) the members[[fn:214]] (which are used) for <i>grasping</i> or for <i>moving from place to place</i>, for they generally designate the acts which emanate from His will.",
    "root.text.Part 1..27.2": "As for these words: <i>And under his feet</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רגליו</span>) <i>there was as it were a work of the brilliance of sapphire</i> (Exodus 24:10), Onkelos, as you know, interprets them in such a way as to consider the pronoun in <span dir=\"rtl\">רגליו</span> (<i>his</i> feet) as referring to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כסא</span>, <i>throne</i> (which would be implied)[[fn:215]]; for he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ותחות כורסי יקריה</span>, <i>and under the throne of his glory</i>. This must be understood, and you will be astonished (to see) how far Onkelos remains from the corporealization (of God) and from everything that can lead to it in even the most remote manner; for he does not say: <i>and under his throne</i>, because if one attributed the throne to (God) Himself, in the sense that would be seen at first glance, it would follow that God is established[[fn:216]] upon a body, and corporealization would result; he therefore attributes the <i>throne</i> to <i>His glory</i>, I mean to the <i>Shekhinah</i>, which is a <i>created light</i>[[fn:217]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..27.3": "He expresses himself after the same manner in the translation of the words: <i>Because the hand</i> (<i>is placed</i>) <i>upon the Throne of God</i> (Exodus, xvii. 16), where he says: «…from before God, whose Majesty rests upon <i>the Throne of His Glory</i>.» Thou findest likewise in the language of the whole Jewish nation the term <span dir=\"rtl\">כסא הכבוד</span>, <i>Throne of Glory</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..27.4": "But we have digressed from the theme of this chapter to touch upon a matter which shall be explained in other chapters.",
    "root.text.Part 1..27.5": "I return, then, to the subject of the chapter. You know, I say, the manner in which Onkelos interprets (the passage in question); but the principal aim for him is to exclude corporealization, and he does not explain to us what they perceived[[fn:218]] nor what was intended by this allegory. Likewise, in all passages (of this nature), he does not address that question at all[[fn:219]]; rather, he limits himself to excluding corporealization, for that is a demonstrable thing, necessary in religious faith; he does so, therefore, in an absolute manner[[fn:220]], and expresses himself accordingly. As for the explanation of the meaning of the allegory, it is a matter of opinion; for the aim (of the allegory) could be this thing or another. These are also very obscure subjects, the understanding of which is not part of the foundations of the Faith, and which the vulgar do not easily grasp; that is why he does not engage in this question.",
    "root.text.Part 1..27.6": "But one, having regard to the object of this treatise, cannot forbear to offer some interpretation (of the passage in question). One therefore states that, by the words <i>under His feet</i>, is signified <i>by its cause and by Him</i>, as has been explained[[fn:221]]; what they perceived was the true condition of <i>prime matter</i>, which proceeded from God, who is the cause of its existence. Observe carefully the words: <span dir=\"rtl\">כמעשה לבנת הספיר</span>, <i>as a work of the brightness of sapphire:</i> had <i>colour</i> been intended, it would have been said <span dir=\"rtl\">כלבנת הספיר</span>, <i>as the whiteness</i> or <i>the brightness of sapphire;</i> but <span dir=\"rtl\">מעשה</span>, <i>a work</i>, was added, because matter, as is known, is ever <i>receptive</i> and <i>passive</i> in relation to its essence, and possesses action only accidentally, just as form is ever <i>active</i> by its essence and <i>passive</i> by accident, as is explained in the treatises of Physics; and it is on this account that <span dir=\"rtl\">כמעשה</span>, <i>as a work</i>, has been said of the former[[fn:222]]. As to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לבנת הספיר</span> (which signify literally: <i>the whiteness of sapphire</i>)[[fn:223]], they denote <i>transparency</i>, and not <i>the white colour</i>, for the whiteness of crystal is not a white colour, but a mere transparency; and transparency is not a colour, as has been demonstrated in the treatises of Physics[[fn:224]], for, were it a colour, it would not permit all colours to pass through[[fn:225]], and would not receive them. It is therefore because the transparent body is deprived of all colours that it receives them all successively; and this resembles <i>prime matter</i>, which, in relation to its true condition, is deprived of all forms, and which, on that account, receives them all successively. Thus, therefore, what they perceived was prime matter and its relation to God[[fn:226]], (to wit) that it was[[fn:227]] the first of His creatures comprising <i>generation</i> and <i>corruption</i>[[fn:228]], and that it was He who had produced it from nothingness[[fn:229]]. Further mention will be made of this subject (in another place)[[fn:230]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..27.7": "Know thou that thou hast need of such an interpretation, even beside that of Onkelos, who says: <i>and under the throne of His glory;</i> I mean that prime matter is also, in truth, beneath the heaven, which is called <i>throne</i>, as has been said aforetime. That which suggested unto me this remarkable interpretation and caused me to find the subject in question, is solely an assertion which I found in the work of R. Eliezer, son of Hyrcanus, and which thou shalt learn in one of the chapters of this treatise[[fn:231]]. The aim, in sum, of every intelligent man, is to remove from God all that may lead to corporeality, and to regard all these perceptions of which we have spoken as appertaining to the intellect, and not unto the senses. This must be understood and pondered upon.",
    "root.text.Part 1..28.0": "<i>’Açab</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עצב</span>) is a homonym which signifies (firstly) <i>pain</i> and <i>suffering;</i> e.g.: <i>In pain</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בעצב</span>) <i>thou shalt bring forth children</i> (Genesis iii. 16). It signifies also the act of <i>provoking;</i> e.g.: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא עצבו אביו מימיו</span>, <i>His father had not</i> <small>VEXED</small> <i>him at any time</i> (I Kings i. 6); <span dir=\"rtl\">כי נעצב אל דוד</span>, <i>For he was</i> <small>GRIEVED</small> <i>on account of David</i> (I Sam. xx. 34). It signifies finally <i>to oppose or to be rebellious;</i> e.g.: <i>They rebelled, and vexed</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ועצבו</span>) <i>His holy spirit</i> (Isaiah lxiii. 10); <i>They provoked Him</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יעציבוהו</span>) <i>in the wilderness</i> (Ps. lxxviii. 40); … <i>if there be in me a rebellious way</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עצֶב</span>) (Ps. cxxxix. 24); <i>Every day they wrest</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יעצבו</span>) <i>my words</i> (Ps. lvi. 6).",
    "root.text.Part 1..28.1": "It is according to the second or the third signification that it has been said (concerning God): <span dir=\"rtl\">ויתעצב אל לבו</span>, <i>And it</i> <small>GRIEVED</small> or <small>VEXED</small> <i>Him at His heart</i> (Genesis vi. 6). According to the second signification, the interpretation thereof would be: That God was wroth with them by reason of their evil deeds. As for the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אל לבו</span>, <i>in His heart</i>, which are likewise employed in the history of Noah, saying: <i>And the Lord said in His heart</i> (Genesis viii. 21), hearken unto the meaning thereof: when it is said, in speaking of man, that he <i>said in his heart</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">בלבו</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">אל לבו</span>, it concerns something that man does not utter nor declare unto another; and in like manner, whenever it concerns something that God has willed, yet has not declared unto a prophet at the moment when the act was performed according to the Divine will, it is expressed: <i>And the Lord said in His heart</i>, by assimilation to human things, and according to that constant rule, that <i>the Scripture speaks according to the language of men</i>[[fn:232]]; and this is clear and manifest. Since, therefore, as touching the rebellion of the <i>generation of the flood</i>, there is no mention in Scripture of any messenger sent unto them, nor of warning, nor of threat of destruction, it was said of them that God was vexed with them <i>in His heart</i>. Likewise, when God willed[[fn:233]] that there should be no more flood, He spake not unto any prophet: Go and announce unto them such a thing; wherefore it was said: <i>in His heart</i> (Genesis viii. 21).",
    "root.text.Part 1..28.2": "To interpret the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ויתעצב אל לבו</span> according to the third signification, they must needs be explained in this sense: that <i>man opposed the will of God within him</i>[[fn:234]]; for the will is also called <span dir=\"rtl\">לב</span>, <i>heart</i>, even as we shall explain in treating of the homonymity of the word <i>leb</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לב</span>) <span dir=\"ltr\">[[fn:235]]</span>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..29.0": "<i>Akhal</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אכל</span>, <i>to eat</i>). — This word, in the Hebrew tongue, is primarily applied, in speaking of animals, to the act of <i>taking food</i>, and this requirs no citation of examples; subsequently, the language has considered in the act of eating two things: first, that the thing eaten is lost and perishes, I mean that its form is first corrupted; secondly, that the animal grows by the food it takes, whereby it continus to preserve itself, prolongs[[fn:236]] its existence, and restors all the powers of the body.",
    "root.text.Part 1..29.1": "With respect to the first consideration, the verb <i>akhal</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אכל</span>) has been employed metaphorically for everything that perishes and is destroyed, and in general for every deprivation of form; as for instance: <i>And the land of your enemies shall eat you up</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ואכלה</span>) (Levit. xxvi. 38); <i>A land that eats up</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אכלת</span>) <i>the inhabitants thereof</i> (Numb. xiii. 32); <i>Ye shall be devoured</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תאֻכּלו</span>) <i>with the sword</i> (Isa. i. 20); <i>Shall the sword devour</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תאכל</span>) … (II Sam. ii. 26)? <i>And the fire of the Lord burnt among them, and consumed</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ותאכל</span>) <i>them that were in the uttermost parts of the camp</i> (Numb. xi. 1); (<i>God</i>) <i>is a consuming fire</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אכׁלה</span>) (Deut. iv. 24), that is to say, He destroys those who are rebellious against Him, even as fire destroys all that it seizes. This use (of the verb <i>akhal</i>) is frequent.",
    "root.text.Part 1..29.2": "With regard to the second consideration, the verb <i>akhal</i> has been employed metaphorically to signify <i>knowledge</i> and <i>instruction</i>, and, in general, the intellectual perceptions whereby <i>the human form</i>[[fn:237]] continues to be preserved in the most perfect state, just as, through nourishment, the body remains in its best condition; for example: <i>Come, buy and eat</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ואכלו</span>), <i>etc.</i> (Isaïe, 55, 1); <i>Hear me, and you shall eat</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ואכלו</span>) <i>that which is good</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 2); <i>To eat</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אכלׁ</span>) <i>too much honey is not good</i> (Prov., 25, 27); <i>Eat</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אכל</span>) <i>honey, my son, for it is good; pure honey, sweet to thy palate. Such is for thy soul the knowledge of wisdom, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 24, 13, 14).",
    "root.text.Part 1..29.3": "This usage is likewise frequent in the words of the Sages—I mean, the denoting of knowledge by the verb <i>to eat;</i> as for instance: “Come and <i>eat</i> fat meat at the house of Raba.”[[fn:238]] They say (elsewhere): “Wheresoever mention is made in this book of <i>eating</i> and <i>drinking</i>, nothing else is intended but wisdom,” or, according to some copies, “the Law.”[[fn:239]] In like manner, wisdom is frequently called <i>water;</i> as for instance: <i>Ho, every one that thirsts, come ye to the waters</i> (Isa. lv. 1)!",
    "root.text.Part 1..29.4": "This use (of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אכל</span>) having become very frequent, and having received such extension that it has, in a manner, taken the place of the primitive signification, the words <i>hunger</i> and <i>thirst</i> have likewise been employed to denote the absence of <i>knowledge</i> and of intellectual <i>perception</i>; as for instance: <i>I will send a famine in the land, not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the Lord</i> (Amos viii. 11); <i>My soul thirsts for God</i>, <i>for the living God</i> (Ps. xlii. 3). Of this there are numerous examples. The words, <i>Therefore with joy shall ye draw water out of the wells of salvation</i> (Isa. xii. 3), Jonathan ben Uziel (in his Chaldaic version) has rendered thus: <i>And ye shall receive with joy a new doctrine from the chosen among the righteous</i>. Observe well that, according to his interpretation, <i>water</i> denots the <i>knowledge</i> which shall be obtained in those days; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מַעַיְנֵי</span> (the wells), he assimilats to <span dir=\"rtl\">מֵעֵינֵי העדה</span> (Numb. xv. 24), signifying <i>the chiefs</i>[[fn:240]], who are the <i>learned</i>. He says: <i>from the chosen among the righteous</i>, because <i>righteousness</i> (or <i>piety</i>) is the true <i>salvation</i>. Thou seest[[fn:241]] how he interprets every word of this verse in the sense of <i>knowledge</i> and <i>instruction</i>. Consider this well.",
    "root.text.Part 1..30.0": "Know[[fn:242]] that for the human intellect there are objects of perception which it is within its faculty and nature to perceive; but there are also<i></i>, in that which exists, beings and things which it is not at all in its nature to perceive in any manner, nor by any cause whatsoever[[fn:243]], and the perception of which is absolutely inaccessible to it[[fn:244]]. Finally, there are things in existence of which it perceivs certain circumstances, whilst remaining in ignorance of others[[fn:245]]. Indeed, from the fact that it is a percipient thing, it does not necessarily follow that it must perceive all things; for the senses likewise have perceptions, yet they cannot perceive objects at every possible distance.",
    "root.text.Part 1..30.1": "The same holds true for other corporeal faculties; for, although man, for example, be capable of lifting two quintals, he is not thereby capable of lifting ten. The superiority which individuals of the (human) species possess one over another, in these <i>sensible</i> perceptions and in other corporeal faculties, is clear and manifest to all; yet it possesses a limit, and the matter does not extend to any distance whatsoever, nor to any measure whatsoever. It is precisely the same with regard to man's <i>intelligible</i> perceptions, in which individuals of the species enjoy a marked superiority one over another, a fact likewise exceedingly clear and manifest to men of science; insomuch that there is a subject which one individual draws forth by his own speculation, whilst another individual could never comprehend the same subject, and even were one to endeavour to make him comprehend it by every manner of phraseology and example and over a long period of time, his mind cannot penetrate thereto, but rather refuses to comprehend it. But the superiority in question extends not to infinity either, and human intelligence, on the contrary, undoubtedly possesses a limit whereat it ceases.",
    "root.text.Part 1..30.2": "For certain things, therefore, man acknowledgs the impossibility of grasping them, and feels no desire to know them, well knowing that this is impossible and that there is no gate whereby one may enter to attain thereunto. Thus, for example, we are ignorant of the number of the stars of heaven, and whether it be an even or an odd number; and we are likewise ignorant of the number of the species of animals, minerals, and plants, and other such-like things. But there be other things which man feels a great desire to grasp, and the efforts of the intelligence to seek the reality thereof and to scrutinize them thoroughly are met with among all the speculative sects of the world and in all ages[[fn:246]]. And concerning these things, opinions are numerous, disagreement reigns among thinkers, and doubts arise, because the intelligence strivs to grasp these things—I mean that it is thither drawn by desire—and because each one believs himself to have found a path whereby to know the reality of the thing, whilst it is not in the power of human intelligence to allege in this regard a demonstrative proof; for every thing whose reality is known by means of a demonstration cannot be the subject of a difference (of opinion), nor of a contention[[fn:247]], nor of a denial, unless it be on the part of an ignorant person who employs that manner of contradicting which is called <i>the demonstrative contradiction</i>[[fn:248]], as thou findest, for instance, folk who contest the sphericity of the earth and the circular motion of the celestial sphere and other like things. These latter find no place in the subject (which now occupies us).",
    "root.text.Part 1..30.3": "The points on which this confusion (in opinions) prevails are very numerous in metaphysical matters, few in physical matters, and none in mathematical matters.",
    "root.text.Part 1..30.4": "Alexander of Aphrodisias[[fn:249]] says that the causes of disagreement concerning certain things are three in number: 1st, ambitious and rival pretensions[[fn:250]] which prevent man from perceiving the truth as it is; 2nd, the subtlety of the perceptible thing in itself, its profundity and the difficulty of perceiving it; 3rd, the ignorance of him who perceivs, and his incapacity to grasp even that which it is possible to grasp. Thus says Alexander. In our own times there is a fourth cause which he mentioned not, because it existed not among them[[fn:251]]: namely, habit and education; for it is in the nature of man to love that which is familiar to him and to be attracted thereunto. Thus thou seest the Bedouins, unclean as they are, deprived of enjoyments and subsisting miserably, feel a repugnance for the cities, being insensible to the pleasures which they offer, and preferring the evil condition whereunto they are accustomed to a better condition whereunto they are not accustomed; so that they have no pleasure in dwelling in palaces, nor in clothing themselves in silk, nor in procuring the delights of the bath, of oils, and of perfumes.",
    "root.text.Part 1..30.5": "In like manner it happens that man loves the opinions which are familiar to him and in which he has been nurtured, that he takes them under his protection, and is affrighted by that which is outside them.",
    "root.text.Part 1..30.6": "And by the selfsame cause, man shutts his eyes to the perception of truths and inclins toward his habits, as befalls the vulgar in the question of corporeality and in many metaphysical matters, even as we shall set forth; all this by reason of habit and because one has been nurtured upon the words of Scripture, the constant object of respect and faith, the literal sense of which would indicate the corporeality of God and imaginary things void of truth, but which were spoken by way of allegory and enigma, and that for reasons of which I shall treat.",
    "root.text.Part 1..30.7": "It must not be supposed that what we have here said concerning the insufficiency of the human intellect, in maintaining that it has a limit at which it must needs stay its course, has been said from the standpoint of religion; it is, on the contrary, a thing which the philosophers have asserted and which they have perfectly understood, without regard to sect or opinion. And it is a true matter which cannot be called into doubt, save by him who is ignorant of things already demonstrated.",
    "root.text.Part 1..30.8": "This chapter we have placed here only to serve as a preparation for that which shall follow.",
    "root.text.Part 1..31.0": "Know, O reader of my treatise! that there occurrs in <i>intelligible</i> perceptions, in so far as they are linked to matter[[fn:252]], something similar to that which occurrs in <i>sensible</i> perceptions. Thus, when thou lookest with thine eye, thou perceivest that which it is within thy visual faculty to perceive. But, when thou strainest thine eyes and fixest thy gaze, endeavouring to see at a great distance, too remote for it to be within thy power to see so far, or when thou considerest a very fine script or a minute engraving, the perception of which is not within thy faculty, so as to strain thy gaze in order to examine it well, then thy sight becoms too weak, not only for that which thou couldst not easily see, but also for that which thy faculty permitted thee to perceive; thy gaze is blunted and thou seest no longer even that which thou wert able to perceive before having fixed thy gaze and fatigued it.",
    "root.text.Part 1..31.1": "In the same condition is he who studies any science whatsoever, when he gives himself over to meditation; for, if he makes efforts[[fn:253]] of meditation and fatigue his whole mind, he becoms hebetated, and then he no longer comprehends even that which it is within his power to comprehend, for all the corporeal faculties[[fn:254]] are, in this regard, in the same position.",
    "root.text.Part 1..31.2": "The like befalls thee in (purely) <i>intelligible</i>[[fn:255]] perceptions; for, if thou haltest before that which is obscure, if thou deceivest not thyself by believing that there is a demonstration for that which is indemonstrable, if thou art not in haste[[fn:256]] to reject and declare to be a lie aught whose contrary is not demonstrated, and if, finally, thou aspirest not to the perception of that which thou canst not perceive, then hast thou attained unto human perfection, and art in the rank of Rabbi Akiba, who <i>entered in peace and went out in peace</i>[[fn:257]] while studying these metaphysical things. But if thou aspirest to a perception beyond thy perceptive faculty, or art in haste to declare those things to be a lie whose contrary is not demonstrated, or which are possible, albeit in a most remote manner, thou joinest thyself unto Élischa’ A’her[[fn:258]], and not only shalt thou not be perfect, but thou shalt become the most imperfect of all; then shall it happen unto thee that thou shalt allow the imaginative faculties to prevail and be led into vice, depravity, and evil, because the intellect will be preoccupied and its light extinguished, even as all manner of vain phantoms present themselves to the sight when <i>the visual spirit</i>[[fn:259]] grows weak in the sick and in those who fix their gaze upon brilliant or very subtle objects.",
    "root.text.Part 1..31.3": "It is in this sense that it has been said: <i>If thou hast found honey, eat so much as is sufficient for thee, lest thou be filled therewith</i>, <i>and vomit it</i> (Prov., 25, 16). Indeed, the doctors have allegorically applied this passage to Élischa’ A’her[[fn:260]]. This allegory is indeed remarkable; for in comparing here knowledge to <i>eating</i>, according to what we have already stated[[fn:261]], the sweetest of foods is mentioned, namely honey. Honey, by its nature, when taken in great quantity, excites the stomach and causes vomiting. This passage intimates that, whatsoever the importance and grandeur of this perception and the perfection found therein, it is in its nature — when one does not stop at a certain limit and does not advance therein with caution — to turn to imperfection, just as in eating honey one is nourished and delighted when partaking with moderation, but when too much is taken, all is lost; (which is why) it was not said: <i>Be not full thereof, for thou wouldst</i> <small>BECOME DISGUSTED</small>, but it was said: <i>for thou wouldst</i> <small>VOMIT IT</small>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..31.4": "Unto this same idea was allusion made in the words: <i>It is not good to eat much honey, etc.</i> (Prov. xxv. 27); likewise in saying: <i>Be not wise over much; why shouldest thou destroy thyself</i> (Ecclesiastes vii. 16)? Allusion was further made therein by these words: <i>Keep thy foot when thou goest to the house of God</i>, etc. (<i>Ibid.</i>, iv. 17); and David also alluded thereto, saying: <i>Neither do I exercise myself in great matters, or in things too high for me</i> (Ps. cxxxi. 1). It is this same idea which they (the Sages) had in view when they said[[fn:262]]: 'Search not out that which is too obscure for thee, and scrutinize not that which is too veiled for thee; study that which is permitted unto thee (to know), and occupy not thyself with obscure things'; which is to say that one must only engage one's intelligence in that which it is possible for man to perceive; but as for that which it is not in the nature of man to perceive, it is most perilous to occupy oneself therewith, even as we have explained. This is also what they had in view when they said: 'Whosoever examins four things, etc.[[fn:263]]', a passage which they concluded with these words: 'and whosoever has no regard for the glory of his Creator'; (which contains) an allusion to that which we have just explained, to wit: that man should not precipitately approach speculation with false imaginations, and that, if doubts arise within him, or if the matter in question be not demonstrated unto him, he must not abandon and reject it, nor hasten to declare it a lie, but, on the contrary, remain calm, <i>respect the glory of his Creator</i>, and abstain and halt. This is a matter sufficiently explained.",
    "root.text.Part 1..31.5": "But the aim of these sentences pronounced by the prophets and the Sages is not to close entirely the door of speculation, nor to despoil the intellect of the perception of that which it is possible to perceive, as believe the ignorant and the slothful, who are pleased to pass off their own imperfection and stupidity for perfection and wisdom, and the perfection of others and their science for imperfection and irreligion, <i>that put darkness for light, and light for darkness</i> (Isaiah v. 20); the whole intention is, on the contrary, to declare that the intellects of mortals have a limit at which they must halt.",
    "root.text.Part 1..31.6": "One must not cavil at certain words spoken concerning the intellect in this chapter and in others; for the aim has been to guide (the reader) toward the (particular) subject that was in view, and not to fathom the nature of the intellect, a matter which shall be examined in other chapters[[fn:264]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..32.0": "Know thou that it would be most perilous to commence one's studies with this science, I mean to say, with metaphysics; likewise would it be dangerous to explain at the outset the meaning of the prophetic allegories and to direct attention to the metaphors employed in discourse, with which the prophetic books are filled. It behooves us, on the contrary, to educate the youth and to strengthen those of lesser capacity according to the measure of their understanding; and as for him who manifests a perfect mind and is prepared for this lofty degree, that is to say, for the degree of demonstrative speculation and the true argumentations of the intellect, he shall be advanced little by little until he attains unto his perfection, whether by the guidance of another or by his own endeavor.",
    "root.text.Part 1..32.1": "But when one commencs with this metaphysical science, there results therefrom not only a disturbance in belief, but absolute irreligion[[fn:265]]. I can but compare this to one who should give to a suckling babe wheaten bread and meat to eat, and wine to drink; for verily he would slay him, not because these are evil nutriments and contrary to the nature of man, but because the recipient is too weak to digest them so as to derive profit therefrom. Likewise, if metaphysical truths[[fn:266]] have been presented in an obscure and enigmatical manner, and if the learned have employed all manner of devices to teach them without pronouncing clearly thereon, it is not because they contain inwardly aught that is evil, or because they overthrow the foundations of religion, as is believed by the ignorant who pretend to have reached the degree of speculation; rather have they been veiled because the intellects, in the beginning, are incapable of receiving them, and they have been shown but dimly, so that the perfect man might know them; wherefore they are called <i>mysteries</i> and <i>secrets of the Law</i>, as we shall hereafter explain.",
    "root.text.Part 1..32.2": "For this reason has <i>Scripture spoken in the language of men</i>, even as we have explained[[fn:267]]. For it is intended to serve as the initial study and to be learned by children, by women, and by the generality of men, who are not capable of understanding things in their reality; wherefore one has confined oneself for their sake to simple authority[[fn:268]] whenever it concerned a true opinion the truth of which it was desired to proclaim, and with regard to every ideal thing[[fn:269]] attention was directed to that which might indicate to the mind that it <i>exists</i>, and not to examine the true nature of its being[[fn:270]]. But when the individual has perfected himself, and <i>the secrets of the Law are revealed unto him</i>[[fn:271]], whether by another or by himself, through their mutual combination[[fn:272]], he attains to the point of recognizing the truth of these sound opinions by the true means of establishing the truth, either by demonstration, when such is possible, or by solid arguments, when this means is practicable; and likewise he represents to himself in their reality those ideal things, which were for him but matters of imagination and figures, and he comprehends their true being.",
    "root.text.Part 1..32.3": "We have already cited on several occasions, in our discourses, this passage: “Neither shall one interpret the <i>Merkabah</i>, even to one alone, unless he be a wise man, understanding by his own knowledge; and then one shall impart to him only the first elements thereof[[fn:273]].” None, therefore, ought to be initiated into this matter, save according to the measure of his capacity and under the two following conditions: first, that he be <i>wise</i>, that is to say, that he possess the knowledge from which are drawn the preliminary notions of speculation; secondly, that he be intelligent, discerning, and of a natural perspicacity, grasping a subject by the slightest indication; and such is the meaning of the words: <i>understanding by his own knowledge</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..32.4": "The reason why it is forbidden to instruct the masses according to the true method of speculation, and to enable them, at the very outset, to form a conception of the true nature of things[[fn:274]], and why it is absolutely necessary that it should be thus and not otherwise[[fn:275]], all this I desire to explain unto thee in the following chapter. I say, then:",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.0": "The causes which prevent the commencement of instruction with metaphysical subjects, and the awakening of attention unto that which merits attention, and the presentation thereof to the common people, are five in number.",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.1": "The <small>FIRST CAUSE</small> is the difficulty of the subject in itself, its subtlety and its depth; as it has been said: <i>That which is far off</i> (<i>from our conception</i>), <i>and exceeding deep, who can find it out?</i> (Ecclesiastes vii. 24); and as it is further said: <i>But where shall wisdom be found?</i> (Job xxviii. 12). Wherefore, one must not begin instruction with that which is most difficult of comprehension and most profound. One of the allegories current in (the traditions of) our nation is the comparison of science unto water;[[fn:276]] the doctors have explained this allegory in divers manners, and (they have said) amongst others: He who knows how to swim draws pearls from the bottom of the sea, but he who is ignorant of swimming is drowned; wherefore none venturs to swim save he who has exercised himself in the learning thereof.",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.2": "The <small>SECOND CAUSE</small> is: the incapacity inherent in the minds of men in general from the outset; for man is not endowed, primarily, with his ultimate perfection, but perfection resides within him <i>potentially</i>, and in the beginning he is deprived of <i>actuality</i> (as Scripture states): <i>And man is born as a wild ass's colt</i> (Job, 11, 12). But when an individual possesses somewhat <i>potentially</i>, it is not necessarily incumbent that this should pass <i>into actuality;</i> on the contrary, the individual sometimes remains in his imperfection, whether through certain impediments, or for want of exercising himself in that which would bring this <i>potency</i> into <i>actuality</i>. It is expressly stated: <i>There are not many who become wise</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 32, 9); and the Doctors have said: «I have seen men of elevation, but they are few in number[[fn:277]];» for the obstacles to perfection are exceeding numerous, and the preoccupations which hinder it are manifold, and when, therefore, can one obtain that perfect disposition and that leisure (necessary) for study, so that what the individual possesses <i>potentially</i> may pass into <i>actuality</i>?",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.3": "The <small>THIRD CAUSE</small> is the length of the preparatory studies; for man naturally feels a desire to seek the highest points, and ofttimes he grows weary of preparatory studies or abandons them. But know thou well, that if one could arrive at any high point (of science) without the preparatory studies which must precede, these would not be preparatory studies at all, but rather useless occupations and mere superfluities. If thou shouldst awaken any man whatsoever, even the most stolid of men, as one awakens a sleeper, saying unto him: Wouldst thou not desire to know at this moment these heavens (and to know) what is their number, what is their form, and what they contain? What are the angels? How the world in its entirety was created, and what is its purpose according to the reciprocal arrangement of its parts? What is the soul, and how did it enter into the body? If the soul of man be separable (from the body), and if it be separable, how, by what means, and to what end it is so?[[fn:278]] and other such inquiries—this man would doubtless answer thee: “Yes,” and he would feel a natural desire to know these things in their reality; only he would wish to appease this desire and arrive at the knowledge of all this by a single word, or by two words which thou shouldst say unto him. Yet, if thou shouldst impose upon him (the obligation) to interrupt his business for a week, in order to comprehend all this, he would not do so, but would rather content himself with false imaginations wherewith his soul is quieted, and it would be unpleasing to him to be declared that there exists something which requirs a multitude of preliminary notions and very prolonged researches.",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.4": "As for thee, thou knowest that the subjects in question are linked one to another. In truth, there is in existence naught else than God and all His works; these latter being all that existence contains besides Him (God). There is no means of perceiving God save through His works; it is they which indicate His existence and what must be believed concerning Him, I mean, what must be affirmed or denied of Him. It is therefore necessary to examine all beings in their reality, so that from each branch (of science) we may draw true and certain principles to serve us in our metaphysical researches. How many principles are there not drawn, indeed, from the nature of numbers and from the properties of geometrical figures, (principles) by which we are led to (know) certain things which we must remove from the Divinity, and the negation of which leads us to divers (metaphysical) subjects![[fn:279]] As for the matters of astronomy and physics, there will be, I think, no doubt in thy mind that these are necessary things for understanding the relation of the universe to the government of God, such as it is in reality and not according to imaginations. There are also many speculative matters which, without furnishing principles for this (metaphysical) science, yet exercise the mind and cause it to acquire the art[[fn:280]] of demonstration and to know the truth in its essence,[[fn:281]] putting an end to the trouble which the confusion of accidental things with essential things generally brings forth in the minds of thinkers, as well as the false opinions resulting therefrom. Let us add to this (the advantage) of well conceiving these other subjects (of which we have just spoken), considered in themselves,[[fn:282]] even though they should not serve as a basis for metaphysical science. Finally, they fail not to have other advantages for certain things which lead to the attainment of this science.[[fn:283]] It is therefore necessary that he who desirs (to obtain) human perfection should first instruct himself in logic, then gradually in mathematics, then in the physical sciences, and after that in metaphysics.",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.5": "It is frequently observed that many individuals confine their intellectual pursuits to a particular branch of science, and even when their minds remain unrelaxed, it often happens that death intervenes whilst they are still engaged in preliminary studies.  If, therefore, we were never to receive an opinion through the channel of traditional authority, and were never guided by allegory in any respect, but were compelled to form a perfect conception of every subject by means of essential definitions, and to admit nothing as true except through demonstration, a condition which is only possible after these prolonged preliminary studies, it would necessarily follow that mankind, in general, would perish without even knowing whether a God exists for the universe or whether none exists, and still less would they attribute to Him a government or reject from Him an imperfection[[fn:284]].  No one would ever escape this calamity, except perhaps <i>one individual in a city or</i> (at most</i>) <i>two in a family</i>[[fn:285]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.6": "As for those who are <i>the remnants which the Eternal One calls</i>[[fn:286]], perfection, which is the ultimate goal, shall be truly acquired only after the preparatory studies. Solomon has already declared that the preparatory studies are absolutely necessary, and that it is impossible to attain true wisdom, unless one has exercised oneself; he said: <i>If the iron is dulled and has not been sharpened, shall it vanquish armies? but even more preparation is required to</i> (<i>acquire</i>) <i>wisdom</i> (Ecclesiastes, Chapter 10, verse 10)[[fn:287]]; and he said further: <i>Heed the counsel and receive instruction, that thou mayest become wise at the end</i> (Proverbs, Chapter 19, verse 20).",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.7": "That which further renders necessary the acquisition of preparatory knowledge is, that a multitude of doubts speedily present themselves to a man during his study, and that he perceivs with equal speed the objections—I mean, how certain assertions may be refuted (for it is in this as in the demolition of an edifice[[fn:288]])—whilst one cannot well establish assertions nor resolve doubts, save by means of many principles drawn from these preparatory branches of knowledge. He, therefore, who enters upon speculation without preparatory study, is like unto one who, running with all his might to reach a place, falls by the way into a deep pit whence he has no means of egress, so that he perishes; had he abstained from running and remained in his place, he would surely have done better.",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.8": "Solomon, in the Proverbs, has extensively delineated the habits of the slothful and their incapacity, all of which constitutes an allegory concerning the inability to pursue knowledge; speaking of him who desires to attain the ultimate principles (of science), and who, neglecting to acquire the preparatory knowledge conducive to these ultimate principles, does nought but desire, he expresses himself thus: <i>The desire of the sluggard kills him, for his hands refuse to labour. All the day he covets greedily: but the righteous gives and spares not</i> (Prov., 21, 25 and 26). His meaning is that, if his desire proves fatal, the cause thereof lies in his neglect to undertake that which might assuage this desire; that, on the contrary, he does nought but ardently desire, and fixes his hopes upon that for the acquisition of which he possesses no instrument; it would, therefore, have been more expedient for him to relinquish this desire. Observe how well the conclusion of the allegory explains its commencement, by stating: <i>but the</i> <small>RIGHTEOUS</small> <i>gives and spares not;</i> for <i>righteous</i> (or <i>pious</i>) can only be opposed to <i>slothful</i> according to what we have expounded. He means, indeed, that the <i>righteous</i> amongst men is he who renders unto everything that which is due to it, that is to say (who dedicates) his entire time to study and reserves nothing for other pursuits; it is as if he had said: <i>but the righteous dedicates his days to science and reserves none thereof</i>, an expression analogous to this: <i>Give not thy strength unto women</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, XXXI, 3).",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.9": "The greater part of the learned—I speak of those who possess a reputation for science—are afflicted with this malady, I mean that of seeking the ultimate ends (of science) and of discoursing thereon, without occupying themselves with preparatory studies. There are some in whom ignorance and the desire for dominion reach such a point as to make them blame those preparatory branches of knowledge which they are either incapable of grasping or too slothful to study, and who endeavour to show that they are injurious or (at the least) useless; but, when one reflects thereon, the truth is clear and manifest[[fn:289]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.10": "The <small>FOURTH CAUSE</small> is to be found in the natural dispositions; for it has been set forth and even demonstrated that the moral virtues are preparatory for the rational virtues[[fn:290]], and that the acquisition of true rational virtues—by which I mean perfect intelligible notions—is possible only unto a man who has well disciplined his morals and is calm and composed. There be many who possess, from their origin, a disposition of temperament wherewith no moral improvement is possible. He, for instance, who by nature has a heart of an exceedingly hot temperature[[fn:291]] cannot refrain from being violent, even though he should exert the greatest efforts upon himself[[fn:292]]; and he whose testicles are of a hot and moist temperament and strongly constituted, and whose seminal vessels produce much sperm, shall hardly be able to remain chaste, even though he should make extreme efforts upon himself. Likewise, thou shalt find certain men full of levity and thoughtlessness, whose very agitated and disordered movements indicate a vicious complexion and a bad temperament of which no account can be rendered[[fn:293]]. In such as these, perfection shall never be seen, and to occupy oneself with them in this matter[[fn:294]] would be pure folly on the part of him who should do so; for this science, as thou knowest, is neither medicine nor geometry, and, for the reasons we have stated, not everyone is prepared for it. It must therefore be preceded by moral preparations, so that man may attain to extreme rectitude and perfection; <i>for the froward is abomination to the Lord: but His secret is with the righteous</i> (Prov. iii. 32). This is why it is deemed ill to teach it to young men; for even these cannot receive it—having a boiling nature and a preoccupied mind, by reason of the flame of youth—until that flame which troubls them be extinguished, and they have obtained calm and tranquillity, and their heart has become humble and submissive by temperament. It is then that they will themselves desire to rise to that high degree[[fn:295]] which is the perception of God, I mean the science of metaphysics which has been designated by the name of <i>Ma’asé mercabâ</i>[[fn:296]], as Scripture says: <i>The Lord is nigh unto them that are of a broken heart</i> (Ps. xxxiv. 18) and elsewhere: <i>I dwell</i> (<i>in the</i>) <i>high and holy place, and with him also that is of a contrite and humble spirit, etc.</i> (Isaiah lvii. 15).",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.11": "It is for this reason that in the Talmud, concerning these words: <i>the first elements thereof shall be transmitted to him</i>, they (the Doctors) say[[fn:297]]: “They do not transmit even the first elements, save to a president of a tribunal, and only if he has a heart of affliction[[fn:298]];” and by this is meant humility, submission, and great piety joined to science.",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.12": "In the same place it is said: “They transmit the secrets of the Torah only to <i>a man of counsel, a wise thinker, and one who expresses himself with intelligence</i>[[fn:299]];” and these are qualities for which a natural disposition is indispensable. Knowest thou not that there are people very weak in giving an opinion, although very intelligent, whilst there is another who has a sound judgment and who knows well how to direct political affairs? It is such a one who is called <span dir=\"rtl\">יועץ</span>, <i>a man of counsel;</i> nevertheless, he might not understand an <i>intelligible</i> thing[[fn:300]], even should it approach the first notions[[fn:301]], and he might even in this respect be very stupid and without any resource: <i>Wherefore is there a price in the hand of a fool to get wisdom, seeing he has no heart to it?</i> (Prov. xvii. 16). There is another who is intelligent, of a natural penetration, and who masters the most obscure subjects, expressing himself with conciseness and accuracy[[fn:302]]—and it is he who is called <span dir=\"rtl\">נכון לחש</span>, <i>one who expresses himself with intelligence</i>—but who has not laboured and has not acquired the sciences. But he who has acquired the sciences <i>in act</i> is he who is called <span dir=\"rtl\">חכם חרשים</span>, <i>a wise thinker</i>. “When he speaks,” say the Doctors, “all become as if they were dumb[[fn:303]].”",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.13": "Observe well how they have laid down as a condition, making use of a sacred text[[fn:304]], that the person be perfectly versed in social government and in the speculative sciences, and possess, in addition thereto, natural penetration, intelligence, and a good elocution to present the subjects in such a manner as to afford a glimpse of them; and it is only then that <i>they transmit to him the secrets of the Torah</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.14": "In the same place it is said: “R. Johanan having said to R. Eleazar: Come, let me teach thee the <i>Ma’asé mercabâ</i>, the latter replied: <span dir=\"rtl\">אכתי לא קשאי</span>,” which signifies: <i>I am not yet old</i>, and I find myself still of a boiling nature and with the levity of youth. Thou seest, therefore, that they have also made age a condition, joined to those virtues of which we have just spoken; and how then could one engage in this matter with the commonalty of men, children, and women?",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.15": "The <small>FIFTH CAUSE</small> is found in the occupations necessitated by the wants of the body, which constitute the <i>first perfection</i>[[fn:305]], especially when thereunto is joined the care for wife and children, and above all when there is added the pursuit of the superfluities of life, which, through custom and evil habits, become a potent natural necessity[[fn:306]]. Verily, even the perfect man, such as we have described, when he occupies himself much with these necessary things, and a fortiori when he concerns himself with things not necessary and desirs them with ardor, his speculative aspirations wax weak and are submerged; he seeks them no longer save with lukewarmness and slackness and with little solicitude; and then he perceivs not even that which he has the faculty to perceive, or else he has a confused perception, mingled of apprehension and incapacity.",
    "root.text.Part 1..33.16": "It is by reason of all these causes that the subjects in question befit but a very small number of the select few, and not the vulgar; wherefore they must be hidden from the beginner and he must be restrained from approaching them[[fn:307]], even as a young child is prevented from taking coarse food and from lifting heavy weights.",
    "root.text.Part 1..34.0": "It is not to be supposed that all which we have preliminarily stated, in the preceding chapters, concerning the importance of the subject, its obscurity, the difficulty of apprehending it, and the reserve to be observed regarding the vulgar, applies likewise to the negation of corporeality and that of the passions[[fn:308]]. Such is not the case; but, on the contrary, just as it is necessary to instruct children[[fn:309]] and promulgate among the masses that God [may He be glorified!] is <i>one</i> and that no other save Him is to be worshipped, so likewise must they learn, by tradition, that God is not a body, that there is no resemblance, in anywise, between Him and His creatures, that His existence resembles not theirs, that His life resembles not that of creatures endowed with life, nor His knowledge that of creatures endowed with knowledge[[fn:310]], and that the difference between Him and them consists not merely in degree, but rather in the very nature of existence. I mean, that it must be established for all that our knowledge and His, or our power and His, differ not by <i>more</i> and <i>less</i>, or in that one is <i>stronger</i> and the other <i>weaker</i>, or by other similar distinctions; for the strong and the weak are necessarily alike in kind, and a single definition comprehends them both; and likewise, all proportional relation obtains only between two things of the same kind, as has also been explained in the physical sciences[[fn:311]]. But all that is attributed to God is distinct from our attributes in every respect, such that the two things cannot be comprised within a single definition; just as His existence and the existence of that which is outside of Him are called, both alike, <i>existence</i> only by homonymy, as I shall explain.",
    "root.text.Part 1..34.1": "And this should suffice for children and for the vulgar to establish in their minds that there exists a perfect Being who is not a body, nor a faculty in a body; that this Being is God, that no kind of imperfection can reach Him, and that on this account He is in no wise subject to passivity.",
    "root.text.Part 1..34.2": "What is to be said concerning the <i>attributes</i>, how they are to be removed from Him (God), what is the meaning of the attributes applied to Him, likewise what is to be said concerning the manner in which He created all things and His manner of governing the world, how His providence extends over that which is outside of Him, what is to be understood by His <i>will</i>, His <i>perception</i>, His <i>knowledge</i> of all that He knows, likewise what is to be understood by <i>Prophecy</i> and what are its different degrees, and finally what is to be understood by the names of God, which, however numerous they be, designate a single Being—all these things are profound subjects; these are, in reality, the <i>secrets of the Torah</i>, and they are the <i>mysteries</i> which are constantly spoken of in the books of the prophets and in the discourses of the doctors. These are the things of which one must teach only the <i>first elements</i>, as we have said, and even then only to such a person as we have described.",
    "root.text.Part 1..34.3": "But if the matter concerns the removal of corporeity, and the exclusion from God of any likeness to creatures or of any passions, then is it a thing whereon one must speak clearly, which must be explained to every man according to his capacity[[fn:312]] and taught as a tradition unto children, unto women, unto simple folk, and unto those who lack natural aptitude; even as they learn by tradition that God is <i>one</i>, that He is eternal, and that none other but He must be worshipped. Verily, there is no <i>unity</i> save when corporeity is removed; for a body is not <i>one</i>, but rather composed of matter and form, which, by their definition, constitute <i>two</i>, and it is moreover divisible and susceptible of being parted. And if, having received this teaching, having become accustomed thereto, and having been nurtured and grown therein, they be troubled by certain texts of the prophetical books, one shall explain the sense thereof unto them, and initiate them into the manner of interpreting them, and call their attention to the homonymies and metaphors wherewith this treatise deals, until they be convinced of the truth of the belief in the unity of God and the veracity of the prophetical books.",
    "root.text.Part 1..34.4": "As for him whose mind refrains from understanding the allegorical interpretation of the texts, and from understanding that there may be a concordance in the name despite the difference in the sense[[fn:313]], thou shalt say unto him: “The interpretation of this text is understood by men of science; but as for thee, thou shalt know that God is not a body and that He is impassible, for passivity implies change, whereas God is not subject to change, resembls nothing of all that is outside of Him, and has absolutely no definition in common with any thing whatsoever[[fn:314]]; and thou shalt also know that such discourse of a prophet is true and must be interpreted allegorically.” There one shall stop with him; but one must not let the belief in corporeity, or the belief in any thing that pertains to bodies, establish itself in any man, any more than one should allow the belief in the non-existence of God, the notion of association[[fn:315]], or the worship of another than He, to take root.",
    "root.text.Part 1..35.0": "I shall explain unto thee, when I speak of the <i>attributes</i>, in what sense it has been said that such a thing <i>pleass God</i>, or <i>provoks</i> and <i>movs Him to wrath;</i> for it is in that sense that it is said of certain persons that <i>God found pleasure in them</i>, or that <i>He was wroth</i>, or that <i>He was provoked against them</i>. This is not the purpose of this chapter; but it has for its purpose that which I am about to say.",
    "root.text.Part 1..35.1": "Know that upon examining the entire Pentateuch and all the books of the prophets, one will find the expressions of <i>wrath</i>, of <i>irritation</i>, of <i>jealousy</i> only when the discourse pertains particularly to <i>idolatry</i>; and one will find that none is designated an <i>enemy</i> of God, or <i>hostile</i> to Him, or His <i>adversary</i>, save the idolater in particular. We read, for example: … <i>and lest ye serve other gods, etc., so that the wrath of the Eternal be kindled against you</i> (Deuteronomy, 11, 16, 17); <i>Lest the wrath of the Eternal, thy God, be kindled</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 6, 15); <i>To provoke Him to anger with the work of your hands</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 31, 29); <i>They have moved Me to jealousy with that which is not God; they have provoked Me to anger with their vanities, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 32, 21); <i>For a jealous God, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 6, 15); <i>Why have they provoked Me to anger with their graven images?</i> (Jeremiah, 8, 19); <i>Because his sons and his daughters have provoked him</i> (Deuteronomy, 32, 19); <i>For a fire is kindled in My anger</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 22); <i>The Eternal</i>[[fn:316]] <i>takes vengeance on His adversaries, and He reserves wrath for His enemies</i> (Nahum, 1, 2; <i>And he repays His adversaries</i> (Deuteronomy, 7, 10); <i>Until he has cast out his enemies</i> (Numbers, 32, 21); … <i>which the Eternal, thy God, hates</i> (Deuteronomy, 16, 22); <i>All that is an abomination unto the Eternal</i>, (<i>all that</i>) <i>He hates</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 12, 31). Examples of this nature are too numerous for enumeration; but should one trace them through all the (holy) books, one will indeed discover them.",
    "root.text.Part 1..35.2": "If the prophetical books have so strongly insisted thereon, it is solely because it concerns a false opinion relating to God, I mean, idolatry. If any one believed that Zeid were standing at the moment when he is sitting, his deviation from the truth would not be so grave as the deviation of him who should believe that fire is below the air, or that water is below the earth[[fn:317]], or that the earth is a plane, and other like things; the deviation of this latter would not be as the deviation of him who should believe that the sun is composed of the element of fire, or that the heaven is a hemisphere, and other like things; the deviation of this third would not be as the deviation of him who should believe that angels eat and drink, and other like things; the deviation of this fourth would not be as the deviation of him who should believe that one must worship some other thing besides God. For in proportion as ignorance and false belief relate to a greater object, I mean to that which occupies a more important rank in Being, they possess more gravity than when they relate to that which occupies an inferior rank. By <i>false belief</i>, I mean: that one believs a thing to be the contrary of what it truly is; by <i>ignorance</i>, I mean: that one is ignorant of what it is possible to know. The ignorance of him who should be ignorant of the measure of the cone or the sphericity of the sun would not be so grave as the ignorance of him who should not know whether God exists or whether the universe has no God; and the false belief of him who should believe that the cone forms the half of the cylinder[[fn:318]] or that the sun is a disk[[fn:319]] would not be so grave as the false belief of him who should believe that God is more than <i>one</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..35.3": "Thou knowest that all those who devote themselves to the worship of idols do not adore them in the sense that there exists no divinity beside them; for never did any man of past generations imagine, nor will any man of future generations imagine, that the figure fashioned by him of metal, stone, or wood, has itself created heaven and earth, and that it is that which governs them. This latter, on the contrary, is worshipped only in the sense that it is the symbol of a thing that is intermediate between us and God, as (Scripture) clearly says: <i>Who would not fear thee, O King of nations? etc.</i> (Jeremiah, x. 7); and elsewhere: <i>And in every place incense is offered unto my name, etc.</i> (Malachi, i. 11), alluding to that which, according to them (the heathens), is the <i>first cause</i>. We have already expounded this in our great work[[fn:320]], and it is a matter which none among our co-religionists disputs.",
    "root.text.Part 1..35.4": "But, since these misbelievers, whilst believing in the existence of God, applied their false belief to something that is due unto God alone,—I mean, to the worship and veneration (due to the Divinity), as it is said: <i>And ye shall serve the Lord, etc.</i> (Exodus, xxiii. 25), to the end that His existence might be well established in the belief of the people,—and since they believed they (could render) this duty to that which is without Him [a thing which contributed to make the existence of God vanish from the belief of the people, the latter perceiving only the practices of worship, without (penetrating) the meaning, nor (knowing) the reality of Him to whom this worship is addressed], this must necessarily cause them to deserve death, as it is expressly said: <i>Thou shalt save alive nothing that breaths</i> (Deuter., xx. 16); and the reason thereof is expressly given, which is, to put an end to this false opinion, lest others be infected thereby, as it is added: <i>That they teach you not to do etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 18).",
    "root.text.Part 1..35.5": "These (the idolaters) have been called <i>enemies, adversaries, hostile</i> (to God), and it has been said that he who acts thus <i>renders</i> (<i>God</i>) <i>jealous, provoks Him to anger, and incits His wrath;</i> what then shall be the condition of him whose infidelity relats to the very essence of God, and whose belief[[fn:321]] is the reverse of what He (God) really is, I mean, who believs not in His existence, or who believs Him to be <i>two</i>, or who believs Him to be a body, or who believs Him subject to passions, or who attributs to Him any imperfection whatsoever? Such a man is indubitably worse than he who worshipps an idol, considering it as an intermediary, or because, in his opinion, it is beneficent or harmful.",
    "root.text.Part 1..35.6": "Thou must know that by believing in corporeality or (by attributing to God) any of the conditions of the body, thou <i>renderest Him jealous</i>, thou <i>provokest Him to anger</i>, thou <i>enkindlest the fire of His wrath</i>, thou art an <i>adversary, an enemy</i>, <i>hostile</i>, much more even than he who devots himself to idolatry. If it should occur to thy mind that he who believs in corporeality might be excused because he had been so reared, or because of his ignorance and the weakness of his intelligence, thou shouldst think the same with regard to him who devots himself to idolatry; for he does it only through ignorance or through education: <i>they maintain the custom of their fathers</i>[[fn:322]]. If thou shouldst say (regarding the former) that the literal sense of Scripture causs him to fall into this doubt, thou shouldst likewise know that he who worshipps idols is led to their worship only by imaginations and false ideas. There is therefore no excuse for him who, being himself incapable of meditation, follows not the authority of thinkers who seek the truth; for, verily, I do not declare him a misbeliever who does not remove corporeality (from God) by means of demonstration, but I declare him a misbeliever who believs not that it must be removed; the more so as we have the version of Onkelos and that of Jonathan ben-Uziel, who do their utmost to remove the idea of corporeality (from God). Such was the aim of this chapter.",
    "root.text.Part 1..36.0": "<i>Panîm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">פנים</span>) is a homonym, and it is so chiefly in its metaphorical relation[[fn:323]]. It is, in the first place, the name of the <i>face</i> (or of the <i>countenance</i>) of any animal; e.g.: <i>And all faces</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">פניס</span>) <i>are turned into paleness</i> (Jeremiah, xxx. 6); <i>Wherefore is your countenance</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">פניכס</span>) <i>sad</i> (Genesis, xl. 7)? The examples thereof are numerous.",
    "root.text.Part 1..36.1": "It signifies also <i>anger;</i> e.g., <i>And her countenance of wrath was no more</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">פניה</span>) (I Sam. i. 18)[[fn:324]]; and, according to this signification, it has been oft employed to denote the <i>wrath</i> and the <i>indignation</i> of God; e.g., <i>The anger</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">פני</span>) </span>of the Lord has divided them<i> (Lament. iv. 16); </i>The face<i> (</i>פני<span dir=\"rtl\">) </span>of the Lord is against them that do evil<i> (Ps. xxxiv. 17); </i>My anger<i> (</i>פנַי<span dir=\"rtl\">) </span>shall pass away, and I will give thee rest<i>[[fn:325]] (Exodus xxxiii. 14); </i>And I will set My face<i> (</i>פנַי<span dir=\"rtl\">) </span>against that man, and against his family<i> (Levit. xx. 5); there be many examples thereof.",
    "root.text.Part 1..36.2": "It signifies also the <i>presence</i> of a person and the place where he abids; e.g., <i>He was established in the presence</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">על פני</span>) <i>of all his brethren</i> (Genesis xxv. 18); <i>Before the face</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">על פני</span>) <i>of all the people I will be glorified</i> (Levit. x. 3), where the sense is: <i>in their presence;</i> .... <i>if he will not curse Thee to Thy face</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">על פניך</span>) (Job i. 11), that is to say, <i>in Thy presence, Thou being there</i>. It is according to this signification that it was said: <i>And the Lord spake unto Moses face to face</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">פנים בפנים</span>) (Exodus xxxiii. 11), which means: <i>in the presence of one another</i>, without intermediary, even as it is said elsewhere: <i>Come, let us look one another in the face</i> (II Kings xiv. 8); and as it has further been said: <i>The Lord talked with you face to face</i> (Deuter. v. 4), which, in another place, has been clearly expressed thus: <i>Ye heard the voice of the words, but ye saw no similitude; only ye heard a voice</i> (<i>Ibid.</i> iv. 12); and this it is which was called <i>face to face;</i> likewise by the words: <i>And the Lord spake unto Moses face to face</i>, naught else was expressed but what is said elsewhere concerning the manner in which God spake unto him[[fn:326]]: <i>He heard the voice of one speaking unto him</i> (Numbers vii. 89). Thus it is manifest unto thee that by <i>face to face</i> it is intended to indicate that he (Moses) heard the <i>voice</i> (divine) without the mediation of an angel. The words: <i>And My face shall not be seen</i> (Exodus xxxiii. 23) contain yet this same sense (of <i>presence</i>), that is to say: the reality of My existence as it truly is cannot be apprehended.",
    "root.text.Part 1..36.3": "<i>Panîm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">פנים</span>) is likewise an adverb of place (signifying <i>before</i>), which is expressed in Arabic by <i>imâm</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(أمام)</span> or <i>beîn yedei</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(بين يدى)</span>, and it is oft employed in this sense when speaking of God; e.g., <span dir=\"rtl\">לפני י״י</span>, <i>before the Lord</i> (Genesis xviii. 22, and <i>passim</i>). It is in this sense also that the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ופני לא יראו</span> (<i>And My face shall not be seen</i>) are taken in the interpretation of Onkelos, who says: <span dir=\"rtl\">ודקדמי לא יתחזון</span>, <i>and those that are before Me shall not be seen</i>, to indicate that there be likewise sublime creatures which man cannot perceive in their reality, and which are <i>the separate Intelligences</i>[[fn:327]]; these have been brought into relation with God, as being constantly <i>before Him</i> and <i>in His presence</i>, because Providence cleavs strongly and constantly unto them[[fn:328]]. That which, according to him—I mean according to Onkelos—can be perceived in reality, are those things which occupy, in Being, a rank inferior to those others—I mean such as have <i>matter</i> and <i>form</i>[[fn:329]]; and it is with regard to these things that he says (in his paraphrase): <span dir=\"rtl\">ותחזי ית דבתראי</span> <i>And thou shalt see that which is behind Me</i>, that is to say, the beings from which, as it were, I turn away and which I leave behind Me, (which is said) allegorically to indicate their remoteness from the Divine Being. Thou shalt hear later my interpretation of the request of Moses[[fn:330]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..36.4": "<i>Panîm</i> is also an adverb of time signifying <i>before</i> (<i>aforetime</i>) or <i>of old;</i> e.g., <i>In former time</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לפנים</span>) <i>in Israel, etc.</i> (Ruth iv. 7); <i>Of old</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לפנים</span>) <i>hast Thou laid the foundation of the earth</i> (Ps. cii. 26).",
    "root.text.Part 1..36.5": "Finally <i>panîm</i> signifies also <i>égard</i> and <i>attention</i> (or <i>soin</i>); for example: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא תשא פני דל</span> <i>Thou shalt not regard the poor</i> (Leviticus, 19, 15); <span dir=\"rtl\">ונשוא פנים</span> <i>And the respected man</i> (Isaiah, 3, 3); <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר לא ישא פנים</span> <i>Who has no regard</i> (Deuteronomy, 10, 17), and many other passages. And it is also in this sense that it has been said: <i>That the Lord turns His face</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">פניו</span>) <i>toward thee and givs thee peace</i> (Numbers, 6, 25), which means that Providence<i> accompanies us.",
    "root.text.Part 1..37.0": "<i>A’hôr</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אחור</span>) is a homonym which signifies <i>dos</i> (or <i>partie de derrière</i>); for example: <i>The derrière</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אחורי</span>) <i>of the tabernacle</i> (Exodus, Chapter 26, verse 12); <i>And the lance issued forth from behind</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מאחריו</span>) (II Samuel, Chapter 2, verse 23). It is also an adverb of time signifying <i>after;</i> for example: <i>And after him</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ואחריו</span>) <i>he did not rise as he did</i> (II Kings, Chapter 23, verse 25); <i>After</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אחר</span>) <i>these things</i> (Genesis, Chapter 15, verse 1); and numerous examples are to be found.",
    "root.text.Part 1..37.1": "It also conveys the sense of <i>following</i>, <i>walking in the footsteps</i> of a person, by imitating their conduct; for example: <i>You shall walk after</i> (post, <span dir=\"rtl\">אחרי</span>) <i>the Eternal, your God</i> (Deuteronomy, 13, 5); <i>They shall walk after</i> (post, <span dir=\"rtl\">אחרי</span>) <i>the Eternal</i> (Hosea, 11, 10), which signifies <i>obeying God</i>, of <i>walking in the footsteps of His actions</i> and of <i>imitating His conduct;</i> (similarly:) <i>He has walked after</i> (post, <span dir=\"rtl\">אחרי</span>) <i>a</i> (vanus, <i>vain</i>) <i>command</i> (ius, <i>Ibid.</i>, 5, 11).— It is in this sense that it has been said: <i>And thou shalt see me behind</i> (post, <span dir=\"rtl\">אחורי</span>) (Exodus, 33, 23), (which signifies) thou shalt grasp that which follows me, that which is akin to me and that which results from my will, namely all my creatures, as I shall explain in one of the chapters of this treatise[[fn:331]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..38.0": "<i>Leb</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לב</span>) is a homonym which denotes (originally) the <i>heart</i>, I mean the member in which, for every being endowed with it, resides the principle of life[[fn:332]]; p. ex.: <i>And he thrust them into the heart</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בלב</span>) <i>of Absalom</i> (II Sam., 18, 14).",
    "root.text.Part 1..38.1": "And as this organ is found in the midst of the body, the <i>midst</i> of any thing has been thus metaphorically so called; e.g.: <i>Unto the heart</i> or <i>the midst</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לב</span>) <i>of heaven</i> (Deut. iv. 11); <i>In the midst</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לבת</span>) <i>of the fire</i> (Exodus, iii. 2).",
    "root.text.Part 1..38.2": "This is also the name of the <i>thought</i>; p. e. g.: <i>My heart</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לבי</span>) <i>has it not followed etc.</i> (II Kings, 5, 26); that is to say, I was present by my <i>thought</i> when such and such a thing occurred. In the same sense (it is said): <i>And you shall not incline after your heart</i> (Numbers, 15, 39), that is to say, you shall not follow your <i>thoughts</i> (and elsewhere:) <i>whose heart turns away today</i> (Deuteronomy, 29, 18), (that is to say) whose <i>thought</i> turns away.",
    "root.text.Part 1..38.3": "It signifies further <i>opinion</i> (or <i>sentiment</i>); e.g.: <i>All the rest of Israel were of one heart</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לב</span>) <i>to make David king</i> (I Chron. xii. 38), that is to say, of the same <i>sentiment;</i> likewise: <i>And fools die for want of heart</i> (Prov. x. 21), which is to say: for want of <i>good sense</i>. The same obtains in this passage: <i>My heart has never turned aside</i> (Job xxvii. 6), which signifies: my <i>sentiment</i> has never turned away nor strayed from this thing; for the beginning of the verse says: <i>My righteousness I hold fast, and will not let it go</i>, (which well accords with these words:) <i>and my heart has never turned aside</i>[[fn:333]]. The sense which I give here to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">יחרף</span>, I find likewise in <span dir=\"rtl\">שפחה נחרפת לאיש</span> (Levit. xix. 20), — the word <span dir=\"rtl\">נחרפת</span> being analogous to the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">منحرفة</span> , <i>changed, turned aside</i>, — that is to say, “a bondmaid whose bonds of servitude have been <i>changed</i> into bonds of marriage[[fn:334]]”.",
    "root.text.Part 1..38.4": "The term <i>Leb</i> (heart) also signifies <i>volonté</i> (or <i>intention</i>); for instance: <i>And I will give you shepherds according to my heart</i> (Jeremiah, 3, 15)[[fn:335]]; <i>Is thy heart upright as mine is</i> (II Kings, 10, 15)? that is to say, is thy <i>volonté</i> (thy <i>intention</i>) in uprightness as mine? In this sense, it is sometimes employed metaphorically when speaking of God; for example: <i>He will act according to what is in my heart and in my soul</i> (I Samuel, 2, 35), that is to say, he will act according to my <i>volonté; And my eyes and my heart shall be there always</i> (I Kings, 9, 3), that is to say, my providence and my <i>volonté</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..38.5": "— It signifies moreover <i>intellect;</i> e.g.: <i>Vain</i> (<i>hollow-minded</i>) <i>man shall be endowed with heart</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ילבב</span>) (Job xi. 12), that is to say, shall become <i>intelligent;</i> likewise: <i>A wise man's heart is at his right hand</i> (Eccles. x. 2), that is, his <i>intellect</i> (turns) towards perfect things. Numerous examples thereof are to be found. It is in this signification that it must be taken wheresoever it is metaphorically applied to God, I mean as designating the <i>intellect</i>, save for those rare exceptions where it designats the <i>will</i>, according to the context of each passage. Likewise the expressions: <i>And thou shalt consider it in thine heart</i> (Deuter. iv. 39), <i>And none considers in his heart</i> (Isaiah xliv. 19), and all other such expressions, have all the sense of <i>intellectual consideration</i>, as it is expressly said: <i>Yet the Lord has not given you an heart to perceive, etc.</i> (Deuter. xxix. 4), which is similar to this other expression: <i>Unto thee it was shewed, that thou mightest know, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, iv. 35)[[fn:336]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..38.6": "As for these words: <i>And thou shalt love the Lord thy God with all thine heart</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, vi. 5), the sense is, in my opinion: <i>with all the forces of thy heart</i>, that is to say <i>with all the faculties of the body;</i> for they all take their origin in the heart. Hereby is meant: thou shalt have as thy goal, in all thy deeds, the perception of God, as we have set forth in the commentary upon the <i>Mishnah</i>[[fn:337]] and in the <i>Mishneh Torah</i>[[fn:338]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..39.0": "<i>Roua’h</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>) is a homonym which designates first of all the <i>air</i>, I mean one of the four elements; for example: <i>And the </i>air<span dir=\"rtl\"> (</span>רוח<i>) </i>of God was hovering[[t:12]] (Genesis, 1, 2)[[fn:339]]. ",
    "root.text.Part 1..39.1": "It is then the name of the <i>wind</i> that blows; p. ex.: <i>And the wind</i> ('ruach' <span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>) <i>of the East bore away the locusts</i> (Exodus, 10, 13); <i>A wind</i> ('ruach' <span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>) <i>of the West</i> ('ibid.' <i>, </i>V<small>. 19); examples of this kind are numerous.",
    "root.text.Part 1..39.2": "It is also the name of the <i>vital spirit</i>[[fn:340]]; e.g.: <i>A spirit</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>) <i>which departs and returns not</i> (Ps. 78, 39); <i>Who has within him a spirit</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>) <i>of life</i> (Genesis, 7, 15).",
    "root.text.Part 1..39.3": "This is still the name of the thing that remains of man after death, and which is not subject to perishing; e.g.: <i>And the spirit</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">והרוח</span>) <i>returns to God who gave it</i> (Eccl., XII, 7).",
    "root.text.Part 1..39.4": "It denots, moreover, the <i>inspiration</i> of divine intellect which emanats (from God) upon the prophets, and wherewith they prophesy [as we shall explain unto thee when we treat of prophecy in such wise as is meet for this treatise]; for example: <i>And I will take of the spirit</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מן הרוח</span>) <i>which is upon thee and I will put it upon them</i> (Numbers xi. 17); <i>And when the spirit</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הרוח</span>) <i>rested upon them</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 25); <i>The spirit</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>) <i>of the Lord spake by me</i> (II Sam. xxiii. 2); of this there be numerous examples.",
    "root.text.Part 1..39.5": "Finally, this word signifies <i>intention</i> and <i>will</i> (or <i>design</i>); for example: <i>A fool utters all</i> (<i>that which is in</i>) <i>his spirit</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רוחו</span>) (Prov. xxix. 11), that is to say, his <i>intention</i> and his <i>purpose</i>. Likewise: <i>The spirit</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>) <i>of Egypt shall fail in the midst thereof, and I will destroy the counsel thereof</i> (Isaiah xix. 3), which is to say: her designs shall be confounded and the art of governance shall be hidden from her; likewise: <i>Who has measured the spirit</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">את־רוח</span>) <i>of the Lord, or where is the man who might make known unto us</i>[[fn:341]] <i>that which He purposs</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, xl. 13)? which signifies: “Who is he that knows the order of His <i>will</i> or grasps the manner of His governance of the universe, that he might make it known unto us?”—a subject which we shall set forth in certain chapters concerning the governance (of the universe)[[fn:342]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..39.6": "Whenever the word <i>ruaḥ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>) is attributed to God, it is according to the fifth signification[[fn:343]], and sometimes it is in the last sense, which is that of <i>will</i>, as we have set forth; it must therefore be explained in every passage according to that which the context of the discourse indicats.",
    "root.text.Part 1..40.0": "<i>Néphesch</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נפש</span>) is a homonym which designates, in the first instance, the <i>animal soul</i> common to all beings endowed with sensibility[[fn:344]]; e.g.: … <i>having a soul</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נפש</span>) <i>living</i> (Genesis, 1, 30). Then it denotes the <i>blood;</i> e.g.: <i>And thou shalt not eat the soul</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הנפש</span>) <i>with the flesh</i> (Deuteronomy, 12, 23). Next, it is the name of the <i>rational soul</i>, that is to say, of (that which constitutes) the <i>form of man;</i> e.g.: <i>By the living God who has made us this soul</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">את־הנפש הזאת</span>) (Jeremiah, 38, 16). It is, moreover, the name of that which remains of man after death[[fn:345]]; e.g.: <i>The soul</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נפש</span>) <i>of my lord shall be bound in the bundle of life</i> (I Samuel, 25, 29). Lastly, this word signifies <i>will;</i> e.g.: <i>To bind his princes at his pleasure</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בנפשו</span>) (Psalm 105, 22), that is to say, at his <i>will;</i> likewise: <i>And deliver him not unto the will</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בנפש</span>) <i>of his enemies</i> (Psalm 41, 3)[[fn:346]], that is to say, deliver him not unto their <i>will</i>. One takes in the same sense (the words): <i>If it be in your soul</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">את־נפשכם</span>) <i>to bury my dead</i> (Genesis, 23, 8), that is to say, if it be in your <i>intention</i> and in your <i>will;</i> likewise: <i>Though Moses and Samuel stood before me, yet my soul</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נפשי</span>) <i>could not be toward this people</i> (Jeremiah, 15, 1), where the sense is: my <i>will</i> would not be for them, that is to say, I would not desire to preserve them. Whenever the word <i>néphesch</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נפש</span>) is attributed to God, it bears the sense of <i>will</i>, as one has stated previously concerning the words: <i>He shall do according to that which is in mine heart and in my soul</i> (I Samuel, 2, 35)[[fn:347]], the meaning of which is: in my <i>will</i> and in my <i>intention</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..40.1": "According to this signification the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ותקצר נפשו בעמל ישראל</span> (Judges, x. 16) must be explained thus: <i>and His will ceased from afflicting Israel</i>. Jonathan ben-Uziel has not translated this passage; for having understood it according to the first signification (of the word <i>nephesh</i>), there resulted for him a passivity (attributed to God), and for that reason he abstained from translating it[[fn:348]]. But if one takes (the word <i>nephesh</i>) in the last signification, the explanation (of our passage) is right clear; for it is said previously that Divine Providence had forsaken them, so that they perished, and that they had implored His succour, but He succoured them not. When, therefore, they showed an exceeding repentance, being in a profound humiliation and under the dominion of the enemy[[fn:349]], He had pity upon them, <i>and His will ceased from causing their affliction and their humiliation to endure</i>. Thou must well consider this, for it is a remarkable (explanation). The <span dir=\"rtl\">ב</span> in <span dir=\"rtl\">בעמל ישראל</span> is in the place of <span dir=\"rtl\">מן</span>, <i>from;</i> it is as if one had said <span dir=\"rtl\">מן עמל ישראל</span>. The grammarians have enumerated many such examples, as: <span dir=\"rtl\">והנותר בבשר ובלחם</span> <i>and that which remains</i> <small>OF</small> <i>the flesh and</i> <small>OF</small> <i>the bread</i> (Levit., viii. 32); <span dir=\"rtl\">נשאר בשנים</span> <i>that which remains</i> <small>OF</small> <i>the years</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, xxv. 52); <span dir=\"rtl\">בגר ובאזרח הארץ</span> (<i>whether he be</i>) <small>OF</small> <i>strangers or</i> <small>OF</small> <i>those born in the land</i> (Exodus, xii. 19)[[fn:350]]. This is frequently found.",
    "root.text.Part 1..41.0": "Hay <i> (['living', 'alive'] </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חי</span> <i>vivant, vivre</i>) is said of that which has growth and sensibility[[fn:351]]; p. ex.: <i>All that moves which is living </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חי</span>) (Genesis, 9, 3). It also signifies <i>to recover</i> from a very violent disease; p. ex.: <i>When he was recovered </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויחי</span>) <i>from his disease </i> (Isaiah, 38, 9); (<i>Iis remained</i>) <i>in the camp until they were recovered </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עד חיותם</span>) (Joshua, 5, 8); similarly: <i>of sound flesh </i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חי</span>) (Leviticus, 13, 10).",
    "root.text.Part 1..41.1": "Similarly the word <i>muth</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מות</span>), which signifies <i>to die</i>, denots also a <i>violent sickness;</i> e.g.: <i>And his heart died</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וימת</span>) <i>within him, and he became as a stone</i> (I Sam., xxv. 37), which denots the violence of the sickness (of Nabal). Wherefore it was explicitly said of the son of the woman of Zarephath: <i>And his sickness was so sore, that there was no breath</i> (<i>of life</i>) <i>left in him</i> (I Kings, xvii. 17); for had it been simply said, <span dir=\"rtl\">and he died</span> ([[t:22]]וימת[[t:23]]), one might have thought that it concerned only a violent sickness bordering upon death, like unto that of Nabal when he heard the news[[fn:352]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..41.2": "In fact, someone from Andalusia said (concerning the son of the woman of Sarepta) that he ceased to breathe, so that no breath was visible in him, as sometimes occurs in cases of apoplexy and <i>hysterical suffocation</i>[[fn:353]], to such an extent that one does not know if (the patient) is dead or alive, a doubt that may last for one or two days.",
    "root.text.Part 1..41.3": "This word (<span dir=\"rtl\">חי</span>) is also frequently employed when treating of the acquisition of knowledge; e. g.: <i>And they shall be life</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חיים</span>) <i>unto thy soul</i> (Prov., 3, 22); <i>For whoso finds me finds life</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 9, 35); <i>For they are life unto those that find them</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 4, 22), and many other passages. In conformity with this, sound opinions have been denominated <i>life</i>, and corrupt opinions, <i>death;</i> the Most High has said: <i>See, I have set before thee this day life and good, and death and evil</i> (Deuteronomy, 30, 15), where the two words are clearly explained by declaring that good is <i>life</i>, and evil, <i>death</i>. I interpret in like manner the words: <i>That ye may live, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 5, 30); and this in accordance with the traditional explanation of this passage: <i>That thou mayest be happy, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 22, 7)[[fn:354]]. It is in conformity with this metaphorical sense (of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חי</span>), so prevalent in the (Hebrew) language, that the doctors have said: “Pious men, even after their death, are called <i>living</i>; and the wicked, even during their life, are called <i>dead</i>[[fn:355]].” It is essential that thou impress this upon thy mind.",
    "root.text.Part 1..42.0": "<i>Canaph</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כנף</span>) is an homonym, and it is so especially in relation to metaphorical usage. It originally denotes the <i>wing</i> of birds; e.g.: … <i>of every bird having wings</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כנף</span>) <i>which soars in the heavens</i> (Deutér., 4, 17).",
    "root.text.Part 1..42.1": "Subsequently, we have employed it metaphorically to signify the extremities and the angles of garments; for example: <i>Aux quatre coins</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כנפות</span>) <i>of thy garment</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 22, 12).",
    "root.text.Part 1..42.2": "It has furthermore been employed metaphorically to denote the extremities and borders of the inhabited part of the earth, remote from our countries; e.g.: <i>That it might take hold of the ends</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בכנפות</span>) <i>of the earth</i> (Job xxxviii. 13); <i>From the uttermost part</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מכנף</span>) <i>of the earth have we heard songs</i> (Isaiah xxiv. 16).",
    "root.text.Part 1..42.3": "Ibn Janah[[fn:356]] says that this word is also found in the sense of <i>to veil</i> (<i>to hide, to conceal</i>), after the manner of the Arabic, in which it is said[[fn:357]] <i>kanaftu al-shei</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(كَنَفْتُ الشَّيْءَ)</span> in the sense of: <i>I have concealed the thing</i>; and he explains <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יכנף עוד מוריך</span> (Isaiah xxx. 20): <i>and he that enlightens thee</i>[[fn:358]] <i>shall no longer hide himself from thee, nor be veiled</i>, which is a right good interpretation. Thence comes, in my judgment, the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יגלה כנף אביו</span> (Deut. xxiii. 1), that is to say: <i>he shall not lift the veil of his father</i>[[fn:359]]. Likewise the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ופרשת כנפך על אמתך</span> (Ruth iii. 9) must be explained, according to my view: <i>spread thy veil over thy handmaid</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..42.4": "It is in this latter sense, as I deem, that the word <i>kanaph</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כנף</span>) has been metaphorically applied to the Creator, and likewise to the angels; for the angels, in our opinion, possess no body, even as I shall hereafter set forth[[fn:360]]. Thus the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר באת לחסות תחת כנפיו</span> (Ruth ii. 12) must be rendered: <i>under whose veil thou art come to trust</i>[[fn:361]]. Likewise, whenever the word <i>kanaph</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כנף</span>) relats to angels, it has the signification of <i>veil</i>. Hast thou not observed these words: <i>With twain</i> (<i>wings</i>) <i>he</i> <small>COVERED</small> <i>his face, and with twain he</i> <small>COVERED</small> <i>his feet</i> (Isaiah vi. 2)? Verily, the cause of its existence—I speak of that of the angel—is enshrouded in obscurity[[fn:362]], and this is what is called <i>his face</i>. In like manner, those things of which the angel is the cause, and which are denoted by <i>his feet</i> [as we have explained in treating of the homonym <i>regel</i>[[fn:363]]], are likewise obscure; for the action of the <i>Intelligences</i>[[fn:364]] is obscure, and their influence[[fn:365]] is not manifest unto us save when we have devoted ourselves to study, and this for two reasons, of which one resids in them and the other in us—I mean the weakness of our understanding and the difficulty of comprehending the <i>Separate Intelligence</i> in its reality. As for the words: <i>and with twain he did fly</i> (<i>ibid.</i>), I shall explain in a separate chapter the reason wherefore the motion of flight has been attributed to angels[[fn:366]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..43.0": "<i>Ayin</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עין</span>) is an homonym signifying a <i>fountain</i> of water; as, e.g.: <i>By the fountain</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עין</span>) <i>of water in the wilderness</i> (Genesis xvi. 7), and it is also the name for the <i>eye</i>, the instrument of vision[[fn:367]]; as: <i>Eye</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עין</span>) <i>for eye</i> (Exodus xxi. 24). It signifies also <i>care</i> (or <i>regard</i>), as it is said in reference to Jeremiah: <i>Take him and look well</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עיניך</span>) <i>to him</i> (Jeremiah xxxix. 12), which is to say: have a care for him. And it is according to this metaphor that it must be understood wherever it is applied to God; e.g.: <i>Mine eyes</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עיני</span>) <i>and mine heart shall be there perpetually</i> (1 Kings ix. 3), that is to say, my <i>Providence</i> and my purpose, even as we have formerly stated[[fn:368]]; ... <i>whereon the eyes</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עיני</span>) <i>of the Lord thy God are always</i> (Deut. xi. 12), that is to say, upon which (watches) His <i>Providence; The eyes</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עיני</span>) <i>of the Lord which run to and fro, etc.</i> (Zechariah iv. 10)[[fn:369]], that is, His <i>Providence</i> encompasses all that is upon the earth, as shall be declared in other chapters treating of <i>Providence</i>[[fn:370]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..43.1": "If there be joined, in speaking of the <i>eyes</i> (of God), the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ראה</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">חזה</span>, <i>to see</i>, as, for instance: <i>Open thine eyes and see</i> (2 Kings xix. 16); <i>His eyes behold</i> (Ps. xi. 4), there is ever denoted thereby an <i>intellectual</i> perception, and not a <i>sensory</i> one; for <i>to perceive by the senses</i> is ever to be passive, to be affected[[fn:371]], as thou knowest, whereas God is <i>active</i>, and not subject to <i>passivity</i>, even as I shall demonstrate.",
    "root.text.Part 1..44.0": "<i>Shama’</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שמע</span>) is an homonym signifying <i>to hear</i> (to hearken) and also <i>to accept</i> (to listen, to obey). For the signification of <i>to hear</i>, one may cite: <i>It shall not be heard</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לא ישמע</span>) <i>out of thy mouth</i> (Exodus xxiii. 13); <i>And the fame thereof was heard</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נשמע</span>) <i>in Pharaoh’s house</i> (Genesis xlv. 16), and many other examples.",
    "root.text.Part 1..44.1": "Examples are also numerous for the employment of <i>schama’</i> in the sense of <i>accueillir</i> or of <i>écouter</i>, such as: <i>But they heeded not</i> (they did not hear <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא שמעו</span>) <i>Moses</i> (Exodus, 6, 9); <i>If they obey Him</i> (they will hear <span dir=\"rtl\">ישמעו</span>) <i>and that they may adore Him</i> (Job, 36, 11); <i>Will you then hearken unto us</i> (will you listen <span dir=\"rtl\">הנשמע</span>) (Nehemiah, 13, 27)? … <i>and who will not obey</i> (and will not hear <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא ישמע</span>) thy words (Joshua, 1, 18).",
    "root.text.Part 1..44.2": "—It is also said (for <i>to understand</i>) in the sense of <i>to know</i> and of <i>to know</i>; p. ex.: <i>A nation of which thou shalt not hear</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לא תשמע</span>) <i>the language</i> (Deutér., 28, 49), that is to say, of which thou shalt not <i>know</i> the language.",
    "root.text.Part 1..44.3": "Whenever the verb <i>shama’</i> is applied to God, and when, according to the literal sense, it would be taken in the first signification, it denots <i>perception</i>, which appertains to the third signification; e.g.: <i>And the Lord heard it</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וישמע</span>) (Numbers, 11, 1), <i>For that He hears</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בשמעו</span>) <i>your murmurings</i> (Exodus, 16, 7), in which it everywhere concerns a <i>perception of knowledge</i>[[fn:372]]. But when, according to the literal sense, it would be taken in the second signification[[fn:373]], it signifies that God has or has not <i>granted</i> the prayer of him that prayed; e.g.: <i>I will surely hear</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אשמע</span>) <i>his cry</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 22, 23); <i>I will hear it</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ושמעתי</span>), <i>for I am gracious</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 27); <i>Incline Thine ear, O Lord, and hear</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ושמע</span>) (II Kings, 19, 16); <i>And the Lord would not hearken</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ולא שמע</span>) <i>to your voice, nor give ear unto you</i> (Deuter., 1, 45); <i>Yea, when ye make many prayers, I will not hear</i> (Isaiah, 1, 15); <i>For I will not hear thee</i> (Jeremiah, 7, 16), and many other examples.",
    "root.text.Part 1..44.4": "Thou shalt find yet further on, concerning these metaphors and anthropomorphisms, that which shall quench thy thirst[[fn:374]] and resolve thy doubts, and all their significations shall be explained unto thee, so that naught shall remain obscure in any respect[[fn:375]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.0": "We have already stated, in one of the chapters of this treatise[[fn:376]], that there is a great difference between leading one to the (mere notion of the) existence of a thing, and searching into its essence and substance. Verily, one may direct the mind toward the existence of a thing, even by means of its accidents or its actions, or indeed by means of very remote relations which may exist between that thing and others.",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.1": "If, for example, thou shouldst desire to make known the sovereign of a realm to some inhabitant of his country who knew him not, thou mightest, in order to make him known and to draw attention to his existence, proceed in manifold ways. Thou wouldst say, for instance: He is a person of lofty stature, white of hue and with grey hair; and thou wouldst thus make him known by his accidents. Or else thou wouldst say: He is that one around whom is seen a great multitude of men on horse and on foot, who is environed by naked swords, above whose head banners are raised, and before whom trumpets are sounded; or again: He is he who dwells in the palace existing in such a city of this realm; or again: He is he who has commanded the raising of this wall or the construction of this bridge; or finally (thou wouldst designate him) by others of his actions and of his relations with certain things. Thou mightest also indicate his existence by circumstances less manifest than these. Someone, for example, might ask thee: Has this country a sovereign? and thou wouldst answer: Yea, without doubt; but (he would rejoin) what is the proof thereof? “The money-changer here present,” thou wouldst reply, “is, as thou seest, a weak man of a puny body, and has before him this great quantity of gold pieces, and that other individual, portly and strong, but poor, standing before him, asks of him the charity of a mite[[fn:377]], a thing which he does not, treating him, on the contrary, with abruptness, and repulsing him with his words; yet (the poor man), were it not for the fear of the sovereign (which restrains him), would hasten to slay him or to thrust him back, and would take the money which he has in his hands: here then is a proof that this state possesses a king.” Thou wouldst thus demonstrate his existence by the good order which reigns in the state, and which has for its cause the fear inspired by the sovereign and the expectation of being punished by him. In all that we have just cited as an example, there is naught which indicats the essence of the sovereign and his true substance in so far as he is sovereign.",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.2": "Such is that which has come to pass in all the books of the prophets, and likewise in the Pentateuch, when it was a question of making God known; for, as there was a necessity to direct all men towards (the knowledge of) the existence of God and (to make them understand) that He possesses all perfections,—that is to say, that He <i>exists</i> not solely as the earth exists and as the heaven exists, but that He exists as a living being possessing knowledge, power, action, and other things which must be believed regarding His existence and which shall be set forth further on,—the minds of men were led by the idea[[fn:378]] of corporeity to (understand) that He <i>exists</i>, and by the idea of motion to (understand) that He <i>is living</i>. In effect, the vulgar consider only the body as a thing of solid, true, and indubitable existence: everything which is not itself a body, but is found within a body, is (considered as) <i>existing</i>, yet of an existence lesser than that of the body, needing the body in order to exist; but that which is in no wise a body nor is found within a body is not, according to what man conceivs at first and especially according to the imagination, a thing that has existence. Likewise, the vulgar form of <i>life</i> no other idea than motion, and everything which movs not with a spontaneous motion in space is not <i>living</i> (in their eyes), although motion be not in the substance of that which lives, but be only an accident inherent therein[[fn:379]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.3": "In like manner, the perception which is most familiar unto us is effected by the senses, notably by hearing and sight; we obtain not the knowledge of anything, nor can we figure to ourselves the transmission of an idea from the soul of one individual to that of another, save by means of language[[fn:380]], that is to say, of the sound articulated by the lips and the tongue, and the other organs of speech. When, therefore, it was desired also to lead our mind to (understand) that God <i>perceivs</i> and that certain things are communicated by Him to the prophets, in order that these might communicate them unto us, He was presented unto us (on the one hand) as if He <i>heard</i> and <i>saw</i>,—which is to say that He <i>perceivs</i> those things which are <i>seen</i> and <i>heard</i> and that He <i>knows</i> them,—and He was presented unto us (on the other hand) as if He <i>spake</i>,—which is to say that certain things are communicated by Him to the prophets—; and therein lies the sense of prophecy, a matter which shall be set forth with particular care[[fn:381]]. Furthermore, as we understand not that we can <i>produce</i> any object otherwise than by <i>making</i> it by manual operation, God was presented as <i>acting</i> (or <i>making</i> things). Likewise again, as the vulgar understand by <i>that which is living</i> naught else but that which is endowed with a <i>soul</i>, God was also presented as having a <i>soul</i>, which [although the name of the soul (<span dir=\"rtl\">נפשׁ</span>) be homonymous, as has been explained[[fn:382]]] signifies that He is <i>living</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.4": "Now, as it would not be conceived that we ourselves could accomplish all these actions otherwise than by means of corporeal organs[[fn:383]], there have been metaphorically attributed unto God all the organs: those by which local motion is effected, that is to say, the feet and the soles of the feet; those by which hearing, sight, and smell take place, that is to say, the ear, the eye, and the nose; those by means of which one speaks, as well as the matter of speech[[fn:384]], that is to say, the mouth, the tongue, and the voice; those, finally, by which each of us operats whilst working, that is to say, the hands, the fingers, the palm, and the arm. It results from all this, in sum, that there have been metaphorically attributed unto God [may He be exalted above all imperfection!] corporeal organs, in order to indicate thereby His actions, and that these actions themselves have been metaphorically attributed unto Him, in order to indicate thereby some perfection which consists not in the action itself. Thus, for example, there have been attributed unto Him the eye, the ear, the hand, the mouth, and the tongue, in order to indicate thereby sight, hearing, action, and speech. But sight and hearing have been attributed unto Him to indicate <i>perception</i> in general. [Wherefore thou shalt find that the Hebrew tongue putts the perception of one sense in the place of the perception of another sense; e.g.: <i>Behold the word of the Lord</i> (Jeremiah ii. 31) for: <i>hearken</i>, for what was intended to be said thereby was: <i>apprehend the sense of His word;</i> likewise: <i>See the smell of my son</i> (Genesis xxvii. 27) for: <i>smell the odour of my son</i>, for it concerns the <i>perception</i> of his smell. It is in accordance with this that it has been said: <i>And all the people</i> <small>SAW</small> <i>the thunderings</i> (Exodus xx. 15),—although, moreover, this scene was a <i>prophetic vision</i>, which is a thing known, proclaimed by national traditions[[fn:385]].] Action and speech have been attributed unto Him to indicate some influence emanating from Him, as shall be set forth[[fn:386]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.5": "Thus, every corporeal organ which thou findest (attributed unto God) in all the prophetic books, is either an organ of locomotion to indicate <i>life</i>, or an organ of sensation to indicate <i>perception</i>, or an organ of touch to indicate <i>action</i>, or an organ of speech to indicate the <i>influence</i> of the Intelligences[[fn:387]] upon the prophets, even as shall be set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.6": "All these metaphors serve, then, to direct us in such wise as to confirm within us the conviction that there exists a <i>living</i> Being who <i>works</i> all that is external to Himself, and who likewise <i>perceivs</i> His own handiwork. We shall explain, when we come to treat of the negation of attributes, how all this is reduced to a single thing, to wit, the sole essence of God; for the aim of this chapter is none other than to explain the meaning of those corporeal organs ascribed unto God [exalted be He above all imperfection!], and to show that they do but indicate the actions pertaining unto them—actions which, for us, constitute a perfection—in order to manifest unto us[[fn:388]] that He possesses all manner of perfections, even as we have been taught by that maxim: <i>The Torah speaks according to the language of the sons of men</i>[[fn:389]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.7.sub_0": "As touching the organs of locomotion ascribed unto God, it is said, for example: <i>The footstool of my feet</i> (Isaiah lxvi. 1); <i>And the place of the soles of my feet</i> (Ezek. xliii. 7). As for the organs of touch ascribed unto God, there is said: <i>The hand of the Lord</i> (Exod. ix. 3 et <i>passim</i>); <i>With the finger of God</i> (<i>Ibid.</i> xxxi. 18); <i>The work of thy fingers</i> (Ps. viii. 4); <i>And thou hast laid thine hand upon me</i> (<i>Ibid.</i> cxxxix. 5); <i>And to whom is the arm of the Lord revealed</i> (Isaiah liii. 1); <i>Thy right hand, O Lord</i> (Exod. xv. 6). Unto Him have been ascribed the organs of speech, it being said: <i>The mouth of the Lord has spoken</i> (Isaiah i. 20 et <i>passim</i>); … <i>and that he would open his lips against thee</i> (Job xi. 5); <i>The voice of the Lord is powerful</i> (Ps. xxix. 4); <i>And his tongue as a devouring fire</i> (Isaiah xxx. 27). Finally, there are ascribed unto Him the organs of sensation, it being said: <i>His eyes behold, his eyelids try the children of men</i> (Ps. xi. 4); <i>The eyes of the Lord run to and fro</i> (Zechariah iv. 10)[[fn:390]]; <i>Incline thine ear, O Lord, and hear</i> (2 Kings xix. 16); <i>Ye have kindled a fire in mine anger</i> (Jeremiah xvii. 4). Of the inward members, none have been ascribed unto Him save the heart, because the name of the <i>heart</i> is a homonym signifying also <i>intellect</i>[[fn:391]], and because the heart is the principle of life in the living being. Verily, by these expressions: <i>My bowels are troubled for him</i> (Jeremiah xxxi. 20); <i>The sounding of thy bowels</i> (Isaiah lxiii. 15), it was likewise intended to designate <i>the heart</i>; for <i>bowels</i>[[fn:392]] is a term employed in both a general and a particular sense, signifying in particular the <i>intestines</i>, and in general any <i>inward member</i>, and consequently the <i>heart</i> also. The proof of which is that the expression has been used: <i>And thy Law is</i> <small>WITHIN MY BOWELS</small> (Ps. xl. 9) as equivalent to: <i>in my heart</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.7.sub_1": "Wherefore it was said in the verses aforesaid[[fn:393]]: <i>My bowels are troubled, the sounding of thy bowels</i>, for the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">המה</span>, <i>to sound</i>, is said rather of the heart than of other members; as for example: <i>My heart makes a noise</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הומה</span>) <i>in me</i> (Jeremiah iv. 19). In like manner, the <i>shoulder</i> has not been ascribed unto Him, because it is commonly regarded as an instrument of burden, and because that which is carried is in contact therewith[[fn:394]]. With yet greater reason have the organs of nutrition not been ascribed unto Him, because they betoken, at the first glance, a manifest imperfection[[fn:395]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.8": "In verity, the condition of all organs, whether external or internal, is the same; they are all instruments for the diverse actions of the soul. Some serve for the preservation of the individual for a certain season, and such are all the internal members; others serve for the preservation of the species, such as the organs of generation; others yet serve to improve the condition of the individual and to accomplish his actions, and such are the hands, the feet, and the eyes, all of which serve for the fulfillment of motion, labor, and perception. As for motion, it is necessary unto the animal that it may go towards that which is convenient for it and flee from that which is contrary. Sensation is necessary for it to distinguish that which is contrary from that which is convenient. Man has need of the handicrafts to prepare his food, his raiment, and his habitation; for all these are necessary to his nature, by which I mean that he has need to prepare that which is convenient for him. There be certain arts found also among divers animals, because they have need of this or that art.",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.9": "— Now, methinks no man doubts that God has no need of anything for the prolongation of His existence, nor that He in any wise improvs His condition; consequently He has no organ, that is to say, He is no body, and His actions proceed solely from His essence, and not by means of an organ. The <i>faculties</i>, it cannot be doubted, are parts of the organs, and consequently He possesses no faculty, I mean that there is in Him[[fn:396]], external to His essence, no thing whereby <i>He acts, He knows</i> or <i>He wills;</i> for the attributes are but faculties, in which the name alone has been changed and nothing more[[fn:397]]. But this is not the purpose of the present chapter.",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.10.sub_0": "The Sages have delivered a maxim of great import[[fn:398]], which refuts all those false notions that might be engendered by the corporeal attributes mentioned by the prophets (when speaking of God); and this maxim shows thee that the corporeality (of God) never entered the minds of the Sages, and that there is naught in their words which could give rise to error or doubt[[fn:399]]. Wherefore thou shalt find that everywhere, in the <i>Talmud</i> and in the <i>Midrashoth</i>, they constantly imitate these expressions of the prophets[[fn:400]], knowing full well that this is a matter in which one is secure from doubt and in which there is no fear of falling into error, and that everything therein is spoken rather by way of allegory and to direct the mind toward a (Supreme) Being. Now, as this allegory has been constantly employed, in which God is compared[[fn:401]] unto a king who commands and forbidds, who punishes and rewards the people of his land, and who has servants and ministers to transmit his orders and to execute that which he wills to be done, they also—I mean the Sages—have everywhere persevered in this comparison, and have expressed themselves according to what this allegory required (by attributing unto God the action) of <i>speaking</i>, of <i>answering</i>, of <i>enjoining commands</i>[[fn:402]], and other similar actions (emanating) from kings; and they did all this with security, being certain that neither confusion nor doubt would result therefrom. The <i>maxim of great import</i> unto which we have alluded is contained in these words of <i>Bereshith Rabba</i>[[fn:403]]: «Great was the boldness of the prophets in comparing the creature to its Creator; as, for example, in saying: <i>And upon the likeness of the throne was the likeness as the appearance of a man</i> (Ezek. i. 26)[[fn:404]].» Thus they (the Sages) have expressly declared that, in general, those figures which all the prophets perceived in the prophetic vision were <i>created</i> figures, of which God was the Creator[[fn:405]]. And this is true; for every figure that is in the imagination is <i>created</i>[[fn:406]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..45.10.sub_1": "This expression «<i>had great boldness</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">גדול כחן</span>)» is passing remarkable; (the Sages speak) as if this thing had appeared to them exceeding grave.— [In sooth, they ever express themselves thus to indicate that which they find grave in a word spoken or an act performed, which has, in appearance, something unseemly; as, for instance, in the following passage[[fn:407]]: «Rabbi Such-a-one caused the act to be performed with a shoe, in private, and during the night[[fn:408]]; great was his boldness (<span dir=\"rtl\">רב גובריה</span>), says another Sage, to do it <i>in private</i>.» <span dir=\"rtl\">רב גובריה</span> (in Aramaic) is the same as <span dir=\"rtl\">גדול כחו</span> (in Hebrew).] — It is therefore as if they had said: How grave is that which the prophets were led to do in indicating God Himself by the creations which He has produced. Thou must well consider this; for they (the Sages) have thus expressly declared that they themselves were free from the belief in the corporeality (of God), and that every figure and circumscribed thing seen in the prophetic vision were created things, but that they (the prophets) <i>compared the creature to its Creator</i>, as the Sages express it. If, however, it should please any one to think ill of them, after these declarations, out of mere malice and to detract from men whom he has not seen and of whose circumstances he knows naught[[fn:409]], no harm would result unto them therefrom[[fn:410]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..46.0": "We have already observed divers times[[fn:411]] that whatsoever the vulgar imagins to be an imperfection, or which cannot be conceived as compatible with God, the prophetic books have in no wise metaphorically attributed unto Him, although it be in the same condition as the things which have been attributed unto Him: the reason is that those things which have been given Him as attributes are deemed, after a fashion, perfections, or, at the least, they may be conceived (as belonging unto God).",
    "root.text.Part 1..46.1": "This being established[[fn:412]], it behoves us to explain wherefore hearing, sight, and smell have been metaphorically attributed unto God, whilst neither taste nor touch have been so attributed; for He stands in the same condition of exaltation with regard to all the five senses: all of them constitute an imperfection with regard to perception, even for (that being) which perceivs only by the senses[[fn:413]], because they are passively affected, impressed (by another thing), interrupted and subject to suffering, like other organs. When we say that God <i>ses</i>, the sense is that He perceivs visible things, and (when we say) that He <i>hears</i>, it signifies that He perceivs the objects of hearing; one might therefore likewise attribute unto Him taste and touch, interpreting it in the sense that He perceivs the objects of taste and those of feeling. In sooth, the condition of perception is the same for all (the senses), and if one removs from God the perception which belongs unto one (of the senses), one must remove the perception of all, I mean of the five senses; but as soon as one affirms of Him the perception of one of them, I mean (as soon as one affirms) that He perceivs what one of the senses perceivs, He must needs perceive the objects of perception of all five. Nevertheless, we find that our (holy) books say: <i>the Lord saw, the Lord heard, the Lord smelled</i>, and that they say not: <i>the Lord tasted</i> nor <i>the Lord touched</i>. The cause thereof is that it is established in the imagination of all that God cannot be in contact with bodies as one body is with another, since (men) cannot see Him; now, these two senses, I mean taste and touch, perceive the objects of their sensation only by touching them, whereas sight, hearing, and smell perceive the objects of their sensation even when the bodies endowed with (perceptible) qualities[[fn:414]] are far removed from them; wherefore, according to the imagination of the vulgar, it was permissible (to attribute them unto God)[[fn:415]]. Furthermore, in metaphorically attributing these senses unto Him, the object and aim were to indicate that He perceivs our actions; now, hearing and sight sufficed for this, I mean that it is by means of these senses[[fn:416]] that one perceivs all that another does or says. It is thus that the Sages, in a collection of exhortations, have said, by way of admonition and warning: «Know what is above thee: an eye that ses and an ear that hears[[fn:417]].»",
    "root.text.Part 1..46.2": "Thou shalt therefore know, upon examining (the matter) closely, that all (the senses) stand in the same condition, and that, even as the perception of touch and taste has been removed from God, so must we, for the same reason, remove therefrom that of sight, hearing, and smell; for all these be corporeal perceptions, <i>passions</i> (<i>πάθη</i>), and mutable conditions, save that some appear as an imperfection, whilst others pass for a perfection. Likewise, the imagination appears as an imperfection, whereas in thought and in understanding the imperfection is not manifest (unto all)[[fn:418]]; wherefore the word <i>ra’ayon</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רעיון</span>), which signifies <i>imagination</i>, has not been metaphorically employed when speaking of God, whilst the words <i>ma’hashabah</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מחשבה</span>) and <i>tebunah</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תבונה</span>), which signify <i>thought</i> and <i>understanding</i>, have been so employed; e.g.: <i>And the thoughts</i> (purposes) <i>that the Lord has meditated</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חשב</span>) <i>the Lord</i> (Jeremiah xlix. 20); <i>And by His understanding</i>, or <i>His intelligence</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בתבונתו</span>), <i>He has stretched out the heavens</i> (<i>Ibid.</i> x. 12). It has therefore happened likewise for the inner perceptions as it happened for the sensible and external perceptions, that is to say, that some are metaphorically attributed (unto God), whilst others are not. And all this <i>in accordance with the language of men</i>[[fn:419]]: that which they (men) believe to be a perfection has been attributed unto Him; but that which is a manifest imperfection has in no wise been attributed unto Him. Nevertheless, if one goes to the root of the matter, He has no essential and real attribute[[fn:420]] joined unto His essence, even as shall be demonstrated.",
    "root.text.Part 1..47.0": "Whenever the idea of <i>hearing</i> is found ascribed unto God, thou shalt find that Onkelos the proselyte has departed therefrom and has explained it in the sense that the matter reached God—that is to say, that He <i>perceived</i> it; and when a prayer is concerned, he explains (the verb <i>to hear</i>) in the sense that God <i>accepted</i> or <i>did not accept</i> (the prayer). He thus ever expresses himself, in rendering the words <i>the Lord heard</i>, by <span dir=\"rtl\">שמיע קדם יי</span>, <i>it was heard before the Lord;</i> and where a prayer is concerned, he translats, e.g.: <i>I will surely hear his cry</i> (Exodus, xxii. 22) by <span dir=\"rtl\">קבלא אקבל</span>, <i>I will surely accept;</i> this he does continually in his paraphrase, without departing therefrom in a single passage. But as touching the passages where <i>sight</i> is ascribed unto God, Onkelos has therein shown an astonishing versatility[[fn:421]], the aim and intention of which are not clear unto me; for in certain passages he translats <span dir=\"rtl\">וירא יי</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">וחזא יי</span>, <i>and the Lord saw</i>, and in other passages he translats these words by <span dir=\"rtl\">וגלי קדם יי</span>, <i>and it was manifest before the Lord</i>. Since he translats by <span dir=\"rtl\">וחזא יי</span>, <i>and the Lord saw</i>, it proves with evidence that the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">חזא</span>, <i>to see</i>, in the Syriac tongue, is homonymous, and that it signifies the perception of the intellect as well as that of the senses; but if such were his opinion, I would fain know why he has avoided (this verb) in certain passages, translating: <i>and it was manifest before the Lord</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..47.1": "Nevertheless, a perusal of the copies which I have found of the <i>Targum</i> (of Onkelos), conjoined with that which I had heard during the period of my studies, has shown me[[fn:422]] that whenever he found the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ראה</span> (to see) relating to an injustice, or to aught that is hurtful, or to an act of violence, he rendered it by: <i>to be manifest before the Lord</i>. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">חזא</span> (to see), in that (Aramaic) tongue, indubitably implies the idea of <i>perceiving</i> and of <i>acknowledging the thing perceived even as it has been perceived</i>[[fn:423]]; wherefore, when he (Onkelos) found the verb <i>to see</i> relating to an injustice, he did not say <span dir=\"rtl\">וחזא יי</span>, <i>and the Lord saw</i>, but <span dir=\"rtl\">וגלי קדם יי</span>, <i>and it was manifest before the Lord</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..47.2.sub_0": "I have thus found that throughout the Pentateuch, wheresoever the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ראה</span> (to see) is applied unto God, he translats it (literally) by <span dir=\"rtl\">חזא</span> (to see), save in the passages which I am about to cite: For <i>For God saw</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראה</span>) <i>my affliction</i> (Genesis, xxix. 32) he putts: <span dir=\"rtl\">ארי גלי קדם יי עולבני</span> <i>For my ignominy is manifest before God;</i> for <i>For I have seen</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראיתי</span>) <i>all that Laban does unto thee</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, xxxi. 12): <span dir=\"rtl\">ארי גלי קדמי</span> <i>For ... is manifest before me;</i> and although he who speaks here is an angel, he has not ascribed unto him the perception signifying the <i>acknowledgment</i> (or <i>approbation</i>) of the thing, because it concerns an injustice; for <i>And God saw</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וירא</span>) <i>the children of Israel</i> (Exodus, ii. 25) he putts: <span dir=\"rtl\">וגלי קדם יי שעבודא דבני ישראל</span> <i>And the servitude of the children of Israel was manifest before God;</i> for <i>I have surely seen</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראה ראיתי</span>) <i>the affliction of my people</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, iii. 7): <span dir=\"rtl\">מגלא גלי קדמי ית שעבודא דעמי</span>, <i>The servitude of my people is manifest before me;</i> for <i>And I have also seen</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראיתי</span>) <i>the oppression</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..47.2.sub_1": "9): <span dir=\"rtl\">ואף גלי קדמי דחקא</span> <i>And also is manifest before me the oppression, etc.</i>; for <i>And that he had seen</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראה</span>) <i>their affliction</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, iv. 31): <span dir=\"rtl\">וארי גלי קדמוהי שעבודהון</span> <i>And that their servitude was manifest before him;</i> for <i>I have seen</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראיתי</span>) <i>this people</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, xxxii. 9): <span dir=\"rtl\">גלי קדמי עמא הדין</span> <i>This people is manifest before me</i>, for the meaning is: <i>I have seen their rebellion</i>, even as in (the words): <i>And God saw the children of Israel</i>, the meaning is: <i>He saw their affliction</i>[[fn:424]]; for <i>The Lord saw</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וירא</span>) <i>and was provoked to anger</i> (Deuter., xxxii. 19): <span dir=\"rtl\">וגלי קדם יי</span> <i>And it was manifest before God, etc.</i>; for <i>For he ses</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יראה</span>) <i>that power is gone</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 36): <span dir=\"rtl\">ארי גלי קדמוהי</span> <i>For it is manifest before him</i>, for here also it concerns an <i>injustice</i> committed against them and the victory of the enemy. In all these instances he has been consistent, and has had regard unto (those words): <i>And thou canst not look on iniquity</i> (Habakkuk, i. 13); wherefore, whenever servitude or rebellion is the matter, he translats by <span dir=\"rtl\">גלי קדמוהי</span> <i>it was manifest before him</i>, or by <span dir=\"rtl\">גלי קדמי</span> <i>it was manifest before me</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..47.3": "Nevertheless, this excellent and useful interpretation, which is in no wise doubtful, fails in three passages[[fn:425]] which, according to the rule in question, he ought to have translated by <span dir=\"rtl\">וגלי קדם יי</span> <i>and it was manifest before the Lord</i>, whereas we find therein, in the copies: <span dir=\"rtl\">וחזא יי</span> <i>and the Lord saw</i>. They are the following: <i>And the Lord saw that the wickedness of man was great</i> (Genesis, vi. 5); <i>And God looked upon the earth, and behold, it was corrupt</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 12); <i>And the Lord saw that Leah was hated</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, xxix. 31). It seems probable unto me that this is an error which has crept into the copies[[fn:426]]; for we possess not the autograph of Onkelos, wherefore we might say that he perchance had a (particular) interpretation for these passages[[fn:427]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..47.4": "If, on the other hand, he has rendered the words: <i>God will provide</i> (<i>choose</i>) <i>for Himself a lamb</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, xxii, 8) by <span dir=\"rtl\">קדם י״י גלי אמרא</span> <i>Before God the lamb is manifest</i>, it was lest this passage (literally translated) might give occasion to believe that God had need to seek the lamb in order to bring it into His presence[[fn:428]]; or else, because he deemed it unbefitting likewise, in that language (the Aramaic), to associate the Divine perception with an individual[[fn:429]] from among the irrational animals.",
    "root.text.Part 1..47.5": "One must diligently seek in this regard the true reading of the manuscripts; and should these passages be found even as we have stated, I know not his intention[[fn:430]] in this matter.",
    "root.text.Part 1..48.0": "Neither have the angels bodies; they are, on the contrary, Intelligences separate from all matter. Nevertheless, they are created beings, and it is God who has brought them into existence, as shall be set forth hereafter. In <i>Bereshith Rabba</i>[[fn:431]] it is said: «This expression, <i>The flame of the turning sword</i> (Genesis, iii, 24), corresponds to this other: <i>His ministers a flaming fire</i> (Ps. civ, 4)[[fn:432]]; the term <i>turning</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">המתהפכת</span>) is used because they (the angels) <i>transform themselves</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מתהפכים</span>): now they are called <i>men</i>, now <i>women</i>, now <i>winds</i> (or <i>spirits</i>), now <i>angels</i>[[fn:433]].» By this passage it is declared that they are by no means material, and that they possess no stable and corporeal form external to the mind[[fn:434]], but that, on the contrary, all this exists only in the prophetic vision and according to the action of the imaginative faculty, as we shall state when treating of the true nature of prophecy. If it be said (in this passage): «now <i>women</i>», it is because the prophets also at times beheld angels in the form of women, and this is an allusion to that passage of Zechariah (chap. v, <small>v</small>. 9): <i>And, behold, there came out two women, and the wind</i> (<i>was</i>) <i>in their wings, etc</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..48.1": "Thou knowest that the perception of that which is exempt from matter and entirely devoid of corporeality is passing difficult for man,— save it be after great exercise, — and particularly for him who distinguishes not between the <i>intelligible</i> and the <i>imaginary</i>, and who, for the most part, relies only upon the perception of the imagination; so that, in his view, everything imagined exists or may exist, whilst that which cannot be grasped by the imagination[[fn:435]] exists not and cannot exist[[fn:436]]. Such men, — and they comprise the majority of those who study, — never possess a precise idea of any subject, and no obscure matter is ever made clear unto them.",
    "root.text.Part 1..48.2": "It is likewise by reason of the difficulty of this matter that the prophetic books present words which, in their literal sense, would lead one to understand that angels are corporeal and possess certain movements; that they have a human form, receive the commands of God, transmit them (unto men), and perform His will by His decree; all this being intended to bring the mind to a knowledge of their existence, and that they are living and perfect, even as we have set forth concerning God. Nevertheless, had they been represented only thus[[fn:437]], their true essence[[fn:438]] would have been, in the imagination of the vulgar, similar to the essence of God; for there have likewise been employed regarding God words whose literal sense would seem to indicate that He is a body possessed of life and motion and of a human form. It is therefore to signify to the mind that the rank of their existence is below the rank of the Deity, that there has been mingled with their form something of the form of irrational animals, in order to make it understood that the existence of the Creator is more perfect than theirs, just as man is more perfect than the irrational animal. But, as regards the animal form[[fn:439]], naught has been attributed unto them save wings; for one cannot imagine flight without wings, even as one cannot imagine walking without feet, and the very existence of the said faculties can only be imagined as residing in the said <i>subjects</i>[[fn:440]]. And if flight has been chosen (as an attribute of angels) to indicate that they are living, it is because this is the most perfect and noble of the local movements of animals, and man perceivs therein a great perfection, desiring himself to be able to fly, that he might easily flee from all that is harmful to him and promptly reach that which is suitable, at whatsoever distance it may be.",
    "root.text.Part 1..48.3": "It is therefore for this reason that such motion has been attributed unto them, and furthermore because the bird, within a right brief space of time, now appears and now conceals itself, now draws nigh and now withdraws; for these be generally the circumstances which must be admitted concerning the angels, as shall hereafter be set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 1..48.4": "This alleged perfection, I mean the motion of flight, is in no wise attributed unto God, forasmuch as it is a motion pertaining unto an irrational animal. Thou must not be misled by these words: <i>And he rode upon a cherub and did fly</i> (Ps. xviii. 11); for herein it is the <i>cherub</i> which <i>flew</i>[[fn:441]]. It was intended by this allegory that the matter in question comes to pass <i>swiftly;</i> even as it has been said elsewhere: <i>Behold, the Lord rids upon a swift cloud, and shall come into Egypt</i> (Isaiah xix. 1), where it is signified that the calamity in question shall fall <i>rapidly</i> upon them.",
    "root.text.Part 1..48.5": "Neither must thou suffer thyself to be led into error by the expressions which thou findest particularly in Ezekiel, such as: <i>the face of an ox, the face of a lion, the face of an eagle</i> (Ezek. i. 10), <i>the sole of a calf's foot</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 7)[[fn:442]]; for there is for all this another interpretation which thou shalt hear later[[fn:443]], and moreover this is but the description of the <i>hayyôth</i>[[fn:444]]. These subjects shall be explained by means of indications which will suffice to awaken the attention[[fn:445]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..48.6": "As for the motion of flight (ascribed unto angels), it is found everywhere in the (sacred) texts, and (as we have said) it cannot be conceived save by means of wings; wherefore wings have been assigned unto them to indicate a circumstance of their existence, and not to designate their true being.",
    "root.text.Part 1..48.7": "It must be known that unto whatsoever movs with a rapid motion, the action of flying is attributed to indicate the swiftness of the movement; it has been said, for example: … <i>as the eagle flies</i> (Deut. xxviii. 49), because the eagle is, among all birds, that which flies and darts most swiftly, so that it has passed into a proverb. It must also be known that wings are the (efficient) causes of flight; wherefore the wings which appear (in prophetic visions) are of the same number as the causes of the motion of that which movs[[fn:446]]. But this is not the purpose of the present chapter.",
    "root.text.Part 1..49.0": "Know[[fn:447]], O reader of this my present treatise, that belief is not a thing which is merely uttered, but a thing which is conceived in the soul, whilst it is believed that the object is even as it is conceived. If, therefore, in the matter of true opinions or those deemed to be such, thou art content to express them in words, without conceiving them or believing them, and, with even greater reason, without seeking therein a certainty, this is a thing right easy; and thus it is that thou findest many foolish men who retain in their memory beliefs of which they conceive absolutely no idea.",
    "root.text.Part 1..49.1": "But if thou art of those whose thought does rise to ascend unto that exalted degree, which is the degree of speculation, and to possess the certainty that God is <i>one</i>, with a real unity, so that there be found in Him naught composite nor aught that is virtually divisible in any manner whatsoever, it behoovs thee to know that God has no <i>essential</i> attribute, under any condition, and that even as it cannot be admitted that He is a body, so is it inadmissible that He should possess an <i>essential</i> attribute[[fn:448]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..49.2": "Whosoever should believe that He is <i>one</i>, yet possessing manifold attributes, would indeed declare by his speech that He is <i>one</i>, but in his thought he would believe Him to be manifold. This would resemble that which the Christians say: «He is <i>one</i>, yet He is <i>three</i>, and the three are <i>one</i>»; for it would be the same thing if one said: «He is <i>one</i>, but He possesses many attributes, and He with His attributes are <i>one</i>», whilst setting aside corporeality and believing in the absolute simplicity (of God), as though our purpose were merely to seek how we should express ourselves, and not what we ought to believe[[fn:449]]. There can be no belief except where there has been conception; for belief consists in admitting as true that which has been conceived, and in believing that the thing exists outside the mind even as it has been conceived in the mind. If there be joined unto this belief the conviction that the contrary of that which is believed is absolutely impossible, and that there exists in the mind no means of refuting this belief, nor of thinking that the contrary might be possible, that is certainty.",
    "root.text.Part 1..49.3": "If thou divestest thyself of desires and habits, if thou art intelligent and ponderest well what I shall say in these following chapters concerning the <i>negation of attributes</i>[[fn:450]], thou shalt necessarily have certainty in this regard, and then shalt thou be of those who <i>conceive</i> the unity of God, and not of those who merely pronounce it with their mouth, without conceiving an idea thereof, and who belong unto that class of whom it has been said: <i>Thou art near in their mouth, and far from their reins</i> (Jeremiah, xii. 2). For it behoovs man to be of those who conceive the truth and understand it, even though they should not utter it, as it has been commanded unto virtuous men, saying unto them: <i>Commune</i> (<i>think</i>) <i>with your own heart upon your bed, and be still</i> (Ps. iv. 4).",
    "root.text.Part 1..50.0": "There are, in existence, many things clear and manifest, of which some are first notions[[fn:451]] and sensible things, and others are such as approach unto these; so that man, even were he left as he is[[fn:452]], would stand in no need of proof for these matters. Such are, for example, the existence of motion, the freedom of action belonging unto man, the evidence of <i>generation</i> and <i>corruption</i>[[fn:453]], and the natural properties of things which strike the senses, as the heat of fire and the coldness of water; many similar things might be cited. But when there arose extraordinary opinions on the part of those who were in error or who had therein some purpose, and who, by these opinions, set themselves in opposition to the nature of existence, denying that which is perceived by the senses or wishing to make believe in the existence of that which exists not, the men of science had need to establish the existence of these manifest things and the non-existence of things of mere supposition[[fn:454]]. Thus we find that Aristotle established motion, because it had been denied[[fn:455]], and demonstrated the non-existence of atoms, because their existence had been affirmed[[fn:456]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..50.1": "Of this same category is the requirement to remove from God all <i>essential</i> attributes;",
    "root.text.Part 1..50.2": "for it is a <i>primary notion</i> that the attribute is distinct from the <i>essence</i> of the qualified subject, that it is a certain circumstance of the essence, and, consequently, an <i>accident</i>. When the attribute is the very essence of the qualified subject, it is naught else than a tautology, as, for instance, if one were to say: <i>man is a man;</i> or else it is the explanation of a name, as, for instance, if one were to say: <i>man is a living being</i> (or <i>animal</i>) <i>rational</i> [for <i>living and rational</i> expresses the essence of man and his reality, and there is no third idea beyond those of <i>living</i> and <i>rational</i>, which constitute man, who is qualified by <i>life</i> and <i>reason</i>, or, to speak more precisely, this attribute is the explanation of a name and nothing else, and it is as if one were to say: that the thing whose name is <i>man</i> is that which is composed of <i>life</i> and <i>reason</i>].",
    "root.text.Part 1..50.3": "It is therefore clear that the <i>attribute</i> must necessarily be one of two things: either it is the very essence of the subject, such that it is the explanation of a name—a thing which, in this respect[[fn:457]], we do not reject regarding God, but rather in another respect, as shall be explained[[fn:458]]; or the attribute is something other than the subject, or rather, it adds something to the subject, which would result in making the attribute an accident of said essence. But by excluding the denomination of <i>accident</i> from the Creator's attributes, one does not exclude the idea; for whatever is <i>added</i> to the essence is accessory to it and is not the complement of its true idea, and this is precisely the meaning of <i>accident</i>[[fn:459]]. Add to this that, if there were many attributes, it would follow that there are many eternal things; but there is <i>unity</i> only on the condition of admitting a <i>one</i> and simple essence, in which there is neither composition nor multiplicity of ideas, but, on the contrary, a single idea, which is found to be <i>one</i> from whatever side one envisions it and from whatever point of view one considers it, which in no way nor by any cause could be divided into two ideas, and in which no multiplicity exists, neither outside the mind nor within the mind (of the thinker)[[fn:460]], as will be demonstrated in this treatise.",
    "root.text.Part 1..50.4": "Certain thinkers[[fn:461]] have even proceeded so far as to affirm that the attributes of God are neither His essence, nor aught external to His essence[[fn:462]]; but this resembles what certain others have averred: «The <i>conditions</i> — for thus do they designate the <i>general ideas</i> — are neither existent, nor non-existent[[fn:463]]»; and what others again have stated: «The simple substance (the atom) is not in space, yet it occupies a circumscribed position[[fn:464]]», and: «Man possesses no <i>action</i>, but he possesses <i>acquisition</i>[[fn:465]]». These are assertions which are all merely <i>spoken</i>, which exist in words but not in minds, and which, à fortiori, can have no existence external to the mind[[fn:466]]. Nevertheless, as thou knowest, and as is known to all who do not deceive themselves, they are shielded by a superabundance of words and by images of spurious brilliance, and upheld by declamations, by invectives[[fn:467]], and by numerous means (of discussion) borrowed alike from dialectics and from sophistry[[fn:468]]. But if, after having enunciated them and supported them by such means[[fn:469]], one recurs within oneself to one's own conviction[[fn:470]], one discovers naught save perplexity and mental impotence, because one strives to impart existence to that which exists not, and to create a middle term between two opposites where none subsists; for is there a middle term between that which exists and that which exists not, or is there one between the identity and the non-identity of two things[[fn:471]] ?",
    "root.text.Part 1..50.5": "What prompted this was, as we have said, that they abandoned themselves to imaginations and always figured that all existing bodies are essences, each of which necessarily has attributes, and that we never find an essence of a body existing alone and without an attribute; persisting thus in this imagination, they believed that God, likewise, is composed of diverse things, (namely) of His essence[[fn:472]] and of the ideas added to the essence. Some, pushing anthropomorphism further, believed Him to be a body having attributes, while others, rising above this lowest degree[[fn:473]], removed the body (from God) and let the attributes remain. What brought all this about was that they followed the literal sense of the books of revelation, as I shall explain in chapters dealing with these subjects[[fn:474]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.0": "Whenever a subject has an affirmative attribute[[fn:475]] and it be said that it is such, this attribute cannot fail to be of one of the following five classes.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.1": "I. The <small>FIRST CLASS</small> is that in which the thing has for its attribute its own definition, as, for example, when man is designated by saying that he is a <i>rational animal</i>. Such an attribute indicats the true essence of a thing[[fn:476]], and we have already set forth that it is the explanation of a name and naught else[[fn:477]]. This kind of attribute must, by the consent of all, be removed from God, for God has no prior causes which have caused His existence, so that they might serve to define Him[[fn:478]]. Wherefore it is generally admitted by all thinkers who express themselves with precision[[fn:479]] that God cannot be defined.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.2": "II. The <small>SECOND CLASS</small> is that in which the thing has for its attribute a part of its definition[[fn:480]], as, for example, when one designats man by the <i>quality of animality</i> or by <i>reason</i>. Herein is the idea <i>of inherence</i>[[fn:481]]; for, if we say: <i>every man is rational</i>, it signifies that in everything possessing the quality of man, reason is found. This kind of attribute must, by the consent of all, be removed from God; for, were He to possess a part of a <i>quiddity</i>, His quiddity would be a composite thing. This class of attributes is therefore as inadmissible in His regard as was the former.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.3": "III. The <small>THIRD CLASS</small> is that in which the thing possesses as an attribute something exterior to its reality and its essence, so that it forms no part of that which completes and constitutes the essence[[fn:482]], and consequently, it forms therein (merely) a <i>quality</i>. But <i>quality</i>, as a <i>higher genus</i>, is one of the accidents[[fn:483]]; if therefore God possessed an attribute of this class, He would be the <i>substratum</i> of accidents, and this alone suffices to demonstrate that to affirm He possesses a quality would be to depart from His reality and His essence[[fn:484]]. Yet one must marvel that those who admit attributes nonetheless avert from God assimilation (to creatures) and <i>qualification</i>[[fn:485]]; for, when they state He cannot be <i>qualified</i>, this signifies nothing other than that He possesses no <i>quality</i>; and yet, every attribute which is ascribed to an essence in an affirmative and essential sense[[fn:486]] either constitutes the essence, — and in that case it is (the essence) itself, — or else is a quality of that essence.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.4": "The species of <i>quality</i> are four in number[[fn:487]], as thou knowest; I shall give thee an example of each of these species in the form of an attribute, in order to show thee the impossibility of admitting for God this kind of attribute.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.5": "<i>First example:</i> when one characteriss man by one of his <i>capacities</i>[[fn:488]] speculative or moral, or by the dispositions which he possesses as a living being[[fn:489]], as, e.g., when one says: such an one, a carpenter, or chaste, or sick. There is no difference (in this regard) between the denomination of <i>carpenter</i> and that of <i>learned</i>, or of <i>wise</i>[[fn:490]], all of which (designate) <i>dispositions in the soul</i>, and likewise there is no difference between saying <i>chaste</i> and saying <i>merciful;</i> for every art, every science, and every permanent moral quality[[fn:491]] is a disposition in the soul. All this is manifest to him who has in any wise occupied himself with Logic.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.6": "<i>Second example:</i> when one characteriss a thing by a natural power or incapacity which is found therein, as, e.g., when one says: the soft and the hard. There is no difference between saying <i>soft</i> and <i>hard</i> and saying <i>strong</i> and <i>weak</i>[[fn:492]]; all these (signify) natural aptitudes.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.7": "<i>Third example:</i> when one qualifies a man by an affective quality or by affections, as, p. ex., when one says: such a person is in a rage, or irritated, or afraid, or compassionate, without, however, this signifying a permanent moral quality[[fn:493]]. Of the same kind is the qualification by colour[[fn:494]], taste, smell, heat, cold, dryness, and moisture.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.8": "<i>Fourth example:</i> when one characteriss a thing by what it is in respect of quantity as such[[fn:495]], as, e.g., when one says: the long, the short, the curved, the straight, and other such things.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.9": "Considering all these attributes and others of like nature, thou shalt find them inadmissible with regard to God: for He has no quantity, that there might be in Him such a quality as exists in <i>quantity as such;</i> He is neither impressed nor passively affected, that there might be in Him a quality of <i>affections;</i> He has no <i>aptitudes</i>, that there might be in Him <i>powers</i> (faculties)[[fn:496]] or aught of a similar kind; finally, God has no soul, that He might have a <i>disposition</i> and that there might be in Him <i>capacities</i> (<i>ἕξεις</i>), such as meekness, shame, and others of a like nature, or that which pertains to the <i>living being</i> as such[[fn:497]], e.g.: health and sickness. It is therefore manifest that every attribute which belongs to the higher genus of <i>quality</i>[[fn:498]] cannot be found in God.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.10": "Thus it is demonstrated that these three classes of attributes—to wit, all that signifies a <i>quiddity</i>[[fn:499]], or a part of a quiddity, or any quality whatsoever that is found within the quiddity—are inadmissible in respect of God; for they all betoken <i>composition</i>, which, as we shall demonstrate[[fn:500]], is inadmissible in respect of God[[fn:501]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.11": "IV. The <small>FOURTH CLASS</small> of attributes is that in which one designats the thing by its relation to another thing, by relating it, for instance, to a certain time, to a place, or to another individual, as, for example, when one designats Zeid (by saying) that he is the father of such a one, or the associate of such a one, or an inhabitant of such a place, or he who existed in such a time. This kind of attributes implies neither multiplicity nor change in the essence of the subject; for the Zeid in question is (at once) the associate of ‘Amr, the father of Becr, the master of Khalid, the friend of (the other) Zeid, the inhabitant of such a house, and he who was born in such a year. These notions of relation are neither his essence nor, like qualities, aught within his essence; and it appears at first glance that one might ascribe to God this kind of attributes; nevertheless, upon verifying the matter and examining it with care, it will be manifest that this cannot be.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.12": "Firstly, it is evident that God is not related to time and space: for time is an accident that pertains to movement, when, in the latter, one considers the notion of antecedence and posteriority, so that it is numbered (by time), — as is explained in the particular places dedicated to this subject[[fn:502]] —; now, movement being a property of bodies, and God not being a body, it follows that there is no relation between Him and time. Similarly, there is no relation between Him and space[[fn:503]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.13.sub_0": "But that which it behoves one to investigate and examine is whether there exists between God and any of the substances created by Him a certain veritable relation, in such wise that it may serve as an attribute unto Him. That there can be no <i>relation</i> (properly so called)[[fn:504]] between Him and any of the things created by Him, this is manifest at first glance: for one of the properties of two correlatives is perfect reciprocity[[fn:505]]; now, God is of a <i>necessary</i> existence, and that which is external to Him is of a <i>possible</i> existence, as shall be expounded[[fn:506]], and, consequently, there can exist no <i>relation</i> (between God and the creature). But that any sort of relation should subsist between them, this is a thing which has been deemed admissible, albeit such is not the case. Indeed, one could not conceive of a relation between intelligence and colour, even though, according to our view, one and the same existence embraces them both[[fn:507]]; and how then could one conceive of a relation between two beings, one of which possesses absolutely nothing in common with that which is external to it[[fn:508]]? — for, in our opinion, it is only by simple homonymy that (the word) <i>to exist</i> is predicated simultaneously of God and of that which is external to Him. There is, therefore, absolutely no relation in reality between Him (God) and any whatsoever of His creatures: for relation necessarily subsists solely between two things which fall under one and the same <i>proximate species</i>[[fn:509]], but, when they are (merely) under one and the same <i>genus</i>, no relation exists between them[[fn:510]]; hence it is not said: \"This red is stronger or weaker than this green, or equal thereto,\" albeit both[[fn:511]] are comprised under one and the same <i>genus</i>, which is colour. If, however, the two things should fall under diverse genera[[fn:512]], then it is clear, even to simple common sense, that no relation subsists between them, even though they might ultimately revert to a single <i>genus</i> (<i>superior</i>). Thus, for example, there is no relation between <i>a hundred cubits</i> and the <i>heat of pepper;</i> for one of the two things pertains to the genus[[fn:513]] of <i>quality</i>, whilst the other pertains to that of <i>quantity</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.13.sub_1": "But neither is there any relation[[fn:514]] between science and sweetness, nor between mildness and bitterness, although all these be under the <i>higher genus</i> of quality. How then could there be a relation between God and aught of His creatures, given the great distance in the reality of being, a distance so great that there is none more marked? If there were a relation between them[[fn:515]], it would also follow that the <i>accident</i> of relation appertains unto God; for, though it be true that this would not be an accident in the very essence of God, it is yet, in short, a kind of accident. There is therefore, in reality, no means[[fn:516]] of ascribing to God an affirmative attribute, even were it (by qualifying Him) by a <i>relation</i>[[fn:517]]. But these attributes of relation are those which one must show oneself more ready to admit with regard to God[[fn:518]]; for they imply no multiplicity in the Eternal Being[[fn:519]], and they imply no change in the essence of God by reason of the change of things wherewith He is placed in relation.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.14": "V. The <small>FIFTH CLASS</small> of affirmative attributes is that in which a thing has for its attribute its action. By <i>its action</i>, I mean not the <i>artistic capacity</i> which resids therein [as, for instance, when one says the carpenter[[fn:520]] or the blacksmith], for this belongs unto the species of <i>quality</i>, as we have formerly stated[[fn:521]]; but I mean thereby the action (in general) which one has accomplished, as, for example, when it is said: Zeid is he who has fashioned this door, builded this wall, and woven this fabric. Attributes of this sort are far removed from the essence of the subject[[fn:522]]; wherefore it is permissible to attribute them unto God, provided it be well understood that these diverse actions do not necessarily proceed from diverse conditions existing within the essence of their author[[fn:523]], as shall be set forth hereafter[[fn:524]]. On the contrary, the diverse actions of God are all performed by His very essence, and not by anything superadded thereto[[fn:525]], even as we have declared.",
    "root.text.Part 1..51.15": "In summary, then, it has been set forth in this chapter[[fn:526]] that God is <i>one</i> in every respect, that there is in Him no multiplicity nor anything superadded to His essence, and that the many attributes of diverse significations employed in the (sacred) books to designate God denote the multiplicity of His actions, and not a multiplicity in His essence[[fn:527]]. Some (are employed) to indicate His perfection in relation to that which we believe to be a perfection, even as we have explained[[fn:528]]. As for knowing whether it be possible that the essence, one and simple, in which there is no multiplicity, may perform varied actions, this shall presently be demonstrated by examples.",
    "root.text.Part 1..52.0": "Men have been led to believe that God has attributes in much the same manner as they were led to believe in (God's) corporeality[[fn:529]]. Verily, he who believs in corporeality was not led thereto by intellectual speculation, but by following the literal sense of the Scriptural texts; and it is likewise with regard to the attributes: finding that the books of the Prophets and those of the Pentateuch[[fn:530]] ascribed attributes unto God, they took the matter literally, and believed that He possessed attributes. They have, so to speak, exalted Him above corporeality, yet without exalting Him above the circumstances of corporeality, which are accidents—I mean, the dispositions of the soul, all of which are <i>qualities</i>[[fn:531]]. In every attribute which, in the opinion of him who believs in attributes, is <i>essential</i> in God, thou shalt find the notion of <i>quality</i>, although they (the Prophets) do not express themselves clearly in this regard, simply assimilating (the attributes of God) unto those conditions familiar to them in every body endowed with a vital soul[[fn:532]]; and concerning all this it has been said: «The Law speaks according to the language of the sons of men.» The aim of all (the attributes of God) is none other than to ascribe unto Him perfection (in general), and not that very thing which is a perfection for such creatures as are endowed with a soul. Most of them are attributes (arising) from His diverse actions;",
    "root.text.Part 1..52.1": "for the diversity of actions does not presuppose the existence of diverse ideas within the agent[[fn:533]]. One shall, in this regard, be presented with an example drawn from those things which exist near unto us, (to demonstrate) that, the agent being <i>one</i>, there nevertheless result diverse actions, even when it possesses no will, and, *a fortiori*, when it acts with will. Thus, for example, fire liquefies certain substances, coagulates others, cooks, burns, whitens, and blackens; and, if one were to ascribe to fire the attributes of <i>whitening</i>, of <i>blackening</i>, of <i>burning</i>, of <i>cooking</i>, of <i>coagulating</i>, and of <i>liquefying</i>, one would speak truly. Now, he who comprehends not the nature of fire believes that there exist within it six distinct virtues: one virtue by which it blackens, another by which it whitens, a third by which it cooks, a fourth by which it burns, a fifth by which it liquefies, and a sixth by which it coagulates, notwithstanding that these are all actions opposed one to another, and that the idea of the one excludes that of the others[[fn:534]]; but he who comprehends the nature of fire well knows that it is by a single active quality that it produces all these actions, to wit, by heat. Now, if this holds true in that which acts by nature, (the same must apply) *a fortiori*, with regard to that which acts with will, and, with yet greater force, *a fortiori*, with regard to God, Who is elevated above all description; and, when one perceives within Him diverse relations of meaning[[fn:535]], because, within ourselves, the idea of <i>knowledge</i> is distinct from that of <i>power</i>, and that of power distinct from that of <i>will</i>[[fn:536]], how might one thence conclude that there exist within Him diverse things which are <i>essential</i> to Him, so that there be in Him something by which He <i>knows</i>, something by which He <i>wills</i>, and something by which He <i>can</i>? Such, nevertheless, is the purport of the attributes which are proclaimed. Some pronounce this clearly, by enumerating the things superadded to the essence; others, without pronouncing it clearly, evidently profess the same opinion, albeit they do not express themselves in this regard by intelligible words, saying, for example, (that God is) <i>powerful by His essence, knowing by His essence, living by His essence, willing by His essence</i>[[fn:537]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..52.2": "I shall further cite for thee as an example the rational faculty which exists in man; for, by this faculty which is <i>one</i>, without multiplicity, man comprehends the sciences and the arts, and by it at once he sews, carpents, weavs, builds, knows geometry, and governs the state[[fn:538]]. Here, then, are diverse actions resulting from a single simple faculty, in which there is no multiplicity; and they are very varied actions, for there is an infinite number of these arts which the rational faculty produces[[fn:539]]. It is therefore not inadmissible, in regard to God, that these diverse actions (which are attributed unto Him) should emanate from a single simple essence[[fn:540]], in which there is neither multiplicity, nor absolutely anything <i>accessory</i>, so that every attribute found in the divine books designats His action, and not His essence[[fn:541]], or indicats an absolute perfection; (and it by no means follows) that there is there an essence composed of diverse things — [for it suffices not to refrain from speaking expressly of <i>composition</i>, for the essence endowed with attributes not to imply the notion thereof[[fn:542]]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..52.3": "Yet there remains here somewhat of obscurity which has given rise to error[[fn:543]], and which I shall lay before thee: to wit, that they who admit attributes do not admit them (solely) by reason of the multiplicity of actions; for verily, say they, a single essence may produce divers actions, but the <i>essential</i> attributes of God are not of (those which proceed from) His actions, since one cannot conceive, (for example), that God should have created Himself[[fn:544]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..52.4": "They differ, furthermore, concerning these attributes which they term <i>essential</i>, I mean as to their number, each following some text of Scripture[[fn:545]]. But I desire to record (only) those which all admit by common consent, claiming that they are dictated by reason, without needing to depend upon the word of a prophet; these are four attributes, namely: <i>living, powerful, knowing, willing</i>. These, say they, are ideas distinct one from another, and perfections of which not one can be wanting in God, and which cannot be numbered among His <i>actions</i>[[fn:546]]. Such is the summary of their opinion.",
    "root.text.Part 1..52.5": "But thou knowest well[[fn:547]] that, in God, <i>knowing</i> signifies the same thing as <i>living;</i> for every (being) that perceivs itself is endowed with <i>life</i> and <i>knowledge</i>, (taken here) in one and the same sense—[provided, of course, that by <i>knowledge</i> we mean self-perception][[fn:548]]. The essence which perceivs is indubitably that very same which is perceived[[fn:549]]; for, according to our opinion, He (God) is not composed of two things, that is to say, of a thing which perceivs and of another which perceivs not, as man, who is composed of a soul which perceivs and a body which perceivs not[[fn:550]]. If, therefore, by <i>knowledge</i> one understands self-perception, life and knowledge (in God) are one and the same thing. But those (who profess the attributes) have not in view this sense (of divine <i>knowledge</i>); on the contrary, they have in view that perception (of God) which has for its object His creatures.",
    "root.text.Part 1..52.6": "Likewise, <i>power</i> and <i>will</i> exist neither the one nor the other in the Creator in relation to His essence; for He exerciss no power over Himself, nor can one attribute unto Him a will having Himself for its object, a thing which no man could imagine. These attributes, on the contrary, they have admitted only with respect to divers relations existing between God and His creatures, that is to say, that He has the <i>power</i> to create that which He creats, the <i>will</i> to bring into existence that which is such as He has produced it, and the <i>knowledge</i> of that which He has produced. It is therefore manifest that these attributes also[[fn:551]] are (ascribed to God), not with regard to His essence, but with regard to created things;",
    "root.text.Part 1..52.7": "Wherefore this is what we affirm, we who truly profess the <i>Unity:</i> even as we do not admit that there is in His essence aught accessory by which He created the heavens, aught else by which He created the elements, and, in the third place, aught by which He created the (separate) Intelligences, so likewise do we not admit that there is in Him aught accessory by which He <i>can</i>, aught else by which He <i>wills</i>, and, in the third place, aught by which He possesses the <i>science</i> of the things created by Him; but His essence is one and simple, and there is in it nothing accessory in any wise. It is this essence[[fn:552]] which created all that it created, and which <i>knows</i> (things), without it being by aught accessory; and it matters little whether these diverse attributes relate to actions or to diverse relations existing between Him and the things made (by Him)[[fn:553]], to say naught of what we have expounded concerning the true meaning of <i>relation</i>, for it has been admitted only by error[[fn:554]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..52.8": "Such is that which it behoves one to believe concerning the attributes mentioned in the books of the prophets[[fn:555]]; or else, one shall admit for some amongst them that they are attributes serving to indicate a perfection, by way of assimilation unto our own perfections, in such manner as they are understood amongst us, even as we shall hereafter expound.",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.0": "Know that the prince of the learned, our master Moses [peace be upon him!], addressed unto God two petitions, and obtained an answer unto these two prayers[[fn:556]]: by the one, he besought God to make known unto him His true essence; by the other [which is that which he addressed unto Him first[[fn:557]]], to make known unto him His attributes. God answered him concerning these two demands, promising to make known unto him all His attributes, which are His actions, and signifying unto him that His essence could not be apprehended in its whole reality; nevertheless He awakened his attention unto a speculative point of view whence he might perceive all that which it is possible for man at last to perceive. That which he himself perceived, no man has perceived either before him or after him.",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.1": "As for his request to know the attributes of God, it is contained in these words: <i>Shew me now Thy ways, that I may know Thee, etc</i>. (Exodus, xxxiii, 13). Consider well what these words contain of remarkable: the words <i>Shew me now Thy ways, that I may know Thee</i>, indicate that one knows God by means of His attributes, for it was after having recognized <i>the ways</i> (quoth Moses) that he should know Him; the words <i>that I may find grace in Thy sight</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>) indicate that he alone who knows God <i>finds grace in His sight</i>, and not he who merely limits himself to fasting and to prayer. Verily, whosoever knows Him is an object of favour, brought nigh unto Him, and whosoever is ignorant of Him is an object of wrath, removed from Him; and it is by reason of knowledge or of ignorance that favour or wrath, approximation or distance, takes place. But we have departed from the subject of the chapter; I shall therefore return thereto.",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.2": "Having then requested to know the attributes, while imploring at the same time pardon for the nation, and having thereafter, upon obtaining this pardon, requested to perceive the essence of God, saying: <i>I beseech Thee, shew me Thy glory</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 18), there was granted unto him that which he had first requested in these words: <i>Shew me now Thy ways</i>, and it was answered him: <i>I will make all my goodness pass before thee</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 19); but unto the second request he received this answer: <i>Thou canst not see my face, etc</i>. (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 20). As for the words <i>all my goodness</i>, they contain an allusion unto the presentation before him (Moses) of all beings, concerning which it has been said: <i>And God saw every thing that He had made, and, behold, it was very good</i> (Genesis, i, 31); by their <i>presentation before him</i>, I mean that (according to the divine promise) he was to understand their nature, their connection one with another, and to know how God governs them in their whole and in their details. It is also unto this idea that allusion was made by these words: <i>He is faithful in all mine house</i> (Numbers, xii, 7)[[fn:558]], that is<span dir=\"rtl\">־</span>to-say: he has of all My universe a true and firm intelligence; for opinions which are not true have no firmness. The matter here, then, is the perception of the actions (of God), which are His attributes[[fn:559]] by means of which He is known; and the proof that the actions of God were the thing of which the perception was promised unto him, is that he was made to know naught but simple <i>attributes of action</i>, such as: <i>merciful, gracious, long-suffering, etc</i>. (Exodus, xxxiv, 6 and 7). It is therefore clear that the <i>ways</i> of which he requested the knowledge, and which were made known unto him, were the actions which emanate from God. The doctors call them <i>middôth</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מדות</span>), and speak of <i>thirteen middôth</i> (of God); this word, in their usage, applies unto <i>moral qualities</i>[[fn:560]]), e.g.: <i>There are four</i> <small>MIDDÔTH</small> (<i>qualities</i>, or <i>manners of acting</i>) <i>amongst those who give alms; There are four</i> <small>MIDDÔTH</small> <i>amongst those who frequent the school</i>[[fn:561]], and yet more frequently. It is not intended here (in speaking of the <i>middôth</i> of God) to say that He possesses moral qualities, but that He produces actions similar unto those which, amongst us, emanate from moral qualities, I mean from dispositions of the soul, not that God has any such dispositions of the soul.",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.3": "If one has limited himself to mentioning these thirteen <i>middôth</i>, although he (Moses) perceived <i>all His goodness</i>, I mean, all His actions, it is only because these are the actions proceeding from God to bring men into existence and to govern them; and that was the ultimate end of his request, for he concluds by saying: <i>that I may know Thee, that I may find grace in Thy sight, and consider that this nation is Thy people</i> (Exodus, xxxiii, 13), that which I must govern by actions imitating those wherewith Thou dost govern it[[fn:562]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.4": "It is thenceforth manifest that the <i>derakhīm</i> (ways) and the <i>middôth</i> (attributes) are one and the same: they be those actions which proceed from God and are displayed within the universe.",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.5.sub_0": "In proportion, then, as any of His actions were perceived, there was attributed unto Him the quality whence that action proceeds, and there was applied unto Him the name derived from that action. Perceiving, for example, the tender care He bestows upon the fashioning of the embryo[[fn:563]] of animals, and the production, within it and within the parent that must rear it after birth, of faculties which may prevent it from perishing or being lost, and preserve it from harm and aid it in its necessary functions—a manner of acting which, among us, would proceed only from a <i>passion</i>[[fn:564]] and a sentiment of tenderness designated by the term <i>mercy</i>—God has been called <span dir=\"rtl\">רחום</span>, <i>merciful</i>, according to that which has been said: <i>Like as a father pities his children, etc.</i> (Ps. 103:13); <i>And I will spare them, as a man spars his own son</i> (Malachi, III, 17); not that God is passively affected or moved to tenderness, but an action similar to that which proceeds from a father toward his child, and which is the result of tenderness, commiseration, and pure passion, proceeds also from God toward His favoured ones, yet without it being by reason of any passion or any sentiment involving a change[[fn:565]]. Likewise, since among us, when we bestow something upon one who has no claim upon us, it is called in our tongue <span dir=\"rtl\">חנינה</span>, <i>grace</i>—e.g., <i>Be favourable</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חנונו</span>) <i>unto them</i> (Judges, 21:22)[[fn:566]]; … <i>which God has graciously given</i> (Genesis, 33:5); <i>because God has dealt graciously with me</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 11), and many other passages—and since God calls into existence and governs those whom He is under no obligation to create or govern, He has for this reason been called <span dir=\"rtl\">חנון</span>, <i>gracious</i> (clement). Likewise, we find among His actions manifested toward men great calamities which fall upon certain individuals to annihilate them, or which involve in their destruction[[fn:567]] whole families, and even an entire region, causing many generations to perish at once[[fn:568]] and leaving neither tillage nor progeny, as, for",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.5.sub_1": "example, floods, earthquakes, destructive tempests, the expedition of one people against another to destroy them by the sword and to blot out their trace, and many other similar actions, which, among us, are not undertaken by one against another[[fn:569]] save as a consequence of fierce wrath, or great hatred, or with the intent of taking vengeance; He has therefore been called, in respect of these actions: <i>jealous, revengeful, keeping rancour, and irascible</i> (Nahum, 1:2), signifying that actions such as those which, among us, proceed from a disposition of the soul, to wit, from jealousy, vengeance, hatred, or wrath, are manifested also on the part of God by reason of the demerit of those who are punished, yet they proceed in no wise from any passion [exalted be He above all imperfection!]. Thus it is that all actions attributed unto God are actions similar to those which, among men, proceed from passions and dispositions of the soul; but on the part of God, they proceed in no wise from anything accidental to His essence.",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.6": "It behoves him who governs the state, if he be a prophet[[fn:570]], to take these divine attributes for a pattern, so that the said actions may proceed from him by just appreciation and according to what is deserved, and not by the mere impulse of passion. He must not give rein to anger, nor suffer himself to be dominated by passions; for every passion is an evil. He must, on the contrary, guard himself therefrom as much as a man is able, so as to be at one time, toward some, <i>clement</i> and <i>gracious</i>, not out of mere tenderness and commiseration, but according to what is due; and at another time, toward others, <i>revengeful, keeping rancour</i>, and <i>irascible</i>, by reason of what they have deserved, and not out of mere wrath, even to the point that he must command the burning of an individual without feeling against him either indignation, or wrath, or hatred, having regard, on the contrary, only to that which he shall appear to have deserved, and considering how the accomplishment of this act is of sovereign utility for the great multitude.",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.7": "Dost thou not perceive that, in the texts of the Law, after having commanded the destruction of the <i>seven nations</i> and having said: <i>Thou shalt save alive nothing that breaths</i> (Deut., 20:16), these words are immediately followed by: <i>That they teach you not to do after all their abominations, which they have done unto their gods; so should ye sin against the Lord your God</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 18)? This signifies: Believe not that this is cruelty or a desire for vengeance; it is, on the contrary, an act required by human reason, namely, to cause the disappearance of all those who turn aside from the ways of truth, and to remove all obstacles which prevent the attainment of perfection, that is to say, the perception of God.",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.8": "Notwithstanding all this, it behoovs that acts of mercy, of pardon, of commiseration and of benevolence, should proceed from him who ruls the state far more frequently than acts of punishment; for the thirteen <i>middôth</i> are all <i>attributes of mercy</i>, with the exception of one alone, to wit: <i>Visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon the children</i> (Exodus, xxxiv, 7); for the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ונקה לא ינקה</span> (<i>Ibid.</i>) signify: <i>and He does not utterly root out</i> (destroy) <i>altogether</i>[[fn:571]], (a sense derived) from the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ונקתה</span> (Isaiah, iii, 26), <i>and she shall be destroyed</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.9": "It must be known that the words <i>visiting the iniquity of the fathers upon the children</i> relate only to the sin of idolatry in particular, and not to other sins; of which the proof lies in these words of the Decalogue: … <i>upon the third and fourth generation of them that hate me</i> (Exodus, xx, 5), for by <i>him that hats</i> (<i>God</i>), none is designated save the idolater[[fn:572]], (who is likewise presented as the object of God’s <i>hatred</i>): <i>For every abomination to the Lord</i> (<i>and all</i>) <i>which He hats, etc</i>. (Deuteron., xii, 31). One has limited it to <i>four generations</i>, because a man can see of his posterity at most but the <i>fourth generation</i>. Thus, when the population of a city given over to idolatry is put to death[[fn:573]], the aged idolater is slain together with his race even unto the great-grandson, who is the child of the fourth generation. It has therefore been, as it were, indicated[[fn:574]] that among the commandments of God, which indubitably form part of His actions, is that of slaying the descendants of idolaters, though they be young children, [[fn:575]]indiscriminately with their fathers and their grandsires. This command we find everywhere repeated in the Pentateuch, as, for instance, it was ordained concerning the <i>seduced city:</i> to <i>devote it to destruction, it and all that is therein</i> (Deut., xiii, 16); all this to the end that every trace of that which produces so great a perdition might be blotted out, even as we have set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.10": "We have digressed from the subject of the chapter; yet it behoved us to explain wherefore, in speaking here of the actions of God, one has confined oneself to these alone[[fn:576]]. The reason is that they are necessary for the governance of states; for the supreme virtue of man is to render himself like unto God as far as he is able, that is to say, that we ought to make our actions like unto His, even as the (Sages) have set forth, in commenting upon the words <i>be ye holy, etc</i>. (Levit., xix, 2): «Even,» say they, «as He is gracious, so also must thou be gracious; even as He is merciful, so also must thou be merciful[[fn:577]].»",
    "root.text.Part 1..53.11": "Our design, in sum, is (to show) that the attributes ascribed unto Him are attributes (derived) from His actions, and in no wise signify that He possesses any quality.",
    "root.text.Part 1..54.0": "We have already observed, in divers places of this treatise, that whatsoever implies corporeality must necessarily be removed from God. Likewise, all passion must be removed from Him; for all passions imply change, and the agent of these passions is indubitably distinct from that which is passively affected. Now, if God were, in any manner, passively affected, there would be somewhat other than He which would act upon Him and change Him. In like manner, one must necessarily remove from Him all <i>privation</i>[[fn:578]] and (not admit) that any perfection whatsoever should at one time be wanting to Him, and at another exist (in Him); for if this were admitted, He would be perfect (only) <i>potentially</i>, but all <i>potentiality</i> is necessarily accompanied by a <i>privation</i>, and everything that passes from <i>potentiality</i> to <i>actuality</i> has absolute need of somewhat else existing <i>actually</i>, which causs it to pass thither. Wherefore it is necessary that all His perfections should exist <i>actually</i> and that He should have nothing whatsoever <i>potentially</i>. Furthermore, that which must necessarily be removed from Him is resemblance to any of the beings that exist; this is a matter known to all, and already in the books of the prophets has assimilation been expressly removed, when it is said: <i>To whom then will ye liken me, or</i> (<i>to whom</i>) <i>shall I be equal?</i> (Isaiah, xl, 25.) <i>To whom then will ye liken God? or what likeness will ye compare unto Him?</i> (<i>Ibid</i>., <small>v</small>. 18.) <i>There is none like unto Thee, O Lord!</i> (Jeremiah, x, 6.) — There are of this many examples.",
    "root.text.Part 1..54.1": "In sum, therefore, everything that tends toward one of these four species must necessarily be excluded from Him by means of clear demonstration; namely, all that tends toward corporeality, or that tends toward a <i>passion</i> and a change, or that tends toward a <i>privation</i>, as, for example, (to admit) that a thing might not exist in Him <i>in act</i>[[fn:579]] and thereafter be <i>in act</i>, or finally that which tends toward assimilating Him to one of His creatures. These matters are among those in which physical science proves useful for the knowledge of God; for, whoever does not possess these (physical) sciences does not apprehend what is defective in the <i>passions</i>, nor does he comprehend what is meant by <i>being in potentiality</i> and <i>being in act</i>, and remains ignorant that <i>privation</i> is inherent in all that is <i>in potentiality</i>[[fn:580]], that which is <i>in potentiality</i> being more imperfect than that which moves in order that this <i>potentiality</i> may pass into <i>act</i>, and that which moves being likewise imperfect in comparison to that towards which it moves in order to arrive at <i>act</i>[[fn:581]]. But, if one comprehends these things without also knowing their demonstrations, one does not apprehend the particularities which necessarily result from these general propositions; hence, one possesses no demonstration for the existence of God, nor for the necessity of excluding from Him these (four) species (of things).",
    "root.text.Part 1..54.2": "Having made these preliminary observations, I proceed to another chapter, in which I shall demonstrate the falsity of that which those who admit in Him <i>essential attributes</i> believe. However, this can only be comprehended by one who has first acquired knowledge of logic and the nature of being.",
    "root.text.Part 1..55.0": "Know thou that similitude is a certain relation between two things; and whenever no relation can be admitted between two things, neither can a similitude be conceived between them. Likewise, whenever there is no similitude between two things, there is also no relation between them. Thus, for example, it is not said: \"Such a heat is similar to such a color\", nor: \"such a sound is similar to such a sweetness\"; and this is a matter manifest in itself[[fn:582]]. Now, as the relation between us and God—I mean, between Him and that which is outside of Him—is (a thing) inadmissible, it follows that similitude is likewise inadmissible. It must be known that, whenever two things come under the same species—I mean, that their quiddity is one—and they differ only in greatness and smallness, or in strength and weakness, or in other things of this kind, they are necessarily similar to each other, although they differ in the said kind of difference. Thus, for example, the mustard seed and the sphere of the fixed stars are similar in having the three dimensions; and although the latter be exceedingly great[[fn:583]] and the other exceedingly small, the notion of the existence of dimensions is the same in both. Likewise, the wax which melts in the sun and the element of fire are similar in having heat; and although the heat of the latter be exceedingly strong and that of the former exceedingly weak, the notion of the manifestation of this quality is nevertheless the same in both.",
    "root.text.Part 1..55.1": "Thus, then, those who posit that there exist <i>essential</i> attributes applicable to the Creator, namely, that He possesses <i>existence</i>, <i>life</i>, <i>power</i>, <i>knowledge</i>[[fn:584]], and <i>will</i>, ought to apprehend that these qualities cannot be ascribed, in the identical sense, to Him and to us, with the sole distinction that these attributes (of God) would be greater, or more perfect, or more enduring, or more stable than our own[[fn:585]], such that His existence would be more stable than ours, His life more durable than ours, His power greater than ours, His knowledge more perfect than ours, and His will more extensive than ours, and that a single definition would encompass both; as indeed those proponents contend[[fn:586]]. This is by no means the case: for the comparative degree is employed solely (when a comparison is drawn) between those things to which the adjective in question applies as a <i>common noun</i>[[fn:587]], and, this being so, there must exist similitude (between these things); but, according to the persuasion of those who believe in <i>essential attributes</i>, it would be necessary to concede that, just as the essence of God cannot resemble other essences, so too the essential attributes they ascribe to Him bear no resemblance to the attributes (of other beings), and that (consequently) the same definition cannot apply to both. Nevertheless, they do not proceed thus, believing, on the contrary, that a single definition encompasses both, notwithstanding the absence of similitude between them[[fn:588]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..55.2": "It is, in truth, evident to him who comprehends the signification of the <i>similitude</i>, that, if the term <i>existing</i> is simultaneously applied to God and to all that lies beyond Him, it is merely a matter of simple homonymy; and similarly, if the <i>science</i>, the <i>power</i>, the <i>will</i>, and the <i>life</i> are attributed simultaneously to God and to all that is endowed with science, power, will, and life, it is merely a matter of simple homonymy, whereby there is no resemblance of signification between the two (kinds of attributes).",
    "root.text.Part 1..55.3": "It must not be believed that they are employed by <i>amphiboly</i>; for the names which are predicated by amphiboly are those which apply to two things between which there is a resemblance in some respect[[fn:589]]. This sense is an <i>accident</i> in them and does not constitute the <i>essence</i> of each of them; but these things attributed to God cannot be accidents, according to any of the thinkers[[fn:590]], whilst the attributes which belong unto us are all accidents, according to the opinion of the <i>Mutakallimīn</i>[[fn:591]]. I would fain know, then, whence the similitude should arise, so that a single definition might embrace both (kinds of attributes) and that they might be designated by a <i>common name</i>[[fn:592]], as they do maintain!—Thus, it is demonstrated in a decisive manner that between these attributes which are ascribed to God and those known to us, there is absolutely no species of community of meaning, and that the community exists only in the name, and naught else. This being so, thou must not admit ideas added to the divine essence and similar to those attributes which are added to our own essence, because there is between the two (sorts of attributes) a community of name.",
    "root.text.Part 1..55.4": "This subject is of great importance amongst those who are conversant with these matters; it behoves thee, therefore, to penetrate into it and to render an account thereof unto thyself as best thou mayest, so that it may serve as a preparation for that which we desire to make thee understand.",
    "root.text.Part 1..56.0": "UPON THE ATTRIBUTES. <br><small>MORE PROFOUND THAN THE FOREGOING</small>[[fn:593]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..56.1": "It is known that existence is an accident supervening upon that which exists; wherefore it is something accessory to the <i>quiddity</i> of that which exists[[fn:594]]. This is an evident and necessary truth in all that of which the existence has a cause; for its existence is a thing superadded to its <i>quiddity.</i> But as for that of which the existence has no cause, and this is God alone, the Most High, [for this is what is signified by saying that God is of a <i>necessary existence</i>,] His existence is His true essence; His essence is His existence, and it is not an essence to which it has <i>happened</i> to exist, so that His existence therein should be something accessory; for He is always of a <i>necessary existence</i>, and (His existence) is not something new in Him, nor an accident which has supervened upon Him[[fn:595]]. Thus, therefore, He <i>exists</i>, but not by <i>existence</i>, and likewise, He <i>lives</i>, but not by <i>life</i>, He <i>can</i>, but not by <i>power</i>, and He <i>knows</i>, but not by <i>knowledge</i>[[fn:596]]; the whole, on the contrary, reverts to a single idea, in which there is no multiplicity, as shall be explained.",
    "root.text.Part 1..56.2": "It is also essential to note that unity and multiplicity are accidents that have befallen that which exists, inasmuch as it is <i>multiple</i> or <i>a unit</i>, a concept that has been elucidated in the <i>Metaphysics</i>[[fn:597]]. And, just as number is not the very things that are enumerated, so too is unity not the very thing that is <i>one</i>; for these are all (two) accidents of the sort of discrete quantity <i>discrète</i>[[fn:598]], which attain to beings capable of receiving analogous accidents.",
    "root.text.Part 1..56.3": "As touching the <i>Necessary Being</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[t:1]</sup> (God), who is verily simple and altogether exempt from composition, even as one could not attribute unto Him the accident of <i>multiplicity</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[t:3]</sup>, so likewise were it false to attribute unto Him the accident of <i>unity;</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[t:5]</sup> I mean to say that unity is in no wise a thing added unto His essence, but that He is <i>one, yet not by unity.</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[t:7]</sup>",
    "root.text.Part 1..56.4": "These subtle subjects, which almost escape the minds, cannot be expressed by the usual language, which is one of the great causes of error; for, in every language, we are (in this regard) extremely limited in expression, so that such a subject, we cannot represent it (by words) except by relaxing ourselves for expression[[fn:599]]. Therefore, when we wish to indicate that God <i>is not multiple</i>, this cannot be said except by (the word) <i>un</i>, although the <i>un</i> and the <i>multiple</i> both belong to the <i>quantity;quantitas c'est pourquoi, résumant l'idée, nous amenons l'esprit à (comprendre) la réalité de la chose, en disant: <i>un, non par l'unité.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..56.5": "It is thus that we express <i>Eternal</i> to signify that God is not something which is <i>born</i>[[fn:600]]. But to say <i>Eternal</i>, is, doubtless, to place ourselves at ease (with the expression); for the word <i>eternal</i> denotes something which is in relation to time, which is an accident of the movement that is inherent in the body[[fn:601]]. Furthermore, this word expresses a <i>relation</i>[[fn:602]]; for one says <i>eternal</i> with regard to time as one says <i>long</i> and <i>short</i> with regard to the line[[fn:603]]. All that does not touch the accident of time cannot, in reality, be said to be either <i>eternal</i>, or <i>born</i> (or <i>created</i>), any more than sweetness can be said to be <i>curved</i> or <i>straight</i>, or sound, <i>salty</i> or <i>insipid</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..56.6": "These matters are not obscure to one who has diligently trained himself to apprehend subjects in their veritable reality, and who examines them in such a manner that the intellect grasps them in their abstraction, and not according to the collective sense which the words denote[[fn:604]]. Whenever, therefore, in the (sacred) books, one discovers that the attributes of <i>first</i> and <i>last</i>[[fn:605]] are ascribed to God, it is as when the <i>eye</i> and the <i>ear</i>[[fn:606]] are attributed to Him; by this is signified that God is not susceptible to change, and that absolutely nothing new accrues to Him, but not that God falls under the dominion of time, such that there should be any analogy between Him and other things existing in time, and that He should thus be <i>first</i> and <i>last.</i> All these words are employed solely <i>in accordance with the language of men;</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..56.7": "so likewise, when we say (that He is) <i>one</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[t:1]</sup>, the meaning is that there is naught like unto Him, but not that the idea of <i>unity</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[t:3]</sup> is added unto His essence.",
    "root.text.Part 1..57.0": "<small>OF A DEEPER NATURE THAN THE FOREGOING.</small>",
    "root.text.Part 1..57.1": "Know thou that the true attributes of God are those in which the attribution is effected by means of negations, which requirs no improper expression, nor gives occasion, in any wise, to ascribe unto God any imperfection whatsoever[[fn:607]]; but an attribution enunciated affirmatively involvs the notion of <i>association</i> and of imperfection, even as we have set forth[[fn:608]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..57.2": "It behoves me first to explain unto thee how negations are, in a certain manner, attributes, and in what wise they are distinguished from affirmative attributes; thereafter shall I explain unto thee how we have no means of assigning an attribute unto God, save by negations, and not otherwise.",
    "root.text.Part 1..57.3": "I say, then: an attribute is not only that which particularizes the subject in such wise that it shars not this attribute with aught else, but an attribute is also at times the attribute of a subject, even though the latter should share it with another and no particularization should result therefrom. If, for example, beholding a man from afar, thou askest what is the object seen, and it be answered thee (that it is) an <i>animal</i>, this is indubitably an attribute of the object seen; for, although it distinguishes it not particularly from all other things, there nevertheless results therefrom a certain particularisation, in the sense that the object seen is a body which belongs neither to the species of plants, nor to that of minerals. Likewise again: if, a man being in a certain house, thou knowest that there is therein a certain body, without knowing what it is, and, having asked what is in that house, someone answers thee that there is therein neither mineral nor vegetable body, there results (from this answer) a certain particularisation, and thou knowest that there is therein an <i>animal</i>, although thou knowest not what animal it be. In this respect, therefore, negative attributes have something in common with affirmative attributes; for they necessarily produce a certain particularisation, although this be limited to the removing, by negation, of all that which we did not at first believe ought to be denied[[fn:609]]. But here is the respect in which negative attributes are distinguished from affirmative attributes: it is that affirmative attributes, even when they do not particularize (the subject), always indicate a part of the thing which one desirs to know, whether it be a part of its substance, or one of its accidents, whilst negative attributes do in no wise make us to know what is in reality the essence that we desire to know[[fn:610]], unless it be accidentally, as we have given examples thereof.",
    "root.text.Part 1..57.4": "Upon this preliminary observation, it is said: It is a thing demonstrated that God, the Most High, is the <i>necessary</i> being, in which, as we shall demonstrate, there is no composition. We apprehend nothing of him, save that <i>he is</i>, but not <i>what he is</i>[[fn:611]]. It is therefore impossible to admit that he has an affirmative attribute: for he has no <i>being</i> outside of his <i>quiddity</i>, whereby the attribute might indicate one of the two things[[fn:612]]; nay, his quiddity cannot be <i>composed</i>, whereby the attribute might indicate its two parts[[fn:613]]; and, a fortiori, he cannot have accidents that might be indicated by the attribute. There is therefore, in any manner, no affirmative attribute of God.",
    "root.text.Part 1..57.5": "Negatory attributes are those which must be employed to guide the mind toward that which it behoovs us to believe concerning God; for there results from their part no multiplicity, and they lead the mind to the term of that which is possible for man to apprehend of God. Since it is demonstrated unto us, for example, that there exists necessarily somewhat beyond these essences perceived by the senses and of which we embrace the knowledge by means of the intellect, we say of this somewhat that it <i>exists</i>, which signifies that its non-existence is inadmissible. Perceiving thereafter that it is not with this Being as it is, for example, with the existence of the elements, which are inanimate bodies, we say that He is <i>living</i>, which signifies that God is not without life. Perceiving thereafter that it is also not with this Being as with the existence of the heaven, which is a <i>body</i> (albeit) living, we say that He is by no means a <i>body</i>. Perceiving thereafter that it is not with this Being as with the existence of the intellect, which, although it be neither a body nor without life, is nevertheless the product of a cause, we say that God is <i>eternal</i>, which signifies that He has no cause which has brought Him into existence. Then we perceive that the existence of this Being, which is His essence[[fn:614]], does not suffice Him in such wise as to <i>exist</i> only (Himself), but that, on the contrary, there emanate from Him many existences; and this, not as heat emanats from fire, nor as light proceeds from the sun, but by a divine action which gives them duration and harmony by governing them well[[fn:615]], as we shall set forth. And it is because of all this that we attribute unto God <i>power</i>, <i>knowledge</i>, and <i>will</i>, meaning by these attributes that He is neither impotent, nor ignorant, nor heedless, nor negligent. If we say that He <i>is not impotent</i>, it signifies that His existence suffices to bring into existence things other than Himself; <i>not ignorant</i> signifies that He <i>perceivs</i>, that is to say, that He <i>lives</i>, for everything that perceivs has life[[fn:616]]; by <i>neither heedless nor negligent</i>, we mean to say that all these beings follow a certain order and a regime, that they are not neglected and delivered unto chance, but that they are as everything which is conducted, with an intention and a will, by Him who wills it[[fn:617]]. Finally, we perceive that this Being has no fellow; if therefore we say[[fn:618]]: <i>He is one</i>, it signifies <i>that there are not many</i>[[fn:619]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..57.6": "It is therefore manifest that every attribute wherewith we invest Him, is either an attribute of <i>action</i>, or else [if its purpose be to make understood the <i>essence</i> of God, and not His <i>action</i>] it must be regarded as the negation of its privative[[fn:620]]. But these negations themselves must not be employed, to apply them unto God, save in the manner that thou knowest; (I mean) that one sometimes denies of a thing that which it is not in its condition to possess, as when we say of the wall that <i>it ses not</i>[[fn:621]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..57.7": "— Thou knowest, O reader of this treatise! that this very heaven — [which yet is a moved body[[fn:622]], and which we have measured by spans and by cubits, even to embracing with our science the measures of certain of its parts and the greater part of its movements[[fn:623]]] —, our intellects are much too weak to comprehend its <i>quiddity</i>, although we know that it has necessarily[[fn:624]] matter and form, save that it is not there a matter such as that which is in us; wherefore we can only qualify it by words without precision[[fn:625]], and not by a precise <i>affirmation</i>. In fact, we say that the heaven <i>is neither light nor heavy</i>, that it is <i>impassible</i>, and that because of this <i>it receivs no impression;</i> that it <i>has neither taste nor smell</i>, and other such negations[[fn:626]]; all this because of our ignorance concerning the said <i>matter.</i> And what shall it be of our intellects, if they seek to apprehend Him who is exempt from matter, who is of an extreme simplicity, the necessary Being, who has no cause and who is affected by nothing which might be added to His perfect essence, whose <i>perfection</i> signifies (for us) <i>negation of imperfections</i>, as we have set it forth? for we apprehend of Him naught else save <i>that He is</i>, that there is a Being whom none of the beings He has produced resembls, who has absolutely naught in common with these latter, that there is in Him neither multiplicity, nor impotence to produce that which is outside of Him, and that His relation to the world is that of the captain to the vessel; not that this be the true relation, nor that the comparison be just, but it serves as a guide to the mind (to understand) that God governs the beings, that is to say, that He perpetuats them and maintains them in order, as is behoovful. This subject shall yet be more amply set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 1..57.8": "Praise be unto Him who (is so highly exalted that), when intellects contemplate His essence, their comprehension is changed into incapacity, and when they examine how His actions result from His will, their knowledge is changed into ignorance, and when tongues desire to glorify Him by attributes, all eloquence becoms a feeble stammering[[fn:627]]!",
    "root.text.Part 1..58.0": "One might here pose the following question: If, in truth, there be no means to perceive the true essence of God, if one can demonstrate the impossibility of perceiving aught else save <i>that He is</i>[[fn:628]], and if affirmative attributes be impossible, as has been demonstrated, in which then consists the relative superiority amongst those who perceive? for then, that which our master Moses and Solomon perceived is the same thing as that which every individual amongst the students perceivs, and it is impossible to add aught thereto. And yet it is generally admitted by the theologians[[fn:629]], or rather by the philosophers, that there are in this regard many gradations[[fn:630]]. Know then that verily it is so, and that there are most great nuances of superiority among those who perceive. In fact, in proportion as one augments the attributes of a subject, the latter is better determined and one apprehends better and better its true nature[[fn:631]]; and likewise, in proportion as thou augmentest the negations in regard to God, thou drawest nigh unto perception and art nearer thereto than he who denies not that which is demonstrated unto thee as behooving to be denied.",
    "root.text.Part 1..58.1": "Wherefore one man may weary himself for many years to understand a science and to verify the principles thereof, so that he may attain unto certitude[[fn:632]]; and then all this science produces no other result than (to teach us) that we must deny of God a certain thing which is known, by demonstration, to be inadmissible in respect of Him[[fn:633]]; whilst for another, among those who are weak in speculation, this is not demonstrated, and it remains doubtful for him whether this thing exists or exists not in God; and another yet, among those who are utterly bereft of vision, affirms of Him this thing whose negation is demonstrated (as indispensable). I, for example, shall demonstrate that He is in no wise a body; another will doubt, and will not know whether He be a body or whether He be not; and yet another will decide that He is a body, and will approach God with such a belief[[fn:634]]. How great shall be the difference between these three persons! The first shall indubitably be the nearest unto God, the second shall be far from Him, and the third, the farthest. Likewise, if we should suppose a fourth, for whom it was demonstrated that passions are inadmissible in respect of God, whereas for the first, who only denies corporeity, this was not demonstrated, this fourth person would indubitably be nearer unto God than the first, and so forth; so that, if there were found a person for whom it was demonstrated that it is impossible (to admit), in respect of God, many things which, according to us, <i>might</i> exist in Him, or emanate from Him, — and with all the more reason, if we went so far as to believe this <i>necessary</i>, — this person would indubitably be more perfect than we.",
    "root.text.Part 1..58.2": "Thus, it is manifest unto thee that, as often as it shall be demonstrated unto thee that a certain thing must be denied of God, thou shalt thereby be more perfect, and that as often as thou shalt affirmatively attribute unto Him a thing added (to His essence), thou shalt liken Him (unto the creatures), and thou shalt be far from knowing His reality. It is in this manner that one must draw near to the perception of God, by means of inquiry[[fn:635]] and study, in order to know the falsity of all that is inadmissible in respect of Him, and not by affirmatively attributing to Him a thing as being added to His essence, or as if this thing were a perfection in respect of Him, because it is found to be one in respect of ourselves; for all perfections are but certain <i>capacities</i>[[fn:636]], and no capacity exists in every (being) endowed with capacity[[fn:637]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..58.3": "Thou shalt know, therefore, that if thou affirmatively attributest unto Him a thing other (than Himself), thou removest thyself from Him in two respects: first, because all that thou attributest unto Him is a perfection (only) for us, and, secondly, because He possesses no thing other (than Himself), but on the contrary, it is His essence itself which forms His perfections, as we have set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 1..58.4": "Now, everyone having perceived that, even as to that which we have the faculty to perceive (of God), there is no means to perceive it otherwise than by negation, and negation making us know absolutely nothing of the reality of the thing to which it is applied[[fn:638]], all, both ancients and moderns, have declared that the intellects cannot perceive God, that He alone perceivs <i>what He is</i>, and that to perceive Him is (to recognize) that one is powerless to perceive Him fully[[fn:639]]. All the philosophers say: We are dazzled by His beauty, and He hids Himself from us by the very force of His manifestation, even as the sun hids itself from the eyes, which are too weak to perceive it. Men have enlarged upon this in (discourses) which it would be useless to repeat here[[fn:640]]; but that which has been said most eloquently in this regard are these words of the Psalmist: <span dir=\"rtl\">לך דמיה תהלה</span> (Ps. 65, 2), the sense of which is: <i>unto Thee silence is praise.</i> This is a most eloquent expression upon this subject; for, whatsoever we may say with the intent of exalting and glorifying (God), we shall find therein something offensive[[fn:641]] in respect of God, and we shall see (expressed) therein a certain imperfection. It is therefore better to be silent and to limit oneself[[fn:642]] to the perceptions of the intellect, as perfect men have recommended, saying: <i>Commune</i> (<i>meditate</i>) <i>with your own heart upon your bed, and be still</i> (Ps. 4, 5).",
    "root.text.Part 1..58.5": "Thou knowest also a celebrated passage of the Sages to which I would that all their words were similar; although it be a passage which is known by heart, I shall cite it to thee textually, in order to call thy attention to the ideas it contains. Behold what they say[[fn:643]]: \"A certain man, coming into the presence of Rabbi Hanina, expressed himself thus (whilst making his prayer): O God, the great, the mighty, the terrible, the magnificent, the strong, the feared, the imposing!... The Rabbi said unto him (interrupting him): Hast thou finished all the praises of thy Lord? Verily, even the first three (attributes), had Moses not pronounced them in the Law[[fn:644]] and had the men of the <i>Great Assembly</i>[[fn:645]] not come to fix them in the prayer, we should not dare to utter them; and thou, thou utterest so great a number! To make a comparison: a mortal king, for example, who should possess millions of pieces of gold, and whom one should praise for (possessing pieces) of silver, would this not be an offense unto him?\" Behold how this good man expressed himself.",
    "root.text.Part 1..58.6": "Observe first how great was his reluctance and his aversion to the accumulation of affirmative attributes[[fn:646]], and observe likewise that he says clearly that, were we left to our own intelligence alone[[fn:647]], we should never utter[[fn:648]] any attributes nor pronounce any of them; but since, by the necessity of speaking unto men in such wise as to give them some idea (of God), one was forced to describe[[fn:649]] God by their (own) perfections — [in accordance with these words: <i>the Law has spoken according to the language of men</i>[[fn:650]]] —, we ought, in the last resort, to confine ourselves to the (three) words in question[[fn:651]], and even then we should never employ them as names of God, save when we read them in the Pentateuch. If, however, the men of the <i>Great Synod</i>, who were (in part) prophets, came afterwards to sanction their use in prayer, we ought ever to restrict ourselves to these words alone[[fn:652]]. In substance, then, he (R. ’Hanînâ) setts forth that two necessities must meet for us to employ them in prayer: the first, that they be found in the Pentateuch; the second, that the prophets should have established them in prayer. Without the first necessity, we should not pronounce them (at all); without the second, we should not have taken them from their (primitive) place to use them in prayer; and thou (added he), dost thou accumulate attributes[[fn:653]]?",
    "root.text.Part 1..58.7": "It is manifest unto thee also by these words (of R. ’Hanînâ) that it is not permitted us to employ in prayer, nor to pronounce, all the epithets which thou findest attributed to God in the books of the prophets; for he says not (merely): “Had not Moses said them, we should not dare to say them”, but (he adds as) another condition: “and had not the men of the <i>Great Synod</i> come to establish them in prayer”, and since then only has it been permitted us to employ them in prayer. But not as have done those men verily inept, who have striven to dwell at length (upon the attributes), in prayers of their own composition and orations of their own fashion, whereby they thought to draw nigh unto God, and in which they have given Him attributes which, even were they given to a human being, would imply an imperfection[[fn:654]]. It is because, not understanding these sublime subjects, which are too foreign to the intelligences of the vulgar, they approached God with their rash tongues, and used toward Him all the attributes and all the addresses which they thought they might permit themselves[[fn:655]], and dwelt thereon, in order to move Him, as they imagined, in such wise that He might be affected (by their words). Especially when they found in this regard some text of a prophetic discourse, they believed themselves permitted to employ these same terms, which, in any case, have need of being allegorically interpreted; they took them in their literal sense, derived therefrom (other terms), formed ramifications thereof, and built discourses thereupon. This species of license is frequent among poets and orators, or among those who pretend to make verses; so that there have been composed discourses which, in part, are pure irreligion, and in part betray a weakness of mind and a corruption of the imagination such as would naturally cause a man to laugh when he listens to them, and to weep when he considers that such language has been held toward God. Were it not painful for me to disparage the authors, I should cite to thee somewhat thereof to draw thy attention to what is there impious[[fn:656]]; but these are discourses whose vice is too evident to him who knows how to understand,",
    "root.text.Part 1..58.8": "and thou must say to thyself in pondering thereon: if it be a grievous sin to <i>speak evil</i> and to <i>slander the reputation of another</i>[[fn:657]], how much more so (is one guilty) in giving free rein to his tongue when it concerns God, and in giving Him attributes above which He is exalted? I should not call this a sin, but <i>an offence, and a blasphemy rashly committed</i> by the multitude that listens and by that fool who utters (such words). But as for him who understands what there is of vice in such discourses, and who (notwithstanding this) pronouncs them, he is, in my judgment, of the number of those of whom it was said: <i>and the children of Israel imagined concerning God words that were not right</i> (II Kings, xvii, 9)[[fn:658]], and elsewhere: <i>and to utter error against the Lord</i> (Isaiah, xxxii, 6). If then thou art of those <i>who respect the glory of their Creator</i>[[fn:659]], thou must by no means lend an ear thereto; and how then wouldst thou dare to pronounce them, and how (much more so) wouldst thou dare to compose the like? For thou knowest how great is the sin of him <i>who hurls words toward heaven</i>[[fn:660]]. One must by no means engage in attributes of God (expressed) by affirmation, believing thereby to glorify Him, and one must not depart from that which the men of the <i>Great Synod</i> have established in prayers and benedictions; there is therein enough for the need, and more than enough, as R. ’Hanînâ has said. As for what is further found (in the way of attributes) in the books of the prophets, one may read it in passing, provided one admit, as we have set forth, that these are attributes of <i>action</i>, or that they indicate the negation of their privation[[fn:661]]. And this, moreover, must not be divulged to the multitude; on the contrary, this kind of study belongs to the select few who do not believe they glorify God by saying that which is not seemly, but by understanding aright.",
    "root.text.Part 1..58.9": "I return now to complete my remarks upon the words of R. ’Hanînâ and their wise arrangement[[fn:662]]. He says not: “To make a comparison, a mortal king, for example, who should possess <i>millions</i> of pieces of gold, and whom one should praise for (possessing) <i>a hundred pieces</i>”; for this comparison (so conceived) would indicate that the perfections of God are greater than those attributed to Him, but that they are nevertheless of the same species, whereas, as we have demonstrated, it is not so. But the wisdom of this comparison consists in these words: “... pieces <i>of gold</i>, and whom one should praise for (possessing pieces) <i>of silver</i>”, which indicate that God has nothing which is of the same species as those perfections which are found in us, and that these, on the contrary, are imperfections in respect to Him, as he explains by saying: “Would this not be an offence unto him?”",
    "root.text.Part 1..58.10": "Thus, I have made known to thee that all those attributes which thou believest to be a perfection[[fn:663]] constitute an imperfection in respect to God, if they be of the same species as those which we possess. Solomon has already sufficiently instructed us in this regard, saying: <i>For God is in heaven, and thou upon earth; therefore let thy words be few</i> (Ecclesiastes, v, 1).",
    "root.text.Part 1..59.0": "In this chapter, I desire to set before thee certain illustrations, by which thou mayest better conceive how necessary it is to ascribe unto God numerous negative attributes, and whereby thou shalt more and more avoid the admission of affirmative attributes in respect of Him. Suppose a man has the notion that there exists (something which is called) a ship, yet knows not whether the thing to which this name appertains be a substance or an accident; thereafter another individual has recognized that it is by no means an accident; another again, that it is not a mineral; another, that it is neither an animal; another, that it is not a vegetable still attached to the earth; another, that it is not a single body forming a natural whole; another, that it is not something having a flat form, such as planks and doors; another, that it is not a sphere; another, that it is not something of a conical form; another, that it is neither something circular, nor something having plane sides[[fn:664]]; and lastly, another, that it is not a solid body; — it is manifest that this last person will have arrived, by means of these negative attributes, almost at the point of figuring to himself the ship as it is, and will find himself, as it were, on a level with him who figurs it unto himself as a wooden body, hollow, oblong, and composed of many pieces of wood, and who represents it by means of affirmative attributes. As for the preceding persons of whom we have spoken in our illustration, each of them is further from forming an idea of the ship than he who follows him, so that the first, in our example, knows naught thereof but the name alone.",
    "root.text.Part 1..59.1": "In such wise do negative attributes bring thee closer unto the knowledge of God and the perception of Him; but thou must above all endeavour that every new negation which thou addest be demonstrated, and thou must not be content merely to pronounce it[[fn:665]]; for, in proportion as it shall be manifested unto thee by a demonstration that a thing which was believed to exist in God must be denied of Him, thou shalt indubitably have drawn closer unto Him by one degree more.",
    "root.text.Part 1..59.2": "It is in this manner that certain men are found very near unto Him, whilst others are extremely remote from Him; yet not that there is herein any local proximity[[fn:666]], so that one might (materially) draw near to or depart from Him, as they believe who are destitute of sight. Comprehend this well, know it, and thou shalt find thyself happy therein. Thou knowest now the path in which thou must walk to draw near unto God, and it depends upon thy will to walk therein[[fn:667]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..59.3": "As for the attributes of God (expressed) by affirmations, they involve a great peril; for it has already been demonstrated that whatsoever we might take for a perfection [even though this perfection should exist in God, according to the opinion of those who admit the attributes], would not be the same kind of perfection that we should imagine, but would only be so called by homonymy, as we have explained[[fn:668]]. This would necessarily lead thee to a negative idea: for, in saying that He <i>knows</i> by a sole knowledge, and that, by this immutable and not multiple knowledge, He knows the multiple and variable things which are incessantly renewed, without there occurring in Him any new knowledge, and that it is by a single immutable knowledge that He knows a thing before it is born, after it has come into existence, and after it has ceased to exist, thou declarest that He knows with a knowledge which is not like ours; and likewise, there must be attached to His <i>existence</i> another idea than unto ours[[fn:669]]. Thou producest therefore necessarily negations, and, far from succeeding in establishing an <i>essential attribute</i>, thou comest to (establish) multiplicity and to admit that God is an essence having unknown attributes; for those which thou pretendest to lend Him affirmatively, thou thyself refusest to assimilate unto the attributes known amongst us, and, consequently, they are not of the same kind. Thus, then, to admit affirmative attributes[[fn:670]] would, as it were, only amount to saying: that God is a subject affected by certain attributes, and that this subject is not the same thing as these attributes[[fn:671]], so that the final result which we should obtain by this belief would be naught else than <i>association</i>[[fn:672]]. Indeed, every <i>subject</i> indubitably possesses attributes, and, although <i>one</i> by existence, it is (divided into) <i>two</i> by definition, the idea of the subject being other than that of the attribute[[fn:673]]. In several chapters of this treatise it shall be clearly demonstrated unto thee that composition is impossible in God, but that He is pure simplicity, in the highest degree.",
    "root.text.Part 1..59.4": "I will not say, moreover, that he who ascribs affirmative attributes unto God apprehends Him imperfectly, or associats (other beings) with Him, or apprehends Him contrary to what He is; but I will rather say that, without perceiving it, he eliminats from his belief the existence of God[[fn:674]]. I explain myself: he who apprehends <i>imperfectly</i> the reality of a thing, is he who apprehends one part thereof and is ignorant of another, as, for example, he who, in the idea of <i>man</i>, apprehends that which pertains to the animal nature, without apprehending that which pertains to reason; now, in the real being of God there is no multiplicity, so that one might understand such a thing thereof and be ignorant of such another. Likewise, he who <i>associats</i> (another thing) with a thing, is he who, whilst representing to himself a certain essence according to its real nature, ascribs a similar nature to another essence; now, the attributes in question, according to the opinion of those who admit them, are not the essence of God, but ideas added to the essence[[fn:675]]. Finally, he who apprehends a thing <i>contrary</i> to what it is must necessarily apprehend something of what it really is[[fn:676]]; for, if any one should figure to himself that savour were a quantity, I should not say that he figurs the thing contrary to what it is, but I should say that he is ignorant even of the existence of savour and that he knows not to what this name appertains. These are very subtle considerations, which must be well understood.",
    "root.text.Part 1..59.5": "By this explanation thou shalt know that he alone apprehends God <i>imperfectly</i>, and is far from knowing Him, who does not recognize the non-existence (in God) of certain things which others have demonstrated must be denied of Him; so that in proportion as one admitts fewer negations, he apprehends Him less perfectly, as we have set forth at the beginning of this chapter. As for him who assigns unto God an affirmative attribute, he knows of Him naught but the mere name, but the object to which, in his imagination, this name applies, is something that has no existence; it is rather an invention and a falsehood, and it is as though he applied this name to a non-entity, for there is in existence nothing like unto it. It is as one who, having heard the name of the elephant and having known that it is an animal, should desire to know its form and true nature, and to whom another, deceived or deceiving, should say: «It is an animal with but one foot and three wings, dwelling in the depths of the sea; it has a transparent body, and a face as broad and of the same form and figure as the human face; it speaks like a man, and now flies in the air and now swimms like a fish.» Verily, I would not say that this man conceivs the elephant <i>contrary</i> to what it is in reality, nor that he has of the elephant an <i>imperfect</i> knowledge; but I would say that the thing which he imagins to be in this fashion is an invention and a falsehood, that naught like it exists, and that, on the contrary, it is a non-entity to which the name of a being has been applied, such as the <i>griffin</i>[[fn:677]], the <i>man-horse</i> (centaur), and other imaginary figures to which the name of some real being has been applied, whether it be a simple or a compound name. It is absolutely the same here: for indeed, God — may He be glorified! — is a Being whose existence has been demonstrated to be necessary, and from <i>necessary</i> existence results (as a consequence) pure simplicity, even as I shall demonstrate; but that this simple essence, of a necessary existence, should have attributes and be affected by other things, as has been pretended, is that which can in no wise take place, as has been demonstrated. If therefore we should say that this essence, for example, which is called <i>God</i>, is an essence containing numerous ideas which serve as its attributes, we should apply this name to a pure non-entity. Consider, consequently, how perilous it is to assign unto God affirmative attributes.",
    "root.text.Part 1..59.6": "Thus, then, that which must be believed regarding the attributes which are encountered in the Pentateuch and in the books of the Prophets, is that they all have no other purpose than to lead us to (recognize) the perfection of God, or else they designate actions emanating from Him, as we have already set forth[[fn:678]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..60.0.sub_0": "All the names of God found in the (sacred) books are generally derived from actions[[fn:679]], which thing is not unknown. One name alone must be excepted, namely (that which is written by) <i>yod, he, waw, he;</i> for it is a name <i>distinctively assigned</i>[[fn:680]] to God, and which, for that reason, is called <i>Shem ha-Meforash</i>[[fn:681]], signifying that it indicats expressly the essence of God, and that therein is no homonymy. As for His other glorious names, they designate Him by homonymy; for they are derived from certain actions of which the like are found amongst us, as we have set forth[[fn:682]]. The name itself (of <i>Adonai</i>), which is substituted for that of <i>yod, he, waw, he</i>[[fn:683]], is likewise <i>derivative</i>, (containing the idea) of <i>lordship;</i> (one reads for example:) <i>The man, the Lord</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אדני</span>) <i>of the country</i>, <i>spake</i>, etc. (Genesis, 42, 30). The difference between <i>Adoni</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אֲדׂנִי</span>, <i>my lord</i>), the <i>nun</i> having the vowel <i>i</i>, and <i>Adonai</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אֲדׂנָי</span>), the <i>nun</i> having a long <i>a</i>, is the same as that which exists between <i>Sari</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שָׂרִי</span>), signifying <i>my prince</i>, and <i>Sarai</i>(<span dir=\"rtl\">שָׂרַי</span>), the name of the <i>wife of Abram</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 12, 17; XVI, 1); for these are <i>emphatic</i> names which embrace also other beings[[fn:684]], and it has also been said (in speaking) to an angel: <i>Adonai</i> (my lord)... <i>pass not away</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 18, 3). If I have given thee this explanation, particularly regarding (the name) <i>Adonai</i>, employed by substitution, it is because, of all the names of God most widely spread, it is that one which is most proper unto Him. As for the others, such as <i>Dayyan</i> (judge), <i>Tzaddik</i> (just), <i>Hannun</i> (gracious, clement), <i>Rahum</i> (merciful), <i>Elohim</i>[[fn:685]], they are evidently names <i>common</i> and <i>derivative.</i> But the name which is spelled <i>yod, he, waw, he</i>, has no known etymology[[fn:686]], and is applied to no other being[[fn:687]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..60.0.sub_1": "It is not doubtful that this glorious name, which, as thou knowest, was to be pronounced only in the Sanctuary, and particularly by the <i>priests sanctified unto the Lord</i>[[fn:688]], in the sacerdotal blessing[[fn:689]], and by the High Priest on the Day of Fasting[[fn:690]], indicats a certain idea in respect of which there is naught in common between God and that which is outside of Him; perchance it indicats — according to the (Hebrew) tongue, of which there remains[[fn:691]] unto us to-day but very little, and according to the manner in which it was pronounced — the idea of <i>necessary existence.</i> In sum, that which causs this name to have so high an importance and that men refrain from pronouncing it, is that it indicats the very essence of God, so that no created being participats in what it indicats[[fn:692]], as the Doctors have said concerning this name: <i>My name, which is peculiar to Me</i>[[fn:693]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..60.1": "As for the other names, they all indicate attributes, (I mean that they indicate) not only an essence, but an essence having attributes[[fn:694]], for they are <i>derivative;</i> and by that very fact they lead to the belief in multiplicity (in God), I mean, they lead to the belief that there exist attributes and that there is an essence and something added to the essence. In effect, such is the significance of every <i>derivative</i> name; for it indicats a certain idea and an unexpressed subject to which this idea is joined[[fn:695]]. Now, as it has been demonstrated that God is not a subject to which certain ideas have come to be joined, it is known that the derivative names are (given to God), either to attribute unto Him <i>action</i>, or to lead us to (recognize) His perfection.",
    "root.text.Part 1..60.2": "Wherefore Rabbi Hanina would have felt a reluctance to say: “The <i>great, mighty,</i> and <i>awful</i> (God),” had there not been the two necessities of which he speaks[[fn:696]]; for these words might lead to the belief in <i>essential attributes</i>, I mean to say, perfections existing in God. These names of God, being derived from His actions, having multiplied, led certain men to believe that He possessed numerous attributes, as many as the actions wherefrom they are derived; therefore was it predicted that men should attain to a perception which shall cause this error to cease for them, and it is said: <i>In that day shall the Lord be</i> <small>ONE</small> <i>and His name</i> <small>ONE</small> (Zechariah xiv. 9); that is to say: even as He is <i>one</i>, so shall He then be invoked by one name only, that which uniquely indicats the essence (of God), and it shall by no means be a derivative name. In the <i>Pirke Rabbi Eliezer</i> (chap. iii.), we read: “<i>Before the world was created, there was none but the Holy One and His name alone.</i>” Observe well how clearly he stats that all these derivative names were born only after the birth of the world; and this is true, for they are all names established in relation to the actions (of God) found in the universe; but if we consider His essence, void and stripped of all action, He has absolutely no derivative name, but a single name <i>properly applied</i> to indicate His essence. We possess no <i>Shem</i> (name) which is not derivative, save that one, to wit, <i>Yod, He, Vav, He</i>, which is the absolute <i>Shem ha-mephorash</i>[[fn:697]];",
    "root.text.Part 1..60.3": "thou must believe naught else, nor give admission in thy mind to the folly of those who write <i>kami’oth</i>[[fn:698]] and to that which thou shalt hear from them or read in their senseless writings, concerning the names they forge[[fn:699]] without offering any sense whatsoever, calling them <i>Shemoth</i> (sacred names), and pretending that they require holiness and purity, and that they work miracles. All these things are fables[[fn:700]] which a perfect man should not so much as listen to; and how then could he believe in them?",
    "root.text.Part 1..60.4": "The term <i>Shem ha-mephorash</i> is therefore applied only to this <i>Tetragrammaton name</i> alone, which is written, but is not read according to its letters. It is clearly stated in the <i>Sifre</i> (upon this verse): <i>Thus</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כּהׁ</span>) <i>ye shall bless the children of Israel</i> (Numbers vi. 23): “<i>Thus</i>, that is to say, in these words; <i>thus</i>, that is to say, by the <i>Shem ha-mephorash</i>[[fn:701]].” It is likewise said there: “In the Holy City, (this name is pronounced) even as it is written; in the province, by the substituted name[[fn:702]].” And in the Talmud it is said[[fn:703]]: “<i>Thus</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כּהׁ</span>) signifies, by the <i>Shem ha-mephorash;</i> if (doubting still) thou shouldst ask: Is it truly by the <i>Shem ha-mephorash</i>, or only by the name substituted for it? there are, to inform thee, these words: <i>And they shall put</i> <small>MY NAME</small> (<i>Ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 27), that is to say, the name which is peculiar unto Me.”",
    "root.text.Part 1..60.5": "It is therefore clear that the <i>schem ha-mephorasch</i> is this name <i>tétragrammate</i>, and that it is he alone who indicates the essence (of God) without association of any other idea; it is for this reason that one has said of him: <i>he who is mine.</i> ",
    "root.text.Part 1..60.6": "In the following chapter, I shall set forth unto thee that which has led men to this belief concerning the <i>Shemoth</i> (or mysterious names); I shall explain unto thee the depth of this question and shall lift the veil therefrom for thee, so that there may remain no obscurity in this regard, unless thou shouldst desire to deceive thyself.",
    "root.text.Part 1..61.0": "The <i>sacerdotal benediction</i> has been enjoined upon us, in which the Name of the Eternal (is pronounced) as it is written, and this is the <i>schem ha-mephorasch.</i> It was not universally known how it ought to be pronounced, nor by what vowel each of its letters should be actuated, nor whether any of its letters, amongst those susceptible of it, should be doubled[[fn:704]]; the learned men transmitted this amongst themselves, that is to say, the manner of pronouncing this Name, which they taught to none, save <i>to the elect disciple, once a week</i>[[fn:705]]. I believe that when the doctors say[[fn:706]]: “the Name of four letters, the sages transmit it to their sons and to their disciples once a week,” it refers not solely to the manner of its pronunciation, but also to the instruction concerning the idea for the sake of which this Name was <i>devised</i>[[fn:707]], and within which there is likewise a divine mystery.",
    "root.text.Part 1..61.1": "There was also possessed a name which comprised <i>twelve letters</i>[[fn:708]], and which was inferior in sanctity to the name of four letters; that which is most probable, in my judgment, is that it was not a single name, but two or three, which being joined together had twelve letters. It was that which was substituted whenever the name of four letters occurred in reading, even as we substitute this day that which commencs with <i>aleph, daleth.</i> This name of twelve letters had doubtless also a more particular sense than that indicated by the name of <i>Adonai;</i> it was by no means forbidden to teach it, nor was a mystery made thereof[[fn:709]] unto any man of science, but on the contrary, it was taught unto whomsoever desired to learn it. It was not so with the <i>tetragrammaton</i> name; for none of those who knew it ever taught it save unto his son and his disciple once in a week. But since certain lawless men, having learned this name of twelve letters, professed in consequence thereof evil beliefs[[fn:710]],—as it happens unto every man who is not perfect, when he learns that the thing is not such as he had at first imagined,—this name was also hidden, and was taught only <i>unto the most pious of the sacerdotal order</i>, to be used in blessing the people in the sanctuary; for already, by reason of the corruption of men, they had ceased to pronounce the <i>shem ha-mephorash</i>, even in the sanctuary: “After the death of Simon the Just,” say the doctors, “the priests, his brethren, ceased to bless by the <i>Name</i> (tetragrammaton)[[fn:711]]”; but they blessed by this <i>name of twelve letters.</i> “At first,” they say elsewhere[[fn:712]], “it was transmitted unto every man; but since temerarious men multiplied, it was transmitted only unto the most pious of the sacerdotal order, and these caused it to be swallowed up in the melodies of the priests, their brethren. Rabbi Tarphon said: One day I followed my maternal grandfather[[fn:713]] upon the dais, and, inclining mine ear toward a priest, I heard that he caused the name in question to be swallowed up in the melodies of the priests his brethren.”",
    "root.text.Part 1..61.2": "There was likewise possessed a <i>name of two-and-forty letters.</i> Now, every man capable of an idea knows well that it is absolutely impossible for two-and-forty letters to form a single word; they could therefore be naught but several words, which formed a total of two-and-forty letters. These words, it cannot be doubted, necessarily indicated certain ideas which were intended to bring man near unto the true conception of the divine essence, by the process of which we have spoken[[fn:714]]. If these words, composed of numerous letters, have been designated as a single <i>name</i>, it is only because they indicate a single thing, as do all <i>improvised</i> proper names; and, to make this thing understood, several words were employed, for at times many words are used to make a single thing understood.",
    "root.text.Part 1..61.3": "Thou must well penetrate this, and know also that what was taught were the ideas indicated by these two names, and not the mere pronunciation of the letters, devoid[[fn:715]] of all idea. Never was the appellation <i>shem ha-mephorash</i> applied unto the said name of twelve letters, nor unto that of two-and-forty;[[fn:716]] this designation denots only the <i>particular name</i> (tetragrammaton), as we have set forth. As for these two other names, they necessarily contained a certain metaphysical instruction, and the proof that one of them contained an instruction of this kind is that the doctors say in this regard[[fn:717]]: “The name of two-and-forty letters is most holy, and is transmitted only unto the pious man who is in his middle age, who is not prone to anger, nor to drunkenness, nor persists in his evil ways, and who speaks gently with all creatures. Whosoever knows this name and guards it with heed and purity is beloved above and cherished here below; he is an object of respect for all creatures, his learning endurs within him, and he has for his portion two worlds, this world and the world to come.” Such is the text of the Talmud; but how far is the sense which men attach to these words from the intention of their author! Indeed, the most part believe that there is naught there but letters which one pronouncs, but whereunto no idea is attached, so that it would be by these letters that one should obtain the said sublime things, and that it would be for their sake that one would have need of those moral dispositions and that great preparation of which mention was made; whereas it is manifest that the matter in all this is only to make known metaphysical subjects, of those subjects which form part of the <i>mysteries of the Torah</i>, as we have set forth[[fn:718]]. In the books which have been composed[[fn:719]] upon the science of metaphysics, it has been explained that it is impossible to forget this science, I mean the perception of the <i>Active Intellect</i>[[fn:720]]; and that is the sense of these words: <i>his learning endurs within him.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..61.4": "But perverse and ignorant men, having found these talmudic texts, saw therein a vast field for falsehood, saying unto themselves[[fn:721]] that they might gather together such letters as they pleased and say that it was a <i>shem</i> (or sacred name) which would act and operate if it were written or pronounced in such or such a manner. Afterward these lies, invented[[fn:722]] by an initial perverse and ignorant man, were committed to writing; the writings having been transmitted into the hands of good men, pious but narrow-minded, and who lacked judgment[[fn:723]] to discern the true from the false, these latter made a mystery of them, and when these writings were found in their succession, they were taken for pure truth. In short, <i>the simple believs every word</i> (Prov. xiv. 15).",
    "root.text.Part 1..61.5": "We have departed from our sublime subject and our subtle speculation to apply ourselves to the refutation of a folly whose absurdity is manifest to any one who has but commenced his studies; yet were we necessarily led thereto whilst treating of the <i>names</i> (of God), of their signification, and of the opinions prevalent regarding them among the vulgar. I return, therefore, to my subject,",
    "root.text.Part 1..61.6": "and, having observed that all the names of God are <i>derived</i>, with the exception of the <i>schem ha-mephorasch</i>, it behoves us to speak, in a special chapter, of the name (contained in these words:) <span dir=\"rtl\">אהיה אשר אהיה</span>, <i>I am that I am</i> (Exodus, iii, 14); for this relats to the subtle subject wherewith we are here occupied, I mean the <i>negation of attributes.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..62.0": "We shall first make a preliminary observation. Concerning those words (of Moses): <i>If they shall say to me: What is His name? what shall I say unto them?</i> (Exodus, iii, 13); (one might first inquire:) how could the matter in hand lead to such a question (on the part of the Hebrews), that Moses should find it necessary to ask what answer he should return? [As for those words: <i>But, behold, they will not believe me, nor hearken unto my voice: for they will say, The Lord has not appeared unto thee</i> (<i>Ibid</i>., iv, 1), they are right clear; for such is that which must be said to any one who claims to be a prophet, until he produce proof thereof.] Furthermore[[fn:724]], if, as it seems (at first sight), it be simply a matter of a name to be pronounced, it must needs be admitted, either that the Israelites already knew this name, or that they had never heard it. Now, if it were known to them, the utterance thereof could not serve Moses as an argument, since he knew naught of it save what they themselves knew; if, on the contrary, they had never heard tell of it, what then would prove that this was indeed the name of God, even supposing that the mere knowledge of the name could serve as a proof (in favour of Moses)? Finally, God, after having taught him the name in question, said unto him: <i>Go, and gather the elders of Israel together..... and they shall hearken to thy voice</i> (<i>Ibid</i>., iii, 16 and 18); then Moses answered Him, saying: <i>But, behold, they will not believe me, nor hearken unto my voice</i>, although God had already said unto him: <i>and they shall hearken to thy voice</i>. And thereupon God said unto him: <i>What is that in thine hand?</i> and he said: <i>A rod</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 4, 2).",
    "root.text.Part 1..62.1": "That which must be known, so that all these obscurities may be enlightened for thee, is what I am about to tell thee. Thou knowest how prevalent in those times were the opinions of the Sabians[[fn:725]], and that all men then, with the exception of a few, were given over to idolatry, I mean that they believed in the spirits (of the stars) and in incantations, and that they fashioned talismans[[fn:726]]. The pretension[[fn:727]] of whosoever arrogated to himself (a divine mission) had from all time been limited to this: either he maintained that by meditating and seeking for proofs it had been demonstrated to him that one single God presided over the whole universe,—and thus did Abraham,—or he claimed that the spirit of a star, or an angel, or some other similar being, had revealed itself unto him. But that any one should claim to be a prophet, (saying) that God had spoken to him and had sent him, is a thing which had never been heard of before Moses, our master. Thou must not let thyself be led into error by what is related of the patriarchs, saying that God addressed His word to them and that He manifested Himself unto them; for thou wilt not find this kind of prophetic mission (which consists) in making an appeal to men or in guiding others, so that Abraham, or Isaac, or Jacob, or those who preceded them, should have said to men: “God has told me that ye must do or not do (such a thing)”, or else: “He has sent me unto you”. Never did such a thing occur; on the contrary, there was spoken to them naught else but what concerned them personally, I mean, (that it was a matter) of making them perfect, of directing them in what they should do, and of announcing to them what would be the future of their race, but naught besides; and they themselves called men by means of study and instruction[[fn:728]], which, according to us, is clearly indicated in these words: <i>and the souls that they had gotten in Haran</i> (Genesis, xii, 5)[[fn:729]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..62.2": "When, therefore, God manifested Himself to Moses, our master, and commanded him to call these men (to the faith) and to bring them the message in question: “But,” he replied, “that which they will first ask of me is to establish for them that there exists a God for the universe, and then I may maintain that He has sent me.” In truth, all men then, with the exception of a few, were ignorant of the existence of God, and their highest meditation went not beyond the celestial sphere, its forces and its effects; for they detached not themselves from sensible things and possessed no intellectual perfection. God, therefore, gave him then a knowledge which he was to communicate to them, in order to establish for them the existence of God, and that is (what is expressed by the words) <small>EHYE</small> <i>asher</i> <small>EHYE</small> (<i>I am that I am</i>); this is a name derived from <small>HAYA</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">היה</span>), which denots <i>existence</i>, for <small>HAYA</small> signifies <i>he was</i>, and, in the Hebrew tongue, no distinction is made between <i>being</i> and <i>existing.</i> The whole mystery lies in the repetition, in the form of an attribute, of this very word which denots <i>existence;</i> for the word <i>asher</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אשר</span>, <i>which</i>), being an incomplete noun which requirs an <i>adjunct</i> and having the sense of the words <i>alladhī</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(الذى)</span> and <i>allatī</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(التي)</span> in Arabic, requirs that the attribute which is <i>conjoined</i>[[fn:730]] to it be expressed; and, by expressing the first noun, which is the subject, by <small>EHYE</small>, and the second noun, which serves as its attribute, by this same word <small>EHYE</small>, it has been, as it were, declared that the subject is identically the same thing as the attribute. This is, therefore, an explanation of this idea: that God <i>exists, but not by existence</i>[[fn:731]]; so that this idea is thus summed up and interpreted: <i>the Being who is the Being</i>, that is to say, <i>the Necessary Being.</i> And it is indeed that which can be rigorously established by the demonstrative way[[fn:732]], (to wit) that there is something whose existence is necessary, which has never been <i>non-existent</i> and which shall never be so, even as I shall set forth the demonstration thereof (elsewhere).",
    "root.text.Part 1..62.3": "God therefore having made known unto Moses the proofs whereby His existence might be established for their <i>learned men</i> [for it is said further on: <i>Go, and gather the</i> <small>ELDERS</small> <i>of Israel</i> together], and having promised him that they would apprehend that which He had taught him[[fn:733]] and would accept it [which is expressed by these words: <i>and they shall hearken to thy voice</i>]: \"If it be true,\" Moses replied, \"that, by these demonstrations proceeding from the intellect, they shall admit that there is a God, by what means shall I prove that this God who exists has sent me?\" Thereupon he was put in possession of a miracle[[fn:734]].—It is therefore manifest that the words <i>what is his name</i> signify naught else but: <i>who is he by whom thou claimest to be sent?</i> If he said: <i>what is his name?</i> 'twas but to express himself in a respectful manner when addressing the Deity[[fn:735]]; 'tis as though he had said: Thine actual essence, no man can be ignorant thereof, but if I be asked[[fn:736]] Thy name, what is the idea signified by that name? For, deeming it unseemly to say, when speaking (to God), that there might be any one to whom that Being was unknown, he attributed their ignorance to His name, and not to Him who was designated by that name.",
    "root.text.Part 1..62.4": "Similarly, the name <small>YAH</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יה</span>) implies the idea of eternal existence[[fn:737]]. — S<small>CHADDAY</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שדי</span>) is derived from <small>DAY</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">די</span>), which signifies <i>sufficiency</i>, — e.g.: <i>the matter was</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">דיס</span> <i>sufficient for them</i> (Exodus, 26, 7)—; the shin has the meaning of <i>asher</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אשר</span>), as, e.g., in <i>she-kebar</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שכבר</span>, <i>who already</i>, Ecclesiastes, IV, 2), so that the meaning (of the said name) is <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר די</span>, <i>who is sufficient</i>, which signifies that He requires no (being) outside of Himself for that which He produces to come into existence and to continue to exist[[fn:738]], but that the mere existence of God <i>suffices</i> for this. ’H<small>ASÎN</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חםין</span>), likewise, is a derived name, (having the meaning) of <i>strength</i>, e.g., <i>and He is strong</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חםׂן</span>) <i>like the oaks</i> (Amos, 2, 9). Similarly <small>ÇOUR</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צור</span>) <span dir=\"ltr\">[[fn:739]]</span> is a homonym, as we have explained.",
    "root.text.Part 1..62.5": "It is therefore clear that all the names (of God) are either <i>derived</i>, or else are pronounced by <i>homonymy</i> like <small>ÇOUR</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צור</span>) and its counterparts[[fn:740]]. Finally, there is no name of God that is not <i>derived</i>, with the exception of the tetragrammaton, which is the <i>schem ha-mephorasch;</i> for this name does not signify an attribute, but a simple <i>existence</i>, and nothing else. Absolute existence encompasses the idea of that <i>which is always</i>, I mean of the <i>necessary Being.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..62.6": "Imprint well upon thy mind all that we have said hitherto.",
    "root.text.Part 1..63.0": "Know that by <i>schem</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שם</span>) or <small>NAME</small> <i>of the Eternal</i> is often designated (in Scripture) the simple <i>name</i>, as, for example, in these words: <i>Thou shalt not take the</i> <small>NAME</small> <i>of the Eternal, thy God, in vain</i> (Exodus, 20, 6); <i>And he that blasphems the</i> <small>NAME</small> <i>of the Eternal</i> (Leviticus, 24, 16)[[fn:741]]. The examples thereof are innumerable. Sometimes is designated thereby the essence of God and His true being, e. g.: <i>If they say unto me, What is His</i> <small>NAME</small> (Exodus, 3, 13)? At other times is designated thereby the <i>ordinance of God</i>, so that, if we say <small>NAME</small> <i>of the Eternal</i>, it is as though we said <small>WORD OR ORDINANCE</small> <i>of the Eternal;</i> thus, e. g., the words <i>for my</i> <small>NAME</small> <i>is in him</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 23, 21) signify: <i>my</i> <small>WORD</small> or <i>my</i> <small>ORDINANCE</small> <i>is in him</i>, which means that he (the messenger) is the instrument of My will and of My desire. I shall explain these words when discussing the homonymy of the word Malakh (<span dir=\"rtl\">מלאך</span>) <span dir=\"ltr\">[[fn:742]]</span>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..63.1.sub_0": "In like manner, by <i>cabôd</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כבוד</span>) or <i>glory of the Lord</i> is sometimes denoted the <i>created light</i> which God, in a miraculous manner, causs to descend into a place to glorify it[[fn:743]]; e.g.: <i>And the glory</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כבור</span>) <i>of the Lord abode upon mount Sinai, and the cloud covered it, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid</i>., XXIV, 16); <i>And the glory of the Lord filled the tabernacle</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 40, 35).—At other times, there is denoted thereby the essence of God and His very being; e.g.: <i>I beseech thee, shew me thy glory</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 33, 18), whereunto it was replied: …<i>for there shall no man see me, and live</i> (v. 20), which indicats that the <i>glory</i> of which mention is here made is His essence. If he said <i>thy glory</i>, it was out of reverence (for the Divinity), according to that which we have set forth regarding these words: <i>if they say to me, What is his name</i>[[fn:744]]? — Finally, there is sometimes denoted by <i>cabôd</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כבוד</span>) the <i>magnification</i> of which God is the object on the part of all men, or rather on the part of all things besides Him, for all things serve to glorify Him[[fn:745]]. Verily, His true magnification consists in the comprehension of His greatness, and whosoever comprehends His greatness and His perfection glorifies Him according to the measure of his comprehension. Man, in particular, glorifies Him[[fn:746]] by words, to indicate that which he has comprehended by his intellect and to make it known unto others; but those beings which have no perception, such as inanimate things, glorify Him also, after a fashion, by manifesting through their nature the power and wisdom of Him who produced them, and thereby he who contemplats them is led to glorify Him, whether by his tongue, or even without speech, if it be a being unto whom speech is not granted[[fn:747]]. The Hebrew language has taken unto itself some latitude in this respect, so that the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר</span>, <i>to say</i>, is applied to the idea in question, and it is even said of that which has no perception that it praiss God; it is said, for example[[fn:748]]: <i>All my bones shall say, Lord, who is like unto thee</i> (Ps. 35, 10)? which signifies that they give rise to this conviction, as though they did pronounce it[[fn:749]], for it is by them also that this is known.",
    "root.text.Part 1..63.1.sub_1": "It is in this sense (of <i>magnification</i>) attributed to the word <i>cabôd</i> (glory) that it was said: <i>The whole earth is full of his</i> <small>GLORY</small> (Isaiah, 6, 3), which is similar to these words: <i>And the earth was full of his</i> <small>PRAISE</small> (Habakkuk, 3, 3); for <i>praise</i> is called <i>cabôd</i> (glory), even as it has been said: <i>Give glory</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כבור</span>) <i>to the Lord your God</i> (Jeremiah, 13, 16); <i>And in his temple does every one speak of</i> <small>GLORY</small> (Ps. 29, 9)! There are numerous examples thereof. Thou must also possess thyself of this homonymity of the word <i>cabôd</i> (glory) and construe it in each passage according to that which is fitting, and thou shalt thereby escape from great difficulties.",
    "root.text.Part 1..64.0": "I do not think that, having arrived at this point and having recognized that God <i>exists, but not through existence</i>, and that He is <i>one, but not through unity</i>[[fn:750]], thou still hast need that there be set forth unto thee the necessity of removing from God the attribute of <i>speech;</i> especially since our nation admitts by common consent that the Law is a <i>created</i> thing[[fn:751]], which signifies that the speech attributed to God is a created thing[[fn:752]], and that, if it has been attributed to God, it is only because the discourse heard by Moses was created and produced by God like all the works of creation[[fn:753]]. Prophecy shall be spoken of more fully hereafter; here it is only intended to show that, if speech be attributed to God, it is as when there are attributed to Him, in general, actions similar to our own. Thus, in order to lead minds to recognize that there is a divine knowledge which the prophets obtain, it was said that God had spoken to them and had said to them such a thing[[fn:754]], so that we might know that those things which they reported to us on behalf of God did not proceed simply from their own thought and reflection, as shall be set forth elsewhere. We have already touched upon this subject previously[[fn:755]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..64.1": "This chapter has for its sole object (to demonstrate) that the verbs <i>dibber</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דבר</span>, <i>to speak</i>) and <i>amar</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אמר</span>, <i>to say</i>) are homonyms. They apply, first, to <i>language</i> properly so called[[fn:756]]; for example: <span dir=\"rtl\">משה ידבר</span>, <i>Moses spoke</i> (Exodus, 19, 19); <span dir=\"rtl\">ויאמר פרעה</span>, <i>And Pharaoh said</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 5, 5). Next, they apply to the <i>thought</i> which one forms in the intellect, without expressing it; for example: <i>And I</i> <small>SAID</small> <i>in my heart</i> (Eccles., II, 15); <i>And I</i> <small>SPOKE</small> <i>in my heart</i> (<i>Ibid</i>.); <i>And thy heart</i> <small>SHALL SPEAK</small> (Prov., 23, 33); <i>My heart</i> <small>said unto me</small> <i>of thee</i> (Ps. 27, 8); <i>And Esau</i> <small>SAID</small> <i>in his heart</i> (Genesis, XXVII. 41); there are numerous examples thereof. Finally, they apply to the <i>will;</i> for example: <i>And he</i> <small>SAID</small> <i>to smite David</i> (II Sam., 21, 16), — which signifies, <i>and he</i> <small>WISHED</small> <i>to smite him</i>, that is to say, <i>he purposed it;</i> — <small>Do you</small> <i>say to slay me</i> (Exodus, 2, 14), — which must be explained in the sense of, <small>Do you</small> <i>wish to slay me?</i> — <i>And all the congregation</i> <small>SAID</small> <i>to stone them</i> (Numbers, 14, 10); there are likewise numerous examples thereof.",
    "root.text.Part 1..64.2": "Whenever the verbs <i>amar</i> (to say) and <i>dibber</i> (to speak) are attributed to God, they possess both latter significations, I mean to say that they signify either the <i>will</i> and the <i>intention</i>, or some thing that is understood (being reported) from God, be it that one has learned it through a <i>created voice</i> or through one of the prophetic means that we shall expound; and (they signify) in no wise that God has spoken by letters and sounds, nor that He has a soul, so that things might be impressed upon His soul[[fn:757]] in such a manner as to be within Him something that is added to His essence. But these things have been attributed to God, and they have been attributed to Him as have all His other actions.",
    "root.text.Part 1..64.3": "However, if the intention and the will (of God) have been designated by the verbs <i>amar</i> and <i>dibber</i>, it is not only because of that which we have set forth concerning the homonymy of these words, but also by way of assimilation to ourselves, as we have previously observed[[fn:758]]. In sooth, man understands not at the first how that which one wills to be done can be executed by the simple will; he rather thinks at the outset that he who wills a thing to be must of necessity either <i>do</i> it himself or <i>command</i> another to do it. Therefore there has been metaphorically attributed unto God a <i>command</i>, to express that what He willed was accomplished, and it was said: «He <i>commanded</i> that such a thing should be, and it was»; and this by way of assimilation to our actions, and having regard also to the fact that this verb (<i>to say</i> or <i>to command</i>) likewise signifies the sense of <i>willing</i>, even as we have set it forth. Thus, whenever there is found, in the narrative of the creation, <span dir=\"rtl\">ויאמר</span>; <i>and</i> (<i>God</i>) <i>said</i>, it signifies <i>He willed</i> or <i>it pleased Him;</i> this is what others have already said before us[[fn:759]], and it is very well known. The proof thereof—I mean (that which proves) that everywhere here the verb <i>amar</i> (to say) designats the <i>will</i>, and not <i>speech</i>—is that speech can be addressed only to a being that is capable of receiving a command[[fn:760]]. Thus these words: <i>By the word of the Lord were the heavens made</i> (Ps. 33, 6), are parallel to these: <i>and all the host of them by the breath of His mouth</i> (<i>ibid</i>.); even as His <i>mouth</i> and <i>the breath of His mouth</i> are a metaphor, so His <i>word</i> and His <i>saying</i> are a metaphor, and the meaning is that they (the heavens) were produced by His intention and His will. This is a thing of which none of our renowned sages was ignorant. I need not set forth that the verbs <i>amar</i> and <i>dibber</i>, in the Hebrew tongue, are also <i>synonymous</i> (which results from this passage): <i>For it has heard all the words</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אמרי</span>) <i>of the Lord which He spake unto us</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דבר</span>) (Joshua, 24, 27)[[fn:761]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..65.0": "It is said that the Tables (thus the Scripture) were the work of God (Exodus, 32, 16): this signifies that they were a production of nature, and not of art[[fn:762]]; for all natural things are called the work of God; for example: They have seen the works of the Eternal (Ps. 107, 24); and in like manner, after having mentioned all natural things, such as plants, animals, winds, rains, etc., (the poet) exclaims: How great are thy works, O Eternal (Ps. 104, 24)! But what is even more expressive than this attribution[[fn:763]], are these words: The cedars of Lebanon, which He has planted (Ps. 104, 16); for, as they are a production of nature, and not of art, it is said that God has planted them.",
    "root.text.Part 1..65.1": "In like manner, when it is said <i>a writing of God</i> (Exodus, 32, 16), it is evident that the <i>writing</i> is attributed unto God; and indeed it is said (that the Tables were) <i>written with the finger of God</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 31, 18). It is here said <i>with the finger of God</i>, even as it is said, in speaking of the heaven: <i>the work of Thy fingers</i> (Ps. 8, 4), although it is expressly said that it was made by the <i>word: By the word of the Lord were the heavens made</i> (Ps. 33, 6). It is therefore clear that the (sacred) texts express metaphorically by the verbs <i>amar</i> (to say) and <i>dibber</i> (to speak) that a thing has been produced (by God), and that this very thing which is said to have been <i>made by the word</i>, is also called the <i>work of the finger</i> (<i>of God</i>); thus the expression <i>written with the finger of God</i> is equivalent to (<i>written</i>) <i>by the word of God</i>, and if, indeed, it had been said <span dir=\"rtl\">בדבר אלהים</span>, <i>by the word of God</i>, it would be equivalent to <span dir=\"rtl\">בחפץ אלהים</span>, that is to say, <i>by the will and the intention of God.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..65.2": "As for Onkelos, he has adopted concerning this matter a strange interpretation[[fn:764]], for he has said (in his version): <span dir=\"rtl\">כתיבין באצבעא די״י</span>, <i>written with the finger of the Lord</i>, regarding the word <i>finger</i> as something which is placed in relation with God, and interpreting <i>finger of God</i> as (he interprets) <i>mountain of God</i> and <i>rod of God</i>; he means thereby that it was a <i>created instrument</i> which engraved the Tables by the will of God[[fn:765]]. I know not what moved him to this; for it were simpler to translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">כתיבין במימרא די״י</span>, <i>written by the</i> <small>WORD</small> <i>of the Lord</i>, conformably to these words: <i>By the word of the Lord were the heavens made.</i> Can one, in sooth, find the existence of the writing upon the Tables more extraordinary than that of the stars within the spheres? Even as the latter is the effect of a primitive will (of God), and not of an instrument that should have fashioned (the stars), so this writing was traced by a primitive will, and not by an instrument. Thou knowest the terms of the <i>Mishnah</i> in (this passage): «Ten things were created (on the eve of the Sabbath) at twilight, etc.»; of their number were the <i>Writing</i> (<i>holy</i>) and the <i>writing</i> (<i>engraved upon the Tables</i>)[[fn:766]], which proves that a thing whereon there has been general agreement is that it is with the <i>writing of the Tables</i> as with all the other works of creation, as we have set forth in the Commentary on the <i>Mishnah.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..66.0": "Since the verb <i>amar</i> (to say) has been employed metaphorically to signify the <i>volonté</i> (manifesting itself) in all that was produced during the six days of creation, so that it was said each time: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויאמר</span>, <i>and God said</i>, the verb <i>schabath</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שבת</span>. [Lat.: to cease, to repose<i>), in speaking of the Sabbath day, during which there was no new creation, and it was said: <i>And he ceased</i> or <i>reposed</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וישבת</span>) <i>on the seventh day</i> (Genesis, 2, 2); for the verb <i>schabath</i> is also employed in the sense of <i>ceasing to speak;</i> for example: <i>And these three men ceased</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וישבתו</span>) <i>to respond to Job</i> (Job, 32, 1).",
    "root.text.Part 1..66.1": "In like manner, one finds the verb <i>noua’h</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נוח</span>, <i>to be quiet, to rest</i>) employed in the sense of <i>ceasing to speak</i>, as, for example, in this passage: <i>And they spake to Nabal in the name of David all those words, then</i> <small>THEY REMAINED QUIET</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וינוחו</span>) (I Sam., 25, 9); which signifies, in my judgment: they ceased to speak in order to hear the response. Verily, it has not been related previously that they were fatigued; so that, even had they been truly weary, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וינוחו</span> (in the sense of <i>they rested</i>) would still be right improper[[fn:767]] in this narrative. But naught is here recounted save that, after having skilfully set forth[[fn:768]] all this discourse, in which there was much affability, <i>they held their peace</i>, that is to say, they added naught else to this discourse, nor any action which might merit the response he made them; for the aim of this narrative is to describe his meanness[[fn:769]], because he was of an exceeding meanness.",
    "root.text.Part 1..66.2": "— It is also in this sense (of <i>cesser</i> or <i>s’arrêter</i>) that it has been said <span dir=\"rtl\">וַיָּנַח ביום השביעי</span> (Exodus, 20, 11)[[fn:770]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..66.3": "However, the Doctors and other commentators have taken this word (<span dir=\"rtl\">וַיָּנַח</span>) in the sense of <i>rest</i> and have rendered it a <i>transitive verb;</i> the Doctors say: “<i>He caused to rest</i> His universe on the seventh day[[fn:771]]”, that is to say, the creation was stayed[[fn:772]] on that day.",
    "root.text.Part 1..66.4": "It is possible that it be a verb having for its first or for its third radical a weak letter[[fn:773]], and that the sense be: “He <i>established</i>, or He <i>made to endure</i>[[fn:774]] the universe such as it was on the seventh day”; which would signify that on each of the six days there occurred events (which were) outside (the law) of nature[[fn:775]] such as it is established and as it now exists in the whole of the universe, but that on the seventh day everything was consolidated and took on its present stability[[fn:776]]. One cannot object against us[[fn:777]] that the word in question is not conjugated as are verbs having for their first or for their third radical a weak letter[[fn:778]]; for there be sometimes verbal forms which deviate (from the rule) and which follow not analogy, and especially in these <i>weak</i> verbs. And moreover, when it concerns the removal of such a cause of error, one cannot oppose a rule of conjugation[[fn:779]], especially whilst we know that we do not today encompass the (complete) knowledge of our tongue, and that (furthermore) the rules of every language are a matter of plurality[[fn:780]]. We find, moreover, in this root, even by giving it a weak letter for its <i>second</i> radical[[fn:781]], the sense of <i>placing</i> and of <i>establishing;</i> for example: <span dir=\"rtl\">וְהֻנִּיחָה שׁם</span>, and <i>it shall be</i> <small>PLACED</small> <i>there</i> (Zechariah, 5, 11); in like manner: <i>And she suffered not the birds of the air to</i> <small>REST</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לנוח</span>) <i>upon them</i> (II Sam., 21, 10). Such is also, in my view, the sense (of this verb) in: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר אָנוּחַ ליום צרה</span>, <i>for I wait</i> <small>STEADFASTLY</small> (or <small>QUIETLY</small>) <i>for the day of trouble</i> (Habakkuk, 3, 16)[[fn:782]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..66.5": "As for the word <i>wayyinnaphasch</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וינפש</span>, Exodus, 31, 17), it is a <i>niphal</i> of <i>nephesh</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נֶפֶש</span>) <span dir=\"ltr\">[[fn:783]]</span>. We have already set forth that <i>nephesh</i> is a homonym, and that it is (employed) in the sense of <i>intention</i> and of <i>will</i>[[fn:784]]; consequently (the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">וינפש</span>) signifies that His intention was accomplished and that all His will was executed.",
    "root.text.Part 1..67.0": "Thou art acquainted with that celebrated proposition which the philosophers have propounded concerning God, to wit, that He is the <i>Intellect</i>, the <i>Intellector</i>, and the <i>Intelligible</i>[[fn:785]], and that these three things, in God, are but one and the same thing, in which there is no multiplicity. We also have already spoken thereof in our great work[[fn:786]]; for this is the foundation of our religion, as we have there set forth—I mean to say, that He is <i>one</i> only, and that no other thing is in <i>relation</i> with Him[[fn:787]], that is to say, that there is nothing <i>eternal</i> save Him. For this reason is it said <span dir=\"rtl\">חי י״י</span> (As the Lord lives!), and it is not said <span dir=\"rtl\">חֵי י״י</span> (By the life of the Lord!)[[fn:788]]; for His <i>life</i> is naught else than His <i>essence</i>, as we have already explained in the rejection of the attributes[[fn:789]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..67.1": "Doubtless, he who has not studied the books treating of the intellect, who has not apprehended the essence of the intellect, who knows not its true being, and who understands it only so far as he understands the notion of white and black, will find great difficulty in comprehending this subject; and when we say that God is the Intellect, the Intellector, and the Intelligible, it will be to him as though we said that whiteness, the thing whitened, and that which whitens are one and the same thing[[fn:790]]. And indeed, how many ignorant persons will there be who shall hasten[[fn:791]] to refute us by this example and others of a like nature! and how many even of those who, while laying claim to science, will find this matter difficult and will believe it to be beyond our mind to acknowledge its absolute truth[[fn:792]]! Nevertheless, this is a subject demonstrable and manifest, according to what the metaphysical philosophers have expounded, and I shall make thee to understand what they have established by demonstration.",
    "root.text.Part 1..67.2": "Know that man, before he <i>thinks</i> a thing, is <i>intelligent in potentiality</i>[[fn:793]]; but when he has thought a certain thing, as, for example, when he has thought the <i>form</i> of this wood in question, having abstracted that which is its form from that which is its matter, and has represented to himself the abstract form [for it is in this that the action of the intellect consists], he has become <i>intelligent in act.</i> The intellect which has passed into <i>act</i> is itself the form of the wood abstracted in the mind of man; for the intellect is naught else but the <i>intelligible</i> object. Thou shalt understand, therefore, that the intelligible thing is the abstract form of the wood, which is itself the intellect passed into act; there are not there two things, to wit, the intellect and the thought form of the wood; for the intellect <i>in act</i> is naught else but that which has been <i>thought</i>, and the thing by which the form of the wood has been thought and abstracted, and which is the <i>Intellector</i>[[fn:794]], is itself indubitably the intellect passed into act. In truth, the essence of every intellect consists in its action, and it cannot be that the intellect in act be one thing and its action another; for the true being of the intellect[[fn:795]] is <i>perception.</i> It must not, therefore, be believed that the intellect in act is something which exists on its own part, separate from perception, and that perception is some other thing (existing) within it; but (that which constituts) the intellect in itself and its reality is perception, and consequently, when thou posest an intellect existing in act, (thou posest) thereby the perception of a thought object. This is very clear to him who has occupied himself with these manner of speculations.",
    "root.text.Part 1..67.3": "Since, therefore, it is manifest that the action of the intellect, which consists in its perception, is (that which constituts) its true essence, it follows that that by which the form of this wood has been abstracted and perceived, to wit, the <i>intellect</i>, is itself the <i>Intellector;</i> for it is this intellect itself which has abstracted the form and which has perceived it, and this is its action for the sake of which it is called <i>Intellector.</i> But its action being itself its essence, there is, in that which has been posited as <i>intellect in act</i>, naught but the form of the wood in question[[fn:796]]. It is therefore clear (on the one hand) that, whenever the intellect exists <i>in act</i>, this intellect is itself the <i>intelligible</i> thing, and (on the other hand) it has been expounded that the action which constituts the essence of every intellect is to be <i>intelligent</i>[[fn:797]]; whence it follows that the <i>intellect</i>, the <i>Intellector</i> and the <i>Intelligible</i> are always one and the same thing whenever there is question of a thought <i>in act</i>[[fn:798]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..67.4": "But when one poss (a thought) in potentiality, there are necessarily two things: the intellect in potentiality and the intelligible in potentiality. If, for instance, thou speakest of that <i>hylic</i> intellect which is in Zeid[[fn:799]], it is an intellect in potentiality, and likewise this wood is intelligible in potentiality[[fn:800]], and there are indubitably two things there. But when (the thought) has passed into act, and the form of the wood has become <i>intelligible in act</i>, then the intelligible form is identical with the intellect, and it is by this intellect itself[[fn:801]], which is <i>intellect in act</i>, that it has been abstracted and thought; (I say <i>intellect in act</i>,) because everything of which there exists a (real) action exists <i>in act.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..67.5": "Thus, therefore, the intellect in potency and the intelligible in potency are ever twain. But all that which is in potency must needs have a <i>substratum</i> which sustains that potency, as, for example, <i>man;</i> so that there shall be three things: the man who sustains the said potency and who is the <i>intelligent being in potency</i>, the potency itself, which is the <i>intellect in potency</i>, and the thing apt to be thought, which is the <i>intelligible in potency;</i> or rather, to speak according to our example: man, the <i>hylic</i> intellect, and the form of wood, which are three distinct things. But when the intellect has attained unto <i>actuality</i>, the three things become but one alone, and there shall never be found in the intellect and the intelligible two different things[[fn:802]], save when they are taken <i>in potency.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..67.6": "Now, as it is demonstrated that God [exalted be He!] is <i>intellect in act</i>[[fn:803]], and as there is in Him absolutely naught that is <i>in potency</i> — which is manifest in itself and shall yet be demonstrated — so that it cannot be that at one time He perceivs and at another He perceivs not, but on the contrary He is ever intellect in act, it follows that He and the thing perceived are one and the same thing, which is His essence[[fn:804]]; and moreover this same act of perceiving, wherefor He is called <i>intelligent</i>, is the very <i>intellect</i> which is His essence. Consequently, He is perpetually <i>intellect, intelligent</i>, and <i>intelligible.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..67.7": "It is clear also that, if it be said that the intellect, the intelligent, and the intelligible form but <i>one</i> in number, this applies not only to the Creator, but to every intellect. In us also, the intelligent, the intellect, and the intelligible are one and the same thing whenever we possess the intellect <i>in act;</i> but it is only by intervals that we pass from potentiality to act[[fn:805]]. Similarly, the <i>separated intellect</i>, by which I mean the active (universal) intellect[[fn:806]], sometimes experiences an impediment to its action; and, although this impediment proceeds not from itself, but from without, it is a certain perturbation which supervenes accidentally upon this intellect[[fn:807]]. But our present purpose is not to explain this subject; our aim is rather to expound that the attribute which belongs to God alone and is peculiar to Him, is to be always <i>intellect in act</i> and to experience no impediment to perception, neither from Himself, nor from any other quarter. Thence it follows that He is always and perpetually intelligent, intellect, and intelligible; it is His very essence which is <i>intelligent</i>, it is that which is the <i>intelligible</i>, and it is also that which is the <i>intellect</i>[[fn:808]], as must be the case in every <i>intellect in act.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..67.8": "If we have frequently iterated ourselves in this chapter, it is because it concerns a matter which minds conceive with exceeding difficulty[[fn:809]]. I deem not that in thee the intellectual conception can be troubled by the imagination, nor that thou mightest, in thy faculty of phantasy, establish in this regard a comparison with sensible things[[fn:810]]; for this treatise was composed only for those who have studied philosophy and who know that which has been set forth concerning the soul and all its faculties.",
    "root.text.Part 1..68.0": "Philosophers, as you are aware, designate God the <i>First Cause</i>[[fn:811]]; but those known by the appellation of <i>Motecallemin</i>[[fn:812]] scrupulously avoid this designation and term God the <i>Agent</i>. They maintain that a significant distinction obtains between affirming <i>cause</i> and affirming <i>agent</i>: for, they assert, were we to declare Him (God) to be a <i>cause</i>, it would necessarily follow that the <i>effect</i> exists concurrently, which would lead to the <i>eternity of the world</i> and (to the admission) that the world is (coexistent with) Him in a necessary manner[[fn:813]]; but if we designate Him an <i>Agent</i>, it does not necessarily follow that the <i>object of the action</i> exists concomitantly with Him, for the <i>agent</i> may precede his <i>action;</i> and they even proceed so far as to imagine that an agent can only be termed an <i>agent</i> on the condition of being prior to his action[[fn:814]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..68.1": "But this is the reasoning of one who knows not how to distinguish between that which is <i>in potentiality</i> and that which is <i>in actuality</i>; for it must be understood that there is no difference in this regard between the terms <i>cause</i> and <i>agent</i>. Indeed, the <i>cause</i> also, if one considers it <i>in potentiality</i>, precedes its <i>effect</i> in time; whereas, if it be a cause <i>in actuality</i>, the effect necessarily coexists with this <i>cause in actuality</i>[[fn:815]]. Similarly, if one considers the <i>agent</i> as an agent <i>in actuality</i>, it necessarily follows that the object of its action exists; for the architect, before constructing the house, is not an architect <i>in actuality</i>, but an architect <i>in potentiality</i>[[fn:816]] [just as the material of the house, before its construction, is a house <i>in potentiality</i>]; but when he builds, he becomes an architect[[fn:817]] <i>in actuality</i>, and it then necessarily follows that something constructed exists (simultaneously). We would therefore gain naught by preferring the denomination of <i>agent</i> to that of <i>cause</i>. And (indeed) the sole purpose here is to establish the equality between these two denominations, and (to show) that, just as we term God <i>agent</i>, even though the object of His action might not yet exist, — and this because there is nothing that can restrain Him or prevent Him from acting when He wills, — so also is it permissible for us to term Him <i>cause</i>, absolutely in the same sense, even though the <i>effect</i> might not yet exist.",
    "root.text.Part 1..68.2": "That which led the philosophers to designate God as <i>cause</i> and not as <i>agent</i>, was not their well-known opinion concerning the eternity of the world; rather, they held other reasons for this, which I shall now recapitulate. It has been set forth in the <i>Physics</i> that there exist diverse <i>causes</i> for everything that possesses a cause[[fn:818]], four in number, namely: matter, form, the agent (or efficient cause), and the final cause[[fn:819]], and that there are both <i>proximate</i> and <i>remote</i> causes[[fn:820]]; each of these four is termed a <i>cause</i>. Among their tenets, one which I do not dispute is this: that God is simultaneously the <i>efficient cause</i>, the <i>form</i>, and the <i>final cause;</i> it is in this sense that they declare God to be the <i>cause</i>, a designation which encompasses these three causes conjointly, signifying that God is the efficient cause of the world, its form, and its final cause[[fn:821]]. My purpose, therefore, in this chapter, is to elucidate to you in what sense it has been affirmed of God that He is the <i>efficient cause</i> (or the <i>agent</i>), and that He is likewise the <i>form</i> of the world and its <i>final cause</i>. Herein, you need not concern yourself with the question of whether God freely produced the world, or whether, as they (the philosophers) conceive, it necessarily coexists with Him[[fn:822]]; for upon this matter, we shall enter into extensive detail, as befits so weighty a subject[[fn:823]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..68.3": "Here the sole purpose is (to establish) that God is the efficient of the particular facts which occur in the world, even as He is the efficient of the world in its entirety. This, then, is what I say: It has been set forth in the <i>Physics</i> that, for each of these four kinds of causes, another cause must be sought, so that one will first find, for the thing that is born, its four immediate causes, and thereafter one will find for these other causes, and, for those (secondary) causes, yet other causes, until one arrivs at the <i>first causes;</i> thus, for example, a certain thing is the effect produced by a certain efficient, which, in its turn, shall have its efficient, and so forth, until one arrivs at a first mover who shall be the true efficient of all these intermediate things[[fn:824]]. Verily, let A be moved by B, B by C, C by D, and D by E; since this cannot extend to infinity, let us stop, for example, at E, and there will be no doubt that E is the mover of A, B, C, and D, and it is with justice that one may say of the movement of A that it is E who has wrought it. It is in this manner that every fact in the universe, whatever be the immediate efficient that has produced it[[fn:825]], is attributed unto God, as we shall set forth (elsewhere); He is therefore the most remote <i>cause</i> thereof, in the capacity of <i>efficient</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..68.4": "Likewise, if we pursue the physical <i>forms</i>, which are born and perish, we shall find that they must necessarily be preceded (each) by another form which prepars such matter to receive such (immediate) form; this second form, in its turn, shall be preceded by another, (and so forth,) until we arrive at the last form which is necessary for the existence of these intermediate forms, which are the cause of the said immediate form. This <i>ultimate form</i> of all being is God. If we say of Him that He is the ultimate form of the whole universe, it must not be believed that this is an allusion to that <i>ultimate form</i>[[fn:826]] of which Aristotle says, in the <i>Metaphysics</i>, that it is neither born nor does it perish; for the form there spoken of is <i>physical</i>, and not a <i>separate intelligence</i>[[fn:827]]. Indeed, when we say of God that He is the <i>ultimate form</i> of the world, it is not as the form having matter is a form for that matter, so that God should be a form for a body[[fn:828]]. It is not in this wise that it must be understood, but in the following manner: even as the form is that which constituts the true being of everything that has form, so that, the form perishing, the being likewise perishes, so God is found to be in an absolutely similar relation with all the most remote principles of being[[fn:829]]; for it is by the existence of the Creator that everything exists, and it is He who perpetuats its duration by something which is named the <i>emanation</i>, as we shall set forth in one of the chapters of this treatise[[fn:830]]. If, therefore, the non-existence of the Creator were admissible, the entire universe would no longer exist, for that which constituts its remote causes would vanish, as would the last effects and that which is intermediate; and, consequently, God is to the universe what the form is to the thing that has form and which thereby is what it is, the form constituting its true being. Such then is the relation of God to the world, and it is from this point of view that it has been said of Him that He is the <i>ultimate form</i> and the <i>Form of forms;</i> which is to say that He is the one upon whom leans, in the last resort, the existence and maintenance of all forms in the world, and that it is through Him they subsist, even as things endowed with forms subsist by their forms. And it is because of this that He has been called, in our tongue, <span dir=\"rtl\">חי העולמים</span>, which signifies that He is the <i>Life of the Universe</i>, as shall be set forth (hereafter)[[fn:831]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..68.5": "It is even so with every <i>end</i>[[fn:832]], for when a thing has a certain <i>end</i>, thou must seek for that end another end. If, for example, thou sayest that the <i>matter</i> of this throne is wood, its <i>efficient</i> the joiner, its <i>form</i> square and of such or such a figure, and its <i>end</i> to sit upon it, thou must thereafter ask: To what <i>end</i> does one sit upon the throne? It shall be answered, that he who sitts thereon may be raised above the ground. But, thou wilt ask again, to what end must he be raised above the ground? and it shall be answered: It is in order that, being thus seated, he may appear great in the eyes of those who behold him. And to what end, thou wilt pursue, must he appear great to those who behold him? It shall be answered, that he may be feared and respected. But, thou wilt ask once more, to what end must he be feared? It shall be said, that his command may be obeyed. And to what end, thou wilt yet pursue, must his command be obeyed? It shall be answered, that he may prevent men from doing harm to one another. And if thou askest yet: To what end? it shall be answered: It is that their existence may continue in good order. It must necessarily be the same with each new <i>end</i>, until one arrivs at last—according to a certain opinion which shall be set forth (elsewhere)—at the simple will of God, so that one shall answer for the end: Thus has God willed it; or else (one shall reach)—according to another opinion—the requirement of the Divine Wisdom, as I shall set forth, so that one shall answer for the end: Thus has His wisdom required it[[fn:833]]. The series of all <i>ends</i> shall therefore, according to these two opinions, terminate at the will and the wisdom of God. But these, according to our opinion, are His essence; for it has been set forth that the intention, the will, and the wisdom of God are not things outside His essence, I mean to say, other than His essence[[fn:834]]. Consequently, God is the <i>ultimate end</i> of all things; everything likewise has for its <i>end</i> to become like unto Him in perfection, as much as that may be, and it is this which must be understood by <i>His will, which is His essence</i>, as shall be set forth[[fn:835]]. It is therefore from this point of view that He has been called the <i>End of ends</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..68.6": "Thus I have explained in what sense it has been said of God that He is <i>efficient, form</i> and <i>end;</i> it is for this reason that they (the philosophers) have called Him <i>cause</i>, and not merely <i>agent</i> (or efficient).",
    "root.text.Part 1..68.7": "Know thou that certain thinkers[[fn:836]] among these <i>Mutakallimūn</i> have carried their ignorance and boldness so far as to maintain that, even were it admitted that the Creator could cease to exist, it would by no means follow that the thing which the Creator has produced, to wit, the world, should likewise cease to exist; for (say they) it follows not necessarily that what has been made must perish because the author, after having made it, has ceased to be. What they have therein asserted would be true if God were solely <i>efficient</i>, and if this thing made had no need of Him to prolong[[fn:837]] its duration; even as, when the carpenter dies, the chest (which he has made) does not perish for that reason, for it is not he who prolongs its duration. But, since God is at the same time the <i>form</i> of the world, as we have expounded, and since it is He who prolongs its permanence and duration, it is impossible (to suppose) that He who gives duration should disappear, and that nevertheless the thing which has no duration save through Him should continue to exist[[fn:838]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..68.8": "Thus, this assertion would lead to a great error: that God is merely <i>efficient</i>, and is neither <i>end</i> nor <i>form</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..69.0": "<i>Rakhab</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רכב</span>) <span dir=\"ltr\">[[fn:839]]</span>. — This word is a homonym which, in its primary signification[[fn:840]], denots the habitual manner of <i>riding upon a beast</i>[[fn:841]]; e.g.: <i>And he was riding</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רכב</span>) <i>upon his ass</i> (Numbers, xxii. 22). Subsequently it was metaphorically employed in the sense of <i>having dominion over a thing</i>, because the rider dominats and governs his mount, and it is in this sense that it has been said: <i>He made him to ride</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ירכיבהו</span>) <i>on the high places of the earth</i> (Deut. xxxii. 13); <i>And I will cause thee to ride</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">והרכבתיך</span>) <i>upon the high places of the earth</i> (Isaiah, lviii. 14), which signifies: ye shall have dominion over the heights of the earth; (likewise:) <i>I will make</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ארכיב</span>) <i>Ephraim to ride</i> (Hosea, x. 11), that is to say, I will make him <i>to reign</i> and <i>to dominate</i>. It is in this sense that it has been said of God: <i>Who rids</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רכב</span>) <i>upon the heaven, in thy help</i> (Deut. xxxiii. 26), which signifies: He who <i>dominats</i> over the heaven. Likewise the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לרוכב בערבות</span>, <i>To Him that rids upon ‘Arabōth</i> (Ps. lxviii. 5), signify: He who <i>dominats</i> over <i>‘Arabōth;</i> for that is the uppermost sphere which encompasses all[[fn:842]], even as the Sages repeat in several places, saying[[fn:843]] that there be <i>seven heavens</i> and <i>that ‘Arabōth</i> is the highest of them, which encompasses them all. Thou shalt not blame them for counting (only) <i>seven heavens</i>, although there be more; for sometimes one counts but as a single globe that which nevertheless contains several spheres, as is manifest to those who study this matter, and as I shall expound (elsewhere)[[fn:844]]. Here the sole intent is (to show) that they always clearly declare that ‘<i>Arabōth</i> is the highest of all (the heavens), and that it is of ‘<i>Arabōth</i> they (speak when it) is said: <i>Him that rids upon the</i> <small>HEAVEN</small> <i>in thy help</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..69.1": "There is a passage in <i>’Haghîgâ</i> where it is stated[[fn:845]]: «Upon ’<i>Arabôth</i> dwells the Most High, for it is said: <i>Extol Him that rids upon</i> ’A<small>RABÔTH</small> (Ps. 68, 5). And whence know we that this is the appellation for heaven? It is because here it is written: <i>He that rids upon</i> ’A<small>RABÔTH</small>, and elsewhere: <i>He that rids upon the</i> <small>HEAVEN</small>.» It is therefore clear that allusion is made everywhere to a single sphere, that which encircles the universe, and concerning which thou shalt yet learn further particulars. Observe well that they say: (<i>the Most</i>-<i>High</i>) <i>dwells</i> <small>UPON</small> <i>it</i>, and that they do not say: <i>dwells</i> <small>WITHIN</small> <i>it;</i> for, had they said <i>within it</i>, it would have been to assign a place to God, or to assert that God is a faculty therein, as the sects of the Sabians[[fn:846]] imagined, namely, that God is the spirit of the celestial sphere. By thus saying <i>dwells</i> <small>UPON</small> <i>it</i>, they declared that God is separate from the <i>sphere</i>, and that He is by no means a faculty within it[[fn:847]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..69.2": "Know thou also that the metaphorical expression whereby God <i>rids upon the heaven</i>, contains a most remarkable comparison. Verily, the rider is superior to the mount [the term <i>superior</i> is here but improperly used[[fn:848]], for the rider is not of the same species as the mount], and moreover, it is the rider who putts the beast into motion and leads it wheresoever he wills; the latter is unto him an instrument which he disposs at his pleasure, whilst he himself is independent of it, and, far from being joined thereto, he remains without it. In like manner, God [blessed be His name!] is the Mover of the highest sphere, by the motion of which all that is moved within it is set in motion; but God is separate from it and is in no wise a faculty within it.",
    "root.text.Part 1..69.3.sub_0": "In the <i>Berēshīth Rabbā</i>, the doctors, in expounding this divine word: <i>A dwelling-place, the eternal God</i> (Deut. xxxiii. 27), express themselves thus[[fn:849]]: «God is the dwelling-place of the world, but the world is not His dwelling-place;» and they thereafter add (comparing God to a warrior mounted on horseback): «The horse is secondary to the rider, but the rider is not secondary to the horse; it is this which is written: <i>When thou didst ride upon thine horses</i> (Habakkuk iii. 8).» Behold their own terms; pay thou diligent heed thereto, and thou shalt understand[[fn:850]] that they have thereby set forth what is the relation of God to the sphere, to wit, that the latter is His instrument whereby He governs[[fn:851]] the universe. Verily, when thou findest among the doctors the assertion that in such a heaven there is such a thing, and in such a heaven such another thing[[fn:852]], it must not be understood in the sense that there are in the heaven bodies other than the heaven itself, but the sense is that the faculties (of nature) which bring forth such or such a thing and maintain the regularity thereof derive from such or such a heaven. The proof of what I have just said is that the doctors say: «<i>’Arābōth</i>, in which are justice, virtue, right, treasures of life, treasures of peace, treasures of blessing, the souls of the righteous, the souls and the spirits of those yet to be born, and the dew wherewith the Holy One shall restore the dead to life.» It is manifest that naught of all they here enumerate is a body so as to be in a place; for <i>the dew</i> is not here a dew[[fn:853]] in the proper sense of the word. Thou shalt also remark that here they say <i>in which</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שֶׁבּוֹ</span>), that is to say, that these things are <i>in ’Arābōth</i>, and that they do not say they are <i>upon</i> it; they have in a manner declared thereby that the things in question, which exist in the world, exist therein only by faculties emanated from <i>’Arābōth</i>, and that it is God who has made the latter the <i>principle</i> of these things and has fixed them therein. Amongst their number are the <i>treasures of life</i>, which is exactly true; for all life found in any living being whatsoever comes only from that life, as I shall set forth further on[[fn:854]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..69.3.sub_1": "Remark also that they include in the number <i>the souls of the righteous</i>, as well as <i>the souls and the spirits of those yet to be born;</i> and this is a subject of high importance for him who knows how to understand it. Indeed, the souls which survive after death are not the same thing as the soul which is born in man at the moment of his birth; for that which is born at the same time with him is only a thing <i>in potentiality</i> and a <i>disposition</i>[[fn:855]], whilst the thing which remains separately after death is that which has become (<i>intellect</i>) <i>in act</i>[[fn:856]]. The <i>soul</i> which is born (with man) is not the same thing as the <i>spirit</i> which is born (with him); wherefore they count separately, as things <i>to be born</i>, the <i>souls</i> and the <i>spirits</i>[[fn:857]], whilst the <i>separate</i> soul is but a single thing[[fn:858]]. We have already set forth the homonymy of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span> (spirit)[[fn:859]], and we have also explained, at the end of the book <i>Madda’</i>, concerning these various homonyms[[fn:860]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..69.4": "Thou seest thereby how these sublime truths, the object of the speculation of the greatest philosophers[[fn:861]], are scattered throughout the <i>Midrashōth</i>, which the inequitable scholar, in reading them superficially, finds ridiculous, because he ses the literal sense to be in contrast with the reality of being; but the cause of all this is that they have expressed themselves in an enigmatic manner, because these subjects were too profound for vulgar intelligences, as we have already said many a time.",
    "root.text.Part 1..69.5": "To conclude the subject of which I have approached the explanation, I add[[fn:862]] that the doctors have undertaken to prove by passages from Scripture[[fn:863]] that the things enumerated exist in <i>’Arābōth</i>, saying: «As for <i>justice</i> and <i>right</i>, it is written: <i>justice and right are the foundation of thy throne</i> (Ps. lxxxix. 15).» And in like manner, they prove that the other things which they have enumerated likewise exist there, by showing that they are brought into relation with God[[fn:864]]. This must be well understood. — In the <i>Pirkē Rabbi Eliʻēzer</i> (chap. 18), it is said: «The Holy One created <i>seven heavens</i>, and among them all He chose none, for the glorious throne of His kingdom, save <i>’Arābōth</i> alone; for it is said: <i>Extol Him that rids upon ’Arābōth</i> (Ps. lxviii. 5).» Such is the textual passage; thou must likewise be thoroughly imbued therewith.",
    "root.text.Part 1..69.6": "Know also that a collection of beasts of burden is called <i>mercabâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מרבבה</span>, <i>chariot</i> or <i>car</i>), a word which is frequently employed; e.g.: <i>And Joseph made ready his</i> <small>MERCABA</small> or <i>his chariot</i> (Genesis, xlvi, 29); <i>in the chariot</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מרבבת</span>) <i>of the second-in-command</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, xli, 43); <i>the chariots</i> or <i>the cars</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מרבבות</span>) <i>of Pharaoh</i> (Exodus, xv, 4). The proof that this name signifies a number of beasts is that it has been said: <i>A</i> <small>MERCABA</small> (or <i>a chariot</i>) <i>came up and went out of Egypt for six hundred pieces of silver, and a horse for an hundred and fifty</i> (I Kings, X, 29), and this proves that <i>mercabâ</i> signifies <i>four horses</i>. Now, I say, as it has been affirmed by tradition[[fn:865]] that the <i>glorious throne</i> (of God) is borne by <i>four living creatures</i>, the doctors have for this reason called it <i>mercabâ</i>, by comparison with the <i>chariot</i> which consists of four individuals.",
    "root.text.Part 1..69.7": "But behold, we have been led far from the subject of this chapter[[fn:866]]. There must necessarily yet be many other observations to be made upon this matter; but we must return to the aim of this chapter, which was to show[[fn:867]] that the words <i>He that rids upon the heaven</i> (Deut., XXXIII, 26) signify: He who, by His power and His will, causs the surrounding sphere to revolve and move[[fn:868]]. Likewise, at the end of the verse, the words <i>and in His excellency, the skies</i>, signify: He who by His excellency causs the heavens to revolve. Thus the first [which, as we have explained, is <i>’Arabôth</i>] has been distinguished by the verb <i>to ride</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רכב</span>), whilst for the others[[fn:869]] (there has been employed) the word <i>excellency</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">גאוה</span>); for it is in consequence of the <i>diurnal motion</i>, accomplished by the superior sphere, that all the (other) spheres move, as the part movs in the whole, and that is the great power which movs all and which, for that reason, is called <i>excellency</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">גאוה</span>).",
    "root.text.Part 1..69.8": "Let this subject be ever present to thy mind for (comprehending) what I shall say hereafter; for it contains the most important proof by which one may know the existence of God [I mean to speak of the convolution of the celestial sphere], as I shall demonstrate it[[fn:870]]. Thou must well weigh this.",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.0": "Know that the many sciences which our nation possessed to fathom these subjects[[fn:871]] have been lost, as well by the length of time as by the dominion which barbarous nations exercised over us, and also because these subjects, even as we have explained[[fn:872]], were not delivered to everyone, the texts of the Scriptures being the only thing that was accessible unto all. Thou knowest that even the traditional Law[[fn:873]] was not formerly committed to writing, because of that saying so widespread in our communion: “The words which I have spoken to thee orally, thou art not permitted to transmit in writing[[fn:874]].” And this was an exceeding wise measure with regard to the Law; for thereby were avoided the inconveniences into which men fell later[[fn:875]], I mean, the manifold ways of seeing, the division of opinions, the obscurities which reigned in the expression of the redactor, the error to which he was subject[[fn:876]], the disunion occurring among men, who divided themselves into sects, and lastly the uncertainty concerning practices. Far from that, the matter remained entrusted in all respects to the <i>Great Tribunal</i>, as we have explained in our Talmudic works and as the text of the Law indicats[[fn:877]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.1": "Now, if with regard to the traditional Law so much reserve has been used[[fn:878]], so as not to perpetuate it in a collection lavished upon everyone, (and that) because of the inconveniences that might result therefrom, with much greater reason could nothing be put in writing of these <i>secrets of the Law</i> to be delivered to everyone; these latter, on the contrary, were only transmitted by some few select men to some other select men, even as I have explained to thee by quoting this passage: “One only delivers the <i>secrets of the Law</i> to a counsellor, a wise craftsman, etc.[[fn:879]].” And this is the cause which made to disappear in our nation those fundamental principles so important, concerning which one finds only some few slight remarks and some indications which present themselves in the Talmud and in the <i>Midrashôth</i>, and which are but a small number of kernels surrounded by many husks; so that men have occupied themselves with these husks, not suspecting that there was any kernel hidden beneath.",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.2": "As for those few matters which thou findest of the <i>Kalām</i>[[fn:880]] in the writings of some of the <i>Gaonim</i> and the Karaites[[fn:881]], concerning the unity of God and that which pertains thereto, they are things which they borrowed from the <i>Mutakallimūn</i> of the Mohammedans, and it is very little in comparison with what the Mohammedans have written thereupon. It also came to pass that, as soon as the Mohammedans had begun to adopt this method, a certain sect was formed, namely that of the <i>Mu'tazilah</i>[[fn:882]], and our coreligionists borrowed much from them and followed their method. Much later, there arose among the Mohammedans another sect, that of the <i>Ash'ariyyah</i>[[fn:883]], professing other opinions, of which nothing is to be found among our coreligionists; not that the latter chose by preference the first opinion rather than the second, but because it had chanced that they received the first opinion and had adopted it, believing it to be founded upon demonstrative proofs[[fn:884]]. As for the Andalusians of our communion, they are all attached to the words of the philosophers and incline towards their opinions, in so far as they are not in contradiction with a fundamental article of religion; and thou shalt not find them walking, in any respect, in the ways of the <i>Mutakallimūn</i>. For this reason, in the few works which remain to us from their modern authors, they follow, in many respects, nearly our system adopted in this treatise[[fn:885]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.3": "Thou must know that all which the Mohammedans, both Mu'tazilites and Ash'arites, have said upon the subjects in question, consists of opinions based upon certain propositions, which are borrowed from the writings of the Greeks and the Syrians, who sought to contradict the opinions of the philosophers and to criticize their words[[fn:886]]. And this for the following reason: When the Christian Church, whose profession of faith is known, had received into its bosom those nations[[fn:887]] among whom the opinions of the philosophers were spread—for from them did philosophy emanate—and when there had arisen kings who protected the religion, the learned men of those ages, among the Greeks and the Syrians, saw that there were therein assertions with which the philosophical opinions were in great and manifest contradiction. Then arose among them this science of <i>Kalām</i>, and they began to establish propositions profitable for their belief, and to refute those opinions which subverted the foundations of their religion.",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.4": "And when the followers of Islam appeared and the writings of the philosophers were transmitted to them, there were also transmitted to them those refutations which had been written against the books of the philosophers. They found, therefore, the discourses of John the Grammarian[[fn:888]], of Ibn-'Adi[[fn:889]] and of others besides, treating of these matters; and they laid hold of them in the belief that they had made a momentous discovery. They chose also from the opinions of the ancient philosophers all that they deemed to be useful unto them[[fn:890]], although the more recent philosophers had demonstrated the falsity thereof, as, for example, the hypothesis of <i>atoms</i> and of the <i>vacuum</i>[[fn:891]]; and they imagined that these were matters of common interest and propositions needed by all who professed a positive religion. Afterwards the <i>Kalām</i> was extended, and they entered into other extraordinary paths, in which the Greek and other <i>Mutakallimūn</i> had never engaged[[fn:892]]; for these latter were more nearly approached to the philosophers[[fn:893]]. Then there arose again, among the Mohammedans, religious doctrines which were peculiar to them and of which it was necessary to take up the defense; and division having further broken out among them in this regard, each sect established hypotheses which might serve it to defend its opinion.",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.5": "There is no doubt that there be matters therein which equally interest the three communions, I mean, the Jews, the Christians, and the Mohammedans, as, for example[[fn:894]], the dogma of the <i>novelty</i> of the world, [upon the truth of which depends that of miracles,] and others besides[[fn:895]]. But the other questions in which the followers of the two religions (the Christian and the Mohammedan) have seen fit to engage, as, for example, the former in the question of the Trinity and certain sects of the latter in that of the <i>Word</i>[[fn:896]] [so that they felt the need to establish certain hypotheses of their own choosing, in order to sustain the questions in which they were engaged[[fn:897]]], as well as all that is peculiar to each of the two communions and has been laid down as a principle by it[[fn:898]], (of all this, I say,) we have no need in any wise.",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.6": "In sum, all the ancient[[fn:899]] <i>Mutakallimūn</i>, as well among the Greeks who became Christians as among the Mohammedans, did not at first adhere, in their propositions, to that which is manifest in existence, but they considered how existence <i>ought</i> to exist in order that it might serve as a proof of the truth of their opinion, or at least not subvert it. This imaginary <i>existence</i> once established, they declared that existence <i>is</i> in such a manner; they set themselves to argue to confirm these hypotheses whence they were to take the propositions by which their system might be confirmed or be sheltered from attack. Thus did even the intelligent men who first followed this procedure; they committed this to books and pretended that pure speculation alone had led them thereto without their having had regard to any system or opinion of the past. Those who later read these books knew nothing of what had transpired; so that, finding in these ancient books weighty arguments and great efforts to establish or to deny a certain thing, they imagined that it was by no means in the interest of the fundamental principles of religion that one had need to establish or deny that thing[[fn:900]], and that the ancients had only done so to show what was confused in the opinions of the philosophers and to raise doubts concerning what the latter had taken for a demonstration. Those who thus reasoned did not suspect that the matter was quite otherwise[[fn:901]]; for, on the contrary, if the ancients took such pains to seek to establish one thing and deny another[[fn:902]], it was only because of the danger which might result therefrom, were it even at the end of a series of an hundred premises, for the opinion which they desired to verify, so that these ancient <i>Mutakallimūn</i> cut off the malady at its very source[[fn:903]]. But I shall tell thee as a general thesis that the matter is even as Themistius has said, to wit: that existence does not accommodate itself to opinions, but that true opinions accommodate themselves to existence[[fn:904]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.7": "Having studied the writings of these <i>Mutakallimūn</i>, so far as I had the facility, even as I have also studied, according to my power, the writings of the philosophers, I have found that the method of all the <i>Mutakallimūn</i> is of one and the same kind, albeit presenting diverse varieties. In sooth, they all hold as a principle that one must have no regard for <i>being</i> as it is, for that is naught but a <i>habit</i>, the contrary of which is ever possible in our reason. Also, in many places, they follow <i>imagination</i>, which they deck with the name of <i>reason</i>. Having thus established the propositions which we shall make known unto thee, they have peremptorily decided, by means of their demonstrations, that the world is <i>created;</i> now, so soon as it is established that the world is created, it is indubitably established that there is an Artificer who created it. They then demonstrate that this Artificer is <i>one</i>, and finally they establish that, being <i>one</i>, He is not a body. Such is the method of every <i>Mutakallim</i> among the Muslims in this kind of questions; and it is the same with those who have imitated them among our coreligionists and who have walked in their ways. As for their modes of argument and the propositions whereby they establish the novelty of the world or deny the eternity thereof, there are various kinds; but the thing which is common to them all is to establish first of all the novelty of the world, by means of which it is proved that God exists.",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.8": "When, therefore, I examined this method, my soul felt a very great repugnance thereat, and it deservs in truth to be rejected; for all that is claimed to be a proof of the novelty of the world is subject to doubt, and these are decisive proofs only for him who knows not how to distinguish between demonstration, dialectics, and sophistry[[fn:905]]. But for him who is acquainted with these different arts, it is clear and manifest that all these demonstrations are doubtful, and that there have been employed therein premises which are not demonstrated.",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.9": "The furthest point to which, in my judgment, the theologian[[fn:906]] who seeks the truth could go, would be to show the nullity of the demonstrations alleged by the philosophers for the <i>eternity</i> of the world; and how magnificent would it be if success were therein achieved! Indeed, every penetrating thinker who seeks the truth and does not deceive himself knows well that this question, I mean that of whether the world be eternal or created, cannot be resolved by a decisive demonstration, and that it is a point whereat the intellect stays. We shall speak thereof at length; but let it suffice thee now, concerning this question, that for three thousand years, and even unto our time, the philosophers of all ages have been divided thereupon, as may be seen in what we find of their works and of their history[[fn:907]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.10": "Now, since it is thus with this question, how then should we take it as a premise whereon to construct the proof of the existence of God? This latter would then be doubtful: If the world be <i>created</i>, one would say, there is a God; but if it be <i>eternal</i>, there is no God. Such would be our plight, unless we should pretend to have a proof for the novelty of the world and should impose it by main force[[fn:908]], in order that we might claim to know God by means of demonstration. But all this would be far from the truth;",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.11": "to my mind, on the contrary, the true manner, that is to say, the demonstrative method in which there is no doubt, consists in establishing the existence of God, His unity, and His incorporeality by the methods of the philosophers, which methods are based upon the eternity of the world. Not that I believe in the eternity of the world, or that I make a concession unto them in this regard; but it is because, by this method, the demonstration becoms sure and one obtains a perfect certainty concerning these three things, namely, that God exists, that He is <i>one</i>, and that He is incorporeal, without it being of any moment to decide aught regarding the world, to wit, whether it be eternal or created. These three grave and important questions being resolved by a true demonstration, we shall thereafter return to the novelty of the world and we shall produce in this regard all possible arguments[[fn:909]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.12": "If then thou art of those who rest content with that which the <i>Motécallemīn</i> have declared, and believest that there exists a solid demonstration for the novelty of the world, verily it is well; and if the matter seems not demonstrated unto thee, yet thou acceptest by tradition from the prophets that the world was created, there is no harm therein. Nevertheless, thou must not inquire (at this present): How can prophecy subsist, if the world be eternal? (and thou must wait) until thou hast heard what we shall say concerning prophecy in the present treatise; for we occupy not ourselves now with that subject. But what must be known is that in the propositions which the <i>men of the roots</i>[[fn:910]]—I mean the <i>Motécallemīn</i>—have established to affirm the novelty of the world, there is a <i>subversion of the world</i> and an <i>alteration of the laws of nature</i>[[fn:911]], as thou art about to hear; for I cannot dispense with speaking unto thee of their propositions and of their manner of arguing.",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.13": "As for my method, it is such as I shall summarily describe unto thee. The world, I say, is necessarily either eternal or created. Now, if it be created, it has indubitably a creator; for it is a primary notion that what is <i>brought into being</i> has not brought itself into being, but that which has brought it into being is other than itself; wherefore (it is said), He who brought the world into being is God. If (on the contrary) the world be eternal, it follows necessarily, by virtue of such and such proof, that there exists a being, other than all the bodies of the universe, who is neither a body nor a faculty in a body, who is <i>one</i>, permanent, eternal, who has no cause and who is immutable; this being is God. It is therefore manifest that the proofs of the existence of God, of His unity and of His incorporeality, must be obtained solely by the hypothesis of the eternity (of the world), and it is only thus that the demonstration shall be perfect, whether (in reality) the world be eternal or whether it be created[[fn:912]]. Wherefore thou shalt find that whenever, in my Talmudic works, I chance to speak of the fundamental dogmas[[fn:913]] and undertake to establish the existence of God, I do so in terms leaning towards the <i>eternity</i> (of matter). It is not that I profess <i>eternity;</i> but I desire to confirm the existence of God in our belief by a demonstrative method whereon there can be no dispute, so as not to support this true dogma, of so great importance, upon a basis which every man might shake and seek to demolish, and which another might even regard as null and void[[fn:914]]. And the more so[[fn:915]] as the philosophical proofs upon these three questions[[fn:916]] are taken from the visible nature of being, which cannot be denied save by favor of certain preconceived opinions, whereas the proofs of the <i>Motécallemīn</i> are drawn from propositions contrary to the visible nature of being, so that they are constrained[[fn:917]] to establish that no thing has a (fixed) nature.",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.14": "I shall give thee in this treatise, whilst speaking of the <i>novelty of the world</i>, a particular chapter in which I shall set forth a certain demonstration upon this question[[fn:918]], and I shall attain the end unto which every <i>Motécallem</i> has aspired, without thereby denying the nature of being, nor contradicting Aristotle in aught that he has demonstrated. For, whereas the proof which certain <i>Motécallemīn</i> have alleged for the novelty of the world, and which is the strongest of their proofs[[fn:919]], could not be established by them without their having denied the nature of all being and contradicted all that the philosophers have set forth, I myself shall arrive at an analogous proof, without being in contradiction with the nature of being, nor having need to contest sensible things[[fn:920]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.15": "I deem it my duty to report unto thee the general propositions by means of which the <i>Motécallemīn</i> establish the novelty of the world, as well as the existence, the unity, and the incorporeality of God; I shall show thee what is their method in this regard, and I shall set forth that which results from each of the said propositions. Thereafter I shall report unto thee the propositions of the philosophers directly relating to this subject, and I shall show thee the method of the latter.",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.16": "Thou shalt not require of me that, in this treatise, I demonstrate the truth of those philosophical propositions which I shall summarily report unto thee; for therein lies the greater part of <i>physics</i> and <i>metaphysics</i>. Likewise, thou shalt not desire that I make thee hear, in this treatise, the arguments alleged by the <i>Motécallemīn</i> to demonstrate the truth of their propositions; for they have spent their lives therein, as will also spend theirs those who are to come, and their books have multiplied. For each of these propositions, with the exception of a small number, is refuted by that which is seen in the nature of being, and doubts arise; so that they have need of making books and controversies to confirm each proposition, to resolve the doubts that arise therein, and even to reject the evidence which refuts it, if there be no other possible means[[fn:921]]. But, as for the philosophical propositions which I shall summarily report unto thee to serve for the demonstration of the said three questions—I mean, of the existence, the unity, and the incorporeality of God—they are, for the most part, propositions which will give thee certainty as soon as thou shalt have heard them and understood their meaning. Some will indicate unto thee in themselves the places in the books of Physics or Metaphysics where they are demonstrated; thou mayest therefore seek the place thereof and verify that which may have need of being verified.",
    "root.text.Part 1..70.17": "I have already made known unto thee that there exists naught else but God and this universe. God can only be demonstrated by this universe (considered) in its entirety and in its details; it is therefore necessary to examine this universe as it is, and to derive the premises (of the proofs) from its visible nature. Consequently, one must be acquainted with its visible form and nature, and only then may one induce therefrom proofs concerning that which lies outside it. I have therefore deemed it necessary to provide first a chapter in which I set forth the whole of the universe, in the form of a simple relation of that which has been demonstrated (elsewhere) and whose truth is beyond doubt. Thereafter I shall provide other chapters, in which I shall report the propositions of the <i>Mutakallimūn</i> and set forth the methods of which they make use to resolve the four questions at issue[[fn:922]]. Then, in yet other chapters, I shall expound to thee the propositions of the philosophers and their methods of argumentation regarding the said questions. Finally, I shall set forth the method which I myself follow, as I have announced unto thee, regarding these four questions.",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.0": "Know that this universe in its entirety forms but a single individual; I mean to say: the globe of the extreme heaven with all that it contains is indubitably a single individual, under the same title of individuality as Zayd and ‘Amr[[fn:923]]. It is with its different substances—I mean the substances of this globe with all it contains—as it is, for example, with the different substances of the members of the human individual. Even as Zayd, for example, is a single individual, though composed of different parts, such as flesh and bones, different humours and <i>spirits</i>[[fn:924]], so this globe in its entirety embraces the spheres and the four elements with that which is composed of them. It has absolutely no void, but is a solid plenum having for its centre the terrestrial globe; the earth is environed by water, this by air, this by fire[[fn:925]], and this last finally is environed by the <i>fifth body</i>[[fn:926]]. This latter[[fn:927]] is composed of numerous spheres, contained one within the other, between which there is no hollow nor void, but which exactly encompass each other, being applied one to the other. They all possess an equal circular motion, and in none of them is there any precipitation or slackening; I mean that none of these spheres movs now rapidly, now slowly, but each, as to its velocity and manner of moving, remains subject to its natural law. Nevertheless, these spheres move more rapidly than one another, and that which among them all has the most rapid motion is the sphere which environs everything, that is to say, that which has the <i>diurnal</i> motion and which causs them all to move with it as the part movs in the whole, for all of them form parts within it. These spheres have different centres; some have for their centre the centre of the world[[fn:928]], others have their centre outside that of the world[[fn:929]]. There be some which perpetually follow their particular motion from East to West, whilst others move continually from West to East[[fn:930]]. Every star within these spheres is part of the sphere in which it remains fixed in its place; it has no motion peculiar to itself, and appears moved only by the motion of the body of which it is a part. The matter of this entire fifth body, which has the circular motion, is in no wise similar to that of the bodies of the four elements which are found in the interior.",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.1": "The number of these spheres which environ the world cannot in any wise be less than eighteen[[fn:931]]; it is possible, however, that there be more, and it is a matter to be examined. As for knowing whether there be <i>spheres of revolution</i> which do not environ the world, this likewise is to be examined[[fn:932]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.2": "In the interior of the inferior sphere which is nearest unto us[[fn:933]], there is a matter different from that of the fifth body, and which has received four primitive forms whereby four bodies have been formed, (which are) earth, water, air, and fire. Each of these four (bodies) has a natural place which is peculiar to it, and is found in no other (place) so long as it remains left to its nature. These are inanimate bodies, having neither life nor perception, and which do not move by themselves, but remain at rest in their natural places. If, however, one of them has been forced to depart from its natural place, then, as soon as the cause which forced it thereto ceass, it movs to return to that natural place; for it has within it the principle by virtue of which it movs in a straight line to return to its place, but it has within it no principle by virtue of which it must (always) remain at rest or move otherwise than in a straight line. The motions in a straight line made by these four elements, when they move to return to their places, are of two kinds: a motion toward the circumference, which belongs to fire and air, and a motion toward the centre, which belongs to water and earth; and each, after arriving at its natural place, remains at rest[[fn:934]]. As for these (celestial) bodies which have circular motion, they are living and possess a soul whereby they move; there is in them absolutely no principle of rest, and they undergo no change save in position, having circular motion. As for knowing whether they have also an <i>intelligence</i> whereby they <i>conceive</i>, this cannot be elucidated save by means of subtle speculation[[fn:935]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.3": "The entire fifth body accomplishing its circular motion, there ariss therefrom continually in the elements a forced motion whereby they depart from their regions—I mean (there ariss a motion) in the fire and the air, which are driven back toward the water, and all of them penetrate into the body of the earth even into its depths, so that there results therefrom a mixture of the elements. Thereafter they begin to move to return to their (respective) regions, and, in consequence thereof, particles of earth likewise quit their places, joining themselves to water, air, and fire. In all this, the elements act upon one another and receive impressions from one another, and the mixture undergoes a transformation, so that there ariss therefrom[[fn:936]] first the different species of vapours[[fn:937]], then the different species of minerals, all the species of plants, and numerous species of animals, according to what the complexion of the mixture requirs. All that which is born and perishes is born only of the elements and returns thereto in perishing. Likewise, the elements are born of one another and are lost in one another; for all has but a single matter, and matter cannot exist without form, even as no physical form of these things which are born and perish can exist without matter. Therefore, the birth and destruction of the elements, as well as of all that which is born of the latter and is resolved therein in perishing, follow (as it were) a circular motion, similar to that of the heaven; so that the motion which this <i>formed</i> matter[[fn:938]] makes, through the forms which successively come upon it, may be compared to the motion which the heaven makes in <i>place</i>[[fn:939]], the same positions being repeated[[fn:940]] for each of its parts.",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.4": "Even as in the human body there be (on the one hand) <i>dominant</i> members, and (on the other hand) <i>subordinate</i> members which require, for their preservation, the governance of the principal member that ruls them, even so there be, in the whole of the universe, dominant parts, such as the <i>fifth body</i> which encompasses (all), and subordinate parts which require a guide, as the elements and that which is composed thereof.",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.5": "Even as the principal member, which is the heart, movs ever[[fn:941]] and is the principle of all movement existing in the body, whilst the other members of the body are governed by it, and it is that which, by its movement, sends unto them the faculties of which they have need for their actions, even so it is the celestial sphere which, by its movement, governs the other parts of the universe, and it is that which sends unto all that is brought into being the faculties found therein; so that every movement existing in the universe has for its first principle the movement of the celestial sphere, and every soul of that which, in the universe, is endowed with a soul, has its principle in the soul of the celestial sphere.",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.6": "It behoves to know that the faculties which proceed from the celestial sphere unto this world are, as has been set forth, four in number, namely: a faculty which produces mixture and composition, and which suffices indubitably for the production[[fn:942]] of minerals; another faculty, which bestows the vegetative soul upon every plant; another, which bestows the vital soul upon all that lives; and finally another, which bestows the rational faculty upon every reasonable being; and all this is accomplished by the mediation of the light and the darkness resulting from the light of the stars and from their revolution about the earth[[fn:943]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.7": "Even as, furthermore, when the heart stays its motion for a single instant, the individual dies and all his movements and faculties cease, even so, if the celestial spheres should stay their motion, it would be the death of the whole universe and the annihilation of all that is found therein.",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.8": "Even as, finally, the animal lives in its entirety solely by the effect of the movement of its heart, although it possesses members void of movement and sensation, as, for example, the bones, the cartilages, and such like, even so this whole universe is a single individual, living by the movement of the celestial sphere which occupies therein the same rank as that held by the heart in that which has a heart, although it contains many bodies without movement and inanimate.",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.9": "Thus must thou represent to thyself the whole of this sphere as a single individual, living, moving, and endowed with a soul; for this manner of representation is highly necessary, or at the least[[fn:944]] most profitable, for the demonstration of the unity of God, even as it shall be set forth[[fn:945]], and in this wise it shall also be manifest that the <i>One</i> has created but a single being[[fn:946]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.10": "In like manner as it is impossible that the members of a man should exist separately as true members of man—I mean, that the liver or the heart should be isolated, or that flesh should exist in isolation—so would it be impossible that the parts of the universe should exist the one without the other, in this well-constituted being of which we speak[[fn:947]], so that fire should exist without earth, or earth without heaven, or heaven without earth.",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.11": "Even as there exists in this human individual a certain faculty which binds his members one to another, governs them, bestows upon each member what is requisite for its preservation in a goodly state, and wards off that which is harmful to it—to wit, that which physicians expressly designate as the <i>directing faculty</i> of the animal body, and which they often term <i>nature</i>[[fn:948]]—so likewise there is in the whole of the universe a faculty which binds the parts thereof together, which prevents the species from perishing and preservs also the individuals of these species so long as it is possible so to do, and which likewise preservs a portion of the individuals of the universe[[fn:949]]. It remains, however, to be considered whether this faculty acts, or no, by the mediation of the celestial sphere[[fn:950]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.12": "Within the body of the human individual there are, on the one hand, things which have a particular purpose: some, like the organs of nutrition, have for their end the preservation of the individual; others, like the organs of generation, have for their end the preservation of the species; yet others, as for example the hands and eyes, are destined to provide for those things of which man has need in the way of nourishment[[fn:951]] and other like matters. And, on the other hand, there exist[[fn:952]] also things which have no end in themselves, but which are attached as accessories to the complexion of certain members, which particular complexion is necessary for these latter to obtain their proper form, that they may fulfill the functions for which they are destined. Thus, to that which is formed for a certain purpose[[fn:953]], there are joined, according to the exigency of matter, certain other things, as, for example, the hair and the colour of the body; wherefore[[fn:954]] these things possess no regularity, and there are even some which oftentimes are wholly wanting, and there exists in this regard a very great difference between individuals[[fn:955]], which is not the case with the members. Verily, thou shalt not find an individual whose liver is ten times as great as that of another individual, whilst thou shalt find such a man void of a beard, or who has no hair in certain parts of his body, or whose beard is ten or twenty times greater than that of another individual; and this is very frequent[[fn:956]] in this order of things—I mean the difference which exists between individuals as to hair and colour. In like manner[[fn:957]], in the whole of the universe, there are, on the one hand, species whose generation has a determined end, which follow with constancy a certain rule and in which there are but small anomalies remaining within the limits of the species in respect of quality and quantity[[fn:958]]; and, on the other hand, species which have no end in themselves, but are a necessary consequence of the general laws of generation and corruption[[fn:959]], such as, for example, the diverse species of worms which are born in dung, the diverse species of animalcules which are born in fruits when they decay, that which springs from the corruption of moist things, the worms which are born in the intestines, and so forth. In general, it seems to me that all that has not the faculty of procreating its like is of this category; wherefore thou shalt find that these things observe no rule, albeit they must of necessity exist, even as it is necessary that human individuals should be of different colours and have different kinds of hair.",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.13": "In like manner as there are in man bodies of an individual permanence[[fn:960]], such as the principal members, and bodies permanent as a species, and not as an individual, like the four <i>humours</i>[[fn:961]], so there are in the whole of the universe stable bodies, of an individual permanence, like the fifth body with all its parts[[fn:962]], and bodies permanent as a species, such as the elements and that which is composed thereof[[fn:963]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.14": "Even as the faculties whereby man is brought into being and is preserved for a certain season[[fn:964]] are the very same that cause his destruction and ruin, so likewise, in the whole of the world of generation and corruption[[fn:965]], the causes of birth are themselves the causes of destruction. Of this is an example: If those four faculties which exist in the body of every living thing that is nourished, to wit, the attractive, the retentive, the digestive, and the expulsive[[fn:966]], could resemble the intellectual faculties, so as to perform only what is requisite, at the time when it is requisite and in the measure that is requisite, man would be preserved from the greatest calamities and from many diseases; but, since this is not possible, and since, on the contrary, they perform physical functions without reflection or discernment, and without in any wise understanding what they do, it follows that they give rise to grievous maladies and calamities, though they be the instruments by which the animal is born and is preserved for a certain season. Thus, to explain my meaning, if the attractive faculty, for example, did attract only that which is suitable in every respect, and only in the measure of need, man would be preserved from many diseases and calamities. But, as it is not so, and as, on the contrary, it attracts every matter that presents itself, provided it be of the kind of its attraction[[fn:967]], even though this matter should deviate somewhat from the just measure in respect of quantity and quality, it results that it also attracts matter which is hotter, or colder, or thicker, or subtler, or in greater quantity than is needful; whereby the veins are gorged, and there occurrs obstruction and putrefaction, the quality of the humours is corrupted and their quantity is altered, and there arise diseases, such as the itch, the scab, and warts, or great calamities, such as cancerous tumours, elephantiasis, and gangrene, so that the form of one or more members is destroyed. And it is the same with the other of the four faculties. — Now, it is precisely the same thing that occurrs in the whole of the universe: the thing which causs to be born that which is born, and prolongs its existence for a certain season, — to wit, the mixture of the elements produced by the faculties of the celestial sphere which set them in motion and are diffused therein, — is itself the cause which brings into the universe harmful things, such as floods, heavy rains, snow, hail, hurricanes, thunder, lightning, and the corruption of the air, or terrible catastrophes which destroy one or more cities or a whole country, such as the sinkings of the earth[[fn:968]], earthquakes, violent storms, and the waters which overflow from the seas and the depths[[fn:969]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.15.sub_0": "It behoves us to know that, notwithstanding all that we have said concerning the similitude which exists between the whole of the universe and the human individual, it is nevertheless not on account of all this that man has been called a <i>little world</i> (microcosm); for all this comparison might be pursued in respect of every individual among the animals of a complete organism, and yet thou hast never heard that any of the ancients said that the ass or the horse was a <i>little world</i>. If this has been said of man, it is solely because of that which particularly distinguishes him; and that is the <i>rational faculty</i>, I mean that intelligence which is the <i>hylian intellect</i>[[fn:970]], a thing which is found in no other species of animals. Of this is the explanation: Every individual among the animals has need neither of thought, nor of reflection, nor of a regime, to prolong its existence; but it goes and comes according to its nature, eats what it finds suitable for itself, shelters itself in the first place it meets, and coupls with the first female it encounters when it is in heat, if indeed it has a season of heat. Thereby the individual endurs for such time as it can endure, and the existence of its species is perpetuated; it has in no wise need of another individual of its species to succour it and help it to preserve itself, in such manner that the latter should do for it things which it cannot do itself. But it is otherwise with man; for, if one were to suppose a human individual[[fn:971]] existing alone, deprived of all regime and become like unto the animals, such an individual would perish straightway and could not even subsist for a single day, unless it were by accident, I mean, that he should by chance find something wherewith to nourish himself. In truth, the foods whereby man subsists require art and long preparations which can only be accomplished by dint of thought and reflection, with the help of many instruments and with the concurrence of a great number of persons, of which each one takes upon himself a particular function. Wherefore it is necessary for men to have someone to guide and unite them, so that their society may be organized and perpetuated, and that they may afford one another mutual succour.",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.15.sub_1": "Likewise, to preserve himself from the heat in the time of heat and from the cold in the cold season, and to guard himself against rains, snows, and winds, man is obliged to make many preparations, all of which can only be accomplished by means of thought and reflection. It is therefore on this account that he has been endowed with this rational faculty whereby he thinks, reflects, acts, and, by the aid of diverse arts, prepars his food and that wherewith to shelter and clothe himself; and it is by it also that he governs all the members of his body, so that the <i>ruling</i> member may do what it ought to do, and that that which is <i>subjected</i> may be governed as it ought to be. Wherefore, if thou shouldst suppose a human individual deprived of this faculty and abandoned to his animal nature alone, he would be lost and would perish at that very instant. This faculty is most noble, more noble than any of the faculties of the animal; it is also most occult, and its true nature cannot be understood at first sight by simple common sense, as are the other natural faculties.",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.16": "— Likewise, there is in the universe something which governs the whole thereof, and which setts in motion the ruling and principal member, unto which it communicats the motive faculty in such manner as to govern thereby the other members[[fn:972]]; and, were it to be supposed that the thing in question could disappear, this entire sphere of the universe, both the ruling part and the subjected part, would cease to exist. It is by this thing that the existence of the sphere and of each of its parts is perpetuated; and this thing is God [exalted be His name!]. — It is in this sense alone that man in particular has been called a <i>microcosm</i>, that is to say, because there is in him a principle which governs his whole being; and it is because of this idea that God has been called, in our language, <i>the life of the universe</i>, and that it has been said: <i>And he sware by the life of the universe</i> (Daniel, 12:7)[[fn:973]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.17": "It behoves us to know that the comparison which we have established between the whole of the universe and the human individual admitts of no contradiction in anything that we have said, save only in three points:",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.18": "The <i>dominant</i> member (or the heart), in every animal that has a heart, derivs benefit from the <i>subjected</i> members, from which utility returns unto it. But naught of the like occurrs in the universal being; on the contrary, every thing which (in the universe) exercs a directing influence or imparts a faculty[[fn:974]] derivs on its own part no benefit from the subject part, but its manner of giving is like that of the beneficent and liberal man, who does good through generosity of character and by an innate goodness, and not in the hope (of a reward)[[fn:975]]; nay, more[[fn:976]], it is to render himself like unto God [may His name be exalted!].",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.19": "In all animals that possess a heart, the same is found in the midst, and all the <i>subjected</i> members environ it to be generally profitable thereto, by guarding and protecting it, lest any mishap from without should befall it. But in the whole of the universe, the matter is the reverse: the more noble part environns that which is less noble, because it is in no wise exposed to receive an impression from that which is without it; and even were it susceptible of impression, it would find no other body outside itself that could act upon it, for it influencs that which is within it, and there reaches it no impression nor any faculty (coming) from another body.—There is, however, herein some resemblance: for whatsoever among the members of the animal is more remote from the <i>dominant</i> member is less noble than that which is nearer thereto; and likewise, in the whole of the universe, according as bodies approach the centre, they become dull, their substance becoms more gross and their movement more difficult, and they lose their lustre and their transparency, by reason of their distance from the noble, luminous, transparent, mobile, subtle and simple body, I mean, the celestial sphere; but, according as a body approaches it, it acquirs something of these qualities, by reason of its proximity, and enjoys a certain superiority over that which is beneath it.",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.20": "This rational faculty (of which we have spoken) is a faculty in a body and inseparable therefrom, whilst God is in no wise a faculty in the body of the universe, but is separate from all the parts of the universe. The governance of God and His providence are joined unto the whole world by a bond the true nature of which is unknown to us, and which the faculties of mortals cannot comprehend; for it can be demonstrated (on the one hand) that God is separate from the world and that He is independent thereof[[fn:977]], and (on the other hand) it can also be demonstrated that the influence of His governance and of His providence extends over each of the parts of the world, however feeble and insignificant it may be[[fn:978]]. Praise be unto Him whose perfection fills us with admiration!",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.21": "Know that we ought to have compared the relation between God and the universe to that which exists between the <i>acquired intellect</i> and man; for this intellect also is not a faculty in a body, being in reality separate from the body upon which it <i>is poured forth</i>[[fn:979]]. As for the <i>rational faculty</i>, one might compare it to the <i>intelligences of the spheres</i>, which are found in bodies. But the question of the intelligences of the spheres, the existence of <i>separate</i> intelligences, and the idea of the <i>acquired intellect</i>, which is likewise <i>separate</i>, are matters which need to be studied and examined, and the demonstrations of which are obscure albeit true; they are subject to many doubts and exposed to the attacks of him who seeks to criticise and cavil[[fn:980]]. We have therefore preferred that one should first be able to represent the universe by its manifest form; and indeed[[fn:981]] naught of what we have advanced in an absolute manner can be denied save by two sorts of persons: either by him who is ignorant even of that which is most evident, as (for example) he who is no geometrician denies mathematical things which are demonstrated; or by him who preferrs to cling to a preconceived opinion and to deceive himself. As for him who wishes to engage in true speculation, he must apply himself to study, so that the truth of all that we have related may become evident to him, and that he may know, without having the least doubt in this regard, that such is (indeed) the form of this being whose existence is (invariably) constituted. If any one wills to accept it from him to whom all has been rigorously demonstrated, let him accept it and let him build thereon his syllogisms and his proofs[[fn:982]]; but if he loves not simple tradition, even for these principles (of things)[[fn:983]], then let him study, and it shall become manifest to him that the matter is so. <i>Lo, this we have searched it, so it is; hear it, and know it for thy good</i> (Job, v, 27).",
    "root.text.Part 1..71.22": "After this preparation, I approach the subject which I promised to set forth[[fn:984]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.0": "The general propositions which the <i>Motécallemîn</i> have established, notwithstanding[[fn:985]] their diverse opinions and their manifold methods, [propositions which are necessary for them to affirm that which they desire to affirm concerning the four questions in point,] are twelve in number. I shall first state them unto thee (summarily); thereafter I shall expound unto thee the meaning of each of these propositions and that which results therefrom:",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.1": "The I<sup>st</sup> proposition affirms (the existence of) the <i>simple substance</i>[[fn:986]];",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.2": "the II<sup>nd</sup>, the existence of the <i>vacuum;</i>",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.3": "the III<sup>rd</sup>, that time is composed of instants[[fn:987]];",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.4": "the IV<sup>th</sup>, that substance is inseparable from manifold accidents;",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.5": "the V<sup>e</sup>, which in the <i>substance simple</i> (or the <i>atome</i>) reside the accidents on which I shall explain myself, and which are inseparable from it;",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.6": "the VI<sup>th</sup>, that the accident does not endure two <i>times</i> (or <i>instants</i>);",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.7": "the VIIth proposition, that the reason for being is the same for the <i>capacities</i> and for their <i>privations</i>[[fn:988]], and that all of them are real accidents which require an efficient cause;",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.8": "the VIIIth proposition, that there is nothing in the entire being [that is to say, in all created things,] other than substance and accident, and that the <i>form physical</i> is also an accident;",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.9": "the IX<sup>th</sup>, in which accidents do not affect one another;",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.10": "the X<sup>th</sup> is, that the <i>possible</i> is not to be considered from the point of view of the conformity of being with any given idea[[fn:989]];",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.11": "the XI<sup>th</sup> is, that, as touching the inadmissibility of the <i>infinite</i>, it matters little[[fn:990]] whether it be so <i>in act</i> or <i>in potentiality</i>, or accidentally; I mean to say, that it matters little whether infinite things exist simultaneously, or whether they be deemed (to be composed) of that which is and that which is no more[[fn:991]] [and this is that which is <i>accidentally</i>]; all this, say they, is (equally) inadmissible;",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.12": "the XII<sup>th</sup> finally, is their assertion: that the senses lead into error, since many things escape their perception[[fn:992]], and that consequently their judgment cannot be admitted, nor can they be taken absolutely as the principles of a demonstration.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.13": "Having thus enumerated them, I shall proceed to explain their sense and set forth that which results therefrom, (approaching them) one by one.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.14": "The <small>FIRST PROPOSITION</small> has the following meaning: They maintained that the whole universe, that is to say, every body which it contains, is composed of very small particles, which, by reason of their subtilty, admit of no division[[fn:993]]. Each of these particles is absolutely without quantity; but when they are joined to one another, the whole has quantity and is then a body. According to the opinion of some, as soon as two of these particles join together, each of them becoms a body, thus making two bodies[[fn:994]]. All these particles are similar and alike to one another, and there is no kind of difference between them. It is not possible, say they, that there should exist any body whatsoever that is not composed of these similar particles, by <i>juxtaposition</i>[[fn:995]]; so that, for them, <i>generation</i> is the union (of atoms), and <i>destruction</i> is their separation. Nevertheless, they do not bestow upon the latter the name of <i>destruction</i>, but they say: <i>generations</i>[[fn:996]] are union, separation, motion, and rest. They say also that these particles are not of a limited number[[fn:997]] in the universe, as Epicurus and others who professed the doctrine of atoms believed; they maintain, on the contrary, that God perpetually creats these substances when He wills, and that they may likewise not exist. I shall make known unto thee their opinions concerning the <i>privation</i> of substance[[fn:998]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.15": "The <small>SECOND PROPOSITION</small> is the assertion of the <i>vacuum:</i> — The <i>men of the roots</i>[[fn:999]] likewise believe that the <i>vacuum</i> exists, that is to say, (that there exist) one or more spaces in which there is absolutely nothing, but which are void of all body and deprived of all substance. This proposition is necessary unto them so soon as they admit the first proposition. Indeed, if the universe were full[[fn:1000]] of these atoms, how then could that which movs be moved? for one cannot imagine that bodies enter into one another, and these atoms can neither unite nor separate save by motion. They are therefore of necessity obliged[[fn:1001]] to admit the <i>vacuum</i>, in order that it may be possible for these atoms to unite and to separate, and that motion may take place in this vacuum, in which there is no body nor any of these (simple) substances.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.16": "The <small>THIRD PROPOSITION</small> says: “that time is composed of <i>instants;</i>” that is to say, that it is composed of many small times which, by reason of their brief duration, admit of no division. This proposition is likewise necessary unto them because of the first proposition; for, having doubtless seen the demonstrations whereby Aristotle has proved that extension, time, and local motion are three corresponding things in being [that is to say, that they stand in the same mutual relation to one another, and that, when one of these things is divided, the other is divided likewise and in the same proportion], they were constrained to acknowledge[[fn:1002]] that if time were continuous and susceptible of division unto infinity, it would follow that this atom which they posited as indivisible is of necessity divisible, and that likewise, if one admitted that extension is continuous, one would be forced to admit the divisibility of that <i>instant</i> of time which was posited as indivisible, even as Aristotle has set forth in the <i>Acroasis</i>[[fn:1003]]. Wherefore they have laid down as a principle that extension is in no wise continuous, but composed of atoms whereat divisibility stays, and that likewise time results in <i>instants</i> which admit of no division. Thus, for example, an hour having sixty minutes, the minute sixty seconds, the second sixty thirds, the matter shall result for them in particles such as, for instance, tenths, or others yet smaller, which can in no wise be subdivided and which, like (the atoms of) extension, shall no longer admit of division. According to this, time would be a thing of position and order[[fn:1004]]; and verily they have in no wise fathomed the true nature of time.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.17": "And this was as it should be; for if the most skilful philosophers have been perplexed regarding time and several have not comprehended the notion thereof,—insomuch that Galen says it is a thing divine, the true nature of which cannot be grasped,—how much more should this be true of those who have no regard for the nature of any thing?",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.18": "Hearken now unto that which they were compelled to admit as a consequence of these three (initial) propositions: Motion, they said, consists in this, that each of these indivisible particles transports itself from one atom (of extension) to the adjacent atom[[fn:1005]], and it follows from this hypothesis that motions are not swifter one than another. Indeed, they say, when thou seest that two things in motion traverse two different distances in the same time, the cause thereof is not that the motion of that which traverss the longer distance is swifter, but the cause is that into that motion which we call slow, there enter more intervals of rest, whilst into that which we call swift, there enter fewer intervals of rest. When there was opposed to them (the example of) the arrow shot from a bow strongly bent[[fn:1006]], they replied: verily, it also has its motions interrupted by intervals of rest, and if thou believest that it movs with a continuous motion, it is by an error of the senses; for many things escape the perception of the senses, even as they have laid down as a principle in the twelfth proposition. It was then said to them: Have ye not seen that when the millstone performs a perfect circular motion, the part which is at its circumference traverss the extent of the largest circle in precisely the same time during which the part which is near the centre traverss the smallest circle? the motion of the circumference is therefore swifter than that of the inner circle; and it is not permitted you to say that in the motion of this latter part there enter more intervals of rest, since the whole body—I mean, the body of the millstone—is one and continuous. And hereupon they replied that in the revolution its parts become separated, and that the intervals of rest which enter into each part turning near the centre are more numerous than those which enter into the part far from the centre. But then, it was said to them, how comes it to pass that the millstone, in which we recognize a single body which cannot be broken with hammers, should break asunder whilst turning and then rejoin itself as soon as it is at rest, and become again as it was? and how is it not perceived that its parts have separated? And in answer thereto, they availed themselves precisely of this twelfth proposition, which says that one must not have regard to the perception of the senses, but to the testimony of the intellect.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.19": "One must not believe, moreover, that what I have just said is the most absurd consequence of these three propositions; for, certes, that which results from the belief in the existence of the <i>vacuum</i> is yet more extraordinary and more absurd; and what I have related to thee concerning motion[[fn:1007]] is no greater absurdity than to admit, as is necessary according to this system, that the diagonal of the square is equal to its side[[fn:1008]], so that there were some among them who maintained that the square is a thing which has no existence. In short, by reason of the <sup>1st</sup> proposition, all the demonstrations of the whole of geometry are found to be destroyed. These may be divided in this regard into two categories[[fn:1009]]: one part shall be entirely annulled, as, for example, the properties of incommensurability and commensurability[[fn:1010]] in lines and planes, the existence of rational and irrational lines[[fn:1011]], and (in general) all that is contained in the <sup>10th</sup> book of Euclid, as well as other similar things. For another part, the demonstrations shall not be absolute, as, for instance, in the problem of the division of a line into two equal parts[[fn:1012]]; for if the atoms of the line be an odd number, it shall not be possible, according to their hypothesis, to effect the division[[fn:1013]]. — Know also that the celebrated <i>Book of Artifices</i>, by the Banu Shakir[[fn:1014]], contains above an hundred <i>artifices</i>, all of which are supported by demonstrations and have been put into practice; now, if the <i>vacuum</i> could exist, not a single one (of these devices) could be carried out, and many hydraulic operations could not take place. Nevertheless, men have spent their lives arguing to confirm these propositions and others like unto them. — I return now to the explanation of the other propositions which I have recorded.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.20": "The <small>FOURTH PROPOSITION</small> says: “that <i>accidents</i> exist, that they are notions added to the notion of <i>substance</i>, and that there is no body whatsoever entirely exempt therefrom[[fn:1015]].” Were this proposition confined to these terms, it would be a true, clear, and manifest proposition, containing naught of doubt or obscurity[[fn:1016]]. But they say that, if a (simple) substance possesses not within itself the accident of life, it must perforce possess the accident of death; for, of two opposite (accidents), that which receivs (accidents) must of necessity possess one of them[[fn:1017]]. Thus, say they, it has (for example) colour and taste, and (moreover) motion or rest, union or separation[[fn:1018]]. If it possess the accident of life, it must likewise perforce possess certain other kinds of accidents, such as knowledge or ignorance, will or its opposite, power or impotence, perception or one of its opposites[[fn:1019]]; in short, every (accident) which may exist in a living being, it must of necessity possess itself, or else possess one of its opposites.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.21": "The <small>FIFTH PROPOSITION</small> says: “that it is in the atom itself that these accidents reside[[fn:1020]] and that from it they are inseparable.” Behold the explanation and the meaning of this proposition: They say that each of these atoms which God creats has accidents wherefrom it is inseparable, as, for example, colour and odour, motion or rest; excepting however quantity, for each atom apart has no quantity, and indeed, they do not call quantity an accident, nor do they recognize therein the notion of an accident[[fn:1021]]. According to this proposition, they deem that one cannot say of any of the accidents existing in any body whatsoever that it is proper to the whole of that body; on the contrary, this accident exists, according to them, in each of the atoms of which the body is composed. In this heap of snow, for instance, whiteness exists not alone in the whole mass, but it is each of the atoms of this snow which is white, and it is for this reason that whiteness is found in their whole. Likewise, they maintain, with regard to a body in motion, that it is each of its atoms which movs, and that for this reason its whole movs. Even so does life also exist, according to them, in each of the atoms of the living body, and likewise, as touching sensibility, each atom in this sensitive whole is, according to them, endowed with sensibility; for life, sensibility, intelligence, and knowledge are, in their view, accidents like unto blackness and whiteness, even as we shall show by the exposition of their opinions.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.22": "As touching the soul, they are divided: according to the prevailing opinion, it is an accident existing in but one of all the atoms of which man, for example, is composed; and if the whole be called <i>animated</i>, it is because this atom forms part thereof. There be some who say that the soul is a body[[fn:1022]] composed of subtle atoms, which atoms are doubtless endowed with a certain accident peculiar to them and whereby it becoms a soul, and these atoms, say they, mingle with the atoms of the body; consequently, they do not renounce the view of the soul as an accident. As for the intelligence, I see them to be in common accord that it is an accident (existing) in one of the atoms of the intelligent whole.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.23": "Concerning the science, there exists amongst them a state of indecision (with regard to the question of) whether it exists as an accident in each of the atoms of the aggregate endowed with science, or (whether it exists only in) a single atom; but both opinions have absurd consequences.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.24": "When it was objected unto them that metals and stones are for the most part found endowed with an intense colour, which nevertheless departs as soon as they are reduced to powder — [for, when we reduce to powder the emerald, which is of a deep green, it is transformed into a white dust, which proves that this accident pertains to the whole[[fn:1023]], and not to each of its parts; and, what is yet more manifest, when parts of a living being are cut off, they are in no wise living, which proves that this notion (of life) pertains to the whole, and not to each of its parts] — they replied thereto that the accident has no duration, but that it is perpetually created; and it is this opinion which I shall set forth in the following proposition.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.25": "The <small>SIXTH PROPOSITION</small> says: “that an accident endurs not for two times.” The meaning of this proposition is as follows: They maintain that God, in creating the (simple) substance, creats simultaneously therein whatever accident He wills, and that one cannot attribute unto God the power to create a substance without an accident, for that is impossible[[fn:1024]]. The true conception of an accident is that it endurs not, and subsists not for two <i>times</i>, that is to say, two <i>instants</i>[[fn:1025]]; the accident, therefore, as soon as it is created, perishes and remains not, and God creats another accident of the same kind. This latter likewise perishes, and God creats a third of the same kind; and thus it continus so long as God wills this kind of accident to endure. If God wills to create another kind of accident in this substance, He does so; but if He abstains from creating, and creats no further accident, this substance ceass to be. Such is the opinion of a portion of the (Mutakallemîn), and it is the majority; and this is the <i>creation of accidents</i> of which they speak[[fn:1026]]. Nevertheless, several amongst the <i>Mu‘tazilites</i> maintain that there be accidents which endure for a certain time, and that there be others which endure not for two instants; yet have they no rule in this regard for their guidance, whereby they might say: Such a kind of accidents endurs, and such another kind endurs not.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.26": "That which has led them to adopt this opinion is that they do not admit (amongst them) that there be a nature (of things)[[fn:1027]], and that, for example, the nature of a certain body requirs that it be affected by this or that accident; for, on the contrary, they will maintain that God has created these accidents instantaneously, without the mediation of a natural law or of any other thing whatsoever. Now, in professing this opinion, they were necessarily driven to this conclusion: that the accident endurs not. Verily, if it were admitted that it endurs for a certain time and thereafter ceass to be, this would give rise to the question, what thing has caused it to cease to be? and if it were answered that it is God who causs it to cease to be when He wills, that could not be true according to their opinion[[fn:1028]]; for the <i>efficient cause</i> works not non-existence, which has no need of an efficient cause, but it is when the efficient cause <i>abstains</i> from acting that the effect ceass to be [which is true in a certain respect[[fn:1029]]]. Wherefore, having desired to maintain that there is no natural law which necessitats the existence or non-existence of anything whatsoever, they have been led thereby to say[[fn:1030]] that accidents are created successively. According to some, therefore, when God wills that the substance should cease to be, He creats no accident therein, and thereby it ceass to be; others, however, say that if God willed to destroy the world, He would create the accident of destruction without the latter having a <i>substratum</i>[[fn:1031]], and this <i>destruction</i> would neutralize[[fn:1032]] the <i>existence</i> of the world.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.27": "By virtue of this proposition, they have maintained that this cloth which we believe to have been dyed red, it is not we who have dyed it, but it is God who has caused this colour to arise in the cloth at the moment when the latter was joined with the red colour; for, say they, although we believe that this colour has (perforce) penetrated the cloth, it is not so[[fn:1033]]. On the contrary (they add)[[fn:1034]], it is God who has established as a <i>customary thing</i>[[fn:1035]] that this black colour, for example, should only arise at the moment when the cloth is joined with the indigo[[fn:1036]]; yet this black, which God has created at the moment when the thing to be blackened was joined with the black colour, remains not, but perishes at that very instant, and God creats another black. In like manner, God has made it His <i>custom</i> to create, after the disappearance of this black, not a red or yellow colour, but a similar black.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.28": "In accordance with this hypothesis, they have maintained that the cognitions which we now possess of a certain thing cannot be the same as those which we possessed yesterday; that these latter, on the contrary, have vanished, and that others like unto them have been created; and if it be so, say they, it is because science is an accident. And likewise, he who believs that the soul is an accident must necessarily admit that there be created in every living being an hundred thousand souls, for example, in every minute; for, according to them, as thou knowest, time is composed of indivisible instants.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.29": "They maintain further, by virtue of this proposition, that when a man movs the pen[[fn:1037]], it is not the man who movs it; for this movement which ariss in the pen is an accident which God has created therein. Likewise, the movement of the hand, which in our opinion movs the pen, is an accident which God has created in the hand which movs[[fn:1038]]; God has only established it as a <i>custom</i> that the movement of the hand joins itself to the movement of the pen, without the hand having for all that any influence whatsoever or any causality in the movement of the pen, for, say they, the accident passes not beyond its <i>substratum</i>[[fn:1039]]. They admit therefore by common consent that this white cloth which has been lowered into the indigo-vat and which has been dyed, it is not the indigo that has rendered it black; for blackness is an accident in the body of the indigo and cannot pass to another body. There is absolutely no body which exerts an action; the ultimate efficient cause is none other than God, and it is He[[fn:1040]] who has caused the blackness to arise in the body of the cloth when it was joined with the indigo, for such is the <i>custom</i> which He has established. In short, one can in no wise say: Such a thing is the cause of such another; that is the opinion of the great majority of the <i>Mutakallemîn</i>. There be some amongst them who have admitted causality, but they have been blamed for it.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.30": "— Nevertheless, touching the actions of men, they are divided. The greater part of them, and (notably) the <i>Asch’ariyya</i> en masse, hold the opinion that, for the movement[[fn:1041]] of this pen, God has created four accidents which are in no wise causes one unto another, and which do but <i>co-exist</i> together. The first accident is my will to move the pen; the second accident is the faculty wherewith I move it; the third accident is the human movement itself, I mean, the movement of the hand; finally, the fourth accident is the movement of the pen. Indeed, they pretend that when a man wills a thing and thereafter performs it [at least, according to his belief], there has been created in him first the will, then the faculty to do that which he willed to do, and finally the action itself; for he acts not by means of the faculty created within him, which has no influence upon the action.— Nevertheless, the <i>Mo’tazales</i> say that he acts by means of the faculty created within him[[fn:1042]]; and a portion of the <i>Asch’ariyya</i> maintain that the created faculty exerts a certain influence upon the action and participats therein[[fn:1043]], an opinion which has been censured[[fn:1044]]. This created will, according to the opinion of all, and likewise the created faculty and the created action, according to the opinion of some, are all accidents without duration; and it is God who creats in this pen movements one after another, which continus so long as the pen movs. That it may rest, it is necessary that God should likewise create therein rest[[fn:1045]], and He ceass not to create rest successively therein, so long as the pen remains at rest.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.31": "Thus it is that in each of these <i>instants</i>, I mean (in each) of these <i>atoms of time</i>, God creats an accident in every individual being[[fn:1046]], whether it be angel, or celestial sphere, etc., and this continus thus perpetually. This, say they, is what is truly called believing that God is <i>efficient;</i> and he who believs not that God acts thus denies, according to them, that God is efficient. But, in my judgment and in that of every intelligent man, it is concerning such beliefs that one may say: <i>Will ye mock Him</i> (God) <i>as one mocks a mortal</i> (Job, xiii. 9)? For this is verily naught but pure <i>mockery</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.32": "The <small>SEVENTH PROPOSITION</small> is that they believe the <i>privations of capacities</i>[[fn:1047]] to be (real) things existing in the body and added to its substance, that they are accidents likewise having a (real) existence and which, consequently, are perpetually created, so that, when one disappears, another is created. Here follows the explanation: They admit not that rest is the <i>privation</i> of movement, nor that death is the privation of life, nor that blindness is the privation of sight, nor (in general) that there be such <i>privations</i> of capacities[[fn:1048]]. On the contrary, according to them, it is with movement and rest as with heat and cold[[fn:1049]]; and even as heat and cold are two accidents existing in two subjects, that which is hot and that which is cold, so movement is an accident created in that which movs and rest an accident which God creats in that which is at rest. This latter likewise[[fn:1050]] endurs not for two times, as has been already seen in the preceding proposition; wherefore, in this body at rest, God, according to them, creats rest for each of its atoms, and one rest having vanished, He creats another, so long as this thing is at rest. It is exactly the same, according to them, with knowledge and ignorance; for ignorance, according to them, is a positive accident, and it ceass not to vanish and to be created anew so long as he who is ignorant of a certain thing remains in his ignorance[[fn:1051]]. It is furthermore exactly the same with life and death. Verily, both the one and the other, according to them, are accidents (in the same right), and they say plainly that, so long as the living being remains alive, there is always an (instantaneous) life which disappears and another which is created; but when God wills that he should die, He creats in him the accident of death, following upon the (final) disappearance of the accident of life, which endurs not for two times. All this they say expressly;",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.33": "and it follows necessarily from this hypothesis that the accident of death created by God disappears likewise at the same instant, and that God creats (forthwith) another death, for otherwise death would not endure. Even as, therefore, life is created upon life, so is death created upon death[[fn:1052]]. Nevertheless, I would fain know how long God shall create the accident of death in the dead! Is it so long as he retains his outward form, or so long as there remains one of his atoms? For the accident of death which God creats, He creats it, as they suppose, in each of these atoms. Now, we find molar teeth of the dead which have been there for thousands of years, which proves that God has not reduced this substance to naught, and that, consequently, He creats therein the accident of death during those thousands of years, so that, one death disappearing, He creats another. And such indeed is the opinion of the greater number (of the Mutakallimūn).",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.34": "Notwithstanding, a portion of the <i>Mo’tazales</i> concede that certain <i>privations of faculties</i>[[fn:1053]] are not positive things; they assert, to the contrary, that impotence is (truly) the <i>privation</i> of power and ignorance the <i>privation</i> of knowledge. However, they do not thus judge[[fn:1054]] in regard to all privations, and they do not admit that darkness is the privation of light, nor that rest is the privation of movement. Far from it, they behold in these privations, in part, positive things and, in part, true privations, according to what suits their creed, as they have also done in the case of the duration of accidents, (saying) that some endure for a certain time, and that others endure not two instants; for their purpose, in general, is to suppose a Being whose nature is in conformity with our opinions and our beliefs.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.35": "The <small>EIGHTH PROPOSITION</small> stats: «that there exists (everywhere) naught but substance and accident, and that <i>physical forms</i> are likewise accidents[[fn:1055]].» Here follows the explanation of this proposition: All bodies, according to them, are composed of similar atoms, as we have set forth in their first proposition, and they differ from one another only by accidents. Wherefore, in their view, the quality of being an animal, that of being a man, sensation, reason, and so forth, are accidents in the same manner as whiteness, blackness, bitterness, and sweetness; so that individuals of diverse species differ among themselves only as do individuals of the self-same species[[fn:1056]]. They perceive, consequently, in the body of the heavens, and even in that of the angels, and even unto that of the so-called[[fn:1057]] celestial throne, the same substance as in any insect of the earth or in any plant whatsoever; all these things differ from each other only by accidents, and all have for their substance the atoms.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.36": "The <small>NINTH PROPOSITION</small> stats: «that accidents do not support one another.» One cannot therefore say, according to them, that a certain accident is supported by another[[fn:1058]], and that other by the substance; but all accidents are supported, immediately and in like manner, by the substance itself. That which they desire to avoid thereby is the necessity of admitting that a second accident could not exist in the substance save with the first which had preceded it; for they deny that such an occurrence takes place for certain accidents[[fn:1059]], and they strive to devise for these certain accidents the possibility of existing in any substance whatsoever, without the latter being determined by another accident, (and this) in accordance with their opinion, to wit, that all accidents are something which determins (the substance). Furthermore, it is necessary that the subject which bears the attribute should remain stable for a certain duration; now, since according to them an accident endurs not for two times, that is to say, two <i>instants</i>, how could it be, upon this supposition[[fn:1060]], that it should serve as a <i>substratum</i> for another thing?",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.37": "The <small>TENTH PROPOSITION</small> concerns that <i>admissibility</i>[[fn:1061]] of which they speak, and which is the foundation of the science of <i>Kalam</i>. Hearken unto its significance: They are of the opinion that whatsoever is <i>imaginable</i> is likewise <i>admissible</i> to the reason, (and they say) for example, that the terrestrial globe might become a sphere revolving in a circle[[fn:1062]] and the sphere be transformed into a terrestrial globe, and that it is <i>admissible</i> to the reason that such might happen; that the sphere of fire might move towards the centre and the terrestrial globe towards the circumference[[fn:1063]], and that, according to this principle of <i>rational admissibility</i>, one of the two places suits each of these bodies as much as the other[[fn:1064]]. Likewise, they say, every thing amongst these beings which we behold might be greater or smaller than it is[[fn:1065]], or might be found, in respect of its figure and place, in a state contrary to its own. Thus, for example, there might be a human individual of the dimension of a great mountain, having several heads and swimming[[fn:1066]] in the air; or there might exist an elephant of the dimension of a gnat and a gnat of the dimension of an elephant; all this, they say, is admissible to the reason. This principle of <i>admissibility</i> is applied consistently to the whole universe, and, for all that they thus suppose, they say: «One may <i>admit</i> that it be so, and it is <i>possible</i> that it be so, and it must not be that such a thing be rather in one manner than in another», without considering whether reality corresponds to their supposition[[fn:1067]]; for, they say, if this being has known forms, determinate dimensions, and fixed conditions, which undergo neither alteration nor change, this is but a mere <i>habit</i>[[fn:1068]]. Thus, for example, it is the <i>habit</i> of the sovereign not to traverse the streets of the city save upon a mount, and he is never seen otherwise, although it be not inadmissible to the reason that he might traverse the city on foot; on the contrary, no one doubts that this be possible, and it is admitted that it may happen. In like manner, they say, it is the <i>course of habit</i> that the terrestrial element should move towards the centre and fire upwards, or that fire should burn and water cool; but it is not inadmissible to the reason that this habit might change, so that fire might cool and move downwards, while yet being fire, and that likewise water might produce heat and move upwards, while yet being water. Such is the basis of all their reasoning[[fn:1069]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.38": "Nevertheless, they agree by common consent that the conjunction of contraries in the same subject and at the same instant is an absurd and impossible thing which reason cannot admit. Likewise, they say that the existence of a substance without any accident, or that of an accident without a <i>substratum</i> [admitted by some[[fn:1070]]], is an impossible thing which reason cannot admit. Finally, they say likewise that substance cannot be transformed into accident, nor accident into substance, nor one body penetrate into another body; and they even assert that these are things rationally impossible.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.39": "Verily, (in saying) that all these things enumerated as impossible cannot be <i>conceived</i> in the mind, and that what they have called possible can be <i>conceived</i>, there is some truth therein. Yet the philosophers say (unto them): If ye call such a thing impossible because it cannot be imagined, and another thing ye call possible because it can be imagined, then that which seems possible to you is so only in the imagination, but not for the reason; consequently, in this proposition, ye consider the necessary, the admissible, and the absurd, sometimes from the point of view of the imagination and not of the reason, and sometimes from that of mere common sense, as Abu-Nasr (Al-Farabi) has said, in speaking of that which the <i>Mutakallimun</i> call <i>reason</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.40": "It is therefore manifest that, unto them, whatsoever may be imagined is possible [whether reality conforms thereto or not], and that whatsoever cannot be imagined is impossible. But this proposition cannot be sustained save by means of the nine preceding propositions, and it was doubtless for the sake of this one that they were constrained to establish those others first. This shall result more clearly from that which I am about to set forth unto thee, by revealing what lies at the foundation of these matters, in the form of a discussion which took place between a <i>Mutakallim</i> and a philosopher:",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.41": "Wherefore, inquired the <i>Mutakallim</i> of the philosopher, do we find the body of this iron to be exceedingly solid and hard and of a black colour, and the body of this butter to be exceedingly tender and soft and of a white colour?",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.42": "It is, the philosopher replied unto him, because every natural body has two species of accidents: some betide it by reason of its matter, as, for example, health and sickness in man; others betide it by reason of its form, as, for example, astonishment and laughter in man[[fn:1071]]. Now, the matters of bodies of a perfected composition[[fn:1072]] vary greatly according to the forms peculiar to those matters, so that the substance of iron becoms the opposite of the substance of butter, and both are accompanied by the different accidents which thou seest therein; the hardness of the one and the softness of the other are therefore accidents which hold to the diversity of their respective forms[[fn:1073]], whilst blackness and whiteness are accidents which hold to the diversity of their <i>ultimate</i> matter[[fn:1074]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.43": "But the <i>Mutakallim</i> overthrew the whole of this reply by means of his aforementioned propositions, even as I shall expound unto thee: There exists no <i>form</i>, said he, which, as ye believe, constituts the substance in such wise as to make of it varied substances, but there are everywhere naught but <i>accidents</i> [according to their opinion, which we have set forth in the VIII<sup>th</sup> proposition[[fn:1075]]]. There is no difference, he continued, between the substance of iron and that of butter; for all is composed of atoms similar to one another. And this is their opinion which we have set forth in the I<sup>st</sup> proposition, from which, as we have explained, the II<sup>nd</sup> and III<sup>rd</sup> propositions necessarily flow; and likewise one has need of the XII<sup>th</sup> proposition to establish the existence of atoms. Neither can it be admitted, according to the <i>Mutakallim</i>, that a certain substance possesses certain accidents which are peculiar to it and by which it is disposed and prepared to receive secondary accidents; for, according to him, an accident cannot bear another accident, as we have set forth in the IX<sup>th</sup> proposition. Neither has the accident any duration, as we have set forth in the VI<sup>th</sup> proposition. — The <i>Mutakallim</i> having thus arrived, by means of his propositions, at the affirmation of all that he desired[[fn:1076]], and having obtained this result: “that the substances of butter and of iron are similar and identical, that there is one and the selfsame relation between every substance and every accident[[fn:1077]], that one substance is no more apt than another to (receive) a certain accident, and that, even as one atom is no more susceptible of motion than of rest, so the atoms are no more apt than one another to receive the accident of life or that of intelligence or that of sensibility [it matters not whether the number of atoms (united) be greater or less[[fn:1078]], for, according to the opinion set forth in the V<sup>th</sup> proposition, the accident exists in each of the atoms]”, it follows from all these propositions that man is no more apt to think than the beetle[[fn:1079]]; and they were forced to arrive at this <i>admissibility</i> of which they treat in the present proposition. It was toward this proposition that all their efforts tended; for it lends itself marvelously to all the hypotheses which one wishes to establish[[fn:1080]], as shall presently be set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.44": "ANNOTATION<small></small>. Know, O reader of this treatise! that if thou art of those who know the soul and its faculties, and if thou dost penetrate into each thing in all the reality of its being, thou shalt know that <i>imagination</i> belongs to most animals; that, at least as regards the perfect animals, I mean those which have a heart, it is evident that they all possess imagination[[fn:1081]], and that it is not by this that man is distinguished (from other animals). The action of the imagination is not the same as that of the intellect, but is opposed to it. Indeed, the intellect analyss composite things, distinguishes their parts, abstracts them, represents them in their reality and with their causes, and thus perceivs in a single object numerous things, as distinct for the intellect as two human individuals are distinct beings for the imagination[[fn:1082]]. It is by the intellect that one distinguishes that which is general from that which is individual, and no demonstration can take place save by means of that which is general[[fn:1083]]; finally, it is by the intellect that one knows (how to distinguish) the essential attribute from the accidental. But the imagination can accomplish none of these actions; for it perceivs only the individual and the composite in its entirety, even as the senses perceive it, or else it composs things which in reality are separate and which it combins one with another, and this aggregate[[fn:1084]] becoms a body or one of the faculties of the body. Thus, for example, one may conceive in the imagination a human individual having the head of a horse and wings, and other similar (creations); and this is what is called a falsified invention, for there is absolutely no being that conforms thereto. The imagination cannot, in its perception[[fn:1085]], rid itself in any wise of matter, even though it should make every effort to abstract a form; wherefore no heed must be paid unto the imagination.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.45": "—Hearken, however the mathematical sciences are instructive for us (in this regard), and how weighty are the propositions which we derive therefrom.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.46": "Know that there are certain things which man, when he considers them by his imagination, can in no wise conceive, and which on the contrary he finds as impossible for the imagination as would be the conjunction of opposites; and yet, such a thing as it is impossible to imagine, one may establish by demonstration that it exists and bring to light the reality thereof[[fn:1086]]. If, for example, thou shouldst imagine a great globe of such dimension as thou wilt, even shouldst thou imagine it as great as the globe of the <i>encompassing</i> sphere, and that then thou shouldst imagine therein an axis which traverss the centre, and finally thou shouldst figure to thyself two men standing upon the two extremities of the axis, in such manner that their feet be placed in the direction of the axis and that the latter form, as it were, with the feet one single straight line, it must necessarily be that this axis be parallel to the horizon or be not so[[fn:1087]]; now, if it be parallel (to the horizon), the two men shall fall, and, if it be not parallel, one of them, he who is below, shall fall, and the other shall remain standing[[fn:1088]]. Such at least is that which the imagination figurs to itself. Now, it is demonstrated that the earth is of a spherical figure, and that at the two extremities of its axis there are inhabited lands[[fn:1089]]. Each of the inhabitants of the two extremities has his head toward heaven and his feet toward those of his antipode[[fn:1090]]; and yet it is neither possible nor imaginable that either of the two should fall, for they are not one above and the other below, but each of the two is above or below in relation to the other.—Likewise (to cite another example), it has been demonstrated, in the II<sup>nd</sup> book of the <i>Conic Sections</i>, that there are two lines proceeding (from certain points) and between which there is, at their point of departure, a certain distance which diminishes as they are prolonged; so that they ever approach one another without ever being able to meet, even should they be extended to infinity, although nevertheless they draw ever nearer as they are prolonged[[fn:1091]]. Behold a thing which one could not figure to oneself and which the imagination could in no wise conceive[[fn:1092]]. These two lines are, the one straight, the other curved, even as it is set forth in the place cited. It is therefore demonstrated that there exist things which one cannot imagine, and which (not only) could not be comprehended by the imagination, but even appear unto it impossible. Likewise, it is demonstrated (on the other hand) that certain things which the imagination would affirm are (in reality) impossible, as, for example, that God be a body or a faculty in a body; for, unto the imagination, naught has existence, save it be a body or something within a body.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.47": "It is therefore manifest that there exists another faculty whereby one judges the necessary, the possible, and the impossible, and that this is not the imagination. And how excellent and of what great profit is this study for him who desirs to avoid the calamity of suffering himself to be guided by the imagination[[fn:1093]]!",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.48": "It must not be believed that the <i>Mutakallimūn</i> perceive nothing of all this; on the contrary, they have indeed some sentiment thereof and know it so well that they call <i>presumption</i> and <i>chimera</i>[[fn:1094]] that which, albeit impossible, is admitted by the imagination, as, for example, that God be a body, and oftentimes they say clearly that these presumptions are <i>mendacious.</i> This is why they have had recourse to the nine propositions which we have reported, in order to be able to affirm this tenth proposition, which enunciats the admissibility of all <i>imaginable</i> things which they wished to declare admissible, and that by favour (of the hypotheses) of the parity of atoms and of the equal nature of all accidents[[fn:1095]], even as we have set it forth.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.49": "Reflecting well thereon, one shall see that this may give rise to a difficult discussion[[fn:1096]]. For indeed, there are certain ideas which one may set forth as conceptions of the intellect, whilst another will maintain that they belong to the domain of the imagination; and, in this case, we should desire to find a <i>criterion</i> for distinguishing intelligible things from the conceptions of the imagination. Now, if the philosopher should say: “I have for witness <i>Being</i>[[fn:1097]] [as he expresses himself] and it is thereby that we examine the necessary, the possible, and the impossible”, the theologian would answer him: “But this is precisely the point in dispute; for I maintain[[fn:1098]] that this <i>Being</i> has been made by the (divine) <i>Will</i>, and that it is not the result of <i>necessity</i>; and, although it be made in such a manner, it is admissible that it might have been made differently, unless the conception of the intellect decide that it could not be otherwise than it is, even as thou dost pretend.”",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.50": "Such is this principle of <i>admissibility</i>, concerning which I reserve to myself to return in other passages of this treatise; nor is it a matter that one ought to be in haste to reject altogether and lightly[[fn:1099]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.51": "The <small>ELEVENTH PROPOSITION</small> says: “that the existence of the infinite (in the universe) is inadmissible, in whatsoever manner it may be.” Here is the explanation thereof: It has been demonstrated that it is impossible that there should exist an infinite magnitude, or that there should exist magnitudes whose number is infinite [even though each of them were a finite magnitude], provided always that these infinite things exist simultaneously[[fn:1100]]. Likewise, it is inadmissible that there should exist causes in infinitum; I mean, that a thing serving as a cause to another should in its turn have another cause, and this latter yet another cause, and so forth in infinitum, so that there would be there things infinite in number existing <i>in act</i>[[fn:1101]]. And it matters not whether they be bodies or <i>separated</i> substances[[fn:1102]], provided they serve as causes one to another. This is the <i>essential</i> physical order[[fn:1103]], regarding which it has been demonstrated that the infinite is therein impossible[[fn:1104]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.52": "As for the infinite existing <i>in potentiality</i> or <i>accidentally</i>, we have in part demonstrated its existence; thus, for example, we have demonstrated that a magnitude is <i>virtually</i> divisible to infinity, and that time is so as well[[fn:1105]]. But in part, this gives rise to speculation[[fn:1106]], as, for instance, the existence of the <i>infinite by succession</i>, which is also called the <i>infinite by accident</i>, that is to say, when one thing exists after another has ceased to exist, and this other after a third has ceased to exist, and so on (ascending) to infinity. This is a very difficult subject for speculation.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.53": "Those who claim to have demonstrated the eternity of the world maintain that time is infinite, and for them naught inadmissible follows therefrom, for, as one part of time is produced, another which preceded it has vanished; and it is the same, according to them, with the succession of accidents that happen to matter in infinitum, without anything inadmissible following therefrom, since they do not all exist simultaneously, but successively, the impossibility of which has in no wise been demonstrated[[fn:1107]]. But, according to the <i>Mutakallimūn</i>, it is indifferent whether one says that an infinite magnitude exists, or whether one says that body and time are divisible in infinitum[[fn:1108]]. It is likewise indifferent, according to them, (to suppose) the simultaneous existence of a series of things infinite in number[[fn:1109]], speaking, for example, of the human individuals existing at this moment, or to say that there happen in the universe things infinite in number, although vanishing successively, as one might say, for instance: Zeid and the son of ’Amr, this one the son of Khaled, this one the son of Becr, and so forth (ascending) in infinitum; for this is, according to them, as inadmissible as the first case[[fn:1110]]. These four classes of the infinite are therefore equal, according to them[[fn:1111]]. As for the last class[[fn:1112]], there be some among them who think it their duty to prove the truth thereof, I mean (that they think they must) demonstrate its impossibility, by a method which I shall set forth to thee in this treatise[[fn:1113]]. Others say that this is self-evident[[fn:1114]], and that it is something known from the first, requiring no demonstration. Now, if it were manifestly inadmissible that there should be things <i>infinite by succession</i>, even though that which now exists thereof be finite, the eternity of the world would appear inadmissible at the first, without the need of any other proposition[[fn:1115]]. But this is not the place to examine this subject.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.54": "The <small>TWELFTH PROPOSITION</small> says: “that the senses do not always yield certainty.” In fact, the <i>Mutakallimūn</i> suspected[[fn:1116]] the perception of the senses in two respects. Firstly, they say, many sensible objects escape them, either by reason of the subtilty of the perceptible body [as they say concerning (the hypothesis of) atoms and that which follows therefrom[[fn:1117]], as we have explained], or because they are too far removed from him who would perceive them; thus man can neither see, nor hear, nor smell, at the distance of several leagues, and likewise the motion of the heavens is in no wise perceptible. Secondly, they say, the senses are deceived in their perceptions. Thus, for example, a thing which is large, man ses as small when it is remote from him; a small thing, he ses as large when it is in the water; finally, he ses as a broken line that which is straight[[fn:1118]], when one part is in the water and the other without. Likewise, he who is afflicted with jaundice ses things yellow, and he whose tongue is imbued with yellow bile[[fn:1119]] finds sweet things to have a bitter taste. They enumerate yet many things of this kind; wherefore, they say, one cannot trust the senses so as to take them for a principle of demonstration.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.55": "It must not be supposed that it was in vain that the <i>Mutakallemim</i> insisted upon this proposition[[fn:1120]], even as the majority of these modern (Mutakallemim) believe that it was without necessity that their predecessors strove to maintain (the hypothesis of) the <i>atom</i>. On the contrary, all that we have hitherto reported on their part is necessary; and, when one assails even a single one of these propositions, the entire object is frustrated. As for this last proposition, it is right necessary; for, when the perception of our senses is in contradiction with what they have established[[fn:1121]], they say that one must pay no heed to the senses so soon as a thing is demonstrated by means of what they call the testimony of reason[[fn:1122]]. It is thus they pretend concerning motion, which nevertheless is continuous, that there enter therein intervals of rest, and that the millstone is sundered whilst turning[[fn:1123]]; and they further pretend that the (previous) whiteness of this cloth has now vanished, and that this is another whiteness[[fn:1124]]. These are things in opposition to what is seen; and (likewise) there results from the existence of the <i>void</i> many things which are all belied by the senses. To all this they answer: “It is a thing which escaps the senses”, whenever, indeed, it is possible so to answer; for other matters[[fn:1125]], they reply that it is one of the many errors of the senses.",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.56": "Thou knowest that these are all ancient opinions professed by the Sophists[[fn:1126]], even as Galen reports in his book <i>On Natural Faculties</i> (speaking) of those who say that the senses are deceptive; he has related therein (on this subject) all that thou knowest[[fn:1127]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..72.57": "Having first spoken of these propositions (of the <i>Mutakallemim</i>), I shall set forth their methods concerning the four questions at issue[[fn:1128]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.0": "In this chapter I shall relate unto thee in summary the proofs of the <i>Mutakallemim</i> (establishing) that the world is <i>created</i>. Thou shalt not ask of me to give the exposition thereof in their own terms and with their prolixity; but I shall make known unto thee what each of them had in view[[fn:1129]] and his method of argumentation for affirming the <i>creation</i> of the world or for denying its <i>eternity</i>, and I shall briefly point out to thee the propositions employed for each method[[fn:1130]]. If thou readest their prolix books and their most celebrated works, thou shalt find therein absolutely nothing more than what my words shall make thee understand of their argumentation on this subject; but thou shalt find therein a more abundant language and fair and brilliant expressions. Sometimes they have employed rhyme and symmetrical periods, and have made choice of the most elegant expressions[[fn:1131]]; and sometimes also they have employed an obscure style, with the intent to astound the hearer and to affright him who approaches the contemplation thereof. Thou shalt also find, in their works, repetitions, doubtful questions which they propose and which they pretend to have resolved, and frequent sallies against their adversaries.",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.1": "<small>FIRST METHOD</small>[[fn:1132]]: There are some among them who maintain that by admitting a single <i>produced</i> thing[[fn:1133]], one may demonstrate that the world is <i>created</i>. Thus, for example, it is inadmissible that this individual Zeid, who at first was a molecule[[fn:1134]] and who thereafter was transformed successively until he attained his perfection, should have thus changed himself, and that it should be he who produced in himself these diverse metamorphoses. On the contrary, there was outside of him something which changed him; and, consequently, it is demonstrated that he had need of an artificer who should organize his structure and cause him to undergo diverse transformations. It is the same, by analogy, with this palm-tree and with every other thing; and it is the same also, it has been said[[fn:1135]], with the whole of the universe. Thou seest, consequently, that this one[[fn:1136]] believs that, from what is found to take place in one body, conclusions must be drawn for every other body[[fn:1137]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.2": "S<small>ECOND MS</small>OD: They further assert that by admitting the <i>creation</i> of even a single individual among those propagated by generation, it may be demonstrated that the whole universe is <i>created</i>. Here is the explanation thereof: This Zeid, who at first existed not, subsequently came into existence; yet, he could only have been born through ’Amr, his father, and the latter, being likewise <i>born</i>, could only have been born through Khaled, the grandfather. Now, the latter being also <i>born</i>[[fn:1138]], this would proceed thus unto infinity; but they have laid down as a principle that the existence of an infinite, even in this manner[[fn:1139]], is inadmissible, as we have set forth in the XI<sup>th</sup> of their propositions. Likewise, if thou shouldst arrive, for example, at a first individual without a father, who were Adam, there would yet follow the question: “whence was this Adam born?” If thou shouldst answer, for example: “from the dust,” it would necessarily be asked: “but whence was this dust produced?” If, for example, one should answer: “from water,” it would again be asked: “but whence was this water produced?” It is therefore absolutely necessary, they say, that this should continue thus unto infinity, which is inadmissible; or else thou shalt arrive at the existence of a thing brought forth out of absolute non-existence. Therein lies the truth[[fn:1140]] and there the questions shall cease. This, therefore, is a proof, they say, that the world was brought forth out of absolute non-existence[[fn:1141]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.3": "T<small>HIRD MS</small>OD: The atoms of the universe, say they, must perforce be either <i>joined</i> or <i>separated</i>[[fn:1142]], and there be some which at one time unite and at another separate. Now, it is manifest and evident that, in respect of their essence, neither union alone nor separation alone appertains unto them; for, if their essence and nature required that they should be only separated, they would never unite, and likewise, if their essence and their true character required that they should be only joined, they would never separate. Thus, then, separation suits them no more than union, nor union more than separation; and consequently, if they be in part joined and in part separated, and if in part they further change their condition, being at one time united and at another separated, this is a proof that these atoms stand in need of one who shall unite that which is to be united and separate that which is to be separated. Consequently, they say, this is a proof that the world is <i>created</i>.—It is clear that the author of this method has availed himself of the I<sup>st</sup> of their propositions and of all that follows therefrom.",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.4": "F<small>OURTH METHOD</small>: The entire universe, they affirm, is composed of substance and accident, and no substance is exempt from one or several accidents[[fn:1143]]; now, as all accidents <i>originate</i>, it follows that the substance which supports them must likewise have <i>originated</i>, for everything that attaches itself to things <i>originated</i> and is inseparable from them is likewise <i>originated</i>[[fn:1144]]. Consequently, the entire world is <i>originated</i> (or <i>created</i>). Should anyone contend: “perchance the substance has not <i>originated</i>, and perchance it is only the accidents which <i>originate</i> by succeeding one another thereupon unto infinity,” they would reply that in such case there must needs be an infinite number of things <i>originated</i> (successively), which they have posited as inadmissible[[fn:1145]].— This method is regarded by them as the most ingenious and the best, such that many have perceived in it a (veritable) demonstration.",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.5": "Three hypotheses have been admitted for this method, the necessity of which shall escape no thinker[[fn:1146]]: 1st, That an infinite <i>by succession</i> is inadmissible; 2nd, That every accident <i>is produced</i>[[fn:1147]]. But our adversary, who maintains the eternity of the world, contradicts us concerning one of the accidents, which is circular motion. Verily, Aristotle maintains that circular motion is neither produced nor perishes; wherefore, according to him, the mobile which has this motion is neither produced nor perishes[[fn:1148]]. We gain nothing, therefore, by establishing the <i>production</i> of other accidents; for our adversary does not contest that these latter are <i>produced</i>, and he maintains that they succeed one another in that which is not <i>produced</i>[[fn:1149]]. Likewise he maintains that this single accident, to wit, circular motion [I mean, the motion of the celestial sphere], is not <i>produced</i> and that it belongs to none of the species of <i>produced</i> accidents[[fn:1150]]. It is therefore this accident alone which it would be necessary to examine, in order to demonstrate that it is (likewise) <i>produced</i>. Finally, the 3<sup>rd</sup> hypothesis which the author of this method has admitted is this: that there is no sensible being other than substance and accident; I mean, the <i>simple substance</i> (or the <i>atom</i>) with the accidents attributed thereto[[fn:1151]]. Nevertheless, if the body were composed of <i>matter</i> and <i>form</i>, as our adversary has demonstrated, it would be necessary to demonstrate that prime matter and prime form are produced and perish, and only then could one establish a demonstration for the <i>newness of the world</i>[[fn:1152]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.6": "A <small>FIFTH METHOD</small> is that of <i>determination</i>[[fn:1153]]. They have a very great predilection for this method, the sense of which returns to that which I have set forth unto thee concerning their X<sup>th</sup> proposition. Fixing the mind[[fn:1154]] upon the whole of the world or upon any one of its parts whatsoever, one says: It is <i>admissible</i> that this thing should be such as it is, in respect of figure and measure, with the accidents found therein and in the time and place where it is found; but it is <i>admissible</i> also that it might have been either larger or smaller, or of a different figure, or (accompanied) by such accidents, or exist before or after the time of its existence, or in some other place. Now, as it is <i>determined</i> by a certain figure, or by a measure, or by a place, or by a certain accident and by a particular time, although it be admissible that all this might have been otherwise, this is a proof that there is (a Being) who <i>determins</i> freely (all things) and who has preferred one of these two admissible cases[[fn:1155]]. Consequently, the whole of the world or one of its parts standing in need of a Being who <i>determins</i>, this proves that the world is <i>created;</i> for it matters little whether thou sayest <i>determining</i>, or <i>efficient</i>, or <i>creator</i>, or <i>producer</i>, or <i>innovator</i>, or <i>acting with intention</i>, all these have but one and the same meaning.",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.7": "Entering upon this method with many general and special details, they say, for example: “It is no more meet for the earth to be beneath the water than to be above it[[fn:1156]]; who then was it that assigned to it this place? It is no more meet for the sun to be round than to be square or triangular, for all figures are equally suited to bodies endowed with figure; who then has particularized the sun with this figure?” And in this manner they contemplate the details of the whole universe; so that when they behold flowers of diverse colours, they are astonished thereat and find therein wherewith to fortify their argument[[fn:1157]]. For, say they, this earth being <i>one</i> and this water <i>one</i>, wherefore then is this flower yellow and that other red? Can this come to pass otherwise than through a <i>determining</i> being? and this <i>determining</i> being is God; wherefore, the whole world has need of a being who <i>determins</i> the whole, as well as each of its parts, by some [[fn:1158]]particularity.",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.8": "All this results (solely) from the acceptance of the <sup>Xth</sup> proposition; and it must be added thereto that, among those very ones who maintain the eternity of the world, there are some who do not contest <i>determination</i> with us, as we shall set forth[[fn:1159]]. But, in short, this method seems unto me the best, and I have thereupon an opinion which thou shalt hear (further on)[[fn:1160]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.9.sub_0": "<small>SIXTH METHOD</small>: One of the moderns has pretended to have lighted upon a very good method, better than all those which precede, and which is (drawn from) <i>the preference</i>[[fn:1161]] accorded to being over non-being. Every man, says he, grants that the existence of the world is but <i>possible</i>, for if it had a <i>necessary</i> existence, it would be God; now we speak here only to him who, whilst professing the eternity of the world, nevertheless affirms the existence of God. That which is <i>possible</i> is that which may exist or may not exist, and being is no more meet for it than non-being. Now, since that whose existence is but <i>possible</i> does (actually) exist, although there be for it an equal reason for being and for not being, this is a proof that there is something which has <i>preferred</i> its existence to its non-existence[[fn:1162]]. This is a method (which may seem) very satisfactory, yet it is but a branch of that of <i>determination</i>, which preceded it, save that this author has changed the word <i>determine</i> into <i>prefer</i>, and that for the <i>conditions</i> of being he has substituted the very <i>existence</i> of being[[fn:1163]]. But, either he has sought to deceive us, or he has himself been deceived as to the sense of this thesis which says: that the world is of a <i>possible existence;</i> for our adversary, who admitts the eternity of the world, when he says: “the world is of a possible existence,” employs the word <i>possible</i> in a sense other than that given to it by the <i>Mutakallim</i>, as we shall set forth[[fn:1164]]. Furthermore, when it is said that the world has need of something which <i>preferrs</i> its existence to its non-existence, there is therein something very erroneous; for <i>preference</i> and <i>determination</i> can only be applied to some being capable of receiving equally one of two opposite or different qualities[[fn:1165]], so that one might say: since we find it in such a condition and not in another, this proves that there is therein an artificer acting with intention.",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.9.sub_1": "Thus, for example, one would say: This copper is no more apt to receive the form of an ewer than to receive that of a candlestick; if, therefore, we find it a candlestick or an ewer, we know thereby of necessity that a <i>determining</i> (artificer) <i>acting with intention</i> has had in view one of these two <i>admissible</i> forms. Now, it is evident that the copper <i>existed</i>, and that before the action of him who gave preference to one of the two <i>admissible</i> forms attributed to it, there was naught non-existent there save these very forms[[fn:1166]]. But, as for that being concerning which there is no agreement whether its existence has always been and shall always be such as it is, or whether it began to exist after (absolute) non-being, it can in no wise give rise to this thought[[fn:1167]]; and one could not ask: “Who is he that has preferred its existence to its non-existence?” save after having recognized[[fn:1168]] that it came into existence after not having existed, which is precisely the matter whereon there is no agreement. For if we took its existence and its non-existence for something ideal[[fn:1169]], we should simply return to the <sup>Xth</sup> proposition, which says that regard must be had only to imaginations and ideas, and not to real and <i>intelligible</i> things[[fn:1170]]; for the adversary, who admitts the eternity of the world, thinks that the imaginary supposition of the non-existence of the world is like unto the supposition of any other impossible thing that might come to our imagination. — But it is not the purpose here to refute their opinions[[fn:1171]]; I have desired only to set forth unto thee that it is not true, as has been pretended, that this method[[fn:1172]] is different from the preceding one, and that on the contrary it is with this as with the former, in that it supposs[[fn:1173]] that <i>admissibility</i> which is known[[fn:1174]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.10": "<small>SEVENTH METHOD</small>: Another among the moderns has maintained that the creation of the world can be established by means of the permanence of souls admitted by the philosophers. If the world, says he, were eternal, the men who have died in the boundless past would be infinite in number; there would therefore be souls infinite in number and existing simultaneously. Now, it has been indubitably demonstrated that this is false; I speak of the simultaneous existence of things infinite in number[[fn:1175]].—This is a most singular method, which demonstrats an obscure thing by means of that which is yet more obscure; and it is to this that one may rightly apply that proverb current among the Syrians[[fn:1176]]: “Thy surety himself stands in need of a surety.” It would seem that for such a one the permanence of souls is a demonstrated thing and that he knows in what manner they survive (the bodies) and what is the thing that survivs[[fn:1177]], so that he may draw arguments therefrom. If, however, he has had no other aim than to raise an objection against the adversary[[fn:1178]] who admitts the eternity of the world whilst admitting the permanence of souls, his objection would be founded only on the condition that the adversary also confessed the idea attributed to him concerning the permanence of souls[[fn:1179]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.11": "Certain modern philosophers have resolved this difficulty, saying: The souls which survive are in no wise bodies, so that they should possess a <i>place</i> and a <i>situation</i>, or that infinity should be incompatible with their manner of existence[[fn:1180]]. But thou[[fn:1181]], thou knowest that those <i>separated</i> things[[fn:1182]], I mean those which are neither bodies nor a faculty within a body, but pure intelligences, do in no wise admit of multiplicity, save (in this sense) that some among them are the <i>cause</i> of the existence of others, so that they are distinguished one from another only in that the one is <i>cause</i> and the other <i>effect</i>[[fn:1183]]. Now, that which survivs of Zeid is neither the cause nor the effect of that which survivs of ‘Amr; wherefore the whole is <i>one</i> in number[[fn:1184]], as has been shewn by Abu-Bekr ibn al-Çâyig, both he and others who have applied themselves[[fn:1185]] to speak of these profound matters. In fine, it is not from things so obscure, which the minds of men are incapable of conceiving, that one must take premises to demonstrate other things.",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.12": "It must be known that all those who strive to establish the <i>creation</i> of the world, or to contest its <i>eternity</i>, by means of these methods of the <i>Kalâm</i>, must of necessity employ therein either one of these two propositions or both at once; to wit, the X<sup>th</sup> proposition [that is to say, <i>rational admissibility</i>], in order to establish that there is a <i>determining</i> Being, or else the XI<sup>th</sup> proposition, which proclaims the inadmissibility of an <i>infinite by succession</i>. This latter proposition they seek to verify in diverse manners. First, the disputant attaches himself to any species whatsoever (of creatures) whose individuals are born and perish, and, ascending in his mind to the time past, he obtains this result: that, according to the opinion which admitts the <i>eternity</i> (of the world), the individuals of the said species (which have existed) from a certain epoch and anteriorly in the eternal past are infinite (in number), and that (on the other hand) the individuals of this same species (which have existed) from a later epoch, for example, a thousand years after that which was first adopted, and ascending into the eternal past, are likewise infinite[[fn:1186]]; now, this latter totality exceeding the former totality by the whole number of those who were born during those thousand years, they pretend, by this consideration, to arrive at this result: that there would be therein one infinite greater than another infinite[[fn:1187]]. They do the like with the revolutions of the celestial sphere[[fn:1188]], and they pretend to obtain this result: that there would be infinite revolutions more numerous than other infinite revolutions. Sometimes also they compare the revolutions of one sphere with those of another which are slower and which are all infinite[[fn:1189]]. They do the same for every kind of accidents that supervene[[fn:1190]]. They count, therefore, (everywhere) the individualities which exist no more, and represent them to themselves as though they existed and as though they were <i>delimited</i> things[[fn:1191]]; thereafter they add to this ideal thing or subtract from it[[fn:1192]]. But these are things purely ideal and not real. Already Abu-Naçr al-Farâbi has destroyed[[fn:1193]] this (eleventh) proposition, shewing the error it presented in all its particular applications; even as, with an impartial examination, thou shalt find it clearly set forth[[fn:1194]] in his celebrated work <i>On Variable Beings</i>[[fn:1195]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..73.13": "These are the principal methods of the <i>Mutakallimūn</i> for establishing the <i>creation of the world</i>. As soon as they have established by these proofs that the world is <i>created</i>, it necessarily follows that it has an Artificer who created it with intention, with will, and of His own free choice[[fn:1196]]. Thereafter they have demonstrated, by (various) methods which we shall set forth unto thee in the following chapter, that He (the Creator) is <i>one</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 1..74.0": "I shall also set forth unto thee, in this chapter, the proofs of the <i>Unity</i> (of God) according to the system of the <i>Mutakallimūn</i>. This Being, say they, whom the universe indicats as being its Artificer and Producer, is <i>one;</i> and their principal methods for establishing the <i>Unity</i> are two in number: the method of <i>mutual hindrance</i> and that of <i>reciprocal diversity</i>[[fn:1197]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..74.1": "The <small>FIRST METHOD</small>, to wit, that of <i>mutual hindrance</i>, is that which is preferred by the great majority. This is the sense thereof: If, it is said, the universe had two gods, it would be necessary that the atom—which (in principle) cannot be exempt from one of two opposite (accidents)[[fn:1198]]—should be devoid of both at once, which is inadmissible, or else that the two opposites should be joined together at the same time and in the same <i>substratum</i>, which is likewise inadmissible. If, for example, the atom or the atoms which the one (of the two gods) wished now to make hot, the other wished to make cold, it would follow either that they would be neither hot nor cold, because the two actions would mutually hinder each other—[which is inadmissible, every body receiving one of the two opposite accidents]—or else that the body in question would be at once hot and cold (which is impossible). Likewise, if one of the two wished to set in motion a certain body, it might be that the other wished to place it at rest; and it would follow that it would be neither in motion nor at rest, or that it would be at once in motion and at rest. This species of argumentation is based upon the question of the atom, which is (the object of) their I<sup>st</sup> proposition, upon the hypothesis of the (perpetual) creation of accidents[[fn:1199]] and upon the proposition which says that the <i>privations of capacities</i> are <i>positive</i> things which require an efficient cause[[fn:1200]]. Verily, if any one should say that the inferior matter, in which, according to the opinion of the philosophers, generation and corruption succeed each other, is other than the superior matter [that is to say, that which serves as a <i>substratum</i>[[fn:1201]] to the celestial spheres], as has been demonstrated[[fn:1202]], and if it were maintained that there are two gods, the one governing the inferior matter and whose action extends not to the spheres, the other governing the spheres and whose action extends not to the elementary matter[[fn:1203]], as the <i>Dualists</i>[[fn:1204]] did pretend, such an opinion would in no wise lead to <i>mutual hindrance</i>[[fn:1205]]. And if it were objected that this would be to attribute an imperfection to each of the two, because the one would not dispose of that which the other disposs of, one might reply that this constituts no imperfection in respect of either of them; for that thing to which his action extends not is in respect of him <i>impossible</i>, and to be unable to do the <i>impossible</i> constituts no imperfection in the artificer[[fn:1206]], even as, for us unitarians, there is no imperfection in the one God in that He cannot join contraries in the same subject, and that His power extends neither to that, nor to other like impossibilities.",
    "root.text.Part 1..74.2": "Having perceived the weakness of this method, although there was something that drew them to it[[fn:1207]], they have passed to another method.",
    "root.text.Part 1..74.3": "<small>SECOND METHOD</small>: If there were two gods, say they, it would behoove them to possess something in common and something else which should belong to the one and not the other, whereby their <i>reciprocal diversity</i>[[fn:1208]] might be established. This is a philosophical and (verily) demonstrative method, provided it be pursued[[fn:1209]] and its premises be set forth. I myself shall set it forth when I relate the opinions of the philosophers upon this matter. Yet this method cannot be followed according to the system of those who admit the attributes[[fn:1210]]; for, according to them, there be in the eternal Being[[fn:1211]] many things distinct from one another[[fn:1212]], the idea of knowledge being for them distinct from the idea of power, and likewise the idea of power distinct from the idea of will[[fn:1213]]. It would not, therefore, be impossible, under this system, that each of the two gods should contain several ideas, so that some he might have in common with the other (god), and by others he might differ therefrom.",
    "root.text.Part 1..74.4": "<small>THIRD METHOD</small>: There is yet another method which requirs one of the hypotheses admitted by the partisans of this system[[fn:1214]]. It is that several among them (the <i>Motécallemîn</i>) — and these are the most ancient — believe that God <i>wills</i> by a <i>will</i>, which is not an idea superadded to the essence of the Creator, but is a will without a <i>substratum</i>. According to this hypothesis[[fn:1215]] which we have just stated, but of which it is difficult, as thou seest, to form a conception[[fn:1216]], they say: The single will which is not in a <i>substratum</i> could not belong to two; for, they add, a single cause cannot produce two results for two (different) essences[[fn:1217]]. This is, as I have already remarked to thee, to explain an obscure thing by that which is yet more obscure[[fn:1218]]. Of this will of which they speak, one cannot form a conception; there be some among them who deem it impossible, and even they who admit it recognize therein innumerable difficulties[[fn:1219]]. And yet they take it for a proof of the unity (of God).",
    "root.text.Part 1..74.5": "<small>FOURTH METHOD</small>: The existence of an action, say they, proves of necessity that there is an agent, but proves not unto us that there be several agents, no matter whether it be claimed that God is two, or three, or twenty, or any number whatsoever[[fn:1220]]; which thing is clear and manifest. Now, if it were objected that this proof does not demonstrate that plurality in God is impossible, but only that it demonstrats that the number thereof is unknown, and that consequently it may equally be that there is <i>one</i> God or that there be <i>several</i>[[fn:1221]], such an one[[fn:1222]] would complete his demonstration by saying: “In the existence of God there is no <i>possibility</i>, but He is a <i>necessary</i> Being, and consequently the <i>possibility</i> of plurality (in God) is inadmissible.” — Such is the manner in which the author of this demonstration reasons; but the error therein is altogether evident. In sooth, it is in the very existence of God that there is no <i>possibility;</i> but, in the knowledge which we have of Him, there is indeed <i>possibility;</i> for to be possible unto science is a different thing from being possible in reality[[fn:1223]]. It might therefore be that God were neither <i>three</i>, as the Christians believe, nor <i>one</i>, as we ourselves believe[[fn:1224]]. — This is clear unto him who has learned to know in what manner conclusions result from premises[[fn:1225]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..74.6": "<small>FIFTH METHOD</small>: One of the moderns has claimed to have found a demonstrative method to (establish) <i>unity;</i> it is that which is called the method of <i>need</i>, of which this is the explanation: Either, says he, it was an easy thing for a single (god) to produce all that exists, and then a second would be superfluous and there would be no need of him; or else this universe could not be completed and ordered save by the two together, and then each of the two, having need of the other, would be affected with impotence and would not be self-sufficient. This is naught else than a branch (of the method) of <i>mutual hindrance</i>[[fn:1226]],",
    "root.text.Part 1..74.7": "And here is what one might object to this genre of argumentation: One is by no means called <i>powerless</i> who does not that which it is not in his nature to do; for we say not of a human individual that he is <i>weak</i> because he cannot move a thousand quintals, and we attribute no <i>impotence</i> unto God for not being able to become corporeal, nor to create His like, nor to create a square of which the side is equal[[fn:1227]] to the diagonal. Likewise, we cannot say that He is powerless because He should not create <i>alone;</i> for (according to the hypothesis) it would be within the conditions of the divine being that there should be two gods[[fn:1228]], and there would be no (mutual) <i>need</i> therein, but rather a <i>necessity</i>, and the contrary would be impossible[[fn:1229]]. Thus even as, according to their system, we cannot say that God is <i>powerless</i> for being unable to produce a body save by first creating atoms and uniting them by accidents which He creats therein, — a thing which we call neither <i>need</i> nor <i>impotence</i>, the contrary being impossible, — so likewise will the dualist[[fn:1230]] say: It is impossible that the one (of the two gods) should act alone, and this constituts no <i>impotence</i> in respect of either of the two, for unto their <i>being</i> it is an essential condition to be two[[fn:1231]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..74.8": "There were some among them who, being weary of inventing artifices, said that the unity of God must be accepted as a religious dogma[[fn:1232]]; but the <i>Motécallemîn</i> have strongly censured this and have shown contempt for him who said it. I, however, believe that he among them who said this was a man of a very upright mind, to whom it was repugnant to accept sophisms[[fn:1233]]; having therefore heard nothing in their words which was truly a demonstration, and his mind being in no wise tranquillised by that which they pretended to be one, he said that this was a thing which must be accepted as religious tradition. Verily, these people have recognised in nature no fixed law[[fn:1234]] whence a true argument might be drawn, nor have they conceded to the intelligence any innate rectitude[[fn:1235]] by means of which true conclusions might be formed. All this has been done with intention, so as to suppose a universe which allows us to demonstrate that which is not demonstrable; the result of which has been to render us incapable of demonstrating that which can be demonstrated. It remains only to appeal unto God and unto men of justice and intelligence[[fn:1236]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..75.0": "<small>OF INCORPOREALITY, ACCORDING TO THE SYSTEM OF THE MOTÉCALLEMÎN.</small> <br>The methods of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> and their argumentations to set aside corporeity (from God) are very weak, weaker than their proofs of unity; for, unto them, incorporeality is as a branch which is necessarily attached to unity as the stem: “The body,” say they, “is not <i>one</i>[[fn:1237]].” — As for him who rejects corporeity for the reason that the body is necessarily composed of matter and form [for that is a (veritable) composition, and it is evident that composition is impossible in the essence of God[[fn:1238]]], I consider him not as a <i>Motécallem;</i> for this proof is not based upon the principles of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, but it is a true demonstration, based upon the doctrine of <i>matter</i> and <i>form</i> and upon the conception of these two ideas[[fn:1239]]. This is a philosophical doctrine of which I shall speak further on, and which I shall set forth whilst relating the demonstrations of the philosophers upon this matter. In this chapter, our sole purpose is to relate the proofs of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> upon incorporeality, according to their propositions and their methods of argumentation.",
    "root.text.Part 1..75.1": "F<small>IRST METHOD</small>: If God, they say, were a body, it would necessarily follow, either that the true idea of divinity resided in all the (simple) substances of that body, I mean in each of its atoms, or else that it resided in but one of the atoms of that body[[fn:1240]]. Now, if it resided in a single atom, of what use would the other atoms be? The existence of that body would be without meaning. If (on the contrary) it resided in each of the atoms of that body, there would be many gods, and not one god alone; but it has already been set forth that there is but one. If thou examinest this demonstration, thou shalt find it based upon the I<sup>st</sup> and the V<sup>th</sup> of their propositions. But one might object to them[[fn:1241]]: that the body of God is not composed of indivisible parts, that is to say, that it is not composed of (small) substances similar to those which He creats, as ye say, but that it is a single and continuous body which is susceptible of division only in thought, and that one must not have regard unto <i>false ideas</i>[[fn:1242]]. It is thus, indeed, that thou imaginest that the body of the heaven is susceptible of being rent and parcelled, whilst the philosopher says that this is but the effect of the imagination and (that it is) judging by that which is visible [i.e., by the bodies which exist near us] of that which is invisible[[fn:1243]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..75.2": "T<small>HE SECOND METHOD</small>, which they consider very important, is (taken from) the <i>impossibility of similitude</i>. God (say they) cannot resemble any of His creatures; but, if He were a body, He would resemble other bodies. They enlarge much upon this subject, and they say (among other things): “If we said (that He is) <i>a body, yet not like other bodies</i>, it would be a contradiction[[fn:1244]]; for every body is similar to every other body in respect of corporeity, and bodies differ among themselves only in other respects, that is to say, in accidents.” It would follow further, according to them, that God would have created His like[[fn:1245]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..75.3": "This proof may be refuted[[fn:1246]] in two manners. First, one might object: «I do not grant the <i>non-similitude</i>, for how shall you demonstrate that God can in no wise resemble any of His creatures? Unless, by God[[fn:1247]]! you rely in this regard [I mean, concerning the negation of similitude] upon some text of a prophetic book; but then the incorporeality of God would be a thing received by tradition, and not an object of the intellect.» And if it were said that God, if He resembled aught among His creatures, would have created His like, the adversary might reply: «But it is not His like in every aspect; for I deny not that the divinity contains many ideas and presents itself under several aspects.» And indeed, he who believs in the corporeality of God contests not this.",
    "root.text.Part 1..75.4": "— A second manner, which is more profound[[fn:1248]], is this: It is established and proven for whosoever has studied philosophy and has immersed himself in the systems of the philosophers that, if the word <i>body</i> has been applied (at once) to the celestial spheres and to these <i>hylic</i>[[fn:1249]] bodies, it is but by simple <i>homonymy;</i> for there is therein identity neither of matter nor of form[[fn:1250]], but, on the contrary, it is likewise by homonymy that the words <i>matter</i> and <i>form</i> are applied to that which is here below and to the celestial spheres. If it be true that the celestial sphere has indubitably dimensions, it is not dimensions in themselves which constitute the <i>body</i>, but the latter is something composed of matter and form. Now, if this be said regarding the celestial sphere, how much more may he who admitts corporeality say it regarding God. He will say, in effect: God is a body having dimensions; but His essence, His true nature and His substance resemble naught among His creatures, and it is only by homonymy that the word <i>body</i> is applied to Him and to them, even as it is by homonymy that, according to true thinkers, the word <i>being</i> is applied to Him and to them. He who maintains corporeality grants not either that all bodies are composed of like atoms, but he says that God is the creator of all these bodies, which vary in substance and in nature; and even as for him the body of dung[[fn:1251]] is not (the same thing as) the body of the solar globe, even so he says that the body of the <i>created light</i>, I mean, of the <i>schekhînâ</i>[[fn:1252]], is not the body of the spheres and stars; nor is the body of the <i>schekhînâ</i>, or of the created <i>pillar of cloud</i>, according to him, the body of God. This body, he says on the contrary, is the perfect and sublime essence which was never composed, which never changed, and of which change is impossible; for, on the contrary, this body has ever necessarily existed such as it is, and it is He who does all that is outside of Him, according to His intention and His will. — I would fain know how this system, however infirm it may be, could be refuted by means of their astonishing methods which I have made known unto thee!",
    "root.text.Part 1..75.5.sub_0": "<small>THE THIRD METHOD</small> is this: If God, say they, were a body, He would be <i>finite</i> [which is true], and if He were finite, He would have a certain measure and a certain determinate figure [which is likewise a true consequence]. Now, say they, whatever be the measure and the figure (one supposs), God, inasmuch as He is a <i>body</i>, could be greater or smaller than that measure and have a figure different from that one; if therefore He be determined by a certain measure and a certain figure, there was required for this a <i>determining being</i>[[fn:1253]]. — This demonstration also I have heard extolled by them, although it be weaker than all that preceds, being based upon the X<sup>th</sup> proposition, of which we have already exposed all the difficulties that it presents even with regard to other beings [since it is supposed they might have a nature different from that which they have[[fn:1254]]], and how much more so with regard to God. There is no difference between this reasoning and what they say concerning the <i>preference</i> accorded to the existence of the world over its non-existence, and which (according to them) would prove that there is an agent who has <i>preferred</i> its existence over its non-existence, both being possible[[fn:1255]]. Now, if one were to ask them: why does this not apply to God, and why say we not that, since He exists, there must be a being who has preferred His existence to His non-existence? they would doubtless answer: it is because that would lead to an (infinite) chain, and that it would be necessary to stop at a <i>necessary</i> being, in whom there is no <i>possibility</i> and who has no need of another being to bring Him into existence. But this same answer may be applied to figure and to measure. In effect, that figures and measures should be of but <i>possible</i> existence, they must apply to a being who exists after not having existed, and of such a being only can it be said[[fn:1256]]: it was possible that he were greater or smaller than he really is and that he had a figure different from that which he has, so that he had absolute need of a <i>determining</i> being.",
    "root.text.Part 1..75.5.sub_1": "But as touching the figure and the measure of God [far from Him be all imperfection and assimilation!], the partisan of corporeality will say: «It was not after not having existed that He arrived at existence, so that He should have had need of a <i>determining</i> being. On the contrary, His essence with the measure and the figure which it has is thus of a <i>necessary</i> existence; it has had no need of a being who should <i>determine</i> it nor who should <i>prefer</i>[[fn:1257]] its existence over its non-existence, for there is in it no possibility of non-existence; and likewise it has had no need of a being who should give it a determinate figure and measure, for its existence was necessary in this manner[[fn:1258]].»",
    "root.text.Part 1..75.6": "And now, O reader! if thou preferrest the quest of truth, if thou castest far from thee passion, belief upon authority, and prejudice for that which thou wast wont to respect, and if thou wilt not deceive thyself, behold to what these thinkers are reduced, what has befallen them, and what has proceeded from them[[fn:1259]]; for they are[[fn:1260]] as one who escaps from burning ashes (only to fall) into the fire[[fn:1261]]. Indeed, they have effaced every natural law[[fn:1262]] and altered the nature of heaven and earth, by pretending that by these propositions one can demonstrate that the world is <i>created</i>. But, far from having demonstrated the creation of the world, they have destroyed for us the demonstrations of the existence, the unity, and the incorporeality of God; for the demonstrations by which all this becoms clear can be taken only from the nature of being, such as it is established, visible, and perceived by the senses and the intellect.",
    "root.text.Part 1..75.7": "Having fully summarized their discourses[[fn:1263]], we shall likewise report the propositions of the philosophers and their demonstrations establishing that God <i>exists</i>, that He is <i>one</i>, and that it is impossible for Him to be a body; and this, by granting them first the hypothesis of the eternity of the universe, albeit we ourselves admit it not[[fn:1264]]. Thereafter, I shall show thee the method which, guided by true speculation, I have myself adopted to complete the demonstration of these three questions[[fn:1265]]; and finally, by the help of the Almighty, I shall return to engage with the philosophers concerning what they have said of the eternity of the universe[[fn:1266]].",
    "root.text.Part 1..75.8": "END OF THE FIRST PART <br>OF THE GUIDE FOR THE PERPLEXED.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.0": "[[fn:1342]]It follows from the twenty-fifth proposition that there is a motor which has set in motion the matter of that which is generated and perishes[[fn:1343]], that it might receive form; and, if one seeks what has set in motion this proximate motor, it must needs be that another motor be found for it in its turn, whether of its own kind or of another: for motion is found in the four categories unto which the term <i>motion</i> is generally applied, even as we have stated in the fourth proposition. Yet this cannot continue ad infinitum, as we have said in the third proposition. Now, we find that all motion here below leads to the motion of the fifth body, whereat it stays[[fn:1344]]. It is from this last motion that all which in the whole inferior world imparts motion and disposs unto the reception of form is derived, and unto it, by a chain of sequence, it returns[[fn:1345]]. The celestial sphere has the motion of translation, which is antecedent to all motions, as has been said in the fourteenth proposition. Likewise, all locomotion here below leads to the motion of the celestial sphere. One may say, for example, that as for this stone which movs, it is the staff which has set it in motion; the staff was moved by the hand, the hand by the tendons, the tendons were moved[[fn:1346]] by the muscles, the muscles by the nerves, the nerves by the natural heat, and this last finally was moved by the form within it[[fn:1347]], which, undoubtedly, is the prime motor. That which has moved this motor to act would have been, for instance, an <i>opinion</i>[[fn:1348]], to wit, to cause this stone, by thrusting it with the staff, to reach a skylight, so as to stop it up, that the wind which blows might not penetrate thereby unto him. Now, that which movs this wind and produces[[fn:1349]] its breath is the motion of the celestial sphere; and thus shalt thou find that every cause of generation and corruption returns to the motion of the celestial sphere[[fn:1350]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.1": "When, by our contemplation, we have at last arrived at this sphere, which is likewise moved, it behoves, say we, that it should have in its turn a motor, according to what has been said in the seventeenth proposition. Its motor can only be either within it or without it; and this is a necessary alternative. If it be without it, it must necessarily be either corporeal or incorporeal; in the latter case, however, one would not say that it is <i>without</i> it, but rather that it is <i>separate</i> from it: for of that which is incorporeal, it is only by extension that it is said to be <i>without</i> the body[[fn:1351]]. If its motor, I mean that of the sphere, be within it, it can only be either a force diffused throughout its whole body and divisible together with the latter, like heat in fire, or else a force situated within it but indivisible, like the soul and the intellect, as has been said in the tenth proposition[[fn:1352]]. Consequently, the motor of the celestial sphere shall necessarily be one of these four things: either another body without it, or a <i>separate</i> being, or a force diffused within it, or an indivisible force.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.2": "The first case, which assums as the motor of the celestial sphere another body without it, is untenable, as I shall presently show. Inasmuch as, being a body, it shall itself be moved whilst imparting motion, as has been said in the ninth proposition; now, as this sixth body[[fn:1353]] shall likewise be moved in communicating motion, it will be necessary that a seventh body move it, and this one again shall be moved in its turn. It would follow, therefore, that there exist bodies of an infinite number, and that it is thereby that the celestial sphere movs. But this is untenable, as has been said in the second proposition.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.3": "The third case, which supposs as the motor of the celestial sphere a force diffused within it, is likewise inadmissible, as I shall show. For the sphere, being a body, is necessarily finite, as results from the first proposition; its force will therefore likewise be finite, as the twelfth says, and it shall be divided by the division of the body, as the eleventh says[[fn:1354]]. It cannot therefore impart a motion which, as we have laid down in the twenty-sixth proposition, would be infinite[[fn:1355]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.4": "As for the fourth case, which supposs as the motor of the celestial sphere an indivisible force which should be within it, as for example the human soul is within man, it is likewise inadmissible that this motor alone be the cause of perpetual motion, albeit it be an indivisible force[[fn:1356]]. For, were it the first motor thereof, this motor nevertheless would be moved itself <i>accidentally</i>[[fn:1357]], as has been said in the sixth proposition; but I add here an explanation[[fn:1358]]. When, for example, man is moved by his soul, which is his form, to ascend from the house to the upper chamber[[fn:1359]], it is his body which is moved <i>essentially</i>, and the soul is the first <i>essential</i> motor. But the latter is moved <i>accidentally:</i> for when the body transports itself from the house to the chamber, the soul, which was in the house, likewise transports itself and is found thereafter in the chamber[[fn:1360]]. Nevertheless, when the soul ceass to move, that which is moved by it, to wit, the body, is likewise found at rest, and (in its turn), by the rest of the body, the <i>accidental</i> motion which had happened unto the soul ceass[[fn:1361]]. Now, all that is moved accidentally will necessarily be at rest, as has been said in the eighth (proposition); and when it shall be at rest, that which is moved by it shall likewise be so. It is therefore necessary that this first motor have another cause, outside of the whole composed of a motor and a thing moved; if this cause, which is the principle of motion, be present, the first motor which is in this whole will set in motion the moved part; but if it be absent, the latter will be at rest. Wherefore the bodies of animals do not move continually, although there be in each of them an indivisible first motor: for their motor does not move continually by its essence, but on the contrary, that which leads it to produce motion are things outside of it, whether (the desire) of seeking what is fitting for it or of fleeing what is contrary to it, or an imagination, or a conception, in (those beings) which have conception[[fn:1362]]. It is by this alone that it movs, and, in giving motion[[fn:1363]], it is itself moved <i>accidentally;</i> it will therefore necessarily return to rest, as we have said. — Consequently, if the motor of the celestial sphere were found within it in this manner, it would not be possible for it to have a perpetual motion[[fn:1364]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.5": "If therefore this motion be continual and eternal, as our adversary has said[[fn:1365]], — which is possible, as has been said in the thirteenth proposition, — it will be necessary, according to this opinion, to admit for the first cause of the motion of the celestial sphere the second case, to wit, that it is <i>separate</i> from the sphere, and even so does the (previous) division require[[fn:1366]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.6": "It is therefore demonstrated that the first motor of the celestial sphere, if the latter have an eternal and continual motion, can in no wise be either a body or a force in a body; so that this motor has no motion, neither essential nor accidental[[fn:1367]], and that by reason thereof it is also not susceptible either of division or of change, as has been said in the seventh and in the fifth propositions. And this is God — glorified be His name! — I mean, (that He is) the first cause which setts in motion the celestial sphere.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.7": "It is inadmissible that it should be <i>two</i> or more: for separated <i>things</i>, which are not corporeal, do not admit of numeration, unless (in this sense) they are causes and effects one of the other, as was said in the sixteenth (proposition). It is also clear that, since movement is not applicable to it, it does not fall under time, as was said in the fifteenth.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.8": "This speculation has therefore led us to establish, by a demonstration, that the celestial sphere[[fn:1368]] cannot give unto itself perpetual motion[[fn:1369]]; that the First Cause which imparts motion thereto is neither a body nor a force residing in a body; and that it is <i>one</i> and not subject to change, for its existence is not bound by time. These be the three questions which the most excellent among the philosophers have determined by way of demonstration.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.9": "<small>SECOND SPECULATION</small> of these same philosophers. — Aristotle has first laid down as a principle that if a thing be found composed of two distinct elements, and one of these elements exists separately without the composite thing, it is necessary that the other also exists separately without that composite thing; for, if it were a necessary condition of their existence that they exist only together[[fn:1370]], as is the case with matter and physical form, neither of the two could in any wise exist without the other. Thus, therefore, the separate existence of one of the two being a proof of their mutual independence[[fn:1371]], it necessarily follows that the other likewise shall exist separately. If, for example, oxymel exists, and at the same time honey exists alone, it necessarily follows that vinegar also exists alone. — After having set forth this proposition, he says: We find many things composed of a mover and of that which is moved—that is to say, which move another thing and, while imparting motion, are themselves moved by something else; this is manifest in all things intermediate in motion[[fn:1372]]. But we find also a thing moved which movs not at all, and this is the last moved thing[[fn:1373]]; consequently, it is necessary that there should also exist a mover that is not moved, and this is the First Mover[[fn:1374]]. — Since, then, motion in Him is impossible, He is neither divisible, nor a body, nor does He fall under the category of time, as has been explained in the preceding demonstration.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.10": "<small>THIRD PHILOSOPHICAL SPECULATION</small> upon this subject, borrowed from the words of Aristotle, albeit he produced it for another purpose[[fn:1375]]. — Here is the sequence of the reasoning: it cannot be doubted that there be things which exist, and these are the beings perceived by the senses. One can admit concerning beings only three cases, and this is a necessary division: either that all beings neither come into existence nor perish; or that all of them come into existence and perish[[fn:1376]]; or that in part they come into existence and perish, and in part they neither come into existence nor perish. The first case is evidently inadmissible; for we see many beings which come into existence and perish. The second case is likewise inadmissible, as I shall explain: Indeed, if every being were subject to generation and corruption, each and every being would have the possibility of perishing; but what is possible for the species cannot but necessarily happen, as thou knowest[[fn:1377]]. It would thence follow that <i>all</i> of them would have necessarily perished, I mean all beings. Now, after they had all perished, it would have been impossible that anything should exist; for there would have remained nothing that could bring anything into existence; whence it would follow that, in effect, there exists absolutely nothing. Nevertheless, we see things which exist, and we ourselves exist. — It follows therefore necessarily from this speculation that, if there be beings which come into existence and perish, as we perceive, there must also be some being which neither comes into existence nor perishes. In this being which neither comes into existence nor perishes, there shall be absolutely no possibility of perishing; on the contrary, it shall be of a necessary existence, and not of a possible existence[[fn:1378]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.11": "— It has been further said[[fn:1379]]: The <i>necessary</i> being can be such only in respect of itself, or else in respect of its cause; so that in this latter case it might, in respect of itself, exist or not exist, whilst it will be of a necessary existence in respect of its cause, and its cause, consequently, will be the true necessary being, as was stated in the nineteenth proposition. It is therefore demonstrated that there must necessarily exist a being whose existence is necessary in respect of itself, and that without it there would exist absolutely nothing, neither that which would be subject to generation and corruption, nor that which would not be so—if indeed there be anything that exists in this latter manner, as Aristotle maintains[[fn:1380]], I mean something not subject to generation and corruption, being the effect of a cause whose existence is necessary. — This is a demonstration which admitts neither doubt, nor refutation[[fn:1381]], nor contradiction, save for him who is ignorant of the demonstrative method.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.12": "We say further: The existence of every being necessary in itself must necessarily have no cause, as was stated in the twentieth[[fn:1382]] proposition; there will be in it absolutely no multiplicity of ideas, as was stated in the twenty-first proposition, whence it follows that it will be neither a body nor a force residing in a body, as was stated in the twenty-second. It is therefore demonstrated, by this speculation, that there is a being which, by its own very essence, is of a necessary existence, and it is that whose existence has no cause, in which there is no composition, and which, for that reason, is neither a body nor a force in a body; this being is God [may His name be glorified!].",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.13": "In like manner, it may be readily demonstrated that it is inadmissible for <i>necessary</i> existence, in relation to the essence itself, to appertain to two (beings): for the <i>species</i> of necessary being would then be an idea superadded to the essence of each of the two, and neither of the two would any longer be a necessary being solely by its own essence; but rather by that singular idea which constitutes the <i>species</i> of being and which would appertain to both[[fn:1383]]. — It may be demonstrated by various means that in the necessary being there can exist no <i>duality</i> whatsoever, neither by similitude nor by contrariety[[fn:1384]]; the rationale for all this resides in the pure simplicity and absolute perfection (of this being), — which permits no room outside of its essence for anything whatsoever of its species[[fn:1385]], — as well as in the total absence of any <i>cause</i>[[fn:1386]]. There is consequently no association (within it).",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.14": "F<small>OURTH SPECULATION</small>, likewise philosophical[[fn:1387]]. — It is well known that we continually behold things which are (at first) in potentiality and pass into actuality. Now, whatsoever passes from potentiality into actuality has outside itself some thing which causs it so to pass, as has been said in the eighteenth proposition. It is likewise manifest that this agent was at first an agent in potentiality before being such in actuality[[fn:1388]]; and the reason why it was at first but in potentiality is either in an obstacle (proceeding) from itself, or in (the absence of) a certain relation initially lacking between it and the thing which it has caused to pass (into actuality), so that, when this relation existed, it did verily cause it to pass (into actuality). Each of these two cases necessarily required (in its turn) an agent, or something which might cause the obstacle to cease; and the same must be said of the second agent, or of that which caused the obstacle to cease. But, as this cannot extend to infinity, it is necessary to arrive at something which causs to pass from potentiality into actuality, whilst always existing in the same state, and without there being in it any <i>potentiality</i> whatsoever, I mean without its having in its very essence any thing (which is) in potentiality: for, if there were in its essence any <i>possibility</i>, it might cease to exist, as has been said in the twenty-third (proposition). It is likewise inadmissible that this being should have matter; but, on the contrary, it shall be <i>separate</i>[[fn:1389]], as has been said in the twenty-fourth (proposition). This <i>separate</i> being, in which there is absolutely no possibility, but which exists (in actuality) by its essence, is God[[fn:1390]]. Finally, it is manifest that, being in no wise a body, He is <i>one</i>, as has been said in the sixteenth proposition.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.15": "These are all demonstrative methods for (establishing) the existence of one God, who is neither a body nor a force in a body, (and this) while yet admitting the eternity of the world.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.16": "There is yet another <i>demonstrative method</i> for removing corporeity and establishing the unity (of God)[[fn:1391]]: it is that, if there were two gods, it would necessarily follow that they should have somewhat in common, — to wit, that thing by which each of the two deserved to be (called) <i>God</i>, — and some other thing likewise necessary, whereby their reciprocal distinction might take place and whereby they should be <i>two</i>. But then, if each of the two had somewhat which the other had not, each would be composed of two notions, neither of the two would be either <i>first cause</i> or <i>a being necessary by itself</i>, and each would have causes, as has been set forth in the nineteenth (proposition). If, on the contrary, the distinguishing thing were found only in one of the two, he that possessed these two things would by no means be a being necessary by himself.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.17.sub_0": "<i>Another method for</i> (<i>establishing</i>) <i>the unity</i>. — It has been established by demonstration that the whole universe is as a single individual, whose parts are linked one to another, and that the forces of the celestial sphere are diffused throughout this inferior matter and <i>dispose</i> it[[fn:1392]]. This being established, it is inadmissible that one god should isolate himself with one of the parts of this being, and that a second god should isolate himself with another part: for they are linked one to the other. There remains, therefore, no other partition to be made, unless it be that one (of the two gods) should act at one time and the other at another time, or else that they should always both act together, so that no action could be accomplished save by both together. — To suppose that one acts at one time and the other at another time is impossible for several reasons. Indeed, if it were possible that, during the time when one of the two acts, the other should act likewise, what would be the cause which would make one to act and the other to be idle? If, on the contrary, it were impossible for one of the two to act at the same time as the other acts, this would suppose another cause which would have made it possible for the one to act, while for the other it was impossible: for in time itself there is no difference, and the substratum of the action also is <i>one</i> alone whose parts are linked one to another, as we have set forth. Furthermore, each of the two would fall under time: for his action would be bound to time. Thence, each of the two would pass from potentiality into actuality at the moment when he should act, so that each would stand in need of somewhat to make him pass from potentiality into actuality. Finally, there would be in the essence of each a <i>possibility</i>. — But to suppose that they always both operate together everything that is done in the universe, so that one acts not without the other, is likewise an impossible thing, as I shall explain. In effect, whenever a certain action cannot be accomplished save by a collective (of individuals), no individual of that collective is an absolute agent by his essence, and none is the first cause for the action in question; but, on the contrary, the first cause is the union of the whole. But it has been demonstrated that the Necessary Being must be absolutely devoid of <i>cause</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.17.sub_1": "Furthermore, the unification of the whole is (in itself) an act which, in its turn, requirs another cause; and it is this cause which units the whole. Now, if that which has unified this whole, without which the action cannot be accomplished, be a unique (being), it is undoubtedly God. If, on the contrary, that which has unified this whole be in its turn another whole, it will be necessary for this second whole that which was required for the first. One must perforce arrive at a unique being, who shall be the cause of the existence of this unique universe, in whatever manner the latter exists: whether it be by a creation <i>ex nihilo</i>, or by necessity[[fn:1393]]. — It is therefore likewise manifest, by this method, that the unity of the entire universe proves unto us that its author is <i>one</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.18": "<i>Another method for</i> (<i>establishing</i>) <i>incorporeity</i>[[fn:1394]]. — 1° Every body is composite, as has been said in the twenty-second (proposition), and every composite necessarily presupposs an efficient cause, which is the cause of the existence of its form within its matter. 2° It is also perfectly manifest that every body is divisible and has dimensions, and that, consequently, it is indubitably a substratum for accidents. Thus, therefore, a body cannot be <i>one</i>, on the one hand, because it is divisible, and on the other, because it is composite—I mean, because there is a <i>duality</i> in the very term (of <i>body</i>)[[fn:1395]]: for every body is only such a determined body by an idea added to the idea of <i>body</i> in general, and, consequently, there are necessarily in it two ideas. But it has already been demonstrated that in the Necessary Being, there is composition in no wise.",
    "root.text.Part 2..0.19": "Having first recounted these demonstrations, we shall begin to set forth our own method[[fn:1396]], even as we have promised.",
    "root.text.Part 2..1.0": "This fifth body, which is the celestial sphere, must perforce be either something which is generated and perishes [and it shall be the same with motion], or else something which is neither generated nor perishes, as the adversary says[[fn:1397]]. Now, if the celestial sphere be a thing which is generated and perishes, that which has caused it to exist after non-existence is God [glorified be His name!]; this is a primary notion, for whatsoever exists after not having existed necessarily presupposs something that has called it into existence, and it is inadmissible that it should have brought itself into existence. If, on the contrary, this sphere has never ceased and shall never cease to move thus by a perpetual and eternal motion, it must needs be, by virtue of the preceding propositions, that that which imparts unto it eternal motion be neither a body nor a force within a body; and this shall still be God [glorified be His name!]. It is therefore manifest that the existence of God [a Necessary Being, without cause, and whose existence is in itself exempt from all <i>possibility</i>] is demonstrated by decisive and certain proofs[[fn:1398]], no matter whether the world be a creation <i>ex nihilo</i>, or whether it be not. Likewise, it is established by demonstrations that He (God) is <i>one</i> and incorporeal, as we have previously stated: for the demonstration of His unity and His incorporeity remains established, whether the world be, or be not, a creation <i>ex nihilo</i>, as we have set forth in the third of the philosophical methods[[fn:1399]], and as we have (afterwards) separately set forth the incorporeity and the unity by philosophical methods[[fn:1400]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..1.1": "It has seemed good unto me to complete the theories of the philosophers by setting forth their proofs for the existence of the <i>separate intelligences</i>, and to show that they are in agreement, in this matter[[fn:1401]], with the principles of our religion: I mean to speak of the existence of the <i>angels</i>. After having concluded this subject, I shall return to the argument which I promised concerning the <i>novelty of the world:</i> for our principal proofs thereupon shall only be solid and clear after one shall have known the existence of the <i>separate intelligences</i> and the proofs upon which it rests[[fn:1402]]. But, before all this, I must make a preliminary observation, which shall be a beacon to enlighten the obscurities of this entire treatise, as well of the chapters which have preceded as of those which shall follow, and this observation is as follows:",
    "root.text.Part 2..1.2": "<small>PRELIMINARY OBSERVATION</small>. <br>Know that, in this treatise, I have not had for my purpose to compose (a work) upon physical science, any more than to analyse according to certain systems the subjects of metaphysical science, or to reproduce the demonstrations of which they have been the object[[fn:1403]]. Neither have I aimed therein to summarise and abridge (the science of) the disposition of the celestial spheres, nor to make known the number of the latter; for the books which have been composed upon all these matters are sufficient, and should they be insufficient for any subject whatsoever, that which I might say myself upon such a subject would be of no more worth than all that has been said. My purpose in this treatise has been none other than that which I made known unto thee in the introduction thereof[[fn:1404]]; namely, to explain the obscurities of the Law and to manifest the true meanings of its allegories, which are above the reach of common understandings. Wherefore, when thou shalt see me speak of the existence[[fn:1405]] and the number of the <i>Separate Intelligences</i>, or of the number of the spheres and the causes of their motions, or of the true notion of matter and form, or of what is to be understood by the divine <i>emanation</i>[[fn:1406]], or of other similar matters, thou must not believe for a single moment[[fn:1407]] that my sole aim has been to examine this philosophical subject; for these subjects have been treated in many books, and the truth thereof has, for the most part, been demonstrated. But my aim is only to report that the understanding of which may serve to clear up certain obscurities of the Law, and to set forth briefly such a subject by the knowledge of which many difficulties may be resolved[[fn:1408]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..1.3": "Thou knowest already, from the introduction to this treatise, that it turns principally[[fn:1409]] upon the explanation of what it is possible to understand of the <i>Ma’asé beréshîth</i> (Account of the Creation) and of the <i>Ma’asé mercabâ</i> (Account of the Chariot)[[fn:1410]], and upon the elucidation of the obscurities inherent in prophecy and in the knowledge of God. Whenever, in any chapter whatsoever, thou shalt see me approach the explanation of a subject which has already been <i>demonstrated</i> either in physical science or in metaphysical science, or which has only been presented as that which is most admissible, or a subject which is connected with what has been set forth in mathematics, — thou shalt know that this subject is necessarily a key for the understanding of some certain thing in the prophetic books, I mean of their allegories and their mysteries, and that it is for this reason that I have mentioned and clearly set it forth, as being useful either for the knowledge of the <i>Ma’asé mercabâ</i> and the <i>Ma’asé beréshîth</i>, or for the explanation of a principle relating to prophecy or to any true opinion whatsoever which one ought to admit in religious beliefs.",
    "root.text.Part 2..1.4": "After this preliminary observation, I return to fulfil the task which I had imposed upon myself[[fn:1411]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..2.0": "Know that the opinions which Aristotle propounds concerning the causes of the motion of the spheres, and from which he has concluded that there exist <i>Separate Intelligences</i>, although they be hypotheses not susceptible of a demonstration, are nevertheless, among the opinions that may be uttered, those which are least subject to doubt and which present themselves with the greatest method[[fn:1412]], as Alexander says in (his treatise) <i>The Principles of All Things</i>[[fn:1413]]. These are also statements which, as I shall set forth, are in accord with many of those of the Law, especially according to the explanation of the most celebrated <i>Midrashim</i>, which, without doubt, belong to our Sages. Wherefore, I shall cite his opinions[[fn:1414]] and his proofs, in order to choose from them that which is in accord with the Law and conformable to the statements of the Sages [may their memory be blessed!].",
    "root.text.Part 2..3.0.sub_0": "That the celestial sphere is endowed with a soul, is what becoms clear when one well examins (the matter)[[fn:1415]]. That which causs him who hears this to believe it a thing difficult to understand, or to reject it utterly, is that he imagins that, when we say <i>endowed with a soul</i>, it concerns a soul like that of man, or like that of the ass and the ox. But such is not the meaning of these words, by which it is intended only to say that its local motion[[fn:1416]] proves that it has indubitably within it a principle by which it movs, and this principle, without any doubt, is a <i>soul</i>. In truth, — to explain myself more clearly, — it is inadmissible that its circular motion should be like the straight motion of a stone downwards, or of fire upwards, so that the principle of this motion should be a <i>nature</i>[[fn:1417]], and not a <i>soul;</i> for that which has this <i>natural</i> motion, the principle which is within it movs it solely to seek its (natural) place, when it finds itself in another place, so that it remains at rest as soon as it has arrived at its place[[fn:1418]], whilst this celestial sphere movs circularly (remaining ever) in the same place. But it suffices not that it be endowed with a soul, that it should necessarily move thus; for everything which is endowed with a soul movs only by a <i>nature</i>, or by a <i>conception</i>. — By <i>nature</i> I mean here (that which leads the animal) to direct itself towards that which suits it and to flee that which is contrary to it, no matter whether that which thus setts it in motion be without it, — as, for example, when the animal fles the heat of the sun, and, being thirsty, seeks the place of water, — or whether its mover be the imagination [for the animal movs also by the mere imagination of that which is contrary to it and that which suits it]. — Now, this sphere movs not at all for the purpose of fleeing that which is contrary to it or of seeking that which suits it; for the point towards which it movs is also its point of departure, and every point of departure is also the point towards which it movs. Furthermore, if its motion had that purpose, it would be necessary that, having arrived at the point towards which it movs, it should remain at rest; for if it moved to seek or to flee something, without ever attaining it, the motion would be in vain.",
    "root.text.Part 2..3.0.sub_1": "Its circular motion cannot, therefore, take place save by virtue of a certain <i>conception</i>[[fn:1419]], which constrains it to move in such a manner. Now, conception takes not place save by means of an <i>intellect</i>; the celestial sphere, consequently, is endowed with an intellect[[fn:1420]]. But everything that possesses an intellect, whereby it conceivs a certain idea, and a soul, whereby it becoms possible for it to move, does not (necessarily) move when it conceivs (an idea); for conception alone does not necessitate motion. This has been set forth in the <i>First Philosophy</i> (or in the <i>Metaphysics</i>), and it is also manifest (in itself). Verily, thou shalt find of thyself that thou conceivest many things toward which thou art likewise able to move, and yet thou movest not toward them until there necessarily supervens a desire (which impells thee) toward that thing which thou hast conceived, and then only dost thou move to obtain what thou hast conceived. It is therefore clear that neither the <i>soul</i> whereby the motion is effected, nor the <i>intellect</i> whereby the thing is conceived, suffices to produce the motion in question until there be joined thereto a <i>desire</i> for the thing conceived. It follows thence that the celestial sphere has the desire (to approach) that which it has conceived, to wit, the beloved object, which is God [exalted be His name!]. It is from this point of view that it has been said that God setts the celestial sphere in motion, that is to say, that the sphere desirs to assimilate itself unto the object of its perception[[fn:1421]], and this is that thing conceived (by it), which is of an extreme simplicity, in which there occurrs absolutely no change, nor any new situation, and from which goodness continually flows. But the celestial sphere, inasmuch as it is a body, cannot accomplish this except because its action is circular motion, and not otherwise [for the highest point of perfection that a body can attain is to have a perpetual action]. This is the simplest motion that a body can possess, and (this being so) there occurrs no change, neither in its essence, nor in the outpouring of the benefits which result from its motion[[fn:1422]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..3.1": "Aristotle, having recognized all this, applied himself to a new investigation, whereby he found demonstratively (that there are) numerous spheres, whose respective motions differ one from another in velocity, in slowness, and in direction[[fn:1423]], albeit they all have circular motion in common. This physical study led him to believe that the thing which such a sphere conceivs, so as to accomplish its rapid motion in one day, must necessarily differ from that which another sphere conceivs, which accomplishes a single motion in thirty years[[fn:1424]]. He has therefore decidedly concluded that there are <i>Separate Intelligences</i>, equal in number to that of the spheres, that each of the spheres feels a desire for that intelligence which is its principle, and that the latter imparts to it the motion which is peculiar to it, so that a (certain) determined intelligence setts a certain sphere in motion. Neither Aristotle, nor any other[[fn:1425]], has decided that the intelligences are ten or a hundred in number; but he has said that they are of the same number as the spheres. Now, as it was believed in his time that the spheres were fifty in number, Aristotle says: “If it be so, the <i>Separate Intelligences</i> are fifty in number[[fn:1426]].” For mathematical knowledge was rare in his time and had not yet reached perfection; it was believed that for every motion a sphere was required, and it was not known that the inclination of a single sphere gave rise to several visible motions, as, for example, the motion in longitude, the motion in declination, and also the (apparent) motion seen upon the circle of the horizon, in the <i>amplitude</i> of the risings and settings[[fn:1427]]. But this is not our aim, and we return to our subject[[fn:1428]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..3.2": "If the modern philosophers have said that the Intelligences are <i>ten</i> in number, it is because they have counted the globes containing stars and the <i>surrounding</i> sphere, although some of these globes contain several spheres. Now, they have counted nine globes, (to wit) the sphere which surrounds all, that of the fixed stars, and the spheres of the seven planets. As for the tenth Intelligence, it is the <i>Active Intellect</i>, the existence of which is proved by our intellects passing from potentiality to actuality, and by the forms supervening upon beings that are born and perish, after they have been in their matter only in potentiality[[fn:1429]]. For everything that passes from potentiality to actuality necessarily has outside of itself something which causs it so to pass; and it behoves that this agent be of the same species as the thing upon which it acts[[fn:1430]]. Indeed, the carpenter fashions not the chest because he is an artisan, but because he has in his mind the <i>form</i> of the chest; and it is the form of the chest, in the mind of the carpenter, which has caused the (objective) form of the chest to pass into actuality and to supervene upon the wood. Likewise, without any doubt, that which gives the form is a <i>separate</i> form, and that which gives existence to the intellect is an intellect, to wit, the <i>Active Intellect;</i> so that the Active Intellect is to the elements and to that which is composed thereof, what each <i>separate intelligence</i>, belonging to any sphere whatever, is to that sphere[[fn:1431]], and the role of the <i>intellect in act</i> existing within us, which is emanated from the <i>Active Intellect</i> and whereby we perceive the latter, is the same as that of the intellect existing in each sphere, which is emanated from the separate intelligence, and whereby it (the sphere) perceivs the <i>separate intelligence</i>, conceivs it, desirs to assimilate itself thereto, and thus arrivs at motion.",
    "root.text.Part 2..3.3": "It follows also for him[[fn:1432]] that which has already been demonstrated, to wit: that God does not do things by contact; when (for example) He burns[[fn:1433]], it is by the mediation of fire, and the latter is moved by the mediation of the motion of the celestial sphere, which in its turn is moved by the mediation of a <i>Separate Intelligence</i>. The Intelligences are therefore the <i>angels that draw near</i> (unto God)[[fn:1434]], and by whose mediation the spheres are set in motion. Now, as separate Intelligences are not susceptible of being numbered in respect of the diversity of their essences,—for they are incorporeal,—it follows that, according to him (Aristotle), it is God who produced the first Intelligence, which setts the first sphere in motion, in the manner we have set forth; the Intelligence which setts the second sphere in motion has for its cause and principle only the first Intelligence, and so forth[[fn:1435]]; so that the Intelligence which setts in motion the sphere nigh unto us[[fn:1436]] is the cause and principle of the <i>Active Intellect</i>. The latter is the last of the separate Intelligences[[fn:1437]], even as bodies also, beginning with the superior sphere, end with the elements and with that which is composed of them. It could not be admitted that the Intelligence which setts the superior sphere in motion is itself the (absolute) <i>Necessary</i> Being; for, as it has one thing in common with the other Intelligences, to wit, the setting in motion of the (respective) bodies, and as they all are distinguished one from another by another thing, each of the ten is (composed) of two things[[fn:1438]], and, consequently, there must be a first cause for the whole.",
    "root.text.Part 2..3.4": "Such are the words of Aristotle and his opinion. His proofs[[fn:1439]] concerning these things have been set forth, as far as they can be[[fn:1440]], in the books of his successors. That which results from all his words is that all the celestial spheres are living bodies, possessing a soul and an intellect; that they conceive and perceive God, and that they also perceive their principles[[fn:1441]]; finally, that there exist <i>Separate Intelligences</i>, absolutely incorporeal, which all are emanated from God, and which are the intermediaries between God and all these (celestial) bodies.",
    "root.text.Part 2..3.5": "— And now I will set forth unto thee, in the ensuing chapters, that which our Law contains, whether in conformity with or in opposition to these opinions.",
    "root.text.Part 2..4.0": "That the celestial spheres are living and <i>rational</i>, that is to say, (beings) which <i>perceive</i>, is that which is also proclaimed by the Law a thing true and certain; (that is to say) that they are not dead bodies, like fire and earth, as the ignorant suppose, but that they are, as the philosophers assert, animated beings, obeying their master, praising and glorifying Him in the most signal manner[[fn:1442]]. It has been stated: <i>The heavens declare the glory of God</i>, <i>etc.</i>. (Ps. <small>XIX</small>, 2); and how far would he be removed from the apprehension of truth, who should believe this to be a mere metaphor[[fn:1443]]! for the Hebrew tongue could not have employed simultaneously the verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">הגּיד</span> (to announce) and <span dir=\"rtl\">ספּר</span> (to relate) save when speaking of a Being endowed with intelligence. That which proves with evidence that (the Psalmist) here speaks of something inherent in themselves, that is to say, in the spheres, and not of something which men attribute unto them[[fn:1444]], is that he says: <i>There is no speech</i>, <i>nor language; their voice is not heard</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, v. 4); he therefore clearly expresses that he speaks of them themselves, (by stating) that they praise God and relate His wonders without the language of lips and of tongue. And this is the truth; for he who praises by speech merely announces that which he has conceived, but it is in this conception itself that true praise consists, and, if one expresses it, it is to impart knowledge thereof to others, or to manifest that one has oneself attained a certain perception. It has been said (in this regard): <i>Commune</i> (<i>meditate</i>) <i>with your own heart</i> <i>upon your bed</i>, <i>and be still</i> (Ps. <small>IV</small>, 5), as one has already expounded[[fn:1445]]. This proof, drawn from Holy Writ, will be gainsaid only by an ignorant man or one who delights in contradiction.",
    "root.text.Part 2..4.1": "As for the opinion of the Doctors in this regard, it requires, I apprehend, neither explanation nor proof. One has but to consider their formulation of the <i>benediction of the moon</i>[[fn:1446]], and that which is frequently reiterated in the prayers[[fn:1447]], as well as the texts of the <i>Midrashim</i> concerning these passages: <i>And the host of heaven worshipps Thee</i> (Nehemiah, <small>IX</small>, 6)[[fn:1448]]; <i>When the morning stars sang together, and all the sons of God shouted for joy</i> (Job, <small>XXXVIII</small>, 7)[[fn:1449]]. They often revert to this theme in their discourses. Thus do they express themselves, in the <i>Berêshîth Rabbâ</i>, concerning this word of God: <i>And the earth was</i> <small>TOHU</small> <i>and</i> <small>BOHU</small> (Gen. <small>I</small>, 2): «It was <i>toha</i> and <i>boha</i> [that is to say, the earth lamented and despaired of its unfortunate fate]; I and they, it said, were created together [that is, the earth and the heavens]; but the superior things are living, whilst the inferior things are dead[[fn:1450]].» They therefore also clearly state that the heavens[[fn:1451]] are living bodies, and not dead bodies, like the elements.",
    "root.text.Part 2..4.2": "Thus it is manifest that, if Aristotle has said that the celestial sphere possesses <i>perception</i> and <i>conception</i>, this is in accord with the words of our prophets and the pillars of our Law[[fn:1452]], who are the Sages.",
    "root.text.Part 2..4.3": "It is also necessary to understand that all philosophers concur that the governance of this terrestrial world is accomplished by the force which emanates from the celestial sphere upon it, as we have stated[[fn:1453]], and that the spheres perceive and comprehend the matters which they govern. And this is what the Law has likewise expressed by saying (concerning the celestial hosts): <i>that God has allotted them unto all the peoples</i> (Deut. <small>IV</small>, 19), which signifies that He made them intermediaries for governing creatures, and not for their adoration. It has further been plainly stated: <i>And to rule</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ולמשל</span>) <i>over the day and over the night</i>, <i>and to divide</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Gen. <small>I</small>. 18); for the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">משל</span> signifies <i>to rule by governing</i>. This is an idea superadded to that of <i>light</i> and <i>darkness</i>, which are the proximate cause of generation and corruption[[fn:1454]]; for concerning the idea of light and darkness (produced by the celestial bodies) it has been said: <i>and to divide the light from the darkness</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>). Now, it is inadmissible that he who <i>governs</i> a thing should lack knowledge of that thing, once one has apprehended the true meaning which the word <i>to govern</i> bears in this context.",
    "root.text.Part 2..4.4": "We shall enlarge further upon this subject in another place.",
    "root.text.Part 2..5.0": "As touching the existence of angels, it is a matter whereon it is not necessary to adduce any proof from Scripture; for the Law declars itself in this regard in many a passage. Thou knowest already that É<small>LOHIM</small> is the name of <i>judges</i> (or of <i>governors</i>)[[fn:1455]]; e.g., <i>before the Elohîm</i> (judges) <i>shall come the cause of both</i> (Exod. <small>XXII</small>, 9). Wherefore this name has been metaphorically employed for (designating) the angels, and also for <i>God</i>, because He is the judge (or the <i>ruler</i>) of the angels; and for this reason also it was said (Deut. <small>X</small>, 17): <i>For the Lord your God</i>, which is an address to all mankind; and thereafter: <i>He is the God of gods</i>, that is to say, the God of angels, <i>and the Lord of lords</i>, that is to say, the Master of the spheres and the stars, which are the <i>lords</i> of all other bodies. This is the true sense, and the words <i>elohîm</i> (gods) and <i>adonîm</i> (lords) cannot here designate human beings[[fn:1456]]; for these would be too insignificant for such a purpose[[fn:1457]]; and furthermore, the words <i>your God</i>[[fn:1458]] already encompass the whole human species, the ruling part as well as the ruled. — Neither is it possible that it was intended thereby that God is the Master of all that which, in the form of (statues of) stone and wood, is reputed a divinity; for it would not be to glorify and magnify God to make Him the Master of stone, wood, and a piece of metal. But what was intended is that God is the Ruler of rulers, that is to say, of the angels, and the Master of the celestial spheres.",
    "root.text.Part 2..5.1": "We have already, in an earlier section of this treatise, presented a chapter expounding that angels are not corporeal[[fn:1459]]. This is also the assertion of Aristotle; only, a difference of nomenclature obtains here: he, for his part, speaks of <i>Separate Intelligences</i>, whereas we speak of <i>angels</i>[[fn:1460]]. As for his dictum that these Separate Intelligences also serve as intermediaries between God and other beings, and that it is through their agency that the spheres are moved — which constitutes the efficient cause of the genesis of all that comes into being[[fn:1461]] — this, too, is proclaimed by all sacred books; for one shall never find therein that God accomplishes aught save through the mediation of an angel. One is aware that the term <small>מַלְאָךְ</small> (angel) signifies <i>messenger;</i> whosoever, therefore, executes a command is a <i>מַלְאָךְ</i>, such that the movements of even the irrational animal are accomplished, according to the text of Scripture, through the instrumentality of a <i>מַלְאָךְ</i>, when this movement conforms to the purpose of God, who has implanted in the animal a force by which it performs this movement. One reads, for example: <i>My God has sent his angel</i> (<small>מַלְאָכֵהּ</small>) <i>and has shut the lions' mouths, that they have not hurt me</i> (Daniel, <small>VI</small>, 22); and similarly all the movements of Balaam’s ass occurred through the agency of a <small>מַלְאָךְ</small>. The very elements are designated <small>מַלְאָכִים</small> (<i>angels</i> or <i>messengers</i>); for instance: <i>He maks his angels winds</i> (<small>מַלְאָכָיו</small>) <i>and his ministers a flaming fire</i> (Ps. <small>CIV</small>, 4). It is thus clear that the term <small>מַלְאָךְ</small> applies: 1° to the <i>messenger</i> among men, for example: <i>And Jacob sent</i> <small>מַלְאָכִים</small> or <i>messengers</i> (Gen. <small>XXXII</small>, 3); 2° to the <i>prophet</i>, for instance: <i>And an</i> <small>מַלְאָךְ</small> <i>of the Lord came up from Gilgal to Bochim</i> (Judges <small>II</small>, 1); <i>He sent a</i> <small>מַלְאָךְ</small> <i>and brought us forth out of Egypt</i> (Num. <small>XX</small>, 16); 3° to the <i>Separate Intelligences</i> which manifest themselves to the prophets in prophetic vision; and finally, 4° to the animal faculties, as we shall further expound.",
    "root.text.Part 2..5.2": "In this place, we speak only of <i>angels</i>, which are Separate Intelligences; and verily our Law does not deny that God governs this world by the mediation of angels. Behold how the Sages express themselves upon the words of the Law: <i>Let us make man in our image</i> (Gen. <small>I</small>, 26), <i>Go to, let us go down</i> (<i>Ibid.</i> <small>XI</small>, 7), where the plural is employed: \"If,\" say they, \"it be permitted thus to speak, the Holy One does nothing without having <i>looked</i> upon the celestial family[[fn:1462]].\" The word <i>looked</i> is right remarkable[[fn:1463]]; for Plato has said, in these same terms, that God having <i>looked</i> upon the world of intelligences, it was from this that being emanated[[fn:1464]]. In some places, they say simply: \"the Holy One does nothing without having <i>consulted</i> the celestial family[[fn:1465]].\" The word <span dir=\"rtl\">פמליא</span> (familia) signifies <i>army</i> in the Greek tongue[[fn:1466]]. It is said again in the <i>Bereshith Rabba</i>, as also in the <i>Midrash Koheleth</i>, (on the words) <i>that which they have already done</i> (Ecclesiastes <small>II</small>, 12): \"It is not said here <span dir=\"rtl\">עשהו</span>, <i>he has done it</i>, but <span dir=\"rtl\">עשדהו</span> <i>they have done it;</i> for, if it be permitted thus to speak, He (God) and His court have consulted together upon each of thy members and have set it upon its base, as it is said: <i>He has made thee</i>, <i>and established thee</i> (Deut. <small>XXXII</small>, 6)[[fn:1467]].\" It is further said in the <i>Bereshith Rabba:</i> \"Wherever it is said: <small>AND</small> <i>the Lord</i>, it is He and His court[[fn:1468]].\"",
    "root.text.Part 2..5.3.sub_0": "All these texts do not aim, as the ignorant believe, (to affirm) that the Most High speaks, or reflects, or examines, or consults to assist Himself with the opinion of others[[fn:1469]]; for how would the Creator seek help from what He has created? On the contrary, all of this clearly expresses that even the (smallest) details of the universe, down to the creation of animal limbs as they are, all of this (I say) was done through the mediation of angels; for all faculties are angels. How powerful[[fn:1470]] is the blindness of ignorance, and how dangerous it is! If you were to say to someone who claims to be among the <i>sages of Israel</i> that God sends an angel who enters the woman's womb and forms the fetus there, this would please him greatly; he would accept it and believe that it attributes greatness and power to God, and recognizes His high wisdom[[fn:1471]]. At the same time, he would also admit that the angel is a body (formed) of a burning fire, and that its size is about a third of the entire universe; and all this would seem possible to him regarding God. But if you told him that God has placed in the semen a formative power that shapes and outlines these limbs, and that this is the <i>angel</i>, or that all forms come from the action of the <i>active intellect</i> and that it is this which is the angel and the <i>prince of the world</i> of whom the doctors always speak, he would reject such an opinion[[fn:1472]]; for he would not understand the meaning of this true greatness and power, which consists in bringing into being within a thing active forces imperceptible to the senses. The doctors have therefore clearly explained, for one who is truly a <i>sage</i>, that each of the corporeal forces is an <i>angel</i>, and all the more so the forces spread throughout the universe, and that each force has a certain determined action, and not two actions. In the <i>Bereshith Rabba</i> we read: 'It has been taught: a single angel does not fulfill two missions, and two angels do not fulfill the same mission[[fn:1473]]'; — and this is indeed a condition of all (physical) forces.",
    "root.text.Part 2..5.3.sub_1": "That which shall further confirm unto thee that all individual forces, whether physical or psychical, are termed <i>angels</i>, is that which they have observed in several places, and primarily in the <i>Berēshīth Rabbā</i>: “Every day the Holy One creats a class of angels, who recite a song before Him and depart thence[[fn:1474]].” Inasmuch as there has been objected to these words a passage indicating that the angels are <i>stable</i>, — and indeed it has been set forth many a time that the angels are <i>living</i> and <i>stable</i>, — this answer was made, that there be some among them who are stable, whilst there be others who are perishable[[fn:1475]]. And so it is in truth; for these <i>individual</i> forces are born and perish continually, whilst the <i>species</i> of these forces are permanent and suffer no decay[[fn:1476]]. — It is further stated therein[[fn:1477]], concerning the history of Judah and Tamar: “Rabbi Jo’hanan said: He (Judah) wished to pass by; but God sent unto him an angel appointed over concupiscence,” that is to say, over the venereal faculty. This faculty, therefore, has likewise been called an <i>angel</i>. And thus shalt thou find that they ever say: <i>An angel appointed over such or such a thing;</i> for every faculty wherewith God has charged any matter whatsoever[[fn:1478]] is (accounted as) <i>an angel appointed over that thing</i>. A passage in the <i>Midrash Koheleth</i> says: “Whilst man sleeps, his soul speaks to the angel, and the angel to the cherubim[[fn:1479]];” here then, for him who understands and reflects, they have clearly declared that the imaginative faculty is likewise called an <i>angel</i> and that the intellect is called a <i>cherub</i>[[fn:1480]]. This shall appear passing fair to the man of learning, but shall greatly displease the ignorant[[fn:1481]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..5.4": "We have already stated elsewhere that whenever an angel manifests himself in any form whatsoever, it is in a <i>prophetic vision</i>[[fn:1482]]. Thou findest[[fn:1483]] prophets who behold the angel[[fn:1484]] as though he were a mere human individual; e.g.: <i>And, lo, three men</i> (Gen. <small>XVIII</small>, 2). To others, he appears as a man redoubtable and fearful; e.g.: <i>And his countenance was like the countenance of an angel of God, very terrible</i> (Judges, <small>XIII</small>, 6). To yet others he appears as fire; e.g.: <i>And the angel of the Lord appeared unto him in a flame of fire</i> (Exodus, <small>III</small>, 2). It has further been remarked in the same place[[fn:1485]]: “To Abraham, who possessed an excellent faculty, they appeared in the likeness of <i>men</i>; but to Lot, who had but a meaner faculty, they appeared in the likeness of <i>angels</i>.” Herein is a great mystery concerning prophecy, of which we shall speak in due season hereafter[[fn:1486]]. — It is further said therein: “Before they had accomplished their mission, (they showed themselves as) men; after they had accomplished it, they resumed their angelic nature[[fn:1487]].” — Observe well that on every hand it is clearly indicated that by <i>angel</i> one must understand some action or other, and that every vision of an angel occurrs only in <i>prophetic vision</i> and according to the state of him who perceivs.",
    "root.text.Part 2..5.5": "In that which Aristotle has said upon this subject, there is likewise naught which stands in contradiction to the Law. But that which is contrary to us[[fn:1488]] in all this is that he (Aristotle) believs all these things to be <i>eternal</i>, and that they proceed from God <i>by necessity</i>; whereas we believe that all this is <i>created</i>, that God has created the separate Intelligences and has placed in the celestial sphere a faculty of desire which draws it towards them; that it is He (in a word) who has created the Intelligences and the spheres, and who has endowed them with these <i>governing faculties</i>[[fn:1489]]. It is in this that we differ from him.",
    "root.text.Part 2..5.6": "Thou shalt hear hereafter his opinion, as also the opinion of the true Law, concerning the <i>newness of the world</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..6.0": "We have therefore explained that the word <i>mal’ākh</i> (angel) is a homonymous term, and that it compriss the Intelligences, the spheres, and the elements; for they all execute a command (of God). Yet it must not be believed that the spheres or the Intelligences are in the same rank as other forces purely corporeal, which are a mere nature[[fn:1490]] and possess no consciousness of their action; on the contrary, the spheres and the Intelligences have consciousness of their actions, and exercise liberty in their governance[[fn:1491]]. Albeit, this is not a liberty like unto ours, nor a rule like unto ours, in which everything depends upon things accidental and newly arisen. The Law contains several passages which awaken our attention thereto. Thus, for example, the angel says unto Lot: <i>For I cannot do anything, etc.</i> (Gen. <small>XIX</small>, 22); and he says unto him in saving him: “<i>Behold, I have accepted thee concerning this thing also</i>” (<i>ibid.</i>, <small>v.</small> 21); and elsewhere it is said: “<i>Beware of him</i> (the angel), <i>and obey his voice, provoke him not; for he will not pardon your transgressions: for My name is in him</i>” (Exodus, <small>XXIII</small>, 21). All these passages indicate unto thee that they act with full consciousness[[fn:1492]] and that they possess will and liberty in the governance committed unto them[[fn:1493]], even as we possess a will in that which has been committed unto us, and the power of which has been given us from our birth. We, however, do sometimes the least possible; our rule and our action are preceded by <i>privation</i>[[fn:1494]], whereas it is not so with the Intelligences and the spheres. These, on the contrary, ever perform that which is good, and in them there is naught but good, as we shall set forth in other chapters; all that pertains unto them is found perfect and ever in actu, from the moment they have existed.",
    "root.text.Part 2..7.0": "It is one of the ancient opinions prevalent[[fn:1495]] amongst the philosophers and the generality of men, that the motion of the celestial spheres produces a great and right fearful sound[[fn:1496]]. To provide proof thereof, they allege that, since the small bodies here below[[fn:1497]], when they be moved with a rapid motion, cause a great noise and a terrifying clangour to be heard, by a much stronger reason must the bodies of the sun, the moon, and the stars, which be so great and so swift, do likewise. The whole Pythagorean sect believed that they possess harmonious sounds, which, notwithstanding their power, are proportioned among themselves, even as musical modulations are; and they bring forward[[fn:1498]] causes why we hear not these sounds so fearful and so loud. This opinion is likewise prevalent within our nation[[fn:1499]]. Perceivest thou not that the Doctors describe the great noise made by the sun whilst daily traversing the celestial sphere[[fn:1500]]? And the same follows for all (the other stars). Nevertheless, Aristotle refuss (to admit) this and shows that they have no sounds. Thou shalt find this in his book <i>On the Heavens</i>, and there mayest thou be instructed upon this subject. Be not offended that the opinion of Aristotle is here in opposition to that of the Doctors; for this opinion—to wit, that they (the stars) have sounds—does but follow the belief (which admitts) \"that the sphere remains fixed and that the stars revolve[[fn:1501]]\". But thou knowest that in these astronomical matters, they acknowledge the preponderance of the opinion of the <i>sages of the nations of the world</i> over their own: for thus they say plainly: \"And the sages of the nations of the world prevailed[[fn:1502]]\". And this is true; for all those who have spoken upon these speculative matters have done so only according to the result to which speculation had led them; wherefore one must believe that which has been established by demonstration[[fn:1503]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..8.0": "We have already expounded unto thee that the number of the spheres had not been determined in the time of Aristotle[[fn:1504]], and that those who, in our time, have reckoned nine spheres, have but considered as a single globe that which embraces several spheres[[fn:1505]], as is manifest unto him who has studied astronomy. Wherefore also one must not find fault with what one of the Doctors has said: \"There are two firmaments, as it is said: <i>Behold, the heaven and the heaven of heavens belong to the Lord thy God</i> (Deut. X, 14)[[fn:1506]].\" For he who said this has reckoned but a single globe for all the stars[[fn:1507]]—I mean, for the spheres containing stars—and has reckoned as the second globe the surrounding sphere, in which there is no star; wherefore he said: <i>there are two firmaments</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..8.1": "I shall make unto thee a preliminary observation which is necessary for the purpose which I have proposed to myself in this chapter; it is as follows:",
    "root.text.Part 2..8.2": "Know that concerning the two spheres[[fn:1508]] of Venus and Mercury, there is a divergence of opinion amongst the ancient mathematicians (as to whether) they be above or below the sun; for the order in which these two spheres are placed cannot be rigorously demonstrated[[fn:1509]]. The opinion of all the ancients was that the two spheres of Venus and Mercury are above the sun, which thing must be known and well understood[[fn:1510]]. Thereafter came Ptolemy, who preferred to admit that they are below, saying that it is more natural for the sun to be in the midst and that there be three planets above him and three below[[fn:1511]]. Subsequently there appeared in Andalusia, in these latter times, men[[fn:1512]] right well versed in mathematics, who showed, according to the principles of Ptolemy, that Venus and Mars are above the sun. Ibn-Aflah of Seville, with whose son I was connected, composed a famous book thereupon[[fn:1513]]; then the excellent philosopher Abu-Bekr ibn-al-Sa’igh[[fn:1514]], under one of whose disciples I have taken lessons, examined this subject, and produced certain arguments [which we have copied from him[[fn:1515]]], by which he represented it as improbable that Venus and Mercury be above the sun; but that which Abu-Bekr has said is an argument to show the improbability thereof, and proves not its impossibility. In short, whether it be so or not, (the fact remains that) all the ancients placed Venus and Mercury above the sun, and by reason of this, they reckoned the spheres[[fn:1516]] (to the number of) five: that of the moon, which, indubitably, is near unto us[[fn:1517]], that of the sun, which is necessarily above it, that of the five (other) planets, that of the fixed stars, and lastly the sphere which surrounds the whole, and in which there are no stars. Thus then, the <i>figured</i> spheres—I mean the spheres with <i>figures</i>, in which there are stars, for it is thus that the ancients called the stars <i>figures</i>, as is known from their writings—these spheres (say I) would be <i>four</i> in number[[fn:1518]]: the sphere of the fixed stars, that of the five planets, that of the sun, and that of the moon; and above all is a naked sphere, in which there are no stars. This number (of <i>four</i>) is for me an important principle for a subject which has come to my mind, and which I have not seen clearly (expounded) in any of the philosophers; but I have found in the discourses of the philosophers and in the words of the Doctors that which has awakened my attention thereto. I shall speak of it in the following chapter, and I shall expound the matter.",
    "root.text.Part 2..9.0": "It is known, and it is a thing widespread in all the books of the philosophers, that when they speak of the <i>governance</i> (of the world), they say that the governance of this inferior world—I mean of the world of generation and corruption—takes place only by means of the forces which flow from the celestial spheres. We have already said this several times, and thou shalt find that the Doctors say likewise[[fn:1519]]: \"There is not even the smallest plant here below which has not in the firmament its <i>mazzâl</i> (that is to say, its star), which striks it and bidds it to grow, as it is said (Job, <small>XXXVIII</small>, 33): <i>Knowest thou the ordinances of heaven, or canst thou set its dominion</i> (its influence) <i>upon the earth?</i>— [By <i>mazzâl</i>, one designats also a <i>star</i>[[fn:1520]], as thou findest clearly at the beginning of <i>Bereshith Rabba,</i> where they say: ‘There is such a <i>mazzâl</i> (i.e., such a <i>star</i> or such a <i>planet</i>) which complets its course in thirty days, and another which complets its course in thirty years[[fn:1521]].’] — They have therefore clearly indicated by this passage that even the <i>individuals</i> of nature[[fn:1522]] are under the particular influence of the forces of certain stars; for, although all the forces of the celestial sphere together be diffused through all beings, each species nevertheless finds itself also under the particular influence of some star whatsoever[[fn:1523]]. It is as with the forces of a single body[[fn:1524]]; for the entire universe is a single individual, as we have said.—Thus it is that the philosophers have said that the moon has an augmentative force which is exercised particularly upon the element of water; that which proves it is that the seas and the rivers increase in measure as the moon waxes and decrease as it wans, and that the flood-tide, in the seas, is (in relation) with the advancement of the moon and the ebb-tide with its retrogradation—I mean its ascent and its descent in the quadrants of the orbit—as is clear and manifest unto him who has observed it[[fn:1525]]. That, on the other hand, the rays of the sun set in motion the element of fire, is a thing most evident, as thou seest by the heat which is diffused in the world in the presence of the sun, and by the cold which prevails as soon as it (the sun) withdraws from a place, or is hidden from it. This is too evident to be expounded at length.",
    "root.text.Part 2..9.1": "Knowing this, it has occurred to me that, albeit from the whole of these four <i>figured</i> spheres there emanate forces (which spread) into all beings that <i>come into existence</i> and of which they are the causes, yet each sphere may have (under its governance) one of the four elements, in such wise that a particular sphere be the principle of force for a particular element, to which, by its own motion, it gives the movement of <i>generation</i>[[fn:1526]]. Thus, the sphere of the moon would be that which movs the water; the sphere of the sun, that which movs the fire; the sphere of the other planets, that which movs the air [and their manifold motion, their inequality, their retrogradation, their directness and their station[[fn:1527]] produce the numerous configurations of the air, its variation and its prompt contraction and expansion]; finally, the sphere of the fixed stars, that which movs the earth; and it is perhaps for this cause that the latter movs with difficulty to receive impression and mixture[[fn:1528]], (I mean) because the fixed stars have a slow motion. To this relation of the fixed stars with the earth, allusion has been made in saying that the number of the species of plants corresponds to that of the individuals of the whole of the stars[[fn:1529]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..9.2": "In this manner, therefore, it is possible that the order (in nature) be this: four spheres, four elements moved by them, and four forces emanated from them (and acting) in nature in general, as we have set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 2..9.3": "Likewise, the causes of all movement of the spheres are four in number, to wit: the figure of the sphere,—I mean its sphericity,—its soul, its intellect whereby it conceivs, as we have explained, and the separate Intelligence, the object of its desire[[fn:1530]]. Thou must well possess thyself of this. Here is the explanation thereof: if it had not this (spherical) figure, it would by no means be possible for it to have a circular and continuous motion; for the <i>continuity</i> of motion ever repeated is possible only in circular motion alone. Rectilinear motion, on the contrary, even should the thing moved return several times over one and the same extent, could not be continuous; for, between two opposite motions, there is always a rest, as has been demonstrated in its place[[fn:1531]]. It is therefore manifest that it is a necessary condition of the continuity of motion ever returning over the same extent, that the thing moved should move circularly[[fn:1532]]. But naught save a living being can move; there must needs, therefore, exist a soul (in the sphere). It is indispensable also that there be something which invits to motion; this is a <i>conception</i> and the desire for that which has been conceived, as we have said. But this cannot take place here save by means of an intellect; for it is a question here neither of fleeing what is contrary, nor of seeking what is convenient. Finally, it is necessary that there be a being which has been conceived and which is the object of desire, as we have set forth. Thus there are four causes for the motion of the celestial sphere; and (there are also) four species of general forces descended from it towards us, which are: the force which brings minerals into being, that of the vegetative soul, that of the vital soul, and that of the rational soul, as we have set forth[[fn:1533]]. Then, if thou considerest the actions of these forces, thou shalt find that they are of two species, (to wit) to bring into being all that is born and to preserve this thing born, I mean to preserve its species perpetually and to preserve the individuals for a certain time. And this is what is meant by <i>nature</i>, of which it is said that it is wise, that it governs, that it takes care to produce the animal by an art similar to the artistic faculty (of man)[[fn:1534]], and that it takes care to preserve and perpetuate it, producing (first) formative forces which are the cause of its existence, and (afterward) nutritive faculties which are the cause whereby it endurs and is preserved as long as possible; in a word, this is that divine thing whence come the two actions in question, through the mediation of the celestial sphere.",
    "root.text.Part 2..9.4": "This number <i>four</i> is noteworthy and gives occasion for reflection. In the <i>Midrash</i> of Rabbi Tanhuma it is said: «How many steps had the ladder? <i>four</i>[[fn:1535]].» Here the passage is concerned <i>and behold, a ladder was set up on the earth</i> (Gen. <small>XXVIII</small>, 12). In all the <i>Midrashim</i> it is related «that there are <i>four</i> legions of angels,» and this is frequently repeated[[fn:1536]]. In some copies I have seen: «How many steps had the ladder? <i>seven</i>»; but all copies (of <i>Midrash Tanhuma</i>) and all <i>Midrashim</i> agree in saying that <i>the angels of God</i> whom he (Jacob) saw <i>ascending</i> and <i>descending</i> were but <i>four</i>, no more, «two ascending and two descending,» that the four stood together upon one of the steps of the ladder and that all four were upon one and the same rank, the two that ascended as well as the two that descended. They have therefore learned thence that the breadth of the ladder in the prophetic vision was like the universe and a third (of the universe); for the space of a single angel, in this prophetic vision, being as the third of the universe,—since it is said: <i>And his body was like a</i> <small>TARSHISH</small> (Daniel, x, 6)[[fn:1537]],—it follows that the space occupied by the four was like the universe and a third (of the universe). — In the allegories of Zechariah, after having described (ch. <small>VI,</small> v. 1) <i>the four chariots coming out from between two mountains; and the mountains were mountains of brass</i> (<small>NE’HOSCHES</small>), he adds to give the explanation thereof (<i>Ibid,</i> v. 5): <i>These are the four spirits of the heavens, which go forth from standing before the Lord of all the earth</i>, and which are the cause of all that is born[[fn:1538]]. In the mention of brass (<small>NE’HOSCHES</small>), as in the words <i>burnished brass</i> (<small>NE’HOSCHES KALAL</small>, Ezek., I, 7), naught was intended but a certain <i>homonymy</i>, and thou shalt hear later an observation thereupon[[fn:1539]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..9.5": "— As to what they say <i>that the angel is the third part of the universe</i>, — which they express textually in the <i>Bereshith Rabbah</i> by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">שהלמלאך שלישו של עולם</span>, — it is very clear, and we have already set it forth in our great work on the traditional law[[fn:1540]]. For indeed, the whole of created things is divided into three parts: 1st, the <i>separate</i> Intelligences, which are the angels; 2nd, the bodies of the celestial spheres; 3rd, prime matter, I mean the continually changeable bodies, which are beneath the celestial sphere.",
    "root.text.Part 2..9.6": "Thus must he understand who desirs to comprehend the prophetic enigmas, to awaken from the slumber of sloth, to be rescued from the sea of ignorance, and to ascend unto things sublime[[fn:1541]]. As for him who delights in swimming in the seas of his own ignorance and in <i>descending ever lower</i>[[fn:1542]], he shall have no need to weary his body; yet shall his heart not be free from agitation[[fn:1543]], and he shall naturally sink to the lowest degree.",
    "root.text.Part 2..9.7": "It behoovs one well to understand all that has been said and to reflect thereupon.",
    "root.text.Part 2..10.0": "Know thou that if a mere mathematician read and understand those astronomical subjects of which mention has been made, he may believe that there is herein a decisive proof (demonstrating) that such are the form and the number of the spheres. However, it is not so, and such is not the quest of astronomical science[[fn:1544]]. Verily, there be some of these matters which are susceptible of demonstration[[fn:1545]]: thus, for example, it is demonstrated that the orbit of the sun declins from the equator, and there is no doubt thereof. But that it has an eccentric sphere, or an epicycle[[fn:1546]], is that which has not been demonstrated, and the astronomer does not trouble himself therewith; for the aim of this science is to establish a system whereby the motion of the star may be uniform and circular, being never accelerated, nor retarded, nor altered, and that the result thereof may accord with that which is observed[[fn:1547]]. Withal, the intent is[[fn:1548]] to diminish the motions and the number of the spheres as much as possible; for if, for instance, we can establish a system whereby the visible motions of a certain star can be justified by (the hypothesis of) three spheres, and another system whereby the same may be justified by four spheres, it is better to abide by the system in which the number of motions is less. Wherefore do we prefer, for the sun, eccentricity unto the epicycle, even as Ptolemy has said[[fn:1549]].—In this view therefore, since we perceive the motions of all the fixed stars as a single invariable motion, and as they change not their positions one toward another, we maintain (<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">5</sup>[[fn:1550]]) that they are all in one single sphere; but it would not be impossible that each of these stars should be in a (particular) sphere, in such wise that they all had a uniform motion and that all these spheres (revolved) upon the same poles. There would then be <i>Intelligences</i> according to the number of the spheres, even as it is said: <i>Can his armies be numbered?</i> (Job, <small>xxv</small>, 3)? that is to say, by reason of their great number; for the Intelligences, the celestial bodies, and all the forces, all these together form <i>his armies</i>, and their species must of necessity be limited by a certain number. But, should it be so, this would in no wise prejudice our classification[[fn:1551]], in that we have counted as one the sphere of the fixed stars, even as we have counted as one the five spheres of the planets with the numerous spheres which they contain; for thou hast well understood that we have had no other aim than to count (as one) the totality of each force which we perceive in nature as a single whole[[fn:1552]], without concerning ourselves with rendering an exact account of the true state of the intelligences and the spheres[[fn:1553]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..10.1": "But our intent, in brief, is (to show): 1st, That all beings besides the Creator are divided into three classes: the first (compriss) the separate intelligences; the second, the bodies of the celestial spheres, which are <i>substrata</i> for stable forms, in which the form passes not from one <i>substratum</i> to another, nor is the <i>substratum</i> itself subject to change; the third, those bodies which are subject to generation and corruption and which are embraced by a single matter. 2nd, That the <i>governance</i> descends[[fn:1554]] from God upon the <i>intelligences</i>, according to their (successive) order, that the Intelligences, from what they have themselves received, pour forth benefits and lights upon the bodies of the celestial spheres, and that the spheres finally pour forth forces and benefits upon this (lower) body which is generated and perishes, (communicating unto it) that which they have received in greatest measure from their principles[[fn:1555]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..10.2": "It must be known that everything which, in this classification, communicats any good, has not for the sole final cause of its existence—though it be a <i>giver</i>[[fn:1556]]—the giving unto that which receivs; for (were it so), there would follow therefrom a manifest absurdity. Verily, the <i>end</i> is more noble than the things which exist for the sake of that end; now, it would follow (from the said supposition) that what is higher, more perfect, and more noble exists for the sake of that which is inferior to it, a thing which no intelligent man could imagine.",
    "root.text.Part 2..10.3": "But the matter is as I shall state: When a thing possesses a certain kind of perfection, sometimes this perfection occupies therein an extent (sufficient) for the thing itself to be perfect, without any perfection being communicated therefrom to another thing; sometimes the perfection has such an extent that there is a surplus wherewith to perfect another thing. Thus, to cite an example, thou wouldst say that there is a certain man who possesses a fortune sufficient only for his needs, and there remains naught in excess of which another may profit, and another who possesses a fortune of which there remains unto him a surplus[[fn:1557]] wherewith to enrich many people, so that he may give unto another person sufficiently for that person to be likewise rich and have enough remaining to enrich a third person. It is the same in the universe: the <i>efflux</i>, which comes from God to produce separate Intelligences, is also communicated from these intelligences so that they produce one another, even unto the Active Intellect, wherewith the production of <i>separate</i> (intelligences) ceass. From each separate (intelligence) there likewise emanats another production[[fn:1558]], until the spheres terminate at that of the moon. After this last comes this (lower) body which is generated and perishes, I mean prime matter and that which is composed thereof. From each sphere there come forces (which are communicated) unto the elements, until their <i>efflux</i> stays at the term of (the world of) generation and corruption. We have already set forth that all these things do in no wise subvert the words of our prophets and the pillars of our Law[[fn:1559]]; for our nation was a learned and perfect nation, even as God has proclaimed by the hand of the Master who perfected us, saying: <i>Surely this great nation is a wise and understanding people</i> (Deut. IV, 6). But when the wicked among the ignorant nations had brought to nought our fair qualities, destroyed our sciences[[fn:1560]] and our books, and put to the sword our wise men,—so that we became ignorant, even as it had been threatened us on account of our sins, saying: <i>The wisdom of their wise men shall perish</i>, <i>and the understanding of their prudent men shall be hid</i> (Isaiah, 29, 14),—we mingled with these nations[[fn:1561]], and their opinions passed unto us, as also did their manners and their actions. Even as it was said, concerning the assimilation of actions: <i>They were mingled among the heathen and learned their works</i> (Ps. 106, 35), so likewise was it said, concerning the opinions of the ignorant nations transmitted unto us: <i>And they please themselves in the children of strangers</i> (Isaiah, 2, 6)[[fn:1562]], which Jonathan ben-Uzziel translats: <i>And they follow the laws of the nations.</i> Since, therefore, we were nurtured in the habit of the opinions of the ignorant peoples, these philosophical matters appeared to be as foreign to our Law as they were to the opinions of the ignorant peoples, though in truth it be not so.",
    "root.text.Part 2..10.4": "Forasmuch as in our discourse mention has been made several times of the <i>emanation</i> (proceeding) from God and from the Intelligences, it behoovs us to set forth the true meaning thereof, I mean the idea designated by the word <i>emanation</i>. Thereafter, I shall begin to treat of the <i>novelty of the world.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 2..11.0": "It is manifest that everything which is <i>produced</i>[[fn:1563]] must of necessity have an efficient cause which caused it to be after it had not been. This proximate efficient cause can be naught else than either corporeal or incorporeal; nevertheless, a body, whatsoever it be, acts not in its capacity as a body, but it exerciss <i>such</i> an action because it is <i>such</i> a body, I mean to say (that it acts) by its form. I shall speak of this further on. This proximate efficient, producer of the thing <i>produced</i>, may itself be <i>produced</i> (of another thing); but this cannot continue unto infinity, and on the contrary, so soon as there is a thing produced, it is necessary that we arrive at last at a primitive, uncreated producer, who has produced the thing. But then there remains the question (to wit) why he has produced now and why he did it not sooner, since he existed. It must needs be, therefore, that this new act was impossible beforetime[[fn:1564]]: either because, the agent being corporeal, there lacked a certain <i>relation</i> between the agent and the object of the action; or because, the agent being incorporeal, there lacked the <i>disposition of the matter</i>[[fn:1565]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..11.1": "The entire exposition is the result of physical speculation<i>physique</i>, without our presently concerning ourselves with the eternity or novelty of the world; for this is not the purpose of this chapter.",
    "root.text.Part 2..11.2.sub_0": "It has been set forth in physical science that every body which exerciss a certain action upon another body acts upon the latter only by approaching it, or by approaching something which approaches it, if this action be exercised by intermediaries. Thus, for example, this body which has now been heated, was so either because the fire itself approached it, or because the fire heated the air and the air surrounding the body heated it, so that it was the mass of hot air which was the proximate agent for heating this body. Thus it is that the loadstone attracts iron from afar by means of a force which proceeds from it into the air which approaches the iron. Wherefore it exerciss not attraction at any distance whatsoever, even as fire heats not at any distance whatsoever, but only at a distance which permitts the modification of the air that is between it and the thing heated by its force; but when the heat of the air coming from this fire is cut off (or removed) from beneath the wax, the latter can no longer be melted thereby; the same holds true concerning attraction. Now, for a thing which was not hot to become so thereafter, it was necessary that there should supervene a cause to heat it, whether a fire <i>was kindled</i>, or whether there were one at a certain distance which was changed. This is the <i>relation</i> which was lacking at first and which thereafter supervened. Likewise, (if we seek) the causes of all that occurrs in this world in the way of new creations, we shall find that the cause thereof is the mixture of the elements, bodies which act upon one another and receive action from one another, I mean that the cause of what is born is the drawing near or the withdrawal of the (elementary) bodies from one another. — As for that which we see brought into being without its being the simple consequence of the mixture[[fn:1566]], — and these are all the forms, — there is required for this also an efficient, I mean something which <i>bestows</i> the form. And this is by no means a body; for the efficient of the form is a form without matter, as has been set forth in its proper place[[fn:1567]], and we have previously indicated the proof thereof[[fn:1568]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..11.2.sub_1": "That which may further serve to explain this unto thee is that every mixture is susceptible of increase and decrease, and comes about by degrees; whereas it is not so with forms, for these do not arrive by degrees, and for this reason they have no motion, but they appear and disappear in an instant[[fn:1569]]. They are not, therefore, the effect of the mixture; rather, the mixture does but <i>dispose</i> matter to receive the form. The efficient cause of the form is a thing not susceptible of division, for its action is of the same kind as itself[[fn:1570]]; wherefore it is evident that the efficient cause of the form—I mean that which bestows it—is necessarily a form, and this is a <i>separate</i> (form)[[fn:1571]]. It is inadmissible that this efficient cause, which is incorporeal, should produce its impression by reason of a certain <i>relation.</i> For, being in no wise a body, it can neither draw nigh nor depart, nor can any body draw nigh unto it or depart from it; for there exists no relation of distance between the corporeal and the incorporeal. It follows thence by necessity that the absence of this action[[fn:1572]] is caused by the lack of disposition in such matter to receive the action of the <i>Separate Being.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 2..11.3.sub_0": "It is therefore manifest that the action which the (elementary) bodies, by virtue of their (particular) forms, exert one upon another, results in disposing the (various) matters to receive the action of that which is incorporeal, that is to say, those actions which are the <i>forms</i>[[fn:1573]]. Now, since the impressions of the <i>Separate Intelligence</i>[[fn:1574]] are manifest and evident in this world—I speak of all those novelties (of nature) which arise not from the <i>mere</i> mixture in itself—it must perforce be acknowledged that this efficient cause acts not by contact, nor at a fixed distance, since it is incorporeal. This action of the <i>Separate Intelligence</i> is ever designated by the word <i>emanation</i> (<small>FÉIDH</small>), by comparison with a fountain of water which <i>overflows</i> on all sides, and which has no fixed sides, neither whence it flows forth, nor whither it spreads itself elsewhere, but it gushes from every part and continually waters all sides around, both what is near and what is far. For it is even so with this Intelligence: no force comes unto it from a certain side nor from a certain distance, nor does its force reach elsewhere by a determined side, nor at a determined distance, nor at one time rather than another; on the contrary, its action is perpetual, and whenever a thing has been <i>disposed</i>[[fn:1575]], it receivs this ever-existing action which has been designated by the word <i>emanation.</i> Likewise, as the incorporeity of the Creator has been demonstrated, and it has been established that the universe is His work and that He is its efficient cause—as we have set forth and shall further set forth—it has been said that the world comes from the <i>emanation</i> of God, and that God has <i>poured forth</i> upon it all that therein occurrs[[fn:1576]]. In like manner it has been said that God has <i>poured forth</i> His knowledge upon the prophets. All this signifies that these actions are the work of an incorporeal Being; and it is the action of such a Being that is called <i>emanation.</i> The Hebrew tongue likewise has employed this word—I mean (the word) <i>emanation</i>—in speaking of God, by comparison with a fountain of water which <i>overflows</i>[[fn:1577]], as we have said.",
    "root.text.Part 2..11.3.sub_1": "Verily, no better expression could be found than that—I mean <i>fÉidh</i> (emanation)—to designate by comparison the action of the <i>Separate Being;</i> for we could not find a word truly corresponding to the real idea, the conception of the action of the <i>Separate Being</i> being a thing most difficult, even as difficult as the conception of the very existence of the <i>Separate Being.</i> Even as the imagination cannot conceive a being otherwise than as a body or as a force in a body, so it cannot conceive that an action can be exerted otherwise than by the contact of an agent, or at least at a certain (limited) distance and from a determined side. Now, as for certain men, even of the vulgar, it is an established thing that God is incorporeal, or even that He <i>draws not nigh</i> unto the thing which He makes, they have imagined that He gives His commands unto the angels, and that these perform the actions by contact and by a corporeal approach, as we ourselves act upon what we make; they have therefore imagined that the angels also are bodies. Some there be who believe that God commands the thing by speaking even as we speak—I mean by letters and sounds—and that then the thing is made[[fn:1578]]. All this is to follow the imagination, which is also, in truth, the <i>yécer ha-ra’</i> (the evil imagination)[[fn:1579]]; for every rational or moral vice is the work of the imagination or the consequence of its action. But this is not the purpose of this chapter. We rather intend to make understood what is meant by <i>emanation</i>, in speaking either of God, or of the Intelligences or the angels, who are incorporeal[[fn:1580]]. It is also said of the forces of the celestial spheres that they <i>emanate</i> upon the (lower) world, and it is said: 'the <i>emanation</i> of the celestial sphere,' although the effects produced by the latter come from a body, and for that reason the stars act at a determined distance—to wit, according as they are near to or far from the centre (of the world) and according to their mutual relation[[fn:1581]]. Herein is the first starting-point of judicial astrology[[fn:1582]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..11.4": "As to what we have stated, that the prophets likewise metaphorically presented the action of God through the idea of <i>efflux</i>, it is, for instance, in this passage: <i>They have forsaken Me</i>, <i>the Fount of living waters</i> (Jeremiah, 2, 13), which signifies <i>efflux of life</i>, that is to say, of <i>existence</i>, which, indubitably, is life. Similarly it has been stated: <i>For with Thee is the Fount of life</i> (Ps. 36, 10), which signifies the <i>efflux of existence;</i> and it is again the same idea which is expressed at the close of that passage by the words: <i>in Thy light we see light</i> (which signifies) that, through the efflux of the <i>intellect</i> (<i>active</i>) which has emanated from Thee, we cogitate, and thereby are we directed and guided[[fn:1583]] and apprehend the (active) intellect. Thou must impress this deeply upon thy mind.",
    "root.text.Part 2..12.0": "Touching the question whether the world be eternal or created, those who admit the existence of God have professed three different opinions[[fn:1584]]:",
    "root.text.Part 2..12.1": "I. The <i>first opinion</i>, embraced by all those who admit the Law of Moses our Master, is this: That the universe in its totality, I mean every being except God, was produced by God from pure and absolute non-existence; that there had existed at first only God alone, and naught beside Him, neither angel, nor sphere, nor that which is within the celestial sphere; that subsequently He produced all these beings, such as they are, by His free will and not <i>out of</i> any thing; finally, that time itself is likewise part of the created things, since it accompanies motion, which is an accident of the thing moved, and that this thing itself, whose motion time accompanies, was created and has <i>come into being</i> after having not existed. For if it be said, “God was before He created the world,”—where the word <i>was</i> denots a time,—and likewise if it follows thence for the mind[[fn:1585]] that His existence before the creation of the world was <i>prolonged</i> unto infinity, there is in all this naught but a supposition or imagination of time, and not the reality of time; for time is indubitably an accident, and it belongs, in our view, to the <i>created</i> accidents as much as blackness and whiteness. Although it be not of the species of <i>quality</i>[[fn:1586]], it is yet, in short, an accident inherent in motion, as is clear unto him who has understood what Aristotle says to explain time and its true being[[fn:1587]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..12.2": "We shall here give an explanation, which will be profitable for the subject we treat, albeit it does not relate thereto directly. That which (say we) has caused <i>time</i> to remain an obscure matter for the most part of the men of science, so that they have been undecided[[fn:1588]] — as for example Galen[[fn:1589]] and others — upon the question of whether it has, or has not, a real existence, is that it is an accident within another accident. Verily, the accidents which exist in bodies in an immediate manner, such as colours and tastes, are understood at first sight and the idea thereof is conceived. But the accidents whose <i>substrata</i> are other accidents, as, for example, the brightness in colour, or curvature and roundness in a line, are a very obscure thing, especially when there is joined thereto this circumstance, that the accident which serves as a <i>substratum</i> is not in a fixed state, but changs its condition[[fn:1590]]; for then the matter is more obscure. Now, in time, both things are united; for at first it is an accident inherent in motion, which is an accident in the thing moved; and subsequently motion is not in the condition of blackness or whiteness, which are somewhat fixed, but on the contrary, it is of the very essence of motion not to remain a single twinkling of an eye in the same state. It is this, therefore, which has caused time to remain an obscure thing.",
    "root.text.Part 2..12.3": "Our purpose is to establish that, for us, it is a thing created and <i>brought into existence</i>, like other accidents and like the substances which bear these accidents. Consequently, the production of the world by God could not have had a <i>temporal</i> beginning, time being itself part of the created things.",
    "root.text.Part 2..12.4": "Thou must meditate deeply upon this subject, so that thou be not exposed to the objections from which he who is ignorant of this cannot escape. Indeed, as soon as thou affirmest that there existed a <i>time</i> before the world, thou art obliged to admit <i>eternity;</i> for time being an accident, for which a <i>substratum</i> is necessarily required, it would follow thence that something existed before the existence of this world which now exists, and it is precisely that which we wish to avoid.",
    "root.text.Part 2..12.5": "Such then is one of the three opinions, which forms indubitably a fundamental principle of the Law of Moses our Master, the second principle after that of the Unity of God; and it must by no means occur to thy mind that it could be otherwise. It was our father Abraham who began to publish this opinion, unto which he had been led by speculation; wherefore he proclaimed <i>the name of the Lord, the Everlasting God</i>[[fn:1591]]. He clearly expressed this opinion in saying: <i>Possessor of heaven and earth</i> (Genesis, xiv, 22).",
    "root.text.Part 2..12.6": "II. The <i>second opinion</i> is that of all the philosophers of whom we have heard tell, or whose words we have seen. It is inadmissible, say they, that God should produce aught from nothing; nor is it possible, according to their view, that a thing be reduced to (absolute) non-existence; I mean to say, that it is not possible that any being whatsoever, having matter and form, should be born without matter having ever existed, nor that it should perish in such wise that matter itself be reduced to absolute non-existence[[fn:1592]]. To attribute unto God the power of (doing) such a thing would be, in their sight, as though one attributed unto Him the power of joining together at the same instant[[fn:1593]] two contraries, or of creating His like, or of becoming corporeal, or of creating a square whose diagonal should be equal to its side, or similar impossible things. What is implied in their words is that they mean to say that, even as He cannot be charged with impotence for not producing impossible things—for the impossible has a stable nature, which is not the work of an agent, and which, because of this, is invariable[[fn:1594]]—likewise one cannot attribute impotence unto Him because He should not be capable of producing aught from (absolute) non-existence; for that belongs to the category of all impossible things. They believe, therefore, that there exists a matter which is eternal even as God is eternal; that He exists not without it, nor it without Him. Nevertheless, they do not believe for all that that it occupies in being the same rank as God; but, on the contrary, God is (according to them) the <i>cause</i> wherefore it exists, and it is unto Him as the clay is unto the potter, or as the iron is unto the smith. He creats therein what He wills: now He forms of it heaven and earth, and now He forms of it somewhat else.",
    "root.text.Part 2..12.7": "The partisans of this opinion believe that the heaven also was born and is perishable, but that it was not born of nothing, nor is it to perish (in such wise as to return) to nothing. On the contrary, even as the individuals among animals are born and perish (issuing) from a matter which exists and (returning) to a matter which exists, likewise the heaven was born and must perish, and it is with its birth and its corruption even as with those of the other beings which are beneath it. Those who belong to this sect are divided into several classes, the divisions and opinions of which it is useless to mention in this treatise; but[[fn:1595]] the universal principle of this sect is that which I have told thee. Plato also professes this opinion; thou shalt find that Aristotle relats of him in the <i>Acroasis</i> (or the Physics) that he believed—that is to say, Plato—that the heaven was born and is perishable[[fn:1596]], and likewise thou shalt find his opinion clearly expressed in his book to Timaeus[[fn:1597]]. Nevertheless, he believs not that which we believe, as is thought by those who scrutinize not opinions and study not with care, and who imagine that our opinion and his are similar. It is not so at all; for we believe that the heaven was born, not of something, but of absolute non-existence, whilst he believs that it existed (virtually) and was formed of something[[fn:1598]]. Such is the second opinion.",
    "root.text.Part 2..12.8": "III. The <i>third opinion</i> is that of Aristotle, of his followers, and of the commentators of his works. He maintains, with the adepts of the sect of which mention has just been made, that no material thing can be produced without a (pre-existing) matter; but he maintains further that the heaven is in no wise subject to generation and corruption. Here is the summary of his opinion: He claims that this entire universe, even as it is, has always been and shall always be thus[[fn:1599]]; that the stable thing which is not subject to generation and corruption, that is to say, the heaven, never ceass to be such (as it is); that time and motion are eternal and permanent, without beginning or end; that that which is born and perishes, to wit, that which is beneath the sphere of the moon, continus ever thus [that is to say, that this prime matter in itself was not born and shall not perish, but that forms succeed one another therein, so that, being despoiled of one form, it invests itself with another]; finally, that this whole order (of the universe), the superior as well as the inferior, shall not be altered and shall not cease, that nothing new shall be produced therein which is not in its nature, and that there shall happen absolutely nothing[[fn:1600]] which departs from the rule.",
    "root.text.Part 2..12.9": "He says [for, although he expresses himself not in these terms, it is yet that which results from his opinion] that it is, in his view, of the category of the impossible that God should change His will or that there should come upon Him a new volition, and that this whole universe, even as it is, God has made to exist by His will, without however having made aught from nothing. Even as, he thinks, it is of the category of the impossible that God should cease to exist or that His essence should change, so is it of the category of the impossible that He should change His will or that there should come upon Him a new desire. It follows consequently that this whole universe, as it is now, even so has it been from all eternity and even so shall it be for evermore.",
    "root.text.Part 2..12.10": "Such is the summary of these opinions and their true sense; and these are the opinions of those for whom it is a thing demonstrated that there exists a God for this universe. As for those who have not acknowledged the existence of God, but who have thought that things are born and perish[[fn:1601]] by <i>aggregation</i> and <i>separation</i>[[fn:1602]], according to chance, and that there is no being who governs and orders the universe—and these are Epicurus, his sect, and those like unto him, as Alexander relats—it is of no utility for us to speak of these sects; for the existence of God has been demonstrated, and it would be useless to mention the opinions of people who have built their system upon a foundation which has already been overthrown by demonstration[[fn:1603]]. It would be equally useless for us to make efforts to establish the truth of what is said by the partisans of the second opinion, namely, that the heaven is <i>born</i> and that it is perishable; for those men admit the eternity (of matter), and there is no difference, in our view, between those who believe that the heaven was necessarily born of something and that it shall return thither in perishing, and the opinion of Aristotle, who believs that it was not <i>born</i> and that it shall not perish. In truth, all who follow the Law of Moses and of our father Abraham, or who walk in their footsteps, tend to naught else but this belief: that there exists absolutely no eternal thing beside God, and that to produce being from (absolute) non-existence is not for God of the category of the impossible; nay, in the opinion of certain thinkers, it is even a necessary thing[[fn:1604]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..12.11": "Having set forth the divers opinions, I proceed to expound in brief[[fn:1605]] the proofs of Aristotle which he adducs for his own opinion, and the manner in which he was led thereto.",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.0": "I need not reiterate in every chapter that I have composed this treatise for thee only because I am mindful of thy attainments[[fn:1606]]; it is therefore not necessary that I should everywhere cite the words of the philosophers verbatim, but it suffices to indicate the subjects thereof, without prolixity, and merely calling thy attention to the methods of demonstration which they had in view, even as I have done regarding the opinions of the <i>Mutakallimūn</i>. I shall pay no regard to those who, besides Aristotle, have reasoned upon these matters; for his opinions alone are those which demand examination, and if that which we object against him, or the doubt which we raise against him upon any point whatsoever, be well-founded[[fn:1607]], it will be yet more so and will have greater force with respect to all others who contradict the fundamental principles of the Law. — I say then[[fn:1608]]:",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.1": "I. Aristotle says that motion had no beginning, neither shall it perish — that is to say, motion <i><i>par excellence</i></i>[[fn:1609]] —; for, says he, if motion were newly come into existence, then, every newly-occurred thing being preceded by a motion, which is its transition from potentiality to actuality and its <i>becoming</i> after not having been, it would follow that there had already existed a motion, to wit, that by virtue of which this later motion exists. Therefore, the first motion is necessarily eternal; otherwise, the matter would regress to infinity[[fn:1610]]. Proceeding from this principle, he further says that time had no beginning, neither shall it perish; for time accompanies motion and is inherent therein, so that motion occurrs only in time, and time cannot be conceived save with motion, as has been demonstrated[[fn:1611]]. — This is a first method of his, from which one may conclude the eternity of the world.",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.2": "II. A <i>second method</i> of his is this: Prime matter, says he, common to the four elements, had no beginning, neither shall it perish; for, if prime matter had <i>come into being,</i> it would in its turn have a matter from which it was produced, whence it would follow that this <i>produced</i> matter would be endowed with form, which is the true condition of <i>becoming.</i> Now, as we have supposed it to be a matter not endowed with form, it necessarily follows that it is not born of anything; it is therefore eternal and shall not perish[[fn:1612]]. And this likewise leads to the eternity of the world[[fn:1613]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.3": "III. His <i>Third Method</i>: In the substance of the entire celestial sphere, he states, no species of <i>contrariety</i> exists, circular motion having no <i>contrary</i>, as has been expounded; contrariety, as has been demonstrated, occurs only in rectilinear motion[[fn:1614]]. Now, he continues, everything that perishes has for the cause of its perdition only the contrariety inherent within it; but, as there is no contrariety in the celestial sphere, the latter does not perish[[fn:1615]], and that which does not perish is likewise not <i>generated</i>. For he enunciates absolutely the following propositions: all that is generated is perishable; all that is perishable is generated; all that is not generated is not perishable; all that is not perishable is not generated[[fn:1616]]. This, then, is yet another method by which he arrives, as is his object, at establishing the eternity of the world[[fn:1617]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.4": "IV. <i>Fourth method</i>: In everything that comes into being (or is born), says he, the possibility of its coming into being temporally preceds that which comes into being; and likewise, in all that changs, the possibility of changing temporally preceds the change. From this proposition he concluds that circular motion is perpetual and has neither end nor beginning; and it is also by this proposition that his modern followers have explained the eternity of the world[[fn:1618]]. Ere the world was, say they, its birth must needs have been either possible, or necessary, or impossible; now, if its birth were <i>necessary</i>, it has always existed; if its birth were <i>impossible</i>, it could never have existed; finally, if it were <i>possible</i>, what then would be the <i>substratum</i> of this possibility? It was therefore necessary that there should exist something which should be the <i>substratum</i> of the possibility, and whereby the thing in question might be said to be <i>possible.</i> — This is a most puissant method for establishing the eternity of the world.",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.5": "Some of the most discerning among the modern <i>Mutakallemīn</i> have sought to resolve the difficulty by saying: possibility resids in the agent and not in the object of the action. But this is void of significance; for there be two distinct possibilities. Verily, in everything that is born, the possibility of being born is anterior to the birth; and likewise, in the agent who has caused it to be born, the possibility of bringing such a thing into being existed before he brought it into being; there be therefore, indubitably, two possibilities: a possibility in the matter, (that) of <i>becoming</i> such a thing, and a possibility in the agent, (that) of <i>doing</i> such a thing.",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.6": "Such are the principal methods followed by Aristotle to establish the eternity of the world, by taking the world itself as his starting-point.",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.7": "But there be certain other methods, mentioned by his successors, who have derived them from his philosophy, and in which they establish the eternity of the world by taking God as their starting-point.",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.8": "V. One of these (is as follows): If, say they, God had produced the world out of nothing, God would have been, before creating the world, an agent in potentiality, and by creating it, He would have become an agent in actuality. God would therefore have passed from potentiality to actuality, and, consequently, there would have been in Him a <i>possibility</i> and He would have required an efficient cause to have made Him pass from potentiality to actuality[[fn:1619]]. — This is again a great difficulty, upon which every intelligent man must meditate, in order to resolve it and to fathom its mystery[[fn:1620]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.9": "VI. <i>Another method:</i> If an agent, say they, at one time acts and at another time acts not, this can only be by reason of obstacles or wants[[fn:1621]] which supervene unto him or which are within him; for obstacles constrain him to desist from doing that which he would have willed, and wants[[fn:1622]] constrain him to will that which he had not willed before. Now, whereas the Creator has no wants that might bring about a change of will, and as there are for Him neither hindrances nor obstacles that might supervene or cease, there is no reason why He should act at one time and act not at another time; on the contrary, His action must perpetually exist in actuality, even as He is Himself perpetual.",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.10": "VII. <i>Another method:</i> The works of God, they say, are very perfect, and there is nothing defective, useless, or superfluous in them. This is what Aristotle continually repeats, saying: natura est sapientia the nature is wise and does nothing in vain, but makes each thing in the most perfect manner possible[[fn:1623]]. From this, they say, it follows that this universe is the most perfect of all, and there is nothing that surpasses it[[fn:1624]]; therefore, it must be eternal, for the wisdom of God is eternal as His essence, or rather His essence is (itself) His wisdom which has required the existence of this universe.",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.11": "All that thou shalt find, in the way of argumentations (proceeding) from those who admit the eternity of the world, derivs from these methods and may be reduced to one of them.",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.12": "They say further, as if to reduce the matter to an absurdity: How could it be that God had been idle, doing absolutely naught and producing naught throughout all past eternity[[fn:1625]], and that after having done naught during the whole duration of His eternal existence which is without end, He should since yesterday have commenced (to create) the universe? For, even shouldst thou say, for example, that before this world God created many others, as numerous as the grains of mustard-seed which the globe of the utmost sphere might contain, and that each of these worlds has existed for as many years as that same content of grains of mustard-seed, all this would still be, in comparison with the infinite existence of God, as though thou saidst that it was but yesterday that God created the world. Verily, so soon as we affirm that the universe <i>commenced</i> after absolute non-existence, it matters little whether thou admittest that this took place hundreds of thousands of years ago[[fn:1626]] or from a time most nigh; for they who admit the eternity (of the world) find this equally absurd.",
    "root.text.Part 2..13.13": "Argument has been made also from that which has at all times been generally received[[fn:1627]] by all peoples, and whence it results that the thing is <i>natural</i> and not (merely) hypothetical; so that men have come to an agreement in this regard. All men, says Aristotle, openly acknowledge the perpetuity and the stability of the heaven, and, as they have perceived that it is not born and that it is likewise not perishable, they have made of it the dwelling-place of God and of spiritual beings, that is to say, of the angels; they have attributed it to God to indicate His perpetuity[[fn:1628]]. He allegs, in the same chapter, other things of this kind, in order to strengthen, by <i>probable opinions</i>[[fn:1629]], the opinion which speculation had caused him to recognize as true.",
    "root.text.Part 2..14.0": "My aim, in this chapter, is to set forth that Aristotle possesses no demonstration touching the eternity of the world according to his opinion.",
    "root.text.Part 2..14.1": "He does not even deceive himself in this matter; I mean that he himself knows that he has no demonstration thereof, and that those argumentations and those proofs which he adducs are but those which possess the greatest semblance of truth and toward which the soul most strongly inclins. They are indeed, as Alexander maintains, those which offer the fewest doubts; yet it must by no means be believed that an Aristotle could have taken these reasonings for a demonstration, since it was Aristotle himself who taught unto men the methods of true demonstration, its rules and its conditions.",
    "root.text.Part 2..14.2": "— That which has induced me to speak of this is that the moderns among the followers of Aristotle claim that he has <i>demonstrated</i> the eternity of the world. Most of those who profess to be philosophers do therefore follow, in this question, the authority of Aristotle, believing that all which he has said is a decisive demonstration in which there is naught of doubt; and they even deem it absurd to gainsay him, or to suppose that anything could have remained hidden[[fn:1630]] from him, or that he could have erred in any matter whatsoever.",
    "root.text.Part 2..14.3": "Wherefore I have deemed it my duty to proceed with them according to their own opinion, and to show them that Aristotle himself does not pretend to give a demonstration upon this question. Thus, for example, he says in the <i>Acroasis</i>: “All the physicists who have preceded us believed that motion had no beginning and is imperishable, with the exception of Plato, who believed that motion had a beginning and is perishable; and likewise the heavens, according to him, had a beginning and are perishable.” Such are his expressions[[fn:1631]]. Now, it is clear that if this question had been demonstrated by rigorous demonstrations, Aristotle would have had no need to support it by the concordant opinion of the ancient physicists[[fn:1632]], nor would he have had need to say all that he has said in the same place to show the absurdity of those who gainsay him and to render their opinion contemptible; for, as soon as a thing is demonstrated, its truth cannot increase, nor can its certainty be strengthened by the common consent of all the learned, and on the other hand, its truth cannot diminish, nor its certainty be weakened, by the contradiction of all the inhabitants of the earth. Thou shalt also find that Aristotle, in the treatise <i>On the Heavens and the World</i>, where he beginns to set forth that the heavens had no beginning and are imperishable, expresses himself thus: “We wish therefore, after this[[fn:1633]], further to make researches concerning the heavens, and we say: dost thou believe they were created from something, or that they were not? that they are subject to corruption, or that they shall never perish[[fn:1634]]?” After having posed this question, desiring (as he says) to report the arguments of those who say that the heavens had a beginning[[fn:1635]], he continus in the following terms: “When we shall have done this, our words will be received with more benevolence by those who excel in speculation; especially when they shall first have heard the arguments of the adversaries. For if, without reporting the arguments of our adversaries, we were to state only our own opinion and our arguments, these would appear to the hearers too weak to be accepted. It is worthy of him who wishes to judge with truth not to be hostile toward him who contradicts him; he ought, on the contrary, to be benevolent and impartial toward him, rendering justice to his arguments as to his own[[fn:1636]].” Such are the textual words of this man.",
    "root.text.Part 2..14.4": "And now, O all ye who are thinkers! is there yet, after this preliminary declaration, aught for which to blame this man? Shall it still be believed, after such words, that he possessed a demonstration upon this question? Can any man whatsoever, and much more so Aristotle, imagine that a thing which has been <i>demonstrated</i> could be feebly received if one has not heard the arguments of those who gainsay it? — Furthermore, Aristotle declaring that this is an opinion[[fn:1637]] of his own and that his proofs thereupon are but dialectical argumentations, — is it an Aristotle who could be ignorant of the difference between argumentations and demonstrations, between opinions which appear to the mind strong or weak and demonstrative things? Finally, this oratorical expression of <i>impartiality toward the adversary</i>, which he adds as if to strengthen his opinion! is there need of all this in a demonstration? Certes, no; but all that he has in view is to show that his opinion is truer than that of his adversaries, or of those who claim that philosophical speculation leads to the admission that the heavens are subject to birth and corruption, but that nevertheless they were never absolutely non-existent, — or that they were <i>formed</i> from something, — and that they shall not absolutely perish[[fn:1638]]; and other similar things which he reports of these opinions. And this is indubitably true; for indeed his opinion is nearer to the truth than theirs, when one seeks to argue from the nature of being. But we do not think thus[[fn:1639]], as I shall set forth. Nevertheless, all the sects, and even the philosophers, have suffered themselves to be led away by passions, so that they have wished to establish that Aristotle has demonstrated this question. Perchance, according to their opinion, Aristotle made a demonstration upon this question without perceiving it himself[[fn:1640]], so that it was only after him that the remark was made! — As for me, it seems to me beyond doubt that all the opinions which Aristotle expresses upon these subjects, — I mean the eternity of the world, the cause of the varied motions of the spheres, and the order of the Intelligences, — that all this, I say, is not susceptible of a demonstration. Thus Aristotle never had the thought that these reasonings could be considered a demonstration; on the contrary, as he himself says, we have no means of approaching these things by demonstrative methods[[fn:1641]], and they possess for us no principle from which we might argue[[fn:1642]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..14.5": "Thou art acquainted with the text of his words, which run thus: 'And there are (problems) concerning which we have no argument, or which appear grave unto us; for it is difficult for us to declare the <i>why</i> thereof, as for instance the question whether the world be eternal or not.'[[fn:1643]] Such are his expressions. But thou knowest how Abu-Naçr (al-Farâbi) interpreted this example, what explanation he gave thereof, and how he repelled (the notion) that Aristotle could have doubted the eternity of the world.[[fn:1644]] He speaks of Galen with sovereign contempt, because the latter had said that this is an obscure question for which no demonstration is known.[[fn:1645]] Abu-Naçr thinks that it is a clear and evident thing, susceptible of a (rigorous) demonstration, that the heaven is eternal, and that what is within it is subject to birth and corruption.",
    "root.text.Part 2..14.6": "In sum, it is not by any of the manners which we have related in this chapter that an opinion may be confirmed, or destroyed, or called into doubt.[[fn:1646]]",
    "root.text.Part 2..14.7": "We have made these citations only because we know that most of those who pretend to be geniuses, albeit they understand no science, pronounce upon the eternity of the world by following the authority of the celebrated scholars who have proclaimed its eternity, and reject the words of all the prophets, because these are not conceived in the didactic style,[[fn:1647]] but in that of a proclamation on the part of God. In this path (of the prophets) are guided only a few whom intelligence has favoured.",
    "root.text.Part 2..14.8": "That which we desire (to establish), for our part, relative to the <i>creation of the world</i>, according to the opinion of our Law, I shall declare in the following chapters.",
    "root.text.Part 2..15.0": "Behold a chapter in which I shall set forth unto thee my thoughts upon this question, and thereafter I shall allege proofs for that which we desire (to establish). I say, therefore, concerning all that is uttered by those of the <i>Mutakallimun</i> who claim to have demonstrated the creation of the world, that I accept not those proofs, and that I will not deceive myself by decorating sophistical methods with the name of <i>demonstrations.</i> If a man claims to demonstrate a certain question by sophisms, he does not, in my judgment, strengthen the belief in that thing which is sought, but, on the contrary, he weakens it and gives occasion to contest the matter; for the nullity of these proofs having become manifest, the soul refuss to believe[[fn:1648]] what one has sought to prove. It were better that the thing upon which there is no demonstration should remain simply in the state of a question, or that one should accept (by tradition)[[fn:1649]] one of the two terms of the contradiction. I have already related the methods whereby the <i>Mutakallimun</i> establish the creation of the world,[[fn:1650]] and I have called thy attention to the criticism to which they give rise. Likewise, all that Aristotle and his successors have said to prove the eternity of the world is not, in my judgment, a rigorous demonstration; they are, on the contrary, but argumentations subject to grave doubts, as thou shalt hear (hereafter).",
    "root.text.Part 2..15.1": "What I desire to effect, for my part, is to demonstrate that the creation of the world ex nihilo, in accordance with the opinion of our Law which I have already set forth, is by no means impossible, and that all those philosophical disputations, wherefrom it seems to follow that the matter is not as we have stated[[fn:1651]],—that all these reasonings (I say) have a side whereby one may demolish them and prevent their being employed as arguments against us. This being ascertained by me, and this question,—to wit, whether the world be eternal or created,—remaining undetermined[[fn:1652]], I accept the solution afforded by <i>prophecy</i>[[fn:1653]], which explains things whereunto the speculative faculty could not attain; for we shall set forth that prophecy is no vain thing, even according to the opinion of him who admitts the eternity (of the world).",
    "root.text.Part 2..15.2": "Having set forth that what we maintain is possible, I shall likewise endeavour, by speculative proof, to make it prevail[[fn:1654]] over the other (opinion); I mean, to make the opinion of <i>creation</i> prevail over that of <i>eternity.</i> I shall show that, if we be led to some absurd consequence by admitting creation, one is driven to a yet more grievous absurdity by admitting eternity.",
    "root.text.Part 2..15.3": "And now I shall attempt to present a method for demolishing the proofs of all those who argue in favour of the eternity of the world.",
    "root.text.Part 2..16.0": "Every new thing that comes into being after having not existed,—albeit its matter existed and the latter did but divest itself of one form and invest itself with another,—possesses, after it is born, perfected, and arrived at its final state, a nature other than that which it had at the moment when it was being born and began to pass from potentiality into actuality, and different also from that which it had before moving to pass into actuality[[fn:1655]]. Thus, for example, the female semen, whilst it is as yet but blood in the vessels, has a nature different from that which it has at the moment of conception, when it has been touched by the male semen and beginns to move; and the nature which it has in that moment is likewise different from that of the perfect animal after its birth. One cannot in any wise argue from the nature which a thing has, after it is born, perfected, and arrived finally at its most perfect state, concerning the state in which that thing was at the moment when it was moving towards birth. Nor can one argue from the state in which it was at the moment of moving concerning that in which it was before it began to move. So soon as thou errest therein and persistest in arguing from the nature of a thing arrived at actuality concerning that which it had whilst being in potentiality, grave doubts arise within thee; things which must be appear unto thee absurd[[fn:1656]], and absurd things seem unto thee as though they must be.",
    "root.text.Part 2..16.1.sub_0": "Let there be made, touching the example which we have alleged, the following supposition[[fn:1657]]: A man was born with a most perfect disposition[[fn:1658]]; his mother having died after having suckled him for some months, the husband[[fn:1659]] alone occupied himself, in a retired island, with completing the education of this child, until he had grown up and become intelligent and instructed. Having never seen either woman or any female of the animals, he asked one day of one of the men who were with him: “How comes it that we exist, and in what manner were we formed?” He to whom he had addressed the question replied: “Every one of us was formed in the womb of an individual of our species, like unto ourselves, and who was a woman having such and such a form; every one of us was a small body in the interior of the womb, moving, feeding, growing little by little, living, until, arrived at such a limit of greatness, there opened for him, in the lower part of the body (of the woman), a door through which he appeared and came forth, and after that he ceased not to grow until he became such as thou seest us.” This orphan child will necessarily inquire anew and say: “This individual among us, whilst he was small in the womb, living, moving, and growing, did he eat? did he drink? did he breathe by the mouth and the nose? did he void excrements?” — Nay, it will be answered him. — But he will undoubtedly hasten to deny this, and will demonstrate the impossibility of all these things, which nevertheless are true[[fn:1660]], by arguing from the perfect being arrived at its final state. “If one of us,” he will say, “were for some moments deprived of breath, he would die, and his movements would cease; and how then can one figure to oneself that any of us could remain for months within a thick membrane[[fn:1661]], enclosed in the interior of a body, and withal live and move? If one of us could swallow a sparrow, verily, that sparrow would die instantaneously as soon as it arrived in the stomach, and much more so in the lower belly. Every one of us, if he took no food by the mouth and if he drank not, would undoubtedly die at the end of a few days; and how then could an individual remain for months without eating or drinking?”",
    "root.text.Part 2..16.1.sub_1": "“If any one of us, after having taken food, were not to void the excrements, he would perish in a few days in the most grievous pains; how then could that being remain for months without voiding any excrement? If one were to pierce the belly of any of us, he would die within a few days; how then can it be believed that this foetus has the umbilicus open? how, finally, does it come to pass that he opens not his eyes, nor extends his hands, nor stretches forth his feet, as ye assert, seeing that all his members are in good condition and suffer no malady?” — And thus he will pursue his reasonings, to prove that it is impossible for man to be formed in such a manner.",
    "root.text.Part 2..16.2": "Examine well this example and reflect thereon, O thou who seekest to think! and thou shalt find that such is likewise our condition in respect of Aristotle. Indeed, all we, the followers of Moses our Master and of Abraham our Father, believe that the world was formed in such and such a manner, that it developed in such a manner[[fn:1662]], and that one thing was created after another; but Aristotle setts himself to contradict us, arguing against us from the nature of being as it has attained its final state, perfect and existing in act, whilst we, on our part, affirm unto him that after having attained its final state and become perfect, it resembls in no wise that which it was at the moment of its birth, and that it was produced out of absolute non-existence. What argument, therefore, can be drawn against us from all that he says? for these arguments strike only him who claims that it is the nature of this being, having reached its final state, which proves of itself that it was <i>created,</i> whereas I have already made known unto thee that, for my part, I do not maintain this.",
    "root.text.Part 2..16.3": "I shall now resume the principles of his methods[[fn:1663]], and I will show thee how there follows therefrom absolutely nothing against us, who maintain that God produced the whole world out of nothing, and formed it (successively) until it became perfect, even as thou seest it.",
    "root.text.Part 2..16.4": "The First Matter, says he, has neither been born nor shall it perish; and, arguing from things born and perishable, he shows that it is impossible it should have been born[[fn:1664]]. And this is true[[fn:1665]]. For we do not maintain that the first matter was formed as man is formed from the semen, nor that it must perish as man perishes by becoming dust; but we maintain, on the contrary, that God produced it out of nothing, and that after its production it is such as it is[[fn:1666]],—I mean to say, that all things are formed therefrom, and that all which is formed therefrom returns thereto in perishing. It exists not divested of form[[fn:1667]], and it is the term of generation and of corruption. As for itself, it was not born (<i>of</i> anything), as everything is born which is formed from it, and it shall not perish (<i>into</i> anything), as that perishes which perishes within it; but on the contrary, it is a thing <i>created,</i> and whenever its Creator shall will it, He shall reduce it to pure and absolute non-existence. We shall say precisely the same thing concerning motion; for arguments have been drawn from the nature of motion to prove that it was not born and shall not perish[[fn:1668]]. And this is likewise true[[fn:1669]]; for we maintain that it is inconceivable that, since motion has existed with its invariable and fixed nature, it could, in its universality, be subject to birth and corruption, even as are the partial motions which are born and perish[[fn:1670]]. The same reasoning applies to all that is inherent in the nature of motion[[fn:1671]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..16.5": "Likewise, when he says of circular motion that it has no beginning, this is true (in the sense) that after the <i>production</i> of the spherical body, which movs circularly, one could not imagine in its motion any beginning whatsoever[[fn:1672]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..16.6": "We shall say the like concerning the <i>possibility</i> which must precede all things brought into existence[[fn:1673]]; for this is necessary only in this (completely) established universe, in which everything that is born proceeds from some being or other. But a thing produced from <i>nothingness</i> does not indicate, whether to the senses or to the intellect, any (anterior) thing, such as would require it to be preceded by a possibility.",
    "root.text.Part 2..16.7": "Finally, we shall reason in the same manner concerning (that which he says) that in the heavens there is no <i>contrariety</i>[[fn:1674]]. This is likewise true; only (it must be noted) that we do not maintain that the heavens were formed as the horse and the palm-tree are formed, and (consequently) we do not maintain that, being <i>composite</i>, they must perish like plants and animals, by reason of the contrariety which would exist therein[[fn:1675]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..16.8": "The essence of the matter is that which we have stated, (namely) that a being being in its perfect and completed state, one cannot reason from its actual state to the state (in which it was)[[fn:1676]] before its perfection.",
    "root.text.Part 2..16.9": "Neither do we find aught of absurdity in that which has been said, that the heavens were formed before the earth, or the earth before the heavens[[fn:1677]], or that the heavens were at first without stars, or (that there existed) such a species of animals without another; for all this applies to the period when this whole (of the universe) was being formed. It is even as with the animal during its formation, the heart being formed before the testicles, as is manifest to the sight, and the veins before the bones, although, in its perfect state, none of its members exists independently of all the others, without which the preservation of the individual were impossible. All this must also (be admitted), if one takes the text (of Scripture) in its literal sense, although it be not so, as shall be explained when we treat thereof more at length[[fn:1678]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..16.10": "— Thou must give good heed to this subject[[fn:1679]]; for it is a great wall that I have built around the Law, and which encompasses it to protect it against the stones cast at it. If Aristotle, — I mean he who adopts his opinion, — should argue against us, saying: Since no proof can be drawn from this (finished) universe, how then do ye yourselves know that it was <i>created</i> and that there was another nature[[fn:1680]] which created it? we should reply: This does not affect us in respect of our present purpose[[fn:1681]]. Verily, we do not seek now to establish that the world was created; but what we desire is (to show) that it is possible it should have been created; and the falsehood of this assertion cannot be demonstrated by arguing from the nature of the universe, with which we do not place ourselves in opposition[[fn:1682]]. The possibility of this assertion being established, as we have set forth, we shall afterwards endeavour to make the opinion of <i>creation</i> prevail. There would then remain in this regard (no other means of refuting us) than to demonstrate to us the impossibility of the creation of the world, not by the nature of the universe, but by that which the intellect judges to be necessary in respect of God; and these are the three methods of which I have spoken to thee previously, and by which men seek to demonstrate the eternity of the world by taking God as the point of departure[[fn:1683]]. I shall therefore show thee, in the following chapter, in what manner they may be called into doubt, so that no proof may result therefrom.",
    "root.text.Part 2..17.0": "The first method of which they speak is that in which they pretend to establish that, according to our view[[fn:1684]], God must have passed from potentiality to actuality, since He would have acted at one certain moment and not at another[[fn:1685]]. — It shall be very easy to refute this objection[[fn:1686]]: For, in truth, one can only reason in this wise[[fn:1687]] concerning that which is composed of matter in a state of <i>possibility</i> and of a form. Without any doubt, if such a body acts by its form after having been inactive, there has been in it something in potentiality which has passed into actuality, and, consequently, it has stood in need of an efficient cause; for, as touching things endowed with matter, this is a demonstrated proposition. But that which is incorporeal and immaterial has in its essence no <i>possibility</i>, and all that is within it is perpetually in act. One cannot, therefore, apply to it the reasoning in question, and for it, it is by no means impossible that at one time it acts and at another it acts not. For the <i>separate</i> being, this is neither a change nor a passage from potentiality to actuality.",
    "root.text.Part 2..17.1": "We have a proof thereof in the <i>Active Intellect</i>, which, according to the opinion of Aristotle and his followers, is <i>separate</i>, and which, nevertheless, at one time acts and at another acts not, as Abu-Naṣr has set forth in his treatise <i>on the Intellect</i>[[fn:1688]]. He expresses himself therein in these terms: “It is evident that the Active Intellect does not act perpetually; but, on the contrary, at one time it acts and at another it acts not.” These be his very words, and it is evidently the truth. But, although it be thus, it is not said, however, that the Active Intellect is subject to change, nor that after having been an agent in potentiality, it has become so in act, because it might have done at a certain moment what it had not done before; for there is no relation between bodies and that which is incorporeal, and there is no similitude between them, neither at the moment of action, nor at the moment when they abstain from acting. If the action of material forms and that of the <i>separate</i> being are both called <i>action</i>, it is only by homonymy; wherefore, if the <i>separate</i> being performs not at a certain moment the action which it will perform later, it does not follow therefrom that it will have passed from potentiality to actuality, as we find it to be in material forms.",
    "root.text.Part 2..17.2": "Perchance it may be believed that in what I have just said there resids some sophistry: If, it will be said, the Active Intellect necessarily acts at one certain moment and acts not at another, this is not because of aught inherent in its essence, but because of the disposition of the materials; on its part, the action is perpetually exercised upon all that is <i>disposed</i>, and if there be aught that hinders the action, that proceeds from the <i>disposition</i> of the matter, and not from the Intellect in itself[[fn:1689]].— Let him therefore who thus thinks[[fn:1690]] well know that our purpose is not to make known the cause for which God acted at one certain moment and not at another; and, in citing this example, we have not concluded therefrom that, since the Active Intellect, which is <i>separate</i>, acts at one time and acts not at another, it must be the same with God[[fn:1691]]. We have not said that, nor have we made such a conclusion; and had we done so, it would indeed have been a sophistry. But what we have concluded therefrom—and it is a true conclusion—is that, although the Active Intellect, which is neither a body nor a force in a body, acts at one certain moment and performs not the same action at another moment, no matter what the cause thereof may be, it is not said on that account that it has passed from potentiality to actuality, nor that there has been in its essence any <i>possibility</i>, nor finally that it has need of an efficient cause to make it pass from potentiality to actuality[[fn:1692]]. Thus is set aside from us that grave objection which has been made against us by those who maintain the eternity of the world; for, as we believe that God is neither a body, nor a force in a body, He is in no wise affected by <i>change</i> in acting after having not acted.",
    "root.text.Part 2..17.3": "The second method is that in which the eternity of the world is concluded from the fact that for God there are neither needs, nor aught that supervens, nor obstacles[[fn:1693]]. The solution of this objection is difficult and withal subtle; hearken thou unto it.",
    "root.text.Part 2..17.4": "Know thou that every agent who possesses a will and who acts for any reason whatsoever must needs act at one time and not act at another, by reason of certain obstacles or needs that supervene. Thus, for example, a certain man who would desire to possess a house will nevertheless not build one, by reason of hindrances, whether it be that he has not the materials at hand, or that these, prepared though they be, are not ready[[fn:1694]] to receive the form, by reason of a lack of instruments. It may also be that the materials and the instruments are ready, and that nevertheless the man builds not, because, having no need of a dwelling, he wills not to build; but when there shall supervene accidents, such as heat or cold, which shall constrain him to seek a shelter, then will he desire to build. It is therefore clear that supervening accidents change the will, and that obstacles oppose the will in such wise that one cannot act. Nevertheless, all this only takes place when actions have for their reason something outside the will itself. But when the action has absolutely no other end than that of obeying a will, that will has no need of an invitation from without; and in such a case it is not necessary either that he who possesses the will, whilst having no obstacles, should always act; for he has no external end which causs him to act, in such a manner that he should be forced to act so soon as there were no obstacles to reaching the end, since the action, in this case, obeys the will alone.",
    "root.text.Part 2..17.5": "It might be objected against us: All this is true; but is there not a change in this very fact, that at one time He <i>wills</i> and at another He wills not[[fn:1695]]? To this we shall reply: Nay; for that which constituts the true idea of the will is to will and not to will. Now, if this will belongs to a material being, and that which is sought thereby be an external end, it shall be a will subject to change, by reason of obstacles and of that which may betide; but the will of the <i>separate</i> being, which is in no wise determined by aught else, is not subject to change; and, if He wills now one thing and on the morrow another thing, that constituts no change in His essence, nor does it require another cause (outside of Him), even as there is no change in that He at one time acts and at another time acts not, as we have already set forth. It shall be shown (further on)[[fn:1696]] that it is only by homonymy that the name <i>will</i> is applied both to our will and to that of the separate being, and that there is no similitude between the two wills. — Thus, then, this objection is likewise destroyed, and it is manifest that naught results for us from this method which is inadmissible[[fn:1697]]. This is what we desired (to obtain), as thou knowest.",
    "root.text.Part 2..17.6": "The third method is that whereby the eternity of the world is proved (by reasoning) thus: When the (Divine) wisdom decids that a thing must appear, it has appeared[[fn:1698]]; now, the wisdom of God being eternal as His essence, that which results therefrom is (likewise) eternal[[fn:1699]]. — But this is a very feeble reasoning; for, even as we are ignorant why His wisdom required that the spheres should be nine (in number), neither more nor less, that the stars should be as numerous as they are, neither more nor less, and (that they should be) neither greater nor smaller, even so we are ignorant why the wisdom, at a (relatively) recent epoch, caused the universe to exist after having not existed[[fn:1700]]. All things conform to His perpetual and invariable wisdom; but we, for our part, are utterly ignorant of the law of this wisdom and of what it requirs[[fn:1701]]. For, according to our opinion, the will likewise conforms to the wisdom; all (in God) is one and the same thing, I mean to say that His wisdom is His essence, for we admit not attributes[[fn:1702]]. Thou shalt hear much upon this subject when we speak of Providence[[fn:1703]]. — By this consideration, therefore, this absurdity (attributed to us) likewise falls[[fn:1704]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..17.7": "As for what Aristotle says, that the peoples, in ancient times, believed with one accord that the angels dwelt in heaven, and that God also was in heaven[[fn:1705]], — a thing which the literal sense of the (sacred) texts also says, — this cannot serve as a proof for the eternity of the world, as he himself wills; but it was said to prove that the heaven indicats to us the existence of the separate Intelligences, which are the spiritual beings and the angels, and that it indicats to us also the existence of God, who setts it in motion and who governs it, as we shall set forth. We shall show that there is no proof which better demonstrats to us the existence of the Creator, according to our opinion[[fn:1706]], than that drawn from the heaven; and the latter, as we have already said, proves also, according to the opinion of the philosophers, that there exists (a Being) who setts it in motion, and that this latter is neither a body nor a force in a body.",
    "root.text.Part 2..17.8": "After having shown thee that what we affirm is admissible, and that (at the least) it is not an impossible thing, as is pretended by those who maintain the eternity (of the world), I shall show, in the following chapters, that our opinion is preferable from the speculative point of view, and I shall reveal the absurd consequences which the other opinion has[[fn:1707]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.0": "[[fn:1708]]It follows evidently from the system of Aristotle, as from the system of all those who profess the eternity of the world, that according to him this universe is emanated from the Creator <i>by necessity</i>, that God is the cause and this world the effect, and that, consequently, the latter is <i>necessary</i>[[fn:1709]]. Even as one could not say of God why He exists, nor how He exists <i>thus</i>, I mean <i>one</i> and incorporeal, so one could not say of the universe in its entirety why it exists nor how it exists thus (as we see it); for it is necessary that all this exist thus, (I mean) the cause and its effect; and it is impossible for both not to exist or to become other than they are. It follows then from this opinion that every thing must necessarily preserve always the nature which it has, and that no thing can, in any wise, change its nature. According to this opinion, the change of nature of any being whatsoever is an impossible thing, and, consequently, all these things could not have been born by the design of a being having an intention and who would have freely willed that they should be thus; for, if they had been born by such a design, they would not have existed thus before the design was determined[[fn:1710]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.1": "But, according to our own opinion, it is manifest that things exist by virtue of a design, and not by necessity. It might therefore be that He who formed the design should change it and form another design. Nevertheless, this could not be, in an absolute sense, <i>any design whatsoever;</i> for there is a nature of the impossible which is stable and which cannot be destroyed[[fn:1711]], as we shall set forth. — My purpose, in this chapter, is to show thee, by proofs which approach <i>demonstration</i>, that this universe necessarily indicates to us a Creator acting with intention[[fn:1712]], without my wishing for all that to undertake what the <i>Mutakallimūn</i> have attempted, by destroying the nature of being and by proclaiming the atom, the perpetual creation of accidents, and all that I have exposed to thee of their principles, whose sole aim is to establish <i>determination</i>[[fn:1713]]. One must not believe that they have also said what I am about to say; but what cannot be doubted is that they have aimed at the same goal as I. They speak also, then, of the things of which I shall speak, having in view <i>determination;</i> but for them, if such a plant be rather red than white, rather sweet than bitter, it is a <i>particularization</i> on the same ground as that of the heaven having by preference that figure which it has, and not a square or triangular figure[[fn:1714]]. They, verily, have established <i>determination</i> by means of their propositions which thou already knowest[[fn:1715]], whilst I shall establish determination, where it is necessary, by means of philosophical propositions drawn from the nature of being.",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.2": "I shall set forth this method, after having first laid down as a principle what follows: whenever a matter[[fn:1716]] is common to things which differ among themselves in any manner whatsoever, there must necessarily have been, apart from this common matter, a cause which brought it to pass that these things should have, some such quality, and others such another, or rather (there must have been) as many causes as there are different things. This is a proposition upon which the partisans of the eternity (of the world) and those of creation do agree.",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.3": "Having laid down this principle, I enter upon the exposition of that which I had in view, (discussing) in the form of question and answer, concerning the opinion of Aristotle.",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.4": "We first address to Aristotle the following question: Thou hast demonstrated to us that all sublunary things have one and the same matter, common to all; what then is the cause of the diversity of species which exist here below, and what is the cause of the diversity of individuals of each of these species? — Thereupon, he will answer us: That which causs the diversity is that the things composed of this matter differ in <i>mixture</i>. This common matter first received four forms, each of which is accompanied by two <i>qualities</i>[[fn:1717]], and by these four qualities it becoms the <i>elements</i> of that which is composed thereof[[fn:1718]]; for they (the elements) commingle first in consequence of the motion of the celestial sphere, and thereafter, having formed a tempered mixture[[fn:1719]], diversity ariss in the mixed things, which are composed (of the elements) in different degrees of heat, cold, moisture, and dryness. Through these diverse mixtures, it (the matter) acquirs diverse dispositions for receiving diverse forms, and these forms, in their turn, dispose it for the reception of other forms, and so forth. The matter (substratum) of a single specific form possesses a great latitude of quantity and quality, and it is by reason of this latitude that there is a variety of individuals of the same species, even as has been set forth in physical science. — All this is true and manifest for him who is equitable towards himself and who desirs not to deceive himself.",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.5": "Next, we shall address once more to Aristotle this other question: If it be true that the mixture of the elements is the cause which disposs the matters to receive the diverse forms, what was it then that disposed this first matter in such wise that one part should receive the form of <i>fire</i>, and another part the form of <i>earth</i>, and that that which is between the two (should become apt) to receive the form of <i>water</i> and of <i>air?</i> Since the whole has a common matter, what was it then that rendered the matter of the earth more fit for the form of <i>earth</i>, and the matter of fire more fit for the form of <i>fire?</i>",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.6": "— To this Aristotle would make the following reply: That which has effected this is the difference of positions[[fn:1720]]; for it is these which have produced in the single matter diverse dispositions. The portion which is nigher to the circumference has received from it an impression of subtilty and rapid motion, and approaches its nature, so that, being thus prepared, it has received the form of <i>fire;</i> but, in proportion as the matter receds from the circumference and is nigher unto the centre, it becoms thicker, more consistent, and less luminous; it then becoms <i>earth,</i> and, for the same reason, <i>water</i> and <i>air</i>. This must necessarily be so; for it would be absurd to say that this matter is in no place, or that the circumference itself is the centre, and <i>vice versa</i>. It is this, therefore, which has caused it to be <i>particularised</i> by diverse forms; I mean to say, that which has disposed it to receive diverse forms.",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.7": "Finally, we shall further ask of him: Is the matter of the circumference, that is to say, of the heaven, the same as that of the elements?",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.8": "— Nay, he will answer; but, on the contrary, that is another matter, and it has other forms[[fn:1721]]. If one gives at the same time to the bodies here below and to those above the name of <i>body</i>, it is but by homonymy, as the moderns have set forth[[fn:1722]]. All this has been demonstrated.",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.9": "Hearken now, O reader of this treatise, unto that which I myself say.",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.10": "— Thou knowest it has been demonstrated by Aristotle that from the difference of actions one may infer the difference of forms[[fn:1723]]. Now, as the motions of the four elements are rectilinear, whilst the motion of the celestial sphere is circular, it is perceived, in the first place, that the matter of the former is not the same as that of the latter, which is a truth resulting from physical speculation; but, as it is also found that those elements which have rectilinear motions differ in direction, some moving upwards and others downwards, and that even those which tend toward the same side have motion more or less rapid or slow, it is recognised that they differ in forms. Thus has it been recognised that the elements are four in number[[fn:1724]]. It is by an argument altogether similar that one comes to the conclusion that all the celestial spheres have one and the same matter; for they all move circularly. But, in respect of <i>form</i>, the spheres differ one from another[[fn:1725]]; for one movs from east to west, and another from west to east[[fn:1726]], and, furthermore, the motions differ in rapidity and slowness. One must therefore yet address to him (that is, to Aristotle) the following question: Since all the spheres have a common matter, and in each of them the substratum has a particular form which is not that of the others, who is it then that has <i>particularised</i> these <i>substrata</i> and has disposed them to receive diverse forms? Is there, beyond the sphere, any other thing to which one may attribute this <i>particularisation</i>, if it be not God, the Most High?",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.11": "I must here call thy attention to the great profundity of Aristotle and to his extraordinary comprehension, and (remark to thee) how much, without doubt, this objection has embarrassed him, and how he has endeavoured to extricate himself by means in which (the nature of) existence afforded him no succour. For, although he has not mentioned this objection, it is nevertheless evident from his words that he desirs to present to us systematically the existence of the spheres, as he has done for that which is beneath the celestial sphere[[fn:1727]], in such wise that all things take place by a physical necessity and not by the intention of a being who pursus the end which he wills and who <i>determins</i> (things)[[fn:1728]], in whatever manner it pleass him. But in this he has not succeeded, nor shall any man ever succeed therein. He endeavours to give the reason: 1st, why the motion of the sphere proceeds from the East and not from the West[[fn:1729]]; 2nd, why (the spheres) possess motions, some rapid, others slow, which depends upon the order of their position over against the superior sphere[[fn:1730]]; 3rd, why each of the seven planets has several spheres, whilst this great multitude (of fixed stars) is in a single sphere[[fn:1731]]. He endeavours to indicate the causes of all this, in order to present the matter unto us according to a physical order (existing) by necessity. Nevertheless, he has succeeded in none of these things; for, if all that he has set forth unto us regarding sublunary things be systematic and conformable to that which (actually) exists and the causes of which are manifest, and if it may be said that everything therein takes place by a <i>necessity</i> (resulting) from the motion and the forces of the celestial sphere, he has been unable to give any evident reason for all that he has said regarding the celestial sphere, and the matter does not present itself in a systematic form, so that one might maintain the <i>necessity</i> thereof. For, as touching the spheres, we perceive that sometimes those which possess the more rapid motion are above those which possess the slower motion, sometimes those which have the slower motion are above those which have the more rapid motion, and sometimes, finally, they have equal motions, although they be one above the other[[fn:1732]]. There are yet other things (which become) very difficult, as soon as one adopts the point of view of <i>necessity</i>[[fn:1733]], and I shall devote to them a particular chapter of this treatise[[fn:1734]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.12": "In short, Aristotle, recognizing doubtless the weakness of that which he says to justify these things and to indicate their causes, has prefixed, when entering upon these researches, words of which this is the text: «We wish now to examine carefully two questions which it is necessary to examine, and we shall say thereof what our intelligence[[fn:1735]], our science and our opinion permit; but no one should for this reason charge us with presumption and audacity. We should, on the contrary, admire our passion and our zeal for philosophy; and when we examine great and noble questions[[fn:1736]] and succeed in giving them a solution even slightly solid, the hearer ought to experience a great pleasure and be in joy[[fn:1737]].» Such are his own terms. It is therefore clear that he indubitably recognized the weakness of what he said in this regard; all the more so since the science of mathematics was yet imperfect in his time, and that which we know today regarding the motions of the celestial sphere was not then known. It seems to me that if Aristotle says, in the <i>Metaphysics</i>, that one must suppose a <i>Separate Intelligence</i> for each sphere, it is likewise because of the subject in question, (that is to say) in order that there may be a thing which gives a particular motion to each sphere[[fn:1738]]. But we shall show that he gains nothing thereby.",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.13": "As for that which he says in the text I have cited: «what our intelligence, our science and our opinion permit,» I shall explain to thee the sense thereof; for I have seen it (set forth) by none of the commentators. By the words <i>our opinion</i>, he indicats the point of view of <i>necessity</i>, that is to say, the opinion of the eternity of the world. The words <i>our science</i> indicate that evident thing upon which there is agreement, (to wit) that each of these (celestial) things necessarily has a cause and does not occur by simple chance. The words <i>our intelligence</i> signify: our inability to indicate, in a completely perfect manner, the causes of such things; nevertheless, he claims to be able to say <i>somewhat</i> thereof. And this is indeed what he has done; for what he says concerning the rapidity of the universal motion and the slowness of the sphere of the fixed stars, (its motion) taking an opposite direction, is a strange and astonishing argument[[fn:1739]]. Likewise he says that as a sphere is more distant from the eighth, its motion must be more rapid; and yet it is not always so, as I have set forth to thee[[fn:1740]]. And what is even more serious than this, is that there are also spheres beneath the eighth which move from East to West; it would therefore be necessary that those which move from East to West should be (each of them) more rapid than those which are beneath them, and that (generally) those whose motion proceeds from the East should be more rapid in proportion as they are nearer to the (diurnal) motion of the ninth[[fn:1741]]. But, as I have already informed thee, the science of astronomy was not in his time what it is today.",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.14": "Know that, according to the opinion of all of us who profess the creation of the world, all this is easy and proceeds well (in accord) with our principles; for we say that there is a <i>Determining Being</i>, who, for each sphere, has determined as He willed the direction and the rapidity of the motion, but that we are ignorant of the mode of that wisdom which has caused such a thing to arise in such a manner. If Aristotle had been able to give us the reason for the diversity of the motion of the spheres, in such wise that all were in harmony with their reciprocal position, as he believed, it would have been marvellous; and then it would have been with the cause of that which is peculiar (to each sphere) in this diversity of motions, as it is with the cause of the diversity of the elements in regard to their (respective) position between the circumference and the centre (of the universe)[[fn:1742]]. But the matter is not so regulated, as I have set forth to thee.",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.16": "But, as soon as it is admitted that all is due to the design of a Being acting with intention, who has fashioned it thus, there remains naught at which one need marvel[[fn:1748]], nor aught that is improbable;",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.17": "and there is no further room for enquiry, unless thou shouldst ask: what is the cause of this <i>design?</i> In essence, all that is known is that this occurs for a reason unknown to us, yet it is not an idle work, nor the result of chance. For it is well known that the veins and nerves of the canine or equine individual are not the product of chance, nor do they possess such measure fortuitously, and that it is not merely by chance that one vein is large and another thin, that one nerve branches out into many, while another does not, that one descends straight, while another folds back upon itself; for none of this occurs without certain advantages whose necessity is well understood. And how, then, could an intelligent man imagine that the positions of these stars, their measurements, their number, and the movements of their various spheres are without reason, or the work of chance? There can be no doubt that each of these things is necessary in relation to the design of the one who acted with intention, and it is very difficult to conceive that this order of things comes from an (blind) necessity, rather than from a <i>design</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.18": "There is not, in my judgment, a greater proof of <i>design</i> than the variety of the movements of the spheres and the stars fixed within them; wherefore thou shalt find that all the prophets have taken the stars and the spheres as proof that there necessarily exists a God. That which the tradition concerning Abraham relats of his observation of the stars is well known[[fn:1749]]. Isaiah says, to call attention to the proofs to be drawn therefrom: <i>Lift up your eyes on high, and behold; who has created these things? etc.</i> (Is., XL, 26). Likewise Jeremiah says: <i>He who has made the heavens</i>[[fn:1750]]. Abraham said: <i>the Lord, the God of heaven</i> (Genesis, 24, 7), and the prince of prophets: <i>He who rids upon the heavens</i> (Deuter., 33, 26), which we have explained[[fn:1751]]. And this is indeed the true proof, in which there is nothing doubtful. I shall explain myself: If there be beneath the celestial sphere so many diverse things, although their matter be <i>one</i>, as we have set forth, thou mayest say that what has <i>particularized</i> them are the forces of the spheres and the different positions of matter with respect to the celestial sphere, even as Aristotle has taught us. But, as touching the <i>diversities</i> which exist in the spheres and the stars, who could have <i>particularized</i> them, save God? for, should any one say (that they are) <i>the separate intelligences</i>, he would have gained naught by this assertion. For the intelligences are not bodies, such that they might have a position in respect to the sphere; why then should this movement of desire (which attracts each sphere) toward its <i>separate intelligence</i>[[fn:1752]], be directed by one sphere toward the east and by another toward the west? Believest thou that one intelligence is on the side of the west and another on the side of the east? Why, again, should one (sphere) be slower and another swifter, without there even being in this a (regular) sequence in relation to their (respective) distance from one another[[fn:1753]], as thou knowest? It must then necessarily be said that it is the very nature of such a sphere and its substance which required it to move toward such a side and with such a degree of speed, and that the result of its desire was such a thing (obtained) in such a manner. And this is indeed what Aristotle says and what he clearly proclaims[[fn:1754]]. We are thus returned to our point of departure, and we say: Since all (the spheres) have one and the same matter, what then can cause them to be distinguished from one another by a particular nature[[fn:1755]], and that some should have a certain desire producing such a species of motion and opposed to the desire of others producing another species of motion? Is there not necessarily required something which <i>particularizes</i> them? — This consideration has led us to examine two questions. The one (is this): can one, or not, conclude from the existence of this diversity that all is necessarily wrought by the design of a Being having an intention, and not <i>by necessity</i>? The second question (is this): Supposing that all this be due to the design of a Being having an intention, who has thus <i>particularized</i> things, can it be concluded therefrom that all has been created after not having existed? or rather, must one not draw this conclusion, and admit on the contrary that this <i>particularization</i> has taken place from all eternity[[fn:1756]]? — for this opinion[[fn:1757]] has been held also by some of those who admit the eternity (of the world). I shall, therefore, in the following chapters, approach these two questions and set forth what is necessary concerning them.",
    "root.text.Part 2..19.0": "Aristotle demonstrats that physical things in general do not happen by chance, and the demonstration which he gives thereof is this: things of chance happen neither continually, nor even most frequently[[fn:1758]]; but all these (physical) things happen either continually or (at least) most frequently. As for the heaven, with all that it contains, it remains continually in certain situations, without undergoing any change, as we have set forth, neither in its very essence, nor by changing its place. But the physical things which are beneath the sphere of the moon take place, some continually, others most frequently: continually, as, for example, fire which heats and the stone which descends downwards; most frequently, as, for example, the forms of the individuals of each species and its actions[[fn:1759]]. All this is manifest. Now, since the partial things (of the world)[[fn:1760]] are not due to chance, how could the whole be so? It is therefore demonstrated that these beings are in no wise the work of chance.",
    "root.text.Part 2..19.1": "Thus speaks Aristotle, whilst refuting those among the ancients who alleged that this world has arisen from chance and that it was born spontaneously, without a cause: “Others,” says he, “have assigned as the cause of this heaven and of all the worlds <i>spontaneity</i>[[fn:1761]]; for, say they, it is spontaneously that the revolution ariss, as well as the movement which has distinguished and constituted all things in this order. But there is in this something passing strange: they say (on the one hand), of animals and plants, that they neither are nor are born by chance, but that they have for their cause either a nature, or an intellect, or some other similar thing; for every thing whatsoever is not born from every seed or from every sperm (at random), but from such a seed an olive-tree is born, and from such sperm a man is born. And (on the other hand), they say of the heaven and of the bodies which (alone) among all visible bodies are (verily) <i>divine</i>[[fn:1762]], that they are born only spontaneously and that they have absolutely no cause, such as animals and plants possess.” Such are his expressions, and he enters into long details to show the falsity of what they have presumed.",
    "root.text.Part 2..19.2": "It is therefore manifest that Aristotle believs and demonstrats that all these beings exist not by chance; that which refuts (the opinion admitting) that they are the work of chance, is that they exist <i>essentially</i>, that is to say, they have (evidently) a cause which wills that they be necessarily thus, and by this cause they exist even as they are. This is what has been demonstrated and what Aristotle believs. But (as to the question of knowing) if, from the fact that they are not born spontaneously, it necessarily follows that they are the work of a design and of a free will[[fn:1763]], it is not proven unto me that Aristotle believs this; for, to unite together existence by necessity and birth by a design and a will, so as to make of them one and the same thing, this appears to me well-nigh the union of two opposite things. Indeed, the idea of <i>necessity</i> admitted by Aristotle is (this): that all among beings that are not the product of art necessarily have a cause which produced and formed them as they are; this cause has a second cause, this latter a third, and so forth, until one arrivs at a first cause from which all has emanated; for one could not admit a chain (of causes) unto infinity. But he does not believe for all that that the existence of the world results necessarily from the Creator, I mean from the first cause, as the shadow results from the body, or as heat results from fire, or as light results from the sun, as is maintained of him by those who understand not his words. He believs, on the contrary, that it is with this necessity much as (when we say that) the intelligible results necessarily from the intellect, the intellect being the efficient of the intelligible in as much as it is intelligible[[fn:1764]]; for, even according to him (Aristotle), this first cause is an intellect in the highest and most perfect degree of being. But, although he says that God <i>wills</i> that which emanats from Him, that He has joy and pleasure therein[[fn:1765]] and that He could not will the contrary, one could not call this <i>design</i>[[fn:1766]], and there is not therein the idea of design. Indeed, man desirs to have two eyes and two hands, he feels joy and pleasure therein, and he could not will the contrary; but if such an individual has two eyes and two hands, it is not by a design (proceeding from him), nor because he particularly wills such a figure and such actions. The idea of <i>design</i> and that of <i>determination</i> apply only to a thing which exists not yet and which may exist or not exist even as it has been projected or determined.",
    "root.text.Part 2..19.3": "I know not (moreover) whether the moderns[[fn:1767]] have understood the words of Aristotle, saying that things have necessarily a cause[[fn:1768]], in the sense of <i>design</i> and of <i>determination;</i> or else, if they have contradicted him on this point and if it be they who have preferred the opinion of <i>design</i> and of <i>determination</i>, believing that it is not in contradiction with the eternity (of the world).",
    "root.text.Part 2..19.4": "— After this exposition, I approach the opinion of these moderns.",
    "root.text.Part 2..20.0": "Know thou that, among the modern philosophers who profess the eternity of the world, there be some who say that God is the efficient cause of the world, whose existence He has preferred (to non-existence), that He has made it with design and has determined it even as it is, but that it is inadmissible that this should have taken place in one time rather than in another, and that, on the contrary, it has always been and shall always be thus. That which causes, say they, that we could not figure to ourselves that an agent has made something without this agent having preceded his action in time, is that in what we ourselves do, it is necessarily thus; for, in every agent of this sort[[fn:1769]], there is a certain <i>privation:</i> he is (first) an agent in potentiality, and, after having acted, he has passed into actuality. But God, in whom there is no privation, nor absolutely anything that is in potentiality, does not precede His action; on the contrary, He has never ceased to act, and even as there is an immense difference between His essence and ours, so also the relation which exists between His action and Himself differs from that which exists between our action and ourselves. They follow the same reasoning concerning <i>determination</i> and <i>will;</i> for, it matters little whether thou sayest <i>agent</i>, or <i>willing</i>, or <i>acting with design</i>, or <i>preferring</i>, or <i>determining</i>, (all these words) having the same sense[[fn:1770]]. It is inadmissible, say they further, that His action or His will should be subject to change, as we have already set forth[[fn:1771]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..20.1": "It is therefore manifest unto thee, O reader of this my present treatise, that those persons have indeed changed the word <i>necessity</i>, yet have allowed the idea thereof to remain. Perchance they intended to choose a more excellent expression, or (at least) to avoid something ill-sounding[[fn:1772]]; for (if it be said) that the universe is intimately bound to its <i>cause</i>, — which is God, as Aristotle says, — and that it participats in His perpetuity, it is absolutely the same idea as when they say that the world proceeds from the <i>action</i> of God, or (that it was made) by His design, His will, His choice, and His <i>determination</i>, but that it has never ceased and shall never cease to be as it is, even as the rising of the sun is indubitably the efficient of the day, without the one preceding the other in time. Yet this is not the conception of <i>design</i>[[fn:1773]] as we understand it; on the contrary, we mean thereby that the world did not <i>necessarily</i> emanate from God, as an effect emanats from its cause, from which it is so inseparable that it cannot change unless the cause itself undergo a change, or at least change in respect of one of its conditions. When thou shalt have thus understood the idea (of <i>design</i>), thou wilt recognize how false it is to say that the world is a necessary consequence of the existence of God, as the effect is of the cause, and (thou shalt know) that it has come from the (free) action of God or (that it exists) by His <i>determination</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..20.2": "Having thus set forth the subject, we proceed to examine the question of that diversity which exists in the heavens and which, as has been demonstrated, must necessarily have a cause; (and we ask) whether this cause, by its very existence, has motivated and rendered necessary this diversity[[fn:1774]], or whether it be the efficient of this diversity which has <i>determined</i>[[fn:1775]] it in the manner that we believe, we the followers of Moses, our teacher? We shall answer this, after having first made a preliminary observation to explain the sense of the <i>necessity</i> admitted by Aristotle, in order that thou mayest conceive the idea thereof; and thereafter I shall set forth to thee the preference which I give to the opinion of the <i>creation of the world</i>, (supported) by speculative and philosophical proofs, pure from all false reasoning.",
    "root.text.Part 2..20.3": "If he says that the first Intelligence necessarily emanated from God, the second Intelligence from the first, and the third from the second, and likewise, if he thinks that the spheres emanated from the Intelligences and (if he proclaims) that well-known order which thou hast been able to study in the passages relating thereto and which we have here set forth in summary[[fn:1776]], it is clear that he means not thereby that one thing existed first, and that subsequently there was <i>born</i> from it that other thing which is its necessary consequence; for he admitts not the <i>birth</i> of any of these things. In saying <i>necessary consequence</i>, he means to speak only of <i>causality</i>, as if he were to say: the first Intelligence is the cause of the existence of the second Intelligence, this latter is the cause of the existence of the third, and so forth; and it is the same with what he says of the spheres and of prime matter[[fn:1777]], for all these things do in no wise <i>precede</i> one another and exist not, according to him, the one without the other[[fn:1778]]. It is, for example, as if one were to say that, from the <i>primary qualities</i>[[fn:1779]], there result necessarily roughness, smoothness, hardness, softness, thickness, and sponginess[[fn:1780]]; for no one doubts that it is those (the primary qualities), I mean heat, cold, moisture, and dryness, which have given birth to roughness, smoothness, hardness, softness, thickness, sponginess, and other such things, and that these latter have necessarily emanated from the primary qualities, although it be impossible that there should exist a body which, possessing the primary qualities, should be devoid of these secondary qualities[[fn:1781]]. It is therefore in absolutely the same manner that Aristotle says[[fn:1782]], of the universe in general, that such a thing has necessarily emanated from such another, until one arrivs at the <i>First Cause</i>, as he expresses it, or the <i>First Intelligence</i>, or[[fn:1783]] by whatever name thou mayest wish to call it. We all have in view one and the same Principle; but he thinks that all that which is outside (of this Principle) emanated from it <i>by necessity</i>, as I have said, whilst we say that it is God who has made all these things with design and by <i>willing</i> this universe, which existed not at first and which has now been called into existence by the will of God. I shall now, in the following chapters, produce the proofs which lead me to give preference to (the opinion which admitts) that the world was <i>created.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.0": "It is a proposition whereon Aristotle and all the philosophers are agreed, that from a simple thing there can emanate (directly) but a single simple thing[[fn:1784]]. If the thing be composite, there may emanate from it several things, according to the number of simple elements which it contains and of which it is composed. Fire, for example, being a composite of two (primary) qualities, heat and dryness, it follows that it heats by its heat and dries by its dryness[[fn:1785]]. Likewise, a thing being composed of matter and form, there may emanate from it, if it be of manifold composition, several things on the side of its matter and several others on the side of its form. — Conformably to this proposition, Aristotle says that there was no primitive emanation from God but a single simple intelligence, and naught else.",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.1": "<i>The Second Proposition</i>[[fn:1786]]<i>:</i> No thing whatsoever can arise fortuitously from any thing whatsoever, but it is always necessary that there be a certain relation between the cause and its effect. Even accidents themselves cannot arise by chance from one another, as for example, quantity from quality, or quality from quantity. In like manner, a form cannot arise from matter, nor matter from form.",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.2": "<i>Third proposition:</i> Every agent that acts with design and will, and not by its nature, may perform diverse and numerous actions.",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.3": "<i>Fourth Proposition:</i> A whole composed of diverse substances <i>juxtaposed</i> forms a <i>composition</i> more truly than a whole composed of diverse substances <i>mingled together.</i> Thus, for instance, bones, or flesh, or veins, or nerves, are more simple than the entire hand or foot, composed of nerves, flesh, veins, and bones. — This is too clear to require further explanation.",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.4": "After these preliminary remarks, I shall say that, if Aristotle asserts[[fn:1787]] that the first Intelligence serves as the cause of the second, the second of the third, and so forth, even were there thousands of degrees, the last[[fn:1788]] of these Intelligences shall ever indubitably be simple. Whence, then, should proceed the <i>composition</i>, which, according to the opinion of Aristotle, would exist by <i>necessity</i> in these beings (of this lower world)? We are well pleased to agree with him when he says that, in proportion as the Intelligences depart (from the First Cause), there is found in them a greater composition of (diverse) ideas, their intelligibles becoming more and more numerous; but, whilst admitting with him this conjectural opinion, (we would ask him) how could the Intelligences become the cause of the spheres emanating from them? What relation is there between the matter (of the spheres) and the separate Intelligence, which is absolutely immaterial? Even should we grant that each sphere has for its cause an Intelligence, as has been said, because that Intelligence is <i>composed</i> in that it thinks itself and (thinks also) that which is outside of it, — so that it is, after a manner, composed of two things, of which one produces the other Intelligence which is below it, and the other produces the sphere[[fn:1789]], — one might yet ask him: This one and simple thing from which (according to you) the sphere emanats, how could it produce the sphere, since the latter is composed of two matters and two forms, which are (on the one hand) the matter and the form of the sphere, and (on the other hand) the matter and the form of the star fixed within the sphere[[fn:1790]]? If, therefore, all proceeded by way of <i>necessity</i>, we should necessarily have to suppose in that composed Intelligence a cause likewise composed of two parts, one of which might produce the body of the sphere and the other the body of the star[[fn:1791]]. And furthermore, it would be necessary that the matter of all the stars be one and the same[[fn:1792]]; yet it may be that the brilliant stars are of one distinct substance, and the nebulae[[fn:1793]] of another substance. Finally, it is known that every body is composed of its matter and its form[[fn:1794]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.5": "It is therefore manifest that these things proceed not by way of <i>necessity</i>, as he says. Likewise, the diversity of the motion of the spheres is not in relation to their successive order[[fn:1795]], one below the other, in such wise that one might maintain in this regard the system of <i>necessity</i>, of which we have already spoken[[fn:1796]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.6": "There is yet (in this system) another thing which overthrows all that has been established regarding physical things, if one considers the state of the celestial sphere: If the matter of all the spheres be <i>one</i>, how comes it that the form of such a sphere is not necessarily transferred to the matter of another, as happens in sublunary things, by reason of the aptitude of matter[[fn:1797]]? Why does such a form remain ever in such (determined) matter, since all have a common matter? — Unless, by God, one would maintain that each sphere has a matter different from that of the others, so that then the form of motion would no longer be a proof for (the unity of) the matter[[fn:1798]]; but that would be the subversion of all principles.",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.7": "Furthermore, if the matter of all the stars be <i>one</i>, by what means (one might inquire) are their individualities distinguished? Is it by forms or by accidents? However, in either of these two hypotheses, it would be necessary that either these forms or these accidents should of necessity be transferred unto each of the stars, unless one would deny the aptitude of matter[[fn:1799]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.8": "Thou shalt therefore understand thereby that, if we speak of the <i>matter of the spheres</i>, or the <i>matter of the stars</i>, this must in no wise be taken in the same sense as that sublunary matter, and that there is therein but a mere homonymy; for each of these celestial bodies has an existence peculiar unto itself, in which the others do not participate. But if it be so, how comes it nevertheless that there is a <i>community</i> touching the circular motion of the spheres or the fixedness of the stars? — However, if we admit that all this takes place by the design of a Being acting with intention, who has made and determined all things as His incomprehensible wisdom has required[[fn:1800]], none of these questions can be addressed unto us, which one has a right to put only to him who maintains that all things come to pass by <i>necessity</i> and not by a free will[[fn:1801]]. But this latter opinion accords not with the order of the universe, and no one has been able to support it by any reason or sufficient proof. Moreover, there follow therefrom things passingly improbable; for God, whom every intelligent man affirms to be endowed with all manner of perfections, would find Himself, with respect to all beings, powerless to innovate aught[[fn:1802]], and if He willed to lengthen the wing of a fly or shorten the foot of an insect, He could not do so. But Aristotle will say to this that God wills it not, and that it would even be inadmissible that He should will it to be otherwise; that (he would say) would not be to attribute unto Him greater perfection, and perhaps it would rather be an imperfection in certain respects[[fn:1803]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.9": "I shall tell thee as a general thesis—for although I know that many passionate men[[fn:1804]] will reproach me for understanding their words but little or for departing from them by design, I shall not for that reason abstain from saying what I have perceived and understood according to my feeble intelligence—and this thesis is as follows: All that Aristotle has said concerning all that exists beneath the sphere of the moon, unto the centre of the earth, is indubitably true; and no one could depart therefrom, save him who understands it not, or he who has preconceived opinions which he desirs to defend at any cost[[fn:1805]], or which lead him to deny an evident thing. But, from the sphere of the moon upwards, all that Aristotle says thereof resembls, well-nigh, mere conjectures; and with greater reason, what he says of the order of the Intelligences, as well as some of those metaphysical opinions which he adopts, without being able to demonstrate them, but which contain great improbabilities, evident and manifest errors prevalent among the nations, and evil doctrines which have been divulged[[fn:1806]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.10": "One must not criticize me for having brought to light the doubts[[fn:1807]] which attach unto his opinion. Is it indeed by doubts, thou wilt say to me, that one can destroy an opinion, or establish the opposite opinion? Verily, it is not so; but we act with this philosopher as his followers have recommended us to act with him. Indeed, Alexander has already set forth that, whenever a thing is not susceptible of being demonstrated, one must needs pose the two contrary hypotheses[[fn:1808]] in succession, see what doubts attach unto each of the two opposite cases, and admit that which offers the fewest doubts. Thus it is, says Alexander, with all that Aristotle says touching certain metaphysical opinions for which one has no demonstration; for all those who have come after Aristotle affirm that what Aristotle has said thereof offers fewer doubts than all that might else be said[[fn:1809]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.11": "And this is what we have done: after it has been verified unto us that, in the question of knowing whether the heaven be created or eternal, neither of the two opposite hypotheses can be demonstrated, and after having set forth the doubts inherent in each of the two opinions, we have shown thee[[fn:1810]] that the opinion of the eternity of the world offers the most doubts and that it is right dangerous for the belief which one must profess concerning God. Let us add thereto that the <i>creation</i> of the world is the opinion of our father Abraham and of Moses, our prophet.",
    "root.text.Part 2..21.12": "Since we have affirmed that opinions must needs be examined by means of the doubts which they contain, I deem it my duty to enter into some details thereupon.",
    "root.text.Part 2..22.0": "Know that, in the comparison to be established between the doubts which attach to a certain opinion and those which attach to the contrary opinion, in order to give preference to that which offers the fewest doubts, it is not a matter of taking into consideration the mere number of doubts, but rather the gravity of the improbability which they reveal and of the difficulties encountered in the nature of existence[[fn:1811]]. For it may be that a single doubt possesses in itself more importance than a thousand other doubts.",
    "root.text.Part 2..22.1": "Furthermore, this comparison can only be profitably established by one who should accord an equal value to the two contrary hypotheses; but if any man, whether by reason of his education or through some interest whatsoever, should prefer beforehand one of the two opinions, he would remain blind to the truth. Verily, a man moved by passion cannot persist in his obstinacy against a thing demonstrable; but, when it concerns such hypothetical matters, it is but too possible to show oneself rebellious.",
    "root.text.Part 2..22.2": "Thou mayest sometimes, if thou wilt, divest thyself of passion, rid thyself of habit, rely solely upon speculation, and choose by preference that which merits to be preferred; but for this, several conditions must be fulfilled: Firstly, to know how to measure the capacity of thy mind and the perfection of thy natural talent; and thou shalt enlighten thyself hereupon by studying all the mathematical sciences[[fn:1812]] and by seeking to understand the rules of logic. Secondly, to know the physical sciences and to fathom them, so as to render unto thyself an exact account of the doubtful points[[fn:1813]]. Thirdly, to watch over thy morals; for as soon as a man, whether by his nature or by acquired habit, finds himself drawn towards appetites and pleasures, or allows himself to yield to violence and anger, letting his irascible faculty prevail and giving it the rein, he will ever stumble and trip whithersoever he goes, seeking opinions that may serve to support his natural inclination.",
    "root.text.Part 2..22.3": "I have directed thy attention thereto, lest thou be seduced; for it may be that someone, some day, shall lead thee into error by raising doubts against the creation of the world, and that thou be too ready to let thyself be deceived by adopting an opinion which undermins religion at its foundation and proclaims a heresy regarding God[[fn:1814]]. Thy mind must therefore be ever on its guard in this respect[[fn:1815]], and thou must follow the two prophets[[fn:1816]] who are the pillar of the improvement of the human race in its beliefs and its social life. Thou shalt not depart from the opinion of the creation of the world save by reason of a true demonstration; but such a thing exists not in nature.",
    "root.text.Part 2..22.4": "Let not the reader of this treatise find fault with me for having here employed rhetorical arguments to support the opinion of the creation of the world; for the prince of philosophers, Aristotle, in his principal writings, has likewise employed rhetorical arguments to support his opinion of the eternity of the universe; and it is in such a case that one may say in truth: “Does not our perfect law excel their frivolous discourses[[fn:1817]]?” If he supports his opinion by the follies of the Sabeans[[fn:1818]], how should we not support ours by the words of Moses and Abraham, and by all that follows therefrom?",
    "root.text.Part 2..22.5": "I have promised thee a chapter[[fn:1819]] in which I should speak to thee of the grave doubts which may be opposed to him who believs that human science can account[[fn:1820]] for the order of the motions of the heavenly sphere, and that these are physical matters which occur by a necessary law, the order and connection of which are clear. I now, therefore, approach the exposition thereof.",
    "root.text.Part 2..23.0": "Thou knowest of astronomy that which, in my lessons, thou hast read and understood of the contents of the book of the <i>Almagest</i>; but time was not sufficient to cause thee to commence another study[[fn:1821]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..23.1": "What thou already knowest is that, in order to account for the regularity of the motions, and that the course of the stars may accord with visible phenomena, one must admit (one of these) two hypotheses[[fn:1822]], either an epicycle or an eccentric sphere, or even both together[[fn:1823]]. But I shall point out to thee that each of these two hypotheses is entirely[[fn:1824]] outside of all rule and contrary to all that has been expounded in physical science. Firstly, to establish an epicycle which revolvs upon a certain sphere, without revolving around the center of that sphere which bears it, as has been supposed for the moon and for the five planets[[fn:1825]], is a thing wherefrom it would necessarily follow that there is a <i>rolling</i>, that is to say, that the epicycle <i>rolls</i> and entirely changs its place, an inadmissible thing which they sought to escape, (to wit) that there should be (in the heavens) anything whatsoever that changs its place[[fn:1826]]. It is for this reason that Abu Bekr ibn al-Sa’igh, in a discourse of his which exists upon astronomy, has said that the existence of the epicycle is inadmissible; and, after having spoken of the said consequence (of <i>rolling</i>), he says that besides this inadmissible thing which would result from the existence of the epicycle, there would follow yet other inadmissible things. I shall expound them to thee: 1st, There would be a revolution around a center which would not be that of the world; and yet it is a fundamental principle of all this universe, that motions are three in number: a motion (proceeding) from the middle, another (tending) toward the middle, and another around the middle[[fn:1827]]. But, if there were an epicycle, its motion would be neither from the middle, nor toward it, nor around it. 2nd, It is one of the principles laid down by Aristotle, in physical science, that there must necessarily be something fixed around which the motion occurrs, and this is why it is necessary that the earth remain fixed; but, if the epicycle existed, it would be a circular motion around naught that is fixed.",
    "root.text.Part 2..23.2": "I have heard it said that Abu Bekr mentioned having found an astronomical system in which there was no epicycle, but (where all was explained) solely by eccentric spheres; yet, I have not heard this (from the mouth) of his disciples[[fn:1828]]. But, even had he succeeded therein, he would not have gained much; for, in (the hypothesis of) <i>eccentricity</i>, one likewise departs from the principles laid down by Aristotle, to which naught can be added. And this is an observation which belongs to me. For, in <i>eccentricity</i> also, we find a circular motion of the spheres which occurrs not around the middle (of the universe), but around an imaginary point which departs from the center of the world; and this is likewise a motion which occurrs not around something fixed. It is true that those who have no knowledge of astronomy pretend that, since these (imaginary) points are within the sphere of the moon, as it appears at first sight, eccentricity also admitts a motion around the middle (of the universe); and we should wish to be able to grant them that it (the motion) occurrs around a point in the fire or in the air, although that be not a motion around something fixed[[fn:1829]]. But we shall expound to them that the measures of the eccentricities have been demonstrated in the <i>Almagest</i>, according to the hypotheses adopted therein; and the moderns having established by a true demonstration, in which there is naught doubtful, what is the measure of these eccentricities relative to the semi-diameter of the earth[[fn:1830]], even as they have expounded all the distances and magnitudes (of the stars), it has been proved that the center of the eccentric of the sun[[fn:1831]] is necessarily outside the concavity of the sphere of the moon and below the convexity of the sphere of Mercury[[fn:1832]]. Likewise, the point around which Mars revolvs, I mean the center of its eccentric, is outside the concavity of the sphere of Mercury and below the convexity of the sphere of Venus. Likewise again, the center of the eccentric of Jupiter is found at this same distance[[fn:1833]], I mean between the spheres[[fn:1834]] of Mercury and Venus. As for Saturn, the center of its eccentric falls between Mars and Jupiter. Behold[[fn:1835]], consequently, how far all these things depart from physical speculation! All this shall become clear to thee, when thou shalt have studied the distances and magnitudes known for each sphere and for each star; and the evaluation of all this is made by the semi-diameter of the earth, so that all (is calculated) according to one and the same ratio, without establishing any between the eccentricity and the respective sphere.",
    "root.text.Part 2..23.3": "But there is something stranger still and far more obscure: it is that, whenever there be two spheres placed one within the other, closely applied on all sides to each other, but having different centres[[fn:1836]], it may happen that the smaller movs within the larger without the latter moving also; but it is impossible that the larger should move upon any axis whatsoever, without the smaller moving also; for whenever the larger movs, it necessarily carries the smaller along by its motion, save only when the motion occurrs upon the axis which passes through the two centres[[fn:1837]]. Now, by reason of this demonstrative proposition, by reason of that which has been demonstrated, that a vacuum exists not[[fn:1838]], and finally, by reason of the hypothesis of eccentricity[[fn:1839]], it would follow that, the superior (sphere) being in motion, it should carry the inferior along, by its motion, around its (own) centre; and yet we find not that it is so, but on the contrary we find that neither of the two spheres, the one containing and the other contained, movs either by the motion of the other, nor around the centre of the latter, nor around its poles, and that each has a motion which is peculiar to itself. Wherefore men have been constrained to admit (that there exist), between the spheres taken in pairs, bodies other than those of the spheres (of the planets)[[fn:1840]]. But, how many obscurities would yet remain there, if it were truly thus! where would one suppose the centres of these bodies to be, which would exist between each couple of spheres? And it would be necessary that these bodies also should have a peculiar motion. — Already Thâbit[[fn:1841]] has set this forth in a special treatise, and he has demonstrated, according to what we have said, that one must necessarily (admit) a spherical body between each couple of spheres. — I did not explain all this unto thee when thou didst follow my lessons, lest I should confuse thee in that which I intended to make thee understand.",
    "root.text.Part 2..23.4": "Regarding the <i>inclination</i> and the <i>obliquity</i> which are spoken of concerning the latitude of Venus and Mercury[[fn:1842]], I have set forth unto thee viva voce and (clearly) shown that it is impossible to conceive how such a thing can exist in the (celestial) bodies[[fn:1843]]. Ptolemy has clearly acknowledged the difficulty thereof[[fn:1844]], as thou hast seen; for he expresses himself in these terms: «Let no man believe that these principles and the like can with difficulty take place, considering what we have presented here as things obtained by artifice and by the subtlety of art, and which can with difficulty take place; for it is not meet to compare human things with things divine[[fn:1845]].» Such are his own words, as thou knowest.",
    "root.text.Part 2..23.5": "I have indicated unto thee the places wherefrom thou mayest verify all that I have told thee, save only what I told thee[[fn:1846]] concerning the observation of those points which are the centres of the eccentrics, (to wit) where they fall; for I have never met with any (author) who has occupied himself therewith. But this shall become clear unto thee, when thou shalt know the measure of the diameter of each sphere, and what is the distance between the two centres, relatively to the semi-diameter of the earth, as Al-Kabîçî has demonstrated in the treatise <i>on Distances</i>[[fn:1847]]; for, when thou shalt examine these distances, thou shalt acknowledge the truth of that which I have remarked unto thee.",
    "root.text.Part 2..23.6": "Observe, therefore, how obscure all this is; if that which Aristotle says in physical science be the truth, there is[[fn:1848]] neither epicycle nor eccentric, and all revolvs around the centre of the earth. But whence then would come to the planets all these diverse movements? Is it possible, in any manner, that the motion should be perfectly circular and uniform, and that it should correspond (at the same time) to the visible phenomena, if it be not (explained) by one of the two hypotheses[[fn:1849]] or by both at once? The more so as, in admitting all that Ptolemy says of the epicycle of the moon and of its deviation toward a point without the centre of the world and also without the centre of the eccentric[[fn:1850]], the calculations made according to these hypotheses are not found wanting by so much as a single minute, and the truth thereof is attested by the reality of eclipses, ever calculated according to these hypotheses and for which one fixes so exactly the epochs, as well as the times and the measurements[[fn:1851]] of the obscuration. — How further can one conceive the (apparent) <i>retrogradation</i> of a planet, with its other movements, without (the hypothesis of) the epicycle[[fn:1852]]? How, finally, can it be imagined that there is there (in the heavens) a <i>rolling</i> or motion around a non-fixed centre? And this is a real perplexity.",
    "root.text.Part 2..23.7": "I have already explained unto thee viva voce that all this concerns not the astronomer; for he has not for his aim to make us know in what form the spheres exist, but his aim is to establish a system whereby it may be possible to admit circular motions, uniform and conformable to that which is perceived by the sight, regardless of whether the matter be (really) thus, or not[[fn:1853]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..23.8": "Thou art aware that Abu-Bekr ibn-al-Çâyeg, in his discourse upon Physics, expresses this doubt: whether Aristotle was cognizant of the eccentricity of the sun, and if, passing it over in silence, he occupied himself only with that which results from <i>inclination</i>, — the effect of eccentricity being in no wise distinct from that of inclination[[fn:1854]], — or whether, perchance, he knew it not at all. The truth is that he knew it not, and had never heard thereof; for the mathematical sciences were imperfect in his time. Had he heard of it, he would most certainly have rejected it with vehemence; and had it been proven unto him, he would have found himself in great perplexity regarding all that he had established upon this matter.",
    "root.text.Part 2..23.9": "That which I have already declared above[[fn:1855]], I shall here repeat: to wit, that all which Aristotle has said concerning sublunary things follows a logical sequence; these are matters of which the cause is known and which are deduced one from another, and the place therein held by the wisdom and providence of nature is evident and manifest. As for all that is in the heavens, man knows naught thereof, save only this small portion of mathematical theories[[fn:1856]]; and thou seest how it stands. I shall say, employing a poetical phrase: <i>The heavens are the Lord's; but the earth has He given to the children of Adam</i> (Ps. cxv. 16), which is to say, that God alone perfectly knows the true nature of the heavens, their substance, their form, their movements, and their causes; but as for that which is beneath the heavens, He has given unto man the power to know it, for that is his world and the habitation in which he has been placed, and of which he himself forms a part. And this is the truth; for it is impossible for us to possess the elements necessary to reason upon the heavens, which are far from us and too exalted[[fn:1857]] by their place and rank; and even the general proof that may be drawn therefrom, saying that they prove unto us the existence of their Mover, is a matter unto the knowledge of which human intelligences cannot attain[[fn:1858]]. But to weary the mind with that which it cannot grasp, having not even the instruments to reach thereto, would be naught but a lack of understanding and a species of folly. Let us therefore stop at that which is within our power; but that which cannot be grasped by reasoning, let us abandon it unto him who was the object of the great divine inspiration, so that he merited that it should be said of him: <i>With him will I speak mouth to mouth</i> (Num. xii. 8).",
    "root.text.Part 2..23.10": "This is all that I am able to say upon this question; but it is possible that another may possess a demonstration which shall render evident the truth of that which has been obscure unto me. The greatest tribute I could pay unto the truth is to have openly declared how much these matters cast me into perplexity[[fn:1859]], and that I have neither heard nor known of any demonstration for any of them.",
    "root.text.Part 2..24.0": "Know thou that, if we refrain from professing the eternity of the universe, it is not because the text of the Law proclaims the world to be <i>created;</i> for the texts which indicate the novelty of the world are not more numerous than those which indicate the corporeality of God. Concerning the novelty of the world likewise, the means of an allegorical interpretation would not be wanting unto us, nor would they be forbidden us[[fn:1860]]; on the contrary, we might employ this mode of interpretation here, even as we have done to do away with the corporeality of God. Perchance it might even be much easier, and we should be well able to interpret the texts in question and establish the eternity of the universe, even as we have interpreted the other texts and rejected the corporeality of God. But two reasons have induced us not to do this and not to admit it. One is this: the incorporeality of God has been demonstrated, and it is necessary to have recourse to allegorical interpretation whenever the literal sense, being refuted by a demonstration, is known beforehand to be necessarily subject to interpretation[[fn:1861]]. But the eternity of the universe has not been demonstrated, and, consequently, it is not fitting to do violence to the texts and to interpret them allegorically, in order to make prevail an opinion of which one might just as well make the contrary prevail by reasoning in another manner[[fn:1862]]. Such, then, is one reason.",
    "root.text.Part 2..24.1": "— The second reason is this: our belief in the incorporeality of God does not subvert any of the foundations of our religion, nor does it give the lie to anything that the prophets have proclaimed. There is in this no inconvenience, save that, according to the ignorant, it would be contrary to the texts of Scripture; but, as we have shown, there is naught therein contrary to it, and it is rather the aim of Scripture. But to admit eternity as Aristotle believs it, that is to say, as a <i>necessity</i>, so that no law of nature can be changed and nothing can depart from its habitual course, would be to undermine religion at its foundation, necessarily to brand all miracles as falsehoods, and to deny[[fn:1863]] all that religion has caused to be hoped or feared, unless, by God! one would also interpret the miracles allegorically, as have done the <i>Bâtenis</i> (or allegorists)[[fn:1864]] among the Muslims, which would lead to a species of madness. — Nevertheless, if one admitts <i>eternity</i> according to the second opinion we have set forth[[fn:1865]], which is that of Plato, and according to which the heavens also are perishable[[fn:1866]], this opinion does not subvert the foundations of religion, and there follows not the negation of the miracle, but, on the contrary, its admissibility. One might interpret the texts in its sense[[fn:1867]] and find[[fn:1868]], in the texts of the Pentateuch and elsewhere, many analogous expressions to which it might be attached and which might even serve as proof for it. However, no necessity compells us thereto, unless this opinion could be demonstrated; but, since it has not been demonstrated, we incline not toward this opinion, nor do we pay any heed thereto[[fn:1869]]. We rather take the texts in their literal sense, and we say that religion has made known to us a thing which we are incapable of conceiving, and the <i>miracle</i> bears witness to the truth of that which we maintain.",
    "root.text.Part 2..24.2": "It must be known that, so soon as one admitts the novelty of the world, all miracles becoming possible, the (revelation of the) Law becoms possible also, and all the questions that might be asked on this subject vanish. If, therefore, it were asked: Why did God reveal Himself to such a man and not to another? why did God give this Law to a particular nation, without giving one to others? why did He give it at such an epoch and give it neither before nor after? why did He command certain things to be done and forbid others? why did He signalize the prophet by such miracles as are related, without there being others? what was God's purpose in this legislation? why, finally, did He not inspire our nature with the sentiment of these things commanded or forbidden, if such were His purpose[[fn:1870]]? — the answer to all these questions would be this: “Even so has He willed it,” or else: “Even so has His wisdom required it.” Just as He caused the world to be born in this form at the moment He willed it, without our being able to account for His will in this respect, nor for the wisdom which caused Him particularly to choose such forms[[fn:1871]] and such an epoch, even so we cannot account for His will, nor for what His wisdom demanded, when He <i>determined</i> all that is the subject of the preceding questions. But, if it were maintained that the world is thus <i>by necessity</i>, it would necessarily be required to ask all these questions, and one could only escape them by sorry answers, which would contain the denial and negation of all those texts of the Law of which a man of understanding could not doubt the literal sense[[fn:1872]]. It is for this reason that men have avoided (professing) this opinion, and for this reason pious men have passed and will pass their lives meditating upon this question; for, if the novelty (of the world) were demonstrated, were it even according to the opinion of Plato, all that the philosophers have said to refute us would fall; and likewise, if they had succeeded in demonstrating the eternity (of the world)[[fn:1873]] according to the opinion of Aristotle, the whole of religion would fall, and one would be led toward other opinions.",
    "root.text.Part 2..24.3": "I have already set forth unto thee that all depends upon this question; mark it well.",
    "root.text.Part 2..25.0": "In the celebrated <i>Aphorisms</i> of Rabbi Eliezer the Great, known by the title of <i>Pirke Rabbi Eliezer</i>, I have seen a passage such as I have never seen more strange in the discourses of any who follow the law of Moses, our master. Hearken in what terms he expresses himself[[fn:1874]]; “From whence were the heavens created? He (God) took of the light of His garment and spread it like a cloth; and thence the heavens went forth unfolding, even as it has been said: <i>Who coverest thyself with light as with a garment, who stretchest out the heavens like a curtain</i> (Ps. civ. 2). — From whence was the earth created? He took of the snow from beneath <i>the throne of His glory</i> and cast it, even as it has been said: <i>For He says to the snow, Be thou on the earth</i> (Job xxxvii. 6).”",
    "root.text.Part 2..25.1": "— Such are the terms of the passage in question. Would that I knew what this sage believed! Did he perhaps believe that it is inadmissible for a thing to be produced from nothing, and that there must necessarily be a matter from which everything that is born is formed? And is it for this reason that he sought for the heaven and the earth (the matter) <i>from whence they had been created?</i> But, in whatever manner his answer be understood, one must necessarily ask him: “From whence was created <i>the light of His garment?</i> From whence was created the <i>snow</i> which is under <i>the throne of glory?</i> From whence was created this <i>throne</i> itself?” — For if, by the <i>light of His garment</i>, he had intended to indicate something uncreated, and if likewise (according to him) the <i>throne of glory</i> were uncreated, this would be (an opinion) most reprehensible[[fn:1875]]; for he would then have affirmed the eternity of the world[[fn:1876]], at the very least in the sense of the opinion of Plato[[fn:1877]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..25.2": "As touching the <i>throne of glory</i>, the doctors say expressly that it is one of the created things, although they express themselves in a singular manner (saying) that it was created before the creation of the world[[fn:1878]]. As for the texts of the (sacred) books, they speak not at all of <i>creation</i> in its regard, with the exception of these words of David: <i>The Lord has prepared His throne in the heavens</i> (Ps. ciii. 19); but this is a passage which lends itself much to allegorical interpretation[[fn:1879]]. What the text declars expressly is its eternal duration: <i>Thou</i>, <i>O Lord</i>, <i>remainest for ever; Thy throne</i> (<i>endurs</i>) <i>from generation to generation</i> (Lam. v. 19).",
    "root.text.Part 2..25.3": "— If, therefore, R. Eliezer had admitted the eternity of the <i>Throne</i>, the same could designate naught but an attribute of God and not a <i>created</i> body[[fn:1880]]; but how then would it be possible for anything to have been born of an <i>attribute?</i> — Yet that which is most marvelous is the expression <i>the light of His garment</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..25.4": "In sum, this is a passage which greatly perplexes the theologian, who is a man of science, in his faith[[fn:1881]]. I could not give a sufficient interpretation thereof, and I have only spoken of it to thee so that thou shouldst not allow thyself to be led into error;",
    "root.text.Part 2..25.5": "but, be that as it may, he (the author) has rendered us a great service therein, by stating clearly that the matter of the heavens is other than that of the earth, and that they are two right distinct matters. The one, by reason of its elevation and majesty, is attributed to God and proceeds from <i>the light of His garment</i>[[fn:1882]]<i>;</i> the other, remote from the light and splendor of God, is the inferior matter, which is made to proceed from <i>the snow which is under the throne of glory</i>[[fn:1883]]. — This is what led me to interpret the words of the Pentateuch, <i>And under His feet there was as it were a paved work of a sapphire stone</i> (Exodus, xxiv. 10), in this sense: that they perceived, in that prophetic vision, the true condition of the inferior <i>prime matter</i>; for Onkelos, as I have explained to thee, considers the words <i>His feet</i> as referring to the <i>throne</i>, which clearly indicats that this <i>whiteness</i>, which was <i>under the throne</i>, is the terrestrial matter[[fn:1884]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..25.6": "Rabbi Eliezer has therefore repeated the same thing, expressing himself more clearly, to wit, that there are two matters, one superior and one inferior, and that the matter of all things is by no means one and the same. This is a great mystery, and one must not disdain what the greatest doctors of Israel have revealed concerning it; for it is one of the mysteries of Being, and one of the <i>secrets of the Torah</i>. In the <i>Bereshith Rabba</i> we read: “R. Eliezer says: the creation of all that is in the heavens proceeds from the heavens, and the creation of all that belongs to the earth proceeds from the earth[[fn:1885]].” Remark well how this doctor says clearly that all which belongs to the earth, that is to say, all that is beneath the sphere of the moon, has one common matter, and that the matter of the heavens and of all that is found therein is another, distinct from the former[[fn:1886]]. In his <i>Aphorisms</i>, he adds this new trait concerning the majesty of one of these matters, nigh unto God, as also the defectiveness of the other and its circumscribed space. Thou must fully possess thyself of this.",
    "root.text.Part 2..26.0": "I have already expounded to thee that the belief in the <i>creation of the world</i> is necessarily the basis of the whole religion; but that this world, a new creation, must also one day perish[[fn:1887]], is not, in our view, an article of faith, and in believing in its perpetuity, one would offend none of our creeds. Perchance thou wilt say: “Has it not been demonstrated that everything which is born is perishable? Therefore, since it was born, it must perish.” But know thou well that we are not bound to reason thus[[fn:1888]]; for we have not maintained that it was born as physical things are born, subject to a natural law[[fn:1889]]. In truth, that which is born according to the natural course of things must needs perish according to the course of nature; for just as its nature required that it should not at first exist such as it is[[fn:1890]] and that afterwards it should become so, even so does it necessarily require that it should not perpetually exist thus[[fn:1891]], since it is proved that, by its very nature, this manner of existence does not suit it perpetually. But according to our religious thesis, which attributs the existence of things and their loss to the will of God, and not to <i>necessity</i>, according to this opinion (I say), naught constrains us to admit that God, after having produced a thing which had not existed, must necessarily destroy that thing. On the contrary, it shall depend either upon His will, which shall be free to destroy it or to preserve it, or[[fn:1892]] upon what His wisdom shall require; it will therefore be possible that He should preserve it eternally and grant it a permanence similar to His own.",
    "root.text.Part 2..26.1": "Thou knowest (for example) that the Sages have expressly declared that <i>the Throne of Glory</i> is a created thing, and yet they have never affirmed that it must cease to be; it has never been heard, in the discourse of any prophet or any sage, that <i>the Throne of Glory</i> must perish or cease to be, and the text of Scripture has even proclaimed its eternal duration[[fn:1893]]. In like manner, <i>the souls of the righteous</i>, according to our opinion, though <i>created</i>, do never cease to exist[[fn:1894]]. According to certain opinions of those who adhere to the literal sense of the <i>Midrashim</i>, their bodies also shall enjoy perpetual delights throughout all eternity, which resembls the well-known belief that certain folk profess concerning the denizens of Paradise[[fn:1895]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..26.2": "In sum, (philosophical) speculation leads to this conclusion: that the world is not necessarily subject to destruction. There remains, therefore, (to be examined) only the point of view of the prediction of the prophets and sages: has it been foretold, or not, that the world shall infallibly be reduced to naught? Verily, the vulgar among us believe, for the most part, that this has been foretold and that this whole world must perish; but I shall manifest unto thee that it is not so, but on the contrary, a great number of texts proclaim its perpetuity, and that everything which, taken in the literal sense[[fn:1896]], seems to indicate that it must perish, is most evidently an allegory, as I shall explain. If any partisan of the literal sense refuss this, saying that he must necessarily believe in the (future) destruction of the world, one must not cavil with him on that account[[fn:1897]]. However, he must be made to know that, if the destruction of the world be necessary, it is not because it is <i>created</i>, and that, if according to him it must be admitted, it is rather by a sincere faith in that which has been foretold by that allegorical expression which he himself has taken in its literal sense. There is in this no kind of danger to religion.",
    "root.text.Part 2..27.0": "Many of our coreligionists believe that Solomon admitted the eternity (of the world). But it is marvelous that one could have imagined that a man who professed the religion of Moses our Master could have admitted <i>eternity</i>. If any one believed—from which may God preserve us!—that he had deserted therein religious opinions[[fn:1898]], how then would all the prophets and sages have accepted him? how would they not have attacked him upon this point and blamed him after his death, as was necessary to be done[[fn:1899]] for the <i>strange women</i> and for other things? That which gave rise to the suspicion concerning him in this regard is that the sages say: “They sought to suppress the Book of <i>Ecclesiastes</i>, because its words lean toward the words of heretics[[fn:1900]].” Such is the case, doubtless; I mean to say that this book, taken in its literal sense, contains things which lean toward heterodox opinions[[fn:1901]] and which stand in need of interpretation. But the eternity (of the world) is not among them; there is (in this book) no passage which indicats it, and still less finds one there a text which manifestly declars the eternity of the world. However, it contains passages which indicate its perpetual duration, which is true; now, having seen therein passages which indicate its perpetuity, it was thought that Solomon believed it uncreated, whereas it is not so.",
    "root.text.Part 2..27.1.sub_0": "The passage concerning perpetuity is this: <i>and the earth abids for ever</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">לעלם</span> (Ecclesiastes, i. 4); and those who have not bestowed their attention upon this curious point have been obliged to have recourse to this explanation: <i>during the time which has been appointed unto it</i>[[fn:1902]]. They have likewise said, concerning these words of God: <i>While the earth remains</i> (Genesis, viii. 22), that it referrs to the duration of the time appointed unto it. But I would fain know what shall be said of the words of David: <i>He laid the foundations of the earth</i>, <i>that it should not be removed</i>, <i>for ever</i> (Ps. civ. 5); for if the words <span dir=\"rtl\">עולם ועד</span>, <i>in sœculum</i>, did not also indicate perpetual duration, God also would have a certain limited duration, since His perpetuity is expressed in these terms: <i>the Lord shall reign for ever and ever</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">לעלם ועד</span> (Exodus, xv. 18)[[fn:1903]]. But what must be known is that <span dir=\"rtl\">עולם</span>, <i>sœculum</i>, indicats perpetual duration only when the particle <span dir=\"rtl\">עד</span> is joined thereto, whether after, as, for example, <span dir=\"rtl\">עולם ועד</span>, or before, as, for example, <span dir=\"rtl\">עד עולם</span>; thus therefore, the expression of Solomon, <span dir=\"rtl\">לעלם עמדת</span> <i>abids for ever</i>, would even say less than that of David, <span dir=\"rtl\">בל תמוט עולם ועד</span>, <i>that it should not be removed for ever.</i> David, in effect, has clearly set forth the perpetuity of the heaven and (declared) that its laws, as well as all that it contains, shall remain invariably in the same state. He said: <i>Praise ye the Lord from the heavens, etc</i>., <i>for He commanded, and they were created; He has also established them for ever and ever</i>; <i>He has made a decree which shall not pass</i> (Ps. cxlviii. 1, 5, 6), which means that those laws which He has fixed shall never be changed; for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חֹק</span> (law) is an allusion to the <i>ordinances of heaven and earth</i>, of which mention was previously made[[fn:1904]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..27.1.sub_1": "But at the same time he declars that they were created, saying: <i>for He commanded, and they</i>[[fn:1905]] <i>were created.</i> Jeremiah said: <i>Thus says the Lord, which gives the sun for a light by day,</i> (<i>who has prescribed</i>) <i>the ordinances of the moon and of the stars for a light by night, etc., if those ordinances depart from before me, says the Lord, then the seed of Israel also shall cease from being a nation.</i> (Jeremiah, xxxi. 35); he has therefore likewise declared that although they were created, namely these laws, they shall not depart. If then one pursus the research, one shall find (also this doctrine) elsewhere than in the words of Solomon. But Solomon (himself) has said further that these works of God, that is to say the world and that which it contains, shall remain perpetually stable in their nature, although they were <i>made: I know that, whatsoever God does</i>, says he, <i>it shall be for ever; nothing can be put to it, nor any thing taken from it</i> (Ecclesiastes, iii. 14). He has thus made known by this verse that the world is the work of God and that it is of perpetual duration, and he has also given the cause of its perpetuity, saying: <i>nothing can be put to it, nor any thing taken from it;</i> for this is the cause why it <i>shall be for ever</i>. It is as if he had said that the thing which is subject to change is only so because of that which is defective in it and which must be completed, or (because) of that which is superfluous and useless in it, so that this superfluity must be retrenched; whereas the works of God, being exceedingly perfect, so that it is impossible to add thereto or to take away therefrom, remain necessarily as they are, naught within them being able to bring about change[[fn:1906]]. It would seem that he wished also to indicate the end of creation, or to justify the changes that occur[[fn:1907]], saying at the end of the verse: <i>And God does it, that men should fear before Him</i>, which is an allusion to the miracles that occur. When he says subsequently (v. 15): <i>That which has been is now</i>, <i>and that which is to be has already been</i>, <i>and God seeks that which is</i> (<i>continual</i>); he means thereby that God wills the perpetuity of the universe and that all things therein follow each other by a mutual concatenation.",
    "root.text.Part 2..27.2": "That which he has said concerning the perfection of the works of God, and the impossibility of adding anything thereto or diminishing anything therefrom, the prince of the learned[[fn:1908]] has already declared in saying: <i>The Rock</i> (the Creator), <i>His work is perfect</i> (Deut. xxxii. 4); which signifies that all His works, to wit, His creatures, are exceedingly perfect, that no defect is mingled therein, and that they contain nothing superfluous nor anything useless. In like manner, all that is accomplished for these creatures and through them is perfectly just and conformable to the requirements of (divine) wisdom, as shall be set forth in certain chapters of this treatise.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.0": "Know that he who understands not the language of a man whom he hears speaking, knows doubtless that this man speaks, but he is ignorant of his meaning. But, what is yet more serious, is that one sometimes hears in the discourse (of a man) words which, in the language of him who speaks, indicate a certain sense, while by chance, in the language of the listener, such a word has a sense contrary to that which the speaker intended (to express); and yet the listener believs that the word has, for him who speaks, the significance which it has for himself. If, for example, an Arab should hear a Hebrew say <i>abâ</i>, the Arab would believe that the other means to speak of one who rejects a thing and wills it not, whereas the Hebrew means, on the contrary, that the thing pleass him and that he wills it. Such likewise is that which befalls the vulgar with the language of the prophets; in part, they understand it not at all, but it is as (the prophet) has said: <i>The vision of all is become unto you as the words of a book that is sealed</i> (Isaiah xxix. 11); in part, they take it backward or in a contrary sense, as (another prophet) has said: <i>And ye have perverted the words of the living God</i> (Jeremiah xxiii. 36). Know also that each prophet has a language peculiar to himself, which is as it were the (particular) tongue of that person; and it is in the same manner that the revelation, which is personal to him, makes him speak to those who can understand him.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.1": "After this preamble, it must be known that that which frequently occurrs in the discourse of Isaiah,—but rarely in that of other (prophets),—is that when he wills to speak of the fall of a dynasty[[fn:1909]] or the ruin of a great nation, he employs such expressions as: <i>The stars have fallen, the heavens have been troubled</i>[[fn:1910]], <i>the sun is darkened, the earth has been devastated and shaken</i>, and many other similar metaphors.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.2": "It is even as they say among the Arabs (speaking) of him whom a great misfortune has smitten: <i>His heaven has been turned upside down upon his earth</i>[[fn:1911]]. Likewise, when he describs the prosperity of a dynasty and a renewal of fortune, he employs metaphors such as <i>the increase of the light of the sun and of the moon</i>, <i>the renewal of the heaven and the earth</i>, and other analogous expressions. Thus it is that (the other prophets), when they describe the ruin of an individual, of a nation, or of a city, attribute unto God dispositions of wrath and great indignation against them; but when they describe the prosperity of a people, they attribute unto God dispositions of joy and gladness. They say (speaking) of His dispositions of wrath against men: <i>He has gone forth</i>, <i>He has come down, He has roared</i>, <i>He has thundered, He has caused His voice to be heard</i>, and many other similar words; they say also: <i>He has commanded</i>, <i>He has said</i>, <i>He has acted, He has made</i>, and so forth, as I shall set forth. Furthermore, when the prophet relats the ruin of the inhabitants of a certain place, he sometimes substituts the whole (human) species for the inhabitants of that place; thus Isaiah says: <i>And the Lord have removed men far away</i> (vi. 12), meaning the ruin of Israel[[fn:1912]]. Zephaniah says in the same sense: <i>I will utterly consume man from off the land</i>, <i>and I will stretch out mine hand upon Judah</i> (i. 3 and 4). Thou must be thoroughly impressed with this.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.3": "Having explained this language (of the prophets) to thee in general, I shall now show that what I say is true and give thee proof thereof[[fn:1913]]. Isaiah,—when God charged him to announce the fall of the Babylonian empire, the destruction of Sennacherib, that of Nebuchadnezzar who appeared after him, and the cessation of his reign, and when (the prophet) beginns to depict the calamities (which were to smite them) at the end of their reign, their routs and that which was to reach them in the way of misfortunes that reach whosoever is routed and fles before the victorious sword,—(Isaiah, I say,) expresses himself thus: <i>For the stars of heaven and the constellations thereof shall not give their light</i>, <i>the sun shall be darkened in his going forth</i>, <i>and the moon shall not cause her light to shine</i> (Isaiah xiii. 10). He says again in the same description: <i>Therefore I will shake the heavens, and the earth shall remove out of her place</i>, <i>in the wrath of the Lord of hosts, and in the day of His fierce anger</i> (<i>ibid</i>., v. 13). I do not think there is a single man in whom ignorance, blindness, and adherence to the literal sense of metaphors and rhetorical expressions, have reached such a point that he would think the stars of heaven and the light of the sun and of the moon were altered when the kingdom of Babylon perished, or that the earth departed from its centre, as the (prophet) expresses it. But all this is the description of the state of a man put to flight, who, without doubt, beholds all light as darkness, finds all sweetness bitter, and imagins that the earth is too narrow for him and that the heaven has been darkened[[fn:1914]] over him.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.4": "In like manner, when he portrays the state of abasement and humiliation into which the Israelites were to fall during the days of the impious Sennacherib, when he should seize <i>all the fenced cities of Judah</i> (<i>ibid.,</i> XXXVI, 1), how they were to be led captive and put to rout, what calamities were successively to descend upon them from the hand of that king, and how the land of Israel was then to perish by his power, — he expresses himself thus: <i>Fear</i>, <i>and the pit, and the snare, are upon thee</i>, <i>O inhabitant of the earth</i>. <i>And it shall come to pass, that he who fles from the noise of the fear shall fall into the pit; and he that comes up out of the midst of the pit shall be taken in the snare</i>; <i>for the windows from on high</i> (<i>celestial</i>) <i>are open, and the foundations of the earth do shake. The earth is utterly broken down, the earth is clean dissolved, the earth is moved exceedingly. The earth shall reel to and fro like a drunkard</i>, etc. (<i>ibid.,</i> XXIV, 17-20). At the conclusion of this discourse, in describing what God will do unto Sennacherib, the loss of his haughty dominion in his expedition against Jerusalem, and the shame wherewith God shall confound him before that city, he says allegorically[[fn:1915]]: <i>Then the moon shall be confounded, and the sun ashamed, when the Lord of hosts shall reign</i>, etc. (<i>ibid.,</i> v. 23). Jonathan ben Uziel has right well interpreted these words; for he says that, when that which is to happen unto Sennacherib shall befall him in his expedition against Jerusalem, the worshippers of the stars shall know that it is a divine act, and they shall be stupified and troubled: <i>Those</i>, says he, <i>who pay worship to the moon shall be confounded</i>, <i>and those who bow down before the sun shall be humiliated, for the kingdom of God shall be revealed,</i> etc[[fn:1916]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.5": "Furthermore, in depicting the tranquillity which the Israelites should enjoy when Sennacherib had perished, the fertility and the repeopling of their lands, and the prosperity of their empire under Hezekiah, he says allegorically that the light of the sun and the moon shall be increased; for, even as it has been said concerning the vanquished, that the light of the sun and moon departs and is changed into darkness in respect of the vanquished, so likewise the light of both luminaries increass for the victor. Thou shalt ever find that, when a great calamity befalls a man, his eyes wax dim, and the light of his vision is not clear, because the <i>visual spirit</i>[[fn:1917]] is troubled by the abundance of vapours, and at the same time it is weakened and diminished by great sorrow and by the contraction of the soul. In joy, on the contrary, when the soul expands and the <i>spirit</i> (<i>visual</i>) becoms bright, man beholds the light, as it were, more powerful than heretofore. — After having said: <i>For</i> <i>the people shall dwell in Zion at Jerusalem: thou shalt weep no more,</i> etc. (the prophet adds), at the end of the discourse: <i>Moreover the light of the moon shall be as the light of the sun, and the light of the sun shall be sevenfold, as the light of seven days, in the day that the Lord binds up the breach of his people, and heals the stroke of their wound</i> (<i>ibid.,</i> XXX, 19 and 26); he means thereby: when He shall raise them up from their fall[[fn:1918]] which they shall have suffered at the hand of the impious Sennacherib. As for these words: <i>as the light of seven days,</i> the commentators say that he means to indicate thereby a great quantity of light; for the Hebrews employ <i>seven</i> for <i>a great number.</i> But it seems unto me that he alludes unto the <i>seven days of the dedication of the Temple</i> which took place in the days of Solomon; for never had the nation enjoyed such happiness, prosperity, and general joy as in those days. He says, therefore, that their happiness and prosperity shall then be as great as in those seven days.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.6": "When he describs the ruin of the impious Edomites, who oppressed the Israelites, he says: <i>Their slain also shall be cast out, and their stink shall come up out of their carcases, and the mountains shall be melted with their blood. And all the host of heaven shall be dissolved, and the heavens shall be rolled together as a scroll: and all their host shall fall down, as the leaf falls off from the vine, and as a falling fig from the fig tree. For my sword shall be bathed in heaven: behold, it shall come down upon Idumea,</i> etc. (<i>ibid.,</i> XXXIV, 3–5). Now, consider, ye who have eyes to see, if there be in these texts aught that is obscure, or that might lead one to suppose that he describs an event which shall happen in the heavens, and if this be aught else than a metaphor to signify that their kingdom shall be destroyed, that the protection of God shall be withdrawn from them, that their fortune shall be cast down, and that the dignities of their great ones shall vanish away[[fn:1919]] with the utmost speed and extreme rapidity. It is as though he said that those personages who were comparable unto the stars for their stability, for their elevation of rank, and for their distance from vicissitudes, shall fall, in the briefest space, <i>as the leaf falls off from the vine</i>, etc. This is too manifest to be discussed in a treatise such as this, and, with even greater reason, to be dwelt upon. Yet necessity has called us thereto; for the vulgar, and even those who are accounted men of distinction, draw proofs from this verse, without paying heed to what preceds or follows, and without reflecting upon what subject it was spoken, regarding it only as a narrative by which the Scripture intended to announce unto us the end of heaven, even as it has related its birth.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.7": "Furthermore, when Isaiah announcs unto the Israelites the ruin of Sennacherib and of all the nations and kings that were with him [as is well known] and the victory they shall gain through the help of God alone, he says unto them allegorically: see how this heaven dissolvs, how this earth waxes old; they that dwell therein die, and ye, ye are succoured. It is as though he said that they who have encompassed the whole earth and who were thought as firm as heaven, — by way of hyperbole[[fn:1920]], — shall perish swiftly and vanish away like smoke, and their monuments which were in sight[[fn:1921]] and appeared as stable as the earth, these monuments shall perish as a garment that is worn out. At the beginning of this discourse, he says: <i>For the Lord shall comfort Zion: he will comfort all her waste places;</i> etc. <i>Hearken unto me, my people,</i> etc. <i>My righteousness is near; my salvation is gone forth, etc. Lift up your eyes to the heavens, and look upon the earth beneath: for the heavens shall vanish away like smoke, and the earth shall wax old like a garment, and they that dwell therein shall die in like manner: but my salvation shall be for ever, and my righteousness shall not be abolished</i> (<i>ibid.,</i> LI, 3-6).",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.8": "In speaking of the restoration of the kingdom of the Israelites, of its stability and duration, he says that God will produce anew a heaven and an earth; for, in his language, he ever speaks concerning the reign of a king as if it were a world peculiar unto himself, to wit: a heaven and an earth. After having commenced the consolations with the words: <i>I, even I, am he that comforts you</i> (<i>ibid.,</i> v. 12), and what follows, he expresses himself thus: <i>And I have put my words in thy mouth, and I have covered thee in the shadow of mine hand, that I may plant the heavens, and lay the foundations of the earth, and say unto Zion, Thou art my people</i> (<i>ibid.,</i> v. 16). To signify that dominion shall remain with the Israelites and that it shall depart from the celebrated mighty ones, he says: <i>For the mountains shall depart,</i> etc. (<i>ibid.,</i> LIV, 10). In speaking of the perpetuity of the reign of the Messiah, and to signify that the kingdom of Israel shall thenceforth no more be destroyed, he says: <i>Thy sun shall no more go down,</i> etc. (<i>ibid.,</i> LX, 20). — In fine, for him who comprehends the sense of this language, these are similar metaphors, oftentimes repeated, which Isaiah employs in his discourse. It is thus that, describing the circumstances of the exile and their particularities, and thereafter the return of power and the disappearance of all those mournings, he says allegorically: I shall create another heaven and another earth; those of the present[[fn:1922]] shall be forgotten and their trace shall be blotted out. Then he explains this in the sequel of the discourse, saying: If I employ the words <i>I create</i> etc., I mean thereby that I will form for you a state of continual joy and gladness in place of these mournings and this affliction, and no more shall men think upon those former mournings.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.9.sub_0": "Hearken unto the concatenation of ideas and the manner in which the verses relating thereto follow one another: first, in commencing this subject, he says: <i>I will mention the lovingkindnesses of the Lord, and the praises of the Lord,</i> etc. (<i>ibid.,</i> LXIII, 7). After this, he depicts first of all the bounties of God toward us (saying): <i>He bare them, and carried them all the days of old,</i> and the whole of the passage (<i>ibid.,</i> v. 9). Then he describs our rebellion: <i>But they rebelled, and vexed his holy Spirit,</i> and that which follows (<i>ibid.,</i> v. 10). Next he describs how the enemy became master over us: <i>Our adversaries have trodden down thy sanctuary; we are</i> (<i>as those</i>) <i>over whom thou never barest rule,</i> and that which follows (<i>ibid.,</i> v. 18 and 19). Then he prays for us, and says: <i>Be not wroth very sore, O Lord,</i> and that which follows (<i>ibid.,</i> LXIV, 8). After this he calls to mind in what manner we have deserved the grievous chastisement wherewith we have been tried, since we were called unto the truth and we answered not; and he says: <i>I am sought of them that asked not for me,</i> etc. (<i>ibid.,</i> LXV, 1). Then he promiss pardon and mercy, and says: <i>Thus says the Lord, As the new wine is found in the cluster,</i> and that which follows (<i>ibid.,</i> v. 8). He then threatens those who have oppressed us, and says: <i>Behold, my servants shall eat, but ye shall be hungry,</i> etc. (<i>ibid.,</i> v. 13). Finally, he adds thereto that the beliefs of this nation shall be amended, that she shall become an object of blessing upon the earth, and that she shall forget all previous vicissitudes; and he expresses himself in these terms: <i>And he shall call his servants by another name; that he who blesses himself in the earth shall bless himself in the God of truth;</i> <i>and he that swears in the earth shall swear by the God of truth; because the former troubles are forgotten, and because they are hid from mine eyes. For, behold,</i> <i>I create new heavens and a new earth: and the former shall not be remembered, nor come into mind. But be ye glad and rejoice for ever in that which I create: for, behold, I create Jerusalem a rejoicing, and her people a joy.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.9.sub_1": "And I will rejoice in Jerusalem</i>, etc. (<i>ibid.,</i> v. 15-19). — Thou hast therefore now a clear explanation of the whole subject. It is that, after having said: <i>For, behold, I create new heavens and a new earth,</i> he explains it immediately, saying: <i>For, behold, I create Jerusalem a rejoicing, and her people a joy.</i> After this preamble, he says: Even as these circumstances of faith and the exultation thereunto attached, circumstances which I have promised to create[[fn:1923]], shall ever subsist, — for faith in God and the exultation caused by that faith are two circumstances which can never cease nor be altered in him to whom they have come[[fn:1924]]; — he says therefore: Even as this state of faith and exultation, which I announce as destined to be universal upon the earth, shall be perpetual and stable, so shall your seed and your name endure. It is this which he says afterward: <i>For as the new heavens and the new earth, which I will make, shall remain before me,</i> <i>says the Lord, so shall your seed and your name remain</i> (<i>ibid.,</i> LXVI, 22). For it happens sometimes that the <i>seed</i> remains, whilst the <i>name</i> remains not; thou findest, for example, many peoples who indubitably are of the seed of the Persians or of the Greeks, and who nevertheless are no longer known by a particular name, but whom another nation has absorbed. There is therein further, according to my view, an allusion to the perpetuity of the Law (of Moses), by reason of which we have a particular name.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.10": "Since these metaphors are frequently encountered in Isaiah, I have been obliged, for that reason, to traverse them all; but they are also found sometimes in the discourse of other prophets.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.11": "J<small>EREMIAH</small> says, in describing the destruction of Jerusalem, occasioned by the crimes of our ancestors: <i>I have seen the land</i>, <i>and there was only void and chaos</i> (IV, 23).",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.12": "EZEKIEL says, in describing the ruin of the kingdom of Egypt and the fall of Pharaoh by the hand of Nebuchadnezzar: <i>I will cover the heaven when I extinguish thee, and make the stars thereof dark; I will cover the sun with a cloud, and the moon shall not give her light. All the bright lights of heaven will I make dark over thee, and set darkness upon thy land, says the Lord God</i> (XXXII, 7–8).",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.13": "J<small>OEL,</small> son of Pethouel, said (in speaking) of the multitude of locusts which arrived in his time: <i>Before them the earth trembls</i>, <i>the heavens are shaken</i>, <i>the sun and the moon are darkened</i>, <i>and the stars withdraw their light</i> (II, 10).",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.14": "A<small>MOS</small> says, in describing the destruction of Samaria: <i>I will cause the sun to go down at noon, and I will darken the earth in the clear day; I will turn your feasts into mourning, etc.</i> (VIII, 9 and 10).",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.15": "M<small>ICAH</small> says, concerning the destruction of Samaria, whilst ever adhering to those oratorical figures generally known: <i>For, behold, the Lord comes forth out of His place, and will come down, and tread upon the high places of the earth; the mountains shall be molten, etc.</i> (I, 3-4).",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.16": "’H<small>AGGAI</small> says (speaking) of the destruction of the kingdom of the Persians and the Medes: <i>I will shake the heavens and the earth, the sea and the dry land; I will shake all nations, etc.</i> (II, 6-7).",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.17": "Concerning the expedition of Joab against the Syrians, when he (David) depicts how weak and abased the nation was aforetime, and how (the Israelites) were vanquished and put to flight, and when he prays that they may be victorious at this season, he expresses himself thus: <i>Thou hast made the earth to tremble; Thou hast broken it: heal the breaches thereof</i>, <i>for it shaks</i> (Ps. 60, 4). Likewise, to admonish us that we ought to fear nothing when nations perish and pass away,—forasmuch as we rely upon the help of the Most High and not upon our own combat and upon our own strength, as (Moses) has said: <i>A people saved by the Lord</i> (Deut., XXXIII, 29),—he says: <i>Therefore will not we fear, though the earth be removed, and though the mountains be carried into the midst of the sea</i> (Ps. 46, 3).",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.18": "Concerning the drowning of the Egyptians, one finds (the following expressions): <i>The waters saw thee and they trembled, and the depths were moved. The voice of thy thunder was in the whirlwind etc., the earth trembled and shook</i> (Ps. lxxvii. 17 and 19). <i>Is the Lord angry against the rivers</i> (Habakkuk iii. 8)? <i>There went up a smoke out of his nostrils etc.</i> (Ps. xviii. 9). Likewise, in the song of Deborah: <i>the earth trembled, etc.</i> (Judges v. 4). Many (other passages) of this kind are met with; that which I have not cited, thou shalt explain in an analogous manner[[fn:1925]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.19": "As for these words of Joel (iii. 3-5): <i>I will shew wonders in the heavens and in the earth, blood, and fire, and pillars of smoke. The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and the terrible day of the Lord come. And it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall call on the name of the Lord shall be delivered; for in mount Zion and in Jerusalem shall be a refuge etc.,</i> — I should be much inclined to believe that he intends to describe the ruin of Sennacherib (in his expedition) against Jerusalem. But if thou wilt not (admit) this, it may be the description of the ruin of Gog (which shall take place) before Jerusalem, in the days of the King-Messiah, albeit there is question, in this passage, only of great carnage, the ravage of flames, and the eclipse of the two luminaries. Perhaps thou wilt say: how is it that, according to our explanation, he calls the day of the ruin of Sennacherib <i>the great and terrible day of the Lord?</i> But it must be known that every day whereon there occurrs a great victory or a great calamity is called <i>the great and terrible day of the Lord.</i> Joel has likewise said (speaking) of the day when those locusts came against them[[fn:1926]] (ii. 11): <i>For the day of the Lord is great and very terrible; and who can abide it?</i>",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.20": "The aim at which we point is already known; it is (to prove) that a (future) destruction of this world, a change in the state in which it is, or even any change whatsoever in its nature, such that it must thenceforth remain in that altered state[[fn:1927]], is a thing which no prophetic text, nor even any discourse of the Sages, does support; for when these latter say: \"The world endurs six thousand years and for one millennium it remains devastated[[fn:1928]],\" it is not (in this sense) that all which exists must return unto nothingness, since those very words: <i>and for one millennium it remains devastated,</i> indicate that <i>time</i> shall remain[[fn:1929]]. For the rest, this is an individual opinion and (conceived) according to a certain way of viewing things[[fn:1930]]. But that which thou findest continually among all the Sages, and which is a fundamental principle whence each of the doctors of the <i>Mishnâ</i> and the <i>Talmud</i> draws arguments, is that, according to this word: <i>There is no new thing under the sun</i> (Ecclesiastes i. 9), no renewal shall take place in any manner or by any cause whatsoever. This is so true, that even he who takes the words (of Isaiah) <i>new heavens</i> and <i>new earth</i> in the sense attributed to them (by error)[[fn:1931]], says nevertheless: \"Even the heavens and the earth that shall be produced one day are already created and subsist, since it is said: <i>they remain before me;</i> it is not said therefore <i>they shall remain</i>, but <i>they remain</i>\"; and he takes for an argument these words, <i>there is no new thing under the sun</i>[[fn:1932]]. Do not believe (moreover) that this be in opposition to what I have set forth; it is possible, on the contrary, that he meant thereby that the physical disposition which must then produce those promised circumstances (by the prophet) has been created since the six days of creation; which is true.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.21": "If I have said that nothing shall change its nature, <i>so as to remain in that altered state</i>, it was solely to make my reservations concerning miracles; for, albeit the rod (of Moses) was changed into a serpent and the water into blood, and the pure and glorious hand became white (with leprosy), without this being the result of a natural cause, these circumstances nevertheless and others like them did not endure and did not become another nature; and it was said on the contrary: <i>The world follows its habitual course</i>[[fn:1933]]. Such is my opinion, and this is what must be believed. Verily, the Sages have expressed themselves concerning miracles in a most extraordinary manner, in a passage which thou shalt find in the <i>Bereshith Rabbah</i> and in the <i>Midrash Koheleth.</i> But the idea they wished to express is this[[fn:1934]]: that miracles also are, in some sort, in nature; for, they say, when God created this universe and placed therein those physical dispositions, He also placed in those dispositions (the faculty) of bringing forth all the miracles that occurred at the very moment when they actually occurred[[fn:1935]]. The <i>sign</i> of the prophet (according to this opinion) consists in God making known to him the time when he must announce such an event[[fn:1936]] and when such a thing shall receive such an action, according to what was placed in its nature from the principle of its creation.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.22": "If it be truly thus, it gives a high idea of the author of this passage and shews us that he found it extremely difficult (to admit)[[fn:1937]] that any physical disposition could be changed after the <i>work of creation</i>[[fn:1938]], or that there could occur another (divine) will after everything had been thus fixed[[fn:1939]]. His opinion seems to be, for example, that it was placed in the nature of water to be continuous and to flow always from above to below, except at the epoch when the Egyptians should be drowned; then only was the water to divide[[fn:1940]]. Thus have I pointed out to thee what is the veritable spirit of the passage in question, and that all this was said to avoid the admission of an innovation in any thing whatsoever in nature. This is what is there related[[fn:1941]]: «Rabbi Jonathan said: God made conditions with the sea, that it should divide before the Israelites; this is what is written: <span dir=\"rtl\">וישב</span> … <span dir=\"rtl\">לאיתנו</span>, <i>the sea returned, toward the morning</i>, <i>to its former</i> <small>CONDITION</small> (Exodus, xiv, 27). R. Jeremiah, son of Eleazar, said: It was not with the sea alone that God made conditions, but with all that was created in the six days of Creation; such is the sense of these words: <i>It was my hands that stretched out the heavens</i>, <i>and all their host have I commanded</i> (Isaiah, xlv, 12). I commanded the sea to divide, the fire not to harm Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah, the lions not to hurt Daniel, and the whale to vomit up Jonah.» And in a like manner must the other miracles be explained.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.23": "Now is the matter clear unto thee, and the opinion which I profess is plainly set forth. It is that we agree with Aristotle in one half of his opinion: we believe that this universe shall exist for ever and perpetually with that nature which God willed to bestow upon it, and that naught thereof shall be changed in any manner whatsoever, save in some particularity and by way of miracle, though God has the power to change it totally or to reduce it to non-existence, or to cause any of its physical dispositions to cease as it may please Him; nevertheless, it had a beginning, and at first there was absolutely nothing in existence save God. His wisdom required that He should produce the creation at the moment when He did produce it, that what He had produced should not be reduced to naught, and that its nature should be changed in nothing, save in some particularities which it might please God to change, which we already know in part, but which, in part, are yet unknown to us and belong to the future.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.24": "Such is our opinion and such is the fundamental principle of our Law. But Aristotle thinks that, even as the universe is perpetual and imperishable, so it is eternal and has not been created. Now, we have already stated and clearly shown that this can only well accord with the law of <i>necessity;</i> but to proclaim <i>necessity</i> would be to profess a heresy[[fn:1942]] regarding God, as we have already demonstrated.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.25": "The discussion having arrived at this point, we shall provide a chapter in which we shall also make some observations upon certain texts found in the <i>account of creation</i> [for the principal object in this treatise is none other than to explain what is possible to be explained in the <i>Ma’aseh Bereshith</i> and the <i>Ma’aseh Mercabah</i>[[fn:1943]]]; but we shall precede it by two general propositions.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.26": "<small>ONE OF THESE</small> is the following proposition: «All that is related in the Pentateuch concerning the <i>Work of Creation</i> must not always be taken in its literal sense, as the vulgar imagine;» for if it were so, the men of science would not have been so reserved[[fn:1944]] in this regard, and the Doctors would not have so greatly recommended the concealment of this subject and the withholding of it from the multitude. In truth, these texts, taken literally, lead to a great corruption of ideas and give currency to evil opinions concerning the Divinity; or even lead to pure <i>irreligion</i>[[fn:1945]] and the denial of the foundations of the Law. The truth is, that one must refrain from considering them with the imagination alone and void[[fn:1946]] of science; and one must not act as those poor <i>Darshanim</i> (preachers) and commentators, who imagine that science consists in knowing the explanation of words, and in whose eyes it is a very great perfection to speak with abundance and prolixity; but to meditate thereon with a true intelligence, after having perfected oneself in the demonstrative sciences and in the knowledge of the prophetic mysteries, is verily a duty. Nevertheless[[fn:1947]], whosoever shall have acquired any knowledge of this subject must not divulge it, as I have set forth several times in the Commentary on the <i>Mishnah</i>[[fn:1948]]. It has been expressly said: «From the beginning of the book (of Genesis) until here, <i>the majesty of God</i> <i>requirs</i> <i>to conceal the thing</i>[[fn:1949]];» this is what was said in the <i>Midrash</i> at the end of the account of the sixth day of creation.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.27": "Thus, what we have said is clearly demonstrated. Nevertheless, as the divine precept necessarily obligs every one who has acquired a certain perfection to bestow it upon others [as we shall explain hereafter in the chapters on prophecy], every learned man who has come to understand aught of these mysteries, whether by his own speculation or by a guide who has led him thereto, cannot refrain from speaking thereof; but, as it is forbidden to speak of them clearly, he shall make only simple allusions[[fn:1950]]. Such allusions, observations, and indications are often found also in the discourses of some of the Doctors; but they are mingled with the words of others and with other subjects[[fn:1951]]. Wherefore thou wilt find that, in speaking of these mysteries, I ever mention only the word which is the basis of the subject, and I leave the rest to those who are worthy thereof.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.28": "A SECOND PROPOSITION</small> is the following: \"The prophets, as we have stated, employ, in their discourses, homonyms and names by which they have not in view what these names signify in their first acceptation[[fn:1952]]; but rather, in employing such a name, they have regard only to a certain etymology.\" Thus, for example, from <i>makkel</i> <small>SCHAKÉD</small> (a staff of almond wood), one deduces <small>SCHÖKED</small> (vigilant, attentive)[[fn:1953]], as we shall explain in the chapters on prophecy[[fn:1954]]. It is from the same idea that, in the narrative of the <i>char</i>, the word ’<small>HASCHMAL</small> (Ezekiel, I, 4) is employed, as has been explained; similarly <small>REGHEL ’EGHEL</small> and <small>NE’HOSCHES KALAL</small> (ibid., v. 7)[[fn:1955]]; similarly what is said by Zechariah (6, 1): <i>And the mountains were of </i> N<small>E’HOSCHES</small> (brass)[[fn:1956]], and other expressions of a similar nature.",
    "root.text.Part 2..28.29": "After these two propositions, I deliver the chapter which I have promised.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.0": "Know thou that there is a difference between <i>first</i> and <i>principle</i>. For the <i>principle</i> exists either <i>in</i> the thing of which it serves as principle, or (simultaneously) <i>with</i> it, albeit it does not precede it in time[[fn:1957]]; it is said, for instance, that the heart is the principle of the animal, and that the element is the <i>principle</i> of that of which it is an element. Men also sometimes apply to this idea the word <i>first</i>. At other times, however[[fn:1958]], <i>first</i> is said of that which is merely anterior in time, without such anteriority being the cause of that which is posterior to it; as when it is said, “<i>the first</i> who dwelt in this house was such a one, and after him was such another,” without the one being called the <i>principle</i> of the other. The word which, in our (Hebrew) tongue, signifies <i>priority</i>, is <small>TE’HILLA</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תחלה</span>); as for example: <i>The beginning</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תחלת</span>) <i>of the word of the Lord by Hosea</i> (Hos. i. 2). That which designats the <i>principle</i> is <small>RÉSCHÎTH</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראשית</span>); for it is derived from <small>RÔSCH</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראש</span>), the <i>head</i>, which is the <i>principle</i> (beginning) of the animal, by its position[[fn:1959]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.1": "Now, the world was not created in a temporal beginning, as we have set forth, forasmuch as time is (itself) of the number of created things[[fn:1960]]; wherefore it is said <small>BE–RÉSCHÎTH</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בראשית</span>, Gen. i. 1), in which the particle <small>BE</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ב</span>) has the sense of <i>in</i>[[fn:1961]]. The true translation of this verse is therefore this: <i>In the principle God created the upper and the lower</i> (<i>of the universe</i>); this being the only explanation which accords with the <i>creation</i> (of the world)[[fn:1962]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.2": "— As for what you find reported on the part of some of the doctors, tending to establish that time existed before the creation of the world, it is very obscure; for that would be, as I have explained to you, the opinion of Aristotle, who thinks that one cannot imagine a beginning for time, which is absurd. What led them to profess such an opinion is that they encountered[[fn:1963]] (the expressions) <i>one day, second day</i> (Gen., I, 5, 8). He who professed this opinion took the matter literally: since, he said to himself, there was as yet no sphere that turned, nor sun, by what thing then would the <i>first day</i> have been measured? Here is the textual passage[[fn:1964]]: '<i>First day</i>[[fn:1965]]: It follows from this, says R. Judah, son of R. Simon, that <i>the order of times</i> had existed previously. R. Abbahu says: it follows from this that the Holy One had already created worlds which He had then destroyed[[fn:1966]].' This latter opinion is even more blameworthy than the first[[fn:1967]]. You understand what seemed difficult to both of them, namely, that time existed before the existence of this sun; but the solution to what may have seemed obscure to both of them will be explained to you shortly[[fn:1968]]. Unless, by God! these (two doctors) intended to maintain that <i>the order of times</i> must necessarily have existed from all eternity[[fn:1969]]; — but then that would be to admit the eternity (of the world), a thing that every religious man must reject far away. This passage seems to me entirely similar to that of R. Eliezer: <i>From where were the heavens created</i>, <i>etc</i>.[[fn:1970]]. In short, one must not regard, in these subjects, what such a person may have said. I have already informed you that it is the fundamental principle of the entire religion that God produced the world from absolute nothingness, and not in a temporal beginning; time, on the contrary, is a <i>created</i> thing, for it accompanies the movement of the celestial sphere, and the latter is <i>created</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.3": "Furthermore, it behoves us to know that, regarding the particle <small>ETH</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אאת</span>) in the verse <span dir=\"rtl\">את השמים ואת הארץ</span> (Gen. I, 1), the Sages have declared in divers places that it signifies <i>with</i>. They mean thereby that God created <i>with</i> the heaven all that is within the heaven, and <i>with</i> the earth all that appertains to the earth[[fn:1971]]. Thou art also aware that they explicitly state that the heaven and the earth were created at once, citing this passage: <i>I call unto them, they stand up together</i> (Isaiah xlviii. 13)[[fn:1972]]. All things, therefore, were created simultaneously, and thereafter did they become distinct from one another in succession[[fn:1973]]. It is, according to their view, as with a husbandman[[fn:1974]] who has sown in the ground, at the same instant, various seeds, of which some have sprouted after one day, some after two days, and others after three days, although the entire sowing took place at the same moment. By this opinion, which is indubitably true, that doubt is dispelled which led R. Judah, son of R. Simon, to say what he said, because it was difficult for him to understand by what means the first, the second, and the third day were measured. The Sages declare themselves clearly upon this in the <i>Bereshith Rabba;</i> in speaking of the <i>light</i> which is said, in the Pentateuch, to have been created on the first day (Gen. I, 3), they express themselves thus: “These are the <i>luminaries</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, v. 14) which were created from the first day, but which He did not suspend until the fourth day[[fn:1975]].” This subject is thus clearly set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.4": "It must further be observed that <span dir=\"rtl\">ארץ</span> (earth) is a <i>homonym</i>, which is employed both in a general and a special sense. It applies, in general, to all that is beneath the sphere of the moon, that is to say, to the four elements; and is also used, in particular, of the last among them alone, which is the earth. The proof thereof is found in the words: <i>And the earth was without form and void</i>, <i>darkness was upon the face of the deep</i>, <i>and the spirit of God</i>, <i>etc</i>. (Gen. I, 2). Thus, they are all called <span dir=\"rtl\">ארץ</span> (earth)[[fn:1976]]; thereafter it is said: <i>And God called the dry land</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">ארץ</span> <i>Earth</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, v. 10).",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.5": "—This is also one of the great mysteries; (I wish to say) that every time you find the expression <i>and God called such a thing thus</i>, one has the purpose of separating it from the other idea (general), in which the name is common to both things[[fn:1977]]. This is why I have translated the (first) verse: <i>In the beginning God created the</i> <small>HAUT</small> <i>and the</i> <small>BAS</small> (<i>of the universe</i>); so that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ארץ</span> (earth) signifies, the first time, the <i>lower world</i>, I wish to say the four elements, whereas in saying: <i>And God called the dry portion</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">ארץ</span> <i>earth</i>, one wishes to speak of the <i>earth</i> alone. This is therefore clear.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.6": "Furthermore, it should be noted that the four elements are mentioned immediately after the heaven; for, as we have said, they are designated by the first name <span dir=\"rtl\">ארץ</span>, earth[[fn:1978]]. In effect, there are enumerated: <span dir=\"rtl\">ארץ</span> (earth), <span dir=\"rtl\">מים</span> (water), <span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span> (spirit or air)[[fn:1979]] and <span dir=\"rtl\">חשך</span> (darkness).—As for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חשך</span> (darkness), it signifies <i>the elementary fire</i>, and one must not think otherwise; (Moses, for example,) having said: <i>And thou heardest His words out of the midst of the fire</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">האש</span> (Deut. iv. 36), says thereafter: <i>When ye heard the voice out of the midst of the darkness</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">החשך</span> (<i>ibid.</i>, v. 20); and elsewhere it is said: <i>All darkness</i> (calamities) <i>shall be hid in his secret places</i>, <i>a fire not blown shall consume him</i> (Job xx. 26)[[fn:1980]]. If the <i>elementary fire</i> has been designated by this name (of <i>darkness</i>), it is because it is not luminous, but only transparent[[fn:1981]]; for, were the elementary fire luminous, we should behold the entire atmosphere inflamed during the night.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.7": "—These elements have been enumerated according to their natural positions: the earth (first), above it the water, the air adheres to the water, and fire is above the air; for, since air is designated as being <i>upon the face of the waters</i> (Gen., I, 2)[[fn:1982]], the <i>darkness</i> which is <i>upon the face of the deep</i> (<i>ibid.</i>) is indubitably situated above the <i>air</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>) <span dir=\"ltr\">[[fn:1983]]</span>. That which motivated the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">רוח אלהים</span>, <i>the breath</i> or <i>the wind of God</i> (to designate <i>the air</i>), is that it was supposed <i>in motion</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">מרחפת</span>, and the movement of the wind is always attributed to God; for instance: <i>And there went forth a wind from the LORD</i> (Numb., 11, 31); <i>Thou didst blow with thy wind</i> (Exodus, 15, 10); <i>The LORD caused a west wind to turn</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, X, 19), and many other passages. —Since the word ’<small>HOSCHEKH</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חשך</span>), when first employed (v. 5) as the name of the element (of <i>fire</i>), is distinct from the ’<small>HOSCHEKH</small> subsequently mentioned, which designates <i>darkness</i>, it is explained and distinguished by stating: <i>and he called the darkness Night</i> (v. 5), in accordance with what we have expounded. This is therefore equally clear.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.8": "Furthermore, it is to be understood that in the passage: <i>And he made a division between the waters etc</i>. (v. 7), there is no mere local separation implied, such that one portion (of the waters) would have been above and another below, both possessing the same nature; the sense, on the contrary, is that He separated them one from another by a physical distinction, that is to say, by <i>form</i>, and that He made of that part which He had first designated by the name of <i>water</i>[[fn:1984]] a distinct entity, by means of the physical form with which He invested it, whilst He imparted to the other part a different form. This latter is the water (properly so called)[[fn:1985]]; for this reason He also stated: <i>And the gathering together of the waters called he Seas</i> (v. 10), thereby revealing to thee that the primordial <i>water</i>, which is referred to in the words <i>upon the face of the waters</i> (v. 2), is not that which is in the seas, but that one part, above the atmosphere[[fn:1986]], was distinguished by a (particular) form, and that another part constitutes this (lower) water. Thus, the expression: <i>And he made a division between the waters which were under the firmament etc</i>. (v. 7), is to be understood similarly to that other: <i>And God divided the light from the darkness</i> (v. 4), where a distinction by <i>form</i> is in question.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.9": "The firmament (<span dir=\"rtl\">רקיע</span>) itself was formed of water, as it has been said: \"The middle drop became solidified[[fn:1987]].\"",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.10": "— [The expression <i>And God called the firmament Heaven</i> (v. 8) has furthermore the purpose which I have set forth unto thee[[fn:1988]], namely, to make manifest the <i>homonymy</i> and (to make it understood) that the heaven which is first mentioned in the words <i>the heaven and the earth</i> (v. 1) is not that which we (vulgarly) call <i>heaven</i>, which has been confirmed by the words <i>in the open firmament of heaven</i> (v. 20), declaring thus that the <i>firmament</i> is a different thing from the <i>heaven</i>. It is because of this homonymy that the true heaven is also sometimes called <i>firmament</i>, even as the true firmament is called <i>heaven;</i> thus, it is said: <i>And God set them</i> (the stars) <i>in the firmament of heaven</i> (v. 17).",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.11": "It is also clear from these words, — a point already demonstrated, — that all the stars, and (even) the sun and the moon, are fixed <i>dans</i> the sphere[[fn:1989]], because there is no void in the world[[fn:1990]]; they are not situated at the surface (inferior) of the sphere, as the common folk imagine, since we say <small>DANS</small> <i>the firmament of the heavens</i>, and not <small>SUR</small> <i>the firmament of the heavens</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.12": "— It is therefore clear that there was at first a certain common matter, called <i>water</i>, which was subsequently distinguished by three forms: one part formed the seas, another the firmament, and a third remained above this firmament; this last is entirely outside the earth[[fn:1991]]. Another method[[fn:1992]] has therefore been adopted for this subject, to (indicate) extraordinary mysteries. — That this thing which is above the firmament has been designated as <i>water</i> by name alone, and that it is not this <i>specific</i> water (of here below), is what the Doctors have also said in that passage: \"Four entered into the garden (of knowledge), etc.[[fn:1993]]. Rabbi ‘Akiba said unto them: When ye arrive at the stones of pure marble, say not <i>Water! water!</i> for it is written: <i>He that tells lies shall not tarry in my sight</i> (Ps. 101, 7)[[fn:1994]].\" Reflect then, if thou art of those who reflect, what light he (R. ‘Akiba) has shed by this passage, and how he has revealed the whole subject, provided that thou hast well examined it, that thou hast understood all that has been demonstrated in the <i>Meteorology</i>, and that thou hast perused all that has been said upon every point[[fn:1995]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.13": "What further merits thy attention is the reason wherefore, on the second day, it is not said <span dir=\"rtl\">כי טוב</span>, <i>that it was good</i>[[fn:1996]]. Thou art acquainted with the opinions which the Doctors have expressed in this regard, according to their method of <i>interpretation</i>[[fn:1997]]; the best that they have said is: “that the work of <i>the water</i> was not completed[[fn:1998]].” According to my view also, the reason thereof is right clear: it is that, whenever mention is made of one of the works of creation[[fn:1999]] whose existence is prolonged and perpetuated and which have attained their definitive state, it is said thereof <i>that it was good</i>. But this firmament (<span dir=\"rtl\">רקיע</span>) and the thing which is above it, called <i>water</i>, are, as thou seest, enveloped in obscurity. Verily, if one takes the matter literally and considers it but superficially[[fn:2000]], it is something which exists not at all; for, between us and the inferior heaven, there is no other body than the elements, and there is no water above the atmosphere[[fn:2001]]. And what would it be, if someone imagined that the firmament in question, with what is upon it, is above the heaven[[fn:2002]]? for then the matter would be most impossible and most incomprehensible. But (on the other hand), if one takes the matter in its esoteric sense and according to what was intended (in truth), it is extremely obscure; for it was necessary to make of it one of the occult mysteries[[fn:2003]], so that the vulgar should not know it. Now, how could it be permitted to say of such a thing <i>that it was good?</i> The words <i>that it was good</i> have no other meaning, save that the thing is of manifest and evident utility for the existence and the prolongation of this universe. But the thing whose (true) meaning is hidden, and which outwardly presents not itself as it is, what utility is there manifest therein unto men, that one might say thereof <i>that it was good?</i> — I must yet give thee another explanation thereof: it is that, although this thing[[fn:2004]] forms a most important part of creation, it is not however a purpose which was intended for the prolongation of the duration of the universe[[fn:2005]], so that one might have said thereof <i>that it was good;</i> but (it was made) for a certain urgent necessity, (that is to say) in order that the earth might be uncovered. Thou must well penetrate thyself with this.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.14": "Thou must further know that, according to the explanation of the Doctors, the herbs and the trees, God caused them not to spring forth from the earth until He had watered it with rain[[fn:2006]], so that the passage: <i>And a mist went up from the earth</i> (Genesis, ii. 6) speaks of a prior circumstance, which preceded (this command:) <i>Let the earth bring forth grass</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, i. 11). Wherefore Onkelos renders: <span dir=\"rtl\">ועננא הוה סליק מן ארעא</span> “and a mist <i>had gone up</i> from the earth.” Moreover, this results clearly from the text itself: <i>No plant of the field was yet in the earth</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, ii. 5). Thus the matter is clear.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.15": "—Thou knowest, O reader! that the principal causes of <i>generation</i> and <i>corruption</i>, after the forces of the celestial spheres[[fn:2007]], are light and darkness, by reason of the heat and the cold which they have as a consequence[[fn:2008]]. It is in consequence of the movement of the celestial sphere that the elements mingle together, and their mixture varies by reason of light and darkness. The first mixture which springs therefrom consists of the two species of <i>exhalations</i>[[fn:2009]] which are the primary cause of all superior phenomena, among which is rain, and which are also the causes of minerals, and thereafter, of the composition of plants, which is followed by that of animals and finally that of man[[fn:2010]]. Darkness[[fn:2011]] is the nature of the being of the whole inferior world, and light supervens thereunto (as an accident)[[fn:2012]]; it suffices thee (to see) that, in the absence of light, all remains in a state of immobility. — The Scripture, in the <i>account of the Creation</i>, follows absolutely the same order, omitting naught of all this[[fn:2013]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.16": "What must further be known is that they (the Doctors) say[[fn:2014]]: “All the works of creation were created in their (perfect) stature, with all their intellect[[fn:2015]] and in all their beauty”; which is to say that everything which was created was so in its quantitative perfection, with its perfect form and with its fairest qualities[[fn:2016]]. It is these latter which the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לצביונם</span> (in their <i>beauty</i>) indicats, which comes from <span dir=\"rtl\">צב׳</span>, <i>beauty</i>, <i>ornament</i>, for example: <i>the glory</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צבי</span>) <i>of all lands</i> (Ezek., xx. 6). Know this well; for it is an important principle, perfectly true and clear.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.17": "What ought to be a subject of serious meditation is that, after having spoken of the creation of man, in the six days of creation, saying: <i>male and female created He them</i> (Gen., i. 27), and after having entirely concluded (the account of) the creation, saying: <i>Thus the heavens and the earth were finished, and all the host of them</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, ii. 1), a new chapter is opened, (to relate) how Eve was created from Adam. Therein is mentioned the <i>tree of life</i> and the <i>tree of knowledge</i>, the adventure of the serpent and what befell therefrom, and all this is presented as having taken place after Adam had been placed in the Garden of Eden. All the Doctors are agreed that this whole event took place on the Friday, and that naught was changed, in any wise, after the six days of creation. One must therefore see naught perplexing in any of these things; for, as we have said, there was as yet until then no fixed nature[[fn:2017]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.18": "Moreover, they have uttered other matters which I must cause thee to understand, gathering them from diverse places, and I must likewise direct thy attention unto certain points, even as they themselves have done toward us<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2018]]</sup>. It must be known that all which I shall here cite from the discourses of the Sages are words of extreme perfection, the interpretation of which was clear unto those to whom they were addressed, and which are of a very great precision. Wherefore I shall not push the explanation thereof too far, nor shall I expound them at length<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2019]]</sup>, lest I <i>reveal a secret</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2020]]</sup>; but it shall suffice, to make them understood by a man such as thou art, that I cite them in a certain order and with a passing observation.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.19": "Thus they say<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2021]]</sup> that Adam and Eve were created together, joined back to back; (this double being) having been divided, He (God) took the half thereof, which was Eve, and she was given unto the other (unto Adam) for a companion<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2022]]</sup>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אחת מצלעותיו</span> (Gen., II, 21) signify (it is said) <i>one of his two sides</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2023]]</sup>, and there has been cited as proof <span dir=\"rtl\">צלע המשכן</span> (Exodus, 26, 20, etc.), which the <i>Targum</i> renders as <span dir=\"rtl\">סטרא דמשכנא</span>, <i>side of the tabernacle</i>, so that here, they say, (one should translate): <span dir=\"rtl\">מן סטרוהי</span>, <i>from his sides</i>. Understand well how it has been clearly stated that they were in some wise <i>two</i> and yet formed but <i>one</i>, according to these words: <i>a member of my members and flesh of my flesh</i> (Gen., II, 23), which has been further confirmed by saying that the two together were designated by a single name: <i>She shall be called</i> <small>ISHA</small>, <i>because she was taken out of</i> <small>ISCH</small> (<i>ibid.</i>); and, to make their union yet more manifest, it is said: <i>He shall cleave unto his wife, and they shall be one flesh</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, v. 25). — How great is the ignorance of those who understand not that there is necessarily at the root of all this a certain idea! Behold then, this is clear<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2024]]</sup>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.20": "Another subject which they have set forth in the <i>Midrash</i> and which it behoovs thee to know is this: The serpent, they say, was ridden by a rider, and it was as great as a camel; it was his rider who seduced Eve, and this rider was <i>Sammael</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2025]]</sup>. This name they apply unto Satan: they say, for example, in several places, that Satan desired to make our father Abraham fail, so that he would not consent to offer Isaac (as a burnt-offering), and likewise he desired to make Isaac fail, so that he would not obey his father; and, upon this occasion, I mean, concerning the sacrifice of Isaac, they express themselves thus: \"<i>Sammael</i> went unto our father Abraham and said unto him: What now, old man, hast thou then lost thy reason, etc.<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2026]]</sup>\" It is therefore clear that <i>Sammael</i> is Satan. This name, even as that of the <i>nachash</i> (serpent), indicats a certain idea; in relating how the latter came to deceive Eve, they say: \"Sammael was riding upon him; but the Holy One laughed at the camel and his rider<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2027]]</sup>.\"",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.21": "That which further merits thy attention is that the serpent had no manner of relation with Adam and addressed him not, but conversed and had communication only with Eve; it was through the mediation of Eve that evil befell Adam and that the serpent caused his ruin. Perfect enmity exists only between the serpent and Eve, and between the seed of the one and the seed of the other, although <i>her seed</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">זרעה</span>) be indubitably that of Adam<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2028]]</sup>. What is yet more remarkable is that what chains the serpent unto Eve, that is to say, the seed of the one unto the seed of the other, is (on the one hand) the <i>head</i> and (on the other) the <i>heel</i>, so that she subdus him <i>by the head</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2029]]</sup>, whilst he subdus her <i>by the heel</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2030]]</sup>. Behold then, this is likewise clear.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.22": "Here is yet another of those wondrous passages, the literal sense of which is exceeding absurd, but in which, so soon as thou shalt have perfectly understood the chapters of this treatise, thou wilt admire the allegory, full of wisdom and in conformity with the nature of being<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2031]]</sup>. \"At the moment,\" say they, \"when the serpent approached Eve, he infused into her impurity. The Israelites having presented themselves at Mount Sinai, their impurity was removed; as for the Gentiles, who presented not themselves at Mount Sinai, their impurity was not removed<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2032]]</sup>.\" Meditate also upon this<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:2033]]</sup>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.23": "Another passage which it behoovs us to know is this: “The tree of life has (an extent of) five hundred years’ journey, and all the waters of creation flow forth from beneath it[[fn:2034]].” Therein it has been declared that the intent of this measure is to designate the thickness of its trunk, and not the extent of its branches: “The purpose of this saying,” say they, “is not its foliage, but its trunk (<span dir=\"rtl\">קורתו</span>) <span dir=\"ltr\">[[fn:2035]]</span> which has (an extent of) five hundred years’ journey.” By <span dir=\"rtl\">קורתו</span> is understood its thick upright timber; they added this supplementary phrase[[fn:2036]] to complete the explanation of the matter and to bestow upon it greater clarity. This, then, is likewise manifest[[fn:2037]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.24": "It is also necessary to know the following passage: “As for the <i>tree of knowledge</i>, the Holy One has never revealed that tree to any man, nor will He ever reveal it[[fn:2038]].” And this is true; for the nature of existence so requirs[[fn:2039]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.25": "The following passage likewise merits that thou shouldst learn it: “<i>And the Lord God took the man</i>” (Genesis, 2, 15), that is to say, He exalted him; “<i>and He placed him</i>” (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויניחהו</span>) “<i>in the garden of Eden</i>,” that is, He gave him rest (<span dir=\"rtl\">הניח לו</span>) <span dir=\"ltr\">[[fn:2040]]</span>. Thus, the text was not understood[[fn:2041]] in the sense that He (God) had removed him from one place and set him in another, but in the allegorical sense that He <i>exalted</i> the rank of his being amidst those creatures which are subject to generation and corruption, and established him in a certain station[[fn:2042]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.26": "Another point that it is essential to draw to your attention is the wisdom with which the two sons of Adam were designated by the names of <i>Kaïn</i> and <i>Hebel</i> (Abel), that it was Kaïn who slew Hebel <i>in the field</i> (Genesis, 4, 8), that both perished, although he who had exercised violence was treated with leniency[[fn:2043]], and finally that there existed only a durable existence for <i>Sches: For God has established me</i> (S<small>CHATH</small>) <i>another posterity</i> (Genesis, 4, 25). All of this is justified[[fn:2044]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.27": "That which merits further attention is the passage: <i>And man imposed names</i>, <i>etc</i>. (<i>ibid.</i>, II, 20), which informs us that languages are <i>conventional</i> and not <i>natural</i>[[fn:2045]], as was previously believed[[fn:2046]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.28.sub_0": "That which, in fine, still merits thy meditation, are the four words employed to designate the relation between the heaven[[fn:2047]] and God, to wit: <span dir=\"rtl\">ברא</span> <i>to create</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">עשה</span> <i>to make</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">קנה</span> <i>to acquire</i>, <i>to possess</i>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">אל</span> <i>God</i>. It is said, for example: <i>God created</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ברא</span>) <i>the heaven and the earth</i> (Gen., I, 1); <i>in the day that the Lord made</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עשות</span>) <i>earth and heaven</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, II, 4); <i>author</i> or <i>possessor</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">קֹנה</span>) <i>of heaven and earth</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, XIV, 19 and 22); <i>the God</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אל</span>) <i>of the universe</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, XXI, 33)[[fn:2048]]; <i>God</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אלהי</span>) <i>of heaven and God of the earth</i> (<i>ibid</i>, XXIV, 3). As for the expressions: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר כוננתה</span> (<i>the moon and the stars</i>) <i>which thou hast</i> <small>ORDAINED</small> (Ps. 8, 4), <span dir=\"rtl\">טפחה שמים</span> (<i>and my right hand</i>) <i>has</i> <small>METED OUT</small> <i>the heavens with spans</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">נוטה שמים</span>, <i>who</i> <small>STRETCHES OUT</small> <i>the heavens</i> (Ps. 104, 2), they are all comprised within <span dir=\"rtl\">עשה</span>, <i>to make</i>. As concerns the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יצר</span>, <i>to form</i>, it is not met with (in this sense)[[fn:2049]]. It seems to me, indeed, that this verb is applied to the formation of figure and lineaments, or to one of the other accidents; for figure and lineaments are likewise accidents. Wherefore it is said: <span dir=\"rtl\">יוצר אור</span>, <i>who forms the light</i> (Isaiah, 45, 7), for the latter is an accident; <span dir=\"rtl\">יוצר הרים</span>, <i>who forms the mountains</i> (Amos, 4, 13), signifies <i>who fashions their figure;</i> and it is the same with <span dir=\"rtl\">וייצר יי׳ אלהים</span>, <i>the Lord God formed, etc.</i> (Genesis, 2, 7 and 19). But, in speaking of that Being which comprehends the whole of the universe, that is to say, the heaven and the earth, the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ברא</span>, <i>to create</i>, is employed, which, according to our view, signifies <i>to produce from nothingness</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.28.sub_1": "We also say <span dir=\"rtl\">עשה</span>, <i>to make</i>, (which is applied) to the specific forms[[fn:2050]] that have been bestowed upon them, I mean to their physical characters. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קנה</span>, <i>to possess</i>, has been applied to them, because God holds dominion over them, even as a master over his slaves; wherefore He is called <i>the Lord of all the earth</i> (Joshua, 3, 11, 13) and (simply) <span dir=\"rtl\">האדון</span>, the <i>Lord</i> (Exodus, 23, 17; XXXIV, 23). But, since[[fn:2051]] there is no <i>Lord</i> without there being at the same time a <i>possession</i>, which would seem to presuppose a certain pre-existent matter[[fn:2052]], the verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">ברא</span>, <i>to create</i>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">עשה</span>, <i>to make</i> have (rather) been employed. When one says <span dir=\"rtl\">אלהי השמים</span>, <i>God of heaven</i>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">אל עולם</span>, <i>God of the universe</i>, it is from the point of view of the perfection of God and the perfection of the latter; He is E<small>LOHÎM</small>, that is to say, <i>governing</i>, and they are <i>governed</i>[[fn:2053]]. One must not perceive therein the idea of <i>dominion</i>, for that is the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">קונה</span>, <i>possessor;</i> (in saying E<small>LOHÎM</small>) it is from the point of view of the <i>rank</i>[[fn:2054]] which God occupies in being and of their own rank[[fn:2055]]; for it is He, and not the heaven, who is God. Thou must well impress this upon thy mind.",
    "root.text.Part 2..29.29": "These summary observations[[fn:2056]], together with what has preceded and what shall yet be said upon this subject, are sufficient in regard to the object proposed in this treatise and in respect of the reader[[fn:2057]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..30.0": "Thou hast perchance already discerned the reason wherefore such stress has been laid upon the law of the Sabbath, and wherefore it has for its penalty <i>stoning</i>, so that the prince of prophets did indeed inflict death because of it[[fn:2058]]. It occupies the third rank after the existence of God and the negation of dualism [for the prohibition against worshipping any other being than Him has no other end than to affirm Unity][[fn:2059]]. Thou knowest already, from my words[[fn:2060]], that ideas are not preserved if they be not accompanied by actions that may fix, publish, and perpetuate them among the vulgar. Wherefore it has been prescribed unto us to honor this day, in order that the principle of the novelty of the world might be established and published in the universe by the rest in which everyone should indulge[[fn:2061]] upon the same day; for, if it were asked what is the cause thereof, the answer would be: <i>For in six days the Lord made etc.</i> (Exodus, 20, 11).",
    "root.text.Part 2..30.1": "However, two different causes have been assigned to this law, which were to have two different consequences: in the first Decalogue (Exodus, ch. XX), it is stated, to justify the glorification of the Sabbath: <i>For in six days the Lord made etc.</i>, while in Deuteronomy (5:15) it is stated: <i>And you shall remember that you were a slave in the land of Egypt</i>[[fn:2062]]...; <i>therefore the Lord your God commanded you to celebrate the Sabbath day</i>. And this is correct[[fn:2063]]. Indeed, the consequence (indicated) in the first passage is the exaltation and glorification of this day, as it is said: <i>Therefore the Lord blessed the Sabbath day and hallowed it</i> (Exodus 20:11), which is the consequence resulting from the cause (indicated by these words): <i>For in six days etc</i>. But, if it was made a <i>law</i> for us, and if we were commanded to observe this day, it is a consequence (resulting) from this other cause: that we were <i>slaves in Egypt</i>, where we did not work according to our own choice and when we wished, and where we were not free to rest.",
    "root.text.Part 2..30.2": "Rest and cessation from labour were therefore prescribed unto us, in order to combine two things: 1st, the adoption of a true opinion, namely, that of the creation of the world <i>de novo</i>, which, at the first glance and by the slightest reflection, leads to the acknowledgment of the existence of God; 2ndly, that we might remember the benefit which God has bestowed upon us by granting us rest <i>from under the burdens of Egypt</i> (Exodus vi. 6, 7). It is, as it were, a benefit which serves at once to confirm a speculative opinion and to produce the well-being of the body[[fn:2064]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..31.0": "The opinions of men concerning prophecy are like unto their opinions regarding the eternity or the novelty of the world; I mean to say that, even as those for whom the existence of God is proven profess three diverse opinions touching the eternity or the novelty of the world, as we have already set forth, so also the opinions concerning prophecy are three in number[[fn:2065]]. I shall not tarry over the opinion of the Epicurean—for he believs not in the existence of a God, and how, <i>a fortiori</i>, should he believe in prophecy?—but my purpose is only to relate the opinions of those who believe in God[[fn:2066]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..31.1": "I. The <i>first opinion</i>, professed by those among the heathen nations[[fn:2067]] who believed in prophecy, is likewise admitted by certain of the vulgar belonging to our religion[[fn:2068]]. God (say they), choosing whomsoever He wills among men, makes him a prophet and entrusts him with a mission; and it matters little, according to them, whether that man be wise or ignorant, aged or young. Nevertheless, they also lay down as a condition that he be a good man and of fair character; for no one has hitherto contended that, according to this opinion, God grants at times the gift of prophecy to a wicked man, unless He has first brought him back to the way of goodness.",
    "root.text.Part 2..31.2": "II. The <i>second opinion</i> is that of the philosophers[[fn:2069]]; namely, that prophecy is a certain perfection existing in human nature; but that the human individual obtains not this perfection save by means of <i>exercise</i>, which brings into <i>actuality</i> that which the species possesses <i>potentially</i>[[fn:2070]], unless it be hindered by some obstacle arising from the temperament or by some external cause. For, whenever the existence of a perfection is only <i>possible</i> in a certain species, it cannot exist to the ultimate degree in every individual of that species[[fn:2071]], but it must necessarily exist at least in some one individual[[fn:2072]]; and if this perfection be of a nature to require a determining cause for its realisation, such a cause is requisite[[fn:2073]]. According to this opinion, it is not possible that an ignorant man should become a prophet, nor that a man, without having been a prophet on the eve, should suddenly be one on the morrow, as one who finds a treasure. But the case is, on the contrary, as follows: if the superior man, perfect in his rational and moral qualities, possesses at the same time the most perfect imaginative faculty, and has prepared himself in the manner of which thou shalt hear further on, he will necessarily be a prophet; for this is a perfection which we possess <i>naturally</i>. It is therefore impossible, according to this opinion, that an individual, being fit for prophecy and having prepared himself therefor, should not be a prophet, no more than it is possible for an individual of sound temperament to be nourished with good food without there being produced therefrom good blood and other like things.",
    "root.text.Part 2..31.3": "III. The <i>third opinion</i>, which is that of our Law and a fundamental principle of our religion, is altogether similar to this philosophical opinion, with the exception of a single point[[fn:2074]]. For we believe that he who is fit for prophecy and prepared therefor may nevertheless not be a prophet, which thing depends upon the Divine Will. In my judgment, this is like unto all miracles, and belongs to the same category[[fn:2075]]; for nature requirs that every man who, by his natural constitution, is fit for prophecy and has exercised himself by education and study, should actually become a prophet; and if this be denied him, it is as when one is hindered from moving his hand, after the example of Jeroboam (I Kings xiii. 4), or as when one is hindered from seeing, like the army of the King of Syria when seeking[[fn:2076]] Elisha (II Kings vi. 18).",
    "root.text.Part 2..31.4": "— As touching what I have said, that it is our fundamental principle that one must be prepared and have attained perfection in the moral and rational virtues, this is what the Sages have said: “Prophecy resids only in the man who is wise, mighty, and rich[[fn:2077]]”. We have already set this forth in the Commentary on the <i>Mishnah</i>[[fn:2078]] and in the Great Work[[fn:2079]], and we have made it known that the <i>disciples of the prophets</i> were constantly occupied with preparation. But that he who is prepared may nevertheless suffer a hindrance and not become a prophet, is what thou mayest learn from the history of Baruch, the son of Neriah: for he had become the attendant of Jeremiah, who trained him[[fn:2080]], instructed him, and prepared him; yet, however much he was animated by the desire to become a prophet, this was nevertheless denied him, as he says: <i>I fainted in my sighing, and I find no rest</i> (Jeremiah xlv. 3), and it was answered him through the mediation of Jeremiah: <i>Thus shalt thou say unto him: Thus says the Lord etc.</i> <i>And</i> <i>seekest thou great things for thyself? Seek them not</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, vv. 4 and 5). Verily, one would be at liberty to say that it was intended to declare thereby that prophecy, in respect of Baruch, was too much of a <i>greatness</i>[[fn:2081]]; likewise might it be said that in the passage of Lamentations (ii. 9): <i>Her prophets also find no vision from the Lord</i>, (it must be understood) because they were in exile[[fn:2082]], as we shall set forth. But we find numerous passages, as well in the biblical texts as in the words of the Sages, all of which insist upon this fundamental principle, to wit, that God makes whomsoever He wills and whenever He wills a prophet, provided it be a man of exceeding perfection and truly superior; for as to the ignorant among the vulgar, that seems not possible unto us, — I mean that God should make one of them a prophet[[fn:2083]], — no more than it would be possible for Him to make a prophet of an ass or a frog. Such is our principle: (I mean) that it is indispensable to exercise oneself and to attain perfection, and that thereby alone ariss the <i>possibility</i> to which the Divine power attaches itself[[fn:2084]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..31.5": "Suffer not thyself to be led into error by this passage: <i>Before I formed thee in the belly</i> (<i>of thy mother</i>), <i>I knew thee</i>, <i>and before thou camest forth out of the womb I sanctified thee</i> (Jerem. i. 5)[[fn:2085]]; for this is the condition of every prophet, (I mean) that he must have a natural disposition from his primary constitution, as shall be set forth. As for these words: <i>I am a child</i>, <small>NA’AR</small> (<i>ibid.</i>, v. 6)[[fn:2086]], thou knowest that the Hebrew tongue calls the pious Joseph <small>NA’AR</small> (a youth), though he was thirty years of age[[fn:2087]], and that Joshua also is called <small>NA’AR</small>, though he then approached his sixtieth year. In fact, it is said (of the latter), at the time of the matter of the golden calf: <i>And his servant Joshua</i>, <i>the son of Nun</i>, <i>a young man</i> (<small>NA’AR</small>), <i>departed not etc</i>. (Exodus xxxiii. 11). Now Moses, our master, was then eighty-one years old[[fn:2088]], and his whole life was an hundred and twenty years; but Joshua, who lived yet fourteen years after him, attained the age of an hundred and ten years. It is therefore clear that Joshua was, at the time in question, fifty-seven years old at the least, and yet he is called <small>NA’AR</small>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..31.6": "Neither must thou suffer thyself to be led into error by what is found in the (prophetic) promises, where it is said: <i>I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh</i>, <i>and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy</i> (Joel ii. 28); for he (the prophet) has explained this and made known what manner of prophecy this should be, saying: <i>Your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions</i> (<i>ibid.</i>). In truth, whosoever foretells an unknown thing[[fn:2089]], whether by means of magic and divination, or by means of a true dream[[fn:2090]], is likewise called a <i>prophet;</i> wherefore the prophets of Baal and those of Asherah are called <i>prophets</i>. Seest thou not that God has said: <i>If there arise among you a prophet, or a</i> <small>DREAMER</small> (Deut. xiii. 1)?",
    "root.text.Part 2..31.7": "As touching the <i>scene of Mount Sinai</i>, although all (the Israelites), by way of miracle, saw the great fire and heard the awesome and terrifying sounds, yet there attained to the rank of prophecy only those who were fit for it, and that in different degrees. Thou seest it well by this passage: <i>Come up unto the Lord, thou, and Aaron</i>, <i>Nadab, and Abihu, and seventy of the elders of Israel</i> (Exodus xxiv. 1). He (Moses) occupies the highest degree, as it is said: <i>And Moses alone shall come near the Lord: but they shall not come nigh</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, v. 2); Aaron is placed beneath him, Nadab and Abihu are beneath Aaron, the seventy elders beneath Nadab and Abihu, and the others beneath these latter, according to their degrees of perfection. A passage of the Sages says: “Moses forms a partition by himself and Aaron a partition by himself[[fn:2091]]”.",
    "root.text.Part 2..31.8": "Since we have been led to speak of the <i>scene of Mount Sinai</i>, we shall call attention, in a separate chapter, to the elucidations which the (biblical) texts, when rightly examined, as well as the discourses of the Sages, provide concerning this <i>scene</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..32.0": "It is manifest unto me that, in the <i>scene of Mount Sinai</i>, all which reached Moses reached not in its entirety the whole of Israel[[fn:2092]]. The word, on the contrary, was addressed to Moses alone [wherefore the allocution, in the Decalogue, is made in the second person singular], and he, having descended to the foot of the mountain, made known unto the people that which he had heard. The text of the Pentateuch (says): <i>I stood between the Lord and you at that time, to shew you the word of the Lord</i> (Deut. v. 5), and it is further said: <i>Moses spake, and God answered him by a voice</i> (Exodus xix. 19); it is expressly stated in the <i>Mekhilthâ</i> that he repeated unto them each commandment as he had heard it[[fn:2093]]. Another passage of the Pentateuch says: <i>That the people may hear when I speak with thee</i>, <i>etc</i>. (<i>ibid.</i>, v. 9), which proves that the speech was addressed unto him; they heard the <i>mighty voice</i>, but they distinguished not the words[[fn:2094]], and it is of this mighty voice, heard (by them), that it was said: <i>When ye heard the voice</i> (Deut. v. 20). It is further said: <i>Ye heard the</i> <small>VOICE</small> <i>of words</i>, <i>but saw no similitude; only ye heard a</i> <small>VOICE</small> (<i>ibid.</i>, iv. 12); but it was not said <i>ye heard words</i>. Whenever, therefore, mention is made of words being heard, it is intended only of the <i>voice</i> which was heard; it was Moses who heard the words and who reported them unto them. This is what is evident from the text of the Pentateuch and from many discourses of the Sages.",
    "root.text.Part 2..32.1": "Nevertheless, (I must cite) from the latter an assertion reported in several places of the <i>Midraschîm</i> and which is found also in the Talmud; it is this: “<small>I AM</small> and <small>THOU SHALT HAVE NO OTHER</small>, they heard them from the mouth of the Omnipotence[[fn:2095]].” They mean thereby that these words reached them (directly), even as they reached Moses our Master, and that it was not Moses who caused them to reach them. Verily, these two principles, I mean the existence of God and His unity, are conceived by (simple) human speculation[[fn:2096]]; and everything that can be known by demonstration is known in the same absolute degree by the prophet and by every other who knows it, without there being therein any superiority of the one over the other. These two principles, then, are not known solely by <i>prophecy</i>, (as says) the text of the Pentateuch: <i>Unto thee it was shewed, that thou mightest know, etc</i>. (Deut. iv. 35)[[fn:2097]]. As for the other commandments, they belong to the category of <i>probable opinions</i> and of things accepted by tradition, and not to the category of <i>intelligibles</i>[[fn:2098]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..32.2": "But, whatsoever they may have said in this regard, that which the (biblical) texts and the words of the Sages imply[[fn:2099]] is that all the Israelites heard in this scene but a single sound, at one time[[fn:2100]]; and it is the sound whereby Moses and all Israel heard (the two commandments) <small>I AM</small> and <small>THOU SHALT HAVE NO OTHER</small>, which Moses caused them to hear (anew) in his own language, by pronouncing distinctly intelligible letters[[fn:2101]]. The Sages have declared themselves in this sense, supporting it by these words[[fn:2102]]: <small>ONCE</small> <i>God has spoken, twice have I heard this</i> (Ps. lxii. 12), and they have clearly said, at the commencement of the <i>Midrasch ’Hazêtha</i>, that they heard no other <i>voice</i> emanating (directly) from God[[fn:2103]], which is also indicated by the text of the Pentateuch: ... <i>with a great voice</i>, <i>and he added no more</i> (Deut. v. 19). It was after having heard this first voice that there occurred that which is recounted of the terror they experienced and of their violent fear, and (that they uttered) the words reported: <i>And ye said, Behold</i>, the <i>Lord our God has shewed us, etc. And now why should we die, etc.? Go thou near, and hear, etc</i>. (<i>ibid.</i>, vv. 21-24). He then advanced, he, the most illustrious of mortals, a second time, received the remainder of the commandments one by one[[fn:2104]], descended to the foot of the mountain, and caused them to hear them in the midst of this grandiose spectacle. They saw the fires and heard the voices, I mean those voices[[fn:2105]] which are (designated by the words) <i>thunders and lightnings</i> (Exodus xix. 16), such as the thunder and the loud sounding of the horn; and wheresoever mention is made (upon this occasion) of several <i>voices</i> being heard, as for example: <i>And all the people perceived</i>[[fn:2106]] <i>the voices</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xx. 15), it signifies naught but the sounding of the horn, the thunder, etc. But the <i>voice of the Lord</i>, I mean the <i>created voice</i>[[fn:2107]], whereby the <i>word</i> (of God) was communicated, they heard but once only, as the Pentateuch textually says and as the Sages have expounded in the place which I have pointed out to thee. It is of this voice (that it was said) that “their soul departed upon hearing it[[fn:2108]],” and by means of which the first two commandments were perceived.",
    "root.text.Part 2..32.3": "It must be known, however, that even for this <i>voice</i>, their degree (of perception) was by no means equal to that of Moses our Master. I must call thy attention to this mystery and inform thee that it is a thing traditionally admitted by our nation and known by its learned men. Verily, all the passages where thou findest (the words): <i>And the Lord spake unto Moses, saying</i>, Onkelos translats them (literally) by <span dir=\"rtl\">ומלל יי</span>, <i>And the Lord spake, etc</i>. And likewise (he translats the words): <i>And God spake all these words</i> (Exodus xx. 1) by <span dir=\"rtl\">ומלל יי יה כל פתגמיא</span>. But these words of the Israelites (addressed) to Moses: <i>And let not God speak with us</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, v. 16), he translats by <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יתמלל עמנא מן קדם יי</span>, <i>and let it not be spoken with us from before the Lord</i>. He has therefore revealed unto thee thereby the distinction which we have established[[fn:2109]]. Thou knowest that these remarkable and important things Onkelos, as it has been expressly said, learned from the mouth of R. Eliezer and R. Joshua[[fn:2110]], who are the <i>Sages of Israel</i> par excellence[[fn:2111]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..32.4": "It behoves thee to know all this and to bear it in mind; for it is impossible to penetrate into the <i>scene of Mount Sinai</i> more deeply than has been done[[fn:2112]], (this scene) being of the number of the <i>secrets of the Law</i>. The true nature of this perception and the circumstances that accompanied it are for us a thing right obscure[[fn:2113]]; for there has never been anything similar thereto aforetime, nor shall there be in the future. Know this well.",
    "root.text.Part 2..33.0": "As touching that passage which is met with in the Pentateuch, and which says: <i>Behold</i>, <i>I send an angel before thee, etc</i>. (Exodus, xxiii, 20), the sense of this passage has been explained in Deuteronomy (xviii, 18), where we read that God said unto Moses, during the <i>scene at Mount Sinai: I will raise them up a prophet, etc</i>.[[fn:2114]]. The proof of which[[fn:2115]] is that it is said (concerning) this angel: <i>Beware of him and obey his voice, etc</i>. (Exodus, xxiii, 21), an injunction which is indubitably addressed unto the multitude; but the angel did not manifest himself unto the multitude, unto whom he communicated (directly) neither commandment nor prohibition, that they should be warned not to be rebellious against him. The meaning of these words can therefore be naught but this: that God made known unto them that there should be among them a prophet, unto whom an angel should come, who should speak unto him, and who should communicate unto him commandments and prohibitions; God therefore commands us not to be rebellious against that angel whose word the prophet would cause to reach us, as has been clearly stated in Deuteronomy (xviii, 15): <i>Unto him ye shall hearken</i>, and again: <i>And it shall come to pass, that whosoever will not hearken unto my words which he shall speak</i> <small>IN MY NAME</small> <i>etc</i>. (<i>ibid.</i>, v. 19), which explains the words <i>for</i> <small>MY NAME</small> <i>is in him</i> (Exodus, xxiii, 21)[[fn:2116]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..33.1": "All this (was said unto them) only to give them the following admonition[[fn:2117]]: This grand spectacle which ye have seen, that is to say the <i>scene</i>[[fn:2118]] <i>at Mount Sinai</i>, is not a thing which is to continue for you; there shall be naught like unto it in the future, nor shall there always be <i>fire</i> or <i>cloud</i>, as there is now <i>continually upon the tabernacle</i>[[fn:2119]]. But an angel whom I shall send unto your prophets shall conquer the lands for you, shall lay open[[fn:2120]] the earth before you, and shall make known unto you what ye ought to do[[fn:2121]]; it is he who shall make known unto you what things are to be pursued and what are to be eschewed.",
    "root.text.Part 2..33.2": "— Hereby also has been given the principle which I have not ceased to set forth[[fn:2122]], to wit, that unto every prophet other than Moses, our Master, revelation came through the mediation of an angel. Know this well.",
    "root.text.Part 2..34.0": "I have already set forth to all[[fn:2123]], in the Commentary on the <i>Mishnah</i> and in the <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, the four differences whereby the prophecy of Moses, our Master, was distinguished from that of other prophets; and I have given proofs thereof and shown the evidence of them[[fn:2124]]. It is therefore not necessary to repeat this, and it is moreover outside the scope of this treatise.",
    "root.text.Part 2..34.1": "I must inform thee that all that I say concerning prophecy, in the chapters of this treatise, relats only to the prophetic quality of all the prophets who were before Moses and of those who were to come after him; but, as for the prophecy of Moses, our Master, I shall not touch upon it in these chapters, not even by a single word, neither expressly nor by allusion. Verily, according to my view, it is only by <i>homonymity</i>[[fn:2125]] that the name <i>prophet</i> is applied at once to Moses and to the others; and it is the same, in my opinion, regarding his miracles and those of the others, for his miracles are not of the same category as those of other prophets.",
    "root.text.Part 2..34.2": "The proof derived from the Law, (to establish) that his prophecy was distinct from that of all his predecessors, is found in these words: <i>I appeared unto Abraham, etc., but by my name of </i>E<small>TERNAL</small> I was not known to them (Exodus, vi. 3); for we are thereby informed that his perception was not like unto that of the patriarchs, but greater, nor, for a stronger reason, (like unto) those of other (prophets) who were before him[[fn:2126]]. But, that his prophecy was also distinct from that of all his successors, is what has been said, in the form of a simple statement[[fn:2127]]: <i>And there arose not a prophet since in Israel like unto Moses, whom God knew face to face</i> (Deut., xxxiv. 10): it is thus clearly stated that his perception was distinct from the perception of all those who should succeed him among the Israelites, — who (notwithstanding) were <i>a kingdom of priests and an holy nation</i> (Exodus, xix. 6), <i>and in the midst of whom was the Eternal</i> (Numbers, xvi. 3), — and, with greater reason, among the other nations[[fn:2128]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..34.3": "That which generally distinguishes his miracles from those of any other prophet whatsoever, is that all the miracles wrought by the prophets, or wrought in their favour, were known only to a few persons, as for instance the miracles of Elijah and Elisha; seest thou not that the King of Israel inquirs concerning them[[fn:2129]], and requests Gehazi to make them known unto him, as it is said: <i>Tell me, I pray thee, all the great things that Elisha has done; and he told, etc. And Gehazi said: My lord, O king, this is the woman and this is her son whom Elisha restored to life</i> (II Kings, viii. 4 and 5). Thus it is with the miracles of every prophet, save for Moses, our master; wherefore the Scripture declars concerning the latter, likewise in the form of a statement[[fn:2130]], that there shall never arise a prophet who shall work miracles publicly, before both friend and adversary[[fn:2131]], as did Moses. This is what is said: <i>And there arose not a prophet etc., in all the signs and the wonders etc., in the sight of all Israel</i> (Deut., xxxiv. 10-12); here, then, are linked and joined together at once[[fn:2132]] two things: that there shall no more arise (a prophet) who shall have the same perception as he, nor who shall do that which he did. Thereafter it is declared that these miracles were wrought <i>before Pharaoh, and all his servants, and all his land</i>, his adversaries, as also in the presence of all the Israelites, his partisans: <i>in the sight of all Israel;</i> this is a thing which had not occurred with any prophet before him[[fn:2133]], and his true prediction has foretold that it should occur with no other.",
    "root.text.Part 2..34.4": "Be not misled by that which has been stated concerning the sun's light, which stood still for Joshua for several hours: <i>And he said in the sight of Israel</i> (Josh., X, 12); for it was not stated <i>of</i> <small>ALL</small> <i>Israel</i>, as was done concerning Moses. Similarly, Elijah, upon Mount Carmel, acted only before a small company of men[[fn:2134]]. Should I state <i>for several hours</i>, it is because the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כיום תמים</span> (<i>about a whole day</i>, Joshua, 10, 13) appear to me to signify <i>as the longest possible day;</i> for <span dir=\"rtl\">תמים</span> denotes <i>complete</i>. It is therefore as if it had been declared that this day in Gibeon was for them as the longest of summer days in those regions[[fn:2135]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..34.5": "After thou shalt have set apart in thy mind the prophecy of Moses and his miracles, — for these pertain to a perception and to acts equally extraordinary[[fn:2136]], — and when thou shalt be convinced that this is a degree which we are unable to comprehend in all its reality, thou shalt hear what I shall say, in all these (following) chapters, concerning prophecy and the different degrees occupied therein by the prophets, setting aside this first degree (of Moses)[[fn:2137]]. Such is what I had to say in this chapter.",
    "root.text.Part 2..35.0": "Know that prophecy, in reality[[fn:2138]], is an emanation from God, which is shed forth, through the medium of the Active Intellect, first upon the rational faculty, and then upon the imaginative faculty; it is the highest degree of man and the term of perfection to which his species may attain, and this state is the highest perfection of the imaginative faculty. It is a thing which can in no wise exist in every man, and it is not a thing unto which one may arrive by perfecting oneself in the speculative sciences and by the improvement of morals, should they all be of the best and the most beautiful, without there being joined thereto the greatest possible perfection of the imaginative faculty in its original formation. Thou knowest that the perfection of these corporeal faculties, among which is the imaginative faculty, depends upon the best possible temperament of the organ bearing such faculty, upon its fairest proportion and the greatest purity of its matter[[fn:2139]]; this is a thing of which it is in no wise possible to repair the loss[[fn:2140]], or to supply the defect, by means of regimen. For an organ whose temperament has been ill-disposed from the beginning of its formation, the restorative regimen can at most preserve it in a certain degree of health, without being able to restore it to the best possible constitution; but, if its infirmity proceeds from its disproportion[[fn:2141]], from its position, or from its substance, I mean from the very matter of which it was formed, then is there no means of remedy[[fn:2142]]. Thou knowest all this well; it would therefore be useless to enter upon long explanations on this matter.",
    "root.text.Part 2..35.1.sub_0": "You also know the actions of this imaginative faculty, which consist in preserving the memory of sensible things, in combining them[[fn:2143]], and—what is (particularly) in its nature—in tracing (images); its greatest and most noble activity[[fn:2144]] occurs only when the senses are at rest and cease to function, and it is then that a certain inspiration comes to it, which is according to its disposition, and which is the cause of true dreams[[fn:2145]] and also that of prophecy. It differs only by degree, and not by species[[fn:2146]]. You know that they (the Sages) said on various occasions: \"a dream is a sixtieth part of prophecy[[fn:2147]]\"; but one cannot establish a proportion between two specifically different things, and it would not be permissible to say, for example, that the perfection of man is so many times the double of the perfection of a horse. They repeated this idea in <i>Bereshith Rabbah</i>, saying: \"The unripe fruit of prophecy is the dream[[fn:2148]].\" This is a remarkable comparison: for the unripe fruit (<span dir=\"rtl\">נובלת</span>) is identically the fruit itself, except that it has fallen before its perfect maturity[[fn:2149]]; likewise, the action of the imaginative faculty during sleep is the same as in the state of prophecy, except that it is still insufficient and has not reached its end. — But why should we instruct you through the words of the Sages and leave aside the texts of the Pentateuch? (As for example:) <i>If there be a prophet among you, I the Lord will make myself known unto him in a vision, and will speak unto him in a dream</i> (Numbers 12:6). Here God has made known to us the true being of prophecy, and has informed us that it is a perfection that occurs in a <i>dream</i> or in a <i>vision</i>. The word <i>vision</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מראה</span>) is derived from <i>to see</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ראה</span>); for it happens that the imaginative faculty acts so perfectly that it sees the object as if it existed externally, and the object which has its origin only within it[[fn:2150]] seems to have come to it through the channel of external sensation. In these two parts, I mean in the <i>vision</i> and in the <i>dream</i>, are contained all the degrees of prophecy.",
    "root.text.Part 2..35.1.sub_1": "It is well known that the matter wherewith a man is much occupied in his waking state, whilst employing his senses, and to which he applies himself and which is the object of his desire, is also that wherewith the imaginative faculty busies itself during sleep, when the <i>Active</i> <i>Intellect</i> overflows upon it, according as it is prepared thereunto. It would be superfluous to cite examples for this or to say more thereof; for it is a manifest thing which everyone knows, and it is even as the perception of the senses, against which no man of sound mind raiss an objection.",
    "root.text.Part 2..35.2.sub_0": "After these preliminaries, it must be known that we here treat of a human individual[[fn:2151]] whose cerebral substance, in its primitive formation, should be exceedingly well proportioned, by the purity of its matter and the temperament peculiar to each of its parts, by its quantity and by its position, and should suffer no derangement of temperament from any other organ. Furthermore, it behoovs that this individual should have acquired science and wisdom, so as to pass from <i>potentiality</i> to <i>actuality</i>[[fn:2152]]; that he should possess an all-perfect human intelligence and pure and <i>equable</i> human morals[[fn:2153]]; that all his desires should be directed towards the science of the mysteries of this universe and the knowledge of their causes; that his thought should ever be fixed upon noble things; that he should occupy himself only with the knowledge of God, the contemplation of His works, and that which must be believed in this regard; and finally, that his thought and his desire should be detached from animal things[[fn:2154]], such as the pursuit of those pleasures afforded by eating, drinking, cohabitation, and, in general, the sense of touch, which sense Aristotle has expressly declared, in the <i>Ethics</i>, to be a reproach unto us[[fn:2155]]. — [And how well has he spoken! and how true it is that this sense is a reproach unto us! for we possess it only in so far as we are animals, like the other brute beasts, and it contains nothing that pertains to the idea of <i>humanity</i>. As for the other sensual enjoyments, such as those of smell, hearing, and sight, although they be corporeal, there is sometimes found therein a pleasure for man as man, as Aristotle has set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 2..35.2.sub_1": "We have been led here to speak of that which is not our present aim, yet was this necessary; for too often the thoughts of distinguished scholars are preoccupied with the pleasures of the sense in question and desire them, and nevertheless they marvel that they are not <i>prophets</i>[[fn:2156]], since, (say they) prophecy is something which is in the nature of man.] — It would also be necessary that the thought and desire of this individual be detached from vain ambitions[[fn:2157]], I mean the desire to dominate or to be exalted by the common people and to win their homage and obedience without any other end[[fn:2158]] [for one ought rather to consider all men according to their stations[[fn:2159]], in respect of which they are assuredly similar, some to domestic cattle, others to wild beasts, upon whom the perfect and <i>solitary</i> man[[fn:2160]] bestows not his thought — if indeed he thinks of them at all — save to preserve himself from the evil they might do him, if perchance he has to do with them, or to derive profit from the advantages they may offer, when he finds himself reduced thereto for some necessity]. — If therefore, in an individual such as we have just described, the imaginative faculty, as perfect as possible, were in full activity, and the Active Intellect overflowed upon it by reason of the speculative perfection of the individual, such a one would indubitably perceive naught but most extraordinary divine things, would see naught but God and His angels, and the science he would acquire would have for its object only true opinions and rules of conduct embracing the goodly relations of men one with another.",
    "root.text.Part 2..35.3": "It is well known that in the three things we have laid down as conditions — to wit, the perfection of the rational faculty by means of study, that of the imaginative faculty in its primitive formation, and that of morals which is obtained when one detaches his thought from all corporeal pleasures and silenced the desire for every kind of foolish and pernicious grandeur — perfect men possess a great superiority over one another, and it is by reason of the superiority in each of these three things that all prophets are superior in rank one to another.",
    "root.text.Part 2..35.4": "Thou art aware that every bodily faculty is at times blunted, weakened, and impaired, and at other times strengthened. Now, this imaginative faculty is indubitably a bodily faculty; wherefore thou wilt find that the prophets, during times of sadness, anger, and such-like feelings, cease to prophesy. Thou knowest that the Sages say: «Prophecy comes neither during sadness nor during dejection[[fn:2161]]»; that our patriarch Jacob had no revelation during the days of his mourning, because his imaginative faculty was occupied with the loss of Joseph[[fn:2162]], and that Moses had no revelation, as heretofore, from the time of the unhappy event[[fn:2163]] of the <i>spies</i> until the generation of the wilderness had utterly perished[[fn:2164]], because he was overwhelmed by the enormity of their crime [although, for the rest, the imaginative faculty had no part in his prophecy, and the <i>Intellect</i> (<i>Active</i>) was poured out upon him without the mediation of that faculty[[fn:2165]]; for, as we have several times observed, he did not prophesy, like the other prophets, through parables[[fn:2166]], a matter which shall yet be explained elsewhere and is not the object of this chapter].",
    "root.text.Part 2..35.5": "Likewise, thou wilt find that certain prophets, after having prophesied for a certain time, were divested of prophecy, which, by reason of some accident that occurred, could not continue[[fn:2167]]. This is, indubitably, the essential and immediate cause wherefore prophecy ceased in the time of the Captivity; can there exist for a man, in any circumstance whatsoever, a weightier cause for <i>dejection</i> or <i>sorrow</i>[[fn:2168]], than to be a slave, the property of another, and subject to ignorant and impious men, who join the absence of true reason to the fullness of animal concupiscences, <i>and to be able to do naught against it</i>[[fn:2169]]? It is this wherewith we have been threatened, and it is this which was meant by these words: <i>They shall wander to seek the word of the Lord, and shall not find it</i> (Amos, viii, 12); and it is further said: <i>Her king and her princes are among the nations, without law; her prophets also find no vision from the Lord</i> (Lam., ii, 9). This is true, and the reason thereof is manifest; for the instrument has ceased to function[[fn:2170]]. It is for this same reason also that prophecy shall return to us in the days of the Messiah [may he be speedily revealed!], as has been promised unto us.",
    "root.text.Part 2..36.0": "It is necessary to call your attention to the nature of the being of this divine emanation[[fn:2171]] which arrives to us, by which we think and (by which) our intelligences are superior to one another. For sometimes it arrives to an individual in a measure sufficient to perfect it itself, without going beyond[[fn:2172]], sometimes what arrives to the individual suffices beyond its own perfection[[fn:2173]], (so that there remains) for the perfection of others. The same is true for all beings: there are some who have enough perfection to govern others (beings), while there are others who have perfection only to the extent that it is necessary for them to be governed by others[[fn:2174]], as we have expounded.",
    "root.text.Part 2..36.1": "This being so, thou must know that, if this emanation from the <i>Intellect</i> (<i>Active</i>) be shed only upon the rational faculty of man, without any of it being shed upon the imaginative faculty [whether because the emanation itself is insufficient[[fn:2175]], or because the imaginative faculty is defective in its original formation, so that it is incapable of receiving the emanation of the Intellect], this constituts the class of learned men who devote themselves to speculation. But if this emanation be shed at once upon both faculties, I mean upon the rational and upon the imaginative [as we have set forth and as others among the philosophers have also set forth][[fn:2176]], and the imaginative faculty has been originally created in all its perfection, this constituts the class of prophets. If, finally, the emanation be shed only upon the imaginative faculty, and the rational faculty remain behind, whether by reason of its original formation or by reason of lack of exercise, this constituts the class of statesmen who make laws[[fn:2177]], of diviners, of augurs, and of those who have true dreams; and likewise, those who perform miracles by extraordinary artifices and occult arts, without being nevertheless learned men, are all of this third class.",
    "root.text.Part 2..36.2": "What thou must be convinced of is that, to certain men of this third class, there occur, even when they are awake, wondrous chimerical visions, dreams and agitations, similar to prophetic visions, so that they believe themselves to be prophets; they take much delight, therefore, in what they perceive of these chimerical visions, believing that they have acquired sciences without having pursued studies, and they bring great confusion into grave and speculative matters, mingling together, in a wondrous manner, true things and chimeras. All this is because the imaginative faculty is strong in them, while the rational faculty is weak and has obtained absolutely nothing; I mean to say that it has not passed into action.",
    "root.text.Part 2..36.3": "It is known that in each of these three classes there are many gradations[[fn:2178]]. Each of the first two classes is divided into two parts, as we have set forth. For the emanation which reaches each of these two classes is either sufficient only for the perfection of the individual and no more, or it is sufficiently puissant that there remains unto that individual enough wherewith to perfect others[[fn:2179]]. As touching the first class, that of the learned, sometimes that which is poured out upon the rational faculty[[fn:2180]] of the individual is sufficient to make of him a man of study and intelligence, possessing knowledge and discernment, yet who feels not himself moved to instruct others, nor to compose works, having for this neither the inclination nor the (requisite) capacity; at other times, that which is poured out upon him is of a force sufficient to stimulate him necessarily to the composition of works and to the office of teaching. It is likewise with the second class: sometimes a prophet has inspirations which serve only for his own perfection; at other times he is inspired in such wise as to be compelled to make an appeal unto men, to instruct them, and to shed upon them (a portion) of his perfection.",
    "root.text.Part 2..36.4": "It is therefore manifest that, without this superabundant perfection, books on the sciences would not have been composed, nor would the prophets have called men to the knowledge of the truth. Verily, a learned man writs nothing for himself, to the end of teaching himself what he already knows; but it is in the nature of this (active) intellect[[fn:2181]] to communicate itself perpetually and to extend successively its <i>emanation</i> from one individual to another[[fn:2182]], until it reaches an individual beyond whom its influence cannot spread[[fn:2183]], and whom it only perfects (personally), as we have explained, by a comparison, in one of the chapters of this treatise[[fn:2184]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..36.5": "The nature of this matter is such that he who has received this superabundant <i>overflow</i> necessarily preaches unto men, whether he be hearkened to or no, even should he expose his person[[fn:2185]]; so that we find prophets who preached unto men even unto being slain, stimulated by this divine inspiration which left them neither quiet nor rest[[fn:2186]], even when they were smitten by great misfortunes. Wherefore thou seest Jeremiah declare[[fn:2187]] that, by reason of the contempt which he endured from those rebellious and incredulous men who lived in his time, he desired to conceal[[fn:2188]] his prophetic mission and no longer call them to the truth which they had rejected, but that this was impossible for him: <i>For the word of the Lord</i>, says he, <i>was made a reproach unto me, and a derision, daily. Then I said, I will not make mention of him, nor speak any more in his name. But his word was in mine heart as a burning fire shut up in my bones, and I was weary with forbearing, and I could not stay</i> (Jeremiah xx. 8, 9). It is in the same sense that another prophet has said: <i>The Lord God has spoken, who can but prophesy</i> (Amos iii. 8)? — Thou must well consider this.",
    "root.text.Part 2..37.0": "Know that every man necessarily possesses a <i>faculty of courage</i>[[fn:2189]]; without this, he would not be moved by thought to avert that which is harmful to him. This faculty, in my judgment, is among the faculties of the soul what the <i>expulsive</i>[[fn:2190]] faculty is among the physical faculties. This faculty of courage varies in strength and weakness, even as do the other faculties: so that thou findest such a man as advancs against a lion, and another who fles before a mouse; one who advancs alone against an army to do battle, and another who trembls and is afraid when a woman utters a cry. There must also be possessed, from one's primitive formation, a certain predisposition of temperament, which, with a certain manner of thinking, shall increase [so that what is <i>in potentiality</i> shall come forth by the effort made to bring it forth], and which, with another manner of thinking[[fn:2191]], shall diminish through lack of exercise. From the earliest age, one recogniss in children whether this faculty[[fn:2192]] be strong or weak within them.",
    "root.text.Part 2..37.1": "Likewise, this <i>faculty of divination</i> (which is found in prophets) exists in all men, but varies in degree; (it exists) particularly concerning those things wherewith man is strongly preoccupied and in which he exerciss his thought. Thou divinest, for example[[fn:2193]], that such a one has spoken or acted in such a manner in such a circumstance, and it is in truth so. Thou findest such a man in whom the faculty of conjecture and divination[[fn:2194]] is so strong and just, that well-nigh everything which, in his imagination, he believs to be, is (really) such as he has imagined it, or is (at least) so in part[[fn:2195]]. The causes thereof are many, (and this occurrs) through a concatenation of many circumstances[[fn:2196]], anterior, posterior, and present; but, by the power of this (faculty of) divination, the mind traverss all these premises and draws the conclusions therefrom in so short a time that one would say it was the work of an instant[[fn:2197]]. It is by this faculty that certain men give warning[[fn:2198]] of grave things which are to come to pass.",
    "root.text.Part 2..37.2": "These two faculties, I mean, the faculty of <i>boldness</i> and the faculty of <i>divination</i>, must necessarily be very powerful in prophets. When the (<i>Active</i>) <i>Intellect</i> emanats upon them, these two faculties assume an exceeding great force, and thou knowest to what extent the effect produced thereby has reached; to wit, that a solitary man should boldly present himself[[fn:2199]], with his staff, before a great king, to deliver a nation from the thraldom imposed by him, and that he should feel neither terror[[fn:2200]] nor fear, because it had been said unto him (by God): <i>For I will be with thee</i> (Exodus, iii, 12). This is a state which varies greatly amongst them (the prophets), yet which is indispensable unto them[[fn:2201]]. Thus it was said unto Jeremiah: <i>Be not afraid of them, etc. Be not dismayed at them, etc. Behold, I have made thee this day a defenced city, etc.</i> (Jeremiah, i, 8, 17, 18); and unto Ezekiel it was said: <i>Be not afraid of them, neither be afraid of their words</i> (Ezek., ii, 6). Thus thou shalt find them all endowed with a mighty boldness. Likewise, by the great development of their faculties of divination, they promptly foretell the future; but, in this respect also, there is amongst them a variation (of degrees), as thou knowest.",
    "root.text.Part 2..37.3": "It must be known that <i>true prophets</i> undoubtedly possess also <i>speculative</i> perceptions, (but of such a nature) that man, by speculation alone, could not grasp the causes which may lead to such <i>knowledge;</i> even as when they foretell things which man could not foretell by means of mere conjecture and vulgar divination[[fn:2202]]. Indeed, that same inspiration[[fn:2203]] which is shed upon the imaginative faculty, so as to perfect it to such a degree that its action reaches even to the foretelling of the future and the perceiving of it as though it were concerning things perceived by the senses and which had reached that imaginative faculty by the way of the senses, (this same inspiration, I say) perfects also the action of the rational faculty to such a degree that it attains by this action to the knowledge of the real essence of things[[fn:2204]] and possesses the perception thereof as though it had obtained it through speculative propositions.",
    "root.text.Part 2..37.4": "Such is the truth which must be admitted by whosoever loves to render an impartial judgment[[fn:2205]]; for all things serve as testimony and proof unto one another. This befitts even much more the rational faculty[[fn:2206]]. Indeed, it is only upon it, in reality, that the <i>Active Intellect</i> (directly) emanats, which brings it into <i>actuality</i>, and it is through the rational faculty that the emanation reaches the imaginative faculty; how then could it be that the imaginative power were perfect to the point of perceiving what comes not to it by way of the senses, without the same being true for the rational faculty, that is to say (without its being perfect to the point) of perceiving what it could not perceive by means of premises, logical conclusion, and reflection? — Such is the true idea of prophecy, and such are the opinions which serve to characterize the prophetic teaching. If, in what I have just said concerning prophets,",
    "root.text.Part 2..37.5": "I have made it a condition that they be <i>true prophets</i>, it has been to make my reservations regarding the folk of the third class[[fn:2207]], who possess neither rational notions nor science, but only chimeras and erroneous opinions. It may also be that what those men perceive is nothing other than (true) ideas which they (formerly) held, and of which their (present) chimeras have preserved the traces[[fn:2208]], alongside all that is in their imaginative faculty; so that, after many of their chimeras have been annihilated and made to disappear, the traces[[fn:2209]] of these (ancient) ideas alone remained and appeared unto them as a novelty[[fn:2210]] and as a thing coming from without[[fn:2211]]. I deem it possible to compare them to a man who had with him, in his house, thousands of animals; these having all withdrawn from the house, save for a single individual from among the number of those that were there, the man, remaining alone with this individual, would imagine that the latter had just entered into the house unto him, whereas it is not so, and on the contrary, it is that very one which has not gone out.",
    "root.text.Part 2..37.6": "This it is which has given rise to the most pernicious errors and which has caused the ruin of many men who claimed to possess discernment[[fn:2212]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..37.7": "Thus[[fn:2213]], thou shalt find folk who base the truth of their notions upon dreams which they have had, imagining that what they beheld in sleep is aught else than the idea which they themselves conceived or heard whilst in a waking state.",
    "root.text.Part 2..37.8": "Wherefore, no attention must be paid unto those whose rational faculty is not perfect, and who have not attained unto the highest speculative perfection; for he alone who has reached speculative perfection can thereafter obtain other (superior) knowledge, when[[fn:2214]] the divine intellect is poured out upon him. It is such a one who is truly a prophet, and this was clearly expressed (by the words) <span dir=\"rtl\">ונביא לבב חכמה</span> (Ps. xc. 12), that is to say, that <i>the true prophet</i>[[fn:2215]] <i>is</i> (<i>he who possesses</i>)<i> a heart full of wisdom</i>. This also is a matter with which one must be thoroughly impressed.",
    "root.text.Part 2..38.0.sub_0": "Having spoken of the essence of prophecy, which we have made known in all its reality, and having set forth that the prophecy of Moses, our Master, is distinguished from that of others, we say that it is this perception alone (that of Moses) which had for its necessary consequence the calling of us unto the <i>Law</i>. Indeed, a call similar to that which Moses made unto us had never been made by any of those whom we know[[fn:2216]], from Adam until him, nor was any such call made after him by any of our prophets. Likewise, it is a fundamental principle of our Law that there shall never be another; wherefore, in our opinion, there has never been, nor shall there ever be, but one single Law, even that of Moses, our Master. Herein follows a (more ample) explanation, according to what has been expressly stated in the prophetic books and what is found in the traditions. It is that, of all the prophets who preceded Moses, our Master, such as the Patriarchs, Shem, Eber, Noah, Methuselah, and Enoch, none ever said unto a class of men: “God[[fn:2217]] has sent me unto you and has commanded me to tell you such and such a thing; He forbidds you to do this thing and commands you to do that other.” This is a thing which is attested by no text of the Pentateuch and which no true tradition relats. Those, on the contrary, received no divine revelation[[fn:2218]] save in the sense which we have already set forth[[fn:2219]]; and if any among them were more powerfully inspired, as, for example, Abraham, he gathered men together and called them, by way of instruction and guidance, unto the truth which he himself had perceived. Thus it was that Abraham instructed men, showing them, by speculative proofs, that the universe had but one God, that it was He who had created all that is outside of Him, and that one must not worship these figures (of the stars), nor any created thing. This is what he inculcated upon men, drawing them by fair discourse and by benevolence; but never did he say unto them: “God has sent me unto you and has commanded or forbidden me (such or such a thing).” This is so true that, when circumcision was commanded unto him, for himself, his children, and those who belonged unto him, he circumcised them, but invited not (other) men, by a prophetic call, to do the like.",
    "root.text.Part 2..38.0.sub_1": "Seest thou not that the text of Scripture says concerning him: <i>For I have known him</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Gen. xviii. 19)? whence it clearly follows that he proceeded only by way of <i>commandment</i>[[fn:2220]]; and it was in the same form that Isaac, Jacob, Levi, Kohath, and Amram addressed their calls unto men. Thou findest likewise that the Doctors, speaking of the prophets prior (to Moses), say: <i>The tribunal of Eber, the tribunal of Methuselah, the school of Methuselah</i>[[fn:2221]]; for all these prophets did but instruct men, after the manner of preceptors, teachers, and guides, but never said: <i>the Lord said unto me: Speak unto the sons of such a one</i>[[fn:2222]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..38.1": "Thus did the matter pass before Moses, our Master. As for Moses, thou knowest what was said unto him and what he said, and (thou knowest) that word which all the people addressed unto him: <i>This day we have seen that God does talk with man, etc.</i> (Deut. v. 24). As for all those of our prophets who came after Moses, our Master, thou knowest in what manner they express themselves in all their relations and that they present themselves as preachers who invite men to follow the Law of Moses, threatening those who should prove rebellious and making promises unto those who should strive to follow it[[fn:2223]]. And we believe likewise that it shall ever be thus[[fn:2224]], as it has been said: <i>It is not in heaven, etc.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xxx. 12); <i>unto us and to our children for ever</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xxix. 29). And so it must needs be; for, so soon as a thing is the most perfect possible of its kind, any other thing of the same kind cannot but be of a lesser perfection, whether by exceeding the just measure or by falling below it. If, for example, an <i>equal</i> temperament is the most equal possible in a species, any temperament which should be outside of this equality would err by deficiency or by excess. It is the same with this Law, which has been declared to be <i>equal</i> (i.e., <i>equitable</i> or <i>just</i>), saying: <i>statutes and judgments</i> <small>RIGHTEOUS</small> <span dir=\"rtl\">צדיקים</span> (<i>ibid.</i>, iv. 8); for thou knowest that <span dir=\"rtl\">צדיקים</span> (righteous) signifies <i>equal</i> (or <i>equitable</i>)[[fn:2225]]. Verily, these are religious practices in which there is neither burden nor <i>excess</i>, such as (exist) in monachism, in the life of the pilgrim[[fn:2226]], etc., nor any deficiency (vice) which leads to gluttony and debauchery, so as to diminish the perfection of man in respect of morals and study, as (do) all the (religious) laws of the ancient peoples[[fn:2227]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..38.2": "Whenever we shall discourse, in this treatise, upon the reasons which may be assigned for the laws (of Moses[[fn:2228]]), thou shalt receive all necessary enlightenment touching their <i>equability</i>[[fn:2229]] and their wisdom; wherefore it has been said of them: <i>The law of the Lord is perfect</i> (Ps. xix. 8). When any man claims that they impose great and heavy burdens and that they cause torments, 'tis verily an error of judgment. I shall demonstrate that, for perfect men, they are easy; wherefore it was said: <i>What does the Lord thy God require of thee? etc</i>. (Deut. x. 12); and again: <i>Have I been a wilderness unto Israel, etc.</i> (Jer. ii. 31)[[fn:2230]]? All this, certes, (has been said) in respect of virtuous men. As for the impious, the violent, and the despotic, they consider it as the most hurtful and grievous thing that there should be a judge to hinder their despotism; and likewise, for men of ignoble passions, 'tis the most grievous thing to be prevented from abandoning themselves freely to debauchery and to incur punishment for having indulged therein[[fn:2231]]. And thus it is that every vicious man considers the hindrance of the evil he loves to commit, by reason of his moral corruption, as a heavy burden[[fn:2232]]. It behoves us not, therefore, to measure the facility or difficulty of the law according to the passion of every wicked, vile, and depraved man; but we must consider this law from the point of view of the perfect man whom it seeks to set as a model for all men[[fn:2233]]. This law alone do we call <i>Divine Law;</i> but all that exists outside of it in the way of political regimes, such as the laws of the Greeks and the follies of the Sabians and of other (nations), is the work of political men and not of prophets, as I have several times set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 2..39.0": "It has been right clearly set forth that man is by nature a social being[[fn:2234]], and that his nature (requirs) that he live in society[[fn:2235]]; he is not like the other animals, for whom the gathering into society is not a necessity. On account of the manifold composition of this (human) species, — for, as thou knowest, it is the most composite of all things[[fn:2236]], — there exists, amongst its individuals, such a varied difference that one scarce finds two individuals in harmony under any moral respect whatsoever, no more than one could find two figures perfectly alike[[fn:2237]]. The cause thereof is the difference of temperament, which produces a difference in the (respective) matters and also in the accidents which accompany the form; for every physical form has certain particular accidents which accompany it, besides the accidents which accompany the matter. Such great variation from individual to individual is not met with in any species of animals; on the contrary, the difference between the individuals of each species is but little sensible[[fn:2238]], save in man. Indeed, one may find two individuals who differ so greatly in every moral quality, that one would say they belong to two (different) species, so that thou shalt find (e.g.) such an individual who has cruelty even to the point[[fn:2239]] of slaying his young son in the violence of wrath, whilst another is moved (at the thought) of killing a gnat or a reptile, having a soul too tender for such an act; and it is the same in most accidents[[fn:2240]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..39.1": "Now, whereas the human species, by its nature, admitts of this variation amongst its individuals[[fn:2241]], and whereas social life is necessary to its nature, it is altogether impossible that society should be perfect unless it possess a guide who can regulate the actions of the individuals, remedying that which is defective and moderating that which is in excess[[fn:2242]], and who can prescribe actions and morals that all must continually practice, according to the same rule, so that the natural variation may be concealed by the great conventional harmony and that society may be in good order.",
    "root.text.Part 2..39.2": "Wherefore I say that the law, albeit it be not <i>natural</i>, yet enters, in certain respects, into the category of the natural[[fn:2243]]; for it was of the Divine wisdom, for the preservation of this species whose existence He willed, to implant in its nature (the condition) that its individuals should possess a <i>faculty of governance.</i> Sometimes the individual is he himself unto whom this governance has been inspired, and such is the prophet or the lawgiver; sometimes he is one who has the power to constrain (men) to practice what those two[[fn:2244]] have prescribed, to follow it and to bring it into action, and such are the sovereign who adopts this law (of the lawgiver) and the pretended prophet who adopts the law of the (true) prophet, either in its entirety or in part. If (the latter) adopts a part and abandons another part[[fn:2245]], it is either because it is easier for him, or because, through jealousy, he wishes to make it believed that these things came to him by (Divine) revelation and that he has not borrowed them from another; for there is such a man as delights in a certain perfection, finds it excellent and loves it, and who wishes that men should imagine that he is himself endowed with this perfection, although he well knows that he possesses no perfection whatsoever. Thus thou seest many men who boast of the poem of another and attribute it to themselves; and this is also what has been done for certain works of the learned and for many scientific details, (I mean) that a jealous and slothful person, having lighted (by chance) upon a thing invented by another, pretended to have invented it. This is also what has happened for this prophetic perfection; for we find that certain men, pretending to be prophets, spake things which had never been revealed by God[[fn:2246]], as (did), for example, Zedekiah, son of Chenaanah[[fn:2247]], and we find others who, arrogating to themselves the prophetic office, spake things which, doubtless, God had spoken, I mean that they had been revealed, but to others, as (did), for example, Hananiah, son of Azur[[fn:2248]], — so that they attributed them to themselves and adorned themselves therewith.",
    "root.text.Part 2..39.3": "All this is recognized and distinguished right clearly; but I shall expound it unto thee, that naught obscure may remain for thee, and that thou mayest possess a <i>criterion</i> whereby thou mayest distinguish between the regimes of conventional laws[[fn:2249]], those of the Divine Law, and those emanating from men who have borrowed from the words of the prophets, boasting thereof and attributing the same unto themselves. As for the laws which their authors have expressly declared to be the work of their own reflection[[fn:2250]], thou hast need[[fn:2251]] for them of no argument, the confession of the adversary rendering all proof unnecessary. I desire, therefore, to make known unto thee only the regimes which are proclaimed as prophetic, and which, in part, are truly prophetic, I mean Divine, in part legislative, and in part plagiarisms[[fn:2252]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..39.4": "If, then, thou findest a law of which the sole end and whose author, in calculating the effects thereof, had no other aim than to establish good order in the State and its affairs, and to remove therefrom injustice and violence—without insistence being laid, in any wise, upon speculative matters, and without regard being had[[fn:2253]] to the perfection of the rational faculty, or any concern for opinions, whether they be sound or infirm, the whole purpose being, on the contrary, to regulate in all respects the mutual relations of men and (to ensure) that they obtain a certain presumed felicity[[fn:2254]] according to the opinion of the legislator—(if, I say, thou findest such a law,) thou shalt know that this law is (purely) legislative, and that its author, as we have said, belongs to the third class, I mean those who possess no other perfection than that of the imaginative faculty[[fn:2255]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..39.5": "But, if thou findest a law of which all the provisions tend[[fn:2256]] (not only) to the improvement of corporeal interests, of which mention has just been made, but also to the improvement of faith, endeavouring first of all to spread sound opinions concerning God and concerning the angels, and tending to render man wise, understanding, and attentive, so that he may know the whole of being according to its true condition, then shalt thou know that this governance emanats from God and that this law is divine.",
    "root.text.Part 2..39.6": "But it will yet remain for thee to know whether he who proclaims it be himself the perfect man unto whom it was revealed, or whether it be a person who has boasted of these discourses and falsely attributed them unto himself. — To make trial thereof, one must examine (the extent of) the perfection of that person, watch[[fn:2257]] his actions, and consider his conduct. The most important <i>criterion</i> which thou canst have is the repulsion and contempt (which that person would have) for corporeal pleasures; for therein lies the first step of men of science, and, with even greater reason, of prophets; particularly regarding that of the senses which is a disgrace unto us, as Aristotle says[[fn:2258]], and notably the pollution of cohabitation which derivs therefrom. Wherefore God has confounded[[fn:2259]] by this latter anyone who arrogated (unto himself prophecy), so that the truth might be known to those who sought it and that they might not be led astray and into error. Thou seest an example thereof[[fn:2260]] in Zedekiah, son of Maaseiah, and Ahab, son of Kolaiah, who arrogated prophecy and drew men after them by delivering prophetic discourses revealed to others[[fn:2261]], but who (at the same time) gave themselves up to the ignoble pleasure of lust, even to the committing of adultery with the wives of their friends and followers, until at last God unveiled them, as He had confounded others, so that the king of Babylon caused them to be roasted in the fire, as Jeremiah clearly says: <i>And of them shall be taken up a curse by all the captivity of Judah which are in Babylon, saying, The Lord make thee like Zedekiah and like Ahab, whom the king of Babylon roasted in the fire; because they have committed villany in Israel, and have committed adultery with their neighbours' wives, and have spoken lying words in my name, which I have not commanded them; even I know, and am a witness, says the Lord</i> (Jerem. XXIX, 22 and 23). Understand well the intent of this[[fn:2262]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..40.0": "I need not explain what a <i>dream</i> is. As for the <i>vision</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מראה</span>), — for example, <i>I will make myself known unto him in a vision</i> (Numb. 12, 6), — which is designated by the name of <span dir=\"rtl\">מַרְאֵה הַנְּבוּאָה</span>, <i>prophetic vision</i>[[fn:2263]], which (in Scripture) is also called <span dir=\"rtl\">יַד יְיָ</span>, <i>hand of the Lord</i>[[fn:2264]], and which also bears the name of M<small>A</small>'<small>HAZÉ</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מחזה</span>) <span dir=\"ltr\">[[fn:2265]]</span>, it is a state of agitation and terror which seizes the prophet whilst he is awake, as is set forth concerning Daniel in these words: <i>I saw this great vision, and there remained no strength in me: for my comeliness was turned in me into corruption, and I retained no strength;</i> and he continus: <i>Then was I in a deep sleep on my face, and my face toward the ground</i> (Daniel 10, 8, 9). When subsequently the angel speaks unto him and causs him to stand, this still occurrs within the <i>prophetic vision</i>. In such a state, the senses cease to function; that emanation (of which I have spoken) spreads over the rational faculty, and thence over the imaginative faculty, so that the latter is perfected and functions[[fn:2266]]. Sometimes the revelation beginns with a prophetic vision; then this agitation and strong emotion, following the perfect action of the imagination, go on increasing, and then the (true) revelation arrivs. This is what took place for Abraham; for (in speaking) of that revelation, it beginns by saying: <i>The word of the Lord came unto Abram in a vision</i> (Genesis 15, 1), and at the end (it is said), <i>and, lo, an horror of great darkness fell upon him</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 12); and thereafter: <i>And he said unto Abram, etc.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 13-16).",
    "root.text.Part 2..40.1": "Know thou that those amongst the prophets who recount having had a revelation, sometimes attribute it (expressly) to an angel, and sometimes to God, albeit it undoubtedly took place through the mediation of an angel; the Doctors have pronounced thereupon, saying: \"<i>And the Lord said unto her</i> (<i>ibid.</i> XXV, 23), through the mediation of an angel[[fn:2267]].\" — It must be known that whenever a passage (of Scripture) says of someone that an angel spoke unto him, or that the word of God was addressed unto him, this could not have taken place otherwise than in a dream or in a prophetic vision.",
    "root.text.Part 2..40.2": "The terms employed in the prophetic books exhibit four modes of expression regarding the word addressed to the prophets[[fn:2268]]: The first mode is when the prophet expressly relats[[fn:2269]] that this speech came from an <i>angel</i>, in a <i>dream</i> or in a <i>vision</i>. The second mode is when he merely reports the discourse addressed unto him by the <i>angel</i>, without expressly declaring it to have been in a dream or a vision, being assured that it is a matter generally known that no revelation exists save in one of two ways: <i>In a vision do I make myself known unto him, in a dream do I speak with him</i> (Numbers xii. 6). The third mode is when he mentions not an angel, but ascribs the speech unto <i>God</i>, as if He Himself had addressed it unto him, yet declaring that this word came unto him in a <i>vision</i> or in a <i>dream</i>. The fourth mode is when the prophet simply says that <i>God</i> has spoken to him, or commanded him to act[[fn:2270]], to do such a thing, or to speak in such a manner, without expressly mentioning either angel or dream, relying upon that which is known and established as a principle[[fn:2271]], namely, that no prophecy nor revelation comes otherwise than in a dream or a vision, and through the mediation of an angel.",
    "root.text.Part 2..40.3": "The first mode is employed in the following passages: <i>And the angel of God said unto me in a dream</i> (Genesis xxxi. 11); <i>And God spake unto Israel in the visions of the night</i> (<i>ibid.</i> xlvi. 2); <i>And God came unto Balaam; And God said unto Balaam</i> (Num. xxii. 9 and 12)[[fn:2272]]. — Concerning the second mode, the following examples may be cited: <i>And God said unto Jacob: Arise, go up to Bethel</i> (Genesis xxxv. 1); <i>And God said unto him: thy name is Jacob</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 10); <i>And an angel of the Lord called unto him out of heaven; And the angel of the Lord called unto him a second time, etc.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xxii. 11 and 15); <i>And God said unto Noah</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, vi. 13); <i>And God spake unto Noah</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, viii. 15). — An example of the third mode is found in this passage: <i>The word of the Lord came unto Abram in a vision, etc.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xv. 1). — For the fourth mode, the following examples are found: <i>And the Lord said unto Abram</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xii. 1); <i>The Lord said unto Jacob: Return into the land of thy fathers</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xxxi. 3); <i>And the Lord said unto Joshua</i> (Joshua iii. 7); <i>And the Lord said unto Gideon</i> (Judges vii. 2)[[fn:2273]]. And thus it is that they (the prophets) for the most part express themselves: <i>And the Lord said unto me</i> (Isaiah viii. 1); <i>And the word of the Lord came unto me</i> (Ezekiel xxiv. 1); <i>And the word of the Lord came</i> (II Sam. xxiv. 11; I Kings xviii. 1); <i>And behold, the word of the Lord came to him</i> (I Kings xix. 9)[[fn:2274]]; <i>The word of the Lord came</i> (Ezek. i. 3); <i>The beginning of the word of the Lord by Hosea</i> (Hos. i. 2); <i>The hand of the Lord was upon me</i> (Ezek. xxxvii. 1; xl. 1). There be many examples of this kind.",
    "root.text.Part 2..40.4": "All that is presented according to one of these four modes constitutes prophecy, and he who utters it is a prophet. But when it is said: \"God <i>came</i> unto such a one in a <i>dream</i> of the night,\" it does not there concern prophecy, nor is that person a prophet. For it is intended (merely) that an admonition from God came to that person, and it is subsequently declared unto us that this admonition occurred by means of a dream; for, just as God causes one person to set himself in motion to save another person or to destroy him, so likewise does He bring forth, by means of what is seen in a dream, certain things which it is His will to bring forth. Assuredly, one doubts not that the Aramean Laban was a perfect villain and at the same time an idolater; and, as for Abimelech, albeit amongst his people he was a pious man, our father Abraham said of his city and his kingdom: <i>Surely there is no fear of God in this place</i> (Genesis, 20, 11); and yet, of each of the two, one means to say of Laban and of Abimelech, it is said (that God appeared unto him in a dream): <i>And God came unto Abimelech in a dream of the night</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 3), and likewise (it is said) of Laban: <i>In a dream of the night</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, XXXI, 24)[[fn:2275]]. One must, therefore, impress this upon one's mind and attend to the difference that obtains between the expressions: <i>God came</i> and <i>God said</i>, and between the expressions <i>in a dream of the night</i> and <i>in the visions of the night;</i> for of Jacob it is said: <i>God</i> <small>SAID</small> <i>unto Israel in the visions of the night</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, XLVI, 2), whereas of Laban and Abimelech (it is said): <i>And God</i> <small>CAME</small> <i>etc. in a dream of the night;</i> wherefore[[fn:2276]] Onkelos translates it by <span dir=\"rtl\">ואתא מימר מן קדם י״י</span>, <i>and there came a</i> <small>WORD</small> <i>from before God</i>, and he does not say (speaking) of the latter two: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואתגלי י״י</span>, <i>and God revealed himself</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..40.5": "It is also to be known that it is sometimes said: <i>the Lord said unto such an one</i>, without it being (directly) concerning that person[[fn:2277]], and without his ever having received a revelation, but where the communication was made through the mediation of a prophet. Thus, for example, regarding this passage: <i>And she went to enquire of the Lord</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xxv. 22), it has been expressly said (that she addressed herself) 'to the school of Eber[[fn:2278]]'; it was he who answered her, so that it is of him it was spoken when it is said: <i>And the</i> <small>L</small>ORD<i> </i>said unto her<i> (</i>ibid.<small>, <small>v</small>. 23)[[fn:2279]]. If it be true that it has also been said: '<i>And the</i> <small>L</small>ORD<i> </i>said unto her<small>, by the mediation of a </small>MALAKH<i> (</i>angel<i> or </i>messenger<small>)[[fn:2280]],' this may be interpreted (in the sense) that it is Eber who is the </small>MALAKH<small>; for the prophet also is called </small>MALAKH<i>, as we shall set forth[[fn:2281]]. It may also be that it was intended to indicate the </i>angel[[t:34]] who brought this prophecy unto Eber; or else (it may be) that it was intended to declare thereby that, wherever a discourse is found simply attributed unto God, the mediation of an angel must be admitted, (and that) for all[[fn:2282]] the prophets (in general), as we have set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 2..41.0": "We have already set forth that, wheresoever mention is made of the appearance of an angel, or of a speech made by him, it can only be a question of a <i>prophetic vision</i>, or of a <i>dream</i>[[fn:2283]], whether or not it be expressly declared, as has been said before. This thou must know and be well persuaded thereof. It matters not whether it be said at first of someone that he saw the angel, or whether it seems to be said that he took him at first for a human individual, and that at the end it became manifest unto him that it was an angel[[fn:2284]]; as soon as thou findest in the conclusion that he who was seen and who spake was an angel, thou shalt know and be certain that from the beginning it was a <i>prophetic vision</i>, or a <i>prophetic dream</i>. For, in the prophetic vision or in the prophetic dream, at one time the prophet ses God who speaks unto him, as we shall set forth, at another time he ses an angel who speaks unto him, at another he hears someone speaking unto him without seeing the person who speaks, and finally, at another time, he ses a human individual who addresses him, and afterwards it becoms manifest unto him that he who spake was an angel[[fn:2285]]. In prophecy of this last kind, he relats that he saw a man acting or speaking, and that thereafter he knew it to be an angel.",
    "root.text.Part 2..41.1": "It is this important principle[[fn:2286]] which has been professed by one of the doctors, and verily one of the greatest among them, to wit, R. 'Hayya the Great, concerning that passage of the Pentateuch: <i>And the Lord appeared unto him in the plains of Mamre</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Genesis, xviii. 1); for, after having first stated summarily that God appeared unto him, the text beginns to explain under what form this appearance took place, and says that he saw first three men, that he ran (to meet them), that they spake and that it was spoken unto them[[fn:2287]]. He, therefore, who offers this interpretation, says that these words of Abraham: <i>My Lord, if now I have found favour in thy sight, pass not away, I pray thee, from thy servant</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <small>v</small>. 3) are likewise a relation of that which, in the prophetic vision, he spake unto one of them: 'It was,' says he, 'unto the greatest among them that he addressed his speech[[fn:2288]].' Thou must also well penetrate this subject, for it contains a profound mystery[[fn:2289]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..41.2": "I say likewise that, in the history of Jacob, when it is said: <i>And there wrestled a man with him</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, XXXII, 25), it treats of a prophetic revelation, since it is clearly stated at the end (verses 29 et seq.) that it was an angel. It is exactly as with the history of Abraham, in which it is first related summarily that <i>God appeared unto him, etc.</i>, and thereafter the text beginns to explain how this came to pass. Likewise, concerning Jacob, it is said (first): <i>The angels of God met him</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 2); then it beginns to set forth what happened until the moment when <i>they met him</i>, and it is said that he sent messengers (unto Esau), and that after having acted and done (such and such a thing), <i>Jacob was left alone, etc.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 25); for here it treats of those same angels of God of whom it was said at first <i>the angels of God met him</i>[[fn:2290]], and this struggle, as well as the whole dialogue (which follows), took place in a <i>prophetic vision</i>. Likewise, all that happened with Balaam <i>in the way</i> (Numbers, 22, 22 et seq.), as well as the speaking of the ass, (all this, I say,) took place in a prophetic vision[[fn:2291]], since it is expressly said at the end (verse 32) that the <i>angel of the Lord</i> spake unto him[[fn:2292]]. In like manner also, concerning these words (of the book) of Joshua (5, 13), <i>he lifted up his eyes and looked, and, behold, there stood a</i> <small>MAN</small> <i>over against him</i>, I say that this took place in the prophetic vision, since it is clearly said thereafter (<small>V</small>. 14 and 15) that it was <i>a prince of the host of the Lord</i>. — [As for this passage <i>And a</i> <small>MALAKH</small> (messenger) <i>of the Lord came up from Gilgal, etc. And when the</i> <small>MALAKH</small> <i>of the Lord spake these words unto all Israel</i> (Judges, 2, 1 and 4), the doctors have written that <i>the</i> <small>MALAKH</small> <i>of the Lord</i> spoken of here is Phinehas; they express themselves thus: 'it is Phinehas, who, at the moment when the Divine Majesty rested upon him, resembled a <i>malakh</i> (angel) of the Lord[[fn:2293]].']",
    "root.text.Part 2..41.3": "[We have already set forth that the name <i>malakh</i> is homonymous, and that the prophet also is called <i>malakh</i>[[fn:2294]], as, for example, in the following passages: <i>He sent a</i> <small>MALAKH</small> <i>and has brought us forth out of Egypt</i> (Numb., 20, 16); <i>And Haggai, the Lord's</i> <small>MALAKH</small>, <i>spake in the Lord's message</i> (Hag., I, 13); <i>And they mocked the</i> <small>MALAKHIM</small> (<i>messengers</i>) <i>of God</i> (II Chron., XXXVI, 16).] — When Daniel says: <i>And Gabriel, the man whom I had seen at the first in a vision, came to me in a swift flight, about the time of the evening oblation</i> (Dan., IX, 21), all this also took place in a prophetic vision; and it must by no means occur to thy mind that an angel could be seen, or the words of an angel heard, otherwise than in a <i>prophetic vision</i> or in a <i>prophetic dream</i>, as it has been laid down as a principle (in this passage): <i>I will make myself known unto him in a vision, and will speak unto him in a dream</i> (Numb., 12, 6). From that which I have cited, thou shalt derive proof for other passages which I have not mentioned.",
    "root.text.Part 2..41.4": "By that which we have previously said concerning the necessity of a preparation for prophecy[[fn:2295]], and by that which we have said of the homonymy of the name <i>malakh</i>, thou shalt know that Hagar the Egyptian was not a prophetess, and that Manoah and his wife were likewise not prophets[[fn:2296]]; for the word which they heard, or which struck their mind, was something similar to that <i>sound of a voice</i> of which the doctors constantly speak, and which designats a certain situation in which an unprepared person may find himself[[fn:2297]]. That which has given rise to error hereupon is solely the homonymy[[fn:2298]]; but it is there (precisely) the principle which removs[[fn:2299]] most of the difficulties of the Pentateuch.",
    "root.text.Part 2..41.5": "— Observe well that the expression: <i>And the angel found her</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וימצאה</span>) <i>near the source of water etc.</i> (Genesis, 16, 7) resembles that employed in the case of Joseph: <i>And a man found him</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וימצאהו</span>) <i>wandering in the field</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, XXXVII, 15), where all the <i>Midrashic</i> commentaries say that it was an <i>angel</i>[[fn:2300]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..42.0": "We have already set forth, in our works, that the prophets sometimes present their prophecies in the form of parables[[fn:2301]]; this is because at times the prophet beholds a thing by way of a parable, and thereafter the meaning of the parable is explained unto him in that same prophetic vision. It is as[[fn:2302]] unto a man who has a dream, and who, in that very dream, imagins that he has awakened, that he has related the dream to another, and that the latter has explained its meaning to him, whilst the whole was naught but a dream. This is what is termed: 'a dream which has been interpreted in a dream[[fn:2303]],' whereas there be also dreams whose meaning is known only after one has awakened. In like manner, the sense of the prophetic parables is sometimes explained within the prophetic vision, as is manifest regarding Zechariah, who says, after having first presented certain parables: <i>And the angel that talked with me came again, and waked me, as a man that is wakened out of his sleep; and said unto me, What seest thou? etc.</i> (Zech. IV, 1 and 2); then he (the angel) explains to him the (new) parable[[fn:2304]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..42.1": "And the same is likewise manifest in the case of Daniel; for it is first said: <i>Daniel saw a dream, and upon his couch</i> (<i>he had</i>) <i>visions of his head</i> (Dan. VII, 1); and after having related all the parables and having expressed how much he was grieved at not knowing the explanation thereof, he finally inquirs of the angel, and the latter makes known unto him the explanation in that same vision: <i>I came near</i>, says he, <i>unto one of them that stood by, and asked him the truth of all this. So he told me, and made me know the interpretation of the things</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <small>V</small>. 16). After having said that he <i>saw a dream</i>, he calls the whole event ’<small>HAZÔN</small> (a vision), because, as he says, it was an angel who explained it to him in a prophetic dream. He says, therefore, after that: <i>A vision</i> (’<small>HAZÔN</small>) <i>appeared unto me, even unto me Daniel, after that which appeared unto me at the first</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, VIII, 1). This is clear[[fn:2305]]; for ’<small>HAZÔN</small> is derived from the verb ’<small>HAZA</small>, even as <small>MAREA</small> (vision) is derived from <small>RAA</small>, and both verbs have the same meaning (that of <i>seeing</i>), so that there is no difference between the words <small>MAREA</small>, <small>MA</small>’<small>HAZÉ</small> and ’<small>HAZÔN</small>. There is no third way (of prophecy) besides those two ways of which the Pentateuch speaks: <i>I will make myself known unto him in a</i> <small>VISION</small>, <i>and will speak unto him in a</i> <small>DREAM</small> (Numbers, 12, 6)[[fn:2306]]; but there be in this certain gradations, as shall be set forth[[fn:2307]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..42.2": "— Nevertheless, among the prophetic parables, there be also many whose meaning is in no wise explained in the prophetic vision, but whose intent the prophet knows after having awakened, as is the case, for instance, with the <i>staves</i> which Zechariah took in a prophetic vision[[fn:2308]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..42.3": "It must be understood that, even as the prophets behold things which have a parabolical sense, — as, for example, the <i>lamps</i> of Zechariah (Zech. 4, 2), the <i>horses</i> and the <i>mountains</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, VI, 1-7), the <i>roll</i> of Ezekiel (II, 9), the <i>wall</i> made by a <i>plumb-line</i> which Amos beheld[[fn:2309]] (7, 7), the <i>beasts</i> which Daniel beheld (ch. VII and VIII), the <i>seething pot</i> which Jeremiah beheld (1, 13), and other similar parables, whereby it is intended to represent certain ideas, — even so they also behold things whereby it is intended to indicate what the <i>name</i> of the thing seen suggests by its etymology or by its homonymy, in such wise that the action of the imaginative faculty consists, as it were, in causing a thing to be seen bearing a homonymous name, by one of the meanings of which one is guided toward another, which also is one of the species of allegory[[fn:2310]]. When, for instance, Jeremiah says: (that he saw) <small>MAKKEL SCHAKED</small>, <i>a rod of an almond tree</i>, his intention is to deduce something from the homonymy of the word <small>SCHAKED</small>[[fn:2311]], and he says thereafter: <i>For I am</i> <small>SCHÔKED</small>, <i>watchful</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Jer. I, 11 and 12); for it concerns neither the idea of the <i>rod</i>, nor that of the <i>almond tree</i>. Likewise, when Amos beholds K<small>ELOUB KAÏÇ</small>, <i>a basket of summer fruit</i>, it is to deduce therefrom the fulfillment of the time; and he says: For <i>the</i> <small>KÈÇ</small> (or <i>the end</i>) <i>is come</i> (Amos, 8, 2).",
    "root.text.Part 2..42.4": "— That which is yet more astonishing occurs when attention is awakened by means of a certain name, the letters of which are also those of another name, albeit in an inverted order, [[fn:2312]] though there exist between these two names no etymological connection, nor any commonality of sense, as is found (for example) in the parables of Zechariah, when, in a prophetic vision, he takes the two <i>staves</i>[[fn:2313]] for pasturing the flock, and designates the one by the name of N<small>O</small>’<small>AM</small> (grace, favour), and the other, by that of ’H<small>ÔBELÎM</small> (destroyers). [[fn:2314]] In this parable, the aim is (to indicate): that the nation, in its primitive state, enjoyed the <i>favour of the Eternal</i> who guided and directed it; that it was joyful in obeying God and derived pleasure therefrom, [[fn:2315]] and that God was propitious unto it and loved it — [as it is said: <i>Thou hast this day magnified the Lord, etc., and the Lord has magnified thee, etc.</i> (Deut. XXVI, 17 and 18)[[fn:2316]]] — when it was guided and directed by Moses and by the Prophets who succeeded him; but that subsequently it so altered its disposition as to conceive an aversion for obedience to God, with the result that God conceived an aversion for it, and made of its leaders <i>destroyers</i>, such as Jeroboam and Manasseh. This is (the sense) according to etymology; for ’<small>HOBELÎM</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חובלים</span>) is from (the same root as) <small>ME</small>’<small>HABBELÎM</small>, <span dir=\"rtl\">מחבלים כרמים</span> (<i>which</i> <small>DESTROY</small> <i>the vineyards</i>, Song of Sol., II, 15). Subsequently, he deduces likewise — that is to say, from the name of ’<small>HOBELÎM</small> — that they held the Law in <i>aversion</i> and that God held them in aversion. But this sense can only be derived from ’<small>HOBELÎM</small> by means of the transposition of the letters ’H, B, L; he therefore states, concerning the idea of <i>aversion</i> and <i>abomination</i> encompassed within this parable: <i>My soul loathed them,</i>[[fn:2317]] <i>and their soul also abhorred</i> (<small>BA</small>’<small>HALA</small>) <i>me</i> (Zech., XI, 8), and he transposes the letters ’H B L (<span dir=\"rtl\">חבל</span>) to form B ’H L (<span dir=\"rtl\">בחל</span>).",
    "root.text.Part 2..42.5": "According to this method[[fn:2318]], extraordinary things, which are likewise mysteries, are found in the words <small>NE</small>-’<small>HOSCHES</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נחשת</span>), <small>KALAL</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">קלל</span>), <small>RÉGHEL</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רגל</span>), <small>’ÉGHEL</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עגל</span>), and ’<small>HASCHMAL</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חשמל</span>) employed in the <i>Mercabâ</i>[[fn:2319]]. In various passages, there are found other words which, after this observation, shall become clear unto thee by the context of the discourse, if thou examinest them well in every passage[[fn:2320]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..43.0": "Prophecy takes not place save by means of a <i>vision</i> or a <i>dream</i>, as we have set forth several times, so that we need no longer repeat it. We shall now say that, when the prophet is inspired, he sometimes ses a parable, as we have frequently explained. At times he believs he ses[[fn:2321]] God speaking unto him in a prophetic vision, as Isaiah said (6, 8): <i>And I heard the voice of the Lord saying: Whom shall I send, and who will go for us?</i> At other times he hears an angel speaking unto him whom he ses, which is very frequent, as in these passages: <i>And the angel of God said unto me etc.</i> (Genesis, 31, 11)[[fn:2322]]; <i>And he said unto me: Knowest thou not what these things mean? And the angel that talked with me answered etc.</i> (Zech., IV, 5)[[fn:2323]]; <i>Then I heard one saint speaking</i> (Daniel, 8, 13); this is too frequent for one to enumerate all the examples. At other times the prophet ses a human personage who speaks unto him, as it is said in Ezekiel (XL, 3 and 4): <i>And, behold, there was a man, whose appearance was like the appearance of brass, etc.; and the man said unto me, Son of man, etc.</i>, after it had been said at first (v. 1): <i>The hand of the Lord was upon me</i>[[fn:2324]]. Finally, at other times, the prophet, in the prophetic vision, ses no figure, but hears only words addressed unto him, as Daniel said: <i>And I heard a man's voice between the banks of</i> (<i>the river</i>) <i>Ulai</i> (Dan., VIII, 16), and as Eliphaz said: <i>There was silence</i>, <i>and I heard a voice</i> (Job, 4, 16), and as Ezekiel also said: <i>And I heard him that spake unto me</i> (Ezek., 2, 2); for that which spake unto him was not that thing which he had perceived in the prophetic vision; but, after having related in detail that wondrous and extraordinary thing which he declars to have perceived, he beginns to set forth the subject and the form of the revelation, and he says: <i>I heard one that spake unto me</i>[[fn:2325]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..43.1": "Having first spoken of this division of prophetic revelations, which is justified by the texts, I shall say that those words which the prophet hears in the prophetic vision, his imagination sometimes presents them unto him as being exceeding loud, as when a man dreams that he hears a great thunder, or that he ses an earthquake or a fire from heaven[[fn:2326]]; for often one has such dreams.",
    "root.text.Part 2..43.2": "At other times, the words which he hears in the prophetic vision are similar to habitual and familiar language, so that he finds therein naught that is strange. This shall become manifest unto thee by the history of the prophet Samuel, who, when God called him in a moment of inspiration, believed that it was the priest Eli who had called him thrice successively; thereafter Scripture explains the cause thereof, and it is said that what produced this effect upon him, so that he believed it to be Eli, was that he knew not then that the word of God was addressed unto the prophets in this form, this mystery having not yet been revealed unto him. It is therefore said, to give the reason thereof[[fn:2327]]: <i>Now Samuel did not yet know the Lord, neither was the word of the Lord yet revealed unto him</i> (I Sam., 3, 7), which means that he knew not yet, and it had not yet been revealed unto him, that it was thus that the <i>word of the Lord</i> was manifested. When it is said[[fn:2328]] that he <i>did not yet know the Lord</i>, it signifies that he had previously had no prophetic inspiration; for it has been said of him who prophesies: <i>I will make myself known unto him in a vision</i> (Num., 12, 6). The translation of the verse, if regard be had only to the sense, would therefore be this: 'Samuel had not prophesied before, and he knew not even[[fn:2329]] that such was the form of prophetic inspiration.' Thou must needs impress this upon thy mind.",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.0": "Having previously set forth the true sense of prophecy, according to what is required by speculation combined with what has been explained in our Law, I must needs enumerate for thee the <i>degrees</i>[[fn:2330]] <i>of prophecy</i> according to these two bases[[fn:2331]]. If I call them <i>degrees of prophecy,</i> it is not that he who occupies any degree whatever is a prophet; for, on the contrary, the first and the second degrees are but steps whereby to arrive at prophecy, and he who has reached one of these steps is not numbered among the prophets of whom we have spoken heretofore. If at times he be called a prophet, it is only by a species of generalization, because he is very nigh unto the prophets.",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.1": "Thou must not be led into error concerning these <i>degrees</i>, if thou findest in the prophetic books that a prophet[[fn:2332]] was inspired under the form of one of these degrees, and that it is afterwards declared of this same prophet that he had a revelation under the form of another degree; for it may happen that such a prophet, after having had a revelation under the form of one of the degrees which I shall enumerate[[fn:2333]], may thereafter, at another moment, have another revelation, at a degree inferior to that of the first revelation[[fn:2334]]. Indeed, just as the prophet does not prophesy throughout his whole life, without intermission, and as, on the contrary, after having prophesied at one moment, the prophetic inspiration forsaks him at other moments, even so may it be[[fn:2335]] that he prophesies at a certain moment under the form of a superior degree, and that afterwards, at another moment, he prophesies under the form of a degree inferior to the first. It may therefore be that he attains this superior degree but once in his life, and that thereafter he be deprived thereof (forever)[[fn:2336]], as it may also be that he retains an inferior degree (of inspiration), until the moment when his prophetic inspiration ceass entirely; for the prophetic inspiration necessarily forsaks all prophets[[fn:2337]] more or less time before their death, as has been declared concerning Jeremiah, saying: <i>When the word of the Lord had ceased</i> (<i>to go forth</i>) <i>from the mouth of Jeremiah</i> (Ezra, i. 1)[[fn:2338]], and as has been declared concerning David, saying: <i>Now these be the last words of David</i> (II Sam., xxiii. 1). And thenceforth one may conclude for all (others).",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.2": "After having made the preceding preliminary observation[[fn:2339]], I begin to enumerate the <i>degrees</i> in question[[fn:2340]], and I say:",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.3.sub_0": "I. F<small>IRST DEGREE</small>: The first step toward prophecy is when a divine assistance accompanies the individual, which setts him in motion and encourags him unto a virtuous action, great and of high importance[[fn:2341]], as, for example, to deliver a company of virtuous men from a company of wicked men, or to save a great and virtuous man, or to diffuse good upon a multitude of folk, so that (this individual) finds within himself something which impells and invits him to act. This is what is called <i>the spirit of the Lord,</i> and it is said of the individual who finds himself in this state: that <i>the spirit of the Lord entered into him</i>[[fn:2342]], or that <i>the spirit of the Lord clothed him</i>[[fn:2343]], or that <i>the spirit of the Lord rests upon him</i>[[fn:2344]], or <i>the Lord is with him</i>[[fn:2345]], and other like expressions[[fn:2346]]. Such was the degree of all the judges of Israel, of whom it has been said in general: <i>And when the Lord raised up judges for them, then the Lord was with the judge, and delivered them etc.</i> (Judges, ii. 18); and such also was the degree of all the virtuous kings[[fn:2347]] of Israel. This has been declared particularly regarding several judges and kings, e.g.: <i>Then the spirit of the Lord came upon Jephthah</i> (<i>ibid.,</i> xi. 29); of Samson it is said: <i>The spirit of the Lord came mightily upon him</i> (<i>ibid.,</i> xiv. 19); it has been further said: <i>And the spirit of God came upon Saul when he heard those tidings</i> (I Sam., xi. 6); likewise, finally, it was said of Amasa, moved by the holy spirit to go to the aid of David: <i>Then the spirit came upon Amasai, who was chief of the captains; thine are we</i> (said he), <i>O David! and on thy side, thou son of Jesse: peace! etc.</i> (I Chron., xii. 18)[[fn:2348]].—Know thou that this kind of power remained inseparable from Moses, from the moment he had reached man's estate[[fn:2349]]; wherefore he felt himself moved to slay the Egyptian and to rebuff him of the two quarrellers who was in the wrong[[fn:2350]]. This power was so violent within him that[[fn:2351]], even after having been afraid and having taken to flight, when, having arrived[[fn:2352]] at Midian a stranger and fearful, he saw some injustice, he could not prevail upon himself not to make it cease and had not the strength to endure it, as it is said: <i>But Moses stood up and helped them</i> (Exodus, ii. 17).",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.3.sub_1": "In like manner, a similar power had attached itself to David since he had been <i>anointed with the oil of unction,</i> as the Scripture says: <i>And the spirit of the Lord came upon David from that day forward</i> (I Sam., xvi. 13); wherefore he courageously encountered the lion, the bear, and the Philistine. But such a <i>spirit of the Lord</i> never inspired, in any of those, any (prophetic) word whatsoever; this power, on the contrary, leads only to impelling the strengthened man to a certain action, not unto just any action, but to succouring an oppressed one, whether it be a great individual or a society, or at least (it impells) unto that which leads thereto. For, just as all those who dream a <i>true dream</i> are not for that reason prophets, even so could one not say of each of those whom a (divine) help assists for any thing whatsoever, as, for example, to acquire a fortune, or to attain a personal end, that <i>the spirit of the Lord</i> accompanies him, or that <i>the Lord is with him,</i> and that it is by the <i>holy spirit</i> that he has done what he has done. We say this, on the contrary, only of him who has accomplished a noble work, of high importance[[fn:2353]], or at least (who has done) that which may lead thereto, as, for example, the <i>prosperous action</i>[[fn:2354]] of Joseph in the house of the Egyptian, which was the first cause of the important events which followed thereafter, as is evident.",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.4.sub_0": "II. The <small>SECOND DEGREE</small> is when it seems to an individual that something has entered into him and that there has come upon him a new power which makes him speak, so that he utters maxims of wisdom, or a song of praise (unto God)[[fn:2355]], or salutary warnings, or discourses relative to political government or metaphysics, and all this in a state of waking, whilst the senses function according to their wont. It is of such a man that it is said that he <i>speaks by the holy spirit.</i> It was by this kind of <i>holy spirit</i> that David composed the Psalms and that Solomon composed the Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, and the Song of Songs. Likewise, it was by this kind of <i>holy spirit</i> that Daniel, Job, the Chronicles, and the other Hagiographa were composed; wherefore they are called <small>KETHOUBÎM</small> (Hagiographa), meaning that they are <i>written by means of the holy spirit.</i> It has been expressly said: “The book of Esther was dictated by the holy spirit[[fn:2356]].” It is in speaking of this kind of holy spirit that David said: <i>The spirit of the Lord spake by me</i>, <i>and his word was in my tongue</i> (II Sam., xxiii. 2), which is to say that it was He who made him utter these words. Of this class were also the <i>seventy elders</i>, of whom it is said: <i>And it came to pass, that, when the spirit rested upon them</i>, <i>they prophesied</i>, <i>and did not cease</i> (Numbers, xi. 25); and likewise Eldad and Medad (<i>Ibid</i>., v. 26). In like manner, every high priest, when questioned by means of the <i>Urim</i> and <i>Thummim</i>[[fn:2357]], belongs to this class, that is to say—as the doctors express it—: “the divine majesty rests upon him, and he speaks by the holy spirit[[fn:2358]].” To this class belongs also Jahaziel, son of Zechariah, of whom it is said in the Chronicles: <i>The spirit of the Lord came upon him</i>, <i>in the midst of the congregation. And he said, Hearken ye</i>, <i>all</i> (<i>ye men</i>) <i>of Judah, and ye inhabitants of Jerusalem, and thou king Jehoshaphat! Thus says the Lord unto you, etc.</i> (II Chron., xx. 14 and 15).",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.4.sub_1": "In like manner, Zechariah, the son of Jehoiada the priest, appertains unto this class; for it is said of him: <i>And the Spirit of God clothed Zechariah, the son of Jehoiada the priest, and he stood above the people, and said unto them: Thus says God, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.,</i> 24:20). Likewise, Azariah, the son of Oded, of whom it is said: “<i>The Spirit of God was upon Azariah, the son of Oded; and he went out to meet Asa, etc.</i>” (<i>Ibid.,</i> 15:1-2). The same holds true of all those concerning whom the like expression is used. It must be understood that Balaam also, whilst he was yet pious, belonged to this category. This is what was intended by the words: <i>And the Lord put a word in Balaam’s mouth</i> (Numbers 23:5), for it is as though it were said <i>that he spake by the Spirit of the Lord;</i> and it is in this sense that he says of himself: <i>he who hears the words of God</i> (<i>Ibid.,</i> 24:4).",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.5.sub_0": "— We must further observe that David, Solomon, and Daniel are likewise of this class, and that they belong not to the class of Isaiah, Jeremiah, Nathan the prophet, Ahijah the Shilonite[[fn:2359]], and their peers; for these—I mean David, Solomon, and Daniel—did not speak or utter all that they said save by the Holy Spirit[[fn:2360]]. As touching those words of David: <i>the God of Israel said, the Rock of Israel spake to me</i> (II Sam. 23:3), they must be understood in the sense that He had made promises unto him through a prophet, whether it were Nathan or another; it is even as this passage: <i>And the Lord said unto her</i> (Genesis 25:23[[fn:2361]]), and as this other: <i>And the Lord said unto Solomon: Forasmuch as this is done of thee, and thou hast not kept my covenant</i> (I Kings 11:11), which undoubtedly is a threat addressed unto him through the mediation of Ahijah the Shilonite, or of another prophet. Likewise, when it is said regarding Solomon: <i>In Gibeon the Lord appeared to Solomon in a dream by night</i>, <i>and God said, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.,</i> 3:5), this concerns not a true <i>prophecy.</i> It is not at all like this passage: <i>The word of the Lord came unto Abram in a vision</i> (Genesis 15:1), nor like this other: <i>And God spake unto Israel in the visions of the night</i> (<i>Ibid.,</i> 46:2), nor like the prophecies of Isaiah and Jeremiah; for, although it was by a dream that each of these[[fn:2362]] received his revelation, the revelation itself made known unto him that it was a prophecy and that a revelation (from God) had come unto him. But, in this narrative concerning Solomon, it is (expressly) said at the end: <i>And Solomon awoke</i>, <i>and, behold, it was a dream</i> (I Kings 3:15); and likewise, it is said in the second narrative: <i>The Lord appeared to Solomon the second time</i>, <i>as he had appeared unto him at Gibeon</i> (<i>Ibid.,</i> 9:2), where it had been expressly stated that it was a dream. This is a degree inferior to that of which it is said: <i>I will speak unto him in a dream</i> (Numbers 12:6); for those who possess a (true) prophetic inspiration in a dream do by no means call <i>a dream</i> that in which the prophecy came unto them, but declare positively that it was a (divine) <i>revelation.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.5.sub_1": "It is in this sense that our father Jacob expressed himself; for, having awakened from his prophetic dream, he said not that it was a dream, but declared positively: <i>Surely the Lord is in this place, etc.</i> (Genesis 28:16), and (further on) he says: <i>God Almighty appeared unto me at Luz</i>, <i>in the land of Canaan</i> (<i>Ibid.,</i> 48:3), thus declaring that it was a <i>revelation</i>, whereas concerning Solomon, it is said: <i>And Solomon awoke; and, behold, it was a dream.</i> And likewise, thou seest Daniel declaring simply[[fn:2363]] that he had <i>dreams;</i> and, albeit he had there seen an angel whose words he had heard, he called them <i>dreams</i>, even after having derived from them the (sublime) instructions he had received: <i>Then,</i> says he, <i>was the secret revealed unto Daniel in a vision of the night</i> (Dan. 2:19). Further on he says: <i>Then he wrote the dream, etc. I saw in my vision by night, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.,</i> 7:1-2); <i>And the visions of my head troubled me</i> (<i>Ibid.,</i> v. 15); and he says again: <i>I was astonished at the vision</i>, <i>which none understood</i> (<i>Ibid.,</i> 8:27). This is undoubtedly a degree inferior to the degrees of those concerning whom it is said: <i>I will speak unto him in a dream</i> (Numbers 12:6). Wherefore it has been agreed[[fn:2364]], in our communion, to range the book of Daniel among the Hagiographa, and not among the Prophets. I was therefore bound to observe unto thee that, although in this kind of prophetic vision which Daniel and Solomon had, they saw[[fn:2365]] an angel in a dream, they nevertheless did not themselves take it for a true <i>prophecy,</i> but for a <i>dream</i> which was to make known the truth of certain things, which falls within the category of those who speak by the <i>Holy Spirit,</i> and forms the <i>second degree.</i> Thus it is that, in the classification of the Hagiographa, no distinction is made between Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, Daniel, the Psalms, and the books of Ruth and Esther, all of which were written by means of the Holy Spirit and (the authors of which) are all called <i>prophets</i> by a general term.",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.6": "III. The <small>THIRD DEGREE</small> [which is the first degree of those who say: <i>The word of the Eternal was addressed unto me,</i> or who employ other expressions having a similar sense], it is when the prophet beholds a <i>parable</i> in a <i>dream</i>, with all the conditions previously posited for true prophecy, and that in the very same <i>prophetic dream</i> it is explained to him the sense which was intended to be indicated by the parable, as occurs in the majority of the parables of Zachariah[[fn:2366]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.7": "IV. The <small>FOURTH DEGREE</small>, that is to say, when he hears, in a <i>prophetic dream</i>, clear and distinct words, without seeing the one who pronounces them; which occurred to Samuel when he received his first revelation, as we have explained in the treatise concerning him[[fn:2367]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.8": "V. The <small>FIFTH DEGREE</small> is when a <i>personage</i> speaks unto him in a <i>dream</i>, as it is said in one of the prophecies of Ezekiel: <i>And the man said unto me, Son of man, etc.</i> (Ezek. xl. 4)[[fn:2368]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.9": "VI. The <small>SIXTH DEGREE</small> is when an <i>angel</i> speaks unto him in a <i>dream;</i> and such is the condition of the greater part of the prophets, as it is said: <i>And the angel of God spake unto me in a dream</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Gen. xxxi. 11)[[fn:2369]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.10": "VII. The <small>SEVENTH DEGREE</small> is when it seems unto him, in a <i>prophetic dream</i>, that <i>God</i> speaks unto him[[fn:2370]], as when Isaiah says: <i>I saw the Lord, etc., and He said, Whom shall I send? etc.</i> (Isa. vi. 1 and 8)[[fn:2371]], and as when Micaiah, the son of Imlah, said: <i>I saw the Lord, etc.</i> (1 Kings xxii. 19; 2 Chron. xviii. 18).",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.11": "VIII. The <small>EIGHTH DEGREE</small>, it is when he has a revelation in a <i>prophetic vision</i> and when he sees <i>parables</i>, as, for example, Abraham in the <i>vision between the pieces</i> (of the slaughtered animals)[[fn:2372]]; for these parabolic visions took place during the day[[fn:2373]], as it has been declared[[fn:2374]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.12": "IX. The <small>NINTH DEGREE</small>, it is when he hears <i>words</i> in a <i>vision</i>, as has been said concerning Abraham: <i>And lo, the word of the Eternal came unto him, saying: This one shall not inherit thee</i> (Genesis, 15, 4).",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.13": "X. The <small>TENTH DEGREE</small> is when, in a prophetic vision, he ses a <i>personage</i> who speaks unto him, as, for instance, Abraham at the Oaks of Mamre, and Joshua at Jericho[[fn:2375]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.14": "XI. The <small>ELEVENTH DEGREE</small> is when, in a <i>vision</i>, he ses an <i>angel</i> who speaks unto him, as, for instance, Abraham at the time of the sacrifice of Isaac. This is, in my opinion, the highest degree reached by the prophets proclaimed in the (Holy) Books[[fn:2376]], provided it be assumed as a fact, as has been established, that the perfection of the rational qualities of the individual is obtained through speculation, and that Moses our Master be excepted from the common rule[[fn:2377]]. As to (the question of) whether it be possible that, in a <i>prophetic vision</i> also, the prophet believs himself to hear the speech of <i>God</i>[[fn:2378]], (I confess that) according to my view, this is somewhat improbable, and the active power of the imaginative faculty[[fn:2379]] extends not so far; for verily, we have not encountered such a state of affairs amongst the ordinary prophets[[fn:2380]]. Therefore it has been expressly said in the Pentateuch: <i>I make</i> <small>MYSELF KNOWN</small> <i>unto him in a vision</i>, <i>I</i> <small>SPEAK</small> <i>unto him in a dream</i> (Numbers xii. 6); thus has speech been placed in the <i>dream</i> only, whilst in the <i>vision</i> has been placed the union[[fn:2381]] and the emanation of the (Active) Intellect, which has been expressed by <span dir=\"rtl\">אתודע</span>, <i>I make myself known</i>, which is a reflexive verb[[fn:2382]] (derived) from <span dir=\"rtl\">ידע</span>, <i>to know</i>[[fn:2383]]. But it has not been clearly stated that, in a <i>vision</i>, one may hear[[fn:2384]] words coming from God.",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.15": "Having encountered texts which bear witness to words that the prophet might have heard, and in which (nevertheless) it is clear that the matter concerns a <i>vision</i>, I submitted, by mere conjecture[[fn:2385]], that perhaps, where it is stated that words can be heard (only) in a dream and that it cannot be so in a <i>vision</i>, this implies (the hypothesis) that the prophet imagines God Himself addressing him[[fn:2386]]; and all this I stated by adhering to the literal sense[[fn:2387]]. Nevertheless, it might be argued that every <i>vision</i> in which mention is made of <i>words</i> heard was indeed, in the first instance, a <i>vision,</i> but that it subsequently culminated in a profound slumber and became a dream, as we have expounded concerning these words: <i>And a deep slumber fell upon Abram</i> (Genesis, 15, 12)[[fn:2388]], of which they (the doctors) have said: “It is the slumber of prophecy[[fn:2389]].” Thus, whenever words were heard, in whatsoever manner, it was in a <i>dream</i>, as the text states: <i>I speak unto him in a dream</i> (Numbers, 12, 6), whereas in the <i>prophetic vision</i>, only <i>parables</i> were perceived, or communications from the intellect[[fn:2390]] making known scientific matters similar to those obtained by means of speculation, as we have set forth, and this is what was intended to be conveyed (by the words) <i>I make myself known unto him in a vision</i> (<i>ibid.</i>). According to this latter interpretation, therefore, the degrees of prophecy would number <i>eight</i>[[fn:2391]], the most elevated and perfect of which would generally be that in which the prophet is inspired <i>by a vision</i>, even though it were but a <i>personage</i> speaking unto him[[fn:2392]], as has been stated.",
    "root.text.Part 2..44.16": "Thou wilt perhaps raise an objection against me, saying: “Thou hast counted, amongst the degrees of prophecy, that in which the prophet would hear words addressed unto him (directly) by God, as, for instance, Isaiah and Micaiah. But how can this be, since we hold as a principle that every prophet hears the word (of God) only through the mediation of an angel, except Moses our Master, of whom it has been said: <i>With him will I speak mouth to mouth</i> (Numbers xii. 8)?” Know then that it is indeed so, and that that which serves here as a mediator is the imaginative faculty; for it is only in the <i>prophetic dream</i> that the prophet hears God speaking unto him[[fn:2393]], whilst Moses our Master heard Him <i>from above the mercy-seat, from between the two cherubim</i> (Exodus xxv. 22)[[fn:2394]], without employing the imaginative faculty. We have already set forth in the <i>Mishneh Torah</i> the differences of this prophecy (of Moses), and we have explained the meaning of the words <i>mouth to mouth</i> (Numbers xii. 8), of the words <i>as a man speaks unto his friend</i> (Exodus xxxiii. 11), and of other expressions[[fn:2395]]. It is thence, therefore, that thou mayest draw thy understanding, and it is not necessary to repeat what has already been said.",
    "root.text.Part 2..45.0": "From a single individual one may conclude regarding all individuals of the same species, and learn that each of its individuals has such a form[[fn:2396]]. In saying this, my purpose is (to establish) that, from the form of one of the relations of the prophets[[fn:2397]], thou mayest conclude regarding all other relations of the same species.",
    "root.text.Part 2..45.1": "Following this preliminary observation, it must be known that, even as a man may seem to see, in a dream, that he has performed a journey into such a country, that he has married there, and that after remaining there a certain space of time, there was born unto him a son whom he named so-and-so, and who was in such a state and in such a circumstance[[fn:2398]], even so those prophetic parables which appear (to the prophet), or which, in the prophetic vision, are translated into action[[fn:2399]] [when the parable requirs[[fn:2400]] any act whatsoever], as well as certain things which the prophet executs, the intervals of time mentioned parabolically between the different actions and the translations from one place to another—all this (I say) exists only in the prophetic vision, and these are not real actions (existing) for the outward senses[[fn:2401]]. Some of these (matters) are related, in the prophetic books, in an absolute manner[[fn:2402]]; for, as soon as it is known that the whole was a prophetic vision, it were superfluous to reiterate at every detail of the parable that it had occurred in the prophetic vision.",
    "root.text.Part 2..45.2": "Thus, when the prophet has said: <i>And the Lord said unto me</i>, he has no need to declare that it was in a dream[[fn:2403]]; but the vulgar imagine that these actions, these removals, these questions and answers—that all this (I say) took place in the state of sense-perception[[fn:2404]], and not in the <i>prophetic vision</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..45.3.sub_0": "I shall now cite unto thee an example concerning which no man can be mistaken, and I shall connect therewith some other examples of the same kind; from these few instances, thou mayest conclude concerning those which I shall not have cited. That which is clear and whereon no man can err, is when Ezekiel says: <i>I sat in mine house, and the elders of Judah sat before me, etc. A spirit lifted me up between the earth and the heaven, and brought me to Jerusalem, in the</i> <small>VISIONS OF</small> G<small>OD</small> (Ezek., 8, 1 and 3). Likewise when he says: <i>I arose and went forth into the plain</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 3, 23), this also was but in the <i>visions of God</i>, even as it is also said regarding Abraham: <i>And he brought him forth abroad</i> (Genesis, 15, 5), which took place <i>in a vision</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, v. 1). Likewise when he says: <i>And set me down in the midst of the valley</i> (Ezekiel, XXXVII, 1), it was again <i>in the visions of God</i>. Ezekiel, in that vision in which he was brought into Jerusalem, expresses himself[[fn:2405]] in these terms: <i>I beheld, and lo, a hole in the wall. Then said he unto me: Son of man, dig now in the wall; and when I had digged in the wall, behold a door</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 8, 7-8). Even as, therefore, it had appeared unto him <i>in the visions of God</i> that he was commanded to dig through the wall in order to enter therein and see what was being done there, and as he subsequently digged, as he says, in those same <i>visions of God</i>, entered by the opening and saw what he saw—all this in a prophetic vision—likewise, when God says unto him: <i>Take thee a tile, etc., lie upon thy left side, etc. Take thou also wheat, and barley, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.</i> 4, 1, 4 and 9), and likewise again, when He says unto him: <i>And cause</i> (<i>the razor</i>) <i>to pass upon thine head and upon thy beard, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 5, 1), all this (signifies that), in a prophetic vision, it appeared unto him that he performed the actions which he had been commanded to do. Far be it from God to render His prophets a laughing-stock unto fools and an object of mockery[[fn:2406]], and to command them to perform acts of madness[[fn:2407]]!",
    "root.text.Part 2..45.3.sub_1": "Let us add to this that he would have been commanded to disobey (the Law); for, as he was a priest, he would have rendered himself guilty of two transgressions for every corner of the beard or of the hair (that he might have cut)[[fn:2408]]. But all this took place only in a <i>prophetic vision</i>. In like manner, when it is said: <i>Like as my servant Isaiah has walked naked and barefoot</i> (Isaiah, 20, 3), this occurred only in the <i>visions of God</i>. None but men of weak understanding believe that in all these passages the prophet relats that it had (really) been commanded him to do such a thing, and that he did it; it is thus that he would relate that it had been commanded him to dig through the wall which was on the mountain of the temple (at Jerusalem), although he was then in Babylon, and he would add that he (really) digged it, as he says: <i>And I digged in the wall</i> (Ezek., 8, 8). Nevertheless, he says expressly that this occurred in the <i>visions of God</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..45.4": "It is even as we read concerning Abraham: <i>The word of the Lord came unto Abram in a vision, saying</i> (Genesis, 15, 1), and it is said in this same prophetic vision: <i>And he brought him forth abroad and said: Look now toward heaven</i>, <i>and tell the stars</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, v. 5); it is therefore clear that it was in the prophetic vision[[fn:2409]] that it seemed unto him to be led forth from the place where he was, so that he saw the heaven, and that subsequently it was said unto him: <i>Tell the stars</i>. Such is the narrative, as thou seest. I shall say as much of the command which was given unto Jeremiah to hide the girdle in the Euphrates; having hidden it, he went to seek it after a long space of time and found it marred and corrupted[[fn:2410]]. All these are parables of the prophetic vision[[fn:2411]]; for Jeremiah had not departed from the land of Israel for Babylon, and had not seen the Euphrates. Likewise, those words addressed unto Hosea: <i>Take unto thee a wife of whoredoms and</i> (<i>have of her</i>) <i>children</i> (<i>born</i>) <i>of whoredoms</i> (Hos., I, 2), and all this narrative of the birth of the children and of the names so-and-so which were given unto them, all this took place in a prophetic vision. Verily, as soon as it has been declared that these are but parables, there is no longer any reason to suspect that any detail therein had any reality[[fn:2412]], unless there be applied unto us that which has been said[[fn:2413]]: <i>And the vision of all is become unto you as the words of a book that is sealed</i> (Isaiah, 29, 11). — In like manner, it seems unto me that what is related, concerning Gideon, of the <i>fleece</i> and of the other (miraculous) thing[[fn:2414]], took place only in a vision. Nevertheless, I shall not call this a <i>prophetic vision,</i> in the absolute sense; for Gideon had not attained unto the rank of the prophets, and how then should he have gone so far as to perform miracles[[fn:2415]]? His greatest merit was to be included among the judges of Israel[[fn:2416]] [for they (the doctors) have even placed him among the men <i>of least account in the world</i>[[fn:2417]]], as we have set forth[[fn:2418]]. But all this took place only in a dream, similar unto the dream of Laban and (unto that) of Abimelech, of which we have spoken[[fn:2419]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..45.5": "— Even so likewise[[fn:2420]], that which Zechariah says: <i>I fed the flock of slaughter, even</i>, <i>the poor of the flock, and I took unto me two staves</i> (Zech. xi. 7)[[fn:2421]], together with the sequel of the narrative, to wit: the wage demanded with gentleness, the acceptance of the wage, and the silver told out and cast into the treasury (<i>Ibid.</i>, vv. 12 and 13); all this, it appeared unto him in a prophetic vision that he was commanded to do, and he performed it in the prophetic vision or in the prophetic dream.",
    "root.text.Part 2..45.6": "This is a matter of which one cannot doubt, and which can be unknown only to him who confounds together the possible and the impossible.",
    "root.text.Part 2..45.7": "From that which I have cited, thou mayest conclude concerning that which I have not cited; for all is of the same kind and follows the same method; all is a <i>prophetic vision</i>. Whenever, therefore, it is said that, in such a <i>vision</i>, he acted[[fn:2422]], or he heard, or he went out, or he entered, or he spake, or it was spoken unto him, or he arose, or he sat, or he ascended, or he descended, or he journeyed, or he questioned, or he was questioned, all this took place in the prophetic vision. Even though the actions designated should have endured for a long season, and should relate to certain specified times, to particular individuals, and to certain places, so soon as it shall be manifest unto thee that such action is a parable[[fn:2423]], thou shalt know with certainty that it occurred in the prophetic vision.",
    "root.text.Part 2..46.0": "It is indubitably clear and manifest that the prophets, for the most part, prophesy by parables[[fn:2424]]; for that which serves as an instrument thereto, I mean the imaginative faculty, produces this effect. It behoves us, likewise, to know something of metaphors and hyperboles[[fn:2425]]; for such are sometimes found in the texts of the prophetic books. If one were to take a given expression literally[[fn:2426]], without knowing it to be a hyperbole and an exaggeration, or if one were to take another expression in the sense which the words indicate according to their primitive acceptation, without knowing it to be a metaphor, there would thence arise divers absurdities. The Sages have said plainly: «The Scripture employs exaggerated language», that is to say <i>hyperbole</i>, and they have cited as proof these words: <i>cities great and walled up to heaven</i> (Deut. i. 28)[[fn:2427]], which is just. To the category of hyperbole belong also these words: <i>for a bird of the air shall carry the voice</i> (Eccl. x. 20). In like manner it is said: … <i>whose height was like the height of the cedars</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Amos ii. 9). This manner of speech is frequently met with in the utterances of all the prophets; I mean expressions employed by way of hyperbole and exaggeration, and not for the purpose of speaking with precision and exactness.",
    "root.text.Part 2..46.1": "Of this category is not that which the Pentateuch says concerning Og: <span dir=\"rtl\">הנה ערשו ערשׂ ברזל וגו׳</span>, <i>his bedstead</i>, <i>a bedstead of iron, etc.</i> (Deut. iii. 11); for <span dir=\"rtl\">ערש</span> signifies <i>bedstead</i>, as in <span dir=\"rtl\">אף ערשנו רעננה</span>, <i>our</i> <small>BED</small> <i>is green</i> (Cant. i. 16). Now, the bed of a man has not the exact measure of the man himself; for it is not a garment wherewith he cloths himself. On the contrary, a bed is always larger than the person who lies therein, and it is customary, as is well known, that it exceeds the length of the person by about one third. If, therefore, the length of this bed be nine cubits, the length of him who lies therein shall be, according to the usual proportion of beds, six cubits, or somewhat more. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">באמת איש</span>, <i>after the cubit of a man</i> (Deut. <i>loc. cit.</i>), signify: according to the cubit of an ordinary person <i>amongst us,</i> I mean, amongst the rest of men, and not according to the cubit of Og; for every individual has ordinarily his limbs proportioned[[fn:2428]]. It was therefore intended to say that the stature of Og was twice[[fn:2429]] that of an ordinary person amongst other men, or a little more[[fn:2430]]. Doubtless, this is an anomaly for individuals of the species; yet is it in no wise impossible.",
    "root.text.Part 2..46.2": "As touching that which the Pentateuch relats concerning the number[[fn:2431]] of the years of certain persons, I say, for my part, that the individual (in each case) specified alone attained that age (which is ascribed unto him), whilst other men reached only the natural and accustomed terms of life. The anomaly in such an individual either proceeded from diverse causes (subsisting) in his manner of nourishment and in his regimen, or else it must be regarded as the effect of a miracle[[fn:2432]]. There is no means of reasoning thereupon in any other wise.",
    "root.text.Part 2..46.3": "Likewise, moreover, great attention must be paid unto those things which have been spoken by <i>metaphor</i>. There be some which are clear and manifest, and which possess no obscurity for any man, as, for example, when it is said: <i>The mountains and the hills shall break forth before you into singing, and all the trees of the field shall clap their hands</i> (Isaiah, lv. 12), which thing is evidently a metaphor. It is the same when it is said: <i>Yea, the fir-trees rejoice at thee, etc.</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, xiv. 8), which Jonathan ben-Uziel has paraphrased thus: 'even the sovereigns have rejoiced because of thee, those who are rich in possessions,' seeing therein an allegory as (in this passage): <i>Butter of kine, and milk of sheep, etc.</i> (Deut. xxxii. 14)[[fn:2433]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..46.4": "These metaphors are exceeding numerous in the prophetic books; there be some in which even the vulgar recognize metaphors, but there be others which they take not for such. Indeed, no man could doubt that these words: <i>the Lord shall open unto thee His good treasure, etc.</i> (Deut. xxviii. 12) are a metaphor, since God has no treasure which contains the rain. Likewise, when it is said: <i>He opened the doors of heaven, and rained down manna upon them</i> (Ps. lxxviii. 23-24), no man will believe that there be in heaven a gate and doors; but this (is spoken) after the manner of <i>similitude,</i> which is a species of metaphor. In this wise must we understand these expressions: <i>The heavens were opened</i> (Ezek. i. 1); <i>if not</i>, <i>blot me, I pray Thee, out of Thy book which Thou hast written</i> (Exodus, xxxii. 32); <i>him will I blot out of My book</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, v. 33); <i>let them be blotted out of the book of the living</i> (Ps. lxix. 29). All this (is spoken) after the manner of <i>similitude;</i> not that God has a book, in which He writs and blotts out, as the vulgar believe, perceiving not that here is a metaphor.",
    "root.text.Part 2..46.5": "All (that I have said) belongs unto the same category. All that I have not cited, thou shalt compare with what I have cited in this chapter; separate and distinguish things by thy reason, and thou shalt understand what has been spoken by allegory, what has been spoken by metaphor, what has been spoken by hyperbole, and what has been spoken exactly according to that which the primary acceptation (of the terms) indicats. Then all the prophecies shall become clear and manifest unto thee; thou shalt have reasonable beliefs, well-ordered and pleasing unto God, for truth alone is pleasing unto God, and falsehood alone is odious unto Him. Let not thy ideas and thy thoughts become confused, so that thou shouldst admit unsound opinions, far removed from the truth, and take them for religion! Religious precepts are but pure truth, if they be understood as they ought to be; it is said: <i>Thy testimonies are righteous for ever, etc.</i> (Ps. cxix. 144), and it is said again: <i>I the Lord speak righteousness</i> (Isaiah, xlv. 19). By these reflections, thou shalt also escape from the imagination of a world which God created not[[fn:2434]] and from those corrupt ideas, some of which may lead to irreligion and to the admission of defect in God, such as the circumstances of corporeality, of attributes, and of passions, as we have set forth[[fn:2435]], unless thou believest that these prophetic discourses be a lie[[fn:2436]]. All the evil which leads to this is that men neglect[[fn:2437]] those things unto which we have called attention; but these are also subjects (which pertain to) the <i>secrets of the Law</i>, and, although we have spoken thereupon but in a summary manner, it will be easy, from what has preceded, to know the details thereof.",
    "root.text.Part 2..47.0": "It is most manifest that every thing <i>produced</i> has necessarily a proximate cause which caused it to be; this cause (in its turn) has a cause, until one reaches the First Cause of all things, that is to say, the free will of God[[fn:2438]]. For this reason, there are sometimes omitted, in the discourses of the prophets[[fn:2439]], all these intermediate causes, and there is attributed directly unto God such individual act as occurrs, by saying that it was God who did it. All this is known; we have already spoken thereof ourselves, as have others among the true philosophers[[fn:2440]], and it is the opinion of all our theologians[[fn:2441]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..47.1": "After this preliminary observation, hearken unto that which I shall set forth in this chapter, and bestow thereupon a right singular attention, over and above the attention which thou owest unto every chapter of this treatise. The matter which I desire to manifest unto thee is this:",
    "root.text.Part 2..47.2": "Know that all proximate causes, wherefrom springs that which is brought into being, whether these causes be essential and natural, or arbitrary, or accidental and due to chance[[fn:2442]] [by <i>arbitrary,</i> I mean that the cause of that which ariss is the <i>free-will</i> of a man], and even should the cause be naught but the will of any animal whatsoever[[fn:2443]], — all these causes (I say) are, in the books of the prophets, attributed unto God; and, in their modes of expression, it is simply said of such an event, that God has done it, or has commanded it, or has spoken it. For all these things, they employ the verbs <i>to say</i>, <i>to speak</i>, <i>to command</i>, <i>to call</i>, <i>to send,</i> and this is the subject unto which I have desired to summon thy attention in this chapter. Indeed, as it is God [even as it has been established[[fn:2444]]] who has stirred such a will in such an irrational animal, as it is He who has caused the rational animal to possess free-will, and as it is He, finally, who has determined the course of natural things [for <i>chance</i> is but an excess of the natural, as has been explained, and for the most part it participats in nature, in free-will and in will[[fn:2445]]], one must, by reason of all this, say of that which results from these causes, that God has commanded to do such a thing, or that He has said: “Let such a thing be.”",
    "root.text.Part 2..47.3": "I shall cite unto thee examples of all this, whereunto thou mayest compare all that which I shall not have mentioned (expressly). In speaking of natural things which ever follow their course, as (for example) of snow which melts when the air is warm, and of the waters of the sea which are agitated when the wind blows, it is thus expressed: <i>He</i> <small>SENDS</small> <i>his word and melts them</i> (Ps. 147, 18); <i>he</i> <small>SPEAKS</small> <i>and raiss a stormy wind which lifts up the waves</i> (Ps. 107, 25); of the rain which falls, it is said: <i>And</i> <small>I WILL COMMAND</small> <i>the clouds that they rain no rain etc.</i> (Isaiah, 5, 6).",
    "root.text.Part 2..47.4": "When speaking of that which has for its cause the free will of man, such as (for example) a war waged by a powerful nation against another nation[[fn:2446]], or an individual setting himself in motion to inflict harm upon another individual, even if he should have done no more than insult him, it is expressed thus: <i>I have</i> <small>COMMANDED</small> <i>my sanctified ones</i>, <i>and I have</i> <small>CALLED</small> <i>my mighty ones for</i> (<i>to execute</i>) <i>my wrath</i> (<i>Isaiah</i>, 13, 3), — where the tyranny of the impious Nebuchadnezzar and his armies is in question[[fn:2447]]; — and elsewhere: <i>I will</i> <small>SEND HIM</small> <i>against a hypocritical nation</i> (<i>Ibid</i>., X, 6). In the matter of Shimei, the son of Gera, it is said: <i>For the LORD has</i> <small>SAID</small> unto him: <i>Curse David</i> (II Sam., 16, 10). Concerning the pious Joseph delivered from the dungeon, it is said: <i>He</i> <small>SENT</small> <i>a king who released him</i> (Ps. 105, 20). Concerning the victory of the Persians and Medes over the Chaldeans, it is said: <i>And</i> <small>I will SEND</small> <i>against Babylon barbarians who will scatter her</i> (Jer., LI, 2). In the history of Elijah, when God commissions a woman to nourish him[[fn:2448]], He says to her: <i>I have</i> <small>COMMANDED</small> <i>there a widow woman to nourish thee</i> (I Kings, 17, 9). The pious Joseph says: <i>It was not you who</i> <small>SENT</small> <i>me hither, but God</i> (Genesis, 45, 8)[[fn:2449]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..47.5": "In speaking of that which has for its cause the will of an animal, which is moved by its animal needs (it is said, for example): <i>And the Lord</i> <small>SPAKE</small> <i>unto the fish</i> (Jonah, 2, 11); for (it is meant that) it was God who stirred in it this will, and not that He made of it a prophet or revealed Himself unto it. Likewise, it is said concerning the locusts which came in the days of Joel, son of Pethuel: <i>For he is strong that executs his</i> <small>WORD</small> <i></i> (Joel, II, 11). In like manner also, it is said concerning the wild beasts which took possession of the land of Edom, when it was laid waste in the days of Sennacherib: <i>And he has cast the lot for them</i>, <i>and his hand has divided it unto them by line</i> (Isaiah, 34, 17). Although none of the expressions <i>to say, to command, to send,</i> have been employed here, the sense is evidently analogous; and thou shalt judge likewise of all phrases which have a similar turn of expression[[fn:2450]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..47.6": "Concerning accidental things, of mere chance, it is said, for example, in the history of Rebekah: <i>And let her be</i> (<i>given</i>) <i>for a wife unto thy master's son, as the Lord has</i> <small>SPOKEN</small> (Genesis xxiv. 51)[[fn:2451]]. In the history of David and Jonathan, it is said: <i>Go thy way; for the Lord has</i> <small>SENT THEE AWAY</small> (1 Sam. xx. 22)[[fn:2452]]. In the history of Joseph (it is said): <i>And God</i> <small>SENT</small> <i>me before you</i> (Genesis xlv. 7). Thou seest therefore clearly that, to designate the disposition of causes—in whatever manner they be disposed, whether they be causes by essence[[fn:2453]], or by accident, or by free choice, or by (animal) will—one employs these five expressions, namely: <i>to command</i>, <i>to say, to speak</i>, <i>to send</i>, and <i>to call</i>. Know this well, and reflect thereon[[fn:2454]] in each passage so as to explain it as it behoves; then many absurdities shall vanish, and thou shalt recognize the true sense of such passages as might be deemed remote from the truth[[fn:2455]].",
    "root.text.Part 2..47.7": "I have now reached the term of what I had to say[[fn:2456]] concerning prophecy, its parables, and its expressions. This is all that I shall tell thee upon this subject in the present treatise[[fn:2457]]; we shall therefore proceed to other matters with the help of the Almighty.",
    "root.text.Part 2..47.8": "END OF THE SECOND PART <br>OF THE GUIDE FOR THE PERPLEXED.",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.0": "IN THE NAME OF THE LORD, THE GOD OF THE UNIVERSE",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.1": "The propositions which are needed to establish the existence of God, and to demonstrate that He is neither a body nor a force in a body, and that He is <i>One</i> [may His name be glorified!], are twenty-five in number, which, being generally demonstrated, contain naught that is doubtful; for Aristotle and the Peripatetics who succeeded him have already undertaken the demonstration of each of them[[fn:1267]]. There is, moreover, one proposition which we grant unto them as a concession[[fn:1268]], because it will be the means of demonstrating the matters in question, as I shall set forth; this proposition is the eternity of the world.",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.2": "F<small>IRST PROPOSITION</small>. — The existence of any infinite magnitude whatsoever is inadmissible[[fn:1269]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.3": "S<small>ECOND PROPOSITION</small>. — The existence of an infinite number of magnitudes is inadmissible[[fn:1270]], if they are to exist (all) simultaneously[[fn:1271]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.4": "T<small>HIRD PROPOSITION</small>. — The existence of an infinite number of causes and effects is inadmissible, even though they be not magnitudes; thus, for example, it is manifestly inadmissible that a certain intelligence should have for its cause a second intelligence, this second a third, this third a fourth, and so on to infinity[[fn:1272]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.5": "Q<small>UARTIÈME PROPOSITION</small>. — Change is found in four <i>categories</i>",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.6": "1° in the category of the <i>substance</i>, and the change to which the substance is susceptible, namely, birth and corruption;",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.7": "2nd in the category of the <i>quantity</i>, and here it is the growth and diminution;",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.8": "3rd in the category of the <i>quality</i>, which is the transformation;",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.9": "4° in the category of the <i>lieu</i>, that which is the movement of translation[[fn:1273]]; it is to this change in the place that the term <i>mouvement</i>[[fn:1274]] is particularly applied.",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.10": "CINQUÈME PROPOSITION. — Every movement is a change and a passage from <small>power</small> to <i>act</i>[[fn:1275]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.11": "SIXTH PROPOSITION<small>. — Movements[[fn:1276]] are sometimes essential (or inherent in the thing itself), sometimes accidental, sometimes due to violence, sometimes partial, and (in the latter case) a species of (movement) accidental. Essential, as the translation of the body from one place to another; accidental, as we would say (for example) of the blackness in some body, that it has transported itself from one place to another; due to violence, as when the stone moves upwards by something that forces it; partial, as the movement of the nail in the ship: for when the ship moves, we also say that the nail moves. And thus, every time a composite thing moves as a whole, we also say that its part moves[[fn:1277]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.12": "SEPTIÈME PROPOSITION</small>. — All that undergoes change is divisible[[fn:1278]]; it is for this reason that all that is moved is divisible and is necessarily a body[[fn:1279]]. All that is not divisible is not moved[[fn:1280]], and, consequently, cannot in any manner be a body.",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.13": "E<small>IGHTH PROPOSITION</small>. — Everything that is moved accidentally shall of necessity come to rest, forasmuch as its motion is not in its essence; wherefore it is impossible that it should perpetually perform this accidental motion[[fn:1281]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.14": "N<small>EITHER</small>. PROPOSITION</small>. — Every body that movs another does not move, except inasmuch as it is moved itself at the moment when it movs[[fn:1282]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.15": "T<small>ENTH PROPOSITION</small>. — Everything of which it is said that it is in a body belongs to one of these two classes[[fn:1283]]: it is either something that subsists by the body, as the accidents, or something by which the body subsists, as the physical form[[fn:1284]]; in both cases, it is a force (which is) in a body[[fn:1285]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.16": "E<small>LEVENTH</small>[[fn:1286]] <small>PROPOSITION.</small> — Certain things which subsist by the body are divided by the division of the body, and are, consequently, accidentally divisible, as, for example, colours and in general the forces diffused throughout the whole body. Likewise, certain things which constitute the being of the body are in no wise divided, as the soul and the intellect[[fn:1287]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.17": "T<small>WELFTH PROPOSITION</small>. — Every force which is diffused within a body is finite, forasmuch as the body (itself) is finite[[fn:1288]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.18": "T<small>HE TENTH PROPOSITION</small>. — Nothing in the various species of <i>change</i>[[fn:1289]] can be continuous, if it is not the motion of translation[[fn:1290]], and in this the (sole) circular motion[[fn:1291]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.19": "F<small>OURTEENTH PROPOSITION</small>. — The motion of locomotion is anterior to all other motions and is the first of them[[fn:1292]] according to nature; for (even) generation and corruption are preceded by alteration; and alteration (in its turn) is preceded by the approach of that which alters toward that which is to be altered; finally, there is neither growth nor diminution, without there being first generation and corruption[[fn:1293]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.20": "Q<small>UINTIES PROPOSITION</small>. — Time is an accident which accompanies motion and is inherent to it[[fn:1294]], and neither of the two exists without the other; a motion exists only in time, and one cannot think of time without motion. Consequently, everything for which there is no motion[[fn:1295]] does not fall under time.",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.21": "S<small>EIZIÈME PROPOSITION</small>. — All that is incorporeal does not admit the idea of <i>number</i>[[fn:1296]]; except in the case of a force within a body, whereby one may number the individual forces by numbering their matters or subjects[[fn:1297]]. This is why things <i>separated</i>, which are neither a body nor a force within a body, do not admit the idea of number, except in the sense that they are causes and effects (one of the other)[[fn:1298]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.22": "S<small>EVENTEENTH PROPOSITION</small>. — Whatsoever is moved has necessarily a mover[[fn:1299]]. Either it has a mover without itself, as the stone which the hand does move; or else it has its mover within itself, as the body of the animal[[fn:1300]]. This latter is composed of a mover and a thing moved; wherefore, when the animal dies, and is deprived of the mover, which is the soul, the thing moved, which is the body[[fn:1301]], albeit remaining such as it was, straightway ceass to have that motion[[fn:1302]]. But, since the mover which exists in the thing moved is hidden and manifests not itself unto the senses, it has been imagined that the animal movs without a mover. Everything moved, which has its mover within itself, is said to move <i>by itself</i>[[fn:1303]]; which signifies that the force which <i>essentially</i> movs that which is moved therefrom is found within its whole[[fn:1304]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.23": "E<small>IGHTEENTH PROPOSITION</small>. — Whenever aught passes from potentiality to actuality, that which causs it to pass is other than itself, and necessarily is without it[[fn:1305]]: for, if that which causs it to pass (unto actuality) were within it, and there were no hindrance therein, it would not remain for an instant in potentiality, but would ever be in actuality[[fn:1306]]. If, however, that which causs a thing to pass (unto actuality) were within it, but there had existed a hindrance which was removed, there is no doubt that that which caused the hindrance to cease is that which caused this potentiality to pass into actuality[[fn:1307]]. Endeavour to understand this well[[fn:1308]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.24": "N<small>INETEENTH PROPOSITION</small>. — Everything of which the existence has a cause is, in respect of its own essence, of a <i>possible</i> existence[[fn:1309]]: for, if its causes be present, it shall exist[[fn:1310]]; but if they have never been present, or if they have vanished, or finally, if the relation[[fn:1311]] which rendered necessary the existence of the thing be changed, it shall not exist.",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.25": "T<small>WENTIES PROPOSITION</small>. — Everything which is of a necessary existence, in respect of its own essence, derivs not its existence, in any wise, from any cause whatsoever[[fn:1312]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.26": "T<small>WENTY-FIRST PROPOSITION</small>. — Everything which is a compound of two different ideas has necessarily, in this very composition, the (immediate) cause of its existence such as it is, and, consequently, is not of a necessary existence in itself: for it exists by the existence of its two parts and of their composition[[fn:1313]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.27": "T<small>WENTY-SECOND PROPOSITION</small>. — Every body is necessarily composed of two distinct notions, and is necessarily affected by accidents. The two notions which constitute its being are its matter and its form[[fn:1314]]; the accidents which affect it are quantity, figure, and position[[fn:1315]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.28": "VIII-THIRD PROPOSITION</small>. — All that is in potency, in such a manner as to have within its very essence a certain <i>possibilité</i>, may, at a certain moment, not exist in act[[fn:1316]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.29": "V<small>INGT-QUATRIÈME PROPOSITION</small>. — All that is a thing of any sort[[fn:1317]] <i>en puissance</i> necessarily has a matter: for the <i>possibilité</i> is always in the matter[[fn:1318]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.30": "T<small>WENTY-FIFTH PROPOSITION</small>. — The principles of the composite and individual substance are matter and form[[fn:1319]], and there must necessarily be an agent, that is to say, a mover, which has moved the substratum in order to dispose it to receive the form[[fn:1320]]; and this is the proximate mover, which disposs any individual matter whatsoever[[fn:1321]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.31": "This is necessarily the point of departure for the inquiry concerning motion, the mover, and that which is moved. All necessary explanations have been given upon this subject[[fn:1322]] and Aristotle says expressly: «Matter does not move itself[[fn:1323]].» Herein lies the important proposition which leads to the inquiry concerning the existence of the First Mover.",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.32": "Of these five-and-twenty propositions which I have placed at the head, some are manifest upon the slightest examination, being demonstrative propositions and primary notions[[fn:1324]], or nearly so, (intelligible) by the simple exposition which we have made of them[[fn:1325]]; others require numerous demonstrations and premisses, yet have all been already demonstrated in a manner that leavs no doubt, (and this) partly in the book of the <i>Acroasis</i>[[fn:1326]] and in its commentaries, and partly in the book of the <i>Metaphysics</i> and in its commentary[[fn:1327]]. I have already made it known unto thee that my purpose in this treatise is not to transcribe the books of the philosophers, nor to set forth the most remote propositions[[fn:1328]], but to record those propositions which are within our reach and necessary for our subject.",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.33": "To the preceding propositions I shall add one which involvs the eternity (of the world), and which Aristotle maintains to be true and the most admissible of all; we grant it unto him by way of hypothesis[[fn:1329]], until such time as we shall be able to set forth our own ideas in this regard[[fn:1330]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.34": "This proposition, which is <small>THE TWENTY-SIXTH,</small> says that time and motion are eternal, perpetual, and ever existent in act[[fn:1331]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.35": "From this proposition, therefore, it necessarily follows, according to him, that there is a body having an eternal motion, ever in act, and this is the <i>fifth body</i>[[fn:1332]]. Wherefore he says that the heaven neither comes into being nor perishes: for motion, according to him, neither comes into being nor perishes. Indeed, says he, every motion is necessarily preceded by another motion, either of the same species or of another species[[fn:1333]]; and when it is imagined that the local motion of an animal is preceded by no other motion whatsoever, it is not true; for the cause which makes it (the animal) to move after having been at rest, goes back to certain things which bring about this local motion: it is either a change of temperament producing (in the animal) the desire to seek what is convenient for it, or to flee what is contrary to it, or else an imagination, or finally an opinion that occurrs to it, so that one of these three things setts it in motion, each of them being in its turn brought about by other motions[[fn:1334]]. He says likewise that in everything which occurrs, the possibility of occurring preceds in time that which occurrs, and he draws thence various conclusions to confirm his proposition[[fn:1335]]. — According to this proposition, the mobile which is finite[[fn:1336]] must move over a finite extent an infinite number of times, returning ever upon the same extent, which is possible only in circular motion, as is demonstrated by the thirteenth of these propositions. It follows that the infinite may exist by way of <i>succession</i> and provided there be no simultaneity[[fn:1337]].",
    "root.text.Part 2.Introduction.36": "This proposition Aristotle endeavours ever to confirm. As for me, it seems to me that he by no means claims to attribute a demonstrative force to the proofs wherewith he supports it[[fn:1338]]; but it is, according to him, that which is most admissible. Nevertheless, his followers and the commentators of his writings maintain that it is necessary and not merely possible, and that it has been demonstrated[[fn:1339]]. Each of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> (on the contrary) endeavours to establish that it is impossible: for, say they, one cannot conceive that there could occur, even <i>successively</i>, infinite facts (in number); and they consider this, in sum, as a primary notion[[fn:1340]]. That which seems to me is that the said proposition is <i>possible</i>, (and that it is) neither <i>necessary</i>, as the commentators on the words of Aristotle say, nor <i>impossible</i>, as the <i>Motécallemîn</i> maintain. It is not my purpose, at this moment, to set forth the proofs of Aristotle, nor to produce my doubts against him, nor to set forth my opinion upon the <i>creation of the world;</i> but my purpose, in this place, has been to enumerate the propositions of which we have need for our three questions[[fn:1341]]. Having placed these propositions at the head and having granted them, I begin to set forth that which results therefrom.",
    "root.text.Part 3..0.0": "It is well known that there exist human individuals whose faces possess forms similar unto those appertaining to one of the other animals, so that thou beholdest such an individual whose face resembls in some wise the face of a lion, and another who has, as it were, the face of an ox, and so forth[[fn:2473]]; it is according to these figures approaching those of the faces of animals that men receive surnames[[fn:2474]]. Thus the words <i>face of an ox, face of a lion, face of an eagle</i> (Ezekiel, i. 10), designate nought else than a human face, inclining toward the figures of those species. Two proofs support this: the first is that after having said of the <i>’hayyôth</i> (figures of animals) in general: <i>And this was their appearance; they had the likeness of a man</i> (<i>ibid., v</i>. 5), he says of each of the <i>’hayyôth</i> that it had <i>a human face, an eagle's face, a lion's face, and an ox's face</i>. The second proof is the explanation he gives in the second <i>mercabâ</i>, aiming to explain certain things which had remained obscure[[fn:2475]] in the first; for in the second <i>mercabâ</i>, he says: <i>every one had</i>[[fn:2476]] <i>four faces</i>, 1° <i>the face of a cherub</i>, 2° <i>the face of a man</i>, 3° <i>the face of a lion</i>, 4° <i>the face of an eagle</i> (Ezekiel, x. 14). He has thus clearly stated that what he had first called <i>the face of an ox</i> is the <i>face of a cherub;</i> but the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כרוב</span> (cherub) signifies a <i>man</i> of tender age[[fn:2477]]. Thence may one likewise conclude concerning the two other faces[[fn:2478]]. If he has here omitted[[fn:2479]] the <i>face of an ox</i>, it is also to call attention to a certain etymology, a matter whereunto we have already alluded[[fn:2480]]. It would not be permissible to say that perhaps the perception of other figures is here intended; for, at the end of this second description, he says: <i>This was the</i> ’<small>HAYYA</small> (the living creature) <i>that I saw under the God of Israel by the river of Chebar</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, x. 20). — Thus is elucidated that which we deemed it necessary to explain in the first place[[fn:2481]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..1.0": "He (the prophet) relats having seen four ’<small>HAYYÔTH</small> (figures of animals) each of which had four faces, four wings, and two hands; the whole configuration of each ’<i>hayyâ</i> was the figure of a man, as he says: <i>they had the likeness of a man</i> (Ezekiel, i. 5)[[fn:2482]]. He likewise says that the hands were <i>hands of a man</i> (<i>v</i>. 8), for it is known that the hands of man are so formed as to be able to perform works of art, a matter of which one cannot doubt[[fn:2483]]. Further, he says that their feet were straight, which signifies that they had no joints; such is the literal sense of the words <i>straight feet</i> (<i>v</i>. 7)[[fn:2484]]. The doctors likewise say: “<i>Their feet were straight feet;</i> this signifies that the superior beings do not sit down[[fn:2485]].” Thou must also well understand this. — He says further that the soles of the feet, the organs of walking, were not like (those) of a human foot; the hands alone resembled the hands of man, but the feet were rounded <i>like the sole of a calf's foot</i> (<i>v</i>. 7)[[fn:2486]]. Then he says that between each pair[[fn:2487]] of these four <i>’hayyôth</i> there was neither interstice nor space, and that, on the contrary, they were joined one to another, as it is said: <i>joined one to another</i> (<i>v</i>. 9)[[fn:2488]]. But, he adds, although they were joined one to another[[fn:2489]], their faces and their wings were separated above, as it is said: <i>their faces and their wings were stretched upward</i> (<i>v</i>. 11). Mark well the expression <i>upward;</i> for the bodies alone were joined one to another, whilst their faces and their wings were separated, yet only from above[[fn:2490]]. Wherefore he says: <span dir=\"ltr\">ופניהם וכנפיהם פרדות מלמעלה</span>[[fn:2491]]. He says again that they were sparkling <i>like the colour of burnished brass</i> (<i>v</i>. 7), and finally that they were also luminous, as it is said: <i>their appearance was like burning coals of fire</i> (<i>v</i>. 13)[[fn:2492]]. This, in sum, is what he says of the configuration of the <i>’hayyôth</i>, to wit, of their figure, their substance[[fn:2493]], their form, their wings, their hands, and their feet.",
    "root.text.Part 3..1.1.sub_0": "Beginning thenceforth to describe after what manner are the movements of these <i>’hayyôth</i>, he expresses himself in this regard as follows: In the movements of the <i>’hayyôth</i>, says he, there was neither turning, nor deviation, nor curvature; it was, on the contrary, a uniform movement, as he says: <i>They turned not when they went</i> (<i>v</i>. 12)[[fn:2494]]. — He says then that each of the <i>’hayyôth</i> followed the direction of its face, which he expresses by these words: <i>every one went straight forward</i> (<i>v</i>. 9). Thus, he clearly says that each <i>’hayyâ</i> walked only (on the side) whither its face was directed[[fn:2495]]; but would that I knew which face is intended, since each <i>’hayyâ</i> had several faces[[fn:2496]]! Howsoever it may be, the four did not walk in the same direction, for otherwise he would not have attributed to each a particular movement by saying: <i>every one went straight forward</i>[[fn:2497]]. — He says further that the manner of moving of these <i>’hayyôth</i> was a (rapid) running, and that it was likewise by running that they returned upon their steps[[fn:2498]]. This is what he expresses by the words: <i>And the ’hayyôth ran and returned</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">רצוא ושוב</span> (<i>v</i>. 14); for <span dir=\"rtl\">רצוא</span> is the infinitive of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">רָץ</span>, <i>he ran</i>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">שוב</span>, is the infinitive of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">שָׁב</span>, <i>he returned</i>. He employs not the verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">הלוך ובוא</span>, <i>to go and to come</i>, but he says that their movement was a running and a returning upon their steps, which he explains by a simile, saying: <i>as the appearance of a flash of lightning</i>, <small>BAZAK</small> (<i>ibid.</i>), for <i>bazak</i> has the same meaning as <i>barak</i> (lightning). He says therefore: like unto lightning, whose movement seems the swiftest that exists, and which extends with rapidity, darting from a certain place, and thereafter instantly withdraws and returns with the same speed to its point of departure.",
    "root.text.Part 3..1.1.sub_1": "Jonathan ben Uziel explains the words <i>ran and returned</i> in these terms: “They went round the world and returned (upon their steps), like a single creature[[fn:2499]], swift as the appearance of lightning.” He (the prophet) then adds that, if the <i>’hayyâ</i> performs this movement of running (forward) and backward toward a certain side, it is a movement[[fn:2500]] which proceeds not from itself, but from another thing, I mean from the divine intention. He says therefore that the side toward which, according to the divine intention, the <i>’hayyâ</i> was to move, was that[[fn:2501]] toward which it performed this rapid movement designated by the words <i>ran and returned</i>. This is what he says in speaking of the <i>’hayyôth: Whither the spirit</i> (<i>divine</i>), <small>HA-RUAH</small>, <i>was to go, they went; they turned not when they went</i> (<i>v</i>. 12). The word <small>RUAH</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>) signifies not here the <i>wind</i>, but has the sense of <i>intention</i>, as we have set forth concerning the word <small>RUAH</small>[[fn:2502]]. Thus, he means that the <i>’hayyâ</i> followed in its course the direction given to it by the divine intention[[fn:2503]]. It is in the same sense that Jonathan ben Uziel has explained this passage: “Whither the will (<span dir=\"rtl\">רעוא</span>) was that they should go, they went; they turned not when they went.” But as the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אל אשר יהיה שמה הרוח וגו׳</span>, <i>whither the intention was etc</i>. might be understood in the sense that, in the future, sometimes God will will that the <i>’hayyâ</i> take such a direction, and sometimes He will will that it walk in such another direction, opposite to the first[[fn:2504]], he (Ezekiel) returns to explain what is therein obscure, and he informs us that it is not so, and that (the aorist) <span dir=\"rtl\">יהיה</span>, <i>it is, it shall be</i>, has here the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">היה</span>, <i>it was, it had been</i>, which frequently occurrs in Hebrew. The direction, says he, in which God willed that the <i>’hayyâ</i> should walk was determined; the <i>’hayyâ</i> walked in the direction in which God had willed that it should walk, and the (divine) will was stable with regard to that direction.",
    "root.text.Part 3..1.1.sub_2": "In order to explain this idea and to complete his discourse, he says in another verse: <i>Where it was the intention that they should go, there they went</i>, <small>AND THE INTENTION WAS</small> (<small>ALWAYS</small>) <small>THAT THEY SHOULD GO THERE</small> (<i>v</i>. 20). Thou must thoroughly penetrate this remarkable explanation. — Thus he describes the manner of motion of the four <i>’hayyôth</i>, after having described their figures.",
    "root.text.Part 3..1.2.sub_0": "One then proceeds to another description, and it is stated that, beneath the <i>’hayyôth</i>, one perceived another body which was attached thereto. This body conjoined with the earth and likewise constituted four bodies, also possessing four faces[[fn:2505]]. No specific figure is attributed to it, neither that of man, nor that of any other animal[[fn:2506]]; but it is merely stated that these are bodies of great magnitude, formidable and terrifying, no figure being assigned to them, and their bodies being declared full of eyes[[fn:2507]]. These are designated by him as <small>OPHANNÎM</small>, or <i>wheels: 'I beheld the ’hayyôth</i>,' he states, '<i>and behold there was an</i> <small>OPHAN</small> (a wheel), <i>upon the earth, beside the</i> <small>’HAYYÔTH</small>, <i>with its four faces</i>' (<i>v</i>. 15). He thus distinctly declares that it constituted a single body, one extremity of which rested nigh the <i>’hayyôth</i>, whilst the other was situated upon the earth, and that this <i>ophan</i> possessed four faces. He proceeds: '<i>The appearance of the</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small> <i>and their work was like unto the colour of a</i> <small>TARSCHISCH</small>[[fn:2508]], <i>and all four had one likeness</i>' (<i>v</i>. 16). Thus, having initially referred to <i>an ophan</i>, he subsequently speaks of <i>four</i>, thereby distinctly indicating that the four faces possessed by the <i>ophan</i> are, in fact, the four <i>ophannîm</i>[[fn:2509]]; and he further states that these four <i>ophannîm</i> presented one and the same form, a point conveyed by the words: '<i>and all four had one likeness</i>'[[fn:2510]]. He thereupon declares concerning these <i>ophannîm</i> that they were conjoined one with another[[fn:2511]], a concept expressed by the words: '<i>their appearance and their work was as it were a</i> <small>OPHAN</small> <i>in the midst of a</i> <small>OPHAN</small>' (<i>v</i>. 16). This is an expression which is not in any wise employed with respect to the <i>’hayyôth;</i> for, when speaking of the <i>’hayyôth</i>, the term <span dir=\"rtl\">תוך</span> (within) is not utilised, but rather it is stated that these were contiguous[[fn:2512]] one to another, employing the phrase <i>joined one to another</i> (<i>v</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 3..1.2.sub_1": "9); whilst he says of the <i>ophannîm</i> that they were <i>interlaced</i> one with another, as <i>if an</i> <small>OPHAN</small> <i>were in the midst of another</i> <small>OPHAN</small>. As touching that which he says, that the whole body of the ophannîm was <i>full of eyes</i>, it may be that he means (verily) that they were filled with eyes[[fn:2513]]. But it may also be (that this signifies) that they have manifold colours, (as in this passage) <i>and the colour thereof</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">ועינו</span>, <i>was as the colour of Bdellium</i> (Numbers xi. 7); or perchance he intended (by <i>eyes</i>) to signify <i>likeness</i>[[fn:2514]], according to the expression which we find in the language of the ancient doctors[[fn:2515]] <span dir=\"rtl\">כעין שגזל ,כעין שגנב</span>, meaning <i>like unto that which has been stolen, like unto that which has been taken by force</i>; or lastly, it signifies various circumstances and qualities[[fn:2516]], as in this passage: <i>It may be that the Lord will look</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">בעיני</span>, that is to say: <i>on mine affliction</i>[[fn:2517]]. Such then is the description which he gives of the form of the <i>ophannîm</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 3..1.3.sub_0": "Regarding the movement of the <i>ophannîm</i>, he states that there likewise was neither curvature, nor turning, nor deviation, but that these were straight movements which did not vary[[fn:2518]]. He states as follows: <i>When walking, they directed themselves toward their four sides</i>[[fn:2519]], <i>without swerving in their progress</i> (<i>v</i>. 17). He then states that these four <i>ophannîm</i> did not move of themselves, as did the <i>’hayyôth</i>, but that, on the contrary[[fn:2520]], they possessed within them no other movement than that which was imparted to them by an impulse from without[[fn:2521]]. He insists upon the repetition of this idea, bringing it forth on several occasions. He declares that that which set the <i>ophannîm</i> in motion was none other than the <i>’hayyôth</i>[[fn:2522]], so that, to employ an illustration, the relation of the <i>ophan</i> to the <i>’hayyâ</i> was as when one attaches a lifeless body to the fore or hind feet of an animal; whenever that animal is in movement, the wood or stone which one has attached to its limb will likewise be in movement. He states therefore: <i>When the</i> <small>’HAYYÔTH</small> <i>walked, the ophannîm walked beside them, and when the</i> <small>’HAYYÔTH</small> <i>rose from off the earth, the</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small> <i>likewise rose</i> (<i>v</i>. 19); and he states further: <i>And the</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small> <i>rose over against them</i> (<i>v</i>. 20), the cause of which he explains by stating: <i>for the spirit</i> (<small>ROUA’H</small>) <i>of the</i> ’<small>HAYYA</small> <i>was in the</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small>. He repeats this idea once more to confirm it and to render it more perspicuous, and states: <i>When they walked, these likewise walked, and when they stood still, these stood still</i>[[fn:2523]], <i>and when they rose from off the earth, the ophannîm likewise rose over against them, for the spirit of the ’hayyâ was in the</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small> (<i>v</i>. 21).",
    "root.text.Part 3..1.3.sub_1": "The order of these motions is therefore as follows: The <i>’hayyôth</i> moved in the direction towards which, according to the divine intention, they were destined to move; and by the motion of the <i>’hayyôth</i>, the <i>ophannîm</i> also were set in motion, following them because they were attached thereto, for the <i>ophannîm</i> did not move spontaneously towards the <i>’hayyôth</i>. This order, he describes by stating: <i>Whithersoever the intention was for them to go, thither they went, and the intention was</i> (<i>always</i>) <i>for them to go thither; and the</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small> <i>rose up over against them, for the spirit</i> (<small>ROUA’H</small>) <i>of the ’hayyâ was in the ophannîm</i> (<i>v</i>. 20). I have already acquainted thee with the paraphrase of Jonathan ben Uziel, who states: “Whithersoever the will (<span dir=\"rtl\">רעוא</span>) was for them to go etc..”",
    "root.text.Part 3..1.4": "After having completed the description of the <i>'hayyôth</i>, their figures and movements, and having spoken of the <i>ophannîm</i> situated below the <i>'hayyôth</i> to which they are attached and follow the movement, he approaches a third perception and returns to another description[[fn:2524]], namely, that which is above the <i>'hayyôth</i>. Above the four <i>'hayyôth</i>, he says, there was <i>a firmament</i> (2nd V. 22); upon this firmament there was the <i>ressemblance d'un trône</i>, and upon the throne <i>something resembling the aspect of a man</i> (2nd V. 26)[[fn:2525].",
    "root.text.Part 3..1.5": "Such is the sum of the description which he affords of the vision which he first beheld by the river of Chaboras.",
    "root.text.Part 3..2.0.sub_0": "Ezekiel, after having furnished the description of the <i>Merkabah</i>, as presented at the commencement of the book, had, upon a second occasion, the self-same perception, when, in a prophetic vision, he was transported to Jerusalem; and here he expounds to us matters which had not been explained the first time. He substitutes, for instance, for the word <i>’hayyôth</i> (animals) the word <i>kheroubîm</i> (cherubim), and thus apprises us that the <i>’hayyôth</i> of which he first spoke are likewise angels, namely the <i>cherubim</i>[[fn:2526]]. He says, therefore: <i>When the cherubim walked</i>, <i>the</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small> <i>walked beside them, and when the cherubim lifted their wings to mount up from the earth</i>, <i>the ophannîm also turned not from beside them</i> (Ezekiel, X, 16); and thus he confirms the nexus of the two movements, as one has already stated[[fn:2527]]. Thereafter he says: <i>This was the</i> <small>’HAYYA</small> <i>that I had seen beneath the God of Israel, by the river Chebar</i>, <i>and I knew that they were cherubim</i> (<i>v.</i> 20), so that, having again described the self-same figures and the self-same movements, he declares that the <i>’hayyôth</i> are the cherubim and that the cherubim are the <i>’hayyôth.</i> He sets forth, in this second description, yet another idea, namely, that the <i>ophannîm</i> are <i>galgallîm</i> (spheres)[[fn:2528]]: <i>The</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small>, he says, <i>I heard them called</i> <small>GALGAL</small> (<i>v.</i> 13). Then he expounds, concerning the <i>ophannîm</i>, a third idea, by saying of them: <i>for they went whithersoever the head was turned</i>, <i>they turned not as they went</i> (<i>v.</i> 11); he states clearly, therefore, that the constrained movement of the <i>ophannîm</i> occurred <i>whithersoever the head was turned</i>, that is to say, as he has explained: <i>wherever it was the intention</i> (divine) <i>that they should go</i>[[fn:2529]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..2.0.sub_1": "He subsequently adds a fourth notion concerning the <i>ophannîm</i>, stating: <i>the</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small> <i>were full of eyes round about</i>, <i>their four</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small> (<i>v.</i> 12), which he had not stated previously[[fn:2530]]. He further states in this second vision, speaking of the <i>ophannîm: their flesh</i>, <i>their body</i>, <i>their hands and their wings</i> (<i>ibid.</i>)<i>;</i> thus, after having at first spoken neither of <i>flesh</i> of the <i>ophannîm</i>, nor of <i>hands</i>, nor of <i>wings</i>, but simply of their bodies, he ultimately declares that they possessed flesh, hands, and wings[[fn:2531]], without, however, attributing to them any specific form[[fn:2532]]. Moreover, he sets forth in this second vision that each <i>ophan</i> bears relation to a cherub[[fn:2533]], and he states: <i>an ophan beside each cherub</i> (<i>v.</i> 9). Finally, he further sets forth here that the four <i>’hayyôth</i> constitute but a single entity, being attached one to another[[fn:2534]]: <i>This was</i>, he states, <i>the</i> <small>’HAYYA</small> <i>that I had seen beneath the God of Israel, by the river of Chaboras</i> (<i>v.</i> 20). It is thus that he designates the <i>ophannîm</i> by these words: <i>and behold there was an</i> <small>OPHAN</small> <i>upon the earth</i> (I, 15), even though there were, as he states[[fn:2535]], <i>four</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small> (I, 16; X, 9); and this was because they were attached one to another, and that <i>all four had one likeness</i> (I, 16)[[fn:2536]]. Such are the elucidations he adds, in this second vision, concerning the form of the <i>’hayyôth</i> and the <i>ophannîm.</i>",
    "root.text.Part 3..3.0.sub_0": "I must needs call thy attention to an opinion adopted by Jonathan ben Uziel. Having seen that it is clearly said[[fn:2537]] (Ezekiel, X, 13): <i>The</i> <small>OPHANNIM</small>, <i>I heard them called</i> <small>GALGAL</small> (wheel or sphere), he affirmed that the <i>ophannim</i> are the <i>heavens;</i> he translated therefore every time <i>ophan</i> by <span dir=\"rtl\">גַּלְגַּלָּא</span>, <i>the sphere</i>, and <small>OPHANNIM</small> by <span dir=\"rtl\">גַּלְגַּלַּיָּא</span>, <i>the spheres.</i> That which doubtless seemed unto him to confirm this interpretation is that Ezekiel (I, 16) says concerning the <i>ophannim</i> that they were like unto the colour of <i>tarshish</i>, a colour which, as is known, is attributed unto the heaven. But having found this passage: <i>I beheld the</i> <small>’HAYYÔTH</small>, <i>and behold, one</i> <small>OPHAN</small> <i>upon the earth</i>, which indicats indubitably that the <i>ophannim</i> are upon the earth, he saw therein a difficulty for this interpretation. Persisting nevertheless in his interpretation, he explains here the word <i>earth</i> as designating the <i>surface</i> of the heaven, which is a floor in respect of that which is above this surface, and he translats (the words) <i>one</i> <small>OPHAN</small> <i>upon the</i> <small>EARTH</small> by <span dir=\"rtl\">מִלְּרַע לְרוּם שְׁמַיָּא</span>, <i>beneath the height of the heaven;</i> it behoves to understand what his interpretation is (of the whole)[[fn:2538]]. It seems unto me that what provoked this interpretation is that Jonathan believed that <small>GALGAL</small> was a name which primarily designats the heaven. But behold, in mine opinion, the truth of the matter[[fn:2539]]: the word <small>GALGAL</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">גַּלְגַּל</span>) expresses the idea of <i>rolling;</i> for example: <i>And I will roll thee down</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וְגִלְגַּלְתִּיךָ</span>) <i>from the rocks</i> (Jeremiah, 51, 25); <i>and he rolled</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וַיָּגֶל</span>) <i>the stone</i> (Genesis, 29, 10). Wherefore it has been said: <i>And like a rolling thing</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וּכְגַלְגַּל</span>) <i>before the whirlwind</i> (Isaiah, 17, 13), because it is <i>rolled</i>, and for this cause also the skull is called <small>GULGOLS</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">גֻּלְגֹּלֶת</span>), because it is well-nigh round[[fn:2540]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..3.0.sub_1": "Now, as every sphere revolves swiftly, every spherical object has been designated <small>GALGAL</small>[[fn:2541]]; it is for this reason that the heavens have been denominated <small>GALGALLÎM</small>, inasmuch as they are <i>round</i>, that is to say, because they are <i>spherical</i>; and (figuratively) the doctors aver (concerning fate): «It is a <i>galgal</i> (a sphere), which revolves[[fn:2542]].» For this same reason is the pulley termed <small>GALGAL</small>[[fn:2543]]. Thus, when Ezekiel states: <i>The</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small>, <i>I heard them called</i> <small>GALGAL</small>, it is to apprise us of their (spherical) figure, for no other shape or form is ascribed to them; (and it is said) merely that they are <small>GALGALLÎM</small> (spheres)[[fn:2544]]. As for his assertion that they were like the <i>Tarschisch</i> (I, 16), he likewise explains it in the second description, saying of the <i>ophannîm: And the appearance of the</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small> <i>was as the colour of a stone of</i> T<small>ARSCHISCH</small> (X, 9), which Jonathan ben Uziel translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">כעין אבן טבא</span>, <i>resembling a precious stone.</i> Now, thou knowest that it is by this same expression that Onkelos renders the words: <i>like the work of the whiteness of sapphire</i> (Exodus, 24, 10), and he states: <span dir=\"rtl\">כעובד אבן טבא</span>, <i>like the work of a precious stone;</i> there is consequently no distinction between the words <i>as the colour of a stone of</i> <small>TARSCHISCH</small> and the words <i>as the work of the whiteness of sapphire</i>[[fn:2545]]. Thou must thoroughly apprehend this.",
    "root.text.Part 3..3.1": "Thou shalt not deem it amiss that I cite the interpretation of Jonathan ben Uziel, whilst I myself have given an interpretation quite contrary. For thou shalt find that many among the Doctors, and even among the (modern) commentators, contradict his interpretation in regard to certain expressions and numerous subjects treated by the prophets. And how should it be otherwise in these profound matters? Furthermore, I do not tell thee to prefer my interpretation[[fn:2546]]; endeavour to understand his interpretation by the observations I have made to thee, and to understand mine also. God alone knows which of the two interpretations has attained the truth[[fn:2547]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..4.0.sub_0": "That which further merits attention is that the prophet speaks <i>of the visions of God</i> (Ezekiel, I, 1); he does not say <i>a vision</i>, in the singular, but <i>visions</i>[[fn:2548]], because these were several perceptions of diverse kinds, namely three perceptions: that of the <i>ophannîm</i>, that of the <i>’hayyôth</i>, and that of the <i>man</i> who was above the <i>’hayyôth.</i> For each of these perceptions, he says: <i>I saw</i>[[fn:2549]]. He says for that of the <i>’hayyôth: I saw</i>, <i>and, behold, a tempestuous wind, etc.</i> (<i>ibid., v.</i> 4); for that of the <i>ophannîm</i>, he says: <i>I saw the</i> <i>’hayyôth, and behold there was an ophan upon the earth</i> (<i>v.</i> 15); finally, for the perception of the <i>man</i>[[fn:2550]], who was above the <i>’hayyôth</i> in rank, he says: <i>I saw as the colour of the</i> <small>HASCHMAL</small> <i>etc.</i>[[fn:2551]], <i>from the appearance of his loins, etc.</i> (<i>v.</i> 27). Except for these three instances, he does not repeat the expression <i>I saw</i> in the description of the <i>Mercabâ.</i> The Doctors of the <i>Mischnâ</i> have already expounded upon this subject, and it is they who have called my attention thereto. The first two perceptions, they say, that is to say, only that of the <i>’hayyôth</i> and of the <i>ophannîm</i>, it is permissible to teach; but the third perception, namely the <i>’haschmal</i> and that which appertains thereto, one must teach only the <i>first principles</i> thereof. Nevertheless, our holy Doctor[[fn:2552]] opines that it is all three perceptions together that are called <i>Ma’asé mercabâ</i>, and of which only the first principles should be taught. This is how they express themselves in this regard[[fn:2553]]: \"How far (may one teach)[[fn:2554]] the <i>ma’asé mercabâ?</i> Rabbi Meir says[[fn:2555]]: Unto the last <span dir=\"rtl\">וארא</span>, I saw (<i>v.</i> 27). Rabbi Isaac says: Unto the word <i>’haschmal:</i> from (the first) <span dir=\"rtl\">וארא</span>, I saw, (<i>v.</i> 4), unto the word <i>’haschmal</i>[[fn:2556]], it is permissible to teach (the disciple); thenceforth, one transmits to him the first principles.",
    "root.text.Part 3..4.0.sub_1": "«There be some who say: from (the first) <span dir=\"rtl\">וארא</span> unto the word <i>’haschmal</i>, one imparts the first elements; from thenceforth, if he be a sage discerning by his own understanding, he may (study it), otherwise, not[[fn:2557]].» It is therefore clear from their expressions that it concerns diverse perceptions, indicated by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וארא</span>, <i>I saw</i>, thrice repeated, that these are different degrees of perception, and that the last perception, of which it is said: <i>I saw as the colour of</i> <small>’HASCHMAL</small> (<i>v.</i> 27), — I mean the figure of the man divided in twain[[fn:2558]], of which it is said: <i>from the appearance of his loins and upward</i>, <i>and from the appearance of his loins and downward</i> (<i>ibid.</i>), — is the end of the perceptions and the highest among them. The Doctors are likewise divided (upon the question) whether it be permitted to teach it by any allusion whatsoever, I mean <i>by imparting the first elements</i>, or whether it be absolutely forbidden to indicate, even by the first elements, the teaching of this third perception, which may only be approached by <i>the sage discerning by his own understanding.</i> Likewise, the Doctors are divided, as thou seest, concerning the first two perceptions, those of the <i>’hayyôth</i> and of the <i>ophannîm</i>, (to wit) whether it be permitted to teach the meaning thereof in plain terms, or whether it be permitted to teach only the first elements thereof, by allusion and in an enigmatical manner.",
    "root.text.Part 3..4.1": "Thy attention must also be fixed upon the order of these three perceptions. If he has placed at the head the perception of the <i>’hayyôth</i>, it is because these take the first place by their nobility, by causality[[fn:2559]] [as he has said: <i>for the spirit of the</i> <small>’HAYYA</small> <i>was in the</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small> (i, 20)], and by other things besides[[fn:2560]]. After the <i>ophannîm</i> comes the third perception, which occupies a higher rank than the <i>’hayyôth</i>, as has been expounded. The cause (of this order) is that the two preceding perceptions[[fn:2561]] necessarily precede, in study, the third perception, which draws its arguments from them.",
    "root.text.Part 3..5.0": "Know thou that this grave and important subject which Ezekiel undertook to teach us in describing the <i>Mercabâ</i> (or the Celestial Chariot), the prophetic inspiration having impelled him to teach it to us, this same subject Isaiah also has taught us summarily, without deeming it necessary to enter into the same details. <i>I saw the Lord</i>, says he, <i>sitting upon a throne, high and lifted up</i>, <i>and his train filled the temple; above it stood the seraphim</i> (Isaiah, vi, 1, 2). Already have the Doctors expounded all this to us, and have made the same remark, saying that the perception which Ezekiel had was absolutely the same as that which Isaiah had. They have compared the matter to two men, the one a townsman and the other a countryman, who have beheld[[fn:2562]] the sovereign mounted on horseback: the townsman, knowing that the inhabitants of the city are acquainted with the manner in which the sovereign rids, dispenss with the description thereof, and says only: I have seen the sovereign; the other, desiring to describe it to the people of the country, who know nothing at all of his equipage, expounds to them in detail the manner in which he rids, the state of his troops[[fn:2563]], of his servants, and of those[[fn:2564]] who execute his commands. This is an observation of exceeding utility. Thus do they express themselves in <i>’Haghigâ</i>[[fn:2565]]: «All that Ezekiel saw, Isaiah also saw; but Isaiah resembls a townsman who has seen the king, whilst Ezekiel resembls a villager who has seen the king.» The author of this text perhaps understood it[[fn:2566]] as I have said at the beginning (of the chapter), to wit, that the contemporaries of Isaiah had no need that all these details should be set forth to them, and that it sufficed him to say to them: <i>I saw the Lord etc.</i>, whilst the people of the captivity had need of these details. But it may also be that the author intended to say that Isaiah was more perfect than Ezekiel, and that this perception, which disquieted Ezekiel and appeared to him so formidable, was to Isaiah a thing so familiar that it required not to be related in an extraordinary manner, being a thing well known to perfect men[[fn:2567]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..6.0": "One of the points which merit being examined is that the perception of the <i>Mercabah</i> is specified by the year, the month, and the day, and that the place is likewise specified.[[fn:2568]] It behoves us, therefore, to seek the reason thereof, and we must not believe that this is a matter which enfolds no idea.[[fn:2569]]",
    "root.text.Part 3..6.1": "That which it further behoves us to consider, and which is the key to the whole, are the words: <i>the heavens were opened</i> (Ezekiel, I, 1)[[fn:2570]]; for it is a thing which is frequently encountered in the prophets, I mean to employ figuratively the expressions <i>opening</i> and <i>opening the gates;</i> as for example: <i>Open ye the gates</i> (Isaiah, 26, 2), <i>And he opened the doors of heaven</i> (Ps., 78, 23), <i>Lift up your heads</i>, <i>ye everlasting doors</i> (Ps., 24, 9), <i>Open to me the gates of righteousness</i> (Ps., 118, 19), and many other passages.",
    "root.text.Part 3..6.2": "Another matter to which one's attention must be drawn is that, although this entire description be a prophetical vision — as it is stated: <i>And the hand of the LORD was upon him</i> (Ezekiel, I, 3)[[fn:2571]], — the manner of expression, in the diverse parts of this narrative, nevertheless presents a very significant difference. Indeed, in speaking of the <i>’hayyôth</i>, it is said <i>a</i> <small>LIKENESS</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דמות</span>) <i>of four ’hayyôth</i> (<i>ibid</i>., <i>v.</i> 5), and not simply <i>four ’hayyôth</i>; likewise, it states, <i>and above the ’hayya there was the</i> <small>LIKENESS</small> of <i>a firmament</i> (<i>v.</i> 22); in like manner, <i>there was as the appearance of a sapphire stone</i>, <i>the</i> <small>LIKENESS</small> <i>of a throne</i> (<i>v</i>. 26), and yet again, <i>something which</i> <small>RESEMBLED</small> <i>the appearance of a man</i> (<i>ibid.</i>]). In all these passages, therefore, the word <i>likeness</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דמות</span>) is employed; but, in speaking of the <i>ophannîm</i>, it does not state, \"the <i>likeness</i> of an <i>ophan</i>,\" nor \"the <i>likeness of</i><small> OPHANNÎM,\" but rather distinctly enunciates a form actually existing[[fn:2572]]. Let one not be misled by the words: <i>They four had one manner of</i> <small>LIKENESS</small> (<i>v</i>. 16); for this passage bears not the same construction and contains not the sense to which one has alluded[[fn:2573]]. In the latter vision, this idea is confirmed and elucidated[[fn:2574]]. Commencing with the firmament, the details of which are enumerated, it is spoken of in an absolute manner[[fn:2575]]. <i>I saw</i>, it states, <i>and behold</i>, <i>in the firmament that was above the cherubims, there appeared over them as a sapphire stone</i>, <i>as the appearance of the</i> <small>LIKENESS</small> <i>of a throne</i> (X, 1). Here, then, the firmament is spoken of in an absolute manner, and it is not stated, \"likeness of a firmament,\" as had been done when assigning its place <i>above the likeness of the</i> <small>’HAYYÔTH</small>[[fn:2576]]. But as for the throne, it is said that <i>the</i> <small>LIKENESS</small> <i>of a throne appeared above them</i> (the cherubim), which indicates that the firmament had first been perceived, and that subsequently there was manifested to him, above it, <i>the likeness of a throne</i>[[fn:2577]]. One must thoroughly apprehend this.",
    "root.text.Part 3..6.3": "What should further fix thy attention is that in the first vision he relats that the <i>’hayyoth</i> had at once wings and the hands of a man, whilst in this second vision, in which he setts forth that the <i>’hayyoth</i> were cherubim, he perceived at first only their wings, and thereafter, in his vision, there appeared unto them hands of a man: <i>And there appeared</i>, says he, <i>in the cherubims the form of a man’s hand under their wings</i> (Ezekiel, X, 8), where the word <i>form</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תבנית</span>) is taken in the same sense as the word <i>likeness</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דמות</span>). The place which he assigns unto them[[fn:2578]] is <i>under their wings</i>. Thou must well penetrate thyself with this[[fn:2579]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..6.4": "— Remark well also how, in speaking of the <i>ophannim</i>[[fn:2580]], he says clearly <i>over against them</i> (X, 19), albeit he has attributed unto them no form[[fn:2581]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..6.5": "He says further: <i>As the appearance of the bow that is in the cloud in the day of rain, so was the appearance of the brightness round about; such was the</i> <small>LIKENESS</small> <i>of the glory of the Lord</i> (I, 28). The matter of the aforementioned rainbow and its true essence are well known[[fn:2582]]. This is indeed most extraordinary in point of comparison and allegory, and it must indubitably be attributed to a prophetic power[[fn:2583]]. Thou must well consider this and let it sink into thy mind.",
    "root.text.Part 3..6.6": "That which also merits thy fixed attention is that the <i>human form</i> which[[fn:2584]] is <i>above the throne</i>, he divids into two parts, of which the upper part was <i>as the colour of ’haschmal</i>, and of which the lower part was <i>as the appearance of fire[[fn:2585]].</i> As touching the word <i>’haschmal</i>, they (the Sages) have explained it as a compound word containing two ideas, <small>’HASCH</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חש</span>) and <small>MAL</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מל</span>), that is to say, the idea of <i>haste</i>, signified by <i>’hasch</i>, and that of <i>cutting</i>, signified by <i>mal</i>[[fn:2586]]; the intent was to combine two different ideas, by making allusion, in an allegorical manner, unto two sides, the upper and the lower. They give us a second indication, saying that it is derived from (two words signifying) <i>to speak</i> and <i>to be silent</i>; «they (the <i>’haschmallîm</i>), say they, are sometimes silent and sometimes they speak» — whence they derive the idea of being silent (<i>’hasch</i>) from <span dir=\"rtl\">החשיתי מעולם</span>, <i>I have long time holden my peace</i> (Isaiah, xlii. 14), — making allusion to the two ideas by attributing to them a wordless speech. In saying: «sometimes they are silent, sometimes they speak,» they indubitably had in view nought but a created thing. Thou shalt therefore recognize that they have declared unto us thereby that this <i>human form above the throne</i>, which is divided in twain, is not the image of God, who is above all composition[[fn:2587]], but the image of a created thing. Thus the prophet himself says: <i>such was the appearance of the likeness of the glory of the Lord</i> (I, 28); but the <i>glory of the Lord</i> is not the Lord (Himself), as we have many times explained[[fn:2588]]. Consequently, all that which he presents allegorically in all these perceptions is nothing else than the <i>glory</i> of the Lord, that is to say, the <i>chariot</i> (<i>mercabâ</i>), and not <i>He who rids thereon</i>[[fn:2589]]; for God cannot be represented. Thou must well consider this and let it sink into thy mind.",
    "root.text.Part 3..6.7": "We have thus given thee also, in this chapter, some <i>first elements;</i> and if thou collectest[[fn:2590]] these elements, they shall form a useful whole for this subject. If thou readest attentively all that we have said in the chapters of this treatise even unto the present chapter, thou shalt understand the greater part of this subject, or even the subject in its entirety, with the exception of a small number of details and certain repetitions, the sense of which is obscure; but it may be that a more profound study shall shed light thereon, and that nothing thereof shall remain obscure.",
    "root.text.Part 3..6.8": "Hope not, after this chapter, to hear from me a single word upon this subject, neither clearly nor by allusion; for all that it was possible to say thereupon has been said, and I have therein even engaged myself too temerariously[[fn:2591]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..6.9": "Let us now proceed to other subjects among those which I hope to set forth in this treatise.",
    "root.text.Part 3..7.0": "All bodies that are born and perish are subject to corruption only on the side of their matter alone; on the side of the form, and considering the form in itself[[fn:2592]], they are not subject to corruption, but are permanent. You see, in fact, that all specific forms <i>are</i> eternal and permanent; corruption only affects the form accidentally, I mean insofar as it is joined to matter. It is in the true nature of matter that it is never separated from <i>privation;</i> this is why it does not conserve any individual form, and it does not cease to strip itself of one form in order to take on another[[fn:2593]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..7.1": "Solomon therefore, in his wisdom, has expressed himself in a most remarkable manner by comparing matter to an adulterous woman[[fn:2594]]; for matter, being unable in any wise to exist without form, is ever like unto a married woman, who is never loosed from the bonds of her husband and is never found free[[fn:2595]]. But the unfaithful woman, albeit married, seeks unceasingly another man to take him in the stead of her husband, and she employs all manner of wiles to entice him, until he obtains from her that which her husband was wont to obtain. And such also is the condition of matter; for, whatsoever form it may possess, that form does but prepare it for the reception of another form, and it (the matter) ceass not to move in order to put off the form it possesses and to obtain another. When it has obtained it, the case is yet the same.",
    "root.text.Part 3..7.2": "It is manifest that all destruction, corruption, or imperfection has no cause save matter. Thus, for example, the deformity of a man, his members fashioned contrary to nature, the weakening, the interruption, or the derangement of his (corporeal) functions—no matter whether all this be innate in him or be the effect of an accident—all this (I say) is an effect of his corruptible matter, and not of his form. Likewise, every animal is subject to death or to sickness only because of its matter, not because of its form. All the faults, all the sins of man, have their cause solely in his matter, and not in his form, whilst all his virtues proceed from his form. If, for instance, man perceivs his Creator, if he has the conception of intelligible things, if he knows how to moderate his passion and his anger, if he reflects upon what must be done[[fn:2596]] and what must be avoided, all this is the effect of his form. But the passion for eating, for drinking, for giving himself up to love, and likewise anger and all the moral vices of man, all these are the effect of his matter. Now, as it is clear that such is the case, since divine wisdom willed that there should be no matter without form, and that none of these forms[[fn:2597]] should exist without matter, and as this most noble human form [which, as we have already set forth, is <i>the image of God and His likeness</i>[[fn:2598]]] is necessarily bound to this earthly matter, turbid[[fn:2599]] and dark, which causs in man all that is imperfect and corruptible, it has been granted unto this human form to exercise a power over matter, to make itself master thereof, to govern[[fn:2600]] and to dominate it, so as to subjugate it, to repress its exigencies, and to render it perfect and equal as far as possible.",
    "root.text.Part 3..7.3.sub_0": "In this respect, men are divided into several classes. There be certain men who strive ever to choose that which is most noble, and to seek immortality, as their noble form demands, and who consequently think only of the conception of intelligible things, of holding a true opinion concerning all things, and of uniting themselves with the divine intellect[[fn:2601]], which is poured out upon them and from which this form draws its existence. Whenever the exigencies of matter[[fn:2602]] invite them to that which it has of foul and notoriously shameful, they experience pain and shame for having abandoned themselves thereto, blushing to have been thus tarnished, and they use all their efforts to diminish this shame and to preserve themselves therefrom in every way. It is as a man unto whom the sovereign, in his wrath, has commanded, in order to abase him, to transport dung from one place to another; this man will use all his efforts to hide himself at the moment of this abasement, and will endeavour to transport but little a short distance, so that he may not soil his hands and his garments, and so that no other may see him. Thus will free men act. But the slave will feel contentment therein and will not think that a great penalty has thereby been laid upon him; he will cast his whole body into the dung and the filth, will soil his face and his hands, and will carry (his burden) publicly, laughing, rejoicing, and clapping his hands. Such also are the (different) conditions of men. As we have said, there be men in whose eyes all the exigencies of matter are a shame, an ugliness, and imperfections under the necessity of which they must pass, and particularly the sense of touch, which, as Aristotle has said, is a disgrace unto us[[fn:2603]], and by virtue of which we desire to eat, to drink, and to give ourselves up to love. It behoovs us, therefore[[fn:2604]], to restrain these things as much as possible, to hide ourselves from them[[fn:2605]], to perform them with sorrow, not to make them a subject of conversation[[fn:2606]], and not to form assemblies for such things; nay more, man must dominate all these exigencies (of matter), reduce them as much as he can, and admit only that which is indispensable.",
    "root.text.Part 3..7.3.sub_1": "He must take for his aim that which is the (true) aim of man, in so far as he is man, to wit, the sole conception of intelligible things, of which the most important and noble object is to understand, as far as possible, God, His angels, and His other works. Such men cease not to be with God, and it is of them that it has been said: <i>Ye are all divine beings and sons of the Most High</i> (Ps. lxxxii. 6). This is what is required of man, I mean to say that this is his final cause. For the others, whom a veil separats from God[[fn:2607]], that is to say, for the multitude of the ignorant, it is the contrary: they abstain from all thought and all reflection upon intelligible things, and consider as their final end (to satisfy) this sense which is our greatest shame, I mean the sense of touch, so that their thoughts and reflections have for their sole object good cheer and love. Thus it has been plainly said of those wretched beings given over to eating, drinking, and love: <i>These also have erred through wine</i>, <i>they are out of the way through strong drink</i> (Isaiah xxviii. 7); <i>for all tables are full of vomit and filthiness, so that there is no place clean</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v</i>. 8); <i>and women rule over them</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, iii. 12), contrary to that which was in the divine intention[[fn:2608]] from the creation: <i>Thy desire</i> (<i>shall be</i>) <i>to thy husband</i>, <i>and he shall rule over thee</i> (Genesis iii. 16). The prophet also depicts their violent passion, saying: <i>Every one neighed after his neighbour's wife</i> (Jeremiah v. 8); <i>for they be all adulterers</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, ix. 1). Wherefore Solomon has devoted the whole book of Proverbs to warnings concerning unchastity and strong drink; for in these two vices are plunged those who are the objects of divine wrath and are far from God, of whom it has been said: <i>For they belong not unto the Lord</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, v. 10); <i>cast them out of my sight</i>, <i>and let them go forth</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xv. 1).",
    "root.text.Part 3..7.4": "As touching this passage: <i>A virtuous woman</i>, <i>who can find her, etc.</i> (Proverbs, xxxi. 10), the whole of this allegory is right manifest. If one possesses a matter good and fitting, which does not prevail over him nor disturb the equilibrium within him, verily this is a divine gift. In general, it is easy to govern a fitting matter, as we have aforetime observed[[fn:2609]]; yet, if it be not fitting, it is nonetheless not impossible to subdue it by dint of discipline. It is unto this that all the moral sentences of Solomon and others are applied[[fn:2610]]; likewise, the prescriptions of the Law and its prohibitions[[fn:2611]] have no other end than to reform all these exigencies of matter. It behoves him, therefore, who desirs to be a true man, and not a beast having the figure and lineaments of a man, to use all his efforts to diminish all the exigencies of matter concerning feasting, physical love, anger, and all the vices resulting from concupiscence and wrath; he must needs blush thereat and impose limits upon them[[fn:2612]]. As for that which is indispensable, such as eating and drinking, he must restrict himself unto that which is most useful and have in view solely the need of sustenance, but never enjoyment. He must also eschew making them an object of conversation or social gathering. Thou knowest how greatly our doctors held in aversion “feasts not consecrated to a religious act,”[[fn:2613]] and that virtuous men, such as Phinehas ben Jair, never ate at the table of any man[[fn:2614]]: our Holy Doctor[[fn:2615]] having desired that the latter should accept a meal at his house, he refused. It is the same with drinking as with food, both having the same end[[fn:2616]]. To form a gathering for the purpose of taking intoxicating drinks ought to be, in thine eyes, a thing more shameful than a gathering of naked persons who, displaying all their nakedness, should satisfy their natural needs in open day and in one and the same place. Indeed, the satisfaction of one's need is a necessary thing which man has no means of avoiding, whilst to become intoxicated is an act which the vicious man committs of his own free will. If it be deemed unseemly to uncover the shameful parts, this is but a matter of mere convention, which pertains not to the domain of reason[[fn:2617]]; but to corrupt the intelligence and the body is a thing reprobated[[fn:2618]] by reason; wherefore he who truly desirs to be a man must hold such a thing in aversion and not even draw the conversation thereto.",
    "root.text.Part 3..7.5": "As for physical love, I need add naught to that which I have delivered in the Commentary on <i>Aboth</i>[[fn:2619]], (in which I have shown) how greatly our wise and pure Law holds it in aversion, how it forbidds to speak thereof, or to make it, in any fashion or under any pretext whatsoever, a subject of conversation. Thou knowest that the doctors say that Elisha was called <i>holy</i>[[fn:2620]] because he abstained from thinking on this thing, so that no impure accident ever befell him; and thou knowest likewise what they say of Jacob, to wit, “that he was never defiled by an impure accident before begetting Reuben.”[[fn:2621]] These are traditions spread among our coreligionists, in order to cause them to acquire human manners. Thou art acquainted with that sentence of the doctors: “The thoughts of sin are worse than the sin itself,”[[fn:2622]] and I have thereupon an explanation right remarkable. It is that the man who committs a sin only sinns by reason of the accidents which attach unto his matter, as we have expounded—I mean to say, he sinns by his <i>animality;</i> but thought is one of the properties of man which belong unto his form. If, therefore, he directs his thought unto sin, he sinns by the most noble of his two parts. Now, he who, by injustice, makes an ignorant slave to labour is not so culpable as he who exacts service from a free and distinguished man; for this human form and all the properties which belong unto it[[fn:2623]] ought not to be employed save for that which is worthy of them, that is to say, for attaching oneself unto that which is most exalted, and not for descending to the lowest degree[[fn:2624]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..7.6": "Thou knowest also with what severity obscenity of language is forbidden amongst us[[fn:2625]], and thus it ought to be; for language is one of the properties of man and a boon granted unto him whereby he is distinguished[[fn:2626]], as it is said: <i>Who has made man's mouth?</i> (Exodus, iv. 11); and the prophet has said: <i>The Lord God has given me the tongue of the learned</i> (Isaiah, l. 4). This boon, then, which has been granted us for our perfection, for learning and teaching, must not be employed for the greatest vice and for the most shameful thing, in such wise that we should say all that the ignorant and impious Gentiles say in their poems and in their narratives[[fn:2627]], which are well-suited unto them, but not unto those regarding whom it has been said: <i>Ye shall be unto me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation</i> (Exodus, xix. 6). And if any man applies his thought and his speech unto a thing relative to this sense which is a shame unto us, in such wise as to think, more than is necessary, on drinking or on physical love, or to recite verses thereupon, he abuss the boon which has been granted him and uss it to revolt against the Benefactor and to disobey His commandments, so that he resembls those of whom it was said: <i>The silver and gold which I multiplied unto her, they prepared for Baal</i> (Hosea, ii. 10).",
    "root.text.Part 3..7.7": "I deem it also possible to assign the reason why our (Hebrew) tongue is called the <i>Holy Tongue;</i> for it must not be believed that this is on our part a vain word[[fn:2628]] or an error, but it is a verity. It is that, in this sacred tongue, there has been created no word to (designate) the sexual organ of men or of women, nor for the act itself which leads to generation, nor for the sperm, nor for the urine[[fn:2629]], nor for the excrements. For all these things, no primitive term has been created in the Hebrew tongue, but they are designated by words taken figuratively and by allusions. It was intended thereby to indicate that one ought not to speak of these things, nor consequently to give them names, but that they are, on the contrary, things upon which one must keep silence, and that, when there is necessity to speak of them, one must extricate oneself by the use of other expressions, even as, when there is necessity to do them, one must surround oneself with the greatest secrecy. As for the organ of man, it has been called <span dir=\"rtl\">גיד</span>, <i>nerve</i>[[fn:2630]], a name employed by similitude, as it is said: <i>Thy neck is</i> (<i>stiff</i>) <i>as an iron sinew</i> (Isaiah, xlviii. 4). It has been called also <span dir=\"ltr\">שָׁפְכָה</span>[[fn:2631]], <i>instrument for pouring</i> (effusorium), because of its action. For the organ of the woman, (one finds) <span dir=\"rtl\">קֳבָתָהּ</span>, <i>her belly</i> or <i>her stomach</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">קֵבָה</span> being the name of the stomach[[fn:2632]]. As for <span dir=\"rtl\">רֶחֶם</span> (employed for <i>vulva</i>), it is the name of the part of the entrails in which the foetus is formed. The name of the excrements is <span dir=\"rtl\">צוֹאָה</span>, a word derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">יצא</span>, <i>to go out;</i> that of urine is <span dir=\"rtl\">מימי רגלים</span>, <i>waters of the feet</i>[[fn:2633]], and that of sperm, <span dir=\"rtl\">שכבת זרע</span>, <i>layer of seed.</i> The act itself which leads to generation has no name, and one uss, to designate it, the verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">ישגכב</span>, <i>he lies</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">יבעל</span>, <i>he marries</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">יקח</span>, <i>he takes</i> (a woman), or <span dir=\"rtl\">יגלה ערוה</span>, <i>he uncovers the nakedness;</i> no other expression is employed. Let not thyself be led into error by the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ישגל</span>, which thou mightest take for the proper term for the act; it is not so, for <small>SHEGHAL</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שֵׁגָל</span>) is only the name of the young woman ready to give herself to love—for example: <i>the consort</i> (<small>SHEGHAL</small>) <i>is placed at thy right hand</i> (Ps. xlv. 10),—and the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ישגלנה</span> (Deuter. xxviii. 30), according to the <i>Kethib</i>[[fn:2634]], signifies: he shall take her as a woman for the thing in question.",
    "root.text.Part 3..7.8": "In the greater part of this chapter[[fn:2635]], we have departed from the purpose of this treatise, to discourse upon moral and religious matters; yet, albeit these things do not wholly enter into the plan of this work, we have been led thereto by a natural sequence of discourse.",
    "root.text.Part 3..8.0.sub_0": "Matter is a great veil[[fn:2636]] which prevents the perception of the <i>separate Intelligence</i>[[fn:2637]] as it is in its true essence, even were it the most noble and pure matter, I mean the matter of the spheres[[fn:2638]], and how much more so this dark and turbid matter which is ours. Wherefore, whenever our intelligence desirs to perceive the Deity, or any of the <i>Intelligences</i> (<i>separate</i>)[[fn:2639]], this great veil comes to interpose itself. To this allusion is made in all the books of the prophets, (when it is said) that a veil separats us from the Divinity, and that He is hidden from us by a cloud, by darkness, by a mist, or by clouds, and other such expressions, alluding to the fact that, by reason of matter, we are unable to perceive God. This is what was intended when it was said: <i>Clouds and darkness are round about him</i> (Ps. xcvii. 2), in which it is intimated that the obstacle lies in the opacity of our substance, and it is not meant that God is a body surrounded by mist and clouds which prevent Him from being seen, as the literal sense of the words of the allegory conveys. The same allegory is again repeated in the words: <i>He made darkness his secret place</i> (Ps. xviii. 12). Likewise, when (it is said that) God manifested Himself <i>in a thick cloud</i>, in <i>darkness</i>, the <i>cloud</i> and the <i>thick darkness</i>[[fn:2640]], one must likewise behold therein an indication of this idea; for all that is perceived in a prophetic vision is but an allegory to indicate a certain idea. Although this grandiose scene (of Sinai) was greater than any other prophetic vision and beyond all analogy[[fn:2641]], it is not, however, without indicating an idea, notably when God manifests Himself <i>in a thick cloud</i> (Exodus xix. 9); for it is intended to point out that the perception of His true being is impossible for us, by reason of the dark matter which surrounds our being, and not His; for He, the Most High, is not a body. It is known, moreover, and it is a thing well-known in our community, that the day of the <i>scene at Mount Sinai</i> was a day of cloud, of thick darkness, and of small rain, as it is said: <i>O Lord!",
    "root.text.Part 3..8.0.sub_1": "When thou didst go forth from Seir</i>, <i>when thou didst advance from the field of Edom</i>, <i>the earth trembled</i>, <i>the heavens poured forth and the clouds distilled water</i> (Judges, 5, 4). It is therefore possible that this is what was intended by the words <i>ténèbres</i>, <i>nuée</i> and <i>brouillard</i> (Deuteronomy, 4, 11), and not that the ténèbres surrounded the Divinity; for near God there are no ténèbres, but rather the resplendent and permanent light, the effusion of which illuminates all ténèbres[[fn:2643]], as it is said in the prophetic allegories: <i>And the earth was illuminated by His glory</i> (Ezekiel, 43, 2).",
    "root.text.Part 3..9.0": "The <i>Motecallemin</i>, as I have apprised you, conceive of <i>non-being</i> (or <i>privation</i>) solely as absolute non-existence; but all <i>privations of capacities</i>[[fn:2644]] they do not regard as <i>privations</i>, believing, on the contrary, that <i>privation</i> and <i>capacity</i>, such as blindness and sight, death and life, must be considered as two opposing entities[[fn:2645]]; for in this, according to them, the case is analogous to heat and cold[[fn:2646]]. Hence they declare, in an absolute sense, that non-being requires no agent, for only action itself necessarily necessitates an agent[[fn:2647]]; which is true from a certain standpoint[[fn:2648]]. Nevertheless, although they assert that non-being needs no agent, they declare, in accordance with their principle, that God renders blind and deaf, and brings to repose that which is in motion[[fn:2649]]; for these privations are, in their estimation, things which exist (positively).",
    "root.text.Part 3..9.1.sub_0": "It is now necessary that we should make known to thee what is our own opinion in this regard, according to that which philosophical speculation requirs. Thou knowest already that he who removs the impediment (to motion) is in a manner the mover[[fn:2650]]; if any one, for example, removs a pillar from beneath a beam, so that the latter falls by its natural weight, we say that he who has removed the pillar has moved the beam, as it has been said in the <i>Acroasis</i>[[fn:2651]]. In this manner also, we say of him who has caused a certain <i>faculty</i>[[fn:2652]] to cease, that he is the author of such <i>privation</i>, although privation be not an existing thing. Thus we say of him who has extinguished the lamp during the night, that he has produced darkness, and of him who has destroyed the sight, that he has produced blindness, albeit darkness and blindness be privations and require no agent. It is in accordance with this exposition that one must explain the words of Isaiah: <i>I form the light, and create darkness</i>, <i>I make peace, and create evil</i> (Isaiah xlv. 7); for darkness and evil are <i>privations.</i> Observe well that he says not: <span dir=\"rtl\">עושה חשך</span>, \"who <i>makes</i> darkness,\" nor <span dir=\"rtl\">עושה רע</span>, \"who <i>makes</i> evil\"; for these are not things of a positive existence, to which the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">עשה</span>, <i>to make;</i> can be applied; but he employs for these two things the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בורא</span>, <i>creating</i>, a word which in the Hebrew language relats to non-existence, as it is said: <i>In the beginning God created</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ברא</span>) <i>etc.</i>, which means: (he brought forth) <i>out of nothingness.</i> Whenever, therefore, non-existence is related to the action of an agent, it is in the manner which we have set forth. It is in this manner also that we must understand these words: <i>Who has made man's mouth? or who makes the dumb, or deaf</i>, <i>or the seeing, or the blind</i> (Exodus iv. 11)[[fn:2653]]?",
    "root.text.Part 3..9.1.sub_1": "But one may also interpret this passage in another manner, to wit: \"Who is he that has created man speaking, or who has created him deprived of speech?\" And it would then refer to the production of a matter unfit to receive such or such a faculty; for, if any one produces a matter incapable of receiving such (determined) faculty, it may be said of him that he has produced such a privation[[fn:2654]]; just as, if any one had been able to save a person from death, but had abstained and had not saved him, it might be said of him that he has killed him. Be that as it may, it is clear unto thee that, in no wise, can the action of an agent relate to a privation, and that <i>producing a privation</i> can only be said in the sense of an indirect action[[fn:2655]], as we have set forth. But that which an agent performs directly[[fn:2656]] is necessarily a thing of a positive existence; for, whatever the action may be, it can relate only to something existent[[fn:2657]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..9.2": "After this preamble, recall unto thyself that it has been demonstrated that evils are only such relatively to a certain thing[[fn:2658]], and that all evil, in relation to any being whatsoever, consists in the non-being of that thing or in the privation of one of its conditions of good. Wherefore it has been said, as a general thesis, that all evils are privations. In man, for instance, death is an evil, and it is his non-existence; likewise, his sickness, his poverty, and his ignorance are evils in relation to him, and all of these are privations of capacity. If thou shouldst pursue all the particular cases of this general thesis, thou shalt find that it never fails[[fn:2659]], save for him who knows not how to distinguish between <i>privation</i> and <i>capacity</i>, nor between two contraries, or who is totally ignorant of the nature of things, as, for example, he who knows not that health in general is a species of symmetry[[fn:2660]], that the latter belongs to the category of <i>relation</i> and that the absence of this proportion is in general sickness[[fn:2661]]. Death is, in relation to every living being, the privation of form; and likewise, for all that perishes among other beings, destruction is nothing else than the privation of its form.",
    "root.text.Part 3..9.3": "After these premises, it shall be acknowledged with certainty that it could in no wise be affirmed of God that He does evil directly; I mean to say, that God has primarily the intention of doing evil. This cannot be; all His actions, on the contrary, are pure good; for He does naught but <i>being</i>, and all being is good. All evils are privations, to which no action is attached, save in the manner we have set forth, (that is to say) inasmuch as God produces matter with the nature proper to it, to wit, to be always associated with privation, as is already known[[fn:2662]], the which renders it the cause of all corruption and of all evil. Wherefore everything, unto which God has not given this matter[[fn:2663]], perishes not and is subject to none of the (various) evils. Thus, the true action of God is good, for it is <i>being.</i> For this cause, the book which has illumined the darkness of the world has stated textually: <i>And God saw every thing that he had made</i>, <i>and, behold, it was very good</i> (Genesis, i, 31); for even the being of this inferior matter, which nevertheless by its nature is associated with privation, the source of death and of all evils, is notwithstanding a <i>good</i>, in view of the perpetuity of birth and the continual and successive reproduction of being[[fn:2664]]. Wherefore Rabbi Meir interprets the words: <i>And, behold, it was very good</i>, by these: <i>And death is a good</i>[[fn:2665]], according to the idea we have indicated.",
    "root.text.Part 3..9.4": "Keep well in mind what I have told thee in this chapter and endeavour to comprehend it. Then shalt thou find clear all that the prophets and the doctors have said (on this subject), to wit, that all good (alone) comes from the direct action of God. We read in <i>Bereshith Rabba:</i> «Nothing evil descends from above[[fn:2666]].»",
    "root.text.Part 3..10.0": "Those great[[fn:2667]] evils which men inflict upon one another, by reason of tendencies, passions, opinions, and beliefs, all flow from a <i>privation;</i> for they all result from ignorance, that is to say, from the privation of knowledge. Even as the blind man, by reason of the absence of sight, ceass not to stumble, to wound himself and to wound others also, when he has no one to lead him in the way, so do the parties among men, each[[fn:2668]] according to the measure of his ignorance, inflict upon themselves and upon others evils which weigh heavily upon the individuals of the (human) species[[fn:2669]]. Had they possessed knowledge, which is to the human form what the visual faculty is to the eye[[fn:2670]], they would be prevented from doing any harm to themselves and to others; for the knowledge of truth causs enmity and hatred to cease, and prevents men from doing evil to one another, as (the prophet) has announced, saying: <i>The wolf also shall dwell with the lamb, and the leopard shall lie down with the kid, etc.; and the cow and the bear shall feed, etc., and the sucking child shall play, etc.</i> (Isaiah, xi, 6-8). He thereafter indicats the cause thereof, saying that that which shall cause these enmities, these discords, and these tyrannies to cease, is that men shall then possess the true knowledge of God. He says therefore: <i>They shall not hurt nor destroy in all my holy mountain: for the earth shall be full of the knowledge of the Lord, as the waters cover the sea</i> (<i>ibid., v.</i> 9). Know this well.",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.0": "Ofttimes the vulgar are prone to imagine that there are, in the world, more evils than goods; so that all nations express this idea in many of their discourses[[fn:2671]] and of their poems, saying that it is rare to find good in this world[[fn:2672]], whilst its evils are numerous and perpetual. This error exists not only among the vulgar, but also with such a one as believs himself to possess some knowledge.",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.1": "There is a celebrated book of Al-Rāzī[[fn:2673]], which he has entitled <i>Al-Ilāhiyyāt</i> (Divine Things or Metaphysics), and in which, amidst a multitude of follies and absurdities, he has propounded this thesis: that evil in the world is more frequent than good, and that if the well-being of man and the pleasures which this well-being affords him be compared with the pains, the grievous sufferings, the infirmities, the paralyses[[fn:2674]], the adversities, the sorrows, and the calamities which befall him, one shall find that his existence—I mean that of man—is a punishment and a great evil inflicted upon him. He seeks to confirm this opinion by reviewing all these misfortunes, that he may combat all that the friends of truth believe concerning the benevolence of God and His manifest goodness[[fn:2675]], affirming that He is absolute Good, and that all that proceeds from Him is indubitably pure good.",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.2.sub_0": "The cause of all this error is that this ignorant man, as well as his fellows among the multitude, judged the universe only by the human individual alone. Every ignorant person imagins that the whole universe exists only for his own person, as if there were no other being but himself alone. If, therefore, that which befalls him be contrary to his desires, he decids that the whole of existence is evil; but if man were to consider and conceive the universe, and if he knew how small a place he occupies therein, the truth would become clear and manifest unto him. Indeed, this notable folly which men proclaim touching the multitude of evils alleged to be in the universe, they profess it neither with regard to the angels, nor with regard to the spheres and the stars, nor with regard to the elements and the minerals or plants composed thereof, nor with regard to the various species of animals; but their thoughts are directed only toward certain individuals of the human species. If someone, for example, having nourished himself with unwholesome food, becoms leprous, they marvel that he should be smitten with this great misfortune, and ask how such evil exists; likewise, they marvel if someone, by dint of debauchery, has become blind, and they find it cruel that this man should be stricken with blindness. I might cite yet other examples. But the true manner of viewing the matter is that all individuals of the human species who exist, and a fortiori those of other species of animals, are a thing of no value in relation to the immutable whole of the universe[[fn:2676]], as it is clearly said: <i>Man is like unto vanity, &c.</i> (Ps. cxliv. 4); <i>the mortal who is but a worm, and the son of man who is but a maggot</i> (Job xxv. 6); <i>how much more them that dwell in houses of clay, &c.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, iv. 19); <i>Behold, the nations are as a drop</i> (<i>that falls</i>) <i>of a bucket, &c</i>. (Isaiah xl. 15); and many other passages from the prophetic books speak of this important subject, which is of great utility[[fn:2677]] in making known to man his little worth. He must not err and believe that the universe exists only for his person; on the contrary, according to us, the universe exists by reason of the will of its Creator, and the human species is a very small thing therein in relation to the superior world, I mean, to the spheres and the stars.",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.2.sub_1": "As for the angels, there exists no true relation between them and man[[fn:2678]]. Man is but the most noble among the beings subject to contingency[[fn:2679]], that is to say, among those of our lower world; I mean that he is more noble than all that has been composed of the elements. Withal, his existence is a great good for him and a benefit from God, by reason of the properties and perfections which He has granted him. The greater part of the evils which smite individuals proceed from themselves, I mean from human <i>individuals</i> who are imperfect. It is our own vices that give us cause to lament and to cry for help. If we suffer, it is through evils which we inflict upon ourselves of our own free will, yet which we attribute to God;—far be such a thing from Him!—This is what God has declared in His book, saying: <i>Is destruction His</i> (that it should be attributed to Him)? <i>No, to His children, to their own faults</i> (Deut. xxxii. 5)[[fn:2680]]. Solomon has expressed the same idea, saying: <i>The foolishness of man perverts his way, and his heart fretts against the Lord</i> (Proverbs xix. 3).",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.3": "To explain myself more clearly, I shall say that all the evils which smite man may be reduced to one of the three following kinds:",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.4.sub_0": "The evils of the <i>first kind</i> are those which happen to man by the very nature of that which is subject to <i>genesis</i> and <i>corruption</i>, I mean because he is a material being. It is because of this that certain individuals are afflicted with infirmities and paralyses, which are innate[[fn:2681]] in them or which befall them through alterations occurring in the elements, such as the corruption of the air[[fn:2682]], the fires of heaven[[fn:2683]], the subsidences of the soil[[fn:2684]]. As we have already set forth, the divine wisdom willed that <i>genesis</i> should take place only as a consequence of <i>corruption</i>[[fn:2685]]; and, without <i>individual</i> corruption, there would be no permanent <i>specific</i> genesis[[fn:2686]]. It is clear thereby that all is pure goodness and benevolence, and that naught proceeds from God but good. He who, while being of flesh and bone, desirs at the same time to be shielded from every impression and not to be subject to any of the accidents of matter, desirs nothing else, though he perceive it not, than to unite two contraries together; for he desirs at once to be subject to impressions and not to be so. Indeed, were there anything that was not susceptible to impression, it would not be the product of <i>genesis</i>, and that which exists of it would be <i>one</i> (single) individual, and not individuals of a species[[fn:2687]]. How true is that which Galen has said in the third book of <i>The Uses of the Parts</i>[[fn:2688]]! \"Give not thyself up to this vain illusion,\" says he, \"that from the menstrual blood and the semen there could be formed an animal that dies not, or suffers not, or which has a perpetual motion, or which is resplendent like the sun.\" This passage from Galen calls attention to a particular case of a general proposition, which is this: \"All that can be formed from any matter whatsoever is formed in the most perfect manner possible that this specific matter allows, and the imperfection wherewith the individuals of the species are tainted is in proportion to the imperfection of the (particular) matter of the individual.\" Now, the most eminently perfect thing that can be formed from blood and semen is the human species, with its well-known nature of being a living, rational, and mortal being; consequently, this latter kind of evil must necessarily exist[[fn:2689]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.4.sub_1": "Notwithstanding this, thou shalt find that the evils of this kind which befall men are very few in number and happen but rarely[[fn:2690]]. Verily, thou shalt find cities which for thousands of years have been neither submerged nor consumed by fire; likewise, thousands of men are born perfectly sound, and a man born infirm is an anomaly[[fn:2691]], or at least—should any one cavil at the word <i>anomaly</i>—he is a very rare exception, and forms not the hundredth nor even the thousandth part of those who are born in a sound state.",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.5": "The evils of the <i>second species</i> are those which men inflict upon one another, as, for example, the tyranny which they exercise over each other. These evils are more numerous than those of the first species, and the causes thereof are manifold and well known; they likewise proceed from ourselves, yet he who suffers therefrom[[fn:2692]] is powerless against them. Nevertheless, in no city whatsoever in the whole world are evils of this sort widespread or frequent[[fn:2693]] among individuals; on the contrary, they are encountered but rarely, as when one individual surpriss another by night to slay him or to rob him. It is only in great wars that evils of this kind embrace a multitude of people; but even this is not frequent in relation to the whole earth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.6.sub_0": "The evils of the <i>third species</i> are those which happen to each of us by his own act, and this occurrs frequently[[fn:2694]]. These evils are far more numerous than those of the second species. All men lament evils of this kind, and very few will be found who do not render themselves guilty thereof toward themselves. They who are smitten by them verily deserve to be blamed[[fn:2695]], and to them may be addressed these words (of the prophet): <i>This has been by your means</i> (Malachi i. 9). Concerning this matter it has been said: <i>He that does it destroys his own soul</i> (Proverbs vi. 32), and it is likewise of the evils of this species that Solomon said: <i>The foolishness of man perverts his way</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xix. 3). Elsewhere he has clearly stated, speaking of evils of this kind, that man brings them upon himself: <i>lo, this only have I found, that God has made man upright; but they have sought out many devices</i> (<i>guilty</i>) (Ecclesiastes vii. 29)[[fn:2696]]; it is these devices that have brought these evils upon them. It is also in respect of this species (of evils) that it has been said: <i>Although affliction comes not forth of the dust, neither does trouble spring out of the ground</i> (Job v. 6). Then, it is declared immediately after that it is man who gives birth to this sort of evils, and it is said: <i>Yet man is born unto trouble</i> (<i>ibid., ver.</i> 7)[[fn:2697]].—This species (of evils) follows in the train of all vices; I speak especially of the passion for gluttony, wine-bibbing, and carnal love, when these things are enjoyed to excess or without order, or when the food is of an evil quality; for therein lies the cause of all the pernicious maladies of the body and of the soul[[fn:2698]]. As for the maladies of the body, it is evident. The maladies of the soul (which result) from this evil regimen have two causes. The first is that the alteration which the body undergoes necessarily influencs the soul, inasmuch as the latter is a bodily faculty[[fn:2699]], and it is in this sense that it has been said that the character of the soul follows the temperament of the body[[fn:2700]]. The second cause is that the soul becoms familiarized with things unnecessary and accustoms itself thereto, so that it acquirs the habit[[fn:2701]] of desiring what is necessary neither for the preservation of the individual nor for that of the species.",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.6.sub_1": "Now, this desire is a thing which has no limit; for, whilst necessary things are all restricted and bounded, the superfluous, on the contrary, is unlimited. Dost thou desire, for example, to possess vessels of silver, it is more beautiful to have them of gold; some there are even who have them of crystal, and perhaps they are also made of emerald and ruby, in so far as these materials are attainable[[fn:2702]]. Thus, every man who is ignorant and of a false reasoning is constantly in pain and in sorrow because he cannot indulge in luxury as another has done; and often he casts himself into great perils, such as voyaging by sea and the service of kings, aiming to procure this useless luxury. But when, having entered upon these paths, he is smitten by misfortunes, he complains of the divine decree and of destiny, murmurs against fortune, and marvells at its lack of justice, because it has not helped him to obtain great riches, by means of which he might procure wine in abundance to make himself drunken and a great number of concubines decked with gold and precious stones to incite him to the enjoyment of love beyond his capacity, as if the pleasure of this base man were alone the end of the universe. To such a point has the error of the vulgar reached; they have gone so far as to accuse the Creator of impotence for having created the universe with this nature which, as they imagine, necessarily produces these evils, because it helps not every vicious man to satiate his ignoble passion and to bring his perverse soul to the term of its desires, which, as we have set forth, are without end. But virtuous and instructed men know the wisdom that presids over the universe and understand it, as David declared, saying: <i>All the paths of the Lord are mercy and truth unto such as keep his covenant and his testimonies</i> (Ps. xxv. 10), which means that they who have regard for the nature of existence and for the precepts of the Law, and who know the aim thereof, understand the mercy and the truth which preside over all; wherefore they propose for their aim the thing for which they were destined as men, that is to say, <i>perception</i>. Constrained by the needs of the body, they seek what is necessary for it: <i>bread to eat, and raiment to put on</i>[[fn:2703]], without aiming at the superfluous. If one limits oneself to the necessary, the matter is very easy and is obtained with little toil.",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.6.sub_2": "Whenever thou perceivest therein difficulty and toil, it is because, in striving to seek what is not necessary, it becoms difficult for us to find even the necessary; for, in proportion as our desires are directed too much toward the superfluous, the matter becoms more painful; we expend our strength and our substance[[fn:2704]] for that which is not necessary, and we find even the necessary no more.",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.7": "It behoves us to consider the conditions in which we stand in regard to that which is to be found[[fn:2705]]. Verily, according as a thing is more necessary to the animal, it is found more frequently and at a more trifling price; and in proportion as a thing is less necessary, it is found less frequently and is exceeding costly[[fn:2706]]. That which, for instance, is necessary to man, is air, water, and nourishment. Nevertheless, that which is most necessary of all is air, for one cannot be deprived thereof for a single moment without perishing, whilst one may dispense with water for a day or two[[fn:2707]]; wherefore air is indubitably that which is easiest to find and most gratuitous. But water is of a more urgent necessity than is nourishment; for certain men, provided they drink[[fn:2708]], can remain four or five days without food. Thus it is that water is found, in every city, more abundantly and at a more trifling price than nourishment. The same holds true of diverse aliments; those which are more necessary are found more easily and at a lower cost, in the same place, than those which are less necessary. But as for musk, amber, rubies, and emeralds, I do not believe that a man of sound sense can deem them very necessary to man, unless it be for a medical treatment[[fn:2709]]; and even then, they may be replaced, as well as other similar things, by several kinds of herbs and earths.",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.8": "It is herein that the bounty and the goodness[[fn:2710]] which God exerciss even toward this frail creature are manifest. But that which above all is most evident is His signal justice and the equality which He has established among animals[[fn:2711]]. In effect, the laws of nature[[fn:2712]] do not permit that an individual of any species of animals[[fn:2713]] should be distinguished from other individuals of the same species by a faculty peculiar to himself, or by a limb which he might possess in addition. On the contrary, all physical, animal (or psychic), and vital faculties[[fn:2714]], as well as the members possessed by one individual, are <i>essentially</i> the same as those possessed by another; and if there exists anywhere a defect, it is <i>accidentally</i> and by reason of some occurrence which is not according to nature, a thing which is rare, as we have already set forth. Between individuals who follow the course of nature, there is absolutely no difference of more or less, save that which results from the diverse disposition of (individual) matters[[fn:2715]]; and this is a necessary consequence of the nature proper to the matter of the <i>species</i>, a thing which concerns not one individual more than another[[fn:2716]]. But, that one should possess many bags of musk[[fn:2717]] and garments adorned with gold, whilst another lacks these superfluities of life, there is therein neither injustice nor violence. He who has obtained this superfluity has gained thereby no prerogative in his substance, and possesses but a lying illusion or a plaything; and he who lacks the superfluities of life is not necessarily diminished thereby[[fn:2718]]: <i>He that gathered much had nothing over, and he that gathered little had no lack; they gathered every man according to his eating</i> (Exodus, 16, 18). This is what occurrs most frequently in all times and in all places, and we must not have regard to the exception, as we have already set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..11.9": "Thou shalt recognize therefore, by the two preceding reflections, the goodness which God exerciss toward His creatures, (on the one hand) in making them find what is necessary according to its relative importance[[fn:2719]], and (on the other hand) in establishing equality, from their very creation, between the individuals of the same species. It is from this true point of view that the prince of the learned[[fn:2720]] has said: <i>For all His ways are judgment</i> (Deut. 32, 4), and that David has said: <i>All the paths of the Lord are mercy and truth</i> (Ps. 25, 10), as we have already set forth. David further said expressly: <i>The Lord is good to all: and His tender mercies are over all His works</i> (Ps. 145, 9); for the great good in the absolute sense is that He has caused us to exist, and in creating the <i>governing faculty</i>[[fn:2721]] in the animal, He has shewn it His mercy, as we have already set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.0": "Ofttimes the minds[[fn:2722]] of perfect men have been perplexed by the question of knowing what is the final end of this universe; but I shall show that, according to all systems, it is an idle question[[fn:2723]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.1": "Whenever, I say, an agent acts with intention, the thing done by him must necessarily have a final end for which he has made it; this is clear from the point of view of philosophical speculation and needs not to be demonstrated. Likewise, it is clear that the thing thus made with intention was brought into being after not having existed[[fn:2724]]. Finally, what is equally clear and admitted by common accord, is that the necessary Being, who has never been non-existent and who shall never be so, has no need of an efficient cause, as we have already set forth[[fn:2725]]. Now, as He has not been <i>made</i>, one cannot seek the final end thereof[[fn:2726]]. Wherefore one cannot ask \"what is the final end of the existence of the Creator?\" for He is not a created thing. It is therefore clear, according to these propositions, that one cannot seek a final end save for a thing <i>brought into being</i>, which has been made with intention by a being endowed with intelligence[[fn:2727]]; I mean to say that, for all that has taken its origin in an intelligence, one must necessarily seek what is the final cause thereof; but when it is not a thing <i>brought into being</i>, one cannot seek for it a final cause, as we have said.",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.2": "After this exposition, thou shalt understand that one cannot seek a final purpose for the whole of the universe, neither according to our own view, who profess the novelty of the world, nor according to the opinion of Aristotle, who believs it to be eternal. Indeed, according to his opinion concerning the eternity of the world, one cannot seek an ultimate final cause for any of the (principal) parts of the universe; for, according to this opinion, it is not permitted to ask what is the final purpose of the existence of the heavens, nor why they have such measure and such number, nor why the prime matter is of such a nature, nor what is the final purpose of such a species of animals or of plants; all things emanating, according to him, from an eternal necessity, for ever immutable[[fn:2728]]. Albeit physical science seeks the final purpose of every being in nature, this is not the <i>ultimate end</i> of which we speak in this chapter. Indeed, it has been set forth in physical science that every being in nature must necessarily have a certain end[[fn:2729]], but that this final cause, which is the most noble of the four causes, is hidden in most of the <i>species</i>[[fn:2730]]. Aristotle constantly declars that nature does nothing in vain[[fn:2731]]; which is to say that every work of nature has necessarily a certain end. He says expressly that plants were created for the sake of animals[[fn:2732]]. Likewise, he has set forth, concerning certain other things, that they exist for the sake of one another, which particularly applies to the members of animals[[fn:2733]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.3": "Know that the existence of this end in physical things has necessarily led the philosophers to admit a principle other than nature, which Aristotle calls the <i>intelligent</i> or <i>divine principle</i>, and it is this which does such a thing for the sake of another[[fn:2734]]. It must also be known that in the eyes of the impartial man, one of the strongest proofs for the novelty of the world is that it has been demonstrated, concerning physical things, that each of them has a certain end, and that such a thing exists for the sake of another, which proves the <i>design</i> of a being acting with intention; but one cannot conceive of <i>design</i> without it being a matter of a new production[[fn:2735]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.4": "I return now to the subject of this chapter, which treats of the final cause. I say then: Aristotle has set forth that, in physical things, the efficient, the form, and the end are but one and the same thing, I mean to say they are specifically <i>one</i>. Indeed, it is the form of Zeid, for example, which makes the form of the individual ’Amr his son; what it does is to give a form of its species to the matter of ’Amr, and the final purpose of ’Amr is to have a human form. It is the same, according to him, with each of the individuals of the physical species which have need to propagate[[fn:2736]]; for the three causes, in these, form but one. But all this is naught but the <i>first</i> end[[fn:2737]]. Nevertheless, all naturalists think that there necessarily exists, for every species, an <i>ultimate end</i>, albeit it be passing difficult to know it; and with greater reason (to know) the final cause of the entire universe. That which seems to emerge from the words of Aristotle is that, according to him, the ultimate end of these <i>species</i> consists in the permanence of generation and corruption, which is indispensable[[fn:2738]] to perpetuate <i>becoming</i> in this (inferior) matter, from which no permanent individual can come forth, and from which nevertheless must be born, in the last resort, all that is possible to be born therefrom, I mean to say the most perfect thing possible; for the ultimate goal is to arrive at perfection[[fn:2739]]. It is clear that the most perfect thing possible which can be born of this matter is man, and that he is the last and most perfect of these composite beings. If therefore one were to say that all sublunary beings exist because of him, it would be true from this point of view, I mean to say (granting) that the movement of variable things takes place in view of (perpetual) <i>generation</i>, so as to arrive at the most perfect degree. But one could not ask of Aristotle, given his doctrine of the eternity (of the world), what is the final cause of the existence of man. Indeed, the first end of every individual <i>born</i> being, according to him, the perfection of the specific form, every individual, in whom the actions resulting from this form are perfect, has perfectly and completely attained his final purpose; and the ultimate end of the species is to perpetuate this form by means of a continual succession of generations and corruptions, so that there always occurrs a new birth having for its goal[[fn:2740]] a greater perfection. It is therefore clear that, according to the doctrine of <i>eternity</i>, there is no occasion to seek the ultimate end of the whole of the universe.",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.5.sub_0": "But there be some who think that, according to our own opinion, who profess that the whole universe was created after not having existed, it is fitting to pose this question, that is to say, to seek the final cause of all this universe. Consequently, it is believed that the entire universe has for its end only the existence of the human species destined to worship God, and that all that has been made was made only for her, so that the celestial spheres themselves would accomplish their circular motion only to be useful unto her and to produce all that she requirs[[fn:2741]]. Certain passages of the prophetic books, taken in their literal sense, serve as a great support to this opinion: <i>He formed it</i> (<i>the earth</i>) <i>to be inhabited</i> (Isaiah, 45, 18); <i>If it were not for my covenant</i> (<i>subsisting</i>) <i>day and night, I should not have appointed the laws of heaven and earth</i> (Jeremiah, 33, 25)[[fn:2742]]; <i>He has stretched them out as a tent to dwell in</i> (Isaiah, 40, 22). Now, if the celestial spheres exist for the sake of man, with greater reason do all[[fn:2743]] the species of animals and plants. But, in examining this opinion as befitts men of understanding, one shall recognize how much it is subject to doubt. Indeed, one might ask of him who professes this opinion: Since the final purpose is the existence of man[[fn:2744]], could the Creator have produced man without all these preparations, or could he only be created following them? If one were to answer that the thing was possible[[fn:2745]] and that God, for example, could have produced man without there being a heaven, one might ask: of what use unto Him[[fn:2746]] were all those things which were not themselves the final purpose, and (which were created) only for the sake of a thing which could exist without them? But, even admitting that the whole was born for the sake of man, and that the final purpose of man, as has been said, is to worship God, one might yet ask: To what <i>end</i> must God be worshipped, since His perfection cannot increase, even should all creatures worship Him and perceive Him in the most perfect manner, and that even though there should exist absolutely nothing besides Him, He would not thereby be tainted with imperfection?",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.5.sub_1": "For if one should answer that it concerns not His own perfection, but ours [for it is our perfection which forms our greatest good], one would yet come to pose this question: To what end must we exist with this perfection? — This question of the final cause will therefore necessarily lead us to (answer in the last resort): “God has thus willed it[[fn:2747]],” or “His wisdom has thus required it”; and that is the truth. Thou findest, indeed, that the Sages of Israel, in their liturgical rituals, have expressed themselves thus: “Thou hast distinguished man from the beginning (of creation) and Thou hast destined him to present himself before Thee. Who could ask Thee for what purpose Thou actest? If man be righteous, what profit accrus unto Thee therefrom[[fn:2748]]?” They have therefore declared thereby that there is no other final cause of the universe than the Will alone. — But, if it be so[[fn:2749]], then the belief in the novelty of the world forcing us to admit that this universe could have been created differently as concerns its (respective) causes and effects[[fn:2750]], there would ensue[[fn:2751]] this absurdity that all beings, except man, would exist without any purpose, since the only end that was in view, and which is man, could exist without all these beings.",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.6": "Wherefore the only true opinion in my judgment, which is at once consonant with religious beliefs and in agreement with speculative opinions, is this: That it must by no means be believed that all beings exist for the sake of man; but on the contrary, all other beings likewise were created for their own sakes, and not for the sake of aught else. Thus, even according to our opinion which admitts the creation of the world, one cannot seek the final cause of all <i>species</i> of beings; for we maintain that it is by His <i>Will</i> that God has created all parts of the universe, and that some have their end in themselves, whilst others exist for the sake of another thing which has its end in itself. Even as He willed that the human species should exist, so likewise He willed that the celestial spheres and their stars should exist, and so again He willed that the angels[[fn:2752]] should exist. In every being, He has intended that being itself; and whenever the existence of a thing was impossible without being preceded by another thing, He produced the latter first, as for example, sensibility which preceds reason. This opinion has likewise been expressed in the prophetic books, as for example: <i>the Lord has made all things for Himself</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">למענהו</span> (Proverbs, XVI, 4), where the pronoun <i>Himself</i> may refer to the object (<i>all things</i>). If, however, the suffix <span dir=\"rtl\">הו</span> referred to the subject (<i>the Lord</i>), the sense (of <span dir=\"rtl\">למענהו</span>) would be: because of the essence of God, that is to say, because of His <i>Will</i>, which is His essence, as has been set forth in this treatise[[fn:2753]]. We have explained that the essence of God is also called <i>glory</i>, as in this passage: <i>I beseech Thee, show me Thy glory</i> (Exodus, xxxiii, 18)[[fn:2754]]. According to this, the passage \"<i>the Lord has made all things for Himself</i> (or <i>because of His essence</i>)\" would resemble this other passage: <i>Even every one that is called by My name: for </i> <small>MY GLORY</small> <i>I have created him, I have formed him; yea, I have made him</i> (Isaiah, xliii, 7); which signifies that, all things whose creation is attributed to Me[[fn:2755]], I have made only because of My Will alone. The words <i>I have formed him; yea, I have made him</i> refer to that which I have set forth, (to wit) that there are beings whose existence is possible only after that of another thing; He would therefore say: I have created this first thing which must necessarily precede, as for example matter (which must precede) every material being; then I have made, <i>within</i> this prior thing, or <i>after</i> it, all that I intended to bring into existence, yet without there being aught else (to guide Me) but the simple Will.",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.7.sub_0": "If thou examinest the book which is an infallible guide for those who desire to be guided, and which for this reason has been called <i>Torah</i>[[fn:2756]], thou shalt recognize therein, from the beginning of the account of creation until the end, the idea which we have in view. For it is in no wise declared therein that any thing whatsoever[[fn:2757]] was made for the sake of another thing; but it is said of each of the parts of the universe that God having produced it, its existence answered the purpose. Such is the sense of these words: <i>And God saw that it was good</i>; for thou knowest what we have set forth concerning this maxim: \"The Scripture speaks according to the language of men[[fn:2758]],\" and <span dir=\"rtl\">טוב</span>, <i>good</i>, is an expression whereby we designate that which is conformable to our purpose[[fn:2759]]. Of the whole it is said: <i>And God saw every thing that He had made, and, behold, it was</i> <small>VERY GOOD</small> (Genesis, i, 31); for everything that was <i>brought forth</i> was so in accordance with the purpose (which was intended), without any defect therein[[fn:2760]], and this is what is expressed by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מאד</span>, <i>very</i>. Verily, it may be that a thing is <i>good</i> and answers momentarily to our purpose, but that afterwards the purpose fails. We are therefore taught that all these works answered the intention and the purpose of the Creator, and ceased not to remain conformable to that which He had intended. — Suffer not thyself to be led into error by what is said of the stars: <i>to give light upon the earth, and to rule over the day and over the night</i> (Genesis, i, 17 and 18), and believe not that this signifies: in order that they (the stars) might accomplish this action[[fn:2761]]; on the contrary, it was intended only to make known their nature such as it pleased the Creator to give them, I mean, to be luminous and to govern (this lower world). Even so it is said in speaking of man: <i>And have dominion over the fish of the sea, etc.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v</i>. 28), which does not mean that man was created for that purpose, but merely indicats the nature wherewith God has impressed him. If it be said of the plants that God gave them to men and to other animals (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v</i>. 29), this is what Aristotle and others have also declared[[fn:2762]], and it is manifest that plants were created only for the sake of animals, which cannot dispense with food.",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.7.sub_1": "But it is not so with the stars, I mean that they do not exist for our sake, and in order that benefits may come to us therefrom; for, as we have set forth, the words <i>to give light, to rule</i>, do but state the utility which results therefrom and which spreads over this lower world, in accordance with what I have already explained to thee of the nature of the benefits which are continually communicated from one thing to another[[fn:2763]]. If this good which perpetually occurrs be considered in relation to the thing to which it happens, it might seem that this thing, being the object of the benefit, is the final cause of that which has communicated to it its goodness and excellence. Even so any citizen might imagine that the final end of the sovereign is to preserve his house from thieves during the night, which is true up to a certain point; for, his house being guarded and this benefit coming to him from the sovereign, it might seem that the final end of the sovereign is to guard the house of that man. It is in this sense that we must explain every text whose literal meaning would indicate that an exalted thing was made for the sake of that which is inferior to it, which signifies only that this latter thing is a necessary consequence of the nature of the other.",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.8": "Thus, we must believe that the creation of all this universe was determined only by the Divine Will; one must seek for it no other cause, nor any other end. Even as we could not seek the final cause of the existence of God, so we could not seek the final cause of His Will, by virtue of which all that has been born and shall be born is such as it is.",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.9.sub_0": "One must not therefore hold this erroneous opinion that the celestial spheres and the angels exist only because of us. Already (the Prophet) has declared to us of what value we are[[fn:2764]]: <i>Behold, the nations are as a drop of a bucket</i> (Isaiah, xl, 15); and indeed, if thou considerest thy substance and that of the celestial spheres, of the stars, and of the Separate Intelligences, thou shalt recognize the truth and thou shalt know that man is indeed the most perfect and noble being that is born of this (inferior) matter, but that, if one compars his being to that of the spheres, and still more to that of the Separate Intelligences, he is but a very small thing, as it is said: <i>Behold, He put no trust in His servants; and His angels He charged with folly: how much less in them that dwell in houses of clay, whose foundation is in the dust</i> (Job, iv, 18 and 19)? It must be known that <i>the servants</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עבדיו</span>) spoken of in this verse are not of the human species. The proof thereof is that it is said thereafter: <i>How much less in them that dwell in houses of clay, whose foundation is in the dust?</i> But the <i>servants</i> mentioned in this verse are the angels; and likewise the <i>angels</i> intended in this verse are undoubtedly the celestial spheres. Eliphaz himself setts forth this idea in another discourse, in other terms: <i>Behold</i>, says he, <i>He putts no trust in His saints; yea, the heavens are not clean in His sight. How much more abominable and filthy is man</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">איש</span>), <i>which drinks iniquity</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עַוְלָה</span>) <i>like water</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xv, 15 and 16)! It is therefore clear that His <i>saints</i> are the same as His <i>servants</i> and that they are not of the human species; His <i>angels</i>, of whom mention is made in the other verse, are the <i>heavens</i>, and the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תֳּהֳלָה</span> (folly/defect) has the same sense as the words: <i>are not clean in His sight</i>, I mean that they are material beings. But, although they be of the purest and most brilliant matter, they are nevertheless, relatively to the Separate Intelligences, turbid, dark, and without clarity.",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.9.sub_1": "If it be said, in speaking of the angels: <i>Behold, He putts no trust in His servants</i>, this signifies that they possess no stable existence; for, according to our opinion, they are <i>created</i>, and even, according to the opinion of those who admit the eternity of the world, they are the effects of a cause, and their role in the universe has neither stability nor fixity, in relation to God, the <i>Necessary Being</i> in the absolute sense[[fn:2765]]. The words <i>how much less</i> (<i>this being</i>) <i>abominable and corrupt</i> correspond to the words <i>how much less them that dwell in houses of clay;</i> it is as though one said: <i>How much less this abominable and corrupt being, man</i>, whom <i>iniquity</i>[[fn:2766]] spread through all his members does infect, that is to say, who is conjoined with <i>privation</i>[[fn:2767]]. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">עולה</span> signifies <i>crookedness</i> (or <i>iniquity</i>), as in <span dir=\"rtl\">בארץ נכחוח יעול</span>, <i>in the land of uprightness he deals unjustly</i> (Isaiah, 26, 10). The word <span dir=\"rtl\">איש</span>, <i>vir</i>, has here the sense of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אדם</span>, <i>homo;</i> for the human species is sometimes designated by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">איש</span>, <i>vir;</i> as for example <span dir=\"rtl\">מכה איש ומת</span>, <i>he that smits a man</i> (virum) <i>so that he die</i> (Exodus, 21, 10).",
    "root.text.Part 3..12.10": "This, then, is what must be believed; for as soon as man knows himself, and is not deceived[[fn:2768]] concerning his own self, and understands every being even as it (truly) is, he finds peace and his thoughts are not troubled in seeking such an <i>end</i> for a thing that has not that end[[fn:2769]], or (in general) in seeking an end for that which has no other end than its existence dependent upon the Divine Will, or, if thou preferrest, upon the Divine <i>Wisdom</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 3..13.0": "That which man must likewise consider, in order to know what he himself is worth and not to suffer himself to be misled, is that which has been set forth concerning the dimensions of the spheres and the stars, and the immense distances which separate us therefrom. Verily, since there has been set forth the manner of measuring all distances in relation to the semi-diameter of the earth[[fn:2770]], (it follows that), the measure of the circumference of the earth, and consequently that of its semi-diameter, being known, all distances shall likewise be known. It has therefore been demonstrated that the distance between the centre of the earth and the summit of the sphere of Saturn is a journey of about eight thousand seven hundred years, of three hundred and sixty-five days each, reckoning for each day's journey forty of our statute miles, of which each has two thousand common cubits[[fn:2771]]. Consider this great and formidable distance; of it the Scripture says: <i>Is not God in the height of heaven? and behold the height of the stars, how high they are!</i> (Job, 22, 12). This signifies: Seest thou not that the height of heaven proves how far we are from conceiving the Divinity? For, whereas we find ourselves at this immense distance from that body from which so great a space divids us, so that its substance and most of the effects which it produces are unknown to us, how much more shall this be the case regarding the perception of its Author, who is no body at all?",
    "root.text.Part 3..13.1": "— This great distance which has been demonstrated is taken only at the <i>minimum;</i> for between the centre of the earth and the concavity[[fn:2772]] of the sphere of the fixed stars, the distance can in no wise be less, but it is possible that it be several times as much. Indeed, the thickness of the bodies of the spheres has only been determined by demonstration at its <i>minimum</i>, as follows from the treatises <i>On the Distances</i>[[fn:2773]]; and likewise one could not determine exactly the thickness of the (intermediate) bodies which, according to Thabit[[fn:2774]], reason constrains us to admit between each pair of spheres, these bodies having no stars by means of which a demonstration might be made. As for the sphere of the fixed stars, its thickness would form a journey of at least four years' march, as may be concluded from the measure of some[[fn:2775]] of its stars, each of which has a volume exceeding ninety times and more that of the terrestrial globe[[fn:2776]]; but it may be that the thickness of this sphere is yet greater. Regarding the ninth sphere, which causs the whole assembly (of the spheres) to accomplish the diurnal motion[[fn:2777]], its measure is not known at all; for, as it has no stars, we have no means whereby to know its magnitude.",
    "root.text.Part 3..13.2": "One must therefore consider how immense are the dimensions of these corporeal beings, and how numerous they are! Now, if the whole earth be but an imperceptible point[[fn:2778]] in relation to the sphere of the (fixed) stars, what shall be the proportion of the human species to the whole of created things? And how then could any one of us imagine that they exist in his favour and for his sake, and that they should serve him as instruments? But this is yet only a comparison between bodies; and what shall it be if thou considerest the being of the (separate) Intelligences?",
    "root.text.Part 3..13.3": "However, one might in this regard raise an objection against the opinion of the philosophers. If we were to contend, it might be said, that the final end of these spheres were, for example, to govern a single human individual or several individuals, it would be absurd from the point of view of philosophical speculation; but as we believe that they have for their final end the government of the <i>human species</i>, there is no absurdity (in supposing) that these great individual bodies are destined to bring into existence individuals belonging to species, whose number, according to the doctrine of the philosophers, is infinite. This might be compared to iron instruments of a hundredweight which the craftsman fashions to manufacture a small needle weighing a grain. Now, if it were a question of a single needle, it would indeed be, from a certain point of view[[fn:2779]], a matter of ill economy, although it be not so in an absolute sense; but if one considers that he manufacturs, by means of these heavy instruments, a great quantity of needles of the weight of several hundredweights, the fabrication of these instruments is, in any case, an act of wisdom and good economy. In like manner, the final end of the spheres is to perpetuate birth and corruption, and the final end of these latter is, as has already been said, the existence of the human species. We find Biblical texts and traditions which may serve as support for this idea. Nevertheless, the philosopher might answer this objection by saying: If the difference between the celestial bodies and the individuals of the species subject to birth and corruption consisted only in greatness and smallness, the objection would be well-founded; but as the former are distinguished from the latter by the nobleness of the substance, it would be highly absurd (to suppose) that the more noble should serve as an instrument for the existence of that which is basest and most vile.",
    "root.text.Part 3..13.4": "In sum, however, this objection may offer some succour to our belief in the creation of the world[[fn:2780]], and it is this subject which I have principally aimed at in this chapter. Furthermore[[fn:2781]], it is that I have ever heard those who have occupied themselves somewhat with astronomy tax with exaggeration[[fn:2782]] that which the doctors have said regarding distances; for they say clearly that the thickness of each sphere forms a journey of five hundred years, and that between each couple of spheres there is likewise five hundred years of journeying[[fn:2783]]. Now, as there are seven spheres[[fn:2784]], the distance between the seventh sphere — I mean to say its convex part — and the centre of the earth, will form a journey of seven thousand years. Whosoever shall hear this will imagine that there is in these words a great exaggeration and that the distance reaches not that measure. But by the demonstration which has been made concerning distances, thou shalt recognize that the distance between the centre of the earth and the lower part of the sphere of Saturn, which is the seventh, forms a journey of about seven thousand and twenty-four years. As for the distance of which we have spoken (above) and which would form a journey of eight thousand seven hundred years, it reaches unto the concavity of the eighth sphere. If the doctors say that between each couple of spheres there is such a distance, this must be understood of the thickness of the bodies which exist between the spheres[[fn:2785]], and not that there is there a vacuum.",
    "root.text.Part 3..13.5": "One must not require that all they have said relative to astronomy be in accord with reality; for the mathematical sciences were imperfect in those times, and if they spoke of these things, it is not that they had received thereon a tradition proceeding from the prophets, but rather because they were the sages of those times in these matters, or because they had heard them from the sages of the epoch. Wherefore, if we find in their writings words conformable to the truth, I shall say neither that they are not true, nor that they are due to chance; on the contrary, the man of a noble character who loves to be just must always hold, as far as possible, to interpreting the words of others in such wise as to reconcile them with that which has been demonstrated of the sublime truths of being[[fn:2786]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..14.0.sub_0": "The impossible has a stable and constant nature which is not the work of an agent and which is variable under no condition; wherefore one cannot attribute to God any power in this regard[[fn:2787]]. This is what none of the thinkers[[fn:2788]] in any wise contest, and it is ignored only by those who understand not intelligible notions. If there be dissent among thinkers, it is only with respect to certain <i>imaginable</i> things which, according to some thinkers, are in the category of the impossible which God Himself has not the power to change[[fn:2789]], and which, according to others, are in the domain of the possible, which it depends upon the Divine omnipotence to bring into existence at will. Thus, for example, the union of contraries at the same instant and in the same subject, the transformation of <i>essentials</i>[[fn:2790]], I mean the change of substance into accident and of accident into substance, or the existence of a corporeal substance without accident, all this is, for every thinker, of the category of the impossible. Likewise, it is impossible that God should call into existence His like, or that He should render Himself non-existent, or that He should become corporeal, or that He should change, and one cannot attribute to Him the power to do aught of all this. As for the question of knowing whether He can produce an accident alone which be not in a substance, a sect of thinkers, namely the Mutazilites, have imagined this and believed it to be in the domain of the possible[[fn:2791]], whilst others have judged it impossible. It is true that those who have professed the existence of an <i>accident without a substratum</i> were not led thereto by simple speculation, but by their regard for certain religious doctrines which speculation violently rejects, so that they had recourse to this hypothesis[[fn:2792]]. Likewise, to produce a corporeal thing without making use for that purpose of any pre-existing matter, is, according to us, in the category of the possible; but, according to the philosophers, it is impossible[[fn:2793]]. Likewise, the philosophers will say that it is in the domain of the impossible to produce a square whose diagonal be equal to the side, or a solid angle which be surrounded by four plane right angles[[fn:2794]], or other like things. But many a man who is ignorant of mathematics and who knows of these things only the simple words, without conceiving the idea thereof, will believe them possible.",
    "root.text.Part 3..14.0.sub_1": "<br>Would that I knew[[fn:2795]] whether this be a door set open at the pleasure of every man, so that it be permitted to each to maintain, concerning everything that should come into his mind, that it is possible, whilst another should maintain that, by its very nature, the thing is impossible! Or else, is there something which closs this door and which defends the entry thereof, so that man be obliged to declare decidedly that such a thing is impossible by its nature[[fn:2796]]? The touchstone by which one must examine this, is it the imaginative faculty or the intellect? and how shall one distinguish between the things of the imagination and the intelligible? In effect, man is often at variance with another or with himself upon a thing which seems to him to be possible, and which he maintains to be possible[[fn:2797]] by its very nature, whilst it may be objected[[fn:2798]] that it is the imagination, and not the intellect, which makes this thing to appear possible. Is there consequently something which may serve as a criterion between the imaginative faculty and the intellect? and is this something outside of the one and the other, or is it by the intellect itself that one distinguishes between the intellect and that which is of the domain of the imagination? All this may give rise to researches which would merit being well deepened[[fn:2799]]; but this is not the aim of our chapter.",
    "root.text.Part 3..14.1": "Nevertheless, it is manifest that, according to all opinions and systems, there exist impossible things the existence of which is inadmissible, and in respect of which no power can be attributed unto God; yet, if it be true that God cannot change them, there is therein on His part neither weakness nor lack of power, and consequently they are necessary[[fn:2800]] (in themselves) and are not the work of an agent. It is manifest also that divergence can only exist in respect of those things which might, by hypothesis, be placed in either of the two categories[[fn:2801]], whether in that of the impossible or in that of the possible. Thou must well possess thy mind of this truth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..15.0": "The philosophers have professed concerning God a most grievous heresy[[fn:2802]], touching the knowledge He may possess of that which is without Him, and they have suffered a fall from which neither they nor those who have embraced their opinion shall be able to arise[[fn:2803]]. I will cause thee to understand the doubts which have cast them into this heresy, as well as the doctrine which our religion professes in this regard, and that which we oppose unto the evil and absurd opinions which they maintain concerning the divine omniscience.",
    "root.text.Part 3..15.1": "That which above all caused them to fall therein, and which led them thither at the first, is the lack of goodly order which seems at first sight to be remarked in the circumstances of human individuals; for, whilst certain virtuous men lead a life full of evils and sorrows, there are wicked men who lead a life happy and pleasant. They were therefore brought to pose the dilemma which thou shalt hear. It must necessarily be admitted, said they, one of two things: either that God knows naught of these individual conditions and perceivs them not, or that He perceivs and knows them. This is a rigorous dilemma. If, they continued, He perceivs and knows them, one must necessarily admit one of these three cases: either that He orders them and establishes therein the best, the most perfect, and the most complete order; or that He is unable to order them and has no power therein; or finally that, while knowing them and being able to introduce rule and goodly order, He neglects it, either because He disdains and despiss them, or because He is envious of them. Thus find we such a one among men who is able to do good unto another and knows the need of the latter to receive his benefit; but yet, through an evil disposition, through passion or jealousy, he envies him this good and does it not. One is evidently constrained to admit one of these diverse cases[[fn:2804]]. Indeed, every man who knows a certain thing either takes care of the governance of that thing of which he has knowledge, or he neglects it, as one neglects, for example, in his house the governance of cats or of things yet more base; but even he who occupies himself with a thing is sometimes incapable of governing it, even should he so desire. Having enumerated these different cases, they judged peremptorily that, of the three hypotheses admissible in respect of him who has knowledge of a thing, two are impossible[[fn:2805]] in respect of God: to wit, (to admit) that He be powerless, or that, being all-powerful, He should not concern Himself (with the things He knows); for this would be to suppose in Him vice or impotence. Far be both from Him! Of all the cases enumerated, there remain therefore but two (admissible in relation to God): either He knows absolutely naught of these conditions of men, or He knows them and orders them in the best manner. But, since we find them without order, without rule, and without a rigorous sequence, this proves that He knows them in no wise. Behold then what caused them to fall at the first into this great heresy.—All that I have just summarized of their different hypotheses, whilst bringing into relief[[fn:2806]] that which gave rise to their error, thou shalt find set forth and commented upon in the treatise of Alexander of Aphrodisias (entitled) <i>On Governance</i>[[fn:2807]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..15.2": "Thou shalt be astonished to see how they have fallen into something worse than that which they sought to avoid, and how they themselves were ignorant of a thing unto which they constantly called our attention and which they incessantly pretended to explain to us. If I say that they have fallen into something worse than that which they sought to avoid, it is because, in wishing to avoid attributing unto God <i>heedlessness</i> (of human affairs), they have declared that He <i>is ignorant</i> (of these things) and that all which passes in this world is for Him a mystery which He perceivs not. If, furthermore, I say that they were themselves ignorant of the thing unto which they were constantly calling our attention, it is because they considered being from the point of view of the conditions of human individuals, who themselves cause the evils wherewith they are afflicted, or receive them from the fatal nature of matter, as (these philosophers) cease not to say and to develop[[fn:2808]]. We have already set forth in this regard that which was necessary[[fn:2809]]. Having established themselves upon a foundation which destroys all goodly principles and which disfigurs the beauty of every true opinion[[fn:2810]], they have endeavoured to put away the absurdity which it presents[[fn:2811]], by pretending that it is impossible, for several reasons, to attribute unto God the knowledge of these individual things. First (say they), particular things are perceived only by the senses and not by the intellect; but God perceivs not by means of a sense. Next, particular things are infinite, whilst science consists in <i>encompassing;</i> but that which is infinite cannot be encompassed by science. Finally, the knowledge of things which happen, and which without contradiction are particular, would cause God to undergo a species of change; for it would be a successive renewal of knowledge. As for that which we maintain, we who are believers, that God knows these things before they are born, they say that we profess therein two absurdities: first, that science can have for its object pure non-being; secondly, that the knowledge of that which is in potentiality and the knowledge of that which is in act are one and the same thing[[fn:2812]]. There has been among them a conflict of opinions[[fn:2813]]: some have said that God knows only the species and not the individuals, whilst others have maintained that He knows absolutely naught outside of His essence, so that, according to this latter opinion, there would not be in Him a multiplicity of knowledges[[fn:2814]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..15.3": "Finally, there have been philosophers who believed as we do, that God knows all things and that absolutely naught is hidden from Him. These are certain great men prior to Aristotle, whom Alexander also mentions in the said treatise, but whose opinion he rejects, saying that what above all refuts it is that we see virtuous men smitten with evils, whilst the wicked enjoy all manner of happiness.",
    "root.text.Part 3..15.4": "In sum, it is manifest that all the philosophers, had they found the circumstances of human individuals so ordered that even the vulgar might discern therein a proper harmony, would have refrained from embarking upon all this speculation[[fn:2815]], and would not have refuted one another. But that which gave the first occasion for this speculation was the consideration of the respective conditions of virtuous and wicked men, which conditions, in their opinion, were not rightly ordered, as the ignorant among us were wont to say: <i>The way of the Lord is not equal</i> (Ezek. xxxiii. 17).",
    "root.text.Part 3..15.5": "Having shown that the theory of divine omniscience and that of providence are interconnected, I shall proceed to set forth the opinions of thinkers concerning Providence; and thereafter I shall endeavour to resolve[[fn:2816]] the difficulties raised against the knowledge which God is said to possess of particulars.",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.0": "The opinions of men touching Providence are five in number. All these are ancient; I mean to say, they are opinions which were heard expressed in the time of the prophets, from the first appearance of the True Law, which illuminats all this darkness.",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.1": "I. The <i>first opinion</i> is that which maintains that there exists no Providence whatsoever occupying itself with anything in all this universe; that all which therein exists, as well the heavens as other things[[fn:2817]], is due to chance and to certain dispositions[[fn:2818]], and that there is no being who regulats, governs, or cars for anything. Such is the opinion of Epicurus, who likewise professes the doctrine of atoms, believing that these intermingle according to chance, and that whatever springs therefrom is the work of hazard[[fn:2819]]. The infidels in Israel have likewise professed this opinion, and it is of them that it has been said: <i>They have belied the Lord, and said, It is not he</i> (Jer. v. 12). Aristotle has demonstrated that this opinion is untenable, and that the existence of things cannot be due to chance, but that, on the contrary, there is a being who ordains and governs them[[fn:2820]]. We have already touched upon this question in what has preceded[[fn:2821]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.2.sub_0": "II. The <i>second opinion</i> belongs to those who believe that certain things pertain to a Providence and are under the government of a being who ruls and ordains them, whilst others are abandoned to chance. Such is the opinion of Aristotle; I shall here set forth unto thee in summary what he thinks concerning Providence. He believs that divine Providence extends over the spheres and over that which they contain, and that by reason thereof their individual bodies (the stars) remain ever as they are[[fn:2822]]. Alexander says expressly that, according to the opinion of Aristotle, divine Providence ends at the sphere of the moon[[fn:2823]], and this is a branch which is attached to the fundamental doctrine of the eternity of the world. Verily, he believs that Providence corresponds to the nature of beings; consequently, the celestial spheres and the bodies which they contain being permanent, that which constituts Providence in their regard is to remain ever invariably in the same state; but, even as these beings give existence to other beings whose species alone, but not the individuals, exist perpetually[[fn:2824]], so there emanats from the Providence in question something which has for its effect the preservation and perpetuation of <i>species</i>, whilst the permanence of <i>individuals</i> is impossible. Nevertheless, the individuals of each species are not destined to an absolute abandonment; on the contrary, as soon as this (sublunary) matter is sufficiently pure to receive the form of <i>growth</i>[[fn:2825]], it is also endowed with forces which preserve it for a certain time, by attracting to itself that which is suitable and by expelling that which can be of no utility[[fn:2826]]. If it be more pure, so as to receive the form of <i>sensibility</i>, it is endowed with other forces which preserve and keep it, and with another faculty which gives it movement to direct itself toward what is suitable and to flee from what is contrary; moreover, each individual is endowed according to the needs of the species. If, finally, it has a purity yet greater, so as to receive the form of <i>Intelligence</i>, then it is endowed with another force, by means of which every man, according to his degree of perfection, governs, thinks, and reflects upon that which may serve to prolong the duration of the individual and to preserve the species[[fn:2827]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.2.sub_1": "As touching the other motions which betide all individuals[[fn:2828]] of a species, they are, according to Aristotle, the result of chance, and not the work of a Being who governs and ordains. Thus, for example, if there blows a wind more or less violent, it shall indubitably cause leaves to fall from a certain tree, shall break the branches of another, shall hurl down stones from a certain wall[[fn:2829]], shall cover with dust a certain plant so as to destroy it, and shall agitate certain waters[[fn:2830]] in such wise that a vessel found therein shall perish and all the crew, or a part thereof, shall be drowned. According unto him (Aristotle), there is no distinction between the falling of a leaf or a stone and the submersion of those virtuous and distinguished men who were within the ship; likewise, he makes no distinction between an ox which causs the death of a host of ants by depositing its excrements thereon, and a building whose foundations give way and which, in its collapse, causs the death of all those who are praying therein. Neither is there any distinction, according to him, between a cat that meets a mouse and tears it, a spider that devours a fly, and a hungry lion that meets a prophet and tears him[[fn:2831]]. In sum, here is the substance of his opinion: All that which he beheld continuing with sequence, without interruption and without its course undergoing any change, such as the conditions of the celestial spheres, or that which follows a certain rule and fails therein only by exception[[fn:2832]], as do physical things, he attributed to a governance, that is to say (he believed) that Divine Providence accompanied it; but that which he beheld not following any rules and not being subject to a certain law, such as the conditions of the individuals of each species, whether plant, or animal, or man[[fn:2833]], he said was the effect of chance and not of a governance, that is to say, that Divine Providence did not accompany it. He even deemed it impossible that these conditions should depend upon Providence, which connects with his opinion concerning the eternity of the world, and according to which it is impossible that all that which is should be otherwise than it is. Amongst us also, there were heretics who admitted this opinion, and they are those who said: <i>The Lord has forsaken the earth</i> (Ezek. ix. 9).",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.3.sub_0": "III. The <i>third opinion</i> is the contrary of the second. It is the opinion of those who believe that there is in the universe absolutely nothing, neither in details nor in the whole[[fn:2834]], which happens by chance, and that all, on the contrary, is the effect of a will, of an intention, and of a governance. Now, it is manifest that all that which is governed is the object of a knowledge[[fn:2835]]. This is what is professed by the Muslim sect of the Ash’arites[[fn:2836]]; and from this opinion there result great absurdities whose burden they have accepted and whose necessity they have endured. Indeed, they agree with Aristotle when he claims that there is equality between the falling of a leaf and the death of a human individual: even so it is, say they; nevertheless, it is not by chance that the wind has blown, it is God, on the contrary, who has set it in motion. Neither is it the wind which has caused the leaves to fall; but each leaf has fallen in consequence of a judgment and a decree of God, and it is He who has caused it to fall at this moment and in this place, so that the time of its fall could be neither hastened nor delayed, and that it could not fall in another place, all this having been decreed from all eternity. According to this opinion, they were constrained to admit that every motion and rest of animals is predestined, and that man has absolutely no power to do or not to do a thing. It follows likewise from this opinion that the nature of the <i>possible</i> is lacking to things of this sort, and that they are all either <i>necessary</i> or <i>impossible;</i> and indeed, they were forced to admit this, and they said that what we call <i>possible</i>, as, for example, that Zeid be standing and that Amr arrive, is <i>possible</i> only in relation to us, but that, in relation to God, there is absolutely nothing <i>possible</i>, and all is either <i>necessary</i> or <i>impossible</i>. It follows further from this opinion that religious laws possess no utility, since the man for whom every religious law was made has not the power to do anything whatsoever, and that he can neither fulfill that which was commanded him, nor abstain from that which was forbidden him.",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.3.sub_1": "The people of this sect pretend that it has pleased God to send (prophets), to command, to forbid, to inspire terror[[fn:2837]], to cause hope or fear, although we have no power to act; He can therefore impose upon us even things impossible, and it may be that, whilst obeying the commandment, we be punished, or that, whilst disobeying, we be rewarded. Finally, it follows from this opinion that the actions of God have no final end. They bear the burden of all these absurdities to safeguard this opinion, and they go so far as to maintain that, if we behold an individual born blind or leprous, to whom we cannot attribute any prior sin whereby he might have deserved this, we must say: God has willed it so. And if we behold the virtuous and religious man suffer death in tortures, we must say: \"God has willed it so,\" and there is in this no injustice; for, according to them, it is permitted unto God to inflict punishments upon him who has not sinned and to do good unto the sinner. Their discourses concerning these things are well known[[fn:2838]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.4": "IV. The <i>fourth opinion</i> is the opinion of those who believe that man has the power (to act); wherefore, according to them, the commandments and the prohibitions, the rewards and the punishments, of which the Law speaks, are altogether in order[[fn:2839]]. They believe that all the actions of God derive from a <i>wisdom</i>, that one could not attribute injustice unto Him, and that He punishes not the man of good. The Mu’tazilites also admit this opinion, although, according to them, the power of man is not absolute[[fn:2840]]. They also admit that God has knowledge of the falling of this leaf and of the motion of this ant, and that Providence extends over all beings. This opinion also contains absurdities and contradictions. As for the absurd, here it is: If a man be infirm from birth, although he has not yet sinned, they say that this is the effect of divine wisdom and that it is better for this individual to be thus fashioned rather than to be well-constituted. We know not (in what consists) this benefit, although this has befallen him, not to punish him, but to do him good. They answer in like manner[[fn:2841]], when the virtuous man perishes, that it is in order that his reward be so much the greater in the world to come. They have even gone further: when it was asked of them why God is just toward man without being so also toward other creatures, and for what sin a certain animal is slaughtered, they had recourse to this absurd answer[[fn:2842]], that it is better for it (the animal), in order that God may reward it in another life[[fn:2843]]. Yea (say they), even the flea and the louse which have been killed must find for this a reward with God; and likewise, if this mouse, which is innocent, has been torn by a cat or by a kite, it is divine wisdom, say they, which required that it should be so with this mouse, and God will reward it in another life for that which has befallen it.",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.5": "I do not believe I ought to blame any of the partisans of these three opinions on Providence, for each of them was led by a grave necessity to the opinion which he professed. Aristotle held to that which seems manifest by the nature of being. The Ash’arites desired to avoid attributing to God ignorance in any matter whatsoever, for it is not fitting to say that He knows one particularity and is ignorant of another. They therefore had recourse to these absurdities (of which we have spoken) and accepted them. The Mu’tazilites, for their part, desired to avoid attributing unto God iniquity and injustice; but they did not think it fitting to set themselves in opposition to common sense, in such wise as to maintain that there is no iniquity in inflicting pains upon him who has not sinned. Neither did they believe they could admit that the mission of all the prophets and the revelation of the Law had no comprehensible reason; they therefore likewise bore the burden of these absurdities (of which we have spoken), and they were involved in contradictions; for they admit at once that God knows all things and that man has the faculty (to act freely), which, as is easily understood, leads to a manifest contradiction.",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.6": "V. The <i>fifth opinion</i> is our own, I mean that of our Law. I shall make known unto thee what the books of our prophets say concerning this matter, and it is likewise that which our doctors have generally admitted. I shall also inform thee of what some of our modern (scholars) have thought, and finally, I shall declare unto thee mine own opinion. I say then, that it is a fundamental principle of the Law of Moses, our master, admitted by all who follow it, that man possesses an absolute power of action; that is to say, that by his nature, by his choice, and by his will, he does all that a man is capable of doing, without there intervening any newly created thing[[fn:2844]]. Likewise (according to this opinion), all species of animals move by their own will alone; for God has so willed it, I mean that it is by the effect of His eternal and primitive will that all animals move according to their free-will, and that man has the power to do all that he wills, or all that he preferrs among the actions of which he is capable. This is a fundamental principle which, thanks be to God, has never been, within our communion[[fn:2845]], the object of any contradiction.",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.7": "In like manner, it is one of the fundamental principles of the Law of Moses, our master, that one cannot, in any wise, ascribe injustice to God, and that all the misfortunes that fall upon men, or the benefits that come unto them, whether individually or to many in common, are, according to what these have deserved, the effect of an equitable judgment, in which there is absolutely no injustice. If, therefore, an individual had his hand wounded by a thorn which he should immediately remove, it would be the effect of a punishment; and if there should happen to him the smallest enjoyment, it would be the effect of a reward. All this would be well-deserved, as the Scripture says, <i>for all His ways are judgment</i> (Deut. xxxii. 4), although we be ignorant of the manner in which it was deserved.",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.8.sub_0": "Behold then the succinct summary of these different opinions: Of all the varied conditions in which we see human <i>individuals</i>, Aristotle recogniss naught but pure chance; the Ascharites see therein the effect of the divine will alone; the Mo’tazales, the effect of divine wisdom; and we (Israelites) see therein the effect of what the individual has deserved according to his works. Wherefore it might be, according to the Ascharites, that God should cause the good and virtuous man to suffer in this lower world, and condemn him forever to that fire which is said to be in the other world; for, they would say, God has so <i>willed</i> it[[fn:2846]]. But the Mo’tazales think that this would be an injustice, and that the being that has suffered[[fn:2847]], were it even an ant, as I have said, shall have a compensation; for it is divine wisdom that has caused it to suffer, so that it might have a compensation. Finally, we ourselves admit that all that happens to man is the effect of what he has deserved[[fn:2848]], that God is above injustice, and that He punishes only him amongst us who has deserved the punishment. This is what the Law of Moses, our master, says textually, (to wit) that all depends upon merit; and it is also in accordance with this opinion that our doctors generally express themselves. These, indeed, say expressly: “No death without sin, no punishment without crime[[fn:2849]]”; and they say again: “Man is measured according to the measure which he has himself employed,” which is the text of the Mischnâ[[fn:2850]]. Everywhere they say clearly that, for God, justice is an absolutely necessary thing; that is to say, that He rewards the pious man for all his acts of piety[[fn:2851]] and uprightness, even if they had not been commanded him by a prophet, and that He punishes every evil action that an individual has committed, even if it had not been forbidden him by a prophet; for it is forbidden him by the natural sentiment which prohibits injustice and iniquity[[fn:2852]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.8.sub_1": "“The Holy One,” say they, “withholds from no creature that which it has merited[[fn:2853]].” They say further: “Whosoever says that God is prodigal (in His pardon) deservs to have his bowels torn out; it is true that God is long-suffering, but He reclaims that which is His due[[fn:2854]].” Elsewhere it is said: “He who performs a duty which is prescribed to him (by religion) is not comparable to him who performs it without it being prescribed unto him[[fn:2855]];” they say then clearly that even he upon whom the thing is not imposed (by religion) is rewarded therefor. This principle is constantly reproduced in their words; but there is found in the words of the doctors something more which is not found in the text of the Law, namely the <i>chastisements of love</i>[[fn:2856]], of which some speak. According to this opinion, man would sometimes be smitten with misfortunes, not for having sinned beforehand, but in order that his (future) reward might be all the greater. This is also the opinion of the Mo’tazales; but no text of the Law expresses this idea[[fn:2857]]. Thou must not allow thyself to be led into error by the idea of the <i>trial</i>, when it is said: <i>God did prove Abraham</i> (Gen. xxii. 1); <i>He afflicted thee and suffered thee to hunger, etc.</i> (Deut. viii. 3). Thou shalt hear further on what we have to say upon this subject[[fn:2858]]. Our Law occupies itself only with the conditions of human individuals; but never, in ancient times, had one heard mention in our communion of this <i>compensation</i> (which would be reserved) for animals. Never did any of the doctors make mention thereof; but some moderns among the <i>Guéônim</i>, having heard this from the Mo’tazales, approved it and made it a matter of belief[[fn:2859]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.9": "I shall now set forth unto thee what I myself think concerning this fundamental principle, to wit, concerning divine Providence. In this belief of which I am about to speak, I do not rely upon demonstrative proofs, but rather upon what has appeared to me to be the manifest intention of the Book of God and of the writings of our prophets. But the opinion which I admit offers less improbability than the preceding opinions and approaches more nearly to the reasoning of the Intellect. It is that I believe that in this lower world, I mean below the sphere of the moon, divine Providence has for its object, as regards individuals, only those of the human species alone; and that it is in this species alone that all the conditions of individuals, as well as the good and evil that happens unto them, are in accordance with merit, as it is said: <i>for all His ways are judgment</i> (Deut. xxxii. 4). As concerns other animals and, with even greater reason, plants, I share the opinion of Aristotle. I believe in no wise that such a leaf has fallen by the effect of a Providence, nor that such a spider has devoured such a fly by reason of a decree of God and by His momentary and particular will, nor that this spittle cast by Zeid fell upon such a midge, in a particular place, and killed it by reason of a judgment and a decree (of God), nor that it is by a particular divine will that such a fish has snatched such a worm from the surface of the water; on the contrary, all this is, in my view, the effect of pure chance, as Aristotle thinks. But, according to my manner of viewing it, divine Providence follows the divine <i>overflow</i>[[fn:2860]]; and the species to which this emanation of the (divine) Intellect is attached, in such a manner as to make of it a being endowed with Intellect and unto whom is manifested all that is manifested unto an intelligent being, (this species, I say) is accompanied by divine Providence, which measurs all its actions, so as to reward or to punish them. Verily[[fn:2861]], if it be true, as he (Aristotle) says, that the foundering of the ship with its crew and the falling of the roof upon the folk in the house were the effect of pure chance, it was nevertheless not, according to our opinion, by the effect of chance that the former entered into the ship and that the latter sat in the house; on the contrary, (this happened) by the effect of the divine will, according to what these people had deserved according to the judgments of God, whose rules are inaccessible to our intellects[[fn:2862]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.10.sub_0": "That which has led me unto this belief is that I have found no text in the prophetic books which speaks of Divine Providence as extending over any <i>individual</i> animal whatsoever, other than the human individual. The prophets even marvel that Providence should extend over human individuals; for man, and a fortiori any other animal, is too insignificant for God to concern Himself with him: <i>What is man, that Thou takest knowledge of him? etc.</i> (Ps. cxliv, 3); <i>what is the mortal, that Thou art mindful of him? etc.</i> (Ps. viii, 5). Nevertheless, there are found texts which manifestly proclaim that Providence extends over all human individuals and watches over all their actions; for example: <i>He that fashions their hearts alike, who considers all their works</i> (Ps. xxxiii, 15); <i>Thou whose eyes are open upon all the ways of the sons of men, to give every one according to his ways</i> (Jeremiah, xxxii, 19); <i>His eyes are upon the ways of man, and He ses all his goings</i> (Job, xxxiv, 21). The Pentateuch also speaks of Providence in regard to human individuals and of the examination to which their actions are subjected; for example: <i>in the day when I visit, I will visit their sin upon them</i> (Exodus, xxxii, 34); <i>whosoever has sinned against Me, him will I blot out of My book</i> (<i>ibid., v.</i> 33); <i>I will destroy that soul</i> (Levit., xxiii, 30); <i>I will set My face</i> (My anger) <i>against that soul</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xx, 6)[[fn:2863]], and many other passages. All the events related of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob are an evident proof of individual Providence[[fn:2864]]. As for the <i>individuals</i> of (irrational) animals, the matter is undoubtedly as Aristotle thinks; wherefore it is permitted, and even commanded, to slaughter them and to employ them for our use as it pleass us. That which proves that the care of Providence extends unto animals only in the sense indicated by Aristotle[[fn:2865]], is that the prophet, having seen the tyranny of Nebuchadnezzar and the great carnage which he made of men, says: «O Lord,» says he, «thou makest men as the fishes of the sea, as the creeping things, that have no ruler over them,» indicating by these words that these species are abandoned.",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.10.sub_1": "Thus does he express himself: <i>Thou makest men as the fishes of the sea, as the creeping things, that have no ruler over them; he takes up all of them with the angle, etc.</i> (Habac., i, 14, 15). Yet the prophet declars that it is not so (with men); it is not that they have been abandoned and that Providence has withdrawn from them, but rather that they were to be punished, having deserved that which befell them: <i>O Eternal</i>, says he, <i>Thou hast ordained them for judgment; and, O mighty God, Thou hast established them for correction</i> (<i>ibid., v.</i> 12).",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.11": "One must not believe that this opinion[[fn:2866]] is refuted by such passages as these: <i>He gives to the beast his food, etc.</i> (Ps. cxlvii, 9); <i>the young lions roar after their prey, etc.</i> (Ps. ciii, 21); <i>Thou openest Thine hand, and satisfiest the desire of every living thing</i> (Ps. cxlv, 16); and likewise by this passage of the Doctors: «Sitting (upon His throne), He feeds all, from the horns of the unicorns even unto the eggs of insects[[fn:2867]].» Thou shalt find many similar passages, yet there is naught therein which refuts my opinion; for in all these passages, it is a question of a Providence watching over the <i>species</i> and not over the <i>individuals</i>, and therein is described, as it were, the Divine goodness[[fn:2868]], which prepars for every species the food necessary for it and the means of subsistence. This is clear and evident, and Aristotle likewise thinks that this kind of Providence necessarily exists. At least, this is what Alexander reports in the name of Aristotle, to wit, that the sustenance of each species is placed at the disposal of the individuals; for otherwise, the species would undoubtedly perish, which is clear to any who reflects thereon. — If the Doctors say that «to cause pain unto animals is a thing forbidden by the Law[[fn:2869]],» — which they connect with this passage: <i>wherefore hast thou smitten thine ass</i> (Numbers, xxii, 32)[[fn:2870]], — it is for the sake of our moral perfection, lest we should contract harsh habits, and lest we should cause (animals) to suffer in vain and without any utility, but that, on the contrary, we should apply ourselves to pity and mercy for any animal individual whatsoever, except in case of necessity, <i>when thy soul longs to eat flesh</i> (Deuter., xii, 20); but we must not slaughter through cruelty or for pleasure. Neither can one oppose unto my opinion this other question: «wherefore does God take care of human individuals, without taking the same care of every other animal individual?» for he who should ask this question might as well ask: «wherefore has God granted Intelligence unto man and has not likewise granted it unto all other species of animals?» Verily, one would answer this latter question, in accordance with one of the three preceding opinions: «God has so willed it,» or «His wisdom has so required it,» or «nature has so required it.» But the same answers may be made unto the first question.",
    "root.text.Part 3..16.12": "It behoovs thee to understand my opinion thoroughly. Verily, I am far from believing that anything whatsoever can be unknown unto God, or from attributing unto Him impotence; but I believe that Providence depends upon Intelligence, whereunto it is intimately bound. Indeed, Providence can emanate only from an intelligent being, and particularly from Him who is a perfect Intelligence in the highest degree of perfection; whence it follows that he alone unto whom there is attached aught of this <i>emanation</i> (of the Divine Intelligence) shall participate in Providence according to the measure wherewith he participats in Intelligence[[fn:2871]]. Such is, in my view, the opinion which accords with reason[[fn:2872]] and with the texts of the Law. As for the preceding opinions, they admit too much or too little: at times it is an exaggeration which results in a veritable confusion[[fn:2873]], in denying the intelligible and in contesting the sensible[[fn:2874]]; at other times it is too great a reserve[[fn:2875]] which produces most pernicious beliefs concerning the Divinity, destroys the good order in human existence[[fn:2876]], and effacs all the moral and intellectual qualities of man; and here I speak of the opinion of those who refuse to admit Providence for human individuals and who place these upon the same level as the individuals of other animal species.",
    "root.text.Part 3..17.0": "Having established that among all species of animals the human species alone is the object of the special care of Providence, this is what I have to add: It is a known thing that there exists no <i>species</i> outside the mind, but that, on the contrary, the <i>species</i> and other <i>universals</i> are things belonging unto the understanding, and that all which exists outside the mind is an <i>individual</i> being, or a collection of individuals[[fn:2877]]. This being known, it will also be known that the <i>Divine emanation</i> which we find united unto the human species, I mean the human intellect, is a thing which has its existence only through individual Intelligences, to wit, through that which has flowed (from the Divine Intelligence) upon Zeid, upon ’Amr, upon Khâled, and upon Becr.",
    "root.text.Part 3..17.1": "Such being the case, it follows, according to what I have said in the preceding chapter, that the more a human individual participats in this emanation by reason of his matter being better or worse predisposed, and by his exercise[[fn:2878]], the more shall he be protected by Providence, if it be true, as I have asserted, that Providence depends upon the Intellect. Wherefore Divine Providence shall not watch over all individuals of the human species in an equal manner; on the contrary, it shall protect some more than others, in proportion as their human perfection is greater or less. From this reflection it necessarily follows that Providence shall watch with exceeding care over the prophets, and shall vary according to the rank which they hold in prophecy[[fn:2879]]; and likewise, it shall watch over superior and virtuous men, according to their degree of superiority and virtue; for it is such a degree of the emanation of the Divine Intellect that has caused the prophets to speak, that has guided the actions of virtuous men, or has perfected by science the knowledge of superior men. As for the ignorant and sinful, being deprived of this <i>emanation</i>, they abide in a contemptible state and are ranked with the other species of animals: <i>He is like the beasts that are void of speech</i> (Ps. xlix. 13 and 21)[[fn:2880]]; wherefore it has been accounted a light thing to slay them, and it has even been commanded for the public good[[fn:2881]]. That which has just been said is one of the foundations of religion; I mean to say that it is based upon this principle[[fn:2882]], that Providence watches over every human individual in particular, according to his merit.",
    "root.text.Part 3..17.2": "Fix thy attention upon the manner in which Scripture expresses itself regarding Providence protecting the conditions of the patriarchs even unto the smallest details of their occupations and their possessions, as also upon the promises made unto them concerning this protection of Providence. To Abraham it was said: <i>I am thy shield</i> (Gen. xv. 1); to Isaac: <i>I will be with thee, and will bless thee</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xxvi. 3); to Jacob: <i>I am with thee, and will keep thee in all places whither thou goest</i> (<i>ibid</i>., xxviii. 15); to the prince of the prophets: <i>Certainly I will be with thee</i> (Exod. iii. 12); to Joshua: <i>As I was with Moses, so I will be with thee</i> (Josh. i. 5). By all these expressions it is declared that Providence watched over them according to the measure of their perfection. — Concerning Providence watching over superior men and neglecting the ignorant, it is said: <i>He will keep the feet of His holy ones, but the wicked shall be silent in darkness; for by strength shall no man prevail</i> (1 Sam. ii. 9). This means that if certain individuals be preserved from misfortunes, whilst others fall thereinto, it is not by reason of their bodily strength and physical dispositions: <i>for by strength shall no man prevail;</i> but it is, on the contrary, by reason of perfection and (moral) imperfection, that is to say, according as they approach or remove themselves from God. Wherefore they that are near unto Him enjoy a perfect protection: <i>He will keep the feet of His holy ones</i>, whilst they that are far from Him are exposed to all the strokes of chance, nothing protecting them against accidents, as happens unto him that walks in darkness and whose destruction is certain. It is said again concerning Providence watching over superior men: <i>He keeps all his bones</i> (Ps. xxxiv. 21); <i>the eyes of the Lord are upon the righteous</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v</i>. 16); <i>when he calls upon Me</i>, <i>I will answer him</i> (Ps. xci. 15). The texts that treat of this subject, I mean of Providence watching over human individuals according to the measure of their perfection and their superiority, are too numerous to be enumerated. The philosophers likewise have spoken in this sense. Abu-Nasr (Al-Farabi), in the introduction to his commentary on the <i>Nicomachean Ethics</i> of Aristotle, expresses himself in these terms: “Those who possess the faculty of causing their souls to pass from one moral quality to another[[fn:2883]] are, as Plato has said, those whom Divine Providence protects the most[[fn:2884]].”",
    "root.text.Part 3..17.3": "Thou seest now how this manner of reasoning has led us to recognize the truth of what all the prophets have said in this regard, namely, that Providence protects every individual in particular, according to the measure of his perfection, and how this is necessary from the viewpoint of speculation, if it be true, as we have said, that Providence depends upon the Intellect. It would not, therefore, be fitting to profess the opinion put forth by some philosophical sects, namely, that Providence exists for the <i>species</i> (human) and not for <i>individuals;</i> for individuals alone having a real existence outside the understanding[[fn:2885]], it is to these individuals that the Divine Intellect is attached, and consequently Providence also exists for these individuals.",
    "root.text.Part 3..17.4": "Examine this chapter with the greatest care; then all the fundamental principles of religion shall appear unto thee perfect and conformable[[fn:2886]] to speculative and philosophical opinions; the improbabilities shall vanish, and thou shalt have a clear and true idea of Providence.",
    "root.text.Part 3..17.5": "Having related the opinion of thinkers concerning Providence and the manner in which God governs the universe, I shall now also summarize the opinion of our communion concerning <i>omniscience</i>, and what I myself have to say in this regard.",
    "root.text.Part 3..18.0": "It is undoubtedly a <i>primary notion</i>[[fn:2887]] that God must unite within Himself all perfections, and that all imperfections must be removed from Him. It is also well-nigh a primary notion that ignorance of aught whatsoever is an imperfection, and that God can be ignorant of nothing. But that which has led certain thinkers, as I have said, to maintain boldly that He knows one thing and knows not another, is their imagination that the conditions of human individuals lack good order; and yet these conditions, for the most part, are not merely natural conditions, but depend at the same time upon man, who possesses free will and reflection[[fn:2888]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..18.1": "Already have the prophets declared that the ignorant, in order to prove that God has no knowledge of our actions, base themselves upon the well-being and tranquility which we behold the wicked enjoy, which may lead the pious man to believe that it is of no utility that he applies himself to good and endurs the pains caused him by the opposition of others[[fn:2889]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..18.3": "All that I purposed in this chapter was to show that this is a very ancient way of thinking, I mean to say this error of the ignorant who deny that God has knowledge (of human things), because the conditions of human individuals, which by their nature are in the category of the possible, lack good order: <i>And the children of Israel</i>, it is said, <i>did secretly those things that were not right against the Lord</i> (II Kings, xvii. 9). In the Midrash (it is said on this subject): “What did they say? This pillar, said they, neither ses, nor hears, nor speaks,[[fn:2897]]” that is to say: they imagined that God knows not these (human) conditions and that He addresses to the prophets neither commandment nor prohibition. The cause of all this, and what, according to them, is a proof thereof, is that the conditions of human individuals are not as each of us believs they ought to be. Seeing therefore that things did not come to pass according to their desire, they said: <i>The Lord ses us not</i> (Ezek. viii. 12), and Zephaniah says in speaking of them: <i>Those who say in their heart</i>, <i>The Lord will not do good, neither will He do evil</i> (Zeph. i. 12).",
    "root.text.Part 3..18.4": "As touching that which is (verily) to be thought concerning the omniscience of God, I shall declare unto thee mine opinion thereon, after having made known unto thee the principles whereon there is general agreement, and which no intelligent man can in any wise contest.",
    "root.text.Part 3..19.0": "One matter whereon there is agreement, is that no new knowledge can accrue to God, in such wise that He should know now that which He knew not aforetime[[fn:2898]]. Neither can He, even according to the opinion of those who admit the attributes, possess manifold and numerous knowledges[[fn:2899]]. This being demonstrated, we, the followers of the Law[[fn:2900]], do say that, by a <i>single</i> knowledge, He knows manifold and numerous things, and that, in respect of God, the variety of things known implies not a variety of knowledges, as is the case in respect of ourselves. Likewise, we say that all these things newly occurred, God knew them before they existed, and He has known them from all eternity. Consequently, no new knowledge has accrued to Him; for, when He knows that such a one, who exists not now, shall exist at such a time and shall return into nothingness after having existed for a certain season, His knowledge receivs no increase when that person comes into existence as He knew beforehand. There is then naught come into being which was unknown to Him; but there is come into being something whose future birth was known to Him from all eternity, even as it has been realized.",
    "root.text.Part 3..19.1": "But, from this belief (it may be objected), it would follow that (Divine) knowledge has for its very object things which exist not, and that it encompasses the infinite[[fn:2901]]. And this is indeed what we believe. We maintain that it is in no wise impossible that the knowledge of God should have for its object things which exist not yet, but whose future existence He knows beforehand and which He is able to bring into being; only that which never exists, that is what is, in regard to the knowledge of God, absolute non-being, which this knowledge cannot have for its object, even as our own knowledge cannot have for its object that which for us has no existence[[fn:2902]]. But that which presents a (real) difficulty, is to admit that it (Divine knowledge) encompasses the infinite. Certain thinkers have had recourse to this assertion: that, in a certain sense, Divine knowledge attaches to the <i>species</i> and extends thereby over all the individuals of the species[[fn:2903]]; such is the opinion to which all theologians have perforce been led by speculation[[fn:2904]]. However, the philosophers have maintained in an absolute manner that Divine knowledge cannot have non-being for its object, and that no knowledge can encompass the infinite; now (said they), as no new knowledge can accrue to God, it is inadmissible that He should learn any of the things newly occurred, and, consequently, He knows only things stable and invariable[[fn:2905]]. Unto some among them, another doubt arose: even though, said they, He should know only stable things, His knowledge would be manifold; for the multitude of things known implies the multiplicity of knowledges, each thing known supposing a special knowledge. Consequently (they concluded), He knows only His own essence[[fn:2906]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..19.2": "For my part, I deem the cause of all these perplexities to be that men have established a relation between our knowledge and that of God, so that each party, considering all that is impossible for our own knowledge, has imagined that it is necessarily the same[[fn:2907]] for Divine knowledge, or at least has found therein difficulties. In sum, the philosophers are in this matter to be blamed far more than any other party; for it is they who have demonstrated that, in the essence of God, there is no multiplicity, that God has no attribute outside His essence, and that, on the contrary, His knowledge and His essence are one and the same thing. It is they also who have demonstrated that our intellects are incapable of grasping His essence in all its reality, as we have set forth; how then can they have the pretension to comprehend His knowledge, since this latter is not a thing outside His essence? When we say that our intellects are incapable of comprehending His essence, do we not say thereby that they are incapable of comprehending how He has knowledge of things?[[fn:2908]] Verily, this knowledge is not of the same species as ours, that we might judge thereof by analogy. It is, on the contrary, a thing totally different; and even as there is there an essence of a necessary existence, an essence wherefrom, according to the opinion of the philosophers, all beings have emanated by necessity, or which, according to our opinion, has produced from nothingness all that is outside itself, in like manner do we say that this essence perceivs all that is outside itself, and that naught of that which exists[[fn:2909]] is unknown to it, but that there is nothing in common between our knowledge and His, even as there is naught in common between our essence and His. It is only the homonymy of the word <i>knowledge</i> which has given rise to the error; for there is therein but community of names, whilst for the real sense there is complete divergence. This, then, is what has led to the absurd, because it was imagined that all that pertains to our knowledge, pertains also to that of God[[fn:2910]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..19.3": "That which, for me, results likewise from the texts of the Law, is that, when God knows that any <i>possible</i> being whatsoever shall come into existence, this in no wise removs that possible being from the nature of the possible; on the contrary, it preservs that nature, and the (anticipated) knowledge of that which shall arise from possible things does not necessarily require that they should subsequently be realized in one of the two possible manners[[fn:2911]]. This is also one of the fundamental principles of the Law of Moses, whereon there is neither doubt nor division of opinions[[fn:2912]]. Were it not so, it would not have been said[[fn:2913]]: <i>When thou buildest a new house, then thou shalt make a battlement for thy roof, etc.</i> (Deuter. xxii. 8), and likewise: <i>Lest he die in the battle, and another man take her</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xx. 7). All the sacred legislation, that which it commands and that which it forbidds, supposs this principle, to wit, that Divine foreknowledge does not remove the possible from its nature; but for our weak intellects, this is right difficult to comprehend.",
    "root.text.Part 3..19.4": "Observe now in how many points, according to the adherents of the Law, the science of God differs from our own: 1st, In that this science, which is one, embraces a multitude of objects of diverse species. 2nd, In that it relats to that which exists not yet. 3rd, In that it relats to that which is infinite. 4th, In that it undergoes no change by the perception of things newly arisen; and yet it might seem that to know a thing shall exist is not the same as to know it has already come into existence, for in the latter case there would be this further circumstance, that what was only in potentiality has passed into act[[fn:2914]]. 5th, In that, according to the opinion of our Law, the divine prescience decids not[[fn:2915]] for one of the two possible cases, although God knows in a precise manner which of the two shall come to pass[[fn:2916]]. — I would fain know in what respect, even according to the opinion of those who consider (divine) science as an attribute added (unto the essence of God), our science resembls His! Is there here aught else than a simple community of names? But certes, according to our own opinion, who say that His science is in no wise a thing added to His essence, there must needs be between His science and our own a substantial difference, such as that which exists between the substance of heaven and that of the earth. This is also what the prophets have clearly stated: <i>My thoughts are not your thoughts</i>, <i>neither are your ways my ways, says the Lord; for</i>, <i>as the heavens are higher than the earth</i>, <i>so are my ways higher than your ways</i>, <i>and my thoughts than your thoughts</i> (Isaiah, 55, 8-9).",
    "root.text.Part 3..19.5": "In sum, thus do I resume my thought: Even as, without comprehending the true essence of God, we nevertheless know that His being is the most perfect being, that He is affected, in no wise, by imperfection, nor change, nor passion; even so, without comprehending what His science is in reality, since it is His essence, we nevertheless know that He cannot now know and now be ignorant; I mean to say that no new science can come unto Him, that His science can have neither multiplicity nor end, that none of the things which exist can be unknown to Him, and that the knowledge He has of these things leavs their nature intact, the possible preserving the nature of <i>possibility.</i> If in the whole of these propositions there be some which seem to involve contradiction, it is because we judge of them by our own science, which has nothing in common with the science of God, save the name. Likewise, the word <i>intention</i> is applied, by simple homonymy, to that which we ourselves have in view, and to that which God is said to have in view. Finally, in like manner, the word <i>providence</i>[[fn:2917]] is used by homonymy of that wherewith we ourselves are preoccupied, and of that wherewith God is said to be preoccupied. The truth is, consequently, that <i>science</i>, <i>intention</i>, and <i>providence</i>, attributed to us, have not the same sense as when they are attributed to God. It is therefore when one takes in one and the same sense the two <i>providences,</i> the two <i>sciences</i> or the two <i>intentions</i>, that the difficulties arise and the doubts of which we have spoken are born[[fn:2918]]; but when it is known that all which is attributed to us differs from that which is attributed to God, the truth becoms manifest. The difference that exists between these things attributed to God and the same things attributed to us has been clearly enunciated by these words: <i>Your ways are not my ways</i>, as we have previously stated.",
    "root.text.Part 3..20.0": "There is a great difference between the knowledge which the artificer possesses of the work he has produced and that which another possesses of this same work. Indeed, if the work has been executed conformably to the science of the artificer[[fn:2919]], then the latter, in executing his work, has but followed his science[[fn:2920]]; but for any other who contemplats this work and acquirs a perfect knowledge thereof, the science follows the work[[fn:2921]]. Thus, for example, the artificer who has made this box, in which, by the flowing of water, weights are moved, so as to indicate the hours which have passed of the day or of the night, knows and understands perfectly the whole quantity of water that must flow, the change of position of this flow, every thread that is pulled and every ball[[fn:2922]] that descends. If he knows all these movements, it is not because he considers the movements which are happening at this moment; it is the contrary which occurrs, for the movements which take place at this moment happen only conformably to his science. But it is not so for him who contemplats this machine; for the latter, at every movement he ses, acquirs a new knowledge, and his knowledge ceass not to increase and to be renewed successively by observation, until he acquirs thereby the knowledge of the whole machine. Wert thou to suppose the movements of this machine infinite, the observer could never acquire a perfect knowledge thereof. It is impossible also that the observer should know any of these movements before it takes place; for what he knows, he knows only in consequence of that which occurrs.",
    "root.text.Part 3..20.1": "Even so it is with the whole of the universe and its relation to our science and to that of God. Verily, what we know, we know only in consequence of the contemplation of beings; wherefore our science extends neither over future things, nor over that which is infinite; but our knowledge is renewed and multiplied according to the things of which we acquire knowledge. It is not so with God; I mean to say that it is not from things that the knowledge He has of them comes to Him, so that there would be therein multiplicity and renewal (of sciences); on the contrary, these things depend upon His science, which has preceded them and has established them as they are, whether <i>separate</i> beings, or material and permanent individuals, or material beings, individually variable, but which (in their whole) follow an imperishable and unalterable order[[fn:2923]]. For God, therefore, there is no multiple science, and nothing new can occur in His science, which is unalterable[[fn:2924]]; for, in knowing the whole reality of His unalterable essence, He knows by that very fact all that must necessarily result from His actions[[fn:2925]]. To put forth efforts to understand how this is done would be as though we put forth efforts that we might be <i>He</i> (God) and that our perception might be His[[fn:2926]]. Thus, therefore, he who sincerely seeks the truth must believe that nothing whatsoever is unknown to God, and that, on the contrary, all is manifest to His science, which is His essence, but that it is absolutely impossible for us to know this kind of perception[[fn:2927]]. If we knew how to account for it, we should ourselves possess the intelligence which gives this kind of perception[[fn:2928]]; but that is a thing which no being, save God, possesses, and which is itself the divine essence. Thou must well penetrate thyself with this; for I affirm that this is a very profound thought and a true opinion, in which, if one goes deep into it, one will find neither error nor false appearance, (an opinion) which offers no improbability, and by which one attributs to God no imperfection. Certes, these sublime and grave questions could in no wise be the object of a demonstration, neither according to the opinion we profess, we the followers of the Law, nor according to the opinion of the philosophers, however much they may be divided elsewhere upon the problem (which occupies us). For all subjects, therefore, which are not susceptible of being demonstrated, one must follow the method we have followed for the problem in question, I mean the problem of the omniscience of God[[fn:2929]]. Understand this well.",
    "root.text.Part 3..21.0": "The history of Job, so strange and so astonishing[[fn:2930]], relats to the subject wherewith we are occupied; I mean that it is a parable which has for its aim to set forth the opinions of men concerning Providence. Thou knowest that certain doctors say expressly: “Job never existed, and this is but a parable[[fn:2931]].” Even those who believe that he existed and that it is a history which (really) happened, know not how to assign to him[[fn:2932]] either time or place. Some doctors say that he existed in the time of the patriarchs; others say, in the time of Moses; others again, in the time of David; others finally say that he was of those who returned from Babylon. But all this does but confirm the opinion of those who say that he never existed. In sum, whether he existed or not, the fact remains that all readers have been cast into perplexity by his history such as it is related to us[[fn:2933]]; so that there have been objected against the science and the Providence of God what I have already mentioned[[fn:2934]], to wit, that the virtuous and perfect man, full of probity in his actions, and who has the greatest care to avoid sins, is yet smitten, stroke upon stroke, with great misfortunes, in his fortune, in his children, and in his person, without having deserved it by any sin whatsoever. According to both opinions again, whether Job existed or not, the prologue of the book, I mean the discourse of Satan, the words which God addresses to Satan, Job delivered into the power of the latter, all this (say I), for every intelligent man, is indubitably a parable. Nevertheless, this is not a parable such as there are many, but a parable to which are attached profound thoughts, <i>things which form the mystery of the universe</i>[[fn:2935]], and which serves to clear up great obscurities and to manifest the highest truths[[fn:2936]]. I shall tell thee of it all that can be told[[fn:2937]], and I shall relate the words of the doctors who have awakened my attention to all that I have been able to understand of this important parable.",
    "root.text.Part 3..21.1": "The first thing that must engage thy attention are the words: <i>There was in the land of</i> 'O<small>U</small>Z (Hus) <i>a man</i> (Job, I. 1), in which a homonym is employed, to wit, <i>Uz</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עוץ</span>); for it is at once the name of a man: <i>his first-born Uz</i> (Genesis, 22, 21), and the imperative of a verb (expressing the idea of) <i>to reflect</i>, <i>to meditate</i>, as for example <span dir=\"rtl\">עוצו עצה</span>, <i>take counsel</i> (Isaiah, 8, 10). It is therefore as if one said: Meditate upon this parable[[fn:2938]], reflect thereon, seek to penetrate its meaning, and behold what the true opinion is[[fn:2939]]. Afterward it is related that the <i>sons of God</i> (the angels) came to present themselves before the Lord, and that Satan presented himself in the midst of them[[fn:2940]]. It is not said “the sons of God and Satan came to present themselves before the Lord,” so that all would have been there by the same title[[fn:2941]]; but the expression used is: <i>The sons of God came to present themselves before the Lord, and Satan also came in the midst of them</i> (Job, 1, 6; II, 1). By this manner of expression is designated one who came without being intended or his presence sought, but who rather, upon the occasion of the arrival of those whose presence was intended, presented himself in the midst of the newcomers[[fn:2942]]. — Then it is said that this Satan was roaming upon the earth and walking through it; there is therefore no relation between him and the superior world, to which he has no access. Such is the meaning of the words: (<i>I come</i>) <i>from roaming upon the earth and from walking through it</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, I, 7; II, 2); for he roams and walks only upon the earth[[fn:2943]]. — Afterward it is related that this upright and perfect man was delivered into the hands of Satan, and that the latter was the cause of all the misfortunes that befell him in his fortune, in his children, and in his person[[fn:2944]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..21.2": "After having thus indicated the underlying idea[[fn:2945]], the exposition beginns of what the thinkers[[fn:2946]] have said upon this subject; first, an opinion is related which is attributed to Job, then other opinions (are attributed) to his friends. I shall clearly set forth these various opinions, which caused among them so great a conflict of ideas[[fn:2947]] concerning this event of which Satan alone was the cause, whilst they all believed, Job as well as his friends, that God Himself had acted, without the mediation of Satan. That which is most astonishing and most remarkable in this narrative is that science is not attributed to Job, nor is he called a <i>wise</i>, or <i>intelligent</i>, or <i>learned man;</i> for, on the contrary, naught is attributed to him but excellent morals and uprightness in actions. Indeed, had he been a <i>wise man</i>, his situation would have held nothing obscure for him, as shall be explained further on.",
    "root.text.Part 3..21.3": "I will further observe that the misfortunes of Job are presented in a certain gradation, according to the different characters of men[[fn:2948]]. Indeed, there be men who are not affrighted[[fn:2949]] by the loss of their fortune, of which they make little account, but who are seized with terror by the death of their children and die thereof from grief. There be others who bear with resignation[[fn:2950]] even the loss of children; but no being that has sensation[[fn:2951]] can endure pains. All men, I mean the vulgar, glorify God with their tongue, and call Him just and beneficent when they are happy and at ease, or even in a state of supportable suffering. But when those misfortunes arrive which are related of Job, then there be some who, in losing only their fortune, blaspheme and believe that the whole universe lacks good order; others, although afflicted by the loss of fortune, continue to believe in (divine) justice and in the good order (of the universe), but if they be tried by the loss of children, they cannot resign themselves; others finally resign themselves and are not troubled in their faith, even when they lose their children, but none of them endurs the pains of the body without complaining and without blaspheming, either with his tongue or in his thought.",
    "root.text.Part 3..21.4": "In speaking of the <i>sons of God</i>, it is said both times that they came <i>to present themselves before the Lord</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, I, 6; II, 1). But as for Satan, although he came in the midst of them the first and the second time, the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">להתיצב</span>, <i>to present oneself</i>, is not used regarding him the first time, whereas the second time it is said (II, 1): “And Satan also came in the midst of them <i>to present himself before the Lord</i>.” It is necessary well to understand this idea, and thou shalt recognize how remarkable it is[[fn:2952]]; thou shalt then be convinced that it is as it were by a divine inspiration that I have found all these ideas[[fn:2953]]. Indeed, the words <i>to present oneself before the Lord</i> signify that they stood there subject to the order proceeding from His will[[fn:2954]]. It is thus that Zechariah[[fn:2955]], speaking of the <i>four chariots coming out etc.</i>, says: <i>The angel answered and said unto me: These are the four winds which go forth from where they presented themselves</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">מהתיצב</span> <i>before the Lord of all the earth</i> (Zechariah, 6, 5)[[fn:2956]]. — It is therefore clear that the sons of God and Satan do not occupy the same rank in the universe; on the contrary, the sons of God are more stable and more durable, but he too (Satan) occupies in the universe a certain rank below theirs[[fn:2957]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..21.5": "What is further remarkable in this parable is that, after having said that Satan roamed particularly upon the earth and having spoken of the acts to which he gave himself, it is declared that he is forbidden to take possession of the <i>soul</i>, that all these earthly things are placed in his power, but that there is a barrier between him and the human soul[[fn:2958]]; such is the meaning of these words: <span dir=\"rtl\">אך את נפשו שמור</span>, <i>only take heed of his soul</i> (Job, 2, 6). I have already explained to thee that, in our language, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">נפש</span>, <i>soul</i>, is a homonym, and that it applies to that thing which remains of man after death[[fn:2959]]; it is over this thing that Satan has no power[[fn:2960]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..21.6": "After these observations, hearken unto this word so instructive emanating from the sages, unto whom one may with justice apply the name of <i>sages</i>, (a word) which has enlightened all that is obscure, laid bare all that was veiled, and revealed most of the <i>mysteries of the Law;</i> I speak of that which they say in the Talmud[[fn:2961]]: «Rabbi Simeon, son of Lakish, says: Satan, the evil inclination, and the angel of death are one and the same thing.» All that we have said, this passage reveals in a manner which shall have naught of obscurity for him who has understanding[[fn:2962]]. It is therefore manifest that these three names designate one and the same idea, and that all the actions attributed unto each of these three things are the action of one and the same thing. This is likewise what the ancient doctors of the Mishnah have expressed: «It has been taught as follows: he descends and seducs, then ascends and accuss, and finally, having obtained permission, he takes away life[[fn:2963]].» Thou shalt now understand that what David beheld in a prophetic vision at the time of the pestilence,—the angel <i>having a drawn sword in his hand stretched out over Jerusalem</i> (I Chron., 21, 16), — appeared unto him only to indicate a certain idea, which idea is the same[[fn:2964]] as that of which mention is also made in a prophetic vision, concerning the sin committed by the sons of the high priest Joshua: <i>And Satan stood at his right hand to resist him</i> (Zechariah, 3, 1)[[fn:2965]]. Following (this latter passage), it is declared how far Satan is removed from God[[fn:2966]]: <i>The Lord rebuke thee</i>, <i>O Satan; even the Lord</i> <i>that has chosen Jerusalem</i> <i>rebuke thee</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v</i>. 2). It is he also whom Balaam, in a prophetic vision, beheld upon his way, and who said unto him: <i>Behold, I went out to withstand thee</i> (Numbers, 22, 52).",
    "root.text.Part 3..21.7": "[It must be known that the word <small>SATAN</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שׂטן</span>) is derived from the verb <small>SATA</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שׂטה</span>, to turn aside), for example <i>turn from</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שְׂטֵה</span>) <i>it, and pass away</i> (Prov., 4, 15), I mean to say that this word contains the sense of <i>turning aside</i>, <i>departing;</i> for Satan is indubitably he who turns aside from the ways of truth and causs one to be lost in the ways of error[[fn:2967]]]. — This same idea[[fn:2968]] is expressed also by these words: <i>for the imagination of man's heart is evil from his youth</i> (Genesis, 8, 21). Thou knowest how widespread in our religion is the idea of the <i>good</i> and the <i>evil inclination</i>, and thou art acquainted with this word of the doctors: «with both thy inclinations[[fn:2969]]». Elsewhere they say that the <i>evil inclination</i> ariss in the human individual from birth: <i>sin lies at the door</i> (Genesis, 4, 7), and as the Scripture says: <i>from his youth</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, VIII, 21), whilst the <i>good inclination</i> comes not unto him until after the perfecting of his intelligence[[fn:2970]]. Wherefore, say they, in the parable concerning the human body and its various faculties, contained in this passage: <i>a little city, and few men within it, etc</i>. (Eccles., IX, 14), the <i>evil inclination</i> is called a <i>great king</i> and the <i>good inclination</i> is called a <i>poor wise man</i>[[fn:2971]]. All these things are found in well-known texts, emanating from the doctors. — Now, as they have declared unto us that the <i>evil inclination</i> is Satan, who indubitably is an <i>angel</i>, — and who indeed is designated as an angel, since he is found among the number of the <i>sons of God</i>, — the <i>good inclination</i> also is in reality an angel[[fn:2972]]. Thus, when the doctors say, as all the world knows[[fn:2973]], that every man is accompanied by <i>two angels</i>, the one at his right hand and the other at his left, they refer to the good inclination and the evil inclination; and indeed they say expressly in the <i>Gemara</i> of <i>Shabbath:</i> «the one good, the other evil[[fn:2974]].» — Thou seest therefore how many wonderful things are revealed unto us by this word[[fn:2975]], and how many false ideas it causs to vanish.",
    "root.text.Part 3..21.8": "I believe that I have now set forth and thoroughly elucidated the history of Job[[fn:2976]]. But I desire also to set forth unto thee what is the opinion attributed unto Job and what is that which is attributed unto each of his friends, by alleging proofs which I shall collect from their respective discourses. One must not pay heed unto other words[[fn:2977]], necessitated by the whole of the discourse[[fn:2978]], as I have explained unto thee at the commencement of this treatise.",
    "root.text.Part 3..22.0": "This history of Job being admitted (as true), the matter upon which from the first[[fn:2979]] the five personages, that is to say Job and his friends, were in agreement, was that God had knowledge of all that had befallen Job, and that it was God who had smitten him with all these misfortunes. All likewise agreed in recognizing that God could not be charged with injustice and that one could not attribute iniquity unto Him. These are ideas which are frequently found repeated even in the words of Job[[fn:2980]]. If one considers the words which the five men exchange in their dialogue, one would be tempted to believe that what one says, all the others say likewise, and that the same ideas are repeated and cross one another. On the part of Job, they are interrupted only by the description which he makes of the violent pains and sufferings which he endurs despite his uprightness, by the painting of his righteousness, of his noble character and of the goodness of his actions. On their part, his friends mingle, in the discourses which they address unto him, exhortations to patience, consolations and gentle words, saying that he ought to be silent and not give rein unto his words, like one who contends with his fellow, but rather submit in silence[[fn:2981]] to the decrees of the Divinity. To which he replies that violent pains prevent one from being patient and steadfast, and from expressing oneself as is meet. All his friends agree in maintaining that they who do good are rewarded for it, and that they who do evil are punished for it. If, say they, one ses a sinner in happiness, one may be certain that the contrary shall take place in the future; he shall perish, and misfortunes shall fall upon him, upon his children[[fn:2982]] and upon his race. If, on the contrary, one ses a pious man in adversity, he cannot fail to obtain a reparation[[fn:2983]]. This idea thou shalt find repeated in the discourses of Eliphaz, of Bildad and of Zophar, and the three are in agreement upon this opinion. But this is not the aim of this whole history, in which it was intended rather to make known what each of them professed in particular and the opinion which he held upon an event where we see the man most upright and of the most perfect integrity smitten with the greatest and most violent calamities.",
    "root.text.Part 3..22.1.sub_0": "According to the opinion of Job, this event proved that the virtuous man and the impious are equal before God, who despiss the human species and abandons it[[fn:2984]]. It is thus that he says among other things: <i>It is all one; therefore I said it, He destroys the perfect and the wicked; if the scourge slay suddenly, He will laugh at the trial of the innocent</i> (Job, 9, 22-23); that is to say, if the torrent[[fn:2985]] arrivs suddenly, causs to perish and carries away all whom it encounters, He (God) laughs at the calamity of the innocent. He thereafter confirms this opinion by saying: <i>One dies in his full strength, being wholly at ease and quiet; his vessels are full of milk, etc. And another dies in the bitterness of his soul, and never eats with pleasure. They shall lie down alike in the dust, and the worms shall cover them</i> (XXI, 23-26). He further allegs for proof the prosperous state of the wicked and their happiness, and he dwells much thereupon: <i>Even when I remember</i>, says he, <i>I am afraid, and trembling takes hold on my flesh. Wherefore do the wicked live, become old, yea, are mighty in power? Their seed is established in their sight, etc</i>. (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 6-8). After having described this perfect happiness, he says unto his interlocutors: Let us admit that it be as ye pretend, that the children of this happy miscreant perish when he is no more and that their trace disappear, what injury results for this happy man from that which shall happen unto his family when he shall be no more? <i>For what pleasure has he in his house</i> (<i>which he leavs</i>) <i>after him</i>, <i>when the number of his months is cut off in the midst</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 21)? Elsewhere he declars that naught is to be hoped for after death, so that there remains naught else to say, save only that there is abandonment[[fn:2986]]. He expresses therefore his astonishment that God, having not neglected, in the beginning, the creation of the human individual, should yet neglect to govern him[[fn:2987]], and he says: <i>Hast thou not poured me out as milk</i>, <i>and curdled me like cheese, etc</i>. (X, 10)? — This is one of the opinions professed concerning Providence[[fn:2988]]. The doctors, as thou knowest, declare this opinion of Job to be extremely blameworthy[[fn:2989]], making use of expressions such as the following[[fn:2990]]: «Dust in the mouth of Job[[fn:2991]]. — Job sought to turn the dish upside down[[fn:2992]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..22.1.sub_1": "—Job denied the resurrection of the dead. —Job had set himself to utter blasphemies.” Yet if God says unto Eliphaz[[fn:2993]]: <i>for ye have not spoken of me the thing that is right, as my servant Job has</i> (42, 7), the doctors, to justify this[[fn:2994]], say: “A man is not held responsible in the hour of his suffering[[fn:2995]]”, which is to say that he (Job) was excusable by reason of his grievous sufferings. Nevertheless, such words accord not with the whole of this parable[[fn:2996]]. The cause (of the discourse in question) is none other than that which I shall set before thee[[fn:2997]]: it is that Job had returned from that most erroneous opinion and had himself demonstrated its falsity. This was but an opinion which arose at the first, especially in a man smitten by calamities and inwardly convinced of his own innocence, and this no man shall dispute; wherefore this opinion is attributed unto Job. However, he uttered all these discourses only so long as he remained in ignorance and knew God only by tradition, even as the multitude of religious men knows Him; but as soon as he attained a certain knowledge of God, he acknowledged that true felicity, which consists in the knowledge of God, is reserved for all those who know Him[[fn:2998]], and that none of all these calamities can disturb it in man. These imaginary felicities, such as health, wealth, and children, Job had regarded as the end, so long as he knew God only by tradition and not by reflection; wherefore he fell into all these wanderings and uttered these (blameworthy) discourses. Such is the meaning of these words: <i>I have heard of thee by the hearing of the ear; but now mine eye ses thee. Wherefore I abhor</i> (<i>all this</i>) <i>and repent in dust and ashes</i> (XLII, 5, 6). These words must be completed thus according to the sense: “Wherefore I despise all that I had formerly desired, and I repent of having been in dust and ashes[[fn:2999]];” for it is this situation which is attributed unto him (in saying): <i>And he sat down among the ashes</i> (II, 8). It is therefore because of this final discourse, which indicats true perception, that it is said of him immediately thereafter: <i>for ye have not spoken of me the thing that is right, as my servant Job has</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 3..22.2": "Eliphaz’s opinion concerning this occurrence is likewise one of the doctrines espoused regarding Providence. According to him, indeed, all the calamities which had befallen Job, he had merited; for he had committed sins which had deserved this fate[[fn:3000]]. This is what he says to Job: <i>Is not thy wickedness great</i>, <i>and thine iniquities infinite</i> (XXII, 5)? Elsewhere he says to Job: “The good deeds and virtuous conduct upon which thou dost rely[[fn:3001]] do not render thee necessarily a perfect man before God, in such wise that thou canst not be punished: <i>Assuredly</i>, <i>He places no confidence in His servants, He finds defects even in His angels</i>. <i>What then shall become of those who dwell in houses of clay, who have their foundation in the dust</i> (IV, 18-19)?” Eliphaz ceases not to reiterate this thought[[fn:3002]]; I mean to say that he believes all that befalls man must have been merited by him, but that the faults by which we earn chastisement elude our perception, and (that we are ignorant) in what manner they have brought us the punishment.",
    "root.text.Part 3..22.3": "The opinion of Bildad the Shuhite upon this question is that which admitts <i>compensation</i>[[fn:3003]]. Verily, he says unto Job: “These great misfortunes, if so be that thou art pure and hast committed no sin, have for their reason to make thee deserve a reward so much the greater; and certes thou shalt have the fairest compensation[[fn:3004]]. All this, therefore, is for thy good, to the end that the happiness which thou shalt enjoy in the future may be so much the greater.” Such is the sense of those words which he addresses to Job: <i>If thou wert pure and upright; surely now he would awake for thee, and make the habitation of thy righteousness prosperous. Though thy beginning was small, yet thy latter end should greatly increase</i> (VIII, 6 and 7). Thou knowest that this opinion concerning Providence is very widespread, and we have already set it forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..22.4": "The opinion of Zophar the Naamathite is that which admitts that all depends upon the sole will of God, that one must seek no reason in the divine actions, and that one must in no wise ask why He has done this thing or that thing[[fn:3005]]. Wherefore, in all that God does, one must seek neither a reason of justice nor a requirement of His <i>wisdom</i>, for it pertains to His greatness[[fn:3006]] and to His true essence to do that which He <i>wills</i>, whilst we are incapable of penetrating into the secrets of His wisdom, which requirs that He act according to His <i>will</i> and without any other reason[[fn:3007]]. This is what he says unto Job: <i>But oh that God would speak, and open his lips against thee; and that he would shew thee the secrets of wisdom, for they are double to that which is</i>[[fn:3008]]! <i>Canst thou by searching find out God</i> (<i>the secret</i>)? <i>Canst thou find out the Almighty unto perfection</i> (XI, 5, 6, 7)?",
    "root.text.Part 3..22.5": "Thou seest, consequently, if thou reflectest thereon, how this history, which has so much disquieted men, has been (wisely) disposed, so as to bring forward the different opinions concerning Providence which we have previously set forth[[fn:3009]]. Each of these opinions is there presented apart[[fn:3010]], and is attributed to one of the men of antiquity, renowned for virtue and science[[fn:3011]], if so be that it is a parable; or else they may indeed have spoken thus in reality, if it be a true history. Thus, the opinion attributed unto Job is conformable to that of Aristotle; the opinion of Eliphaz to that of our religion; the opinion of Bildad corresponds to the doctrine of the Mutazilites; and lastly, the opinion of Zophar corresponds to the doctrine of the Asharites. These are the ancient opinions concerning Providence.",
    "root.text.Part 3..22.6.sub_0": "Thou perceivest then a new opinion, which is that attributed to Elihu. For this reason[[fn:3012]] is the latter deemed superior to those others[[fn:3013]], and it is declared that, albeit he was the youngest amongst them, he surpassed them in knowledge. He beginns by reprimanding[[fn:3014]] Job, whom he taxes with folly for having displayed pride and for being astonished at the misfortunes which befell him, although he had done naught but good; for he had long boasted of his deeds. Then he likewise taxes with dotage[[fn:3015]] the opinion of the three friends of Job concerning Providence, and he employs expressions so singularly enigmatic, that the reader, in considering his words, is at first astonished, believing that he adds absolutely nothing to that which Eliphaz, Bildad, and Zophar had said, and that, on the contrary, he does but repeat their ideas in other terms and with greater development. Verily, he does naught else but reprimand[[fn:3016]] Job, describe the divine justice and the wonders of the universe, and (proclaim) that God is affected neither by the virtue of the pious man, nor by the sin of the impious[[fn:3017]]; yet all these things the friends of Job had already spoken. Nevertheless, by reflecting thereon, thou shalt understand the new idea which he has introduced and which was his aim, an idea which none of them had expressed aforetime. With this idea, however, he has repeated all that those others had said, even as all of them, Job and his three friends, each repeats the idea expressed by the others, as I have already told thee; and this has for its purpose to conceal that which is peculiar to the personal opinion of each, so that it seems to the vulgar that they all meet in one and the same opinion, although it be not so. The idea which Elihu adds and which none of them had expressed, is that which he presents allegorically by <i>the intercession of an angel</i>. It is, says he, a thing attested and well known, that when a man is sick unto death and when he is despaired of, if he has an angel, no matter which, to intercede for him[[fn:3018]], the intercession of the latter shall be accepted, the sick man shall recover[[fn:3019]], shall be saved, and shall return to the best possible state. Nevertheless, this cannot continue alway, and there can be no continual intercession for evermore, but only twice or thrice[[fn:3020]]. This is what he says: <i>If there be a messenger with him, an interpreter, etc</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 3..22.6.sub_1": "(XXXIII, 23); and, after describing the progressive state of the convalescent and the joy occasioned by his return to perfect health[[fn:3021]], he adds: <i>All this</i>, <i>God does twice or thrice for man</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 29).— This is an idea which is expounded by Elihu alone; but what he further adds, before (expressing) this idea, is that he commences by describing[[fn:3022]] how prophetic inspiration arises, saying: <i>For God speaks once, yea twice, though man perceive it not. In a dream</i>, <i>a vision of the night</i>, <i>when deep sleep falls upon men etc.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 14 and 15). Subsequently, he confirms this opinion[[fn:3023]], and to demonstrate in what manner it must be understood, he describes a series of physical phenomena, such as thunder, lightning, rain, the breath of the winds. He also intermingles therewith many matters concerning living beings, speaking, for example, of the outbreak of pestilence; <i>in a moment they die in the midst of the night etc.</i> (XXXIV, 20), of great wars which erupt: <i>He breaks in pieces mighty men without number, and setts others in their stead</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 24), and of many other similar matters[[fn:3024]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..22.7": "Thou shalt find likewise that, in the revelation which Job had[[fn:3025]], and whereby it became clear unto him that he had been mistaken in all that he had imagined, there is naught but a constant description of physical things, whether they be elements, or meteors, or the nature of different species of animals, and naught else. If mention be there made of the ethereal regions, of the heavens, of Orion, and of the Pleiades, it is because of their influence upon our atmosphere; for all the objects unto which God calls the attention of Job belong only to the sublunary world. Thus Elihu also draws his admonitions from the different species of animals: <i>Who teaches us</i>, says he, <i>more than the beasts of the earth</i>, <i>and makes us wiser than the fowls of heaven</i> (XXXV, 11). In this discourse (of God), the description of the <i>Leviathan</i> is mainly dwelt upon, which is an assemblage of various corporeal properties belonging to animals that walk, swim, or fly[[fn:3026]] — By all these things it is intended to signify that our intelligences arrive not at the understanding of how these physical things came to be, which exist in the world of <i>generation</i> and <i>corruption</i>[[fn:3027]], nor at the conception of the origin of the physical force existing within them. These are not things which resemble that which we ourselves do; and how could we desire to establish a comparison between the manner in which God governs and cars for them, and the manner in which we govern and care for that which is committed to our government and care? Verily, it behoves us to stay our steps at this little (which we know thereof) and to believe that nothing can be hidden from God, as Elihu says: <i>For his eyes are upon the ways of man, and he ses all his goings. There is no darkness</i>, <i>nor shadow of death</i>, <i>where the workers of iniquity may hide themselves</i> (XXXIV, 21-22). But the idea of the Providence of God is not the same as our own[[fn:3028]], and the idea of the <i>government</i> of which creatures are the object on the part of God is not the same as the government which we exercise; these two (respective things) fall not under one and the same definition, as is believed through error, and have nothing in common save the mere <i>name</i>, even as our <i>action</i> and that of God resemble not each other and fall not under one and the same definition. Just as the works of nature differ from the works of art, so does the divine government, the divine Providence, and the divine intention, of which these physical things are the object, differ from our human government, from our foresight and our intention regarding the things which are the object thereof.",
    "root.text.Part 3..22.8": "The whole book of Job has for its purpose to establish this article of faith and to call attention to the proofs which may be deduced from physical things, so that thou mayest not err and desire, in thy imagination, to establish a comparison between the knowledge of God and our own, or (to believe) that the intention, the Providence, and the government of God resemble our intention, our foresight, and our government. The man who shall have been penetrated with this shall easily endure every calamity. Misfortunes shall no longer cause him to doubt concerning God, nor (shall he ask himself) whether God has knowledge thereof or not, whether He cars (for man) or whether He forsaks him; on the contrary, they shall inspire him with more love, as is said at the end of this revelation: <i>Wherefore I abhor myself, and repent in dust and ashes</i>[[fn:3029]], and as the doctors express it: «Those who act from the love of God and rejoice in sufferings, etc.[[fn:3030]]»",
    "root.text.Part 3..22.9": "If thou considerest all that I have just said with the attention which the reading of this treatise requirs, and if thou then examinest the book of Job, thou shalt understand the sense thereof, and thou shalt find that I have summarized all its ideas, leaving aside only that which is a mere ornament of style and serves only to complete the allegory[[fn:3031]], as I have expounded unto thee several times in this Treatise.",
    "root.text.Part 3..23.0": "The conception of <i>trial</i>[[fn:3032]] is likewise most obscure, and constituts one of the greatest difficulties of religion. The Law speaks thereof in six passages, as I shall set forth unto thee in this chapter[[fn:3033]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..23.1": "As for the manner in which the vulgar generally understand the notion of <i>trial</i>—to wit, that God sends calamities upon a man, although he has committed no sin, and for the purpose of granting him a reward all the greater—this is a principle which is nowhere expressly mentioned by any text of the Law; and, among the six passages of the Pentateuch, there is but one alone which, taken literally, might lead one to believe such a thing[[fn:3034]]; I shall explain the sense thereof further on. This opinion is even in opposition to that which the Law lays down as a principle; for it is said: <i>a God of truth and without iniquity</i> (Deut. xxxii. 4).",
    "root.text.Part 3..23.2": "Neither is this vulgar opinion admitted by all the doctors; for there be some who say: “No death without sin, and no punishment without transgression[[fn:3035]].” Verily, it is this latter opinion that every religious man endowed with intelligence must admit; nor ought he to attribute injustice unto God, so as to believe that Zeid is pure from all sin, that he is a perfect man, and that he has in no wise deserved that which has befallen him.",
    "root.text.Part 3..23.3": "As touching the <i>trials</i> which the Pentateuch mentions in the passages in question, their object, in appearance, is to make an experiment and an inquiry, in order to know the degree[[fn:3036]] of faith of such a man or such a nation, or the degree of his piety. But herein lies precisely the great difficulty[[fn:3037]], and particularly in the history of the sacrifice of Isaac, which was known only to God and to the two personages[[fn:3038]], to one of whom it was said[[fn:3039]]: <i>for now I know that thou fearest God</i> (Gen. xxii. 12). It is the same with this passage: <i>for the Lord your God proves you, to know whether ye love etc.</i> (Deut. xiii. 4), and with this other passage: <i>to know what was in thine heart</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, viii. 2). I shall now resolve for thee all these difficulties.",
    "root.text.Part 3..23.4": "Know that whenever, in the Pentateuch, mention is made of a <i>trial</i>, it has no other end and no other object than to make known unto men what they ought to do or what they ought to believe. Consequently, the <i>trial</i> consists, so to speak, in the performance of a certain act in which one has not in view the act itself, which, on the contrary, is proposed only as an example that men should follow and take for a model. Thus, when it is said: <i>to know whether ye love etc.</i> (Deut. xiii. 4), it signifies not: <i>that God might know it</i>, for He already knew it; but it is similar[[fn:3040]] to this other passage: <i>that ye may know that I am the Lord that does sanctify you</i> (Exod. xxxi. 13). There[[fn:3041]], the meaning is: <i>that the nations may know;</i> and likewise He says here: “If there arise a man who arrogats to himself prophecy, and ye see his wonders[[fn:3042]] which cause men to believe that he speaks truly, ye shall know that this is a thing <i>whereby God wills to make known unto the nations</i> to what degree ye are imbued with His Law, how far ye are capable of understanding the true being of God, that ye suffer not yourselves to be deceived by the guile of an impostor, and that your faith in God is not shaken; and this shall serve as a support for all those who aspire unto the truth, so that they shall seek beliefs which are sufficiently firm that, in their presence, no further regard be paid to the worker of miracles[[fn:3043]]. Indeed, such a one would invite belief in that which is impossible[[fn:3044]]; but it can only be of use to have recourse to miracles when that which is proclaimed is possible, as we have set forth in the <i>Mishneh Torah</i>[[fn:3045]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..23.5": "— Since, therefore, it is manifest that the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">לדעת</span>, <i>to know</i>, signifies here: <i>that men may know</i>, it shall be likewise with that which is said concerning the manna: <i>to humble thee, and to prove thee, to know what was in thine heart, whether thou wouldest keep his commandments, or no</i> (Deut. viii. 2); that is to say, that nations may know it, and that it may be published throughout the whole world that they who devote themselves to the service of God receive their sustenance in an unexpected manner. It is in exactly the same sense that, where the fall of the manna is first spoken of, it is said: <i>that I may prove them, whether they will walk in my law, or no</i> (Exodus xvi. 4); which is to say, that every man may draw therefrom a lesson and see whether it be profitable to devote oneself to the service of God, and whether this be sufficient, or no. As touching that which is said concerning the manna for a third time: <i>Who fed thee in the wilderness with manna, which thy fathers knew not, that he might humble thee, and that he might prove thee, to do thee good at thy latter end</i> (Deut. viii. 16), this passage might lead one to believe that God sometimes afflicts man that he may subsequently obtain a reward so much the greater; but, in reality, it is not so. This passage expresses rather one of the two following ideas: 1st, the idea repeated concerning the manna in the first and second passages, that is to say, that it may be known whether it suffices, or no, to be devoted to God in order to have food and to be sheltered from fatigues and pains; or else 2nd, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הסותך</span> would here signify <i>to accustom thee</i>, a sense which the same verb has in this passage: <i>Who had not been accustomed</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נִסְּתָה</span>) <i>to adventure to set the sole of her foot, etc.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xxviii. 56), so that it would have been said here: God first accustomed you to hardship in the wilderness that you might enjoy a greater well-being when you should have entered the land (of Canaan); and this is true, for it is sweeter to pass from hardship unto rest than to be always at rest. It is also known that, had they not suffered misery and hardship in the wilderness, they would not have been able to conquer the land, nor to fight; which the Pentateuch says expressly: <i>for God said, Lest perhaps the people repent when they see war, and they return to Egypt: But God led the people about, through the way of the wilderness of the Red Sea</i> (Exodus xiii. 17 and 18). Indeed, ease and prosperity do cause valiancy to vanish, whilst privations and fatigues do engender it, and therein lies the <i>good</i> which the passage in question promiss them <i>at their latter end</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 3..23.6": "As for this passage: <i>for God has come to test you</i> (Exodus 20:20), it has the same meaning as that of Deuteronomy, where it is said, concerning the one who prophesies in the name of a false god: <i>for the Lord your God is testing you</i> (Deut. 13:4), the meaning of which we have already explained. Here then, in the scene at Mount Sinai, he says to them: Fear not, for this great spectacle you have seen took place solely so that you might, by your own sight, acquire a certain conviction, and so that, if the Lord your God, to manifest your great faith, should test you by a false prophet who would invite you to overturn what you have heard, you would remain firm, without wavering[[fn:3046]]; for if I had presented myself to you as a prophet, as you wished[[fn:3047]], and if I had reported to you what had been said to me, without you having heard it yourselves, it might be that you would deem true what was reported to you by another, even if he came to overturn what I had announced, since you would not have heard it yourselves in this spectacle.",
    "root.text.Part 3..23.7": "As for the story of Abraham regarding the sacrifice of Isaac, it contains two great ideas that are fundamental to religion. The first is to inform us to what limit the love and fear of God must extend. He was commanded (Abraham) to do something to which neither the sacrifice of wealth nor even the sacrifice of life can be compared; it was indeed the most extraordinary thing[[fn:3048]] that can happen in the world, one of those things that human nature cannot be believed capable of accepting. Imagine a childless man[[fn:3049]], animated by an extreme desire to have children, possessing great fortune and status, and desiring that his lineage become a nation: if he has a son, after having despaired of having one, what love, what passion he will have for this son! However, fearing God and desiring to obey His command, he sets little store[[fn:3050]] by this beloved son, renounces all he had hoped for from him, and consents to sacrifice him after several days of travel. And indeed, if he had hastened to do it at the very moment he received the command, it would have been an act of recklessness and haste, without much reflection; but to do such a thing several days after having received the command was an act that presupposed thought and mature reflection, the respect that the command of God deserved, as well as the love and fear of God. Certainly, one must not assume other circumstances, nor suppose (in Abraham) any impression whatsoever[[fn:3051]]; for if our father Abraham hastened to sacrifice Isaac, it was not out of fear that God would put him to death or make him poor, but solely because it is the duty[[fn:3052]] of mortals to love and fear God, independent of any hope of reward or any fear of punishment, as we have explained in several places. Thus, if the angel says to him: <i>for now I know that thou fearest God</i> (Genesis 22:12), it means: this act, by which you merit, in the absolute sense, (the epithet of) <span dir=\"rtl\">ירא אלהים</span>, <i>God-fearing</i>, will make known to all mortals how far the fear of God must go.",
    "root.text.Part 3..23.8": "Thou shalt know that this idea has been confirmed and set forth in the Law, where it is said that the sum of the whole Law, all that it contains in the way of commands, prohibitions, promises, and narrations, has but one single object, which is the fear of God. These are the terms: <i>If thou wilt not observe to do all the words of this law that are written in this book, that thou mayest fear this glorious and fearful name, etc.</i> (Deut. xxviii. 58). — Such is one of the two ideas which were intended in the (narrative of the) sacrifice of Isaac.",
    "root.text.Part 3..23.9": "The second idea is to let us know that prophets must accept as real what revelation brings them from God; for one must not imagine that, because this revelation takes place—as we have explained—in a dream or in a vision, and by means of the imaginative faculty, it follows that what the prophets hear or what is presented to them in a parable might not be certain, or at least that some element of doubt is mixed therein. It was therefore intended to let us know that everything the prophet sees in the prophetic vision is real and certain to him, that he doubts none of it, and that he considers it in the same manner as all real things perceived by the senses or the intellect. The proof of this is that Abraham hastened to sacrifice his <i>only son whom he loved</i>[[fn:3053]], as he had been commanded, although this command had reached him in a dream or in a vision. But if the prophetic dream had been obscure to the prophets, if some doubt or uncertainty[[fn:3054]] had remained in them regarding what they perceived in the prophetic vision, they would not have hastened to do what is repugnant to human nature, and Abraham would not have consented to perform, in doubt, an act of such high gravity[[fn:3055]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..23.10": "Verily, it was meet that this event—I mean the sacrifice in question—should occur through the mediation of Abraham and unto a man such as Isaac[[fn:3056]]; for our father Abraham was the first to make known the unity of God and to establish prophecy[[fn:3057]], so as to perpetuate this belief and to draw men towards it, even as it is said: <i>For I have known him, to the end that he may command his children and his household after him, that they may keep the way of the Lord, to do righteousness and justice</i> (Genesis, xviii. 19). Even as they followed the true and profitable opinions which they had heard from him, so ought one to follow the opinions derived from his acts[[fn:3058]], and notably from this act whereby he established the fundamental principle of the truth of prophecy[[fn:3059]], and whereby he has made known unto us the measure to which the fear and love of God must extend.",
    "root.text.Part 3..23.11": "Such are the notions that one must form concerning <i>trials</i>, and it must not be believed that God wills to try and to test a thing[[fn:3060]], in order to know that which He knew not before. How far is He exalted above that which ignorant and foolish men imagine in their perverse thoughts! Thou must well possess thyself of this truth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..24.0": "Actions, with respect to their end, may be divided into four kinds: the idle action, the frivolous action, the vain action, and the good and useful action. That which is called a <i>vain</i> action is that which is performed for a certain end, which end, however, is not attained, because certain hindrances oppose it. Ofttimes wilt thou hear one say: “I have laboured in <i>vain</i>[[fn:3061]]”, when he has wearied himself in seeking a person whom he has not found, or when he has undertaken a toilsome journey without having made a gainful traffic. It is likewise said: “Our trouble for this sick man has been <i>vain</i>”, when the latter has not returned to health. It is the same with all actions whereby one seeks to attain an end; (they are called <i>vain</i>) when the end is not reached. — The <i>idle</i> action is that whereby one aims at no end whatsoever[[fn:3062]], even as certain folk, whilst meditating, play with their hands, and as do the distracted and the heedless. — The <i>frivolous</i> action is that in which one has in view an insignificant end, I mean, whereby one aims at a thing which is not necessary and which has not even any great utility; thus, for instance, when one dancs without having for his end the taking of exercise, or when one does things which aim at provoking laughter, these are indubitably things that shall be called <i>frivolous</i>. But herein a distinction must be made, according to the purpose and the worth of those who act; for there be many things which, in the eyes of some, are necessary or very useful, whereas, according to others, there is no need of them. Thus, for example, in the eyes of those who understand medicine, the different kinds of bodily exercise are necessary for the preservation of health; and, in the eyes of the learned, writing is a very useful thing. He, therefore, who, for the sake of his health, betaks himself to exercises, such as the game of ball, wrestling, boxing, or the holding of the breath, or who, for the sake of writing, mends the <i>kalam</i> (reed) or prepars paper, performs in the eyes of the ignorant a <i>frivolous</i> act, which yet is not frivolous in the eyes of the learned. — The <i>good and useful</i> action is that which is performed for a noble end, I mean necessary or useful, and which causs that end to be attained.",
    "root.text.Part 3..24.1": "— This, methinks, is a classification against which no objection can be raised. Verily, he who performs any act whatever, at one time aims at a certain end, and at another has no end; and the end in view is sometimes noble, sometimes insignificant, and sometimes it is attained, and sometimes it is not. This classification is therefore of absolute necessity.",
    "root.text.Part 3..24.2": "After this exposition, I say: An intelligent man could not maintain that any action of God can be vain, or idle, or frivolous. According to the opinion of all of us who follow[[fn:3063]] the Law of Moses our master, all His actions are good and useful, even as it is said: <i>And God saw every thing that He had made, and, behold, it was very good</i> (Genesis, i. 31). Consequently, all that God does for the sake of aught is either necessary or very useful for the existence of that thing which He has in view. Thus, for instance, food is necessary to the animal for its preservation, and eyes are very useful to it for that purpose; wherefore food has no other end than to preserve the animal for a certain time, and the senses have no other end than the utility which their perceptions afford to the animal. Such is also the opinion of the philosophers, to wit, that there is nothing idle[[fn:3064]] in any of the physical things; that is to say, that all things which are not artificial presuppose actions having a certain end, whether we know that end or be ignorant of it. But according to that sect among the thinkers[[fn:3065]] which claims that God does nothing for the sake of another thing, that there are neither causes nor effects, but that, on the contrary, all the actions of God are but the result of His will alone, that one must not seek an end for them, nor ask why He has done such a thing, that He does what pleass Him and that it is not the result of <i>wisdom</i>—according to those (I say), the actions of God would enter into the category of <i>idle</i> things, or rather they would be below the idle action; for, if it be true that the author of such an action aims at no end, at least he cars not for what he does; whereas God, according to them, knows well what He does, and yet He does it without any end, nor for any utility.",
    "root.text.Part 3..24.3": "But, that which appears inadmissible from the very outset, is that there should be in the actions of God aught that is <i>frivolous;</i> and one must pay no heed to the folly of those who have contended that the ape was created for the diversion of man. That which has given rise to such notions, is that they were ignorant of the nature of <i>generation</i> and of <i>corruption</i>, and that they forgat a fundamental principle, to wit, that it is with intention that God has brought into being all <i>possible</i> things such as we behold them; His wisdom willed not that they should be otherwise; and, consequently, that would be <i>impossible</i>, for things must be such as His wisdom has required.",
    "root.text.Part 3..24.4.sub_0": "As for those who have said that God, in all that He has wrought, had in view no end, they were necessarily led thereto by considering the whole of being from the standpoint of their opinion[[fn:3066]]; for having asked themselves what might be the end of the existence of the world in its entirety, they necessarily answered, as do all those who maintain the novelty of the world: “Even so has He <i>willed</i> it, without having any other reason.” Thereafter they continued this reasoning with respect to all the details of the universe, so that, for example, far from agreeing that the perforation of the uvea and the transparency of the cornea had for their end to give passage to the <i>visual spirit</i>[[fn:3067]] in order to produce perception, they denied on the contrary that such was the cause of vision. It is not, said they, in view of vision that this membrane was perforated and that which is above was rendered transparent, but it is thus that God willed it, although vision were possible had it been otherwise. We have certain biblical passages whose literal sense, at first sight, might give rise to such a notion. It is said, for instance: <i>The Lord has done whatsoever He pleased</i> (Ps. 135, 6); <i>and what His soul desirs, even that He does</i> (Job 23, 13); and elsewhere: <i>and who shall say unto Him, What doest Thou?</i> (Eccles. VIII, 4.) But the sense of these passages and other like ones is this: That which God <i>wills</i> is necessarily done, and there is naught that can hinder the accomplishment of His will. [However, God wills only that which is possible; not all that is possible, but only that which is required by His wisdom[[fn:3068]]]. Likewise, the absolutely good work which God wills to perform cannot be stayed by any obstacle, and naught can prevent it[[fn:3069]]. Such is the opinion of every theologian and that of the philosophers, and such also is our own; for, although we believe that the world was <i>created</i>, all our doctors and all our sages admit that this event took place not by the sole will of God; but they say that divine wisdom, which we are unable to comprehend, necessitated the existence of this whole universe at the moment when it came into being, and that this same invariable wisdom had necessitated non-existence before the world existed.",
    "root.text.Part 3..24.4.sub_1": "Thou shalt find this idea often repeated[[fn:3070]] among the doctors, for example in the explanation of this passage: <i>He has made every thing beautiful in his time</i> (Ecclesiastes 3, 11)[[fn:3071]]. By all this, they sought to avoid that which it is indeed meet to avoid, to wit, (admitting) that the Agent (that is to say, God) might accomplish an act, without having in view any end whatsoever. Such is the belief of the doctors of our Law, and it is this also which our prophets have declared, namely, that the actions of nature, even unto the smallest details, are wisely ordered and are linked one to another, that they are all causes and effects (of one another), and that none of them is either idle, or frivolous, or vain, but that on the contrary they are actions of a perfect wisdom, as it is said: <i>O Lord, how manifold are Thy works! in wisdom hast Thou made them all</i> (Ps. 104, 24); and elsewhere: <i>And all His works are solid</i> (Ps. 33, 4); and yet elsewhere: <i>The Lord by wisdom has founded the earth</i> (Prov. 3, 19). Such expressions are frequent, and the contrary opinion cannot be admitted. Philosophical speculation likewise decids that, in all the works of nature, there is naught idle, nor frivolous, nor vain, and with even greater reason in the nature of the celestial spheres; for these, by reason of their noble matter, are more solid and more regular.",
    "root.text.Part 3..24.5": "It must be known that the greater part of the false opinions which have brought such perplexity into the researches concerning the final cause, whether of the whole universe or of each of its parts, have no other source[[fn:3072]] than, on the one hand, the error in which man was concerning himself, imagining that the whole universe exists only for him, and, on the other hand, his ignorance both as touching the nature of this inferior matter and as touching the primary end of the Creator, which was to bring into existence all that whose existence was possible, existence being indubitably the good[[fn:3073]]. It is from this error and from the ignorance of these two things that doubts and perplexity arise, so that one imagins that, among the actions of God, there be some that are frivolous, or idle, or vain. Know that those who have resigned themselves to this absurdity, so that for them the divine actions resemble idle actions which have absolutely no end, have sought thereby only to avoid making them depend upon a <i>wisdom</i>, fearing lest that should lead to professing <i>the eternity of the world</i>[[fn:3074]]; they have therefore closed the door to this opinion. But I have already informed thee what is in this regard the opinion of our Law, and that it is this opinion which must be admitted; for we say naught absurd in maintaining that the being and non-being of all these acts depend upon the divine wisdom, but that we are often ignorant how this wisdom manifests itself in the works of God. It is upon this opinion that the whole Law of Moses, our master, is based. It says at the beginning: <i>And God saw every thing that He had made, and, behold, it was very good</i> (Genesis 1, 31), and it says toward the end: <i>The Rock</i> (the Creator), <i>His work is perfect</i> (Deut. 32, 4.) Thou must well penetrate thyself with this. If thou examinest this opinion, as well as the philosophical opinion, by studying all the preceding chapters of this treatise which relate to this subject, thou shalt find that the two opinions differ in respect of none of the smallest details of the universe[[fn:3075]]. Thou shalt find only the single difference which we have already set forth[[fn:3076]], to wit, that, according to them (the philosophers), the world is eternal, whereas, according to us, it is created. Understand this well.",
    "root.text.Part 3..25.0.sub_0": "Even as the speculative theologians differ upon the question of whether the actions of God depend upon His wisdom or whether they depend solely upon His will without having absolutely any end, likewise they differ in the manner of considering the laws which He has prescribed for us. In effect, there be those who attribute to these latter no reason and who maintain that all laws depend upon the sole will (of God), whilst others maintain that all that is prescribed or forbidden depends upon divine wisdom and aims at a certain end, that all laws have a reason and that they have been prescribed in view of some utility. However, we (Israelites), all of us, whether of the common people or of the learned, believe that they all have a reason, but that in part we are ignorant of the reasons, not knowing in what manner they are conformable to the divine wisdom[[fn:3077]]. Passages of Scripture say this clearly: <i>statutes and judgments righteous</i> (Deut. IV, 8); <i>the judgments of the Lord are truth, they are righteous altogether</i> (Ps. 19, 10). The <i>statutes</i> or regulations called <span dir=\"rtl\">חֻקּׅים</span>, for example those relating to garments of heterogeneous materials, to meat seethed in milk, to the scapegoat[[fn:3078]], and concerning which the doctors express themselves in these terms: “Things which I have prescribed for thee, upon which thou art not permitted to reflect, of which Satan makes the object of his criticism, and which the gentiles refute[[fn:3079]],” — (these regulations, I say), the doctors in general do not consider them as things which are absolutely without reason and for which an end must not be sought; for that would lead us to (attribute to God) <i>idle</i> actions, as we have said. All the doctors believe on the contrary that they necessarily have a reason, I mean an end of utility; but this reason escaps us because of the weakness of our intelligence or our lack of instruction. According to them, therefore, all the commandments have a reason, I mean that every prescription or prohibition has an end of utility; sometimes the utility thereof is evident to us, as that of the prohibition of murder and theft; sometimes the utility thereof is not so evident[[fn:3080]], as for example when one forbidds the use of the first products of trees[[fn:3081]] or the mixing of the vineyard (with other plants[[fn:3082]]).",
    "root.text.Part 3..25.0.sub_1": "Those commandments, the utility of which is evident to all, are termed <span dir=\"rtl\">משפטים</span>, <i>laws</i> or <i>ordinances</i>, and those, the utility of which is not generally manifest, are styled <span dir=\"rtl\">חקים</span>, <i>statutes</i> or <i>regulations</i>. They frequently declare: «<i>for it is not a vain thing for you</i> (Deut., XXXII, 47), and if it be vain, it is of your own accord[[fn:3083]];» signifying thereby: This legislation is not a vain enactment devoid of useful purpose, and should it appear thus to you regarding certain precepts, the fault lies with your own apprehension. You are acquainted with that widely prevalent tradition amongst us, that Solomon comprehended the reason for all the commandments, with the exception of those pertaining to the <i>red heifer</i>[[fn:3084]]; and similarly with this opinion of the doctors, namely, that God concealed the reason for the commandments, lest they should be neglected, as befell Solomon in the case of the three commandments whose reason is expressly indicated[[fn:3085]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..25.1": "This principle they do constantly proclaim in all their discourses, and the texts of the Sacred Books do likewise indicate it. Nevertheless, I have found a passage of the Doctors in <i>Bereshith Rabba</i> which seems at first sight to say that certain commandments had no other reason than that of prescribing something[[fn:3086]], without any other end or real utility being in view. Thus it is there said: «What matters it to the Holy One whether one slays animals by the throat or by the nape of the neck? Thou mayest therefore infer thence that the commandments have no other purpose than that of refining men[[fn:3087]], as it is said: <i>The word of the Lord is pure</i> (Ps., 18, 31)[[fn:3088]].» Albeit these words be passing strange, the Doctors nowhere else pronouncing in a like sense, I have interpreted them, as thou shalt hear, in such wise as not to abandon the doctrine which they have constantly proclaimed[[fn:3089]], and not to depart from the principle upon which all are agreed, to wit, that all commandments have a truly useful end: <i>for it is not a vain thing</i> (Deut., XXXII, 47), and as it is said elsewhere: <i>I have not said unto the seed of Jacob: Seek ye Me in vain; I am the Lord, speaking righteousness, declaring things that are right</i> (Isaiah, 45, 19). That which every man of sound reason ought to believe in this regard is as follows: the general enactments of the commandments[[fn:3090]] have necessarily a reason and have been prescribed in view of a certain utility; but the <i>particular details</i>, it has been said, have no other end than to prescribe something[[fn:3091]]. Thus, for example, the precept of slaying animals for the need of good nourishment is of manifest utility, as we shall set forth[[fn:3092]]. But, if it be said that they must be slain by the upper part of the throat, and not by the lower[[fn:3093]], and that the gullet and the windpipe must be cut in a determined place, these and similar regulations have no other end than that of <i>refining men</i>[[fn:3094]]. This is what results from their manner of expression: «Whether one slays by the throat or by the nape.» Verily, I have only cited this example because we read in the passage of the Doctors these words: «Whether one slays by the throat or by the nape;» but if the matter be well examined, the truth is this[[fn:3095]]: As there is a necessity to feed upon the flesh of animals, the intention was to inflict the easiest death upon them and at once to obtain this in the simplest manner [for, to decapitate, a sword or other similar instrument would be required, whereas one may slay with any instrument whatsoever], and to bring about death more easily, it was made a condition that the knife be passing sharp.",
    "root.text.Part 3..25.2": "A more exact example of the <i>particular details</i> is found in the sacrifices. Indeed, the precept of offering sacrifices has a great and manifest utility, as I shall expound[[fn:3096]]. But, whether the victim be now a lamb, now a ram, and that the victims be of a fixed number, these are things for which no reason can ever be given. In my judgment, all those who take the trouble to seek reasons for any of these particular details betray a great folly; and so far from removing thereby any absurdity they might possess, they do but increase the absurdities. He who imagins that these details can be motivated is as far from the truth as he who believs that the general precept has no real utility.",
    "root.text.Part 3..25.3": "It must be known that it is the Divine Wisdom that willed,—or, if thou preferest, thou shalt say it is necessity that required,—that there should be particular details for which no reason could be assigned; and it was in some wise impossible that there should not be in the Law things of this nature. I say that this was impossible; for if thou shouldst ask, for example, why (one must offer) a <i>lamb</i> and not a <i>ram</i>, the very same question might be asked if <i>ram</i> had been said instead of <i>lamb</i>, since some species was necessary. Likewise, if thou shouldst ask why <i>seven</i> lambs and not <i>eight</i>, the same question might be asked if <i>eight</i>, or <i>ten</i>, or <i>twenty</i> had been said, since some number was necessarily required. This resembls in some wise the nature of the <i>possible</i>[[fn:3097]], where it must needs be that one of the possible things should happen,",
    "root.text.Part 3..25.4": "without one having the right to demand why this one among those things took place and not another among the possible things; for the same question might be asked if another possible thing had been realised instead of that one. Thou must well imbibe this notion. If the Doctors say often that all the commandments have their reasons, and likewise (if it be said) that these reasons were known to Solomon, it pertains to the utility of such a commandment considered in its generality, without pursuing all the details thereof.",
    "root.text.Part 3..25.5": "This being so, I have deemed it right to divide the six hundred and thirteen commandments into several classes, of which each contains a certain number of commandments of the same kind, or at least of a like nature. I shall make known unto thee the rationale of each of these classes, by demonstrating that it has an indubitable and incontestable utility. Thereupon I shall return to each of the commandments comprised in that class, and explain the reason thereof, so that there shall remain but a very small number of commandments<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3098]]</sup>, the motive of which is hitherto unknown to me. I have also been able to explain the particular ordinances and conditions pertaining to certain commandments, of which it is possible to give the reason. Thou shalt hear all this hereafter.",
    "root.text.Part 3..25.6": "But all these motives (of the commandments), I shall be unable to set forth until I have first provided several chapters containing useful preliminaries to serve as preparation for the subject I have in view; these be the chapters which I shall now begin.",
    "root.text.Part 3..26.0": "The Law as a whole aims at two things, to wit: the well-being of the soul and the well-being of the body.",
    "root.text.Part 3..26.1": "As for the well-being of the soul, it consists in this: that all men should possess sound ideas according to their respective faculties<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3099]]</sup>. Regarding this, the Scripture speaks sometimes in plain terms, and sometimes by way of allegories<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3100]]</sup>; for it is not in the nature of the vulgar sort to possess the capacity requisite for comprehending such a subject in its full reality.",
    "root.text.Part 3..26.2": "As for the well-being of the body, it is attained through the improvement of the manner of life of men one with another. This result is achieved by two means: first, by removing mutual violence from among men, so that the individual may not permit himself to act according to his own pleasure and the power he possesses<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3101]]</sup>, but shall be constrained<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3102]]</sup> to do that which is profitable unto all; secondly, by causing each individual to acquire morals useful for social life<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3103]]</sup>, in order that the interests of society may be well ordered.",
    "root.text.Part 3..26.3": "It must be known that, of these two aims (of the Law), the one is indubitably of a more exalted order, to wit, the well-being of the soul, or the acquisition of sound notions. But the second preceds it in the order of nature and of time; it is the well-being of the body, which consists in the society being well governed and in the condition of all the individuals composing it being improved as far as possible. The second aim is the more urgent, and it has been set forth with exceeding precision even in its smallest details; for it is only after having attained this second aim that one can reach the first. Verily, it has been demonstrated that man is capable of a double perfection, to wit, a first perfection, which is that of the body, and an ultimate perfection, which is that of the soul. The first consists in his enjoying perfect health throughout the whole bodily economy[[fn:3104]], which he can only obtain by always finding what is necessary when he seeks it, namely, his food as well as the other things which pertain to the regimen of the body, such as raiment[[fn:3105]], the bath, and the like. Man, alone and isolated, could not achieve this, and the individual can only reach this point through the union of society, for it is a well-known maxim that man is by nature a sociable being[[fn:3106]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..26.4": "The second perfection is to become rational in actu, that is to say, to possess <i>intellect in actu</i>[[fn:3107]], so that, by this second perfection, he may have of all that exists such knowledge as man is capable of. It is evident that in this second perfection there is no question of actions nor of manners, but solely of notions, unto which one is led by speculation and which are the fruit of reflection.",
    "root.text.Part 3..26.5": "It is evident likewise that one cannot attain to this ultimate and sublime perfection until after having obtained the first; for it is impossible that man, whilst harassed by pain, by hunger, thirst, heat, or cold, should even grasp the ideas that one would have him understand[[fn:3108]]; and how much less, for a stronger reason, could he form them of his own motion? But, after having arrived at the first perfection, it is possible to reach the second, which is indubitably the most noble, for it is by it alone that man is immortal.",
    "root.text.Part 3..26.6": "The true Law, which, as we have said, is unique[[fn:3109]]—I mean the Law of Moses, our Master—has come unto us only to bring us this double perfection. It regulats, on the one hand, the mutual relations of men, by making reciprocal violence to cease among them and by refining them with noble and generous manners, so that the peoples may endure, that a stable order may be established among them, and that thereby each individual may reach the first perfection; on the other hand, it improvs beliefs and produces sound notions, whereby one may attain the ultimate perfection. The <i>Torah</i> speaks of both, and it teaches us that the aim of the whole Law[[fn:3110]] is to cause us to reach these two perfections. It is there said: <i>The Lord commanded us to do all these statutes</i>, <i>to fear the Lord our God</i>, <i>for our good always, that He might preserve us alive, as it is at this day</i> (Deut. vi. 24). Here the ultimate perfection is spoken of first, because it is the most noble; for, as we have explained, it is the final aim. It is indicated by the words: <i>For our good always</i>; for thou knowest that the Sages explain these words of Scripture: <i>That it may be well with thee, and that thou mayest prolong thy days</i> (<i>ibid.</i> xxii. 7), as follows: \"That it may be well with thee in a world of perfect bliss, and that thou mayest prolong thy days in a world of eternal duration[[fn:3111]].\" Likewise, in our passage, the words <i>for our good always</i> express absolutely the same idea, that is to say, that we should attain to a world entirely of bliss and duration, which signifies perpetual permanence (immortality); but the words <i>that He might preserve us alive, as it is at this day</i> relate to the first corporeal existence, which is prolonged for a certain time, and whose perfect order can only be established by social union, as we have explained.",
    "root.text.Part 3..27.0": "One thing whereon it is necessary to call thy attention is that the (metaphysical) truths whereby one reaches the ultimate perfection, the Law has communicated to us only the most important points thereof, whilst inviting us in a general manner to believe therein. These points are: the existence of God, His unity, His omniscience, His power, His will, and His eternity. All these notions are the ultimate ends (of science), which can only be understood in detail and in a very clear manner[[fn:3112]] after the knowledge of many other ideas[[fn:3113]]. The Law has likewise invited us to believe certain things the belief in which is necessary for the good organization of the social state, as for example the belief that God is exceedingly wroth against those who disobey Him, and that for this reason one must fear Him, respect Him, and beware of disobeying Him[[fn:3114]]. As for the other truths relative to all that exists[[fn:3115]], which form the numerous branches of the speculative sciences and whereby the notions which are the ultimate end are consolidated, the Law, without proclaiming them clearly as it has proclaimed the former[[fn:3116]], has summarized them in these words: <i>To love the Lord</i>[[fn:3117]]. Thou knowest with what energy this love is expressed: <i>With all thine heart</i>, <i>and with all thy soul, and with all thy might</i> (Deut. vi. 5). We have already explained, in the <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, that this love can only take place through a profound understanding of the whole of being and through the contemplation of the divine wisdom manifested therein, and we have said there also that the Sages have called attention to this subject[[fn:3118]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..27.1": "From all that we have hitherto stated concerning this matter, it results that, whenever a commandment, whether affirmative or negative, has for its object to put an end to mutual violence, or to inculcate good morals conducing to right social relations, or to inspire a true idea which must be accepted, either for its own sake or because it is necessary for the cessation of violence or for the acquisition of good morals, such a commandment has an evident reason and a manifest utility, and there is no occasion to ask what its end may be. Indeed, no person has ever been so perplexed as to inquire why we have been commanded to believe[[fn:3119]] that God is One, or why we have been forbidden to slay and to steal, or why we have been forbidden to exercise vengeance and retaliation, or why we have been commanded to love one another. But the commandments which have perplexed men and concerning which they have held divers opinions [so that some have said they possessed no utility other than that of prescribing something[[fn:3120]], whilst others have maintained that they possessed an utility unknown to us], these are they which, taken literally, appear not to be useful for any of the three things of which we have spoken; I mean those which neither inspire any idea, nor ennoble the morals, nor put an end to violence. It would seem, in truth, that these commandments have no relation either to the welfare of the soul, since they inculcate no belief, or to the well-being of the body, since they provide no useful rules for politics or economics; such are the precepts which forbid garments of divers materials, heterogeneous seeds, meat seethed in milk[[fn:3121]], and those which command the covering of blood, the breaking of the neck of a heifer, the redeeming of the firstborn of an ass[[fn:3122]], and others of like nature.",
    "root.text.Part 3..27.2": "But thou shalt hear further on my explanation of all these precepts, for which I shall give the true and well-demonstrated reasons, save for certain precepts of detail, as I have said. I shall show that all these precepts and others of like nature must necessarily relate to one of the three things of which we have spoken, and that they must either rectify a belief, or improve the conditions of society, which is attained by two things: the cessation of mutual violence and the acquisition of good morals.",
    "root.text.Part 3..27.3": "—One must be imbued with that which we have said concerning beliefs: sometimes the commandment inculcats a true belief which is in itself the sole end, as for example the belief in the unity, the eternity, and the incorporeality of God; at other times it is a belief necessary for the cessation of mutual violence, or for the acquisition of good morals, as for example the belief that God is greatly wroth against him who has committed violence, as it is said: <i>My wrath shall wax hot, and I will kill you etc.</i> (Exodus, 22, 24), and the belief that God hearkens instantly to the prayer of him who has suffered violence or has been frustrated: <i>And it shall come to pass, when he cries unto me</i>, <i>that I will hear; for I am gracious</i> (<i>ibid., v.</i> 26).",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.0": "It is well known that our father Abraham [peace be upon him!] was brought up in the religion of the Sabians[[fn:3123]], who believe that there is no God but the stars[[fn:3124]]. When, in this chapter, I shall have made known to thee their books which, being translated into Arabic, are now in our hands, as well as their ancient annals[[fn:3125]], and when by means of these documents I shall have revealed to thee their doctrine and their chronicles, thou shalt recognize that they therein[[fn:3126]] expressly declare that the stars are (that which constituts) the divinity[[fn:3127]], and that the sun is the supreme god[[fn:3128]]. All the seven planets[[fn:3129]], they say elsewhere, are gods; but the two luminaries (the sun and the moon) are the greatest among them. Thou shalt see that they say clearly that it is the sun which governs the upper and the lower world; such is what they state textually.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.1": "In their books and annals, thou shalt find the history of our father Abraham which they recount in these terms: Abraham, who was brought up in Cutha[[fn:3130]], having set himself in opposition to all the world by maintaining that there exists an efficient cause (of the universe) other than the sun, divers arguments were alleged against him, and there were cited to him, among other proofs, the manifest and evident action which the sun exerts upon the universe. “Ye are right,” Abraham answered them: “it is as the axe in the hand of the carpenter.” Some of his arguments against them are then reported, and, at the end of the narrative, it is related that the king caused our father Abraham to be imprisoned, and that he, even in prison, persisted long in assailing their opinions. Finally the king, fearing lest Abraham should prejudice his government and turn the people from their religious beliefs, exiled him to Syria[[fn:3131]], after having confiscated all his substance.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.2": "Such are the tales they recount[[fn:3132]], and thou shalt find this narrative with fuller developments in the <i>Nabataean Agriculture</i>[[fn:3133]]. They make no mention of that which our true traditions[[fn:3134]] relate concerning Abraham, nor of the revelation that came unto him; for they accused him of falsehood because he combated their pernicious opinion. Since he stood in opposition to the belief of all the world, there can be no doubt, I think, but that he was for those erring men an object of malediction, of reprobation, and of contempt. Now, as he endured this for the love of God, choosing rather to profess the truth than to be held in honour[[fn:3135]], it was said unto him: <i>I will bless them that bless thee</i>, <i>and him that curss thee will I curse</i>, <i>and in thee shall all families of the earth be blessed</i> (Genesis, xii, 3); and, in sooth, we see in this day[[fn:3136]] the greater part of the inhabitants of the earth glorifying him with one accord and blessing themselves by his memory, so much so that even they who are not of his race claim descent from him. He has no longer any adversaries, and none remains ignorant of his greatness, save only the last sectaries of that extinct religion[[fn:3137]] who yet abide at the extremities of the earth, such as the infidel Turks in the far north, and the Hindus in the far south; for these remain attached to the religion of the Sabians, a religion which once embraced the whole earth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.3": "The loftiest degree[[fn:3138]] reached by the speculation of the philosophers in those times was to imagine that God is the spirit of the celestial sphere; that is to say, that the celestial sphere and the stars are the body of which God is the spirit[[fn:3139]]. This is what Abu Bakr ibn al-Sayigh says in his commentary upon the <i>Acroasis</i>[[fn:3140]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.4": "Wherefore all the Sabians admitted the eternity of the world; for, according to them, the heavens are God.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.5": "They maintain that Adam was a person born of a man and a woman, like other human individuals[[fn:3141]]; yet they glorify him, saying that he was a prophet, an apostle of the Moon, who invited men to the worship of the Moon, and that he composed books upon agriculture[[fn:3142]]. The Sabians likewise say that Noah was a husbandman, and that he approved not the worship of idols; wherefore thou shalt find that Noah was an object of reprobation unto all the Sabians, who say that he never worshipped any idol[[fn:3143]]. They say also in their books that he was smitten and incarcerated because he devoted himself to the worship of God, and they recount yet other like tales. They maintain that Seth combated the opinion of his father Adam concerning the worship of the Moon[[fn:3144]], and they put forth lies exceedingly ridiculous, which denote a great defect of reasoning and show that they were further removed from philosophy than all other men; and verily[[fn:3145]] they were in a state of extreme ignorance. They recount, for example, that Adam, having come forth from the clime of the sun[[fn:3146]], near unto India, and penetrating into the region of Babylon, brought with him marvelous things; amongst others, a tree of gold which flourished and had leaves and branches, a similar tree of stone, and a green leaf of a tree which the fire could not consume. He (Adam) spake also of a tree which could shelter ten thousand persons, albeit it had but the height of a man[[fn:3147]]; he brought with him two leaves thereof, of which each could enwrap two persons. They recount yet a multitude of other such fables; and it is a matter of wonder that people who believe the world to be eternal should nevertheless admit the existence of these things, acknowledged as naturally impossible by those who apply themselves to physical studies[[fn:3148]]. That which they say of Adam, and all that they attribute unto him, has no other purpose than to fortify their opinion concerning the eternity of the world, in order to draw the conclusion that the stars and the celestial sphere are the divinity[[fn:3149]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.6": "But when he grew up who was the <i>pillar of the world</i>[[fn:3150]], having recognized that there exists a <i>separate</i> God[[fn:3151]], who is neither a body nor a force in a body, and that all these stars and all these spheres were His works, and having understood the absurdity of all those tales wherewith he had been reared, he began to refute their doctrine and to show the falsity of their opinions; he declared himself publicly their adversary, and proclaimed <i>the name of the Eternal, the God of the universe</i>[[fn:3152]], a proclamation which embraced at once the existence of God and the creation of the world by that same God.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.7": "In accordance with these <i>Sabian</i> opinions, they erected statues unto the planets, statues of gold unto the sun and statues of silver unto the moon[[fn:3153]], and they allotted the metals and the regions unto the planets[[fn:3154]], saying that such a planet is the God of such a region. They built temples in which they placed statues, and they asserted that the forces of the planets were poured forth upon these statues, so that the latter spoke[[fn:3155]], understood, thought, and inspired men, making known unto them that which is of use. They speak in the same wise of the trees which fell to the lot of these planets: if (say they), one consecrate such a tree to such a planet, planting it in the name of the latter, and employing for it and with it[[fn:3156]] this or that procedure, the spiritual force[[fn:3157]] of that planet is poured forth upon that tree, inspires men and speaks unto them in their sleep. Thou shalt find all this textually in their writings, unto which I shall call thy attention. Such were the <i>prophets of Baal</i> and the <i>prophets of Asherah</i>, of whom it is spoken amongst us[[fn:3158]] and in whom these ideas had become so fortified that they <i>forsook the Eternal</i>[[fn:3159]] and cried out: <i>O Baal</i>, <i>hear us!</i> (I Kings, 18, 26.) The cause thereof was that these opinions were most common, that ignorance was widespread, and that the world was then generally plunged into foolish imaginations of this kind; thus there arose among them (the Hebrews) ideas which gave birth to soothsayers, augurs, sorcerers, enchanters, evocators, magicians, and necromancers[[fn:3160]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.8.sub_0": "We have already expounded, in our great work <i>Mishneh Torah</i>[[fn:3161]], that our father Abraham began to refute these opinions by arguments and by a preaching full of gentleness which won men over, and that he drew them unto the worship of God by treating them with benevolence. When subsequently the prince of the prophets appeared[[fn:3162]], he fulfilled the intention (of Abraham), by commanding that these men (idolaters) should be slain, that their traces be made to disappear and their root be destroyed [<i>Ye shall demolish their altars, etc</i>.[[fn:3163]]], and by forbidding to follow in any wise their customs: <i>and ye shall not walk in the manners of the nation</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Levit., XX, 23). Thou knowest by many passages of the Pentateuch that the Law had principally for its aim the cessation of idolatry, the effacement of its trace, (the removal of) all that is connected therewith, even unto the very memory thereof, and all that may lead to one of its practices [such as evocation, magic, passing through the fire[[fn:3164]], divination, the art of soothsaying and augury, sorcery, incantation, and necromancy], and finally to warn that one must beware of performing even the semblance of these practices, and a fortiori of imitating them themselves. It is expressly declared, in the Pentateuch, that all things whereby they believed they rendered worship unto their divinities and approached them were in hatred and abomination unto God; it is this which this passage says: <i>for every abomination to the Eternal, which he hats, have they done unto their gods</i> (Deut., XII, 31). It is related, as thou shalt find in their books which I shall make known unto thee, that, under certain circumstances, they offered to the sun, their supreme god, seven beetles, seven mice, and seven bats. Verily, this alone suffices to inspire disgust in human nature.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.8.sub_1": "— All the commandments, therefore, which have for their object the prohibition of idolatry and all that depends therefrom, which may lead thereto, or which is in relation therewith, are of a manifest utility; for they all aim at preserving us from these pernicious opinions, which turn us aside from all that is useful for attaining the two perfections[[fn:3165]], by giving us those foolish preoccupations in which our ancestors were reared, — <i>Your fathers dwelt on the other side of the flood in old time</i>, <i>Terah</i>, <i>the father of Abraham, and the father of Nachor</i>, <i>and they served other gods</i> (Joshua, 24, 2), — and of which the veracious prophets have spoken, saying: <i>They followed after vain things which profit not</i>[[fn:3166]]. Great, therefore, is the utility of every commandment which preservs us from this grave error and which leads us back to the true belief, namely, that there is a God, the Creator of all these things, that it is He who must be worshipped, loved, and feared, and not these imaginary divinities, and that, to approach the true God and to conciliate His benevolence, one has no need of all these painful practices, but that it suffices to <i>love</i> Him and to <i>fear</i> Him, two things which are the true object of divine worship, as we shall set forth: <i>And now</i>, <i>Israel, what does the Eternal thy God require of thee</i>, <i>etc.?</i> (Deuter., 10, 12.) Further on, I shall enlarge more upon this idea.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.9": "— Returning now to my theme, I say that what has enabled me to understand the meaning of a great number of commandments and what has made known unto me the reason thereof, is the study I have made of the doctrines of the Sabians, of their opinions, their practices, and the ceremonies of their worship. This is what thou shalt see, when I shall expound what has motivated these commandments, which are believed to be without any reason whatsoever. I shall therefore speak unto thee of the books[[fn:3167]] whereby thou mayest learn all that I myself know of the doctrines and opinions of the Sabians, in order that thou mayest acquire the certainty that all that I shall say to motivate these commandments is the truth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.10": "The greatest work upon this subject is <i>the Nabatean Agriculture</i>, a work translated by Ibn-Wa’hshiyya[[fn:3168]]. I shall inform thee, in the following chapter, why the Sabians have inscribed their religious doctrines in treatises on agriculture. Thus, the book in question is filled with the foolish conceits of idolaters and with that which may entice and captivate the minds of the multitude; therein they speak of talismans, of the cooperation of spirits (of the stars)[[fn:3169]], of magic, of demons, and of ghouls that inhabit the deserts. Therein are related incidentally great follies, which move intelligent men to laughter and by which they pretended to insult the manifest miracles, which made known to the inhabitants of the earth that there is a God who governs them all, as it is said: <i>To the end thou mayest know that the earth is the Eternal's</i> (Exodus, 9, 29), and in another passage: <i>That</i>, <i>I am the Eternal</i>, <i>in the midst of the earth</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, VIII, 18).",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.11": "It is likewise related therein that Adam, the first man[[fn:3170]], recorded in his book that there existed in India a tree of which the branches, if taken and cast upon the ground, move each like unto creeping serpents; that another tree, the root of which has a human form, gives forth a hoarse sound and utters distinct words; that a man, by taking the leaves of a certain herb [of which a description is given], and placing them in his bosom, renders himself invisible, so that it is not seen whither he enters nor whence he departs; and finally that if, with this selfsame herb, fumigations be made in the open air, there is heard in the atmosphere, so long as the smoke ascends, a noise and sounds most terrifying. Divers fables of a like nature are therein recounted at great length in the style[[fn:3171]] of a simple exposition[[fn:3172]] concerning the remarkable qualities of plants and the peculiarities of nature, so that they seem to offer an insult unto miracles and to induce the belief that these latter were accomplished by means of artifices[[fn:3173]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.12": "One of the fables of this book (of the <i>Nabataean Agriculture</i>) is that relating to the shrub of the <i>Althæa</i>, one of those plants which were employed as <i>Asheroth</i>[[fn:3174]], even as I have informed thee. It is related that this shrub, after having been stationed for twelve thousand years at Nineveh, had a quarrel with the mandrake, which sought to take its place, and that the person whom this shrub (the <i>Althæa</i>) inspired remained for some time deprived of his revelations; thereafter, when inspiring him anew, it recounted unto him that it had been occupied in pleading with the mandrake, and it commanded him to write unto the Chaldeans[[fn:3175]], that they might judge their cause and declare which of the two plants, the <i>Althæa</i> or the mandrake, is preferable for their magic and of more frequent employment. 'Tis a right long fable[[fn:3176]], and if thou readest it, thou shalt thereby be able to judge of the intelligence of the men of those times and of the state of their sciences. Such were in those days of darkness the <i>sages of Babylon</i> unto whom allusion is made[[fn:3177]]; for such were the religious beliefs in which they had been nurtured. Had not the belief in the existence of God been so generally acknowledged in present religions, there would be in our days a darkness yet thicker than that which reigned in those times; nevertheless, there is such in other respects[[fn:3178]]. But let us return to our subject.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.13": "In the book in question, it is related concerning a personage from among the prophets of idolatry, who was called <i>Thammuz</i>, that he invited a certain king to worship the seven planets and the twelve signs of the Zodiac. This king put him to death in a cruel manner[[fn:3179]]; and it is related that, on the night of his death, all the idols from the different regions of the earth assembled in the temple of Babylon, before the great golden statue, which is that of the sun. This statue, which was suspended between heaven and earth, came to station itself[[fn:3180]] in the midst of the temple, and all the other statues placed themselves around it. It began to pronounce the funeral oration of Thammuz and to recount that which had befallen him; all the idols wept and lamented throughout that whole night, and at morn they flew away and returned unto their temples in the different regions of the earth. Thenceforth comes that perpetual custom of lamenting and weeping for Thammuz, on the first day of the month of Thammuz (July); it is the women[[fn:3181]] who weep for him and who recite his funeral eulogy[[fn:3182]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.14": "— Direct thy attention unto all this, and thou shalt understand what were the ideas of the men of those times; for this legend of Thammuz is of a most high antiquity among the Sabians. By the book in question, thou mayest know the greater part of the foolish notions of the Sabians, as well as their practices and their festivals.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.15": "As for that which they relate concerning the adventure of Adam, the serpent, and the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, in which allusion is also made to an unaccustomed manner of clothing[[fn:3183]], thou must be well on thy guard lest thy mind be troubled and lest thou imagine that what they say ever happened unto Adam or unto any other. It is by no means a real history, and the slightest reflection will cause thee to perceive that all which they have related in this fable is naught but falsehood. Thou shalt perceive that it is a story which they have copied from the Pentateuch. When that book had spread among the religious sects[[fn:3184]], and when these, having heard the text of the narrative of the creation, took it entirely in its literal sense, they (the Sabians) forged[[fn:3185]] this story in question, to the end that the inexperienced men who should hear it might be led to believe that the world is eternal and that this history, related in the Pentateuch, had actually occurred even as they recounted it[[fn:3186]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.16": "Albeit a man such as thou hast no need of this observation[[fn:3187]], — for thou art possessed of sufficient science to prevent thy mind from cleaving unto the fables of the Sabians and unto the follies of the Casdeans and the Chaldeans[[fn:3188]], who are devoid of all science truly deserving of that name, — yet have I deemed it my duty to offer a warning for the preservation of others; for the vulgar are but too prone to give credence unto fables.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.17": "Amongst the number of these (pagan) books is likewise the book <i>Istimakhis</i>[[fn:3189]], which is attributed unto Aristotle, though it be passing far from being his work; likewise the writings relating unto talismans, such as the book of Tomtom[[fn:3190]], the book <i>Al-Sarb</i>[[fn:3191]], the book “Of the Degrees of the Celestial Sphere and of the Figures appearing in each Degree[[fn:3192]]”, another book on talismans attributed unto Aristotle, another attributed unto Hermes[[fn:3193]], and finally a book of the Sabian Is’hâk for the defense of the religion of the Sabians[[fn:3194]], and his great work upon the laws of the Sabians, upon various details of their religion, their festivals, their sacrifices, their prayers, and other religious subjects.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.18": "All the books which I have just enumerated are books of idolatry which have been translated into Arabic. It is indubitable that they form but a small portion (of that literature), in comparison with those which have not been translated or which no longer even exist, but have been lost and have perished in the course of years. Those which still exist amongst us at this day contain the greater part of the opinions of the Sabians, as well as their practices, which, in part, are still spread throughout the world; I speak of the construction of temples, of the statues of metal and stone which are erected therein, of the construction of altars, of what is offered there in the way of sacrifices or different kinds of food, of the institution of festivals, of gatherings for prayers or for other ceremonies performed in these temples, [in which are reserved places which they hold in great honour and which they call the chapels of the <i>intelligible forms</i>[[fn:3195]]], of the images which they place <i>upon the high mountains</i> (Deut. xii. 2), of the honours rendered unto the <i>Asheroth</i>[[fn:3196]], of the erection of monumental stones[[fn:3197]], and finally of other matters which thou mayest read in the books whereunto I have called thy attention.",
    "root.text.Part 3..28.19": "The knowledge of these opinions and practices is of the utmost importance for the understanding of the reasons of the (divine) commandments; for the foundation of our whole Law and the pivot upon which it turns, is the effacing of these opinions from minds and the destruction of their monuments; “to efface them from minds,” as it is said: <i>Lest your heart be deceived</i> (Deut. xi. 16),… <i>whose heart turns away this day etc</i>. (<i>ibid.</i>, XXIX, 17), “and to destroy their monuments,” as it is said: <i>Ye shall destroy their altars… and cut down their Asheroth</i> (Deut. vii. 5)[[fn:3198]], <i>and destroy the names of them out of that place</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, XII, 3). These two points are found repeated in several passages; for such is the principal end of the whole Law, even as the doctors have informed us by their traditional explanation of these words: <i>All that the Lord has commanded you by the hand of Moses</i> (Numbers, xv. 23); “Whence thou mayest learn, say they[[fn:3199]], that he who professes idolatry denies the whole Law, and he who denies idolatry acknowledges the whole Law.” Thou must well possess thyself of this truth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..29.0": "By considering these antiquated and unreasonable opinions, thou shalt recognize that it was a notion generally prevalent among men, that the worship of the stars resulted in the prosperity of the earth and the fertility of lands. The learned, as well as the virtuous and pious men of those times, preached in this sense and taught that agriculture, whereby alone man subsisted, could not be accomplished and succeed according to desire save by means of the worship[[fn:3200]] of the sun and the (other) planets, and that, if they were provoked by disobedience, the lands would become desert and be laid waste. They (the Sabians) relate in their books, that Jupiter[[fn:3201]] had smitten with his wrath the desert and uncultivated places, which, for that cause, are deprived of water and trees and inhabited by ghouls. They held in great honour husbandmen and tilers of the soil, because these occupy themselves with the cultivation of the earth, which responds unto the will of the stars and is[[fn:3202]] well-pleasing unto them. The reason why the idolaters esteemed oxen so highly is none other than because these are useful for agriculture. They even said that it is not permitted to slaughter them[[fn:3203]], because, albeit being endowed with strength, they yield themselves unto man for agriculture[[fn:3204]]. If they act thus, if they submit unto man despite their strength, it is solely (they say) because their service in agriculture is pleasing unto the gods. As these opinions were widely spread, they linked idolatry unto agriculture, the latter being a thing necessary for the subsistence of man and of most animals; the idolatrous priests preached unto the men assembled in the temples and confirmed them in this notion, that by means of this worship (of the stars), the rains would descend, the trees would bear fruit, and the lands would be fertile and populous. One must read what is said in the <i>Nabatean Agriculture</i> in the place where the vine is spoken of; thou shalt find there these very words of the Sabians: “All the ancient sages and prophets have prescribed it as a duty to play musical instruments, on festival days, before the idols; they said with reason that the gods take pleasure therein and grant the fairest reward unto those who do so. They have made many goodly promises for this act, promising amongst others the prolongation of life, the removal of calamities, the disappearance of infirmities, the fertility of seeds, and the abundance of fruits[[fn:3205]].” Such are the literal words of the Sabians.",
    "root.text.Part 3..29.1": "Now, as these opinions were so generally diffused that they were believed to be true, and as God, in His mercy towards us, desired to efface this error from our minds and to relieve our bodies of these toils, by bringing to an end these wearisome and profitless ceremonies and by giving us His laws through Moses, the latter announced to us in the name of God that, if these stars and idols were worshipped[[fn:3206]], their cult would result in the failure of rain, that the soil would be desolate and yield nothing, that the fruits of the trees would fall, that calamities would befall social relations[[fn:3207]], and infirmities would afflict the person, and that human life would be shortened. Such is the object of <i>the words of the covenant which the Lord has made</i>[[fn:3208]]. Thou shalt find this same subject repeated throughout the whole Pentateuch, to wit: that the worship of the stars brings about the cessation of rain, the devastation of the soil, the destruction of social relations, diseases of the body, and brevity of life; whilst by forsaking their worship and embracing the service of God, one obtains the descent of rain, the fertility of the soil, the improvement of social relations, health of body, and length of days. This is the contrary of that which the worshippers of false gods preached, in order to propagate their cult[[fn:3209]]; for the principal aim of the Law is to cause this belief to cease and to efface every trace thereof, as we have set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..30.0": "There be certain persons who are loath to perceive a motive in any of the (divine) laws; they prefer to find no rational sense in the commandments and prohibitions[[fn:3210]]. That which leads them to this is a certain weakness which they experience in their souls, but upon which they cannot reason, and of which they could not well give an account. This is what they think: If the laws were intended to profit us in this (temporal) existence, and if they had been given to us for such or such a motive, it might well be that they were the product of the reflection and thought of a man of genius; if, on the contrary, a thing has no comprehensible sense and produces no advantage, it emanats from the Divinity, for human reflection would not lead to such a thing. It would seem that, according to these weak minds, man is more perfect than his Creator; for man (according to them) would speak and act with a view to a certain end, whilst God, far from acting likewise, would command us, on the contrary, to do that which is of no utility to us, and would forbid us actions which can bring us no harm. Far from Him be such a thought! The contrary is the case, and it is always our good that the Divinity has in view, as we have shewn by the words of Scripture: <i>That we might be happy always, and that He might preserve us alive, as it is at this day</i> (Deuter., vi. 24)[[fn:3211]]. Elsewhere it is said: <i>Surely they that shall hear all these statutes will say</i>, <i>this great nation is a wise and understanding people</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, iv. 6). Here it is clearly stated that even all the <i>statutes</i> (or ordinances)[[fn:3212]] shall appear to the nations as emanating from wisdom and intelligence. But if a thing[[fn:3213]] has no discernable motive, if it produces no advantage, nor averts any evil, why should it be said of him whose object of belief or rule of conduct it is, that he is <i>wise</i> and <i>intelligent</i> and that he occupies an exalted rank? What would there be in that which could astonish the peoples?",
    "root.text.Part 3..30.1": "But nay; the matter is indubitably as we have said, namely, that each of the six hundred and thirteen commandments must either produce a sound opinion, or destroy an erroneous opinion, or establish a rule of justice, or cause injustice to cease, or train man in good morals, or preserve him from depraved morals. The whole of the commandments, therefore, relats to three things: to opinions, to morals, and to the practice of social duties. If we do not here include speech, it is because the words which Scripture commands or forbidds to be uttered[[fn:3214]], sometimes enter into the class of social duties, and sometimes cause certain opinions or certain morals to be contracted. Wherefore here, where the question is to indicate the <i>motive</i> of each commandment, we confine ourselves to the three classes which we have just indicated.",
    "root.text.Part 3..31.0": "If thou considerest the divine works, I mean the works of nature, thou shalt understand what providence, what wisdom God has manifested in the creation of living beings, in the arrangement of the movements of the members, and in the position of these latter in respect to one another; likewise, thou shalt acknowledge the wisdom and providence of God in the different conditions through which He causs the whole of the individual (animal) successively to pass[[fn:3215]]. As for the arrangement of its movements and the relative position of the organs, I shall cite the following example: The anterior part of the brain is extremely soft, whilst the posterior part has more consistency; the spinal marrow is yet more consistent, and, as it extends, it becoms firmer. The nerves are the organs of sensation and motion; consequently, the nerves which serve for simple sensory perception or for a movement of little difficulty, such as that of the eyelid and the jaw, proceed from the brain, whilst those which are necessary for the movement of the limbs issue from the spinal marrow. Now, as the nerves, even those which issue from the spinal marrow, could not, by reason of their softness, set the joints in motion, it has been skillfully remedied in the following manner: the nerves are ramified into fibres, which, being filled with flesh, have become muscles; then the nerve, having passed beyond the extremity of the muscle[[fn:3216]] and having become firm by fragments of the ligaments which are mingled therewith, has become a tendon[[fn:3217]]. The tendon joins the bone[[fn:3218]] and is attached thereto: only then can the nerve, in consequence of this gradual transformation, set the limb in motion. I cite but this single example, because it is the most manifest among the marvels set forth in the treatise on the <i>Utility of the Parts</i>[[fn:3219]], all of which are clear, manifest, and well known to him who examins them with a penetrating mind. Likewise, God has used providence toward the individuals of the mammals; for, as these are born with extreme delicacy and cannot nourish themselves with dry food, there have been prepared for them teats which give them milk, that they may be able to nourish themselves with a succulent food, appropriate to the constitution of their members, until these latter become little by little and gradually firm and solid.",
    "root.text.Part 3..31.1": "Many things in our Law have been thus ordained by the Supreme Disposer. Indeed, as it is impossible to pass suddenly from one extreme to the other, man, according to his nature, cannot abruptly quit all his habits. When, therefore, God sent Moses, our master, in order to make of us, by the knowledge of God, <i>a kingdom of priests and an holy nation</i> (Exodus, xix. 6) [as He declared by saying: <i>Unto thee it was shewed that thou mightest know, etc.</i> (Deuteron., iv. 35), <i>Know therefore this day, and consider it in thine heart</i>, <i>etc.</i> (<i>Ib.</i>, v. 39)], and in order to make us devoted to His worship, as it is said: <i>and to serve him with all your heart</i> (<i>Ib.</i>, xi. 13), <i>ye shall serve the Lord your God</i> (Exodus, xxiii. 25), <i>and him shall ye serve</i> (Deuteron., xiii. 5), then (I say)[[fn:3220]] it was a widespread custom, familiar to the whole world,—and we ourselves had been reared in this universal cult,—to offer diverse species of animals in those temples where the idols were placed, to worship these latter and to burn incense before them. Religious men and ascetics were the only ones who devoted themselves to the service of these temples consecrated to the stars[[fn:3221]], as we have set forth. In consequence, the wisdom of God, whose providence is manifested in all His creatures, did not judge it fitting to command us to reject all these species of worship, their abandonment and their suppression; for that would have appeared then inadmissible to human nature, which always affects that which is habitual to it. To demand then such a thing would have been as if a prophet in these times, in exhorting to the worship of God, should come to tell us: “God forbidds you to address prayers to Him, to fast, and to invoke His help in misfortune; but your worship shall be a simple meditation, without any practice.”",
    "root.text.Part 3..31.2": "This is why God allowed these various kinds of worship to remain; but, instead of being directed toward created objects and imaginary things without reality, He transferred them to His name and commanded us to practice them toward Himself. He thus commanded us to build Him a temple: <i>Let them make Me a sanctuary</i> (Exodus 25:8), to raise the altar in His name: <i>An altar of earth thou shalt make unto Me</i> (<i>Ib.</i>, XX, 21), to offer sacrifices to <i>Him: If any man of you bring an offering unto the Lord</i> (Leviticus I, 2), to prostrate oneself before Him, and to burn incense before Him. He forbade the performance of any of these actions for any other than Himself: <i>He that sacrifics unto any god shall be utterly destroyed</i> (Exodus 22:19); <i>for thou shalt worship no other god</i> (<i>Ib.</i>, XXXIV, 14). He designated priests for the service of the sanctuary, saying: <i>That they may minister unto Me in the priest's office</i> (<i>Ib.</i>, XXVIII, 41); and, as they were occupied with the temple and its sacrifices, it was necessary to establish for them revenues that would suffice for them, which are called the dues of the Levites and the priests. The result of this divine providence[[fn:3222]] was to erase the memory of idolatrous worship and to consolidate the great and true principle of our belief[[fn:3223]], namely, the existence and unity of God, without human minds being repulsed and startled by the abolition of ceremonies that were familiar to them and outside of which none were known.",
    "root.text.Part 3..31.3.sub_0": "I know that at first glance your mind will refuse to admit this idea and that you will feel a sense of repugnance toward it. You will mentally pose these questions to me: How can one suppose precepts, prohibitions[[fn:3224]], and important acts, minutely detailed and prescribed for fixed times, which yet would not have their purpose in themselves, but in something else, as if this were merely an expedient devised by God to achieve His primary goal? What, then, prevented Him from revealing to us (directly) what His primary goal was and making us capable of conceiving this goal, without needing these means that you suppose to be only a secondary goal? But listen to the answer I have to give you[[fn:3225]]; it will remove this anxiety from your heart and manifest to you the truth of what I have observed. Indeed, the very text of the Pentateuch presents us with something analogous, saying: <i>God led them not through the way of the land of the Philistines</i>, <i>although that was near</i>, <i>etc</i>. <i>But God led the people about, through the way of the wilderness</i>, <i>by the Sea of Reeds</i> (Exodus 13:17 and 18). Just as God, for fear of an obstacle that their bodies naturally could not overcome, caused them to deviate from the direct path[[fn:3226]] that had first been intended, toward another path, so that the primary goal might be attained, so too, fearing (to reveal directly to them) what the soul naturally could not conceive, He prescribed for them these laws of which we have spoken, so that the primary goal might be reached, namely, the conception of the true God and the abolition of idolatry. For just as it is not in the nature of man that, after having been raised in servile labor, that of clay and bricks, etc., he should suddenly wash the stain from his hands and immediately fight the <i>descendants of Anak</i>[[fn:3227]], so it is not in his nature that, after having been raised in very diverse kinds of worship and in habitual practices with which minds become so familiar that they become, as it were, a primary notion, he should abandon them all at once.",
    "root.text.Part 3..31.3.sub_1": "On the one hand, God employed providence by making these men wander in the desert until they had become valiant [for it is known that the life of the desert and physical privations[[fn:3228]] produce valor, while the opposite engenders cowardice], and, moreover, there were also born men who were not accustomed to degradation and servitude; all this was done by divine orders given through the mediation of Moses, our teacher: <i>At the commandment of the Lord they rested</i>, <i>and at the commandment of the Lord they journeyed; they kept the charge of the Lord</i>, <i>at the commandment of the Lord by the hand of Moses</i> (Numbers 9:23). On the other hand, the laws of this class[[fn:3229]] were given to them by divine providence so that they might maintain that kind of practice to which they were accustomed, and that thereby faith—which was the primary goal—might be strengthened. You will ask (secondly): \"What then prevented God from revealing to us (directly) what His primary goal was and making us capable of conceiving this goal?\" But this second question can be retorted against you, saying: What then prevented God from making them take the path of the land of the Philistines and making them capable of engaging in wars, without needing to make them take this detour <i>with the pillar of cloud by day and the pillar of fire by night</i> (Exodus 13:21, 22)? Similarly, a third question could be addressed to you regarding the details of promises and threats relating to the entire law[[fn:3230]], and one could say to you: \"Since the primary goal and intention of God were that we should believe in this law and observe the practices it prescribes[[fn:3231]], why did He not give us the faculty to always conceive this goal[[fn:3232]] and to act accordingly, rather than using an indirect means, (by warning us) that He would reward us for our obedience and punish us for our disobedience? And why realize all these rewards and all these punishments? For[[fn:3233]] this is yet another indirect means employed toward us to obtain from us what was His primary goal. What then would have prevented Him from fixing in us a natural inclination to perform[[fn:3234]] the acts of piety He desired and to repudiate the sins He detested?\"",
    "root.text.Part 3..31.4": "To these three questions and all other similar ones, a single general answer can be given, which is as follows: Although all miracles consist in the change of the nature of any being among existing things[[fn:3235]], God does not change the nature of human individuals by miracle. It is because of this important principle that it is said: <i>O that there were such an heart in them</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Deuteron. 5:26)[[fn:3236]]. This[[fn:3237]] is the reason for the precepts, the prohibitions, the rewards, and the punishments. We have already, in several places in our works, expounded this fundamental principle, supporting it with proofs[[fn:3238]]. If we profess this principle, it is not because we believe that changing the nature of any human individual is difficult for God; on the contrary, it is possible and depends upon the power (of God). However, according to the principles contained in the Law of the Pentateuch[[fn:3239]], He never <i>willed</i> to do so and never will; for if it were His will to change the nature of the human individual each time because of what He wants to obtain from that individual, the mission of the prophets and the entire legislation would be useless[[fn:3240]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..31.5": "Returning now to my subject, I observe: As this species of worship, — by which I refer to sacrifices, — possessed but a secondary aim, whilst invocations, prayers, and other religious observances approximate more closely to the principal end and are requisite for its attainment, God instituted a marked distinction between the two classes (of worship). Indeed, the worship of the former species, — I mean that of sacrifices, — though directed towards God, was nevertheless not enjoined upon us as it had initially been, that is to say, to offer sacrifices in every place and at every time. Temples could not be erected everywhere, nor could any casual individual be appointed as a sacrificer, permitting <i>whosoever would to officiate</i> (I Kings, XIII, 33). All this, on the contrary, He (God) prohibited, and He established[[fn:3241]] a single temple: <i>in the place which the LORD shall choose</i> (Deuteronomy, 12, 26); sacrifices could not be offered elsewhere: <i>Take heed to thyself that thou offer not thy burnt offerings in every place that thou seest</i> (<i>Ib.</i>, <i>v.</i> 13), and only a particular family was permitted to exercise the priesthood. All this was intended to restrict this species of worship, and to permit only such portions thereof to subsist as divine wisdom deemed not to be wholly relinquished. But invocations and prayers are performed in every place and by whomsoever; the same applies to the <i>tzitzit</i>[[fn:3242]], the <i>mezuzot</i>[[fn:3243]], the <i>tefillin</i>[[fn:3244]] and other similar objects of worship.",
    "root.text.Part 3..31.6": "By reason of this notion which I have revealed unto thee, there are oftentimes found in the books of the prophets reproaches addressed unto men concerning their great assiduity[[fn:3245]] in offering sacrifices, and it is declared unto them that these latter have no end which is essential in itself[[fn:3246]], and that God has no need of them. Samuel said: <i>Has the Lord as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices, as in obeying the voice of the Lord</i> (I Sam. xv. 22)? Isaiah says: <i>To what purpose is the multitude of your sacrifices unto me</i>, <i>says the Lord, etc</i>. (i. 11). Jeremiah says: <i>For I spake not unto your fathers</i>, <i>nor commanded them in the day that I brought them out of the land of Egypt, concerning burnt offerings or sacrifices</i>: <i>but this thing commanded I them, saying, Obey my voice, and I will be your God</i>, <i>and ye shall be my people</i> (Jer. vii. 22, 23). This passage has appeared difficult unto all those whose discourses I have seen or heard. How, said they, could Jeremiah say of God that He prescribed nothing unto us concerning burnt offerings and sacrifices, seeing that a great number of commandments relate only to that? But the sense of this passage accords with what I have set forth unto thee: “That which I have principally for my object,” says He, “is that ye should perceive Me and that ye should worship none other than Me: <i>I will be your God, and ye shall be my people</i>. The prescription to offer sacrifices and to repair to the temple had no other end than to establish this fundamental principle, and it is for this reason that I have transferred these ceremonies unto My name, so that the trace of idolatry might be blotted out and the principle of My unity firmly established. But ye have neglected this end and have attached yourselves to the means[[fn:3247]]; for ye have doubted My existence: <i>They have belied the Lord, and said, It is not he</i> (Jer. v. 12); ye have given yourselves up to idolatry: …. <i>to burn incense unto Baal</i>, <i>and walk after other gods... and come and stand before me in this house</i> (<i>Ib.</i>, vii. 9, 10)[[fn:3248]]; ye continue to repair to the temple and to offer sacrifices, which were not the end primarily held in view.”",
    "root.text.Part 3..31.7": "I have yet another manner of interpreting this verse, which likewise leads unto the idea that we have just set forth. For indeed, the biblical text and tradition agree in declaring that in the first laws which were prescribed unto us, there was no question whatever of burnt offerings and sacrifices; for thou must not occupy thyself with the <i>Passover lamb in Egypt</i>[[fn:3249]], which had a clear and manifest reason, as we shall set forth[[fn:3250]], and which, moreover, was prescribed in Egypt itself, whereas the legislation alluded to in the verse (of Jeremiah) concerns that which was prescribed unto us after the departure from Egypt. This is why there is made in this verse this express restriction: <i>In the day that I brought them out of the land of Egypt;</i> for the first precepts given <i>after the departure from Egypt</i> were those prescribed at Marah, where He says unto us: <i>If thou wilt diligently hearken to the voice of the Lord thy God, etc.</i> (Exod. xv. 26); <i>there he made for them a statute and an ordinance, etc.</i> (<i>Ib.</i>, <i>v</i>. 25). The true tradition says: “At Marah, there were prescribed the Sabbath and the civil laws[[fn:3251]]”; therefore, by <i>statute</i>, allusion is made to the <i>Sabbath</i>, and by <i>ordinance</i>, to the <i>civil laws</i>, which have for their object the cessation of injustice. Here, then, it is a question of the <i>primary object</i>, as we have explained, I mean to say (that it concerns) firstly the highest truths of faith, such as the newness of the world[[fn:3252]]; for thou knowest that the law of the Sabbath was prescribed unto us especially to consolidate this fundamental principle, as we have set forth in this treatise[[fn:3253]]. Besides true ideas, it was also intended to cause injustice to cease among men. It is therefore clear that, in the first laws, there was no question of <i>burnt offerings and sacrifices</i>, for these have but a <i>secondary object</i>, as we have stated.",
    "root.text.Part 3..31.8": "The same idea which Jeremiah expressed is also set forth in the Psalms in the form of an exhortation unto the whole nation, which then ignored the <i>primary object</i>, and distinguished it not from the secondary object: <i>Hear, O my people, and I will speak; O Israel, and I will testify against thee: I am God, even thy God. I will not reprove thee for thy sacrifices or thy burnt offerings, to have been continually before me</i>. <i>I will take no bullock out of thy house, nor he-goats out of thy folds</i> (Ps. l. 7-9). Wheresoever this idea has been repeated, the end which I have indicated was intended. It behoves thee to be well penetrated with this and to reflect upon it.",
    "root.text.Part 3..32.0": "That which, among other things, the perfect Law had furthermore for its object, was to make us repress and despise our appetites, and to restrict them as much as possible, so as to satisfy them only in that which is necessary. Thou knowest that the passion unto which the multitude most frequently gives itself up[[fn:3254]] is intemperance in food, drink, and physical love. It is this which destroys the ultimate perfection of man, and which is also detrimental to his primary[[fn:3255]] perfection, by corrupting the majority of social and domestic relations. For, by following only one’s passion, as do the ignorant, one destroys one’s speculative aspirations, the body is corrupted, and man perishes before his physical constitution demands it[[fn:3256]]; cares and sorrows multiply, mutual jealousy and hatred increase, and men come to blows to despoil one another. That which brings all this about is that the ignorant man considers pleasure as the only essential end to be sought. Therefore God (blessed be His name) has used foresight in giving us laws proper to destroy this end and to turn our thoughts from it in every manner. He has forbidden us all that leads to greed and to mere pleasure, and this is one of the most pronounced tendencies of this Law. Seest thou not that the textual words of the Law command to put to death him who manifests an excessive leaning for the pleasure of good cheer and drink? This is the <i>stubborn and rebellious son</i> (Deut. xxi. 18) whom they call a <i>glutton and a drunkard</i> (<i>Ib.</i>, <i>v.</i> 20). It is commanded to stone him and to cut him off promptly (from society), before the matter[[fn:3257]] takes on more gravity and he can cause many people to perish and destroy the position of virtuous men by his violent greed[[fn:3258]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..32.1": "That which the Law furthermore had in view was (to inspire us with) gentleness and docility[[fn:3259]]; it wills that man, far from being harsh and coarse, should on the contrary show himself flexible, obedient, and condescending[[fn:3260]]. Thou knowest this divine precept: <i>Circumcise therefore the foreskin of your heart</i>, <i>and be no more stiffnecked</i> (Deut. x. 16); <i>Take heed, and hearken</i>, <i>O Israel</i> (<i>Ib.</i>, xxvii. 9); <i>If ye be willing and obedient</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Isaiah i. 19). In speaking of the docility to accept that which is obligatory, the expression is employed: <i>We will hear it, and do it</i>. The same idea is expressed allegorically by the words: <i>Draw me</i>, <i>we will run after thee</i> (Song of Sol. i. 4)[[fn:3261]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..32.2": "Another object which the Law had in view was purity and holiness, which consists in the repression of physical love, in eschewing it, and in indulging therein as little as possible, as I shall hereafter set forth[[fn:3262]]. When God commanded the sanctification of the nation for the reception of the Law, as it is said: <i>Sanctify them to-day and to-morrow</i> (Exodus xix. 10), He said[[fn:3263]]: <i>Come not at your wives</i> (<i>Ib.</i>, <i>v.</i> 15), declaring thereby that holiness consists in the repression of physical love. Likewise, it has been declared that abstinence from wine is <i>holiness</i>, for it is said of the Nazirite: <i>He shall be holy</i> (Numbers vi. 5). In the Siphra, we read: “<i>Ye shall sanctify yourselves, and ye shall be holy</i> (Lev. xi. 44); this is the sanctification by the commandments[[fn:3264]].” Even as the Law calls obedience to the commandments <i>holiness</i> and <i>purity</i>, so does it designate the transgression of the commandments and shameful actions by the name of <i>impurity</i>, as I shall set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..32.3": "Cleanliness of garments, the washing of the body, and the removal of its defilements, are likewise matters which the Law has had in view; yet these follow only after the purification of actions and after the heart has been cleansed of impure ideas and morals[[fn:3265]]. To content oneself with keeping the exterior clean by washing and wearing clean garments, whilst remaining eager for pleasures and indulging in gluttony and physical love, would be exceedingly blameworthy. Isaiah has said concerning this: <i>They that sanctify themselves, and purify themselves in the gardens</i>, <i>behind one in the midst</i>[[fn:3266]], <i>eating swine's flesh, etc.</i> (Isaiah lxvi. 17); by which is meant that they show themselves pure and holy in open and public places, but thereafter, when they are alone in their chambers and in the interior of their houses, they persist in their sins, permitting themselves to eat forbidden things, such as the swine, the mouse, and other abominations. It may also be that, by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אחר אחת בתוך</span>, <i>behind one that is within</i>, it was intended to indicate that, in seclusion, they give themselves up to forbidden love. In short, it was meant that their exterior is clean and shows neatness and purity[[fn:3267]], but that within they persist in indulging their passions and the enjoyments of the body. Such, however, was not the intent of the Law, which, on the contrary, has had as its chief object the restraint of passion, and willed that the purification of the exterior should only take place after that of the interior. Solomon has already called attention to those who take care to wash their bodies and purify their garments, whilst their actions are impure and their morals depraved: <i>There is a generation</i>, says he, <i>that are pure in their own eyes</i>, <i>and yet is not washed from their filthiness; there is a generation, O how lofty are their eyes! and their eyelids are lifted up</i> (Prov. xxx. 12, 13). By considering the intentions of the Law which we have mentioned in this chapter, thou wilt understand the reasons for a great number of commandments, which had remained unknown before the knowledge of these intentions, as I shall hereafter set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..33.0": "Furthermore, it must be known that the Law has no regard for that which is exceptional. Legislation has not been made with a view to that which happens but rarely; but in all that it has sought to inculcate in the way of ideas, morals, and useful actions, it has had in view only the most frequent cases, without regard to that which occurrs but seldom, nor to the harm that might result from such a legal provision and regime for a single individual. Verily, the Law is a divine thing; yet we must consider the things of nature, which embrace those general advantages existing in the Law, and from which nevertheless individual harms result, as has been set forth by ourselves and by others[[fn:3268]]. Upon this reflection[[fn:3269]], thou wilt no longer marvel that the purpose of the Law is not accomplished in every individual. On the contrary, there must necessarily exist individuals whom this regime of the Law does not render perfect, since the physical forms of the <i>species</i> do not produce, in every <i>individual</i>, all that is necessary[[fn:3270]]; for all these things have emanated from one God, from one agent[[fn:3271]], and <i>were given from one shepherd</i>[[fn:3272]]. The contrary would be impossible[[fn:3273]], and we have already set forth that the <i>impossible</i> has a stable nature that never varies[[fn:3274]]. It further follows from this reflection that laws cannot adapt themselves exactly[[fn:3275]] to the diverse circumstances of individuals and of times, like medical treatment, which, for each individual in particular, must be in conformity with his present temperament. It is necessary, on the contrary, that the legal regime be absolute and embrace the generality of men, although it may, while suiting certain individuals, not suit others; for, if it were to conform to individuals, the generality would suffer, and “thou wouldst make of it something relative[[fn:3276]].” Wherefore that which the Law has chiefly aimed at is independent of the circumstances of time and place; the legal provisions are absolute and general, as it is said: <i>One ordinance shall be both for you of the congregation</i> (Numbers xv. 15), and they have in view only that which is useful generally and in the most frequent cases, as we have set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..33.1": "After these preliminary observations, I proceed to the exposition which I had in view[[fn:3277]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.0": "To this end, I have divided all the commandments into fourteen classes[[fn:3278]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.1": "The I<sup>st</sup> class compriss those commandments which relate to fundamental principles; these are they which we have enumerated in the treatise <i>Yesode ha-Torah</i> (of the Foundations of the Law). To this class also appertain repentance and fasts, as I shall hereafter set forth. In the matter of inculcating these sublime truths, which are to inspire in us belief in the Law, there is no occasion to inquire into their utility, even as we have already declared.",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.2": "The II<sup>nd</sup> class compriss the commandments pertaining to the prohibition of idolatry, and these are they which we have enumerated in the treatise <i>Abodah Zarah</i> (of Idolatry). It behoves one to know that the commandments relating to garments of diverse materials, to the first-fruits of trees, and to the mixture in the vineyard (with other plants)[[fn:3279]] belong likewise unto this class, as shall be explained. The motive of this class also is easily discerned; for its general purpose is to establish true ideas and to perpetuate them among the people during the course of years.",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.3": "The III<sup>rd</sup> class compriss the commandments relating to the improvement of moral qualities; these are they which we have enumerated in the treatise <i>De’oth</i> (of Ethics). It is well known that by good morals the intercourse of men and their society are brought to perfection, which is a thing necessary for the proper regulation of the social state.",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.4": "The IV<sup>th</sup> class compriss the commandments relating to alms, to loans, to gifts, and to all that appertains thereto; such are the <i>valuations</i> and <i>consecrations</i>[[fn:3280]], the ordinances concerning loans and slaves, and all the commandments which we have enumerated in the book <i>Zera‘im</i> (of Seeds), with the exception of diverse mixtures and the first-fruits of trees. The reason for all these commandments is manifest; for all men, in their turn, derive benefit therefrom. Indeed, if a man be rich to-day, he may be poor to-morrow, either he or his seed; and if he be poor to-day, to-morrow he or his son may be rich.",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.5": "The V<sup>th</sup> class comprehends the commandments which aim at the prevention of violence and injustice. These are they which, in our work, form the subject of the book <i>Nezikin</i> (of Damages). The utility of this class is self-evident.",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.6": "The VI<sup>th</sup> class comprises the commandments relating to criminal punishments[[fn:3281]], such as the penalties of the thief, the robber, false witnesses, and in general all that we have enumerated in the book <i>Schofetim</i> (of Judges). The utility thereof is manifest and evident; for, should the offender not be punished, crime would never cease, and those who breathe only violence would not be restrained. There are only weak-minded persons who contend[[fn:3282]] that the abolition of punishments would be mercy towards men; whereas it would rather be true cruelty towards them and the destruction of society. Mercy, on the contrary, resides in this order given by God: <i>Judges and officers shalt thou make thee in all thy gates</i>. (Deut., <small>XVI</small>, 18.)",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.7": "The VII<sup>th</sup> class comprises the property rights which pertain to the mutual transactions of men, such as lending[[fn:3283]], hiring for wages, deposits, sales, purchases, etc.; inheritances also belong to this category. These are the commandments which we have enumerated in the books <i>Kinyân</i> (of Acquisition) and <i>Mischpatim</i> (of Rights). This class is of manifest and evident utility; for these pecuniary relations are necessary for men in every State, and it is essential to establish equitable rules in these transactions and to subject them to a useful appraisal.",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.8": "The VIII<sup>th</sup> class comprises the commandments relating to sacred days[[fn:3284]], I mean to the Sabbaths and the festivals[[fn:3285]]. Scripture has already assigned the motive for each of these days and indicated the reason thereof, which is either to give rise to a certain idea[[fn:3286]], or to procure for us the rest of the body, or to produce both these effects at once, as we shall subsequently set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.9": "The IX<sup>th</sup> class comprises the other religious practices imposed upon all[[fn:3287]], such as prayer, the reading of the <i>Schema</i>, and the other matters which we have enumerated in the book <i>Ahabâ</i> (of Love), with the exception of Circumcision. The utility of this class is evident; for all these practices serve to confirm the ideas relating to the love of God, to that which must be believed concerning Him, and to that which must be attributed unto Him.",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.10": "The X<sup>th</sup> class comprises the commandments relating to the sanctuary, to its (sacred) utensils, and to its ministers. These are the commandments which we have enumerated in a part[[fn:3288]] of the book <i>’Abôdâ</i> (of Worship). We have previously spoken of the utility of this class[[fn:3289]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.11": "The XI<sup>th</sup> class compriss the commandments relative to sacrifices; these be, for the most part, the commandments which we have enumerated in the book <i>Abôdâh</i> (of Service) and in the book <i>Korbanôth</i> (of Sacrifices). We have already stated in the preceding chapter (ch. xxxii) what utility lay in the ordaining of sacrifices in general, and what was the necessity thereof in those times.",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.12": "The XII<sup>th</sup> class compriss the commandments relative to cases of cleanness or uncleanness. The general object of all these commandments was that one should abstain, whilst in a state of uncleanness, from visiting the Sanctuary, to the end that one might be impressed with its grandeur, and that it might be an object of fear and reverence, as I shall hereafter set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.13": "The XIII<sup>th</sup> class compriss the commandments relative to the prohibition of certain foods and that which appertains thereto; these are the commandments which we have enumerated in the treatise <i>Maakhalôth Assurôth</i> (of Forbidden Foods); but the ordinances concerning vows and Naziriteship likewise belong unto this class. All these have for their object to subdue the passion which leads us to seek after delicacies, and to prevent feasting and drinking from being regarded as the end of life[[fn:3290]], even as we have set forth in the Commentary on the Mishnah, in the introduction to the treatise <i>Abôth</i>[[fn:3291]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.14": "The XIV<sup>th</sup> class compriss the commandments relative to the prohibition of certain kinds of intercourse. These be they which we have enumerated in the book <i>Nashim</i> (of Women), and in the treatise <i>Issure Biâh</i> (of Forbidden Unions); the hybridizing of animals (of two species) likewise belongs unto this class. These commandments also have for their object to diminish carnal intercourse with women, to restrain, so far as possible, the unbridled desire for cohabitation, and to prevent its being regarded, as is done by the ignorant, as the end (of human existence), even as we have set forth in the Commentary on the treatise <i>Abôth</i>[[fn:3292]]. Circumcision likewise belongs unto this class.",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.15": "It is well known that the whole of the commandments may be divided into two parts, the one concerning the relations between man and man, the other concerning the relations between man and God[[fn:3293]]. In our division, the classes of which we have just enumerated, the fifth, sixth, seventh, and a portion of the third class, relate to the relations between man and man; the other classes concern the relations between man and God. For every commandment, whether positive or negative, which has for its object to endow us with a certain moral quality, or idea, or to correct our actions, and which concerns[[fn:3294]] only the individual himself whom it serves to perfect, has been called by the Sages <i>the relation between man and God</i>, although in reality it may sometimes lead unto relations between man and man; but this latter case only occurrs after many intermediate steps and from general points of view, and at first sight naught is perceived therein which might affect other men. Thou must well understand this.",
    "root.text.Part 3..34.16": "Having made known the reasons for these several classes of commandments, I shall proceed to treat of the precepts comprised within each of them, especially those which are deemed to be of no utility, or which are taken for ordinances lacking any rational principle; I shall set forth the reasons for them and the instances of their utility, save for a small number of which, until this present hour, I have not yet grasped the purpose[[fn:3295]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..35.0": "The commandments comprised in the first class, to wit, the <i>doctrines</i>, and which we have enumerated in the treatise <i>Yesode ha-Torah</i> (of the Foundations of the Law), are all clearly reasoned. If thou dost examine them one by one, thou shalt ever find therein a true notion susceptible of demonstration. Likewise, everything which serves as an incentive and encouragement to study and teaching is of manifest utility; for if one has not acquired knowledge, one shall possess neither practical virtues nor sound notions[[fn:3296]]. It is equally of manifest utility to honour those who are the <i>supporters of the Law</i>[[fn:3297]]; for if they be not an object of high consideration[[fn:3298]], their words will not be hearkened to when they seek to direct our thoughts and our actions. In the commandment expressed by the words: <i>Thou shalt rise up before the hoary head</i> (Levit. xix. 32)[[fn:3299]], is also contained the duty of deporting oneself with modesty[[fn:3300]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..35.1": "Unto this first class belong also the precepts which command us to swear by His name and which forbid us to violate an oath, or to swear in vain[[fn:3301]]. All this has a manifest reason, and has for its object the reverence due unto the Divinity; for these are actions which strengthen the belief in His greatness.",
    "root.text.Part 3..35.2": "To this class belongs furthermore the precept to invoke God in moments of distress, as it is said: <i>Ye shall blow</i><i> </i>an alarm with the trumpets</i> (Numbers x. 9)[[fn:3302]]; for this is an act which serves to strengthen a true notion, namely, that God perceivs our condition, that it depends upon Him to improve it if we obey Him, and to make it evil if we disobey Him, and that we must not regard this as the effect of chance or of a mere accident. Such is the import of these words: <i>If ye walk with Me</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">קרי</span>, <i>contrary unto Me</i> (Leviticus xxvi. 21)[[fn:3303]], which must be understood thus: “If ye consider these calamities which I bring upon you for your punishment as a mere chance, I shall increase for you this pretended chance in that which is most grave and most cruel,” as it is said: <i>And if ye walk with Me contrary unto Me, then I will walk contrary unto you also in fury</i> (<i>Ib. v.</i> 27-28). Verily, their belief that it was but a pure chance must have the effect of leaving them, without return, to persist in their pernicious opinions and in their impious actions, as it is said: <i>Thou hast stricken them, but they have not grieved</i> (Jeremiah v. 3). Wherefore God has commanded us to invoke Him, to have recourse unto Him[[fn:3304]] and to address our supplications unto Him in every calamity.",
    "root.text.Part 3..35.3": "It is evident that repentance likewise belongs to this class; I mean to say that it forms part of those notions which must be admitted so that there may exist a well-ordered religious society. Indeed, it is impossible that man should not sin and stumble, whether by adopting through ignorance an opinion or a conduct which cannot be approved, or by suffering himself to be overcome by concupiscence or passion. If, therefore, man could believe that this evil is for ever past remedy[[fn:3305]], he would persist in his error, and perhaps would he sin yet more, having no means of reparation; but, by believing in repentance, he will amend himself and become better once more, and even more perfect than he had been before he sinned. Wherefore the acts which are to strengthen this true and most useful notion are numerous; I refer to confessions[[fn:3306]], sacrifices for error[[fn:3307]] and for certain sins committed with intent, and fasts. That which characteriss repentance in general for every sin is the forsaking thereof; and it is this which this notion has ultimately for its object. All these things, therefore[[fn:3308]], are of manifest utility.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.0": "The commandments contained in the second class are all those which we have enumerated in the treatise <i>Aboda Zarâ</i> (concerning idolatry). It is manifest that they all aim at preserving us from the errors of idolatry and from other false conceits which idolatry brings in its train, such as prognostications, auguries, sorcery, incantation[[fn:3309]], and other like things. When thou shalt have read all the books of which I have spoken unto thee, thou wilt see clearly that what thou hearest called <i>magic</i> consists of practices exercised by the Kasdim and the Chaldeans[[fn:3310]], and which were especially frequent among the Egyptians and the Canaanites. They desired to make it believed, or believed themselves, that these practices exercised a miraculous or extraordinary influence in the world, whether upon an individual or upon the inhabitants of a city; whilst logic and reason cannot admit that the practices exercised by magicians produce any effect whatsoever[[fn:3311]], as, for example, when they seek to gather such a plant at such a moment, or when they take such a quantity of one thing and such a quantity of another. These are very varied procedures, which I shall summarize into three kinds: 1st, those which relate to some being, whether plant, animal, or mineral; 2nd, those which relate to a determined time in which such practices must be exercised; 3rd, certain actions performed by men, as for example dancing, clapping the hands, shouting, laughing, hopping upon one leg[[fn:3312]], lying upon the ground, burning a certain thing, or making a certain fumigation, or pronouncing certain words, whether intelligible or unintelligible. Such are the different species of magical operations.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.1": "There be certain magical operations which are not accomplished save by the conjunction of all these acts at once. They say, for instance: One shall take such or such a quantity of leaves of such a plant, at the moment when the moon is found under such a sign of the zodiac, in the east or at one of the other cardinal points[[fn:3313]]; one shall also take the horns of such an animal, or its excrements, or its hair, or its blood, in such a quantity, when the sun shall be found, for example, in the midst of heaven, or at another determined place; finally, one shall take such a metal, or several metals, which one shall melt under the ascendant of such a sign, at the moment when the stars shall be found in such a situation[[fn:3314]]; thereafter, one shall speak, saying such a thing, and one shall make with these leaves, etc., a fumigation unto this figure of metal, and, as a consequence of all this, such a thing shall come to pass. But there are other magical operations which, as they believe, are accomplished by means of but one of these three kinds of acts. In the most part of these magical operations, they lay down as a necessary condition that they be executed by women. Thus, for example, they say that, to obtain that water should gush forth, it is needful that ten virgin women, adorned with jewels and clad in red robes, should dance and approach one another, going now forward, now backward, and pointing to the sun, etc., etc.[[fn:3315]]; by this means, as they imagine, the water will gush forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.2": "They further assert that if four women, lying upon their backs, should lift their legs and spread them asunder[[fn:3316]], and if, in this unseemly posture, they should utter such words and perform such an act, the hail would cease to fall in those places. There are yet many other of these absurdities and follies, the practice of which, according to their express condition, belongs only unto women. In all magical operations, it is also necessary to observe the state of the stars; for they pretend that such a plant has fallen as a portion to such a star, and likewise they attribute every animal and every mineral to a certain star[[fn:3317]]. According to their opinion also, the practices exercised by magicians[[fn:3318]] are a species of worship rendered to such or such a star, which, finding pleasure in such a practice, in such words, or in such a fumigation, does for us that which we desire.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.3.sub_0": "After this preliminary observation, which thou mayest verify by reading their books which are now in our hands and which I have made known unto thee, hearken to what I shall say: That which formed the aim and, as it were, the centre of the Law, was the destruction of idolatry, the trace of which it desired to make disappear; it willed not that one should imagine that any star whatsoever could exercise an influence, whether harmful or beneficent, upon anything concerning human individuals, for it is this opinion which has led to the worship of the stars. In consequence, every magician was to be put to death[[fn:3319]]; for the magician is indubitably devoted to idolatry, although he follows particular and strange ways, different from those which the multitude follows for the worship of false gods. Now, as they have laid down as a principal condition, in all these magical operations, or at least in the most part, that they should be exercised by women, it is said: <i>Thou shalt not suffer a witch to live</i> (Exodus, xxii. 17). Furthermore, as a natural pity prevents men from slaying women, it is expressly said, in speaking of idolatry: <i>a man or a woman</i> (Deut., xvii. 2), and the words <i>that man or that woman</i> are repeated (<i>ibid., v.</i> 5), an expression which is employed neither concerning the profanation of the Sabbath, nor concerning any other commandment. The reason thereof is the great pity which women naturally inspire. — The magicians attributed to their art such efficacy, that they pretended, by their operations, to be able to expel dangerous animals from the fields[[fn:3320]], such as lions, serpents, and the like. They also pretended to be able, by their magic, to guarantee plants against all manner of dangers, and they had, for example, operations by which they pretended to prevent the hail from falling, and others which, they said, caused the worms in the vineyards to die, so that these should not be destroyed. The Sabeans, in the book of <i>Nabataean Agriculture</i>, have even entered into long details upon the manner of killing the worms of the vineyards by means of those <i>Amorite</i> usages[[fn:3321]] of which we have spoken. Likewise, they pretended to possess operations to prevent the leaves of trees and the fruits from falling.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.3.sub_1": "It is by reason of all these superstitions, then widely prevalent, that in the <i>words of the Covenant</i>[[fn:3322]] it is declared, among other things, that the worship of false gods and those magical operations, whereby it was believed[[fn:3323]] these dangers might be averted, would on the contrary become the cause of these misfortunes. It is said, for example: <i>I will also send wild beasts among you, which shall rob you of your children</i> (Levit., xxvi. 22); <i>I will also send the teeth of beasts upon them, with the poison of serpents of the dust</i> (Deut., xxxii. 24); <i>All thy trees and fruit of thy land shall the locust consume</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xxviii. 42); <i>Thou shalt plant vineyards, and dress them, but shalt neither drink of the wine, nor gather the grapes</i><i>, </i>for the worms shall eat them<i> (<i>ibid., v.</i> 39); <i>Thou shalt have olive trees throughout all thy coasts, but thou shalt not anoint thyself with the oil; for thine olive shall cast his fruit</i> (<i>ibid., v.</i> 40). In short, in the presence of all the artifices invented[[fn:3324]] by the worshippers of false gods to perpetuate the worship thereof, by making men believe that, by these means, one may avert certain misfortunes and obtain certain advantages, it has been declared in the <i>words of the Covenant</i> that, as a result of the worship of these gods, these advantages shall be wanting and these misfortunes shall arrive. — Thou wilt now understand, O reader! wherefore the Scripture has insisted upon these particular curses and blessings, contained in the <i>words of the Covenant</i>, and has made them stand forth more than the others; thou shalt therefore know how to appreciate the great utility thereof.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.4.sub_0": "To the end that we might be kept far from all magical operations, we are forbidden (in general) from practicing any of the customs of the idolaters, even those which pertain to agricultural and pastoral practices[[fn:3325]] and others of a similar nature; I refer to all things which they allege to be profitable, and which, in their opinion, belong solely to the domain of certain occult forces[[fn:3326]], without being required by the study of physical science. It is of this that the Scripture says: <i>Ye shall not walk in the customs of the nation, etc.</i> (Levit. xx. 23), and this is what the Doctors term <i>ways of the Amorites</i>. Verily, these are but the branches of certain practices of magicians; for they are things which proceed not from physical reasoning, and which lead unto magical operations, which, relying necessarily upon astrology, result in the glorification of the stars and the rendering of worship unto them[[fn:3327]]. The Doctors expressly declare: “Whatever is practiced as a medicinal remedy is not considered a way of the Amorites;”[[fn:3328]] signifying thereby: whatsoever the study of physics requirs is permitted, but other practices are forbidden. Wherefore, after having said: “An tree whose fruits fall off, men load it with stones and mark it with red chalk,” this practice is met with the following objection: “As for loading it with stones, it is in order that its sap may diminish[[fn:3329]]; but wherefore mark it with red chalk? etc.” It is therefore manifest therefrom that it would be forbidden to mark it with red chalk, or to do any other such thing devoid of reason[[fn:3330]], because these would be ways of the Amorites. Likewise, concerning the “foetus of sacred animals which must be buried,”[[fn:3331]] it is said: “It is not permitted to hang it upon a tree, nor to bury it at a cross-road, because these are ways of the Amorites.” Thence one may conclude regarding other analogous cases[[fn:3332]]. Thou shalt not be scandalized by certain things which have been permitted, as for example the nail of one hanged and the tooth of a fox[[fn:3333]]; for in those times these things were considered as proven by experience.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.4.sub_1": "They fell, therefore, into the category of medicines, in the same manner as, amongst us, peony is suspended over an epileptic, or as the excrements of a dog are employed against swellings of the throat, and fumigations of vinegar and <i>marcasite</i>[[fn:3334]] against hard tumors of the tendons[[fn:3335]]; for all remedies which, like these, are proven by experience, albeit they be not rational, it is permitted to employ them, and they enter the category of medicines by the same title as purgative remedies[[fn:3336]]. Thou must well imbue thyself, O reader! with the remarkable observations I have just made, and keep them in thy memory: <i>for they shall be an ornament of grace unto thy head, and chains about thy neck</i> (Proverbs i. 9).",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.5": "As touching the prohibition against rounding the corners of the hair and the beard, we have already set forth in our great work that this was a custom of idolatrous priests[[fn:3337]]. It is for the same reason that fabrics of heterogeneous materials were forbidden; for it was the custom of idolatrous priests to join together, in the stuffs which served them for raiment, vegetable and animal matters, and to carry at the same time in their hand a seal made of some mineral. Thou shalt find this textually in their writings.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.6": "It is further for the same reason (of idolatry) that it is said: <i>The woman shall not wear that which pertains unto a man, neither shall a man put on a woman's garment</i> (Deut. xxii. 5). Thou shalt find this in the book of Tomtom[[fn:3338]], which prescribs that a man, when appearing before the planet Venus, should be clad in a variegated female garment, and that a woman should cover herself with a breastplate and weapons of war when appearing before Mars. This prohibition, I believe, has yet another reason: namely, that such an action excits the passions and leads unto all manner of debaucheries.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.7": "As to the prohibition of deriving any profit whatsoever from idols, the reason thereof is right clear; for sometimes, in taking them to break them, one might preserve them and they might become a <i>snare</i>. Even when they had been broken and melted, or sold unto a pagan, it would be forbidden to make use of the price received therefrom. The reason for this is that often the vulgar see in accidental things essential causes; thus, for example, thou shalt often find such a man who says that, since he has dwelt[[fn:3339]] in such a house, or since he has bought such a beast of burden, or such a piece of furniture, he has grown rich and has acquired a great fortune, and that these objects have been for him a cause of blessing. It might therefore happen that a person, whose commerce, by chance, had become flourishing, or who had gained much money by this price (of the idols), should see in the latter a <i>cause</i>, imagining that the blessing attached to the price of that sold statue has produced this result. He would, consequently, profess a belief which the Law in its entirety has endeavored to combat, as results from all the texts of the Pentateuch. It is further for the same reason that it is forbidden to derive profit from the ornaments which cover an object of worship[[fn:3340]], as well as from the offerings or the vessels of idolatry[[fn:3341]], so that we may be preserved from this erroneous opinion; for, great in those times was the belief in the stars; it was believed that they gave life or death, and that all good and all evil proceeded from them. Wherefore the Law, to be more sure of causing such an opinion to cease, has employed the symbols of the Covenant[[fn:3342]], the invocation of witnesses[[fn:3343]], the vehement adjurations and the imprecations of which we have spoken[[fn:3344]]; and God has warned us to take nothing of that which belongs unto idols, nor to derive any profit therefrom, and He has made known unto us that, if the least thing of the price received therefrom were mixed with the fortune of a man, it would bring about the loss and the ruin of that fortune. Such is the sense of these words: <i>Neither shalt thou bring an abomination into thine house, lest thou be a cursed thing like it, etc.</i> (Deut. vii. 26); with all the more reason, one must not believe that there is in such a thing a blessing. If therefore thou pursuest in detail all the commandments relating unto idolatry, thou shalt find that they evidently have for their reason the cessation of these pernicious opinions and the directing of our thoughts elsewhere[[fn:3345]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.8": "What we must further observe is that the authors of these false opinions, which are devoid of foundation or utility, seek[[fn:3346]] to procure their acceptance and to establish them by spreading abroad among men the notion that whosoever should fail to perform a certain act serving to perpetuate a particular belief, should be smitten with such or such a calamity. It may perchance happen one day by accident to some individual, so that he will thereafter desire to perform the act in question and will admit the belief of which it treats. It is well known that it is in the nature of men, in general, to experience the greatest dread, the most violent terror, concerning the loss of their fortune and their children. Wherefore, in those times, the fire-worshippers proclaimed that he who did not cause <i>his son and his daughter to pass through the fire</i>[[fn:3347]], would behold the death of his children. This absurd belief undoubtedly had the effect of making everyone hasten to perform the act in question, because of the great commiseration and the fear which he felt for the fate of his children; the more so as this act was a small thing and very easy, for they required naught else but to make them <i>pass</i> through the fire[[fn:3348]]. Let us add thereto that the care of young children is committed unto women, and it is known how readily these latter allow themselves to be impressed[[fn:3349]], and what is generally the weakness of their understanding. Wherefore the Law rose up strongly against this act and branded it with more energy than other species of idolatry, (employing these words): <i>To defile my sanctuary and to profane my holy name</i> (Levit., 20, 3); thereafter the truthful (prophet) made known, in the name of God, that although by performing this act they believe they prolong the life of their children[[fn:3350]], God will cause those who so act to perish and will destroy their race: <i>I</i>, says He, <i>will set</i>[[fn:3351]] <i>my face against that man and against his family, etc.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 5). Know that the traces of this act, once so widespread in the world, have been preserved even unto this day. Thou mayest behold the midwives taking small children in their swaddling-clothes, casting into the fire incense of an unpleasant odour, and waving the infants over this incense, (holding them) above the fire. This is undoubtedly a manner of <i>passing through the fire</i>, the practice of which is by no means permitted. Thou seest, consequently, how much malice the authors of this idea have employed in perpetuating it by means of a chimera[[fn:3352]], so that, despite the opposition made to it by the Law for thousands of years, its trace is not yet effaced.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.9": "The partisans of idolatry acted in like manner with respect to property[[fn:3353]]. They prescribed that there should be consecrated to the object of their worship[[fn:3354]] a certain tree, to wit, the <i>aschérâ</i>[[fn:3355]], and that its fruits should be taken, of which a part would be used as offerings, and the remainder would be eaten in the temple of idolatry, as they have set forth in the rites of the <i>aschérâ</i>. They prescribed the like for the firstfruits of every tree whose fruits serve for food, I mean that a part thereof should be used as offerings, and another part consumed in the temple of idolatry; and they also spread this belief that every tree, whose first produce had not been employed for this purpose, would wither away, or cast its fruit, or produce but little, or be smitten with some other calamity, even as they spread the notion that every child who was not made to pass through the fire would die. Men, therefore, fearing for their goods, hastened to act thus. But the (divine) Law rose up against such a notion, and God commanded that all which the fruit-tree should produce in the space of three years should be burned[[fn:3356]]; for there be trees that produce at the end of one year, others that bear their first fruits after two years, and others finally that produce only after three years. This is what most frequently occurrs when one <i>plants</i>, as is generally the custom, in one of the three known ways, which are planting, layering, and grafting[[fn:3357]]. No provision was made for the case where one might have <i>sown</i> a stone or a pip; for the ordinances of the Law relate only to the most frequent cases[[fn:3358]], and planting, in Palestine, gives the first products, at the latest, at the end of three years. God has therefore promised us that, in consequence of the loss and the corruption of this first product, the tree would produce all the more, as it is said: <i>That it may yield unto you the increase thereof</i> (Levit., 19, 25); and He commanded us to consume the fruit of the fourth year <i>before the Lord</i>[[fn:3359]], in opposition to the custom of consuming the firstfruits[[fn:3360]] in the <i>temple of idolatry</i>, as we have set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.10": "The ancient idolaters further relate, in the <i>Nabatean Agriculture</i>, that they suffered certain substances which they enumerate to fall into putrefaction, observing the entry of the sun into certain signs of the ecliptic and engaging in numerous magical operations. They pretended that everyone ought to make these preparations, and that whenever a fruit-tree was planted, a portion of this putrefied mixture should be scattered around the tree, or in the very place it was to occupy; by this means (they said), the tree would sprout promptly and bear fruit in a much shorter period than is customary. This, they add, is a marvelous process, of the talismanic kind[[fn:3361]], and one of the most efficacious magical devices for hastening the production of fruit in all things that can produce it. I have already explained to thee how much the Law held in abhorrence all these magical operations; wherefore it has forbidden all that fruit-trees produce during three years, from the day of their planting. It is therefore not necessary to hasten their production, as they pretend; for, in Syria, most fruit-trees, according to the course of nature, produce their fruits perfectly at the end of three years, without it being necessary to have recourse to that famous magical operation which they employed. Imprint well also upon thy mind this remarkable observation.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.11": "Another opinion, very widespread in those times and which the Sabians have perpetuated, is that concerning the grafting of trees of different species; they pretended that by operating under the ascendant of a certain constellation, by making a certain fumigation and pronouncing a certain invocation, at the moment of grafting, the product of this graft would be very useful in diverse respects. What is most notorious in this regard is what they have said, at the commencement of the <i>Agriculture</i>, concerning the grafting of the olive upon the citron-tree. For my part, I doubt not that the <i>Book of Medicines</i>, suppressed by Hezekiah[[fn:3362]], contained similar things. — They say further that, if one wishes to graft one species upon another species, it is necessary that a young damsel, holding in her hand the branch which one wishes to graft, should surrender herself to a man in a shameful manner of which they give the description, and that at the moment they accomplish this act, the woman should graft the branch upon the tree[[fn:3363]]. This custom was doubtless very widespread, so that no one acted otherwise; the more so as therein was found the sensual pleasure of love joined with the lure of gain[[fn:3364]]. Wherefore the mixture of two species was forbidden, that is to say, the grafting of one tree upon a tree of another species[[fn:3365]], in order to keep us far removed from everything that might give occasion[[fn:3366]] to idolatry, as well as from their abominable and unnatural amours. It is because of grafting[[fn:3367]] that it is forbidden to join together two species, of whatever plant it may be, and even to bring them near to one another. If thou examinest what the Talmudic tradition says upon this commandment[[fn:3368]], thou wilt find that, according to the written law, grafting is in every place punishable by corporal chastisement[[fn:3369]], for it is this which the prohibition chiefly had in view, whilst the mixtures of heterogeneous seeds, I mean their juxtaposition, is forbidden only in the Holy Land[[fn:3370]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.12": "It is also expressly stated, in that <i>Agriculture</i>, that it was the custom to sow together barley and grapes; for it was believed that this process alone could make the vine prosper. Wherefore the Law has forbidden the mixture of the vineyard (with other plants), and has commanded that the whole be burned[[fn:3371]]; for all the <i>customs of the gentiles</i>, to which certain occult properties were attributed, were forbidden, even when they contained no trace of idolatry, as we have explained concerning that passage of the doctors: \"It is not permitted to suspend it from a tree, etc.[[fn:3372]]\" All these customs, called <i>ways of the Amorites</i>, were forbidden because they lead unto idolatry. If thou examinest their customs relating to agriculture, thou wilt find that in such cultivation they turn toward such stars, and in such other toward the two luminaries (the sun and the moon). Often they fix, for the sowing, the moment of the ascendants (of certain constellations), perform fumigations, and he who plants or sows walks in a circle; there be those who believe they must make five turns for the five planets[[fn:3373]], others believe they must make seven for the five planets[[fn:3374]] and the two luminaries. They pretend that there are in all this particular virtues, very useful for agriculture, in order to bind men to the worship of the stars. Wherefore all these customs of the gentiles were generally forbidden, as it is said: <i>Ye shall not walk in the manners of the nation, etc.</i> (Levit., 20, 23); and that which was very well known or very widespread, or which was expressly designated as a species of idolatrous worship, was the object of a particular prohibition, such as for example the firstfruits of trees, the mixture of heterogeneous seeds, and the mixture of the vineyard (with other plants). What is astonishing is the opinion of Rabbi Josiah relative to the mixture of the vineyard, and which is accepted as a doctrinal decision, to wit \"that one is only guilty on condition of having sown, with a single cast, wheat, barley, and grape-stones[[fn:3375]].\" Doubtless, he had read somewhere that this process had its origin in the <i>ways of the Amorites</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 3..36.13": "It is therefore manifest and beyond all doubt that the weaving of heterogeneous materials, the first-fruits of trees, and the commingling of diverse seeds, were forbidden only by reason of paganism; and that the said heathen customs were prohibited because they lead unto idolatry, even as we have set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..37.0": "The commandments comprised within the third class are those which we have enumerated in the treatise <i>De’oth</i> (of Ethical rules). They are all of an evident and manifest utility; for they generally concern those morals which serve to improve social relations, a matter which is so[[fn:3376]] clear that I need not dwell thereon.",
    "root.text.Part 3..37.1": "It must be known that there are likewise certain commandments which, whilst having for their object the purification of morals, yet prescribe acts seemingly without purpose, and which might be taken for mere <i>caprices of the Law</i>. These we shall set forth, each separately, in their proper places[[fn:3377]]; but those which we have enumerated[[fn:3378]] in the treatise <i>De’oth</i> do expressly declare themselves as having for their aim these noble (social) morals.",
    "root.text.Part 3..38.0": "The commandments comprised within the fourth class are those which we have enumerated in the book <i>Zera‘im</i> (of Seeds) of our work, with the exception of <i>hetero</i><i>geneous seeds;</i> it further contains the laws relating to <i>estimations</i> and <i>consecrations</i>[[fn:3379]], as well as the commandments which we have enumerated in the treatise <i>Malwe-we-lowe</i> (of the lender and the borrower), and in the treatise <i>‘Abadim</i> (of Slaves). If thou shalt examine all these commandments one by one, thou wilt find them of an evident utility for (inspiring us with) sentiments of commiseration toward the wretched and the poor, and (for engaging us) to afford the indigent succour of every kind, that we may not oppress him who is in need, nor afflict the heart of those who find themselves in an unhappy condition[[fn:3380]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..38.1": "The duty of <i>giving unto the poor</i> is a matter which is self-explanatory. The motive for the <i>heave-offerings</i> (for the priests) and the <i>tithes</i> has been clearly indicated: <i>For he</i> (<i>the Levite</i>) <i>has no part</i>, <i>nor inheritance with thee</i> (Deut. xiv. 29); and thou knowest the reason thereof: namely, that this entire tribe (of the Levites) might devote itself unto the Divine service and the study of the Law, and that it should have no need to occupy itself with either ploughing or harvesting[[fn:3381]], but should belong unto God alone, as it is said: <i>They shall teach Thy judgments unto Jacob, and Thy Law unto Israel</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xxxiii. 10). Thou findest in several passages of the Pentateuch these words: <i>the Levite</i>, <i>the stranger, the fatherless, and the widow</i>; for the Levite, having no possession, is ever reckoned among the number of the poor.",
    "root.text.Part 3..38.2": "—As touching the <i>second tithe</i>, it was merely commanded to expend the same in feasts at Jerusalem[[fn:3382]], the which must needs have led to the bestowing of alms; for, inasmuch as it could only be employed for feasts, it was an easy matter for each man to distribute it[[fn:3383]] by little and little. This gathering together in a single place was likewise intended to have the effect[[fn:3384]] of establishing amongst men firm bonds of brotherhood and love.",
    "root.text.Part 3..38.3": "As for the precept concerning the fruit of the fourth year[[fn:3385]], although by its connection with the law regarding the first-fruits it be related to an idolatrous custom[[fn:3386]], as we have aforementioned, it nevertheless enters into the category of those ordinances relative to the heave-offering of the produce of the earth and the dough, the first ripe fruits, and the first of the fleece[[fn:3387]]; for the first-fruits in general were consecrated unto God in order to confirm generosity within us, and to diminish intemperance and the greed of lucre. For the same reason the priest receivs the <i>shoulder</i>, the <i>jaws</i>, and the <i>stomach</i>[[fn:3388]]; for the jaws constitute one of the principal parts of the animal body; the shoulder, to wit the right one, is the first of the members that issue from the trunk, and the stomach is the first of all the entrails.",
    "root.text.Part 3..38.4": "In the <i>recitation</i> which must accompany the presentation of the first-fruits[[fn:3389]], there is likewise a manifestation of humility; for it is performed by him who bears the basket <i>upon his shoulders</i>[[fn:3390]]. Therein is expressed gratitude for the goodness of God and for His benefits, to the end that man may know it is a religious duty for him, when he finds himself in prosperity, to recall his seasons of distress. The Law insists right often upon this: <i>Remember that thou wast a bondman</i>, <i>etc</i>. (Deut. v. 15; xvi. 12); for it was feared lest those habits so common to all who have been reared in affluence should prevail, namely, self-sufficiency, vanity, and the neglect of true ideas: <i>Lest when thou hast eaten and art full, etc., thine heart be lifted up</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, viii. 12-14); <i>Jeshurun waxed fat and kicked</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xxxii. 15). It was in this fear that it was commanded to perform the <i>recitation of the first-fruits</i> each year before God and in the presence of His Majesty. Thou knowest also that the Law strongly enjoins to remember ever the plagues that fell upon the Egyptians: <i>That thou mayest remember the day when thou camest forth</i>, <i>etc</i>. (<i>ibid.</i>, xvi. 3); <i>And that thou mayest tell in the ears of thy son</i>, <i>etc</i>. (Exodus x. 2). And there were just reasons for so doing; for these are events which confirm the truth of prophecy, as well as the doctrine of retribution[[fn:3391]]. Thus, every commandment that serves to recall the memory of one of the miracles, or to perpetuate a certain belief, is of recognized utility.",
    "root.text.Part 3..38.5": "It is expressly said concerning the sanctification of the first-born of man and of beasts: <i>And it came to pass, when Pharaoh would hardly let us go, etc., that I sacrifice to the Lord</i> (Exodus xiii. 15). The reason why the ox, the small cattle, and the ass are particularly designated is right clear[[fn:3392]], for these are domestic animals which are reared and found in most places, especially in Syria, and above all amongst us Israelites, who were all shepherds, from father to grandsire: <i>Thy servants are shepherds</i> (Genesis xlvii. 3)[[fn:3393]]. But horses and camels are not habitually found with shepherds, nor do they exist everywhere; if thou readest, for example, of the expedition against Midian[[fn:3394]], thou findest there mentioned, in the matter of animals, only the ox, the small cattle, and the ass. Verily, the species of the ass is necessary to all men, and particularly to those occupied with the labors of the field: <i>I have oxen and asses</i> (Genesis xxxii. 6), whereas camels and horses are ordinarily found only with certain persons and in certain localities. — As for the prescription to break the neck of the first-born of an ass (Exodus xiii. 13), the reason thereof is that it shall necessarily constrain the owner to redeem it; wherefore it is said: “The commandment which enjoins to redeem it has the preference over that which enjoins to break its neck[[fn:3395]].”",
    "root.text.Part 3..38.6": "The various commandments that we have enumerated in the treatise <i>Shemitah we-Yobel</i> (of the Sabbatical year and the Jubilee) have for their object, at times to prescribe commiseration and liberality toward men in general, — as it is written: <i>That the poor of thy people may eat: and what they leave the beasts of the field shall eat</i> (Exodus xxiii. 11), — and to cause the earth to become more fertile by strengthening itself through rest[[fn:3396]]; at other times to inspire benevolence toward slaves and the poor, I mean the release of debts and the manumission of Hebrew slaves; and at yet other times to provide in perpetuity for the necessaries of life, by making the land an inalienable estate, so that it cannot be sold absolutely[[fn:3397]], — <i>The land shall not be sold for ever</i> (Levit. xxv. 23), — so that consequently the fortune of each man remains as a fund reserved for him and his heirs, and he can enjoy only the usufruct thereof[[fn:3398]]. Thus, then, have we assigned the reasons for all that is contained in the Book of <i>Zera’im</i> of our work, with the exception of the mingling of heterogeneous animals, the reason of which shall be set forth hereafter[[fn:3399]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..38.7": "The commandments which we have enumerated in the treatise <i>'Arkhin wa-Ḥaramim</i> (concerning Valuations and Vows of Devotion) likewise have for their object acts of liberality. Some of these (gifts) belong unto the priests; others are destined for the repair of the Temple. By all these, man accustoms himself to generosity and learns to despise wealth and not to be niggardly in matters pertaining to God; for most of the evils which disturb human societies[[fn:3400]] arise only from the thirst for riches, the desire to increase them, and the greed for gain.",
    "root.text.Part 3..38.8": "— In like manner, all the commandments which we have enumerated in the treatise <i>Malveh ve-Loveh</i> (concerning Lender and Borrower), if thou examine them one by one, thou shalt find that they breathe forth benevolence, mercy, and clemency toward the unfortunate; it is forbidden to deprive any man of an object of utility necessary for his subsistence; for instance, <i>no man shall take the nether or the upper millstone to pledge</i> (Deut. xxiv. 6).",
    "root.text.Part 3..38.9.sub_0": "Likewise, the commandments which we have enumerated in the treatise <i>‘Abadim</i> (concerning Slaves) breathe forth mercy and benevolence for the unfortunate. That which especially denots a great humanity is that the Canaanite slave must be set at liberty when he is deprived of one of his members[[fn:3401]], so that his servitude be not aggravated by any mutilation[[fn:3402]], even were one but to cause him the loss of a tooth, and with even greater reason if another injury be caused him[[fn:3403]]. Moreover, it is not permitted (to the master) to strike him otherwise than with a whip or a rod, or with other such things, as we have explained in the <i>Mishneh Torah;</i> and furthermore, if he strike him violently so as to kill him, he is punished with death like any other man (who should have struck him)[[fn:3404]]. — The words <i>thou shalt not deliver unto his master the servant</i> (Deut. xxiii. 16), besides recommending pity (for slaves), contain another precept of great utility, namely, that we ought to possess the generosity to protect him who implors our protection, to defend him, and not to deliver him up to him from whom he has fled. It suffices not even that thou accordest him thy simple protection, but thou hast towards him the obligation[[fn:3405]] of providing for his needs and of doing him good, and thou must not, by a single word, afflict his heart. It is this which God has said: <i>He shall dwell with thee</i>, <i>even among you</i>, ... <i>in that place which he shall choose in one of thy gates, where it liks him best: thou shalt not oppress him</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 17). If this duty has been imposed upon us toward the lowest and most base of men, that is to say, the slave, what shall it be if a man of high worth implors thy protection? what must thou not do in his regard[[fn:3406]]? — But also, on the other hand, the criminal, the impious man, who implors our protection, must be neither protected, nor pitied, nor relieved in any wise from the penalty which he has deserved, even should he place himself under the protection of the most illustrious[[fn:3407]] and most eminent of men.",
    "root.text.Part 3..38.9.sub_1": "It is this which the Scripture indicats by these words: <i>Thou shalt take him from mine altar, that he may die</i> (Exodus xxi. 14); for, although such a one has put himself under the protection of God and has taken refuge near an object consecrated to His name, God protects him not, but commands, on the contrary, to deliver him to him who is the master to do himself justice[[fn:3408]] and before whom he has fled. And with much greater reason, (the culprit) who implors the help of a <i>man</i> ought to find with him neither protection nor pity; for pity for impious and criminal men is but harshness toward the whole world. These are, without contradiction, equitable manners, which ought to be numbered among the <i>statutes and righteous judgments</i>[[fn:3409]]; they resemble in no wise the manners of the heathens[[fn:3410]], among whom it was considered as a virtue worthy of praise to be proud and to become the passionate champion of the first comer[[fn:3411]], whether he were an oppressor or oppressed, as is everywhere found in their chronicles and in their poems[[fn:3412]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..38.10": "Thus, then, all the commandments which belong unto this class are clearly motivated and of manifest utility.",
    "root.text.Part 3..39.0": "The commandments contained in the fifth class are those which we have enumerated in the Book of <i>Nezikin</i> (of Damages); their common object is to cause injustices to cease and to prevent the infliction of injury upon others. To the end that men might avoid with the utmost care the causing of damage, man is held responsible for every injury proceeding from his property, or occasioned by his own act, provided it was possible for him to be circumspect and to use precautions[[fn:3413]] so as not to do harm. Wherefore are we held responsible for the damages proceeding from our beasts, that we may watch over them; as also for those caused by <i>fire</i> or the <i>pit</i>[[fn:3414]], which are the work of man and may be guarded and watched by him, lest any harm result therefrom. Nevertheless, these provisions impose upon us a certain equity[[fn:3415]] whereunto I must call attention. Thus, one is not responsible for the tooth and the foot in the public thoroughfare[[fn:3416]]; for it is a thing against which no precaution can be taken, and moreover it is rare[[fn:3417]] that any damage should happen in such a place. He, furthermore, who placs aught upon the public way is guilty against himself and exposs his property to loss; wherefore one is responsible for <i>tooth</i> and <i>foot</i> only within the property of him who suffers the damage[[fn:3418]]. — But the damage caused by the horn or any similar thing, which may be foreseen everywhere and against which those who walk in the public way can take no precaution, is subject in every place to the same law[[fn:3419]]. Here, however, a distinction must be made between the docile animal and that which is notoriously dangerous[[fn:3420]]. If the act be exceptional, one is responsible for but half the damage; but if the animal which causs the damage has made a habit thereof and be known for it, one is responsible for the whole damage[[fn:3421]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..39.1": "The price of a slave is generally fixed at the half of that which a free man is generally worth[[fn:3422]]; for thou findest that, for the <i>estimations of men</i>, the maximum is <i>sixty shekels</i>[[fn:3423]], whilst the average value of a slave is <i>thirty shekels of silver</i> (Exodus, 21, 32).",
    "root.text.Part 3..39.2": "If it has been commanded to put to death the beast which shall have killed a man[[fn:3424]], it is not to inflict a punishment upon the animal, — an absurd opinion which the heretics attribute to us[[fn:3425]], — but to punish its master. For this reason has it been forbidden to derive any profit from the flesh of the animal, so that its master may watch it with care, knowing full well that, if the beast killed a child or an adult, free man or slave, he would inevitably lose the price thereof, and that, if it were a notoriously dangerous animal, he would even be obliged to pay a ransom over and above the loss of the price. It is for the same reason that the beast must be put to death which has served for bestiality[[fn:3426]], in order that the master may take heed to his animal and watch over it as over his own family, lest he lose it[[fn:3427]]. Verily, men are as anxious for their property as for their persons; there are even some who set their property above their persons, but the most part attach an equal value to both: <i>to take us for bondmen, and</i> (<i>to take also</i>) <i>our asses</i> (Genesis, 43, 18).",
    "root.text.Part 3..39.3": "That which further belongs to this class is the commandment to put to death the <i>pursuer</i>[[fn:3428]]. This commandment, I mean to kill him who meditats a crime before he has executed it, applies to these two cases only, namely, if any one pursue his neighbour to kill him, or if he pursue a person to violate her chastity; for these are crimes which it is impossible to repair when they are accomplished[[fn:3429]]. As for other transgressions which entail a capital sentence, such as, for example, idolatry and the profanation of the Sabbath, they do no wrong to other persons, and affect only ideas; wherefore the transgressor is not put to death for the mere intent, but only for the accomplished deed.",
    "root.text.Part 3..39.4": "Desire, as is well known, is forbidden, because it leads to covetousness, and this, because it leads to rapine; thus the doctors have expounded[[fn:3430]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..39.5": "The duty of restoring a lost thing[[fn:3431]] is self-explanatory. Not only is it an excellent virtue beneficial to society[[fn:3432]], but it is also a thing of reciprocal utility; if thou dost not restore what another has lost, thou shalt not be restored what thou shalt have lost, just as he who does not honour his father shall not be honoured by his son. There are many similar cases.",
    "root.text.Part 3..39.6": "If he who committs an involuntary homicide be condemned to exile[[fn:3433]], it is in order to appease the mind of the <i>avenger of blood</i>[[fn:3434]], by withdrawing from his sight the person by whom this misfortune has occurred. His return (from exile) depends upon the death of the man who is the greatest and most beloved in Israel, an event which must needs calm the unfortunate man[[fn:3435]] whose kinsman has been slain. For it is in human nature that he who has been smitten by a calamity finds consolation in a similar or greater misfortune wherewith another has been struck; and, among the instances of death which may befall us, none is a greater calamity than the death of the High Priest.",
    "root.text.Part 3..39.7": "As for the precept of <i>breaking the neck of a heifer</i>[[fn:3436]], its utility is manifest; for this duty devolvs upon the city nearest to (the place where) the murdered person was found, and most frequently the murderer is from among its inhabitants. The elders of that city, therefore, invoke God as a witness that they have neglected naught in the maintenance and safety of the highways and in the protection of travellers[[fn:3437]], as the (traditional) explanation[[fn:3438]] says. If, then (say they), this man has been murdered, it is not because we have neglected the public interests; moreover, we know not who has slain him. Necessarily, in most cases, the investigation, the departure of the elders, the measuring (of distances), and the presentation of the heifer[[fn:3439]] will give rise to much discourse and conversation; the matter being thus bruited abroad, one may come to know the murderer, for anyone who knows him, or who has heard of him, or who by certain circumstances[[fn:3440]] has some inkling thereof, will come and say: The murderer is such a one. Indeed, as soon as any person, were it even a woman or a slave, declars that such a one is the murderer, the neck of the heifer is not broken[[fn:3441]]. It is certain that if the murderer were known (to any person whatsoever) and silence were kept regarding him, whilst God was taken to witness that he is not known[[fn:3442]], there would be therein great temerity and a grievous sin. Consequently, even a woman who should know him must declare it. As soon as he is known, the object is attained; for even should the tribunal not condemn him to death[[fn:3443]], the sovereign, who has the power to condemn upon probability, would have him put to death; and if the king does it not, it will be the <i>avenger of blood</i> who will seek to slay him, and he will employ ruses to surprise him in order to put him to death. It is therefore clear that the precept of breaking the heifer's neck has for its object the discovery of the murderer. That which confirms this idea is that the place where this ceremony[[fn:3444]] is accomplished must never be tilled nor sown[[fn:3445]]; the owner of this land will therefore employ all manner of ruses and make researches to know the murderer, so that this ceremony may not take place and his field may not be forbidden unto him forever.",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.0": "The commandments contained in the sixth class concern criminal punishments[[fn:3446]]. Their utility in general is well known, and we have already spoken thereof. Hearken now unto the details and the manner of judging the extraordinary cases[[fn:3447]] which present themselves therein.",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.1": "In general, the penalty to be inflicted upon whosoever committs a crime against his neighbour is to act towards him exactly as he has acted; if he has caused a bodily injury, he shall suffer a bodily injury, and if he has attacked the fortune of another, he shall suffer a pecuniary penalty, albeit it be permitted unto the owner to be generous and to forgive. The murderer alone, by reason of the enormity of his crime, can by no means obtain pardon, and no ransom must be accepted from him: <i>And the land cannot be cleansed of the blood that is shed therein, but by the blood of him that shed it</i> (Numbers, xxxv. 33). Wherefore, even should the victim survive for an hour or for some days, speaking and having all his presence of mind, and should he say: “I desire that my murderer be released, I have forgiven and pardoned him,” he would not be hearkened unto. On the contrary, life must needs be given for life, regarding as equal the child and the adult, the slave and the freeman, the sage and the ignorant; for, among all the crimes of man, there is none greater than that. He who has deprived another of a limb shall be deprived of the same limb: <i>As he has caused a blemish in a man, so shall it be done to him again</i> (Leviticus, xxiv. 20). Thou must not be troubled by the fact that, in this case, we inflict only a pecuniary penalty; for my present purpose is to assign reasons for the Biblical texts and not to assign reasons for the traditional explanation[[fn:3448]]. Moreover, I have also regarding the tradition in question an opinion which must be delivered orally[[fn:3449]]. For wounds where it was impossible to return exactly the like, one was condemned to a pecuniary fine: <i>He shall pay for the loss of his time, and shall cause him to be thoroughly healed</i> (Exodus, xxi. 19).",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.2": "He (we have said) who makes an attempt upon the fortune of any man shall suffer a pecuniary penalty in a measure exactly similar: <i>Whom the judges shall condemn, he shall pay double unto the other</i> (Exodus, xxii, 8), (to wit) the amount of that which he has taken, whereunto shall be added as much from the fortune of the thief. — It must be known that, the more frequent a kind of crime[[fn:3450]] is and the more easy to perpetrate, the more severe must the penalty be, in order that men may abstain (from committing it); and the more rare it is, the lighter must be the penalty. It is for this reason that the fine paid by him who steals sheep is double that which is paid for other moveable objects, I mean to say (that it is) fourfold, provided however that he has parted with them by sale or has slaughtered them[[fn:3451]]. For at all times they are frequently stolen[[fn:3452]], because they are in the fields, where they cannot be watched as one watches things that are within the cities; wherefore also those who steal them are wont to sell them promptly, so that they may not be recognised in their possession, or to slaughter them, so that their appearance may vanish. Thus, therefore, the fine for the most frequent cases (of theft) is the heaviest. The fine to be paid for the theft of an ox is further increased by one more, because this theft is yet far more easy. Verily, sheep graze together (in flocks), so that the shepherd may encompass them with his sight, and they can scarce be stolen save during the night; but oxen, as is observed in the book of <i>Agriculture</i>, graze very far apart from one another, wherefore the herdsman cannot encompass them with his sight, and they are very frequently stolen.",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.3": "Likewise, the law concerning <i>false witnesses</i>[[fn:3453]] wills that there be done unto them exactly as they intended to do; if they intended to cause a sentence of death, they shall be put to death; if they intended to cause the infliction of stripes, they shall be scourged; if they intended to cause a fine to be imposed, they shall be punished with a like fine. All this has for its object to render the punishment equal to the crime, and it is in this sense also that the laws are called <i>just</i>[[fn:3454]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.4": "The reason wherefore the <i>highwayman</i> is not compelled to pay anything in addition, by way of fine [for the fifth part is but an expiation for the false oath[[fn:3455]]], is that brigandage occurs but rarely. Indeed, the act of theft is more frequent than brigandage: 1<sup>st</sup>, because theft may be committed in any locality, whereas brigandage can but with difficulty be executed within the precincts of towns; 2nd, because theft may be perpetrated both upon objects in plain sight and upon those surrounded by secrecy and surveillance, whereas brigandage is only practicable upon objects manifest and evident[[fn:3456]], so that one may take precautions against the highwayman, put oneself on guard, and offer him resistance[[fn:3457]], which cannot be done in regard to the thief; 3rd, because the highwayman is known, so that he may be summoned (to justice) and one may seek to reclaim that which he has taken, whereas the thief is unknown. For all these reasons, the thief is condemned to a fine, whereas a similar condemnation does not apply to the highwayman.",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.5": "<i>Preliminary observation</i>[[fn:3458]]. — Know thou that for the penality, whether it be grave and very painful, or lesser and easy to endure, four things are taken into consideration:",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.6": "1<sup>o</sup> The gravity of the crime; for those actions from which a great injury results entail a heavier penalty, whereas those which cause but an inconsiderable injury are punished more lightly.",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.7": "2ndly, the frequency of the occurrence; for that which transpirs more frequently must be restrained by a more severe penalty, whereas a lighter punishment suffices to repress a crime which presents itself but seldom[[fn:3459]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.8": "3rdly, the strength of the temptation; for a thing unto which man is impelled, whether by a passion that violently excits him, or by the force of habit, or lastly, by the great grief he feels in abstaining therefrom, nothing can cause him to renounce it save the dread of a grievous chastisement.",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.9": "4thly, the facility of perpetrating the deed in secret and with mystery, so that others perceive it not; for such an action can only be repressed by the fear of a punishment both grave and rigorous.",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.10": "After this observation, it must be known that the classification, according to the punishments of which the Pentateuch speaks, compriss four categories[[fn:3460]]: 1st, that which causs the offender to be condemned to the penalty of death inflicted by the tribunal[[fn:3461]]; 2ndly, that which entails <i>excision</i>, consisting (for us) merely in stripes, assuming however that the crime in question is one of the most heinous[[fn:3462]]; 3rdly, that which entails the penalty of stripes [and in which the sin, far from being accounted among the most heinous, is but a simple transgression], or else the penalty of death inflicted by God[[fn:3463]]; 4thly, that which contains simple prohibitions giving no occasion even to the <i>penalty of stripes.</i> Of this latter class are all transgressions in which there is no act, with the exception however of the following[[fn:3464]]: <i>a</i>) <i>a vain oath</i>[[fn:3465]], because of the high idea which must be entertained of the reverence due to the Divinity; <i>b</i>) the <i>exchange</i> (of animals designated for sacrifices)[[fn:3466]], lest one be led thereby to despise the sacrifices consecrated unto God; <i>c</i>) <i>the curse which one might pronounce against his neighbour</i> by invoking the name of God[[fn:3467]], because men are generally far more sensitive to a curse than to a bodily injury. Save for these exceptions, all transgressions in which there is no act can cause but a trifling injury; moreover, one can hardly guard against them, since they consist but in words, and if they were to be punished[[fn:3468]], men would be continually smitten. Furthermore, <i>admonition</i>, in these cases, is scarcely possible[[fn:3469]]. — In the number of stripes there is likewise wisdom, for they are fixed at a maximum, but are indeterminate with respect to the persons. Indeed, each individual can only be smitten according to what he can endure; but the maximum of stripes is forty, even though he might be able to endure a hundred[[fn:3470]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.11": "As for capital punishment, thou shalt find it in none of the cases relating to prohibited foods; for there results therefrom no great evil, nor are men strongly impelled thereto as they are to the pleasures of love. One incurrs the penalty of <i>excision</i> for the use of certain foods: for the use of blood (for example)[[fn:3471]] which men were, in those times, very eager to eat, practicing thereby a certain idolatrous rite, as is set forth in the book of <i>Tomtom</i>[[fn:3472]]; wherefore it was so severely forbidden. In like manner, the use of <i>fat</i>[[fn:3473]] is punished by <i>excision</i>, because men delight therein; it has also a distinct role in the sacrifice, which it was intended thereby to honour[[fn:3474]]. Likewise again, the penalty of <i>excision</i> applies to him who uss leavened bread during the Passover and to him who takes food on the day of the Great Fast[[fn:3475]], (things forbidden) as much to impose upon us a painful privation as to lead us unto faith; for these are acts serving to consolidate beliefs which are the foundations of religion, namely (on the one hand) the belief in the Exodus from Egypt and in its miracles, and (on the other hand) that relating to repentance: <i>for on this day shall he make an atonement for you</i> (Levit. xvi. 30). Finally, one incurrs the penalty of <i>excision</i>, for having eaten the <i>remnant</i> of the sacrifice, or the <i>abominated</i> sacrifice, or for having, in a state of impurity, eaten holy things[[fn:3476]], which is as reprehensible as eating fat. The purpose is to give importance to the sacrifice, as shall be set forth hereafter.",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.12": "Capital punishment thou shalt find only in grievous cases, such as the subversion of faith, or a (social) crime of extreme gravity; I refer to idolatry, adulterous or incestuous intercourse, the shedding of blood, and all that leads unto these crimes (such as the following instances): 1st, the Sabbath (the profanation of which is punished with death), because it serves to establish the belief in the creation of the world[[fn:3477]]; 2nd, the false prophet and the rebellious elder[[fn:3478]], (who are punished with death) by reason of the great corruption which they spread; 3rd, he that smits or curss his father or his mother[[fn:3479]], forasmuch as this betokens great effrontery and destroys the organization of families, which is the principal basis of the State; 4th, the stubborn and rebellious son[[fn:3480]], because of what he may hereafter become, for he will of necessity become a murderer[[fn:3481]]; 5th, he that steals a person[[fn:3482]], because he exposs him to death; likewise, lastly, 6th, he that comes to steal by breaking in, forasmuch as he disposs himself to murder, as the doctors have explained[[fn:3483]]. The three last, I mean the stubborn and rebellious son, he that steals a person and sells him, and he that steals by breaking in, will certainly end by becoming murderers. Thou shalt find capital punishment in no other case save these grievous crimes. Not all incests are punished with death, but only those which are more easily committed, or which are most shameful, or towards which one is more strongly impelled; those which meet not these conditions are punished only with <i>excision.</i> Likewise, not all kinds of idolatry are punished with death, but only the principal acts of such worship, as, for instance, the adoring of idols, prophesying in their name, causing (children) to pass through the fire, the practice of necromancy, magic, or sorcery.",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.13": "It is manifest also that, since punishments cannot be dispensed with, it is likewise indispensable to appoint judges, distributed throughout all the cities. There is also need of the testimony of witnesses. Finally, there must be a sovereign who is feared and respected, who may exercise all manner of restraints, strengthen the authority of the judges, and be (in turn) strengthened by them[[fn:3484]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.14": "Having set forth the reasons for all the commandments which we have enumerated in the book <i>Shophetim</i> (of Judges), we must, according to the purpose of this treatise, direct the attention to certain provisions mentioned therein,",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.15": "and notably to those which relate to the <i>rebellious elder</i>[[fn:3485]]. I say then: As the Almighty knew that the provisions of the Law, in all times and in all places[[fn:3486]], would require, according to the diversity of places, events, and circumstances[[fn:3487]], sometimes to be extended and sometimes to be restricted, it was forbidden to add anything thereto or to diminish anything therefrom, and it was said: <i>Thou shalt not add thereto, nor diminish from it</i> (Deut. xiii. 1); for this might have led to the corruption of the legal ordinances and to the belief that they proceeded not from God. Nevertheless, God permitted the learned of every age, I mean the <i>Great Tribunal</i>, to take care for the strengthening of these legal provisions by means of new regulations which should prevent their alteration[[fn:3488]], and to perpetuate these preservative cares, even as the doctors say: “Make a hedge about the Law[[fn:3489]].” Likewise, it was granted unto them, under certain circumstances or in consideration of certain events, to suspend certain practices prescribed by the Law, or to permit certain things which it had forbidden[[fn:3490]]; yet such a suspension was not to be made perpetual, as we have explained in the Introduction to the <i>Commentary</i> upon the <i>Mishnah</i>, concerning the <i>temporary ruling</i>[[fn:3491]]. By this method, the unity of the Law was preserved, and at the same time the circumstances of the moment could always be taken as rules of conduct[[fn:3492]]. But, had it been permitted unto every one of the learned to indulge in these partial considerations[[fn:3493]], men would have been exposed to numerous divisions and schisms. Wherefore God has forbidden all the learned, save the <i>Great Tribunal</i> alone, to undertake such a thing, and He commanded that whosoever should oppose this tribunal be put to death; for, if every thinker had been able to revolt against it, the object in view would have been frustrated and the benefit (of these provisions) would have been destroyed.",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.16": "It must further be known that, for the transgression of the prohibitions of the Law, four categories may be established: 1<sup>st</sup> that which is under compulsion, 2nd that which is committed through inadvertence, 3rd that which is committed with premeditation, 4th that which is committed with effrontery[[fn:3494]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.17": "As for him who is <i>compelled</i> (to sin), it is explicitly stated that he will not be punished and that he bears no fault. God has said: <i>and to the young woman thou shalt do nothing</i>, <i>the young woman has not committed a sin worthy of death</i> (Deutér., 22, 26).",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.18": "He who sinns <i>unwittingly</i> is culpable, for had he taken due heed to remain tranquil and to watch over himself, he would not have fallen into error. Nevertheless, he can by no means be punished, albeit he requirs an atonement, which consists in the offering of a sacrifice. Herein the law has made a distinction between the private person, the king, the high priest, and the teacher of the law[[fn:3495]]. We learn thereby that he who acts, or who renders a doctrinal decision, according to his individual doctrine—unless it be the Great Sanhedrin or the high priest—is of the class of those who sin with premeditation, and is not numbered among those who sin unwittingly[[fn:3496]]; wherefore the rebellious teacher is put to death, although he acted or rendered decisions according to his personal doctrine[[fn:3497]]. To the members of the Great Sanhedrin alone it appertains to decide according to their own doctrine[[fn:3498]]; therefore, if they have erred, they are considered as having sinned unwittingly, even as it is said: <i>if the whole congregation of Israel sin through ignorance</i> (Lev. iv. 13). It is by reason of this principle that the Sages have said: “An error in doctrine is accounted as a wilful sin[[fn:3499]]”, which signifies that he whose knowledge is circumscribed, and who yet acts or gives decisions according to that circumscribed knowledge, is regarded as sinning with premeditation. Verily, it is not the same with him who eats a portion of the fat of the kidneys, believing it to be the fat of the tail[[fn:3500]], as with him who eats the fat of the kidneys wittingly, but ignorant that such fat is forbidden; for the latter, although an expiatory sacrifice suffices for him, comms nigh unto a voluntary sin. Yet this is only so when he alone committs the sin[[fn:3501]]; but he who renders decisions (erroneous) proceeding from his ignorance[[fn:3502]] must indubitably be regarded as sinning with premeditation, for the text (of the law) excuss an erroneous decision only in the case of the Great Sanhedrin alone.",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.19": "He who sinns <i>with premeditation</i> shall undergo the prescribed punishment, whether it be the capital penalty, or the legal stripes[[fn:3503]], or the stripes for rebellion[[fn:3504]] in cases of transgressions where legal stripes are not applicable, or finally a pecuniary penalty. If, for certain transgressions, premeditation has been assimilated unto ignorance, it is because they are committed frequently and with ease, consisting solely in words and not in deeds, as, for example, the oath of testimony[[fn:3505]] and the oath of deposit[[fn:3506]]. The same holds for intercourse with a betrothed bondwoman[[fn:3507]], which (sin) is regarded as lighter because it frequently occurrs, seeing that she (the bondwoman) yields herself up[[fn:3508]], being neither entirely a slave, nor entirely free, nor entirely under the power of a husband, as the tradition says in explaining this commandment[[fn:3509]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.20": "The <i>insolent</i> sinner is he who not only acts with premeditation, but who is sufficiently impudent and audacious to transgress the law in public. Such a one sinns not out of mere passion, nor because his perverse inclinations make him seek pleasures which the law has forbidden, but to resist the law and to rise in rebellion against it. Therefore is it said of him: <i>he reproaches the Lord</i> (Num. xv. 30), and he indubitably deservs death. He who so acts does it only because he has formed an opinion of his own, in consequence of which he rersists the law. Wherefore the traditional interpretation says that Scripture here speaks of idolatry[[fn:3510]], a system which undermins the very foundation of the law; for no man will ever worship a star without believing it to be eternal, as we have several times explained in our works. The same, in my judgment, applies to every transgression whereby one manifests the intention to overthrow the law and to rebel against it. According to my view, if an Israelite should eat meat cooked in milk, or clothe himself in garments of diverse materials[[fn:3511]], or shave the corners of his hair[[fn:3512]], with the intent to show his contempt for these prohibitions and to demonstrate that he believs not in the truth of this legislation, he would be guilty of <i>blasphemy against the Lord</i> and would deserve death, not as a punishment (for his sin), but for his infidelity; even as the inhabitants of a seduced city are put to death for their infidelity, and not as a punishment for their crime, which is the reason why their goods are delivered to the flames and pass not to their heirs, as do those of others condemned to death[[fn:3513]]. I would say the same of any community of Israelites who by common consent[[fn:3514]] transgress any commandment whatsoever and who act insolently. They all deserve death, as thou mayest learn from the history of the sons of Reuben and the sons of Gad, concerning whom it is said: <i>And the whole congregation decided to go up to war against them</i>[[fn:3515]]. In the warning given unto them, it was set forth that, having committed this sin by common consent, they were guilty of infidelity and had shown themselves rebellious against the whole religion, and it was said unto them... <i>turning away this day from following the Lord, etc.</i> (Josh. xxii. 16), whereunto they replied on their part: <i>God</i>, <i>the Lord God knows... if it be in rebellion, etc.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v</i>. 22). Thou must also well possess thyself of these principles concerning criminal punishments.",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.21": "Furthermore, the book <i>Schophetim</i> (of Judges) contains also the commandment to destroy the race of Amalek[[fn:3516]]. Verily, even as the individual is punished, so must a whole tribe or nation be punished, to the end that all tribes may be intimidated and may not mutually assist one another in doing evil, and that they may say: “One might deal with us as they dealt with such a tribe[[fn:3517]]”. In this manner, if there should arise in their midst a wicked and destructive man, having a soul so depraved as not to care for the evil he does and not to reflect thereon, he would find no one to assist him in executing the evil designs he desirs to accomplish. Amalek, then, having been eager to draw the sword, it was commanded to exterminate him by the sword; but Ammon and Moab, who had acted with baseness and had employed craft to do harm, suffered no other punishment than to be excluded from Israelitish marriages, and to see their friendship rejected with contempt. All these provisions show that God has proportioned the punishments, to the end that they should be neither too severe nor too lenient, but as God has expressly said: <i>according to his fault</i> (Deut. xsv. 2).",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.22": "This book (<i>Schophetim</i>) contains furthermore the commandment to prepare a place apart (without the camp) and a paddle[[fn:3518]]; for one of the objects which the law aims at, as I have erst made known unto thee[[fn:3519]], is cleanliness and the removal of defilements and impurities, to the end that men should not be as the beasts. By this commandment[[fn:3520]], it was also intended to strengthen in the warriors, by the prescription of these acts, the confidence that the Divine Majesty dwells in their midst, as it is set forth in assigning the motive for this commandment: <i>For the Lord thy God walks in the midst of thy camp</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, XXIII, 15). This leads further to this other idea: <i>That He see no unclean thing in thee, and turn away from thee</i> (<i>ibid.</i>), which is an admonition not to give oneself over to debauchery, which, as is well known, prevails in a camp of war when soldiers remain too long absent from their homes. God therefore, to preserve us from such conduct, has prescribed acts which should remind us that the Divine Majesty dwells among us, and He has said: <i>Therefore shall thy camp be holy: that He see no unclean thing in thee.</i> Even he that has been defiled only by a nocturnal accident must go abroad out of the camp, whereinto he may not return until after the going down of the sun[[fn:3521]], so that every man may be well imbued with this thought, that the camp is as a sanctuary of the Lord, and that its mission is not, like the armies of the heathen, to destroy, to ravage, to do evil unto others, and to take their goods; for we, on the contrary, aim to prepare men for the worship of God and to establish order among them. I have already made known unto thee that I indicate the motives of the commandments only according to the literal sense of the text[[fn:3522]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..40.23": "Finally, this same book (<i>Schophetim</i>) contains also the law concerning the <i>beautiful captive woman</i>[[fn:3523]]. The doctors say, as thou knowest: “The Law has herein spoken only in respect of human passion[[fn:3524]].” Nevertheless, I must observe that this commandment also contains noble moral lessons which virtuous men ought to take as rules of conduct. Thus, although under the dominion of an indomitable passion, he (the warrior) must be alone with this woman in a retired place, as it is said: <i>within thine house</i> (Deuter., XXI, 12), and he must not violate her during the war[[fn:3525]], as the doctors have explained. Furthermore, it is not permitted unto him to have commerce with her a second time, until her affliction be calmed and her grief assuaged; and he must not hinder her from yielding herself up to sorrow, from neglecting her raiment, and from weeping, as it is written: <i>she shall bewail her father and her mother</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 13). For indeed, those who are overwhelmed with sadness find relief in weeping and in exciting their grief, until their physical powers be too much enfeebled to bear this agitation of the soul, even as those who are transported with joy are calmed by all manner of diversions. Wherefore the Law, full of benevolence towards her, grants her in this regard full liberty[[fn:3526]], until she be weary of weeping and of giving herself over to sadness. Thou knowest that he may have commerce with her a first time while she is yet a heathen[[fn:3527]]. Likewise, for thirty days, she may publicly profess her religion, and even give herself to idolatry, without any man being able, during all that time[[fn:3528]], to seek a quarrel with her concerning her belief. After this, if he succeeds not in converting her to the precepts of the Law, it is not permitted unto him to sell her, nor to use her as a slave. The law has therefore respected this woman who has become inviolable in consequence of cohabitation, and, although the act was in some sort a sin[[fn:3529]],—for she was then a heathen,—it is nevertheless said: <i>Thou shalt not make merchandise of her, because thou hast humbled her</i> (<i>ibid., v.</i> 14). Thou seest what noble morality is contained in this commandment[[fn:3530]]. And now the motives of all the commandments of this book are sufficiently elucidated.",
    "root.text.Part 3..41.0": "The commandments comprised in the seventh class, relating to the rights of property, are those which we have enumerated in certain portions of the book <i>Mischpâtim</i> (of Judgments) and of the book <i>Kinyân</i> (of Acquisition). They all possess a manifest motive; for they contain provisions of equity for the transactions which of necessity take place among men, and (they recommend them) to yield unto one another a succour profitable to both parties[[fn:3531]], in such wise that neither of the two interested parties should desire to have the larger share in the whole and to be alone advantaged in every respect.",
    "root.text.Part 3..41.1": "Before all things, one must abstain from all fraud in buying and selling, and be content with the customary profits of an acknowledged legitimacy[[fn:3532]]. Certain conditions have been established for the validity of the transaction, and fraud has been forbidden, even should it consist but in words[[fn:3533]], as is well known.",
    "root.text.Part 3..41.2": "Next comes the law relating to the <i>four guardians</i>[[fn:3534]], which is of manifest equity and justice. Verily, he who keeps a deposit without fee, having absolutely no interest in this matter and acting out of pure kindness, is responsible for naught, and every damage which occurrs must be borne by the owner[[fn:3535]]. The borrower, who alone has all the profit, while the owner does him a kindness, is responsible for everything and must bear all the damages which arise[[fn:3536]]. If any man chargs himself with a deposit for a wage, or takes a thing on hire, both of them—I mean the depositary and the owner—have a common interest therein, and consequently the damages must be divided between them; those which arise from a lack of care in supervision must be borne by the depositary, as, for example, if the object has been stolen or lost, for theft and loss show that he has neglected to bestow upon it great care and extreme foresight[[fn:3537]]; but the damages which it is impossible to prevent,—as, for example, if the animal (lent) has been maimed, or driven away, or if it be dead, which are cases of <i>vis major</i>,—must be borne by the owner.",
    "root.text.Part 3..41.3": "Stress is further laid upon the benevolence due to the hireling, by reason of his poverty; and it is prescribed to pay him his hire promptly, and in no wise to defraud him of that which is his due, that is to say, to recompense him according to the value of his labor[[fn:3538]]. Benevolence toward him proceeds so far that one ought not to prevent him, nor even the beast (whilst engaged in labor), from eating of the food which is the object of their toil, according as the (traditional) ordinances relating to this law require[[fn:3539]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..41.4": "The laws concerning property encompass likewise the matter of inheritances. Sound morality requirs that a man should not refuse to do good unto him who is worthy thereof. He ought not, therefore, at the moment of his departure, to be envious of his (natural) heir, nor should he squander his fortune, but leave it unto him among men who has the greatest right thereto, namely, unto the next of kin: <i>unto his kinsman that is next to him of his family</i> (Numbers, xxvii. 11). It has been expressly stated, as is well known, that this is first the child[[fn:3540]], then the brother, and thereafter the uncle (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 8-10). He must give preference to the first-born of his sons, the first object of his love, and must not suffer himself to be led by his passion: <i>he may not make the son of the beloved first-born</i> (Deut., xxi. 16). The equitable Law has desired to preserve and fortify within us this virtue, I mean that of having regard for kinsmen and protecting them. Thou knowest the word of the prophet: <i>He that is cruel troubls his own flesh</i> (Prov., xi. 17); the Law, in speaking of alms, says: <i>Unto thy brother</i>, <i>to thy poor</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Deut., xv. 11), and the doctors greatly extol the virtue of the man “who is attached to his kinsfolk and who marries his sister’s daughter[[fn:3541]].”",
    "root.text.Part 3..41.5": "The Law has taught us that we must proceed to the utmost degree in the practice of this virtue, that is to say, that a man must ever have regard for his kinsman and hold the ties of family in high esteem; and though his kinsman should have shown himself hostile and malicious toward him, and should have manifested an exceedingly vicious character, he must nevertheless treat him with all the regard due to kinship[[fn:3542]]. God has said: <i>Thou shalt not abhor an Edomite</i>, <i>for he is thy brother</i> (Deut., xxiii. 8). Likewise, he of whom thou hast one day stood in need, from whom thou hast derived benefit and whom thou hast found in a moment of distress, even should he have done thee evil thereafter, thou art bound to take account of the past. God has said: <i>Thou shalt not abhor an Egyptian, because thou wast a stranger in his land</i> (<i>ibid.</i>). Nevertheless, it is well known how much evil the Egyptians did unto us thereafter[[fn:3543]]. — Thou seest how many noble virtues we learn by these commandments. The two last-cited passages, it is true, do not belong at all to this seventh class; but having spoken of the regard due to kinsmen in inheritances, we were led to say a word concerning the Egyptians and the Edomites.",
    "root.text.Part 3..42.0": "The commandments comprised in the eighth class are those which we have enumerated in the book <i>Zemannim</i> (of Times or Seasons). All, save a small number, are clearly justified by their motives in the (Biblical) text.",
    "root.text.Part 3..42.1": "As touching the institution of the Sabbath, the reason thereof is too well known to require exposition. It is understood that, on the one hand, it is for rest; it was desired that every person might devote the seventh part of his life to pleasure, and rest from the toils and troubles from which no one, neither small nor great, can escape. On the other hand, it was intended to perpetuate throughout the generations a great and most important doctrine, namely, that of the <i>creation of the world in time</i>[[fn:3544]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..42.2": "The institution of the fast of the Day of Atonement is likewise well-grounded, for it serves to establish the idea of repentance[[fn:3545]]. It is the day whereon the Prince of the Prophets brought down from Sinai the second tables unto the Israelites and announced unto them the pardon of their great sins[[fn:3546]]. This day became, therefore, in perpetuity a day of penitence uniquely consecrated to Divine worship. For this reason must one abstain on this day from all bodily gratification and from all occupation relating to material interests—I speak of industrial labors. One must confine himself (on that day) to confessions, that is to say, to confessing his sins and repenting of them.",
    "root.text.Part 3..42.3": "The <i>festivals</i> are all intended for rejoicings and for pleasant assemblies, which generally are necessary unto man, and have also the advantage of cementing the friendships which ought to be established among men in civil societies. Each of these days is particularly grounded.",
    "root.text.Part 3..42.4": "The subject of the <i>Passover</i> is well known; if it lasts seven days, it is because the period of seven days is a mean period between the natural day and the lunar month[[fn:3547]]. Thou knowest also that this period plays a great part in physical things[[fn:3548]]. Wherefore it is the same in religious matters; for religion ever imitats nature and, in a manner, complets physical things. Verily, nature has neither thought nor reflection, whereas religion is the rule and the regimen emanating from God, from whom every intelligent being derivs his intelligence. But this is not the subject of this chapter; we return, therefore, to the matters wherewith we are here occupied.",
    "root.text.Part 3..42.5": "The Feast of <i>Weeks</i> is the day of the Revelation of the Law[[fn:3549]]. To glorify and honor this day, one counts[[fn:3550]] the days from the first of the festivals until then, even as one who awaits the arrival of his best friend and counts the days and the hours. Such is the reason why one <i>counts the ’omer</i>[[fn:3551]] from the day of the departure from Egypt until the day of the revelation of the Law, which was the true object of that departure: <i>And I brought you unto myself</i> (Exodus xix. 4). This great spectacle lasted but one day, and likewise is the memory thereof celebrated each year during one day. But if one ate unleavened bread only for one day, it would not be perceived, and the thing which it is intended to recall would not become manifest; for it often happens that one partaks of the same kind of food for two or three days. It is only by continuing to eat it during a complete period that the matter which it has for its object becoms clear and the signification manifest.",
    "root.text.Part 3..42.6": "Likewise, the feast of the <i>New Year</i>[[fn:3552]] lasts but one day; for it is a day whereon men must do penance and awaken from their indolence. It is for this reason that the <i>shofar</i> (horn) is sounded on this day, as we have explained in the <i>Mishneh Torah</i>[[fn:3553]]; it is, as it were, a preparation and an opening for the day of fasting; thus thou seest that it is a traditional custom, very widespread in our communion, to observe the <i>ten days</i> from the commencement of the year until the Day of Atonement.",
    "root.text.Part 3..42.7": "The Feast of <i>Tabernacles</i>, being dedicated unto mirth and rejoicing, endurs seven days, to the end that the object thereof be well understood. The reason why it is celebrated in this season[[fn:3554]] is set forth in the Law: <i>When thou hast gathered in thy labours out of the field</i> (Exodus xxiii. 16); that is to say, at the time when, being free from care, thou restest from necessary toils. Aristotle has already remarked in the ninth book of the <i>Ethics</i> that such, it would seem[[fn:3555]], was a custom most widely diffused among the nations of antiquity. Thus he expresses himself: “The sacrifices and the (solemn) assemblies, among the ancients, took place after the ingathering of the fruits; they were, as it were, sacrifices of thanksgiving for the leisure[[fn:3556]].” Such are his words. Furthermore, one may easily dwell in the <i>succah</i> (booth) during this season, in which there is neither oppressive heat nor troublesome rain.",
    "root.text.Part 3..42.8": "The two festivals of <i>Tabernacles</i> and of the <i>Passover</i> have each for their object a religious belief and a moral thought. As touching belief, the Passover is intended to recall the miracles of Egypt and to perpetuate the memory thereof throughout all generations; and the Feast of Tabernacles is to perpetuate for ever the memory of the miracles of the wilderness. The moral thought is that man, in his prosperity, ought to remember the days of distress, so that he may manifest unto God his full gratitude and derive therefrom lessons of submission and humility. Therefore must we eat unleavened bread and bitter herbs during the feast of Passover, in order to remind ourselves of what befell us. Even so, we must quit our houses and dwell in booths, as do the unhappy inhabitants of the fields and deserts, to the end that we may remember that such was formerly our condition—(<i>that your generations may know</i>) <i>that I made the children of Israel to dwell in booths</i> (Leviticus xxiii. 43)—and that, through the goodness of God, we were brought forth thence to dwell in splendid houses in one of the fairest and most fertile regions of the earth, by virtue of the promises which He had made unto our ancestors, Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, men perfect in their beliefs and their virtues. Indeed, this is also one of the pivots of religion; I mean (the belief) that every benefit which God bestows or has bestowed upon us is due only to the merit of the patriarchs who <i>kept the way of the Lord to do justice and judgment</i>[[fn:3557]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..42.9": "The reason why the Festival of Cabanes terminates in a second festival, which is the <i>eighth day of closing</i>, is so that on this day one may complete the rejoicings to which one cannot give oneself over in the huts, but only in spacious habitations and in grand edifices.",
    "root.text.Part 3..42.10": "As for the four species (of plants) constituting the <i>lulab</i>[[fn:3558]], the doctors have assigned a reason for them after the manner of the <i>drashoth</i>[[fn:3559]], the method of which is known unto all who understand the words of the Rabbins; for these are with them as mere poetic allegories, and they mean not that such is the actual sense of the text[[fn:3560]]. Men have regarded the <i>drashoth</i> in two different ways: some have imagined that what the doctors have therein stated is the explanation of the true meaning of the texts; others, despising these explanations, have made them a subject of mockery, since it is most manifest that such is not the sense of the text. The former[[fn:3561]] have obstinately contended to defend, according to their own opinion, the truth of the <i>drashoth</i>[[fn:3562]], believing that this was the true sense of the (biblical) text, and that one should attribute to the <i>drashoth</i> the same authority as to the traditional laws. But neither of these two parties has been willing to understand that these were but poetic allegories, the meaning of which is by no means obscure to the intelligent man. This method was very prevalent in those times, and everyone employed it, even as poets use poetical locutions. Thus, for example, the doctors say: “Bar-Kappara taught that where it is said: <i>thou shalt have a paddle upon thy weapon</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">אזנך</span> (Deut. xxiii. 14), we should not read <small>AZENEKA</small> (thy <i>weapon</i>), but <small>OZNEKA</small> (thine <i>ear</i>); and this teaches us that a man, when he hears an unseemly thing, should put his finger in his ear[[fn:3563]].” Now, I would fain know whether, in the opinion of the ignorant, the doctor in question truly believed that this passage should be so explained, that such was the object of this commandment, and that by <small>YATHED</small> (<i>paddle</i>), one must understand the <i>finger</i>, and by <small>AZENEKA</small>, the <i>ears</i>. I do not think that a single man of sound sense could believe this. Rather is it a very beautiful poetic allegory whereby he wished to inculcate a noble moral, to wit, that it is forbidden to hear obscene words, even as it is forbidden to utter them; and he has attached this unto a biblical passage, after the manner of poetic allegories. Likewise, whenever it is said in the <i>drashoth:</i> “thou must not read in such a manner, but in such another,” it must be understood in this sense.—I have wandered from my subject; yet is this a useful observation, of which all intelligent theologians and Rabbins may have need. I now resume the continuation of our subject.",
    "root.text.Part 3..42.11": "In my opinion, the four species constituting the <i>lulab</i> indicate the mirth and joy which the Hebrews felt when they left the wilderness, which was <i>no place of seed, or of figs</i>, <i>or of vines, or of pomegranates</i>; <i>neither was there any water to drink</i> (Numbers xx. 5), to go unto places where there were fruit trees and rivers. To celebrate the memory thereof, they took the fairest and most fragrant fruit of those places[[fn:3564]], their foliage[[fn:3565]] and their most beautiful verdure, to wit, willows of the brook. These four species are distinguished by three peculiarities: 1st, they were in those times very frequent in the land of Israel, so that everyone could procure them. 2ndly, they are of a beautiful appearance, full of freshness, and have in part a goodly odour, such as the citron and the myrtle; as for the branches of the palm-tree and the willow, they have no odour, neither bad nor good. 3rdly, they keep their freshness for a week, a quality which peaches, pomegranates, quinces, pears, etc., possess not.",
    "root.text.Part 3..43.0": "The commandments comprised within the ninth class are those which we have enumerated in the book <i>Ahabâ</i> (Of the Love of God). They are all clearly motivated, and the reason for them is manifest[[fn:3566]]; for all these religious practices (which they prescribe for us) have for their object to cause us ever to think of God, to cause us to love and fear Him, to the end that we may obey His commandments in general, and believe concerning God that which every religious man must of necessity believe. These practices are: prayer[[fn:3567]], the reading of the <i>Schema</i>’[[fn:3568]], the blessing of the meal[[fn:3569]] and their appurtenances, the blessing of the priests[[fn:3570]], the phylacteries[[fn:3571]], the inscription upon the door-posts[[fn:3572]], the acquisition of the book of the Law and the reading thereof at certain seasons[[fn:3573]]. All these practices are of a nature to give rise to profitable thoughts; this is clear and evident, and it would be bootless to say a word more, for I could but repeat myself.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.0": "The commandments comprised within the tenth class are those which we have enumerated in the treatises <i>On the Chosen House</i> (or of the central sanctuary), in that <i>On the Utensils of the Sanctuary and its Ministers</i>, and in that <i>On the Entrance into the Sanctuary</i>[[fn:3574]]; we have already made known, in general, the utility of this class.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.1.sub_0": "It is well known that the idolaters sought to build their temples and to erect their idols in the highest place they could find: <i>upon the high mountains</i> (Deut. xii. 2). Therefore did our father Abraham choose Mount Moriah, which was the highest mountain in those regions[[fn:3575]], and there proclaimed the unity of God, and designated the <i>Kiblâ</i>[[fn:3576]], fixing it exactly to the West. Verily, the Holy of Holies was in the West, and it is this which the doctors indicate when they say: “The Divine Majesty is in the West[[fn:3577]].” The doctors have already explained in the Gemara of the treatise <i>Yômâ</i> that it was our father Abraham who determined the <i>Kiblâ</i>, that is to say, the site of the Holy of Holies[[fn:3578]]; and here, in my opinion, is the reason: Since it was then a very widespread opinion that worship should be rendered to the sun, which passed for God, and as doubtless all the world turned, in praying, towards the East[[fn:3579]], our father Abraham took for his <i>Kiblâ</i>, upon Mount Moriah, that is to say, upon the place of the sanctuary, the western side, in order to turn his back upon the sun. Dost thou not see what the Israelites did, when their defection[[fn:3580]] and their infidelity caused them to return to those ancient perverse opinions? <i>They turned their backs against the temple of the Lord and their faces toward the East, prostrating themselves toward the East before the sun</i> (Ezekiel viii. 16). Thou must well ponder this remarkable observation. I doubt not, moreover,",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.1.sub_1": "that this place chosen by Abraham in a prophetic vision was known to Moses our master and to many other persons; for Abraham had recommended that this place should be consecrated to worship, as the (Chaldean) translator expressly says[[fn:3581]]: “Abraham worshipped and prayed in this place and said before the Lord: Here future generations shall worship, etc.” If, in the Pentateuch, this is not expressly said in a positive manner, and if it is only alluded to by the words <i>which God shall choose</i>[[fn:3582]], there were for this, as it seems to me, three reasons: 1st, lest the (pagan) nations should seek to seize this place and wage violent war for its possession, knowing that it was the most important place upon earth for the religion (of the Israelites); 2nd, lest those who then possessed it should destroy it by devastating it as much as possible; 3rd, and this is the weightiest reason, lest each tribe should seek to have this place in the portion it was to possess, and to conquer it, which would have caused such disputes and troubles as there were concerning the priesthood. Wherefore it was commanded not to build the central sanctuary until after the establishment of the monarchy, so that it might belong to one alone to give orders[[fn:3583]], and that all strife might cease, as we have explained in the book <i>Schophetim</i>[[fn:3584]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.2.sub_0": "It is further known that those men (the idolaters) built temples to the planets[[fn:3585]] and that there was placed in each temple the statue which it was agreed to worship, that is to say, a statue consecrated to a certain planet forming part of a sphere[[fn:3586]]. We were therefore commanded to build a temple to the Most High and to deposit therein the holy Ark containing the two tables which comprised (these two commandments): <i>I am the Lord, etc.</i> and <i>Thou shalt have no other gods</i>, <i>etc.</i>— It is known that the article of faith concerning prophecy must precede the belief in the Law; for, without prophecy, there is no Law. The prophet receivs revelation only through the medium of an angel; for example: <i>And the angel of the Lord called</i> (Genesis xxii. 15), <i>And the angel of the Lord said unto her</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xvi. 9-11), and other passages innumerable. Moses himself was initiated into his prophetic mission by an angel: <i>The angel of the Lord appeared unto him in the midst of the fire</i> (Exodus iii. 2)[[fn:3587]]. It is therefore clear that the belief in the existence of angels must precede the belief in prophecy, and that the latter must precede the belief in the Law. Now, as the Sabians were ignorant of the existence of God and imagined that the Eternal Being, who could never not have existed, was the celestial sphere with its stars, whose forces were poured out upon idols and upon certain trees, such as the <i>Aschêrôth</i>[[fn:3588]], they believed that it was the idols and the trees that inspired the prophets, made revelations to them by speaking to them, and made known to them what was useful or harmful; and these opinions we have already set forth, in speaking of the <i>prophets of Baal</i> and of the <i>prophets of Aschêrâ</i>[[fn:3589]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.2.sub_1": "But when the truth was manifested to men, and it was known by means of demonstrations that there exists a Being who is neither a body nor a force within a body, to wit, the true and only God; that, furthermore, there exist other beings, <i>separate</i>[[fn:3590]] and incorporeal, upon whom <i>is poured forth</i> the divine being[[fn:3591]] and who are the angels, as we have set forth[[fn:3592]], and finally, that all these beings are without the celestial sphere and its stars, then were men convinced that it was these angels who in reality made revelations unto the prophets, and not the idols and the <i>Aschérôth</i>. Thus, it is manifest from the foregoing that the belief in the existence of angels is joined to that which has for its object the existence of God, and that it serves to establish the truth of prophetic revelation and of the Law. To confirm this article of faith, God commanded the placing above the ark the image <i>of two angels</i>[[fn:3593]], so as to consolidate the belief of the people in the existence of angels, a true belief, which is the second after the belief in the existence of God, as well as the principle of prophecy and of the Law, and the negation of idolatry, as we have set forth. Had there been but a single figure, I mean the figure of a single cherub, it might have given rise to error, and one might have believed that it was a figure under which God was worshipped, as the idolaters were wont to do, or also that there was but a single individual angel[[fn:3594]], which would have led to a kind of dualism. But, as two cherubim were made, alongside the express declaration that <i>the Lord our God is one</i> (Deut. vi. 4), the belief in the existence of angels was thereby confirmed, and it was established that they were several. One ran no risk, therefore, of being deceived and of taking them for God, since God is <i>one</i> and it is He who has created this plurality (of Intelligences).",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.3": "Before[[fn:3595]] it was placed the <i>candlestick</i> as a sign of honour and respect for the temple; for this temple, ever illumined by lamps and separated (from the Holy of Holies) by a vail[[fn:3596]], was intended to impress the soul mightily. Thou knowest what importance the Law attaches to our being struck with the majesty of the sanctuary and the respect which is due thereunto, so that in beholding it man may have the sentiment of his own frailty and become humble. It is said: <i>Ye shall reverence my sanctuary</i> (Levit. xix. 30); and, to give more force to this recommendation, it has been joined to that of the observance of the Sabbath.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.4": "— The altar of incense, the altar of burnt offerings and their vessels were of an evident necessity[[fn:3597]]. As for the table and the bread which was to be continually set forth thereon[[fn:3598]], I know not the reason thereof, and until this moment I have found naught to which I might attribute this usage.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.5": "As for the prohibition of <i>hewing</i> the stones of the altar[[fn:3599]], thou knowest the reason which the Doctors have given for it: “It is not fitting,” say they, “that that which shortens life should be lifted up upon that which prolongs it[[fn:3600]].” This is good after the manner of <i>drashôth</i>, as we have said; but this prohibition has another manifest reason. It is that the idolaters constructed their altars with polished stones; wherefore it was forbidden to do as they did, and, to avoid this imitation, it was commanded to make the altar of earth, as it is said: <i>An altar of earth thou shalt make unto me</i> (Exodus xx. 21). Nevertheless, if it became indispensable to make it of stone, these stones must at least have their natural form and be not polished. Thus was it also forbidden to erect stones adorned with figures and to plant trees near the altar[[fn:3601]]. All this has one and the same purpose, to wit, that we should not worship God under the individual forms of the cults[[fn:3602]] which they (the heathens) rendered unto their divinities, and this has been forbidden in a general manner in these terms: <i>How did these nations serve their gods? even so will I do likewise</i> (Deuter. xii. 30), which signifies that one must not act thus toward God, for the reason thereafter stated: <i>Every abomination to the Lord</i>, <i>which he hats</i>, <i>have they done unto their gods</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 31).",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.6": "Thou knowest also how widespread in those times was the worship of <i>Pe’ôr</i>, which was celebrated by uncovering one's shameful parts[[fn:3603]]. Wherefore it was commanded unto the priests to make for themselves breeches <i>to cover their nakedness</i> (Exodus xxviii. 42) during the service, and nevertheless they were not to go up to the altar by steps (as it is said): <i>that thy nakedness be not discovered thereon</i> (Exodus xx. 23).",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.7": "The Temple was to be continually guarded, and a watch maintained round about it, for the sake of its honor and that it might be held in reverence<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3604]]</sup>, and lest it should be encroached upon by the profane<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3605]]</sup>, the unclean, and those in a state of impurity<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3606]]</sup>, as shall be hereafter explained. That which, among other things, was intended to glorify the Sanctuary and to cause it to be honored in such wise as to ensure our respect, was the prohibition of entry unto men intoxicated<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3607]]</sup>, the unclean, and those of ill-kept appearance—to wit, having their hair in disarray or their garments rent—and the requirement that every officiating minister should sanctify his hands and his feet<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3608]]</sup>.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.8": "It was further for the honor of the Temple that it was ordained to honor its ministers; the priests and Levites were specially designated (for the service), and the priests were provided with splendid vestments, of great beauty and elegance: <i>holy garments for glory and for beauty</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3609]]</sup>. No man who possessed a physical blemish was admitted to the service, and herein it is not only a question of one afflicted with an infirmity, for deformities likewise rendered the priests unfit (for service)<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3610]]</sup>, as has been set forth in the traditional explanation of this commandment<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3611]]</sup>; for the vulgar do not esteem a man according to his true form<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3612]]</sup>, but rather by the perfection of his members and the beauty of his raiment. All this is designed to the end that the Temple be honored and respected by all people.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.9": "— As for the Levite, who was not charged with the offering of sacrifices and was not deemed to make atonement for sins—as it is said of the priests: <i>He shall make an atonement for him</i>, or <i>for her</i> (Leviticus, iv. 26; xii. 8, and <i>passim</i>)—but had no other function than the recitation of hymns, he became unfit for service only upon the loss of his voice<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3613]]</sup>. Verily, the purpose of song is to ensure that the words exert a more profound impression upon the soul; now, the soul is only so impressed by sweet melodies, with the accompaniment of musical instruments, as was ever the custom in the Sanctuary.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.10": "— Even unto those priests fit for service who remained in the Sanctuary, it was not permitted to sit therein, nor to enter the interior of the Temple at all times, nor ever to enter the Holy of Holies, with the exception of the High Priest (who might enter it on the Day of Atonement four times, and no more)<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3614]]</sup>; and all this was out of reverence for the Sanctuary.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.11": "Since, in this holy place, many animals were daily slaughtered, and flesh was therein cut up and consumed by fire, and the inwards were washed<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3615]]</sup>, it is certain that, had it been left in such a state, it would have yielded an odor like unto that of a shambles. Wherefore it was commanded to burn incense there twice daily, in the morning and in the afternoon, to diffuse a pleasant odor and to perfume the garments of all who performed the service. Thou knowest what the Sages say: “From Jericho the odor of the incense was perceived”<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3616]]</sup>. This served equally to maintain the respect for the Sanctuary. But had it not possessed a pleasant odor, and still more so if the contrary had been the case, there would have resulted the opposite of respect; for the soul is cheered by goodly odors and is attracted thereby, whilst it is repelled by foul odors and fles from them.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.12": "— As for the <i>oil of anointing</i>[[fn:3617]], it possessed a twofold advantage: on the one hand, it imparted a sweet savour unto that which was imbued therewith, and on the other, it inspired reverence for the object anointed, sanctifying it and distinguishing it from other things of like kind, whether it were a human individual, a garment, or a vessel. All this was intended to lead to the veneration of the Temple, which, in its turn, should inspire the fear of God; for, upon entering therein, one was deeply impressed, and hearts of stone were softened and rendered pliable. It is for the purpose of softening them and making them humble that God, by His <i>remote decrees</i>[[fn:3618]], has employed all this foreseeing wisdom, so that, by frequenting the Temple, they might become accessible unto the divine precepts, which serve us as guides, and might attain unto the fear of God, as is clearly set forth in the text of the Pentateuch: <i>Thou shalt eat before the Lord thy God, in the place which He shall choose to place His name there, the tithe of thy corn, of thy wine, and of thine oil, and the firstlings of thy herds and of thy flocks; that thou mayest learn to fear the Lord thy God always</i> (Deut. xiv. 23). Thou wilt now perceive what was the end in view in prescribing all these things. — The reason why it was forbidden to imitate the oil of anointing and the perfumes is right manifest: it was, on the one part, to the end that this odour should be perceived only in that holy place, and that the impression thereof might be the more profound; and, on the other part, lest it might be believed that all who were anointed with this oil, or with an oil similar thereto, were men of distinction, which might have given rise to grave inconveniences and contentions.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.13": "If it was commanded to bear the Ark upon the shoulders, and not upon wagons, it is manifest that this was to show respect unto it[[fn:3619]]; nought was to be altered in its form, and the staves were not even to be removed from the rings[[fn:3620]]. In like manner, one was not to alter the form of the Ephod and of the Breastplate, nor even to separate them one from the other[[fn:3621]]. All the garments of the priests were to be woven of a single piece[[fn:3622]], being neither cut nor fashioned by division, to the end that the form of the fabric might not be altered.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.14": "— It was also forbidden to each of the ministers of the Sanctuary to take upon himself the functions of others[[fn:3623]]; for when duties are entrusted unto several persons, without assigning unto each[[fn:3624]] a particular office, it comes to pass that all neglect them and become remiss.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.15": "— It is evident also that this gradation established for the different places (of the Sanctuary)[[fn:3625]], by prescribing special ordinances for the Temple Mount, for the <i>ḥēl</i> or rampart[[fn:3626]], for the Court of the Women, for the Courtyard, and so forth even unto the Holy of Holies—all this, I say, had for its object to render greater homage unto the Temple and to inspire a deeper reverence in all who approached it.",
    "root.text.Part 3..44.16": "And now we have set forth the reasons for all the particular commandments which are comprised within this class.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.0": "The commandments comprised in the eleventh class are those which we have enumerated in the remainder of the book <i>’Abôdâ</i> (of Worship) and in the book <i>Korbanôth</i> (of Sacrifices). We have already treated of their general utility[[fn:3627]], and we shall now undertake to set forth the reasons thereof in detail, so far as we have been able to apprehend them. Thus, then, we say.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.1": "It is related in the text of the Pentateuch, according to the exposition of Onkelos, that the ancient Egyptians worshipped the constellation of Aries; wherefore it was forbidden among them to slay sheep, and they held shepherds in abomination, as it is said: <i>Lo, shall we sacrifice the abomination of the Egyptians, etc.</i> (Exodus, viii, 22)[[fn:3628]]; <i>For every shepherd is an abomination unto the Egyptians</i> (Genesis, xlvi, 34). In like manner, certain sects of the Sabians who worshipped demons believed that these latter assumed the form of he-goats; for which reason they bestowed upon the demons the name of he-goats. This opinion was very prevalent in the days of Moses, our teacher: <i>and that they no more offer their sacrifices unto he-goats</i> (Levit., xvii, 7); wherefore these sects likewise abstained from the eating of he-goats[[fn:3629]]. As for the slaughtering of oxen, it was held in abomination by well-nigh all idolaters, and all held this species in great honour[[fn:3630]]. Hence thou wilt find that the Hindus even unto our own day never slaughter the bovine species, even in those lands where they sacrifice[[fn:3631]] other kinds of animals. It was, therefore, to obliterate the vestiges of these unwholesome opinions that we were commanded to sacrifice specifically these three kinds of quadrupeds: <i>of the herd and of the flock ye shall bring your offering</i> (Levit., i, 2), so that men might draw nigh unto God by the very act which they had deemed the most heinous of crimes, and that they might seek in that act the remission of their sins. Thus was it sought to heal these corrupt notions, which are the maladies of the human soul, by means of the contrary extreme.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.2": "It was precisely for this same purpose that we were commanded to sacrifice the Paschal lamb, and, in Egypt, to sprinkle the blood thereof upon the exterior of the doors, that we might be delivered from those opinions, and that by proclaiming the contrary we might make (the Egyptians) share in the belief that the act which they regarded[[fn:3632]] as a cause of death was, on the contrary, that which saved from death: <i>And the Lord will pass over the door, and will not suffer the destroyer to come in unto your houses to smite you</i> (Exodus, xii, 23), as a reward for having publicly performed their worship and rejected the follies professed by idolaters[[fn:3633]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.3": "— Such, then, is the reason for which these three species were particularly chosen for the sacrifices. Moreover, these species are domestic animals[[fn:3634]] which exist in great number; whereas the idolaters were wont to sacrifice[[fn:3635]] lions, bears, and other wild beasts, as is related in the book <i>Tomtom</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.4": "Inasmuch as the greater part of the people possess not the means to offer a quadruped, it was ordained to offer likewise as a sacrifice those birds which are most common in Syria, being the best and most easy to obtain: namely, turtledoves and young pigeons[[fn:3636]]. He that was unable to offer even a bird might offer baked oblations, prepared in any of the divers manners of cooking known in those times, whether in the oven, or upon the griddle, or in a frying-pan; and he who found it difficult to offer baked cakes might offer fine flour[[fn:3637]]. All these ordinances were directed unto those who had the voluntary desire (to offer sacrifices)[[fn:3638]]. — Furthermore, it is expressly stated that, if we did not perform this manner of worship, I mean that of sacrifices, we should not thereby be stained by any sin: <i>But if thou shalt forbear to vow, it shall be no sin in thee</i> (Deuter., xxiii, 23).",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.5": "Since the idolaters offered but leavened bread, and frequently offered sweet things and mingled honey in their offerings,—as is often seen in the books of which I have spoken to thee,—and since in none of their offerings did they make use of salt[[fn:3639]], God, on the one hand, forbade the offering of any manner <i>of leaven</i> or <i>of honey</i> (Levit. ii. 11), and, on the other hand, He commanded that salt should ever be offered: <i>With all thine offerings thou shalt offer salt</i> (<i>ibid., v</i>. 13).",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.6": "All sacrifices were to be without blemish and in the most excellent state, to the end that one should not come to disdain the sacrifice nor to hold in contempt that which was to be offered to the Divinity, as it is said: <i>Offer it now unto thy governor; will he be pleased with thee, or accept thy person?</i> (Malachi i. 8.) It is also for the same reason[[fn:3640]] that it was forbidden to offer in sacrifice an animal that has not yet accomplished seven full days[[fn:3641]], forasmuch as its species is not yet perfectly formed and it is found to be repulsive; it is indeed like unto an untimely birth. It is likewise for the same reason that it is forbidden to offer the hire of a harlot and the price of a dog[[fn:3642]], by reason of the turpitude of these two things. For the same reason also, full-grown turtle-doves and young pigeons were offered[[fn:3643]], both being the best of their kind, for full-grown pigeons possess no flavour. Finally, for the same reason, the offerings were to be kneaded with oil and composed of fine flour[[fn:3644]], for these are the things most perfect and sweet. The frankincense (which was placed therein) was chosen because of the goodly fragrance which its smoke diffused in places where there was an odour of burnt meat.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.7": "It is further out of reverence for the sacrifice, and that it should not be regarded with aversion and loathing, that it was prescribed to flay the burnt-offering and to wash the inwards and the legs, although they were to be burnt in their entirety[[fn:3645]]. Thou wilt find that this was a matter of constant concern and from which they sought to preserve themselves: <i>But ye have profaned it, in that ye say, The table of the Lord is polluted; and</i> (<i>ye disparage it</i>) <i>saying,</i>[[fn:3646]] <i>the fruit thereof, even His meat, is contemptible</i> (Malachi i. 12). For the same reason also, an uncircumcised or unclean man[[fn:3647]] may not eat of the sacrifice; nor can it be eaten when it has been rendered unclean, nor after the prescribed delay, nor when it has been profaned by thought[[fn:3648]], and it must be eaten in a specified place[[fn:3649]]. The burnt-offering, which pertains wholly unto God, may in no wise be eaten; that which is offered in atonement for a fault, to wit, the <i>sin-offering</i> and the <i>guilt-offering</i>[[fn:3650]], must be eaten within the court, and only on the very day of the slaughtering and the night following. The peace-offerings, which are of a lower degree and of lesser holiness, must be eaten only within the whole city of Jerusalem and may yet be eaten on the morrow (of the slaughtering), but no later; for after that delay, they spoil and become corrupt.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.8": "It is also to cause us to reverence the sacrifice and all that has been consecrated in the name of God, that the Law declars guilty whosoever shall have derived enjoyment from holy things; he must offer a trespass-offering and pay a fifth part more thereto[[fn:3651]], even though he should have committed the sin through inadvertence.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.9": "In like manner, it was forbidden to work with sacred animals or to shear them[[fn:3652]]; all this, out of reverence for the sacrifices. The law relating to the exchange (of animals)[[fn:3653]] was given by way of precaution; for, had it been permitted to substitute a good animal for a bad one, men would also have substituted a bad one for a good one, pretending that it was better. The Law therefore pronounced that: <i>both</i> (<i>the beast) itself and that which was put in its place should be holy</i> (Leviticus xxvii. 10 and 33). — If it was prescribed that he who would redeem any of the things he had consecrated must add a fifth part of the value[[fn:3654]], the reason thereof is manifest. Verily, (as the proverb says) \"the nearest kinsman to a man is himself\"[[fn:3655]]; being therefore ever inclined by his nature to be parsimonious of his money, he would not inquire diligently[[fn:3656]] into the price of the consecrated thing, nor would he submit it to a rigorous estimation[[fn:3657]], in order to truly ascertain its value. Wherefore we have been secured against him by the requirement of an augmentation[[fn:3658]], to the end that the consecrated object might be sold to another for its true value[[fn:3659]]; all this, to preserve from contempt that which has been consecrated to God and that which is to serve in obtaining His favour[[fn:3660]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.10": "The reason wherefore <i>the offering of the priest</i>[[fn:3661]] was to be burnt, is that every priest was bound to present his offering with his own hand[[fn:3662]]; if, therefore, he had himself eaten the offering presented by him, it would have been as though he had offered nothing at all. Verily, of every offering of a private individual, there was offered upon the altar only the incense and a handful (of flour)[[fn:3663]]. If then, not content with the scantiness of this sacrifice, he who offered it had further been permitted to eat it, there would not have even remained a semblance of worship; for which cause this offering was to be burnt.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.11": "The ordinances which particularly concern the paschal lamb, to wit, that it must be eaten only <i>roasted with fire</i>, in <i>one</i> house, and <i>without breaking a single bone thereof</i> (Exodus, xii, 8 and 46), have all an evident reason. Indeed, even as the unleavened bread is motivated by haste, so also the roasting had for its motive precipitancy[[fn:3664]]; for they had not leisure to make diverse dishes nor to prepare dainties. They would even have feared to tarry for the breaking of the bones and the taking out of the contents; for, to sum up all this[[fn:3665]], it is said: <i>and ye shall eat it in haste</i> (<i>ibid., v</i>. 11). Now, in haste, one cannot stop to break the bones, nor to send portions from one house to another and await the return of the messenger; for all such actions denote negligence and delay, whilst the object was to secure themselves by speed and precipitancy, lest any person should be late and miss the opportunity of departing with the multitude, so that harm might have befallen him by being taken unawares. These customs were thereafter perpetuated in commemoration of the event, as it is said: <i>Thou shalt therefore keep this ordinance in his season from year to year</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xiii, 10). If it was said that \"the paschal lamb could only be eaten by those who had been numbered to partake of it[[fn:3666]],\" it was to inculcate the duty of purchasing it, and so that no one should rely upon a kinsman, a friend, or the first comer who might have offered it to him, lest he should have failed to provide for it beforehand. As for the prohibition against giving it to be eaten by the uncircumcised[[fn:3667]], the doctors have already explained it[[fn:3668]], saying that the Hebrews, during their long sojourn in Egypt, had neglected the commandment of circumcision, in order to assimilate themselves unto the Egyptians[[fn:3669]]. When, therefore, the paschal lamb was ordained and it was made a condition that no one should slaughter it save after having practiced circumcision upon himself, upon his children, and upon the men of his house, and that only then <i>he might come near and keep it</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xii, 48), they all underwent circumcision. The multitude of the circumcised, say they, caused the blood of circumcision to be mingled with the blood of the paschal lamb, and it is to this that the prophet alludes when he says: <i>weltering in thy blood</i> (Ezekiel, xvi, 6), to wit, the blood of the paschal lamb and that of the circumcision[[fn:3670]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.12.sub_0": "It must be known that the Sabians regarded blood as a thing most unclean, and, despite this, they ate it, because they believed it to be the food of demons, and that, if any person ate thereof, he did thereby fraternize with these evil spirits, who would come unto him and make known to him future things, as the vulgar imagine concerning demons. There were, however, some people to whom it seemed hard to eat blood, for it is a thing repugnant to human nature. These, therefore, having slaughtered an animal, collected the blood into a vessel or into a pit, and ate the flesh of that animal near the blood; they imagined, in so doing, that the demons ate this blood, which was their food, whilst they themselves ate the flesh, and that, by this means, fraternization might be obtained, since they all ate at the same table and in the same gathering. According to their opinion, the demons would then appear unto them in a dream, make known to them hidden things[[fn:3671]] and render them services. These were the opinions followed in those times, accepted with eagerness and generally spread abroad, the truth of which was beyond doubt in the eyes of the vulgar[[fn:3672]]. The perfect Law undertook to bring to an end these inveterate maladies in those who acknowledge it, by forbidding the eating of blood; it insisted upon this prohibition as much as upon that of idolatry; God has said: <i>I will set my face</i> (My anger) <i>against that soul that eats blood</i> (Leviticus, xvii, 10), even as He said concerning him who gives of his seed unto Molech: <i>I will set my face against that man</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xx, 5)[[fn:3673]]. There exists no third commandment concerning which one expresses himself in this manner, which is only employed toward those who give themselves up to idolatry or who eat blood; for by eating of the latter, one is led to a species of idolatry, which is the worship of demons. Nevertheless, it (the Law) declared blood to be pure and made of it a means of purification for him who should undergo contact with it: <i>Thou shalt sprinkle it upon Aaron, and upon his garments, etc., and he shall be hallowed, and his garments</i> (Exodus, xxix, 21).",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.12.sub_1": "It commanded the sprinkling thereof upon the altar, and made the whole ceremony consist in pouring it there, not in gathering it: <i>And I</i>, it is said, <i>have given it to you upon the altar to make an atonement, etc.</i> (Levit., xvii, 11); it was poured there, as it is said: <i>And he shall pour out all the blood</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, iv, 18), and elsewhere: <i>The blood of thy sacrifices shall be poured out upon the altar of the Lord thy God</i> (Deuter., xii, 27). Finally, it was even commanded to pour out the blood of every animal that one should slaughter, without it being a sacrifice, as it is said: <i>Thou shalt pour it upon the earth as water</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xii, 16 and 24; xv, 23). Next, it was forbidden to assemble around the blood and to eat there, as it is said: <i>Ye shall not eat anything with the blood</i> (Levit., xix, 26)[[fn:3674]]. As they persisted in sinning and in following the well-known custom in which they had been nurtured, of fraternizing with demons by eating around the blood, God absolutely forbade them to eat in the wilderness <i>flesh of desire</i>[[fn:3675]], but willed that every (animal intended for consumption) should be offered as a <i>peace-offering</i>[[fn:3676]], declaring unto us that the reason thereof was that the blood should be poured upon the altar and that they should not assemble around it; He said therefore: <i>To the end that the children of Israel may bring, etc.</i>, <i>and they shall no more offer their sacrifices unto devils</i>, or <i>demons</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xvii, 5 and 7). But there yet remained (to prescribe a rule of conduct) concerning the wild beast and the fowl, for the wild beast could never serve as a sacrifice and the fowl could not be offered as a <i>peace-offering</i>[[fn:3677]]; God therefore prescribed, following this, that when one had slaughtered a wild beast or any fowl whatsoever of which it is permitted to eat the flesh, one should cover the blood thereof with dust[[fn:3678]], so that they should not assemble to eat around the blood. It was thus that the end of <i>severing the fellowship</i>[[fn:3679]] between those who were truly possessed and their demons was completely attained.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.13": "— It must be known that this belief was well-nigh contemporary with Moses, our teacher, that it was much followed and that it led men astray. Thou findest this textually in the song <small>HAAZINU</small>: <i>They sacrificed unto devils, not to God; to gods whom they knew not, etc.</i> (Deuter., xxxii, 17). The doctors have thus explained the meaning of the words <i>not to God:</i> not content, say they, with worshipping real beings, they even worship imaginary beings. Thus it is expressed in the <i>Sifri:</i> \"It suffices them not to worship the sun, the moon, the planets and the constellations, but they even worship the reflections thereof (<span dir=\"rtl\">בבואה</span>).\" The word <span dir=\"rtl\">בבואה</span> is the name for the shadow (or the <i>reflection</i>)[[fn:3680]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.14": "— I now return to our subject. It must be known that <i>meat of desire</i> was prohibited in the wilderness alone; for it was one of those opinions then current that demons inhabited the deserts, and that there they did speak and appear, whilst in cities and inhabited places they did not manifest themselves; wherefore such of the inhabitants of cities as desired to practice any of those follies went forth from the city and betook themselves to desert and solitary places. Hence it was that <i>meat of desire</i> was permitted (unto the Hebrews) after their entry into the land (of Canaan). Moreover, this malady must then have lost its strength, and the adherents of such opinions must have diminished. Furthermore, it would have been passing difficult and well-nigh impossible for all who desired to eat of the flesh of a (domestic) animal to repair unto Jerusalem. For all these reasons, <i>meat of desire</i> had been forbidden only in the desert.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.15": "Furthermore, it is to be known that the more grave a sin, the more does the sacrifice required for it diminish in value as touching its species[[fn:3681]]. For this reason, the sin of idolatry committed through inadvertence especially demands a she-goat[[fn:3682]], and the other sins of an individual require a ewe or a she-goat[[fn:3683]]; for in every species the female is of less account than the male, and there is no sin greater than idolatry, nor any species[[fn:3684]] lower than the she-goat. By reason of the distinguished rank occupied by the king, the sacrifice which he offers for a sin of inadvertence is a he-goat[[fn:3685]]; as for the high priest and the congregation, since their sin of inadvertence consists not in a mere (personal) act, but in a legal decision[[fn:3686]], their sacrifice was distinguished by prescribing for them the offering of bullocks[[fn:3687]], and, for the sin of idolatry, he-goats[[fn:3688]]. — Inasmuch as the sins for which an <i>asham</i> (trespass-offering) was offered are less grave than those for which a <i>hattâth</i> (sin-offering) was brought, the <i>asham</i> sacrifice was a ram or a young lamb[[fn:3689]]; thus was chosen a species and a sex more distinguished, and it was ordained to be a male from among the sheep. Perceivest thou not that for the burnt-offering likewise, which belongs entirely unto God, a more distinguished sex was chosen, and it can be naught but a male[[fn:3690]]? It is further by the same principle that the offering of the sinner and that of the unfaithful wife suspected of a sin[[fn:3691]] were deprived of embellishment and sweet savour, and neither oil nor frankincense was to be put with these offerings. This embellishment was suppressed therein because the person offering it had not followed a seemly conduct; and, as if there were a movement of repentance, it was said, as it were, unto him[[fn:3692]]: By reason of thy evil actions, thy offering shall be in an inferior state. As for the unfaithful wife, whose action is more shameful than a sin of inadvertence, her offering is of an inferior substance; for it consists of barley meal. These particulars which have just been reviewed[[fn:3693]] possess a most remarkable significance.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.16.sub_0": "The Sages say that the reason why, on the eighth day of the consecration (of the priests), <i>a young calf as a sin-offering</i> was offered (Levit. ix. 2), was to make atonement for the golden calf; and that likewise the sin-offering of the Day of Atonement was <i>a young bullock for sin</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, XVI, 3), to make atonement for the golden calf. In accordance with the idea they have expressed, it seems to me that the reason why all sin-offerings, as well for the individual as for the congregation, were he-goats, — I speak of the he-goats offered on the festivals, on the New Moons, and on the Day of Atonement, as well as the he-goats offered for the sin of idolatry[[fn:3694]], — the reason, I say, is that their principal sin at that time was to offer sacrifices unto the he-goats (demons), as the text of Scripture expressly says: <i>and they shall no more offer their sacrifices unto he-goats</i> (demons) <i>after whom they have gone astray</i> (Levit. xvii. 7). As for the Sages, they think that the reason why the atonement for the sins of the congregation was constantly made by the sacrifice of he-goats was that the he-goat is connected with the sin which the whole congregation of Israel had committed of old. They allude to the selling of Joseph the righteous, in whose history it is said: <i>And they killed a kid of the goats, etc.</i> (Genesis xxxvii. 31)[[fn:3695]]. This reason must by no means be considered weak; for the aim of all His actions is that every sinner should be convinced that he must ever remember his sin and confess it, as it is said: <i>And my sin is ever before me</i> (Ps. li. 5), and that he and his posterity must seek to obtain pardon for that sin by a religious act of the same kind as the sin itself. This is what I would say: If he has sinned in a matter of money, the reparative act must also consist in a sacrifice of money; if he has sinned by corporeal enjoyments, he must impose upon himself a religious act which fatigus and afflicts his body, by fasting and watching in the night; if he has committed a moral sin, he must repair it by an opposite moral act, as we have explained in the treatise <i>De'ôth</i> (of Morals) and elsewhere[[fn:3696]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.16.sub_1": "Finally, if he has committed a speculative error, that is to say if, through his incapacity or his negligence in betaking himself to research and speculation, he has admitted a false idea, he must combat it by banishing it from his mind and by preventing it from dwelling[[fn:3697]] upon aught worldly, to occupy himself only with matters of the intellect and the serious examination of things which ought to be believed. It is in this sense, well-nigh, that it was said: <i>If my heart has been secretly enticed, my hand has been laid upon my mouth</i> (Job xxxi. 27)[[fn:3698]], which is an allegorical expression signifying that one must <i>abstain</i> and <i>halt before that which is obscure</i>, as we have set forth in the first book of this treatise[[fn:3699]]. Thus, thou seest that when Aaron had failed in making the golden calf, it was imposed upon him, and upon all those of his race who were to succeed him, to offer a bullock and a young calf[[fn:3700]]. Likewise, where the sin is brought into relation with a he-goat, the religious act was accomplished by means of a he-goat[[fn:3701]]. If the soul be well penetrated with these ideas, man shall be led thereby to hold sin in abhorrence and to depart from it, that he may not be obliged, in falling therein, to submit himself to a long and painful atonement; sometimes even the atonement cannot be accomplished[[fn:3702]], so that man shall avoid sin in advance and flee from it, which evidently is of great utility. It behoovs thee to penetrate thy mind well with this subject.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.17": "I deem it my duty here to call thy attention to a most remarkable thing, albeit it may seem foreign to the purpose of this treatise. The he-goat offered at the New Moons is alone called <i>sin-offering</i> <small>UNTO</small> <small>THE</small> LORD</small> (Numbers xxviii. 15), an expression which is employed neither for any of the he-goats offered at the festivals, nor for the other sin-offerings; the reason of which, in my judgment, is very clear: it is that the sacrifices which the congregation offered at certain seasons, that is to say, the additional sacrifices (of the festivals), were all burnt-offerings, and there was each day a he-goat as a sin-offering. This he-goat was eaten, whilst the burnt-offerings were entirely consumed by fire; wherefore they are expressly called <i>offering made by fire</i> <small>UNTO</small> <small>THE</small> LORD</small>[[fn:3703]], whilst it is never said either <i>sin-offering</i> <small>UNTO</small> <small>THE</small> LORD</small>, nor <i>peace-offering</i> <small>UNTO</small> <small>THE</small> LORD</small>, because these sacrifices were eaten. Even those sin-offerings which were burned[[fn:3704]] could not be called <i>offerings made by fire unto the Lord</i>, the reason of which I shall explain in this chapter. One could not, therefore, (all the more so) call the he-goats (of the festivals) <i>sin-offerings unto the Lord</i>, for they were eaten, and were not burned in their entirety. But, as it might be feared that the he-goat of the New Moons should be considered as a sacrifice offered unto the moon, after the example of the Egyptians, who offered sacrifices unto the moon at the beginnings of the months[[fn:3705]], it is expressly said in speaking of this he-goat that it is consecrated <i>unto God</i>, and not unto the moon. This fear could not be entertained concerning the he-goats offered at the festivals and on other solemn days[[fn:3706]], for these days were neither beginnings of months, nor marked by any phenomenon of nature, but had been instituted by the decrees of the Law. On the contrary, the beginnings of the lunar months were not instituted by the Law; but the nations offered on those days sacrifices unto the moon, even as they offered them unto the sun when he rose and when he entered into certain degrees (of the ecliptic), as is known from those books (of the Sabians). It is for this reason that there is used, in speaking of this he-goat (of the New Moons), a particular expression[[fn:3707]], saying <i>unto the Lord</i>, in order to destroy the errors which were rooted in the grievously sick hearts (of the Israelites). Penetrate thy mind well with this remarkable idea.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.18": "It must also be known that every sin-offering, whereby it is believed that great sins are expiated, or even a single sin—as, for instance, the sacrifice for the <i>sin of ignorance</i>[[fn:3708]] and other like offerings[[fn:3709]]—is burnt entirely without the precincts, and not upon the altar; for naught was burnt upon the altar save the burnt-offering and that which resembls it[[fn:3710]], and for this cause is it called the <i>altar of burnt-offering</i> (Exodus, xxx, 28, and <i>passim</i>). Verily, the consumed burnt-offering was (accounted) a <i>sweet savour unto God</i>, and likewise every <i>Azcarâ</i>[[fn:3711]] offered a <i>sweet savour unto God</i>. It must indubitably have been thus, since this ceremony was intended to abolish idolatrous beliefs, as we have already set forth. But the custom of burning those <i>sin-offerings</i> (of which we have spoken) signifies naught else than that the trace of such sin was blotted out and had vanished even as that body which had just been consumed by fire, and that no vestige of that action remained, even as there remained no vestige of that sin-offering which had been destroyed by the flames. Consequently, the latter, when it was burnt, could not yield a <i>sweet savour</i> unto God; but, on the contrary, it was a smoke which God must needs detest and abhor; wherefore it was burnt entirely <i>without the precincts</i>. Dost thou not perceive that, concerning the offering of the woman suspected of adultery, it is said that it is an <i>offering of memorial to bring iniquity to remembrance</i> (Numbers, v, 15), and not that it is a thing favourably accepted?",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.19": "The <i>scapegoat</i> being ordained for the total expiation of great sins, so that there exists no public sin-offering which expiats so many as it, and that it, in a manner, bears away all sins, it was not to be slaughtered, nor burnt, nor offered as a sacrifice[[fn:3712]]; but it was to be removed as far as possible and cast into a land called <small>GUEZÉRA</small> (Levit., xvi, 22), that is to say, a place remote from habitations. It is indubitable to all that sins are not bodies[[fn:3713]] which can be transferred from the back of one individual unto that of another. But all these acts are but symbols intended to make an impression[[fn:3714]] upon the soul, to the end that this impression may lead to penitence; the meaning being: we are delivered from the burden of all our former actions, which we have cast behind us and hurled to a great distance.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.20": "As regards the offering of wine, it has hitherto left me in perplexity. How is it that an offering of wine was prescribed, which was also presented by idolaters[[fn:3715]]; I have not been able to comprehend this; but another has given the reason, which is as follows: The faculty of <i>appétition</i>, which has its source in the liver, finds nothing better than flesh; the faculty <i>vitale</i>, which has its source in the heart, finds nothing better than wine; in like manner, the faculty which has its source in the brain, namely the faculty <i>psychique</i>, delights in songs accompanied by instruments[[fn:3716]]. It is for this reason that each faculty endeavours to approach God by means of the thing it loves most, so that one offers (to God) flesh, wine, and sounds, that is to say, songs.",
    "root.text.Part 3..45.21": "The pilgrimage has a manifest utility; that which serves as its motive is that this assembly and the impression it produces result in a renewed zeal for the Law and in the fraternity which is established among men. It is thus especially with the commandment to assemble the people[[fn:3717]], the reason of which is clearly indicated: <i>that they may hear, etc.</i> The money given for the second tithe was destined to be expended there[[fn:3718]], as we have already set forth[[fn:3719]]. It was likewise thus with the fruit of the fourth year[[fn:3720]] and the tithe of the cattle. Thus, therefore, one had there the meat of the tithe, the wine of the fourth year, and the money of the second tithe, so that provisions were there in abundance; for it was permitted to sell naught of all these things, nor to defer them from one season to another; but, as God has said: <i>Year by year</i> (Deuter., xiv, 22). These things shall therefore of necessity be used for the giving of alms, and, in truth, it is strenuously enjoined to give alms during the festivals, saying: <i>Thou shalt rejoice in thy feast</i>, <i>thou, and thy son, and thy daughter, etc., and the stranger, and the fatherless, and the widow</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xvi, 14). Now, we have traversed the particular commandments belonging to this class, and we have touched upon many of their details.",
    "root.text.Part 3..46.0": "The commandments comprised in the twelfth class are those which we have enumerated in the book <i>Tohorâ</i> (of Purification). Although I have already briefly spoken of their utility[[fn:3721]], we must here give fuller explanations; and, after having justified this class as is meet, I shall give the reasons for these details, so far as they are clear unto myself.",
    "root.text.Part 3..46.1": "I say, then, that this Divine Law, which was given to Moses, our master, and which has been attributed unto him, had no other end than to render the ceremonies of worship more facile and to alleviate[[fn:3722]] the burden thereof; and if there be any that may appear to thee grievous and most heavy, this proceeds from the fact that thou art unacquainted with the usages and rites that existed in those times. Let a comparison, therefore, be made between a cult in which a man burns his child and that in which one burns a young pigeon[[fn:3723]]! It is said in the Pentateuch: <i>for even their sons and their daughters they have burnt in the fire to their gods</i> (Deuter., 12, 31); such was the worship they offered to their gods, and that which is analogous thereto, in our worship, is the burning of a young pigeon, or even a handful of fine flour. It was in this regard that our nation was rebuked in the time of its rebellion, and that it was said unto it: <i>O my people, what have I done unto thee? and in which have I wearied thee? testify against me</i> (Micah, VI, 5); it is said again, in the same sense: <i>Have I been a wilderness unto Israel? a land of darkness? wherefore say my people, We are lords; we will come no more unto thee, etc.</i> (Jeremiah, 2, 31), which signifies: what then is the grievous burden they have seen in this law, that they should forsake it[[fn:3724]]? Elsewhere God apostrophizes us, saying: <i>What iniquity have your fathers found in me, that they are gone far from me</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v</i>. 5)? All these passages have but one and the same end.",
    "root.text.Part 3..46.2": "After this preliminary observation, which is of great import and which thou must not lose from thy sight, I say: We have already set forth that all which was sought to be obtained through the sanctuary was that it should produce an impression upon him who came to visit it, and that it should inspire fear and reverence, as it is said: <i>and ye shall reverence my sanctuary</i> (Levit., 19, 30). But, when one continually approaches any object of respect, the effect which it produces upon the soul does diminish, and the impression received therefrom is lessened. The doctors have already called attention to this subject, saying that it is not well to enter at every moment into the sanctuary, and they cite in support these words: <i>Withdraw thy foot from thy neighbour's house; lest he be weary of thee, and so hate thee</i> (Prov., 25, 17)[[fn:3725]]. It was with this intention that God forbade the unclean to enter the sanctuary; and the cases of uncleanness being very numerous, one could but very rarely find a person who was pure. Indeed, even if one had preserved himself from the contact of a carcass, he might not have escaped contact with one of the <i>eight creeping things</i>[[fn:3726]] which often fall into the house, as well as into food and drink, and against which one often stumbls[[fn:3727]]. Having avoided that, one might still not have escaped contact with a woman in her courses or one afflicted with an issue of blood, a man afflicted with gonorrhœa, a leper, or their bed[[fn:3728]]; even had one escaped these, one could not always avoid cohabitation with his wife, or having a nocturnal accident[[fn:3729]]. Even when one had purified himself from these uncleannesses, it was not permitted to enter the temple before the setting of the sun[[fn:3730]]. Now, as one could not enter the temple during the night, as results from the treatises <i>Middoth and Tamid</i>[[fn:3731]], it might happen for the most part that he (who had purified himself) would cohabit that very night with his wife, or that there might come upon him one of the other causes of uncleanness, and he would find himself the next morning at the same point as the day before.",
    "root.text.Part 3..46.4": "In measure as the case of uncleanness might happen more frequently, the purification was more difficult and lasted longer. To be under the same roof with dead bodies, and especially with those of kinsmen and neighbours, is a case more frequent than any other uncleanness; one could not, therefore, become pure again except by means of the ashes of the <i>red heifer</i>, which are exceeding rare, and after seven days[[fn:3733]]. The flux (of blood or gonorrhœa) and the menses are more frequent than contact with an unclean thing; wherefore these uncleannesses required seven days (of purification)[[fn:3734]], and he who came into contact with them was unclean for a single day[[fn:3735]]. If the purification of the man afflicted with gonorrhœa, of the woman afflicted with an issue of blood, and of the woman in childbirth, is not completed but by a sacrifice, it is because these cases happen more rarely than the menses[[fn:3736]]. — All these uncleannesses, I mean those of the menses, gonorrhœa, issue of blood, leprosy, a dead body, a carcass, a creeping thing, and of semen, are things most foul. The legal dispositions relative thereto have, therefore, various ends: 1st, to remove from us all foulness; 2nd, to preserve the sanctuary; 3rd, to have regard for customs generally spread abroad, for thou shalt hear presently what grievous ceremonies the Sabians imposed upon themselves in these cases of uncleanness; 4th, to alleviate this heavy burden and to ensure that the question of what is pure or impure should not fetter man in any of his occupations, for this question concerns only the sanctuary and holy things: <i>She shall touch no hallowed thing, nor come into the sanctuary</i> (Levit., 12, 4). For the rest, one is guilty of no sin in remaining unclean as long as one wills, and in nourishing oneself as much as one wills with profane things tainted with uncleanness. According to the customs spread among the Sabians until our time in the countries of the Orient, I mean among the remnants of the Magi[[fn:3737]], the woman having her menses remains isolated in an apartment, the places upon which she walks are burnt[[fn:3738]], he who speaks to her becoms unclean, and if the wind that blows passes over the woman and over a pure man, the latter becoms unclean. Thou seest, consequently, how far these usages are from what we say: “All the works that a woman does for her husband, the woman having her menses may do likewise, except the washing of his face, etc.[[fn:3739]]”; one is only forbidden to cohabit with her during the days of her defilement and uncleanness. — Another custom spread among the Sabians until our time is that they repute as unclean all that is separated from the body, whether hair, or nail, or blood; wherefore every barber, among them, is unclean, because he touches the blood and the hair[[fn:3740]]. Whosoever has himself shaved must plunge into water springing from a fountain. They have many of these inconvenient usages, whilst we pay no attention to what is pure or impure save in regard to holy things and the sanctuary. However, if the Scripture says: <i>Ye shall therefore sanctify yourselves, and ye shall be holy; for I am holy</i> (Levit., 11, 44), this is by no means said in regard to what is pure or impure; the <i>Siphra</i> says expressly that it referrs therein to a “sanctification by the commandments[[fn:3741]]”, and likewise the doctors say, concerning these words: <i>Ye shall be holy</i> (Levit., 19, 2), that it referrs to a sanctification by the commandments. Wherefore the transgression of the commandments is also called <span dir=\"rtl\">טומאה</span> (defilement or uncleanness), an expression employed in regard to the fundamental commandments[[fn:3742]], which are idolatry, incest, and murder. In speaking, for example, of idolatry, it is said: <i>because he has given of his seed unto Molech, to defile my sanctuary</i> (Levit., 20, 3); of incest, it is said: <i>defile not ye yourselves in any of these things</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, XVIII, 24); of murder, it is said: <i>so ye shall not pollute the land, etc.</i> (Numbers, 35, 34). It is seen, consequently, that the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">טומאה</span> (defilement or uncleanness) is an homonymous term which is used in three different senses. It is said: 1st, of disobedience and the transgression of the commandments in matters of action or opinion; 2nd, of foulness and filth: <i>her filthiness is in her skirts</i> (Lamentations, I, 9); 3rd, of those things reputed (unclean), I mean when one touches or carries such a thing, or when one is found under the same roof with such a thing[[fn:3743]]. It is in this third sense that we say: “The words of the Law are not susceptible of uncleanness[[fn:3744]].” Likewise, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קדושה</span>, <i>holiness</i>, is said, as an homonym, in three senses, opposed to the three significations of which we have just spoken.",
    "root.text.Part 3..46.5": "Inasmuch as one cannot be purified from the uncleanness caused by a dead body except at the end of seven days, and for this purpose the ashes of the <i>red heifer</i> must be found; and since, on the other hand, the priests have constant need to enter the Sanctuary to offer the sacrifices, it has been particularly forbidden to every priest to expose himself to the impurity arising from a dead body, save in a case of extreme necessity, where it would be repugnant to human nature to abstain; I refer to contact with the bodies of father and mother, children and brethren[[fn:3745]]. As it is highly necessary that the High Priest be continually in the Sanctuary, even as it is said: <i>it</i> (the diadem) <i>shall be always upon his forehead</i> (Exodus, xxviii. 38), it has been absolutely forbidden unto him to defile himself by a dead body, were it even that of his own parents[[fn:3746]]. Perceivest thou not that this prohibition does not embrace the women? <i>the sons of Aaron</i> (it is said), and not the <i>daughters</i> of Aaron[[fn:3747]], because there is no need of women in the service of the sacrifices.",
    "root.text.Part 3..46.6": "As it is inevitable that persons tainted with impurity should enter the Sanctuary by error, or eat, in their state of uncleanness, of holy things, and since there be some who do this with premeditation—the most impious of men committing the gravest sins of deliberate purpose—it has been commanded to offer sacrifices to expiate the defilement of the Sanctuary and of the holy things; these are of divers kinds, some for premeditated sin, others for that committed through inadvertence. These are the goats of the festivals, those of the New Moons, and the scapegoat[[fn:3748]], as is set forth in its place, to the end that he who sinns of deliberate purpose may not believe that he has not committed a great sin in defiling the Sanctuary of the Eternal, but may know, on the contrary, that his sin has been expiated by the sacrifice of the goat; as it is said: <i>and that they die not in their uncleanness</i> (Levit., xv. 31); <i>and Aaron shall bear the iniquity of the holy things, etc.</i> (Exodus, xxviii. 38)[[fn:3749]], an idea which is frequently repeated.",
    "root.text.Part 3..46.7": "As touching the impurity of leprosy, we have already set forth its significance[[fn:3750]]. The doctors also have expounded it and have made us to know that it is established as a principle that this malady is a chastisement to punish evil-speaking[[fn:3751]]. At first, this alteration is remarked in the walls[[fn:3752]]; if the man repent, the end is attained; but if he continue to sin, the alteration extends to his bed and to the vessels of his house[[fn:3753]], and if he yet persist in his sin, it extends to his garments and thereafter to his body. This was a miracle which was perpetuated in the nation, like that of the bitter waters of the woman suspected of adultery[[fn:3754]]. It is manifest that this is a most profitable belief, especially if one reflects that leprosy is contagious and that all men feel toward it an almost instinctive loathing[[fn:3755]]. The reason why the purification was performed with cedar wood, hyssop, scarlet wool, and two birds[[fn:3756]], has been indicated in the <i>Midraschôth;</i> but it suits not our purpose, and hitherto I have been unable to account for any of these things. Neither know I for what reason cedar wood, hyssop, and scarlet wool are employed in the ceremony of the <i>red heifer</i>, nor why a bunch of hyssop is used for the sprinkling with the blood of the Paschal lamb[[fn:3757]]; I find naught whereby to justify the preference given to these species.",
    "root.text.Part 3..46.8": "The reason why the <i>red heifer</i> is called ’<i>hattath</i> (sin-offering)[[fn:3758]], is because it complets the purification of him who has been defiled by a dead body, so that he may enter into the Sanctuary[[fn:3759]]; I mean that, from the moment one has defiled himself by a corpse, it would be for ever forbidden him to enter the Sanctuary or to eat of holy things, were there not this heifer to take away (symbolically) this sin[[fn:3760]]. It is even as the diadem (of the High Priest) which expiats defilement[[fn:3761]] and as the goats which are burnt[[fn:3762]]. Wherefore he who was occupied with the red heifer and the goats that were to be burnt rendered his garments unclean[[fn:3763]], even as he who was occupied with the scapegoat, which, by reason of the great sins it was supposed to carry away, rendered unclean those who touched it[[fn:3764]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..46.9": "— Thus have we assigned motives, in this class, to all the commandments of which we have deemed it possible to conjecture the reasons.",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.0": "The commandments comprised in the thirteenth class are those which we have enumerated in the treatises <i>Maakhalôth assourôth</i> (of forbidden foods), <i>Sche’hîtâ</i> (of the manner of slaughtering animals), <i>Nedarim ou-nezirouth</i> (of vows and the Nazirite state). We have already, in this treatise[[fn:3765]] and in the commentary upon <i>Aboth</i>, sufficiently and at large expounded the utility of this class; we shall here provide more ample explanations, whilst reviewing the particular commandments therein enumerated.",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.1": "I say, then, that all the foods which the Law has forbidden us constitute unwholesome nourishment. In all that has been forbidden us, there be only the swine and the fat which are not deemed injurious[[fn:3766]]; yet it is not so, for the swine is (a food) more humid than is meet, and of an exceeding superfluity[[fn:3767]]. The principal reason why the Law holds it in abomination is that it is right filthy and feeds upon unclean things. Thou knowest how much the Law cars to remove the sight of filth, even in the open field, in a camp of war[[fn:3768]], and much more so in the interior of cities; but if the flesh of swine were eaten, the streets and even the houses would be filthier than latrines, as is now to be seen in the land of the Franks[[fn:3769]]. Thou knowest the saying of the Sages: «The snout of the swine is as walking filth[[fn:3770]].»",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.2": "Likewise, the fat of the intestines[[fn:3771]] is over-nourishing, hinders digestion, and engenders cold and thick blood; wherefore it is more fitting that it be burned[[fn:3772]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.3": "In like manner, blood and the beast that dies of itself are difficult of digestion and provide base nourishment; and it is also known that the beast <i>teréphâ</i> is very nigh unto being a dead beast[[fn:3773]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.4": "— As for the characteristic signs (of a clean animal), to wit, for quadrupeds, the chewing of the cud and the cloven hoof, and for fishes, the possession of fins and scales[[fn:3774]], it must be known that the existence of these signs is not the cause why it is permitted to eat thereof, nor is the lack of these signs the cause why these animals are forbidden. They be merely signs whereby one may recognize the good species and distinguish it from the bad[[fn:3775]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.5": "— The reason for the commandment concerning the <i>sciatic nerve</i> is set forth in the text itself[[fn:3776]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.6": "— The prohibition of eating a limb of a living animal[[fn:3777]] is based upon the fact that such an act accustoms one to cruelty. The kings of the heathens were wont to do so in those times, and it was likewise an idolatrous practice to sever a certain limb from a quadruped and to devour it[[fn:3778]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.7": "Concerning the prohibition of eating meat cooked in milk, apart from the fact that it constituts a very gross food, generating a superfluity (of blood), it is not improbable that idolatry had some part therein. Perhaps it was eaten during some idolatrous ceremony, or at one of the festivals of the heathens; and what confirms me in this latter opinion is that where the Law twice forbidds the eating of meat cooked in milk[[fn:3779]], it mentions it beside the precept concerning the pilgrimage: <i>Three times in the year, etc.</i>[[fn:3780]]. It is as though it said: At the time of your pilgrimage, when ye enter the temple of the Lord your God, ye shall not cook anything after the manner aforesaid, as the idolaters were wont to do. This is, I believe, the most plausible reason for this prohibition; yet have I found no mention thereof in those books of the Sabians which I have perused.",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.8": "The precept regarding the slaughtering of animals is necessary. For the natural food of man consists only of vegetable substances and the flesh of animals, and the best of meats are those which are permitted unto us, a fact of which no physician is ignorant. Now, since the necessity of obtaining good nourishment requirs that the animal be slain, it was intended that it should die in the easiest manner, and it was forbidden to torment it, whether by an improper slaughter, or by piercing the lower part of the neck[[fn:3781]], or by severing a limb, as we have already explained.",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.9": "— In like manner was it forbidden to slaughter on the same day the mother and her young[[fn:3782]], to the end that we might take care not to slay the young before the eyes of the mother; for the animal would, in such a case, endure too great a grief. Verily, in this respect, there is no difference between the pain felt by man and that of other animals; for the love and tenderness of a mother for her offspring depend not upon reason, but upon the action of the imaginative faculty, which most animals possess as well as man. If this commandment has been given specifically regarding the bovine and ovine species, it is because these are the domestic animals which we are permitted to eat and which men are generally accustomed to consume, and these are also the species in which the mother and her young can be distinguished[[fn:3783]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.10": "— The precept of dismissing the mother from the bird’s nest[[fn:3784]] has an analogous reason. Verily, for the most part, eggs that have been sat upon and young birds that yet require the mother are not fit for food; if, therefore, one must dismiss the mother so that she flies away, not only shall she be spared the grief of seeing her young taken, but often even this shall lead to the leaving of the whole, since that which one might take is generally not good to eat[[fn:3785]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.11": "If the Law has had regard unto these sorrows of the soul in the case of quadrupeds and birds, how much more so in regard to all the individuals of the human race? One must by no means object to me that which the Doctors say: “He who says: Thy mercy extends unto the nests of birds, etc.[[fn:3786]]”; for that is one of the two opinions of which we have spoken, to wit, the opinion of those who think that the Law has no other motive than the mere <i>will</i> (of God), whilst we, for our part, follow the second opinion[[fn:3787]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.12": "We have already observed that the Law itself explains wherefore it is necessary to cover the blood, and that this precept concerns particularly the clean wild beast and the clean fowl[[fn:3788]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.13": "— Besides the precepts which have been given us to interdict the use of certain foods, there have been prescribed unto us precepts relating to vows of (voluntary) abstinence[[fn:3789]]. If any man say that this bread, or this meat, be for him a thing forbidden, he is prohibited from eating thereof. All this has for its end to exercise man in sobriety and to moderate his desire for eating and drinking. The Doctors have said: “Vows are a hedge around abstinence[[fn:3790]].” But as women, being easily impressionable and of a feeble soul, are prone to be moved by passion, there would be in the house grave difficulties, strifes, and disorder, if they were mistresses of making vows; for such kind of food would be permitted to the husband and forbidden to the wife, and another would be forbidden to the daughter and permitted to the mother. Therefore has the matter been entrusted to the head of the family, in all that which may concern him[[fn:3791]]. On the other hand, thou seest that the woman who governs herself, and who is not under the dependence of a head of family, is subject in respect of vows to the same rule as men; I speak of her who has neither husband nor father, or who has attained the age of puberty and is called <i>boghers</i>[[fn:3792]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..47.14": "The <i>Nazirite vow</i> has a motive very clearly indicated, which is that of abstaining from the drinking of wine[[fn:3793]], which in all times has made victims[[fn:3794]]: <i>many and mighty were those whom it has slain</i>[[fn:3795]]; <i>and these also have erred through wine</i> (Isaiah, xxviii. 7). The law of the Nazirite, as thou mayest see, forbidds among other things the use <i>of all that proceeds from the vine</i>[[fn:3796]], with much strictness, to the end that men should content themselves with that which is necessary. Indeed, he who abstains from wine is called holy and is placed in the same rank of holiness as the High Priest, so that, like the latter, he dars not even make himself unclean by contact with (the corpse of) his father or his mother[[fn:3797]]. All this greatness comes unto him from his abstinence from drink[[fn:3798]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.0": "The commandments comprised within the fourteenth class are those which we have enumerated in the book <i>Nashim</i> (of Women) and in the treatises <i>Issure Biah</i> (of Unlawful Unions) and <i>Kil'e Behemah</i> (of the Mixing of Animals of Two Species); the commandment of circumcision belongs likewise unto this class. We have already previously made known the object of this class[[fn:3799]]; now, I shall set forth the details thereof.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.1": "I say then: It is well known that friends are a thing of which man has need throughout his whole life, as Aristotle has already set forth in the ninth book of the <i>Ethics</i>[[fn:3800]]. In seasons of health and prosperity, he enjoys their familiarity; in seasons of adversity, they serve him as a refuge; finally, in old age, when his body waxes weak, he seeks succour at their hands. These advantages man finds in a far higher degree in his children, and likewise in his parents. Fraternity, friendship, and reciprocal devotion exist in perfection only among kinsfolk descended from the same family, so that men of the same tribe having a common ancestor, even though remote, are imbued with love, devotion, and sympathy for one another, which is one of the principal tendencies of the Law.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.2": "Wherefore the Law has forbidden prostitution, which is the destruction of the family[[fn:3801]]; for the child that is born thereof is a stranger unto all the world, no family is known unto him, and none of his kinsfolk[[fn:3802]] knows him, which is the most grievous condition for himself and for his father. There was yet another grave reason for prohibiting intercourse with a harlot: it was to prevent men from surrendering themselves too passionately and with too much persistence to carnal love. Verily, the diversity of prostituted persons augments passion; for man is not excited by one single person to whom he is continually accustomed as he is by persons ever new, differing in countenance and in manners. Finally, there is in the prohibition of the harlot another great utility, to wit, the avoidance of mischiefs; for, if it were permitted to have intercourse with a harlot, several men might, by chance, approach the same woman at the same time, which would inevitably cause strife, and, most often, they might slay one another, or slay the woman, which, as is well known, did frequently happen: <i>And they assemble themselves by troops in the harlot's houses</i> (Jeremiah v. 7). It is, therefore, to avoid these great mischiefs and to obtain the general advantage (already mentioned), which is the knowledge of the family, that the Law forbidds intercourse with the harlot and with the <i>cinaedus</i>,",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.3": "so that for the purpose of surrendering oneself in a lawful manner to carnal love, there is no other mean than to take a wife for oneself alone and to espouse her[[fn:3803]] publicly; for, if it sufficed to dwell alone with her, man would most often take a harlot into his house for a certain time, by agreement with her, and would say that she is his wife[[fn:3804]]. Wherefore a bond[[fn:3805]] and an act have been prescribed whereby he appropriats the woman, and these are the betrothal; then a public act, which is the marriage: <i>And he</i> (<i>Boaz</i>) <i>took ten men, etc.</i> (Ruth iv. 2).",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.4": "As it may sometimes happen that perfect concord reigns not in their union and that their household be not well-ordered, divorce has been permitted. But, if divorce could be accomplished by a mere word, or by the dismissal of the woman from the house, she would watch for a moment when she was not observed[[fn:3806]] and would go forth, pretending that she is repudiated; or else, if a man had had intercourse with her, she and the seducer would pretend that she had been repudiated beforehand. Wherefore the Law wills that divorce be valid only by means of a writing which attests it: <i>and he shall write her a bill of divorcement</i> (Deut. xxiv. 1).",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.5": "As the suspicion of infidelity and the doubts which may arise in this regard are frequent concerning a wife, the Law has prescribed unto us provisions touching the woman suspected of adultery[[fn:3807]]; and this procedure had perforce for its consequence that every married woman, fearing the terror of the <i>bitter waters</i>, observed herself with extreme care and took good heed[[fn:3808]] not to cause grief to the heart of her husband. Verily, if the woman were pure and could entirely reassure her husband concerning herself, most men would certes have given all they possessed to redeem her from the act to which she was to be subjected[[fn:3809]], and would even have preferred death to this great ignominy—to wit, to have the head of the woman bared, her hair dishevelled, her garments rent so that her bosom be exposed, and to make her go round the sanctuary in the presence of the public, both women and men, and in the presence of the Great Tribunal[[fn:3810]]. Thus, by inspiring this dread, great misfortunes have been prevented[[fn:3811]] which might trouble the order of many a household.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.6": "Inasmuch as the young virgin may marry whomsoever she first encounters, there has been imposed upon her seducer naught but the duty to espouse her[[fn:3812]]; for he is the one who is most suitable for her, and this marriage is better calculated to rehabilitate her[[fn:3813]] than one which another might contract with her. But if she, or her father, refuss, he (the seducer) shall nevertheless pay the <i>mohar</i>[[fn:3814]]. For him who is guilty of violation[[fn:3815]], an additional punishment has been added: <i>he may not put her away all his days</i> (Deut. xxii. 29).",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.7": "As for the motive of the levirate, it is written (in the Pentateuch) that it was an ancient custom, prior to the revelation of the Law[[fn:3816]], and which the latter allowed to subsist[[fn:3817]]. Touching the ceremony of the <i>drawing off of the shoe</i>[[fn:3818]], the reason thereof is that these acts (of which it consisted) were deemed ignominious according to the manners of those times, and that thereby the brother-in-law, to avoid such acts, might be induced to perform the levirate. This results clearly from the text of the Pentateuch: <i>So shall it be done unto that man, etc., and his name shall be called in Israel, etc.</i> (Deut. xxv. 9-10).",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.8": "By the history of Judah[[fn:3819]] one may learn to maintain a decent conduct and to observe equity in one's dealings; I refer to those words (of Judah): <i>let her take it to her, lest we be shamed</i> (Gen. xxxviii. 23). The explanation thereof is as follows: Before the legislation (of Moses), intercourse with a harlot was what marriage has been since that legislation[[fn:3820]]; I mean to say that it was a permitted act, for which there was absolutely no repugnance. To pay a harlot the hire upon which one had agreed was then what the payment of a woman's dowry is now in case of divorce[[fn:3821]]; that is to say, it was a right of the woman which the man was bound to pay. When, therefore, Judah says: <i>lest we be shamed</i>, he teaches us thereby that it is shameful for us to speak in general of matters relating to cohabitation, even though it be permitted, and that one must, on the contrary, pass it over in silence and conceal it, even should this lead to a loss of money. This is, as thou seest, what Judah did when he said: It is better that we suffer a loss and that she keep what she has received, rather than reveal our search and reap shame therefrom. Such is the decent conduct which we learn from this history. As for the lesson of equity which we may derive from it, it is when he says, to assure that he is free from all violence toward the woman, that he did not retract and did not diminish the price whereon he had agreed with her: <i>Behold, I sent this kid, etc.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>); for there is no doubt that this kid was one of the best of its kind, and for that reason he employs the demonstrative <span dir=\"rtl\">הזה</span>, <i>this</i>. Such is the equity of which Jacob, Isaac, and Abraham had set them the example—namely, that one must neither change his word (once pledged), nor alter his promise; that one must pay in full what is due; that there is no difference between that which has been entrusted to thee of another's fortune, by way of loan or deposit, and what thou owest him in any manner whatsoever, by way of hire or otherwise; finally, that the dowry of every woman is as the hire of any hireling, so that he who withholds what is due to his wife is as guilty as he who withholds the hire of a hireling; for it matters little whether one cavills with the hireling and seeks pretexts to dismiss him without hire, or whether one acts thus toward his wife to dismiss her without a dowry.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.9": "I must here point out to thee the great equity of these <i>righteous statutes and judgments</i> (Deut. iv. 8), in the judgment pronounced against the slanderer of his wife[[fn:3822]]. Without doubt, this wicked man loved not his wife whom he has slandered, and found her unpleasing: if, therefore, he had desired to divorce her according to the manner of all those who repudiate their wives, naught would have prevented him; but, had he divorced her[[fn:3823]], he would have been obliged to pay her what was her rightful due. He therefore brought an <i>evil name</i> upon her, in order to rid himself of her without payment; he calumniated and slandered her with lies, in order to withhold from her the sum which she had the right to demand of him and which is fifty shekels of silver[[fn:3824]], for that is the <i>mohar</i> of virgins, fixed in the Law. Wherefore God has condemned him to pay <i>an hundred shekels of silver</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, xxii. 19)[[fn:3825]], according to this principle: <i>Whom the judges shall condemn, he shall pay double unto his neighbour</i> (Exod. xxii. 8), and in conformity with the judgment of false witnesses, as we have already set forth[[fn:3826]]. It is the same with this slanderer, who, having desired to make her lose the fifty shekels which are due to her from him[[fn:3827]], is condemned to pay an hundred. Such is his punishment for having wished to withhold the indemnity imposed upon him and having sought to seize it; but his punishment for having assailed the honour of the woman and having accused her of fornication consisted in being himself dishonoured by scourging: <i>and they shall chastise him</i> (Deut. xxii. 18). Finally, his punishment for having obeyed his concupiscence and having sought only pleasure alone consisted in remaining perpetually bound to her: <i>He may not put her away all his days</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 19); for the cause of all that occurred was that he found her unpleasing.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.10": "Thus are evil morals healed, if they have the Divine precept for their physician; and in all the dispositions of this Law, if thou examine them well, thou shalt not cease to behold on every side a manifest and resplendent equity. Observe well how equality has been established between the judgment of the slanderer who sought to withhold the indemnity imposed upon him, and the judgment of the thief who has seized the property of another, and how the false witness, who only sought to inflict an injury but did not succeed therein, has been treated as equal to him who has done harm and exercised violence, I mean (equal to) the thief and the slanderer; all three are judged according to <i>one law and one statute</i>[[fn:3828]]. The wisdom of the laws of God must inspire the same astonishment as that which He has displayed in His works: <i>the Rock</i>, it is said, <i>His work is perfect</i>, <i>for all His ways are justice</i> (Deuter., 32, 4). This signifies: even as His works are of an extreme perfection, so His laws are of an extreme justice; but our intelligences are too weak to grasp the perfection of all that He has made and the justice of all that He has decreed; and, even as we understand certain wonders of His works, in the members of animals and the movements of the celestial spheres, so we understand the justice of a part of His laws. But that which remains hidden from us, in both respects, is far more considerable than that which is manifest unto us. — But let us return to the subject of the chapter.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.11": "The commandments relating to unlawful unions[[fn:3829]] have all for their object to render cohabitation more infrequent, and to cause one to feel a repugnance for it, and to seek it as rarely as possible. As for the prohibition of pederasty and bestiality, the motive thereof is evident; for if it be only with repugnance and by necessity that one yields to natural union, how much more (ought one to shun) that which is outside the course of nature and which has no other end than mere voluptuousness.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.12": "The women whom it is forbidden to wed (by reason of kinship) are all found in the same position, that is to say, that for the most part, each of them is continually with the man[[fn:3830]] in the same house; she will easily yield to his desire, will be prompt to suffer herself to be taken, and he may cause her to come into his presence without difficulty, without any judge being able to reproach the man because she is found with him. If, therefore, it were with this familiar woman as with any other unmarried woman, I mean, if it were lawful to wed her and she were only forbidden (unto that man) because she is not his wife, the greater part of men would be constantly exposed to intimate commerce with such women. But since it is absolutely forbidden to cohabit with them, since we are prevented therefrom in the most energetic manner, being threatened with the penalty of death or that of excision[[fn:3831]], and since there is no means of union with these women, it might be assured that man would not seek to approach them and that he would turn away his thoughts from them.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.13": "As for this facility (of intercourse), it is right manifest that it exists for each of the women whom it is forbidden to wed[[fn:3832]]. Verily, it is a thing well known that as soon as a man has taken a wife, her mother, her grandmother, her daughter, her granddaughter, and her sister are for the most part near unto this husband, who encounters them continually, in coming in, in going out, and in occupying himself with his affairs; likewise, the wife has frequent relations with her husband's brother, with his father, and with his son. It is also known that man is very frequently with his sisters, with his maternal and paternal aunts, with the wife of his uncle, and that often he has been brought up with them. These are all the kindred women whom it is forbidden to wed; and if thou reflectest thereon[[fn:3833]], thou shalt find that the reason indicated (to wit, intimacy) is one of those why marriage between kindred is forbidden.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.14": "I find a second reason in considerations of modesty; for it is only in consequence of a very great immodesty that the act in question can take place between the root and the branch[[fn:3834]], I mean the cohabitation (of a man) with his mother or with his daughter[[fn:3835]]. Wherefore mutual commerce between the root and the branch has been forbidden, and it matters little whether it be the root that wedds the branch, or whether it be the branch that wedds the root[[fn:3836]], or whether the root and the branch wed the same third person[[fn:3837]], that is to say, that the same person yields to cohabitation with the root and the branch. Therefore is it forbidden to take at once a woman and her mother, and to wed the wife of one’s father or that of one’s son; for in all these cases, one and the same person would uncover his nakedness before that of the root and the branch. Brothers and sisters are assimilated to root and branch; now, as it is forbidden to wed one's sister, it is also forbidden to wed the sister of one's wife and the wife of one's brother; for in this manner two persons, who are as root and branch, would both wed the same third person.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.15": "As the union between brothers and sisters is severely forbidden, and as they have been likened unto stock and branch—or rather, the brother and the sister[[fn:3838]] being as one soul—there was likewise forbidden the marrying of one's maternal aunt, who is in the stead of the mother, and one's paternal aunt, who is in the stead of the father. But, even as it has not been forbidden to marry the daughter of one's uncle or aunt, so, by analogy, it has not been forbidden to marry the daughter of one's brother, or the daughter of one's sister. If it be permitted for the uncle to marry the wife of his nephew, whilst it is forbidden for the nephew to marry the wife of his uncle, this is explained according to the first reason. Indeed, the nephew is frequently found in the house of his uncle, and he forms a bond with the wife of his uncle, even as he bonds with the wife of his brother; but the uncle is not so frequently found in the house of his nephew and has no such intimacy with the wife of the latter. Seest thou not that, the father being bonded with the wife of his son, even as is the son with the wife of the father, both marriages are equally forbidden[[fn:3839]] and punished with the same death[[fn:3840]]?",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.16": "As for the prohibition of having commerce with a woman during her courses or with a married woman, the reason thereof is too manifest for one to have need to seek it out.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.17": "Thou knowest also that it is forbidden unto us to enjoy, in any manner whatsoever, a woman whom the Law interdicts unto us, were it even to look upon her with a view to pleasure, as we have set forth in the treatise <i>Issuré Biâ</i> (of illicit unions)[[fn:3841]]. Therein have we said that our Law in no wise permitts us to occupy our thoughts with carnal love[[fn:3842]], nor to excite concupiscence in any manner, and that man, if he feels himself excited against his will, must occupy his mind with other thoughts and reflect upon other matters, until this excitation shall have passed away. Behold what the Doctors say in their sentences, which serve to perfect even virtuous men: «If this hideous thing[[fn:3843]] encounters thee, drag it to the house of study; if it be of iron, it shall melt, and if it be of stone, it shall be shattered, as it is said: <i>Is not my word like as fire? says the Lord</i>, <i>and like a hammer that breaks the rock in pieces</i> (Jeremiah, xxiii, 29)[[fn:3844]]?» The Doctor here gives unto his son this rule of conduct: If thou feelest thyself excited to concupiscence and if thou sufferest therefrom, go to the house of study, devote thyself to study and to reading, question and allow thyself to be questioned, and this suffering shall undoubtedly vanish. The expression <i>this hideous thing</i> is remarkable, and in truth, there is naught more hideous. This morality is not only prescribed by religion, but is also recommended by the philosophers. I have already quoted unto thee textually the words of Aristotle[[fn:3845]], who says: «this sense which is a shame unto us», meaning the sense of touch which invits us to seek after good cheer and carnal love. In his writings, he calls those people abject who give themselves up to carnal love and to good cheer, and he lavishes upon them blame and mockery, as thou shalt find in his treatise on <i>Ethics</i> and in that on <i>Rhetoric</i>[[fn:3846]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.18": "It is in view of this virtuous conduct, which we must propose unto ourselves as the goal of all our efforts, that the Doctors have forbidden us to look upon «the quadrupeds and the birds at the moment of their coupling[[fn:3847]].» According to my view, this is also the reason why it is forbidden to couple animals of different species[[fn:3848]]; for it is known that ordinarily an individual of one species is not inclined to couple with one of another species, unless it be driven thereto by force, as one ses continually practised by those abject men who desire to obtain the birth of mules. The Law therefore willed not that the Israelite should descend to such a practice, which reveals so much abjection and impudence, and that he should occupy himself[[fn:3849]] with things of which religion abhorrs the simple mention, and a fortiori the execution, unless it be by necessity; but there is no necessity to effect this coupling. It seems also to me that the prohibition of yoking together two species for any labor whatsoever has for its motive to distance us from the coupling of two species; if therefore it be said: <i>thou shalt not plow with an ox and an ass together</i> (Deut. xxii, 10), it is because, being joined together, they might sometimes couple one with another. The proof thereof is that this provision embraces also animals other than the ox and the ass: «Whether it be an ox and an ass, or other animals of two species; but the Scripture speaks of what is habitual[[fn:3850]].»",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.19": "I believe likewise that one of the motives for circumcision is to diminish cohabitation and to weaken the (sexual) organ, in order to restrain its action and to leave it at rest as much as possible[[fn:3851]]. It has been claimed that circumcision had for its purpose to complete what nature had left imperfect[[fn:3852]], which has given occasion to criticize (this precept); for, it was said, how could the things of nature be imperfect, so as to stand in need of a completion coming from without, the more so since it is known how useful the prepuce is to the member in question? But this precept has in no wise for its goal to supply a physical imperfection; on the contrary, it concerns only the remedying of a moral imperfection. The true goal is the bodily pain to be inflicted upon this member, which in no way derangs the functions necessary for the preservation of the individual, nor destroys procreation, but which diminishes passion[[fn:3853]] and excessive concupiscence[[fn:3854]]. That circumcision weakens concupiscence and sometimes diminishes voluptuousness is a thing of which one cannot doubt; for if, from birth, one causs this member to bleed by removing its covering, it shall undoubtedly be weakened. The Doctors have expressly said: «The woman who has given herself to love with an uncircumcised man finds it difficult to separate from him[[fn:3855]];» this is, according to me, the most important motive for circumcision. And who then first practised this act? Was it not Abraham, so renowned for his chastity? as the Doctors say concerning this passage: <i>Behold now, I know that thou art a fair woman to look upon</i> (Genesis xii, 11)[[fn:3856]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.20": "Circumcision has, in my judgment, another motive of great moment: it causs those who profess the idea of the Unity of God to be distinguished by a common bodily sign impressed upon them all, so that he who pertains not thereto, being a stranger, may not claim to belong to them<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3857]]</sup>; for there might be men who would so act with the intent of deriving profit therefrom, or of deceiving those who profess this religion (of Unity). This act<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3858]]</sup>, no man would perform upon himself or upon his son, save by reason of a sincere conviction; for it is no mere incision in the leg, nor a cautery upon the arm, but a matter exceeding hard. It is known likewise how much men love and succour one another when they bear the same distinctive mark, which serves them as a species of covenant and pact; and in like manner, circumcision is a covenant concluded by Abraham our father for the belief in the Unity of God, so that all they who are circumcised alone enter into the covenant of Abraham. By this covenant, one is pledged<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3859]]</sup> to believe in the Unity: <i>to be a God unto thee, and to thy seed after thee</i> (Genesis, xvii. 7). This is yet another important motive which may be assigned for circumcision, and it is perchance of greater weight than the former.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.21": "Religion cannot be truly accomplished, nor perpetuated, unless circumcision take place during the years of infancy; and for this there are three reasons<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3860]]</sup>: 1. If the child were permitted to grow up, it might be that he would not perform it (circumcision). 2. He suffers not so much as a grown person would suffer, seeing that his membrane is tender and his imagination yet weak; for a grown person finds that thing terrible and cruel, before it occurrs, which his imagination figurs to him beforehand. 3. Parents have not yet a great affection for the child at the moment of its birth; for the <i>imaginative form</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3861]]</sup> which produces in parents the love for the child has not yet become consolidated within them. Verily, this imaginative form augments by habitual contact<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3862]]</sup> and increass as the child grows, and it is only later that it beginns to decline and to fade away. Wherefore the father and the mother feel not for the new-born babe that love which they feel for a child of one year, nor do they love the child of one year as much as that of six years. If, therefore, the child were left for two or three years (without being circumcised), the consequence would be the neglect of circumcision, by reason of the affection and love which would be felt for the child. But at the time of its birth, this <i>imaginative form</i> is very weak, especially in the father, unto whom this commandment is enjoined<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3863]]</sup>. The reason why circumcision takes place on the eighth day is that every animal, at the moment of its birth, is very weak and exceeding tender, as though it were still in its mother's womb; only after seven days is it numbered among those beings which are in contact with the air. Seest thou not that for quadrupeds also regard was had to this circumstance? <i>Seven days it shall be with its dam, etc.</i> (Exodus, xxii. 30). Before this term, it is considered as an abortion, and likewise man may not be circumcised until after the term of seven days. In this manner also the matter remains fixed, and \"thou dost not make of it something variable<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3864]]</sup>.\"",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.22": "That which also enters into this class is the prohibition against mutilating the organs of generation of any male among animals<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3865]]</sup>, a prohibition which is linked to this principle of <i>righteous statutes and judgments</i> (Deut. iv. 8)<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3866]]</sup>, that is to say, of the golden mean in all things; one must not indulge overmuch in physical love, as we have said, but neither must it be utterly annihilated. Has not God pronounced this command: <i>Be fruitful and multiply</i> (Genesis, i. 22)? This organ must therefore be weakened by circumcision, but not entirely uprooted; on the contrary, that which is natural must be left in its nature, yet one must guard against excesses. It is forbidden for the castrated and the eunuch to marry an Israelite woman<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3867]]</sup>; for it would be a cohabitation lost and without purpose, and such a marriage would also become a stumbling-block for the woman and for him who seeks her<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3868]]</sup>, which is right manifest.",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.23": "To remove us from illicit unions, it is forbidden for the bastard to marry the daughter of an Israelite<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3869]]</sup>. It was intended that the adulterous man and woman should well know that, by committing this act, they impress beforehand upon their children a stain forever irreparable<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3870]]</sup>. Bastards being held in contempt according to the custom of all nations<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3871]]</sup>, the race of Israel has been judged too noble to unite with them. — Unto the priests it is forbidden, by reason of their nobility, to marry a harlot, a divorced woman, or a woman born of such a marriage<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3872]]</sup>; unto the high priest, who is the noblest among the priests, it is forbidden even to marry a widow, or a woman who should not be a virgin<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3873]]</sup>. The reason for all this is evident. — If it be forbidden to admit bastards into the <i>congregation of the Lord</i> (Deut. xxiii. 2), with much more reason, male and female slaves<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3874]]</sup>. As for the prohibition against alliance with the Gentiles, it is motivated in the text of the Pentateuch: <i>Lest... thou take of their daughters unto thy sons, etc.</i> (Exodus, xxxiv. 16).",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.24": "The majority of the <i>statutes</i> (or ordinances)<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3875]]</sup>, the reason of which is unknown to us, have no other end than to remove us from idolatry. If for certain details the motives be unknown to me, and if I know not their utility, the reason is that it is not with things known only by tradition as it is with those which have been seen<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3876]]</sup>. Therefore the little that I know of the opinions of the Sabians, having drawn it from books, cannot be compared to that which was known by those who knew their acts from having seen them, especially now that these opinions have vanished for two thousand years or more. If we knew all the particulars of these acts, and if we had heard all the details of these opinions, we should understand all the wisdom contained in the details of the practices relating to sacrifices, to impurities, etc., the reason of which does not appear easy to me (to comprehend)<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3877]]</sup>. For my part, I doubt not but that all this had for its end the effacing from our minds of these false ideas and the cessation of these useless practices, which caused the time of life to be lost in vain and idle occupations. These ideas did but hinder the human mind from seeking the conceptions of the intellect, or useful actions, as our prophets have expounded to us, saying: <i>They have followed after vain things, which cannot profit</i><sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3878]]</sup>; Jeremiah says: <i>Our fathers have inherited nought but lies, vanity, and things in which there is no profit</i> (Jeremiah, xvi. 19). Thou wilt understand how pernicious all this is, and whether it was not a thing that had to be brought to an end at any cost<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3879]]</sup>. Thus, then, the majority of the commandments, as we have expounded, have no other end than to cause these opinions to cease, and to lighten the great and grievous burdens, the fatigues and the pains which those men imposed upon themselves for the celebration of worship<sup class=\"footnote-marker\">[[fn:3880]]</sup>. Consequently, every precept of the Law, whether affirmative or negative, the reason of which thou art ignorant of, has no other end than to heal one of those maladies which, thanks be to God, we no longer know this day. This is what every perfect man must believe, who understands the true sense of this divine word: <i>I said not unto the seed of Jacob, Seek ye me in vain</i> (Isaiah, xlv. 19).",
    "root.text.Part 3..48.25": "Now, we have traversed, one by one, all the commandments comprised within these several classes[[fn:3881]], and we have indicated the reasons thereof. There remain but a few[[fn:3882]] for which I have not been able to assign a motive, as well as certain unimportant particulars; but in truth, we have virtually given the reason for them in a manner that lies within the reach of every studious and intelligent man[[fn:3883]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..49.0": "There are yet other matters which belong to the <i>mysteries of the Law</i>, and which, having perplexed many persons, stand in need of explanation. These are certain narratives related in the Pentateuch, in which no utility is apparent, such as, for instance, when there are enumerated the peoples descended from Noah, their names and the places of their habitations (Genesis, ch. X), <i>the Horites, descendants of Seir</i>, as also <i>the kings that reigned in the land of Edom</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 36, 20, 31 et seq.), and other like accounts. The Doctors say, as thou knowest, that the impious Manasseh did naught but continually occupy[[fn:3884]] his ignoble council with the criticism of these passages: “He sat there,” say they, “interpreting the <i>Haggadôth</i>[[fn:3885]] in a mocking spirit; he would say, for example: Had Moses need to write, ‘<i>And Lotan's sister was Timna</i>’ (<i>Ibid., v.</i> 22)?” I shall first make known unto thee a general principle, and thereafter I shall return to the details, even as I did in assigning motives for the commandments.",
    "root.text.Part 3..49.1": "Know that, whenever thou findest in the Pentateuch any narrative whatsoever, it has of necessity a certain utility for religion, whether it confirms one of the fundamental ideas of religion, or whether it teaches us a certain rule of conduct, so that there be among men neither mutual violence nor injustice. I shall now pass through the different cases[[fn:3886]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..49.2": "Since it is a fundamental belief of religion that the world was <i>created</i>, that in the beginning there was created but a single individual of the human species, who is Adam, and that the ancient times from Adam until Moses our Master comprised but a space of about two thousand five hundred years, these facts, set forth in a plain and simple manner, would have been promptly called into doubt; for even then men were found scattered to all the ends of the earth, forming diverse nations, speaking diverse tongues, widely remote from one another. These doubts were therefore silenced by indicating the genealogy and extraction of them all, mentioning the names of the best known among them, “such an one, the son of such an one,” as also their ages, and making known the place of their habitation, as well as the reason why they were scattered to the ends of the earth and why their languages were so different, although they were all originally in the same place and spoke the same tongue, as it behooved them to do, seeing they were the children of a single individual. Likewise, the recital of the history of the Deluge and of that of Sodom and Gomorrah was intended to show the truth of this idea: <i>Verily there is a reward for the righteous: verily he is a God that judges in the earth</i> (Ps. lviii. 11).",
    "root.text.Part 3..49.3": "Likewise, the description of the war of the nine kings has for its object to make known that miracle, namely, the victory achieved by Abraham, with a small number of men having no king with them, over four powerful kings[[fn:3887]]. We are also informed how he defended[[fn:3888]] his kinsman (Lot), who held the same belief as he, and how he braved the perils of war to rescue him. Finally, we are shown how reserved[[fn:3889]] he was, moderating his desires, despising gain, and applying himself to noble conduct, as it is said: <i>That I will not take from a thread even to a shoelatchet, etc.</i> (Genesis xiv. 23).",
    "root.text.Part 3..49.4": "If the tribes of the Seirites be all enumerated, and their particular genealogy[[fn:3890]] set forth, it is solely by reason of one commandment: for God specifically ordained the extermination of the seed of Amalek. Now, Amalek was none other than the son of Eliphaz, whom he had by Timna, the sister of Lotan[[fn:3891]]. But God had not commanded the slaying of the other sons of Esau; and whereas Esau had allied himself with the Seirites, as the Scripture plainly says[[fn:3892]], he had children by that race. He reigned over them, and his progeny became mingled with theirs; whence it followed that the whole land of Seir, with its tribes, was attributed to the preponderant tribe, which was that of the descendants of Esau, and especially that of Amalek, the most noble amongst them. Had not all these families been designated in detail, they might perchance have been slain through error. Therefore does the Scripture expressly designate their tribes, as if to say: those whom ye behold this day in the land of Seir and in the kingdom of Amalek are not all the descendants of Amalek, but the descendants of such and such an one; and if they be traced back to Amalek, it is because his mother belonged to their race[[fn:3893]]. All this betokens the divine justice, which willed not that one tribe should be slain indiscriminately[[fn:3894]] with another; for the divine decree struck only the race of Amalek in particular. We have already set forth what wisdom there was in this measure[[fn:3895]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..49.5": "The reason wherefore are enumerated <i>the kings that reigned in the land of Edom</i> (Genesis, 36, 31), is that there is a commandment which says: <i>Thou mayest not set a stranger over thee, which is not thy brother</i> (Deuteron., 17, 15). Now, among the kings mentioned, there was not a single one who was a native of Edom[[fn:3896]]; seest thou not that their family is indicated as well as their country—such an one, from such a place; another, from another place? It appears to me right probable that their acts and their history were generally known—I mean the acts of these kings of Edom—and that they tyrannized over and humbled[[fn:3897]] the descendants of Esau; for which cause they are recalled[[fn:3898]] to the memory of the Israelites. It is as though it were said: «Take ye warning from your brethren, the descendants of Esau, who had for their king such or such an one [whose acts were then well known]; for never has a man of a strange race reigned over a nation without exercising over it a tyranny more or less grievous.»",
    "root.text.Part 3..49.6": "In sum, that which I have told thee concerning the interval which now separats us from the religious practices of the Sabeans[[fn:3899]] may also be applied to the history of those times which is now unknown to us; for, if we knew it, and if the events which then occurred were manifest to us, we should understand, in respect of many details, the ground which caused them to be mentioned in the Pentateuch.",
    "root.text.Part 3..49.7": "It must further be well understood that written narratives are not regarded in the same light as the events of which one is a witness; for in the latter, there are certain details having necessary consequences which cannot be related without prolixity[[fn:3900]]. Wherefore, in reading these narratives, the reader finds therein prolixity or repetitions; but had he been a witness of that which is related, he would understand that what has been said is necessary. Whenever, therefore, thou seest narratives in the non-legislative portion of the Pentateuch, it seems to thee at times that such or such a narrative had no need to be written, or that it contains either tedious lengths or repetitions[[fn:3901]]; the reason thereof is that thou hast not been a witness of the details which led the author to frame his narrative as he has done.",
    "root.text.Part 3..49.8": "Of this category is the enumeration of the <i>stations</i>[[fn:3902]]. It might appear at first sight that a thing altogether useless had been related; it is therefore by reason of this false notion which might be held that it is said: <i>And Moses wrote their goings out according to their journeys by the commandment of the Lord</i> (Numbers, 33, 2). And this was of a very great necessity; for all miracles are certain to him who has seen them, but for posterity, the account thereof becoms a mere tradition, and for him who hears it, it is easy to deny it[[fn:3903]]. It is known to be impossible and unimaginable that a miracle should be certified and attested by all men in the course of ages. Now, one of the miracles related in the Pentateuch, and even one of the greatest, is the sojourn of forty years which the Israelites made in the desert, where they found manna every day. In that desert, as the Pentateuch says, there were <i>fiery serpents, and scorpions, and drought, where there was no water</i> (Deuter., 8, 15). These were places very remote from a cultivated land and which were not suited to the nature of man: <i>no place of seed, or of figs, or of vines, or of pomegranates, etc.</i> (Numbers, 20, 5); these places are also called <i>a land that no man passed through</i> (Jeremiah, 2, 6); and the text of the Pentateuch says: <i>Ye have not eaten bread, neither have ye drunk wine or strong drink</i> (Deuter., 29, 5). All these miracles were manifest and visible. Now, God knew that in the future, it would befall these miracles as it befalls traditions, and that it would be thought they sojourned in a desert nigh unto inhabited places, where man can abide, and similar to those deserts which the Arabs inhabit this day, or that they were places where one might plow and reap, or sustain oneself by some plants found therein, or that it was in the nature of the manna to fall continually in those places[[fn:3904]], or that there were in those places cisterns with water. Wherefore, to remove all such doubts, the account of all these miracles has been confirmed by the enumeration of these stations, so that future generations might see them and recognize the magnitude of the miracle by which the human species was able to sojourn in those places for forty years.",
    "root.text.Part 3..49.9": "It is for the same reason that Joshua pronounced a perpetual anathema against whosoever should rebuild Jericho[[fn:3905]], to the end that the miracle might be certified and established; for whosoever should behold those walls sunken into the earth would perceive that it was not the wall of a demolished edifice, but that it had been engulfed by a miracle.",
    "root.text.Part 3..49.10": "Likewise, when it is said: <i>at the commandment of the Lord they encamped, and at the commandment of the Lord they journeyed</i> (Numbers ix. 20), this might have sufficed for the narrative; and it might appear at first sight that all which is subsequently related upon the same subject is but an unnecessary prolixity, as, for example, the words: <i>And so it was, when the cloud abode from even unto the morning, etc.</i> (<i>v.</i> 19), <i>and so it was, when the cloud abode, etc.</i> (<i>v.</i> 21), <i>or whether it were two days, etc.</i> (<i>v.</i> 22). I shall now impart unto thee that which has occasioned all these details: the reason thereof is that the Scripture desired to insist upon this account, in order to destroy the notion then held by foreign nations, and which they hold even unto this day, to wit, that the Israelites were lost upon the way and knew not whither they should go, as it is said: <i>They are entangled in the land</i> (Exodus xiv. 3). Even thus the Arabs unto this day call that desert <i>Al-Tîh</i>, and imagine that the Israelites were wandering astray (<i>tâhou</i>)[[fn:3906]] and were ignorant of the path. The Scripture, therefore, setts forth with emphasis that these irregular stations, the return to several among them, and the diverse duration of the stay in each—in such wise that they remained in one station eighteen years[[fn:3907]], in another a day, and in yet another but a single night[[fn:3908]]—that all this (I say) was decreed by God and was not a mere wandering upon the road, but depended upon the lifting of the <i>pillar of cloud</i>. For this cause are all these details given, after having declared, in the Pentateuch, that this interval of the way was short, well known, much frequented, and by no means unknown; I speak of the interval between Horeb [whither they had repaired with intent, according to that which God had commanded them: <i>ye shall serve God upon this mountain</i> (Exodus iii. 12)] and Kadesh-barnea, where the inhabited land beginns, as the Scripture says: <i>Behold, we are in Kadesh, a city in the uttermost of thy border</i> (Numbers xx. 16). This interval is traversed in eleven days, as it is said: <i>There are eleven days' journey from Horeb by the way of mount Seir</i>, <i>unto Kadesh-barnea</i> (Deut. i. 2); it is not, therefore, a path in which one might wander for forty years, and this must be attributed solely to the causes expressly written in the Pentateuch.",
    "root.text.Part 3..49.11": "Thus it is that whenever thou art ignorant of the reason why any history whatsoever has been related (in the Pentateuch), there was for it a grave motive, and to all such cases may further be applied that principle which the Doctors have brought to our notice: «<i>For it is not a vain thing for you</i> (Deut. xxxii. 47); and if it be vain, it is because of you[[fn:3909]].»",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.0": "The chapter which we are now about to set forth adds no new subject to those contained in the other chapters of this treatise. It is, as it were, a <i>conclusion</i>, setting forth the worship to which he must devote himself who understands the true duties to be practiced toward God[[fn:3910]], after having rightly comprehended His true being; it is intended to guide man to that worship which is the true end of man, and to make him know how Providence watches over him in this world until the moment when he passes into life eternal[[fn:3911]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.1": "I commence my discourse, in this chapter, by presenting unto thee the following parable: The sovereign was in his palace, and his subjects were partly within the city and partly without. Of those who were in the city, some turned their backs upon the habitation of the sovereign and journeyed in another direction; others turned toward the habitation of the sovereign and directed their steps toward him, seeking to enter his dwelling and to present themselves before him, but hitherto they had not yet perceived the wall of the palace. Of those who were repairing thither, some, having reached the palace, went round about it seeking the entrance; others had entered and were walking in the vestibules; others, finally, had succeeded in entering the inner court of the palace and had arrived at the place where the king was, that is to say, at the sovereign's own habitation. These, however, although they had arrived in this habitation, could neither see the sovereign nor speak with him; but, after having penetrated into the interior of the dwelling, they had yet to perform other indispensable steps, and then only could they present themselves before the sovereign, see him from afar or from near, hear his word, or speak unto him. — I shall now explain unto thee this parable which I have devised:",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.2": "As for \"those who were outside the city,\" these comprise all such men as possess no religious belief, neither speculative nor traditional, such as the furthest of the Turks in the extreme north[[fn:3912]], the Negroes in the extreme south, and those who resemble them in our climates. These are to be accounted as irrational animals; I place them not in the rank of men, for they occupy among beings a rank inferior to that of man and superior to that of the ape, since they possess the figure and lineaments of man and a discernment above that of the ape.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.3": "\"Those who were within the city, but turned their backs upon the habitation of the sovereign,\" are men who hold an opinion[[fn:3913]] and who think, but who have conceived ideas contrary to the truth, whether in consequence of some grave error that has befallen them in their speculation, or because they have followed those who were in error. These, by reason of their opinions, the more they walk, the further they recede from the habitation of the sovereign; they are far worse than the first, and there arrive moments when it even becoms necessary to slay them and to efface the traces of their opinions, lest they lead others astray.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.4": "— \"Those who turned towards the habitation of the sovereign and sought to enter therein, but who had not yet beheld the habitation of the sovereign,\" are the multitude of religious men, that is to say, the ignorant who occupy themselves with religious observances.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.5": "— \"Those who had arrived at the palace and went round about it,\" are the casuists[[fn:3914]] who admit, by tradition, true opinions, and who discourse upon the practices of worship, but who engage not in speculation concerning the fundamental principles of religion, nor seek in any wise to establish the truth of any belief whatsoever.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.6": "— As for those who plunge into speculation on the fundamental principles of religion, they are 'those who had entered the vestibules', where men are undoubtedly admitted to varying degrees.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.7": "They who have comprehended the demonstration of all that is demonstrable, who have attained unto certainty in metaphysical matters, wheresoever this be possible, or who have approached unto certainty where one can but approach it, these are they \"who have arrived within the interior of the palace, in the presence of the Sovereign.\"",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.8": "Know, my son, that so long as thou occupiest thyself but with mathematical sciences and with logic, thou art of those who go round about the palace (of the Sovereign) and seek the entrance thereof, even as the Doctors say allegorically: «Ben-Zôma is still without[[fn:3915]]»; after having understood the objects of physics, thou hast entered the palace and walkest in its vestibules[[fn:3916]]; finally, after having completed the physical sciences and studied metaphysics, thou hast entered into the presence of the Sovereign, into the inner court, and thou findest thyself with Him in the same apartment. This latter degree is that of the (true) sages, but even here there are (to be distinguished) different degrees of perfection. They who, after having perfected themselves in metaphysics, occupy their thought with God alone, devoting themselves entirely unto Him, and withdrawing from all that is without Him, and who make the whole action of their intelligence to consist in reflecting upon the (created) beings, in order to draw from these latter the proof of the existence of God and to know in what manner He may govern them, these (I say) are found in the hall where the Sovereign sitts; such is the degree of the prophets. There was one whose perception was so powerful and who knew so well how to isolate himself from all that is without God, that it could be said of him: <i>And he remained there with God</i> (Exodus, 34, 28), questioning, receiving answers, speaking and receiving the word (of God), in that holy abode. By reason of his great contentment in that which he perceived, <i>he did neither eat bread nor drink water</i> (<i>ibid.</i>); for the intelligence so far prevailed, that it annihilated every material faculty in the body, I mean the various faculties of the sense of touch. There have been other prophets who <i>beheld</i> only, some from near at hand, others from afar, as it is said: <i>From afar the Lord appeared unto me</i> (Jeremiah, 31, 3). Having already spoken previously of the degrees of prophecy[[fn:3917]],",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.9.sub_0": "we return to the purpose of this chapter, the object of which is to encourage man to occupy his thought with God alone, after he has learned to know Him, as we have set forth. This is the true worship which befitts those who have perceived the transcendent truths; the more they meditate upon God and abide with Him, the more does He become the object of their worship. As for those who meditate upon God and who speak much of Him without possessing science, but rather attaching themselves to a mere being of their imagination, or to a belief which they have received by tradition, these, I say, finding themselves[[fn:3918]] without the palace and far removed from it, do not really think of God nor meditate upon Him. Verily, that being who exists only in their imagination and of whom their mouth speaks responds to naught that is real and is but an invention of their fancy, as we have set forth when speaking of the <i>attributes</i>[[fn:3919]]. One must not give oneself unto this species of worship save after having conceived (the idea of <i>God</i>) by means of the intellect; it is only after having understood God and His works, as far as the intelligence requirs[[fn:3920]], that thou canst entirely consecrate thyself unto Him, seek to draw nigh unto Him and strengthen the bond which exists between thee and Him, to wit, the intellect[[fn:3921]], even as it is said: <i>Unto thee it was shewn to know that the Lord, etc.</i> (Deuter., 4, 35); <i>know therefore this day and consider it in thine heart, etc.</i> (<i>ibid., v.</i> 39); <i>know ye that the Lord He is God</i> (Ps. 100, 3). Already the Pentateuch setts forth that this supreme worship, whereon we call attention in this chapter, can only take place following upon perception: <i>To love the Lord your God</i>, it is said, <i>and to serve Him with all your heart and with all your soul</i> (Deuter., 11, 13). We have already set forth several times[[fn:3922]] that <i>love</i> (of God) is in proportion to perception; it is only following upon love that there can come this worship whereon the Doctors also have called attention, saying: «c’est le culte du cœur[[fn:3923]].» This consists, in my view, in applying the thought to the principal object of the intellect[[fn:3924]] and in devoting oneself unto Him as much as one is able.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.9.sub_1": "Wherefore thou findest (in Scripture) that David, in dictating to Solomon his last wishes, recommends unto him especially these two things, namely, to strive to arrive at the knowledge of God and then to render unto Him a worship (worthy of that knowledge): <i>And thou</i>, says he, <i>my son Solomon, know thou the God of thy father, and serve him, etc. If thou seek him, he will be found of thee, etc.</i> (I Chron., 28, 9). This whole exhortation can have for its object naught but <i>intelligible conceptions</i>, and not the creations of the imagination; for thoughts relating to things of the imagination are not called <span dir=\"rtl\">דעה</span>, <i>knowledge</i>, but are called <span dir=\"rtl\">העולה על רוחכם</span>, <i>that which comes into your mind</i> (Ezekiel, XX, 32). It is therefore clear that after having acquired the knowledge of God, one must aim at devoting oneself unto Him and constantly occupy the thought and the intelligence[[fn:3925]] with the love which is His due. This is attained, for the most part, only through solitude and isolation; wherefore every superior man seeks often to isolate himself and joins with no one, save in case of necessity[[fn:3926]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.10": "[A<small>NNOTATION</small>. We have already set forth unto thee that this intellect which flows down upon us from God is the bond which exists between us and Him[[fn:3927]]. It depends upon thee, either to strengthen and consolidate this bond, or to weaken and relax it little by little, until it be undone[[fn:3928]]. This bond can only be strengthened when one makes use of it to love God and to draw nigh unto Him, as we have set forth; it is weakened and relaxed when thou occupiest thy thought with that which is without Him. Know thou that, even though thou wert the man most learned in true metaphysics, if thou turnest thy thought from God and occupiest thyself entirely[[fn:3929]] with thy food or other necessary affairs, thou hast severed this bond which exists between thee and God, and thou art no longer with Him, even as He is no longer with thee; for this relation which existed between thee and Him has ceased in fact in those moments. Wherefore superior men employed but rare moments in occupying themselves with other things than Him[[fn:3930]], from which they wished to preserve us by this warning: «Turn ye not unto that which comes of your own thought[[fn:3931]].» David said: <i>I have set the Lord always before me, because He is at my right hand, I shall not be moved</i> (Ps., 16, 8); that is to say: I never turn my thought from God, and He is as my right hand which I forget not for a moment by reason of the swiftness of its movements; wherefore I shall not be moved, that is to say, I shall not fall.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.11": "— Ye must know that all the ceremonies of worship, such as the reading of the Law, prayer, and the practice of other commandments, have no other end than to train thee to occupy thyself with the commandments of God instead of occupying thyself with worldly matters, and, so to speak, to occupy thyself with God alone and none other. But, if thou prayest by moving thy lips and turning thy face toward the wall, whilst thou art thinking of what thou hast to sell or to buy, or if thou readest the Law with thy tongue, whilst thy heart is occupied with the building of thy house, without reflecting upon what thou readest; in fine, if thou performest any commandment whatsoever with thy limbs, like one who diggs a pit in the earth or who cutts wood in the forest, without reflecting either upon the meaning of this practice, nor upon Him who has ordained it, nor upon that which it has for its end, then thou must not believe that thou hast attained any end whatsoever; on the contrary, thou then drawest nigh unto those of whom it has been said: <i>Thou art near in their mouth, and far from their reins</i> (Jeremiah, xii, 2).",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.12": "Now, I shall indicate unto thee the manner of training thyself to arrive at this important end:",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.13": "The first thing to which thou must attach thyself is to keep thy mind free from all other things at the moment when thou devotest thyself to the reading of the <i>Schemâ’</i> and to prayer, and that thou contentest not thyself with reciting with attention the first verse of the <i>Schemâ’</i> and the first <i>benediction</i> of the prayer[[fn:3932]]. When thou shalt have succeeded therein and shalt have made it a habit for years, thou must endeavour, every time thou readest in the Pentateuch or hearest it read, to apply thy whole thought unceasingly to reflect upon that which thou shalt hear or that which thou shalt read. When thou shalt have likewise made this a habit, thou shalt endeavour to have thy mind always free in all that thou readest of the other discourses of the prophets and even in all the benedictions, and to apply thyself to reflect upon all that thou shalt pronounce and to understand the meaning thereof. When thou shalt have performed these religious acts and thy thought, at the moment of practicing them, shall have remained pure from all preoccupation with worldly things, then only shalt thou be able to begin to occupy thyself with the necessary things or the superfluities of life. In general, thou shouldst not apply thy thought to worldly things[[fn:3933]] save at the moment of eating and drinking, or when thou art in the bath, or when thou conversest with thy wife or thy young children, or when thou speakest with any man whomsoever. Thus, I leave thee sufficient and ample time to think of all that is necessary for thee in matters of business, of domestic economy, and of bodily needs; but, in the moments when thou occupiest thyself with religious things, thy thought must be entirely upon that which thou doest, even as we have set forth.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.14": "When thou art quite alone, or when thou art awake in thy bed, beware well, in those precious moments, of thinking of anything else than of this intellectual worship which consists in drawing nigh unto God and in presenting thyself before Him in the true manner which I have made known unto thee, and not by the fantastic action of the imagination[[fn:3934]]. At this stage may arrive, according to my view, every man of science who has prepared himself therefor by this kind of exercise (of which we have spoken).",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.15.sub_0": "If a human individual could succeed in perceiving the high truths and in enjoying that which he has perceived, to such a degree as to be able to converse with men and to occupy himself with his bodily needs, whilst his whole intelligence were turned toward God and that, in his heart, he were always in the presence of God, albeit being externally with men,—much as it is said in the poetical allegories composed upon these subjects: <i>I sleep, but my heart waks; it is the voice of my beloved that knocks, etc.</i> (Canticles, V, 2),—that would be a degree (of perfection) which I would not attribute even to all the prophets, and I would rather say that it is the degree of Moses our master, of whom it is said: <i>Moses alone shall come near the Eternal, but they shall not come near</i> (Exodus, 24, 2), <i>and he was there with God</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, XXXIV, 28); and to whom it was said: <i>But as for thee, stand thou here by Me</i> (Deuter., 5, 28), according to the sense that we have attributed to these verses[[fn:3935]]. This is also the degree attained by the patriarchs, who were to such a degree near unto God, that it was from them that was derived the denomination by which He was known in the world[[fn:3936]]: <i>The God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob; ... this is my name for ever</i> (Exodus, 3, 15). Their intelligence having perceived God even unto uniting with Him, it resulted therefrom that He concluded with each of them a perpetual covenant: <i>Then will I remember my covenant with Jacob, etc.</i> (Leviticus, XXVI, 42). Indeed, the sacred texts attest clearly that these four, I mean the patriarchs and Moses our master, were united with God, that is to say, they perceived Him and loved Him; likewise, Divine Providence watched with care over them and over their posterity after them. Notwithstanding this, they occupied themselves sometimes with the government of men, with the increase of their fortune, and with the seeking of possessions; and this proves, in my opinion, that while busying themselves with their affairs, they were occupied therewith only with their body, whilst their intelligence was unceasingly with God.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.20": "The philosophers have likewise set forth that, in youth, the corporeal forces constitute an obstacle to the majority of moral virtues[[fn:3949]], and a fortiori to that pure meditation resulting from the perfection of ideas which leads man to love God passionately. It is impossible that this meditation should arise whilst the ebullition of the bodily humours persists; but in proportion as the forces of the body weaken and the fire of desire is quenched, the intellect is strengthened, its light increass, its comprehension gains in clarity, and it experiencs a greater delight in that which it has apprehended; so that, in the man bowed down under the weight of years and drawing nigh unto death, this comprehension receivs a vast increase, yields an exceeding great joy, and inspirs a true passion for Him who is its object, until at length, in the midst of this delight, the soul is sundered from the body.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.21": "It is unto this state that the doctors have alluded when speaking of the death of Moses, of Aaron, and of Miriam, and saying that all three died by a kiss. This passage, say they, <i>And Moses, the servant of the Eternal, died there in the land of Moab by the mouth</i> (the command) <i>of the Eternal</i> (Deut. xxxiv. 5), teaches us that Moses died by a kiss; in like manner it is said of Aaron: … <i>by the mouth</i> (the command) <i>of the Eternal, and he died there</i> (Numbers, xxxiii. 38); likewise they say of Miriam that she also died by a kiss, but concerning her, it is not said: <i>by the mouth of the Eternal</i>, because she was a woman, and it was not seemly to employ, regarding her, this allegory[[fn:3950]]. They intend thereby that all three died in the delight which this comprehension caused them to experience and by the violence of love. The doctors have employed in this passage the well-known allegorical method, according to which this comprehension, resulting from the violent love which man feels for God, is called a <i>kiss</i>, as it is said: <i>Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth, etc.</i> (Song of Sol., i. 2). This manner of death, say they, whereby man escaps true death, befell none but Moses, Aaron, and Miriam; the other prophets and pious men are below this degree. But in all, the comprehension of the intellect is strengthened at the moment of parting (from the body), as it is said: <i>Thy righteousness shall go before thee, and the glory of the Eternal shall follow thee</i> (Isaiah, lviii. 8). After this, the intellect remains for evermore in the same state; for the obstacle which sometimes hid (its object)[[fn:3951]] has been removed; it then continually tasts this great delight, which is of a nature quite other than the delights of the body, as we have set forth in our works and as others have set forth before us.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.22": "Apply thyself to understand this chapter, and exert thy whole endeavour to multiply the moments in which thou mayest be with God, or seek to rise towards Him, and to diminish the moments in which thou art occupied with other things, without seeking to reach Him. These counsels[[fn:3952]] suffice for the purpose I had in this treatise.",
    "root.text.Part 3..51.0": "A man alone in his house sitts, movs, and busies himself in a manner he would not do in the presence of a king; when he finds himself with his family or with his kinsfolk, he speaks freely and at his ease, as he would not speak in the king's hall. He, therefore, who desirs to attain human perfection and to be truly a man of God, will be well imbued with this idea[[fn:3953]], that the great King who accompanies him and who cleavs to him constantly is greater than any human person[[fn:3954]], though it were even David or Solomon. This King, who cleavs (unto man) and accompanies him, is the intellect which emanats upon us and which is the bond between us and God; and even as we perceive Him by means of this light which He pours upon us, as it is said: <i>by Thy light do we see light</i> (Ps. xxxvi. 10), so by means of this light does He observe us, and thereby is He ever with us, enveloping us with His gaze: <i>Can any hide himself in secret places that I shall not see him</i> (Jeremiah, xxiii. 24)? This must be well understood,",
    "root.text.Part 3..51.1": "and it must be known that perfect men, so soon as they are imbued therewith, will attain, by the ways of truth and not of imagination[[fn:3955]], to such a degree of piety, humility, fear, and reverence for the Divinity, and will feel such shame before God, that they will conduct themselves in their private life, whether being with their wives or in the bath, as they would conduct themselves in public before all men. Thus, for example, it is said of the conduct of our celebrated doctors with their wives: “Uncovering a span and covering a span[[fn:3956]].” Elsewhere it is said: “Who is a modest man? He who performs his needs by night even as he would do in the broad day[[fn:3957]].” Thou knowest also that the doctors have forbidden “to walk with an upright stature, <i>the whole earth being full of the glory of God</i> (Isaiah, vi. 3)[[fn:3958]].” All this aims at confirming the idea of which I have spoken, to wit, that we find ourselves always before God, and that it is in the presence of His Majesty that we go and come. The greatest doctors even refrained from baring their heads, because the Divine Majesty envelops man[[fn:3959]]. It is for the same reason that they spoke little; in the treatise <i>Aboth</i> we have already sufficiently set forth why it is good to speak little[[fn:3960]]: <i>For God is in heaven, and thou upon earth; therefore let thy words be few</i> (Ecclesiastes, v. 1).",
    "root.text.Part 3..51.2": "The idea whereunto I have awakened thy attention contains likewise the design of all the practices prescribed by the Law; for, by applying themselves unto all these practical details and by repeating them, certain men of excellence shall exercise themselves to attain unto human perfection, so as to fear God, to respect and to revere Him, knowing Him who is with them, and in this manner they shall thereafter perform that which is necessary. God Himself has set forth that the end of all the practices prescribed by the Law is to receive thereby those impressions of which we have, in this chapter, demonstrated the necessity unto those who know the high truths, (which impressions consist) in fearing God and respecting His precepts. He says: <i>If thou wilt not observe to do all the words of this law that are written in this book</i>, <i>that thou mayest fear this glorious and fearful name, THE LORD THY GOD</i> (Deuter., 28, 58). Thou seest how clearly He says that <i>all the words of this Law</i> have but one single end, to wit, to cause one <i>to fear this name</i>, <i>etc.;</i> that this end must be obtained through the practices, this thou mayest recognize by the words of this verse: <i>if thou wilt not observe to do</i>, in which it is clearly stated that it is the result of the practices[[fn:3961]], (that is to say, of the performance) of the affirmative and negative precepts. As for the <i>ideas</i> which the Law teaches us, to wit, those of the existence of God and of His unity, they must inspire us with <i>love</i> (for God), as we have several times set forth, and thou knowest with what energy the Law insists upon this love: <i>With all thine heart</i>, <i>and with all thy soul, and with all thy might</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 6, 5). In truth, these two ends, to wit, the love and the fear of God, are attained by two things: unto <i>love</i> one arrivs by the ideas which the Law contains concerning the true doctrine of the existence of God[[fn:3962]]; unto <i>fear</i> one arrivs by means of all the practices of the Law, as we have set forth.—Thou must well understand this brief exposition.",
    "root.text.Part 3..52.0": "This chapter contains the exposition of the meaning of three words which it is necessary to explain; these are the words ’H<small>ÉSED</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חסר</span>), M<small>ISCHPAT</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">משפט</span>), C<small>EDAKA</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צדקה</span>).",
    "root.text.Part 3..52.1": "We have already set forth in the commentary upon <i>Abôth</i> that ’H<small>ÉSED</small> signifies <i>excess</i> in all things, whatsoever they be[[fn:3963]]; but it is more often employed to express <i>an excess of beneficence.</i> It is known that <i>to be liberal</i> is said in two senses: it is, first, to do good unto him to whom one ows absolutely naught; and second, to do good unto him who has deserved it, beyond that which he has deserved. The prophetic books most often employ the word ’H<small>ÉSED</small> in the sense of “doing good unto him to whom one ows absolutely naught”; wherefore every benefit which comes from God is called ’H<small>ÉSED</small>, as it is said: <i>I will mention the loving-kindnesses</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חסדי</span>) <i>of the Lord</i> (Isaiah, 63, 7). Wherefore also all this universe, I mean its production by God, is called ’H<small>ÉSED</small>, as it is said: <i>For I have said, Mercy</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חסד</span>) <i>shall be built up for ever</i>[[fn:3964]] <i></i> (Ps. 89, 3), which is to say: the construction of the universe is a benefit. God says again in enumerating His attributes: <span dir=\"rtl\">ורב חסד</span>, <i>abundant in goodness</i> (Exodus, 34, 6).",
    "root.text.Part 3..52.2": "—As for the word C<small>EDAKA</small>, it is derived from C<small>ÉDEK</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">צדק</span>), which signifies <i>equity.</i> Equity consists in doing justice unto whomsoever may invoke a right, and in giving unto every being according to its merit. Nevertheless, in the prophetic books, the discharge of debts which are incumbent upon thee towards another is not called C<small>EDAKA</small>, according to the first sense; for if thou payest unto the hireling his wages, or if thou payest thy debt, that is not called C<small>EDAKA</small>. But that which is there called <small>CEDAKA</small> is the fulfillment of duties which are incumbent upon thee towards another from the standpoint of a good moral character, as, for example, to succour him who suffers from any evil whatsoever[[fn:3965]]. Wherefore it is said, concerning the duty of restoring the pledge (Deuter., 24, 13): <span dir=\"rtl\">ולך תהיה צדקה</span>, “and it shall be <i>righteousness unto thee;”</i> for if thou walkest in the way of the moral virtues, thou art <i>equitable</i> toward thy rational soul, since thou fulfillest thy duty toward it. Every moral virtue being called C<small>EDAKA</small>[[fn:3966]], it is said (Genesis, 15, 6): <i>And he believed in the Lord; and He counted it to him for</i> C<small>EDAKA</small> (virtue), in which one means the virtue of faith. It is the same with this passage: <i>And it shall be our</i> C<small>EDAKA</small> (<i>virtue</i>), <i>if we observe to do, etc.</i> (Deuter., 6, 25).",
    "root.text.Part 3..52.3": "—As for that which concerns the word M<small>ISCHPAT</small>, it signifies the fit judgment pronounced in respect of him who is judged, whether it be to his advantage or to his detriment.",
    "root.text.Part 3..52.4": "In summary, the word ’H<small>ESED</small> is applied unto that which constitutes absolute liberality; C<small>EDAKA</small> is predicated of every good which thou performest from the standpoint of moral virtue, whereby thou dost perfect thy soul. Finally, that which is termed M<small>ISCHPAT</small> has for its result at times a punishment, and at times a benefit.",
    "root.text.Part 3..52.5": "We have already set forth, whilst treating of the negation of (divine) attributes, that every attribute ascribed unto God in the books of the prophets is an <i>attribute of action</i>[[fn:3967]]. Thus, inasmuch as He bestows existence upon all things, He is called ’H<small>ASSÎD</small>[[fn:3968]] (beneficent); in respect of His tenderness toward weak beings[[fn:3969]], that is to say, in so far as He governs living beings by means of their faculties, He is called Ç<small>ADDÎK</small> (equitable); finally, in respect of the relative benefits and the great relative calamities[[fn:3970]] which occur in the universe, and which are a result of the judgment emanating from (divine) wisdom, He is called S<small>CHOPHET</small> (judge). These three words are found in the text of the Pentateuch, for example: <i>Shall not the</i> <small>JUDGE</small> <i>of all the earth</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Genesis, 18, 25); <i>He is</i> <small>EQUITABLE</small> <i>and just</i> (Deut., 32, 4); <i>abundant in</i> <small>LOVING-KINDNESS</small> (Exodus, 34, 6).",
    "root.text.Part 3..52.6": "In explaining the signification of these words, our purpose was to prepare (the reader) for the succeeding chapter.",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.0": "The word ’H<small>OKHMA</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חכמה</span>), in the Hebrew language, is employed in four senses[[fn:3971]]: 1st. It signifies the perception of truths (philosophical) which have for their ultimate aim the perception of God, (and in this sense) it is said: <i>But whence shall one draw the</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמה</span>, <i>wisdom</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Job, 28, 12); <i>if thou seekest her as silver, etc.</i> (Prov., 2, 4), and many other passages. 2nd. It refers to the possession of any art (or industry) whatsoever; for example: <i>All the artists</i> (or <i>industrious ones</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">חכם לב</span>) <i>among you</i> (Exodus, 35, 10); <i>and all the industrious women</i> (<i>ibid</i>., <i>v.</i> 25). 3rd. It applies to the acquisition of moral virtues; for example: <i>and that he might make wise</i> (or <i>instruct</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">יחכם</span>) <i>his elders</i> (Ps. 105, 22); <i>in the aged is wisdom</i> (Job, 12, 12), for that which is acquired by old age alone, is the disposition to receive moral virtues[[fn:3972]]. 4th. It is also employed in the sense of shrewdness and cunning; for example: <i>Come, let us deal shrewdly</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נתחכמה</span>) <i>with him</i> (Exodus, 1, 10). It is in this sense that it is said: <i>And he sent thence for a wise woman</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">אשה חכמה</span> (II Samuel, XIV, 2), which signifies: endowed with shrewdness and cunning. Similarly, in this passage: <i>they are wise</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמים</span>, <i>to do evil</i> (Jeremiah, 4, 22). —It may be that the word ’H<small>OKHMA</small>, in the Hebrew language, bears (originally) the sense of <i>shrewdness</i> and <i>application of thought</i>, in such wise that this shrewdness or sagacity shall have for its object now the acquisition of intellectual qualities, now that of moral qualities, now that of a practical art, and anon evil designs and wickednesses. Thus it is clear that the adjective ’H<small>AKHAM</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חכם</span>) is applied to him who possesses intellectual qualities, or moral qualities, or any practical art whatsoever, or cunning for wickednesses and evil designs.",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.1": "According to this explanation, he who is instructed in the whole Law, and who knows its true sense, is called ’H<small>AKHAM</small> from two points of view, because it embraces at once intellectual and moral qualities. But, as the intellectual truths of the Law are accepted by tradition, without being demonstrated by speculative methods, it happens that, in the books of the prophets and in the words of the Doctors, the science of the Law is made a thing apart and absolute science a thing apart. This absolute science is that which provids demonstrations for all those intellectual truths which we have traditionally learned through the Law; and whenever the (Holy) Scriptures speak of the greatness and wonder of science, and of the rarity of those who possess it, — for example: <i>Great men are not always wise</i> (or <i>learned</i>) (Job, 32, 9); <i>where shall wisdom be found</i>, (or <i>science</i>) (<i>Ibid</i>., XXVIII, 12)? and many other similar passages, — it always pertains to that science which provids us with the demonstration of the (philosophical) ideas contained in the Law. In the words of the Doctors, this also occurrs very frequently; I mean that they make the science of the Law a thing apart and philosophy a thing apart. Thus, for example, they say of Moses that he was “father of the Law, father of science, and father of the prophets[[fn:3973]]”; and when it is said of Solomon: <i>For he was wiser</i> (more learned) <i>than all men</i> (I Kings, 4, 31, or V, 11), the Doctors remark: “but not (wiser) than Moses[[fn:3974]]” [for in saying: <i>than all men</i>, one intends to speak only of his contemporaries; wherefore are mentioned Ethan, Heman, Calcol, and Darda, the sons of Mahol, who were then celebrated as men of learning].",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.2": "The Doctors further declare that from man is first required the knowledge of the Law, thereafter (philosophical) science, and finally the knowledge of the tradition which is linked unto the Law, that is to say, the knowing how to derive thence rules for his conduct[[fn:3975]]. Such must be the successive order of studies: first, one must know the ideas in question traditionally, then one must know how to demonstrate them, and finally one must render an exact account of those actions which constitute good conduct[[fn:3976]]. Hearken how they express themselves concerning the questions which are successively addressed unto man regarding these three things: «When man appears before the (celestial) tribunal, he is first asked: Hast thou appointed set hours for the study of the Law? Hast thou engaged in the discussion of science? Hast thou learned to understand one thing from another[[fn:3977]]?» It is therefore manifest that, in their view, the knowledge of the Law is one thing and philosophy another; the latter consists in confirming the truths of the Law by means of true speculation.",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.3": "— Having premised these matters, hearken to that which we shall say. The philosophers, both ancient and modern, have set forth that there are found in man four species of perfections[[fn:3978]]:",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.4": "The first species, which is of the least value, yet unto which the inhabitants of the earth devote their entire lives, is perfection in the matter of possession; it embraces what man possesses in the way of goods, garments, furniture, slaves, lands, and the like; and even the attainment of regal dignity belongs to this species. This is a perfection which appertains in no wise to the person; it is but a certain relation which affords unto man, at the most, naught but an imaginary enjoyment, to wit, the saying: this house is mine, this slave is mine, this fortune is mine, these troops are mine. But if he considers his own person, he shall find that all this is external to his essence, and that each of his possessions is in itself what it is; wherefore, when this relation ceass, it is found[[fn:3979]] that there is no difference between him who was a great king and the meanest of men, without any of the things which were in relation with him undergoing a change. The philosophers have shown that he whose every effort tends to obtain this kind of perfection toils but for a thing purely imaginary; for it is a thing that has no stability, and should this possession even remain with him his whole life long, no perfection would thereby accrue to him in his essence.",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.5": "The second species appertains, more than the first, unto the essence of the person; it is the perfection in the conformation and the constitution of the body; I mean to say, that the temperament of the person be of a perfect equilibrium, and that his members be well-proportioned and possess the necessary strength. Neither can this species of perfection be adopted as the final end; for it is a bodily perfection which man possesses not in so far as he is man, but in so far as he is an animal, and which he has in common with the meanest of animals. Moreover, albeit the strength of a human individual should reach its utmost limit, it would not yet equal that of a vigorous mule, and, much less, that of a lion or an elephant. By this perfection, according to that which we have just said[[fn:3980]], one attains at most to the bearing of a heavy burden or the breaking of a hard bone, or the doing of other similar things, in which there is no great utility for the body; as for the advantages for the soul, this species (of perfection) has absolutely none.",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.6": "The third species is, more than the second, a perfection in the essence of man; it is the perfection of the moral qualities, which signifies that the morals of such a man are good in the highest degree. The greater part of the commandments have no other end than to bring us unto this species of perfection. Yet this itself is but a preparation for another perfection, and is not an end in itself. Indeed, all the moral virtues concern only the relations of men one with another, and the moral perfection which a man possesses does but, as it were, dispose him to be useful unto others, so that he becoms thereby an instrument for the service of others. If, for example, thou shouldst suppose a human individual isolated, having naught to do with any man, thou shalt find that all his moral virtues are vain and idle, that he has no need of them, and that they perfect his person in no wise; he has need of them and derivs profit therefrom only in respect of others[[fn:3981]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.7": "The fourth species is the true human perfection; it consists in the acquisition of the intellectual virtues, to wit, in the conception of <i>intelligibles</i> which may afford us sound notions regarding metaphysical subjects. This is the ultimate end (of man), which gives unto the human individual a true perfection; it belongs unto him alone, through it he attains immortality, and by it man is (truly) man. If thou considerest each of the three preceding perfections, thou shalt find that they profit others and not thyself[[fn:3982]], albeit, according to vulgar notions, they necessarily profit thee and others[[fn:3983]]; but this last perfection profits thee alone, and none other shars the benefit thereof with thee: <i>let them be only thine own, etc</i>. (Prov. v. 17)[[fn:3984]]. Wherefore it behoves thee to be desirous of obtaining this thing which remains thine own, and that thou shouldst bestow neither fatigue nor toil upon that which profits others, while neglecting thine own soul, so that its lustre be tarnished by the preponderance of the bodily faculties[[fn:3985]]. It is in this sense that it is said, at the commencement of those poetic allegories composed upon these subjects: <i>My mother's children were angry with me</i>, <i>they made me the keeper of the vineyards; but mine own vineyard</i> <i>have I not kept</i> (Song of Solomon, i. 6). It is in the same sense further said: <i>lest thou give thine honour unto others, and thy years unto the cruel</i> (Proverbs v. 9).",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.8": "The prophets likewise have set forth these same matters unto us and have explained them, even as the philosophers have explained them, by declaring that neither possession, nor health, nor morals, are perfections in which one should glory or which one ought to desire, and that the only perfection which may be the object of our pride and our desires is the knowledge of God, which is the true science. Jeremiah expresses himself thus concerning these four perfections: <i>Thus says the Lord: Let not the wise man glory in his wisdom</i>, <i>neither let the mighty man glory in his might</i>, <i>let not the rich man glory in his riches; but let him that glories glory in this</i>, <i>that he understands and knows me</i> (Jeremiah ix. 22-23). Thou wilt observe how he has enumerated these things according to the degree of value attributed unto them by the vulgar[[fn:3986]]. In effect, that which, for the vulgar, is the greatest perfection, is to be <i>rich;</i> to be <i>strong</i> is a lesser quality, and that which is still less esteemed (by the multitude) is the <i>wise man</i>, that is to say, he who possesses good morals[[fn:3987]]. Nevertheless, the latter likewise is honoured by the vulgar, unto whom the discourse (of the prophet) is addressed. This is why he has enumerated them in this order.",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.9": "The doctors also have understood this verse in the sense of the ideas which we have just presented, and they have clearly stated what I have set before thee in this chapter, namely: that wheresoever mention is made of <i>wisdom</i> (’H<small>OKHMA</small>) par excellence, which is the ultimate end, it signifies the knowledge of God; that the treasures so ardently desired[[fn:3988]] which man possesses, and whereby he thinks to become perfect, are not perfection; and that, finally, all the practices of religion, to wit, the various ceremonies of worship, and likewise the morals which are useful unto men in general in their mutual relations—that all this (I say) is not connected with this ultimate end and has not the same value, but that these are merely preparatory things for the attainment of that end.",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.10": "Hearken unto their very words, whereby they express these ideas; ’tis a passage from <i>Bereschith Rabbâ</i>, in which we read as follows: “One text says (of wisdom): <i>all the things that may be desired are not to be compared to it</i> (Prov. viii. 11), and another text says: <i>all the things thou canst desire are not to be compared unto her</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, iii. 15); by <i>desirable things</i> is meant the practice of the commandments and good works; by <i>things thou canst desire</i>, precious stones and pearls; neither desirable things, nor the things thou canst desire, are equal unto wisdom. But <i>let him that glories glory in this</i>, <i>that he understands and knows me</i> (Jeremiah ix. 25)[[fn:3989]].” Thou wilt observe how concise these words are, and how perfect was their author, having omitted nothing of all that we have said and presented through a long exposition and long preliminaries.",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.11": "Having discussed this verse (from Jeremiah) and the notable ideas it encompasses, and having mentioned what the doctors have said upon this subject, we shall now complete its exposition: In expounding, in this verse, what constitutes the noblest of ends, the prophet did not confine himself merely to (indicating as such) the knowledge of God [for if such had been his intent, he would have said: <i>but in which one may glory</i>, <i>is in understanding and knowing Me</i>, and would have stopped there; or else, he would have said: <i>it is in understanding and knowing Me</i> (<i>knowing</i>) <i>that I am one</i>, or <i>that I have no figure</i>, or <i>that there is none like unto Me</i>, or other similar expressions]; but he said: “in which one may glory, is in comprehending Me and knowing My <i>attributes</i>,” by which he intended the <i>actions</i> of God, as we have expounded concerning those words: <i>Make known to me Thy ways</i>, <i>etc.</i> (Exodus, 33, 13)[[fn:3990]]. He has therefore declared unto us in this verse that these actions, which one must know and take as a model, are ’H<small>ÉSED</small> (benevolence), M<small>ISCHPAT</small> (justice), and C<small>EDAKA</small> (equity).",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.12": "He then subjoins another essential idea, saying: <span dir=\"rtl\">בארץ</span>, <i>upon the earth</i>, and this idea is the pivot of religion. For it is not as the unbelievers[[fn:3991]] maintain, who believe that Divine Providence stopps at the sphere of the moon and that the earth, with all that is found therein, is neglected by God: <i>The Lord has forsaken the earth</i> (Ezekiel, ix. 9)[[fn:3992]]; but on the contrary, even as it has been declared unto us by the Prince of Sages[[fn:3993]], <i>the earth is the Lord's</i> (Exodus, ix. 29), which signifies that the earth also, in certain respects, is the object of Providence, even as is the heaven in other respects[[fn:3994]]. Such is the sense of these words (of Jeremiah): <i>for I am the Lord who exercise loving-kindness</i>, <i>judgment and righteousness</i> <small>UPON THE EARTH</small>. Thereafter, he complets his thought, saying: <i>for in these things I delight</i>, <i>says the Lord</i>, which signifies: “it is my intention that loving-kindness, righteousness, and judgment should emanate from you upon the earth,” according to what we have set forth concerning the <i>thirteen attributes</i> of God[[fn:3995]], namely, that we must have for our aim to imitate them and to regulate our conduct after them. Thus, that which he had for his final purpose to express by this verse, was to declare that the perfection in which man may truly glory, is to have acquired[[fn:3996]], according to his faculty, the knowledge of God and to have recognized His Providence watching over His creatures and revealing itself in the manner in which He produces and governs them[[fn:3997]]. Such a man, after having acquired this knowledge, will ever conduct himself[[fn:3998]] in such wise as to aim at loving-kindness, equity, and justice, by imitating the actions of God, as we have set forth at divers times in this treatise.",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.13": "These are all the questions which I have deemed it necessary to approach in this treatise, and the development of which has appeared to me most profitable for men such as thou art. I trust that, with profound meditation, thou wilt well comprehend all the subjects I have treated therein with the help of God.",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.14": "May He fulfill for us and for all our brethren, the Israelites, that promise which He has made unto us: <i>Then the eyes of the blind shall be opened, and the ears of the deaf shall be unstopped</i> (Isaiah, xxxv. 5); <i>The people that walked in darkness have seen a great light; they that dwell in the land of the shadow of death, upon them has the light shined</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, ix. 1). Amen.",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.15": "God is nigh unto all them that call upon Him in sincerity, without turning aside. He suffers Himself to be found by all those who seek Him, and who walk straight before them without going astray.",
    "root.text.Part 3..53.16": "END OF THE THIRD AND LAST PART OF THE <i>GUIDE FOR THE PERPLEXED</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 3.Introduction.0": "GOD OF THE UNIVERSE <br>PRELIMINARY OBSERVATION.[[fn:2458]] <br>We have already set forth many a time that the principal object we pursued in this treatise was to explain, so far as explanation is possible, the <i>Ma’asé beréschith</i> (the Account of the Creation) and the <i>Ma’asé mercabâ</i> (the Account of the Chariot)[[fn:2459]], out of consideration for those for whom this work was composed[[fn:2460]].",
    "root.text.Part 3.Introduction.1": "We have likewise set forth that these subjects belong to the <i>secrets of the Law;</i> and thou knowest how severely the Sages rebuke him who reveals these secrets, even declaring plainly that he who conceals the secrets of the Law, which are clear and manifest to speculative minds, shall receive a very great reward. At the end of the treatise <i>Pesa’him</i>, in interpreting these words: <i>For her merchandise shall be for them that dwell before the Lord, to eat sufficiently and for durable clothing</i>, <small>WE-LIMEKHASSÉ ATHÎK</small> (Isaiah, xxiii. 18), they say: «And for him who covers (<i>limekhassé</i>) the things revealed by the Ancient (<i>’attik</i>) of Days, to wit, the secrets of the Law[[fn:2461]].» Thou must therefore understand, if thou possessest intelligence, what lesson they intended to impart to us thereby. They have explained elsewhere[[fn:2462]] how profound is the <i>Ma’asé mercabâ</i>, and how inaccessible it is to vulgar minds; and it has been declared that even that which is clear therein to him who has been admitted to understand it[[fn:2463]], is forbidden by religion to be taught or communicated to others, unless it be by word of mouth and to a single individual possessing certain qualities, and even then one shall only impart to him the <i>first elements</i>[[fn:2464]]. This is the reason why this science has entirely vanished from our community, so that not even the slightest trace thereof is to be found[[fn:2465]]. And indeed, so it must needs have been, for it was only transmitted by tradition from one sage to another[[fn:2466]], and was never committed to writing.",
    "root.text.Part 3.Introduction.2": "Since this is so, how shall I proceed to awaken attention to that which has appeared to me well-nigh clear, or to that which is for me of an indubitable evidence, according to what I have understood thereof[[fn:2467]]? Nevertheless, I should have deemed it a great cowardice towards thee and towards every perplexed man, to refrain from writing aught of that which is clear to me, so that my inevitable death would have caused its loss; it would have been, as it were, to rob of the truth him who is worthy of it, or to grudge the heir his inheritance[[fn:2468]], both of which things betoken a blameworthy character.",
    "root.text.Part 3.Introduction.3": "On the other hand, we have already stated that religion forbidds us to expound this subject clearly, to say nothing of what simple judgment imposs upon us[[fn:2469]]. Add to this that what I believe myself to possess thereof is but simple conjecture and a personal opinion[[fn:2470]]. I have had no divine revelation therein which might have made me know that this is indeed what was intended, nor have I learned[[fn:2471]] from any master that which I think of it; but it is the texts of the prophetic books and the discourses of the Sages, as well as those speculative propositions which I possess, that have led me to believe that the matter is indubitably thus. Nevertheless, it is possible that it may be otherwise, and that something quite different was intended.",
    "root.text.Part 3.Introduction.4": "Guided by a right thought and by divine assistance, I have taken in this regard the course of which I shall now speak: I shall give thee concerning the words of Ezekiel an explanation which, when heard by the first comer, might seem to him to add nothing to what the text expresses, as though I were merely translating words from one language into another, or summarizing the literal sense of the discourse; but if it be examined with perfect care by him for whom this treatise was composed and who has well understood every chapter thereof, the whole matter shall be clear to him, even as it is clear and manifest to me, so that naught therein shall any longer be a mystery to him. This is all that it has been possible to do to be useful to every one, whilst refraining from teaching clearly, and as it ought to be done, anything whatsoever concerning this subject.",
    "root.text.Part 3.Introduction.5": "After this preliminary observation, apply thy mind diligently unto those chapters which shall treat of this noble, sublime, and grand subject, which is <i>a peg whereon all is suspended and a pillar whereon all is stayed</i>[[fn:2472]].",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.15.sub_1": "It appears to me, furthermore, that which occasioned these four personages to remain in the most perfect communion with the Almighty, and that which procured for them the constant protection of His Providence, even in those moments when they were engaged in the augmentation of their worldly fortunes, that is to say, during the moments devoted to pastoral pursuits, to agriculture, and to the administration of their families, was that in all these actions they had as their objective the attainment of proximity to the Divine as far as possible; for the principal aim they sought in this life was to bring into being a nation that would know and worship the Almighty: <i>for I have distinguished him, to the end that he may command</i> (Genesis, 18, 19). Hence, it is patent that all their endeavours were directed towards this sole objective, namely, to disseminate throughout the world the belief in the unity of God and to lead mankind to the love of God. Wherefore, they attained to this lofty degree of perfection; for these occupations constituted a grand and veritable form of worship. This lofty degree, a person such as myself cannot pretend to guide men towards its attainment; but the degree previously alluded to, one may strive to attain by means of the exercise of which we have spoken. It is necessary to address to the Almighty our humble supplications, that He may remove the obstacles which separate us from Him, although the greater part of these obstacles arise from ourselves, as we have expounded in diverse chapters of this treatise[[fn:3937]]: <i>Your iniquities have established a separation between you and your God</i> (Isaiah, 59, 2).",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.16": "At present, a most singular reflection has occurred unto me, whereby certain doubts may be assuaged and by which various metaphysical enigmas are revealed. We have heretofore expounded, in the chapters <i>of Providence</i>, that Providence watches over every being endowed with intelligence, in accordance with the measure of its intelligence[[fn:3938]]. Consequently, the individual possessed of perfect perception, whose Intelligence never ceass to occupy itself with the contemplation of God, is ever under the guardianship of Providence; but the individual who, notwithstanding his perfect perception, permits his thought, at certain moments, to be unoccupied with the consideration of God, is under the guardianship of Providence solely during those moments in which his thought is directed towards God, whilst it abandons him during the moments of his preoccupations. Nevertheless, Providence does not abandon him in the manner it abandons one who never directs his thoughts towards God; it merely becoms less effective, owing to the fact that this individual, endowed with perfect perception, does not possess the intellect <i>in actuality</i> during the moments of his preoccupations, and who, in those moments, is intelligent only in <i>proximate potentiality</i>[[fn:3939]], resembling a skilled writer at the moment when he is not engaged in writing. Thus, the individual who never occupies his thoughts with the consideration of God is analogous to one who finds himself in darkness and who has never beheld the light, as we have expounded upon the words: <i>And the wicked perish in the darkness</i> (I Samuel, II, 9)[[fn:3940]]; the individual who perceivs God and dedicats his entire being to the consideration of this object of his thought is similar to one who finds himself surrounded by the radiance of the sun. Finally, the individual who thinks, yet is preoccupied, resembls, during the moments of his preoccupations, one who finds himself on a day of mist and who does not receive the rays of the sun owing to the clouds that intercept the light.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.17": "It is for this reason that it appears unto me, that all those amongst the prophets, or amongst the pious and perfect men, who were afflicted with one of the evils of this world, were thus afflicted only in a moment in which they forgot God, and that the magnitude of the misfortune was in proportion to the duration of this forgetfulness, or to the unworthiness of the thing in which they were so preoccupied. If [[fn:3941]] it were indeed thus, this would resolve the great difficulty which has led philosophers to deny that Divine Providence watches over each individual man, and to assimilate, in this respect, human individuals to those of other species of animals; for the proof which they allege in support of this notion, is that pious and virtuous men are sometimes afflicted with great misfortunes. The mystery which lies beneath this, would thus be clarified, even according to the opinions of philosophers; for Divine Providence would perpetually watch over the man who is favoured with that <i>outpouring of the Divine</i>, which is bestowed upon all those who labour to attain it[[fn:3942]]. For, when the thought of man is perfectly pure, when he perceivs God, by employing the true means, and when he enjoys that which he perceivs, it is not possible for any kind of evil to ever afflict this man; for he is with God, and God is with him. But, when he turns away from God, and in some sort withdraws himself from His gaze, God withdraws Himself from him, and he then remains exposed to all the evils which may, by accident, come upon him; for that which calls forth Providence, and savs from the floods of chance, is that outpouring of the Divine Intelligence, which has, for a certain time, withdrawn itself from such a pious and virtuous man, or which has never come unto another, who is vicious and wicked, and it is for this reason that they have both been afflicted by the blows of chance.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.18": "This conviction, I daresay, is further corroborated by the textual narrative of the Pentateuch, in which the Almighty has spoken thus: <i>I shall hide My countenance from them; he</i> (the people) <i>shall be devoured, and numerous evils and calamities shall befall him, and he shall say on that day: Is it not because the Lord is not with me that these misfortunes have befallen me</i> (Deut., 31, 17)? It is patently evident, my dear fellow, that if <i>He hids His face</i>, we ourselves are the causa causans, and that this veil (which serves to conceal Him from us) is the direct result of our own actions, as it is written: <i>And I will hide My face on that day, because of all the evil which he has done</i> (<i>ibid., v.</i> 18). There can be no doubt, nay, 'tis an absolute certainty, that that which is stated concerning the community applies equally to an individual; hence, it is crystal clear that if a human being is abandoned to chance and exposed to being devoured like the beasts of the field, the reason for this state of affairs is that he is separated from the Divine Presence by a veil. However, if <i>his God is with him</i>, no evil can befall him[[fn:3943]], as it is written: <i>Fear not, for I am with thee</i>[[fn:3944]], <i>be not dismayed, for I am thy God</i> (Isaiah, 41, 10), and as it is written again: <i>When thou walkest through the waters, I will be with thee; the rivers, they shall not overflow thee</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, XLIII, 2), in which the sense is: \"when thou walkest through the waters, accompanied by Me, the rivers shall not carry thee away[[fn:3945]].\" Indeed, whosoever has rendered himself worthy of receiving the effusion of this Intelligence[[fn:3946]] finds himself under the guardianship of Providence and secure from all evils, as it is written: <i>The Lord is with me, I fear not; what can man do unto me</i> (Ps., 118, 6)? and as it is written again: <i>Trust in Him and thou shalt be at peace</i> (Job, 22, 21), that is to say: turn thyself towards Him and thou shalt be preserved from all evil.",
    "root.text.Part 3..50.19": "If thou dost peruse the <i>Canticle of Misfortunes</i>[[fn:3947]], thou shalt discover that it treats of that sublime Providence which watchfully presids over mankind and preservs it from all corporeal calamities[[fn:3948]], both those that are general and those that are particular to diverse individuals (in such a manner that no calamity befallenth them), neither those that are inherent to the nature of being in general, nor those that emanate from the malice of men. Behold, what is said therein: <i>For He shall deliver thee from the snare of the fowler, from the noisome pestilence</i>. He shall cover thee with His feathers, and thou shalt take refuge under His wings; His truth shall be thy shield and buckler. Thou shalt not be afraid of the terror by night, nor of the arrow that flies by day, nor of the pestilence that walks in darkness, nor of the destruction that wasts at noonday</i> (Ps., 91, 3-7). Speaking thereafter of the protection (of God) against the malice of men, it is said: If it were to befall thee, whilst thou art journeying, to pass through a vast battlefield, in such a manner that a thousand dead were to fall on thy left and ten thousand on thy right, no evil would befall thee; but thou wouldst behold and observe with thine eyes how God has judged and punished these wicked men whom death has smitten, whilst thou hast been preserved. Such is the import of these words: <i>A thousand shall fall at thy side, and ten thousand at thy right hand, but the evil shall not come nigh thee</i> (<i>ibid., v.</i> 7 and 8). After having subsequently expounded in detail how the righteous man is preserved, it indicats the reason for this lofty protection, and says what is the cause whereby the sublime Providence watchfully presids over this man: <i>Because he has set his passion</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חשק</span>) <i>upon Me, I will deliver him; I will set him on high, because he has known My name</i> (<i>ibid., v.</i> 14). — We have already expounded in preceding chapters that <i>to know the name of God</i> signifies to comprehend God; he (the Psalmist) says, therefore, in a manner of speaking: If such a man enjoys this protection, it is because he has learned to know Me and has thereafter set his passion upon Me. Thou art also acquainted with the difference that exists between <span dir=\"rtl\">אוהב</span> (loving) and <span dir=\"rtl\">חושק</span> (being passionate); for the love that is borne to such an excess that one cannot think of anything else but the beloved object, that is <i>passion</i>.",
    "root.text.Part 2..18.15": "That which renders yet more evident the existence of <i>determination</i>[[fn:1743]] in the celestial sphere, so that no man could find for it any other <i>determining</i> cause than the design of a being acting with intention, is the manner of existence of the stars. In effect, the sphere being always in motion and the star remaining always fixed[[fn:1744]], this proves that the matter of the stars is not the same as that of the spheres. Already Abu Nasr (Al-Farabi), in his glosses upon the <i>Acroasis</i>, has expressed himself in the following terms: «Between the sphere and the stars there is a difference; for the sphere is diaphanous, whilst the stars are not so. The cause thereof is that there is between the two matters and between the two forms a difference, though it be <i>small</i>.» Such are his expressions. I, however, do not say <i>small</i>, but (I say) that they differ <i>greatly</i>; for I derive the proof thereof, not from the transparency, but from the motions. It is therefore clear to me that there are three matters and three forms: 1st, bodies which, in themselves, are always at rest, and these are the bodies of the stars; 2nd, bodies which are always in motion, and these are the bodies of the spheres; 3rd, bodies which sometimes move and sometimes are at rest, and these are the elements. Now, I should like to know what could have joined together these two matters[[fn:1745]], — between which there is an extreme difference, as it seems to me, or (at the very least) a small difference[[fn:1746]], as Abu Nasr says, — and who is he that has prepared this union? In short, two diverse bodies, of which one is fixed in the other, without being mingled therein, and being, on the contrary, circumscribed in a particular place of the latter and strongly attached, (all this) without the design of a being acting with intention, would be an astonishing thing[[fn:1747]]. But, what is yet more astonishing, are those numerous stars which are found in the eighth (sphere), all of them globes, some small, others large, here a star, there another [apparently at the distance of a cubit], here ten (stars) clustered together, there a great space with nothing. What then is the cause which particularly distinguishes this space by ten stars and that other by the lack of stars? Finally, the body of the sphere is a single simple body, without diversity; for what cause, then, does such a part of the sphere suit the star located therein, rather than another? All this, like all things of the same kind, would be most improbable, or rather would border upon the impossible, were it admitted that all proceeds from God <i>by necessity</i>, as Aristotle supposs.",
    "root.text.Part 3..18.2": "But a prophet (Asaph) tells us that after having long reflected upon this subject, he understood that things must be considered by their final issue, and not by their beginning. Behold how he depicts the series of his reflections: <i>They say: How does God know? And is there knowledge in the Most High? Behold, these are the ungodly, who prosper in the world; they increase in riches. Verily I have cleansed my heart in vain, and washed my hands in innocency</i> (Ps. lxxiii. 11-13). Then he says: <i>When I thought to know this, it was too painful for me; until I went into the sanctuary of God; then understood I their end. Surely Thou didst set them in slippery places, etc. How are they brought into desolation, as in a moment! etc.</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v</i>. 16-19). Malachi makes precisely the same reflections: <i>Your words have been stout against Me, etc. Ye have said, It is vain to serve God: and what profit is it that we have kept His ordinance, and that we have walked mournfully before the Lord of hosts? And now we call the proud happy, etc. Then they that feared the Lord spake often one to another, etc. Then shall ye return, and discern, etc.</i> (Malachi, iii. 13-18). David also speaks of this opinion[[fn:2890]] widespread in his time, and which necessarily resulted in injustice and the reciprocal violence of men[[fn:2891]]. He seeks to produce arguments to destroy this opinion and to establish that God has knowledge of all this: <i>They slay</i>, says he, <i>the widow and the stranger, and murder the fatherless. Yet they say, The Lord shall not see, neither shall the God of Jacob regard it. Understand, ye brutish among the people: and ye fools, when will ye be wise? He that planted the ear, shall He not hear? He that formed the eye, shall He not see?</i> (Ps. xciv. 6-9). I shall explain to thee the sense of this last argument, after having first shown thee how little those who assail with their attacks the words of the prophets have understood these words (of David). Years ago, some physicians, men of wit of our own community, expressed to me their astonishment at these words of David. From his reasoning, said they, it would follow that He who created the mouth eats, that He who created the lungs cries out, and the same would hold for the other organs. But thou shalt see, O reader of this treatise, how far these persons were from understanding the import of this argument; harken to its meaning: It is clear that he who fashions any instrument whatever, if he possessed not the idea of the work which that instrument is to serve, would find it impossible to fashion an instrument for that use. If, for example, the smith did not form a just idea of sewing, he could not fashion the needle in a form which alone can make it serve for sewing, and the same is true of other instruments; for, as certain philosophers believed that God perceivs not individual things, which are things perceived by the senses, whilst God perceivs not by a sense, but by an intelligible perception, he (David) argus against them from the existence of the senses. If, says he, the manner in which the eye perceivs were for God a mystery which He were incapable of knowing, how could He have produced this organ, destined for visual perception? Was it pure chance that caused a limpid humor to be born, and then another similar humor, then a membrane which chance alone likewise would have perforated, and that finally before the opening there should be placed a transparent and hard membrane[[fn:2892]]? In short, can an intelligent man imagine that the humors, the membranes, and the nerves of the eye, which are so wisely organized[[fn:2893]] and whose whole has for its end this visual action, are a simple effect of chance? Assuredly not, and there is therein necessarily an <i>intention</i> of nature, as all physicians and all philosophers have declared. Now, nature has neither intelligence, nor (consequently) organizing faculty[[fn:2894]], whereupon the philosophers are agreed; but this artistic organization[[fn:2895]] emanats, according to the opinion of the philosophers, from an intellectual principle, and, according to us, it is the work of an intelligent Being who has impressed such faculties upon all that possesses a natural faculty. If, therefore, this Intelligence perceived not the object in question and knew it not, how, in that case, could it have produced or caused to emanate from itself a nature tending toward a goal which it knew not? It is therefore with reason that he (David) calls these men <i>brutish</i> and <i>fools.</i> Thereafter he setts forth that this is a defect of our perception. God (says he), who has given us this Intelligence by which we perceive, whilst our incapacity to grasp His true essence gives rise within us to these grave doubts, God knows this defect which exists in us, and we must not take account of the attacks which are the result of the weakness of our reflection[[fn:2896]]: <i>He</i>, says he, <i>that teaches man knowledge</i>, the <i>Lord, knows the thoughts of man, that they are vanity</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v</i>. 10-11).",
    "root.text.Part 3..46.3": "All this, therefore, contributed to the end that one should keep far from the sanctuary and not enter therein at every instant. Thou knowest, moreover, what the doctors say: “No person, were he even pure, shall enter the court for the service until he has bathed[[fn:3732]].” These acts, then, maintained reverence and served to produce the impression which was to lead to the piety that was the aim.",
    "root.text.Munk's Introduction.0": "The work of Maimonides that is the subject of this publication is sufficiently well-known and appreciated. It is understood what importance it holds for biblical exegesis and theology, and how rich it is in information regarding the history of medieval philosophy, particularly among the Arabs. None of the questions that interest the theologian are passed over in silence; and, although the solutions to these questions are generally based on allegorical exegesis and a metaphysics that has had its time, they often offer us glimpses of light that can still guide our research today and serve as a beacon to penetrate the elevated regions that, for the human mind, are shrouded in mystery. As the true founder of a rational theology, in which philosophical thought maintains all its rights, Maimonides has exercised a decisive influence on his co-religionists, the consequences of which are still felt today; and the principles he laid down, eagerly embraced by some and passionately rejected by others, have given rise to a struggle in which human reason has emerged victorious, after having brought about a reconciliation between faith and thought that the great genius of Maimonides, anticipating the centuries, had set as the noblest goal of all his efforts. But the high reputation of this illustrious man was not confined to the synagogue, and this spirit of such incontestable superiority found appreciators among the elite men of all confessions. Muslim scholars did not disdain to read the Guide, and one of them even wrote a commentary on certain parts of this work, which we still possess and in which he lavishes praise on the author. In Egypt, where Maimonides lived, Coptic theologians studied his work and made it accessible to their community by having copies made in Arabic characters. It must have been known early on",
    "root.text.Munk's Introduction.1": "It is known in Europe through a Latin version; for it is often cited by the scholastics, and men such as Saint Thomas and Albert the Great invoke its authority. The fame enjoyed by Maimonides' work shows that there is more to it than just Jewish theology; and, indeed, although this theology was the author's primary goal, he dealt with a wide range of subjects, which he used to support his system, and his work, based on extensive reading and solid erudition, abounds in information of all kinds that would be sought in vain elsewhere. Without mentioning his exposition of the Arab Peripatetic system, drawn from the works of Ibn Sina, we must particularly highlight the precious details that the author provides at the end of the first part regarding the system of the Motécallemin or Muslim scholastics, and the curious information that, in the second part, he provides about the books of the Sabians, which are still so little known today and whose Arabic translation exists only very incompletely in a few European libraries. I must not anticipate here what I will have to say later in the Prolegomena that I intend to publish, regarding the era of Maimonides, the life of this illustrious man, his works, the manuscripts that exist, and the editions that have been published; but I wanted to draw attention to the great importance of his Guide for the Perplexed and the general interest that this work offers, which deserves, for so many reasons, to occupy one of the first places among the monuments of Arabic literature, and which, however, has not been the subject of any critical work by orientalists until now. While our century has seen the publication of so many other Arabic works, far less worthy of occupying minds, the original Arabic of the Guide has remained buried in a few libraries; the Hebrew version by Samuel ibn Tibbon has been the only one published in several editions, generally very faulty. It is on this version that two Latin translations have been made, one of which, attributed to the Jewish physician Jacob Mantino and published in Paris in 1520, is almost unknown, and the other, attributed to Jean Buxtorf the son (Basel, 1629, in-4), has been the only one to make Maimonides' masterpiece accessible to the European reader until now. It will be easily understood that Buxtorf's work, whatever its merit, cannot meet the requirements of criticism. Ibn Tibbon's version, which can be called a true copy of the original Arabic, can only be fully understood by one who possesses",
    "root.text.Munk's Introduction.2": "PREFACE. At the same time, knowledge of Arabic and that of rabbinic Hebrew, and who has acquired sufficient notions of Muslim philosophy and its terminology. Buxtorf, an excellent Hebraist, was completely foreign to the language and literature of the Arabs and lacked all the indispensable resources for the interpreter of a work of this nature. Thus, his translation, if it allows the reader to grasp the entirety of Maimonides' work, leaves much to be desired in the details, disfigured by numerous misinterpretations. Moreover, it is not accompanied by any commentary that could aid the reader in understanding the many details that were easily grasped by the author's contemporaries but are full of obscurity for us. Since Buxtorf, the Guide has been the subject of no work, except for a few modern commentaries written in Hebrew by authors who were far beneath such a task, and an edition of the second part of Ibn-Tibbon's version, accompanied by a German translation and explanatory notes, by M. Simon Scheyer (Frankfurt am Main, 1838, in-8). This partial publication, for which the Arabic text was consulted, is the only one that offers a truly scientific character and responds to some extent to the needs of the European reader. For more than twenty years, I had formed the project of publishing the Arabic text of the Guide, of accompanying it with a French translation, and of adding more or less developed annotations, which could provide the reader with all the necessary clarifications for understanding the text and by which he could be enabled to place himself always from the author's point of view and to judge him with perfect intelligence of the era that saw the birth of this work and the studies that served as its basis. I announced this project, as early as 1833, in my Reflections on the worship of the ancient Hebrews, p. 80 (t. IV of M. Cahen's Bible), where I gave as a specimen two chapters of the second part; but I found in Paris only incomplete manuscripts of the first and second parts of the Arabic text. Later, I was put in possession of a copy of the Hebrew version of the Venice edition, which had in the margin a partial copy of the Arabic text, notably of the first part, from chapter XXXIII to chapter LXXI. A trip I made to Oxford in 1835 allowed me to collate this fragment with six manuscripts from the Bodleian Library and to complete the first part; but I could not prolong-",
    "root.text.Munk's Introduction.3": "PREFACE. During my stay at Oxford, I was able to devote all the necessary care to this work and definitively establish all parts of my text. It was the liberality of the curators of the Leyden Library that generously came to my aid to complete my critical work on the Arabic text of the Guide. The late Mr. Weyers, whose premature loss is lamented by Oriental scholars and whose memory will be preserved with pious respect, spontaneously offered to put at my disposal the two manuscripts of the aforementioned library, which were sent to me in Paris. His learned successor, Professor Juynboll, was kind enough to continue this remarkable favour, without which it would have been impossible for me to execute my work with the meticulous exactness that the importance of the subject made a sacred duty for me. In making this acknowledgement, I feel the need to add an expression of the deepest gratitude. Finally, having obtained the most indispensable materials, I found myself hindered by the numerous material difficulties that oppose an undertaking of this importance. Unfortunately, I was only able to contemplate the execution of the work at the very moment when Providence subjected me to the most severe trial that could paralyse the efforts of a writer for whom the most meticulous readings and research are at once a need and an imperative duty. The total loss of sight seemed to render impossible the continuation of the work to which I had already devoted so many sleepless nights and from which it was hard for me to renounce forever. But, having recovered from my initial despondency and supported by the encouragement of friendship and generous protection, I saw in the new difficulties that I would have to overcome a diversion from the pain, and I hoped that, through perseverance, I could create a consolation for myself from the few literary fragments saved from the shipwreck. I must now render to the reader an account of the first fruits of my efforts, which I am permitted to present to him today. This first volume contains the first part of the Guide, which is entirely devoted to preliminary questions and is intended to prepare the reader for the solution of the lofty questions of theology and philosophy treated in the other two parts. The author first explains a certain number of homonymous words found in the Holy Scripture, sets forth their various meanings, and particularly insists on the figurative sense that must be attributed to them when they are applied to God.",
    "root.text.Munk's Introduction.4": "PREFACE. The author intermingles general observations on theological studies and the manner in which one ought to prepare oneself for them. Subsequently, he addresses the question of divine attributes, demonstrating that all manner of attributes must be removed from God, and he expounds upon the sense in which the various attributive terms employed by sacred writers and philosophers ought to be understood. Finally, he summarises the system of Muslim theologians, which was partly adopted by the Jewish theologians of the East, and, illustrating the paradoxical and arbitrary nature of this system, he reveals its impotence in providing a rational foundation for the most important dogmas of religion, particularly with regards to demonstrating the existence, unity, and incorporeality of God, as well as creation ex nihilo. It has been observed what resources were at my disposal for the publication of the Arabic text of this part. I had it printed in Hebrew characters: firstly, because the author himself, like all Jewish Arabic writers, employed the Hebrew script in writings related to Judaism, and this script has been preserved in all the manuscripts that remain to us of the Guide; secondly, to avoid the numerous typographical difficulties and the unappealing appearance presented by an Arabic text often bristling with Hebrew citations. The change in script cannot present any serious difficulty to the Arabist, provided that he has familiarised himself with the mode of transcription, which is all the more simple and natural given that the Hebrew and Arabic letters, belonging to languages of the same root, correspond exactly to one another and can easily be substituted for one another. Indeed, the conventional orthography of the manuscripts, which I have scrupulously preserved, presents nothing that could hinder the Arabist, except for the substitution of the punctuated càph (îp) for the khà (^) and the guimel without a point (3) for the ghaïn (p) (i), a substitution also commonly used by the Christians of Syria, who write Arabic in Syriac characters. I have merely followed the example set by Pococke in his Porta Mosis, and by Schnurrer in the extracts he published from several Jewish Arabic authors. Nevertheless, to avoid presenting the less practised reader with certain difficulties found in the publications of these two scholars, I deemed it necessary to introduce, with regards to grammatical forms, ",
    "root.text.Munk's Introduction.5": "VI PREFACE. notably the desinences, a more regular orthography, by avoiding the irregularities and inconsistencies that most Jewish Arabic manuscripts and often even those of Muslims present. Thus, for example, I have often added the accusative termination, which is almost always lacking in manuscripts, and at other times I had to suppress this same termination that ignorant copyists often placed at random; in words derived from roots whose third radical is a weak letter, I have suppressed this letter whenever the grammatical rules required it, writing, for instance, ])îé (^) for ••y-IÛ, I^DO for \"^I^DD, and I have made the same suppression in the future apocopes, adding Arabic vowels, which I have often used for clarity and which are also found here and there in the manuscripts of Jewish Arabs and Syrian Christians; in the hamzated verbs, which the copyists, in accordance with the usage of vulgar Arabic, frequently confuse with the verbs with quiescent letters, I have restored the hamzated letter, and I have written, for example, pnni\"' for iirni\"» (fol. 17 6), ^vin^ for pinS^ (fol. 97 6) (i). Nevertheless, I have preserved in my text a few slight inaccuracies that evidently emanate from the author himself and which may be considered as anomalies of the local dialect; thus, I have always written \"•ît^, i<:x, although the more correct orthography is '^iîi^ (^')ï Ï<i3t< (^L')• The imperative n^< (for j or Tij) falls into the same category (2). — As for the variants of the different manuscripts, I have limited myself to indicating, in the notes accompanying the translation, all those that may offer some interest for the critique of the text or for the interpretation of certain passages; I have accepted in my edition the reading that seemed to me the best, and my choice has been guided by a conscientious examination of both the original text and the Hebrew versions. In the French translation, I have aimed above all at strict fidelity, without however allowing myself to be led into a servile literalness that could have harmed clarity. I have sought, as far as possible, to enable the reader, by means of my translation, to understand each word of the Arabic text, and.",
    "root.text.Munk's Introduction.6": "PREFACE. When the genius of the language absolutely refused the fidelity that I had imposed upon myself, I have given in a note the word for word of the original (*). It is all too easy, when it comes to the interpretation of profound and obscure passages, to conceal one's ignorance behind a vague translation with elegant turns of phrase, and it seemed to me that, in a work of the nature of this one, elegance must give way to the most precise expression of the author's thought. I had to make the greatest efforts to unite fidelity with clarity and to compensate for certain negligences of style that can be reproached in our author, as in all Arab writers in general, and I do not disguise from myself that, if on the one hand my translation can fully satisfy the need of one who seeks in it an aid for the study of the original text or of the Hebrew version of Ibn-Tibbon, on the other hand it will often require the indulgence of the French reader who neither wants nor can have recourse to the text, and who, uninterested in the philological part of my work, seeks only to become familiar in an easy manner with the subjects treated in this work. I must add, however, that as I advanced in my translation and could suppose the Arabist reader to be sufficiently familiar with certain turns of phrase of the original, I sought more and more to consider those who will read only the translation, and, without ceasing to be faithful, I departed a little from that literalness that will be particularly noticeable in the introduction and the first chapters. I have accompanied the translation with annotations, more or less numerous, more or less developed, according to what seemed to me to be required by the subjects treated in the various chapters and the terms more or less explicitly employed by the author. The latter, as he often declares, addressed himself to readers versed in the different branches of theological studies and in the relative literature, as well as in the philosophy of the time; the different classes of readers for whom this translation is intended will therefore necessarily require, in order to understand it in all its parts, information (1) Sometimes, to render the phrase clearer, I have added between ( ) explanatory words that are not found in the text; the parentheses of the text have been indicated by [ ].",
    "root.text.Munk's Introduction.7": "VII PREFACE. varied which may, as far as possible, enable them to place themselves at the author's point of view and follow him everywhere in his reasoning. To some, it was necessary to explain what is particularly within the domain of biblical exegesis and Jewish theology; to others, all that supposes a notion more than superficial of Aristotelian philosophy in the form it had taken in the Arab schools through the works of Al-Farabi and Ibn-Sina. Often it was necessary to recall certain opinions of Jewish theologians prior to the author, or even to quote in extenso unpublished passages. I believed it necessary to provide all literary and historical information that the reader could not easily find elsewhere, and which is indispensable for the prompt understanding of certain passages; therefore, I think that even in my most extensive notes, there will be no unnecessary digressions. Where it was sufficient to provide simple indications, I was sparing of textual citations. I have indicated, as far as possible, all passages, whether from the Talmud and the Midrashim, or from Aristotle and a few other authors, to which Maimonides alludes; these indications, of incontestable utility, have generally been neglected by commentators. A certain number of critical notes are devoted to the variants of the Arabic text and the divergences offered by the two Hebrew versions. With a view to those who will use my translation to study the Hebrew version of Ibn-Tibbon, I believed it necessary to rectify, with the help of manuscripts, the main errors that disfigure the editions of this version. One will read with interest several notes that Ibn-Tibbon added to his version, as well as a few others emanating from the author himself (ch. LXXIV), and which until now had remained unpublished. In short, I have desired to make (1) The importance I believed I had to attach to these indications did not allow me to shrink from the difficulties that my current situation opposes to such a task, and I have not hesitated in all my research, often taking some vague memories as a starting point, to undertake long readings to reach the desired goal. There are, I think, in this first volume, only three citations for which I could not indicate the location: page 14, a passage from Midrash or Haggadah (Exposing the power, etc.), which is also cited by R. Moses ben-Nachman, in his Commentary on Genesis, but which may no longer exist in our Midrashim; page 107, a passage from Alexander of Aphrodisias, which I did not have at my disposal; page 381, words attributed by the author to Galen and related to time.",
    "root.text.Munk's Introduction.8": "PREFACE. ix so that this work could serve as a repository for those seeking information on Jewish theology and Arabic philosophy; to the details given in the notes, I hope to add a more systematic exposition that I reserve for the Prolegomena. I must conclude this preface without expressing my deep gratitude to those who have kindly assisted me in the accomplishment of my difficult task. I shall not attempt to express all the sentiments that the warm devotion of a tried friend inspires in me, who has more than once supported my flagging courage. Mr. Albert Cohn has not only hastened the material execution of this publication through his zeal, but his profound knowledge of the Arabic language has also enabled him to actively cooperate in the revision of the text proofs and to spare me errors that could have escaped my notice during reading. Another friend, Rabbi Trénel, has kindly collated my translation with the Hebrew version of Ibn-Tibbon, and has often had occasion to draw my attention to the errors committed by the editors that needed to be rectified in the notes. Finally, a modest scholar, Mr. Clément-Mullet, who applies his solid knowledge of Oriental languages to important research related to the history of sciences, has assisted me, with devoted friendship, in the revision of the translation proofs and in the verification of numerous citations made in the notes. I must not forget those who, with unwavering devotion and patience, have written under my dictation and have made the long and laborious readings that my research required. I pay here a painful tribute of regrets to the memory of the young and studious Isidor Stillmann, who assisted me for the greater part of this volume, and who, suddenly taken away by premature death, has left me a successor equally devoted in his friend Mr. Joseph Mistowski, with whose help I have been able to continue and complete this part. I have had to struggle against very great difficulties in undertaking a task that may be beyond my strength, and I feel that I must arm myself with courage and patience to accomplish the mission that I have given myself, consulting rather my zeal than my physical and intellectual capacities. The gravity of this mission and the duties that the memory of the illustrious doctor imposes upon me, whose interpretation I dare to attempt,",
    "root.text.Munk's Introduction.9": "IX PREFACE. Thought does not permit me to abuse the rights that I may have to the indulgence of the learned world. It is my duty to invite serious and impartial criticism from competent men, and I shall welcome with sincere gratitude the critical observations and advice that will be addressed to me in public journals or that will be kindly sent to me directly. I shall be happy to profit from all warnings to rectify the errors I may have committed and to avoid new ones; and if this first part of my work can deserve, on some points, the approval of scholars, it will be for me a powerful encouragement to persevere, with the aid of Providence, in the long and difficult career that remains for me to pursue. S. MUNK. Paris, April 1856.",
    "root.text.Munk's Introduction.10": "TABLE OF CHAPTERS."
