  "footnotes": {
    "fn.0": "We have translated here the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אל עולם</span> in the sense that Maimonides himself lends to them in several places, and notably in chapter 29 of the third part of the <i>Guide</i>, although in the biblical passage (Genesis, 21, 33) these words mean <i>the Eternal God</i>.",
    "fn.1": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">כנת</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(كُنْتَ)</span> that begins the sentence relates to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">עטׄם שאנך</span>, which it serves to put in the pluperfect.",
    "fn.2": "See my aforementioned notice on Joseph ben Judah. We will return to this disciple of Maimonides in the general introduction to this work.—The letters <span dir=\"rtl\">ש״צ</span>, which follow the name of Joseph, are the abbreviation of <span dir=\"rtl\">שְׁמָרָהוּ צוּרוֹ</span>, the Hebrew preterite being used, in the Arabic manner, in the sense of the optative.",
    "fn.3": "Literally: <i>of the ends of the countries</i>. Joseph had come from Sabta or Seuta, located in the northwest of Africa, which the Arabs called <i>Al-Maghreb al-Akça</i> or the farthest West. See the aforementioned Notice, pages 6 and 14.",
    "fn.4": "The <i>Makâmât</i> among the Arabs are short stories or tales written in rhyming prose interspersed with verse; the famous <i>Makâmât</i> or <i>Séances</i> of Harizi are well-known. This genre of composition was imitated in Hebrew by the Jews as early as the first half of the 12<sup>th</sup> century, and they were given the name of <span dir=\"rtl\">מחברות</span>; it is indeed this word that R. Iehouda al-Harizi uses here in his translation, which renders the meaning of the Arabic word much better than the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חרוזיך</span> used by Ibn Tibbon. Al-Harizi, in his collection of Makâmât known under the title of <i>Ta’hkemôni</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תחכמוני</span>), himself cites one of our Joseph's <i>Makâma</i> that had become famous. See my aforementioned Notice, p. 48 and following. [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.5": "Several manuscripts read <span dir=\"rtl\">במאלך</span>, others <span dir=\"rtl\">במא לך</span>, and still others <span dir=\"rtl\">כמאלך</span>; it is the latter reading that appears to be expressed by Al-’Harizi's translation, which bears <span dir=\"rtl\">שכלך השלם</span>. We must undoubtedly read <span dir=\"rtl\">במאלך</span> (from <span dir=\"rtl\">مآل</span>), which Ibn-Tibbon has accurately rendered as <span dir=\"rtl\">אחריתך</span>. I am unaware of where Hyde obtained the reading of <span dir=\"rtl\">עקלך</span>, which he claims to have altered to <span dir=\"rtl\">עקבך</span>. (See Hyde's <i>Syntagma dissertationum</i>, published by Sharpe, vol. I, p. 436.) This reading is not found in any of the Oxford manuscripts, nor in the two Leyden manuscripts [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.6": "This name designates the religious philosophers or the scholastics of the Arabs. On its origin, see my Notice on Rabbi Saadia Gaon, p. 16 and following (Bible of M. Cahen, t. IX, p. 88 and following). The author himself provides numerous details on the <i>Motécallemîn</i> in several places in this work, and notably in the <sup>first</sup> part, ch. 71, 73 and following. I have given a brief overview of their doctrines in the <i>Dictionnaire des sciences philosophiques</i>, article <i>Arabes</i>.",
    "fn.7": "All manuscripts, even those with diacritical points, bear <span dir=\"rtl\">בדתך</span>; I believe one must pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">بِذَٰلِكَ</span>, from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">بَنّ</span> filaments <i>prœvertit, vicit</i>, such that the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדהשה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">ברח</span> mean: <i>trouble</i> or <i>confusion had taken hold of you</i>. Ibn-Tibbon has correctly rendered the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בלחך</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">דפקתך</span>.",
    "fn.8": "These last words, which the author wrote in Hebrew, are taken from Ecclesiastes, ch. 12, <small>V</small>. 10.",
    "fn.9": "By <i>names</i> the author means here not only substantives and adjectives, but also verbs in the infinitive or <i>names of action</i>.",
    "fn.10": "The common <i>noun</i> or <i>appellative</i> is called by the Arabs <span dir=\"rtl\">الاسم المتواطئ</span> , or <span dir=\"rtl\">الاسم المقول بتواطؤ</span> , that is to say <i>conventional name</i> or <i>said by convention</i>. The rabbis translated these terms as <span dir=\"rtl\">השם המוסכם</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">השם הנאמר בהסכמה</span>; one can see the definition in Maimonides' <i>Compendium of Logic</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">באור מלות ההגיון</span>), ch. 13. The Arabic term <span dir=\"rtl\">بتواطؤ</span> corresponds to the Greek words <i>κατὰ συνθήκην</i>, and is borrowed from the Arabic translation of Aristotle's <i>Organon</i>; see the treatise <i>On Interpretation</i>, at the beginning of ch. II, where the words: <i>Ὄνομα μὲν οὖν ὲστι φωνὴ σημαντικὴ κατὰ συνθὴκην</i> are thus rendered in Arabic: <span dir=\"rtl\">فالاسم هو لفظة دالة بتواطؤ</span> (Arabic Ms. of the National Library, n° 882 A).",
    "fn.11": "The word <i>man</i>, for example, can apply to a living man, as well as to a dead man, or to a statue, or to a portrait. If one considers only the external form, the word <i>man</i>, taken in these various senses, can be regarded as a true appellative, or common noun, designating everything that externally presents the human form; but if one considers what constitutes the true essence of man, the word <i>man</i>, taken in the various senses indicated, will be a homonym, since it designates things of diverse nature. From this point of view, the word <i>man</i> is called <span dir=\"rtl\">اسم مشكك</span>, <i>ambiguous name</i> or <i>amphibological</i>. See Maimonides, <i>locus citatus</i>, and the present work, I<sup>st</sup> part, ch. 56 (fol. 68 <i>b</i> of our Arabic text).",
    "fn.12": "By <span dir=\"rtl\">פקה</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">فقه</span> the Arabs understand what we call canon law and, in general, all legislation related to the Koran and traditions, including all religious ceremonies and prescriptions. Arab Jews, both Karaite and Rabbanite, also use this word to designate the body of doctrines and practices based on tradition, and Arab rabbis apply this word to the Oral Law or the Talmud. Thus, translators generally render the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">פקה</span> as <span dir=\"rtl\">תלמוד</span>, and it is this word that Ibn-Tibbon has employed here.",
    "fn.13": "The author intends to refer to other works that he himself had composed on this subject, such as certain parts of his commentary on the <i>Mischnâ</i> and the initial treatises of his <i>Mischné Torâ</i> or Compendium of the Talmud.",
    "fn.14": "That is to say, what he has understood in a superficial manner, by adhering solely to the literal meaning, which will lead him to corporify the divinity and to admit other absurdities. [[t:1]] [[fn:2]]",
    "fn.15": "Literally: <i>for the one with whom he would converse</i>.",
    "fn.16": "See above, page 7, notes 1 and 2.",
    "fn.17": "The Talmud speaks of profound mysteries contained in the 1<sup>st</sup> chapter of Genesis, beginning with the word <i>beréschîth</i>, in the 1<sup>st</sup> chapter of Ezekiel and in several passages of Isaiah and Zechariah. The visions of these prophets, and particularly those of Ezekiel, are designated by the name of <i>Ma’asé mercabâ</i>, or <i>account of the chariot</i> (<i>celestial</i>). The mysteries of <i>Beréschîth</i> and of the <i>Mercabâ</i> play a major role in the writings of the Kabbalists, who are in little agreement as to what is to be understood by these mysteries, the Talmud not having expressed itself on this point. Maimonides, who was not a partisan of the Kabbalah, nevertheless speaks, as a Talmudist, of the <i>Ma’asé beréschîth</i> and of the <i>Ma’asé mercabâ</i>, and, imbued with the philosophical doctrines of the Arabic schools, he explains one and the other in such a way as to find in them the principal parts of Peripatetic philosophy, seeing in the one <i>physics</i> and in the other the <i>metaphysics</i> of Aristotle. It is in this sense that he already speaks of them in his Talmudic works. See <i>Commentary on the Mishnah</i>, 2<sup>nd</sup> part, tractate ’Haghîgâ, ch. II, § 1; <i>Abridgment of the Talmud</i>, book I, 1<sup>st</sup> tractate (or <i>Yesodé ha-Torâ</i>), ch. 1 to 4; [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.18": "Voy. Mischnâ and Talmud of Babylon, treatise ’Haghîgâ, ch. 2, fol. 12 and 13. In enumerating various biblical subjects that must not be interpreted in public, the Talmud prohibits interpreting the <i>mercabâ</i>, even to a single pupil in particular, unless he be a subject of superior intelligence.",
    "fn.19": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתצריח</span>, one of the Leiden manuscripts reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">תצריחהא כל אלתצריח</span>, a lesson that Ibn Tibbon seems to have rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">לגלותם · · · · · בביאור</span>.",
    "fn.20": "Voy. Mishnah and Babylonian Talmud, <i>loc. cit.</i>.",
    "fn.21": "Literally: <i>up to their extremity and their end</i>.",
    "fn.22": "Literally: <i>matters and habits</i>, that is to say, things pertaining to matter and daily life. Ibn-Tibbon rendered the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמואדّ</span> (matters) by <span dir=\"rtl\">הטבעים</span>, and Al-’Harizi by <span dir=\"rtl\">כחות הטבע</span>, which seems to me inaccurate.",
    "fn.23": "Ibn Tibbon adds here, in his Hebrew version, the explanatory words <span dir=\"rtl\">with a slight difference between them</span> <i>with small intervals</i>.",
    "fn.24": "The Hebrew version of Ibn-Tibbon adds here the following passage: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויש מי שיהיה לו בין ברק וברק הפרש רב והיא מדרגת רוב הנביאים</span> This passage is not found in any of the Arabic manuscripts; it is also not found in the Hebrew version of Al-’Harizi, nor in the excerpts of R. Schem-Tob ibn-Falaquera. See his <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, printed in Presbourg (1837, in-8), p. 9. However, this passage also exists in the manuscripts of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, and it is reproduced by the commentators of this version, as well as by Samuel Zarza in his <i>Mekor ’hayyîm</i> or commentary on the Pentateuch in the book of Numbers, ch. 11, <small>V</small>. 25. This passage, moreover, is superfluous; for the one that follows the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ויתנבאו ולא יספו</span> says almost the same thing.",
    "fn.25": "All Arabic manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">עלינא</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">עליו</span> in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version is an error; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">עלינו</span>, as the manuscripts of this version indeed do.",
    "fn.26": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">רבאני</span> does not have here the meaning of <i>rabbinic</i> or <i>rabbanite</i>, but that of <i>theologian</i> or <i>metaphysician</i>, roughly like the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאהי</span> that precedes it; it is derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">רَبّ</span>, in the sense of <i>master</i> par excellence, or of <i>God</i>. This is how Ibn-Tofaïl refers to the intellectual faculty of man as <span dir=\"rtl\">امر ربانی الاهی</span> (<i>Epistola de Hai Ebn-Yokdhan</i>, ed. Pocock, p. 137). See also the observations of R. Schem-Tob Ibn-Falaquera on the version of Ibn-Tibbon, <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 148.",
    "fn.27": "All the Oxford mss. have fi <span dir=\"rtl\">למעניין מתקארבה̈</span> which is incorrect. In one of the two Leyden mss. one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">למעאני</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">למעניין</span>; in the other one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">למעניין מתקארבין</span> and it is this latter reading that we have adopted as the most correct.",
    "fn.28": "<span dir=\"rtl\">מתאכׄם</span> participates in the third<sup>e</sup> form of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תכׄם</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">تخم</span>  This lesson is found in six manuscripts of the Bodleian Library in Oxford, and it is the one expressed by the version of Ibn-Tibbon, which bears <span dir=\"rtl\">מצרנית</span> <i>limitrophe</i>. The version of Al-’Harizi states: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי המרע הטבעי הוא אחרון למדע האלהי</span>. This translator has read <span dir=\"rtl\">מתאבׄר</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(متأخر)</span>  a lesson that is indeed found in the two manuscripts of the library of Leyde, but which does not offer a plausible meaning and does not agree with the words that immediately follow. The author means to say that there is no intermediate science between physics and metaphysics, and that in the order of studies the first must precede the second. The entire course of philosophical studies was divided, among the Arab Peripatetics, notably since Avicenna, into three parts: 1° logic; 2° physics (including mathematics and astronomy); 3° metaphysics. Compare Maimonides, in the present work, I<sup>st</sup> part, ch. 34 (at the 3<sup>rd</sup> <i>cause</i>), fol. 39 <i>a</i> of our Arabic text. See also my article <i>Ibn-Sina</i> in the <i>Dictionary of Philosophical Sciences</i>, vol. III, p. 175.",
    "fn.29": "The lesson we have adopted is that of the majority of the mss.; the one that has been expressed in the two Hebrew versions is found only in one of the Leyde mss., which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">עטׄם אמור עלי מא הי עליה</span>.",
    "fn.30": "See the introduction to the X<sup>th</sup> (XI<sup>th</sup>) chapter of the treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, on the seventh article of faith; <i>Porta Mosis</i>, by Pococke, p. 170.",
    "fn.31": "That is to say, allegorical interpretations and legends contained in the <i>Midraschîm</i> and in the <i>Haggadôth</i> of the Talmud.",
    "fn.32": "All the mss. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאםמא אלמשכלהֹׄ ואלאמתׄאל</span>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">והמושאלים</span>, which is read in most editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">והמשלים</span>, as the mss. and <i>the princeps edition</i> of 1480 have it. It is singular that the same error has crept into the unique ms. that remains to us of Al-’Harizi's version, where one also reads <span dir=\"rtl\">והמושאלים</span>. It would appear that it is the preceding word <span dir=\"rtl\">המםופקים</span> that has given rise to the mistake, the copyists having believed that this word designated <i>amphibologous</i> names; but the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמשכלה̈</span> generally designates the <i>obscure</i> names of the different categories mentioned at the beginning of this introduction.",
    "fn.33": "That is to say, in other words, that, on the one hand, what is an allegory is not considered as such, and that, on the other hand, what should be taken in its literal sense is considered as an allegory. The commentary <i>Éphodi</i> cites, for example, on the one hand, the image of the adulterous woman (Prov., ch. VII), which, according to our author, designates matter, and which many commentators take in the literal sense, and, on the other hand, the account relative to Bathsheba (II. Sam., ch. XI, <small>V</small>. 2 and following), which evidently should be taken in its literal sense and in which certain commentators have seen an allegory.",
    "fn.34": "This passage and the following one are found, with a few variants, in the Midrash on <i>Canticle of Canticles</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שיל השירים רבה</span>, fol. 1 <i>d</i>).",
    "fn.35": "The verb is implied, and the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ורד</span> that is found in some modern editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version must be deleted. This word is not present in the old editions, nor in the Arabic and Hebrew manuscripts of the <i>Guide</i>. In the passage from the Midrash indicated in the previous note, one reads more regularly: <span dir=\"rtl\">כך מדבר לדבר יממשל למשל עמד שלמה על סודה של תורה</span>.",
    "fn.36": "The religious doctrine, says the author, does not consist essentially in the ceremonial laws, such as the prescriptions relating to the festival of Tabernacles and other similar things, nor in the civil laws, as, for example, the provisions concerning the four kinds of depositaries (See <i>Mishnah</i>, 2nd<sup> part, tractate </sup>Shebu'oth<i> or of </i>Oaths<i>, ch. VIII); but it consists in the fundamental principles of faith and in the profound subjects with which Theology is concerned [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.37": "We translate this verse according to the explanation that the author will himself provide.",
    "fn.38": "The root of this Chaldaic word, according to the author, is the same as that of the Hebrew word <i>Maskiyyôth</i>.",
    "fn.39": "The form <span dir=\"rtl\">ארי</span> (instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ز</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">هنر</span>) is incorrect; however, as the author uses it very frequently and it is found in all the manuscripts we have consulted, we deemed it necessary to preserve it.",
    "fn.40": "By <i>proximate</i> or <i>immediate</i> matter, the author means everything that constitutes the animal organism, such as, for example, the limbs of the body, flesh, blood, humors, etc.; for, in this respect, man perfectly resembles animals, and forms with them a single and same species. The elements form a more <i>remote</i> and more general matter, embracing all sublunary bodies. The most remote and most general matter is <i>prime matter</i> or hylé <span dir=\"rtl\">ג</span> which encompasses all creation.",
    "fn.41": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon adds here the words: <span dir=\"rtl\">למצוא כל עניני המשל בנמשל</span>, the equivalent of which is not found in any of the mss. of the original Arabic.",
    "fn.42": "Literally: <i>and in which they compose</i> (books).",
    "fn.43": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יתהאפת ללרד</span> are thus rendered by Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יהרום ויקפוץ עצמו להשיב על דברי</span>. It was Maimonides himself who had advised him to translate in this manner, as can be seen in the supplement to the letter that begins with the words: <span dir=\"rtl\">לפי שכלו יהולל איש וכוי</span>. This supplement has been preserved in a manuscript from the Bodleian Library (Cod. Poc., n° 74).",
    "fn.44": "Literally: <i>and if</i> (this treatise) <i>quenches his thirst</i>. Ibn Tibbon translated it somewhat freely: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואם ירפא לו מדוה לבבו</span>, <i>and if it heals the sickness of his heart;</i> however, Maimonides himself had advised the translator to render these words by <span dir=\"rtl\">ואם רוה צמאו</span>, as can be seen in the supplement that we just discussed in the previous note. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מדוה</span>, a manuscript of the Hebrew version bears <span dir=\"rtl\">מחום</span>, and in the margin one reads the variant <span dir=\"rtl\">ואם ירוה לו צמאו</span>. One can compare below, I<sup>r</sup> part, at the end of chapter 45, where the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מא ישפי גלילך</span> are rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">מה שירוה צמאך</span>. — Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">שפת</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(شَفَتْ)</span>, as we have written in our text, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">שפאת</span> in all the manuscripts that we have consulted (a total of <i>eight</i>), as well as in the supplement to Maimonides' letter that we just discussed [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.45": "The author means that they will flee from the true doctrines because these will reveal to them the complete falsity of the hypotheses and theories they have imagined in order to silence their doubts and calm the turmoil of their soul, and which are, so to speak, their sole treasure, prepared in advance to save them from distress. It is undoubtedly a question here of those who followed the doctrines of the <i>Motécalemîn</i>. See above p. 5, note 1.",
    "fn.46": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתי ענדנא מא אלף פיהא</span>, which are found in all Arabic mss., were omitted by the two Hebrew translators.",
    "fn.47": "It is known that the rabbis lend to verse 126 of Ps. 119 the following meaning: <i>When it is time to act for God</i>, <i>it is even permissible to transgress the Law</i>, that is to say one may permit oneself to violate some secondary precepts when it is a matter of consolidating the religious edifice in general.",
    "fn.48": "That is to say, when the two statements, or one of them, must not be understood in an absolute manner, but under a certain implicit reserve or condition. Thus, for example, when it is said, on the one hand, that God punishes the sins of the fathers on the children (Exod., 20, 5; XXXIV, 7), and on the other hand, that one will not put the fathers to death for the children, nor the children for the fathers (Deut., 24, 16), the rabbis, seeing a contradiction in these passages, say that the punishment of the children for the sins of the fathers takes place <i>under the condition</i> that the children persist in the evil way of the fathers. — To cite another example, when one reads, on the one hand, that Moses entered <i>into the midst of the cloud</i> (Exod., 24, 18), and on the other hand, that Moses could not enter the tent of meeting <i>when the cloud rested upon it</i> (<i>Ib</i>., XL, 35), the rabbis say that he could enter into the cloud under the condition that he was expressly called there by the divinity.",
    "fn.49": "According to some commentators, the author intends to speak here of two propositions containing homonymous subjects, where, by taking the homonyms in the same sense, one could find an apparent contradiction. But the author's words seem to have a more general meaning. If, for example, it is said, on the one hand, that God reveals Himself to certain prophets in a dream (Numb., 12, 6), and, on the other hand, that dreams only speak falsehoods (Zechariah, 10, 2), the doctors, in order to eliminate the contradiction, say that it is a matter of dreams of different natures, some true, others false. See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Berakhôth</i>, fol. 55 <i>verso</i>, Albo, ’<i>Ikkarîm</i>, 1. III, ch. 10.",
    "fn.50": "Moses of Narbonne, in his commentary, cites as an example the notions of <i>species</i> and <i>genus</i> about which Aristotle had to say a few words when wanting to explain the category of substance (<i>Categories</i>, ch. III), although the exact explanation of these notions only comes later (in the <i>Topics</i>). The dual nature, logical and ontological, of the categories and various other notions dealt with in the <i>Organon</i>, often requires the provisional and summary explanation of certain notions that are only truly explored in depth in metaphysics.",
    "fn.51": "The author states further that, in his own work, certain contradictions are found resulting from the fifth cause. Let us cite an example: in chapter LXX of the I<sup>st</sup> part, the author presents God as the immediate mover of the higher sphere, whereas at the end of chapter IV of the II<sup>nd</sup> part, he states that the first of the <i>intelligences of the spheres</i>, produced by God, sets the first sphere in motion. This is because, in the first passage, the author's sole intention is to present God as the <i>mover</i> of the universe in general, without entering into the details of the movement of the different spheres and their <i>intelligences</i>, as he does in the second passage. It thus results in a contradiction that is only apparent. [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.52": "By the author's own admission, his work also contains contradictions resulting from this seventh cause. One of the most serious appears to be the following: the author, who argues against the eternity of the <i>prime </i>matter, as admitted by the Peripatetics, nonetheless admits it as a premise in demonstrations related to the most important metaphysical questions, as he himself declares in chapter LXXI of the I<sup>st</sup> part and at the beginning of the II<sup>nd</sup> part.",
    "fn.53": "That is to say Rabbi Judah the Saint, the redactor of the Mishnah. [[t:1]] [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.54": "It is known that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אמוראי</span> designates the doctors who appear in the <i>Guemarâ</i>, and who discuss the opinions of the Tannaîm or doctors of the Mishnah.",
    "fn.55": "The mss. of the original Arabic generally have both examples; the Hebrew version has only that of <i>Rabâ</i>.",
    "fn.56": "It is known that Rabbi Ashi is one of the main editors of the Babylonian Talmud.",
    "fn.57": "Voy. Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Schabbâth</i>, fol. 30. Various passages are cited in which Solomon is in contradiction with David or with himself, and an attempt is made to resolve the difficulty by establishing that in the contradictory passages different subjects are being referred to. Thus, for example, it is written in the Proverbs (ch. XXVI, <small>V</small>. 4): <i>Do not answer a fool according to his folly</i>, and immediately thereafter (v. 5): <i>Answer a fool according to his folly</i>. To remove the contradiction, the Talmud states that in one of the two passages it is a matter of religious things, and in the other, of secular things.",
    "fn.58": "Literally: <i>And one must not conjecture about it</i>. Ibn-Tibbon has rendered the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אן לא יגׄזף פי דׄלך</span> with a long periphrasis; Al-’Harizi translates more faithfully: <span dir=\"rtl\">וצריך לבל יגזרו עליו כפי מה שיזדמן</span>.",
    "fn.59": "See above, page 28, note 1, and page 29, note 1.",
    "fn.60": "One would hardly believe that Jewish doctors could have fallen into such errors, if we did not have the positive testimony of Maimonides, as well as that of his son Abraham and several of his contemporaries, who were obliged to defend Maimonides against the attacks of several Talmudists, and notably against a portion of the rabbis of France, who believed they had to take the anthropomorphisms of the Bible literally. We content ourselves with recalling in this regard the unsuspicious testimony of a fierce adversary of Maimonides, R. Abraham ben-David of Posquières, in his critical notes on the <i>Mishneh Torah</i> or <i>Abrégé du Talmud</i> (book I, treatise <i>Teshuvah</i> or on Repentance, ch. 3, § 7). Maimonides having counted among the heretics (<span dir=\"rtl\">מינים</span>) one who admits the corporeality of God, R. Abraham asks: \"Why does he call this one a <i>heretic</i>, since men greater and better than he (Maimonides) have followed this opinion, according to what they had seen in the texts of Scripture, and even more in the <i>Haggadot</i> which disturb thought?\" [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.61": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בדפע</span> (with <i>daleth</i>) several mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ברפע</span> (with <i>resch</i>), which does not alter the meaning.",
    "fn.62": "The author means that he does not use the word <i>form</i> here in the philosophical sense, but in that of common language.",
    "fn.63": "That is to say, the particular form of the human species, or what characterizes man and distinguishes him from other animals.",
    "fn.64": "Literally: <i>Their meaning</i> (<i>their idea</i>) <i>that one imagined</i>, that is to say the false idea that one formed of them or the virtue that one attributed to them in error.",
    "fn.65": "It is generally believed that the disease of the Philistines, designated by the word <i>te’horîm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">טחרים</span>) or, according to the reading of the <i>khetîb, ’apholîm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עפלים</span>), consisted of a type of tumors or pustules in the private parts; the rabbis see in it <i>varices hémorroïdales</i>, and it is probably in this sense that Maimonides himself understands this word. The Jewish physicians of the Middle Ages commonly employ the word <span dir=\"rtl\">טחורים</span> to designate <i>hémorroïdes</i>.",
    "fn.66": "See above page 6, note 3.",
    "fn.67": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ׂגארחה̈</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">ׂגאנחה̈</span> particularly designate here the members that serve to grasp something or to perform a task. <span dir=\"rtl\">ׂגארחה̈</span>, derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">ׂגרח</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(جرح)</span> <i>to wound</i>, is used to designate <i>the hand</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">ׂגאנחה̈</span> is used to designate <i>the ribs</i>, and also appears to be used for <span dir=\"rtl\">ׂגנאח</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">جناح</span> <i>wing</i>, and, like the latter, in the sense of <i>arm</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא מעשה גוף ילא יד ולא רגל</span>, which is not entirely accurate; but this translation is preferable to that of Al-’Harizi, which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא אבר ולא נתח</span>.",
    "fn.68": "The author is alluding here to what the Arab philosophers call <span dir=\"rtl\">الاتصال</span>, <i>the conjunction</i>, or the union of <i>the active intellect</i> (coming from God through the intermediary of the Intelligences of the spheres) with <i>the passive intellect</i>. This subject will be explained in several places in this work. See below ch. LXVIII; II<sup>nd</sup> part, ch. IV; III<sup>rd</sup> part, ch. LI, and other places.",
    "fn.69": "An allusion is probably being made here to some Oriental fable; I suppose it is intended to refer to Nimrod, who, according to Oriental traditions, revolted against God and had the <i>Tower of Babel</i> built, and is said to have been placed in the heavens, being identified with the constellation of the <i>Giant</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(الجبّار)</span> or of <i>Orion</i>. See the <i>Chronicon Paschale</i>, p. 36; on the <i>Djebbâr</i> or <i>Orion</i>, see the commentary on the treatise of Ulug-Beigh, by Hyde, in the <i>Syntagma dissertationum</i>, vol. I, pp. 42 et seq., and p. 57.",
    "fn.70": "Literally: <i>with the beginnings of his thoughts and ideas</i>, that is to say according to what first presents itself to his mind.",
    "fn.71": "The distinction that the author establishes here between things of intelligence and things that are within the realm of probable opinions is borrowed from Aristotle (<i>Topiques</i>, l. I, ch. 1). The Stagirite recognizes four species of syllogisms, of which the first two are: <i>the demonstrative</i>, which is based on principles of absolute truth and evident in themselves, being founded in intelligence, and <i>the dialectical</i>, which proceeds from <i>probable opinions</i> (<i>ἐξ ἐνδόξων</i>), and which is based on the opinions of the totality or the majority of men, or only those of the totality or the majority of wise men, or the most illustrious among them. Good and evil, virtues and vices are within the realm of <i>probable opinions</i>, and have no relation to God or pure intelligence (See <i>Morale à Nicemaque</i>, l. VII, ch. 1). The Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמשהוראת</span>, which means <i>things generally known</i> or <i>famous things</i>, clearly designates here things admitted by opinion and corresponds to the Greek term <i>τὰ ἔνδοξα</i>; Hebrew versions render this word as <span dir=\"rtl\">המפורסמות</span>. The Greek word <i>ἔνδοξος</i> having both meanings that we have just indicated, the Arabs have rendered it by a word that means <i>generally known</i> or <i>famous</i>, although this word does not exactly convey Aristotle's idea. Thus, for example, these words: Δ<i>ιαλεϰτιϰὸς δέ συλλογισμὸς ὁ ἐξ ἐνδόξων συλλογιζόμενος</i> (<i>Top.</i>, I, 1), \"the dialectical syllogism is the one that results <i>from probable opinions</i>\", have been rendered in the Arabic version as: <span dir=\"rtl\">والقياس الجدلى هو الذى ينتج من مقدمات ذائعة</span>. For the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ذايعة</span>, a marginal variant in the ar. ms. n° 882 A gives the synonym <span dir=\"rtl\">مشهورة</span>, which is the term generally used by Arab philosophers. This is how Ibn-Roschd expresses himself in his <i>Abrégé de l’Organon</i>, at the beginning of the <span dir=\"rtl\">كتاب الجدل</span> (book of dialectic), corresponding to Aristotle's Topiques.",
    "fn.72": "That is to say: he manifested his disobedience by a <i>act.</i> The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פלמא עצא</span> refer to the change that occurred in him; the second, <span dir=\"rtl\">עצא</span>, to the material disobedience.",
    "fn.73": "That is to say, with regard to the realm of intelligence and <i>necessarily true</i> in itself; the author means that pure intelligence has only to concern itself with the true and the false. Good and evil are the domain of probable opinion [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.74": "The author follows the opinion of the ancient rabbis who apply this verse to Adam: God, they say, had initially endowed Adam with immortal strength (<span dir=\"rtl\">תתקפהו לנצח</span>); but having neglected God's advice and followed that of the serpent, he was expelled from paradise. See <i>Beréschîth rabba</i>, sections 14 and 21.",
    "fn.75": "That is to say, the punishment was entirely conforming to the sin; the Hebrew words <span dir=\"rtl\">מדה כנגד מדה</span> which the author intercalates here in the Arabic phrase, are a well-known proverbial locution, often employed by the rabbis.",
    "fn.76": "See above, page 6, note 3.",
    "fn.77": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's Hebrew version generally bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ובצדו קצת מקריו</span>; one must read, according to the Arabic: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובצד וקצת מקריו</span>, as is indeed the case with the mss. of the aforementioned version.",
    "fn.78": "That is to say, they criticized and judged them with malice. The author alludes to various passages from the Talmud and the Midrashim. Thus, for example, the Jerusalem Talmud (<i>Biccourîm</i>, ch. III, and <i>Schekalîm</i>, ch. V) speaks of two doctors, one of whom had explained the passage in question in a sense of praise, and the other in a sense of blame; according to the latter, the Israelites said, speaking of Moses' stoutness: \"Behold his thighs, behold his legs; he eats of what belongs to the Jews, he drinks of what belongs to the Jews, all that he has comes from the Jews.\" <span dir=\"rtl\">ומאן דאמר לגנאי חזי שאקי חזי כרעין אכיל מן יהודאי שתי מן יהודאי כל מרליה מן יהודאי</span>. See also <i>Midrasch Tan’houma</i>, sect. <span dir=\"rtl\">כי תשא</span> (Venice edition, fol. 40 <i>d</i>); <i>Schemôth rabba</i>, sect. 51; Babylonian Talmud, <i>Kiddouschîn</i>, fol. 33 <i>b</i>. There were even some, says the Talmud elsewhere, who went so far as to suspect him of adultery: <span dir=\"rtl\">שחשדוהו מאשת איש</span>. See <i>Synhedrîn</i>, fol. 110 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.79": "One can easily guess that the author intends to refer to Aristotle. In what follows, allusion is made to a passage from the <i>Treatise on the Heavens</i>, book II, at the beginning of chapter 12, where Aristotle, addressing some questions related to the movement of the spheres, expresses himself in terms roughly similar to those attributed to him here by Maimonides [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.80": "Literally: <i>after having obtained true and certain premises and having known them.</i> The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מקדמאת</span> here designates the propositions that serve as premises in the syllogism; the author enumerates here everything that is part of the science, of logic, that one must delve into before tackling metaphysical subjects.",
    "fn.81": "The author often uses the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">מצאפא אלי</span>, <i>joined to, added to</i>, when he wishes to convey that the literal meaning may be admitted alongside the allegorical meaning.",
    "fn.82": "In the Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Berakhôth</i>, fol. 7 <i>a</i>, it is stated, among other things, regarding Moses: <span dir=\"rtl\">בשכר מהביט זכה לותמונת ה׳ יביט</span>, \"as a reward for having <i>refrained from looking</i>, he merited to <i>behold the countenance of God.</i>\" See also <i>Midrasch Tan’houma</i>, section <span dir=\"rtl\">שמות</span>, Venice edition, fol. 23 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.83": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">כליה</span> is Hebrew, a noun of action of <span dir=\"rtl\">כלה</span>; in two mss. we find <span dir=\"rtl\">בליה</span> with <i>beth</i>, an Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">(بلية)</span> which has roughly the same meaning.",
    "fn.84": "According to rabbinic tradition, those who were burned at <i>Tab'erâ</i> (Numbers, 11, 1–3) are the same as the 70 elders, who, along with Nadab and Abihu, burned in the sanctuary (Leviticus, 10, 2), are designated by the words: <i>the elect among the sons of Israel.</i> See <i>Midrash Tan'huma</i>, section <span dir=\"rtl\">בהעלותך</span>, fol. 72 <i>a, b; Wayyikra Rabbah</i>, section 20, near the end.",
    "fn.85": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">יתקדם</span> some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">יתקדם</span> (with <i>samekh</i>), <i>it will be sanctified</i>",
    "fn.86": "That is to say, after having seen God in their own way, they surrendered to corporeal pleasures.",
    "fn.87": "The author intends to refer to verse 10: <i>And they saw the God of Is etc</i>.",
    "fn.88": "See this I<sup>re</sup> part, chap. XXVIII, and III<sup>e</sup> part, chap. IV.",
    "fn.89": "Literally: <i>the woman to her sister</i>, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">יריעה</span> being of the feminine gender.",
    "fn.90": "Homonymy occurs with respect to the figurative sense, analogous to that indicated lastly for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אשה</span>.",
    "fn.91": "The mss. read: <span dir=\"rtl\">יגׁתזון</span>, which is nothing other than a vulgar orthography for <span dir=\"rtl\">יגׁתזיון</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(يجتزئون)</span>, the VIII<sup>th</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">جزأ</span>. Maimonides takes the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ישׂפיקו</span> in the sense of <i>to be content, to be satisfied;</i> similarly Saadia, who, in his Arabic version of Isaiah, renders it by <span dir=\"rtl\">יכתפון</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(يكتفون)</span>. In one of the Leyden mss., this last word has been substituted in our passage for <span dir=\"rtl\">יגׁתזון</span> which is found in all the other mss. [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.92": "One sees that the author had the intention of devoting a chapter, in this I<sup>re</sup> part, to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בן</span>; but he did not do so.",
    "fn.93": "See above, chap. I, p. 37.",
    "fn.94": "Voy. Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>’Eroubîn</i>, fol. 18 <i>a</i>; cf. <i>Beréschîth rabba</i>, sect. 20 and 24, where the same thing is stated in slightly different terms.",
    "fn.95": "That is to say, it applies at times to a particular <i>place</i> (<i>ἴδιος</i>) or to the <i>place</i> occupied by a particular body, at times to a <i>common</i> <i>ϰοινός</i> place, more or less vast and enclosing several particular places (such as, for example, a house enclosing dwellings, a city enclosing houses, a country enclosing cities, and so on), and finally to the universal place or to <i>space</i> in general. Cf. Aristotle, <i>Physics</i>, book IV, chapter II.",
    "fn.96": "Literally: <i>according to his rank and his high dignity in Being</i>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ועטׄם חטׄה</span> are rendered in the version of Ibn-Tibbon by <span dir=\"rtl\">ועוצם חלקו</span>, <i>and his great part;</i> but Ibn-Falaquera observes with reason that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חטׄ</span> does not have here the meaning of <i>part</i>, but that of <span dir=\"rtl\">כבוד</span>, <i>honor, dignity;</i> for one cannot say of God, creator of the entire universe, that he has <i>a part</i> in what exists. See the appendix of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 149. [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.97": "The author means that, in the explanation of words, his purpose is solely to indicate their various philosophical senses, and that he does not concern himself with philological explanations, or the diverse acceptations of words in common language [[t:1]] [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.98": "Literally: <i>an existing thing;</i> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מוגוד</span> appears to have the meaning here of: <i>existing in a visible, perceptible manner</i>.",
    "fn.99": "That is to say, one who has scientific knowledge, who knows the spheres and their movements.",
    "fn.100": "Some of the best mss. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי עטׄים אהל לה</span>, which does not offer a very satisfactory meaning; Ibn-Tibbon appears to have also read <span dir=\"rtl\">עטׄים</span>, but this passage is somewhat corrupted in his version and presents variants in the different mss. and editions. Al-’Harizi translates <span dir=\"rtl\">על גדולת מי שהוכן לו</span>; it is evident that he read <span dir=\"rtl\">עטׄם</span>, but he poorly rendered the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אהל</span>. The reading we have adopted (<span dir=\"rtl\">עלי עטׄם מן</span>) is that of three mss.; a fourth bears <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי עטׄמה̈ מן</span>, which is the same thing.",
    "fn.101": "The author means: If we have spoken of a throne of God in passages where it is a question neither of the sanctuary nor of heaven. [[t:1]] [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.102": "See further on the chapters dealing with the attributes of God.",
    "fn.103": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מן דׄלך אלמוצׄע</span> some manuscripts read <span dir=\"rtl\">מנה</span>; similarly the two Hebrew versions <span dir=\"rtl\">ממנו</span>.",
    "fn.104": "That is to say, it is permissible to assign a higher rank to sacred things; but it is not allowed to lower them further. Thus, for example, one reads in the Mishnah (2nd<sup> part</sup>, tractate <i>Shekalim</i>, chapter VI, § 4) that the <i>shewbread</i> was first placed on a marble table and then on a golden table; but it would not have been permitted to do the opposite. A community may sell land to use the proceeds to build a synagogue; similarly, with the money received from selling copies of the <i>Prophets</i>, one may purchase copies of the <i>Pentateuch</i>; but it is forbidden to do the contrary (<i>Ibid</i>., tractate <i>Megillah</i>, chapter III, § 1).",
    "fn.105": "As an example of this allegorical sense, the author cites, at the end of the chapter, the words: <i>And Moses went up to God</i>.",
    "fn.106": "By <i>surrounding sphere</i>, the author means the superior sphere that surrounds all the others, and which, in his opinion, is designated in the Bible by the word <i>araboth</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ערבות</span>). See this I<sup>st</sup> part, chap. LXX, and chap. LXXII at the beginning; II<sup>nd</sup> part, chap. VI and following.",
    "fn.107": "The author notes here that the expression <i>descendre</i> is used to indicate that the punishment comes from the supreme divine will; for it cannot be admitted that God decrees punishment solely as a result of the evil actions of men, which would suppose that God can be influenced and change His will. On the important problem of God's prescience and man's free will, see the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. XVII and following.",
    "fn.108": "That is to say, the meaning of an elevation of <i>thought</i>, which the author mentioned in the third place.",
    "fn.109": "That is to say, a light specially created to represent divine majesty (<span dir=\"rtl\">שכינה</span>). See below, chap. LXIV.",
    "fn.110": "That is to say, God, in giving him children, assures him a stable place in the house. [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.111": "See below, chap. LVI.",
    "fn.112": "All the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">תגירא</span>; one must pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">تغيراً</span> and consider this word as an adverbial accusative.",
    "fn.113": "By <i>the individuals of the heavens</i>, the author means the celestial spheres and the stars fixed in these spheres. See this I<sup>st</sup> part, chap. LXXII.",
    "fn.114": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">כאינאת אלארץׄ ופאםדיהא</span> mean the same thing as <span dir=\"rtl\">אמור אלארץׄ אלכאינה̈ אלפאםדה̈</span>. The author expressed himself in a somewhat irregular manner; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ופאםדיהא</span>, he should have said <span dir=\"rtl\">ופאםדאתהא</span>.—The words <span dir=\"rtl\">בון</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">פםאד</span> are Peripatetic terms, borrowed from the Arabic versions of Aristotle, and correspond to the Greek words <i>γένεσις</i> and <i>φθορά</i>.",
    "fn.115": "That is to say, by the homonymy of which we speak in this chapter.",
    "fn.116": "The author plays on the double meaning of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">דור</span> (as a noun and as an adverb), and we have attempted to render this play on words by employing the word <i>tour</i>. Ibn-Tibbon used for the adverb <span dir=\"rtl\">דוראֹ</span> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חלילה</span>, taken in the sense it has in the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">חוזר חלילה</span>, and from this he formed, in order to render the noun <span dir=\"rtl\">דור</span>, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חלילות</span>. The obscurity of the two words used by Ibn-Tibbon has led copyists to often alter them, and the manuscripts, as well as the editions of the Hebrew version, present many variations here; one must read: <span dir=\"rtl\">אל סבוב הארץ ר״ל חלילותה רמוז לענינים ההוים בה חלילה</span>. Al-’Harizi translates <span dir=\"rtl\">דורא</span> as <span dir=\"rtl\">בהקפת הגלגל</span>, <i>by the revolution of the sphere</i>, which is a misinterpretation.",
    "fn.117": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אליה</span> is used for <span dir=\"rtl\">אלי שי</span>. Ibn-Tibbon paraphrased the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אליה</span> with <span dir=\"rtl\">אל כל אחד מן הענינים ההם</span>; Al-’Harizi has again made a mistake here by relating the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">אליה</span> to God, and by translating this word with <span dir=\"rtl\">אליו לא לזולתו</span>.",
    "fn.118": "Some commentators wonder why the author, when speaking of the proper and material sense of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קום</span>, said: <i>one of its meanings</i>, instead of saying: <i>its primitive meaning</i>, or: <i>it primitively means</i>, as he usually does in the explanation of homonyms. But they have not considered that the primitive sense of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קום</span> is <i>to rise</i>, whereas the author's explanations relate to another meaning of the same verb, that of <i>being upright</i>; it is this latter sense that he gives to the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קום</span> in the biblical passages he is about to cite. One will no longer be surprised, with the commentator Schem-Tob, that the author, instead of citing passages from the Pentateuch, went to seek an example of the proper sense in the book of Esther; in the examples proposed by Schem-Tob, namely: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויקם אברהם</span> (Genesis, 23, 3), <span dir=\"rtl\">ויקם פרעה</span> (Exodus, 12, 30), the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קום</span> means <i>to rise</i>, whereas in the passage from Esther it can be translated by <i>being upright</i>. Al-’Harizi made a mistake in translating <span dir=\"rtl\">וענינו לקום היושב ממקומו</span>; by emphasizing the sense of <i>to rise</i>, he did not understand the author's intention, and he even altered his words. Ibn-Tibbon translated literally <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחד מעניניו הקימה</span>, taking <span dir=\"rtl\">הקימה</span> in the sense of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">קיאם</span>, <i>being upright</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מעניני</span>, which is found in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">מעניניו</span>, as found in the manuscripts [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.119": "By the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תׄם</span>, <i>there</i>, the author means <i>in the presence of God, when speaking of God</i>.",
    "fn.120": "Literally: <i>May</i> (God) <i>be exalted!</i> Instead of the elliptical expression <span dir=\"rtl\">תעאלי</span>, one of the Leyden mss. reads <span dir=\"rtl\">תעאלי אללה ען דׄלך</span>, <i>may God be exalted above this</i>, which appears to be a gloss that was inserted into the text at a later time.",
    "fn.121": "Voy. Talmud of Babylon, tractate ’<i>Haghîgâ</i>, fol. 15 <i>a;</i> one sees that Maimonides admits in this passage the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עמידה</span>, which the editors of the Talmud have erased in order to conform to the opinion of Rashi. Cf. Samuel Yaphé, <i>Yephé mareh</i>, at the beginning of the tractate <i>Berakhôth</i>.",
    "fn.122": "See below, chap. XXVIII.",
    "fn.123": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">קולה לה תעאלי</span> are for <span dir=\"rtl\">קולה תעאלי למשה</span>.",
    "fn.124": "The author does not clearly explain his thought; according to the commentators, Maimonides means that the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">עמד</span>, in the two last passages, applies to the stable and permanent part of Moses, i.e., to his intellectual soul, and to the union of the latter with the active intellect and with God.",
    "fn.125": "The first <span dir=\"rtl\">משתק</span> is found in all the mss.; it has not been rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version.",
    "fn.126": "Maimonides, as well as other commentators, finds the indication of this etymology in Genesis, chapter 2, <small>V</small>. 7, and chapter III, <small>V</small>. 23.",
    "fn.127": "On the different acceptations of the word <i>Élohîm</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אלהים</span>), see ch. II, at the beginning.",
    "fn.128": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">יתםלק</span> found in all the mss. was not rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version.",
    "fn.129": "The adverb <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄרורה̈</span> does not relate to <span dir=\"rtl\">ידרך</span>, but to <span dir=\"rtl\">מן עליה</span>; the meaning is: <i>that which</i> is <i>necessarily</i> above, the absolute and necessary Being.",
    "fn.130": "The author means that, by rendering the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עליו</span> as <i>above it</i>, and by placing God in a sort of local and material relationship with the ladder, he has merely harmonized this word with the literal meaning of the passage, without regard for the allegory it contains; for the head of the ladder signifies the higher sphere set in motion by the first mover, or God, who is said allegorically <i>to stand above it</i>. See ch. LXX of this <sup>book</sup> part.",
    "fn.131": "The author states that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מלאך</span> (angel) must be taken here in its primitive sense of <i>messenger</i>.",
    "fn.132": "See above, ch. X.",
    "fn.133": "The author sees in this passage an allusion to the perpetual contemplation of God by Moses. See the following chapter on the word <span dir=\"rtl\">צור</span>.",
    "fn.134": "That is to say, the two verbs are used figuratively when it concerns something stable and imperishable.",
    "fn.135": "By this last example, the author intends to show that the verb <i>'amad</i>, in relation to the substantive <span dir=\"rtl\">צור</span>, <i>rock</i>, forms an allegory representing the permanence of God's action in the universe and of His inspiration being communicated to Moses; he infers from this that, in the previously cited verse, the word <i>naçab</i>, in relation to the same substantive, must form an analogous allegory. Here it is God's spirit that penetrates Moses, there it is Moses' intelligence uniting with God through contemplation.",
    "fn.136": "Literally: <i>mountain</i>. By mountain the author appears to be designating here a lofty rock, a mountain formed by a rock; if he does not state it explicitly, it is because he believes himself to be sufficiently understood through the example he cites.",
    "fn.137": "See above, ch. VIII. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">לח</span>, which refers to Moses, is missing in several mss., as well as in the two Hebrew versions.",
    "fn.138": "All the mss. without exception bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמטׄנון</span> (with <i>ts</i>), but the sense of the phrase requires <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמצׄנון</span>. The substitution of the <span dir=\"rtl\">טׄ</span> for the <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄ</span> is a very frequent orthographical error in the mss.",
    "fn.139": "The author, having just explained the word <span dir=\"rtl\">צור</span> in the sense of <i>principle of things</i>, which pertains to physics, believes it necessary to recall that the subjects of physics have been presented, just like those of metaphysics, by means of metaphors. Such appears to be the purpose of this brief chapter, by which the author interrupts his explanations of homonymous words.",
    "fn.140": "The author states in various places in his Talmudic works and in the introduction to the present work that the <i>Ma’assé beréschîth</i> is physics. See above, p. 9 and 10.",
    "fn.141": "Literally: <i>among the people of the Law</i> or of the revealed religion; the author means by <span dir=\"rtl\">אהל אלשריעה̈</span> the doctors of all the religious sects.",
    "fn.142": "The expressions of <i>male</i> and <i>female</i> that one encounters here and there among the Neoplatonists and Gnostics are not precisely those used by Plato. The latter employs, among other terms, to designate matter, the words <i>nurse</i> (<i>τίθνη</i>), <i>mother</i>, etc., while form, that is to say the intelligent principle or <i>reason</i> (<i>λόγος</i>), is presented by him as the <i>father</i> of the universe. See the <i>Timaeus</i>, p. 49 <i>a</i>, 28 <i>a</i> and <i>passim</i>. — Ibn-Roshd also attributes to Plato the designation of matter and form by the words <i>female</i> and <i>male</i>. In his Epitome of the Organon (towards the end of the book of Demonstration, corresponding to the Posterior Analytics), speaking of the inconvenience presented by the figures and images employed in philosophical reasoning, he cites as an example Plato, <i>who says that matter is the female, and form is the male, an assertion that is far from making the essence of matter understood</i>. <span dir=\"rtl\">مثال ذلك قول افلاطون في المادة انها الانثى \nوفي الصورة انها الذكر فان هذا القول بعيد من ان يفهم جوهر المادة</span>  The passage that follows (from the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ואנת תעלם</span> to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי אלעלם אלטביעי</span>) appears to be a parenthesis, having no other purpose than to justify the image employed by Plato.",
    "fn.143": "That is to say, privation considered in relation to a <i>determined</i> form.",
    "fn.144": "See the physics of Aristotle, book I, ch. 6 and 7. Cf. <span dir=\"rtl\">רוח חן</span>, ch. 9.",
    "fn.145": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">عزب</span>, which means <i>to be removed</i>, <i>to be set apart</i>, and is constructed with the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">ען</span>, is here employed by the author in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">صعب</span> (to be difficult) and constructed like the latter verb.",
    "fn.146": "The lesson <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמראד בנא</span> that we have adopted is based on six mss. from the Bodleian library; the two mss. from Leyde have: <span dir=\"rtl\">בה</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בנא</span>.",
    "fn.147": "That is to say, its judgment has come before God, who has had <i>knowledge</i> of the sins of Babel and has decreed its punishment.",
    "fn.148": "See this I<sup>st</sup> part, ch. XXI, and II<sup>nd</sup> part, ch. XLI.",
    "fn.149": "That is to say, of the highest sphere. On the number of the spheres, see ch. IV of the II<sup>e</sup> part of this work.",
    "fn.150": "Literally: <i>a very great reciprocal struggle for superiority</i>.",
    "fn.151": "See what the author says about negative attributes in chap. LX of this I<sup>re</sup> part.",
    "fn.152": "We have printed in the text <span dir=\"rtl\">מלא העמר לאחד</span> (a full Omer <i>for each one</i>), as borne by almost all the Arabic and Hebrew mss. that we have had before our eyes, although the quotation is inaccurate. This is a lapse of memory that must undoubtedly be attributed to the author himself. See the commentaries of Joseph Caspi and Ephodi.",
    "fn.153": "See above, chap. X, page 58, note 2.",
    "fn.154": "Almost all the manuscripts, both Arabic and Hebrew, bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">ותרם התבה מעל הארץ</span>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">התבה</span> is not found in the passage that the author had in view. Here again we have deemed it necessary to reproduce, in our Arabic text, the quotation as it appears to have been made from memory by the author himself, although it is inaccurate.",
    "fn.155": "The citation <span dir=\"rtl\">מתוך העפר</span> found in the Ar. mss., as well as in the two Hebrew versions, is inaccurate; one must read: <span dir=\"rtl\">מן העפר</span>.",
    "fn.156": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חטׄ</span>, see above, ch. VIII, p. 52, note 2.",
    "fn.157": "We have written, in our Arabic text, <span dir=\"rtl\">וׂגלאלה̈</span> with <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>, as do the majority of the manuscripts; some bear <span dir=\"rtl\">רפעה̈ גׁלאלה̈</span>, which would need to be translated as <i>elevation in terms of majesty.</i> Ibn-Tibbon appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">רפעה̈ מנזלה̈ וׂגלאלה̈ ועזה̈</span>, as can be read immediately thereafter.",
    "fn.158": "That is to say, to a word that should have only one well-determined meaning, especially when it applies to God. The author responds here, once and for all, to an objection that could also be made regarding the explanations he provides for many other homonyms, and he refers to the chapters where he will discuss the attributes of God, which, however numerous they may be in biblical language, always express only one thing, <i>the divine essence</i>.",
    "fn.159": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">מענאה אלאול</span> signify <i>the primitive idea</i> expressed by the verb; by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מׄתאלה אלאול</span>, the author designates the <i>principal meaning</i> in which the verb is ordinarily employed. In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, these last words are rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">ועקר הנחתו</span>; it is better to read <span dir=\"rtl\">ודמיונו הראשון</span>, as several mss. do [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.160": "The author, in choosing this example, has neglected the grammatical sense of the passage, for the subject of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">עברו</span> is <span dir=\"rtl\">יין</span> (wine); but he considered the quoted words as equivalent to these: <i>and as a man who has drunk wine immoderately</i>.",
    "fn.161": "All the mss. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">תׄכטי</span>, which must be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">تَخَطَّى</span>, like the <sup>V</sup> form of the root <span dir=\"rtl\">خطو</span> meaning: <i>to take steps, to advance, to pass before</i>. Ibn-Tibbon translated <span dir=\"rtl\">שיחטיא</span>, taking <span dir=\"rtl\">תׄכטי</span> in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">תׄכטא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(تخطا)</span>, <i>to miss the mark</i>. Although nothing is more common in the mss. than to confuse the <i>hamzéd</i> verbs with the verbs having quiescent letters, a thing which is also done in vulgar Arabic, it has seemed to us more in conformity with the entirety of all that follows to take <span dir=\"rtl\">תכטי</span> in the sense of: <i>to pass before</i>, <i>to pass over</i>.",
    "fn.162": "The author appears to be alluding to a passage from the Talmudic treatise <i>Rôsch ha-schanâ</i> (fol. 17 <i>b</i>), where Rabbi Io’hanan explains the biblical passage in question in the sense that God <i>enveloped his face:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">ויעבר ה׳ על פניו ויקרא א״ר יוחנן</span> ·⋯ <span dir=\"rtl\">מלמד שנתעטף כשליח צבור וכוי</span>. Although this explanation is far from the one that Maimonides will provide, it serves to show that the ancient doctors considered the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">פניו</span> as referring to God.",
    "fn.163": "Book I, treatise <i>Yesodé ha-Torâ</i>, ch. I, § 10.",
    "fn.164": "That is to say in the passage: <i>And the Lord passed before his face</i>.",
    "fn.165": "See below, ch. LIV.",
    "fn.166": "Literally: <i>behind this</i>, that is to say beyond what he perceived.",
    "fn.167": "The word that we have spelled <span dir=\"rtl\">יבחל</span> is written in several mss. <span dir=\"rtl\">יכׄתל</span> (with a <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄ</span> punctuated), which must be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">يختلّ</span> when considering it as the VIII<sup>th</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">خَلّ</span> offerings, in the sense of: <i>being diminished, pierced, defective</i>. In some mss. one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">יכתל</span> (without a point on the <span dir=\"rtl\">כ</span>) and this lesson is perhaps the true one, although in the mss. one cannot attach importance to the absence of diacritical points, such that the pronunciation remains always doubtful. <span dir=\"rtl\">יכתל</span> (pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">يكتل</span>) would be the VIII<sup>th</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">كُلّ</span> having the sense of: <i>becoming weary, growing dull</i>. Both lessons are admissible in our passage. — As for the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יהלך</span>, the two Hebrew translators have rendered it by <span dir=\"rtl\">ימות</span>, <i>he will die</i>, considering as the subject of this verb the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כל אנסאן כאמל</span>; but it is far more rational to refer the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יהלך</span> to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לאדראכה</span> <i>his perception</i>, all the more so since the author does not seem to mean that man, in seeking to perceive what is inaccessible to him, exposes himself to death.",
    "fn.168": "See below, ch. XXXII.",
    "fn.169": "By <span dir=\"rtl\">מצׄאף</span>, <i>annexe</i>, one understands a word that is in the <i>construct state</i>, that is to say, which is followed by a genitive.",
    "fn.170": "That is to say to something which is determined by the name of God in the genitive. In short, the author means that the Chaldaic paraphrase admits certain ellipses, in order to avoid anthropomorphisms. [[t:1]] [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.171": "All the mss., with the exception of one of the two mss. of Leyde, bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלרוס</span> (action noun of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">دام</span>, <i>to desire</i>, <i>to seek</i>), which Ibn-Tibbon has very well rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">וההשׁתדלות</span> (not <span dir=\"rtl\">וההשׂתכלות</span> as some editions have it). It is wrongly that R. Schem-Tob-ibn-Falaquera (<i>Morè ha-Moré</i>, appendix, p. 149) criticizes Ibn-Tibbon's version by admitting the reading of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלחס</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלרוס</span>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלחס</span> which we have only encountered in one of the mss. of Leyde, presents no plausible meaning here. Al-’Harizi's version, which expresses the reading of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלחס</span>, and which bears <span dir=\"rtl\">וההרגש כלו</span>, is devoid of sense here.",
    "fn.172": "The author means that, even granting the intervention of the sense of sight, it is not necessarily the case that Moses actually saw, in some <i>created</i> phenomenon, the reflection of divine majesty; for everything could have been merely a vision, and existed only in Moses' exalted imagination.",
    "fn.173": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">عدم</span> (pl. <span dir=\"rtl\">أحلام</span>), as seen above, ch. XVII, corresponds to the Aristotelian term <i>στερησις</i>, <i>privation</i>, which designates <i>non-being</i>, or the negation of what is positive. Thus, in the biblical passage cited by the author, <i>evil</i> and <i>darkness</i> are nothing other than the <i>privation</i> or <i>negation</i> of good and light.",
    "fn.174": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">או</span> several mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אי</span>, <i>that is to say, knowledge</i>. and this is the lesson followed by the two Hebrew translators who have <span dir=\"rtl\">כלומר</span>; the words <span dir=\"rtl\">תׄכאת מואעידה</span> would then be the explanation of <span dir=\"rtl\">חלול אמרה</span>.",
    "fn.175": "By the word <span dir=\"rtl\">באטבא</span>, accusative adverbial (for which the manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄפאב</span>), the author means that the suffix <span dir=\"rtl\">ך</span> in <span dir=\"rtl\">עמך</span> refers to the people of Israel, and that it is to them that the prophet Zechariah addresses his words. Ibn Tibbon translated <span dir=\"rtl\">מדבריס</span> (<i>who spoke</i> to Israel), which is inaccurate; perhaps he read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמכׄאטין</span>, a variant reading found in the margin of one of the Leiden manuscripts [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.176": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">נפודׄ אלאמר</span> (<i>the transmission</i> or <i>the divulgation of the thing</i>) have been rendered, in the version of Ibn-Tibbon, by <span dir=\"rtl\">עבור המצוה</span>, <i>the transmission of the command</i>, and in that of Al-’Harizi by <span dir=\"rtl\">קיום מצות המלך</span>, <i>the execution of the king's command</i>. According to this, Maimonides would have understood the words <span dir=\"rtl\">דבר המלכה</span> in the sense of: <i>the command concerning the queen</i>. We believe that the two translators were mistaken in giving the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר</span> the meaning of <i>command</i>, <i>order</i>; this word simply means <i>thing, matter</i>. The meaning that we have given to the cited verse is simpler and more natural, and it is doubtless in this sense that Maimonides understood it; it is in the same sense that the passage in question has been interpreted by Rashi, Ibn-Ezra, and other commentators [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.177": "Literally: <i>whose instrument, to transmit their will,</i> (<i>is</i>) <i>speech</i>. We have written <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתי אֿלתהם</span>, as it is found in all the mss.; but, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתי</span>, it would be more correct to say <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄין</span>.",
    "fn.178": "The two Hebrew versions read <span dir=\"rtl\">ואין לו דבור</span>; in the original Arabic <span dir=\"rtl\">לה</span> is perhaps implied, but it is found in no ms. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">לן</span> is also missing in several mss. of Ibn-Tibbon's version.",
    "fn.179": "See below, ch. LXV.",
    "fn.180": "All the mss. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתי תבעהא</span>, and it is thus without a doubt that the author wrote, thinking of the feminine word <span dir=\"rtl\">שכינה</span>, although it would have been more logical to say <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי תבעה</span>; for these words refer to <span dir=\"rtl\">ארתפאע</span> which is masculine.",
    "fn.181": "Literally: <i>It is of the voice that it is said that it was</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">מתהלך</span> (<i>spreading</i>). The author means that one should not believe that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מתהלך</span> refers to God <i>strolling</i> in the garden.",
    "fn.182": "Ibn-Tibbon translates:<span dir=\"rtl\">אם להתפשט הענין</span>; Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">אם לדבר המתפשט</span>. The two translators have taken <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאמר</span> in the sense of <i>thing;</i> but it is far more probable that the author employs this word here in the sense of <i>word, order, command</i>.",
    "fn.183": "Literally: <i>What</i> (<i>is done on the part</i>) <i>of the animal by walking</i>. In the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי דׄלך מן אלחיואן באלהליכה</span>, a verb must be understood; one of the two Leyden mss. adds in the margin the word <span dir=\"rtl\">יכון</span> after <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי</span>, and the two Hebrew versions have <span dir=\"rtl\">יהיה</span>. In two Oxford mss., one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי כני דׄלך</span> (what <i>has been</i> named, designated); but this reading is hardly plausible [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.184": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">וילך</span>, which pertains to <i>the Eternal</i>, can also, according to the author, pertain to <i>wrath</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אף</span>).",
    "fn.185": "It is to be recalled that, in the cited passage, it is a matter of Ahron and Miriam.",
    "fn.186": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי דׄלך אלמכאן</span>, which are not rendered in the two Hebrew versions, are found in all the Arabic mss. that we have consulted [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.187": "See above, at the beginning of ch. VIII, page 52, note 1.",
    "fn.188": "The Hebrew word <span dir=\"rtl\">שכינה</span> itself, which means <i>residence</i>, and which designates the presence of divine majesty, is derived from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">שכן</span> in the metaphorical sense under consideration here.",
    "fn.189": "Literally: In whatever place it remains permanent. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">דאמת</span> refers to both <span dir=\"rtl\">סכינתה</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">ענאיתה</span>, and it is wrongly that Ibn-Tibbon added here the word <span dir=\"rtl\">השכינה</span>, of which none of the mss. of the Arabic text offers any trace. Al-’Harizi's translation is more faithful here [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.190": "The words of the text literally mean: <i>Or to anything in which Providence is permanent;</i> but the author, by inverting the words, has expressed himself here in a manner that could seem somewhat inexact; for the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">שכן</span> does not apply to the thing, but to Providence. The entire passage means that the verb in question applies to the majesty and divine Providence manifesting itself in any place, or even to Providence manifesting itself over any individual. Ibn-Tibbon, who, as we have stated in the previous note, added the word <span dir=\"rtl\">השכינה</span>, did not fully grasp the meaning of the passage, and, even assuming that this word should be added to the Arabic text, it would be necessary to read immediately after <span dir=\"rtl\">או בכל אמר</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">או לכל אמר</span>.",
    "fn.191": "The first of these three examples relates to the manifestation of the <i>Schekhînâ;</i> the second to the divine Providence manifesting itself over the entire people of Israel; the third appears to relate to the Providence manifesting itself over an individual object, that is to say, over the bush that, though entirely aflame, was not consumed, or to the divine benevolence that Moses, in this verse, invokes upon the tribe of Joseph. The commentators generally see in the third example, as in the first, an allusion to the manifestation of the <i>Schekhînâ</i>. See also Abravanel, in his commentary on Deuteronomy, at the verse in question.",
    "fn.192": "That is to say, the sentence of the ancient rabbis. See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Ichamôth</i> fol. 71 <i>a; Babâ Meci’â</i>, fol. 31 <i>b</i>, and <i>passim</i>. — The author interrupts here again his explanations of homonyms to discuss the meaning that should be attached to the <i>movement</i> attributed to God. This chapter and the following one, which in several manuscripts form only one, are connected to the previous chapter; for it is undoubtedly with regard to the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">הלך</span> (to go) that the author is led to speak of movement.",
    "fn.193": "That is to say with regard to the subject that concerns us here; the author intends to speak of the anthropomorphisms which Holy Scripture uses in speaking of God. [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.194": "All the mss. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אגׄמע</span>, which is here used for the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">אגׄמעיי</span>.",
    "fn.195": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אליה</span> depends on <span dir=\"rtl\">באלאצׄאפה̈</span>, which is implied; Ibn-Tibbon repeated the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בערך</span>.",
    "fn.196": "Literally: <i>But if they imagined that such human perfection is lacking in God, this would be, in their view, an imperfection with regard to Him</i>.",
    "fn.197": "That is to say, what is eliminated through transpiration. Cf. the <i>Canon</i> of Avicenna, Arabic text, vol. I, p. 75, line 27: <span dir=\"rtl\">انما اقامتها الاستبدال ما</span>  <span dir=\"rtl\">يتحلل منها وهو الغذا</span>.",
    "fn.198": "Literally: <i>Eating and drinking would, in their view, be an imperfection with regard to God, and movement would not be an imperfection with regard to Him</i>.",
    "fn.199": "Cf. Arist., <i>Phys.</i>, 1. VIII, ch. V: A <i>ναγϰαῐον δή το ϰινούµενον ἃπαν εἶαι διαιρττὀν εἰς ἀεἰ διαιρετά</i>. Compare also what Aristotle says about movement with regard to the soul, treatise <i>On the Soul</i>, 1. I, ch. III. See the details in the introduction to the II<sup>nd</sup> part of the <i>Guide</i>, at the 7<sup>th</sup> Proposition. — Ibn-Tibbon's version substitutes here and in several other passages the word <span dir=\"rtl\">גשם</span>, <i>body</i>, for the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">עטׄס</span>, <i>magnitude</i>, which refers only to geometric body, i.e., <i>the space circumscribed by limits</i>.",
    "fn.200": "Of the eight mss. that we have consulted, there are six that bear <span dir=\"rtl\">מן שאנה</span>: one of the Oxford mss. has <span dir=\"rtl\">מא מן שאנה</span>, and one of the two Leyden mss. <span dir=\"rtl\">מא שאנה</span>. According to this variant, the translation should be: <i>that which has as a condition movement</i>.",
    "fn.201": "That is to say, by conforming to the ideas and language of the common people. [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.202": "That is to say, on account of the ideas and expressions to which the common people have become accustomed [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.203": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אליהם מנהס</span> are found placed in all the mss. at the end of the sentence; <span dir=\"rtl\">אליהם</span> refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">אלסאבקה̈</span>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">מנהם</span> is connected to <span dir=\"rtl\">ואזאלה̈</span>. The regular construction would be: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואזאלה̈ מנהם הדׄה <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> אוהאם אלסאבקה̈ אליהם מן סן אלטפוליה̈</span>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלסאבקה̈ אליהם</span> literally mean: <i>what comes to them in advance</i>; it is a matter, as Ibn-Falaquera explains very well (while criticizing Ibn-Tibbon's version), of <i>preconceived opinions</i> to which one abandons oneself at the outset, before having reflected. See <i>Moré-ha-Moré</i>, p. 150.",
    "fn.204": "On the use of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">סריאני</span> to designate both of the two main <i>Aramæan</i> dialects, Chaldean and Syriac, see my edition of R. Tan'hûm's Commentary on the book of Habakkuk (in vol. XII of M. Cahen's Bible), p. 99, note 14 [[fn:14]]. The author speaks, in this chap., of the periphrases employed by Onkelos, in his celebrated Chaldean version of the Pentateuch, to avoid anthropomorphisms relative to <i>movement</i>.",
    "fn.205": "All the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אעתני</span>, with the exception of one of the two Leyden mss., which has <span dir=\"rtl\">אעתנא</span>, which is more correct; it is evident that it is an infinitive, and that one must pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">عشاء</span>.",
    "fn.206": "The majority of Ar. and Heb. mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחזי</span>; some bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ואדון</span>, as is indeed found in our editions of Onkelos' paraphrase.",
    "fn.207": "Literally: <i>he has opened </i> to us.",
    "fn.208": "<span dir=\"rtl\">והו אלצחיח</span> literally: <i>and this is the truth</i>, that is to say Onkelos was right in acting thus. Ibn-Tibbon translates <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא ספור האמת</span>, which does not yield a suitable meaning; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ספור</span> is superfluous. Al-’Harizi has translated it better: <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא האמת והנכון</span>.",
    "fn.209": "Literally: <i>the description</i> or <i>relation of what has occurred in your existing things</i>. The author means: as it was a matter of a real fact here, and not words spoken in a nocturnal vision, Onkelos, faithful to his system of avoiding anthropomorphisms, had to modify the terms of the text [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.210": "All the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אבקאה בהסבה</span> with the masculine suffix, which is irregular; for the suffix relates grammatically to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאמור אלכׄיאליה̈</span>, whereas the author has connected it with <span dir=\"rtl\">מא קיל</span>.",
    "fn.211": "We shall see, in another place (II<sup>e</sup> part, ch. XLV), that the author establishes, in prophetic revelations, different degrees, some higher than others, and which, according to him, are characterized by the expressions noted in our passage. The author finds an allusion to these distinctions in the procedure of Onkelos, which he has just indicated.",
    "fn.212": "See above, ch. XXI, p. 78, note 1.",
    "fn.213": "See above, ch. XIII, p. 63.",
    "fn.214": "On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄארחה̈</span>, see above, p. 36, note 1.",
    "fn.215": "Several commentators, who have only had the Hebrew version before their eyes, have understood this passage in the sense that, according to Onkelos, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">רגליו</span> (his feet) is a <i>denomination</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כנוי</span>) of the throne of God; for Onkelos renders the words <i>and under his feet</i> by these: <i>and under the throne of his glory</i>. According to this explanation, the suffix, in <span dir=\"rtl\">רגליו</span>, would refer to God; but the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄמיר</span> can leave no uncertainty about the meaning, and the author states very positively that, according to Onkelos, the <i>suffix</i> in <span dir=\"rtl\">רגליו</span> refers to the <i>throne</i>, that is to say that the words <i>under his feet</i> mean <i>under the feet of the throne</i>, which Onkelos renders by <i>under the throne of his glory</i>. Maimonides thus appears to say that Onkelos considered the idea of the <i>throne</i> to be implicit, and that he explained the text as if it read <span dir=\"rtl\">ותחת רגלי כסא כבודו</span>; but that by introducing the words <i>throne of glory</i> into his translation, he suppressed the word <span dir=\"rtl\">רגלֵי</span>, which had become superfluous.",
    "fn.216": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מַסְתּוִיָּא (مستويًا)</span> is imitated from these words of the Koran (chap. VII, <small>V</small>. 52): <span dir=\"rtl\">استوى على العرش</span> , <i>he established himself on the throne</i>. The commentators of the Koran give to the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">استوى</span> the meaning of <i>to seize, dominate</i>, or that of <i>to settle</i>. Ibn-Falaquera, in his critical notes on Ibn-Tibbon's version, cites the aforementioned passage from the Koran. See <i>Moré-ha-Moré</i>, p. 150.",
    "fn.217": "See above, p. 58, note 2.",
    "fn.218": "It must be recalled that in the passage in question (Exodus, 24, 9-11) it is a matter of a vision of the seventy elders, called <i>the elect from among the sons of Israel</i>. Cf. above, p. 48 and 49.",
    "fn.219": "That is to say, the explanation of the meaning of the allegory.",
    "fn.220": "Literally: <i>He decides</i> or <i>he decrees</i>, that is to say, he declares in an absolute manner, by his manner of translating, that corporality must be set aside.",
    "fn.221": "That is to say by taking here the word <span dir=\"rtl\">רגל</span> in the sense of <i>cause</i>, which was attributed to it at the beginning of this chapter.",
    "fn.222": "The author sees in the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מעשה</span>, <i>work, labor</i>, an allusion to the matter <i>receiving the action</i> of the form, and which, according to him, is designated by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לבנת הספיר</span>, <i>splendor of the sapphire</i>, as he will explain.",
    "fn.223": "The author here does not take the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ספיר</span> in the sense of <i>sapphire</i> properly so called, but discerns therein, in general, a <i>transparent</i> substance. Immediately thereafter, he does substitute the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">بلور</span>, <i>crystal</i>. The Hebrew translators of the Middle Ages oft employ the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ספירי</span> to render the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">دمشق</span>, which corresponds, in the Arabic versions, to the Greek word <i>διαφανεὶς</i>, <i>transparent</i>.",
    "fn.224": "Voy. Aristotle, treatise <i>on the Soul</i>, book II, chapter 7: <i>Ἄχρουν δ’ἐστὶ τὸ διαφανὲς</i>, <i>ϰ. τ. λ</i>.",
    "fn.225": "Literally: <i>she would not show all the colors behind her;</i> the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אורי (أورى)</span>  is a vulgar form for <span dir=\"rtl\">أرى</span>  (IV<sup>th</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">رأى</span> ).",
    "fn.226": "That is to say, in what condition it stands with regard to God. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ויחסו</span>, which is found herein in the version of Ibn-Tibbon, has caused several commentators to mistake the sense of this passage by pronouncing <span dir=\"rtl\">וְיׅחֲסוּ</span> as a verb; one must pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">וְיַחֲסוֹ</span> as a substantive, with a suffix (<i>and its relation</i>), as the commentator Joseph Caspi does rightly observe, or rather read <span dir=\"rtl\">ויחוסו</span>, as several manuscripts bear.",
    "fn.227": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon reads <span dir=\"rtl\">להיותו</span>; but one must translate <span dir=\"rtl\">והיותו</span>, for the Arabic manuscripts generally have <span dir=\"rtl\">וכונהא</span>, with the exception of one of the two Leyden manuscripts, which reads <span dir=\"rtl\">לכונהא</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">וכונהא</span> (<i>et esse eam</i>) indicates, as does the preceding, the object of the perception of the <i>Elect</i>.",
    "fn.228": "It is matter that is the principle of all beings that <i>are born</i> and <i>perish</i>. — We have already stated above (p. 60) that the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כון</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">פסאר</span> correspond to the Greek words <i>γἑνεσιϛ</i> and <i>φθορά</i>.",
    "fn.229": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">بدع</span> or (in the IV<sup>th</sup> form) <span dir=\"rtl\">أبدع</span>, <i>novum produxit</i>, implies the idea of creation <i>ex nihilo</i>.",
    "fn.230": "See the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chap. XXVI.",
    "fn.231": "See at the beginning of the chapter indicated in the previous note.",
    "fn.232": "See above, at the beginning of chap. XXVI.",
    "fn.233": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אראדתה</span> is preceded, in one of the Leyde mss., by the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">ענד</span>, which is not found in any of the other mss.",
    "fn.234": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">באיה</span> (<i>in it</i>, that is, in God) is found in all mss.; similarly, in the mss. of the Hebrew version of Ibn-Tibbon, one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">אגרואיי אעוביא עוביאיו ארויי</span>, and it was wrong to suppress the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אר</span> in the editions.",
    "fn.235": "See below, chapter XXXIX.",
    "fn.236": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואסתמראר</span> (with <i>rêsch</i>), some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ואסתמדאד</span> (with <i>daleth</i>); Cf. this I<sup>re</sup> part, towards the end of chapters LXIII and LXIX (fol. 82 <i>b</i>, line 15, and fol. 91 <i>a</i>, lig. 3 and following of our Arabic text).",
    "fn.237": "See above, chap. I, p. 35, note 1.",
    "fn.238": "Vide  Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Babâ Bathrâ</i>, fol. 22 <i>a</i>. It concerns a doctor who, extolling the superiority of Rabâ’s teaching over that of Abaï, spake unto his pupils: «Instead of gnawing bones with Abaï, come ye and eat fat meat with Rabâ.» Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תא אכלו</span>, as one reads in all the Arabic and Hebrew manuscripts of the <i>Guide</i>, the editions of the Talmud bear, more correctly, <span dir=\"rtl\">תו אכלו</span>.",
    "fn.239": "This refers to the book of <i>Kohéleth</i> (Ecclesiastes); the cited passage is found in the <i>Midrasch rabbâ</i> on <i>Kohéleth</i>, chap. III, vers. 13, and it is conceived in these terms: <span dir=\"rtl\">כל אכילה ושתיה שנאמרה במגלה הזאת בתורה ובמעשיס טובים הכתוב מדבר</span>.",
    "fn.240": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">מעיני העדה</span> mean, according to Maimonides, <i>by the principals of the community</i>, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עינים</span> being used, in his view, like the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">احیان</span>, <i>the principals</i> or <i>chiefs</i>, and it is in the same sense that Jonathan would have taken the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מַעַיְנֵי</span>, which is of the same root as <span dir=\"rtl\">עין</span> (eye, source). Ibn Tibbon, in the notes he appended to certain passages of his version of the <i>Guide</i>, which have remained unpublished, criticizes Maimonides' opinion in the following terms: [[fn:1]]",
    "fn.241": "Literally: <i>Behold;</i> on <span dir=\"rtl\">ארי</span>, used as an imperative, see above, p. 19, note 2.",
    "fn.242": "The author, ere he concluds his explanations of homonyms, which he does resume only in chapter XXXVII, enters into general considerations upon man’s faculty for perceiving intelligible things, upon the limits of this faculty, upon the difficulties of approaching metaphysical studies, and upon the necessity of first accepting  <i>a priori</i> certain doctrines relative to the nature of the Divinity, lest one should mistake certain expressions whereby Scripture appears to attribute human passions unto God.",
    "fn.243": "That is to say, there are things that human intelligence can neither perceive directly, nor indirectly, nor through an auxiliary cause. According to the commentator Schem-Tob, the words <i>by a cause</i> are an allusion to divine inspiration. Indeed, there are limits that, according to Scripture, Moses himself was unable to transcend, as will be seen later [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.244": "Literally: <i>but rather the doors of their perception are closed before her</i>, that is to say before the intelligence.",
    "fn.245": "The author is referring to the spheres and their movements, which are in part demonstrable and in part hypothetical, as he will explain in other places.",
    "fn.246": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon is herein somewhat corrupted in most editions; the manuscripts bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">והתגברות השכל לבקש אמתתם ולחפשה נמצא בכל כת מעינת מבני אדם ובכל זמן</span>. One perceivs that Ibn-Tibbon referrs the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">ענהא</span> to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חקיקתהא</span>, so that one should translate: <i>and for</i> <small>IT</small> <i>to scrutinise;</i> one preferrs to refer <span dir=\"rtl\">ענהא</span> to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אשיא</span>, <i>the things</i>, as Al-’Harizi has done, who translats <span dir=\"rtl\">והחקירה עליהם</span>. The latter furthermore differs in another point from the version of Ibn-Tibbon, for he has considered the word <span dir=\"rtl\">והמלט</span> as a <i>preterite</i>, and one should translate, according to him, <i>the intellect makes efforts to seek their reality, and the researches into these things are found</i>, etc. This translation is likewise admissible; it does seem unto one, however, that to translate thus, one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">ויחלט</span> in the <i>future tense</i>.",
    "fn.247": "Ibn Tibbon translates <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא הכזבה</span>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מגׄאדׄבה̈</span> he appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">מכאדׄבה̈</span>, <i>démenti</i>, which I have not found in any manuscript [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.248": "By <i>demonstrative contradiction</i>, the author means here <i>demonstrative reasoning</i> based on false principles, or sophistical reasoning. Ibn-Tibbon renders the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלענאר אלברהאני</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">המחלקת המופתית</span>, and in his glossary of technical terms, he states that one thus calls <i>the contradiction that attacks what has been established by a demonstration:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">החולק על מה שבא עליו מופת תקרא מחלקתו מחלקת מופתית</span>. Ibn-Roschd, in his abridgment of the Organon, toward the end of the Book of <i>Demonstration</i> or the <i>Posterior Analytics</i>, opposes the reasoning called <i>demonstrative contradiction</i> to that which is called <span dir=\"rtl\">مخاطبة التعليم والتعلم</span> (<i>oratio docendi et discendi</i>), and which has the purpose of <i>instructing</i> by means of demonstration (Cf. Arist., <i>Refutation of the Sophists</i>, chap. II: <i>λόγοι διδασϰαλιϰοὶ ϰαὶ ἀποδειϰτιϰοί</i>). The reasoning called <span dir=\"rtl\">مخاطبة العناد البرهاني</span> is defined by Ibn-Roschd in these terms:",
    "fn.249": "This celebrated commentator upon Aristotle, who flourished at the close of the second and the commencement of the third century, enjoyed amongst the Arabs, as amongst the Greeks, a very great authority. Maimonides, in a letter addressed to the Hebrew translator of his <i>Guide for the Perplexed</i>, R. Samuel Ibn-Tibbon, does particularly recommend unto the latter the study of Alexander’s commentaries. Concerning the Arabic versions of Alexander’s numerous works, vide  Casiri, <i>Biblioth. arab. hisp.</i>, vol. I, p. 243 et seq.; Wenrich, <i>De Auctorum Grœcorum versionibus etc.</i>, p. 273 et seq.",
    "fn.250": "Literally: <i>the desire to dominate</i> or <i>to take precedence, and</i> (<i>that</i>) <i>to conquer</i> or <i>to prevail</i> (<i>φιλονϛιϰία</i>).",
    "fn.251": "That is to say, <i>amongst the Greeks</i>. The author, by these words, does anticipate that which he shall state a little further on concerning the powerful influence exercised upon most men by the perusal of religious books and the habit of taking literally the words of Scripture containing images and allegories. This cause of error, he would imply, exists only amongst those who believe in the authority of sacred books and who profess a religion revealed by God. Yet this source of error existed likewise amongst the Greeks; for the populace admitted the truth of mythological fables. Aristotle himself speaks here and there of the power of habit and beliefs, which are sometimes an impediment to the knowledge of truth. Vide , e.g., <i>Metaph.</i>, bk. II, chap. III.",
    "fn.252": "Intelligible perceptions do not have their seat in matter, but they are <i>attached</i> to matter through the faculty of thought, which is a physical faculty. The intellect in itself, which receives the impression of intelligible things, is entirely impassible (<i>ἀπαθήϛ</i>) and free from all mixture (<i>ἀμιγήϛ</i>) with any material form. Other Arab philosophers, and notably Ibn-Roschd, express themselves like our author, saying that the intellect is not in matter, but is <i>attached</i> to matter in some way. See Ibn-Falaquera, <i>Moré-ha-Moré</i>, p. 17.",
    "fn.253": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אנעם</span>, which here means <i>intendit, summa conatus est</i>, one of the Leyden mss. reads <span dir=\"rtl\">אמען</span> (<i>aller trop loin, s’appliquer fortement</i>).",
    "fn.254": "The author means that the faculty of thinking is in the same position as all other physical faculties in this regard.",
    "fn.255": "That is to say, in the perceptions having as their object the metaphysical things to which <i>the intellect in act</i> seeks to elevate itself.",
    "fn.256": "Ibn-Tibbon translates, in error, <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא תתחיל</span> (if you do not <i>begin</i>); the same error is repeated a little further on and in other places in Ibn-Tibbon's version.",
    "fn.257": "The author here alludes to a celebrated allegorical passage in the two Talmuds, in which mention is made of four doctors who entered into paradise (of science), namely: Ben-’Azaï, ben-Zôma, A’her (Elischa’), and R.’Akiba. Of the first two, one perished for having suffered his gaze to penetrate too far, the other was afflicted (with madness); A’her ravaged the plants; R. ’Akiba alone <i>entered in peace and departed in peace</i>. Vide  Jerusalem Talmud, treatise <i>’Haghîgâ</i>, chap. II; Babylonian Talmud, same treatise, fol. 14 <i>b</i>. The author has followed the redaction of the Jerusalem Talmud; in that of Babylon, the words <i>entered in peace</i> are not read.",
    "fn.258": "That is to say, you wreak havoc and manage to deny everything, just as A'her did [[fn:12]].",
    "fn.259": "Several editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version bear <span dir=\"rtl\">כח הראה</span>; but we must read, according to the Arabic, <span dir=\"rtl\">הרוח הרואה</span>, as do, in fact, the mss. of the Hebrew version and the <i>princeps edition. The visual spirit</i>, among the Scholastics: <i>spiritus visivus</i> (<i>ὀπτιϰὸν πνεῦμα</i>, Alex. Aphrod., <i>Problem</i>., I, 74), is, according to the ancients, the most subtle of the <i>animal spirits</i> that concur to form sensations, and which have their common center in the brain. In the XXIX<sup>th</sup> chapter of the II<sup>nd</sup> part of the <i>Guide</i>, our author also speaks of the <i>visual spirit</i> which becomes troubled and weakened by the agitations of the soul. — Ibn-Roschd, in the analysis of the treatise <i>On Sense and the Sensible</i> (or more accurately <i>Parva naturalia</i>), speaking of the organ of sight, expresses himself thus:",
    "fn.260": "The author appears to have made an error of memory here; for, in our editions of the two Talmuds, the passage in question is not applied to A’her, but to one who was afflicted with madness, that is, in the Talmud of Jerusalem, to ben-’Azaï, and in the Talmud of Babylon, to ben-Zôma.",
    "fn.261": "See above, chap. XXX, p. 101.",
    "fn.262": "The passage cited here is reported in the Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>’Haghîgâ</i>, fol. 13 <i>a</i>, according to the book of <i>Ben-Sîrâ</i>, or of Jesus, son of Sirach; cf. <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. VIII. It is indeed found in chap. III of the Ecclesiasticus, verses 21 and 22 of the Greek text: Χ<i>αλεπώτερά σου μή ζήτει, ϰαὶ ἰσχυρότερά σου μὴ ἐξέταζε</i>. <i>ἅ προσετάγη σοι</i>, <i>ταῦτα διανοῦ</i>. <i>οὐ γάρ ἐστί σοι χρεία τῶν ϰρυπτῶν</i>. — In the citation of Maimonides, as it is found in all the Arabic mss. of the <i>Guide</i>, there are two words that differ from our editions of the Talmud, where one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">התבונן</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">דרוש</span>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">בנסתרות</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בנפלאות</span>.",
    "fn.263": "This passage, which is found in the <i>Mischnâ</i> (II<sup>nd</sup> part, treatise <i>’Haghîgâ</i>, chap. II, § 1), is conceived in the following terms: \"Whoever examines four things would have done better not to have come into the world; (these four things are:) what is above and what is below (the universe), what was before (the world) and what will be after. And whoever does not respect the glory of his Creator would have done better not to have come into the world.\"[[fn:1]]",
    "fn.264": "See below, chapters LXVIII and LXXII.",
    "fn.265": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">עטל (عطّل)</span> , which means <i>to strip, empty, or cause to cease</i>, is also employed in the sense of <i>denying religious dogma</i>. Al-Makrizi, in his Description of Egypt, reproaches Maimonides himself for having rendered the Jews <i>mo'attila</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(معطّلة)</span>. Silvestre de Sacy has several times translated the participle <span dir=\"rtl\">معطل</span> as <i>atheist;</i> however, he notes that there may be some rigor in translating it thus, for, he adds, the dogma of <span dir=\"rtl\">تعطيل</span> consists rather in denying the attributes of God, and presenting Him as inaccessible to human intelligence and foreign to the governance of the universe, than in directly denying His existence (see <i>Chrest. ar.</i>, 2<sup>nd</sup> edition, vol. I, p. 325, and vol. II, p. 96). The word <i>irreligion</i> seems to me to respond better than any other to the idea that Maimonides attaches to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">تعطيل</span> ; it is <i>the negation of what is written in religious books</i>, as can be seen in several other passages of the <i>Guide;</i> for example, II<sup>nd</sup> part, chapter XXV: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתכדׄיב ואלתעטיל לגׄמיע טׄואהר אלשריעה̈</span> \"The denial and <i>negation</i> of all the texts of the Law\"; and <i>ibid.</i>, chapter XXIX, toward the end: <span dir=\"rtl\">או לתעטיל מחץׄ וכפר בקואעד אלשריעה̈</span> \"... or else (these expressions lead) to <i>pure irreligion</i> and to deny the foundations of the Law.\"",
    "fn.266": "Literally: <i>These true opinions</i>.",
    "fn.267": "See above, chap. XXVI.",
    "fn.268": "That is to say, we have only followed the authority of tradition without entering into the substance of the matter.",
    "fn.269": "By <span dir=\"rtl\">(تصوُّر)</span> , the author appears to understand here in general everything that pertains to the realm of thought or <i>idea</i>, all metaphysical beings, as well as the ideas associated with them.",
    "fn.270": "Literally: <i>to what directs the mind towards its existence</i> (that of the ideal thing), <i>and not to the reality of its quiddity</i>. The preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי</span> that follows the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תצור</span> depends on the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אקחצר</span>, <i>one was limited, one</i> <i>was content;</i> it is the same for the second preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי</span> that precedes the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חקיקה̈</span>, and which depends grammatically on the same verb, although it is only there by a kind of <i>attraction</i>, for the meaning would require a different construction. The author means that, every time it was a matter of an <i>idea</i> or a metaphysical thing, one was content to use expressions that could make the mind suspect that this thing <i>is</i>, without examining what it really is. The translation of Ibn-Tibbon is a bit obscure; it reads: (ar.<span dir=\"rtl\">מא שיישר השכל אל</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נחו</span> (understood; <span dir=\"rtl\">ובכל ציור</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הספיק עמהם אמתת מהותו</span> (ar. <span dir=\"rtl\">מציאותו לא על</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עלי</span>. In several mss. one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">אל אמתת</span>, and the two prepositions, although quite distinct in the original ar., seem, according to the translation, to depend on the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">(וישר) יסדר</span>. The commentators thought that the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">מציאותו</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">מהותו</span> referred to God, and this is also the opinion of Al-’Harizi, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">שיורה הרעיון על מציאות</span> (read <span dir=\"rtl\">ובכל מחשבה וציור השכל כמו (במא הבורא לא להשיג אמתת מהותו</span>. But if the author had intended to speak of God in particular, he would have expressed himself more clearly, and he would not have failed, at least, to add after <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄודה</span> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תעאלי</span>. The meaning that we have given to this passage seems to us to be fully confirmed by the way the author expresses himself a little further on: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכדלך יתצור תלך אלאמור אלתי כאנת לה כיאלאת ומחאלאת בחקאיקהא ויפהם מאהיתהא</span>.",
    "fn.271": "Literally: <i>delivered, transmitted</i>. These words, which the author wrote in Hebrew, contain an allusion to this Talmudic passage (<i>’Haghîgâ</i>, fol. 13 <i>a</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">אין מוסרין סתרי תורה וכו׳</span>. See the following chapter towards the end.",
    "fn.272": "Literally: <i>when they</i> (these secrets) <i>give him insight into one another</i>, that is to say when he himself guesses them by combining them with one another. The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">בעצׁהא</span> refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">סתרי תורה</span>.",
    "fn.273": "See above, p. 9.",
    "fn.274": "Literally: <i>the quiddities of things as they are</i>.",
    "fn.275": "Literally: <i>that it should not be if it be not thus</i>. The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version commonly bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">שלא יהיה אלא אס כן אביאהו</span>; one must erase <span dir=\"rtl\">אס</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">אכיאהו</span>, and read: <span dir=\"rtl\">שלא יהיה אלא כן</span>, as indeed the MSS. have it. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי פצל</span> connect to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וסאכין לך</span>, which are at the beginning of the sentence.",
    "fn.276": "See above, chap. XXX, towards the end.",
    "fn.277": "These words are attributed to R. Siméon ben-Io’haï. See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Succâ</i>, fol. 45 <i>b; Sanhedrin</i>, fol. 97 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.278": "The commentators thus explain these latter questions: If the soul of man is separable from the body and exists after death as a <i>separate</i> substance, then 1° <i>how</i> is this existence, that is, does the soul of each man exist <i>individually</i>, or do all souls form only <i>one single substance?</i> 2° <i>by what means</i> does it attain immortality? Is it through philosophical speculation, or through the practice of religious precepts? And finally 3° <i>to what end</i>, that is, what is the outcome of the soul's permanence? Is it union with <i>the active intellect</i>, or union with God? Indeed, these are the questions that have primarily occupied the Arab Peripatetics, and which have given rise to interminable discussions among both Arab philosophers and Scholastics.",
    "fn.279": "Ibn-Falaquera cites as an example, among numbers, <i>the unit</i>, and among geometric figures, <i>the circle</i>. All the powers and roots of the number <i>one</i> are <i>one</i>, and this number gives us the idea of that which cannot be either multiplied or divided without ceasing to be what it is. It is the same with the circle; for the circumference, the image of the unit, cannot be either increased or diminished without ceasing to be a circumference, whereas a straight line can be increased and diminished while remaining a straight line. Thus, the number <i>one</i> and the <i>circumference</i> give us the idea of absolute unity, excluding the ideas of the multiple and the divisible, which we must exclude from the Divinity. If the author particularly insists on what <i>we must exclude from the Divinity</i>, it is to be remembered that, according to him, the attributes of God must be expressed by <i>negations</i>, and that he insists on the <i>negative attributes</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אלסואלב</span>), in order to establish, in its greatest purity, the principle of the unity of God. See below, chapter LVIII [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.280": "<span dir=\"rtl\">מלכה̈</span> signifies an <i>inherent quality</i>; the author means that, through these speculative matters, the mind becomes accustomed to the art of demonstration, which becomes, in a sense, an inherent faculty of its nature.",
    "fn.281": "<span dir=\"rtl\">In the expression al-dātiyya </span>literally: <i>by the things that are essential to it;</i> the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">lahu</span> refers, in my opinion, to <span dir=\"rtl\">al-ḥaqq</span>, <i>the truth</i>, and it is incorrect, it seems to me, that some commentators of Ibn Tibbon's version relate the pronoun <i>it</i> to <i>the demonstration</i>. The author means that these speculative things (by which he means the different branches of logic) serve to distinguish, in the truth to be demonstrated, what is <i>essential</i> and what is merely <i>accidental</i>. Al-Ḥarīzī translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">maʿānīn ha-morim ʿal ʿeṣem ha-borā</span>, <i>by the things that indicate the essence of the Creator</i>, relating the pronoun <i>it</i> to the word God or <i>Creator</i>, which would be implied; but his translation does not offer a satisfactory meaning and seems to me to be entirely inadmissible (cf. above, chap. XXXIII, page 117, at the end of note 4 of the previous page).",
    "fn.282": "This passage is somewhat obscure; one opines that, by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">תלך אלאמור</span>, the author intends to designate <i>the matters of astronomy and physics</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אמור אלהיאה̈ אלפלכיה̈ ואלעלם אלטביעי</span>) of which he has just spoken. He means that the study of the various parts of logic also contributes, by rectifying the judgement and by rendering ideas more precise, to a better comprehension of the subjects of astronomy and physics, and to their being viewed from their true perspective. Indeed, these sciences presuppose the general notions acquired through logic, such as the notions of <i>substance</i>, of <i>accident</i>, of <i>the necessary</i>, of <i>the possible</i>, etc., as well as the knowledge of syllogism and demonstration.",
    "fn.283": "That is to say, in addition to the advantages enumerated, the aforementioned <i>speculative things</i> have others relative to certain things by which we arrive at metaphysical sciences. This passage also lacks clarity, and no commentator, to my knowledge, has provided an explanation. The author, in stating that logic has the advantage of making known things more directly related to metaphysics, is alluding, it seems to me, to the ontological nature of categories and various other subjects treated in the <i>Organon</i> of Aristotle. Cf. above, p. 27, V<sup>e</sup> cause, and note 2 [[fn:2]].",
    "fn.284": "The author means that men, not even knowing if God exists, would know even less what to accept or not accept regarding Him; for example, whether to attribute to Him the governance of the world, whether to remove from Him all sorts of imperfection, and other similar questions. The Arab word <span dir=\"rtl\">חכם</span> that we have rendered by <i>governance</i> is somewhat vague; the two Hebrew translators have rendered it by <span dir=\"rtl\">דבר</span>, <i>something</i>, which is inaccurate and offers little sense; however, in a manuscript of Ibn-Tibbon's version (Oratoire fonds, n° 46) we read <span dir=\"rtl\">דין</span> <i>judgment</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">حكم</span> indeed means <i>judgment</i>, or <i>regime, governance</i>; the author having just used the expression: <i>if there is a God for the universe</i> or <i>the world</i>, we believe he intended to speak here of God's intervention in the governance of the world[[fn:1]].",
    "fn.285": "The author refers to the words of Jeremiah, chap. 3, <small>V</small>. 14.",
    "fn.286": "That is to say, the small number of the elect; these words are taken from the book of Joel, chapter 3, <small>V</small>. 5.",
    "fn.287": "We have translated this verse in the sense that Maimonides appears to attribute to it.",
    "fn.288": "The author means that doubts and objections present themselves promptly to the mind, just as the demolition of a building constructed with much effort occurs easily and with promptness.",
    "fn.289": "That is to say, for one who reflects on it well, it is evident that preparatory studies are necessary.",
    "fn.290": "On the division <i>of virtues</i> (<i>ἀρεταί</i>) into <i>moral</i> (<i>ἠθιϰαί</i>) and <i>rational</i> or <i>intellectual</i> (<i>διανοητιϰαί</i>) ones, and on their mutual relationships, see Aristotle, <i>Nicomachean Ethics</i>, book I, chapter 13; book II, chapter 1 and following; book VI, chapter 2. Also compare what Maimonides himself says in this regard in the <i>Eight Chapters</i>, placed at the beginning of his commentary on the treatise <i>Abôth</i>; in chapter II, it is written: \"The virtues are of two species, <i>moral virtues</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">פצׄאיל כׄלקיה̈</span>) and <i>rational virtues</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">פצׄאיל נטקיה̈</span>), and opposed to them are two species of vices, etc.\" See Pococke's <i>Porta Mosis</i>, p. 191.",
    "fn.291": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">קויא</span>, the MSS. generally bear <span dir=\"rtl\">קוי</span>, and the two Hebrew translators have considered this word as an adjective; but the concern here is solely with the <i>heat of the heart</i> and by no means with <i>force</i>. One prefers to read <span dir=\"rtl\">קויא</span>, as one MS. bears, and one considers this word as an adverb; the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ג֯דא קויא</span> signify <i>very strongly</i>. The reading of <span dir=\"rtl\">קוי</span> which most MSS. present does not oppose this interpretation; for it is known that the <span dir=\"rtl\">א</span> of the adverbial accusative is often omitted in the MSS.",
    "fn.292": "Literally: <i>when he had exercised himself in the greatest exercise</i>.",
    "fn.293": "That is, something that cannot be explained with precision and escapes analysis.",
    "fn.294": "That is, from metaphysics.",
    "fn.295": "Literally: <i>They will raise or they will stir up their souls to that degree</i>.",
    "fn.296": "See above, p. 9.",
    "fn.297": "Voy. Talm. of Bab., treatise <i>’Haghigâ</i>, fol. 13 <i>a;</i> cf. above, p. 9.",
    "fn.298": "That is, to a man who has both gravity and humility. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא</span> our editions of the Talmud read <span dir=\"rtl\">ולכל מי</span>; according to this reading, it should be translated: <i>and to all those who have a troubled heart</i>.",
    "fn.299": "These words, taken from Isaiah, chapter 3, <small>V</small>. 3, had to be translated here in the sense of the Talmudists, which will be explained.",
    "fn.300": "That is, a thing that belongs to the domain of intelligence and philosophical thought.",
    "fn.301": "<span dir=\"rtl\">אלמעקולאת אלאוול</span>  (<i>intelligibilia prima</i>) are the <i>first notions</i> or the <i>axioms</i> that do not require demonstration.",
    "fn.302": "According to the two Hebrew versions, one ought to translate: <i>who is capable of concealing</i> or <i>of enveloping the subjects, by expressing oneself etc.</i>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אכפי (أَخْفَى)</span>, the two translators have read <span dir=\"rtl\">אִכְפָא (إِخْفَاءٌ)</span>, as some MSS. have it; the sense would then be: and who knows, by his intelligent discourse, to present the subjects in such a manner as to <i>envelop</i> that which ought to remain hidden from the vulgar.",
    "fn.303": "See <i>’Haghigâ</i>, fol. 14 <i>a;</i> the rabbis play on the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חָרָשִׁים</span> (<i>men of genius, artists</i>) that they pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">חֵרְשִׁים</span> (<i>deaf, deaf-mutes</i>). When the scholar speaks, they say, all become like mute and have nothing to respond to him.",
    "fn.304": "<span dir=\"rtl\">בנץ כתאב</span>, literally: <i>by the text of a book</i> (<i>sacred</i>). Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">בספריהם</span> <i>in their books;</i> but Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">מדברי הכתוב</span>, <i>by the words of the Scripture</i>, and, according to him, the author would be alluding to the text of Isaiah from which the words in question are borrowed, an opinion that seems plausible to us.",
    "fn.305": "See the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. XXVII.",
    "fn.306": "On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מלכה̈</span>, see above, p. 121, note 2; <span dir=\"rtl\">מלכה̈ מתמכנה̈</span> signifies <i>a natural quality firmly established</i>.",
    "fn.307": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon adds here the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מי שאינו ראוי להם</span>, which are not expressed in our Arabic text.",
    "fn.308": "That is, what has been said about the difficulty of metaphysical matters and the impossibility of presenting them to the masses should not lead one to believe that it is necessary to leave them unaware that God is incorporeal, and that He is not subject to <i>passions</i> (<i>πάθη</i>) or passivity. See further, chap. LV.",
    "fn.309": "Literally: <i>just as it is necessary</i> <small>TO RAISE</small> <i>children… in</i> (<i>this idea</i>) <i>that God etc.</i> The construction is irregular, because the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי</span> is adapted to <span dir=\"rtl\">ירבי</span>, and not to <span dir=\"rtl\">ויעלן</span>.",
    "fn.310": "Literally: <i>that his life is not similar to the life of what is living among them, and that his science is not similar to the science of what has science among them</i>.",
    "fn.311": "The author appears to be alluding to chapter IV of the VII<sup>th</sup> book of Physics, where Aristotle, in discussing the comparison of different species of movements, arrives at the conclusion that things comparable to one another must not only not be mere homonyms, but also must not be distinguished from one another by any difference, either with regard to what they are or with regard to that in which they are, or, in other words, they must coincide both in <i>genus</i> and in <i>difference</i>, that is to say, be of the same <i>species</i>. See further on, chapter LII, p. 201, note 4[[fn:4]].",
    "fn.312": "That is to say, according to his faculties and intelligence.",
    "fn.313": "That is to say, that the same words can be applied to different things or ideas, as occurs in homonyms.",
    "fn.314": "That is to say, no species of definition can apply to God and something other than him at the same time.",
    "fn.315": "That is, the idea of beings associated with him, dualism or polytheism.",
    "fn.316": "In the text, the quotation is not altogether exact. One must read <span dir=\"rtl\">י״י</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">הוא</span>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">ונוטר</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ומשלם</span>. This is, doubtless, an error of memory which must be ascribed to the author; for the fault exists in almost all the Arabic MSS., as in those of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, and in the <i>Moré-ha-Moré</i> (p. 21).",
    "fn.317": "It is known that, according to Aristotle, the four elements have their respective regions; these are spheres that surround one another, like those of the planets. The earth is surrounded by water, which, in turn, is enveloped by air, which is itself surrounded by fire. See Aristotle's <i>Physics</i> book IV, chapter 5, and the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, book IV, chapter 5. Cf. below, chapter LXXII.",
    "fn.318": "Literally: <i>the cone of the cylinder is half of it</i>, that is, the cone forms half of a cylinder with the same base and height; we know that the measure of solidity of the cone is only one-third of that of the cylinder with equal base and height.",
    "fn.319": "Literally: <i>a circle;</i> the author wishes to speak of one who would believe the sun to be such as it appears to our eyes, that is to say, who would see therein only something <i>circular</i>, and who would be ignorant that the sun is a <i>sphere</i>. Al-’Harizi, having not rightly comprehended the word <span dir=\"rtl\">دايرة</span> (circle), which he took in the sense of <i>round</i>, deemed it necessary to add a negation and translated <span dir=\"rtl\">או כי אין השמש עגול</span>, <i>or that the sun is not round</i>. This is a serious misinterpretation, and M. Scheyer, in the critical notes with which he accompanied the edition of the first part of Al-’Harizi’s version (p. 37, note 5), erred in here preferring this version over that of Ibn-Tibbon, and in asserting that a negation must be added to the Arabic text. It is likewise erroneously that M. Scheyer relies upon Ibn-Falaquera's version, which, he says, bears <span dir=\"rtl\">או שהשמש איננה מסיבה</span> (vide <i>Moré-ha-Moré</i>, p. 21); the word <span dir=\"rtl\">איננה</span> has been added, doubtless, by some copyist or perhaps by the editor of the <i>Moré-ha Moré</i>, for the MSS. generally bear <span dir=\"rtl\">או שהשמש מסיבה</span>.",
    "fn.320": "See Mischné Torâ or Abrégé du Talmud, book I, treatise on the Idolâtrie, chap. I.",
    "fn.321": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואעתקאדה</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ואעתקדה</span>, <i>and who believes</i>. The Hebrew versions agree with the latter reading, which is perhaps preferable.",
    "fn.322": "These words, which the author wrote in Hebrew, are a proverbial locution commonly used in rabbinic books; for example: <span dir=\"rtl\">הזהרו במנהג אבותיכם בידיכם</span>, Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Béçâ</i>, fol. 4 <i>b</i>. In the tractate <i>’Houllin</i> (fol. 13 <i>b</i>), it is said regarding idolaters: <span dir=\"rtl\">נכרים שבחוצה לארץ לאו עוברי ע״א הן אלא מנהג אבוחיהם בידיהם</span> \"The Gentiles outside the Holy Land are not true idolaters, but <i>they maintain the usage of their fathers</i>.\" See also tractate <i>’Abôdâ Zarâ</i>, fol. 2 <i>a</i>, in the <i>Tosaphôth</i> or Glosses.",
    "fn.323": "Literally: <i>and the greater part of its homonymy</i> (<i>takes place</i>) <i>by way of metaphor</i>, that is to say the majority of the meanings of this word are metaphorical.",
    "fn.324": "Raschi similarly explains, in this verse, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ופניה</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">פנים של זעם</span>, <i>face expressing anger</i> or <i>irritation;</i> Kimhi and others take it, with more reason, in the sense of <i>ill countenance</i>.",
    "fn.325": "The meaning of <i>anger</i> that the author gives to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פני</span> in this latter passage is not the one generally adopted by translators and commentators of the Pentateuch; the author appears to have followed the opinion reported in the Babylonian Talmud (<i>Berakhôth</i>, fol. 7 <i>a</i>), and perhaps also the version of Onkelos, which, in various manuscripts, bore <span dir=\"rtl\">רוגזי יהך</span>, as can be seen in the commentary of Abravanel on the <i>Moré</i> (Prague, 1831), fol. 61 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.326": "Literally: <i>on the form of the allocution</i>.",
    "fn.327": "That is to say, the <i>Intelligences of the spheres</i>, or the superior and <i>abstract</i> spirits, which, according to the Arab philosophers, preside over the different <i>spheres</i> of the celestial realm; the Arabs, as well as the Scholastics, believed that Aristotle had designated these <i>Intelligences</i> by the words <i>τἀ ϰεχωρισμένα, the separated things</i> (Treatise <i>On the Soul</i>, Book III, Chapter 7). Albert the Great, speaking of <i>separated substances</i>, expresses himself thus: \"And therefore, that (substance) which is not divided by the division of the body, nor moved by the motion of the body, nor operates with the instruments of the body, that <i>separated</i> (substance) is, not by place, but by the obligation of corporeal matter, however simple. Now, all these characteristics belong to the <i>substances of the heavens</i>, etc.\" (Parva naturalia, <i>On the Motions of Animals</i>, Book I, Treatise I, Chapter 4). See also Maimonides, in the present work, Part II<sup>e</sup>, Chapter IV, and my article <i>Ibn-Rushd</i>, in the <i>Dictionary of Philosophical Sciences</i>, Vol. III, p. 168[[fn:1]].",
    "fn.328": "That is to say, because they are the object of the immediate and particular providence of the deity. Cf. the IIIrd part of this work, ch. XVII.",
    "fn.329": "For the <i>separated</i> or <i>abstract</i> intelligences are <i>pure forms</i>.",
    "fn.330": "See below, chapter LIV.",
    "fn.331": "See further, chap. LIV. Cf. above, chap. XXI, p. 76.",
    "fn.332": "Literally: <i>in which is the principle of the life of all that has a heart</i>, that is to say of all animals that have blood. Cf. Aristotle, treatise <i>On the Parts of Animals</i>, book III, chap. IV; Hist. <i>of Animals</i>, book II, chap. XV.",
    "fn.333": "The author wants to justify his translation of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יחרף</span>, which he takes to mean: <i>to deviate, to turn away</i>, from the Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">حرف</span>, whereas it is generally translated as <i>to blame</i>.",
    "fn.334": "Literally: <i>turned away from the possession of slavery to the possession of marriage</i>.",
    "fn.335": "This example, as some commentators have reasonably pointed out, should have been placed a bit further on, where the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לב</span> is discussed, applied metaphorically to God.",
    "fn.336": "The author adds this last passage to show that <span dir=\"rtl\">דעת</span> (to know) means <i>to understand, to comprehend</i>, and that, consequently, it is also a matter of intellectual knowledge in the preceding passage.",
    "fn.337": "See <i>Huit chapitres</i> or Introduction to the tractate <i>Abôth</i>, chapter V.",
    "fn.338": "See the treatise <i>Yesodé ha-Torâ</i>, chap. II, § 2.",
    "fn.339": "The author deviates from the opinion of all commentators in here explaining the word <span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span> as <i>air</i>. One must recall that the author discovers in the first two verses of Genesis the Aristotelian theory concerning the four elements and the respective position of their spheres. Vide what he says in this regard at the commencement of chap. XXX of the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work. Cf. supra, chap. XXXVI, p. 134, note 2.",
    "fn.340": "For further details on <i>vital spirits</i>, see a note at the beginning of chapter LXXII.",
    "fn.341": "The author, as we will see, explains <span dir=\"rtl\">יודיעֶנּו</span> as if there were <span dir=\"rtl\">יודיעֵנו</span>, taking the suffix <span dir=\"rtl\">נו</span> for the first person plural.",
    "fn.342": "See chapters XVIII and following in the III<sup>e</sup> part of this work, which deal with divine Providence and its intervention in human affairs.",
    "fn.343": "That is, in the sense of <i>inspiration</i> emanating from God.",
    "fn.344": "Cf. Aristotle, <i>Treatise on the Soul</i>, Book II, Chapter III and V.",
    "fn.345": "If the author distinguishes here from <i>the rational soul</i> what remains of man after death, it must be remembered that he sees in the rational soul only a <i>physical disposition</i>; it is the intellect in potentiality which, through meditation and the influence of the universal active intellect, becomes <i>acquired intellect</i> and <i>intellect in act</i>, and, as such, is imperishable. See below, chap. LXX and chap. LXXII, towards the end. On this doctrine and its various nuances among Arab philosophers, see my articles <i>Ibn-Bâdja</i> and <i>Ibn-Roschd</i> in the <i>Dictionary of Philosophical Sciences</i>, vol. III, p. 157, 166 and following. On Maimonides in particular, see Seheyer, <i>The Psychological System of Maimonides</i>, p. 30 and following. We will have the opportunity to return to this in other places [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.346": "In both Hebrew versions, the citation reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">אל תתנני בנפש צרי</span>, <i>deliver me not over unto the soul of mine enemies</i> (Ps. 27, 12). The author appears to have written, through an error of memory: <span dir=\"rtl\">אל תתנהו בנפש צריו</span>, as it is found in several MSS. of the Arabic original and of Ibn-Tibbon's version. Some copyists have changed <span dir=\"rtl\">צריו</span> to <span dir=\"rtl\">אויביו</span>, others have substituted (in Ibn-Tibbon's version) the passage from Psalm XXVII, verse 12; but that which proves the author to have written <span dir=\"rtl\">תתנהו</span> with the third-person suffix, is that he himself explains this word by the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">תםלמה</span>, which one reads in all the MSS. In most MSS. of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one likewise reads <span dir=\"rtl\">תםגירהו</span>, and this word has been arbitrarily changed to <span dir=\"rtl\">תםגירני</span>.",
    "fn.347": "See above, chapter XXXIX, page 143.",
    "fn.348": "R. David Kim’hi, in his commentary upon the book of Judges, likewise states that Jonathan ben-Uziel, in his Chaldaic paraphrase, passed this passage over in silence; nevertheless, Ibn-Caspi, in his commentary upon the <i>Moré</i>, affirms that the words in question were rendered in the MSS. which he had before him as they are indeed in our editions of the Chaldaic paraphrase. According to Ibn-Caspi, the author may have intended to convey that Jonathan did not modify the expressions of the passage, but rendered it word for word, without causing the anthropomorphism to disappear; but the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לם יתרגׄמה בוגׄה</span> by no means admit of this interpretation, and it evidently results from the terms employed by Maimonides that Jonathan had not translated this passage. Indeed, there still exist MSS. of Jonathan's paraphrase, in which the passage in question is not translated into Chaldee, but preserved in Hebrew. Here is the entire verse according to a MS. from the Imperial Library (anc. Heb. fund, n°57, fol. 118 <i>a</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">ואעדיאו ית טעות עממיא מביניהון ופלחו קדם י״י ותקצר נפשו בעמל ישראל</span>.",
    "fn.349": "Literally: <i>Their downfall being great and the enemy having become master over them</i>.",
    "fn.350": "In all these examples, the prefix <span dir=\"rtl\">ב</span>, <i>dans</i>, is in place of the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">מן</span>, <i>de</i>. The third example is missing in our editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, but it is found in the manuscripts of this version.",
    "fn.351": "That is, beings with the faculty of nutrition and at the same time sensitivity. The Hebrew word <span dir=\"rtl\">חי</span> is never applied to plants.",
    "fn.352": "See chapter XXV of the I<sup>st</sup> book of Samuel, whence the preceding biblical passage is drawn.—The author appears to signify that the entirety of the passage concerning the son of the woman of Zarephath, in which one speaks first explicitly of a most violent illness, and subsequently of the extinction of the breath of life, indicates that the child was indeed truly dead. He then cites, without adopting it, the opinion of an author from Andalusia, who deemed that it concerned, in this narrative, an apparent death. Several commentators, such as Ibn-Caspi, Moses of Narbonne, Ephodi, insinuate that Maimonides wished to veil his thought, and that he himself adopts the opinion of the author from Andalusia. They support themselves by another analogous narrative (II Kings, 4, 19 and 20), in which one expresses: <i>and he died</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וימת</span>), which would destroy Maimonides' reasoning; but Abravanel, in his commentary upon the <i>Moré</i> (fol. 66), observes that there also one speaks first of the illness and then of the death, which indicates that it concerns a real death, in conformity with Maimonides' reasoning on the passage relative to the son of the widow of Zarephath. Be that as it may, one cannot admit that Maimonides, with the aim of veiling a heresy, should have presented a reasoning devoid of logic and contradicted himself. One could not admit Abravanel's opinion, who, to justify Maimonides, pretends that the words of the author from Andalusia refer to Nabal; it appears quite evident to one, from the whole, that it concerns here the son of the woman of Zarephath; but, as one has said, Maimonides cites this opinion without himself adopting it. It is certain, moreover, that this passage is one of those which Maimonides' adversaries seized upon to render his orthodoxy suspect. See the response of R. Jehuda ibn-al-Fakhâr to R. David Kim’hi, in the Collection of Maimonides' Letters, Amsterdam edition, in-12, fol. 25 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.353": "The disease called by the ancients <i>strangulation</i> or <i>suffocation of the matrix</i> (<i>πνιγμοì … ἐν ταĩς ὑστϵ́ρϰις</i>, Arist., <i>Histoire des animaux</i>, l. VII, chap. 2; <i>ὑστεριϰὴ πνίξ</i>, Galien, <i>Comment. sur les Aphorismes d’Hippocrate</i>, V, 35; <i>strangulationes vulvœ</i>, Pline, <i>Hist. nat.</i>, XX, 57) is an affliction of this organ in which women experience spasmodic movements and believe they feel a ball (<i>globus hystericus</i>) in their throat that is choking them. See Dict. des sc. méd., articles <i>Hystérie, Strangulation</i> and <i>Suffocation</i>.",
    "fn.354": "The author intends to convey that, just as in the aforementioned passage from Deuteronomy (XXX, 15) life and good are evidently the same thing, so likewise in this passage: <i>That ye may live, and that it may be well with you</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">וטוב לכם</span>), life and well-being (<span dir=\"rtl\">טוב</span>) designate the same thing, and that it concerns here the life of the immortal soul or the life of pure intelligence, in conformity with what the Talmud states concerning these words: <i>That it may be well with thee, and that thou mayest prolong thy days</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>. 22, 7): <span dir=\"rtl\">למען ייטב לך לעולם שכולו טוב ולמען יאריכון ימיך לעולם שכולו ארוך</span> «That it may be well with thee in a world of perfect happiness, and that thou mayest prolong thy days in a world of eternal duration.» See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Kiddushin</i>, fol. 39 <i>b; ’Hullin</i>, fol. 142 <i>a;</i> cf. the third part of this work, at the close of chap. XXVII.",
    "fn.355": "Voy. Talm. of Bab., treatise <i>Berakhôt</i>, fol. 18 <i>a, b</i>. The author appears to understand this passage in the sense that pious men who, through study, have formed their intelligence, and in whom the <i>potential intellect</i> has become, through meditation, <i>actual intellect</i>, attain true eternal life, whereas the impious, even during their lifetime, must be regarded as dead, not seeking to establish a connection with the higher world through meditation and speculative studies.",
    "fn.356": "It is the celebrated grammarian and lexicographer R. Ionâ, called in Arabic Abou’l-Walîd Merwân ibn-Djanâ’h. See my <i>Notice</i> on this illustrious man in the <i>Journal asiatique</i>, year 1850, April issue, etc.",
    "fn.357": "The mss. read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי יקולון</span>; in one ms. we read <span dir=\"rtl\">אנה יקול</span>. We have written more correctly <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי יקול</span>; in a few mss. of the version of Ibn-Tibbon the verb is also in the singular (<span dir=\"rtl\">שיאמר</span>).",
    "fn.358": "All the MSS. of the <i>Guide</i> bear most distinctly <span dir=\"rtl\">מנירך</span>; in the original passage of Ibn-Djanâ’h’s Dictionary, as cited by Gesenius in his Commentary on Isaiah and in his <i>Thesaurus</i> (<i>rad.</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כנף</span>), one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">מנורך</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(منوّرك)</span>. Nevertheless, the copy of this passage of Ibn-Djanâ’h, which M. Goldberg was pleased to send one, based on the Oxford MS., bears <span dir=\"rtl\">מטרך</span>; if this reading be accurate, Ibn-Djanâ’h would have here taken the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מורה</span> in the sense of <i>rain</i> (<i>early</i>), which, indeed, would accord with the explanation of R. Salomon Par’hon and R. David Kim’hi, borrowed, doubtless, from Ibn-Djanâ’h. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מטרך</span> could easily, through a copyist’s error, have been changed into <span dir=\"rtl\">מנירך</span>.",
    "fn.359": "Or: <i>he will not discover the mystery of his father</i>, the thing that his father surrounds with mystery, that is to say, his wife.",
    "fn.360": "See below, chap. XLIX.",
    "fn.361": "The reading varies in the different MSS.; most bear <span dir=\"rtl\">לתסתכן</span>, some <span dir=\"rtl\">לתסכן</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">לתסתר</span>; but the verb must be in the feminine. The true reading appears to one to be <span dir=\"rtl\">לתםתכני</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">( لتستكنى)</span>, the X<sup>e</sup> form of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">كن</span>. It is by this same verb that Saadia renders the Hebrew verb <span dir=\"rtl\">חםה</span>. See, for example, at the end of Ps. 2 (Ewald, <i>Beitrœge etc.</i>, p. 10).",
    "fn.362": "Literally: <i>is veiled and very hidden</i>.",
    "fn.363": "See above, chapter XXVIII, page 94.",
    "fn.364": "That is to say, the action that the <i>intelligences of the spheres</i> exert on the sublunar world. It must be recalled that Maimonides identifies angels with the <i>separated</i> intelligences or the intelligences of the spheres. See the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chapters VI and XII. Cf. above, chapter XXXVII, p. 140, note 1.",
    "fn.365": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אתׁרהא</span> (their <i>trace</i> or <i>impression</i>) has been rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version by <span dir=\"rtl\">ענינם</span> (their <i>things, what they are</i>); the translator undoubtedly read <span dir=\"rtl\">אמרהא</span>. Elsewhere, he renders the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אתׁר</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">מעשה</span>; but Ibn-Falaquera observes that it would be better rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">רישום</span> (<i>impression</i>). See Ibn-Falaquera's critical notes on chapter LXXII of this <sup>book</sup>'s <i>Moré ha-Moré</i> (p. 153) [[fn:1]].",
    "fn.366": "See below, chap. XLIX.",
    "fn.367": "The author expresses <span dir=\"rtl\">אלעין אלבאצרה̈</span> (the <i>seeing</i> eye), because in Arabic the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עין</span> also has the two meanings of <i>source</i> and <i>eye</i>. If he places the primary meaning, which is that of <i>eye</i>, after that of <i>source</i>, it is doubtless to bring it closer to the metaphorical meaning of <i>Providence</i>, which comes immediately after.",
    "fn.368": "See above, chap. XXXIX, where the author cites this same passage regarding the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לב</span>.",
    "fn.369": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">wandering</span>, as the Arabic mss. and those of Ibn-Tibbon's version generally have, it should be read, according to the biblical text: <span dir=\"rtl\">wandering</span>; the author has confused in his memory the passage from Zechariah with another similar one (2 Chronicles 16:9), where one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">wandering</span>.",
    "fn.370": "See the IIIrd part of this work, chap. XVII and following.",
    "fn.371": "Literally: <i>all feeling</i> or <i>every sensation is a passion</i> (<i>πϰ́θος</i>) <i>and an impression</i> (<i>received</i>).",
    "fn.372": "That is to say, where the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">שמע</span> means everywhere <i>percevoir</i> in the sense of <i>savoir, connaître</i>.",
    "fn.373": "That is, in welcoming the words of someone.",
    "fn.374": "See above, p. 23, note 2.",
    "fn.375": "This last sentence does not relate only to the metaphors pertaining to <i>hearing</i>, but in general to all those expounded in the preceding chapters, particularly to those relating to the senses, and on which the author elaborates in the subsequent chapters.",
    "fn.376": "See above, chap. XXXIII.",
    "fn.377": "Literally: <i>of a carob-seed</i>, that is to say, of a thing of small value. The seed contained within the carob-pod figures in the weights of Arab apothecaries and is equivalent to four grains of barley. See Casiri, <i>Biblioth. arab. hisp.</i>, Vol. I<sup>er</sup>, p. 281. Ibn-Tibbon has, therefore, inaccurately rendered the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄרובהֹׁ</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">משקל שעורה</span> (weight of a grain of barley).",
    "fn.378": "Literally: <i>by imagining</i> or <i>by being imagined</i>, that is to say we have presented things as if one <i>imagined</i> that God was corporeal. The mss. bear some <span dir=\"rtl\">בתכׄייל</span> in the II<sup>nd</sup> form, others <span dir=\"rtl\">בתכׄיל</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(بتخيل)</span> in the V<sup>th</sup> form; both readings are equally plausible. Ibn-Tibbon, by translating with <span dir=\"rtl\">בדמיון</span>, appears to have read in the V<sup>th</sup> form; but Ibn-Falaquera, in his critical notes (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, page 150), prefers to translate <span dir=\"rtl\">בּֽדַמּוֹת</span> in the active voice, that is, <i>by imagining</i> or <i>by letting imagine</i>.",
    "fn.379": "Voy. Aristotle, <i>Physics</i>, book V, chap. II: Κ<i>ατ’οὐσίαν δἐ οὐϰ ἔστι ϰίνησις, ϰ. τ. λ.</i> Cf. above, chap. XXVI, pag. 90.",
    "fn.380": "Literally: <i>we do not know</i> (<i>something</i>) <i>etc.</i>, that is to say, knowledge of what occurs in another's soul can only be transmitted to us through speech, and we cannot imagine it to be otherwise. The author, after having spoken of <i>existence</i> and <i>life</i>, speaks here of <i>science</i> following the order in which he enumerated a little earlier everything that constitutes the existence of God, saying that God <i>exists</i> as a <i>living</i> being having <i>science</i>, etc. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">לא נעלם</span> (we do not <i>know</i>) refer to <span dir=\"rtl\">עאלמא</span> (having <i>science</i>).",
    "fn.381": "The author elaborates at great length on propheticism in the II<sup>e</sup> part of this work. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אגיא</span>, which should be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">أشياء</span>, is the noun of action of the IV<sup>e</sup> form of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">غنى</span> used in the sense of: <i>pushing to the extreme</i>, and derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">غاية</span>, <i>extremity</i>; this meaning of the verb is not indicated in dictionaries. See my edition of the <i>Commentary of R. Tan'houm on Habakkuk</i>, p. 98, note 13 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.382": "The author means by this parenthesis: Although, moreover, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">נפש</span>, <i>soul</i>, is a homonym and when applied to God it signifies <i>will</i>, as stated above, chap. XLI, it was nonetheless intended to designate God as a <i>living being</i>, in accordance with the primitive sense of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">נפש</span>.",
    "fn.383": "Literally: <i>as all these actions are conceived in us only through corporeal organs</i>. We are referring to the <i>actions</i> that have just been mentioned, namely, those of <i>moving</i>, <i>seeing</i>, <i>hearing</i>, <i>speaking</i>, and <i>performing</i> things.",
    "fn.384": "By <i>matter</i> of the word the author understands the <i>voice</i>, or the inarticulate sound to which the organs of speech give the <i>form</i>.",
    "fn.385": "Literally: <i>as is known and prevalent among the nation</i>. According to the author, the passage: <i>And all the people</i> <small>SAW</small> <i>the thunder</i>, must be explained in accordance with what precedes, that is to say, by giving to the verb <i>to see</i> the more general sense of <i>to perceive;</i> although, moreover, he adds, it is not absolutely necessary to have recourse to this interpretation, for the verb <i>to see</i> may apply here to the entire scene of the revelation upon Sinai, which falls within the category of prophetic <i>visions</i>. The author appears to allude to the tradition which states that the first two commandments reached all the people directly through the voice of God, and not through the intermediary of Moses: <span dir=\"rtl\">אנכי ולא יהיה לך מפי הגבורה שמענום</span>. Cf. the II<sup>e</sup> part of this work, chap. XXXIII. — As for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמקאם</span>, which one reads in the Arabic original, Ibn-Tibbon’s version bears <span dir=\"rtl\">המאמר</span>; the translator appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמקאל</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמקאם</span> (statio), by which the author designates the scene of the revelation, ought to be translated into Hebrew by <span dir=\"rtl\">המעמד</span>, as Al-’Harizi has done. Cf. II<sup>e</sup> part, <i>ibid.</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">מעמד הר סיני</span>.",
    "fn.386": "See II<sup>nd</sup> part, chapter XII.",
    "fn.387": "That is to say, the inspiration coming from God or the angels who, according to the author, are the <i>Intelligences of the spheres</i>.",
    "fn.388": "Literally: so that we may be guided, that is, so that we may be led to recognize[3]. The verb לנדל[5] must be read in the passive (لِنُدَلَّ)[7]; some editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have incorrectly שנודה[9] with daleth for שנוּרֶה[11].",
    "fn.389": "See above, at the beginning of chap. XXVI.",
    "fn.390": "The citation we have reproduced as it appears in the Arab manuscripts and in the version of Ibn-Tibbon is not entirely accurate; it should read: <span dir=\"rtl\">‘Eynay YHWH hameh mashuttim</span>. Cf. above, chap. XLIV, pag. 154, note 4.",
    "fn.391": "See above, chap. XXXIX.",
    "fn.392": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מעי</span> must be considered as the Arabic <span dir=\"rtl\">(نعي)</span>; if it were Hebrew, as the two Hebrew translators seem to have believed, one would have to read <span dir=\"rtl\">מעים</span>.",
    "fn.393": "In the text, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">פי הדׄא אלפסוק</span> in the singular, and what proves that it is the author who wrote thus is that, in the two Hebrew versions, one also reads <span dir=\"rtl\">בזה הפםוק</span>. It would have been more exact to write in the dual: <span dir=\"rtl\">פי הדׄין אלפםוקין</span>, because it is a question of two verses.",
    "fn.394": "That is, the functions of the shoulder are considered too material to be, even metaphorically, attributed to God; one must especially exclude from God the idea of a <i>contact</i> with things. See above, chapter XVIII.",
    "fn.395": "See above, chapter XXVI, p. 89.",
    "fn.396": "All mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אן יכון</span> without negation; similarly, the two Hebrew versions. The sense is: <i>de manner that there be in him</i>.",
    "fn.397": "That is, what is called the attributes of God is nothing other than a set of faculties that differ from one another only by their names, and which all return to a single and the same thing: <i>the essence of God</i>.",
    "fn.398": "Literally: <i>who embraces</i> or <i>who encompasses</i> (<i>much</i>).",
    "fn.399": "Some mss. add פי הדׄא אלמעני (on this subject); similarly Al-’Harizi: בזה הענין.",
    "fn.400": "Literally: <i>They persist in these external words of the prophets</i>, that is to say: they employ the same expressions, which, taken in the <i>literal</i> sense, may give rise to the corporification of God.",
    "fn.401": "Literally: <i>as the allegory has been fixed in that God has been compared</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">lana</span>, some manuscripts read <span dir=\"rtl\">bana</span>, a reading also admissible.",
    "fn.402": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלתרדד</span>, one MS. bears <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלתכרר</span>, which signifies <i>repetition</i>, which, indeed, appears to be the sense of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלתרדד</span> here. Ibn-Tibbon translates the final words by <span dir=\"rtl\">והחזרה בענין</span> and Al-’Harizi by <span dir=\"rtl\">ולשוב בענין</span>, which lacks clarity; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאמר</span> appears to one to have here the sense of <i>precept, command</i>, and not that of <i>thing</i>.",
    "fn.403": "See the beginning of section 27.",
    "fn.404": "Cf. the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, at the end of chap. II.",
    "fn.405": "That is, the doctors have declared by this that the divinity revealed itself to the prophets through figures it created in the soul or imagination of the prophets.",
    "fn.406": "That is to say, everything that the imaginative faculty shows us is a divine creation, just like this faculty itself.",
    "fn.407": "Talmud of Babylon, tractate Iébamôth, fol. 104 a.",
    "fn.408": "The present matter concerns the symbolic act of the <i>’halîçâ</i> or the unshoeing which dissolves the bonds of the <i>levirate;</i> see Deut., chap. 25, <small>v.</small> 9. The ceremony, according to the provisions of the Talmudic code, must be performed with a sandal or a leather shoe, in public, and during the day, and the doctor here in question dispensed himself from observing these three conditions, of which the second, especially, that of publicity, [is indispensable. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מוק</span> designates a kind of felt slipper. According to the <i>’Aroukh</i>, it is the footwear called in Arabic <i>mouk</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">سوق</span>, ocrea crassior).",
    "fn.409": "That is, whose studies, method, and manner of speaking are absolutely unknown to him.—The verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">ישאהד</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">עלם</span> should be read in the active form, as Al-’Harizi has done; the version of Ibn-Tibbon renders them in the passive.",
    "fn.410": "That is, they are too high above blame to be reached by it.",
    "fn.411": "See above, chap. XXVI, p. 89, and chap. XLVI, p. 164.",
    "fn.412": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתקריר</span> (with <i>resch</i>), some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתקדיר</span> (with <i>daleth</i>), and it is this latter reading that Al-’Harizi expressed, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">לפי השיעור הזה</span> (estimating <i>, or </i>judging<i> thus), which does not offer a suitable meaning here.",
    "fn.413": "That is to say, the senses always give only an imperfect perception; they are imperfect, even with regard to sensible perceptions and even for beings that have no other perceptions.",
    "fn.414": "Literally: <i>who bear these qualities</i>, that is, who are the <i>substratum</i> of the necessary qualities for producing the sensations of sight, hearing, and smell; these qualities are: color, sound, and odor.",
    "fn.415": "The words: <i>to attribute them to God</i>, which one adds in one’s translation, are expressed in some manuscripts which bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">פכאן נםבתהא לה תעאלי םאיגה̈ פי כׄיאל אלגׄמהור</span>; likewise Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">על כן היה נאות ליחםם לשם בדמיון ההמון</span>. Al-’Harizi, in agreement with the reading one has adopted, translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">והיה זה מקובל במחשבת ההמון</span>, <i>so that this was permissible in the imagination of the vulgar</i>.",
    "fn.416": "The text reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">בה</span>, <i>by him</i>, which pertains to each of the two senses in particular, as does the preceding word <span dir=\"rtl\">כאף</span>; but it would have been more regular to render these two words in the dual, and to say <span dir=\"rtl\">כאפיאן</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">בהמא</span>. The Hebrew versions likewise present the singular. The ensuing word <span dir=\"rtl\">ידרך</span> ought to be read in the passive <span dir=\"rtl\">(يُدْرَك)</span>; the two Hebrew versions have rendered <span dir=\"rtl\">שישיג</span> in the active voice; such that the verb's subject would be <i>God</i>, which yields no suitable meaning.",
    "fn.417": "See <i>Mischnâ</i>, IV<sup>e</sup> part, tractate <i>Abôth</i>, chapter II, § 1. The author cites this passage to show that the doctors have also spoken of the eye and the ear of God, to indicate that God knows our actions and words.",
    "fn.418": "That is, the common people recognize well that imagination is an imperfect faculty that one cannot attribute to the divinity, but thought and understanding appear to them as faculties of all perfection.",
    "fn.419": "See above, chap. XXVI.",
    "fn.420": "See below, chap. L, p. 180, note 1.",
    "fn.421": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תלוון (تلوّن)</span> signifies <i>to assume diverse colours, to change colour</i>, and figuratively: <i>to be variable, inconstant</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's translation reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">פירש אונקלום בו פרושים מופלאים</span>, <i>Onkelos has given in this regard astonishing explanations;</i> this translation is exceedingly inexact, as Ibn-Falaquera has already observed (<i>Moré-ha-Moré</i>, pp. 150, 151), who on this occasion elucidats the true sense of the Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تلوّن</span>, by recalling that in Arabic proverbs, the inconstant and versatile man is compared unto the chameleon, which does frequently change its hue. Cf. Freytag, <i>Prov. ar.</i>, 1, 409; de Sacy, Comment. ar. upon the <i>Séances de Hariri</i>, 22<sup>nd</sup> séance (p. 206 of the new edition).",
    "fn.422": "Literally: <i>after examining the copies etc…, I found etc.</i>",
    "fn.423": "That is, this verb indicates not only the <i>perception</i>, but also the <i>acknowledgment</i> and the <i>approval</i> of the perceived thing; one cannot apply it to God when the object of perception is evil, for God cannot approve of evil.",
    "fn.424": "The author means that Onkelos avoided, in these two passages, the verb <i>to see</i>, because it involves a <i>wrong</i>, that is, in one case, the <i>acts of rebellion</i> of the Hebrew people, and in the other, their <i>affliction</i>.",
    "fn.425": "Literally: <i>was pierced</i> or <i>damaged by three passages</i>.",
    "fn.426": "In fact, in the first of these three passages, some editions have <span dir=\"rtl\">וגלי קדם י״י</span>; these same words are found, in the third passage, in the paraphrase attributed to Jonathan ben-Uziel, and which the author had before him the one of Onkelos.",
    "fn.427": "The author means to say: If we were certain that Onkelos had really written, in these three passages, <span dir=\"rtl\">וחזא י״י</span>, we would have to suppose that he had some particular reason for doing so, and seek the interpretation he might have given to these passages; but, as it is much more probable that these are merely copying errors, we should not attribute any particular intention to him with regard to these passages.",
    "fn.428": "Literally: <i>so that it not be thought that God was for approaching His research and production</i>, or: <i>that God was in front of seeking and producing</i>.",
    "fn.429": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">שבׄץ</span>, <i>individual</i>, which Ibn-Tibbon has rendered too feebly by <span dir=\"rtl\">אחד</span>, <i>one</i>, instead of by <span dir=\"rtl\">איש</span>, and which Al-'Harizi has suppressed in his version, is not without significance in this context. According to the author's system, the <i>individuals</i> of the human race alone are guided by Providence; as for other animals, Divine Providence extends only to the species, abandoning individuals unto chance. See the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. XVII.",
    "fn.430": "That is to say, the intention of <i>Onkelos</i>, to whom the pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">לה</span> referrs; it is in this sense that Al-'Harizi translated: <span dir=\"rtl\">איני יודע מה היתה כונתו</span>. Some manuscripts present <span dir=\"rtl\">להא</span> referring to <span dir=\"rtl\">מואצׄע</span>, and it is this reading which, in several manuscripts and editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, is expressed by <span dir=\"rtl\">להם</span>; nevertheless, the editio princeps has <span dir=\"rtl\">לו</span>.",
    "fn.431": "See at the end of section 21.",
    "fn.432": "That is to say, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">להט</span>, <i>flame, </i>outburst, designates the angels, called elsewhere <span dir=\"rtl\">אש לוהט</span>, <i>a blazing fire</i>.",
    "fn.433": "The author will cite a passage where the angels are designated by the name <span dir=\"rtl\">נשים</span>, <i>women</i>; the name of <span dir=\"rtl\">אנשים</span>, <i>men</i>, is found, for example, Genesis 18:2, that of <span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>, <i>wind</i> or <i>spirit</i>, Psalms 104:4, and 1 Kings 22:21.",
    "fn.434": "That is to say, the corporeal figures under which they appear exist solely in the mind of the beholder, and possess no real existence. One does frequently encounter the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄארגׄ אלדׄהן</span>, <i>outside of the mind</i>, to indicate <i>objective</i> reality, even as one says that something is <span dir=\"rtl\">פי אלדׄהן</span>, <i>in the mind</i>, to indicate <i>subjective</i> conception, whether it corresponds or not to aught real. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">דׄהן</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(ذهن)</span> embraces the sum of all spiritual faculties and dispositions, even imagination, whilst <span dir=\"rtl\">עקל</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">عقل</span> designats the intellect or intelligence; one may therefore say that a thing is <span dir=\"rtl\">פי אלדׄהן</span> <i>in the mind</i>, even when it exists solely in the imagination. See, for example, above, chap. III (pp. 43 and 44), where the perception of the senses, <i>independently</i> or <i>outside of the mind</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כׄארגׄ אלדׄהן</span>), is opposed at once to imagination and to the perception of intelligence (<span dir=\"rtl\">עקל</span>).",
    "fn.435": "Literally: <i>and what does not fall into the net of imagination.</i>",
    "fn.436": "The author is alluding here to the system of <i>motecallemîm;</i> see above, chapter LXXIII, 10<sup>e</sup> proposition.",
    "fn.437": "Literally: <i>if one had stopped, for them, at this imagination</i>, that is, at this way of presenting them to the imagination.",
    "fn.438": "Literally: <i>their truth</i> (<i>reality</i>) <i>and their essence</i>.",
    "fn.439": "A manuscript contains <span dir=\"rtl\">from the intellect</span>; similarly, Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">according to a pattern</span> (with the prefix <span dir=\"rtl\">from</span>). After the word <span dir=\"rtl\">Chayuan</span>, one of the Leiden manuscripts adds <span dir=\"rtl\">Gir Natak</span>, and this reading has been followed by Ibn-Tibbon, who has <span dir=\"rtl\">they do not speak</span>.",
    "fn.440": "That is to say, in the wings and feet that serve as the <i>substratum</i> for these two faculties.",
    "fn.441": "That is, the subject in the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ויעף</span>, <i>and he flew</i>, is not <i>God</i>, but the <i>cherub</i>, so that one should translate: <i>he was mounted on a cherub who flew</i>.",
    "fn.442": "The author means that, in these passages, Ezekiel appears to attribute to the angels, in addition to the bodies of animals, other parts than the wings, contrary to what was said earlier.",
    "fn.443": "See IIIrd part, chap. Ier, where the author says that it is here a question of human faces resembling the faces of certain animals.",
    "fn.444": "That is to say, <i>the celestial animals</i> of Ezekiel, which, according to the author, designate the spheres themselves, and not the angels or the <i>Intelligences of the spheres</i>.",
    "fn.445": "See the first chapters of the III<sup>rd</sup> part.",
    "fn.446": "Maimonides, as the commentators observe, makes particular allusion here to the movement of the celestial spheres, represented, according to him, by the <i>’hayyôth</i> or celestial animals of Ezekiel's vision; the ’hayyôth possess <i>four wings</i>, and likewise the causes of the spheres' motion are four in number, namely: their sphericity, their soul, their intelligence, and the supreme <i>separated intelligence</i> or <i>God</i>, the object of their desire. See the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chaps. IV and X.",
    "fn.447": "This chapter serves as an introduction to what the author will set forth, in the ten ensuing chapters (LI to LX), concerning the <i>attributes</i>. Ere he addresses his wholly spiritualistic theory of the <i>attributes of God</i>, a theory so far removed from vulgar beliefs, he deems it incumbent upon himself to offer a definition of <i>belief</i>, that is to say, of such belief as truly merits this appellation, and which is not a mere <i>profession of faith</i>, but an intimate <i>thought</i>, an <i>idea</i> which, even though it may not be objectively true, is at least so in the mind of him who has conceived it, and contains nothing absolutely inadmissible.",
    "fn.448": "The question of <i>attributes</i> is one of those which most occupied the Muslim and Jewish doctors of the Middle Ages. Certain doctors, whilst professing the absolute unity of God, yet believed themselves able to admit a certain number of eternal and <i>essential</i> attributes, that is to say, attributes inherent in the divine essence, such as life, knowledge, power, etc.; the sect of the <i>Mo’tazales</i>, in general, denied <i>attributes</i> as incompatible with absolute unity; there were, however, among them, those who implicitly admitted <i>essential</i> attributes by asserting that God is living through His essence, and not through the <i>attribute</i> of life; that He knows through His essence, and not through the <i>attribute</i> of knowledge, and so forth. (Cf. Pococke, Spec. hist. Arab., p. 214 et seq., and <i>ibidem</i>, the text of Abou’l-Faradj, p. 19). These latter, according to our author, are no more in the right than those who openly profess the attributes of God. Faithful to the principles of the philosophers, he rejects <i>essential</i> attributes in an absolute manner. See further details below, in chap. LIII.",
    "fn.449": "Those, says the author, who assign to God numerous attributes whilst proclaiming His unity, His incorporeality, and His absolute simplicity, are in contradiction with themselves, and it would appear that, according to them, the matter concerns rather a particular mode of expression than to penetrate themselves with the true sense of beliefs. The <i>Mo’tazales</i>, in arguing against the proponents of eternal and essential attributes, reproach them, as does our author, with falling into an error similar to that of the Christians, who admit three persons in God. Cf. Pococke, <i>loco citato</i>, p. 216: <i>Christians are accused of infidelity because they establish three eternal things; what then is to be pronounced concerning those who establish seven or more?</i>",
    "fn.450": "That is, on the necessity of excluding from God all affirmative attributes.",
    "fn.451": "See above, p. 128, note 3.",
    "fn.452": "<span dir=\"rtl\">The same as </span>כמא</span>, which is found in some manuscripts. The author means that these things are so clear that even an uneducated man, whose mind has not been cultivated, accepts them at once, without needing to be demonstrated.",
    "fn.453": "See above, p. 59, note 5, and p. 98, note 2.",
    "fn.454": "Literally: to affirm these evident things and to nullify (or to set aside) the existence of these supposed things.",
    "fn.455": "See the <i>Physique</i> of Aristotle and particularly the refutation of the proofs alleged by Zénon against the existence of movement, book VI, chap. 2, and book VIII, chap. 8.",
    "fn.456": "See <i>Ibid.</i>, book 6, chap. 1 and following.",
    "fn.457": "That is to say, by considering the attribute as the simple explanation of the name that is its subject.",
    "fn.458": "See the next chapter, where the author shows that God cannot be <i>defined</i>.",
    "fn.459": "The author here addresses those who, whilst attributing to God attributes and affirming that these attributes are something which is <i>added</i> to His essence, nevertheless pretend to admit that God has no accidents: it suffices not, he says, to avoid the word <i>accident</i> in order to efface also the idea thereof, and as soon as one admits that God possesses attributes distinct from His essence, one necessarily admits that He possesses <i>accidents;</i> for that which is <i>added</i> to the essence and is distinct therefrom can only be accessory to it, and cannot form its essential complement.",
    "fn.460": "That is, which in itself has no multiplicity and cannot even appear multiple to the mind. See above, page 175, note 5.",
    "fn.461": "By <span dir=\"rtl\">אהל אלנטׄר</span>, lit.: <i>people of speculation</i>, the author here understands not philosophers properly so called, but theologians who apply philosophical speculation to religious dogma; the various propositions he is about to cite pertain to the <i>Motécallemîn</i> or Muslim scholastics (cf. above, p. 5, note 1), whom the Karaite Ahron ben-Elie, in his <span dir=\"rtl\">עץ חיים</span>, often designates under the denomination of <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמי המחקר</span>, similar to that employed by our author here.",
    "fn.462": "An Arab theologian, cited by Reland, thus expresses himself concerning the attributes of God: «All these eternal attributes are contained within His essence and subsist in Him from eternity to eternity, without division or variation, <i>yet so that one cannot say that these attributes are He Himself, just as one cannot say that they are essentially different from Him</i>, each of the said attributes being conjoined with another, such as life with knowledge, or knowledge with power, etc.» Cf. <i>La Religion des Mahométans, tirée du latin de Reland</i> (The Hague, MDCCXXI), II<sup>e</sup> lesson; Cf. Brucker, <i>Hist. crit. philos.</i>, vol. III, p. 160.",
    "fn.463": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמעאני <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמעאני אלכליה</span> </span> signify the <i>universals</i>, or the <i>general ideas</i>, expressing genera, species, etc.; cf. Maimonides, <i>Abridgment of Logic</i>, chap. X. One perceives, by this passage, that the question which agitated between the <i>nominalists</i> and the <i>realists</i> likewise occupied the Arab thinkers, and that there were, amongst them, those who sought to reconcile the two opinions and to discard that which each of them possessed of too absolute a character, much as the <i>conceptualists</i> have done. It is natural that this question, which has its true origin in the theories of Plato and Aristotle and which Porphyry touched upon at the commencement of his <i>Isagoge</i>, should have been agitated by the Arab philosophers; yet it did not possess, amongst them, the same importance as it did amongst the Christian philosophers of the Middle Ages. As for Maimonides himself, a faithful disciple of Aristotle, he declares that the <i>universals</i> possess no existence outside the mind. See the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, at the commencement of chap. XVIII.",
    "fn.465": "Several doctors of the sect of the <i>Ash’arites</i> (which professes an absolute fatalism), whilst admitting that the actions of men emanate solely from the will and omnipotence of God (see the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. XVII, 3<sup>rd</sup> opinion), nevertheless sought to attribute to man, in the causality of actions, a small part by which he acquires merit or demerit; this concurrence of man in the action created by God, an hypothesis intangible and void of meaning, is designated by the word <i>acquisition</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">اكتساب كسب</span>. See Pococke, Specimen hist. Arab., pages 239, 240, 248 et seq.), and one’s article <i>Arabes dans</i> the <i>Dictionnaire des sciences philosophiques</i>, volume I, page 176. Cf. hereinafter, chap. LXXIII, towards the end of the 6<sup>th</sup> proposition, and Ahron ben-Elie, <span dir=\"rtl\">עץ חיים</span>, chap. IV and LXXXVI (pages 17 and 115 of the Leipzic edition), where <i>acquisition</i> is designated, in Hebrew, by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ריוח</span>.",
    "fn.466": "That is to say, the spirit cannot attach any idea to these assertions, which, all the more so, do not correspond to anything real and objective outside the spirit.",
    "fn.467": "The author alludes to the verbose style and bombastic imagery which the <i>Motecallemin</i> employed in their works, as well as to the vehemence they manifested in discussion, supplying the place of arguments with invectives and sophisms. Cf. hereinafter, at the commencement of chap. LXXIV. Ibn-Tibbon’s version does not entirely accord with the text; one must efface therefrom the words <span dir=\"rtl\">במאמרים שמשתדל אומרם לשמרם ברוב דברים</span>, which appear to be naught but an explanatory gloss that the copyists have introduced into the text. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ובהוצאת דִּבּות והרחקות</span> constitute a double translation of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלתשניעאת</span>, and one believs that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">והרחקות</span> ought to be suppressed; the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">באלציאח ואלתשניעאת</span> signify literally: clamoribus et infamationibus. The version of Al-Ḥarizi is here preferable to that of Ibn-Tibbon; it has: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי יעשו להם חיוק ברוב הטלות והדמיונים המזוייפים ויחזקו אותם בקולות ובגערות</span>.",
    "fn.468": "Literally: <i>et par de nombreux modes</i> (<i>d’argumentation</i>) <i>composés de dialectique et de sophistique</i>.",
    "fn.469": "Ibn-Tibbon's version reads <span dir=\"rtl\">באלו הדברים</span>, which appears to be an ancient error of the copyist; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">באלו הדרכים</span>, as Al-’Harizi has indeed done.",
    "fn.470": "That is, if one then inquires into his inner conviction.",
    "fn.471": "Literally: <i>between</i> (<i>this alternative</i>) <i>that of two things one is the other or is something else</i>. The author is alluding to those who maintained that God's attributes are neither identical with His essence, nor something outside of His essence.",
    "fn.472": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">self</span> that the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have here must be changed to <span dir=\"rtl\">himself</span>, as the mss. and the ed. <i>princeps</i> have, in fact.",
    "fn.473": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">דרך</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(دَرُك)</span> signifies <i>bottom, depth, lowest degree;</i> one reads, for example, in the Korân (IV, 144): <span dir=\"rtl\">إنّ المنافقين في الدرك الاسفل من النار</span> <i>Verily, the hypocrites shall be in the lowest bottom of the fire</i> (<i>of hell</i>]). The two Hebrew translators, as Ibn-Falaquera has already observed, have ill rendered the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דרך</span>: Ibn-Tibbon, who appears to have taken it for a Hebrew word, has translated: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואנשים סרו מזה ה ד ר ך</span>; Al-Ḥarizi, taking <span dir=\"rtl\">דרך</span> in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">אדראך</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">إدراك</span> , <i>perception</i>), has yet more ill rendered this passage, translating: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואנשים לא רצו זאת ה ה ש ג ה ורחקו מעליה</span>. The same error has been committed by the two translators in chap. VIII of the III<sup>rd</sup> part of the <i>Guide</i>, where the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לא ללאנחטאט ללדרך אלאספל</span> (<i>not to descend to the lowest degree</i>) are rendered, in both versions, by: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא לרדת ל ה ש י ג השפל</span>.",
    "fn.474": "See below, chapter LIII.",
    "fn.475": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">תוגׄב לה צפה̈</span> signify: <i>to which an attribute is affirmatively given</i>. In this chapter, the author commencs his researches into the attributes which are, or are not, befitting unto God, and he surveys the various classes of <i>affirmative</i> attributes; one shall see hereinafter that he admitts, in regard to God, only <i>negative</i> attributes.—The word <span dir=\"rtl\">صفة</span>, employed in the sense of <i>attribute</i>, signifies literally <i>description</i>, from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">וصف</span>, <i>to describe</i>, which, in philosophical language, does also signify: <i>to designate by an attribute</i>. One translats this verb, as the context of the sentence demands, now by <i>to give</i> or <i>to have an attribute</i>, now by <i>to designate</i>, now lastly by <i>to qualify</i>, taking this latter word in a general sense, and not in the special sense of <i>to attribute a quality</i>, that is to say, of <i>to give an attribute of the</i> <small>CATEGORY</small> <i>of</i> <small>QUALITY</small>. In this latter sense, the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">كيف</span> is employed, as one shall see further on, in the III<sup>rd</sup> class of attributes.",
    "fn.476": "Literally: <i>the quiddity of a thing and its reality</i>, that is, what a thing is in reality.",
    "fn.477": "See the preceding chapter, p. 183.",
    "fn.478": "It must be remembered that, according to Aristotle, the best definition, that is to say, that which reveals the essence of a thing, is one which at the same time reveals its cause (<i>Posterior Analytics</i>, Book II, chap. 10). Definition is effected by <i>genus</i> and <i>differentia</i>, and it is requisite that it proceed from things anterior and better known; to weaker intellects, one does sometimes demonstrate the anterior by the posterior, yet these are not true definitions (Vide <i>Metaph.</i>, Book VII, chap. 12; <i>Topics</i>, Book VI, chap. 4). It follows thence that whatsoever is primitive and simple cannot be defined, for definition does presuppose somewhat general encompassing the particular object to be defined, whereas the primitive being has naught general preceding it or constituting its cause. Vide <i>Metaph.</i>, Book VIII, chap. 6: <i>Ὅσα δὲ μὴ ἔχει ὕλην, μήτε νοητὴν μήτε αἰσθητήν, εὐθὺς ὅπερ ἕν τι εἶναί ἐστιν ἕϰαστον, ὥςπερ ϰαὶ ὅπερ ὄν τι, τὸ τόδε, τὸ ποιόν, τὸ ποσόν. διὸ ϰαὶ οὐϰ ἔνεστιν ἐν τοῖς ὁρισμοῖς οὔτε τὸ ὂν οὔτε τὸ ἕν · ϰαὶ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι εὐθὺς ἕν τί ἐστιν ὥςπερ ϰαὶ ὄν τι. διὸ ϰαὶ οὐϰ ἔστιν ἕτερόν τι αἴτιον τοῦ ἓν εἶναι οὐθενὶ τούτων, οὐδὲ τοῦ ὄν τι εἶναι</i>. Cf. <i>ibid.</i>, chap. 3: <i>ὥστ’ οὐσίας ἔστι μὲν ἧς ἐνδέχεται εἶναι ὅρον ϰαὶ λόγον, ϰ. τ. λ</i>. God, consequently, cannot be defined; for He has no anterior cause, He does not fall within a <i>genus</i> nor is He distinguished by a <i>differentia</i>, and, generally, He exists not under the conditions of true definition.",
    "fn.479": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמחצלין למא יקולונה</span> signify: <i>those who reduce what they say to its true meaning</i>, that is to say, <i>those who employ terms in their precise sense and express themselves with clarity and exactness</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמחצלין</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(المحصّلين)</span> is the participle of the II<sup>nd</sup> form of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">حصل</span>, taken in the sense of: <i>ad summam ac sensum suum redegit</i> (<i>sermonem</i>]). Some MSS. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמכׄלצין</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(المخلصين)</span>, that is to say: <i>those who explain</i> or <i>expound with clarity</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's version (<i>mss.</i> and edit. <i>princeps</i>) reads <span dir=\"rtl\">המבררים</span>; in several editions, this word has been corrupted to <span dir=\"rtl\">המדברים</span>. Al-’Harizi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">המשכילים למה שהם אומרים</span>, that is to say, <i>those who reflect well upon what they say, who understand it thoroughly</i>.—By the words in question, the author intends to imply that those who possess not an exact comprehension of what <i>definition</i> truly is, imagine that God may be defined; for, by employing a defective definition and demonstrating the anterior by the posterior, one may say, for example: <i>God is the being who moves the universe</i>, or: <i>God is the form of the universe</i>. This is the example cited by Ibn-Roschd when speaking of definitions in which the thing, possessing no anterior, is defined by that which is posterior to it (and which are not true <i>definitions</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">مثال ذلك من حدّ البارى تعالى بانه المحرّك للعالم او انه</span>  <span dir=\"rtl\">صورة الكل</span> . Vide <i>Abridgement of the Organon</i> (Book of <i>Demonstration</i>, chapter on <i>Definitions</i>); cf. Ibn-Falaquera, <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 25. Elsewhere Ibn-Roschd does observe that, if the philosophers have stated that the prime being cannot fall within the same <i>genus</i> with another being, nor be distinguished therefrom by a <i>differentia</i>, this is true only when one takes the words <i>genus</i> and <i>differentia</i> in their proper sense, referring to things which possess both a general form and a particular form, and of which a true definition may be given; but if, by homonymy, one were to take the word <i>genus</i> in an improper sense, that is to say, by taking the posterior for the anterior, one might also encompass God within a genus, by saying, for instance, that He is the being or the substance, etc., and then one might give a definition of Him, albeit an insufficient one. Vide <i>The Incoherence of the Incoherence</i>, at the commencement of the VII<sup>th</sup> <i>Question;</i> cf. the Commentary of Moses of Narbonne, on our passage.",
    "fn.480": "That is, where the thing is qualified by the <i>genre</i> alone or by the <i>différence</i> alone.",
    "fn.482": "Literally: <i>the means by which the essence is terminated and subsists</i>.",
    "fn.483": "By <i>superior genus</i>, the author signifies the category of quality <i>in its entire generality;</i> for one will observe further on that this category comprehends four diverse species. It is in the same sense that the words <i>superior genus</i> are employed by Al-Farâbi, who, in his <i>Logic</i>, when speaking of the category of <i>quality</i>, expresses himself thus (according to the Hebrew version): <span dir=\"rtl\">ותתחלק האיכות אשר הוא הסוג העליון אל ארבעה סוגים אמצעיים</span> 'Quality, which is the <i>superior genus</i>, is divided into four <i>middle genera</i>.' See Heb. ms. of the Imp. Library, Oratory collection, no. 107. Maimonides, in his <i>Compendium of Logic</i> (chap. X), designates the categories, in general, under the denomination of <i>superior genera</i>. Aristotle himself frequently gives to the categories the name of <i>genera</i> (<i>γένο</i>), because they represent the most general notions designated by words. See, for instance, the treatise <i>On the Soul</i>, book I, chap. I (§ 3), where the words <i>ἐν τίνι τῶν γενῶν</i> signify evidently: <i>in which of the categories;</i> see also <i>Categories</i>, at the end of chap. 8: <i>έν ἀμφότεροις τοῖς γένεσι</i>, <i>in both categories</i> (of <i>quality</i> and <i>relation</i>); likewise, in several passages of the <i>Metaphysics</i>.—The author intends to convey that <i>quality</i>, considered firstly in its generality, as one of the ten categories, forms part of the nine <i>accidents</i>, and cannot be attributed to God. He will then provide a specific demonstration for each of the four genera of <i>quality</i>.",
    "fn.484": "Literally: <i>and this would be enough to be far from its reality and essence, I mean</i> (<i>to admit</i>) <i>that it possesses a quality</i>. The sense is: God, becoming, through qualifying attributes, a substratum of accidents, is a sufficient reason to exclude this type of attributes.",
    "fn.485": "Although it be evident, says the author, that one cannot ascribe <i>qualifying</i> attributes to God, one ought to be astonished that the proponents of attributes have rejected <i>qualification</i> from God; for, from their point of view and to be consistent, they must necessarily admit qualifying attributes, inasmuch as every attribute, unless it be the very essence and, consequently, a tautology (as has been stated above, chap. LI), is necessarily <i>qualifying</i>.",
    "fn.486": "See above, and chapter L, page 180, note 1.",
    "fn.487": "These four genera, enumerated by Aristotle, are as follows: 1° capacity (or talent) and disposition (<i>ἕξις ϰαί διάθεσις</i>); 2° that which is said with respect to natural power or impotence (<i>ὅσα ϰατὰ δύναμιν φυσιϰὴν ἤ ἀδυναμίαν λέγεται</i>); 3° affective qualities and affections, or passions (<i>παθητιϰαὶ ποιότητες ϰαὶ πάθη</i>); 4° the figure and external form which each thing possesses (<i>σχῆμά τε ϰαὶ ἡ περὶ ἕϰαστον ὑπάρχουσα μορφή</i>). See the details in Aristotle's <i>Categories</i>, chap. 8; the examples Maimonides is about to cite are connected with the words of Aristotle.",
    "fn.488": "The word <i>ἕξις</i> here translated as 'queen' is the Greek term used by Aristotle (<i>Catégorie</i>, l. c.) to denote something more durable and solid (<i>χρονιώτερον ϰαὶ μονιμώτερον</i>) than disposition; cf. above, p. 121, note 2, and 129, note 3.",
    "fn.489": "The words <i>ἕξις ϰαί διάθεσις</i>, which the Arabic version of Aristotle renders as <span dir=\"rtl\">الملكة والحمال</span> (Heb. <span dir=\"rtl\">הקנין והענין</span>), are thus explained by Al-Farâbi (l. c.): <span dir=\"rtl\">כל תכונה <span dir=\"rtl\">هيئة</span> בנפש וכל תכונה בבעל נפש במה שהוא בעל נפש</span> 'Every <i>disposition in the soul</i>, and every <i>disposition in the animate being</i> inasmuch as it is an animate being.' Dispositions in the soul, or <i>ἕξεις</i>, are acquired capacities, such as sciences, arts, virtues, or natural capacities, such as the knowledge of axioms or the arts possessed by certain animals; the dispositions possessed by the animate being as such, or <i>διϰθεσέις</i>, are, for example, warmth, cooling, health, sickness, etc. Ibn-Roschd, in his <i>Compendium of the Organon</i>, distinguishes these two species of dispositions in the same terms, calling the one <span dir=\"rtl\">الهيآت التي في النفس</span>, and the other <span dir=\"rtl\">الهيآت الحاصلة</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">في المتنفس بها هو متنفس</span>.",
    "fn.490": "The two Hebrew versions bear <span dir=\"rtl\">הרופא</span>, but one does not believe that the author employed the word <span dir=\"rtl\">حكيم</span> here in the sense of <i>physician</i>, which it sometimes has in vulgar parlance; the author intends to convey that the words <i>learned</i> and <i>wise</i> designate <i>dispositions in the soul</i> (see the preceding note), as readily as <i>carpenter</i>, and that all these dispositions can no more be attributed to God than can the others. Immediately thereafter, he makes the same observation concerning moral dispositions, intending to convey that one can no more attribute to God the quality of <i>merciful</i> than that of <i>chaste</i>.",
    "fn.491": "Literally: puissante or qui s’est emparé (of the man). Cf. above, note 3.",
    "fn.492": "The author intends to convey that one can no more ascribe to God the attribute of <i>strong</i> than that of <i>hard;</i> for <i>strong</i> and <i>weak</i> designate, like <i>hard</i> and <i>soft</i>, a natural aptitude or inaptitude to do or to suffer certain things. Cf. Aristotle, <i>Categories</i>, loc. cit. (concerning the second species of <i>quality</i>).",
    "fn.493": "Literally: <i>when the moral quality has not become potent</i>, or <i>has not consolidated itself</i> (in man), that is to say, when it is but the effect of a fleeting impression; for, in this case, it is not a <i>quality</i> properly so called, but a mere <i>affection</i>. Cf. Arist., <i>ibid.</i>: <i>ὅσα δὲ ἀπὸ ῥᾳδίως διαλυομένων ϰαὶ ταχὺ ἀποϰαθισταμένων γίνεται πάθη λέγεται, ποιότητες δὲ οὔ. ϰ. τ. λ</i>.",
    "fn.494": "Colors are said to be <i>qualities affective</i>, inasmuch as they come from an affection (<i>from a passion</i>), like, for example, the redness that comes from shame, the pallor that comes from fear. See Arist., <i>ibid</i>.",
    "fn.495": "Literally: <i>by that which affects it</i> or <i>touches it on the side of quantity in so far as it is quantity</i>. Our author designats the fourth genus of <i>quality</i> by terms which differ entirely from those employed by Aristotle, although the Arabic translation of the <i>Categories</i> is here wholly conformable to the Greek text; the words <i>σχῆμά τε ϰαὶ ἡ περὶ ἕϰαστον ὑπάρχουσα μορφή</i> are thus rendered in Arabic: <span dir=\"rtl\">الشكل والخلقة الموجودة</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">في واحد واحد</span>. The terms of which Maimonides makes use are the same as those which have been employed by Al-Farâbi and, generally, by all the Arabian philosophers. Thus expresses Al-Farâbi himself, in his Logic (Hebrew version): <span dir=\"rtl\">והסוג הרביעי האיכויות אשר ימצאו במיני הכמות במה שהוא כמות</span> “The fourth genus (compriss) the qualities which are found in the several species of quantity, in so far as it is quantity.” Al-Farâbi adducs as examples the straightness and the curvature of lines, the convexity and concavity of surfaces, the <i>figure</i> (<i>σχῆμα</i>) geometrical, such as the circle, the triangle, the square, etc., and the <i>form</i> (<i>μορφη</i>), which, as he says, is a species of figure in the surface of the animate body, lastly, the even and the odd in number. Ibn-Roschd, in his <i>Epitome of the Organon</i>, expresses himself thus: <span dir=\"rtl\">والضرب الرابع من اجناس الكيف هو مثل الاستقامة</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">والانحناء والشكل وبالجملة الكيفيات التى فى الكمية بها هى كيفية</span> . “The fourth of the genera of <i>quality</i> (compriss), e.g., straightness, curvature, and (geometrical) figure, and, in general, the qualities which are in quantity in so far as it is quantity.” One perceivs that the words <i>σχῆμα</i> and <i>μορφή</i>, of which Aristotle makes use, have been generally interpreted by the Arabian philosophers in the sense of a <i>quantitative quality</i>, or, as they themselves express it, of a quality situated in quantity in so far as quantity, that is to say, which qualifies the thing from the standpoint of abstract quantity or of geometrical or arithmetical form in general, without designating the more and the less.",
    "fn.496": "The two Hebrew versions have הכח, in the singular; similarly, some Arabic manuscripts read: אלקוה̈.",
    "fn.497": "See above, p. 195, note 2.",
    "fn.498": "That is to say, who belongs to the category of <i>quality</i>, in general. See above, page 193, note 2.",
    "fn.499": "See above, p. 190, note 1.",
    "fn.500": "See the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chap. I.",
    "fn.501": "Some manuscripts add: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולדׄלך קיל אנה תעאלי ואחד באטלאק</span> ‘That is why it has been said that God is absolutely <i>one</i>’; similarly the version of Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולזה נאמר שהוא אחד גמור</span>. The version of Al-Harizi did not render these words.",
    "fn.502": "Time, says Aristotle, is not itself motion, for change and motion are in the thing alone which changs and movs, whereas time is everywhere and in every thing. Every change is more or less rapid or slow; time is neither the one nor the other, for it is by time that rapidity and slowness are indicated (See <i>Physics</i>, Book IV, at the end of chap. 10). Not being itself motion, it must necessarily be <i>something of motion</i> (<i>ἀνάγϰη τῆς ϰινήσεώς τι εἶναι αὐτόν</i>), or, as our author says, an accident which pertains to motion. One apprehends time by determining motion through the determination <i>of the anterior</i> and <i>of the posterior</i> (<i>ὅταν ὁρίσωμεν τῆν ϰίνησιν τὺ πρότερον ϰαὶ ὕστερον ὁρίζοντες</i>), that is to say, by that which is before and after an intermediate point, which is the <i>present</i> (<i>το νὺν</i>]). Time is therefore the <i>number</i> of motion, according to the <i>before</i> and the <i>after</i> (<i>ἀ ρ ι θ μ ὸ ς ϰινήσεως ϰατὰ τὸ πρότερον ϰαὶ ὕστερον. Ibid.</i>, chap. 11). Time being the measure or the number of motion, it follows that <i>that which is always</i> (<i>τὰ ἀεὶ ὄντα</i>) is not in time; for it is not contained within time, and its being is not measured by time (<i>Ibid.</i>, chap. 12). Cf. the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chap. XIII.",
    "fn.503": "For space is not something that is contained, but something that contains (See Aristotle, <i>ibid.</i>, chap. 2); it cannot therefore be related to God.",
    "fn.504": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">נסב̈ה</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(نسبة)</span> denots any <i>ratio</i> or <i>relation</i> whatsoever, notably a proportional ratio, whereas the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אצׄאפה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(إضافة)</span> is employed particularly to designate the <i>Category of Relation</i> (<i>τὰ προό τι</i>), or the relation between two things said to be such in respect of one another, as, for example, <i>greater</i> and <i>less, father</i> and <i>son, master</i> and <i>slave</i> (See Arist., <i>Categories</i>, chap. 7). It is this <i>relation</i> which the author here affirms to be absolutely inapplicable to God.",
    "fn.505": "Literally: <i>the inversion with mutual equality</i>. The <i>relatives</i>, or the words which fall under the <i>category</i> of <i>relation</i>, possess this property, that they are predicated of reciprocal things (<i>πάντα δὲ τὰ πρός τι πρὸς ἀ ν τ ι σ τ ρ έ φ ο ν τ α λέγεται</i>, Arist., l. c.). Thus, the slave is the slave of the master, as the master is the master of the slave; both alike, they are what they are solely by their reciprocal condition, and one could not exist without the other. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אנעכאס</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">انعكاس</span>, <i>inversion</i>), employed by our author here, is derived from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">عكس</span>, which, in the Arabic versions, corresponds to the Greek verb <i>ἀντιστρέφειν</i>. Ibn-Roschd, in the <i>Compendium of the Organon</i>, in speaking of the two <i>relatives</i>, expresses himself similarly: <span dir=\"rtl\">ومن خواصها ايضا ... ان</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">يرجع كل واحد منهما على صاحبه منعكساً</span> \"Which is also one of their properties …, that they relate to one another <i>reciprocally</i>.\"",
    "fn.506": "See the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, introduction (Propositions XIX and following) and chapter I.",
    "fn.507": "That is to say, albeit the existence of intelligence and that of colour be of the same nature. The author here alludes to that which he shall expound further on, namely, that with the exception of the existence of God, all existence is an <i>accident</i> of the thing which exists. This thesis, maintained by Ibn-Sînâ and disputed by others, is adopted unreservedly by our author; wherefore he here adds the words: <i>according to our opinion</i>. See hereafter, at the commencement of chap. LVII.",
    "fn.508": "More literally: <i>between that which has absolutely naught in common with what is outside of it</i>. One perceives that the author has expressed himself in an incomplete manner, and that the words: <i>and another being</i> must be understood. The sense is: How could one conceive a relation between two beings which possess absolutely nothing in common, not even the genus of existence? for in the one, existence is its substance, and in the other, it is an accident.",
    "fn.509": "The <i>proximate</i> or <i>immediate</i> species is that which immediately follows individuality, and which, in no respect, can be considered as a <i>genus;</i> it is the species specialissima (<i>τὸ εἰδιϰώτατον εἶδος</i>). Cf. Porphyry's <i>Isagoge</i>, chap. 2: <i>ϰαὶ πᾶν τὸ πρὸ τῶν ατόμων π ρ ο σ ε χ ῶ ς ϰατηγορούμενον εἶδος ἄν εἴη μόνον, οὐϰέτι δὲ ϰαὶ γένος</i>.",
    "fn.510": "As Moïse de Narbonne does remark, the author here refers to that which has been expounded by Aristotle at the end of the VII<sup>th</sup> book of the <i>Physics</i> (chap. 4). Aristotle therein treats of the different species of motion and the relations which they may bear one to another; the question is to ascertain which motions admit of comparison, and which differ amongst themselves solely by quantity. In order that two motions may be compared one with another, it is requisite that they be susceptible of being equally swift, that is to say, they must differ solely by quantity, and no qualitative difference ought to exist between them. In general, for two things to be comparable one with another, it suffices not that they be not mere homonyms, but it is necessary that the two things be distinguished by no essential <i>difference</i>, neither in themselves nor in their <i>substratum</i>; that is to say, that they be not only comprehended within the same <i>genus</i>, but also characterised by the same <i>difference</i>, in a word, that they be of the same <i>species</i>: A’λλ’ ἀρὰ οὐ μόνον δεῖ τὰ σνμβλητὰ μὴ ὁμώνυμα είναι ἀλλὰ ϰαὶ μὴ ἔχειν δ ι α φ ο ρ ά ν, μήτε ὅ μήτ’ἐν ᾧ. Thus, for example, says Aristotle, colour possesses a <i>division</i> (into different colours). Colours as such are therefore not comparable one with another; one cannot assert that such-and-such a black is more intense than such-and-such a white. Comparison can only be established for one and the same colour, for example, between two white or black objects. Cf. above, page 131, note 2.",
    "fn.511": "The mss. read <span dir=\"rtl\">כאנת</span>; but it may be necessary to read <span dir=\"rtl\">כאנא</span> or better <span dir=\"rtl\">כאנתא</span>.",
    "fn.512": "Ibn-Tibbon's version bears <span dir=\"rtl\">תחת שני סוגים עליונים</span> (under two <i>superior</i> genera); it is necessary to efface the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עליונים</span>, albeit it is also found in the manuscripts of that version. One must likewise efface the words …<span dir=\"rtl\">אין ספק ש</span>, and a little further on, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">שאין ספק לשום אדם</span>, which are found neither in the manuscripts of Ibn-Tibbon's version nor in the Arabic text.",
    "fn.513": "The word <i>genre</i> is used here in the sense of <i>catégorie</i>. See above, p. 193, note 2.",
    "fn.514": "After having given an example of two things belonging to two <i>superior genres</i> or to two different categories, the author gives here an example of two <i>intermediate genres</i> belonging to the same category.",
    "fn.515": "The feminine suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">בינהא</span> refers to God and creatures (<span dir=\"rtl\">מכׄלוקאתה</span>).",
    "fn.516": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">תבלע</span>, which one pronounces <span dir=\"rtl\">تخص</span> as the infinitive of the V<sup>th</sup> form, signifies <i>deliverance, means of salvation;</i> the sense is: there is no means of extricating oneself by desiring to attribute any quality to God, and, however one may proceed, difficulties are encountered. This is what Al-’Harizi has expressed by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ואי אפשר להמלט מחטוא</span>. Ibn-Tibbon, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">הנה לא תנצל</span>, has pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">تَخَلَّصَ</span>, as the 2<sup>nd</sup> person future passive of the II<sup>nd</sup> form.",
    "fn.517": "Literally: <i>même du côté du rapport</i>, that is to say, whether this attribute is taken in the relation that exists between God and his creatures.",
    "fn.518": "More literally: <i>but they</i> (the relational attributes) <i>are among the attributes that most deserve to be indulgent in their employment as qualifications of God</i>.",
    "fn.519": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version generally carry <span dir=\"rtl\">שלא יתחייב רבוי הקדמות</span>; one must read, with the manuscripts: <span dir=\"rtl\">הקדמון שלא יחייב רבוי</span>.",
    "fn.520": "The editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon add <span dir=\"rtl\">הצייר</span> (the painter), a word that is not found in the mss. of this version.",
    "fn.521": "See above (pag. 194) the first example of the IIIe class of attributes.",
    "fn.522": "Literally: <i>of the essence of that to which they refer</i> or <i>to which they are attributed</i>. One of the Leyden manuscripts bears <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמוצוף</span>, a word which designates the <i>subject</i> of the attribute; it is this latter reading which has been adopted by Al-’Harizi, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">המתואר</span>. The sense is that actions, while emanating from the sole divine essence, as shall presently be stated, are not, however, something permanent within the essence, as would be <i>artistic capacity</i>, and thought separates them from the essence by limiting them to a past, present, or future moment. Cf. the Commentary of Ibn-Caspi: <span dir=\"rtl\">וזה לפי שלא נתארהו בתאר מיושב נח בו כמו שמות הבינוני והפעול ויתר שמות התארים אבל זה פעל אם עבר ואם עתיד וכו׳</span>.",
    "fn.523": "Literally: <i>qu'il ne faut pas que ces actions diverses soient faites par des choses</i> (or <i>des idées</i>) <i>diverses dans l'essence de l'agent</i>.",
    "fn.524": "See the following chapter, where the author expands on the <i>attributes of action</i>. We will see that the author shares this opinion with other Arab philosophers.",
    "fn.525": "A ms. reads <span dir=\"rtl\">זאיד עלי דׄאתה</span>; similarly Ibn-Tibbon <span dir=\"rtl\">מוסף על עצמו</span>.",
    "fn.526": "Literally: <i>thus, a brief summary of what is</i> (<i>contained</i>) <i>in this chapter is</i> etc.",
    "fn.527": "Literally: <i>… who are found in the books</i> (<i>sacred</i>) <i>and by which one indicates God, are</i> (<i>employed</i>) <i>in terms of the multiplicity of His actions, and not due to a multiplicity in His essence</i>.",
    "fn.528": "See above, chapter XXVI, XLVI (page 160) and XLVII.",
    "fn.529": "Literally: <i>that which has led to believe in the existence of attributes of the Creator, among those who believe in them, is almost what has led to believe in corporality, among those who believe in it</i>.",
    "fn.530": "Literally: <i>and the books of revelation;</i> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתנזיל</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(التنزيل)</span> is here particularly applied to the books of Moses and employed in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">תורה</span>. Cf. above, chap. XLVI, page 158: “In all the books of the <i>prophets</i> and likewise in the <i>Pentateuch</i>”, where the text bears (fol. 50 <i>a</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">פי גׄמיع כתب אלאנביא ופי אלתורה</span>. At other times, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתנזיל</span> applies to the whole of Holy Writ in general; see, for instance, at the close of chap. LI: <span dir=\"rtl\">כתב אלתנזיל</span>.",
    "fn.531": "See what the author says in the previous chapter about the III<sup>rd</sup> class of attributes.",
    "fn.532": "In the verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">יצרחוא</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">עהדוה</span>, one must implicitly understand, as the subject, <i>the prophets</i>, and not <i>those who believe in the attributes</i>, as some commentators have supposed; for, in this latter case, the verbs should be in the singular, like the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מעתקד</span>. Instead of the words <span dir=\"rtl\">יבארהו בו</span>, which are found in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">יבארוהו</span>, as the manuscripts of this version possess. The sense of this passage is as follows: the attributes which the prophets ascribe to God, and in which one has claimed to discern <i>essential attributes</i>, that is to say, forming a part of the divine essence (See above, page 180, note 1), are at bottom naught but qualities, and the prophets, without pronouncing clearly in this regard, and by metaphorically assimilating God to animated beings, have ascribed to Him attributes akin to the conditions of these beings, and which are familiar to all.",
    "fn.533": "Literally: <i>by the diversity of actions the ideas which exist in the agent are not diverse</i>. Here the author approaches the discussion on <i>attributes of action</i>, to which he has alluded at the close of the last chapter, and he makes comprehensible, by examples, the distinction which one ought to establish between this sort of attributes, applicable to God, and those which have been termed <i>essential attributes</i>. The question of attributes, as one has stated higher up (page 180), had already occupied the Muslim theologians and philosophers, and one finds among them likewise the distinction between <i>essential attributes</i> and <i>attributes of action</i>. See Pococke, Specimen hist. Ar., page 223; Schahrestâni, History of Religious and Philosophical Sects, Arabic text (published by M. Cureton), page 64; German translation by M. Haarbrücker, volume I, page 95. Cf. Ba’hya, <i>Duties of the Hearts</i>, book I (On the Unity of God), chap. 10.",
    "fn.534": "Literally: <i>and that the idea of no action of either of them is the idea of the other</i>.",
    "fn.535": "That is, when our perception makes us see in God things to which we attach various ideas, because, in us, they are perfectly distinct from one another.",
    "fn.536": "As one shall observe further on, <i>knowledge</i>, <i>power</i>, and <i>will</i> are, besides <i>life</i>, the principal attributes which the ancient Muslim theologians, and, following them, certain Jewish theologians (notably the Karaites), ascribed unto God as <i>essential</i> and <i>eternal</i> attributes. Cf. Schahrestâni, German transl., vol. I, pp. 42, 95, and <i>passim;</i> Pococke, <i>Specimen hist. Ar.] </i>, p. 223; Schmœlders, <i>Essai sur les écoles philosophiques chez les Arabes</i>, pp. 187 and 196. These are the same attributes cited by Al-Ghazali, in his <i>Destruction of the Philosophers</i> (at the commencement of the sixth <i>Question</i>), when discussing the opinion of the philosophers and the <i>Muʿtazila;</i> one cites the Hebrew version: <span dir=\"rtl\">הסכימו הפלוסופים על שקרות קיום המדע והיכולת והרצון בהתחלה הראשונה כמו שהסכימו עליו המעתזלה וכו׳</span> «The philosophers are agreed that it is false to affirm <i>knowledge</i>, <i>power</i>, and <i>will</i> in the first principle (or in God), and the <i>Muʿtazila</i> likewise concurred thereupon, &c.» Cf. Saadia, <i>Book of Beliefs and Opinions</i>, II, 1 to 4; Ahron ben-Elie, <i>Tree of Life</i>, chap. LXVII.",
    "fn.537": "The author alludes, on the one hand, to the <i>Ṣifātiyya</i> or partisans of attributes, who, without circumlocution, ascribe eternal attributes to God, and, on the other hand, to a portion of the <i>Muʿtazila</i>, who believed to satisfy the principle of absolute unity by declaring that God is <i>powerful</i>, not by power, but by His <i>essence</i>; that He is <i>living</i>, not by life, but by His <i>essence</i>; and so forth. Cf. Pococke, <i>l. c.] </i>, p. 214, and the text of Abū’l-Faraj, p. 19; Schahrestâni, <i>l. c.] </i>, p. 42; Schmölders, <i>l. c.] </i>, pp. 196, 197. The discussion concerning attributes recurred amongst Jewish theologians; there were those (notably within the Karaite sect) who, following the example of certain <i>Muʿtazila</i>, admitted essential attributes (<span dir=\"rtl\">ספורים עצמיים</span>, cf. Ahron ben-Elie, <i>Tree of Life</i>, at the end of chap. LXVII). Amongst the Rabbinic doctors, Maimonides himself names the Spaniard R. Joseph ibn-Ṣaddīq as having followed the opinion of the <span dir=\"rtl\">בעלי התארים</span> or <i>partisans of attributes;</i> cf. the collection of Maimonides' Letters (letter to R. Samuel ibn-Tibbon), Amsterdam edit., fol. 14 <i>b</i>. — As to the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">لذاته</span>, <i>by His essence</i>, the Muslim theologians themselves were not entirely agreed upon its precise meaning (Cf. Pococke, <i>ibid.] </i>, p. 215 et seq.); it is for this reason that Maimonides states that <i>they do not express themselves in intelligible terms</i>. According to Ibn-Falaquera (<i>Moré-ha-Moré</i>, p. 151), the <i>Mutakallimūn</i> understood by this expression that the power, knowledge, &c., attributed to God, are something which pertains <i>to His essence alone</i>, and that He possesses therein nothing in common with any other being.",
    "fn.538": "It is well to recall here what the author states elsewhere concerning the rational part of the soul, in accordance with Peripatetic theories. In the first of the <i>Eight Chapters</i> serving as an introduction to the treatise <i>Abôth</i>, after having discoursed upon nutrition, sensibility, imagination, and appetition, one finds him expressing himself thus concerning the fifth part of the soul, or the rational faculty (Vide Pococke’s <i>Porta Mosis</i>, pp. 188 and 189): “The <i>rational part</i> is that faculty existing in man by which he is <i>intelligent</i>, by which reflection is performed, by which he acquires science, and by which he distinguishes between that which is foul and that which is fair, in respect of actions. The actions (of the rational faculty) are some practical, others speculative. The practical pertains to the domain of art or of reflection; the speculative is that by which man knows invariable beings as they are, and this is what is termed <i>science</i> in an absolute sense. The artistic faculty is that by which man acquires the arts, as, for example, carpentry, agriculture, medicine, and navigation. Reflection is that by which one examines, in a thing one desires to do, and at the moment one desires to do it, whether it be possible or not to perform it, and, supposing it to be possible, how it ought to be performed.” One perceives that what the author designates as the <i>rational</i> or <i>reasonable</i> part of the soul is the totality of all those faculties of the soul which belong solely to man; all other faculties, forming the irrational part of the soul, appertain equally to animals. This division of the soul’s faculties into two parts, one reasonable (<i>τὸ λογιστιϰὸν</i> or <i>τὸ λόγον ἔχον</i>), the other unreasonable (<i>τὸ ἄλογον</i>), belongs rather to Plato than to Aristotle; the latter, indeed, seems openly to disapprove of it, in criticising those who affirm that the soul possesses parts (Vide <i>Treatise on the Soul</i>, Bk. III, chap. 9, and Trendelenburg’s Commentary, on the same chapter, §§ 2 and 3, p. 528). Nevertheless, Aristotle himself makes use of this division in his classification of virtues. See <i>Eth. Nicom.</i>, Bk. I, chap. 13; Bk. VI, chap. 2: <i>Πρότερον μέν οὖν ἐλέχθη δύ’ εἶναι μέρη τῆς ψυχῆς, τό τε λόγον ἔχον ϰαὶ τὸ ἄλογον · νῦν δὲ περὶ τοῦ λόγον ἔχοντος τὸν αὐτόν τρόπον διαιρετέον. ϰαὶ ὑποχείσθω δύο τὰ λόγον ἔχοντα, ἕν μὲν ᾧ θεωροῦμεν τὰ τοιαῦτα τῶν ὄντων ὅσων αἱ ἀρχαὶ μὴ ἐνδέχονται ἄλλως ἔχειν, ἕν δὲ ᾧ τὰ ἐνδεχόμενα · ϰ. τ. λ</i>. It is this classification which has served as the basis for what Maimonides states concerning the <i>rational part</i> of the soul in the passage just cited.",
    "fn.539": "Literally: <i>and their number has almost no end, I mean the number of arts produced by the rational faculty</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">the number of actions</span> that one reads in all the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">the number of crafts</span>, as in the mss. and Al-’Harizi's version.",
    "fn.540": "The author does not concur with what he states elsewhere to be the opinion of Aristotle and of all Peripatetic philosophers, namely, that the First Cause, being absolute unity, can have but unity as its effect, and that from the absolute <i>One</i> the manifold cannot emanate. One shall have occasion to revert to this point in the second part of this work, Chapter XXII, where this question is particularly treated.",
    "fn.541": "Literally: <i>a characteristic</i> (<i>coming</i>) <i>from his action, and not a characteristic of his essence</i>.",
    "fn.542": "Literally: <i>for because they</i> (the adherents of attributes) <i>do not relinquish the word</i> <small>COMPOSITION</small>, <i>the idea thereof is not thereby removed from the essence endowed with attributes;</i> that is to say: as soon as one admits an essence possessing <i>essential</i> attributes, one implicitly admits <i>composition</i>, whereas by admitting only attributes of <i>action</i>, it does not follow that the essence from which the actions emanate is something composite. — The words <span dir=\"rtl\">יבטל מענאה</span> are rendered in Ibn-Tibbon’s version as <span dir=\"rtl\">יבטל ענינה</span>, the feminine suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">ענינה</span> referring to <span dir=\"rtl\">מלת ההרכבה</span>; it is by a printer’s error that the editions bear, some <span dir=\"rtl\">ענינם</span>, others <span dir=\"rtl\">ענינים</span>.",
    "fn.543": "Literally: <i>but there is here a place of doubt that has led them to this</i>, that is, which has led certain theologians to admit essential attributes<i>.",
    "fn.544": "The author has here expressed himself in a truncated and obscure manner, and his words have been diversely interpreted. One would doubtless infer that, according to the adherents of the attributes, a distinction must be drawn between the <i>transitive</i> attributes, which indicate an action produced upon an object, and those which one may term <i>neutral;</i> these latter alone are what they call <i>essential attributes, which proceed not from His actions</i>, whilst the <i>transitive</i> attributes, on the contrary, as, for example, that of <i>creator</i>, cannot be <i>essential</i>, relating necessarily to something external to God, for it would be absurd to assert <i>that God is the creator of Himself</i>. Behold how Moses of Narbonne explains these latter words: <span dir=\"rtl\">ירצה אבל היותו בורא הוא בורא לזולתו לא לעצמו ולכן לא יהיה תאר עצמי לו יתעלה לפי שהוא מכלל פעולותיו</span>. «He intends: but <i>to be creator</i> signifies that He creats that which is external to Himself, and not <i>Himself;</i> wherefore this is not an <i>essential</i> attribute of God, for it does form part of His <i>actions</i>.» Joseph Caspi says that the author here speaks of the arguments of little worth conceived by the subtlest amongst the <i>Motecallemîn</i> to defend their opinion concerning the attributes: they have, he says, divided the attributes into <i>transitive</i> and <i>neutral</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">והוא שיעשו חלוקה בין תארים יוצאים ובין מה שהם בודדים</span>), which the author indicats by citing as example the attribute derived from the verb <i>to create</i>, to which (adds Caspi) one might substitute any other transitive verb, such as <i>to build, to write</i>, etc.",
    "fn.545": "The numerous attributes admitted by the <i>Cifâtiyya</i>, according to the texts of the Koran, were subsequently reduced unto seven, which are: life, knowledge, will, power, speech, hearing, and sight. Vide Schahrestâni, German transl., vol. I, p. 95; Schmœlders, l. c., p. 187; cf. Pococke, Sp. hist. Ar., p. 216. Ibn-Roschd does observe that these seven attributes are equally suitable unto the soul, and that the essence of God, as determined by these attributes, would be of the same genus as the soul, with this difference, that it is not found within a body. Vide the observations added by Moses of Narbonne, at the conclusion of his Commentary, upon this chapter.— The word <span dir=\"rtl\">כתאב</span>, <i>Scripture</i>, does not here signify the <i>Bible</i>, but, generally, a book considered as divine, and notably the Koran; for the matter at hand does chiefly concern the doctrines professed by the Muslim theologians, and which certain Jewish doctors had borrowed from them.",
    "fn.546": "These are, as we have said above, <i>neutral</i> attributes, indicating no action produced on an object.",
    "fn.547": "The author does now seek to demonstrate that it is erroneously that one has believed it possible to separate these four attributes from the other attributes indicating <i>actions</i> and to constitute them <i>essential</i> attributes. Herein, in substance, is his reasoning, which he has not presented with all desirable clarity: Firstly, if by <i>to know</i> one does understand <i>to perceive oneself</i>, then <i>life</i> and <i>knowledge</i> are absolutely identical in God, indicating both <i>a perception of oneself</i>, and there is no cause to make of them two distinct attributes. But if one does admit the sense which the partisans of the attributes here attach to the word <i>knowledge</i>, by which they signify the knowledge God possesses of created things, there is no reason whatsoever to make of knowledge an <i>essential</i> attribute, and the same holds true for power and will; for all these attributes do necessarily indicate, according to them, relations existing between God and created things, and are found under the same conditions as the attributes of action, as, for example, that of <i>creator</i>. Thus, the <i>essential</i> attributes, reduced to their true value, are but a vain hypothesis.",
    "fn.548": "Life, says Aristotle, consists principally in sensation and in intelligence (<i>ἔοιϰε δὴ τὸ ζἠν εἶναι ϰυρίως τὸ αἰσθάνεσθαι ἤ νοεῖν</i>, Eth. ad Nic., l. IX, chap. 9). To affirm of a being that it lives, it suffices that there be within it intelligence, or sensation, or motion (Vide <i>Treatise on the Soul</i>, l. II, chap. 2). In God, there can be question only of an <i>intelligible</i> perception, of the perception of Himself; His life is intelligence, and for Him knowledge and life are absolutely identical. Vide Metaph., XII, 7: <i>ϰαὶ ζωὴ δέ γε ὑπάρχει · ἡ γὰρ νοῦ ἐνέργεια ζωή, ϰ. τ. λ</i>.",
    "fn.549": "This is what the author will explain in greater detail in chapter LXVIII. — The first <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמדרכה̈</span> is the active participle <span dir=\"rtl\">(المُذَكِّرَة)</span> ; the second, the passive participle <span dir=\"rtl\">(الْمُدْرَكَة)</span> .",
    "fn.550": "The inanimate body has no perception, and all perceptions, both sensible and intelligible, belong to the different faculties of the soul.",
    "fn.551": "That is, the attributes called <i>essential</i>.",
    "fn.552": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא</span>, which several editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version carry, one must read, according to the Arabic: <span dir=\"rtl\">העצם ההוא</span>, as indeed the manuscripts and the <i>princeps</i> edition have it.",
    "fn.553": "That is to say, one cannot establish a difference between the attributes of action and the attributes of relation; the latter cannot, any more than the former, be called <i>attributes essential</i>.",
    "fn.554": "Literally: <i>and with what one has also set forth regarding the reality of relation and</i> (<i>one says</i>) <i>that it is erroneously admitted</i>. The author intends to convey: Besides that the attributes of relation cannot be accounted <i>essential</i> attributes, one has shown above, in elucidating the true import of <i>relation</i>, that it is only through error that one has deemed it possible to admit of any relation whatsoever between God and created things. See, in the preceding chapter, the IV<sup>th</sup> class of attributes.—By the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ואנהא מטׄנונה̈</span>, the author makes allusion to that which he has stated in the indicated passage (fol. 60 <i>b</i> of our text): <span dir=\"rtl\">אמא אן חכון בינהמא נםבה̈ מא פהו אמר <span dir=\"rtl\">יָסֶךְ</span> בה אנה <span dir=\"rtl\">יעח</span> ולים כדׄלך</span> <i>But that there should be any relation between them, this is a matter which has been deemed admissible, albeit it is not so in reality</i> (p. 200).",
    "fn.555": "That is to say, these are attributes relating to <i>actions</i>.",
    "fn.556": "The author here takes in hand the interpretation of sundry significant passages of the Pentateuch, in which, after an allegorical manner, mention is made of the perception of the divine essence and the attributes of God. The commentators do observe that Moses is here styled <i>prince of the learned</i>, and not, as elsewhere, <i>prince of the prophets</i>, inasmuch as the present discourse concerns the most elevated metaphysical questions, the solution of which Moses did seek, and which were intended for him to be the object of philosophical speculation.",
    "fn.557": "It is likely that the author intentionally inverted the order of the two prayers of Moses, in order to mention first the most important one, which had as its object the very essence of God. As for Moses, says Abravanel, he followed the usual order of studies, beginning with the easier one, in order to pass on to what is more difficult.",
    "fn.558": "The author, as we will see, does not take the word <span dir=\"rtl\">faithful</span> here in the sense of <i>faithful</i>, but in that of <i>true</i> and <i>stable</i> or <i>solid</i>, and he sees an allusion to true <i>thoughts</i> and <i>solid</i> thoughts.",
    "fn.559": "The Arabic text expresses itself here in an irregular manner, stating literally: <i>thus then the perception of these actions are His attributes etc.</i>. One might be tempted to believe that some scribal error has here occurred, and, indeed, the MSS. present diverse variants which evince that this passage has been corrupted by the copyists. One of the Leyden MSS. has: <span dir=\"rtl\">לאן אלארא אלגיר צחיחה̈ אדׄא לא תתׄבת תלך אלאפעאל ותלך אלאפעאל הי צפאתה תעאלי</span>; in another manuscript, one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">לאן אלארא אלגיר צחיחה̈ לא תתׄבת אדראך תלך אלאפעאל אלתי הי צפאתה תעאלי</span>. It is observed that the reading one has adopted is still the least incorrect; the version of Ibn-Tibbon is in perfect accord with our reading, and reproducs the same incorrection. The sole MS. of Al-’Harizi’s version has: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי הדעות שאינם אמתיות לא תתקיים השגתם והפעלים ההם הם מדותיו</span>; in the edition which was made at London of the first part of this version (pag. 53), this passage has been arbitrarily altered; therein one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי הדעות שאינם אמתיות לא תתקיים השגתם אם כן השגת הפעלים ההם הם מדותיו</span>.",
    "fn.560": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מדה</span>, which means <i>measure</i>, is used, in rabbinic language, in the sense of <i>property, quality, characteristic</i>.",
    "fn.561": "See <i>Mischnâ</i>, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Abôth</i>, chap. V, §§ 13 and 14.",
    "fn.562": "Literally: <i>through actions by which I must imitate your actions</i> (<i>employed</i>) <i>in their governance</i>.",
    "fn.563": "All mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אגׄנה̈</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">أجنة</span> , pl. of <span dir=\"rtl\">جنين</span> ), <i>the embryos</i>, although all the suffixes related to them in the following words are in the singular.",
    "fn.564": "It is essential to remember that we are employing here, as elsewhere, the term <i>passion</i> in the sense of the Greek term <i>πάθος</i>, rendered in Arabic as <span dir=\"rtl\">انفعال</span>, and which designates <i>passivity</i> in general, or the affection by an impression coming from outside.",
    "fn.565": "Passion, or passivity, implies a change in the one who is the object of the impression, and, consequently, no passivity or affective quality can be attributed to God. See toward the end of chap. XXXV (pag. 133), and above, at the beginning of chap. LV (pag. 225).",
    "fn.566": "That is, grant us the women we have taken; <i>accordez-nous les femmes que nous avons enlevées;</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">אותם</span> refers to the women, and the masculine suffix is irregular, like <span dir=\"rtl\">אבותם</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">אחיהם</span>. In several editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, another example has been substituted, taken from Job (19, 21); but the princeps edition is in agreement with our text.",
    "fn.567": "Literally: <i>or a general thing that causes to perish</i>, or <i>that destroys</i>.",
    "fn.568": "Literally: <i>the children, and the children of children</i>.",
    "fn.569": "Literally: <i>which do not emanate from any of us against another</i>.",
    "fn.570": "That is, if he wants, like Moses, <i>to know the ways of God</i>, and to conform his actions to those that emanate from God.",
    "fn.571": "The author here departs from the interpretation of Onkelos and the Talmud (<i>Yômâ</i>, fol. 86 <i>a</i>) and from that of all commentators, who concur in assigning to the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">נַקֵּה</span> the signification of <i>to declare pure</i> or <i>innocent, to absolve</i>. In the passage from Isaiah, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וְנׅקׇּתׇה</span> signifies literally: <i>she shall be rendered void</i>, which is to say: <i>she shall be stripped of her inhabitants, devastated;</i> it is therefore inopportunely that the author adducs this passage in support of the meaning of <i>to uproot, to destroy</i>, which he here assigns to the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">נַקֵּה</span>.",
    "fn.572": "Literally: <i>and one calls not</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">שׂונא</span> (<i>hating one, foe</i>) <i>but the idolater</i>. Ibn-Tibbon adds the word <i>alone</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לבד</span>), which is not found in our Arabic text, the which likewise expresses not the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כאשר אמר</span>, found in both the Hebrew versions. One has deemed it fitting to append the words: <i>who is likewise presented as an object of God’s hatred;</i> for, in the cited passage, the hatred is not ascribed to the idolater, but unto God, and this passage accords not well with that which preceds.",
    "fn.573": "See Deuteronomy, chapter XIII, verses 13-19.",
    "fn.574": "That is, in the enumeration of the thirteen <i>middôth</i> and in the Decalogue, related to the passage in Deuteronomy concerning the idolatrous city.",
    "fn.575": "The reading does vary somewhat in the MSS.: the majority present <span dir=\"rtl\">גמאר</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(غبار)</span>, some few <span dir=\"rtl\">גמר</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">خُمُرُ</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">دُور</span>), and one of the Leyden MSS., <span dir=\"rtl\">אגמאר</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(أغمار)</span>; all these words, some being singular, others plural, signify <i>mixed multitude, throng</i> (miscella hominum turba). The version of Ibn-Tibbon (MSS. and edition princeps) has <span dir=\"rtl\">בתוך</span>, <i>in the midst</i>, which, in several editions, has been arbitrarily altered to <span dir=\"rtl\">בחטא</span>, <i>for the sin</i>. The sole MS. of Al-’Harizi’s version has <span dir=\"rtl\">בחיי</span>, <i>in the lifetime;</i> the editor of London has, one knows not why, substituted <span dir=\"rtl\">בחטא</span>, following the erroneous reading of the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version.",
    "fn.576": "That is, those designated by the thirteen <i>middôth</i>.",
    "fn.577": "The author appears here to have made an error of memory in stating that these words of the Rabbins serve as a commentary upon this passage of Leviticus: <i>Be ye holy; for I, the Lord your God, am holy</i>. Elsewhere the author himself states that the words in question apply to this passage of Deuteronomy (XXVIII, 9): <i>and thou shalt walk in His ways</i>; cf. <i>Mischné Tôrâ</i>, or <i>Abridgement of the Talmud</i>, treatise <i>Dé’ôth</i>, chap. I, § 6. Indeed, the aforesaid words are found in the commentary upon Deuteronomy known under the name of <i>Siphrî</i>, not in the passage indicated by Maimonides, but in another analogous passage: <i>to walk in all His ways, etc.</i> (Deut., X, 12); cf. also the <i>Yalkout</i>, upon this latter passage, and compare <i>Babylonian Talmud</i>, treatise <i>Sôtâ</i>, fol. 14 <i>a</i>, where, in a similar manner, the words are explained: <i>Ye shall walk after the Lord your God</i> (Deut, XIII, 5). The author, in stating here that the aforesaid words of the doctors serve as an explanation for the words <i>be ye holy, etc.</i>, has perchance confounded in his memory the passage from the <i>Siphrî</i> with a passage from the <i>Midrasch, Wayyikrâ rabbâ</i> (sect. 24), where one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">כשם שאני פרוש כך תהיו פרושים כשם שאני קדוש כך תהיו קדושים הה״ד קדושים תהיו</span>.",
    "fn.578": "It goes without saying that the word <i>privation</i> is used here in the Aristotelian sense of the word <i>στέρησις.</i> Cf. above, chap. XVII, p. 69.",
    "fn.579": "One of the Leyden manuscripts bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">מתׄל אן יכון לה שי באל קוח̈</span> <i>as, for example,</i> (<i>to admit</i>) <i>that one has a thing</i> <small>IN POTENCY</small>. Al-’Harizi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">כדי שלא יהיה לו שום דבר בכח</span>. It is observed that Al-’Harizi has preferred to read <span dir=\"rtl\">באלקוה̈</span>, but he has made an antithetical error by retaining the negation <span dir=\"rtl\">שלא</span>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כדי</span> is doubtless a copyist’s error, for <span dir=\"rtl\">כמו</span>.",
    "fn.580": "Cf. above, p. 69.",
    "fn.581": "This is evident from the entire ensemble of Aristotelian doctrines on movement. See, among other things, <i>Physique</i>, book III, ch. 2: Ἥ <i>τε ϰίνησις ἐνέργεια μέν τις εἶναι δοϰεῖ, ἀτελὴς δέ · αἴτιον δ’οτι ἀτελὲς τὸ δυνατόν, οὗ ἐστίν ἡ ἐνέργεια ϰίνησις.</i>",
    "fn.582": "See above, chap. LII, pp. 200 and 201.",
    "fn.583": "The sphere of fixed stars, which surrounds the spheres of planets and forms the limit of the universe, is (after the superior or <i>environnante</i>) the most extensive body that can be imagined. See, on the celestial spheres and their number, the second part of this work, chap. IV; <i>cf.</i> III<sup>e</sup> part, chap. XIV.",
    "fn.584": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read <span dir=\"rtl\">ויודע וחכם</span>; one must erase the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ויודע</span>, which is not found in the manuscripts.",
    "fn.585": "Literally: (<i>in this way</i>) <i>that the difference between these attributes and our attributes would consist</i> (<i>only</i>) <i>in the greatest, etc.</i>.",
    "fn.586": "That is, the attributes of God and our own would fall under the same category, and the same definition would apply to both; for their <i>quiddity</i> would be the same, and they would differ only in degree. Cf. above, chapter XXXV, p. 131.",
    "fn.587": "Literally: <i>for one does not express oneself by the form</i> <small>AF’ALOU</small> <span dir=\"rtl\">(أَفْعَلُ)</span> <i>save amongst those things of which that which is in question</i> (the adjective) <i>is predicated</i> <small>CONVENTIONALLY</small>, that is to say, to which the adjective in question applies in some sort as a <i>common</i> or <i>appellative name;</i> thus, for instance, <i>living</i> is a common name for all that has life; <i>powerful</i>, for all that has power. Concerning the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בתואטא (بتواطؤ)</span>, cf. above, page 6, note 2.",
    "fn.588": "Here, in summary, is the sense of this entire passage: The adherents of <i>essential attributes</i>, says the author, ought to comprehend that the difference between the attributes of God and our own cannot consist solely in degree; for, in such a case, there would exist between them a proportional relation and, consequently, a similitude, which they could no more admit than the similitude between the divine essence and our own. But where there is no similitude, neither is there a common definition; consequently, the adherents of attributes, who, whilst rejecting similitude, yet admit that the same definition embraces the attributes of God and our own, are in contradiction with themselves.",
    "fn.589": "Cf. above, p. 6, note 3.",
    "fn.590": "Cf. above, p. 184, note 3.",
    "fn.591": "See above, chap. LXXIII, 4<sup>th</sup> proposition.",
    "fn.592": "See above, p. 228, note 4.",
    "fn.593": "In the preceding chapters, the author has contended against those who believe themselves able to admit as <i>essential attributes</i> of God, life, knowledge, power, and will. Here he will demonstrate that one cannot even admit as <i>attributes</i> of God, <i>existence</i>, <i>unity</i>, and <i>eternity</i>, which are more generally considered as attributes inherent in the divine essence. Cf. Ba’hya, <i>Duties of the Hearts</i>, book I, chap. 10.",
    "fn.595": "That is to say, something which has newly supervened upon its quiddity and which is posterior thereto. The manuscripts generally bear <span dir=\"rtl\">טאריא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(طاريا)</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">עארצׄא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(عارضاً)</span>, and these two words must be regarded as the <i>predicate</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(خبر)</span> of the negation <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span>, which here possesses the force of a verb, implying <span dir=\"rtl\">وجودة</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">هو</span>; it is as if one had said: <span dir=\"rtl\">ليس وجوده طارئاً</span>.",
    "fn.596": "It is erroneously that Pococke (<i>Specimen hist. Ar.</i>, p. 214) compares these words of Maimonides with what certain Mu'tazilites affirmed, to wit, that God is <i>powerful by His essence, knowing by His essence, etc.</i>; one has seen higher up (chap. LIII, p. 209) that Maimonides himself reproves these words of the Mu'tazilites, which he finds scarcely intelligible and in which he discerns an indirect admission of the <i>essential attributes.</i> The author, in saying: <i>existent, not by existence; living, not by life</i>, etc., evidently avoids expressing himself, like the Mu'tazilites, by affirmative terms. Cf. the last of the <i>Eight Chapters</i> with which our author prefaces his commentary on the treatise <i>Abôth;</i> touching upon the same question, he likewise expresses himself by negative terms, saying: <span dir=\"rtl\">אן אללה תעאלי לים הו עאלמא בעלם ולא חיא بחיأه̈</span> (Vide Pococke, <i>Porta Mosis</i>, p. 254).",
    "fn.598": "<i>Quantity</i>, says Aristotle (<i>Categories</i>, chap. 6), is either <i>discrete</i> (<i>διωριτμένον</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">منفصل</span> ) or <i>continuous</i> (<i>συνε χές</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">متصل</span> ); discrete quantity, he adds, compriss number and speech, of which the parts have no common term in which they unite. It is manifest that the author, in here asserting that the <i>one</i> is, like number, of the genus or category of <i>discrete</i> quantity, has, like Ibn-Sīnā, confounded numerical unity with absolute unity. See the preceding note.",
    "fn.599": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تسامح</span> (VI<sup>th</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">جسم</span> ), which signifies: <i>to act with gentleness, to show oneself easy</i> or <i>indulgent</i>, is likewise employed, as a technical term, in the sense of <i>to permit oneself an improper expression</i>, that is to say, to be lax concerning the words one employs and not to adhere to their rigorous meaning, when one desirs to express an idea for which the proper terms are not readily found (cf. above, page 203, note 5). According to the definition of the <i>Kitāb al Ta’rīfāt</i> (also reported in Freytag’s Dictionary), this term signifies: that the idea one aims to express does not issue forth from the words employed, and that it is necessary, in order to divine it, to supply another expression by ellipsis.",
    "fn.600": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">חאדתׄ</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(حادث)</span>  means <i>what has newly occurred, what has been produced</i> or <i>created</i>, and is opposed to <span dir=\"rtl\">קדים</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">قديم</span> , <i>ancient, eternal.</i>",
    "fn.601": "Literally: <i>for</i> <small>ETERNAL</small> <i>is said only of that which time does affect, which etc.</i> See above, chap. LII, page 199, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1. It must not be overlooked that the author here reasons concerning the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">(قديم)</span> , employed in the sense of <i>eternal</i>, but which signifies primitively <i>that which precedes, prior, ancient</i>, and which evidently designats somewhat that stands in relation to time.",
    "fn.602": "Literally: <i>And it is also of the class of the relative;</i> that is to say, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">قديم</span> (prior, eternal) belongs to the category of <i>relation</i>, which embraces, according to the expression of Aristotle (<i>Categ.</i>, chap. 7), all that is said to be what it is by reason of things other than itself, or, in other terms, all that relats to a thing other than itself (cf. Maimonides, <i>Compendium of Logic</i>, chap. 11). Indeed, the <i>prior</i> is said in relation to that which is <i>posterior;</i> the <i>ancient</i>, in relation to that which is <i>new;</i> the <i>eternal</i>, in relation to that which has not always been and which is <i>originary</i> of somewhat. The author doubtless intends that the word <i>eternal</i>, being a <i>relative</i>, cannot be applied to God, who cannot be placed in <i>relation</i> with any thing. Cf. above, page 200, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1.",
    "fn.603": "That is to say: just as <i>long</i> and <i>short</i> are <i>relatives</i> which apply to the line, so likewise <i>eternal</i> and <i>created, ancient</i> and <i>new</i>, are relatives which apply to time. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי ערץׄ אלזמאן</span> here signify: <i>within the extent</i> or <i>in the region of time</i>, and similarly, <span dir=\"rtl\">פי ערׄץ אלכׄט</span>: <i>within the extent of the line.</i> One must here pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">عرض</span> , and not <span dir=\"rtl\">عرض</span> , as the two Hebrew translators have done, rendering the words in question by <span dir=\"rtl\">במקרה הזמן</span> (in the <i>accident</i> of time) and <span dir=\"rtl\">במקרה הקו</span> (in the <i>accident</i> of the line), which offers scarcely any sense. Ibn-Falaquera has already observed that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ערץׄ</span> ought to be rendered here in Hebrew by <span dir=\"rtl\">רוחב</span> (breadth), taken here in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">מדה</span> <i>measure, extent.</i> See <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, page 28, and the appendix, page 151. Cf. further on, chap. LXXII (fol. 101 <i>b</i>, line 6, of our text): <span dir=\"rtl\">בקדר ערץׄ דׄלך אלנוع</span>, <i>according to the </i> <small>EXTENT</small> <i>of this species.</i>",
    "fn.604": "More literally: <i>and who examines them, the intelligence apprehending and abstracting them, and not with the total idea which the words indicate</i>, that is to say, one who examines them with a profound intelligence, capable of grasping these matters in themselves, in all their abstract nature, and does not guide oneself by the general sense which certain words possess. Thus, for example, he who comprehends these metaphysical subjects will know that, when it is said of God that He <i>exists</i>, that He is <i>one</i> and <i>eternal</i>, these words here bear an abstract sense, entirely independent of that which they possess in their general application. — Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">באלתגׄמל</span>, several manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">באלתגׄםים</span> (with <i>corporification</i> or the <i>corporeal idea</i>); but our reading is confirmed by the two Hebrew versions, one of which bears <span dir=\"rtl\">בכללות</span>, the other <span dir=\"rtl\">בחבור</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">תגׄמל</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(تجمّل)</span> is a <i>noun of action</i> (of the V<sup>th</sup> form), derived from the substantive <span dir=\"rtl\">جهالة</span> , totality, and signifies here <i>generality, ensemble.</i>",
    "fn.605": "See, for example, Isaiah, chap. 44, <small>V</small>. 6.",
    "fn.606": "That is to say: the words <i>first</i> and <i>last</i>, applied to God, must be taken figuratively, just as the <i>eye</i> and the <i>ear</i> are attributed to Him.",
    "fn.608": "Literally: <i>as for His description by means of affirmations, there is in this, regarding association and imperfection, that which one has already expounded;</i> that is to say: by attributing affirmative qualities unto God, one implicitly acknowledges that diverse ideas are therein associated, and one likewise ascribes imperfection unto Him. By the word <span dir=\"rtl\">שרך</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(شُرَك)</span> , <i>association</i>, the author would seem to allude to the comparison he established earlier (chap. L, p. 181) between the belief in <i>essential</i> attributes and the Christian dogma of the Trinity. For the Mo’tazals also, to ascribe an attribute unto God is to give unto Him an <i>associate</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(مشريك)</span> . Their chief did expressly state: He who admits an eternal attribute together with the (divine) idea, admits two gods. Cf. Pococke, <i>Specimen hist. Ar.</i>, p. 216.",
    "fn.609": "Literally: <i>whatever there is in them by way of particularization, that is, </i> (<i>that </i>) <i>excluding what is negated from everything we believed should not be negated</i>, that is, stripping the subject of certain qualities that could be attributed to it and confining it within a narrower circle through negations, in order to determine it in a certain way.",
    "fn.610": "Literally: <i>do not reveal to us anything at all about the essence of which we desire to know what it is.</i>",
    "fn.611": "Literally: <i>one apprehends only His</i> <small>THAT</small> <i>alone, not His</i> <small>WHAT</small> (for God, being indefinable, one cannot declare <i>what He is;</i> vide supra, p. 190). The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אניח̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(أنيّة)</span> is derived, doubtless, from the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">أن</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">أَنَّ</span> , <i>that</i> (quod), and, to render this Arabic term precisely, one would need to form the word <i>quoddity</i> (analogous to <i>quiddity</i>). It is the <i>ὅτ׳</i>, which Aristotle places at the head of those objects that the intellect contemplates in every science, and which designates the pure <i>existence</i> of the thing (vide <i>Posterior Analytics</i>, bk. II, chap. 1). It is only after having recognised <i>that</i> the thing <i>is</i>, that one inquires into <i>what it is</i> (<i>μετὰ ταῦτα γνόντες, ὅ τ ι ἐστί τι, τ ί οὖν τοῦτ’ ἐστι, ζ<span dir=\"rtl\">ד</span> τοῦμεν. Ibid.</i>, chap. 2). In the <i>Kitâb al-Ta’rifât</i>, one reads the following definition: <span dir=\"rtl\">الانية تحقق الوجود العينى</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">من حيث رتبة الذاتية</span> “The <i>anniyya</i> ascertains existence in itself, considered in the degree of pure essence.” The meaning of this definition is as follows: the <i>ὅτι</i> (the <i>that</i> or the <i>quoddity</i>) confines itself to ascertaining pure and abstract existence, from the standpoint of <i>being</i>, or of <i>essence</i> (to render <span dir=\"rtl\">الذاتية</span> precisely, one would need to form the word <i>essentiality</i>); it is <i>being</i> considered in itself, abstracting from all that may serve to determine it, such as <i>quiddity</i> (<i>τί ἐστιι</i>), <i>quality</i> (<i>πσ̃ς ἐστι</i>) and <i>cause</i> (<i>τὸ διότι</i>]). Cf. one's Extraits de la <i>Source de la vie</i> of Ibn-Gebirol, or Avicebron, bk. V, § 30. M. Silv. de Sacy has given a translation of the said definition entirely different from one's own; but he himself does avow that he cannot guarantee the accuracy of his rendering, and he offers upon the etymology of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">الإنية</span> a conjecture of but scant plausibility. Vide Notices et extraits des manuscrits, vol. X, p. 85.",
    "fn.612": "As the author stated at the commencement of the preceding chapter, <i>existence</i> and <i>quiddity</i> in God are one and the same, and cannot be separated one from the other; one cannot, therefore, make of one the attribute of the other, and, as has been said aforetime, one cannot even ascribe unto God as an attribute either <i>being</i> or <i>existence.</i> It has, moreover, been established that God cannot be defined, and that, consequently, no attribute indicating His quiddity can be ascribed unto Him (vide chap. LII, p. 190).—The editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">מפני שאין לו מהות חוץ לישותו</span>; one must read, according to the Arabic, <span dir=\"rtl\">ישות הוץ למהותו</span>, as indeed several manuscripts possess, and likewise Ibn-Falaquera, <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 29.",
    "fn.613": "Beings which possess an anterior cause fall into a <i>genus</i> and are distinguished by a <i>difference</i>; their <i>quiddity</i>, consequently, is composed of two elements. Man, for instance, is <i>animal</i> and <i>rational</i>, and his quiddity is contained within these two attributes, one indicating the genus and the other the difference. God has no anterior cause and falls into no genus, and His quiddity can only be of absolute simplicity. Cf. above, p. 190, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 3. — The editions of Ibn Tibbon’s version generally exhibit <span dir=\"rtl\">על שני חלקים</span>; one should read: <span dir=\"rtl\">על שני חלקיה</span>, as found in the manuscripts and the editio princeps. Al-’Harizi and Ibn Falaquera (loco citato) have, the one <span dir=\"rtl\">על הלקים</span>, the other <span dir=\"rtl\">על החלקים</span>, which is incorrect.",
    "fn.614": "That is to say: which is the very essence of that being and has no <i>cause</i> external to itself (cf. above, at the commencement of chap. LVII). — There is a certain obscurity in this phrase, the literal translation of which is as follows: <i>then we comprehend that</i> (<i>as for</i>) <i>this being, its existence, which is its essence, suffices it not merely that it should exist, but that, on the contrary, there</i> <small>FROM IT</small> <i>emanates, etc.</i> It is not altogether clear whether the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ענה</span>, which one has rendered by <i>from it</i>, signifies <i>from him</i> (referring to <i>this being</i>), or rather <i>from her</i> (referring to <i>existence</i>).\nIn the editions of Ibn Tibbon’s version, there are here certain inaccuracies; one must read as follows, according to the manuscripts: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחרי כן השגנו שזה הנמצא אין מציאותו אשר הוא עצמו מםפיק לו שיהיה נמצא בלבד אבל שופעות מאתו מציאויות רבות</span>. It is designedly that the translator has here given to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מציאות</span> the masculine gender, in order, as he himself states, to allow the ambiguity, which one has pointed out in the Arabic text, to subsist; here is how he expresses himself in a note, at the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר הוא עצמו</span> (MS. from the Sorbonne collection, no. 108): <span dir=\"rtl\">שמתי הנה מלת מציאות על לשון זכר בהכרח כנוי וכנוי מאתו אפשר שהוא שב אל הנמצא או אל המציאות וראי וה לא כראי זה ואילו הייתי עושה שם מציאות לשון נקבה הייתי מכריע הדרך האחד וברחתי מזה והעתקתי מלה במלה</span>.",
    "fn.615": "Literally: <i>but it is an effluence which constantly prolongs their duration and order, by a well-established regimen;</i> that is to say, that God, in producing these existences, acts with freedom and with full consciousness, and not without will, as nature does. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">פיץׄ</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">فيض</span> <i>effusion, emanation</i>) designates the outpouring of divine grace, the inspiration by which God manifests Himself in the prophets, and, generally, the divine action manifesting upon created beings. The author himself explains elsewhere why the divine action is termed <span dir=\"rtl\">فيض</span>, by comparison with water gushing from a spring and spreading forth on all sides. See the Second Part of this work, chap. XII.",
    "fn.616": "See above, p. 214, note 1.",
    "fn.617": "Literally: <i>that they are not neglected and existing as it happens</i> (<i>by chance</i>), <i>but</i> (<i>that they are</i>) <i>as is all that which one who wills guides with intention and volition.</i> One must read <span dir=\"rtl\">ככון</span> (with <i>câf</i>), and not <span dir=\"rtl\">בכון</span> (with <i>béth</i>), as some manuscripts present it; similarly, in Ibn Tibbon’s version, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">כהיות</span>, as the editio princeps has it, and not <span dir=\"rtl\">בהיות</span>, as the other editions exhibit.",
    "fn.618": "In several manuscripts, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">פקלנא אנה ואחד ואלמעני</span>… (<i>we thus say that He is unique, which means…</i>), which is more in line with the way the author expressed himself in the preceding passages.",
    "fn.619": "Literally: <i>the negation of plurality.</i> The Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחד</span> signifies at once <i>one</i> (that is to say, <i>not multiple</i>) and <i>unique;</i> it necessarily bears this latter sense here, since the matter concerns the denial that there is <i>another being similar to God</i>, and, for the same reason, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כתׄרה̈</span> must here be translated as <i>plurality</i>, and not as <i>multiplicity.</i> In order to expound more fully the idea attached to the Hebrew word <span dir=\"rtl\">אחד</span> and the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">واحد</span>, the author ought to have said: “Finally one comprehends that this being has no like, <i>and that there is nought composite in him.</i>” Thus does the author, in his <i>Thirteen Articles of Faith</i>, express himself concerning <i>the unity of God:</i> “For He who is the (first) cause of all things is <i>one</i>, not as the <i>one</i> of a <i>genus</i>, nor as the <i>one</i> of a <i>species</i>, nor as the <i>composite</i> individual, which is divided into several units, nor even as the <i>simple body, one</i> in number, but which is susceptible of being infinitely apportioned and divided; but God is of a unity to which no unity whatsoever resembles in any manner.” Vide commentary on the <i>Mishnah</i>, Part IV<sup>e</sup>, treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, chap. X (Pococke, Porta Mosis, p. 165).",
    "fn.620": "Literally: <i>or else its sense is the negation of its privation;</i> that is to say: the affirmative attribute ascribed to God, if it does not designate an action emanating from God, must be regarded as the negation of a privative adjective. Thus, for example, <i>powerful</i> must be taken in the sense of <i>not impotent; living</i>, in the sense of <i>not inanimate; He is</i> <small>ONE</small> signifies <i>He is not</i> <small>NOT ONE</small> (or <i>multiple</i>), and so forth. Al-Ghazali, in his <i>Maqasid al-Falasifa</i> (section of <i>Metaphysics</i>, book III), speaking on behalf of the Peripatetics, thus expresses himself concerning the attributes of <i>action</i> (which he also calls <i>relative</i>) and the <i>negative</i> attributes — (one cites the Hebrew version)—. <span dir=\"rtl\">ויולדו מתארי הצירוף והשלילה לראשון שמות לא יחייבו רבוי בעצמותו כי כאשר נאמר אחד הנה ענינו שלילת השתוף והדמיון ושלילת החלוק וכאשר נאמר קדמון הנה ענינו שלילת ההתחלה ממציאותו וכאשר נאמר טוב ונכבד ורחמן הנה ענינו צירופו אל פעולות םודרו ממנו.</span> “From the <i>relative</i> and <i>negative</i> attributes, there result for the first being (God) names which imply no multiplicity in His essence; for, when one says <i>one</i>, one intends to deny association and assimilation (of other beings to God), as well as divisibility; when one says <i>eternal</i>, one intends to deny that His existence had a commencement; when one says <i>bon, glorious, merciful</i>, this expresses a relation to the actions emanating from Him.”",
    "fn.621": "The author intends to convey that the negative attributes of God must ever bear the sense of <i>universal</i> and <i>absolute negations</i>, and not that of <i>particular negations;</i> the negation contained within the negative attribute must resemble, says the author, this proposition: <i>the wall sees not</i>, which signifies: <i>no wall ever sees</i>, because it is not in its nature to see, whereas this other proposition: <i>the man sees not</i>, signifies that such a man sees not, because he is blind or sleeps, or by some other <i>accident</i> whatsoever, although it be in his nature to see (cf. Aristotle, <i>Categories</i>, chap. 11; treatise on <i>Hermeneia</i>, chap. 7; and the <i>Compendium of Logic</i>, by our author, ch. 2 and 11). It is evident that the negative attributes, by which one aims to preclude all imperfection from God, cannot be negations of the latter kind, that is to say, <i>particular negations;</i> for imperfection must here be denied in an absolute sense, as a thing which can never exist in God.",
    "fn.622": "That is, which is moved by something else, and which, consequently, does not have its cause within itself.",
    "fn.623": "The suffix <span dir=\"rtl\">הא</span>, in <span dir=\"rtl\">חרכאתהא</span>, which one has referred to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">הדׄה אלםמא</span> <i>this heaven</i>, could also refer to <span dir=\"rtl\">אגׄזא</span> <i>parts</i> (a word which here designates the different spheres), such that it would be necessary to translate: <i>and most of</i> <small>THEIR</small> <i>mouvements.</i> Indeed, the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version bear <span dir=\"rtl\">תנועותיהם</span>, with the plural suffix, referring to <span dir=\"rtl\">חלקים</span>; but in the manuscripts one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">תנועותיו</span>, where the suffix refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">רקיע</span>.",
    "fn.624": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄרורה̈</span> (<i>necessarily</i>) is missing in several manuscripts; it is also omitted in Al-’Harizi’s version.",
    "fn.625": "On the meaning of the participle <span dir=\"rtl\">מחצלת̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(محلة)</span> , cf. above, p. 190, note 4.",
    "fn.626": "It is almost in the same negative terms that Aristotle expresses himself concerning <i>aether</i>, which, according to him, forms the substance of the celestial spheres; vide his treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, Book I, Chapter 3: <i>τὸ δὲ ϰύϰλῳ σῶμα, φερόμενον ἀδύνατον ἔχειν βάρος ἢ ϰουφότητα, ϰ. τ. λ.</i>, and further on: <i>ὥστ’ εἴπερ τὸ ϰύϰλῳ σῶμα μήτ’ αὔξησιν ἔχειν ἐνδέχεται μήτέ φθίσιν, εὔλογον ϰαί ἀναλ-λοίωτον εἶναι. Διὸτι μἐν οὖν αίδιον ϰαί οὔτ’ αὔξησιν ἔχον οὔτε φθίσιν, ἀλλ’ ἀγή-ρατον ϰαί ἀναλλοίωτον ϰαί ἀπαθές ἐστι τὸ πρῶτον τῶν σωμάτων, ϰ. τ. λ.</i> Cf. the Second <sup>Part</sup> of this work, Chapter XIX.",
    "fn.627": "Literally: <i>becomes stammering and impotence.</i>",
    "fn.628": "Literally: <i>if the demonstration leads</i> (<i>to this conviction</i>) <i>that</i> <i>the perceived thing is only</i> <small>THAT IT EXISTS.</small>",
    "fn.629": "See above, chap. XVII, p. 68, note 3.",
    "fn.630": "Literally: <i>that the gradual superiority in this regard is multiple.</i>",
    "fn.631": "Literally: and he who attributes (that is to say, he who makes the attributes), approaches perception, reality.",
    "fn.632": "Literally: <i>in order to possess it with certainty</i> or <i>with certainty.</i>",
    "fn.633": "Literally: <i>of which it is known, by demonstration, that it is false that this thing can be attributed unto it.</i> The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תעלם</span> is the preterite of the Fifth <sup>Form</sup>, and one ought to pronounce it <span dir=\"rtl\">تَعَلَّمَ</span>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">והודיע</span>, which the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version bear, one reads, in the manuscripts, <span dir=\"rtl\">ונודיע</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">ונודע</span>, and it is this latter reading that one ought to adopt. Al-’Harizi likewise has <span dir=\"rtl\">שנודע</span>.",
    "fn.634": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">הכונה</span>, which one reads here in all editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, is an error; the manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">האמונה</span>.—It is mistakenly that Ibn-Falaquera does here presume to rectify Ibn-Tibbon's version, by rendering the Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">וילקי</span> as <span dir=\"rtl\">וישליך</span>, such that one would be obliged to translate: <i>and He casts God into this belief</i>, which, he says, is a figurative expression; Ibn-Falaquera has been led into error by a faulty reading, for he has read <span dir=\"rtl\">פי הדׄה אלעקידח̈</span>, whereas the true reading is <span dir=\"rtl\">בהדׄה אלעקידה̈</span>. Vide the Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 151.",
    "fn.635": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">באלבחתׄ</span> is not expressed in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version; the manuscripts of this version carry <span dir=\"rtl\">בדרישה ובחקירה</span>.",
    "fn.636": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">malkeh</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(malakah)</span> , which we render by <i>capacity</i>, and which corresponds to the Greek word <i>hexis</i>, cf. above, p. 195, notes 1 and 2.",
    "fn.638": "Literally: <i>of the thing of which one denies what one denies.</i>",
    "fn.639": "That is, our perception of God consists in recognizing that we are powerless to perceive his true essence.",
    "fn.640": "The author may be alluding to a passage from Ba’hya (Devoirs des cœurs, book I, chapter 10), where the same subject has been extensively developed. In establishing that it is impossible for the intelligence to perceive directly the divine essence, which manifests itself to us only through its works, Ba’hya also uses the comparison taken from the light of the sun, which the sight cannot perceive directly.",
    "fn.641": "Literally: <i>a charge</i>, <i>a burden</i>, or <i>an attack</i> (from <span dir=\"rtl\">حَبَلَ</span>, impetum fecit)<i>;</i> the author intends to convey: by believing one praises God, one charges Him with something unworthy of His true being, and one offends Him. Ibn-Tibbon renders the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חמל</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">מעמם</span> (charge). Ibn-Falaquera prefers to render it by <span dir=\"rtl\">טורח</span> or by <span dir=\"rtl\">משא</span>, which is nigh the same thing; vide the Appendix of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, where one reads concerning our passage: <span dir=\"rtl\">והנכון להעתיק נמצא בו משא מה או טורח מה</span>. This passage, drawn from Hebrew ms. No. 352 of the ancient collection, is wanting in the edition of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i> and in most manuscripts.",
    "fn.642": "One should read, in Ibn-Tibbon’s version, <span dir=\"rtl\">וההםתפקות</span>, and not <span dir=\"rtl\">וההשתקפות</span>, as several editions have.",
    "fn.643": "Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Berakhôth</i>, fol. 33 <i>b.</i> This passage, as cited in our text, is not entirely conformable to our editions of the Talmud, and the various Arabic and Hebrew manuscripts of the <i>Guide</i> likewise offer numerous variants. The same holds true for nearly all Talmudic citations, and occasionally for Biblical citations, which authors ordinarily made from memory, and which have often been corrected by scribes. Ba’hya, in his treatise on the <i>Duties of the Hearts</i> (Book I, chap. 10), cites the same passage with other variants. One has followed several manuscripts which are well-nigh conformable, and one's reading differs but little from that of Al-’Harizi.",
    "fn.644": "See Deuteronomy, chapter X, <small>V</small>. 17.",
    "fn.645": "On the assembly called the <i>great Synod</i> or the <i>great Synagogue</i>, to which the composition of daily prayers is attributed, see my History of the <i>Land of Palestine</i>, p. 479 and 480.",
    "fn.646": "Some manuscripts read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאיגׄאב</span>, with the article, and similarly the two Hebrew versions, <span dir=\"rtl\">החיוב</span>.",
    "fn.647": "That is to say: if the matter were abandoned to one's sole judgment, and if certain attributes had not been consecrated by Holy Writ and by the ritual of prayers. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תרכנא</span> is in the passive voice, and one ought to pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">تُرِكْنَا</span>; Ibn-Tibbon has rendered it with great exactitude by <span dir=\"rtl\">הונחנו</span> (mss. and edit. princeps), and it is erroneously that, in most editions, this word has been altered to <span dir=\"rtl\">הונחו</span>.",
    "fn.648": "Instead of Kalnehah, one of the Leiden manuscripts has Aqlenehah; this reading, expressed by Al-’Harazi (We have not understood them), does not convey a suitable sense.",
    "fn.649": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אן יוצף</span> depend on the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אלגׄאת</span>, of which the words <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄרורה̈ אלכׄטאב</span> form the subject; the literal translation would be: <i>since the necessity of speaking to men… has forced one to describe God, etc.</i>.",
    "fn.650": "See above, at the beginning of chap. XXVI.",
    "fn.651": "That is, we should be limited to the attributes of grand, powerful, and formidable.",
    "fn.652": "Literally: <i>notre terme</i> (<i>devrait être</i>) <i>de les dire</i> (<i>eux</i>) <i>seulement;</i> that is to say: we should, in the prayer, limit ourselves to pronouncing these three attributes alone.",
    "fn.653": "Literally: <i>you persist in the attributes.</i>",
    "fn.654": "Literally: <i>who have extended and prolonged</i> (<i>the attributes</i>) <i>and have made efforts</i> (<i>in this regard</i>), <i>in prayers which they have composed and orisons which they have fashioned, by which, in their opinion, they drew nigh unto God, and in which they described God by such attributes, that, were one thus to describe an individual amongst men, this would be</i> (<i>to express</i>) <i>an imperfection concerning him.</i> The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אטנבוא</span>, which signifies <i>they have extended themselves</i> or <i>they have insisted</i>, has been rendered, in Ibn-Tibbon's version, by <span dir=\"rtl\">המריצו בשבחים</span> (they have insisted <i>upon praises</i>); the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בשבחים</span> is an addition by the translator, and likewise, further on, he has rendered <span dir=\"rtl\">ויטנבון</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">וימריצו לשבח</span>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואגׄתהדוא</span> (<i>and they have made efforts</i>), some manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ואםתועבוא</span> (X<sup>e</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">ועב</span>), <i>and they have accumulated</i>; it is this latter reading which Ibn-Tibbon appears to have followed, rendering: <span dir=\"rtl\">והרבו דברים</span>, <i>and they have accumulated words</i>, whereas Al-’Harizi has <span dir=\"rtl\">וישתדלו</span>, which accords with our reading.",
    "fn.655": "Literally: <i>they took God the Most High as the step of their tongues, and described Him and spoke to Him through all that they considered permitted.</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">מדרםה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">مَدْرَسَة</span> here means <i>the place that is trodden;</i> this word is missing from dictionaries.",
    "fn.656": "Literally: <i>On the site of the transgression</i> or <i>the sin.</i> Some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">מואצׄע</span>, <i>the places</i>, and similarly Al-’Harizi, <span dir=\"rtl\">places</span>.",
    "fn.657": "The author here intentionally employs the <i>Hebrew</i> words <span dir=\"rtl\">לשון הרע</span> (<i>the evil tongue</i> or <i>slander</i>), and <span dir=\"rtl\">הוצאת שם רע</span> (<i>propagation of an evil reputation</i>), which are in the mouth of all Hebrew moralists; it is thus that a little further on he inserts into the Arabic phrase the Hebrew words <span dir=\"rtl\">חרוף וגדוף בשגגה</span>, and yet others.",
    "fn.658": "We deviate slightly from the true sense of this passage, and translate it as demanded by the application made here by Maimonides.",
    "fn.659": "These words contain an allusion to a passage from the <i>Mischnâ</i>. See above, chap. XXXII, p. 113, note 2.",
    "fn.660": "The author alludes to the words of R. Éléazar as reported in the Babylonian Talmud, <i>Succâ</i>, fol. 53 <i>a</i>, and <i>Ta’anîth</i>, fol. 25 <i>a.</i>",
    "fn.661": "See the previous chapter, p. 245, note 3.",
    "fn.662": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אחכאם</span>, as Ibn-Falaquera observed (Moré ha-Moré, p. 151), should be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">إحكام</span> (the nomen actionis of the IV<sup>th</sup> form), and signifies <i>arrangement, good order;</i> the author intends to speak of the sagacious manner in which R. ’Hanînâ had disposed his words, so as to bring forth several important teachings. Ibn-Tibbon translates <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחכאמה</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">וחכמותיו</span>; it is thus manifest that he pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">أَحْكَامُ</span> (plural of <span dir=\"rtl\">حكمُ</span>, <i>principle of wisdom</i>).\nAccording to Ibn-Falaquera, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אחכאם</span> should be rendered, in Hebrew, by <span dir=\"rtl\">תקון</span>, as Ibn-Tibbon himself rendered it in the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. XIX, where there is question of the sagacious disposition of the organs of sight, and where the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתי הי מן אלאחכאם עלי מא קר עלם</span> are translated by <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר בהם מן התקון מה שנודע</span>.— Al-’Harizi has entirely neglected, in his translation, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחכאמה</span>.",
    "fn.663": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">כמאלא</span>, several manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">כמאל</span> (or <span dir=\"ltr\">אנהא</span>) <span dir=\"rtl\">אנה</span>, or <span dir=\"rtl\">אנהא כמאלאת</span>; the reading we have adopted, and which is more correct, agrees with the version of Ibn-Tibbon.",
    "fn.664": "By these last words, the author designates different species of circular-based bodies, such as the cylinder, the ellipsoid, etc., and all bodies with plane surfaces, such as the cube and all prisms in general.",
    "fn.665": "Literally: … <i>that you add the negation of a thing through demonstration, and that you do not deny only by the word</i>.",
    "fn.666": "Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא שיש לשם מקום</span>, which is not entirely accurate; Ibn-Falaquera translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא ששם קרבת מקום</span> (<i>Moréha-Moré</i>, p. 33). Similarly Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא שיש למעלה קרבת מקום</span>.",
    "fn.667": "Literally: <i>goes there therefore, if you want</i>.",
    "fn.668": "See above, chapter LVI, p. 229.",
    "fn.669": "Literally: <i>and similarly, it must exist, not in the sense of existence</i> (<i>which is</i>) <i>in us</i>.",
    "fn.670": "In Ibn-Tibbon's version, one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">הענין בחיוב התארים</span>, and not <span dir=\"rtl\">העיון בהיות התארים</span>, as in the editions.",
    "fn.671": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">محمول</span>, which the author here employs, designates the <i>logical attribute</i> (prædicatum), whilst <span dir=\"rtl\">صفة</span> designates the <i>metaphysical attribute</i>, or the real quality of a subject; the discussion here concerns the <i>subject</i> and the <i>attribute</i>, taken in their most general sense. Instead of these words: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא דׄלך אלמוצׄוע מתׄל הדׄה אלמחמולאת</span>, one reads in most manuscripts: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא דׄלך אלמוצׄוע מתׄל הדׄה אלמוצׄועאת ולא תלך אלמחמולאת מתׄל הדׄה אלמחמולאת</span>, <i>and that this subject is not like these other subjects, nor are these attributes</i> (<i>are</i>) <i>like these other attributes</i>, that is to say, that one is here concerned with a subject and attributes which cannot be compared to anything familiar to us in the realm of subjects and attributes. Ibn-Tibbon’s version confirms this latter reading; but that which one has deemed proper to adopt is guaranteed by the better of the two Leyden manuscripts, as well as by Al-’Harizi’s version, which has: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואין המוםד ההוא כאלו הנשואים</span>. It is erroneously that the editor of this version conceived it necessary to complete the passage in the sense of Ibn-Tibbon’s version. See also the following note.",
    "fn.672": "That is to say: the belief which admits affirmative attributes would lead, ultimately, to admitting diverse ideas associated together within God. Concerning the word <span dir=\"rtl\">شُرْك</span>, cf. above, chap. LVIII, p. 239, note 1. This word, rendered in Hebrew by <span dir=\"rtl\">שתוף</span>, has generally been taken, by the commentators of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, in the sense of <i>homonymy</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(اشتراك)</span>; that which appears to have given rise to this error is the variant of which one has spoken in the preceding note, and which was reproduced by Ibn-Tibbon. The sense which the commentators attribute to Maimonides’ words is this: with affirmative attributes, one arrives merely at ascertaining that God is a subject possessing attributes, and that <i>subject</i> and <i>attribute</i>, when it concerns God, ought not to be taken in the same sense as elsewhere, so that one would have, as the sole result, merely words employed by <i>homonymy</i>, the true meaning of which would remain unknown. This interpretation is very little plausible, and the word <span dir=\"rtl\">شرك</span>, taken in the sense of <i>association</i>, appears to confirm fully the reading which one has adopted.",
    "fn.673": "That is to say: as soon as a thing is designated as <i>subject</i>, it necessarily possesses one or more <i>attributes</i>, and, although the subject and the attribute together form but a single being, these are nevertheless two distinct things, each of which has its own definition. If one says, for example: <i>this man is learned</i>, one speaks of a single being who is at once <i>man</i> and <i>learned;</i> but to the words <i>man</i> and <i>learned</i> are attached two distinct ideas, each of which possesses its separate definition.",
    "fn.674": "The author means that he will not confine himself to reproaching the adherents of affirmative attributes for entertaining an imperfect idea of God, or for implicitly associating other beings with God and admitting in some wise a plurality in the divine idea, or finally for forming an idea of God contrary to truth. On the one hand, these reproaches might not appear to them sufficiently well-founded from their own perspective; on the other hand, especially regarding the third reproach, it would be to concede to them a certain notion concerning the existence of God which, in reality, they do not possess. The grave reproach that one may justly address to them is that of rendering God an imaginary being and lapsing into atheism.",
    "fn.675": "The adherents of attributes, the author remarks, might repel the charge of <i>association</i> by subtle distinctions regarding the meaning of this term, demonstrating that they associate no other being with God, inasmuch as they ascribe to no being an <i>essence</i> similar to the Divine essence, and that the attributes they impute to Him, whilst forming part of the Divine essence, are not that essence itself. If, somewhat higher up, the author hesitated not to declare that affirmative attributes lead to <i>association</i>, he intended thereby the <i>multiplicity</i> within the Divine idea, or the association of diverse ideas collectively forming the essence of God, whereas here the discussion pertains to <i>plurality</i>, or the opinion which would admit of other essences similar to the essence of God. This distinction is essential. Cf. above, chap. LVIII, p. 245, note 2.",
    "fn.676": "For example, someone who declares <i>amer</i> what is <i>doux</i>, would always agree that it is a thing affecting the taste, and that it is a <i>quality;</i> they would therefore have an exact notion of taste, whereas someone who says that taste is a <i>quantity</i> would show a complete ignorance of what taste is.",
    "fn.677": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ענקא מגרב</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(عَنْقَاءُ مُغْرِبِ)</span> designate, amongst the Arabs, a fabulous bird, similar to our <i>griffin</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">مغرب</span> is an epithet concerning the meaning of which the Arabs themselves are not in agreement; but it is most generally believed that this epithet was bestowed upon the bird <i>’ankâ</i> because, it is said, it <i>bears far away</i> all the prey which it seizes. See <i>Les oiseaux et les fleurs</i>, by M. Garcin de Tassy, p. 218 et seq., and the <i>Séances de Hariri</i>, edit. of Messrs. Reinaud and Derenbourg, p. 678, and the French notes, <i>ibid.</i>, p. 202. — The two Hebrew translators have rendered <span dir=\"rtl\">העזניה הנפלאה</span>, taking the word <span dir=\"rtl\">مغرب</span> in the sense of <i>marvellous</i>, and perceiving in the bird ’<i>ankâ</i> one of the species of eagle, that which, in the Pentateuch (Levit., XI, 13, and Deut., XIV, 12), is denominated <span dir=\"rtl\">עזניה</span>. Indeed, Saadia, in his Arabic version of the Pentateuch, renders this word by <span dir=\"rtl\">عنقاء</span>; but he was criticised by Ibn-Ezra, who justly observes that the bird <i>’ankâ</i>, being a fabulous creature, could not possibly be enumerated amongst the animals whose flesh was forbidden to the Hebrews. It appears nevertheless to result from the whole of our passage that Maimonides also believed the word <span dir=\"rtl\">عنقاء</span> originally denoted a real being; for, according to Damiri, as cited by Bochart, this word designates a species of eagle, and is synonymous with <span dir=\"rtl\">عُقَاب</span>. See Bochart, <i>Hierozoïcon</i>, vol. II, p. 812 (Rosenmüller’s edit., vol. III, p. 803).",
    "fn.678": "The author, in concluding his discussion on attributes, once again summarizes in this final phrase his theory regarding attributes found in the Holy Scripture. Cf. above, at the end of chap. LIII. — On <span dir=\"rtl\">כתאב אלתנזיל</span>, see above, pag. 206, note 1.",
    "fn.679": "The author, having demonstrated that every species of attributes must be excluded from God, adds, in this chapter and in the three succeeding ones, some observations upon various names of God found in Holy Writ, which might be regarded as attributes. He shows that all names are derived from the <i>actions</i> of God, with the exception of one alone, which designates the Divine essence itself. Cf. the <i>Khozari</i>, book II, §2. Already, in a treatise attributed to Aristotle, and which perhaps formed part of his <i>exoteric</i> works, one discovers an analogous attempt to explain the names of the Greek divinities by applying them all to a unique being, in conformity with philosophical doctrines. See the treatise <i>On the Universe</i>, chap. 7: Eῖς δἐ ὢ ν πολυὤνομὸς ἐστι, ϰατονομαζόμενος τοῖς πάθεσιν ἅπ’ρ αὐτὸς νςοχοῖ, ϰ. τ. λ.).",
    "fn.680": "The Arabs call <i>improvised name</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(ism murtajal)</span>  that which has been, from its origin, the <i>proper name</i> of an individual, and which is not derived from an appellative name or a verb. See Silv. de Sacy, <i>Arabic Grammar</i>, t. I, p. 268 (2<sup>nd</sup> edition).",
    "fn.681": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">שם המפורש</span> (<i>Mishnah</i>, II<sup>nd</sup> part, treatise <i>Yoma</i>, chap. VI, § 2) signify doubtless: <i>the name of God distinctly pronounced</i>, that is to say, the <i>Tetragrammaton</i>, written and read by the four letters <i>yod, he, waw, he</i>, and which is also denominated <span dir=\"rtl\">השם המיוחד</span>, or <i>the peculiar name</i> (Babylonian Talmud, <i>Sanhedrin</i>, fol. 56 <i>a; Shebu'ot</i>, fol. 36 <i>a</i>). The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ונקב שם י״י</span> (Lev., XXIV, 16) are rendered, in Onkelos’s Chaldaic version, by <span dir=\"rtl\">ודי פרש שמא די״י</span>, <i>and he who</i> <small>PRONOUNCES</small> <i>the name of the Eternal;</i> cf. Ibn-Ezra’s commentary: <span dir=\"rtl\">והנה הטעם בעת שיקלל אם יפרש השם יומת</span> 'The sense is: if, in blaspheming, he <i>pronounces</i> the name of God, he shall be put to death.' Our author apprehends the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מפורש</span> in the sense that this name <i>expressly</i> designates the Divine essence, and is by no means an <i>homonym</i>, that is to say, that it does not apply simultaneously to God and to other beings. This interpretation of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מפורש</span>, generally adopted by the theologians who succeeded Maimonides (cf. Albo, <i>Ikkarim</i>, II, 28), was certainly not present in the thought of the ancient rabbis.",
    "fn.682": "See above, chap. LIV, p. 219 and following.",
    "fn.683": "It is known that, from the most ancient times, the Jews, by a pious reverence for the proper name of God, durst not pronounce this name, and always read <span dir=\"rtl\">אדני</span> in the stead of <span dir=\"rtl\">יהוה</span>; wherefore the Septuagint already does render this latter name by <i>ὁ ϰύρις the Lord.</i> The <i>Masoretes</i> have, for the same reason, assigned unto it the vowels of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אֲדׂנָי</span>, so that one does read <i>Iéhova</i>, which is not the true pronunciation of the name <span dir=\"rtl\">יהוה</span>. It is by the same scruple that the author, instead of inditing this name in its veritable form, does spell out its letters, and ever writs: <i>yod, hé, wâw, hé</i>.",
    "fn.684": "Literally: <i>for therein is emphasis and generality;</i> that is to say, names such as <i>Adônaï</i> and <i>Saraï</i> are <i>emphatic</i> words, or, as grammarians term them, <i>plurals of majesty</i>, and their very form does indicate that they are not primitively proper names appertaining unto a single individual, but common nouns applicable to a generality of individuals. Thus, the name <i>Adônaï</i>, which signifies <i>my lords</i>, does necessarily derive from an appellative noun belonging to several individuals.",
    "fn.685": "On this last name, see above, chap. II, p. 37.",
    "fn.686": "The author, following the example of other theologians (cf. <i>Khozari</i>, book IV, § 3), deems it proper to isolate the <i>Tetragrammaton</i> from all other names of God, and to perceive therein a proper name, without known etymology, albeit it is evident that the name <span dir=\"rtl\">יהוה</span> does contain the sense of <i>being</i> (from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">הוה</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">היה</span>), even as that of <span dir=\"rtl\">אהיה</span>, which the author himself derivs from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">היה</span>, as shall be observed in chapter LXIII.",
    "fn.687": "Literally: <i>And He</i> (God) <i>has not shared it with another.</i>",
    "fn.688": "These words, which the author puts in Hebrew, seem to refer to a passage from 2<sup>e</sup> book of Chronicles, chap. XXVI, <small>V</small>. 18.",
    "fn.689": "In the sanctuary of Jerusalem, the priests, while giving the people the blessing prescribed by the Law (Numbers, chap. 6, <small>V</small>. 24-26), pronounced the tetragrammaton as written, while outside the sanctuary, they substituted the name of <i>Adônaï</i>. See <i>Mischnâ</i>, III<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Sôtâ</i>, chap. VII, § 6.",
    "fn.690": "That is, on the <i>day of atonement</i>, when the high priest, in the confession of sins, also pronounced the name <span dir=\"rtl\">YHWH</span> with its own consonants. See <i>Mischna</i>, treatise <i>Yômâ</i>, l.c.; Talmud of Bab., same treatise, fol. 39 <i>b.</i>.",
    "fn.691": "The manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ליםת ענדנא</span>, which is incorrect; one must write <span dir=\"rtl\">לים</span>.",
    "fn.692": "The manuscripts generally bear <span dir=\"rtl\">לא ישארך אחד</span>; it mayhap one ought to read <span dir=\"rtl\">אחר״א</span>, in the accusative case, so that the literal translation would be: <i>inasmuch as He</i> (<i>God</i>) <i>has no community with any of the creatures in this indication.</i> In one manuscript, one does read <span dir=\"rtl\">ישארכה</span> with the suffix. According to this reading, it would be necessary to translate: <i>…that none of the creatures has community with Him</i>, etc.",
    "fn.693": "The author will cite the Talmudic passage he has in view later on. See p. 272.",
    "fn.694": "Instead of דׄאת צפאת, some manuscripts read דאת להא צפאת, which may be preferable.",
    "fn.695": "Thus, for example, the words <i>clement, just, all-powerful</i>, indicate the ideas of clemency, justice, and power, and a subject to which these ideas are attached, and which is implied; therefore, these words, used as names of God, would make God a composite being.</i>",
    "fn.696": "See above, chap. LIX, p. 254 and 255.",
    "fn.697": "See above, p. 267, note 3.",
    "fn.698": "That is, <i>amulets</i>, containing magical formulas and imaginary names of the deity, and that were worn as preservatives against diseases. See <i>Mischnâ</i>, II<sup>nd</sup> part, treatise <i>Schabbâth</i>, chapter VI, § 2, and <i>Talmud of Babylon</i>, same treatise, fol. 61 <i>a. b.</i>.",
    "fn.699": "Literally: <i>qu'ils cousent ensemble.</i> Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ילפקונהא</span>, some manuscripts read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלפוהא</span>, <i>qu'ils ont composés.</i>",
    "fn.700": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אבׄבאר</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(اخبار)</span> which means here <i>fictitious stories</i>, or fables, one of the manuscripts reads <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄראפאת</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(خرافات)</span> , which has the same meaning; this word has not been rendered in either of the two Hebrew versions.",
    "fn.701": "That is to say: the word <i>ainsi</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כה</span>) signifies that the priests must recite the blessing in the prescribed terms, without changing anything, and by pronouncing the name <i>tétragrammate</i> as it is written.",
    "fn.702": "That is to say: in the Jerusalem sanctuary, the priests, in reciting the prescribed blessing, pronounce the true name <i>tetragrammate;</i> but outside the central sanctuary, they substitute the name of <i>Adônaï</i>.",
    "fn.703": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Sôtâ</i>, fol. 38 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.704": "Literally: <i>if it is a letter that receives the</i> <small>TESCHDÎD</small>. In the name of <span dir=\"rtl\">YHWH</span>, only the letters <span dir=\"rtl\">Y</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">V</span> can receive the <i>teschdîd</i> or <i>daghesch;</i> because the <span dir=\"rtl\">H</span> never receives it.",
    "fn.705": "These last words, written in Hebrew, refer to the Talmudic passage that the author is about to cite.",
    "fn.706": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate Kiddouschîn, fol. 71 a.",
    "fn.707": "See the previous chapter, p. 267, note 2.",
    "fn.708": "The Talmud, as will be seen later, speaks of a divine name composed of twelve letters and another of forty-two letters, without explaining either the meaning of these names or the letters of which they are composed.",
    "fn.709": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא מצינא בה</span>, which signify literally <i>and one was not sparing thereof</i> (cf. above, at the commencement of chapter XVII), have been omitted in the version of Ibn-Tibbon, which bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא היה נמנע מעולם מאחד וכו׳</span>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מעולם</span> is the translation of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">קט</span>, which one does indeed find in one of the Leyden manuscripts, in which it is read: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומא כאן קט ממנועא</span>.",
    "fn.710": "Literally: <i>perdirent</i> or <i>corrompirent des croyances;</i> the sense is, that these men, not having grasped the profound metaphysical mysteries hidden in this name, were troubled in their faith and arrived at false beliefs.",
    "fn.711": "The author relats this fact, in well-nigh the same terms, in his <i>Abridgement of the Talmud</i>, book II, treatise <i>Tephillâ</i>, chap. XIV, § 10; one does doubt, however, that the citation be textual. The fact is reported in the Babylonian Talmud, <i>Yômâ</i>, fol. 39 <i>b</i>, and <i>Mena’hôth</i>, fol. 109 <i>b</i>, and these are the two passages which the author appears to have had in view, and in which he perchance had a reading differing from that of our editions.",
    "fn.712": "See Talmud of Babylon, Kiddouschîn, fol. 71 a.",
    "fn.713": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אבי אמא</span>, <i>the father of the mother</i>, our editions of the Talmud read: <span dir=\"rtl\">אחי אמי</span>, <i>the brother of my mother.</i>",
    "fn.714": "That is to say, by denying unto God every species of imperfection; for that is, according to the author, the sole manner of approximately conceiving the divine essence. One does perceive, moreover, by the manner in which the author expresses himself here, that he either knew not, or believed not authentic, the name of forty-two letters which figurs in the books of the Cabbalists, and which commencs with the letters <span dir=\"rtl\">אבגיתץ</span>, albeit this name was already known, two centuries before Maimonides, to R. Hâya Gaôn, who speaks thereof in one of his <i>Consultations</i>, relative to the divine names and addressed to the school of Kairawân. R. Hâya, speaking expressly of the name <span dir=\"rtl\">אבגיתץ</span>, says that, though its letters be known, there is no agreement concerning the manner of its pronunciation, which is known only by tradition: <span dir=\"rtl\">וזה בן מ״ב אותיות אע״פ שאותיותיו ידועות אין מםור בהגיונו וקריאתו אלא בקבלה וכוי</span>. Consult the collection of unpublished pieces recently issued under the title of <span dir=\"rtl\">טעם זקנים</span>, by M. Éliézer Aschkenazi (Frankfurt on the Main, 1854, in-8°), fol. 57 <i>a.</i> — Certain Cabbalists have perceived in the forty-two letters, commencing with <span dir=\"rtl\">אבגיתץ</span>, the initials of so many attributes of God; the enumeration thereof is to be found in the commentary of Moses of Narbonne, upon our passage.",
    "fn.715": "The adjective <span dir=\"rtl\">אלערי</span>, which is in the singular masculine, refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">אלנטק</span> (<i>the pronunciation</i>), and it was an error for the two Hebrew translators to render it in the plural, as if it referred to <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאחרף</span> (<i>the letters</i>).",
    "fn.716": "In all manuscripts, both Arabic and Hebrew, the <i>nom de douze lettres</i> precedes that of <i>quarante-deux</i>, and it is an error that, in all editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the order has been reversed.",
    "fn.717": "See Talmud of Babylon, l. c.",
    "fn.718": "Cf. above, chap. XXXV, p. 132. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אוצׄחנא</span>, some manuscripts carry <span dir=\"rtl\">בוא</span>.",
    "fn.719": "In some manuscripts, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמוצׄועה̈</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמדונה</span>.",
    "fn.720": "The doctrine of Aristotle concerning <i>intellect</i>, which he divides into <i>active</i> and <i>passive</i> (vide <i>De Anima</i>, Book III, chapter 5), received significant developments amongst the Arabs; there was discussion regarding the nature of the <i>material intellect</i> or <i>passive intellect</i> and the manner in which this intellect passes from <i>potency</i> to <i>act</i>, and that which chiefly engaged the Arabian philosophers was the question of whether and how the intellect in act may unite with the universal <i>active intellect</i>, emanated from God, and which, in the system of the Arabian philosophers, constitutes one of the <i>separated intelligences</i>, namely, that which presides over the lunar orb. See at the places indicated above, p. 146, note 2. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אדראך אלעקל אלפעאל</span> (<i>the perception of the active intellect)</i> admit of a twofold meaning: one might understand thereby the perception of which the human intellect is capable, once it has become <i>intellect in act</i>, or else the perception which has the universal active intellect for its object, and which consists in comprehending it and identifying with it. It is evidently this latter sense that one must adopt, and it is likewise in this sense that our passage was explained by Moses of Narbonne: <span dir=\"rtl\">השגת השכל הפועל ירצה בו שישוב הוא הוא ושישיג הנמצאות באופן שישיגם הוא</span> 'The <i>perception of the active intellect</i> is to become <i>it</i>, and to perceive beings in the manner in which it perceives them.' This sublime perception, the result of metaphysical speculation, and in which the intelligent and the intelligible are completely identified, is by no means subject to oblivion, inasmuch as the inferior faculties of the soul, such as imagination and memory, have no part therein. Abou-Becr ibn-al-Çâyeg, or Ibn-Bâdja, treating the same subject in his <i>Farewell Letter</i>, likewise states (verse, Hebr.): <span dir=\"rtl\">אולם החכמה האחרונה אשר הוא הציור השכלי והוא מציאות השכל הנאצל אי אפשר בו השכחה אלא אם יהיה זה יוצא מהטבע וכו׳</span> 'The ultimate science, which is pure intelligible conception and the very existence of the <i>emanated intellect</i>, cannot be forgotten, unless it be through an exception of nature, etc.' Vide the commentary of Moses of Narbonne, on our passage, and the Appendix to the <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, p. 142 (where, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">וכתב אבונצר</span>, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">וכתב אבובכר</span>), and cf. the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chapter LI. Concerning the entirety of the theory of the <i>intellect</i>, one will find further details below, in chapter LXVIII.",
    "fn.721": "Literally: He expands for them (the faculty) of lying and saying, etc.",
    "fn.722": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אבתדעהא</span>, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">אכׄתרעהא</span>, which does not make a difference in terms of meaning.",
    "fn.723": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מיןאן</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(ميزان)</span>, which signifies <i>balance</i>, is employed figuratively in the sense of <i>intelligence</i> or <i>judgment.</i> Ibn-Falaquera read <span dir=\"rtl\">מיאז</span>, or rather <span dir=\"rtl\">מין</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">تميز</span>, <i>discernment</i>); vide <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, at the end of p. 151. It is this same reading that the version of Al-’Harizi expresses, which renders <span dir=\"rtl\">הכרה</span>, whereas that of Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">מאזנים</span>, which accords with the reading one has adopted based on eight manuscripts; only one, one of the Leyden manuscripts, bears <span dir=\"rtl\">מיאזן</span>, a word which signifies naught and which is merely a copyist's error for <span dir=\"rtl\">מיזאן</span>.",
    "fn.724": "The preceding phrase should be considered as a parenthesis, where the author, in order to better highlight the obscurity of the words of Moses cited at the beginning of the chapter, opposes to them the clarity of other words reported in the same biblical account. Here he reprises the difficulties offered by the words cited first.",
    "fn.725": "By the term <span dir=\"rtl\">צאבה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(صابة)</span> , <i>Sabiens</i> or <i>Sabéens</i>, our author, following the example of other Arab writers, refers to the worshippers of the stars, and in general, idolaters; details on the Sabians can be found in the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. XXIX, where the author states that the Sabian religion <i>embraced the entire earth.</i>",
    "fn.726": "By <span dir=\"rtl\">روحانية</span>, one understands the <i>spirit</i> which presides over a star or a constellation, as well as the appearance of this spirit; the faculty of causing the spirits of the stars to descend into idols or talismans is termed <span dir=\"rtl\">استنزال الروحانيات</span> or simply <span dir=\"rtl\">استنزال</span>. <i>Talismans</i> (<i>τε έσματα</i>) are figures engraved upon a stone or a plate, representing certain constellations under whose influence one desires to place oneself.",
    "fn.727": "In the version of Ibn-Tibbon one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">davar</span>, and not <span dir=\"rtl\">kabhar</span>, as most editions have; one must also read <span dir=\"rtl\">bazmanim kolam</span> and erase the pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">hem</span>.",
    "fn.728": "That is to say: in order to summon men to them and to cause them to partake of their own conviction, they employed the means of study and instruction. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ידעון</span>, Ibn-Tibbon perhaps read <span dir=\"rtl\">ירשדון</span>, for his translation bears <span dir=\"rtl\">היו מישרים</span>, <i>they directed.</i> Al-’Harizi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">היו מיהרים</span>, which offers no suitable meaning; one may presume that there is here a copyist's error in the unique manuscript of Al-’Harizi's version.",
    "fn.729": "The author understands by these words the persons whom Abraham and Sarah had instructed and converted to their belief. The Chaldaic version of Onkelos similarly states: <span dir=\"rtl\">וית נפשתא דשעבדו לאוריתא בחרן</span>, '<i>and the persons whom they had brought into subjection to the religious Law in Haran.</i>' It is in the same sense that this passage has been explained by the ancient rabbis; vide Babylonian Talmud, <i>Sanhedrin</i>, fol. 99 <i>b:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">כל המלמד את בן חברו הורה מעלה עליו הכתוב כאילו עשאו שנאמר ואת הנפש אשר עשו בחרן</span> Cf. <i>Bereshit Rabbah</i>, section 39, towards the end.",
    "fn.730": "In the system of the Arab grammarians, the relative pronoun is a species of <i>conjunction</i> or of <i>conjunctive adjective</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(اسم موصول)</span> , which necessarily requirs after it, for complement, a conjunctive proposition, termed in Arabic <span dir=\"rtl\">صِلَةُ</span> , <i>adjonction;</i> vide Silv. de Sacy, <i>Grammaire arabe</i>, Vol. I, pp. 443 and 444 (2<sup>e</sup> Edition). In the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אהיה אשר אהיה</span>, the second <span dir=\"rtl\">אהיה</span> forms the <i>adjonction</i> of the conjunctive adjective <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר</span>.",
    "fn.731": "See above, chapter LVII, p. 232.",
    "fn.732": "Literally: <i>and it is to this that the demonstration necessarily leads.</i>",
    "fn.733": "The Arabic text reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">מא אלקיתה אליך</span> , <i>that which I have taught thee</i>, and likewise the version of Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">מה שהורעתיו לך</span>; concerning this irregular construction, in which the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">أنّ</span> serves to introduce direct discourse, and where it replacs the verb <i>saying</i>, vide Silv. de Sacy, <i>Grammaire arabe</i> (2<sup>e</sup> Edition), Vol. I, p. 568, and Vol. II, p. 468 (No. 843). One finds the same construction a little further on: <span dir=\"rtl\">כאנה יקול אן דׄאתך וחקיקתך לא יגׄהלהא אחד</span>. The Greek conjunction <i>ὅτι</i> is sometimes employed in the like manner, especially in the New Testament, and likewise the Syriac conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">די</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">ד</span>.",
    "fn.734": "This is the miracle of the transformation of the staff, to which the author has alluded earlier.",
    "fn.735": "Literally: <i>to exalt and glorify</i> (<i>God</i>) <i>by speaking to Him.</i>",
    "fn.736": "All manuscripts bear  but, this verb being obviously in the passive, we have written more correctly  (sūʾilat).",
    "fn.737": "The author, who does not express himself with clarity, appears here to indicate that the name <small>YAH</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יה</span>) is, like <small>EHYÉ</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אהיה</span>), derived from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">היה</span>, <i>to be;</i> elsewhere, however, he does declare that <span dir=\"rtl\">יה</span> is <i>a part of the tetragrammaton</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מקצת שם המפורש</span>, vide Compendium of the Talmud, treatise <i>Yesôdé ha-Tôrâ</i>, Chap. VI, § 4). Be that as it may, this name, says the author, indicats eternal existence and expresses the idea of the <i>necessary being</i> which the author finds in the name <small>EHYÉ</small>; it is this selfsame meaning that the author, at the close of this chapter, does expressly attribute to the <i>schem ha-mephorasch</i>, albeit higher up (Chap. LXI) he pronounced himself on this matter but doubtfully (vide above, pp. 269 and 270). Concerning these diverse names, cf. <i>Khozari</i>, Book IV, § 3.",
    "fn.738": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אםתמרארה</span>, several manuscripts carry <span dir=\"rtl\">אםתמרארה</span> (with <i>resch</i>), which is roughly the same thing; cf. below, at the end of chap. LXIX.",
    "fn.739": "That is, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">צור</span>, which means <i>rock</i>, and is sometimes used to designate God. See above, chap. XVI.",
    "fn.740": "In Ibn-Tibbon's version, one does read <span dir=\"rtl\">צור ואמת</span>; but the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אמת</span>, <i>truth</i>, is never employed as a <i>name</i> of God. It is true that some Arabic manuscripts likewise possess <span dir=\"rtl\">צור ואמת</span>; but <span dir=\"rtl\">ואמת</span> appears to be but an abbreviation of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואמתׄאלה</span>, which one does read in several Arabic manuscripts. Al-’Harizi has not rendered this word.",
    "fn.741": "Cf. above, p. 267, note 3.",
    "fn.742": "See on the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מלאך</span> (messenger, angel, II<sup>e</sup> part of this work, chapter VI, and <i>ibid.</i>, chapter XXXIV, where the author explains the entire passage from Exodus to which he is referring here.",
    "fn.744": "See the previous chapter, p. 284.",
    "fn.745": "The author having expressed himself in too concise a manner, we have had to deviate slightly from the text in order to render more completely his thought; a literal translation would be: <i>or rather, all that is outside of him glorifies him.</i>",
    "fn.746": "The text reads Ya’tam without a suffix; similarly, in the manuscripts of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, one reads Yagdil, while the editions have Yagdilhu.",
    "fn.747": "By these last words, the author is alluding to the celestial spheres, which have life and perception, but not speech. See the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chap. V.",
    "fn.748": "In some manuscripts one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">אנה םבח וקאל כדׄא כמא קאל</span>, <i>he praises and says such and such a thing, as has been said</i>, etc.; the version of Ibn-Tibbon expresses the same lesson: <span dir=\"rtl\">he praises and says thus, as he has said</span>.",
    "fn.749": "That is to say, the bones give us the conviction that no being is similar to the one who has so wisely arranged them, and it is as if they were expressing themselves in words to that effect.",
    "fn.750": "The author here mentions two of the most difficult questions he has hitherto treated (vide chap. LVII), and he intends to convey that the reader, after having followed him through subjects so profound and subtle, has scarce need for an explanation of the question forming the subject of the present chapter. Having concluded the discussion upon the <i>attributes</i> and <i>names</i> of God, the author adds, in this chapter and the two ensuing, certain observations concerning the <i>speech</i> and <i>writing</i> attributed to God, and concerning what one must understand by the <i>rest</i> of the seventh day of creation.",
    "fn.751": "According to the ancient rabbis, the <i>Tôrâ</i> or the Law revealed unto Moses had been created long prior to the creation of the world (that is to say, it was from all eternity within the Creator’s designs and predestined to enlighten successively the whole human race); vide <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, 1<sup>st</sup> section; Babylonian Talmud, <i>Schabbâth</i>, fol. 88 <i>b</i>, and passim.</i>",
    "fn.752": "That is to say: that the divine word, a real and objective thing existing outside the soul of the prophet, is a miraculous creation, God miraculously causing the air to resound with sounds which strike the prophet's ear. Vide Saadia, <i>Beliefs and Opinions</i>, Book II, chap. 8 (Berlin edit., fol. 33 <i>b</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">וענין הדבור שהוא ברא דבור הגיע באויר אל שמע הנביא או העם</span> «By <i>word</i> (divine) one must understand that God created words which arrived through the air to the ear of the prophet or of the people.» Likewise Abou-’Ali (commentary on Exodus, at the beginning of chap. XX): <span dir=\"rtl\">ואנמא אלכׄאלק תבארך יהרה אלבלאם פי אלהוא כמא ישא ויקרע בה ארׄן אלםאמעין</span> «The Creator causes the word to be born in the air, according to His will, so as to strike the ear of those who listen, etc.» R. Yehudah ha-Levi expresses himself in the same sense (<i>Khozari</i>, Book I, § 89): <span dir=\"rtl\">וכן יצטיר האויר המגיע אל אזן הנביא בצורות האותיות שהם מורות על הענינים שהוא חפץ להשמיעם אל הנביא או אל ההמון</span> «The air arriving at the prophet’s ear modifies itself in conformity with the letters which indicate the subjects God desires to make heard by the prophet or by the people.» The same question likewise occupied the ancient Muslim theologians, who sought to resolve it in diverse manners. Vide Pococke, <i>Specimen hist. Arab.</i>, page 217; Scharestâni, German transl., tome 1, pages 42 and 43; Schmœlders, <i>Essay etc.</i>, pages 187, 188 and 198. Regarding our author's opinion, cf. higher up, page 286, note 3, and hereafter, page 296, note 1.",
    "fn.753": "Literally: <i>as He has created all that He has created and produced.</i>",
    "fn.754": "Literally: <i>the spirits were therefore brought etc. ... by this means</i> (<i>that it was said</i>) <i>that God had spoken to them etc.</i> The word <span dir=\"rtl\">באן</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(بِأَنَّ)</span> is connected to the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">פאַרשרת</span>, and one must understand <i>that it was said</i>, as if there were <span dir=\"rtl\">באן קיל אן</span>; Ibn-Tibbon added the word <span dir=\"rtl\">באמרם</span>.",
    "fn.755": "See above, chap. XLVI, p. 159.",
    "fn.756": "Literally: <i>language</i> (<i>expressed</i>) <i>by means of language.</i>",
    "fn.757": "That is to say: so that it receives impressions that it needs to express by speech.",
    "fn.758": "See above, chap. XLVI, pp. 165 and 166. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">נבהנא</span>, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">בינא</span>; similarly Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">כמו שביארנו</span>.",
    "fn.759": "According to Moses of Narbonne, the author would be alluding to Saadia and the grammarian Ibn-Djanâ’h; indeed, Saadia, in his Arabic version of the Pentateuch, always translates the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ויאמר אלהים</span> (Genesis, chapter I) by <span dir=\"rtl\">وشاء الله</span> , <i>and God willed.</i>",
    "fn.760": "All the works of creation issued from nothingness solely by means of this divine word itself; consequently, there existed as yet no being to whom this word could address itself, and the words <i>God said</i> can signify naught else save <i>God willed.</i> — Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאקאויל</span>, some manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אקאליל אלאמר</span>; likewise Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">מאמרי הצווי</span>.",
    "fn.761": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אִמְרֵי</span> being used here in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">דִּבְרֵי</span>, and as the regimen of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">דִּבֶּר</span>, it results that the roots <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">דבר</span> are synonyms.",
    "fn.762": "Literally: <i>that their existence was natural, and not artificial.</i> — The author, desiring to set forth in this chapter that by <i>the writing of God</i> engraved upon the Tables of the Law one understands, as by <i>the word of God</i>, the eternal and immutable will of the divinity, must first demonstrate that the words <i>the work of God</i>, applied to the Tables, designate, in general, a work of nature, that is to say, a work created simultaneously with all other works of creation, and not a work newly produced at a certain moment, such as artificial things.",
    "fn.763": "That is, the attribution contained in the words <i>works of the Eternal</i>, and by which everything that nature produces is attributed to the Creator.",
    "fn.764": "The author means that Onkelos, who, in his Chaldean version, always seeks to eliminate the anthropomorphisms of the Hebrew text, deviated from his usual method here by preserving the expression of the original.",
    "fn.765": "The sense of this entire passage is as follows: Onkelos, in permitting the words <i>finger of God</i> to subsist in his version, must have regarded the word <i>finger</i> as designating something material brought into relation with God, and not something inherent in the divine essence, such as <i>the will;</i> he must, consequently, have held for the words <i>finger of God</i> an interpretation analogous to that which he accords to other expressions in which something material is brought into relation with God. Thus, for example, regarding the words <i>the mountain of God</i> (Exodus, 3, 1), Onkelos renders: <i>the mountain upon which the glory of God was revealed;</i> for <i>the rod of God</i> (<i>Ibidem.</i>, 4, 20), he states: <i>the rod with which miracles had been wrought on the part of God;</i> and in like manner the author supposes that, in the thought of Onkelos, the words <i>finger of God</i> designate an instrument which miraculously engraved upon the Tables the commandments of God, and which, like the Tables themselves, formed a part of the works of creation.",
    "fn.766": "Vide <i>Mischnâ</i>, fourth part, treatise <i>Abôth</i>, Chapter V, § 6. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">הכתב והמכתב</span>, which, each in turn, signify <i>writing</i>, have been diversely interpreted; several commentators have discerned in <span dir=\"rtl\">כתב</span> the figures of the letters traced upon the Tables, and in <span dir=\"rtl\">מכתב</span> the manner in which these letters were traced, legible from all sides of the Tables. One has been constrained to adopt here the interpretation which our author does himself provide in his <i>Commentary on the Mischnâ</i>, and which he does precede with a general observation upon the pre-existence of the <i>ten things</i> under consideration and of all other miracles (Cf. supra, Chapter LXIV, page 287, at the close of the note). One does here quote verbatim this passage from the Commentary, according to the Arabic original: <span dir=\"rtl\">קד דׄכרת לך פי אלפצל אלתׄאמן אנהם לא יעתקדון בחרותׄ אלמשיה̈ פי כל חין בל פי אול עמל אלאשיא גׄעל פי טביעתהא אן ינעמל פיהא כל טא אנעמל סוי אן אלשי אלדׄי אנעמל אכתׄרי והו אלאמר אלטביعي או כאן פי אלנאדר והי אלמעגׄزه̈ ולדׄלך קאלוא אן פי אליום אלםארם גׄעל ללארץׄ אן תכׄםף בקרח וללחגׄר אן תכׄרגׄ אלמא וללאתון אן תכלם וכדׄלך םאירהא וכתב הי אלתורה אלמכתובה̈ בין ידיה תעאלי ולם נעלם כיף והו קולה ואתנה לך את לוחות האבן ומכתב הי אלבתאבחׄ אלתי עלי אללוחות כמא קאל והמכתב מכתב אלהים הוא</span> «One has already stated, in the <i>eighth<sup> chapter</i> (cf. the aforementioned note), that they did not concede there to be, upon each occasion, a new intervention of the (divine) will, but, rather, that it was held that, from the moment when things were produced (by the Creator), they were naturally so disposed as to lend themselves to all that has subsequently operated therein, whether the matter destined to operate did occur frequently, and this is (what is termed) a <i>natural</i> thing, or whether it did occur rarely, in which instance it is the <i>miracle.</i> Hence it is they affirm that, from the sixth day (of Creation), all was so arranged that the earth might swallow Korah, that the stone might cause water to gush forth, that the ass might speak, and so forth. — By <i>kethâb</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בתב</span>) is understood the <i>Law</i>, which was inscribed before God, in a manner unknown to us, as does appear from this passage: <i>And I will give thee tables of stone, etc.</i> (Exodus, 24, 12); by <i>mikhtâb</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מכתב</span>) is understood the <i>writing</i> that was upon the Tables, as it is stated: <i>And the writing was the writing of God</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, 32, 16).»",
    "fn.767": "A <span dir=\"rtl\">foreign</span> word or expression is called <span dir=\"rtl\">ajnabi</span> , that is to say, a word or expression that does not fit with the rest of the sentence, and which disrupts either the grammatical structure or the sequence of ideas. Cf. Silv. de Sacy, <i>Arabic Grammar</i> (2<sup>nd</sup> edition), vol. II, p. 161, note 1, and p. 208, note 1.",
    "fn.768": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">םרדוא</span> (from <span dir=\"rtl\">سرد</span> , <i>bene disposuit narrationem</i>), a manuscript has <span dir=\"rtl\">טרדוא</span>, and another <span dir=\"rtl\">םמעוא</span>; these two variants do not offer a suitable meaning.",
    "fn.769": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">לום</span> ought to be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">لَوْمَ</span> (deriving from <span dir=\"rtl\">לأم</span>); one manuscript has <span dir=\"rtl\">לאמה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">לأمة</span>, which is the self-same thing. Al-’Harizi has rendered this word by <span dir=\"rtl\">נבלות</span>, and his translation is here more exact than that of Ibn-Tibbon, which bears <span dir=\"rtl\">גנות</span>; this latter word does correspond to <span dir=\"rtl\">زوم</span>, <i>blame.</i>",
    "fn.770": "The author means that these words must be translated: and he ceased (or stopped) the seventh day.",
    "fn.771": "Voy. <i>Beréschîth rabba</i>, sect. 10, at the end: <span dir=\"rtl\">All the time that their hands were still in use, they would be tense and move; as soon as their hands were idle, He would give them rest and cause them to rest in the seventh day</span>.",
    "fn.772": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אנקטע</span>, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">אנקטאע</span>, in the infinitive; similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">הִפָּםֵק</span>. Al-’Harizi has <span dir=\"rtl\">פםקה</span>, which agrees with our reading.",
    "fn.773": "That is, an irregular verb of the class <span dir=\"rtl\">פ״י</span> or of the class <span dir=\"rtl\">ל״ה</span>, so that the root would be <span dir=\"rtl\">ינח</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">נחה</span>.",
    "fn.774": "In the first case, <span dir=\"rtl\">וַיָּנַח</span> would be the aorist of <span dir=\"rtl\">הִנִּיחַ</span>, <i>he placed</i> or <i>strengthened</i> (which grammarians derive from the root <span dir=\"rtl\">ינח</span>); in the second case, it would come from <span dir=\"rtl\">הִנְחָה</span>, <i>he led, extended, prolonged</i>.",
    "fn.775": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄארגׄא ען הדׄה אלטביעה̈</span> signify <i>outside of this nature;</i> the sense is: there occurred extraordinary events, which were in no wise conformable to the laws of nature as they present themselves to us in the completed creation, for these laws were not yet established. Ibn-Tibbon does translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">יוצאים מזה הטבע</span>, which may be interpreted in the sense one has just indicated; yet it would have been preferable to say <span dir=\"rtl\">חוץ מזה הטבע</span>, for <span dir=\"rtl\">יוצאים</span> would correspond to <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄארגׄה̈</span>, whereas all manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄארגׄ</span>, which is but an orthographical error for <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄארגׄ״א</span> (for the <span dir=\"rtl\">א</span> of the adverbial accusative is oft omitted in manuscripts). Al-’Harizi has made a mistranslation by rendering: <span dir=\"rtl\">יוצאים מבח זה הטבע</span>, id est, <i>which issued forth</i> or <i>emanated from the faculty of this nature.</i>",
    "fn.776": "Literally: <i>the thing became stable and was strengthened so that it is now as it is.</i>",
    "fn.777": "One must needs read <span dir=\"rtl\">ינקץׄ</span> (with <span dir=\"rtl\">ץׄ</span> pointed), a verb signifying <i>to overturn, to refute;</i> Ibn-Tibbon, who does translate this word by <span dir=\"rtl\">יפחות</span>, has taken it in the sense of <i>to diminish</i>, reading <span dir=\"rtl\">ינקץ</span> (without the point upon the <span dir=\"rtl\">ץ</span>), as indeed it is found in several manuscripts. The subject of this verb is <span dir=\"rtl\">כון חצריפה</span>, and the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קולנא</span> constituts its object; the meaning is: <i>this circumstance, that it is not conjugated as etcetera... does not gainsay our assertion.</i>",
    "fn.778": "In fact, the aorist of <span dir=\"rtl\">הִנִּיחַ</span> would be <span dir=\"rtl\">וַיַּנַּה</span>, and that of <span dir=\"rtl\">הִנְחָה</span> would be <span dir=\"rtl\">וַיֶּנַח</span> (as <span dir=\"rtl\">דַיֶּגֶל</span>); thus, assuming the interpretation that has been given, the form <span dir=\"rtl\">וַיָּנַח</span> would be irregular.",
    "fn.779": "Literally: <i>And to remove</i> or <i>to set aside a thing which so greatly misleads</i> (<i>and a thing which</i>) <i>could not be rendered void by a rule of conjugation;</i> that is to say: the necessity in which one finds oneself to put aside from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">וינח</span> the notion of <i>repose</i>, so as not to give occasion to believe that God is subject to fatigue and stands in need of rest, this necessity is far too weighty for it to yield before a rule of conjugation.—Instead of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">רפע</span>, at the beginning of the sentence, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">דפע</span>, with <i>daleth</i>, which in no wise modifies the sense.",
    "fn.780": "That is to say: rules are never general and apply solely to the <i>plurality</i> of cases, such that it is permissible to admit of exceptions. The adjective <span dir=\"rtl\">أَكْثَرِيُّ</span> (derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">أَكْثَرَ</span>) designates <i>that which pertains to the greatest number, that which is most frequent.</i> Cf. further on, chap. LXXII (fol. 101 <i>b</i> of our text): <span dir=\"rtl\">והדׄא אכתׄרי פי הדׄא אלצנף</span>, <i>and this is very frequent in this species;</i> likewise, in the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, at the commencement of chapter XX: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאמור אלאתפאקיה̈ ליםת דאימה̈ ולא אבתׄריה̈</span>, <i>the things of chance occur neither continually, nor even most frequently;</i> III<sup>rd</sup> part, at the commencement of ch. XXXIV: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאמור אלאכתׄריה̈</span>, <i>the things which occur most often</i> (in opposition to <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאמר אלקליל אלוקוע</span>, <i>the thing which occurs rarely</i>]. See also, in the passage cited in the preceding chapter, p. 296, note 1, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">םוי אן אלשי אלדׄי אנעמל אכתׄרי</span>.—Ibn-Tibbon does not appear to have grasped, in our passage, the true sense of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אכתׄריה̈</span>, which he translates by <span dir=\"rtl\">רבים</span>; Ibn-Falaquera (Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 132) proposes to render this word by <span dir=\"rtl\">רֻבִּיִים</span>, an adjective derived from the word <span dir=\"rtl\">רׄב</span> signifying <i>the majority, the great number.</i> Al-’Harizi renders the word in question by <span dir=\"rtl\">מוםכמות</span>, that is to say, that the rules of every language are <i>conventional</i>, which likewise deviates from the true sense of the passage.",
    "fn.781": "That is, even if one admits that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וַיָּנַח</span> is a verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ע״ו</span>, coming from the root <span dir=\"rtl\">נוח</span>.",
    "fn.782": "It is not an easy matter to say how the author understood the entire passage from Habakkuk; but the essential point for him is to explain the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אנוח</span> in the sense of <i>to remain firm</i>, or <i>calmly positioned</i>, and not in that of <i>to rest.</i> Cf. the Commentary of R. Tan’houm of Jerusalem (p. 85 of my edition): <span dir=\"rtl\">נםתקר נחן ונבקא חתי ננטׄר יום שדתהם</span>, <i>We shall be tranquil and we shall remain there awaiting the day of their misfortune.</i>",
    "fn.783": "That is, a passive, or neutral, verb derived from the noun <span dir=\"rtl\">נפש</span>, <i>âme.</i>",
    "fn.784": "See above, chapter XLI.",
    "fn.785": "In other words: He is the <i>thought</i>, the subject <i>which thinks</i> and the object <i>which is thought</i>, that is to say, He is absolute thought having Himself as its object. The celebrated proposition of which the author speaks, and which has been extensively developed by the Arabian philosophers, harks back to Aristotle, who, in his Metaphysics (Book XII, chaps. 7 and 9), sets forth that in every intelligence in <i>act</i>, and consequently in the first mover, which is absolute <i>energy</i>, there is always perfect identity between that which thinks and that which is thought. The first mover, or God, is the supreme thought thinking itself; in Him, thought is itself its object, and divine thought, says Aristotle, is <i>the thought of thought</i> (<i>αὑτòν ἄρϰ νοει̃, εἴπερ ὲστὶ τὸ ϰράτιστον, ϰαὶ ἔστιν ἡ νόησις νοήσεως νόνσις</i>, chap. 9).— The author, after having dwelt at length, in the preceding chapters, upon the necessity of removing from God every species of attribute and all that might impair His absolute unity and simplicity, demonstrates in this chapter that the proposition in question implies no multiplicity in the divine idea, and that, in it, the <i>intellect</i>, the <i>intelligent</i> and the <i>intelligible</i> are one and the same thing. Here, as elsewhere, the author follows in the footsteps of the Arabian Peripatetics, and notably of Ibn-Sînâ, his accustomed guide. Cf. the analysis of Ibn-Sînâ’s philosophy provided by Schahrestâni, in his History of Religious and Philosophical Sects, p. 376 et seq. of the Arabic text (German trans., vol. II, p. 255 et seq.).",
    "fn.786": "The author wishes to speak of his <i>Mischné Tôrâ</i>, or <i>Abrégé du Talmud</i>, where, from the very first lines, he establishes the existence and absolute unity of God (see treatise <i>Yesôdé ha-Tôrâ</i>, chap. 1).",
    "fn.787": "Cf. above, chap. LII, p. 200.",
    "fn.788": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">חַי י״י</span> signify, according to the author: <i>by the living God</i>, in such wise that the two words designate but one thing, and express that <i>God</i> and <i>His life</i> are perfectly identical; His life being His essence. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">חֵי י״י</span>, <i>life of God</i>, would establish a <i>relation</i> between <i>God</i> and <i>life</i>, as between two things which would be distinct in thought. Hence, says the author, one never finds in Scripture the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">חֵי י״י</span>, <i>by the life of God!</i> which would be analogous to <span dir=\"rtl\">חֵי פרעח</span> (Genesis, 42, 15), <i>by the life of Pharaoh!</i> but it is ever said <span dir=\"rtl\">חַי י״י</span>, and likewise <span dir=\"rtl\">חַי אלהים</span> (II Sam., 2, 27), <span dir=\"rtl\">חַי אל</span> (Job, 27, 2). Maimonides' observation is especially confirmed, in a striking manner, by passages where the two expressions are found side by side, as, for example: <span dir=\"rtl\">חַי י״י וחֵי נפשך</span> (I Sam., 20, 3; XXV, 26), <i>by the living God and by thine own soul!</i>",
    "fn.789": "See above, chap. LIII (p. 213), chap. LVII (p. 232), and <i>passim.</i>",
    "fn.790": "Moïse de Narbonne observes that this example is not well chosen, for whiteness does not exert an action on the whitened object, like intelligence on the intelligible object; but it is by design, it seems to me, that the author takes an example from things familiar to even the most vulgar intelligences, and makes the ignorant say absurd things when they dare to tackle the most elevated philosophical questions.",
    "fn.791": "Ibn-Tibbon’s version reads <span dir=\"rtl\">יתחילו</span>, <i>they will begin</i>, which is inaccurate; cf. above, p. 110, note 4.",
    "fn.792": "Literally: <i>and they will believe that recognizing the truth of the necessity of this</i> (that is, <i>recognizing that the aforementioned proposition is necessarily true</i>), <i>is a thing that transcends the minds.</i>",
    "fn.794": "That is to say, the intelligent<i></i> part of the human soul.",
    "fn.795": "Literally: <i>the reality of the intellect and its quiddity.</i>",
    "fn.796": "That is, since the essence of intellect is its action, and the object of its action is itself, it follows, in the given example, that the <i>intellect in act</i> is absolutely nothing other than the <i>form</i> of the wood in question.",
    "fn.797": "That is, <i>to think</i>. There is a slight inversion in the text here; a literal translation would be: …<i>that the action of every intellect, which is to be intelligent</i> (or <i>to think</i>), <i>is</i> (<i>at the same time</i>) <i>its essence</i>.",
    "fn.798": "Literally: <i>in all that is thought in act</i>, that is, every time thought has truly identified itself with the thing it has as its object and has grasped its form or true being.",
    "fn.799": "That is, from the passive intellect which is in any individual. See above, p. 306, note.",
    "fn.800": "That is, the wood itself, considered as being able to become an object of thought, is a thing <i>intelligible in potentiality.</i>",
    "fn.801": "One must read <span dir=\"rtl\">ובדׄלך</span> (with <i>bêth</i>); in Ibn-Tibbon's version one reads similarly <span dir=\"rtl\">ובשכל</span>, whilst that of Al-’Harizi bears <span dir=\"rtl\">וכן השכל</span>. The reading expressed by Al-’Harizi (<span dir=\"rtl\">וכדׄלך</span> with <i>câph</i>) is found in the two manuscripts of Leyden, but it offers no suitable sense. In our edition (fol. 87 <i>b</i>, line 6), <span dir=\"rtl\">וכדׄלך</span> is a typographical error, for <span dir=\"rtl\">וכדׄלך</span>.",
    "fn.802": "Literally: <i>you will never find the intellect a thing, and the intelligible another thing.</i>",
    "fn.803": "Some editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version add the word always, which is not found in any of the Arabic text manuscripts.",
    "fn.804": "Voy. Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i>, book XII, chap. 7; cf. above, p. 301, note 4.",
    "fn.805": "Cf. Aristotle, l. c.: <i>εἰ οὖν οὖτως εὖ ἔχει, ὡς ἡμει̃ς ποτέ, ὁ θεὸς ἀεί θαυμαστόν ϰ. τ. λ.</i>",
    "fn.806": "See above, p. 277, note 3, and p. 308, note.",
    "fn.807": "As the author stats in other places, the <i>separated intellect</i>, whence forms emanate, does sometimes undergo an interruption in its action, the cause of which is not within itself, but ariss from the matter upon which it acts not being apt to receive the form. If, then, the action of the <i>active intellect</i> does not always manifest itself in an equal manner, one must not conclude therefrom that it can be sometimes <i>in potentia</i> and sometimes <i>in actu;</i> for it is essentially <i>energy</i>, and the interruption of its action has its cause in external obstacles. See the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chapter XII and chapter XVIII. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">חרכה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(حركة)</span> has not here the sense of <i>motion</i>, but rather that of <i>agitation</i> or <i>perturbation.</i>",
    "fn.808": "In other words: the divine essence is the <i>thought</i> which has itself as its object; it is at the same time thought, the thinking subject, and the thought object.",
    "fn.809": "Literally: <i>we have often repeated this subject in this chapter, because minds are very foreign to this conception.</i>",
    "fn.810": "More literally: <i>and by taking for comparison the sensible, in the imaginative faculty.</i> The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ואכׄדׄ מתׄל אלמחםום</span> are somewhat obscure; one pronouncs: <span dir=\"rtl\">وأَخْذِ مَثْلِ الْمَحْسُوسِ</span>, considering the genitive <span dir=\"rtl\">أَحَدٍ</span> as dependent upon the prefix in <span dir=\"rtl\">بالتخيل</span>, so that <span dir=\"rtl\">وأخذُ</span> would be for <span dir=\"rtl\">وَأَخَذَ</span>. It is in this sense also that Ibn-Tibbon appears to have understood the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אכׄדׄ</span>, which he translats into Hebrew by a verbal substantive; his version bears <span dir=\"rtl\">ולקיחת משל המוחש</span>. Al-’Harizi translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">והנני מדבר על דמיון המורגש וכו׳</span>; one perceivs that he has taken the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אכׄדׄ</span> for a verb <span dir=\"rtl\">أَحَدٌ</span>; but his translation is somewhat unintelligible. The sense is: One deems not that thou allowest thyself to be disquieted by the imagination, and that thou comparest the subject in question with things purely sensible, calling to thy aid the imaginative faculty, which ought not to intervene in that which pertains to the domain of intelligence. The author addresses his disciple (see above, page 3), stating that he is confident of being well understood by him; otherwise he would not have addressed this treatise to him, which has been composed solely for those who have studied philosophy.",
    "fn.811": "The Arabic text expresses here and further on the word <i>cause</i> by two different words, <span dir=\"rtl\">علة</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">سبب</span> , which the two Hebrew versions render by <span dir=\"rtl\">עלה</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">סבה</span>; these two words, in the philosophical terminology of the Arabs, correspond to the Greek word <i>αἰτíα</i> or <i>αἲτιον</i> (Arist., <i>Metaph.</i>, V, 2, and passim</i>), and are completely synonymous, as Ibn-Roschd states in his <i>Epitome of Metaphysics</i>, book I (Hebrew verse): <span dir=\"rtl\">הסבה והעלה שני שמות נרדפים והנה יאמרו על הסבות הארבעה אשר הם הפועל והחומר והצורה והתכלית</span> «<i>Sebeb</i> and ’<i>illa</i> are two synonymous names which are applied to the <i>four causes</i>, namely: the efficient, the material, the formal, and the final.» — The denomination of <i>first cause</i>, of which the author here speaks, being very familiar to the Arabian philosophers and to the Scholastics, is intimately connected with the system of Aristotle, which reveals to one in the prime mover, or in God, the ultimate term to which one’s intellect necessarily arrives in ascending the series of beings and causes. This first cause is, according to Aristotle, a necessary condition of science, which would be impossible if causes extended to infinity; for the illimitable escapes science. Cf. <i>Physics</i>, book VIII, chap. 5; <i>Metaphysics</i>, book II, chap. 2; book XII, chap. 7 et seq. — This chapter, like the preceding, serves as a complement to what the author has stated concerning the attributes; it is therein demonstrated that God is at once the efficient cause, the formal cause, and the final cause of the universe, and that these three causes, in Him, are entirely identical and signify but one single thing, that is to say, His very <i>essence</i>.",
    "fn.812": "See above, p. 5, note 1 and further on, chap. LXXI.",
    "fn.813": "The existence of the <i>cause</i> as such necessarily implies that of the <i>effect</i>, and, as soon as one denominates God the <i>first cause</i>, one thereby declares that the world, which is the <i>effect</i> of this cause, has ever existed. This reasoning of the <i>Motekallemin</i> is well-nigh the same as that made by Plotinus when he states that in denominating God <i>the cause</i>, one asserts something which pertains not to Him, but to one’s self, because one receives something from Him, whilst He remains in Himself: <i>ἐπεὶ ϰαὶ τὸ αἲτιον λέγειν οὐ ϰατηγορεῖν ἐστι συμβεβηϰός τι αὐτῷ, ἀλλ’ ἡμῖν, ὃτι ἒχομέν τι παρ’ αὐτοῦ, ἐϰεὶνου ὂντος ἐν αὑτῷ</i> (<i>Enneads</i>, VI, 9, 3). Cf. Ritter, <i>Geschichte der Philosophie</i>, vol. IV, p. 579. — The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ואן אלעאלם לה</span>, which are found in most of the Oxford manuscripts, signify: <i>and that the world belongs to Him</i>, that is to say, that it stands in a necessary relation to God, and that its existence is a condition of God’s own. Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ושהעולם מאתו</span>, <i>and that the world comes from Him</i>, which is not entirely exact. Al-’Harizi perpetrates a veritable mistranslation in rendering: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכי העולם עלה</span>, <i>and that the world is a cause</i>. He has perhaps followed an incorrect reading (<span dir=\"rtl\">ואן אלעאלם עלה̈</span>), which one indeed finds in one of the two Leyden manuscripts, whilst the other bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואן ללעאלם עלה̈</span>, <i>and that the world has a cause</i>. This latter reading, albeit less at variance with the passage as a whole, is likewise incorrect.",
    "fn.814": "More literally: <i>they do not even conceive the idea of an agent as an agent except insofar as it is anterior to its action;</i> that is to say, they believe that the very idea attached to the word <i>agent</i> implies anteriority.",
    "fn.815": "Literally: <i>son effet existe nécessairement par son existence comme cause en acte</i>. In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">במציאות העלה</span>, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">במציאותה עלה</span>.",
    "fn.816": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version here add the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וכשיבנה אז הוא בונה בפעל</span>, <i>and when he builds he is an architect in act</i>. These words are found neither in the Arabic text, nor in the manuscripts of Ibn-Tibbon’s version; they were evidently added by the copyists, and that owing to a misunderstanding to which this same version gave rise, which, a little further on, bears <span dir=\"rtl\">אז הוא נבנה</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אז הוא בונה</span>, so that, without the added words, something would be wanting in the passage as a whole, inasmuch as no mention would therein be made of the <i>architect in act</i>. Cf. the succeeding note.",
    "fn.817": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon renders <span dir=\"rtl\">נבנה</span> (<i>built</i> or <i>constructed</i>); it appears that the Hebrew translator here read <span dir=\"rtl\">מבני</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בנא</span>, or else that he deemed it proper to pronounce this latter word <span dir=\"rtl\">بِنِسَآءِ</span> (construction), and not <span dir=\"rtl\">يَشَاءُ</span> (architect). He did not perceive that the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כמא אן מאדה̈ דׄלך אלבית קבל אן יבני בית באלקוה̈</span> ought to be regarded as a parenthesis, and he imagined that the words <span dir=\"rtl\">פענד מא יבני</span> were a continuation thereof, so that he considered this latter verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יבני</span> as a passive verb <span dir=\"rtl\">(دِيُوبَنْدِي)</span>, referring to the <i>house</i>, whereas it is an active verb <span dir=\"rtl\">(يُبْنَى)</span>, referring to the <i>architect</i>. In truth, a Leyden manuscript favours Ibn-Tibbon's version, for it reads <span dir=\"rtl\">בית</span> (house) instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בנא</span>; but this is merely an arbitrary and unintelligent correction. — Al-’Harizi has here been more exact, rendering <span dir=\"rtl\">בנא</span> by <i>architect</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בנאי</span>); but he has entirely suppressed, in his version, the words which one deems to be a parenthesis.",
    "fn.818": "That is, for every being, except for the first mover, or God.",
    "fn.819": "Cf. Aristotle, <i>Physics</i>, Book II, chap. 7, where these four causes are designated as follows: <i>ἡ ὓλη</i> (the matter), <i>τὸ εἶδος</i> (the form), <i>τὸ ϰινἡσϰν</i> (that which moves, or the moving cause), <i>τὸ οὗ ἓνεϰα</i> (the wherefore or the final cause). The <i>formal</i> cause, which makes a thing to <i>be what it is</i> and which forms the <i>essence</i> of the thing, is also termed <i>τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι</i> and <i>οὐσία</i> (vide <i>Posterior Analytics</i>, II, 11; <i>Physics</i>, II, 3; <i>Metaph</i>., I, 3); the <i>moving</i> cause is also called <i>τὸ ποιοῦν</i>, that is to say, <i>the efficient</i>, and the <i>final</i> cause is called <i>τὸ τέλος</i>, <i>the end</i> (<i>Metaph</i>., V, 2 and passim]), and it is to these two words that the Arabic terms <span dir=\"rtl\">الغاية الفاعل</span> correspond.",
    "fn.820": "Cf. Aristotle, <i>Dern. Anal</i>., book I, chap. 13 (ed. of Bekker); <i>Métaph</i>., book VIII, chap. 4.",
    "fn.821": "That is to say, that God is at once the efficient cause, the formal cause, and the final cause of the world. The notion of the prime mover, which is itself unmoved and is pure <i>energy</i>, excluds the material cause; this latter is often presented by Aristotle as that which exists by <i>necessity</i> (<i>τὸ ἐξ ὰνάγϰης</i>), and is opposed to the three other causes, representing motion and true being, and which is summed up in a single cause, namely the <i>end</i> (<i>τὸ οὗ ἓνεϰα</i>). Vide <i>Physics</i>, Book II, chap. 7-9, and cf. the treatise <i>On the Parts of Animals</i>, Book I, chap. 1. Likewise the notion of the <i>soul</i>, which is <i>entelechy</i>, excluds the material cause; wherefore Aristotle says that the soul is the cause according to the <i>three</i> modes of cause (Treatise <i>On the Soul</i>, Book II, chap. 4).",
    "fn.822": "Literally: <i>or if</i> (<i>the world</i>) <i>necessarily results from it</i>, that is, if the existence of the world is a necessary consequence of that of God, so that the world would be eternal, and not <i>created</i> by the free will of God.",
    "fn.823": "The question at issue here is extensively treated in the II<sup>e</sup> part of this work.",
    "fn.824": "See the passages of Aristotle indicated above, p. 313, note 1.",
    "fn.825": "Literally: <i>whomever it may have been that did it among the next efficient ones</i>.",
    "fn.826": "The version of Al-’Harizi reads <span dir=\"rtl\">היא הצורה האחרונה</span>, <i>that this be that last form;</i> one reads similarly in one of the Leyden manuscripts: <span dir=\"rtl\">הי אלצורה̈</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">הי אשארה̈ ללצורה̈</span>. Ibn-Falaquera (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 37) likewise suppresses the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אשארה̈</span> <i>allusion;</i> but the version of Ibn-Tibbon conforms to the reading one has adopted.",
    "fn.827": "Aristotle demonstrates, in sundry passages of his <i>Metaphysics</i>, that primary matter and primary form neither come into being nor perish. Throughout all being, <i>generation</i> (<i>γένεσις</i>) and <i>destruction</i> or <i>corruption</i> (<i>φθορά</i>) are naught else but <i>change;</i> that which comes into being, comes into being from somewhat (which is the matter), by receiving the form (through which alone it is what it is), and it perishes by losing its form. It is manifest, therefore, that the idea of <i>matter</i>, like that of <i>form</i>, excluds generation and destruction; for, were matter or form capable of coming into being or perishing, it would be requisite that each of the twain should in turn be compounded of matter and form, a process extending to infinity, did one not halt at a primary matter and a primary form, which, consequently, can neither come into being nor perish. — Maimonides appears here to have especially in view a passage in the VII<sup>th</sup> book of the <i>Metaphysics</i> (chap. 8), in which Aristotle does summarily state thus: Since that which comes into being does so <i>by</i> somewhat (the efficient cause) and <i>from</i> somewhat (the matter), and becoms somewhat (by the <i>form</i>), the efficient cause produces neither matter alone nor form alone. He, for instance, who fashions a sphere of bronze, makes neither the bronze nor the spherical form in itself, but does impart this form unto the bronze, which is its <i>substratum</i>. Were the efficient cause truly to produce this form in itself, the latter would in turn come into being <i>from</i> somewhat, and would likewise be compounded of matter and form, and <i>generations</i> (<i>γενέσεις</i>) would continue ad infinitum. It is manifest, therefore, that <i>form</i>, or that which in sensible things one may denominate <i>figure</i>, does not <i>come into being;</i> there is, consequently, no <i>generation</i> for it, nor for the <i>what</i> in itself (<i>τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι</i>), which always comes into being in something else, be it by art, or by nature, or by a faculty. Here are Aristotle's own words: <i>εἰ οὖν ϰαὶ τοῦτο ποιεῖ αὺτό</i> (sc., <i>τὸ εἶδος</i>), <i>δῆλον ὃτι ὡσαύτως ποιήσει ϰαί βαδιοῦνται αἱ γενέσεις εἰς ἄπειρον. φανεὸν ἄρα ὃτι οὐδέ τὸ εἶδος, ἢ ὁτιδήποτε χρὴ ϰαλεῖν τὴν ἐν τῷ αἰσθητῷ μορφήν, γίγνεται, οὐδ’ ἒστιν αὐτοῦ γένεσις, οὐδὲ τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι. τοῦτο γάρ ἐστιν ὃ ἐν ἄλλῳ γίγνεται ἢ ὑπὸ τέχνης ἢ ὑπὸ φύσεως ἢ δυνάμεως</i>. Vide also <i>Metaph</i>., VII, 15; VIII, 3, 5; XII, 3. It is perceived that the matter at hand is indeed, as Maimonides does state, all form in general, or the <i>physical</i> form, inseparable from matter, and not merely the absolute <i>primary form,</i> or the prime mover.",
    "fn.828": "God, while setting the celestial sphere in motion, to which he gives form, is yet entirely distinct from this sphere, as our author will explain in other places. See the following chapter, and II<sup>e</sup> part, chap. IV.",
    "fn.829": "One has been obliged, in this passage, to deviate somewhat from the Arabic text, the construction of which is somewhat irregular, and indeed scarce logical. Here follows the literal translation: <i>It is not in this wise that it has been stated; but, even as every formed being is what it is only by its form, and when its form perishes its being perishes and ceass, so also a relationship absolutely similar to this is the relation of God unto all the remotest principles of being</i>. The purport is that God is designated the <i>ultimate form,</i> because He causs to exist the principles of all that is, which principles are remote from the causes one does recognise as immediate.",
    "fn.830": "See the II<sup>e</sup> part, chap. XII. On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פיץׄ</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">فيض</span> which we render as <i>épanchement</i>, see above, pag. 244, note 1.",
    "fn.831": "Vide hereafter, towards the close of chap. LXXII (fol. 103 <i>b</i> of our text), where the author does cite these words from the book of Daniel (12, 7): <span dir=\"rtl\">וישבע בחי העולם</span>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">חי העולמים</span> are not found in the Holy Scripture, but in the ritual of prayers; Ibn-Falaquera does appear also to have read in our passage: <span dir=\"rtl\">חי העולם</span> (vide <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, pag. 37).",
    "fn.832": "That is to say, for any <i>final cause;</i> the author will show that final causes, as well as efficient and formal causes, all lead to a last cause, or to a <i>final end</i>, which is God.",
    "fn.833": "See the III<sup>e</sup> part of this work, chap. XIII, where the author expands more fully on this matter; on the two opinions to which allusion is made here, see <i>ibid.</i>, chap. XVII (2<sup>e</sup> and 3<sup>e</sup> opinions), and cf. II<sup>e</sup> part, chap. XVIII.",
    "fn.834": "Vide above, chap. LIII, pag. 213 et seq. — One has been unable to follow exactly the construction of the text, of which here follows the word for word rendition: <i>wherefore the series of every end shall culminate, according to these two opinions, in His will and in His wisdom, concerning which it has been set forth, according to our opinion, that they are His essence, and that His intention, His will, or His wisdom, are not things which issue from His essence, that is to say, which are other than His essence</i>.",
    "fn.835": "See Part III, chap. XIII, and cf. ibid., chap. XXVII and LIII, and above, at the end of chap. LIV, what the author says about human perfection.",
    "fn.836": "See above, p. 184, note 3.",
    "fn.837": "In most manuscripts, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">אםתמדאד</span> (with <i>daleth</i>), and similarly, in what follows, <span dir=\"rtl\">יםתמדה ,אלמםתמד ,אלממד ,ימרה ,ימדהא</span>; in a few manuscripts, all these words are written with <i>resch</i>, which makes little difference in terms of meaning. Cf. above, p. 285, note 1.",
    "fn.838": "Literally: <i>it is impossible that he who prolongs may go, and that</i> (<i>nevertheless</i>) <i>he who is prolonged remains, and has duration only by virtue of what he receives from prolongation</i>.",
    "fn.839": "In this chapter, as we will see, the author explains the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">רכב</span>, applied allegorically to God as <i>first mover</i> or as <i>efficient cause</i> and <i>formal cause</i> of the universe; this explanation is closely related to the previous chapter, which deals with God considered as <i>cause</i>.",
    "fn.840": "On the meaning of the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מתׄאלה אלאול</span>, see above, p. 75, note 1.",
    "fn.841": "Literally: <i>the riding of a man on beasts, according to the usual manner</i>.",
    "fn.842": "See the II<sup>nd</sup> part, chap. IV and following, and cf. above, page 57, note 1.",
    "fn.843": "Literally: <i>a text of the doctors, repeated everywhere</i>, (<i>said</i>) <i>that there is etc</i>. On the <i>seven heavens</i>, see Talmud of Babylonia, tractate <i>’Haghîgâ</i>, fol. 12 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.844": "See the second part, chapter IV, where the author says that, in the time of Aristotle, there were counted up to fifty spheres (cf. <i>Metaphysics</i>, XII, 8), but the moderns count only ten, although some of them contain several spheres.",
    "fn.845": "See Talmud of Babylon, <i>l. c</i>.",
    "fn.846": "See above, p. 280, note 2.",
    "fn.847": "See the previous chapter, p. 320, note 1.",
    "fn.848": "On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תםאמח</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(تماسيح)</span> , see above, pag. 235, note 1. To understand the meaning of this parenthesis, one must recall what the author observed earlier about the <i>comparative</i>, which can only be used when dealing with two homogeneous things; see chap. LVI, pag. 228, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 4.",
    "fn.849": "According to the <i>Midrash</i> of Genesis, or the <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i> (sect. 68, concerning the verse <span dir=\"rtl\">ויפגע במקום</span>, Gen., XXVIII, 11), these words of Deuteronomy: <span dir=\"rtl\">מעונה אלהי קדם</span>, signify: <i>the eternal God is a habitation</i> or <i>a refuge,</i> and correspond to this passage: <i>Lord, thou hast been our dwelling place</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מעון</span>) <i>in all generations</i> (Ps. 90, 1).",
    "fn.850": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">והבין</span> , found in almost all manuscripts, should be considered as an imperative of the V<sup>th</sup> form <span dir=\"rtl\">(تَبَيَّنَ)</span> , which has the sense of <i>to understand</i> here; one of the Leiden manuscripts has <span dir=\"rtl\">פתאמלה ותאמל</span>.",
    "fn.851": "Most manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">דבר</span>; a few have <span dir=\"rtl\">ידבר</span> in the aorist, and likewise the two Hebrew versions, of which one has <span dir=\"rtl\">ינהיג</span>, and the other <span dir=\"rtl\">יתקן</span>.",
    "fn.852": "The author, reverting to the explanation which he has given of the passage from <i>’Haghîgâ</i>, and which he finds confirmed by the passage from the <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, insists anew upon the functions of the superior sphere called <i>’Arabôth</i>, which is, as it were, the instrument employed by the prime mover, or God, to impart motion unto the other spheres. These, in their turn, successively communicate motion one unto another, and are designed solely for the celestial bodies; if, therefore, in the continuation of the passage from <i>’Haghigâ</i>, the <i>seven heavens</i> be made the seat of diverse other things, by placing, for instance, in one the thrones of angels, in another, reservoirs of snow and hail, and so forth, this must be understood figuratively and applied unto the faculties of nature emanating from the celestial spheres and producing certain phenomena. This becoms especially evident by the attributions of <i>’Arabôth</i>, which the author is about to enumerate according to the Talmudic text, and which, doubtless, are naught but allegories.",
    "fn.853": "The manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">לים הו טל</span>; but the second <span dir=\"rtl\">טל</span> should be considered as an Arabic word, and one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">طَلَّ</span>.",
    "fn.854": "See the chapter LXXII, where the celestial sphere is presented as the principle of life of the entire universe, just as the heart is the principle of life in man. Cf. the II<sup>nd</sup> part, chap. X.",
    "fn.855": "Literally: <i>the potency of disposition</i>, that is to say, that thing in potentia which has been designated as a simple <i>disposition</i>. The author intends to speak of that which the Arabic philosophers have termed <i>the hylic intellect</i> or <i>material</i>, and he here declars himself in favour of the opinion professed by Alexander of Aphrodisias concerning the <i>passive intellect</i>. See above, page 306, note.",
    "fn.856": "Cf. above, p. 146, note 2.",
    "fn.857": "The author does not clearly explain the distinction to be drawn here between the words <i>soul</i> and <i>spirit;</i> in the <i>soul</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">נשמה</span>), he evidently discerns either the <i>disposition</i> of which he has just spoken or the <i>hylic intellect</i>, or the sum of the rational faculties; the word <i>spirit</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>) appears here to designate, according to him, the <i>vital spirit</i>. See above, chapter XL, page 144.",
    "fn.858": "The author intends to say that in speaking of the <i>souls of the righteous</i> one makes not the same distinction of <i>souls</i> and <i>spirits</i>, because by this is understood the <i>immortal soul</i>, which is naught else but the <i>acquired intellect</i> (see above, page 307, note), and which consequently is no longer, like the <i>hylic intellect</i>, one of the numerous faculties of the <i>rational soul</i>, which perishes with man. Cf. above, page 146, and see also what our author says upon the same subject in his <i>Mischné-Tôrâ</i> or Abridgment of the Talmud, treatise <i>Yesôdé ha-Tôrâ</i>, chapter IV, §§ 8 and 9. — Here and in other passages besides, the author indicats with sufficient clarity that the soul, to attain immortality, must have arrived in this life at the degree of the <i>acquired intellect;</i> in chapter XXVII of the II<sup>e</sup> part, the author expressly attributs permanence solely unto the <i>souls of superior men</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אנפם אלפצׄלא</span>).",
    "fn.859": "See above, chapter XL.",
    "fn.860": "More literally: <i>and one has also set forth….. that which exists in these names in the way of homonymy</i>. The book entitled <i>Madda’</i> (Science) is the first of the fourteen books of which the <i>Mischné-Tôrâ</i> is composed; the author makes allusion, doubtless, to a passage from the treatise <i>Teschoubâ</i> (chap. VIII, § 3), in which, speaking of the <i>soul</i>, he indicats the meaning of the words <span dir=\"rtl\">נפש</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">נשמה</span>.",
    "fn.861": "Literally: <i>regard therefore these extraordinary and true subjects, to which the speculation of the highest among those who have philosophized has reached</i>.",
    "fn.862": "Literally: <i>I am returning to complete what I had undertaken to make understood, and I say etc</i>.",
    "fn.863": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">beneitz po'emik</span> signify: <i>by texts of verses</i> (<i>biblical</i>); <span dir=\"rtl\">po'emik</span> is an irregular plural, of Arabic form, of the rabbinic word <span dir=\"rtl\">pamuk</span>, <i>verse</i>.",
    "fn.865": "Literally: <i>as it has been said, according to what has been said</i>, that is, according to the traditional explanation given by the rabbis to the vision of Ezekiel, where it is a matter of the <i>four animals</i> and the celestial throne. See Ezekiel, chap. I, verses 5 and following, and cf. the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. II.",
    "fn.866": "Literally: <i>this is to what extent the word has been led in this chapter</i>.",
    "fn.867": "Literally: but the purpose of this chapter, towards which the discourse was intended to be directed, was to show that etc.",
    "fn.868": "Cf. above, p. 28, note 1.",
    "fn.869": "Several manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">ובקיתה</span> with the masculine suffix; similarly, Ibn-Tibbon and Ibn-Falaquera (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 41): <span dir=\"rtl\">ושאריתו</span>. One must read <span dir=\"rtl\">ובקיתהא</span>, as some manuscripts indeed have, and translate, in Hebrew, <span dir=\"rtl\">ושאריתם</span>.",
    "fn.870": "See the II<sup>e</sup> part, chap. I.",
    "fn.871": "That is to say, the metaphysical subjects which have been discussed in the preceding chapters, and which have been designated by the Rabbins under the denomination of <i>Ma’asé mercabâ</i> (consult the preceding chapter, and supra, p. 9). — Before exposing, in the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, the doctrines of the philosophers concerning the existence of God, His unity, and His incorporeality, the author will, in the final chapters of this I<sup>st</sup> part, present an exposition of the system of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>. The present chapter, serving as an introduction to this exposition, does contain sundry historical indications regarding the origin of the said system, the influence of which is perceptible amongst certain Jewish theologians of the Orient. The author does intimate, at the commencement of this chapter, that philosophical sciences had been cultivated by the ancient Jews, and that the metaphysical subjects in question were not familiar to them solely through religious tradition. Whatever may be the value of this assertion, it is in conformity with the opinion of sundry ancient authors, and one has elsewhere demonstrated that this opinion has been upheld by pagan, Christian, and Muslim writers. Consult one's article <i>Juifs</i>, in the <i>Dictionnaire des sciences philosophiques</i>, t. III, p. 352, and the notes appended thereto in the <i>Archives israélites</i>, number for March 1848 (the whole published separately in German, with additions, by Dr. B. Beer, under the title of: <i>Philosophie und philosophische Schriftsteller der Juden</i>, Leipzig, 1852, octavo, pp. 7 and 96). In Maimonides' time, the said opinion was generally accredited, as may be seen in the following passage from Ibn-Roschd, towards the end of his <i>Destruction de la Destruction</i> (Hebr. version): <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יםפק אחד שהנה היו בבני ישראל חכמים רבים וזה גלוי מהםפרים אשר ימצאו אצל בני ישראל המיוחםים אל שלמה ולא םרה החכמה ענין נמצא באנשי הנבואה והם הנביאים ע״ה</span> “None does doubt that there were amongst the Israelites many philosophers, and this is evident from the books found amongst the Israelites and attributed unto Solomon. Science has continually existed amongst the inspired men, who are the prophets.”",
    "fn.872": "See above, chap. XXXIV, p. 127 and following.",
    "fn.873": "On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פקה</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">פקה</span> , which the two Hebrew translators render as <span dir=\"rtl\">תלמוד</span> (Talmud), see above, p. 7, note 1.",
    "fn.874": "See the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Guittîn</i>, fol. 60 <i>b</i>, where the verse: <i>Écris-toi ces paroles, etc</i> (Exodus 34:27), is interpreted in the sense that it is not permitted to teach the written Law verbally or to transmit the oral Law in writing.",
    "fn.875": "Literally: <i>for it was to avoid what we fell into later</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">הרב</span> must be considered as a noun of action <span dir=\"rtl\">(هَرَب)</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">וקע</span> as a passive impersonal verb <span dir=\"rtl\">وُقِعَ</span>. Ibn-Tibbon's translation, which renders these two words as active preterites, is not clear.",
    "fn.876": "Ibn-Tibbon's version reads <span dir=\"rtl\">וםפקות נופלות בלשון המחובר בםפר ושגגה תתחבר לו</span>. It is observed that the translator took <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמדון</span> for a passive participle <span dir=\"rtl\">(المدوّن)</span> signifying <i>that which has been committed to writing;</i> it is in the same sense that this word has been rendered by Al-’Harizi. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ושגגה תתחבר לן</span> (<i>the error which attached itself thereto</i>) would, according to sundry commentators, signify: the errors of the copyists which were conjoined with the obscurity of the redaction; but the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">سہوُ</span> does rather designate an error of <i>thought</i> or of <i>opinion</i>, a <i>misapprehension</i>. It appears to one preferable to consider <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמדון</span> as an active participle <span dir=\"rtl\">المدون</span> , signifying <i>redactor;</i> the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וםהו יצחבה</span> signify literally <i>and the misapprehension</i> or <i>the error which accompanied it</i>, that is to say, which lay within the mind of the redactor.",
    "fn.877": "Voy. Deutéronome, chap. XVII (v. 8-12), and Maimonides, <i>Mischné Tôrâ</i>, preface.",
    "fn.878": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אעהיהאאואואא</span> (as one reads in all manuscripts), or better <span dir=\"rtl\">אעהיהאא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(المعاحة)</span> , is the name of action of the III<sup>rd</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">fabaœ</span> , having the sense of <i>to be stingy with something, to abstain with care</i>.",
    "fn.879": "See above, chap. XXXIV, p. 127.",
    "fn.881": "It is known that the title of <i>Gaôn</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">גאון</span> (in the plural <i>Geonim</i>) was borne by the heads of the Jewish academies of Babylonia, under Arab dominion, from the close of the seventh <sup>century</sup> until approximately the middle of the eleventh <sup>century</sup>. The period of the <i>Geonim</i> coincides with that of the development of the <i>Kalām</i> amongst the Arabs; the example afforded by the Muslim theologians was followed by certain Jewish doctors of the East, who, like the <i>Mutakallimūn</i>, endeavoured to uphold religious dogmas through philosophical speculation, and laid the foundations of a systematic and rational theology. It was the doctors of the Karaite sect who first embarked upon this path and themselves adopted the appellation of <i>Mutakallimūn</i> (vide the <i>Khozari</i>, book V, § 15), without, however, assenting to all the hypotheses of Muslim <i>Kalām</i>, which shall be expounded further on (chap. LXXIII). Some Rabbanite doctors amongst the <i>Geonim</i> imitated the example of the Karaites (cf. above, p. 286, note 3, and p. 290, note 2). There remains to us, in the <i>Book of Beliefs and Opinions</i> by Rabbi Saadia Gaon, a significant monument of what one may term Jewish <i>Kalām</i>, and it is to this celebrated doctor that Maimonides appears here to allude, as Moïse de Narbonne observes in his commentary upon our passage. In vol. III of the <i>Dictionnaire des sciences philosophiques</i>, under the article <i>Juifs</i> (p. 355-357), one has provided further details on this subject. One would add, moreover, that Arab authors of the tenth <sup>century</sup> expressly speak of Jewish <i>Mutakallimūn</i>. Al-Masʿūdī cites several whom he had personally known (vide Silv. de Sacy, <i>Notices et Extraits des manuscrits</i>, vol. VIII, p. 167). Isa ibn-Zara’a, a Christian Arab author from Baghdad, speaking, in his theological opuscules, of a certain Abu’l-Khayr Daud ibn-Mushajj, states that he was one of the foremost Jewish <i>Mutakallimūn</i> and a profound thinker <span dir=\"rtl\">(...من متكلّمي اليهود وكان متقدّما فيهم نظّارا)</span> . Vide ms. ar. of the Imperial Library, old collection, no. 98, fol. 40 <i>b</i> and 42 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.882": "The sect of the <i>Muʿtazilites</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(المعتزلة)</span>, whose name signifies <i>separatists</i> or <i>dissidents</i>, had as its founder Wāṣil ibn-ʿAṭā (born in the year 80 A.H., or 699-700 C.E., and deceased in the year 131 A.H., or 748-749 C.E.). Wāṣil, a disciple of Al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī (from Basra), having <i>separated</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(اعتزل)</span> from the school concerning some religious dogma, constituted himself the head of a new school, and established a doctrine whose elements were borrowed from various preceding sects (cf. Shahristānī, p. 18 et seq.; Ger. trans., vol. I, p. 25 et seq.; Pococke, <i>Specimen hist. ar</i>., p. 211 et seq.). The Muʿtazilites, subdivided into several branches, are all in agreement upon the two following points: 1° Man possesses perfect liberty in his actions; he performs good and evil by his own volition, and thereby accrues merits or demerits. 2° God, absolutely <i>one</i>, possesses no attributes distinct from His essence (cf. above, p. 209, note 1). It is on account of these two principal tenets of their doctrine, aiming to establish the absolute justice and unity of God, that the Muʿtazilites designate themselves by the appellation of <span dir=\"rtl\">أصحاب العدل والتوحيد</span> (partisans of <i>justice</i> and <i>unity</i> ). These are precisely the same expressions which the Arab historian Al-Masʿūdī employs to designate the doctrine of the disciples of ʿAnān (vide de Sacy, <i>Notices et Extraits</i>, loc. cit.; <i>Chrest. ar</i>., vol. I, p. 349-351), a circumstance which proves that the Karaite theologians principally took as their model the Muslim doctors of the sect of the <i>Muʿtazilites</i>. In accord with Maimonides, the Karaite author Ahron ben-Eliya expressly states that the Karaite scholars and a portion of the Rabbanites adhered to the doctrines of the <i>Muʿtazilites</i> (vide <span dir=\"rtl\">עץ חיים</span> or <i>Tree of Life</i>, Leipzig edition, p. 4).",
    "fn.883": "The <i>Ash'ariyyah</i> or Ash'arites are the disciples of Abu'l-Hasan 'Ali ibn-Isma'il al-<i>Ash'ari</i> of Basra (born around the year 880 of the Christian era, and deceased in 940). This individual, brought up in the principles of the Mu'tazilah, and already one of their principal doctors, publicly declared, upon a Friday, in the great mosque of Basra, that he repented having professed heretical doctrines, and that he acknowledged the pre-existence of the Qur'an, the attributes of God, and the predestination of human actions. The Ash'arites, therefore, unreservedly admitted attributes of God distinct from His essence and denied the free will of man. It is evident that their doctrine was diametrically opposed to that of the Mu'tazilah; they nevertheless made certain reservations to avoid falling into anthropomorphism and not to deny all species of merit and demerit in human actions (vide the article <i>Arabes</i> in the <i>Dictionnaire des sc. philos</i>., vol. I, page 176, and cf. above, page 186, note 1). Several details of the Ash'arite doctrine shall be expounded further on, chap. LXXIII (Prop. 6, 7 etc.); concerning the absolute fatalism professed by this sect, vide the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. XVII (3<sup>rd</sup> opinion).",
    "fn.884": "Literally: <i>and they believed it to be a demonstrable thing</i>, that is, they believed that the opinions of the <i>Mo’tazales</i> were truths that could be rigorously demonstrated.",
    "fn.885": "It would appear to result from Maimonides' statement here that, prior to his era, a certain number of Jewish theologians or philosophers had existed in Andalusia or Muslim Spain (cf. above, chap. XLII, page 149, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1), but that already in his time, only a small number of works, emanating from a few recent writers, of the philosophical literature of the Jews of Spain were still extant. Those which remain to us even at present generally justify the judgement rendered by our author, with the exception of two who have drawn various borrowings from the Mutakallimun. Solomon ibn-Gabirol of Malaga has bequeathed unto us, in his <i>Source of Life</i>, an important document which proves that from the XI<sup>th</sup> century, and before Peripatetic philosophy had found a worthy representative amongst the Muslims of Spain, the Jews of that country had successfully devoted themselves to philosophical studies and had approached the highest metaphysical questions; and from that same epoch there were also in Spain writers who deemed it their duty to defend religion against the encroachments of philosophy. Cf. my <i>Notice sur Abou’l-Walîd Merwân ibn-Djanâ’h</i>, page 81 et seq. (<i>Journal asiatique</i>, July 1850, page 45 et seq.). Around 1160, Abraham ben-David of Toledo, an adversary of Ibn-Gabirol, undertook, in his work entitled <i>The Sublime Faith</i>, to reconcile religion and philosophy; and one discerns in him a great admirer of Aristotle's philosophy, which he introducs into religious questions in almost the same measure as Maimonides. Around the same period, Judah ha-Levi of Castile had, in his celebrated <i>Kuzari</i>, declared war upon philosophy, in which he was profoundly versed; yet at the same time, he had rejected the reasonings of the <i>Kalam</i>, which he regarded as useless subtleties (vide his aforesaid work, book V, § 16). Amongst his contemporaries, Moses ben-Jacob ibn-Ezra appears to belong to the philosophical school, whereas Joseph ibn-Saddiq was in diverse respects under the influence of the <i>Kalam</i> (cf. above, page 209, note 1). This same influence is discernible, at the close of the XI<sup>th</sup> century, in the celebrated work of Ba'hya ben-Joseph of Saragossa, entitled <i>Duties of the Hearts;</i> in the I<sup>st</sup> book, which treats of the <i>Unity of God</i>, one discovers several arguments borrowed from the <i>Mutakallimun</i> (see notably chapter V), although, in respect of divine attributes, Ba'hya does show himself entirely in accord with the philosophers (cf. above, page 238, note 1).",
    "fn.886": "The author makes allusion to the ecclesiastical writers, Greek and Syriac, who, from the VI<sup>th</sup> century onwards, were constrained to defend Christian dogmas against the increasingly dominant Peripatetic philosophy and against the numerous heresies issuing from that same philosophy. The defenders of religion availed themselves of the weapons of dialectic which the study of Aristotle's philosophy had placed in their hands. Cf. Brucker, <i>Hist. crit. philos</i>., vol. III, page 534; Ritter, <i>Geschichte der Philosophie</i>, vol. VI, page 458 et seq. (<i>Philos. chrét</i>., book VII, chap. 1). The same method, as the author is about to explain, was pursued by the Muslim <i>Mutakallimun</i>, who were acquainted with the writings of several authors of the Greek Church, such as John Philoponus, John Damascene, and others, and who were even more capable of drawing from the writings of Christian theologians of Syria and Mesopotamia. Learned Christians, both Nestorians and Jacobites, lived in great numbers amongst the Muslims, who owed to them their knowledge of the philosophy and sciences of the Greeks. Cf. Wenrich, De auctorum Grœcorum versionibus et commentariis syriacis arabicis etc., page 7-22; E. Renan, De Philosophia peripatetica apud Syros, Paris 1852.",
    "fn.887": "Literally: <i>when the Christian nation embraced</i> (<i>within its bosom</i>) <i>these nations</i> [<i>and the assertion of the Christians is that which is known</i>] <i>etc.</i> The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מלה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(ملة)</span>, <i>nation</i>, denotes chiefly a great religious community. In lieu of <span dir=\"rtl\">דעוי</span> (assertion), some manuscripts bear the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אדעוא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(ادّعوا)</span>; likewise Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">וטענו הנוצרים מה שכנר נודע</span>, <i>and the Christians affirmed</i> (or <i>maintained</i>) <i>that which is known</i>. The author intends to speak of the mysteries of the Incarnation and of the Trinity.",
    "fn.888": "This author is John Philoponus, surnamed <i>the grammarian</i>, who flourished in Alexandria in the sixth and seventh centuries; his commentary upon Aristotle's <i>Physics</i> bears the date of the year 333 of the era of Diocletian, or of the Martyrs, which corresponds to the year 617 of the Christian era (vide Fabricius, Bibliotheca Græca, vol. X, p. 640, 4th edition). The discourses of Philoponus, to which Maimonides here alludes, are, doubtless, his <i>Refutation</i> of Proclus's treatise on the eternity of the world, and his <i>Cosmogony of Moses</i>.",
    "fn.889": "Abu-Zakariyya Yahya <small>IBN</small>-'A<small>DI</small>, a Jacobite Christian, from Tikrit in Mesopotamia, lived in Baghdad in the tenth century. He was a disciple of Al-Farabi, and rendered himself celebrated by his Arabic translations of several works by Aristotle and his commentators. According to Al-Kifti's <i>Tarîkh al-’hocamâ</i>, he died on the 13th of August of the year 1285 of the Seleucids (974 of the Christian era), aged 81 years. Confer. Aboul-Faraj, Historia Dynastiarum, Arabic text, p. 317, Latin version, p. 209. Ibn-'Adi likewise composed theological writings, in which he sought to reconcile philosophy with Christian dogmas; thus, for instance, he perceived in the <i>Trinity</i> the unity of the intellect, the intelligent, and the intelligible (confer above, chap. LXVIII), which are the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit (vide Hammer, Encyklopœdische Ubersicht der Wissenschaften des Orients, p. 423). It would appear that Maimonides was not well acquainted with the epoch in which Ibn-'Adi had lived, and believed him to be of greater antiquity, inasmuch as he seems to suppose that the first Muslim <i>Motécallemîn</i> had drawn from his writings.",
    "fn.890": "Literally: <i>all that the one who chose believed to be useful to him.</i>",
    "fn.891": "See below, chap. LXXIII, propositions 1 and 2.",
    "fn.892": "The majority of the Oxford manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אלם</span>, and this word was taken by Ibn-Tibbon in the sense of <i>to suffer, to be ill</i> or <i>to be affected by something</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">ألم</span>, for he translats it by <span dir=\"rtl\">נחלו</span> (id est <span dir=\"rtl\">נֶחֱלוּ</span>, the <i>niph'al</i> of <span dir=\"rtl\">הלה</span>). In the two Leyden manuscripts one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">עלם</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">علم</span>, and it is this reading that Al-Harizi expresses, who has <span dir=\"rtl\">נודעו</span>; in a manuscript of Ibn-Tibbon's version (Oratory fund, no. 46), one reads likewise <span dir=\"rtl\">ידען</span> in lieu of <span dir=\"rtl\">נחלו</span>. Neither of the two versions expresses the true sense of the Arabic text, in which one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">أَلَمْ</span> (the IV<sup>th</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">الله</span>), a verb which signifies <i>to enter, to approach, to engage in something;</i> confer above, chap. XXVIII, <span dir=\"rtl\">לאילּם בהדֿא אלמעֿני פֿלדֿך לא</span>, <i>therefore he does not engage in this question</i> (fol. 31 <i>b</i> of our text and p. 96 of the translation), where Ibn-Tibbon renders the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ירושלם</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">הבנים עצמו</span>.",
    "fn.893": "Id est, their era was more approximate to that of the ancient philosophers, to whom they were bound by sundry ties. This is, at the least, how this passage has been understood by Al-Harizi, who translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי החבמים ההם היה זמנם קרוב לדור הפילוםופים</span>. However, according to certain commentators, the sense would be that the Greek theologians <i>held themselves closer</i> to the philosophers, id est, did not deviate so greatly from their doctrines; the Arabic text, as well as Ibn-Tibbon's version, does indeed admit of this interpretation, and it is moreover favoured by a variant in some Arabic manuscripts which bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אלפלספח</span>, <i>of philosophy</i>, in lieu of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלפלאםפה̈</span>, <i>of the philosophers</i>.",
    "fn.894": "All Arabic manuscripts have the feminine form והי, referring to אשיא; the two Hebrew versions have והוא.",
    "fn.895": "<span dir=\"rtl\">וגירהא</span> cannot grammatically relate but to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמעגׄזאת</span>, and the same is true of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וזולתם</span> in the Hebrew versions; but one believs that the author has inadvertently placed <span dir=\"rtl\">וגירהא</span>, in lieu of <span dir=\"rtl\">וגירה</span>, and that this word relats to <span dir=\"rtl\">אלקול</span>. In this wise is also justified the employment of the feminine <span dir=\"rtl\">והי</span>, of which one has spoken in the preceding note.",
    "fn.896": "The author alludes to the discussion which arose amongst Muslim theologians concerning the <i>divine word</i> addressed to the prophets, and notably to Mohammed. According to some, the word of God is <i>eternal</i>, and the Qur’an, in which it is deposited, has existed from all eternity; according to others, it is <i>created</i> within the subject in which it reveals itself, and which clothes it with letters and sounds. Vide Pococke, <i>Specimen hist. ar</i>., p. 217 et seq.; Schahrestâni, p. 30 and 156 (Germ. trans., Vol. I, p. 42 and 236); cf. above, p. 290, note 2. It is in this sense likewise that Ibn-Tibbon explains the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דבור</span>, by which he here renders the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">בלאם</span>; in certain manuscripts of his version one reads this note from the translator: <span dir=\"rtl\">רוצה בו דבור השם לנביאים כי האריבו לדבר במהותו אם הוא קדמון או מחודש</span> «He intends to speak of the word of God (addressed) to the prophets; for they (the Muslims) have long discussed the question whether it be eternal or created.»",
    "fn.897": "Literally: <i>certain hypotheses, by means of which hypotheses, which they had chosen, they supported the things in which they had committed themselves</i>.",
    "fn.898": "Literally: <i>of that which has been laid down with her</i>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ממא <span dir=\"rtl\">ממיא אחֿצֿע פיהא</span> פיהא</span> are rendered in Ibn-Tibbon’s version (according to the editions) by <span dir=\"rtl\">ממה שחובר בהם לה</span>, <i>of that which has been</i> <small>COMPOSED</small> <i>in this regard by her</i>, that is to say, of that which has been consigned in writings; one preferrs the reading of several manuscripts which bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ממה שהוצע לה</span>.",
    "fn.899": "All manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאול</span> in the singular, which is irregular.",
    "fn.900": "Literally: <i>that we had no need whatsoever to establish or deny this thing, in so far as it pertains to the necessary foundations of religion</i>.",
    "fn.901": "Literally: <i>they did not perceive and did not know that the thing was not as they believed</i>.",
    "fn.902": "Literally: <i>in order to establish what was being sought to be established, and to deny what was being sought to be denied</i>.",
    "fn.903": "That is to say: They even rejected innocuous proposals as soon as they realized that, from consequence to consequence, they could lead to a proposal that was dangerous for the religion.",
    "fn.904": "Moses of Narbonne expresses his astonishment that the author here cits Themistius (commentator of Aristotle of the fourth <sup>century</sup>), instead of citing Aristotle himself, who, he says, has treated this subject at length in letter Γ (Book IV) of the Metaphysics, where he expresses himself thus: «If every thought and every opinion were true, then all things would necessarily be at once true and false; for many men think the contrary of what others think, and believe that those who do not think as they do are in error.» Which means (adds Moses of Narbonne) that things would needs be true and false at once, were they to accommodate themselves to the opinions of men; for oftentimes men hold opposing opinions concerning one and the same thing. The passage to which allusion is made is found in Book IV, chap. 6, of the Metaphysics, where Aristotle, combating the doctrine of Protagoras (who asserted that man or individual opinion is the measure of all things), expresses himself in these terms: <i>εἴτε γἀρ τὰ δοϰοῖντα πάντα ἐστὶν ἀληθῆ ϰαὶ τὰ φαινόμενα, ἀνὰγϰη πάντα ἃμα ἀληθῆ ϰαὶ ψευδῆ εἶναι. πολλοὶ γὰρ τἀναντία ὑπολαμβάνουσιν ἀλλή-λοις, ϰαὶ τοὺς μὴ ταὐτὰ δοξάζοντας ἑαυτοῖς διεψεῦσθαι νομίζουσιν, ϰ. τ. λ</i>. After having made some other citations from the same chapter, Moses of Narbonne adds: «It seems to one that the author (Maimonides), in composing this treatise, consulted particularly the moderns.» It appears indeed that Maimonides, in studying Peripatetic philosophy, did not always revert to the sources, and confined himself to reading the analyses of the commentators; amongst these latter, Themistius was one of the most accredited amongst the Arabs, and it is chiefly in the works of this commentator and in those of Ibn-Sînâ that many Arab scholars studied the philosophy of Aristotle. Cf. Schahrestâni, loco citato, p. 312 and 326 (Germ. trans., Vol. II, p. 160 and 181).",
    "fn.905": "Dialectic and sophistic are concerned with the same genus of questions that philosophy treats, and they have for their object <i>being</i>. But, says Aristotle, dialectic does but <i>examine</i> where philosophy <i>cogniss;</i> sophistic <i>appears</i> (to be science), without being so: <i>ἒστι δὲ ἡ διαλεϰτιϰὴ πειραστι-ϰὴ περὶ ὧν ἡ φιλοσοφία γνωριστιϰή ἡ δὲ σοφιστιϰὴ φαινομένη, οὖσα δ’οὔ</i>. Metaph., Book IV, chap. 2.",
    "fn.906": "<span dir=\"rtl\">אלמתשרעון</span> (properly: <i>ceux qui s’occupent de la Loi</i>) means the same as <span dir=\"rtl\">אהל אלשריעה̈</span>; see above, p. 68, note 3.",
    "fn.907": "In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, one must add, after <span dir=\"rtl\">their writings</span>, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">and their words</span>, which is found in the manuscripts of this version and that of Al-’Harizi; the sense is, as one can convince oneself, either by their own works or by what is reported about them.",
    "fn.908": "Literally: <i>and that we should not fight for this with the sword</i>. There may be an ironic allusion here to the manner in which Islam sought to propagate its doctrines.",
    "fn.909": "Literally: <i>and we will say about it all that by which it is possible to argue</i>, that is, we will produce all the arguments that can be alleged in favor of the novelty of the world, or at least against its eternity. See the II<sup>e</sup> part, chap. XV and following.",
    "fn.910": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">أصوليون</span> designats those who occupy themselves with the <i>roots</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(اصول)</span> or the fundamental doctrines of religion; it is these <i>roots</i> which form the subject of the <i>calâm</i>, whilst the <i>branches</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(فروع)</span>, that is to say, all that is secondary, and notably the practices, belong to the domain of jurisprudence <span dir=\"rtl\">(فقه)</span>. Vide Pococke, <i>Specimen hist. ar</i>., text of Abou’l-Faradj, p. 16; Schahrestâni, p. 28 (Germ. trans., Vol. I, p. 38 and 39).",
    "fn.911": "The Hebrew expressions <span dir=\"rtl\">הִפּוּךְ עולם</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">שנוי םדרי בראשית</span>, which the author here employs, are borrowed from well-known Talmudic locutions; e.g.: <span dir=\"rtl\">עולם הָפוך ראיתי</span>, Bab. Talm., treatise <i>Pesa’hîm</i>, fol. 50 <i>a;</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">נשתנו לו םדרי בראשית</span>, ibidem, treatise <i>Schabbâth</i>, fol. 53 <i>b;</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">גדולה חבתן של ישראל שהקב״ה משנה םדרי בראשית בשביל טובתן</span> <i>Schemôth rabbâ</i>, sect. 38.",
    "fn.912": "This is the author's reasoning: If one admits that the world is <i>created</i>, it doubtless follows that a creator exists; but one thereby succeeds in establishing naught save the mere <i>existence</i> of God, whereas by admitting the <i>eternity</i> of the world, one shall possess rigorous demonstrations for establishing simultaneously the immateriality, the unity, and the immutability of God, as shall be seen in the first chapters of the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work. It is therefore necessary to borrow in the first instance the arguments of those who profess the eternity of prime matter, so that no doubt may remain concerning the true nature of the Supreme Being, reserving the subsequent refutation of the hypothesis of the eternity of matter. One readily perceives the vicious character of this reasoning; it contains the implicit confession that the believer who admits the dogma of Creation <i>ex nihilo</i> must admit the unity and incorporeality of God as objects of religious faith, and renounce all <i>demonstration</i> in this regard. This is how Ibn-Falaquera (<i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, p. 43) expresses himself concerning our passage: «Here one might object: How is one to demonstrate a subject of such import by means of a dubious thing, and a fortiori if that thing be not true? For if the premises of the demonstration are not true, how can the conclusion be true, and how can one, with such premises, form a demonstration that is not dubious?... Doubtless, this cannot have escaped our master, who has sagely ordered all his words.» Cf. above, p. 29, note 1.",
    "fn.913": "Literally: <i>Therefore thou shalt ever find in that which I have composed concerning the books of </i> F<small>IKH</small> (or <i>the Talmud</i>), <i>whenever I happen to speak of bases</i> (or <i>of fundamental principles</i>), <i>etc.</i> Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">قואעד</span>, one of the Oxford manuscripts (Uri's catal., no. 359) bears <span dir=\"rtl\">قوااعد אלשריעה̈</span>, <i>the bases of religion</i>. It is this reading which has been followed in the two Hebrew versions; that of Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">יםודות הדת</span>, and that of Al-’Harizi <span dir=\"rtl\">יםודות האמונה</span>. Ibn-Falaquera (<i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, p. 43) has put <span dir=\"rtl\">פנה מפנות התורה</span>, which would be in Arabic: <span dir=\"rtl\">קאערה̈ מן קואער אלשריעה̈</span>.",
    "fn.914": "Literally: <i>and that another may claim to have never been built</i>.",
    "fn.915": "That is to say, I have all the more reason to act in this way.",
    "fn.916": "That is, those of the existence, unity, and incorporality of God.",
    "fn.917": "The manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ילתגׄון</span> and further on <span dir=\"rtl\">פילתגׄוא לתואליף</span>; one has deemed it necessary to write, in both passages, <span dir=\"rtl\">يلحقون</span>, for the construction indicates that this verb derives from the root <span dir=\"rtl\">لجأ</span>, which signifies <i>to have recourse, to be reduced to something</i>. In the manuscripts, the <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span> and the <span dir=\"rtl\">י</span> <i>hamzas</i> are often suppressed. Cf. above, p. 50, note 3.",
    "fn.918": "See the II<sup>e</sup> part, chap. XIX.",
    "fn.919": "The author appears to be alluding to the fifth chapter of the proofs of the Motécallemîn enumerated later, chap. LXXIV.",
    "fn.920": "That is to say, to deny self-evident things. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">مكابرة</span> denotes an <i>obstinate negation</i>, a discussion whose purpose is not truth, but rather dispute and contradiction. Vide the Appendix to the <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, chap. III (p. 152); men of science, says Ibn-Falaquera, call him who denies the self-evident <span dir=\"rtl\">سكابر العيان</span>. Cf. the work <i>Ta’rifàt</i>, cited in Freytag's Dictionary, under the root <span dir=\"rtl\">كبر</span>.",
    "fn.921": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">QUOIQU</small>’<i>il n’y ait pas de moyen possible pour cela;</i> This version is in accordance with one of the manuscripts of Leyde, which reads <span dir=\"rtl\">QUOIQU</small>’<i>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">il n’y a pas de moyen possible</span>.",
    "fn.922": "That is to say, the questions of God's existence, unity, and incorporality, and that of the creation of the world.",
    "fn.923": "Already Plato, in the Timaeus, presents the universe as a single individual, as an animated and organic whole possessing the spherical form, and this is likewise the basis of Aristotle's cosmology, expounded in the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i> and in that <i>On the World</i>. The comparison which the author is about to establish between the organism of the universe and that of the human individual is but the reproduction of an idea widely diffused amongst Jewish writers of all epochs, and which finds its source rather in the doctrines of Pythagoras and Plato than in those of Aristotle. The idea of the <i>macrocosm</i> and <i>microcosm</i>, which appears here and there in the allegories of the Talmud and the <i>Midrashim</i>, was especially developed in the book <i>Avot</i> of <i>Rabbi Nathan</i> (chap. 31) and in the book <i>Yetsirah</i>, and embraced by several Jewish philosophers of the Middle Ages; see, for example, the Extracts from the <i>Fountain of Life</i> by Ibn-Gabirol, Book III, §§ 6 and 44, and the <i>‘Olam Katan</i>, or <i>The Microcosm</i>, by R. Joseph Ibn-Tzaddik (recently published by M. Ad. Jellinek, Leipzig, 1854, octavo), Book II, sect. 1<sup>st</sup>. In our author's work, the comparison aims only to establish the individuality of the universe; it confines itself to ingenious rapprochements and possesses nothing of the symbolic and mystic character which the Kabbalists and certain moralists have imparted to the theory of the microcosm. — The author will present in this chapter the principal points of <i>physics</i>, as it was taught in the Arabic schools; Aristotle's theories therein are supplemented and developed here and there by those of Ptolemy, Galen, and the Neoplatonists. One cannot enter into the details of the diverse theories summarised by the author, and one must needs confine oneself, in these notes, to elucidating certain points whose comprehension is more particularly requisite for the reader of this work. Various details relating to Peripatetic cosmology and to the system of Ptolemy will be more amply expounded by the author himself in the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, and it would be superfluous to dwell upon them at this juncture.",
    "fn.925": "See above, p. 134, note 2.",
    "fn.926": "That is, by a fifth element, which is the <i>ether</i> and which forms the substance of the celestial spheres. See above, pag. 247, note 3, and cf. the II<sup>nd</sup> part, chap. II, XIX and <i>passim</i>.",
    "fn.927": "The pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">והו</span> refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">אלגׁםם אלכׄאמם</span>. In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">והם</span> is an error; manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא</span>.",
    "fn.928": "That is to say, the center of the earth, which, in the system of the ancients, is the common center of the entire universe.",
    "fn.929": "The author is referring to the <i>excentriques</i>, which, in Ptolemy's system, serve to explain certain inequalities in the movement of the stars.",
    "fn.930": "The author here discourseh concerning the hypotheses of the ancients relative to the two opposed motions, of which one is the <i>diurnal</i> motion, from the orient to the occident, imparted to the planets and fixed stars by the movement of the superior sphere, and the other, that which the celestial bodies accomplish, in certain periods, from the occident to the orient. Cf. our author, in his Abridgment of the Talmud, <i>Yesôdé ha-Tôrâ</i>, chap. III, § 2, and Isaac Israeli, <i>Yesôd ’Olàm</i>, book II, chap. 1 and 7. One shall have occasion to revert to these theories in chapter IV of the II<sup>nd</sup> part.",
    "fn.931": "In this, we include the <i>excentrics</i>, attributing three spheres to the Moon, three to Mercury, and two to each of the five other planets, which, with the sphere of the fixed stars and that of diurnal motion, makes a total of <i>eighteen</i>.",
    "fn.932": "Under the denomination of <i>sphere of circumvolution</i>, the Arabs designate the <i>epicycle</i>, which, in Ptolemy's system, is a small sphere in which the celestial body is fixed, and whose centre movs, upon the surface of the great sphere, on the circumference of a great circle denominated the <i>deferent circle</i> (because it <i>carries</i> the epicycle). Cf. the treatise <i>Yesôdé ha-Tôrâ</i>, chap. III, § 4. From the commencement of the XII<sup>th</sup> century, the Arab astronomers of Spain recognised that which was improbable in this hypothesis, by means of which Ptolemy endeavours to explain certain anomalies in the motion of various planets. Ibn-Bâdja was the first to rise against the hypothesis of epicycles, and Ibn-Tofail rejected both eccentrics and epicycles (vid. my Article <i>Tofail</i> in the <i>Dict. des sc. philos.</i>, vol. VI, p. 907). For this reason, Maimonides here states that the question of epicycles ought to be submitted to examination; he himself addresses this question in chap. XXIV of the II<sup>nd</sup> part. A little later, Abou-Is’hâk al-Bitrôdji, or Alpetragius, attempted to substitute other hypotheses for those of Ptolemy.",
    "fn.933": "The author wishes to speak of the sphere of the Moon.",
    "fn.934": "The author summarizes here the theories of Aristotle on the elements. See the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, book IV, chap. 3 and following; <i>Physics</i>, book IV, chap. 5; cf. the treatise <i>Yesôdé ha-Tôrâ</i>, chap. III, §§ 10 and 11.",
    "fn.935": "The author will return to this question in chapter IV of the second part; the purpose he proposes here does not require the immediate solution of this question.",
    "fn.936": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">יכון</span>, one of the Leyden manuscripts has <span dir=\"rtl\">יהבון</span>, in the V<sup>th</sup> form; likewise Ibn-Tibbon and Ibn-Falaquera (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 45): <span dir=\"rtl\">שיתהוה</span>, whilst Al-’Harizi has <span dir=\"rtl\">יהיה</span>. The same Leyden manuscript has <span dir=\"rtl\">מנהא</span>, which would refer to the <i>elements;</i> Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harizi have indeed <span dir=\"rtl\">מהם</span>, in the plural; but the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">מנה</span> referrs to <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמכׄתלט</span>, <i>the mixture</i>, and Ibn-Falaquera likewise has <span dir=\"rtl\">ממנו</span>, in the singular.",
    "fn.937": "According to Aristotle's theories, the primary transmutations of elementary substances are exhalations and vapours, which produce the various phenomena above the earth and within the terrestrial globe; vide Aristotle's <i>Meteorologica</i>, books I-III. Vapours and exhalations which find no egress first contribute to the formation of minerals (see <i>ibid.</i>, at the end of book III), and subsequently, by degrees, to that of plants and animals. Cf. the exposition of Al-Kazwîni, in Silv. de Sacy's <i>Chrestomathie arabe</i>, Vol. III, p. 389 et seq., the citation from the <i>Ayîn Acbéri, ibid.</i>, p. 454 et seq., and the Analysis of Ibn-Sînâ's <i>Physica</i>, in Schahrestâni's work, p. 413 (German transl., Vol. II, p. 310). See also Part II<sup>e</sup> of this work, chap. XXX.",
    "fn.938": "That is to say, <i>who receives the form</i>, or who is capable of receiving it.",
    "fn.939": "Literally: in the where, that is, in relation to the category of πόν̃; for, as the author said earlier, celestial bodies undergo no change except that of position.",
    "fn.940": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">בתכרר</span> (by the <i>répétition</i>) is rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version as <span dir=\"rtl\">בהשתנות</span> (by the <i>changement</i>); this lesson, which is also found in the manuscripts, may be an error, for <span dir=\"rtl\">בהשנות</span>.",
    "fn.941": "As Ibn-Falaquera observes in <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, p. 48, it is not accurate to say that the heart<i> moves itself</i> (for it has no movement of <i>locomotion</i>, but only of <i>pulsation</i>); according to him, the author is speaking of the movement of the blood that passes from the heart into the arteries.",
    "fn.942": "Instead of תוליד, one of the Leiden manuscripts has הרכיב, and it is this latter reading that is reproduced by the two Hebrew versions, as well as the Moré ha-Moré (p. 46), which have בהרכבת.",
    "fn.943": "The author intends to convey, as he himself explains in chap. XXX of Part II<sup>e</sup>, that the genesis and dissolution of sublunar entities occur under the influence of the variation of light and darkness, or of heat and cold; this variation results from the revolution of the celestial bodies, by consequence of which light and heat are imparted to and withdrawn from the various regions of the earth in turn. The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">לנורהא ודורתהא</span> is irregular; for it can only refer to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלפלך</span>, which stands at the beginning of the sentence, and it would have been more regular to write <span dir=\"rtl\">לנורה</span>, with the masculine suffix. It is perhaps for this reason that Ibn-Tibbon rendered <span dir=\"rtl\">הגלגלים</span>, in the plural, although all Arabic manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אלפלך</span>, in the singular. One must necessarily understand in <span dir=\"rtl\">לנורהא</span> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאפלאך</span>, or <span dir=\"rtl\">אלכואכב</span>. In most editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לנורהא</span> is rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">אחר יושרם</span>, whereas one ought to read <span dir=\"rtl\">אחר אורם</span>, as is found in the manuscripts and the editio <i>princeps</i>.",
    "fn.944": "All Arabic manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">or</span>; Ibn-Tibbon and Ibn-Falaquera read <span dir=\"rtl\">if</span>, which they rendered as <span dir=\"rtl\">to mean</span>; but this reading does not offer a suitable sense.",
    "fn.945": "See the II<sup>e</sup> part, chap. I.",
    "fn.946": "That is, God, who is <i>one</i>, created only a being that is also <i>one</i>. The author is alluding to a question that he will address in chapter XXII of the II<sup>nd</sup> part, and which we touched on earlier, p. 211, note 2.",
    "fn.947": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon is here disfigured, in the editions, by some printing errors; the manuscripts and the edition <i>princeps</i> read: <span dir=\"rtl\">ken ei afshar shimtsu cholam ha-olam qatsam me-lay la-qatsam ba-zeh ha-metsiut ha-yashuv asher dibarenu bo</span>.",
    "fn.948": "By this is understood the principal faculty of the soul, which has its seat in the brain; it is designated as that which <i>guides</i> and <i>governs</i> the animal body (<i>τὸ ἡγεμονιϰὸν</i>, or <i>τὸ βασιλεῦον ϰαὶ ἐπ τἁσσον</i>). Vide Galen, <i>Definitiones medicæ</i>, CXIII (Kühn's edition, Vol. XIX, p. 378). This faculty is sometimes denominated <i>nature</i>, as Galen states, when discoursing upon the various acceptations of the word <i>φύσις</i>, <i>ibid.</i>, XCV (p. 371): <i>φύσις ϰαἰ ἡ διοιϰοῦσα τὸ ζῶον δύναμις λέγεται</i>. Cf. Cicero, <i>De natura Deorum</i>, II, 9.",
    "fn.949": "That is to say, the celestial spheres and stars, which do not perish, while in sublunar things only the species are preserved and the individuals perish.",
    "fn.950": "The author means that there is no agreement on the question of whether this faculty that governs the universe comes directly from God, or is communicated through the intermediary of the intelligences of the spheres; this question will be treated elsewhere. See the II<sup>e</sup> part, chap. X and XII.",
    "fn.951": "More literally: … <i>has for purpose the need to which it is constrained for its food</i>.",
    "fn.952": "The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">ופיה</span> refers to the <i>corps</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בדן</span>). Only one of our manuscripts has the incorrect reading <span dir=\"rtl\">ופיהא</span>, which was reproduced by the Hebrew translators; Ibn-Tibbon and Ibn-Falaquera have <span dir=\"rtl\">ובהם</span>, and Al-’Harizi <span dir=\"rtl\">ויש מהם</span>.",
    "fn.953": "Literally: <i>to its being</i> (or <i>to its nature</i>) <i>which one has purposed</i>. The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">כונה</span> (<i>its</i> being) appears to one to refer to the <i>body</i>, like that in <span dir=\"rtl\">ופיה</span> (see the preceding note); the sense is: that there are joined to the formation of the body, where all is constituted with a certain purpose and according to a particular design, various accessory matters, resulting from the material quality of certain parts of the body.",
    "fn.954": "That is, because these things are only accessories.",
    "fn.955": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתפאצׄל בין אלאשכׄאץ</span> signify that individuals <i>surpass</i> one another, and that the things in question are found in greater quantity among some than among others.",
    "fn.956": "On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אכתׄרי</span>, cf. above, pag. 300, note 2.",
    "fn.957": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">כדׄלך</span> corresponds to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וכמא אן</span>, <i>de même que</i>, which are found at the beginning of this long period, and which we have suppressed in our translation, in order to render the construction of the sentences less cumbersome.",
    "fn.958": "Literally: <i>in which there is only a slight difference, according to the measure of the extent of the species in its quality and quantity;</i> that is to say, according to the limits that nature has established, in terms of quality and quantity, for the species in question. On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ערץׄ</span>, see above, p. 236, note 2.",
    "fn.959": "Literally: <i>but are linked to the nature of general generation and destruction</i>. The author signifies that these species are produced by nature without intention and without a particular end, and that they form themselves in consequence of certain accidents resulting from the general physical laws which govern that portion of the universe subject to <i>generation</i> (<i>γένεσις</i>) and to <i>destruction</i> or <i>corruption</i> (<i>φθορά</i>]).",
    "fn.960": "Literally: <i>dont les individus sont stables</i> or <i>permanents;</i> that is to say, of bodies that remain individually constituted in each individual.",
    "fn.961": "The ancient physicians reckon four principal <i>humours</i> in the human body, namely blood, phlegm, yellow bile, and black bile. These humours possess not, like the members, an individual existence; for they are intermingled, and it is sometimes one, sometimes another, which predominates. The nature of the mixture constitutes the <i>temperament</i>, which is either sanguine, or phlegmatic, or bilious, or melancholic. Vide Galen, <i>Definitiones medicæ</i>, LXV-LXX (Kühn's edition, Vol. XIX, p. 363 et seq.). Cf. Sprengel, <i>Versuch einer pragmatischen Geschichte der Arzeneykunde</i>, Vol. II, p. 164.",
    "fn.962": "That is to say, the material of the spheres or the ether, the spheres and the stars, which have an individual permanence in the universe.",
    "fn.963": "Since everything is composed of the four elements, the elements exist everywhere as the element in general, but not as a particular element.",
    "fn.964": "Literally: <i>And just as the faculties of man produce his birth and duration as long as he lasts</i>.",
    "fn.965": "That is, in the sublunar world, where everything is subject to the perpetual change of <i>naissance</i> and <i>destruction</i>, in opposition to the superior world, where nothing is born or perishes.",
    "fn.966": "By means of the <i>attractive faculty</i>, each part of the animal body extracts from the aliments and draws to itself those parts which are suitable to its nature; by the <i>retentive faculty</i> or <i>coërcitive faculty</i>, the diverse parts of the body retain this attracted substance; by the <i>digestive faculty</i>, they dispose it to be converted into their own substance; finally, by the <i>expulsive faculty</i>, the body ejects those residues which are not conducive to its organisation. Cf. Al-Kazwîni, in Silv. de Sacy's <i>Chrestomathie arabe</i>, Vol. III, pp. 487-488; Sprengel, loc. cit., p. 162.",
    "fn.967": "That is, provided the material is of the kind that the animal body can assimilate and is of a nature to be attracted by the attractive faculty and absorbed by the body.",
    "fn.968": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">خسف</span> designates a convulsion of the soil, by reason of which entire mountains and cities are sometimes swallowed up into the earth; according to the Arab physicists, this convulsion, related to earthquakes, occurs when the vapours enclosed within the globe's bosom violently erupt, tearing the soil and carrying with them earthy matter, such that vast subterranean cavities are formed and the ground subsides. See Al-Kazwîni, <i>Adjâyib al-makhloukât</i>, published by M. Wüstenfeld (Gœtting, 1849, in-4°), page 149. It was by inadvertence that further above, in ch. LIV (page 220), the word <span dir=\"rtl\">خسوف</span> was rendered by <i>inondations</i>.",
    "fn.969": "By these last words, the author is alluding to the invasions of marine and subterranean waters, phenomena that sometimes accompany earthquakes, and of which Aristotle speaks in the second book of the Meteorology (at the end of chapter VIII): and already waters are bursting forth in the course of earthquakes, etc.",
    "fn.970": "See above, p. 306, note.",
    "fn.971": "Literally: <i>As for man only, if one were to suppose an individual, etc</i>.",
    "fn.972": "Literally: <i>de sorte qu’il gouverne par elle</i> (i.e. by this motor faculty) <i>ce qui est en dehors de lui</i> (i.e. outside the main member). The author wants to speak of the motor faculty communicated by the first motor to the upper sphere that sets all the other spheres in motion.",
    "fn.973": "Cf. above, chap. LXIX, pag. 321, note 1. The author takes the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עולם</span> (eternity) here, as elsewhere, in the sense of <i>monde, universe</i>; cf. above, pag. 1, note 2.",
    "fn.974": "That is to say, all the principal parts of the universe that govern other parts or communicate certain faculties to them.",
    "fn.975": "<span dir=\"rtl\">תַּרְגֵּ (تَرْجِ)</span> is the infinitive of the V<sup>th</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">رجا</span>, <i>to hope;</i> the manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">הרגׄי</span>. In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, this passage is disfigured by several errors; one must read, according to the manuscripts: <span dir=\"rtl\">לנדיבות טבעים ולמעלתם לא לתוחלת</span>. Ibn-Falaquera's version is more exact; it states (in the manuscripts of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">לנדיבות טבע ומעלת יצירה לא לתוחלת גמול</span>.",
    "fn.976": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">but not this</span>, several manuscripts read <span dir=\"rtl\">but all this</span>, and this is the reading expressed by the versions of Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harizi; Ibn-Falaquera combines the two readings: <span dir=\"rtl\">but all this</span> (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, pag. 54).",
    "fn.977": "See above, chap. LXIX, p. 320, note 1; chap. LXX, p. 325; II<sup>e</sup> part, chap. IV.",
    "fn.978": "See the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. XVII.",
    "fn.979": "Regarding what is called the <i>acquired intellect</i>, see above, pages 307 and 308, note. Ibn-Tibbon here renders <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמסחפאד</span> by the two words <span dir=\"rtl\">הנקנה הנאצל</span>, <i>acquired</i> and <i>emanated;</i> he states himself, in his Glossary of technical terms (letter <span dir=\"rtl\">ס</span>, under the word <span dir=\"rtl\">שׂכל</span>), that either of these two words is equally employed, but that oftentimes he has united them together to precisely convey the sense of the Arabic philosophical term.",
    "fn.980": "Literally: <i>It is beset by many doubts, and here the critic has something to criticize and the litigant has something to litigate over</i>.",
    "fn.981": "The construction of the text here is irregular; the literal sense is: <i>which</i> (<i>form</i>) <i>is such that there are but two persons who could deny, etc</i>. The author intends to convey that he has preferred to set aside, in his exposition, all that can only be comprehended after profound philosophical studies, and that he has confined himself to presenting being in a form intelligible to all.",
    "fn.982": "That is to say, he should admit, as by tradition, what we have said about the constitution of the universe in general, and then construct his proofs based on this to demonstrate the four questions at issue.",
    "fn.983": "That is to say: not even in the case of physical science, which deals with the principles (ἀρχαὶ) of all things.",
    "fn.984": "Literally: <i>I begin to report what I have promised to report and explain</i>, namely, the propositions of the <i>Motecallemin</i> and their demonstrations relative to the four questions that were discussed at the end of the preceding chapter.",
    "fn.985": "The two Hebrew translators have taken the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(على)</span> in the sense of <i>selon</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כפי ,לפי</span>), which is inadmissible here; the author means that the propositions he is about to enumerate are common to all <i>Motécallemîn</i>, regardless of the diversity of their opinions and methods.",
    "fn.986": "That is to say, the existence of <i>atoms</i>; see above, p. 185, note 3.",
    "fn.987": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">آن</span> , in the language of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, designates an indivisible parcel of time, which is to time what the geometric point is to space. It is the <i>moment</i> or the ideal point between what is before and what is after, and which Aristotle calls <i>τὸ νῦν</i>; cf. above, p. 199, note 1.",
    "fn.988": "Literally: <i>that the reason</i> (or <i>the nature</i>) <i>of capacities is also that of their privations;</i> that is, what we consider as the <i>privation</i> of a certain quality or <i>capacity</i> (<i>ἕς́ις</i>) is also a real and positive quality.",
    "fn.989": "That is, as we will see later, it is sufficient for something to be possible that one can form an idea of it, and it is not necessary that the real being conforms to this idea.",
    "fn.990": "Literally: <i>there is no difference between etc</i>.",
    "fn.991": "In other words: it makes little difference whether it involves a number of infinite things existing <i>simultaneously</i>, or a series of things existing <i>successively</i> one after the other.",
    "fn.992": "Literally: <i>and that it escapes them a great deal of their objects of perception</i>.",
    "fn.993": "The hypothesis of <i>atoms</i>, to which that of the <i>void</i> is intimately linked, was borrowed by the <i>Motécallemîn</i> from the ancient Greek philosophers, as the author has observed above (page 342). However, in re-establishing the hypotheses of Democritus and Epicurus and by adding thereto that of the <i>atoms of time</i>, the <i>Motêcallemîn</i>, far from dreading the consequences, sought to reconcile this doctrine with the dogma of creation ex nihilo, by divesting atoms of eternity and supposing them created by God. It was in much the same form that atomism later reappeared in Gassendi’s doctrine.—See also, concerning these propositions of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, Ahron ben-Élie, <i>Tree of Life</i>, chapter IV.",
    "fn.994": "According to this opinion, each atom in itself becomes quantitative as a result of composition. Doubtless, one desired thereby to explain how atoms, which possess no dimensions, can form bodies through composition, and thus one believed to escape one of the most serious objections made against atomism. Cf. Saadia's objections, <i>Beliefs and Opinions</i>, book I, chap. 4 (Berlin edition, fol. 10 <i>b</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">והשלישית שאני מרחיק ואחשוב לשקר <sup>.....</sup> הצטיירות מה שאינו לא ארוך ולא רחב ולא עמוק עד שיהיה ממנו האורך והרוחב והעומק ·</span>",
    "fn.995": "That is to say: atoms join one another, but do not form a <i>mixture;</i> for, being indivisible, they are not susceptible of being confused by fusion.",
    "fn.996": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אכואן</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(أكوان)</span> must be considered as the plural of <span dir=\"rtl\">כין</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(كون)</span>, <i>generation, birth</i> (<i>γένεσις</i>); Ibn-Tibbon's version bears <span dir=\"rtl\">ההויה</span>, in the singular; but in some manuscripts, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">ההויות</span>, in the plural. Al-’Harizi's version bears <span dir=\"rtl\">האחיס</span>, <i>the brothers;</i> it is evident that this translator pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאכׄואן</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(الاخوان)</span>, which is absurd.—The author intends to convey that the <i>Motécallemîn</i> avoid employing the word <span dir=\"rtl\">فساد</span>, <i>corruption</i> or <i>destruction</i> (<i>φθορά</i>), because, in all physical transformations, the atoms remain indestructible; they therefore prefer to designate all transformations by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">كون</span>, <i>birth</i>. All physical phenomena are reduced by them to these four occurrences: conjunction, separation, motion, and repose. Cf. Schmœlders, <i>Essay on the Philosophical Schools among the Arabs</i>, page 167 et seq.",
    "fn.997": "That is to say, they are not the same from all eternity, such that their number cannot change. All manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">מחצורה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(محصورة)</span>, that is: <i>contained within a number, bounded</i> or <i>limited;</i> Ibn-Tibbon has here freely translated: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי אלה החלקים אינם נמצאים מאז</span>, <i>that these particles</i> <small>DO NOT EXIST FROM ALL TIME</small>.",
    "fn.998": "That is to say, the <i>privation</i> of the atom, or the <i>void</i>, and how they generally understand the <i>privation</i> or the <i>non-being</i>. See propositions II and VII.",
    "fn.999": "Vide supra, chap. LXXI, pag. 349, note 1. Ibn-Tibbon, who, in the passage just indicated by one, renders the word <span dir=\"rtl\">الاصوليون</span> very aptly by <span dir=\"rtl\">היטרשיים</span>, here explains it by this inexact paraphrase: <span dir=\"rtl\">קדמוני המדברים שהיו עיקר חכמת הדברים</span>. It appears nevertheless that this paraphrase was not present in all manuscripts; the commentaries of Moses of Narbonne and Joseph Caspi here likewise contain the word <span dir=\"rtl\">השרשיים</span>.",
    "fn.1000": "The word full, found in all manuscripts, is a colloquial form for complete.",
    "fn.1001": "The manuscripts read <span dir=\"rtl\">ילתגׄון</span>; see above, pag. 351, note 4.",
    "fn.1002": "Literally: They knew necessarily. The translation of Ibn-Tibbon is inaccurate; instead of and we knew, one should read and they knew.",
    "fn.1003": "That is to say, in the <i>Physics;</i> the Arabs designate Aristotle's Physics under the title of <span dir=\"rtl\">السماع</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">السماع الطبيعي</span>, corresponding to the Greek title: <i>φυσιϰὴ ἀϰρὸασις</i> (physica auscultatio</i>). — The author intends to refer to the VI<sup>th</sup> book of the Physics (chap. 1 et seq.), in which Aristotle explicates that which is <i>continuous</i> cannot be composed of indivisible parts, and in which, after having established the continuity and divisibility of <i>magnitude</i> or <i>extension</i> (<i>μέγεθος</i> or <i>μῆϰος</i>), of <i>time</i> and of <i>motion</i>, he demonstrates that these three continuous entities are bound together by a mutual concatenation, and that what is affirmed of one of them is necessarily also affirmed of the others. Maimonides chiefly has in view the following passage from chapter II: <i>ἔτι δὲ ϰαὶ ἐϰ τῶν εἰωθότων λóγων λέγεοθαι φανερὸν ὡς εἴπερ ὁ χρόνος ἐστὶ συνεχής, ὅτι ϰαὶ τὸ μέγεθος, εἴπερ ἐν τῷ ἡμίσει χρόνῳ ἥμισυ διέρχεται ϰαὶ ἁπλῶς ἐν τῷ ἐλάττονι ἔλαττον · αἱ γὰρ αὐταὶ διαιρέσεις ἔσονται τοῦ χρόνου ϰαὶ τοῦ μεγέθους. ϰαὶ εὶ ὁποτερονοῦν ἄπειρον, ϰαὶ θάτερον, ϰαὶ ὡς θάτερον, ϰαὶ θάτερον, ϰ. τ. λ</i>.",
    "fn.1004": "That is to say: according to the hypotheses of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, time would be something positive and real, composed of perceptible elements which would succeed one another in a certain order (Cf. Schmœlders, <i>Essai, etc.</i>, pag. 163 et seq.); but in reality, time possesses but two parts, of which one is no more and the other is not yet, for the present moment (<i>τὸ νῦν</i>) is merely an ideal limit between the past and the future and cannot be considered as a part of time capable of serving to measure it. Vide Aristotle, <i>Physics</i>, book IV, chap. 10, and cf. supra, pag. 199, note 1.",
    "fn.1005": "Literally: <i>Movement is the translation of a simple substance from amongst these particles</i> (<i>transporting itself</i>) <i>from one simple substance to a</i> (<i>further</i>) <i>simple substance which adjoins it;</i> that is to say: according to the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, when a body moves, each of the atoms of that body touches successively the atoms of the path it has to traverse; whence it follows that all movements are equal, and that, if they appear to one more slow or more rapid than others, it is in consequence of more or fewer intervals of rest which interrupt the movement.",
    "fn.1006": "That is to say: if they object that, in the rapid movement of the arrow, one cannot admit intervals of rest, as they are forced to do. For, if they were to claim that, in fact, it is a continuous movement without any interval of rest, they would be obliged to admit that the movements of two arrows are always absolutely equal, which is also inadmissible.",
    "fn.1007": "The author is referring to what he said earlier about the way the <i>Motécallemîn</i> explain movement.",
    "fn.1008": "One necessarily arrives at this result, as soon as one denies the continuity of extension, as do the atomists. By considering the square as a discrete quantity, composed of a number of atoms = <i>a</i> <sup>2</sup>, the number of atoms contained within each of the sides, as well as in the diagonal, will be = <i>a</i>. Let, for instance, the square be composed of <i>sixteen</i> atoms placed in the following order: <span dir=\"rtl\">ם</span> one shall have in all directions, as well horizontally and:::: vertically as diagonally, <i>four</i> atoms; and, as the atoms are all equal and equally near to one another, it will necessarily follow that the diagonal is equal to each of the sides of the square, which is absurd. This is one of the principal arguments adduced by the Arab Peripatetics against the atomism of the <i>Motécallemîn;</i> Al-Ghazali, in his <i>Makâcid al-Falâsifa</i> (at the commencement of the section on <i>Metaphysics</i>), enumerates six, amongst which, in the fourth rank, figures that of which one speaks. Two of Al-Ghazali's arguments are cited by Mr. Schmœlders (<i>Essai etc.</i>, pag. 224); these are the first and the sixth.",
    "fn.1009": "Literally: <i>The matter divides, as regards</i> <small>ITSELF,</small> <i>into two parts</i>. One attributes the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">פיהא</span> and in <span dir=\"rtl\">בעצׄהא</span> to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלהנדסה̈</span> (geometry), and not to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בראהין</span> (demonstrations), as Ibn-Tibbon has done, who places the suffix in the plural (<span dir=\"rtl\">קצתם ,בהם</span>); it is evident, by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ובעצׄהא תכון בראהינה</span>, that the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">בעצׄהא</span> cannot possibly refer to the <i>demonstrations</i>.",
    "fn.1010": "There can be no doubt as to the sense here borne by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">تباين</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">اشتراك</span>; in the Arabic version of Euclid's Elements (Book X, definitions I and II), <i>commensurable</i> magnitudes (<i>σύμμετρα</i>) are termed <span dir=\"rtl\">المشتركة</span> and <i>incommensurable</i> ones (<i>ἀσύμμετρα</i>), <span dir=\"rtl\">المتباينة</span>. — It is readily understood, from what has just been said concerning the diagonal of the square, that under the hypothesis of atoms, it cannot be a question of demonstrating the theories of commensurable and incommensurable magnitudes, of rational and irrational lines, &c., and almost all geometrical demonstrations become impossible.",
    "fn.1011": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מרובקים</span>, in our editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, is a misprint; one ought to read <span dir=\"rtl\">מדוברים</span>, as found in the manuscripts and the edition <i>princeps</i> first edition. From the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דבור</span>, employed in the sense of <i>reason</i>, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מדובר</span>, <i>rational</i>, has been formed, in imitation of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">منطق</span> (<i>ῥητóϛ</i>), derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">نطق</span>, which denots at once <i>speech</i> and <i>reason</i>.",
    "fn.1012": "Literally: <i>As when we say: we want to divide etc</i>. <span dir=\"rtl\">نريد أن</span>  or <span dir=\"rtl\">لنا أن</span>  is the formula used to present a problem.",
    "fn.1013": "That is to say: according to the hypothesis of the <i>indivisible portions</i> or the <i>atoms</i>, it will be impossible to divide a line into two exactly equal parts, as soon as the number of atoms attributed to it is supposed to be odd.",
    "fn.1014": "These are the three sons of Musa Ibn-Shakir, named Mohammed, Ahmed, and Al-Hasan, who flourished in the middle of the IX<sup>th</sup> century. They favoured by every means the introduction of Greek sciences amongst the Arabs, and rendered themselves famous by their own writings on various mathematical sciences. The book known by the title <span dir=\"rtl\">حيل</span> or <i>Artifices</i> contained, as it appears, ingenious inventions relative to the various branches of mechanical science <span dir=\"rtl\">(عام الرحيل)</span>, and especially to hydraulics and pneumatic machines, which were based upon the hypothesis of the <i>horror vacui</i>. Cf. Hammer, <i>Encyclopœdische Übersicht der Wissenschaften des Orients</i>, pp. 340 and 508. Concerning the Beni-Shakir, vide the notice in the <i>Tarîkh al-’Hocamâ</i>, in Casiri's <i>Biblioth. arab. hisp</i>. Bibliotheca Arabico-Hispana, vol. I, p. 418; Abu’l-Faraj, <i>Hist. dynast.</i> Historia Dynastiarum, Arabic text, p. 280, Latin version, p. 183; Abu’l-Fida, <i>Anal. moslem.</i> Annales Moslemici, vol. II, p. 241.",
    "fn.1015": "Literally: <i>and that no body can be detached</i> (or <i>free</i>) <i>from one of them</i>, that is, in each body the substance must be accompanied by some accident whatsoever.",
    "fn.1016": "That is to say: if the <i>Motécallemîn</i> were to limit themselves to saying that substance is necessarily accompanied by accidents, they would be saying something true; for, in fact, accidents are inherent in all substances subject to contingency.",
    "fn.1017": "That is to say: it is necessary that one of the two opposite accidents should exist in the simple substance, or in the atom, in an absolute manner, and there is no simple substance that is simultaneously exempt from both of the two opposites, or that possesses an accident holding a middle position between the two opposites. Accidents in general, according to the <i>Mutakallimūn</i>, are of two sorts: 1° accidents pertaining exclusively to animate objects; 2° accidents proper also to inanimate objects (vide Schmœlders, <i>Essai etc.</i> Essay etc., p. 167). In both species of beings, there are such accidents which are in direct opposition to other accidents, as affirmation is opposed to negation. This proposition is in relation to the VII<sup>th</sup>, according to which negative qualities are likewise real accidents.",
    "fn.1018": "That is, apart from accidents that have no opposite, such as color and taste, the substance always has those that are the absolute negation of other accidents. Cf. p. 378, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.1019": "See Schmœlders, l. c., p. 168 and following.",
    "fn.1020": "Ibn-Tibbon's version reads <span dir=\"rtl\">תשלם עמידת מציאותו וכו׳</span>; that is to say, <i>that the existence of the atom is accomplished by these accidents</i>. This translation, albeit conforming to the ordinary sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">قام به</span>, <i>peregit</i> completed, performed (cf. chap. LXXVI, fol. 125 <i>b</i> of our text, <i>l. </i> 9 et seq.), is not here entirely exact, as Ibn-Falaquera observs in the Appendix to the <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, p. 153. It seems to one that the author has here employed the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">تقوم به</span> for <span dir=\"rtl\">تقوم فيه</span>. It is evident that this expression has here a sense differing from that which the author does assign unto it further on, in the phrase: <span dir=\"rtl\">אן הדׄא אלערץׄ تَقُومُ بهِ الجُمْلةُ</span>.",
    "fn.1021": "That is to say: they do not recognize the idea of a real attribute, and they see only an abstraction, a thing existing in our idea, and not in objects. Cf. Schmœlders, <i>Essai</i>, pag. 162 and following.",
    "fn.1022": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">Gassam</span>, which is not rendered in the two Hebrew versions, is found in most Arabic manuscripts; it is missing in the two Leiden manuscripts, which nonetheless contain <span dir=\"rtl\">Merkeb</span>, in the masculine form, which may suggest that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">Gassam</span> was omitted by the copyists.",
    "fn.1023": "Literally: <i>what is constituted</i> (<i>or subsists</i>) <i>the ensemble</i>.",
    "fn.1024": "That is to say, because it is inherent in the very idea of substance or the atom to be inseparable from accidents.",
    "fn.1025": "Or, to put it more accurately, two atoms of time. See above, p. 375, note 5.",
    "fn.1026": "All manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתי יקולونهא</span>, and one must believe that the author wrote thus by inadvertence; for these words can grammatically relate only to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאעראץׄ</span> (<i>of accidents</i>), whereas they ought to relate to <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄלק</span> (<i>creation</i>). Ibn-Tibbon has corrected this error, by translating: <span dir=\"rtl\">בריאת המקרים אשר יאמרו אותה</span>; for the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">אותה</span> referrs to <span dir=\"rtl\">בריאת</span> (creation).",
    "fn.1027": "See above, p. 351 and following.",
    "fn.1028": "That is to say, according to this opinion: that there exists no <i>nature of things</i>, nor any causality, and that all which one observes to occur is the immediate act of the divine will. In this system, therefore, were one to concede to accident a certain duration, after which it ceased to exist, this disappearance of the accident, not being the effect of a natural cause, would necessitate God's <i>effecting</i> non-being, which, however, is not <i>effected</i>. — Some commentators have observed that this reasoning stands in contradiction to the VII<sup>th</sup> proposition of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, according to which <i>privations</i> are likewise real accidents that stand in need of an <i>efficient;</i> but this proposition speaks of the privation of some determinate quality, or, so to speak, of <i>negative capacities</i> (<i>ἕς́ειϛ</i>), and these, according to the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, ought not to be confounded with absolute non-being. It is not, therefore, necessary to suppose, with Ibn-Caspi, that the author here reasons in the sense of certain <i>Motécallemîn</i> who admit not the VII<sup>th</sup> proposition. Vide the commentaries of Ephodi and Schem-Tob.",
    "fn.1029": "The author probably means that, in principle, it is true to say that the non-being does not need an efficient cause, but one must not, as the Motécallemîn do here, confuse absolute non-being with the non-existence of what has existed, for the latter does require an efficient cause.",
    "fn.1030": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אן קאלוא</span> are connected with the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אפצׄי בהם אלקול</span>; literally: <i>the discourse has led them forth</i>, (<i>in such wise</i>) <i>that they said</i>. The sense is: having had an interest in maintaining that there exists neither natural law nor causality, they were thereby compelled to affirm that accidents do not endure, and that they are created by God successively, the one after the other.",
    "fn.1031": "According to this latter opinion, which is that of the <i>Mo’tazales</i>, it would not suffice for the destruction of the world that God <i>should abstain</i> from creating accidents in the atoms, but it would be requisite for God expressly to create the accident of destruction, without, however, this accident existing in a subject or in a substance; for, were it in a subject, it would be the conjunction of being and non-being, which is impossible. — Ibn-Roschd has brought forth all that is absurd in this hypothesis, which, he remarks, merits no serious refutation. In the first place, <i>destruction</i> and <i>non-being</i> are synonymous, and if God creates not non-being, neither does He create destruction. Furthermore, an accident without <i>substratum</i> is in itself an absurd thing; and, moreover, how can one conceive that non-being should effect non-being? Vide <i>Destr. de la Destruction</i>, II<sup>nd</sup> question, in which one finds further details relating to this VI<sup>th</sup> proposition of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>.",
    "fn.1032": "Literally: <i> would rise up as an adversary</i>, or <i> would be raised against</i>.",
    "fn.1033": "Literally: <i>the color which we believe to have penetrated into the fabric, whereas, they say, it is not the case</i>.",
    "fn.1034": "Ibn-Tibbon's version here adds these words: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא זו בלבד אמרו אבל אמרו גם כן</span>, <i>and they said not merely this, but they said furthermore</i>. Of all the Arabic manuscripts one has consulted, there is but a single one (one of the two Leyden manuscripts) that possesses this addition: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולים הדׄא קאלוא פקט בל קאלוא</span>. Neither are these words to be found in the version of Al-’Harizi.",
    "fn.1035": "Literally: <i>who has caused to run</i> <small>THE HABIT</small>; that is to say, God willed that this should occur <i>habitually</i>, without constituting it a <i>law</i> of nature. The <i>Motécallemîn</i> thus purported to destroy every species of <i>causality</i>, in order to attribute directly to the sole will of God all that occurs in the world. This opinion found a zealous champion in Al-Gazâli (vide one's article <i>Gazâli</i> in the <i>Dict. des sc. philos.</i>, t. II, p. 511). Ibn-Roschd has demonstrated that this hypothesis of <i>habit</i> is an elusive and meaningless concept. Habit, being a capacity one acquires by repeating an action several times, cannot possibly be attributed to God, an immutable being, in whom no change can possibly take place. But neither can it reside in beings in general; for habit, when taken in its true signification, can only be ascribed to animate being, and, if attributed to inanimate being, it signifies the same as <i>nature</i> or <i>natural law</i>. Finally, one cannot constitute of it something subjective residing in one's judgment, which would possess the <i>habit</i> of judging things in a certain manner; for this judgment itself is the action of the intellect, likewise subject to a <i>natural law</i>. Vide <i>Destr. de la Destruction</i>, XVII<sup>th</sup> question, and confer Ibn-Falaquéra, <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, pag. 57.",
    "fn.1036": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">נילגׄ</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(نيلج)</span> is generally employed in the Orient to designate <i>indigo</i>, which yields the <i>blue</i> colour; if, therefore, the author here speaks of the <i>black</i> colour, it is perchance in the sense of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, according to whom blue is a species of black. They reckon generally but five colours: white, black, red, yellow, and green; white and black are the fundamental colours, the others being but a greater or lesser admixture of black upon a white ground. Vide Schmœlders, <i>Essays etc.</i>, page 167.",
    "fn.1037": "Properly, <i>the reed</i>, of which the Orientals make use for writing.",
    "fn.1038": "All manuscripts carry <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמתחרכה̈</span> (the participle of the V<sup>e</sup> form), which the two Hebrew translators inaccurately rendered as <span dir=\"rtl\">המניעה</span>; it is necessary to put <span dir=\"rtl\">המתנועעה</span>.",
    "fn.1039": "That is, the accident does not pass from one <i>substratum</i> to another, and, consequently, the accident of movement that is in the hand cannot be communicated to the pen.",
    "fn.1040": "The author means that the ultimate efficient cause, or God, is, according to the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, the sole and true efficient cause, acting without intermediary.",
    "fn.1041": "Several manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אֲנִי עָנִי הַחֲרִיךְ</span>, which is an irregular construction, for <span dir=\"rtl\">אן ענד תחריכי</span>, <i>that, when I move, etc.</i> That which speaks in favour of this reading is the suffix of the first person in the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אראדתי</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">קדרתי</span>, for which the Hebrew translators have substituted the third person: <span dir=\"rtl\">רצונו</span> (Al-’Harizi <span dir=\"rtl\">יכלתו</span> ,(<span dir=\"rtl\">רצון המניע</span>).",
    "fn.1042": "It has already been observed above (page 337, note 1) that the <i>free will</i> of man is an essential tenet of the doctrine of the <i>Mo’tazales;</i> they were therefore necessarily bound to acknowledge that man acts voluntarily of himself, by means of the faculty of action which God creats in him, to the end that he may execute his will. Cf. the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. XVII, 4<sup>th</sup> opinion.",
    "fn.1043": "More literally: <i>and that she attaches herself</i> to it. The author alludes to what the Ascharites call the <i>acquisition</i>. See above, p. 186 and 187.",
    "fn.1044": "Lit.: <i>et ils</i> (the Ascharites in general) <i>ont blâmé cela</i>. The two manuscripts of Leyde have: <span dir=\"rtl\">לכן אבתׄרהם אםתשנעוא דׄלך</span>, <i>mais la plupart ont blâmé cela;</i> this lesson has been reproduced in the two Hebrew versions.",
    "fn.1045": "Literally: <i>and when it rests, it only rests when there is also created rest</i>.",
    "fn.1046": "That is to say, in all the atoms of which each individual being is composed.",
    "fn.1047": "See above, p. 376, note 1, and cf. p. 195, notes 1 and 2.",
    "fn.1048": "The text is expressed in an irregular and truncated manner; it says, word for word: <i>ni rien qui ressemble à cela en fait de privations de capacités</i>, and one must infer: <i>n’est une véritable privation</i>.",
    "fn.1049": "That is to say: just as heat and cold arise from two different causes, so too the mere absence of the cause of movement is not sufficient for there to be rest, and two distinct causes are required for movement and rest.",
    "fn.1050": "That is to say, the accident of rest.",
    "fn.1051": "<i>In other words, </i> ignorance, according to them, exists <i>as something real </i> and <i>positive </i>, <i>at the same time being an accident, and it never ceases to disappear and be replaced by another ignorance continuously, as long as the ignorant remains unaware of a particular thing </i>.",
    "fn.1052": "That is to say: just as the accident of life is reproduced ceaselessly in the living being by the direct action of the Creator, so too the accident of death requires ceaseless reproduction by a new creation.",
    "fn.1053": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must replace the words the natural affairs with the acquisitions, as the manuscripts have.",
    "fn.1054": "Literally: <i>and they do not pursue this</i>, that is: they do not persist in the same thought. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">יטרד</span> must be considered as an active verb <span dir=\"rtl\">يَطْرُدُ يَطْرِدُ</span> , with the subject <span dir=\"rtl\">בעשאלאמעולה</span> . Ibn-Tibbon rendered it very accurately by <span dir=\"rtl\">ימשיך</span>.",
    "fn.1055": "That is to say, that all things in nature are composed of simple substances, or of atoms, and of their accidents, and not of matter (<i>ὔλη</i>) and form, as the philosophers assert; and that even what these latter term the <i>physical forms</i>, that is to say, the forms which constitute genera and species and which determine the essence of things, are likewise but simple accidents created in the atoms.",
    "fn.1056": "Literally: <i>so that the difference between an individual of one species and an individual of another species is as the difference between individual and individual of the same species;</i> that is to say, there exists no generic or specific difference, and every difference between any two individuals whatsoever is purely <i>accidental</i>, and not <i>essential;</i> for the <i>physical forms</i>, as has been seen, are but simple <i>accidents</i>.",
    "fn.1057": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי מא יתוהם</span>, <i>according to what one imagines</i>, refer to the <i>throne</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">العرش</span> , cf. above, p. 95, note 1), in which the common people see something real, but which, according to our author, is only an image of divine majesty, or sometimes designates the upper sphere. See above, chap. IX and LXX.",
    "fn.1058": "For example, the figure and color, which are accidents borne by quantity, and time, which is an accident borne by that of movement.",
    "fn.1059": "More literally: <i>if they flee from this</i> (i.e., if they refuse to admit that accidents are borne one by another), <i>it is because it would follow that this second accident could not exist in the substance save after the first accident had preceded it, which they are unwilling to admit for certain accidents, etc.</i> The purport of this passage is as follows: the <i>Motécallemîn</i> are unwilling to admit with the philosophers that there are certain accidents which are linked to the substance only through the medium of other accidents, as, for example, figure and colour, through the medium of quantity; they maintain, on the contrary, that all accidents in general are borne immediately by the atoms and serve to <i>determine</i> the substance, that is to say, to form its essence and to make it what it is; for it has been seen, in the preceding proposition, that it is the accidents which determine genera and species and that the <i>physical forms</i> are likewise accidents (Cf. above, page 398, note 1). They endeavour, therefore, to present, as existing in the substance itself, or in the atoms, those accidents which are evidently borne by other accidents.",
    "fn.1060": "All the manuscripts read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתקדיר</span> (with <i>daleth</i>); Ibn-Tibbon, who reads <span dir=\"rtl\">היםוד</span>, appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתקריר</span> (with <i>resch</i>).",
    "fn.1062": "That is to say, the terrestrial globe, centre of the universe and always at rest, could become one of the celestial spheres that surround the centre, around which they accomplish their circular motion.",
    "fn.1063": "Cf. above, p. 134, note 2, and p. 356. The element of the earth, with an <i>absolute gravity</i>, always tends towards the center, while that of fire, with an <i>absolute lightness</i>, flees the center and always tends towards the circumference of the universe. See Aristotle, treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, book IV, chapters 4 and 5.",
    "fn.1064": "Literally: <i>and that no place is more suitable to such a body than another</i>.",
    "fn.1065": "The construction of the Arabic text is here somewhat irregular; hereunder is approximately the word-for-word rendering: <i>likewise, say they</i>, (as to) <i>every thing among these visible beings, that each of these things be greater, etc…, as for instance that there be an individual, etc…, all this, say they, is admissible to reason</i>. The infinitive <span dir=\"rtl\">כון</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(كَوْنُ)</span> must be considered as a <i>subject</i> of which the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כל הדׄא גׁאיז</span> form the <i>attribute</i>.",
    "fn.1066": "Most manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">YiTaf</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon translates as <span dir=\"rtl\">YipraH</span> (which <i>flies</i>); we have written <span dir=\"rtl\">YiTpo</span>, as in one of the two Leiden manuscripts, and this is the reading expressed by Al-’Harizi, who translates this verb as <span dir=\"rtl\">YishoT</span> (which <i>drifts</i>).",
    "fn.1067": "Literally: <i>without regard to the conformity of being with what they suppose</i>.",
    "fn.1068": "More literally: <i>these are thus only by the course of habit;</i> vide above, p. 392, note 1. The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">כונהא</span> (<i>their</i> being) refers to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">צור</span>, etc.; some editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version possess the abbreviation ‘<span dir=\"rtl\">היות</span>, from which <span dir=\"rtl\">היותה</span> has been fashioned; one ought to read <span dir=\"rtl\">היותם</span>, as the manuscripts have it.",
    "fn.1069": "Literally: <i>And it is on this that the whole affair has been constructed;</i> that is to say: it is on such reasoning that the whole edifice of their system rests.",
    "fn.1070": "One must recall that there are <i>Motécallemîn</i> who admit, in certain exceptional cases, the accident without <i>substratum;</i> see above, pag. 391, and <i>ibid</i>., note 1.",
    "fn.1071": "Health and disease depend on the physical constitution of man or on the matter that is common to him with other animals, while wonder and laughter depend on the rational soul, which is the <i>specific form</i> of man.",
    "fn.1072": "Literally: <i>the composite bodies of a final composition</i>, that is, complex bodies, composed of all the diverse elements that contribute to forming their nature and give their matter a particular character.",
    "fn.1073": "That is, of what constitutes the respective essence of the two substances. — In one of the Leiden manuscripts, this phrase is constructed slightly differently; it reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">Ma tar’eh min al-shiddah fi al-daw’</span> … <span dir=\"rtl\">Wa’llayn fi al-daw’ wa-huwa ’a’ratah</span>. Similarly Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">Mah she-atah ro’eh min ha-hozek be-zeh va-harifyon be-zeh she-hem maqarim</span>.",
    "fn.1074": "That is, of the proximate or immediate matter, which is particular to them and is the result of the last composition, of which mention has just been made. Cf. above, p. 21, note 1.",
    "fn.1075": "See above, page 398, and <i>ibid</i>., note 1.",
    "fn.1076": "Literally: <i>Therefore, when it has become apparent to the Motécallem all that it wanted, in accordance with its propositions</i>.",
    "fn.1077": "That is to say, that every substance, or every atom, is equally apt to receive any accident whatsoever, and that there is always a direct relation between substance and accidents. Cf. above, page 399, and <i>ibid</i>., note 3. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מנהא</span> (<i>of them</i>), which follows the word <span dir=\"rtl\">גׁוהר</span> and which appears superfluous, has the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">מן אלגׁואהר</span>; if the pronoun were to refer to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלזבד ואלחדיד</span> (which, moreover, would not yield a suitable meaning), it would have been necessary to say <span dir=\"rtl\">מנהמא</span>.",
    "fn.1078": "Literally: <i>and the multitude or the small number of atoms adds nothing to this;</i> that is to say: the greater or lesser number of atoms contained in a substance is in no way responsible for the aptitude that this substance may have to receive the accidents in question.",
    "fn.1079": "For, both the one and the other being composed of atoms of the same nature, and all atoms being equally apt to receive every species of accidents, it follows that the accident of <i>thought</i> may as readily supervene upon the scarab as upon man.—The manuscripts generally bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אלכׄנפם</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(الخنفس)</span> and some few <span dir=\"rtl\">אלכׄנפםה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">الخمسة</span>, a word which is the generic name for the <i>scarab;</i> Ibn-Tibbon has rendered it <span dir=\"rtl\">העטלף</span>, and Ibn-Falaquéra, in criticising this translation, does observe that the translator mayhap read, in his Arabic text, <span dir=\"rtl\">אלכׄפאש</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(الخُفَّاش)</span>, a word signifying <i>bat</i>. See the Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, page 153.",
    "fn.1080": "Literally: <i>for it is the most firm thing to establish everything one wants to establish</i>.",
    "fn.1081": "Cf. Aristotle, treatise <i>de l’Ame</i>, book III, chapter 10: <i>καὶ ἐν τοῖς ἄλλοις ζώοις οὐ νόησις οὐδέ λογισμός ἐστιν, ἀλλἀ φαντασία</i>. See also the end of the same chapter and chapter 11.",
    "fn.1082": "The author means that intelligence alone, by analyzing things, can recognize the various elements of which they are composed and distinguish form and matter, as well as efficient and final causes, all of which are inaccessible to the imagination.",
    "fn.1083": "That is to say, only intelligence knows how to distinguish, in individuals, the genres and species by which the premises of demonstrations are formed.",
    "fn.1084": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version generally have <span dir=\"rtl\">ויעשה מהבל</span>; one must read simply <span dir=\"rtl\">והבל</span>, as the mss. and the edition <i>princeps</i> have.",
    "fn.1085": "One must erase, in the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">להשיג הדבר הבללי</span>, which are not found in the mss. of this version, nor in the <i>princeps</i> edition.",
    "fn.1086": "Literally: <i>and the being makes it appear in the day</i> (or <i>it manifests</i>); that is, reality shows that this thing exists. At least, this is how these words have been understood by Al-ʾHarizi and Ibn-Falaquera (<i>More ha-More</i>, p. 60), who translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובייאיו אוביי אעוביי אוביי אאיי</span>.",
    "fn.1087": "That is to say: it will be necessary for it to traverse the globe horizontally or vertically.",
    "fn.1088": "If the axis is horizontal, the two men placed horizontally will not be able to maintain themselves and will fall from the globe; if, on the other hand, the axis is vertical, one of the two men will be at the top and will be able to stand, while the other, being at the bottom of the globe, with his feet directed upwards, will necessarily fall.",
    "fn.1089": "That is to say, at the extremities of the axis which traversith the earth from east to west and which corresponds to the horizontal axis of the imaginary sphere of which mention has just been made. — In Ibn-Tibbon's version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ושמוה מיושבת</span> must be changed to <span dir=\"rtl\">ושמן המיושבת</span>, as several manuscripts possess; likewise, Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובי יש מן הישוב</span>, and Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>M. ha-M</i>., p. 60): <span dir=\"rtl\">ושיש מהנושב</span>.",
    "fn.1090": "More literally: <i>and his feet are on the side that faces the feet of the other that is diametrically opposite</i>.",
    "fn.1091": "It is evident that the two lines here in question, which, as the author is about to state, are, the one curved, the other straight, are none other than the <i>hyperbolic curve</i> and the <i>asymptote</i>. The treatise on <i>Conic Sections</i> cited by the author is that of Apollonius, entitled Κ<i>ωνιϰὰ στοιχεῖα</i>, and in Arabic <span dir=\"rtl\">كتاب المخروطات</span>; the theorem in question is the XIII<sup>th</sup> of Book II. Concerning the Arabic versions of Apollonius’s treatise, see Wenerich, <i>De auctorum grœcorum versionibus, etc</i>., page 198 et seq.",
    "fn.1092": "Literally: <i>and which does not fall into the net of imagination</i>.",
    "fn.1093": "Literally: <i>… who desirs to return unto himself from this faintness, I mean</i> (<i>from that</i>) <i>of following imagination</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אגמא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(إغماء)</span> is the nomen actionis of the IV<sup>th</sup> form of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">قمی</span>, which signifies <i>to fail, to swoon</i>. Figuratively, this same verb (applied to day or night) signifies: <i>to be covered with clouds</i> or <i>with darkness;</i> wherefore Ibn-Tibbon has rendered the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אגמא</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">חשך</span>, <i>darkness</i>.",
    "fn.1094": "All the manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">והמא ןכׄיאלא</span>, <i>error and imagination;</i> the author intends that the <i>Motécallemîn</i> themselves confess that these are false notions and vain imaginations, taking the word <i>imagination</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(خيال)</span> in its true acceptation. The editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version generally bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">עולה על רוח מחשבה לא דמיון</span>, and the editio princeps, as well as some manuscripts: <span dir=\"rtl\">עולה על רוח לא דמיון</span>; but this version offers no proper sense. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">לא דמיון</span>, for which several manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">ל״א דמיון</span>, or <span dir=\"rtl\">לשון אחר דמיון</span>, are a marginal gloss which unintelligent copyists have caused to enter into the text; Ibn-Tibbon had rendered the word <span dir=\"rtl\">והמא</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">עולה על רוח</span>, and the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בִּיאלוֹא</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">מחשבה</span> or by <span dir=\"rtl\">דמיון</span>; this latter word, which perchance the translator himself had written in the margin, has given rise to the error which one has just indicated. In some manuscripts of this version one finds: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויקראו ….. במו היות השם גשם מחשבה ודמיון</span>.",
    "fn.1095": "Literally: <i>and of the equality of accidents in the quality of accident</i>. The author is alluding to the 8th and 9th propositions, which declare that everything that is an accident is so in the same way, and that all accidents are immediately borne by the atoms.",
    "fn.1096": "Literally: <i>Consider therefore, O reader! and see that from this there arises a path</i> (or<i> an opportunity</i>) <i>for a difficult speculation</i>. On the imperative <span dir=\"rtl\">ארי</span>, cf. above, pag. 19, note 2.",
    "fn.1097": "That is to say: the being in general or the law of nature serves as a witness and a <i>criterium</i>. On the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">אן</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(أن)</span> used here in direct speech, cf. above, p. 283, note 4.",
    "fn.1098": "All manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">I know you</span>; the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">they say</span> is used here for the singular <span dir=\"rtl\">he brought</span>, according to the usage of common Arabic.",
    "fn.1099": "The author deems it necessary to make a reservation concerning this X<sup>th</sup> proposition; for he himself acknowledges this principle of<i> </i>admissibility for a part of the universe, and he opines that, for all that lies above the sphere of the Moon, it is not given to one to recognise an immutable natural law, and that many a thing might be otherwise than it actually is. The author bases his proofs of Creation upon a reasoning analogous to that which the <i>Motecallemin</i> have drawn from this X<sup>th</sup> proposition. Cf. the ensuing chapter (V<sup>th</sup> method), and the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chaps. XIX et seq.—The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פתאום ברגע קטן</span>, which are read here in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, contain a double translation of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">באלהוינא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(بِالْهُوَيْنَا)</span>; one must efface the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פתאום</span>, which is not found in the manuscripts of the said version. Ibn-Falaquera renders the word <span dir=\"rtl\">באלהוינא</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">בדבר קל</span>, <i>with little concern</i>, that is to say, <i>inconsiderately</i> or <i>lightly;</i> vide the Appendix to the <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, p. 153.",
    "fn.1100": "That is to say, the existence of things infinite in number is demonstrated to be impossible only inasmuch as these things are supposed to exist simultaneously; for it will presently be seen that the inadmissibility of the infinite <i>by succession</i> is by no means demonstrated.—On the various propositions here enunciated by our author, vide Aristotle, <i>Physics</i>, Book III, chaps. 4-8; <i>Metaphysics</i>, Book II, chap. 2; Book XI, chap. 10. One reserves the right to revert thereto in the notes to the introduction of the II<sup>nd</sup> part, propositions 1, 2, 3, and 26.",
    "fn.1101": "We will see that the infinite <i>in power</i> is admissible.",
    "fn.1102": "That is to say, purely immaterial and spiritual substances, such as the intelligences of the celestial spheres. It is always impossible, the author says, to admit causes ascending to infinity, whether these causes are corporeal or incorporeal.",
    "fn.1103": "That is, this succession of causes and effects takes place in nature through a necessary and <i>essential</i> chain, and it must be carefully distinguished from the accidental succession that will be discussed shortly.",
    "fn.1104": "The manuscripts bear, some <span dir=\"rtl\">פיה</span>, others <span dir=\"rtl\">לה</span>; one must unite the two readings and read <span dir=\"rtl\">לה פיה</span> (Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">לו בו</span>). There is here a slight inversion, and the true construction of this clause is as follows: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי תברהן פיה אמתנאע מא לא נהאיה̈ לה</span>, which stands for <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי תברהן אן ימתנע פיה מא לא נהאיה̈ לה</span>.",
    "fn.1105": "Vide Arist., <i>Physics</i>, III, 6: <i>ἄλλως ρἐν οὖν οὐκ ἔστιν, οὕτως δ’ἔστι τὸ ἄπειρον, δυνἀμει τε καὶ ἐπὶ καθαιρἐτει, κ. τ. λ</i>. On the infinite divisibility of continuous magnitudes, vide <i>ibid</i>., chap. 7, and Book VI, chap. 2 (cf. supra, p. 380, note 2). That which is here termed the infinite <i>in potency</i> might also be designated as infinite <i>in intensity</i>, whereas the infinite <i>in act</i> is infinite <i>extension</i>.",
    "fn.1106": "That is to say, it is a dubious subject that calls for meditation and on which opinions are divided.",
    "fn.1107": "See on these questions the introduction of the II<sup>nd</sup> part, at the 26<sup>th</sup> proposition.",
    "fn.1108": "That is to say, they do not make a distinction between the infinite <i>in act</i> and the infinite <i>in potential</i>.",
    "fn.1109": "Literally: <i>the existence of infinite things in number ranged together</i>.",
    "fn.1110": "That is to say, they do not admit more the <i>infini by succession</i> or <i>by accident</i> than the simultaneous existence of an infinite series of things.",
    "fn.1111": "That is to say: they deem them equally inadmissible, and they make no distinction, in continuous magnitude, between the infinite <i>in act</i> and the infinite <i>in potency</i>, nor, in number, between the infinite <i>simultaneous</i> and the infinite <i>by succession</i>. — All the manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ממא לא נהאיה̈</span>; a single Oxford manuscript adds <span dir=\"rtl\">להא</span>. One must write <span dir=\"rtl\">ממא לא נהאיה̈ לה</span>.",
    "fn.1112": "That is to say: by succession <i>par</i>.",
    "fn.1113": "See below, at the end of chap. LXXIV.",
    "fn.1114": "That is to say: they somehow admit as an axiom that the <i>infinite by succession</i> is impossible.",
    "fn.1115": "In other words: if it were clearly evident that one could not admit a series of infinite finite things arising and disappearing successively one after the other, this single proposition alone would suffice to immediately recognize that the world could not be eternal.",
    "fn.1116": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version generally carry <span dir=\"rtl\">חשבו</span>; one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">חשדו</span>, as the manuscripts and the edition <i>princeps</i> have it.",
    "fn.1117": "By the words: <i>and what follows</i>, the author appears to be alluding to the theory of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> on the movement of atoms; see above, III<sup>rd</sup> proposition (pages 382 and 383).",
    "fn.1118": "According to the reading of all the Arabic manuscripts, as well as of both Hebrew versions, one ought to translate: <i>what is bent</i> (or <i>broken</i>) <i>he sees as straight;</i> but one believes that the author has inadvertently inverted the words. Whilst preserving in our text the reading of the manuscripts, one has translated as if there were <span dir=\"rtl\">וירי אלמםתקים מעוגׄא</span>, which is more natural; for it is only under certain determined conditions that the broken stick can be seen as straight. Indeed, one reads in Ephodi’s commentary: <span dir=\"rtl\">ר״ל ויתחייב מזה שיראה הישר מעוקם</span> «He means: it follows (from what precedes) <i>that what is straight will be seen as bent</i>.",
    "fn.1119": "In Al-’Harizi’s version (<i>ms</i>.), these latter words are thus paraphrased: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואשר גבר על טבעו המרה הירוקה ונשתקעה בלשונו</span>, <i>and he in whose nature the yellow bile has gained ascendancy, so that it has permeated his tongue</i>. In Ibn-Tibbon’s version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ואשר בלשונו</span> are a typographical error; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">ואשר שוקה לשונו</span>, as found in the manuscripts and the edition <i>princeps</i>. In the same version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מרה אדומה</span> (bile <i>red</i>) are employed in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">מרה ירוקה</span> (bile <i>yellow</i>); for, according to the ancient physicians, the humour termed <i>yellow bile</i> is divided into five species, one of which is <i>reddish</i>. Vide R. Meir al-Dabi, <i>Shebile Emunah</i>, introduction to Book IV (Amsterdam edition, fol. 41 <i>b</i>), and cf., on the humours, supra, p. 366, note 4.",
    "fn.1120": "Literally: whether the Motécallemîn have fought for this proposition in play; i.e., whether they have made it a simple game or amusement without utility. The version of Ibn-Tibbon, which renders the verb (ضاربوا) by (הםכימו), is not exact.",
    "fn.1121": "Literally: <i>when we perceive through our senses things that overturn</i> (or <i>refute</i>) <i>what they have established</i>.",
    "fn.1122": "Literally: <i>as soon as it has been demonstrated the thing of which they claim that the testimony of reason proves</i>.",
    "fn.1123": "See above, IIIrd proposition (p. 382).",
    "fn.1124": "That is, the whiteness that one sees now is another. See the VI<sup>th</sup> proposition (p. 392).",
    "fn.1125": "That is, when it comes to things that cannot be said to be entirely beyond the reach of the senses.",
    "fn.1126": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">انتحل</span> (derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">نخلة</span> , <i>sect</i>) signifies <i>to profess a</i> <i>doctrine</i> or <i>an opinion</i>. This same verb is likewise employed in the sense of <i>attributing to oneself the words of another</i>, and it is in this sense that Ibn-Tibbon has here taken it, who paraphrased our passage in these terms: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכבר ידעת שאלו כולם דעות קדמוניות היו מתפארים בהם הםופםטאנין ואומרים שהם אמרום תחלה</span> \"Thou knowest that these are all ancient opinions, <i>of which the Sophists boasted</i>, <i>pretending that they had been the first to express them</i>.\"",
    "fn.1127": "Ibn-Tibbon renders: <span dir=\"rtl\">יספר דברים כבר ידעתם</span>; it is seen that instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">כל מא</span>, he has read <span dir=\"rtl\">כלאמא</span>, as indeed some manuscripts have it. — As for the passage of Galen to which allusion is made, it is found in the treatise entitled: <i>περὶ δυναμέων φυσικῶν</i>, book I, chap. 2 (Kühn's edit., vol. II, p. 4). Galen, in speaking of the diverse acceptations of the word <i>movement</i> (<i>κίνησις</i>), which does likewise embrace the notions of <i>change</i> and <i>transformation</i>, says that, according to general opinion, the transformation of aliments into blood is a matter perceptible to the senses of sight, taste, and touch. Then he adds: <i>ὃτι δὲ καὶ κατ’ ἀλήθειαν οὐκέτι τοῦθ’ ὁμολογοῦσιν oἱ σοφισταί. οἱ μὲν γάρ τινες αὐτῶν ἅπαντα τὰ τοιαῦτα τῶυ ἠμετέρων αἰσθήσεων ὰπάτας τινὰς ϰαἰ παραγωγὰς εἶναι νομίζουσιν, ἄλλοτε ἄλλως πασ-χουσῶν τῆς ὑποϰειμένης οὐσίας μηδἐν τούτων, οἷς ἐπονομάζεται, δεχομένης</i>.",
    "fn.1128": "That is to say, the methods of demonstration employed by the <i>Motécallemîn</i> to establish the creation of the world, as well as the existence, unity, and incorporality of God.",
    "fn.1129": "<span dir=\"rtl\">כל מנהם</span> is the same as <span dir=\"rtl\">כל ואחד מנהם</span> (a lesson found in one of the Leiden manuscripts); by these words, the author designates the various heads of school among the <i>Motécallemîn</i>.—One of the Oxford manuscripts reads <span dir=\"rtl\">במקצד כלאמהם</span>, and another (the oldest), <span dir=\"rtl\">במחץׄ כלאמהם</span>.",
    "fn.1130": "Literally: <i>which the author of this method</i>. has employed.",
    "fn.1131": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">شجع</span> designats rhymed prose; by <span dir=\"rtl\">فقرة</span> (a word which properly designats the <i>vertebrae</i>), one understands the consonances or words which present amongst themselves a certain symmetry by their form and their termination. By the verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">םגׄעת ופקרת</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(سُجِعَتْ وَفُقِرَتْ)</span> , the author means that the writings of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> were composed in rhymed prose and adorned with consonances, a manner of writing much in vogue amongst Arab authors, and which has often been imitated in the Middle Ages by Jewish authors who prided themselves on elegance. The most celebrated Hebrew compositions of this genre are those of Al-’Harizi and Immanuel. One has oneself endeavoured to convey an idea of this Arabic style in one's Essay toward a translation of the <i>Séances de Hariri</i> (Journal Asiatique, December 1834); therein it is said, for example, when speaking of a preacher:",
    "fn.1132": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">طريق</span> being of the common gender, the author put the adjectives and verbs related to it sometimes in the masculine, sometimes in the feminine, and these variations, as will be seen, often occur in a single and the same phrase. This inconsistency, which must be attributed to the author himself and not to the copyists, we have always exactly reproduced the readings of the manuscripts.",
    "fn.1133": "That is to say: <i>newly produced</i> or <i>created;</i> see above, p. 235, note 2.",
    "fn.1134": "Properly: gutta (sc. seminis).",
    "fn.1135": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קאל</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(قال)</span> , <i>he said</i>, refers to the author of this first method; the two Hebrew translators have written <span dir=\"rtl\">אמרו</span>, in the plural.",
    "fn.1136": "That is, the one who employs this method of demonstration.",
    "fn.1137": "By these last words, the author indicates what is imperfect and inconclusive about this demonstration <i>par analogie</i>. Furthermore, as Ephodi observes, it does not exclude the existence of a prime matter; it would at most establish that the world has not always been as it is, but it does not prove creation <i>ex nihilo</i>.",
    "fn.1138": "In the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon the reading is incorrect; one must read, according to the manuscripts: <span dir=\"rtl\">הנה אביו גם כן מחודש ואם אי אפשר היות אביו אלא מיצחק זקנו והנה יצחק גם כן מחודש והנה ילך וכו׳</span>.",
    "fn.1139": "That is to say: even <i>the infinite by succession</i> that the <i>Motécallemîn</i> also call <i>the infinite by accident</i>.",
    "fn.1140": "Such is the lesson of almost all Arabic manuscripts and the two Hebrew versions; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלחק</span>, <i>la vérité</i>, the oldest of the two Leiden manuscripts bears <span dir=\"rtl\">אלחד</span>, <i>la limite</i>, and this may be the original reading.",
    "fn.1141": "Literally: <i> that the world has existed after the pure and absolute non-being</i>.",
    "fn.1142": "Literally: <i>they would not be able to escape</i> (<i>from this alternative</i>) <i>of being either united or separated</i>. See the previous chapter, I<sup>st</sup> proposition.",
    "fn.1143": "See the propositions IV and VIII in the preceding chapter.",
    "fn.1144": "Since the substance, or the atom, cannot be for an instant without an accident, it follows that, if the accident is a thing <i>born</i> or newly come to be, the substance must be so as well.",
    "fn.1145": "See the previous chapter, the XIth proposition.",
    "fn.1146": "Literally: <i>which are necessary for what is not hidden from thinkers</i>; that is, that every thinker easily guesses why the three following hypotheses were necessary for the author of this method.",
    "fn.1147": "That is, the accident is a newly produced thing.",
    "fn.1148": "That is to say, the celestial sphere, which has a circular motion, has neither beginning nor end. See Aristotle, <i>Métaph.</i>, XII, 7: <i>ϰαὶ ἔστι τι ἀϵὶ ϰινούμενον ϰίνησιν ἄπαυστον, αὕτη δ’ἡ ϰύϰλῳ · ϰαὶ τοῦτο οὐ λόγῳ μόνον ἀλλ’ ἔργῳ δῆλον ὥστε ἀΐδιος ἂν εἴη ὁ πρῶτος οὺρανός</i>.",
    "fn.1149": "That is, in the elements; for everything is born and perishes, is composed and decomposed, through the mixture of the elements, which is brought about by the movement of the celestial sphere, as we have seen in chapter LXXII, p. 358 and following.",
    "fn.1150": "As Ibn-Tibbon does observe (in a note accompanying our passage in some manuscripts of the Hebrew version), there is here a superfluous repetition; for the author has already stated that, according to Aristotle, circular motion neither beginns nor perishes. According to Ibn-Tibbon, one ought to efface the words \"<i>has not come into being, and that it</i>\", so that the author would merely state that, according to Aristotle, circular motion falls into none of the categories of true accidents. Here is the note in question: <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר שמואל בן תבון נראה לי בזה המקום מותר אין צורך לו שכבר קדם לו המאמר שהתנועה הםבובית בלתי מתחדשת והטוב שנפיל מלות שהיא בלתי מחודשת ולכתוב במקום ואינה שאינה ׃</span>",
    "fn.1151": "We have seen above that the <i>Motécallemîn</i> recognize only atoms and <i>accidents</i> in any body, and that they reject the Peripatetic theory of <i>matter</i> and <i>form</i>. See the previous chapter, VIII<sup>th</sup> proposition (p. 398, note 1).",
    "fn.1152": "That is to say: from nothing <i>ex nihilo</i>.",
    "fn.1153": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">تخصيص</span> , which signifies <i>particularisation, appropriation, determination</i>, is a term whereby, as one shall presently perceive, the <i>Motécallemîn</i> designate the divine action, creating freely and <i>determining</i> each thing in the universe, without being constrained by any natural law whatsoever; it is by His sole will that God fashions each thing in a <i>particular</i> manner, all things being capable of existing otherwise than they do.",
    "fn.1154": "The subject that one must infer in is the Motécallem or the partisan of this method.",
    "fn.1155": "Cf. <i>Khozari</i>, book V, § 17, art. 3, where the same argumentation of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> is reported, applied particularly to <i>time</i>, that is, to the fixed epoch that gave birth to things. See also Ahron ben-Éli, <i>Tree of Life</i>, chapter XI, 2<sup>nd</sup> demonstration.",
    "fn.1156": "One recalls that the earth, occupying the last rank among the elements, is placed below the sphere of water. See above, p. 134, note 2, and p. 356.",
    "fn.1157": "Literally: and this proof is strengthened among them.",
    "fn.1158": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">באחד אלגׄזאיאת</span>, several manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">באחד אלגׄאיזאת</span>, that is to say: <i>by one of the things</i> <small>ADMISSIBLE</small>. The two Hebrew versions, in agreement with the reading that we have adopted, bear <span dir=\"rtl\">באחד מן הפרטים</span>.",
    "fn.1159": "The author intends to convey that this argumentation possesses two weak aspects: first, it has no other foundation than the X<sup>th</sup> proposition, which, as has been observed in the preceding chapter, often leads to absurd consequences; secondly, that which the <i>Motécallemîn</i> term <i>determination</i> is also admitted by certain philosophers, who profess the <i>eternity</i> of the world and who recognise in all particular things the effects of the universal and immutable law established by God; so that one could not thence derive a decisive proof for the dogma of Creation. Cf. the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chap. XXI.",
    "fn.1160": "See the II<sup>nd</sup> part, chap. XIX, and cf. above, pag. 413, note 1.",
    "fn.1161": "Properly: the <i>preponderance</i>; the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">رجّح</span> , whose <span dir=\"rtl\">ترجيح</span>  is the <i>nominal action</i>, means <i>to make the balance incline</i>.",
    "fn.1162": "Cf. Schmœlders, <i>Essai</i>, pp. 155 and 156: \"Possible beings can neither exist nor not exist without a distinct cause. This quality of the possible being is admitted by all; one merely disputs concerning its value, that is to say, one questions whether it be an immediate notion, or whether this assertion still stands in need of proof. Now, it is evident that one apprehends this immediately; for, reality having as much sway over a possible being before its appearance as non-reality, it is absolutely essential to suppose a <i>motive</i> which determins the existence or non-existence of that being.\"",
    "fn.1163": "That is, in the previous method, one argues by the conditions or modes of being of the universe and its parts, conditions <i>determined</i> by the Creator, whereas here one argues by the existence itself of the world, which the Creator <i>preferred</i> to non-existence.",
    "fn.1164": "The author signifies that the <i>Mutakallim</i> takes the word <i>possible</i> in an absolute sense, understanding thereby that which in reality may either be or not be, whereas the philosopher understands by <i>possible</i> that which is <i>in potency</i> and that which a necessary cause causes to pass from <i>potency</i> into <i>act</i>. Consequently, that which the philosopher designates as <i>possible</i> is likewise necessary, inasmuch as it possesses an efficient cause which is necessary. Vide the Introduction to the II<sup>nd</sup> part of the <i>Guide</i>, prop. XIX et seq.",
    "fn.1165": "That is to say: in order to be able to say that a worker or an efficient agent acting with intention has given a certain form to a being rather than another, it is first necessary that this being exists and that its existence itself is not in question.",
    "fn.1166": "Literally: <i>and that</i> (<i>only</i>) <i>the two</i> (<i>forms</i>) <i>admissible which are attributed unto it were lacking unto it before the action of him who gave the preference</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מעדומאן</span>, the manuscripts generally bear <span dir=\"rtl\">מעדומה̈</span>; one alone (one of the two Leyden manuscripts) has <span dir=\"rtl\">מעדומין</span>, which is less incorrect. This same manuscript bears, like all the others: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלגׄאיזין אלמנםובין</span>; but one must render all these words in the nominative case.",
    "fn.1167": "Literally: <i>one could in no wise form this notion concerning it;</i> that is to say: there can be no question, concerning this being, of that which has been termed <i>determination</i> or <i>preference</i>. This notion of <i>determination</i>, as the author is about to declare, can present itself only after it has been established that this being has issued forth from absolute nothingness, which is precisely the question one seeks to resolve, so that one would here encounter a <i>begging of the question</i>.",
    "fn.1168": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read <span dir=\"rtl\">ההוראה בשהוא</span>; one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">ההודאה בשהוא</span>.",
    "fn.1169": "That is, for something purely subjective, which was only within the domain of the idea and thought and outside the questions related to being in general and the laws of nature.",
    "fn.1170": "That is to say: to beings as they exist in reality and to what falls within the domain of intelligence.",
    "fn.1171": "That is, the opinions of the Motécallemîn.",
    "fn.1172": "We have deemed it necessary to reproduce exactly in our text the lesson of all manuscripts, although it is incorrect; one should read: <span dir=\"rtl\">אן הדׄה אלטריק אלתי טׄן בהא</span>. Cf. above, p. 421, note 1.",
    "fn.1173": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תקדיר</span>, <i>supposer</i>, some manuscripts read <span dir=\"rtl\">תקריר</span> (with <i>rêsch</i>), <i>confirm;</i> similarly, several manuscripts of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, as well as the <i>princeps</i> edition, read <span dir=\"rtl\">מישב</span> (i.e., <span dir=\"rtl\">מִיֵּשֵּׁב</span>), instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מִשֵּׁעֵר</span>.",
    "fn.1174": "That is to say: the principle of <i>admissibility</i>, posited by the X<sup>th</sup> proposition.",
    "fn.1175": "See the preceding chapter, XI<sup>e</sup> proposition.",
    "fn.1176": "By <i>Syrians</i>, the author here signifies the ancient doctors of the Talmudic epoch, who spake a Chaldean or Syriac dialect; cf. above, p. 91, note 1. The proverb here cited is much in use among the Talmudists; vide, for example, Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Sukkah</i>, fol. 26 <i>a</i>. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">הערביים</span> (the <i>Arabs</i>), in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, is a misprint; the manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">הארמיים</span> (the <i>Syrians</i>).",
    "fn.1177": "The obscure and dubious manner in which Aristotle expresses himself concerning the immortality of the intelligent part of the soul (vide treatise <i>De Anima</i>, I, 4; II, 2; III, 5; <i>Metaph.</i>, XII, 3) has given rise among the Arabian philosophers to divergent opinions upon this important question; the majority deny the individual permanence of the soul, or attribute immortality solely to those who have succeeded in elevating themselves to the degree of the <i>acquired intellect</i>. Vide mine article <i>Ibn-Rushd</i>, in the <i>Dict. des sc. philos.</i>, t. III, p. 170; cf. above, p. 146, note 2, and p. 328, note 4.",
    "fn.1178": "Literally: <i>to bring out the doubt for the adversary</i>; that is, to show what is doubtful and contradictory in the opinion of the philosopher who admits both the eternity of the world and the immortality of the soul.",
    "fn.1179": "Literally: this would not be inconsistent (i.e., there would be no contradiction) only if the adversary also granted to the one who raises the objection what the latter has imagined concerning the words (i.e., the words of the philosopher) on the permanence of souls.",
    "fn.1180": "As Moses of Narbonne observes, the author alludes to Ibn-Sînâ. This philosopher, who admits the individual permanence of souls after death, perceives the soul as a <i>substance</i> entirely independent of the body, yet one to which neither the category of <i>place</i> or of the <i>where</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">الاين</span> , <i>τὸ ποί</i>), nor that of <i>situation</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">الوضع</span> , <i>τὸ ϰεîσθαι</i>) applies. See the abridgement of Ibn-Sînâ’s <i>Physics</i>, in <i>Schahrestâni</i>, p. 419 (Ger. transl., vol. II, p. 318). Ibn-Sînâ believed himself justified in contending that, as souls possess neither <i>place</i> nor <i>situation</i>, nought opposes their number being infinite. Ibn-Roschd, rising against those who pretended to demonstrate the dogma of Creation by means of the permanence of souls, as admitted by the philosophers, first refuses to admit with Ibn-Sînâ the existence of these individual substances, pure forms devoid of matter; for he adopts concerning the permanence of the soul the opinion of Ibn-Bâdja, of whom mention shall presently be made. Then, speaking of Ibn-Sînâ’s solution, to which Maimonides here alludes, he expresses himself in these terms (<i>Destr. of the Destruction</i>, I<sup>st</sup> question, Heb. vers.): <span dir=\"rtl\">ועוד המנע מה שאין תכלית לו על מה שהוא נמצא בפעל שרש ידוע מדעות הפלוםופים בין שהיו גשמים או בלתי גשמים ולא ידענו אחד יפריש בין מה שיש לו מצב ומה שאין לו מצב בזה הענין אלא אבן םינא לבד <sup>.....</sup> והוא מהבלי הטפלות בי הפלוםופים ירחיקו מציאות מה שאין תבלית לו בפעל בין שהיה גשם או בלתי גשם לפי שיתחייב ממנו שיהיה מה שאין תבלית לו יותר ממה שאין תכלית לו ואולי אבן םינא אמנם כיון בו לפיים ההמון במה שהורגלו לשמעו מענין הנפש וכו׳</span> “Furthermore, the impossibility of the <i>infinite</i> for that which exists <i>in actuality</i> is a principle well known, taught by the philosophers, whether it concerns bodies or incorporeal entities. One knows of none who has, in this regard, drawn a distinction between that which possesses <i>situation</i> and that which possesses it not, save only Ibn-Sînâ..… But these are vain puerilities; for the philosophers reject the existence of the <i>infinite in actuality</i>, both for that which is corporeal and for that which is incorporeal, inasmuch as it would thence result that there is an infinite greater than another infinite. Perchance Ibn-Sînâ did but aim to accommodate himself to the multitude in that which they were accustomed to hear said concerning the soul, &c.”",
    "fn.1181": "The author is addressing his disciple, to whom he dedicated this work, here as in many other passages.",
    "fn.1182": "That is, the abstract beings entirely separated from corporality, in Greek τὰ ϰεχωρισμένα (Arist., treatise on the Soul, book III, chapter 7). Cf. above, p. 373 and 374.",
    "fn.1183": "See the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, introduction, XVI<sup>th</sup> proposition, and cf. Abridgement of the Talmud, treatise <i>Yessôdé ha-Tôrâ</i>, chap. II, § 5. The proposition in question applies not only <i>to the intelligences of the spheres</i>, but likewise to the acquired intellect, which, as the author has stated, is by no means <i>a faculty within a body</i>, and which is the sole thing remaining of the human soul after death. See above, at the close of chap. LXXII (p. 373), and cf. p. 328, note 4.",
    "fn.1184": "That is to say: the aggregate of all souls forms but a single <i>unity</i>. This doctrine of the <i>unity of souls</i> was professed by Abou-Becr ibn-al-Çâyeg, or Ibn-Bâdja, in his <i>Epistle of Farewell</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(رسالة الوداع)</span> and in other writings. See the commentary of Moses of Narbonne upon our passage, and Ibn-Falaquéra, <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 65. — Ibn-Roschd proclaims the same doctrine: “Zeid and ‘Amr,” he states (<i>l. c.</i>), “are numerically two, yet they are <i>one</i> by their form, which is the <i>soul;</i> now, were the souls of Zeid and ‘Amr numerically distinct, as are their persons, their souls would be <i>two</i> in number, but a <i>unity</i> by form, such that the soul would possess a soul. Seeing then that the soul of Zeid and ‘Amr is necessarily <i>one</i> by form, and that which is <i>one</i> by form receives numerical multiplicity or division solely through matter, it thence follows that, if souls perish not with bodies, they necessarily form, after having separated themselves from bodies, a numerical <i>unity</i>.” Developed by Ibn-Roschd in several of his writings, this doctrine caused a great sensation amongst Christian theologians of the XIII<sup>th</sup> century; Albert the Great refuted it in a particular treatise entitled: Libellus contra eos qui dicunt quod post separationem ex omnibus animabus non remanet nisi inlellectus unus et anima una (see <i>Alberti Opera</i>, vol. V, p. 218 et seq., Jammy’s edition).",
    "fn.1185": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تجرد</span>, which signifies <i>to divest oneself of one's garments</i>, here bears the sense of <i>to apply oneself with ardour to a matter;</i> the Hebrew verb <span dir=\"rtl\">נחלץ</span>, which the two Hebrew versions here employ, corresponds precisely to the Arabic verb, and Ibn-Falaquéra explains it by these words: <span dir=\"rtl\">בלומר שהכין נפשו כמי שחולץ בגדיו לעשות דבר בזריזות</span>. See the Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, chap. I (page 142).",
    "fn.1186": "The text here contains, twice in succession, a grammatical solecism. All manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">כל שכׄץ ····· גיר מתנאהיה̈</span>; the words <span dir=\"rtl\">בל שכׄץ</span>, <i>every individual</i>, are here taken in the sense of <i>the aggregate of all individuals</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's version reproduces the same solecism; it bears <span dir=\"rtl\">בל איש ····· אין תכלית להם</span>. Al-’Harizi deemed it better to write <span dir=\"rtl\">אין לו תכלית</span>, so that one should be obliged to translate: <i>every individual, etc. ..… is infinite</i>, which is evidently a contrary sense.",
    "fn.1187": "That is: they show in this way that the one who professes the eternity of the world necessarily arrives at the result that there are infinite things greater than others, which is absurd.",
    "fn.1188": "That is to say: they apply the same reasoning to the celestial sphere, which, from two different and earlier epochs, will have completed obviously more numerous revolutions in one case than in the other, and yet both are infinite. Cf. <i>Khozari</i>, book V, §18, article 1<sup>st</sup>.",
    "fn.1189": "The slower revolutions are obviously fewer in number than the faster ones, and yet both kinds would be infinite, in the hypothesis of the eternity of the world. Cf. <i>Khozari, l. c</i>.",
    "fn.1190": "That is to say: they still apply the same reasoning to accidents of all kinds, occurring one after the other, and which, in the hypothesis of the eternity of the world, would form an infinite series.",
    "fn.1191": "The author's observation applies generally to the several cases which have just been enumerated; he intends to convey that, both in the series of creatures of the same species and in the series of the revolutions of the spheres and in that of accidents, and, generally, in the infinite <i>by succession</i> or <i>by accident</i>, not only are all individualities and all particular manifestations which have ceased to exist counted as actually subsisting, but one also conceives them as things well-delimited, so as to be capable of being counted as an infinite multitude of <i>units</i> within this infinite series one seeks to establish. By the words <i>and as if they were delimited things</i>, the author particularly indicates the revolutions of the celestial spheres, which the <i>Motécallemîn</i> likewise consider as a numerical multitude composed of well-determined units, whereas in reality circular motion possesses neither beginning nor end, and, consequently, the movements of the spheres cannot possibly be numbered. Cf. above, page 425, note 1. As for the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ذات بداة</span>, which signify literally <i>having a beginning</i>, and which one has rendered by <i>delimited things</i>, thus does the author himself explain them in his letter to Samuel ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">דׄאת בדאה̈ מענאהא אנהם יטׄנון אמורא להא אבתדא מחדוד לאן כל מא הו מוגׄוד בגׄמלתה מחדוד אלטרפין הו אלדׄי יקאל פיה אנה דׄאת בדאה̈ והו אלדׄי ימכן אן יזאד עליה או ינקץ מנה אמא מא יחדתׄ אולא אולא ולים פיה מבדא מחדוד מתׄל אלחרכאת אלדורيه̈ פלא יתצור פיה זיאדה̈ ולא נקצאן</span> “The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ذات بداة</span> signify that they conceive therein things having a determined beginning; for, whatsoever exists as an aggregate limited at both extremities, that is called <span dir=\"rtl\">ذات بداة</span>, and that it is which can be augmented or diminished. But, in that which arises gradually and possesses no determined beginning, as, for example, the circular movements, one cannot possibly conceive either augmentation or diminution.” It was upon the author's own counsel that Ibn-Tibbon rendered the words in question by <span dir=\"rtl\">דברים שיש להם התחלה מםוימת</span>.",
    "fn.1192": "That is to say: they add to this imaginary numerical multitude, or subtract from it, in order to show that, in the hypothesis of the eternity of the world, one would arrive at the result that there are two or several <i>infinis</i> greater than one another.",
    "fn.1193": "Literally: <i>struck upon the brain</i> or <i>upon the head</i>. Thus has the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">דמג</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(دمغ)</span> been explained by the author himself, in his letter to Samuel ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">ם<span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> תני ען מעני הדׄה <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> לפטׄה̈ אעני דמג והו פי נםכׄתהם אדמג והו כׄטא אנמא אלצחיח דמג אעני צׄרבהא פי דמאגהא מתׄל קולהם ז״ל מחו לה אמוחא כדלך תקול <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span> ערב למן צׄרב שכׄצא עלי ראםה פאהלכה דמג פלאן פלאנא פבאן גרצׄי אן אבא נצר ברהן עלי פםאד תלך <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span> מקדמה̈ אלתי אתכׄדׄוהא <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span> מתכלמון אצלא כבירא</span> “Thou hast questioned me concerning the meaning of this word, namely <span dir=\"rtl\">دماغ</span>; for in their copy (that is to say, in that of the Jews of Provence) there was <span dir=\"rtl\">אדםג</span>, which is an error. The correct reading is <span dir=\"rtl\">דמג</span>, that is to say: <i>he struck it upon its brain</i>, an expression analogous to that of the doctors: <span dir=\"rtl\">מחו לה אמוחא</span>. It is thus that the Arabs employ the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">دمغ</span>, with the direct regimen, when speaking of one who has struck a person upon the head, so as to cause his demise. I wished thereby to convey that Abou-Naçr has demonstrated the fallacy of that proposition which the <i>Motécallemîn</i> have adopted as a significant principle.” — Of eight manuscripts which one has consulted, two only bear <span dir=\"rtl\">דמג</span>; the six others possess the reading <span dir=\"rtl\">אדםג</span>, which the author himself pronounces erroneous. As for the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">מחו לה אמוחא</span>, perfectly analogous to the Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">دمغ</span>, the Talmudists likewise employ it when speaking of a proposition whose nullity has been demonstrated. See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Meghillâ</i>, fol. 19 <i>b;</i> cf. the treatise <i>Sopherîm</i>, chap. 2, § 11.",
    "fn.1194": "Literally: <i>and he has revealed the places of error in all their particulars, as thou wilt find it clear and evident, with an examination devoid of partiality, etc.</i> The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تعصب</span> signifies <i>to defend a thing with ardour, to become heated for or against an opinion, to display partiality</i>. Cf. the II<sup>nd</sup> part, towards the end of chapter XXII: <span dir=\"rtl\">בתׄיר מן אלמתעצבין</span> (Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">הרבה מן העוזרים לאוהביהם</span>); by these words Maimonides intends, as he states in his letter to Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלו המתחזקים לקיים דברי הפלוםופים בתאותם</span>, <i>those who strive to passionately support the sayings of the philosophers</i>.",
    "fn.1195": "One possesses no information regarding this work of Al-Farâbi, and already Moses of Narbonne, in the XIV<sup>th</sup> century, had been unable to procure it; this commentator opines that it is from Al-Farâbi’s work that Ibn-Roschd borrowed the principal arguments he adduces against the <i>Motécallemîn</i> concerning the question which occupies one here. Vide <i>Destr. de la Destruction</i>, quest. I<sup>st</sup> and III<sup>rd</sup>, and cf. the commentary of Moses of Narbonne upon our passage. The principal argument is indicated by our author himself in these words: <i>and as if they were delimited things</i>. See the note upon these words and cf. the commentary of Éphodi upon the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ובבר הבה</span>.",
    "fn.1196": "It is apparent that the proofs adduced by the <i>Motécallemîn</i> to establish the existence of God are of the kind that have been denominated <i>physical proofs</i>. It might appear that the methods enumerated in this chapter, and notably the I<sup>st</sup> and II<sup>nd</sup>, are founded upon the <i>principle of causality</i>; yet, one has observed that the <i>Motécallemîn</i> reject this principle in an absolute manner. But the contradiction is merely apparent. For the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, indeed, the proof of the Creator's existence would result immediately from every thing <i>newly produced</i> or <i>created</i>, which, according to them, requires the direct action of the divinity to pass from non-being to being; and if, in order to reach the Creator, they cause one to ascend through a series of creations that appear to produce one another, this is only to demonstrate that the series of transformations would be infinite were one not to halt at a first creation, and that those very individuals who admit in nature a series of causes and effects are compelled to halt at a first <i>created thing</i> which possesses no other cause than the Creator's will. For them, nothing is <i>caused</i>, but everything in nature is <i>made</i> or <i>created;</i> thus, they carefully avoid, in their arguments, employing the word <i>cause</i> and refuse to adopt the denomination of <i>first cause</i> used by the philosophers to designate God. Vide above, at the commencement of chapter LXIX. — Several of the methods enumerated in this chapter have been adopted in substance by the Jewish theologians of the Middle Ages; cf. Saadia, <i>Beliefs and Opinions</i>, book I, chap. 1; Ba’hya, <i>Duties of the Hearts</i>, book I, chap. 5. The Karaïtes, especially, imitated on this point, as on many others, the arguments of the Arabic <i>kalām</i>. Vide Juda ha-Lévi, <i>Khozari</i>, book V, § 18.",
    "fn.1197": "It will be seen from what follows that these two methods (to which three others are likewise joined) are <i>indirect;</i> for they demonstrate the unity of God by showing that the supposition of the contrary, or of dualism, leads to the impossible or to the absurd. It is difficult to render exactly the two Arabic terms employed by the <i>Motécallemîn</i> to designate these two methods; the two words are <i>noms d’action</i> of the sixth form, indicating reciprocity. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تمانع</span> signifies <i>to prevent one another, to be an obstacle to one another;</i> Ibn-Tibbon doubtless attached the same meaning to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">המנעות</span> which he employs in his version, whilst Al-’Harizi makes a mistranslation by rendering it: <span dir=\"rtl\">דרך הנמנע</span>, <i>the method <span dir=\"rtl\">of</span> the impossible</i>. Ba’hya, who provides a combined demonstration of the first and fifth methods of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> concerning the unity of God, likewise employs the word <span dir=\"rtl\">تهازع</span> therein in the sense which one has just indicated; vide <i>Duties of the Hearts</i>, book I, ch. 7 (seventh demonstration), where one reads, in the Arabic original: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולו גׄאז אן יכון אכתׄר מן ואחד לאמכן אן יכון בינהם תמאנע ואכׄתלאף פי כׄלקה̈ אלמכׄלוקין</span> (the Hebrew version bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלו היה אפשר להיות יותר מאחד היה אפשר שתהיה ביניהם מחלוקת בבריאת הברואים</span>). — The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تغاير</span> signifies <i>to differ reciprocally</i>, and expresses the <i>non-identity</i> of two things. In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">דרך ההמנעות ודרך ההשתנות והחלוק</span>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">והחלוק</span> is found neither in the manuscripts nor in the editio <i>princeps</i>. Some manuscripts bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">ודרך ההשתנות ודרך החלוף והחלוק</span>; it is evident that Ibn-Tibbon’s version rendered the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתגאיר</span> in two different ways, and that the copyists united the two translations, one of which was probably written in the margin.",
    "fn.1198": "See chapter LXXIII, IVth proposition (pp. 385 and 386).",
    "fn.1199": "See <i> ibid.</i>, VI<sup>th</sup> proposition (p. 389 and following).",
    "fn.1200": "See ibid., VIIth proposition, to which, as we have just seen, the IVth proposition is linked.",
    "fn.1201": "In all manuscripts, <span dir=\"rtl\">מוצׄועאת</span>, in the feminine plural form with a neutral meaning; in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">נושא</span>; but the mss. and the edit. <i>princeps</i> have <span dir=\"rtl\">נושאי</span>, in the plural.",
    "fn.1202": "On the two <i>substances</i>, see chap. LXXII, p. 356 and following; the author wants to show here that this I<sup>deal</sup> method is based on the doctrine of atoms and has no value for the philosopher who recognizes as principles of the universe two substances, that of the elements and that of the celestial spheres or the <i>ether</i>.",
    "fn.1203": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">هيولى</span> , formed from the Greek <i>ὕλη</i>, primarily designates sublunar matter, or that of the four elements, opposed to the <i>cinquième corps</i>.",
    "fn.1204": "The name <span dir=\"rtl\">ثنوية</span> (dualists), amongst Arabic authors, embraces the different sects which admitted two sovereign, eternal, and necessary principles, entirely independent one of the other, that of light and that of darkness, and which distinguished themselves from the <i>Magi</i> or disciples of Zoroaster, in that these latter <i>subordinated</i> the principle of darkness to that of light. Vide Schahrestâni, p. 188 et seq. (German trans., vol. I, p. 285 et seq.). The author signifies: If one were to maintain that the two matters are governed by two intelligences or two divinities, entirely independent one of the other, like the two principles admitted by the <i>dualists</i>.",
    "fn.1205": "That is to say: it would not result in the difficulty pointed out by the <i>Motécallemîn</i> atomists; for the two gods could very well act alongside one another without interfering with each other.",
    "fn.1206": "Literally: <i>and there is no imperfection in the craftsman because he has no power over the impossible</i>.",
    "fn.1207": "That is to say: The <i>Motécallemîn</i> perceived that this method held no value for the philosopher who recognised matter and form as the principles of all things, and who distinguished between the matter of the elements and that of the celestial spheres; although for themselves this method possessed something eminently plausible, and they must doubtless have been led thereto by the hypothesis of atoms, according to which the entire universe is composed of indivisible particles, completely similar and susceptible of the same accidents.",
    "fn.1208": "The author does not express himself explicitly here, nor does he complete the explanation of this method; but, as will be seen, he designates it as <i>a philosophical and demonstrative method</i>, promising to expound its meaning later. The passage to which allusion is made is found in chapter I of the second part, where the author expresses himself in these terms: «There is yet another method for demonstrating incorporeality and establishing the unity (of God): Namely, that if there were two gods, it would necessarily follow that they must possess something belonging to them in common — to wit, that by which each of the two might merit to be (called) <i>God</i> —, and some other thing equally necessary whereby their reciprocal distinction <span dir=\"rtl\">(التباين)</span> might occur, and whereby they might be <i>two;</i> whether each of the two possessed something which the other did not, and then each of the two would be composed of two things, and neither of them would be a <i>first cause</i> nor a <i>necessary being</i> in itself, but each would have causes, as has been set forth in the nineteenth proposition (of the philosophers); or whether the distinctive thing were found in but one of the two, and then that which possessed both things would not be a necessary being in itself.» Cf. the introduction to the second part, propositions XIX to XXI.",
    "fn.1209": "That is, <i>provided that we complete its explanation</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">חחבע</span> , two manuscripts from Oxford have <span dir=\"rtl\">תתׄבת</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">تَشَبَّثَ</span> , that is, provided that we have well <i>established</i>.",
    "fn.1210": "The author means: If it is true that on one hand this method might even be suitable for philosophers, on the other hand it could not be suitable for the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, who admit attributes; for, in the system of attributes, it could be possible that there were two gods with different attributes.",
    "fn.1211": "The optative <span dir=\"rtl\">תעאלי</span> (<i>may He be exalted!</i>) is connected to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלקדים</span>, <i>the Eternal One</i>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">ענדה</span> refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">כל מן יעתקד</span>; the reading of our text is that of all manuscripts, and the inversion it contains has been reproduced by Al-’Harizi, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">שהקדמון אצלו יתברך</span>, whereas Ibn-Tibbon's version states: <span dir=\"rtl\">שהקרמון יתעלה אצלו</span>.",
    "fn.1212": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מתגאירה̈</span>, one of the Leiden manuscripts reads <span dir=\"rtl\">מתנאהיה̈</span>, i.e., <i>finies</i> or <i>having a term</i>; similarly Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">בעלי תכלית</span>, which does not convey a suitable meaning.",
    "fn.1213": "Cf. above, chap. LIII, p. 208 and following. — The reading we have adopted agrees with Ibn-Tibbon's version; in most manuscripts one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומעני אלעלם גיר מעני אלקדרה̈ ענדה גיר מעני אלאראדה̈</span>, and in one of the Leiden manuscripts: <span dir=\"rtl\">וגיר מעני אלאראדה̈</span>; similarly Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">וענין המדע זולת ענין היכולת אצלו וזולת ענין החפץ</span>.",
    "fn.1214": "That is to say: of the system of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלטריק</span> (in the singular), which is found in all manuscripts, appears to one to refer here to the <i>system</i> of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> in general; Ibn-Tibbon rendered <span dir=\"rtl\">הדרבים</span> (in the plural), a word which in the editions has been altered to <span dir=\"rtl\">הדברים</span>. The author means that this III<sup>rd</sup> method necessitates a new hypothesis, which is not found amongst the propositions of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> enumerated above.",
    "fn.1215": "The two Hebrew versions have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלו ההקדמות</span>, in the plural, and similarly one of the Leiden manuscripts, <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמקדמאת</span>; all the other manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמקדמה̈</span>, in the singular. Cf. the Commentary of Ibn-Kaspi, p. 84, and the note, p. 86.",
    "fn.1216": "Literally: <i>but whose conception is such as you see</i>, that is, which is of such a nature that one cannot conceive it. The version of Ibn-Tibbon adds the word <span dir=\"rtl\">far</span>, <i>unlikely, difficult;</i> this reading is found only in one of the Leiden manuscripts, which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">form in the beginning</span>.",
    "fn.1217": "That is to say: this divine will, possessing no <i>substratum</i>, can be nought but a cause of absolute simplicity and is incapable of producing two disparate effects; consequently, it cannot appertain to two gods. This hypothesis of <i>will without substratum</i> bears resemblance to another hypothesis, equally elusive, which has been discussed above; one refers to the <i>accident without substratum</i> which, according to certain <i>Motécallemîn</i>, God would create to demolish the world. See chapter LXXIII, VI<sup>th</sup> proposition (page 391).",
    "fn.1218": "See chapter LXXIV, VII<sup>th</sup> method (page 432).",
    "fn.1219": "Literally: <i>and according to whoever admits it by opinion, doubts arise in his regard that cannot be recounted</i>. In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לא תנחצר</span> are inaccurately rendered as <span dir=\"rtl\">שאי אפשר לדחותם</span>, <i>that it is impossible to repel</i> or <i>to refute;</i> Al-’Harizi says more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא יםפרו</span>.",
    "fn.1220": "That is: the action that appears in the entirety of the universe generally proves that there is an agent, but one cannot demonstrate by this <i>action</i> that there are multiple agents of a fixed number, whatever that number may be. Consequently, the plurality in God being indemonstrable, it follows that God is <i>one</i>.",
    "fn.1221": "Literally: <i>and it is possible that He</i> (<i>God</i>) <i>be</i> <small>ONE</small>, <i>and it is possible</i> (<i>also</i>) <i>that He be</i> <small>MANY</small>; that is to say: naught hinders one from admitting, generally, the existence of <i>several</i> gods, albeit one cannot fix their number.",
    "fn.1222": "That is, the author of this method.",
    "fn.1223": "The divinity itself is what it is; unity or plurality, it is the necessary <i>being</i>. If we see in it the <i>possibility</i> of being of such or such a determined number, this possibility is only in our thought and does not affect the divine essence.",
    "fn.1224": "Literally: <i>And perchance, just as Christians believe that He is</i> <small>THREE</small>, <i>without this being so, so do we believe that He is</i> <small>ONE</small>, <i>without the matter being thus;</i> that is to say: the argumentation in question being insufficient to establish the <i>non-plurality</i> of God, it might be that our belief is no truer than that of the Christians, and that God be neither <i>one</i> nor <i>three</i>, but some plurality whatsoever. — In Ibn-Tibbon’s version, one must read: <span dir=\"rtl\">שהם שלשה ואינו בן</span>, as is found in the manuscripts and the <i>editio princeps;</i> the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ואינו בן</span> are absent from our editions.",
    "fn.1225": "That is to say, for one who has studied the rules of demonstration well and does not make false syllogisms.",
    "fn.1226": "Indeed, these two methods (the first and the fifth) have been conflated into one by several Jewish theologians who have borrowed from the Motécallemîn; see Saadia, Croyances et Opinions, book II, at the beginning of chapter 3; Ba’hya, Devoirs des cœurs, book I, chapter 7, 7th demonstration.",
    "fn.1227": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מאן</span>  (mss. <span dir=\"rtl\">מםאויא</span>), one of the Leiden manuscripts has <span dir=\"rtl\">מםתויא</span>.",
    "fn.1228": "Literally: <i>for it would be a necessity of their existence that they were two;</i> that is to say: the duality would be in the very nature of divinity and would form an essential condition of the divine being, so that the participation of the two gods in the work of creation could not be attributed to a <i>need</i> reciprocal, but would be a true <i>necessity</i>.",
    "fn.1229": "See Ahron ben-Élie, <span dir=\"rtl\">עץ חיים</span> or <i>Tree of Life</i>, chapter LXIV (page 78 of the Leipzig edition); this author endeavours to respond to the objection here raised by Maimonides against this V<sup>th</sup> method, by demonstrating that that which one of the two gods could not perform would constitute in him a veritable <i>impotence</i> and could not be confounded with that which is genuinely and objectively <i>impossible</i>, as, for example, the conjoining of contraries within the same subject.",
    "fn.1230": "Literally: <i>he who associates</i>, that is, he who gives God an associate. Cf. above, p. 239, note 1.",
    "fn.1231": "Literally: <i>for their being necessary</i> (<i>wants</i>) <i>that they be two</i>.",
    "fn.1232": "Literally: <i>Already some of them had become so fatigued by the artifices that they said the unity is accepted religiously;</i> that is, several <i>Motécallemîn</i>, fatigued by inventing all sorts of subtleties to establish the unity of God by artificial demonstrations, completely renounced demonstrating it and accepted it as a religious dogma.",
    "fn.1233": "Literally: <i>distant from the acceptance of sophism</i>.",
    "fn.1234": "Literally: <i>did not leave</i> (or <i>conceded</i>) <i>any established</i> (or <i>fixed</i> nature to the being.",
    "fn.1235": "Literally: <i>no natural right disposition</i>.",
    "fn.1236": "Literally: <i>There is no</i> (<i>cause for</i>) <i>complaint save to God and to those amongst intelligent men who possess justice</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">שכיה̈</span>, some manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">שכוה̈</span>. In Ibn-Tibbon’s version, one must read: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא לאל ולמודים על האמת</span>; some editions incorrectly present: <span dir=\"rtl\">לאלו למודים</span>, or <span dir=\"rtl\">לאלו המודים</span>.",
    "fn.1237": "That is to say: the body is necessarily something <i>composé</i> and cannot form an <i>unité</i> absolute; consequently, God, who is <i>un</i>, cannot be a body.",
    "fn.1238": "In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, one should read, according to the mss. and the ed., <i>princeps</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">וזאת הרכבה ויבאר המנע ההרכבה</span>; the editions generally carry <span dir=\"rtl\">וזאת ההרבבה תבאר ובו׳</span>.",
    "fn.1239": "As seen above, the <i>Motécallemîn</i> did not admit the ideas of <i>matter</i> and <i>form</i>. See p. 398, note 1.",
    "fn.1240": "More literally: <i>it would be indispensable for the idea of divinity and its reality that that which constitutes it should be the aggregate of the substances of this body, meaning each of its atoms, or else that it should be a single one of the atoms of this body which constitutes it</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">למעני</span>, most manuscripts present <span dir=\"rtl\">מעני</span>; yet the two Hebrew versions likewise exhibit <span dir=\"rtl\">לענין</span>. It would have been more regular to say: <span dir=\"rtl\">פלא יכׄלו מעני ····· מן אן יבון</span>; the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">מן</span> has been supplied by the two Hebrew translators, who rendered it by <span dir=\"rtl\">מבלתי</span>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אן יכון יקום בה גׄמלה̈</span> <small>(in which</small> the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">בה</span> refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">מעני</span>) are rendered, in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, by <span dir=\"rtl\">מבלתי שיתוקן בו שתשלם מציאותו בכלל וכו׳</span>. It is evident that one has here confounded two distinct translations of the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">יקום בה</span>; for, indeed, several manuscripts present <span dir=\"rtl\">מבלתי שיתוקן בו כלל וכו׳</span>, and in the margin one finds the <i>variant</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">שתשלם מציאותו (בבלל)</span>. — To comprehend the import of this passage, one must recall the V<sup>th</sup> proposition of the <i>Motecallemin</i>, according to which the accident or quality pertains not to the whole of the body, but to each of its atoms, or indeed to one amongst them, as, for instance, the accident of the soul, or that of intelligence, or that of knowledge (vide supra, pp. 387 and 388); if, therefore, God were a body, and consequently composed of atoms, it would be requisite for the idea or quality of <i>divinity</i> to reside in each of the atoms of the divine body, or else for it to reside in one of its atoms, as is the case, for example, with the accident of the <i>soul</i> or that of <i>intelligence</i>, which, according to the <i>Motecallemin</i>, resides in one of the atoms of the <i>animated</i> or <i>intelligent</i> body.",
    "fn.1241": "The text literally says: <i>but if one were to say etc</i>. The phrase is elliptical and one must infer: <i>what would they say?</i> The meaning is: what would they have to respond, if the one who maintains the corporeity of God were to object, etc.? Cf. Silv. de Sacy, <i>Arabic Grammar</i> (2<sup>nd</sup> ed.), vol. II, p. 464, n° 838.",
    "fn.1242": "We have seen that the <i>Maimonideans</i> themselves, even as they maintain that nothing is impossible provided one can form an idea of it, nonetheless acknowledge that there are certain <i>presumptions</i> or <i>false ideas</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">delusions</span> that are absolutely inadmissible. See above, p. 411 and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.1243": "That is to say: You, the <i>Moteccallem</i>, imagine also that the celestial sphere is composed of atoms and susceptible to division to infinity; but the philosopher declares that this is an error and a vain imagination, and that you judge thus only by establishing an analogy between the celestial sphere, inaccessible to our senses, and the bodies that are near us.",
    "fn.1244": "Literally: <i>you would be refuting yourself;</i> that is to say, these words would contain within themselves a contradiction.",
    "fn.1245": "That is, as soon as one admits that God is a body, all bodies being similar in terms of corporality, it would follow that God created his likeness, i.e., a being <i>necessary</i> like him, which is absurd. Cf. Ahron ben-Eli, <i>Arbre de la vie</i>, chap. XV (pag. 43), the 7<sup>th</sup> demonstration of incorporality.",
    "fn.1246": "Literally: <i>is pierced</i> or <i>defective</i>.",
    "fn.1247": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version generally carry <span dir=\"rtl\">the divine</span>, which would be an adjective of <span dir=\"rtl\">things</span>; but one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">God</span>, as a vocative, as the mss. and the ed. <i>princeps</i> indeed have.",
    "fn.1248": "Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">יותר נאותים</span>, <i>more suitable;</i> this translator has taken the comparative <span dir=\"rtl\">أَشْكَلُ</span> in the sense of <i>decentior, venustior</i>. Al-’Harizi has <span dir=\"rtl\">יותר משובש</span>, <i>more confused</i>, or better <i>more obscure, more profound</i>. The author certainly means that the second refutation is more difficult to understand.",
    "fn.1249": "That is to say, to sublunar bodies, composed of the four elements, and whose matter (<i>ὕλη</i>) is different from that of celestial spheres. See above, p. 356 and following, and cf. p. 442, note 2.",
    "fn.1250": "Literally: <i>for this matter is not that matter, nor these forms are not that form;</i> that is, the matter and form of sublunar things are not the same as those of celestial spheres.",
    "fn.1251": "Two of the best Oxford manuscripts (codd. Poc. 212 and 345) bear very distinctly <span dir=\"rtl\">אלארואתׄ</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">الأرواث</span>, plural of <span dir=\"rtl\">רَوْثُ</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">רَوْثَةُ</span>, <i>horse-dung;</i> this reading is confirmed by Ibn-Tibbon’s version, which, in most manuscripts and in the <i>editio princeps</i>, has <span dir=\"rtl\">גשם הצואות</span> (corpus stercorum), and it is erroneously that in most editions <span dir=\"rtl\">הנצוצות</span> has been printed. Some manuscripts of this version have <span dir=\"rtl\">גשם הצמחים</span>, and likewise the ancient Latin version (Paris, 1520, in-folio): corpus plantæ. Several Arabic manuscripts present <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאדואת</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאדׄואת</span> (with <i>daleth</i>), a word which offers no intelligible meaning. Only one of the Oxford manuscripts (No. 359 of Uri’s Catalogue) bears <span dir=\"rtl\">אלנוריאת</span>, a word whose sense is not clear, but which might well bear some relation to the Hebrew word <span dir=\"rtl\">הנצוצות</span>. — It is doubtless the singularity of the expression chosen by the author which has given rise to the various readings presented by the manuscripts of the Arabic original and those of Ibn-Tibbon’s version. The author intends to convey that the proponents of God’s corporeality establish between the body of God and that of even the most elevated things of creation, such as the <i>created light</i> and the celestial spheres, a distance as vast as that existing between the resplendent orb of the sun and <i>dung</i> or the most abject things of the earth.",
    "fn.1252": "See above, chapter LXIV, page 286, note 3.",
    "fn.1253": "That is to say: God could not thus be <i>determined</i> save by a being external to Himself, so that there would exist a being anterior to God and superior to Him, which is an absurdity. Cf. Ahron ben-Eliya, <i>Tree of Life</i>, chap. XV (p. 43), 6<sup>th</sup> demonstration. This reasoning of the <i>Motecallemin</i>, as the author is about to state, is founded upon their X<sup>th</sup> proposition; it is analogous to that which forms the basis of some of their demonstrations of Creation; vide chap. LXXIV, V<sup>th</sup> and VI<sup>th</sup> methods.",
    "fn.1254": "Literally: <i>since they are estimated</i> (<i>existent</i>) <i>contrary to their nature</i>. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קדר</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(قَدَّرَ)</span> , in the masculine and the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">טביעתה</span> relate to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">םאיר</span>.",
    "fn.1255": "Vide chap. LXXIV, VI<sup>th</sup> method. — All manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">לאמבאן וגׄודה ועדמה</span>, save one of the two Leyden manuscripts, which has merely <span dir=\"rtl\">לאמבאן וגׄודה</span>; according to this latter reading, one ought to translate: “its existence being (merely) <i>possible</i>,” that is to say, not being a <i>necessary</i> thing. It is this reading which has been reproduced by the two Hebrew translators; Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">לאפשרות מציאותו</span>, and Al-’Harizi, <span dir=\"rtl\">בעבור שמציאותו אפשרית</span>.",
    "fn.1256": "The construction of this sentence is irregular; here is roughly the word-for-word translation: <i>For all figures and measures of a possible existence</i> (<i>are</i>) <i>in this sense that something had</i> (<i>at first</i>) <i>not existed and then existed; this is what one would say etc</i>.",
    "fn.1257": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">וְלֹא לְמַרְגֵּחַ</span> are found only in one of the Oxford manuscripts (Uri, No. 359) and are not rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version; but these words appear to one necessary for completing the sense, and the negation <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span>, which, in all manuscripts (save in one of the Leyden MSS.), precedes the word <span dir=\"rtl\">למכׄצץ</span>, sufficiently indicates that something is wanting here. Al-’Harizi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא יצטרך למגביל אשר יבריע המציאות על האפיםה</span>.",
    "fn.1258": "That is to say: God, according to the proponents of corporeity, is, as He is, <i>the necessary being;</i> everything in Him, the attribute as the essence, is of absolute necessity, and nothing in Him supposes a being anterior that would have <i>determined</i> it in such or such a manner.",
    "fn.1259": "Literally: <i>considers</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">פתאמל</span>) ... <i>the condition of these thinkers</i> (i.e. the <i>Motécallemîn</i>) <i>and what has happened to them and from them</i>.",
    "fn.1260": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, there was an error made by inserting <span dir=\"rtl\">שהוא</span>; the manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">שהם</span>.",
    "fn.1261": "That is to say: who, in order to avoid an evil, falls into an even greater evil.",
    "fn.1262": "Literally: <i>they have destroyed the nature of being</i>.",
    "fn.1263": "Literally: <i>Après avoir achevé</i> (<i>d’exposer</i>) <i>le but extrême de leurs paroles;</i> that is to say, after having made known in substance the opinions and demonstrations of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>.",
    "fn.1264": "Cf. above, p. 350, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1.",
    "fn.1265": "Literally: <i>our method to us in that to which a true speculation has led us to complete etc</i>.",
    "fn.1266": "That is to say: After having first reasoned on the hypothesis of the eternity of the world, I will return to this very hypothesis itself and I will guarantee this in a discussion with philosophers.",
    "fn.1267": "All the Arabic mss. carry <span dir=\"rtl\">over Barhan, all and each of them</span>. Ibn-Tibbon deviates slightly from the original, translating: <span dir=\"rtl\">he has already done ··· a sign over each of them</span>. The version of Al-’Harizi carries: <span dir=\"rtl\">he has already examined ··· a sign over each of them</span>.",
    "fn.1268": "That is to say, we provisionally grant it to them as a hypothesis; see below the <small>XXVI</small><sup>e</sup> proposition, and cf. t. 1, pag. 350, note 1.",
    "fn.1270": "This second proposition enunciates concerning discrete magnitude that which, in the first proposition, was enunciated concerning continuous magnitude. Actual numerical infinity is as inadmissible as infinite extension: for the units which compose discrete quantity may all together form a continuous quantity, and it is clear that, since the latter cannot be infinite, the former can no more be so. One may, moreover, as the commentator Schem-Tob observes, apply directly to this proposition the demonstration which one has given of the first proposition, according to Ibn-Sina. Indeed, by diminishing the discrete quantity, supposed infinite, by a certain number of units, the remainder will be either infinite or finite; now, were it infinite, the part would be equal to the whole, which is impossible; were it finite, the whole would likewise be finite, which is contrary to the hypothesis.",
    "fn.1271": "We have already seen that, according to our author, the inadmissibility of the infinite <i>by succession</i> is not demonstrated. See t. I, p. 414 and 415, and cf. below proposition 26<sup>th</sup>.",
    "fn.1272": "This proposition was developed by Aristotle, in the <i>Metaphysics</i>, Book II, chap. 2, in which it is shown in general that, in the four species of causes, one necessarily arrives at a final term, and that these causes cannot continue ad infinitum: Α’<i>λλὰ μέν ὅτί γ’ ἐστὶν ἀρχή τις ϰαὶ oὐϰ ἄπειρα τὰ αἴτια τῶν ὄντων, οὔτ’ εἰς εὐθυωρίαν οὔτε ϰατ’ εἶδος, δῆλον, ϰ. τ. λ</i>. Cf. Volume I of this work, page 313, note 1.",
    "fn.1273": "Vide Aristotle, <i>Metaphysics</i>, Book XII, chap. 2: E<i>ἰ δὴ αἱ μεταϐολαὶ τέτταρες, ἢ ϰατὰ τὸ τί ἢ ϰατὰ τὸ ποιὸν ἢ ποσὸν ἢ ποῦ, ϰαὶ γένεσις μὲν ή ἁπλῆ ϰαὶ φθορὰ ἡ ϰατὰ τόδε, αὔξησις δὲ ϰαὶ φθίσις ἡ ϰατὰ τὸ ποσόν, ἀλλοίωσις δὲ ἡ ϰατὰ τὸ πάθος, φορὰ δε ἡ ϰατὰ τόπον, εἰς ἐναντιώσεις ἂν εἶεν τὰς ϰαθ’ ἕϰα-στον αἱ μεταϐολαὶ, ϰ. τ. λ</i>. Cf. <i>Physics</i>, Book III, chap. 1: Μ<i>εταϐάλλει γάρ τὸ μεταϐάλλον ἀεὶ ἢ ϰατ’ οὐσίαν, ἢ ϰατὰ ποσὸν, ἤ ϰατὰ ποιὸν, ἢ ϰατὰ τόπον</i>. One observes that by <i>change</i> (<i>μεταϐολή</i>), one must understand the mutual passage of opposites from one to another, and one must not confound it with the idea of <i>motion</i>, which, as Aristotle states elsewhere, applies only to the categories of quantity, quality, and place, and not to that of substance. Vide <i>Physics</i>, Book V, at the end of chap. 1, and at the commencement of chap. 2. Cf. the treatise <i>On the Soul</i>, Book I, chap. 3, § 3, where Aristotle speaks of four species of <i>motion</i>, which, fundamentally, form but three, belonging to three categories, namely: <i>φορά</i>, to the category of <i>place</i>; <i>ἀλλοίωσις</i>, to that of <i>quality</i>; <i>φθίσις</i> and <i>αὔξησις</i> to that of <i>quantity</i>. Nevertheless, in the six species of <i>motion</i> (<i>ϰινήσεως</i>) enumerated at the commencement of chap. 14 of the <i>Categories</i>, Aristotle also includes generation and corruption (<i>γένεσις ϰαὶ φθορά</i>), which apply to the category of <i>substance</i>. The other four are: <i>increase</i> (<i>αὔξησις</i>), <i>decrease</i> (<i>μείωσις = φθίσις</i>), <i>alteration</i> (<i>ἀλλοίωσις</i>), and change of place (<i>ϰατὰ τόπον μεταϐολή</i>); the first two species pertain to the category of <i>quantity</i>, the third to that of <i>quality</i>, and the fourth to that of <i>place</i>. One perceives that this passage from the <i>Categories</i> corresponds exactly to that from the <i>Metaphysics</i>, and that Aristotle there employed the word <i>ϰίνησις</i> in the more extended sense of <i>μεταϐολή</i>. Cf. infra, note 2.",
    "fn.1274": "One has observed in the preceding note that, in general, the changes here in question, with the exception of the first, are also designated as <i>motions;</i> but this is only because, fundamentally, all these different changes are in some wise a local motion: <i>πᾶσαι γὰρ αἱ λεχθεῖσαι ϰινήσεις ἐν τόπῳ</i> (<i>Treatise On the Soul</i>, l. c.); thus, for instance, in growth and decay, one may attribute to the different parts of the body a local motion. Cf. infra the 14<sup>th</sup> proposition. — All editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version here add the words: <span dir=\"rtl\">ועל שאר השנויים בכלל</span>, <i>and to the other changes</i> (it applies) <i>in general;</i> the mss. of the version do not contain this addition. Cf. at the commencement of chap. I.",
    "fn.1275": "The author here reproduces Aristotle's definition of motion. And here, the term <i>motion</i> embraces all species of <i>changes</i> mentioned in the preceding proposition; comprising both the change of generation and corruption, which occurs instantaneously and, as it were, without motion, and also those other changes which come to pass gradually and by a veritable motion. In this sense, therefore, motion is the change which may be designated, in the most general manner, as the passage from potency to act. See Aristotle, <i>Physics</i>, book III, chap. 1: Ὥ<i>στε ϰινιήσεως ϰαὶ μεταϐολῆς ἐστὶν εἴδη τοσαῦτα ὅσα τοῦ ὄντος. διῃρῆμένου δὲ ϰαθ’ ἔϰαστον γένoç τοῦ μὲν ἐντελεχείᾳ τοῦ δὲ δυνάμε, ἡ τοῦ δυνάμει ὄντς ἐντελέχεια, ᾖ τοιοῦτον, ϰίνησίς ἐστιν, ϰ. τ. λ</i>. Cf. <i>Metaphysics</i>, book XI, chap. 9.",
    "fn.1276": "The author is speaking here of movement par excellence, that is, of local movement<i>.",
    "fn.1277": "The various distinctions which the author here draws in local motion are borrowed from Aristotle, and are intended to demonstrate that all particular motions, whatsoever their nature, have their source in a first eternal motion upon which they depend. That which is moved, Aristotle states, is moved either <i>per se</i> (<i>ϰαθ’ αὑτό</i>) or <i>accidentally</i> (<i>ϰατὰ συμϐεϐηϰός</i>). Among that which is moved accidentally, he distinguishes things which might also be moved per se, as, for instance, the parts of an animal body and the nail in a ship, and other things which are always moved accidentally, such as whiteness (in the body) and knowledge (in the soul): for these only change place with the thing in which they subsist. Finally, in that which is moved <i>per se</i>, he further distinguishes that which is moved by itself and that which is moved by another, that which is moved naturally and that which is moved <i>by violence and contrary to nature</i> (<i>βίᾳ ϰαί παρὰ φὑσιν</i>), See Aristotle, <i>Physics</i>, book IV, chap. 4 and book VIII, chap. 4; cf. the treatise <i>On the Soul</i>, book II, chap. 3 (§§ 2 and 3), and the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, book III, chap. 2.",
    "fn.1278": "See Aristotle, <i>Physics</i>, book VI, at the commencement of chap. 4: Τ<i>ò δὲ μεταϐάλλον ἅπαν ἀνάγϰη διαιρετὸν εἶναι</i>. The demonstration given by Aristotle may be summarised thus: Everything that undergoes a change passes from one state of affairs to another; it cannot be for a single instant in either of the two states, for then it would not change; nor can it be wholly in one of the two states, for then it would either not yet be changing, or it would already be changed. It is, therefore, necessarily required that it be partly in the one state and partly in the other, and consequently it is divisible.",
    "fn.1279": "The fifth proposition establishes that all motion is a change; consequently, everything that is moved undergoes change and is necessarily divisible. Aristotle, who had already established the divisibility of magnitude, time, and motion (cf. vol. I of this work, p. 380, note 2), shows, in the chapter cited in the preceding note, that divisibility must also apply <i>to that which is moved</i>: Ἐ<i>πεὶ δὲ πᾶν τὸ ϰινούμενον ἔν τινι ϰινεῖται ϰαὶ χρόνον τινά, ϰαὶ παντός ἐστι ϰὶνησις, ἀνάγϰη τὰς αὐτὰς εἰναὶ διαιρέσεις τοῦ τε χρόνου ϰαὶ τῆς ϰινήσεως, ϰαὶ τοῦ ϰινεῖσθαι, ϰαὶ τοῦ ϰινουμένου, ϰαὶ ἐν ᾧ ἡ ϰίνησις, ϰ. τ. λ</i>. Cf., book VIII, chap. 5 (ed. Bekker, page 257 <i>a</i>, line 33): Ἀ<i>ναγϰαῖον δὴ τὸ ϰινούμενον ἄπαν εἶναι διαιρετὸν εἰς ἀεὶ διαιρετά, ϰ. τ. λ</i>.",
    "fn.1280": "As, for example, the geometrical point and the intellect, which possess no <i>essential</i> motion, but only an <i>accidentall</i> motion. This thesis, which the author adds here as a corollary, is a necessary consequence of what precedes. Nevertheless, Aristotle has entered into some details to demonstrate that the indivisible is immobile <i>per se</i>, and has only an accidental motion, and he observes in particular that if one were to admit the motion of the <i>point</i>, one would thereby arrive at establishing that the line is composed of points and time of small instants or of <i>present moments</i> (<i>ὲϰ τῶν νῦν</i>), which is false. See <i>Physics</i>, book VI, chap. 10, at the commencement: Λ<i>έγομεν ὅτι τὸ ἀμερὲς οὐϰ ἐνδέ-χεται ϰινεῖσθαι πλὴν ϰατὰ συμϐεϐηϰός, ϰ. τ. λ</i>. And further on: <i>ῶστ’ οὐϰ ἐνδε-χεται τὸ ἀμερὲς ϰινεῖσθάι οὐδ’ ὅλως μεταϐάλειν, ϰ. τ. λ</i>.",
    "fn.1282": "This proposition has been extensively developed by Aristotle, who, in order to establish the existence of a prime unmoved mover, demonstrats that that which movs, if it be not the prime mover itself, can be but an intermediate cause of motion, which is necessarily moved itself by another cause. It follows naturally that the physical body cannot communicate motion to another body save by being moved itself. Vide <i>Physica</i>., book VIII, chap. 5; Cf. <i>Metaphysica</i>., book XII, chap. 6.",
    "fn.1283": "Literally: <i>divides into two parts</i>.",
    "fn.1284": "That is to say, the form that constitutes the genus or species, and which makes something what it is. Cf. t. I, p. 398, and <i>Ibid</i>., note 1.",
    "fn.1285": "That is to say: what one does designate <i>a force in a body</i> may be either an accident, as for example heat and coldness in bodies which, by their nature, are neither hot nor cold, or indeed a physical form, such as, for instance, the heat or the igneous nature of fire, or the coldness of ice. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">قوة</span> (potency), which the Arabian philosophers employ in the various senses that Aristotle attributs to the word <i>δύναμɩς</i>, must be taken here not in the sense of <i>possibility</i> or <i>faculty of being</i> as opposed to <i>act</i> (<i>ἐνέργεɩα</i>), but in its primitive and absolute sense which Aristotle defins as «the principle from which emanats motion or change produced in another thing inasmuch as it is another thing» (<i>Metaphysica</i>., book V, chap. 12, commencement and end), or in other words, in the sense of <i>force</i> or of <i>active faculty</i>. This force may be found outside the body upon which it acts, or within that body itself; and in this latter case, it is a <i>force in a body</i>.",
    "fn.1286": "The mss. read here and in the following propositions <span dir=\"rtl\">עשר</span>; we have written more correctly <span dir=\"rtl\">עשרה̈</span>.",
    "fn.1287": "The more precise meaning of this proposition is as follows: amongst the accidents or qualities which subsist only within the body, there are some which divide with the body, such as, for instance, the heat of a warm body or the colour inherent in a body; for every particle of the body retains the same heat and the same colour. Others do not follow the division of the body, such as, for instance, figure, which does not always remain the same when the body is divided. On the other hand, even amongst those things which constitute or perfect the being of the body, there are some which cannot be divided in any manner, neither in reality nor even in thought, and such pre-eminently are the rational soul and the intelligence; others, like certain physical forms, divide with the body to which they belong. — By <i>soul</i> and <i>intelligence</i>, the author signifies not only the rational soul of man and the <i>hylitic intellect</i>, but also the souls of the celestial spheres and the intelligence by which they <i>conceive</i> the particular aim of their motion: for it will be seen further on (chap. IV) that the author, following the theory of Ibn-Sīnā, attributes to the celestial spheres not only a soul, but also a thought inherent in them, which must not be confounded with the <i>separated intelligences</i> which are the object of desire for their respective spheres, and which determine their motion.",
    "fn.1288": "Aristotle, having established that the prime mover is not moved, endeavours to demonstrate that it possesses neither parts nor extension. Starting from the proposition already demonstrated, that there is no infinite extension or magnitude, he shows that the prime mover cannot be a finite magnitude, for the motion emanating from it being infinite, it would follow that <i>in a finite magnitude there can be an infinite force;</i> yet, this is impossible, for infinite force ought to produce its effect in a time less than that which any finite force would require to produce the same effect, that is to say, infinite force would produce its effect in no time or instantaneously, which is inadmissible, as every transformation occurs within a certain time. Will it be said that infinite force also produces its effect within a certain time? But then one could discover a finite force which would produce the same effect in the same time, and it would follow that this finite force would be equal to an infinite force, which is impossible. Such is, in substance, the demonstration by which Aristotle establishes that <i>in a finite magnitude there cannot be an infinite force</i>. See <i>Physics</i>, book VIII, ch. 10 (Bekker's ed., p. 266 <i>a</i>): <i>ὅτι δ’ὅλως οὐϰ ἐνδέχεται ἐν πεπε-ρασμένῳ μεγέθει ἄπειρον εἶναι δύναμιν, ἐϰ τῶνδε δῆλον. ϰ. τ. λ.</i>",
    "fn.1289": "See above the IV<sup>th</sup> proposition.",
    "fn.1290": "The first three kinds of changes enumerated above (prop. IV) all indicate the passage from one state to another opposite state; now, the two opposite states are necessarily separated one from the other by an interval of time, and consequently the change is not continuous. Nothing of the like takes place in the fourth kind of change, or in local motion, which alone can be continuous. See Arist., <i>Phys</i>., book VIII, ch. 7 (p. 261 <i>a</i>): ὅ<i>τι μὲν οὖν τῶν ἄλλων ϰινή-σεων οὐδεμίαν ἐνδέχεται συνεχῆ εἷναι, ἐϰ τῶνδε φανερόν</i>. Ἅ<i>πασαι γὰρ ἐξ ἀντι-ϰειμένων εὶς ἀντιϰείμενά εἰσιν αἱ ϰινήσεις ϰαὶ μεταϐολαὶ, ϰ. τ. λ.</i> Cf. <i>ibid.,</i> book V, chap. 4 (p. 228 <i>a</i>, <i>b</i>.): Κ<i>εῖται γὰρ τὸ συνεχές, ὧν τὰ ἔσχατα ἕν... πολλαὶ οὖν ϰαὶ οὐ μία ἡ ϰίνησις, ὧν ἐστίν ὴρεμία μεταξύ</i>.",
    "fn.1291": "In local motion itself, only circular motion is truly continuous; for motion in a straight line, being unable to continue to infinity, must necessarily have a stopping point from which it will turn, either to take another direction, or to return in the opposite direction. See <i>ibid</i>., book VIII, chap. 8, at the beginning: <i>Ὅτι δ’ἐνδἑχεται εἶναί τινα ἄπειρον, μίαν οὖσαν ϰαὶ συνεχῆ, ϰαὶ αὔτη ἐστίν ἡ ϰύϰλῳ, λέγωμεν νῦν... ὅτι δὲ τὸ φερόμενον τήν εὐθεῖαν ϰαὶ πεπερασμένην οὐ φέρεται συνεχῶς, δῆλον. ’Α’ναϰάμπτει γάρ, τὸ δ’ ἀναϰάμπτον τὴν εὐθεῖαν τὰς ἐναντίας ϰινεῖται ϰινήσεις</i>. See also <i>Metaphysics</i>., book XII, chap. 6, where our proposition is enunciated in these terms: <i>Κίνησις δ’οὐϰ ἔστι συνεχής ἄλλ’ ἢ ἡ ϰατὰ τόπον, ϰαὶ ταύτης ἡ ϰύϰλῳ</i>.",
    "fn.1292": "The mss. carry <span dir=\"rtl\">ואולהא</span> in the masculine, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואולאהא</span>.",
    "fn.1294": "It is, as Aristotle expresses it, <i>something of movement</i> (<i>τῆς ϰινήσεώς τι</i>). See on this proposition vol. I of this work, p. 199, n. 1, and p. 380, n. 2.",
    "fn.1295": "That is to say, all that which is not moved, but which is itself the cause of motion, or in other terms, all that which is outside the celestial sphere, such as God and the separated intelligences. See Aristotle, Treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, book I, chap. 9: Ἄ<i>μα δὲ δῆλον ὅτι οὐδὲ τόπος οὐδὲ ϰενὸν οὐδὲ χρόνος ἐστὶν ἔξω τοῦ οὐρανοῦ..... ϰίνησις δ’ ἄνευ φυσιϰοῦ σώματος οὐϰ ἔστιν · ἔξω δὲ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ δέδειϰται ὅτι οὔτ’ ἔστιν οὔτ’ ἐνδέχεται γενέσθαι σῶμα, ϰ. τ. λ.</i> Cf. <i>Phys</i>., book IV, chap. 12: Ὤ<i>στε φανερὸν ὅτι τὰ ἀεὶ ὄντα ῇ ἀεὶ ὄντα, οὐϰ ἔστίν ἐν χρόνῳ, ϰ. τ. λ</i>.",
    "fn.1296": "Literally: <i>In all that is not a body, one cannot think of numeration</i>… Cf. Arist. <i>Metaph.</i>, book XII, chapter 8: Α<i>ll’ ὄσϰ, ἀρι-θμῷ πολλά, ὕλην ἔχει, ϰ. τ. λ</i>.",
    "fn.1297": "That is to say, the different matters or subjects in which they are found.",
    "fn.1298": "See vol. I of this work, p. 434, and ibid., notes 2, 3, and 4, and below, the beginning of chap. 1, p. 31, note 2.",
    "fn.1299": "See Arist., <i>Phys</i>., book VII, chap. 1: Ἅ<i>παν τὸ ϰινούμενον ἀνάγϰη ὑπό τινος ϰινεῖσθαɩ</i>. Aristotle demonstrates this proposition by arguing chiefly from the infinite divisibility of that which is moved (See the VIII<sup>th</sup> propos.), which does not permit one to stop at any part whatsoever of the moved thing to perceive therein the motor principle of the whole; whence it follows that this mover is necessarily something other than the entirety of the moved thing. Cf. book VIII, chap. 6, towards the end.",
    "fn.1300": "See Aristotle, <i>Phys</i>. book VIII, chap. 4. After distinguishing what is moved accidentally with something else from what is moved in itself (<i>ϰαθ’ αὑτό</i>), Aristotle adds: Τ<i>ῶν δὲ ϰαθ’ αὑτά τὰ μὲν ὑφ’ ἑαυτοῦ τὰ δ’ ὑπ’ ἄλλου... ϰινεῖται γὰρ τὸ ζῷον αὐτὸ ὑφ’αὑτοῦ, ϰ τ. λ.</i>",
    "fn.1301": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלגׄסד</span>, which better designates the inanimate body, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלגׄסם</span>.",
    "fn.1302": "That is, the local movement which came to it from the soul.",
    "fn.1303": "The Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">מן חלקאיה</span> , <i>from the side</i> or <i>from his own part</i>, correspond to the Greek words <i>ὑφ’ ἑαυτοῦ</i>.",
    "fn.1304": "In other words: that the inherent motive force, by which any part of the moving body receives an essential and not an accidental movement (such as the movement received by the hand through an external impulse), resides in the entirety of that body.",
    "fn.1305": "This proposition results from the combination of propositions V and XVII. Movement having been defined as the passage from potency to act (cf. <i>Phys</i>., III, 1 and 2; <i>Metaph</i>., XI, 9), and every movement presupposing a mover which is other than the thing moved, it thence follows that everything in potency requires an external impulse to pass into act. Potency is a faculty of acting or a faculty of receiving action (cf. <i>Metaph</i>., IX, 1); in both cases, potency passes into act only by virtue of something which proceeds from without. Thus, for instance, the artist, who possesses the faculty of producing a work of art, requires an external material to realise this faculty; and similarly, bronze, which possesses the faculty of becoming a statue, requires the artist's labour for this faculty to be realised.",
    "fn.1306": "Thus, for example, what is of absolute lightness, such as fire, or of absolute heaviness, such as earth, not only has the <i>faculty</i> to move upwards and downwards, but this faculty or power is always in act, unless there exists an obstacle that prevents the natural movement and produces a contrary movement.",
    "fn.1307": "If, for instance, someone removes a column that supports a heavy object, causing it to fall, we can say in some sense that it was this person who made the object fall by removing the obstacle that prevented gravity from following its natural law. See Aristotle, <i>Phys</i>., book VIII, end of chapter 4. Cf. Maimonides, III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chapter 10.",
    "fn.1308": "Literally: <i>and understand this</i>. Commentators observe that the author here adds these words on account of the profound import of this proposition, which appears to subvert the dogma of creation: for God, being absolute energy always in act, and nothing being capable of obstructing His action, could not, at a given moment, have created the world, or passed from potency to act. See, upon this question, chapter XVIII of this II<sup>e</sup> part.",
    "fn.1309": "This proposition and the two succeeding ones are borrowed from Ibn-Sinâ, who was the first to make, within the idea of <i>necessary being</i> (as opposed to the absolute <i>possible</i>, which comes into being and perishes), this distinction between that which is <i>necessary in itself</i> or the absolute necessary, and that which is <i>necessary through another</i>, being by its own essence in the category of the possible. Of the second species are, according to Ibn-Sinâ, the celestial spheres, in which one distinguishes potency and act, matter and form, and which derive the quality of <i>necessary beings</i> solely from their relation to the first cause, or God. Ibn-Sinâ departs, in this respect, from Aristotle, who expressly extends the idea of <i>necessary being</i> to that which is moved eternally, or to the celestial spheres, which, he states, are in no wise in potency and possess no matter properly so-called, that is to say, no matter subject to generation and corruption. See <i>Metaph</i>., book IX, chapter 8: O<i>ὐθέν ἂρα τῶν ἀφθάρτων ἁπλῶς δυνάμει ἐστὶν ὄν ἁπλῶς οὐδὲ τῶν ἐξ ἀνὰγϰης ὄντων, ϰαί τοι ταῦτα πρῶτα.... διὸ ἀεὶ ἐνεργεῖ ἥλιος ϰαὶ ἄστρα ϰαὶ ὅλος ὁ οὐρανὸς, ϰ. τ. λ</i>. Cf. Treatise <i>on the Heavens</i>, I, 2. Ibn-Roschd contended against Ibn-Sinâ's theory in several passages of his works. Cf. my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, pp. 358-359.",
    "fn.1310": "Ibn-Sinâ gives the example of the number <i>four</i>, which exists only due to the number <i>two</i> being taken <i>two</i> times, and which, consequently, ceases to exist as soon as the number <i>two</i>, which is its cause, no longer exists. See Schahrestâni, p. 373 (all. trans., Vol. II, pag. 250), and <i>Al Nadjâh</i>, [[t:10]]Métaph[[t:11]]., at the beginning of Book II, p. 62.",
    "fn.1311": "That is to say, the relation between cause and effect, or the necessary condition under which alone a given cause produces a given effect. «Everything,» states Ibn-Sinâ (loco citato), «whose existence is necessary through another, is in itself of a possible existence: for the necessity of its existence depends upon a certain <i>relation</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(نسبة)</span> , in which one considers something other than the very essence of the thing in question.» Thus, for instance, the sun becomes the cause of day for a part of the earth only when it is found in a certain position relative to that part.",
    "fn.1312": "That is: it has neither an external cause nor even an internal cause, which would imply a composition. See the preceding proposition.",
    "fn.1313": "It is evident, and the author frequently insists thereupon (See, in volume I, the chapters concerning the attributes, and hereinafter, ch. I), that the absolute and necessary being cannot be composed of two distinct things, and that thought cannot even therein distinguish two distinct ideas, or two intelligible things. Consequently, every existence which presents itself, in our thought, as a compound of two ideas, as for example matter and form, cannot be, as it presents itself, <i>necessary in itself</i>, inasmuch as it is, at the very least for thought, the result of a composition: for, as Ibn-Sinâ observes, it is impossible to admit that the whole is, by its essence, anterior to the parts, but it is either posterior, or coexistent therewith. See <i>Al-Nadjâh</i>, <i>loco citato</i>., p. 63, line 9.",
    "fn.1314": "The notions of potency and act, of matter and form, are so familiar to the Peripatetics that the proposition here in question requires no explanation. It is merely to be observed that the author here understands by <i>body</i>, not only that which is subject to generation and corruption, but also the celestial bodies; these, according to Aristotle, whilst not possessing matter susceptible of generation, do possess one which serves as a substratum for translatory motion. See Arist., <i>Metaph.</i>, IX, 8, at the end, and XII, 2, and Cf. <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 4, note 1, and p. 18, note 1. Maimonides adopted the opinion of Ibn-Sina, who purported to furnish a general demonstration of the existence of matter and form in bodies, applicable to <i>all bodies</i>, including the celestial spheres. Cf. Schahrestàni, p. 366 (Germ. trans., II, p. 239-240). This opinion, by which one might be led to attribute to the celestial bodies also an <i>being in potency</i> (which would be contrary to Aristotle's theories), was contested by Ibn-Roschd; the latter considers the celestial bodies as simple bodies which discover their form or their entelechy in the <i>separated intelligences</i>. Cf. Ibn-Falaquéra, <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, on this proposition (p. 71-72).",
    "fn.1315": "These three accidents are inherent in every body: one cannot imagine a body without <i>quantity</i>, and it necessarily has limits that constitute the <i>figure</i>; finally, its parts are in a certain <i>situation</i> relative to one another, and the entire body is in a certain situation relative to what is outside of it.",
    "fn.1316": "There is a nuance between <i>potency</i> and <i>possibility;</i> the former may exist solely in our thought, whilst the latter inheres in the things themselves. Thus, one often distinguishes potency and act in a purely ideal manner, even when in reality the two notions are inseparable one from another; prime matter, for instance, is a <i>potency</i>, but this potency exists separately only in thought, for prime matter is inseparable from form. <i>Possibility</i>, on the contrary, resides in the object itself, and designates that which may or may not be; thus, for example, bronze may or may not be a statue, and the statue may cease to be what it is by losing its form. Thus, then, the author who seeks to characterize, in this proposition, that which, at a certain moment, may not exist in act, must add to <i>potency</i> the condition of <i>possibility</i> within the very essence of the thing, intending thereby that whatsoever is in potency, not merely in our thought, but because the thing itself encompasses the notion of the <i>possible</i>, may also be conceived as not existing in act at a certain moment. In sum, this proposition reverts to what Aristotle declared, namely, that whatsoever is <i>possible</i> may not be in act, and consequently it may both be and not be. See <i>Metaphysics</i>, Book IX, Chapter 8 (Brandis's edition, p. 187-188): Ἕ<i>στι δ’οὐθὲν δυνάμει ἀΐδιον..... Tὸ δυνατὸν δὲ πᾶν ἐνδέχεται<br/>μὴ ἐνεργῖν· τὸ ἄρα δυνατὸν εἶναι ἐνδέχεται εἶναι ϰαὶ μὴ εἶναι</i>. An explanation which the author himself gave concerning this XXIII<sup>rd</sup> proposition is cited in the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i> (p. 72, lines 9-18): <span dir=\"rtl\">ופירש בו מורנו ז״ל כי הדבר בהיות תאר מהתארים וכו׳</span>. This explanation is drawn from the letter addressed by Maimonides to R. Samuel ibn-Tibbon, and of which one has spoken in other places (cf. Vol. I, p. 23, note 1). — One will observe in Chapter I (at the <i>fourth speculation</i>) the application which the author makes of this proposition, in order to demonstrate the necessity of ascending to a prime mover, within which there is absolutely no notion of <i>possibility</i>.",
    "fn.1317": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">שי מא</span> are rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">דבר אחד</span>, which one must be careful not to translate here as <i>a single thing</i>, and which bear the sense of <i>something</i> or <i>any thing whatsoever</i>. These two words, omitted in almost all editions, are found in the edition <i>princeps.</i> Ibn-Falaquéra has with reason replaced them with <span dir=\"rtl\">דבר מה</span>, and Al-’Harizi with <span dir=\"rtl\">שום דבר</span>.",
    "fn.1318": "This proposition, which forms one of the principal tenets of Peripateticism, requires no explanation. <i>Potency</i> is the principle of contingency or the faculty of becoming something, and this faculty necessarily resides in a substratum, which is matter. Whatever is subject to any change whatsoever possesses matter (<i>πὰντα δ’ ὕλην ἔχει ὅσα μεταϐάλλει</i>, <i>Metaph.</i>, XII, 2). One has already observed that Aristotle also attributes to the celestial spheres a certain matter as a substratum for translatory motion. Cf. above, p. 20, n. 1.",
    "fn.1319": "See <i>Physics</i>, book I, chapter 7.",
    "fn.1320": "Ibn-Tibbon's version editions read in that form; the word that is not in the manuscripts must be erased.",
    "fn.1321": "That is to say, who disposes a particular matter to receive a particular form, such as, for example, the artist, who gives bronze the form of a statue.",
    "fn.1322": "Literally: <i>And already has been exposed with regard to all this what it is necessary to expose</i>. The author wants to speak of the developed explanations, given by Aristotle in the <i>Physics</i> and in the <i>Metaphysics</i>.",
    "fn.1323": "See <i>Metaph</i>., book XII, chapter 6: For <i>if it is not in actuality, it will not be moved; for it will not move itself, but rather the artificer, etc. Cf. </i>ibid<i>., book I, chapter 3: For </i>it is the thing that is subject that makes itself change.",
    "fn.1324": "Most manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">מעקולאת</span> without the copulative <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>, and likewise the two Hebrew versions, as well as the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, have <span dir=\"rtl\">מושכלות</span>, as an adjective of <span dir=\"rtl\">הקדמות מופתיות</span>, so that one should translate: <i>demonstrative propositions</i>, <i>intelligible at first glance;</i> but then the form <span dir=\"rtl\">מעקולאת</span> would be incorrect, for the <i>adjective</i> ought to assume the feminine singular form <span dir=\"rtl\">מעקולה̈</span>. One therefore considers this word as a neuter substantive, in the sense of intelligibilia, so that the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מעקולאת אול</span> signify, as ever, <i>primary notions</i> or <i>axioms</i>. These words cannot be a mere appositive of the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מקדמאת ברהאניה̈</span>; for <i>demonstrative propositions</i> cannot be qualified as <i>axioms</i>. One has therefore added a copulative <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>, and has written <span dir=\"rtl\">ומעקולאת</span>, as is indeed found in one of the two Leyden manuscripts (no. 221).",
    "fn.1325": "Literally: <i>by what we have summarized from their arrangement</i> or <i>their enumeration</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">Bema</span> depends on <span dir=\"rtl\">Ma’qulat</span>, <i>the</i> (<i>notions</i>) <i>intelligible by, etc</i>.",
    "fn.1326": "See vol. I, p. 380, n. 2.",
    "fn.1327": "All manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ושרחה</span> in the singular; likewise the version of Al-’Harisi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ופירושו</span>, whereas the version of Ibn-Tibbon has the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">ופירושיו</span>, <i>and its comments</i>. It is known that the Greek commentaries upon the <i>Metaphysics</i> were few in number; the Arabs knew but of an incomplete commentary by Alexander of Aphrodisias upon the XII<sup>th</sup> book, and a paraphrase by Themistius on the same book. Ibn-Roschd expresses himself thus in this regard at the commencement of his introduction to Book XII of the <i>Metaphysics</i> (Hebrew version, manuscript from the Oratory collection, no. 114, fol. 139 <i>a</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">לא ימצא לאלכסנדר ולא למי שאחריו מהמפרשים פירוש במאמרי זאת החכמה ולא ביאור אלא בזה המאמר כי אנחנו מצאנו לאלכסנדר בו פירוש משני שלישי המאמר ומצאנו לתמסטיוס בו ביאור כפי הענין ·</span>",
    "fn.1328": "All the manuscripts one has been able to consult bear <span dir=\"rtl\">וחתביין אבער טמקדמאת</span>; this reading is confirmed by the version of Ibn-Tibbon, which bears <span dir=\"rtl\">ולבאר הרחוקות שבהקדמות</span>. It appears, nevertheless, that the Hebrew translator here entertained a doubt upon which he consulted the author; for this is what one reads in the letter addressed by Maimonides to R. Samuel Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמקדמה̈ אלכ\"ה ט̇ננת אן פיהא נקצ̇א ולא נקץ̇ פיהא בל נצ̇הא כמא\nענדכם ואנמא תקדיר אלכלאם הד̇א ליס גרץ̇ הד̇ה אלמקאלה̈ נקל\nכתב אלפלאספה̈ פיהא אלא תביין בעץ̇ אלמקדמאת</span>. “Thou supposedst that, in the XXV<sup>th</sup> proposition, something was wanting; but nothing is wanting therein, and on the contrary the reading is such as ye have it. My words signify naught else than this: The aim of this treatise is not to transcribe therein the books of the philosophers, <i>but to set forth certain propositions</i>.” — If these are indeed the terms of Maimonides, and if there be no fault in the unique manuscript one possesses of the letter in question, one ought to continue the sentence thus: <i>or rather to recount the propositions which are within our reach</i>, <i>etc</i>. — The version of Al-’Harizi bears <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא לבאר ההקדמות הרחוקות והקרובות שאנו צריכין עליהם וכו׳</span>. This version, in any case, is inexact; but the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא</span> offers a trace of the reading given in Maimonides’ letter.",
    "fn.1329": "Cf. above, p. 3, note 2.",
    "fn.1330": "Literally: <i>Until it has been exposed what we propose to expose</i>.",
    "fn.1331": "See especially <i>Phys</i>., Book VIII, chapter 1, where Aristotle establishes the eternity of motion as a necessary consequence of the eternity of time, which, as Aristotle expresses it, is <i>the number of motion</i>: E<i>ἰ δή ἐστίν ὁ χρόνος ϰινήσεως ἀριθμὸς ἢ ϰίνησίς τις, εἴπερ ἀεὶ χρόνος ἐστίν, ἀνάγϰη ϰαὶ ϰίνησιν ἀἴδιον εἶναι</i>. Cf. <i>Metaph</i>., XII, 6: <i>’</i>Α<i>λλ’ ὰδύνατον ϰίνησιν ἢ γενέσθαι ἢ φθαρῆναι · ἀεί γαρ ἦν · οὐδὲ χρόνον. ϰ. τ. λ.</i> See also above, the XV<sup>th</sup> proposition and the passages indicated in the accompanying notes.",
    "fn.1332": "That is to say, the body of the celestial sphere, which is above the four elements, and whose substance has been designated by the name of `<i>aether</i>`. Vide the treatise `<i>On the Heavens</i>`, Book I, chapters 2 and 3; `<i>Meteorologica</i>`, Book I, chapter 3; and cf. Vol. I of this Work, p. 247, note 3, and p. 425, note 1. — The expression `<i>fifth body</i>` (`<i>πέμπτον σῶμα</i>`) is familiar to the commentators of Aristotle. Vide, for instance, Simplicius, on the treatise `<i>On the Heavens</i>`, Book I, chapter 3 (`<i>Scholia in Aristotelem</i>`, collected by Brandis, p. 475 `<i>a</i>`). Aristotle himself does rather employ the expressions `<i>τὸ ἄνω σῶμα</i>` (treatise `<i>On the Soul</i>`, II, 7, and `<i>passim</i>`; cf. the commentary of Trendelenburg, p. 373 et seq.), `<i>τὸ πρῶτον σῶμα</i>` (`<i>On the Heavens</i>`, II, 12), `<i>τὸ πρῶτον στοιχεῖον</i>` (`<i>Meteorologica</i>`, I, 2 and 3). It is doubtless of this celestial substance that the treatise by Empedocles, entitled “On the `<i>Fifth</i>` Substance” (`<i>περὶ τῆς πέμπτης οὐσίας</i>`), did treat; and which, as it appears, was refuted by Plutarch in a writing mentioned by Lamprias. Cf. Sturz, `<i>Empedocles agrigentinus</i>` (Lipsiae, 1805, in-8°), p. 73.",
    "fn.1333": "Thus, for example, the circular motion of each celestial sphere, considered in itself, is caused by a motion of the same kind that precedes it; the birth of the elements and their motion proceed from the circular motion of the celestial spheres, which is not of the same kind.",
    "fn.1334": "Vide `<i>Physics</i>`, Book VIII, chapter 2. After having spoken of this objection, drawn from the motion of animals, which appears to be spontaneous and to proceed from no movement originating from without, Aristotle does observe that this is but a false appearance, and that one does ever remark, in that which composs the animal organism, certain motions whose cause ought not to be sought within the animal itself, but in that which does environ it from without, so that there are external movements which act upon the intellectual and appetitive faculties: `Ὁ<i>ρῶμεν γὰρ ἀεί τι ϰινούμενον ἐν τῷ ζῴῳ τῶν συμφύτων.... οὐδὲν οὖν ϰωλύει, μᾶλλον δ’ ἴσως ἀναγϰαῖον τῷ σώματι πολλὰς ἐγγίγνεσθαι ϰινήσεις ὑπὸ τοῦ περιέχοντος, τούτων δ’ ὲνίας τὴν διὰνοιαν ἢ τὴν ὄρεξιν ϰινεῖν, ϰ. τ. λ.</i>` Cf. the treatise `<i>On the Motion of Animals</i>`, chapter 6: `Ὁ<i>ρῶμεν δὲ τὰ ϰινοῦντα τὸ ζῷον διάνοιαν, ϰαὶ φαντασίαν ϰαὶ προαίρεσιν, ϰαὶ βούλησιν, ϰαὶ ἐπιθυμίαν · ταῦτα δε πάντα ἀνάγεται εἰς νοῦν ϰαὶ ὄρεξιν</i>`. — In order that one may better comprehend the terms which Maimonides here reports in the name of Aristotle, one shall moreover cite the passage from the `<i>Physics</i>` according to the Arabic-Latin version, with the explication of Ibn-Roschd (Works of Aristotle with the commentaries of Averroes, Vol. IV, fol. 161, col. 3): `Semper enim invenimus aliquid moveri in animali, quod est naturale in eo; et causa istius motus animalis non est anima ejus, sed aer qui continet animal in eo, quod reputo. Et cum dicimus ipsum moveri a se, non intendimus omni motu, sed motu locali. Et nihil prohibet, immo dignum est ut sit necessarium, ut in corpore fiant plures motus a continente, quorum quidam movent voluntatem et appetitum, et tunc ista movebunt animal secundum totum.` Here follows Ibn-Roschd's explanation of the conclusion of this passage: `Et dixit <i>et nihil prohibet</i>, <i>etc</i>., id est et necesse est ut principium motuum animalis sit ex continente: immo hoc est necessarium. Et quia posuit quod in corpore animalis fiunt plures motus ex continente, narravit quomodo accidit ex istis motibus ut animal moveatur in loco, et dixit <i>quo rumquœdam moventur</i>, <i>etc</i>., id est, et hoc est ita, quod in animali fiet ab eo, quod accidit sibi, opinio, voluntas, et appetitus ad motum, aut ad expellendum nocumentum contingens ex continente, aut ad inducendum juvamentum. Et intendit hic per <i>opinionem</i> aliquid commune virtuti imaginativæ, et rationali. Animali enim non accidit appetitus, nisi ex imaginatione; v. g. quod, cum patitur et cum timet, fugit: et cum auferuntur appetitus, quiescit: aut, cum accidit ei fatigatio, et appetit quietem.` Concerning `<i>imagination</i>`, vide also further on, at the commencement of chapter IV.",
    "fn.1335": "See <i>Phys</i>., VIII, 1 (p. 251 <i>a</i>). Aristotle, after having recalled the definition of motion given above, adds that, even without this definition of motion, one will concede that, with regard to every motion, that which moves must be capable of motion, as for example, that which is transformed must be capable of transformation, and that which is transported must be capable of changing place, <i>so that a thing must be combustible before it burns, and capable of kindling before it kindles</i>. (<i>ὤστε δεῖ πρότερον ϰαυστὸν εἶναι πρὶν ϰάεσθαι, ϰαὶ ϰαυστιϰὸν πρὶν ϰάειν</i>]). According to Ibn-Rushd (l. c., f. 155, c. 3) this passage signifies nothing else than that motion (which, according to Aristotle, is <i>the actuality of a mobile thing in so far as it is mobile</i>) must exist in potentiality in every mobile thing. But the Arab commentator informs one that Al-Farabi understood this passage in the sense that every potentiality must <i>temporally</i> precede the act, not only in motion, but generally in everything that comes into being: “Dico secundum hanc expositionem intellexit Alpharabius et alii hoc capitulum, scilicet quod induxit definitionem motus ad declarandum potentiam esse ante actum; et hoc non est proprium motui, secundum quod est motus, sed est proprium novo facto secundum quod est novum factum, scilicet ut potentia et posse novi præcedat ipsum <i>secundum tempus</i>.” It is evident that Maimonides borrowed the terms of Al-Farabi’s explanation, which Ibn-Rushd declares erroneous: “Et hoc quod dixit <i>et sine hac definitione</i>, etc., hoc decepit homines in hoc: et existimaverunt ipsum declarare potentiam esse ante actum in tempore, et ipse intendebat dicere quod non dicitur moveri nisi illud in cujus natura est motus, scilicet corpus mobile: et quod non invenitur in immobili.”",
    "fn.1336": "That is, the celestial sphere, which is a finite body.",
    "fn.1337": "That is, one can admit the existence of the infinite in number (see the second proposition), provided that the units that compose it do not exist simultaneously, but successively, one after the other, like, for example, the instants that succeed each other in time and the successive and uninterrupted movements of the celestial sphere. See book I, chapter LXXIII, pp. 413-415.",
    "fn.1338": "Literally: <i>does not cut</i> (or <i>does not decide</i>) <i>whether his proofs about them are a demonstration</i>.",
    "fn.1339": "See on this question, the chap. XV of this II<sup>nd</sup> part.",
    "fn.1340": "Literally: <i>The force of their words</i> (i.e., that which results therefrom in sum) <i>is that it is there, according to them</i>, <i>a primary notion;</i> that is to say: they generally consider it a simple axiom that <i>the infinite by succession</i> is impossible. Cf. Vol. I, p. 416. — All the manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אנהא ענדהם</span>, and one must take the feminine suffix of <span dir=\"rtl\">אנהא</span> in the neuter sense, or else refer it to a word <span dir=\"rtl\">מקדמה̈</span>, which would be understood, that is to say, the proposition which declares the infinite by succession inadmissible. Ibn-Tibbon’s version bears <span dir=\"rtl\">שהוא</span> in the masculine, that of Al-’Harizi <span dir=\"rtl\">שהיא</span> in the feminine.",
    "fn.1341": "That is, the existence, incorporality, and unity of God.",
    "fn.1342": "The author provides, in this chapter, various demonstrations of the existence of a single and immaterial God. His demonstrations are of those which have been termed <i>physical</i> or <i>cosmological</i>, and which lead one from the contingent existence of the world to the conception of a necessary being. His proofs are principally founded upon <i>motion;</i> it is demonstrated that, inert matter being unable to move itself, and the causes of motion being unable to ascend to infinity, it is necessary to acknowledge a <i>first mover</i> who is himself immobile. The argumentation is, in substance, borrowed from Aristotle (<i>Phys.</i>, Book VIII, chapters 5 et seq.; <i>Metaph.</i>, Book XII, chapters 6 and 7); but it has been, on various points, completed and modified by the Arab philosophers, and one will recognise, notably in the 3<sup>rd</sup> <i>Speculation</i>, theories peculiar to Ibn-Sīnā.",
    "fn.1343": "That is to say, the matter of all sublunar things. The two Hebrew versions (cf. <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 74), according to which the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ההוה והנפםד</span> would refer to <i>matter</i>, are incorrect; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">החמר הזה</span>, one ought to write <span dir=\"rtl\">חמר זה</span> without the article, and consider <span dir=\"rtl\">חמר</span> as a <i>construct state</i>, of which <span dir=\"rtl\">זה</span> is the complement.",
    "fn.1344": "The author, I believe, means that this is where the movement proper to sublunar things comes to an end, in order to continue by an impulse emanating from a movement of a different kind.",
    "fn.1345": "That is to say, everything in this world that serves as a proximate or immediate mover, and disposes the particular matter to receive the particular form. See above, the XXV<sup>th</sup> proposition.",
    "fn.1346": "It is by inadvertence that in our text we have written <span dir=\"rtl\">חרכתהא</span>, as several mss. have; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">חרכהא</span>, the more correct reading that some other mss. have, because from the following words, <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלעצׄל חרכה</span>, it is clear that the author constructed the collective noun <span dir=\"rtl\">עצׄל</span> as a singular masculine.",
    "fn.1347": "Here the author understands by form the vital soul.",
    "fn.1348": "See above, p. 26, note 1.",
    "fn.1349": "Instead of the participle <span dir=\"rtl\">ומלה</span> , several mss. have the infinitive <span dir=\"rtl\">יולד</span> ; similarly, several mss. of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have <span dir=\"rtl\">והולדת</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ומוליִד</span>.",
    "fn.1350": "See vol. I, p. 362, and ibid., note 2.",
    "fn.1351": "The author means that the expression <i>outside</i> implies the idea of <i>place</i> and of corporeality, and that when speaking of an incorporeal thing, of a pure intelligence, one ought not to say that it is <i>outside</i> the body, but rather that it is <i>separated</i> from it. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">مفارق</span> , <i>separated</i>, is employed by the Arab philosophers to designate purely spiritual substances, separated from every species of matter, and to which, in no respect, applies the idea of <i>being in potentiality</i>, nor any other category than that of <i>substance</i>. They have understood in this sense what Aristotle (treatise <i>On the Soul</i>, Book III, chapter 7) calls <i>τὰ ϰεχωρισμένα, the separated things</i> (<i>from extension</i>)<italic/>, and it is there that one must seek the origin of the Arabic term. See my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 449, and cf. Vol. I, p. 434.",
    "fn.1352": "The word <i>tenth</i> is read in most of the Arabic MSS., as well as in the two Hebrew versions; but what the author states here relates rather to the <i>eleventh</i> proposition, and indeed one of the two Leyden MSS. (cod. 18) bears <span dir=\"rtl\">אלחאדיהֹׁ עשר</span>. In several editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, after the word <span dir=\"rtl\">העשירית</span>, the numerical value <span dir=\"rtl\">וי״א</span> has been added, which exists neither in the MSS., nor in the edition princeps.",
    "fn.1353": "We have already seen that the body of the celestial sphere is called the <i>cinquième</i> body (see above, p. 25, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1); therefore, the body that would set the celestial sphere in motion would be a <i>sixième</i> body.",
    "fn.1354": "One might ask, at first glance, why the author introduced here, as a premise, the XI<sup>th</sup> proposition; for the XII<sup>th</sup> appears quite sufficient to demonstrate that the force diffused within the celestial sphere could not impart an infinite motion to the same. Samuel Ibn-Tibbon having submitted this question to the author, the latter replied to him, in the letter already cited, with details which it would be too lengthy to reproduce herein. He states, in substance, that the XII<sup>th</sup> proposition applies in an absolute manner only to a divisible force (such as, for example, the heat of fire, which is diffused only to a certain limited distance), whereas certain indivisible forces found within a finite body are not necessarily finite; thus, for instance, the thought of man ascends beyond the ninth sphere, and it is not demonstrated that it possesses a limit, albeit it is situated within a finite body. It was therefore necessary here, to demonstrate that the prime mover of the sphere could not be a force diffused within it, to conjoin, as premises, the XII<sup>th</sup> and the XI<sup>th</sup> propositions. The author will subsequently demonstrate that this mover cannot, furthermore, be an indivisible force.",
    "fn.1355": "Literally: <i>It shall therefore not be able to move infinitely, as one has posited in the XXVI<sup>th</sup> proposition</i>. It must be remembered that the author admitted the XXVI<sup>th</sup> proposition only as a <i>hypothesis;</i> it is for this reason that he states here: <i>as one has</i> <small>POSITED</small>, an expression which he does not employ when citing the other propositions, all of which are rigorously demonstrated.",
    "fn.1356": "Literally: <i>although it be indivisible</i>. The feminine forms <span dir=\"rtl\">באנת</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">מנקםמהֹׁ</span> would appear, according to the construction of the phrase, to refer to <span dir=\"rtl\">אלחרבהֹׁ</span>, <i>the motion;</i> but the sense requires one to supply the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלקוהֹׁ</span>, <i>the force</i>, which the author has evidently understood implicitly. It is therefore incorrectly that Ibn-Tibbon, in his Hebrew version, likewise employed the feminine, <span dir=\"rtl\">ואע״פ שהיא בלתי מתחלקת</span>: for, in Hebrew, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כח</span>, <i>force</i>, is masculine. Ibn-Falaquera (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 74) translated more accurately <span dir=\"rtl\">ואע״פ שאינו מתחלק</span>. Al-’Harizi, who uses the feminine (<span dir=\"rtl\">שהיא בלי מתחלקת</span>), may nevertheless have correctly apprehended the meaning: for he employs the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בח</span> as feminine.",
    "fn.1357": "That is to say: How can we suppose that this is its primary mover, since this mover itself is moved <i>by accident</i>, as we will expose later.",
    "fn.1358": "That is to say: I will explain myself more clearly on the subject of the application of the VI<sup>th</sup> proposition.",
    "fn.1359": "The term <span dir=\"rtl\">غُرْفَة</span>  refers here to the pavilion or the <i>upper chamber</i> which, in the East, is located on the platform of houses, and which, in Arabic as in Hebrew, also bears the name of ’<i>aliyya</i>. See my work, <i>Palestine</i>, p. 364.",
    "fn.1360": "One of the two Leiden manuscripts (cod. 18) simply states: <span dir=\"rtl\">אנתקלת אלנפם מעה איצׄא וצארת פי אלגרפהֹׁ</span>; similarly, the Hebrew version of Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">התנועעה הנפש עמו אל העליה</span> «The soul moves with him towards the pavilion.»",
    "fn.1361": "The accidental <i>movement</i> of the soul is that which it shares with the body after having itself put it in motion by the essential impulse it gives it; the local displacement is accidental for the soul. See, on our passage, Aristotle, Treatise <i>on the Soul</i>, book I, chap. III (§§ 6 and 7) and chap. IV (§ 9).",
    "fn.1362": "See above, p. 26, note 1.",
    "fn.1363": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version generally have <span dir=\"rtl\">ובשיתנועע</span>; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">וכשיניע</span>, as in the <i>princeps</i> edition.",
    "fn.1364": "Aristotle also notes that the soul one would suppose to be in the celestial sphere, condemned to perpetually impose a violent movement, would only have a painful existence and would be more wretched than the soul of any mortal animal, to which it is granted to rest and recreate through sleep; it would have the fate of Ixion, attached to the wheel that turns perpetually. See the treatise <i>on the Heavens</i>, book II, chapter I.",
    "fn.1365": "That is, Aristotle, whose opinion regarding the eternity of the world Maimonides will later combat.",
    "fn.1366": "That is to say, the division into four cases, of which the first, third, and fourth have proven impossible, so that only the second case remains admissible.",
    "fn.1367": "That is, it is not driven by any other motor, neither essentially nor accidentally, and it is itself immobile.",
    "fn.1368": "For the construction to be more regular, it would be necessary to add, before the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אן אלפלך</span>, the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">אלי</span>. Ibn-Tibbon translated, so as to alleviate the ellipsis of the preposition: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכבר יצא לנו מן העיון הזה במופת שהגלגל ובו׳</span>; nevertheless, some MSS. bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובבר הביא העיון הזה במופת שהגלגל ובו</span>. Al-’Harizi supplied a verb: <span dir=\"rtl\">והנה הביא זה העיון בדרך המופת להאמין בי הגלגל ובו׳</span>.",
    "fn.1369": "That is to say, the movement comes from outside, and therefore there must be a first mover.",
    "fn.1370": "Literally: <i>if their existence required that they exist only in conjunction</i>.",
    "fn.1371": "Literally: <i>the lack of necessary connection</i> (between the two). On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תלאזם</span>, see vol. I, p. 191, note 2.",
    "fn.1372": "That is, in the universal movement of the world. See above, at the beginning of this chapter. In Ibn-Tibbon's version, the constructed state <span dir=\"rtl\">in motion</span> is inaccurate; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">in motion</span>. Al-’Harîsi's version carries <span dir=\"rtl\">in motion</span>.",
    "fn.1373": "This is, in the universe, the matter of what is born and perishes; or, for example, in the movements emanating from the soul, and of which the author spoke earlier, the stone that is moved by the hand, and which no longer moves anything else.",
    "fn.1375": "The author intends to convey that this demonstration does not, strictly speaking, belong to Aristotle, but rather that it was he who furnished its principal elements. It is when he seeks to demonstrate the eternity of the world as a whole that Aristotle enters into particulars concerning the idea of the perishable and the imperishable, and concerning that which, in itself, is or is not subject to genesis and corruption; see, above all, the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, Book I, Chapter X et seq. One shall perceive that this third demonstration is based upon the theories of Ibn-Sina.",
    "fn.1376": "These words are missing in several editions of the Hebrew version, where one must add, with the <i>princeps</i> edition, <span dir=\"rtl\">או יהיו בולם הוות נפםדות</span>.",
    "fn.1377": "The author, interrogated by the translator Ibn-Tibbon concerning the precise meaning of this passage, explains himself almost thus in the letter cited above: When the <i>possible</i> is predicated of a species, it is requisite that it should exist actually in certain individuals of that species; for, were it never to exist in any individual, it would be <i>impossible</i> for the species, and by what right, then, could it be said to be <i>possible?</i> If, for example, one avers that writing is a thing possible for the human species, it is necessarily consequent that there should be men who write at some given time; for, should it be contended that there is never any man who writes, this would be tantamount to affirming that writing is impossible for the human species. It is not so with the <i>possible</i> which is predicated of an individual; for, if one says that this child may either write or not write, it does not follow from this possibility that the child must necessarily write at some particular moment. Thus, therefore, the <i>possible</i> predicated of a species is not, strictly speaking, within the category of the possible, but is in some wise <i>necessary</i>. — This explanation does not yet suffice to convey a clear apprehension of the conclusion which the author is about to draw from this proposition, to wit, that all beings would have necessarily perished. There is here, perchance, an obscure point upon which the author was unwilling to express himself more clearly, as indicated by the commentator Ephodi. Cf. the introduction to the first part, vol. I, p. 28, VII<sup>th</sup> cause. According to the indications of the commentator Schem-Tob, the author’s thought would be thus: the possibility attributed to an entire species is, like the species itself, an eternal thing; one cannot, strictly speaking, attribute a possibility to an eternal thing, and for such a thing, all that is possible shall be at the same time necessary. — In a word, the author evidently intended to convey that the hypothesis of contingency, for the universality of beings, is inadmissible; and if he did not clearly state this thesis, it is perchance because he feared to offend certain readers, by explicitly acknowledging that this demonstration, which he declares to be the strongest, is founded upon the principle of the eternity of the world.",
    "fn.1378": "The author wants to speak of the celestial sphere, which has a necessary existence and is not subject to contingency, although it does not have in itself the necessary cause of its existence, according to the distinction made by Ibn-Sinâ (see above, the XIX<sup>th</sup> proposition), and on which the author returns in the course of this demonstration.",
    "fn.1379": "The author, by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קאל</span>, dixit, evidently alludes to Ibn-Sīnā, who, as one has already stated (p. 18, n. 3), was the first to distinguish, in being, between that which is necessary in itself and that which is necessary by another. Cf., concerning the demonstration which is to follow, the analysis of the Metaphysics of Ibn-Sīnā, in Shahrastānī, pp. 375-376 (German transl. vol. II, pp. 253-255).",
    "fn.1380": "The author makes his reservations for the celestial sphere, in which, as will be seen later, he does not want to see, with Aristotle, an uncreated being.",
    "fn.1381": "<span dir=\"rtl\">Middaf</span>, which should be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">Midfa‘</span>, means literally <i>a means of repelling</i>. In the Hebrew version of Ibn-Tibbon, this word is rendered as <span dir=\"rtl\">dichah</span> (which should be placed before <span dir=\"rtl\">machloqs</span>, as the manuscripts have it).",
    "fn.1382": "Some mss. have the incorrect form <span dir=\"rtl\">אלעשריניה</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon imitated in Hebrew by <span dir=\"rtl\">העשרימיה</span>.",
    "fn.1383": "In other words: As soon as the <i>necessary existence</i> would be supposed to belong to several beings, these would form a <i>species</i> characterized by necessary existence, and, consequently, the idea of <i>necessary being</i> would be that of the species and would no longer belong to the essence of each of these beings.",
    "fn.1384": "That is to say, that Divinity cannot be composed of two principles, neither similar nor contrary to one another.",
    "fn.1385": "Literally: <i>dont il ne reste rien de redondant en dehors de son essence</i> (<i>qui soit</i>) <i>de son espèce</i>. The relative pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי</span> refers to <i>the necessary being</i>, which is implied here.",
    "fn.1386": "That is to say, that the absolute being has not emanated from any prior cause. — On the two words <span dir=\"rtl\">עלהֹׁ</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">סבב</span>, which are perfectly synonymous, see t. I, p. 313, note 1.",
    "fn.1387": "As the commentator Schem-Tob observes, this fourth speculation is fundamentally identical with the first demonstration, with the difference that, in the latter, the author, attaching himself particularly to the idea of movement in the universe, leads us to the first mover, whereas here he leads us back, in a more general manner, the series of effects and causes, to arrive at the absolute first cause.",
    "fn.1388": "Literally, <i>that which causes to pass was first causing to pass</i> (<i>or efficient</i>) in potentia, <i>then it became causing to pass in actu;</i> that is to say, that which caused a thing to pass from potency to act possessed first itself, in potency, the faculty of causing to pass to act, before this faculty was realised upon the object which it caused to pass to act.",
    "fn.1389": "See above, p. 31, note 2.",
    "fn.1390": "It should be recalled that, according to Ibn-Sīnā, whose theories the author has adopted, the other <i>separated</i> intelligences are, in relation to their own essence, within the category of the <i>possible</i>, and derive only from their cause or from God the quality of <i>necessary</i> beings; they do not constitute an absolute unity, for they may be numbered, as causes and effects. See the XVI<sup>th</sup> proposition.",
    "fn.1391": "The demonstration the author is about to give is borrowed from <i>Motécallemîn</i>, as can be seen in the first part of this work, chap. LXXV, II<sup>nd</sup> method (t. I, pag. 443).",
    "fn.1392": "See the I<sup>st</sup> part of this work, chap. LXXII. — On the sense that the verb 'to dispose' has here, see the XXV<sup>th</sup> proposition, and cf. p. 30, note 2.",
    "fn.1393": "Cf. t. I, p. 314, and ibid., note 1.",
    "fn.1394": "This method aims to demonstrate that, every body being composite, God, who is absolute simplicity, cannot be a body. It is comprised of two distinct demonstrations. In the first, based upon the XXII<sup>nd</sup> proposition, the body is considered from the perspective of its composition of matter and form, which presupposes an efficient cause, and which, consequently, precludes the perception in any body whatsoever of the absolute being, independent of all cause. In the second demonstration, the body is considered as a <i>composite being</i>, from the perspective of its divisible quantity, its composition of substance and accidents, and its quality as a complex being, in which there is simultaneously the idea of corporeality in general and that of any determined body whatsoever.",
    "fn.1395": "Literally: <i>parce qu’il est deux par l’expression</i> (or <i>the term</i>); for the word <i>corps</i> encompasses the idea of <i>corporéité</i> in general, and the idea of <i>such a body</i> in particular.",
    "fn.1396": "That is to say, the one based on creation <i>ex nihilo</i>, and which the author will explain later, after having given details on the celestial spheres and the intelligences.",
    "fn.1397": "See above, p. 35, note 4.",
    "fn.1398": "As the commentator Ephodi here observes, there can be no rigorous demonstration for the existence of God, based upon well-defined premises, since, as the author has stated elsewhere, God has no <i>anterior causes</i> and cannot be defined. The proofs adduced for the existence of God are thus of those which are based upon imperfect definitions, in which the anterior is defined by the posterior. See vol. I of this work, p. 190, and <i>ibid.</i>, notes 3 and 4. Cf. <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, pp. 192 and 193.",
    "fn.1399": "See the previous chapter, at the end of the 3<sup>e</sup> <i>speculation</i>, where the author has shown that, the existence of the <i>necessary being</i> having been established, it is easy to demonstrate that this being is <i>one</i> and incorporeal.",
    "fn.1400": "The author intends to convey that the proofs adduced at the close of Chapter I, for establishing the unity and incorporeality of God, are likewise independent of the theory of the world's eternity, and retain their full force even with the doctrine of <i>creation</i>. — Only one of our MSS., and that the least correct, bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכמא בינא פי תביין נפי אלגסמאניה</span>; Ibn-Tibbon likewise read the words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי תביין</span>, which he rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">בפירוש</span>.",
    "fn.1401": "All mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">metabeka delak</span>, <i>the conformity of this</i>. The <i>princeps</i> edition of the version of Ibn-Tibbon renders these words by <span dir=\"rtl\">hassavim</span> (for <span dir=\"rtl\">hasavmat zeh</span>); the other editions, as well as several mss., substitute <span dir=\"rtl\">hasshavucham</span>. Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">how can this matter be</span>.",
    "fn.1402": "Literally: <i>and how one has proven their existence;</i> i.e., and after one shall have ascertained how the philosophers proceeded to prove the existence of these intelligences. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אסהדל</span> ought to be pronounced as a past passive <span dir=\"rtl\">(أستدلّ)</span>. Ibn-Tibbon, by error, took it for an active future <span dir=\"rtl\">(أُسْتُدِلَّ)</span>, and translated <span dir=\"rtl\">ואיך אביא ראיה</span>, <i>and how shall I prove?</i> which has much perplexed the commentators. Al-’Harîzi rendered the sense accurately by translating: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואיך הביאו ראיה</span>, <i>and how they proved</i>.",
    "fn.1403": "Literally: <i>or to demonstrate what has been demonstrated of them</i>, that is to say, of these metaphysical subjects.",
    "fn.1404": "The author is referring to the general introduction placed at the head of the I<sup>rst</sup> part.",
    "fn.1405": "Literally: <i>from the affirmation</i>, that is to say, from the doctrine that affirms the existence of separate Intelligences.",
    "fn.1406": "On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פיץׄ</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">فيض</span>, see t. I, p. 244, note 1, and below, chap. XII.",
    "fn.1407": "Literally: <i>what you believe, nor that it comes to your mind</i>.",
    "fn.1408": "Such appears to be the sense of the Arabic phrase, irregularly constructed and which means literally: <i>and many difficulties will be resolved by the knowledge of this subject that I will summarize</i>.",
    "fn.1409": "Literally: <i>that its pole turns only on, etc</i>.",
    "fn.1410": "See vol. I, p. 9, note 2.",
    "fn.1411": "Literally: <i>I am returning to complete what I had attached myself to, or what I had committed to</i>.",
    "fn.1412": "More literally: <i>and who run most regularly</i>. <span dir=\"rtl\">أَجْرَاهَا</span> is a comparative <span dir=\"rtl\">(أُجْرِيَ)</span> with suffix, derived from the participle <span dir=\"rtl\">چاپ</span> , <i>running</i>, <i>marching</i>.",
    "fn.1413": "This treatise by Alexander of Aphrodisias, which no longer exists in Greek, appears to be the same one mentioned by Casiri, under the title of <i>De Rerum creatarum principiis</i>, and whose Arabic translation is found in the Arabic manuscript no. <small>DCCXCIV</small> of the Escorial. See Casiri, <i>Biblioth</i>. <i>arab</i>. <i>hisp</i>., vol. I, p. 242.",
    "fn.1414": "That is to say, the opinions of Aristotle.",
    "fn.1415": "Cf. the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, Book II, Chapter 2, in which Aristotle terms the heaven an animated being, possessing within itself a principle of motion (<i>ὁ δ’οὑρανὸς ἒμψυχος ϰαί ἒχει ϰινἠσεως ἀρχήν</i>). Elsewhere, however, Aristotle appears to attribute the circular motion of the heavens, like the rectilinear motion of the elements, to a nature inherent in the celestial body (<i>Ibid.</i>, Book I, Chapter 2), and at other times, he attributes it to a desire awakened within it by the supreme intelligence, towards which it is drawn (<i>Metaph</i>., XII, 7). Herein, as in the theory of the diverse intellects (see Vol. I, p. 304 et seq.), the obscurity and vagueness prevalent in Aristotle's theories have given rise to various interpretations. The Arabian philosophers, and notably Ibn-Sīnā, by combining the opinions of the Neoplatonic commentators with astronomical theories, have formulated, concerning the motion of celestial bodies and the order of the spheres and Intelligences, the doctrine developed by Maimonides in this and the succeeding chapters, and which, upon sundry points, was gainsaid by Ibn-Rushd. See Avicennae opera (Venice, 1495, in-fol.), Metaphysica, Book IX, Chapters <small>II</small>-<small>IV</small>; Shahrestānī, p. 380 et seq. (Ger. transl., Vol. II, p. 261 et seq.); <i>Al-Najāt</i>, or <i>Epitome of the Philosophy of Ibn-Sīnā</i> (at the end of the <i>Canon</i>), p. 71 et seq.; Averroes, Epitome in libros Metaphysicorum Aristotelis, Tract IV. Cf. Albertus Magnus, De Causis et processu universitatis, Book I, Tract IV, Chapters 7 and 8, and Book II, Tract II, Chapter 1 (opera omnia, Vol. V, p. 559 et seq., p. 586 et seq.); Saint Thomas Aquinas, De Substantiis separatis, Chapter II (opp. Vol. XVII, fol. 16 <i>verso</i> et seq.).",
    "fn.1416": "That is, the local movement of the celestial sphere.",
    "fn.1417": "The principle of motion proper to the elements, which is gravity or lightness, is designated by Aristotle as a <i>nature</i> (<i>φύσις</i>) inherent in the elements. These move in a straight line, sometimes in conformity with their nature (<i>ϰατἀ φύσιν</i>), as for example earth downwards and fire upwards, sometimes, and by an exterior force, contrary to their nature (<i>παρἀ φύσιν</i>]). See the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, I, 2; III, 2; IV, 3, and passim. Cf. above, p. 8, note 1, and p. 10, note.",
    "fn.1418": "Cf. the First Part of this work, chapter LXXII (t. I, p. 359).",
    "fn.1419": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تصورُ</span> is derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">صورة</span>, <i>form</i>, and signifies, according to the book <i>Ta’rîfât</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">حصول صورة الشى فى العقل</span>, <i>to receive into the intellect the form of a thing</i>. The infinitive, which one here renders by <i>conception</i>, might also be expressed by <i>thought</i> or <i>idea</i> (cf. Vol. I, p. 116, note 3); in the Arabic versions of Aristotle, the words <i>τὸ νοεῖν, νόησις</i> and <i>νόημα</i> are often rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">تصوّر بالعقل</span>, or simply by <span dir=\"rtl\">تصوُّر</span>. See, for example, the treatise <i>De Anima</i>, Book I, chap. 1 (§ 9): <i>Mἀλιστα δ’ ἒοιϰεν ἰδίῳ τὸ νοεῖν</i>; in Arabic: <span dir=\"rtl\">والذى يشبه ان يكون يخصها هو التصور بالعقل</span> (Hebraic MS. of the Imperial Library, ancient collection, n° 317, fol. 104 <i>recto</i>, col. <i>b</i>). <i>Ibid.</i>, chap. 4 (§ 14): <i>ϰαί τὸ νοεῖν δὴ</i>; in Arabic: <span dir=\"rtl\">والتصور بالعقل</span> (same MS., fol. 113, col. d). <i>Metaph.</i>, Book XII, chap. 7: <i>ἀρχὴ δὲ ἡ νόησις</i>; Hebraic version: <span dir=\"rtl\">וההתחלה הוא הציור בשבל</span>; Arabic-Latin version (in-folio, fol. 150, col. a): \"Principium autem est <i>imaginatio per intellectum</i>.\" The word <span dir=\"rtl\">تصور</span> also denotes the simple <i>notion</i> (<i>νόημα</i>), in which there is neither truth nor falsehood, and in this sense it is opposed to <span dir=\"rtl\">تصديق</span> (affirmation), indicating the combination of thoughts (<i>σύνθεσίς νοημάτων</i>), in which error and truth obtain. See Arist., <i>Categories</i>, chap. 2 beginning, and chap. 4 (II) end; treatise <i>De Interpretatione</i>, chap. <small>I</small>; treatise <i>De Anima</i>, Book III, chap. 6 (§ 1); and <i>ibid.</i> the commentary of Averroës (in-folio edition, fol. 171, col. <i>c</i>).",
    "fn.1420": "That is, of an intelligence it possesses within itself, similar to the rational faculty of man, and which should not be confused with the separated intelligence <i>séparée</i>, which is outside the sphere. Cf. the I<sup>re</sup> part of this work, chap. <small>LXXII</small> (p. 373-374).",
    "fn.1421": "Cf. Aristotle, <i>Metaph.</i>, Book XII, chap. 7 (Brandis edition, p. 248): <i>Ẻπιθυμητὸν γἁρ τὸ φαινόμενον ϰαλόν, βουλητὀν δὲ πρῶτον τὸ ὂν ϰαλόν</i>. Conformably to the doctrine of Ibn-Sinâ, Maimonides considers God and the other separated Intelligences as the <i>final causes</i> of the movement of the celestial spheres, which, as has been observed, possess within themselves the <i>efficient causes</i> immediate to their motion, namely their <i>souls</i> and their <i>intellects</i>.",
    "fn.1422": "That is to say, in the benevolent influence that the celestial body exerts on the sublunar world. Cf. book I, chapter <small>LXXII</small>, p. 361 and following.",
    "fn.1423": "That is to say, the movement in some of them is faster or slower than in others, and they are not all directed in the same direction. See for this paragraph the <i>Métaphys</i>. of Aristotle, book XII, chapter 8. The author has mixed the considerations of Aristotle with the astronomical theories that were current among the Arabs, particularly those of Ptolemy.",
    "fn.1424": "The author refers, on the one hand, to the <i>diurnal</i> movement that the upper sphere, or the ninth sphere, performs from east to west in the space of 24 hours, and, on the other hand, to the <i>periodic</i> movement of the planet Saturn, from west to east, which lasts 30 years. Cf. vol. I<sup>er</sup>, p. 357, note 3.",
    "fn.1425": "That is to say, none of his contemporaries.",
    "fn.1426": "This is not altogether exact, and the author merely intended to provide a round number. Aristotle states that, according to one system, one must admit of fifty-five spheres, and according to another, forty-seven. See <i>Metaph.</i>, XII, 8. Ibn-Sinâ, whom our author doubtless followed, expresses himself with greater exactitude by stating that, according to Aristotle, if the Intelligences correspond to the spheres, there will be <i>approximately</i> fifty of them: \"et sequetur secundum sententiam magistri primi quod sunt <i>circiter quin-quaginta et amplius</i>.\" See <i>Avicennœ opera</i>, <i>Metaphysica</i>, Book IX, chap. 3 (fol. 104 <i>recto</i>, col. <i>b</i>).",
    "fn.1427": "The author intends to convey, as it appears to one, that by positing for a planet an <i>inclined</i> sphere – that is, a sphere whose axis is oblique to the ecliptic, and whose poles consequently diverge from those of the sphere of the fixed stars – one accounts simultaneously for 1° the periodic motion in longitude, or from west to east, 2° the motion of declination towards the north or south, and 3° the motion discernible upon the circle of the horizon in the arcs comprised between the equator and the limit of the rising and setting of each planet (<i>ortive amplitude</i> and <i>occident amplitude</i>); for, owing to the motion of declination, the points of the risings and settings of the planets vary from day to day within the extent of these arcs of the horizon.",
    "fn.1428": "Literally: <i>in that into which we were</i>.",
    "fn.1430": "Literally: <i>and it must be that what causes to go out</i> (<i>or to act</i>) <i>is of the same kind as what is caused to go out</i>.",
    "fn.1431": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s Hebrew version bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">יחם בל שכל נבדל המיוהד לגלגל ההוא</span>; one observs that there is lacking the equivalent of the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">בכל פלך</span>. In the manuscripts of this version one reads, as in that of Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">המיוחד בגלגל לגלגל ההוא</span>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בגלגל</span> renders the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">באלפלך</span>, which some Arabic manuscripts bear instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בבל פלך</span>.",
    "fn.1432": "That is, for <i>Aristotle</i>, to whom our author attributes the core of the theory he has just exposed.",
    "fn.1433": "Literally: <i>but</i> (<i>it is</i>) <i>as</i> (<i>when</i>) <i>it burns by means of the intermediary</i>, <i>etc.</i>—Ibn-Tibbon, having taken the words <span dir=\"rtl\">פכמא אן</span> in their ordinary sense of <i>just as</i>, wrote, to complete the sentence, <span dir=\"rtl\">כן הגלגל</span> (<i>likewise</i> the sphere), whereas all the Arabic MSS. simply bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלפלך</span> (<i>and</i> the sphere).",
    "fn.1434": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">الْمَلَائِكَةُ الْمُقَرَّبُونَ</span> are borrowed from the Qur'an (chap. IV, v. 170), where they designate the first class of angels, or the <i>cherubim</i>. One could not admit that Maimonides, in a work intended for Jews, should have intentionally reproduced an expression from the Qur'an. The entire passage is doubtless borrowed from one of the Arab philosophers, probably from Ibn-Sînâ, or from Al-Gazàli. The latter, speaking of the philosophers, expresses himself in these terms (<i>Destruction of the Philosophers</i>, XVIth question, verse, Hebrew): <span dir=\"rtl\">וכבר חשבו שהמלאבים השמימיים הם נפשות הגלגלים ושהמלאבים הנקראים ברובים המתקרבים הם השבלים המופשטים</span>. “They contend that the <i>celestial angels</i> are the souls of the spheres, and that the <i>approaching angels</i>, called <i>cherubim</i>, are the Abstract Intelligences (or <i>separated ones</i>).” Cf. Averroes, The Destruction of the Destruction, disputation <small>XVI</small>, at the commencement. — Regarding the identification of angels with the separated Intelligences, one may consult hereinafter, chap. <small>VI</small>.",
    "fn.1435": "In other terms: As the Intelligences do not constitute diverse essences, distinct one from another, so as to be numbered as diverse units, it follows that they can only be so numbered in so far as they are causes and effects one of another; so that God is the immediate cause only of the first Intelligence, which in its turn is the cause of the second Intelligence, and so forth. One may consult the <small>XVIth</small><sup></sup> of the propositions placed at the head of this II<sup></sup> part.",
    "fn.1436": "That is, the sphere of the moon.",
    "fn.1437": "Literally: This leads to the existence of separate Intelligences.",
    "fn.1438": "That is to say: Since, on the one hand, the first Intelligence shares this in common with the others, that it sets its respective sphere in motion, and since, on the other hand, all the intelligences are distinguished one from another in that they are causes and effects one of another, one may distinguish in the first, as in all the others, two different ideas, for it is at once the mover of the first sphere and the efficient cause of the second Intelligence; it is therefore <i>composite</i>, and it cannot be considered as the <i>Necessary</i> Being, which is of an absolute simplicity. Cf. chap. XXII of this II<sup></sup> part.",
    "fn.1439": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">וראיותיו</span> is missing here in almost all editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s translation, but it can be found in the manuscripts and in the <i>princeps</i> edition.",
    "fn.1440": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">שׄבלם</span>, which all editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have here, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">םבלם</span>, the name of the action of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">םבל</span>, which, like the Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">احتمل</span> , has the sense of <i>support, sustain, be admissible</i> or <i>possible</i>. The suffix pronoun refers to <i>his proofs</i>.",
    "fn.1441": "That is to say, by the intellect they possess within themselves (and which must not be confounded with the separated Intelligence), they have the <i>conception</i> or the <i>thought</i> of the Divine Being, which they afterwards <i>perceive</i> through the <i>desire</i> that attracts them towards it. At the same time, they perceive their principles; that is to say, each of them perceives the Intelligences which are superior to it, and from which they more directly emanate.",
    "fn.1442": "Literally: <i> who praise and glorify it; and what praise! and what glorification!</i>",
    "fn.1443": "Literally: <i>he who would believe that this is the language of the state</i> or <i>of the attitude;</i> that is to say, the mute and figurative language which, in our thought, one attributes to objects. “The expression <span dir=\"rtl\">لسان الحال</span>,” says Silvestre de Sacy, “is a metaphor employed when speaking of things of which the mere sight proves as well, and often better than all words, the truth of a fact.” It is thus that one says in French: <i>Facts speak with evidence</i>. “Thus the Arabs say that the leanness of a man, his haggard and emaciated mien, his worn and torn raiment, declare, <i>by the language of their state</i>, that he has been the sport of ill fortune, and implore for him the commiseration of generous men.” One may consult <i>Chrest. ar.</i> (2<sup></sup> edition), vol. I, p. 461. Maimonides signifies that those are far from the truth who imagine that in the words of the Psalmist it is a question of a language which exists only in the imagination of the poet, and which by metaphor he attributes to the heavens; for the celestial spheres, being living and intelligent entities, possess truly a language within themselves, and not merely in our thought, albeit their language consists not in words. — The words <span dir=\"rtl\">לשון הענין</span>, by which Ibn-Tibbon’s version renders the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">לםאן אלחאל</span>, are of scant intelligibility; Al-’Harizi, in order to intimate the true sense, has supplied: <span dir=\"rtl\">לשון הענין הנראה מהם</span>.",
    "fn.1444": "Literally: <i>he describes their state in themselves, I mean the state of the spheres, and not the state of human reflection regarding them</i>, that is to say, the reflection that men make in contemplating them.",
    "fn.1445": "See t. I, at the end of chapter L and chapter LXIV (p. 288).",
    "fn.1446": "The author alludes to the prayer that one must recite after the appearance of the new moon, and where one says, speaking of the stars: <span dir=\"rtl\">ששים ושמחים לעשות רצון קונם ובו׳</span>, <i>they rejoice in doing the will of their creator, etc</i>.; which proves that they are attributed with intelligence.",
    "fn.1447": "For example, in the morning prayer: <span dir=\"rtl\">פנות צבאות קדושים ובו׳</span>, \"the chiefs of the armies of saints, exalting the Almighty, constantly recount the glory and sanctity of God.\"",
    "fn.1448": "In the Talmud, it is said allegorically that the sun traverses the sky, rising in the east and setting in the west, in order to greet the Creator daily, as it is said: <i>And the celestial armies prostrate themselves before you</i>. See the treatise <i>Synhedrîn</i>, fol. 91 <i>b</i>, and the <i>Midrash Yalkout</i>, n° 1071.",
    "fn.1449": "According to the Talmud (tractate ’<i>Hullin</i>, fol. 91 <i>b</i>), it is the Israelites whom one designates here allegorically by the words <i>morning stars</i>, whilst by <i>the sons of God</i> one understands the angels, who, according to Maimonides, are naught else but the <i>Intelligences of the spheres</i>. The verse from Job is therefore explained thus: <i>After the believers have chanted their morning hymns</i>, <i>the angels likewise intone their celestial songs</i>.",
    "fn.1450": "Vide the Midrash, <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. II (fol. 2, col. <i>c</i>). According to our author, the words <i>tohou</i> and <i>bohou</i> in Genesis (1, 2), which signify <i>formless</i> and <i>void</i>, or <i>in a chaotic state</i>, are considered by the <i>Midrash</i> as participles bearing the sense of <i>lamenting, grieving</i>. —One has reproduced the words of the <i>Midrash</i> as they are found in all Arabic and Hebrew manuscripts of the <i>Guide</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אני והן</span>, <i>I and them</i>, the editions of the <i>Midrash</i> bear <span dir=\"rtl\">העליונים והתחתונים</span>, <i>the superior and the inferior;</i> it is the editors of Ibn-Tibbon's version who, to render the phrase more correct, changed the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">נבראו</span>, <i>have been created</i>, to <span dir=\"rtl\">נבראנו</span>, <i>we have been created</i>.",
    "fn.1451": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">הגלגלים</span>, <i>the spheres</i>; the mss. have, in accordance with the Ar. text, <span dir=\"rtl\">השמים</span>, <i>the heavens</i>.",
    "fn.1452": "Literally: <i>Of those who carry our Law</i>, i.e., who are its depositaries and have the mission to watch over its conservation. Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וחבמי תורתנו</span>, <i>the sages of our Law;</i> Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומקבלי תורתנו</span>, <i>those who have received our Law;</i> It would be better to translate in Hebrew: <span dir=\"rtl\">ונושׂאי תורתנו</span>.",
    "fn.1453": "See vol. I, chap. <small>LXXII</small>, p. 361 et seq., and infra, chap. <small>X</small> et seq., and Cf. above, p. 55, note 2.",
    "fn.1454": "See vol. I, p. 362, note 2.",
    "fn.1455": "See the I<sup>st</sup> part, chap. <small>II</small>, p. 37.",
    "fn.1456": "Literally: <i>The</i> E<small>LOHIM</small> <i>and the</i> A<small>DONIM</small> <i>could not be of the human species</i>, that is, taking these words in the sense of <i>judges</i> and of <i>rulers</i>.",
    "fn.1457": "That is to say: human beings, even distinguished individuals, are of a rank too low to be directly related to God, and for one to believe that glorifying God consists in saying that He is their judge and ruler.",
    "fn.1458": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלהים</span> in several editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version is an error; one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלהיכם</span>.",
    "fn.1459": "See the I<sup>st</sup> part, chap. <small>XLIX</small>.",
    "fn.1460": "Albertus Magnus, who contends against this identification of angels with separate Intelligences, states that this is a theory belonging particularly to Isaac Israeli, to Maimonides, and to other Jewish philosophers: “Ordines autem intelligentiarum quos non determinavimus quidam dicunt esse ordines angelorum, et intelligentias vocant angelos; et hoc quidem dicunt Isaac et Rabbi Moyses et cæteri philosophi Judæorum. Sed nos hoc verum esse non credimus. Ordines enim angelorum distinguuntur secundum differentias illuminationum et theophaniarum, quæ revelatione accipiuntur et fide creduntur, et ad perfectionem regni cœlestis ordinantur in gratia et beatitudine. De quibus philosophia nihil potest per rationem philosophicam determinare.” Vide <i>De causis et processu universitatis</i>, liv. I, tract. <small>IV</small>, cap. 8 (opp. t. V, pag. 563 <i>a</i>). One has, however, seen that the Arab philosophers profess the same opinion concerning angels as Maimonides. Cf. above, pag. 60, note 3.",
    "fn.1461": "That is, of sublunar things that are born and perish: For the words <span dir=\"rtl\">עבע הייו היי</span>, <i>what is</i>, one reads in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon. <span dir=\"rtl\">עובי וביי היי</span>, <i>whose movement is</i>; the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">עובי איי</span>, which is in accordance with the Arabic text.",
    "fn.1462": "See note 4 below. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מסתכל</span> (having <i>regardé</i>), on which the author lays particular stress here, is found neither in the Talmudic passages cited below nor in analogous passages in the <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. 8; perhaps this reading existed in some <i>Midrasch</i> that has not come down to us.",
    "fn.1463": "Literally: <i>be astonished at that which they say: having</i> <small>LOOKED</small>. <span dir=\"rtl\">אעגׄב</span> is here the imperative, and it is erroneously that Ibn-Tibbon rendered this word by the aorist <span dir=\"rtl\">אתמה</span>, <i>I am astonished</i>. Al-’Harizi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויש לתׄמוה</span>, <i>one must be astonished;</i> Ibn-Falaquéra employs the imperative <span dir=\"rtl\">ותמה</span> (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, pag. 86).",
    "fn.1464": "Cf. Munk, Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe, pp. 100-102 and pp. 253-254.",
    "fn.1465": "Vide Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Synhedrîn</i>, fol. 38 <i>b</i>, and Jerusalem Talmud, same tractate, chap. I. According to Moses of Narbonne and other commentators, the difference would consist in the suppression of the formula <span dir=\"rtl\">כביכול</span>, <i>if one may so speak;</i> but one believes that it consists rather in the employment of the word <i>consulted</i>, which bears not the same import as the word <i>looked</i>, and which would not occasion a comparison with the words of Plato.",
    "fn.1466": "The author means that by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">פמליא של מעלה</span> the Talmud understands the <i>upper army</i> or <i>celestial</i>; it is probably in favor of the rapprochement he made with certain words of Plato that he likes to give the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פמליא</span> a Greek origin, whereas it is clearly the Latin word <i>familia</i>.",
    "fn.1467": "See <i>Bereshit Rabbah</i>, section 12 (fol. 10, col. <i>b</i>), and the <i>Midrash</i> of <i>Kohelet</i>, or the Ecclesiastes (fol. 65, col. <i>b</i>), where, in the obscure words of Ecclesiastes, God is considered the subject of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">asah</span>, <i>they made</i>.",
    "fn.1468": "Vide <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. 51 (fol. 45, col. <i>d</i>). — The purport of this passage is as follows: whenever in Holy Scripture one reads (<span dir=\"rtl\">ויהוה</span>) <i>and</i> the Eternal, without one being able rigorously to justify the employment of the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>, <i>and</i>, this latter indicates that the action is attributed at once to God and to the angels who compose His tribunal.",
    "fn.1469": "Literally: <i>that there is there</i> (i.e., nigh unto God) <i>language</i>, — <i>how far is He above that!</i> — <i>or reflection, or examination, or consultation and desire to avail oneself of the opinion of another</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's translation, <span dir=\"rtl\">שיש לו יתעלה דברים</span>, is not altogether literal. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">או התבוננות</span> ought to be placed before <span dir=\"rtl\">או שאלת עצה</span>, as indeed the manuscripts have it.",
    "fn.1470": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר</span> , Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harizi have read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלוף</span>  (with <i>resch</i>), because they translate both: <span dir=\"rtl\">מה מאד רע</span>; Ibn-Falaquéra translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">מה מאד קשה</span> (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, pag. 87).",
    "fn.1471": "More literally: <i>and one would discern therein a greatness and a might regarding God, and a wisdom from the Most High</i>. In several manuscripts, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">עטׄמה̈ קדרה̈</span> without the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>, similarly in the versions of Ibn-Tibbon and Ibn-Falaquéra <span dir=\"rtl\">עוצם יכולת</span>, <i>the greatness of power</i>, whilst that of Al-’Harizi bears <span dir=\"rtl\">גדולה ותפארת</span>.",
    "fn.1472": "Literally: <i>he would flee from this</i>.",
    "fn.1473": "See <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, section 50 (folio 44, column <i>d</i>).",
    "fn.1474": "See <i>ibid.</i>, sect. 78 (fol. 68, col. <i>a</i>); cf. <i>Ekhâ rabbathi</i>, or <i>Midrasch</i> of the Lamentations of Jeremiah, at chap. 3, v. 22 (fol. 56, col. <i>b</i>), and Babylonian Talmud, tractate ’<i>Haghîgâ</i>, fol. 14 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.1475": "See <i>Beréchîth rabbâ</i>, <i>l. c.</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">זה מיכאל וגבריאל שהן שרים של מעלה דכולא מתחלפין ואנון לא מתחלפין</span>; that is to say, that Micael and Gabriel are among the <i>superior princes</i>, or the angels of the <sup>first</sup> class, who are not transitory like the others and who recite hymns every day.",
    "fn.1476": "On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הבל</span> , cf. t. I, pag. 77, note 5.",
    "fn.1477": "That is, in the <i>Beréschîth rabbâ;</i> see section 85 (folio 75, column <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.1478": "That is, God destined it for some physical function.",
    "fn.1479": "See the <i>Midrasch Kohéleth</i> (fol. 82, col. <i>a</i>) on the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כי עוף השמים ובו׳</span>, <i>for the bird of the heaven shall carry the voice, and that which has wings shall tell the matter</i> (Ecclesiastes, 10, 20): <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר ר׳ בון בשעה שהאדם ישן הגוף אומר לנשמה והנשמה לנפש והנפש למלאך ומלאך לכרוב והכרוב לבעל כנפים מי הוא זה השרף והשרף יוליך דבר ויגיד לפני מי שאמר והיה העולם</span>. «R. Boun says: Whilst man sleeps, the body speaks unto the sensitive soul, the latter unto the rational soul, this unto the angel, this unto the cherub, and this last unto the winged creature, which is the seraph; this last, finally, carries away the word and repeats it before Him <i>who spake, and the world was</i>.» Cf. <i>Wayyikra rabbâ</i>, or <i>Midrasch</i> of Leviticus, sect. 32 (fol. 172, col. <i>b</i>).",
    "fn.1480": "According to the author, the <i>Midrasch</i> would have designated by the <i>soul</i> the senses in general, or, if one prefers, the <i>common sense;</i> this transmits to the imagination, here called <i>angel</i>, the impressions received, and the imagination, in its turn, transmits them to the intelligence, designated by the name of <i>cherub</i>. Nevertheless, the entirety of the passage cited in the preceding note appears to us but little favourable to this interpretation, and it is more probable that the words <i>angel</i>, <i>cherub</i> and <i>seraph</i> here designate superior beings, intermediate between God and man. See the <i>Yephé toar</i>, or the commentary of Samuel Yaphé on the <i>Wayyikrâ rabbâ</i>, sect. 32, § 2.",
    "fn.1481": "Literally: <i>How fair is this for him that knows, but how foul shall it be for the ignorant!</i> The author intends that the explanation he has just given of the passage from the <i>Midrasch</i> shall be greatly approved by learned men, but shall much displease the ignorant, who will prefer to take the words <i>angel</i> and <i>cherub</i> in their literal sense and to believe in a mysterious converse of the soul with superior beings.",
    "fn.1482": "That is to say, the form that the prophet sees exists only in his imagination and has no objective reality. See the I<sup>st</sup> part, chap. <small>XLIX</small>.",
    "fn.1483": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אנת</span>, some manuscripts read <span dir=\"rtl\">אנך</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(إِيَّكَ)</span> .",
    "fn.1484": "All mss. carry <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמלאבים</span> in the plural, and <span dir=\"rtl\">כאנה</span> with the singular suffix; similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">יראו המלאכים כאלו הוא איש</span>.",
    "fn.1485": "That is, in the <i>Beréschîth rabbâ;</i> see section 50 (folio 44, column <i>d</i>), where it is explained why the celestial messengers appeared to Abraham as men <i>(Gen., <small>XVIII</small>, 2), and to Lot, as birds <i>(Ibid.</i>, <small>XIX</small>, 1).",
    "fn.1486": "Literally: <i>and</i> (<i>further on</i>) <i>the discourse shall fall</i> (or <i>return</i>) <i>upon prophecy concerning what is meet</i> (<i>to say thereof</i>). — The mystery which, according to our author, would be indicated in the passage from the <i>Midrasch</i>, appears to be this: that visions possess no objective reality and are but the effect of imagination, and that, the stronger and more perfect the imaginative faculty, the more the objects one believs to see depart from their vague and uncertain form and approach reality. Abraham, therefore, a true prophet and endowed with great imaginative power, saw before him the divine messengers in a form human and well-defined, whilst Lot beheld them only in the vague and nebulous shape of those redoubtable and fantastic beings, created by a distempered imagination. Commentators remark that the sense which the author here attributs to the passage from the <i>Midrasch</i> appears to be in contradiction with what he says further on, in Chapter <small>XLV</small>, where, in enumerating the different degrees of prophetic visions, he placs visions of <i>angels</i> above visions of <i>men</i>. One may reply with Joseph ibn-Kaspi that here there is question of a distinction in the very nature of the vision, which may supervene upon man either in a waking state, or in a dream. For Abraham the vision was clear and distinct, whilst for Lot it was confused: the one was awake, the other dreamed; but, in each species of visions, the appearance of a human figure is inferior to that of an angel; that is to say, the perception of a sublunary being is beneath the perception of the superior Intelligences, called <i>angels</i>.",
    "fn.1487": "Literally: «they clothed themselves with <i>angelicity</i>.» In our text, one has reproduced this passage from the <i>Midrasch</i> as it is found in all the Arabic and Hebrew MSS. of the <i>Guide</i>. In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">קראם אנשים</span>. In the editions of the <i>Midrasch</i> (<i>loc. cit.</i>), the passage is thus conceived: <span dir=\"rtl\">ער שלא עשו שליחותן קראן אנשים משעשו שליחותן מלאבים</span>. The author, who often cited from memory, appears to have taken the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לבשו מלאבות</span> from another passage, which, in the <i>Midrasch</i>, preceds ours.",
    "fn.1488": "That is, what, in the view of Aristotle, is contrary to ours. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">יכׄאלפנא</span>, some mss. read <span dir=\"rtl\">יכׄאלפוא</span>, which is clearly a mistake. Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harizi appear to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">יכׄאלפה</span>; the former translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל אשר יחלוק בזה כולו</span>; the latter: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל מחלקתו שהוא חולק בזה הענין בולו</span>.",
    "fn.1489": "Cf. the t. I, p. 363-364, and below, chap. <small>X</small>.",
    "fn.1490": "The author means that the celestial spheres and intelligences do not act without will, like the blind forces of the sublunar nature. Cf. above, p. 52, n. 2.",
    "fn.1491": "Literally: <i>they choose</i> (<i>freely</i>) <i>and govern;</i> that is to say: in the governance of the world entrusted to them, they act with full liberty. The two participles <span dir=\"rtl\">מכׄתארה̈</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">ומרבה</span> are connected, and the first should be considered in some sort as an adverb to the second, as if there were <span dir=\"rtl\">ומדברה באחתיאר</span>; this becoms evident from what is said further on: <span dir=\"rtl\">מלוכהם להם אראדה ואכתיאר פי מא פוץ' להם</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">מן אלתדביר</span> «and that they possess the will and the liberty in the governance entrusted unto them».",
    "fn.1492": "Literally: <i>that they perceive</i> (or <i>comprehend</i>) <i>their actions</i>. The pronominal suffix must refer to the spheres and the intelligences. The author has employed the masculine plural, having in mind the <i>angels</i>, of which the text makes mention, and which, according to him, are nought else than the forces emanating from the celestial spheres and their intelligences. In the passages cited, one doubtless ascribs to these angels a perfect freedom of action.",
    "fn.1493": "Ibn-Tibbon renders: <span dir=\"rtl\">במה שהושפע להם</span>, “which has been <i>inspired</i> unto them,” and likewise immediately thereafter <span dir=\"rtl\">במה שהושפע לנו</span>. Ibn-Falaquera has already noted this error, remarking that the translator confounded together the roots <span dir=\"rtl\">فوضى</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">فيض</span>, of which the second is construed with <span dir=\"rtl\">على</span>, and not with <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span>. Vide <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, appendix, p. 154.",
    "fn.1494": "The word <i>privation</i> has here the Aristotelian sense of the Greek word <i>στέρησις</i>; the author means that for us the <i>power</i> precedes the <i>act</i> (for, although we have the faculty to act, we do not always act in reality), whereas the spheres and intelligences are always <i>in act</i> in all respects.",
    "fn.1495": "The manuscripts have, some <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדאעיה̈</span>, while others <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדאיעה̈</span>. Al-’Harîzi, who has <span dir=\"rtl\">המחייבות</span>, appears to have expressed the first of these two readings. We prefer the second, which should be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">الذائعة</span> , from the root <span dir=\"rtl\">ذيع</span> , and which Ibn-Tibbon has correctly translated as <span dir=\"rtl\">המתפשטים</span>.",
    "fn.1496": "Literally: <i>a des sons fort effrayants et grands</i>. In most mss., the adverb <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄדא</span> is placed before <span dir=\"rtl\">עטׄימה̈</span>; the manuscript of Leiden, n° 18, has <span dir=\"rtl\">האילה̈ עטׄימה̈ גׄדא</span>, a reading that has been followed by the two Hebrew translators.",
    "fn.1497": "Literally: <i>which are nigh unto us</i>. The Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">التي لدينا</span> exactly reproduce the Greek words <i>τῶν παρ’ ἡμῖν</i> which are found in the passage of Aristotle whereto allusion is here made. Vide the treatise <i>De Coelo</i>, Book II, chap. 9: <i>δοϰεῖ γάρ τισιν ὰναγϰαῖον εἶναι τηλίϰούτων φερομένων σω–μάτων γίγνεσθαι ψόφον, ἐπεὶ ϰαὶ τῶν παρ’ ἡμῖν οὔτε τοὺϛ ὄγϰουϛ ἐχόντων ἴσουϛ οὔτε τοίούτῳ ταχει φερομἐνων. ϰ. τ. λ.</i>",
    "fn.1498": "Literally: <i>and they have an allegation of causes</i>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">ויש להם לתת עלה</span>, is not exact. The cause they allege is that we are accustomed from birth to these perpetual sounds, which no contrast of silence makes resound for our ears. See Aristotle, <i>l. c.</i>",
    "fn.1499": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version bear <span dir=\"rtl\">באמונתינו</span>, <i>“in our belief;”</i> but the MSS., as likewise the commentaries, have <span dir=\"rtl\">באומתנו</span>. According to certain commentators, the author does make allusion to a passage from Ezekiel (ch. I, <small>v</small>. 24): <i>“And I heard the noise of their wings, like the noise of great waters, as the voice of the Almighty.”</i> It is more than probable that he had in view a Talmudic passage, which one cits in the succeeding note.",
    "fn.1500": "The author doubtless intends to speak of a passage from the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Yoma</i>, fol. 20 <i>b</i>, in which one reads the following: <span dir=\"rtl\">שלש קולות הולכין מםוף העולם ועד םופו ואלו הן קול גלגל חמה וקול המונה של רומי וקול נשמה בשעה שיוצאה מן הגוף</span> “Three voices resound from one extremity of the world unto the other; these are: the voice of the sphere of the sun, the tumult of the city of Rome, and the cry of the soul as it quitts the body.” Cf. <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. 6 (fol. 5, col. <i>d</i>).",
    "fn.1501": "Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Pesa’hîm</i>, fol. 94 <i>b</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמי ישראל אומרים גלגל קבוע ומזלות חוזרין וחכמי אומות העולם אומרים גלגל חזזר ומזלות קבועין</span> “The sages of Israel say: The sphere remains fixed and the stars revolve; the sages of the nations of the world say: the sphere revolvs and the stars remain fixed.” Aristotle likewise relats the opinion of the Pythagoreans with that which ascribs motion to the stars themselves and not to the sphere in which they are fixed. Vide <i>De Coelo</i>, Book II, chap. 9: <i>Ὅσα μὲν γὰρ αὐτὰ φέρεται, ποιεῖ ψόφον ϰαἰ πληγήν. ὅσα δ’ἐν φερομένῳ ἐνδέδεται ἢ ἐνυπάρχει, ϰαθάπερ ἐν τῷ πλοίῳ τὰ μόρια οὐχ οἶόν τε ψοφεῖν, ϰ. τ. λ.</i>. According to Aristotle, who contends against the opinion of the Pythagoreans, the stars remain fixed within their respective spheres, which carry them along in their motion. Vide <i>ibid.</i>, chap. 8.",
    "fn.1502": "These words are not found, in our editions of the Talmud, following the passage from <i>Pesa’hîm</i> which one has cited in the preceding note; hence certain Jewish authors have expressed their astonishment at this citation by Maimonides. Vide R. Azariah de’ Rossi, <i>Meôr’Enaïm</i>, chap. XI (edit. of Berlin, f. 48), and the <i>Sépher ha-berîth</i> (Brünn, 1797, in-4°), Part <sup>I</sup>, Book II, chap. 10 (fol. 14 <i>b</i>]). Nevertheless, several authors do state with Maimonides, and by citing the same passage from the Talmud, that, upon this point, the sages of Israel confessed themselves vanquished by the sages of the other nations. Vide Isaac Arama, <i>’Akédà</i>, chap. XXXVII (edit. of Presburg, 1849, in-8°, vol. II, fol. 39 <i>a</i>); David Gans, in his astronomical work entitled <i>Ne’hemâd we-na’îm</i>, §§ 13 and 25. The latter, after having cited the passage from the treatise <i>Pesa’hîm</i>, adds that the great astronomer Tycho-Brahe had told him that the sages of Israel were in error to confess themselves vanquished and to adopt the opinion of the pagan savants, which was evidently false. — One must suppose that the words cited by Maimonides were to be found, at least in his day, in certain MSS. of the Talmud; the other authors who cite them may have drawn them from Maimonides’ work without verifying the citation.",
    "fn.1503": "Literally: This, whose demonstration has been proven and established.",
    "fn.1504": "See above, p. 56.",
    "fn.1505": "Literally: <i>they only counted a single globe, embracing several spheres</i>. This turn of phrase is irregular and somewhat obscure, and it is probably to make it clearer that Ibn-Tibbon added the words: <span dir=\"rtl\">וחשבוהו לאחד</span>, <i>and they considered it a single</i>. The sense is: 'They often counted a certain globe as one, although it encompassed several spheres.'",
    "fn.1506": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>’Haghigâ</i>, folio 12 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.1507": "The reading which one has adopted in one's text, albeit somewhat irregular, is that of most manuscripts. In one of the Leyden manuscripts (no. 18), the second <span dir=\"rtl\">כרה̈</span> has been suppressed, and it is likewise omitted in the version of Ibn-Tibbon. It would have been more regular to place, instead of the first <sup> </sup> <span dir=\"rtl\">כרה̈</span>, the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">אכר</span>, and this is what Al-’Harîzi has done, who translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">היה מונה כידורי הבכבים בלם אחד</span>.",
    "fn.1508": "All mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">פלך</span> in the singular; it would have been more regular to say <span dir=\"rtl\">מַלְכִּי</span> , in the dual.",
    "fn.1509": "Literally: for there is no demonstration that indicates the order of these two globes.",
    "fn.1510": "The author emphasizes this point, which will be established later, as necessary to confine all the spheres within four globes, which, according to the author, would be indicated by the <i>four animals</i> of Ezekiel. Cf. the next chapter and the III<sup>rd</sup> part, chapter II.",
    "fn.1511": "See <i>Almagest</i>, or <i>Great Composition</i> of Ptolemy, book IX, chap. I.",
    "fn.1512": "Literally: <i>Then came men of the last</i> (or <i>modern</i>) <i>men in Andalusia</i>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">other men</span> <i>other men</i>, which is incorrect; one should say <span dir=\"rtl\">last men</span>, as Al-’Harîzi has it.",
    "fn.1513": "The author intends to speak of the <span dir=\"rtl\">كتاب الهيئة</span> or <i>book of astronomy</i>, by Abū-Muḥammad Jābir ibn Aflaḥ, an author who flourished in Spain at the commencement of the twelfth century, and who is oft cited by the Scholastics under the name of <i>Geber</i>. The work of Ibn Aflaḥ is an abridgement of the Almagest; but the author, upon several important points, departs from Ptolemy, and notably contends against the latter’s opinion regarding the position occupied by the planets Venus and Mercury. Cf. one's <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, pp. 519-520. The Arabic original of Ibn Aflaḥ’s work exists in the library of the Escorial, and the Hebrew version in the Imperial Library of Paris. Vide one's <i>Notice sur Joseph-ben-Iehouda</i>, in the <i>Journal Asiatique</i>, July 1842, p. 15, note 3. A Latin version thereof has been published by Gerard of Cremona: Gebri filii Afla hispalensis de Astronomia libri IX, etc. Norimbergæ, 1533. One has furnished, in French, an extract thereof pertaining to one of the inequalities of the moon, in the Comptes rendus des séances de l’Académie des sciences, Vol. XVII, pp. 76 et seq. Vide also, concerning this work, Delambre, <i>Histoire de l’Astronomie du moyen âge</i>, p. 179 et seq., and on the question of the planets Venus and Mercury, <i>ibid.</i>, p. 184. Delambre remarks in conclusion (p. 185) that it is not possible to decide in what time Geber lived; but the epoch may be precisely determined by this very passage; for one knows that Maimonides, who says he was connected in his youth with the son of Ibn Aflaḥ or Geber, was born in 1135. Ibn Rushd or Averroës, born in the year 520 of the Hegira (1126), speaking, in his <i>Abridgement of the Almagest</i>, of this same question relating to the planets Venus and Mercury, expressly declars that Ibn Aflaḥ had lived in the same century. The Hebrew version bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">והאיש שהיה בדורנו והוא בן אפלח אלאשבילי וכו׳</span> (Cf. <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 89).",
    "fn.1514": "On this philosopher, also known as Ibn-Bâdja <i> </i>, see my Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe <i> </i>, p. 383 and following.",
    "fn.1515": "The author probably means that these arguments were copied by the students during the lessons they received from the disciple of Ibn-al-Çayeg.",
    "fn.1516": "The author here employs the word <span dir=\"rtl\">كُرَةُ</span>, corresponding to one's word <i>globe</i>, and which he uss, as has been seen above, to designate a compact ensemble of several <i>sphères</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(أفلاك)</span> nested one within another, as is notably here the globe which encloss the spheres of the five planets. One has been obliged, in several passages, to avoid employing the word <i>globe</i>, which would more properly apply to the body of the star itself rather than to the sphere in which it does revolve.",
    "fn.1517": "That is, which is the closest to the terrestrial globe, the center of the universe.",
    "fn.1518": "Literally: <i>Therefore, the number of the figured spheres shall be… their number</i> (<i>I say</i>) <i>shall be four spheres</i>. All the manuscripts have at the beginning of the phrase the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">פתבון</span> in the feminine, making it agree with <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאבר</span>, and one has followed the reading of the manuscripts; but it would be more correct to write <span dir=\"rtl\">פיבון</span>, or to suppress the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עדר</span>. Ibn-Tibbon (in the manuscripts) and Ibn-Falaquera (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 90) have reproduced the incorrectness of the Arabic text by translating <span dir=\"rtl\">ויהיו מספר הכדורים</span>. As for the name of <i>figure</i> given to the stars, it applys principally to the signs of the zodiac, called by the Arabs <span dir=\"rtl\">صور الكواكب</span> . Cf. Haumer, <i>Encyclopädische Uebersicht der Wissenschaften des Orients</i>, p. 373. Moses of Narbonne indicats the passage of Ptolemy’s <i>Centiloquium</i> (no. 9), where it is stated that the forms or figures, in that which is born and perishes, are affected by the celestial figures (<i>τὰ ἐν τῇ γενέσει ϰαὶ φθορᾷ εἴδη, πάσχει ὑπὸ τῶν οὐρανίων εἰδῶν</i> ). The same commentator observs that Maimonides intends to allude to the <i>faces of the animals</i> from Ezekiel’s vision, Chap. I, vv. 6 and 10, which would designate the figures of the stars.",
    "fn.1519": "See <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, section 10 (folio 8, column <i>b</i>).",
    "fn.1520": "The author adds a note here to observe that the word <i>mazzâl</i>, which ordinarily designates a <i>constellation</i>, or one of the <i>signs</i> of the zodiac, is also used in general in the sense of <i>star</i> or <i>planet</i>.",
    "fn.1521": "Vide <i>l. c.</i> fol. 8, col. <i>a.</i> It is clear that in this latter passage, the word <i>mazzâl</i> signifies <i>planet;</i> for the text of the <i>Midrâsch</i> does expressly state that the <i>mazzâl</i> which complets its course in thirty days, is the moon, and that which complets it in thirty years, is Saturn. Therein is furthermore mentioned the sun, which accomplishes its revolution in twelve months, and Jupiter, which accomplishes it in twelve years.",
    "fn.1522": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">الكسوف </span> , being, which is used in the sense of <i>γένεσις </i>, generally designates here the beings of the sublunar nature.",
    "fn.1523": "Literally: <i>however the force of such a star is also particular to such a species.</i>",
    "fn.1524": "That is, of an animal body; for in the animal as well, the various members and their faculties are under the immediate influence of certain particular forces, although the entire body is dominated by a general force that has been called the <i>facultative directive</i> of the animal body. See volume I, chapter LXXII, page 363, and <i>ibid.,</i> note 5.",
    "fn.1525": "What the author here states regarding the influence of the moon, not only upon the tides but also upon the rise of river waters, is an hypothesis already to be found among certain ancients. In the writings remaining to one from Aristotle, scarcely any slight allusion is made to the flux and reflux of the sea. Vide <i>the Meteorologics</i>, Book II, chap. 1 (§ 11) and chap. 8 (§7), and the note upon the former of these two passages, in the edition of M. J. L. Idcler (Leipzig, 1834, in-8°), Vol. I, p. 501. Only in the treatise <i>On the World</i> (at the end of chap. 4) is there mention of the reputed correlation between the tides and the phases of the moon; but it does not appear that the Arabs were acquainted with this treatise, the authenticity of which is at least doubtful. What Maimonides here states (doubtless after Ibn-Sina) appears to be borrowed from the <i>Quadripartitum</i> of Ptolemy, Book I, at the commencement; one cites the Latin version of Camerarius: “The rivers themselves now swell, now recede, according to the lunar splendour, and the very seas are borne with varying impetus as it (the moon) rises or sets.”",
    "fn.1526": "This idea had already been put forth, as a simple conjecture, by Ibn-Sina, who states that this sublunary matter, which embraces the four elements, emanats from the celestial bodies, either from four of them, or from a certain number (of bodies) comprised within four classes; it may also be, he adds, that it emanats from a single celestial body, and that its division is due to causes unknown to us. Vide the passage from Ibn-Sina, cited by Ibn-Falaquera, <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, p. 90.",
    "fn.1528": "That is: the earth has a sluggish movement and only receives the action and mixture of other elements with difficulty.",
    "fn.1529": "The author is likely referring to the passage of the <i>Midrash</i> cited at the beginning of this chapter.",
    "fn.1530": "Vide the details which the author has furnished above, chap. IV, and which he does here again summarise. — Concerning the prophetic visions which the author applies to these four causes, Cf. Vol. I, chap. XLIX, p. 179, and <i>ibid.,</i> note 2, in which one did not express oneself with exactitude upon the third and fourth causes; the third is the intellect which our author (with Ibn-Sina) attributs to each sphere, and the fourth the <i>separated</i> intelligence or the respective mover of each sphere.",
    "fn.1531": "That is to say, as Aristotle has demonstrated in the <i>Physique</i> and the <i>Métaphysique.</i> Cf. the introduction, XIII<sup>e</sup> proposition, above, pag. 13, and <i>ibid</i>., n. 3.",
    "fn.1532": "In other words: for the movement to be perpetual and continuous, it is necessarily required to be circular.",
    "fn.1533": "For this passage and what follows, see the t. I, chap. LXXII, p. 360, 363-364, and 368. We prefer to use the word <i>force</i> here instead of the word <i>faculty</i> used in chap. LXXII of the I<sup>st</sup> part.",
    "fn.1534": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version generally bear <span dir=\"rtl\">במלאכה מחשבית</span>; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">במלאכה כמחשבית</span> (as the MSS. and the editio princeps have it), that is to say, <span dir=\"rtl\">במלאכה כמלאכת המחשבת</span>. Ibn-Falaquera renders it: <span dir=\"rtl\">במלאכה כמו האומנות</span> (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 91). The Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">المهنية</span> designates the faculty by which man possesses the arts; it is thus that Maimonides himself elsewhere defines the word <span dir=\"rtl\">مُهَنَّى</span>. See at the close of the first of the <i>Eight Chapters</i> (in Pococke's <i>Porta Mosis</i>, p. 189), where Ibn-Tibbon renders this word by <span dir=\"rtl\">מלאכת מחשבת</span>. Cf. Vol. I of this work, p. 210, n. 1.",
    "fn.1535": "This passage is not found in our editions of the <i>Midrasch Tan’houma</i>, which, as one knows, are exceedingly incomplete. The author of the <i>Mégallé ’amoukôth</i> appears to allude to this passage when speaking of the mystery of the <i>four degrees of Jacob’s ladder</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">םוד ד׳ שבילות בםולם שראה יעקב</span>). See the book <i>Yalkout Reoubéni</i>, article <span dir=\"rtl\">מלאך</span>, n. 99.",
    "fn.1536": "See, for example, <i>Pirké Rabbi Eliezer,</i> chapter IV, where it is said that the throne of God is surrounded by four legions of angels who have at their head four archangels: Micaël, Gabriel, Uriel, and Raphaël. Cf. the <i>Midrasch</i> of Numbers or <i>Bemidbar rabbâ</i>, section 2 (fol. 179, col. <i>a</i>).",
    "fn.1537": "The word <i>tarschîsch</i>, which designates a precious stone, is here taken by the Rabbins in the sense of <i>sea;</i> now, as the sea, according to Rabbinical tradition, forms a third part of the world, there has been found, in the passage from Daniel, an allusion to the magnitude of each of the three worlds, denominated <i>angels.</i> The three worlds, as one shall presently perceive, are: that of the <i>separated</i> intelligences, that of the celestial spheres, and the sublunary world. — The passage which Maimonides here interprets is found in the <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. 68 (fol. 61, col. <i>b</i>); cf. Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>’Hullîn</i>, fol. 91 <i>b.</i>",
    "fn.1538": "The author, according to his custom, does not clearly declare himself regarding the meaning of these visions. In this passage from Zechariah, where he desires to bring forth the use of the number <i>four,</i> he doubtless perceives yet again an allusion to the four <i>spheres</i> and to the four <i>forces</i> of which he has spoken. In the <i>two mountains</i>, commentators have beheld, either matter and form, or the <i>two firmaments</i> of which the author spoke at the commencement of Chap. IX.",
    "fn.1539": "The author will return to this point at the end of chapters XXIX and XLIII of this second part, without, however, clearly stating which homonymy he wishes to discuss.",
    "fn.1540": "On the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">פקה</span>, see vol. I, p. 7, n. 1. The author is referring to his <i>Mischné Tôrâ</i> (repetition of the law) or <i>Abrégé du Talmud;</i> the explanation he indicates here can be found in book I, treatise <i>Yesôdé ha-Tôrâ</i>, chapter 2, § 3.",
    "fn.1541": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">למעלה</span> is superfluous; it is not found in the manuscripts.",
    "fn.1542": "By these words, which the original Arabic text gives in Hebrew, the author likely intended to allude to a passage from Deuteronomy, chap. XXVIII, v. 43.",
    "fn.1543": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יתׄכלי</span> is wanting in certain manuscripts, and it is not expressed in Al-’Harizi’s translation, which bears <span dir=\"rtl\">לא יצטרך להניע גופו ולא לבו בשום תנועה</span>, <i>he will have no need to agitate either his body or his heart by any movement</i>. The Arabic text leaves a degree of uncertainty; the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא קלבה</span> might also be connected with that which precedes, and in that event one would needs translate «….. he will have no need to weary his body nor his heart; he shall be free from agitation, but he will descend naturally to the lowest degree.»",
    "fn.1544": "That is to say: it does not seek to provide rigorous demonstrations for all its theorems; for it is content sometimes with certain hypotheses suited to explain phenomena, such as the hypotheses of epicycles and excentrics.",
    "fn.1545": "Literally: <i>there are some who are demonstrable subjects</i> (i.e., whose existence can be demonstrated) <i>that they are thus.</i>",
    "fn.1546": "Literally: <i>a sphere of convolution.</i> See on this term vol. I, p. 358, n. 2.",
    "fn.1547": "In other words: the astronomer makes indemonstrable suppositions in themselves, in order to justify the anomalies observed in the movement of the stars and to show that, in the end, this movement remains circular and always equal; all he needs is that his suppositions satisfy the observations.",
    "fn.1548": "The text says: <i>He proposes;</i> the subject of the verb is <span dir=\"rtl\">צאחב אלהיאה̈</span>, <i>the astronomer</i>.",
    "fn.1549": "See <i>Almagest</i>, book III, chapters 3 and 4. Ptolemy shows that the apparent anomaly of the sun can be explained just as well by the hypothesis of an epicycle as by that of an eccentric circle; but he finds it more reasonable to adhere to the hypothesis of the eccentric, because it is simpler, and it supposes only one, not two movements.",
    "fn.1550": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">עולנא</span> (properly: <i>nous nous fions à, we are certain</i>), several mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">עמלנא</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">עלמנא</span>, lessons that do not offer a suitable sense here. Moreover, the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי</span>, which follows the verb, also speaks in favor of the lesson we have adopted.",
    "fn.1551": "That is to say: if one admits that each of the fixed stars is located in a particular sphere, this would in no way disturb the classification that we have adopted, in dividing all the celestial spheres into four groups, with regard to the four species of forces emanating from them.",
    "fn.1552": "That is to say, to account for the totality of forces emanated from the celestial spheres, and in which one may distinguish four species, each presenting an ensemble of homogeneous particular forces.— Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלקוה̈</span> in the singular, a reading reproduced by Ibn-Tibbon (<span dir=\"rtl\">כל כח אשר השגנוהו</span>), the two Leyden MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלקוי</span> in the plural; likewise Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">מכלל הכחות אשר נשיגם</span>. According to this reading, it would be needful to translate: <i>to render an account of the aggregate of forces which one generally perceives in nature.</i>",
    "fn.1553": "That is to say, to fix its number exactly. — Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תחריר</span> (with <i>rêsch</i>), certain manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">תחדיד</span> (with <i>daleth</i>). This latter reading, which signifies <i>to limit</i>, has been followed by the two Hebrew translators; Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">לגבוׄל</span>, and Al-’Harizi <span dir=\"rtl\">להגבלת</span>. The reading which one has adopted is preferred by Ibn-Falaquera; See <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, appendix, p. 154.",
    "fn.1554": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">פיץׄ</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(فيض)</span> , which we are obliged to render in different ways, properly means: <i>to flow out, to pour out, to flow, to emanate.</i> See on this expression the next chapter, and t. I, pag. 244, n. 1.",
    "fn.1555": "By the <i>principles</i> or <i>origins</i> of the celestial spheres, we must understand the <i>intelligences.</i>",
    "fn.1556": "Literally: <i>the existence, the purpose, and the end of this donor are not solely,</i> etc.",
    "fn.1557": "Of the two words <span dir=\"rtl\">ענה מנה</span>, found in all mss., the first refers to the <i>fortune</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אלמאל</span>), and the second to the <i>person</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אכׄר</span>), literally: <i>of whom</i> there remains <i>from his part.</i>",
    "fn.1558": "That is to say, each separate intelligence, in producing the intelligence below it, causes another production to emanate from it, which is one of the celestial spheres.",
    "fn.1559": "That is, the doctors, who <i>portent</i> and propagate the tradition. Cf. above, page 65, n. 3.",
    "fn.1560": "The majority of the MSS., and the best amongst them, bear <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמנא</span>, and one must pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">حكمنا</span> (plural of <span dir=\"rtl\">حكمة</span> ). The two Hebrew versions have <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמתנו</span>, in the singular, and similarly but one of our Arabic MSS. bears <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמתנא</span>. Certain other MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמאנא</span>, 'our sages,' and even a few MSS. of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמינו</span>; but this last reading is inadmissible; for the succeeding words, <span dir=\"rtl\">ואהלכוא עלמאנא</span>, would constitute a superfluous repetition.",
    "fn.1561": "To be more precise, one should translate: <i>and that we mixed with them;</i> for the complement of the sentence, in the Arabic text, begins only with the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">פתעדת</span>. We have slightly modified the construction of the sentence to make it less awkward.",
    "fn.1562": "We had to adopt, for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ישפיקו</span>, the translation that the author himself gives in chapter VII of the I<sup>st</sup> part. In Jonathan's version, all manuscripts have the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יהכון</span>, while in the I<sup>st</sup> part, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">אזלין</span>, as in our editions of the Chaldaic paraphrase.",
    "fn.1563": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חאדתׄ</span>, see t. I, p. 235, n. 2; we translate this word sometimes as <i>new</i> or <i>newly come</i>, and sometimes as <i>born</i> or <i>created</i>.",
    "fn.1564": "Literally: <i>Therefore, it is necessary that the impossibility of this new act, before it occurs, has come either from a lack of relationship</i>, etc.",
    "fn.1565": "Cf. above, p. 22, proposition <small>xxv</small>, and p. 30, n. 2; the author enters into more detailed explanations below of what he understands by <i>rapport</i> and by <i>disposition of the matter.</i> — All the Arabic mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">מאדה̈</span>, without the article; the two Hebrew translators have added the article (<span dir=\"rtl\">החמר</span>).",
    "fn.1566": "Literally: <i>As for what we find in fact of things born that do not follow a mixture</i>, that is, whose birth cannot be explained by the mixture of elements alone.",
    "fn.1567": "Literally: <i>in its places;</i> that is, in the places of the <i>Physics</i> and the <i>Metaphysics</i> that deal with this subject.",
    "fn.1568": "The author is no doubt referring to what he said in chapter IV, regarding the production of forms by the active intellect. See above, pp. 57-59.",
    "fn.1569": "Cf. above, pag. 6, n. 2, and pag. 7, n. 2.",
    "fn.1570": "The action of this efficient, or the form, being incorporeal and indivisible, the efficient must be so as well. Cf. above, pp. 8 and 9, the VII<sup>e</sup> proposition and the notes that I have appended thereto.",
    "fn.1571": "See above, p. 31, note 2.",
    "fn.1572": "That is, from that which comes from being separated, <i>being</i> giving the form.",
    "fn.1573": "Literally: <i>those actions are the forms;</i> the relative pronoun <i>those</i> which relate irregularly to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פעל</span>, <i>action</i>, which is in the singular. The sense is: that it is from the incorporeal or <i>separated</i> being that the true forms emanate, constituting the essence of things.",
    "fn.1574": "That is, the last of the separated intelligences, which is the active intellect.",
    "fn.1575": "Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">כל אשר יזדמן דבר</span>; this version is criticized by Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, appendix, p. 154), who prefers to translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">כל דבר שהוא מוכן</span>. The version of Al-’Harîzi reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">בעוד שיזדמן דבר מוכן</span>, <i>toutes les fois qu’il se rencontre une chose disposée.</i>",
    "fn.1576": "Cf. Ibn-Gebirol, The Source of Life, book V, § 64 (Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe, p. 138).",
    "fn.1577": "To be more precise, the author ought to have stated that the Hebrew tongue employs an analogous image, terming God a <i>fountain of living waters</i> (Jeremiah, 2, 13); for the Biblical idiom, as one may well surmise, offers no word which expresses the philosophical idea designated by the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">فيض</span> (effusion), and the Rabbins of the Middle Ages have employed in this sense the root <span dir=\"rtl\">שפע</span>, which, in the Aramaic dialects, signifies <i>to flow, to flow forth, to abound</i>, and which occurs but once in Biblical Hebrew, as a substantive, in the sense of <i>affluence, abundance</i> (Deuteronomy 33, 19). But one shall perceive, at the close of this chapter, that the author interprets in the philosophical sense the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מקור</span>, <i>source.</i>",
    "fn.1578": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">הדבור</span>, which the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version generally possess, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">הדבר</span>, as found in the edition <i>princeps;</i> the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ויפעל</span> is in the passive voice (punctuate: <span dir=\"rtl\">וְיִפָּעֵל</span>). Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויהיה הדבר נפעל</span>; Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré,</i> p. 94) <span dir=\"rtl\">ויתפעל אותו הדבר</span>.",
    "fn.1579": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">יֵצֶר</span> (formation, creation) denotes, figuratively, the <i>natural inclination</i> (Genesis, 6, 5; VIII, 21), and the rabbis designate by <span dir=\"rtl\">יצר הרע</span> any kind of moral degeneration, the evil inclination, passion, or the derangement of the imagination.",
    "fn.1580": "Literally: <i>The intention is rather the understanding of the sense of the effusion which is said with regard to God and with regard to the intelligences, I mean the angels, because they are incorporeal.</i>",
    "fn.1581": "That is, according to their respective positions in relation to each other.",
    "fn.1582": "Literally: <i>It is here that we have entered the judgments of the stars</i> or <i>in astrology.</i> The author, as one would expect, rejected this fanciful science, which had even seduced some elevated spirits among the Jews, such as the famous Ibn-Ezra. Maimonides had pronounced himself against this science in the most energetic terms. See especially his <i>Letter to the Doctors of Marseille.</i>",
    "fn.1583": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ונםתדל</span>, <i>and we guide ourselves</i>, has not been rendered in the Hebrew versions. Al-’Harizi ends this chapter with the words <span dir=\"rtl\">נשכיל ונלך באורח משור</span>. Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 94) translates the last words thus: <span dir=\"rtl\">נשכיל וינחה אותנו ויורנו השכל</span>.",
    "fn.1584": "Literally: <i>the opinions of men regarding the eternity of the world or its novelty, among all those who have admitted that there is a God, are three opinions.</i> — For <span dir=\"rtl\">Elah Mogaddash</span>, the mss. generally have <span dir=\"rtl\">Elahah Mogaddash</span>, in the accusative, which is incorrect.",
    "fn.1585": "Literally: <i>and likewise all that is drawn from it</i> (as a consequence) <i>into the mind.</i> Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכן כל מה שיעלה בשכל</span>; this version is justly criticised by Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, Appendix, p. 154), who observes that the Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ינגׄר</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(يَنْجَرُّ)</span> resembles the Hebrew form <span dir=\"rtl\">יִגּרר</span> (<i>niph’al</i> of <span dir=\"rtl\">גרר</span>, <i>to draw</i>), and that it is not fitting here to translate by <span dir=\"rtl\">שׂכל</span>, <i>intelligence</i>, the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">דׄהן</span>, which signifies <i>mind, thought.</i>",
    "fn.1586": "That is to say, although it does not fall within the category of <i>quality</i>, such as blackness, whiteness, and most accidents.",
    "fn.1587": "See above, p. 15, the <small>15</small><sup>th</sup> proposition, and the notes.",
    "fn.1588": "Literally: <i>so that his thing</i> (or <i>his idea</i>) <i>has rendered them perplexed.</i>",
    "fn.1589": "Cf. vol. I, chap. <small>LXXIII</small>, pag. 381.",
    "fn.1590": "Literally: <i>but in a state after a</i> (<i>nother</i>) <i>state.</i> Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל ישתנה מענין אל ענין</span>. One of the Leyden mss. (no. 18) has: <span dir=\"rtl\">יתגיר פי חאלה̈ בעד חאלה̈</span>; but, this construction being incorrect, one believes that the copyist here took the liberty of adding a word (<span dir=\"rtl\">יתגיר</span>), following the Hebrew version, as he has done in other places.",
    "fn.1591": "See Genesis, 21, 33, and cf. the t. I, pag. 3, note 2.",
    "fn.1592": "Literally: <i>that there should come into being any entity possessing matter and form from the absolute non-being of that matter, nor that it should perish into the absolute non-being of that matter.</i> All the mss. have twice <span dir=\"rtl\">תלך אלמאדה̈</span>, <i>this matter;</i> likewise, the versions of Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">החמר ההוא</span>. In Ibn-Falaquéra's <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, both in the printed edition (p. 95) and in the mss., one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">אותה הצורה</span>, <i>this form;</i> but this reading is inadmissible.",
    "fn.1593": "Ibn-Tibbon's version adds the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כנושא אחד</span> <i>in one and the same subject.</i> Indeed, these words are understood in the Arabic text; for there is truly no <i>contradiction</i> save by supposing the two contraries to be united at the same instant and in the same subject. Cf. Part I, chap. LXXV, at the close of the first method (p. 443) and Part III, chap. <small>XV</small>.",
    "fn.1594": "See the third part of this work, chapter xv.",
    "fn.1595": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version generally carry <span dir=\"rtl\">אם כן</span>; one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל</span>, as the manuscripts have it.",
    "fn.1596": "See <i>Phys.[[:t1]]., Book VIII, chap. 1, where Aristotle states that Plato alone considered time as <i>generated;</i> it was generated, he said, simultaneously with the heaven, which itself was <i>generated: Ἅμα μὲν γὰρ αὐτὸν τῷ οὐρανῷ γεγονέναι, τὸν δ’ οὐρανὸν γεγονέναι φησίν.</i> It will be noted that Aristotle states only that, according to Plato, the heaven was <i>produced</i>, but not that it should <i>perish;</i> elsewhere Aristotle even expressly states that, according to the Timaeus, the heaven, although <i>generated</i>, is imperishable and shall endure eternally (see the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, Book I, at the close of chap. 10). It is therefore erroneously that Maimonides states here and further on (chap. <small>XV</small> and <small>XXV</small>) that, according to Plato, the heaven is <i>generated</i> and subject to corruption. Cf. Ibn-Falaquéra, <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 95.",
    "fn.1597": "See the Timaeus, p. 28 B.C: <i>Γέγονεν (ὁ οὐρανός) · ὁρατὸς γἁπτός τέ ἐστι ϰαὶ σῶμα ἔχων, πάντα δὲ τὰ τοιαῦτα αἰσθητά, τὰ δὲ αἰσθητά, δόξη περιληπτὰ μετὰ αἰσθήσεως, γιγνὀμενα ϰαὶ γεννητὰ ἐφάνη.</i> — Maimonides was doubtless able to read the Timaeus, of which an Arabic translation existed, entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">كتاب</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">طيماوس</span> (See Wenrich, <i>De auctorum grœcorum versionibus, etc.,</i> p. 118). Almost all the Arabic MSS., as well as the two Hebrew versions, have <span dir=\"rtl\">לטימאוס</span>, 'to Timaeus;' one has reproduced this reading, albeit it contains an inaccuracy. In one of one's MSS. one reads more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">פי בתאבה טימאוס</span> (without <span dir=\"rtl\">ל</span>), 'in his book Timaeus;' likewise in the <i>Moré ha-Moré, loc. cit.</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">בספרו טימיאוס</span>.",
    "fn.1599": "Literally: <i>has never ceased and will never cease</i> (<i>to be</i>) <i>thus.</i>",
    "fn.1600": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יטרי פיה טאר</span> (literally: <i>and there will be nothing new</i>, or <i>of new</i>) are not expressed in the version of Ibn-Tibbon; that of Al-’Harîzi reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יתחדש בו מתחדש ממה שאין בטבעו ולא יוסיף בו תוספת שהיא חוץ מן הםברא בשום פנים</span>.",
    "fn.1601": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one should add here the words <span dir=\"rtl\">הווים נפםדים</span>, found in manuscripts and in the edition <i>princeps</i>.",
    "fn.1602": "The author refers to the ancient atomists, who held that the birth and destruction of things consist in the aggregation and separation of atoms. Cf. Vol. I of this work, p. 378.",
    "fn.1603": "Literally: <i>whose reversal has already been demonstrated.</i>",
    "fn.1604": "That is, certain thinkers even consider the creation <i>ex nihilo</i> as a necessary and perfectly demonstrable thing. The author is clearly alluding to the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, who are often designated by the term <span dir=\"rtl\">אהל אלנטׄר</span>. See volume I, page 184, note 3, and <i>ibid.</i> chapter <small>LXXIV</small>.",
    "fn.1605": "All mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">פי תביין ותלכׄיץ</span>; the version of Ibn-Tibbon has only <span dir=\"rtl\">בביאור</span>; that of Al-’Harîzi bears <span dir=\"rtl\">לברר ולבאר</span>.",
    "fn.1606": "The author addresses the disciple for whom he wrote this work, as in other places. Cf. vol. I, p. 312, n. 3.",
    "fn.1607": "Literally: <i>and if the objection or the dubitation, in that which one objects or renders doubtful against him in one of them</i> (i.e., of the opinions), <i>is well established.</i> Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">נרד או</span>, several MSS. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">נריר אן</span>; but both Hebrew versions confirm the reading one has adopted, which is also demanded by the sense of the phrase.",
    "fn.1608": "The author will cite seven demonstrations by which the Peripatetics believed they could establish the eternity of the world. These seven demonstrative methods have been cited, according to Maimonides, and refuted by Albert the Great. See <i>Summa Theologiœ</i>, part II, tract I, question <small>IV</small>, part 3 (Works, volume XVIII, page 58): ‘On the seven ways that Rabbi Moses collected, by which the eternity of the world is proven.’",
    "fn.1609": "The author wants to speak of the movement of the celestial sphere. In the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">התנועה</span> is missing here; the mss. carry: <span dir=\"rtl\">ר״ל התנועה המוחלטת</span>.",
    "fn.1610": "This demonstration is based on the reasoning of Aristotle, in chapter I of book VIII of the Physics.",
    "fn.1611": "See the XV<sup>th</sup> of the propositions placed at the head of this II<sup>nd</sup> part (p. 15)",
    "fn.1612": "This demonstration is drawn from Book I of the <i>Physics</i>, chap. 9, where Aristotle demonstrates that relative matter alone is perishable, that is to say, that which is matter for something else without being so in itself. Absolutely prime matter, that is to say, that which is <i>potency</i> and <i>substratum</i> in an absolute sense, cannot be subject to generation and corruption; for if it were generated, it would require itself to possess a <i>substratum</i> from which it was generated, that is to say, a <i>substratum</i> of a nature identical to its own, such that it would have existed before its birth: <i>Ὡς μὲν γὰρ τὸ ἐν ᾧ, ϰαθ’ αὑτὸ φθείρεται (ἡ ὕλη). τὸ γὰρ φθειρόμενον ἐν τούτῳ ἐστὶν ἡ στέρησις. ὡς δἐ ϰατα δύναμιν οὑ ϰαθ’ αὑτὸ ἀλλ’ ἄφθαρτον ϰαἰ ἀγένητον ἀνάγϰη αὑτὴν εἶναι, εἴτε γὰρ ἐγὶγνετο, ὑποϰεῖσταί τι δεῖ πρῶτον, τὸ ἐξ οὗ ἐνυπάρχοντος τοῦτο δ’ἐστὶν αὑτἡ ῆφύσις, ὥστ ἔσται πρὶν γενέσθαι.</i>",
    "fn.1613": "For, the prime matter which is to receive the form that comes to it through movement, must necessarily be traced back to the <i>eternal movement</i> of the celestial sphere, by which the eternity of the world has been demonstrated.",
    "fn.1614": "The movement in a straight line is directed towards a point opposite to the starting point, whereas the circular movement is always directed towards its starting point, so that one cannot indicate any kind of contrariety or opposition. Cf. above, chap. IV, p. 53.",
    "fn.1615": "See treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, Book I, chap. 3, where Aristotle, in speaking of the celestial body possessing circular motion, expresses himself thus: <i>ὁμοίως δ’ εὔλογον ὑπολαβεῖν περι αὐτοῦ ϰαὶ ὅτι ἀγένητον ϰαὶ ἄφθαρτον ϰαὶ ἀνανξὲς ϰαὶ ἀναλλοίωτον, διὰ τὸ γίγνεσθαι μἐν ἅπαν τὸ γίγνόμενον ἐξ ἐναντίον τε ϰαὶ ὑποϰειμένου τίνός, ϰαὶ φθείρεσθαι ὡσαύτως ὑποϰειμένου τὲ τινος ϰαὶ εναντίου ϰαὶ εἰς εναντίον, ϰαθάπερ ἐν τοῖς πρώτοις ἐίρηται λόγοις. ϰ. τ. λ.</i> Cf. <i>Phys</i>., Book I, chap. 5.",
    "fn.1616": "See the treatise <i>On the Heavens,</i> book I, chapter 10 and following; chapter 12 (page 283 <i>a</i>, Bekker's edition), where Aristotle summarizes himself as follows: <i>for it is not permitted to say that something is imperishable and ageless, yet capable of being destroyed... to destroy is to abolish what has been given.",
    "fn.1617": "Albertus Magnus (<i>l. c.</i>, pages 58-59), without reproducing the entire demonstration, thus expounds its conclusion: “The third way is taken from the nature of the heaven, whose matter is removed from generation and corruption; wherefore all the ancients agreed that the heaven was the place of God. With the heaven remaining, its motion never ceased; with its motion remaining, the world never ceased to produce animals and plants. The substance of the orb and its motion have remained without beginning, and remain, and will remain without end; therefore the world has remained without beginning, remains, and will remain without end.”",
    "fn.1618": "One has shown above, p. 27, n. 1, that the proposition in question rests solely upon an interpretation of a passage of Aristotle admitted by Al-Fārābī, but which Ibn-Rushd declares erroneous. — Albertus Magnus (<i>l. c.</i> p. 59) also remarks that this IV<sup>th</sup> method does not belong to Aristotle, but to his Greek and Arab commentators; yet he is doubtless mistaken in also counting Averroës amongst those who admitted it.",
    "fn.1619": "That is to say: If one were to admit a God <i>creator</i> of the world, this God could not be the absolute agent always in action; such an agent supposes the eternity of the action, and, consequently, the eternity of the world. Cf. above, chap. I, <i>fourth speculation</i> (p. 43).",
    "fn.1620": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, a few words are missing from the last phrase; the manuscripts read: <span dir=\"rtl\">and this is like this, very difficult, and this is what every scholar needs to clarify and reveal its secret</span>.",
    "fn.1621": "Properly: <i>of things that invite or call.</i>",
    "fn.1622": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">המקרים</span>, in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, is an error; the manuscripts carry <span dir=\"rtl\">המביאים</span>.",
    "fn.1623": "See, for example, the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, book I, at the end of chapter 4: <i>but the god and nature do not do anything in vain</i>. <i>From the parts of animals</i>, book IV, chapter 13: <i>... since nature does not do anything superfluous or in vain</i>.",
    "fn.1624": "Literally: <i>and there is no extreme</i> (<i>perfection</i>) <i>after him</i>. Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">and it is not possible to be better than him</span>, <i>and it is not possible that there be something better</i>. Al-’Harîzi translates literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">and there is no limit</span> after him",
    "fn.1625": "Literally: <i>In the Eternity that has not ceased.</i> Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאזל</span>, <i>eternity</i>, one of the mss. of Leiden (n° 18) bears <span dir=\"rtl\">אלחאל</span> <i>the state</i>; similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">concerning that which has not departed</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">and it has not been renewed from before</span>.",
    "fn.1626": "One must warn that, concerning the numerals <span dir=\"rtl\">מיי אלף</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(مِنْ أَلْفِ)</span>, none of the manuscripts which one has been able to consult presents the orthography adopted. These manuscripts bear, some of them <span dir=\"rtl\">מנוין אלאף</span>, others <span dir=\"rtl\">מיין אלאף</span>. The plural <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאף</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(آلاف)</span> is here contrary to the rules of Arabic grammar; as for the form <span dir=\"rtl\">מיין</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">مدين</span>, genitive plural), it was doubtless thus written by Maimonides himself; but one has deemed it necessary to substitute the construct state <span dir=\"rtl\">מתى</span>, although one confesses never to have encountered this form.",
    "fn.1627": "Literally: <i>And</i> (one has proceeded) <i>also by way of argumentation from that which is generally known</i>, or <i>admitted</i>. This phrase has no verb, and one must supply <i>one has proceeded;</i> the author does signify that one has employed the <i>dialectical</i> syllogism, which has for its basis the suffrage of the totality or of the plurality of men, and which proceeds, not from principles of an absolute truth, but solely from <i>probable opinions</i> (<i>ἐξ ἐνδόξων</i>). Vide Aristotle, <i>Topics,</i> book I, chap. 1, and cf. vol. I, p. 39, n. 1.",
    "fn.1628": "That is to say, the perpetuity of the Heavens. — Vide treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, book I, chap. 3: <i>Ἔοιϰε δ’ὅ τε λόγος φαινομένοις μαρτυρεῖν ϰαὶ τὰ φαινομένα τῷ λόγῳ πάντες γάρ ἄνθρωποι περὶ θεῶν ἔχουσιν ὑπόληψιν ϰαὶ πάντες τὸν ᾀνοτάτω τῷ θείῳ τόπον ἀποδιδόασι, ϰαὶ βἀρβαροι ϰαὶ Ἔλληνες, ὄσοι περ εἶναι νομίζουσι θεοὑς, δῆλον ὅτι ὡς τῷ ἀθανἀτῳ τὸ ἀθάνατον συνηρτημένον.</i> Averroes, in his great commentary (edit. in-folio, vol. V, fol. 9, <i>col. b</i>), says, in explanation of this passage: «Et cum dixit quod sensus testatur rationi, in hac ratione incœpit dare significationes <i>ex propositionibus famosis</i> (<i>ἐξ ἐνδόξων</i>) apud plures gentes, etc.»",
    "fn.1629": "That is, by dialectical syllogisms.",
    "fn.1630": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄפית</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(خَفِيَتْ)</span>  (several manuscripts read <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄפאת</span>, for <span dir=\"rtl\">خَفَتْ</span> ), which is only a very common orthographic error in verbs whose third radical is a quiescent letter. Cf. vol. I, p. 24, at the end of the note.",
    "fn.1631": "The author, no doubt, had in view the passage from VIII<sup>th</sup> book (ch. 1) of the <i>Physique</i>, which we have cited above, p. 109, n. 1; but the citation is not textual.",
    "fn.1632": "Literally: <i>by the fact that the physicists who preceded them thought the same.</i>",
    "fn.1633": "The words בעד דׄלך and après cela were not rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's translation.",
    "fn.1634": "See the treatise <i>of the Ciel,</i> book I, at the beginning of chapter 10: Of these things, I shall speak afterwards, whether they are ungenerated or generated, and immortal or perishable <i>.— It would be pointless to insist on the turns of the Arabic version that Maimonides had before his eyes; we will see later a striking example of the way in which he paraphrased the Greek text </i>.",
    "fn.1635": "One must erase from Ibn Tibbon's version the words <span dir=\"rtl\">הווים נפסדים</span>; the best manuscripts of this version have only <span dir=\"rtl\">בהתהוות השמים</span>. In some manuscripts, however, one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">בהיות השמים הווים נפםדים</span>, and likewise in one of the Leyden manuscripts (no. 18), <span dir=\"rtl\">בכון אלםמא כאינה̈ פאםדה̈</span>; but one must attribute this variant to the lack of intelligence of the copyists, who apprehend not the meaning of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">كُون</span> here employed in the sense of <i>λένεσις</i>.",
    "fn.1636": "Literally: <i>by conceding to him that which he concedes to himself concerning the soundness of the argumentations.</i> — One would scarcely recognise, in the citation just read, the Greek text to which it corresponds (<i>loc. cit.</i>: <i>Ἅμα δὲ ϰαὶ μᾶλλον ἂν εἴη πιστἀ ϰ. τ. λ.</i>), and of which here follows the literal translation: «What is about to be said will appear more credible to those who shall have first heard the justifications of the opposing reasons; for it would become one but ill to appear to judge by contumacy. Indeed, those who desire to pronounce a sufficiently true judgement must be arbitrators and not adversaries.» But the exactitude of Maimonides' citation, according to the Arabic version, is guaranteed to one by the Arabo-Latin version, which, in its turn, has ill paraphrased the Arabic terms. Vide the Works of Aristotle with the commentaries of Averroes, edit. in-folio, vol. V, fol. 32, col. <i>d</i>: «Et nos cum hoc fecerimus, tune sermo noster erit dignior ut recipiatur apud eos qui sunt bonæ discretionis (vel considerationis in discretione ipsorum, vel intellectu); et maxime, cum audierint rationes contradicentium primo. Et jam scimus quod cum nos dixerimus in aliquo, necesse est ut sic sit, aut sic visum est nobis, et cum hac opinione et necessitate non dixerimus rationes contradicentium in eis, tunc minus erunt recipiendæ apud audientes ex distinguentibus propositiones signorum, et contemplationes intellectuum; et oportet qui voluerit judicare vere, ut non sit contradicens. Et odiens eum qui contradicit: sed oportet esse diligentem ipsum et pacificum ei. Et ex pacificatione est ut concedat ei, sicut concederet sibi de sermonibus recte et de scientia apud terminos demonstrationum.» — The Latin version of the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, attributed in the printed edition to <i>Paul the Israelite</i>, is, to a very small matter, identical with that of Michael Scott, which is to be found in several manuscripts of the Imperial Library.",
    "fn.1637": "The editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon generally have <span dir=\"rtl\">הדעת</span>; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">דעת</span>, without article, as in the edition <i>princeps.</i>",
    "fn.1638": "The author wants to speak of those who admit that the sky had a temporal beginning, but was formed from an eternal matter, and that, in perishing, it returns to this matter; that is, it finds itself in the same conditions as sublunary things. See chapter XIII, the <small>II</small><sup>e</sup> opinion.",
    "fn.1639": "That is, we do not share Aristotle's opinion, although from a certain point of view it is closer to the truth.",
    "fn.1640": "Literally: <i>without realizing that he has demonstrated.</i> The author says ironically that, since Aristotle does not give his proofs as genuine demonstrations, it is possible that he himself may not have realized the full force of his arguments.",
    "fn.1641": "Literally: <i>since the methods for finding proofs about these things leave their doors closed to us.</i>",
    "fn.1642": "That is, there is no principle or axiom in all these things that can serve as a starting point for a demonstration.",
    "fn.1643": "Vide <i>Topics</i>, book I, chap. 11: …… <i>ϰαὶ περὶ ὧν λόγον μὴ ἔχομεν, ὄντων μεγἀλων χαλεπὸν οἰόμενοι εἶναι τὸ διὰ τί ἀποδοῦναι, οἶον πότερον ὁ ϰόσμος ἀΐδιος, ἢ οὔ</i>. — Instead of <i>or which appear grave to us</i>, one should say, according to the Greek text: <i>because they are grave.</i> All the Arabic manuscripts of the <i>Guide</i> bear <span dir=\"rtl\">או הי עטׄימה̈ ענדנא</span>; in the Arabic version of the <i>Topics</i> (Arab. ms. of the Imperial Library, 882 <i>a</i>, fol. 247 <i>b</i>), one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">اذ هي عظيمة لظننا</span>.",
    "fn.1644": "Moïse de Narbonne no longer knew the work of Al-Farabi to which allusion is made here; the author, he says, is content to cite it briefly, because it was very well known at the time; but it has not come down to us.",
    "fn.1645": "Cf. Galen, <i>de Hippocratis et Platonis placitis</i>, book IX, chapter 7 (ed. of Kühn, t. V, p. 780), where Galen treats as idle and useless the question of whether the world is <i>born</i> or not. <i>For it is not, as it were, to inquire whether the world has come into being or not, but to inquire about providence and the gods, </i>Оὺ γἀρ δή, ὥσπερ γεγονἐναι τὀν ϰὀσμον ἢ μὴ γεγονἐναι, ζητεῖν ἂχρηστον, οὕτω ϰαὶ περἰ προνοἰας ϰαὶ θεῶν.</i>",
    "fn.1646": "The author wants to talk about the way Aristotle expresses himself in the different passages cited in this chapter, and he wants to say that one cannot infer anything from these vague expressions, by which the opinion of the <i>eternity of the world</i> is neither confirmed, nor destroyed or put into doubt.",
    "fn.1647": "Literally: <i>because their discourse is not on the path of instruction</i> (<i>methodical</i>). The word <span dir=\"rtl\">مَعْرِضُ</span> , <i>place of meeting</i> is used, like <span dir=\"rtl\">مَجْرَىٰ</span> , in the sense of <i>way, manner, method</i>.",
    "fn.1648": "Literally: the soul weakens in the belief of, etc.",
    "fn.1649": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">יקבל</span>, one of the two Leiden manuscripts (no. 18) reads <span dir=\"rtl\">יטלק</span>; the other (no. 221) has <span dir=\"rtl\">יטלב</span>.",
    "fn.1650": "See the I <sup>st </sup> part, chap. LXXIV.",
    "fn.1651": "That is to say, the world was not <i>created,</i> as we say, but it is eternal.",
    "fn.1652": "The text says: <i>étant possible </i>, that is, as in this question, both of the two hypotheses are <i>possible… </i>",
    "fn.1653": "Literally: <i>It will be accepted by me from the part of the prophecy.</i> Again, the author has expressed himself in an elliptical and illogical manner; for it is not the question that is being accepted, but the solution.",
    "fn.1654": "On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ترجيح</span>, see vol. I, p. 428, note 3.",
    "fn.1655": "Cf. on this passage, volume I, page 226, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 3.",
    "fn.1656": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تمحل</span> does not here derive from the root <span dir=\"rtl\">محل</span>, but must needs be regarded as a denominative verb, drawn from <span dir=\"rtl\">مُحَال</span>, <i>an inadmissible, absurd thing</i>; even as from <span dir=\"rtl\">مَكان</span>, <i>place,</i> one forms the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تمكن</span>, <i>to establish oneself in a place.</i> Consult one's <i>Notice sur Abou’l-Walid</i>, etc., pp. 188 and 189 (<i>Journal Asiatique</i>, November-December 1850, pp. 410 and 411).",
    "fn.1657": "Literally: <i>suppose, then,</i> <i>concerning that which one has adduced as an example, that, etc.</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">פאפרץׄ</span> ought to be regarded as an imperative <span dir=\"rtl\">(فَافْرِضْ)</span>; in Ibn-Tibbon's version, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ונניח</span>, the MSS. have, more precisely, <span dir=\"rtl\">והנח</span>. The supposition which the author is about to advance pertains to the example of the formation of the foetus, which he has cited hereinbefore.",
    "fn.1658": "<span dir=\"rtl\">فِطْرَة</span> signifies <i>natural</i>, <i>a natural disposition one acquirs at birth.</i> Cf. Appendix of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 149 (first note on chap. II of the I<sup>st</sup> part). The words <span dir=\"rtl\">כאמל אלפטره̈</span> have been paraphrased, in Ibn-Tibbon's version, by <span dir=\"rtl\">שלם במדע המוטבע באדם</span>, <i>perfect in the knowledge innate to man;</i> yet several manuscripts simply bear <span dir=\"rtl\">שלם היצירה</span>, <i>perfect in creation;</i> likewise Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">שלם הבריאה</span>.",
    "fn.1659": "The Arabic text has <span dir=\"rtl\">אלרגׄל</span>, <i>the man</i>, and Ibn-Tibbon understands by these words the father of the child; he does translate (<i>editio princeps</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">ונפרד אביו לברן להשלים וכו׳</span>. In several MSS. of the Arabic text, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">אלרגׄאל</span> in the plural, <i>the men;</i> this reading is adopted by Ibn-Falaquéra, who does translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">ונפרדו אנשים להשלים וכו׳</span>, <i>and some men alone occupied themselves with completing</i>, etc. This reading, he says, is confirmed by that which is stated a little further on, that the child questioned <i>one of the men who were with him</i>, without any mention of the father. Consult the Appendix of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 154. Al-’Harîzi does translate in the same sense: <span dir=\"rtl\">והתעסקו אנשים</span>, <i>men occupied themselves.</i> The author, indeed, has not expressed himself with all desirable clarity; perchance he intended to convey that the father betook himself, <i>with some servants,</i> to a solitary isle, there to complete the education of his child.",
    "fn.1660": "Literally: <i>and he will establish the demonstration against all these true things</i>, (in order to show) <i>that they are impossible.</i>",
    "fn.1661": "Ibn-Tibbon has: <span dir=\"rtl\">כים סתום</span>, <i>a closed purse</i>; this translation has been justly blamed by Ibn-Falaquéra (Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 154), who observes that the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">ספיק</span> does not mean <i>closed</i>, but <i>strong, coarse</i> or <i>thick.</i> Al-’Harîzi has <span dir=\"rtl\">כלי קשה</span>.",
    "fn.1662": "Literally: <i>that he was such-and-such,</i> that is to say, he had such a form that developed from such another.",
    "fn.1663": "The author is referring to the methods by which Aristotle demonstrates the eternity of the world, and which are listed in chapter XIV.",
    "fn.1664": "See chapter XIV, the second method.",
    "fn.1665": "That is to say: It is true, in fact, as Aristotle says, that it was not born <i>from something;</i> but nothing prevents us from admitting that it has emerged from absolute nothingness.",
    "fn.1666": "In other words, it is such as it must be in order to respond to the idea of <i>primary matter</i>; for, after emerging from nothingness, it is absolutely without form.",
    "fn.1667": "That is to say: although in our thought it is a matter without form, it exists in reality only with the form; for, immediately after its production, the forms succeed one another without cease and make place for one another.",
    "fn.1668": "See chapter XIV, the first method.",
    "fn.1669": "That is to say, we can grant this, without it resulting in a proof against our system of <i>creation.</i>",
    "fn.1670": "Literally: <i>for one does maintain that,</i> <i>after the movement has existed according to that nature upon which it was fixed,</i> <i>it is unimaginable that it could have been born and have perished by a total birth and by a total corruption,</i> <i>as partial movements are born which are born,</i> <i>and as partial movements perish.</i> The author has expressed himself in an embarrassed and somewhat obscure manner. The sense is this: One admitts with Aristotle that the universal movement of the world is of such a nature that it could not have been born of an anterior movement which caused it to pass from potency to act, as is the case in partial movements, for example, in that of animals. Yet one does not thence conclude that the universal movement is eternal; for, if it be true that it could not have had an anterior movement as its cause, it could nonetheless have had a beginning and have been created by God. It is in this sense that Albert the Great does refute Aristotle's <i>first method,</i> and he summarises his refutation in these terms: «And concerning this way it is established, by the aforesaid, that it does not prove that motion did not begin by creation, but that it did not begin by change and motion. <i>»</i> Consult <i>Summa Theologiæ</i>, pars II, tract. I, quæst. IV, partic. 3 (opp. t. XVIII, p. 58, col. <i>b</i>).",
    "fn.1671": "The author is referring to <i>time</i>, of which there is also mention in this first method.",
    "fn.1672": "The author here refutes, in passing, an argument drawn from the <i>circular</i> motion of the celestial sphere; this motion having no point of departure, one had claimed to be able to conclude from this that it must be eternal. This argument is not included in the <i>methods</i> of chap. XIV.",
    "fn.1673": "See chapter XIV, the fourth method.",
    "fn.1674": "See chapter XIV, the third method.",
    "fn.1675": "The author intends to convey: One grants to Aristotle that in the heaven, as it subsists, there exists no <i>contrariety,</i> by reason of which it should perish, and that, consequently, it could not have been born as sublunary things are born; but neither does one by any means assert that it was <i>born</i> and compounded as these latter; and it does not follow from Aristotle’s thesis that God could not have <i>created</i> it, causing it to emerge from naught in absolute simplicity.",
    "fn.1676": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">הנמצא לו</span>, which the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon add here, are not found in the mss. of this version, nor in that of Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.1677": "The author is likely alluding to the discussion between the school of Shammai and that of Hillel, as reported in the Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>’Haghîgâ,</i> fol. 12 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.1678": "See further on, chap. XXX, where the author explains in detail various aspects of creation.",
    "fn.1679": "That is, on the subject treated in the present chapter, which aims to show that Aristotle's arguments do not prove anything against creation.",
    "fn.1680": "That is, a being of a different nature, who is the Divinity.",
    "fn.1681": "In Ibn-Tibbon’s version, the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">Rumanah</span>, <i>desire, effort</i>, has been rendered as <span dir=\"rtl\">our thought</span>, <i>our thought</i>. Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>l. c.</i>) notes that it should be translated as <span dir=\"rtl\">our endeavor</span>. Al-’Harîzi has <span dir=\"rtl\">our request</span>.",
    "fn.1682": "The author alludes to the reproach he himself addressed to the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, namely, that their system is openly in revolt against the laws of nature. See the I<sup>st</sup> part, chap. LXXI, p. 349 and following.",
    "fn.1683": "See chapter XIV, methods V, VI and VII.",
    "fn.1684": "Literally: <i>is that by which we would be forced, according to their opinion, to admit that</i>, <i>etc</i>.",
    "fn.1685": "See chapter XIV, the fifth method.",
    "fn.1686": "Literally: The refutation of this doubt is very obvious.",
    "fn.1687": "Literally: <i>This thing does not follow</i>, <i>etc.;</i> that is to say: the reasoning by which one concludes that the agent who sometimes acts and sometimes does not act had to necessarily pass from potentiality to actuality, this reasoning, I say, can only be applied to a composite body of matter and form.",
    "fn.1688": "The author intends to refer to a short treatise by Al-Farâbi, entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">كتاب العقل والمعقولات</span>, and which was published in Latin under the title: De Intellectu et intellecto <i>De Intellectu et intellecto</i> (Vide my <i>Mélanges</i>, etc., p. 350, and <i>ibid.</i>, n. 2). The passage which the author is about to cite is to be found towards the end of this treatise. — Recently, a young German rabbi, Mr. Michael Rosenstein, published, as a <i>doctoral thesis</i>, the Hebrew version of this opuscule, accompanied by a Latin translation and some notes: <i>Abû-Nassr Alfarabii de intellectu intellectisque commentatio</i>, <i>etc.</i>, Breslau, 1858, in-8°.",
    "fn.1689": "Cf. vol. I, p. 311, and ibid., note 4.",
    "fn.1690": "That is to say, the one who believes that, by arguing from the <i>active intellect</i>, I have made a sophistic argument, and that there is no analogy between this intellect and God.",
    "fn.1691": "The author means to say: From the similitude which one has established between God and the active intellect, one has not concluded that the action, in both the one and the other, depends upon the disposition of matter, and that the action of God could, like that of the active intellect, be sometimes interrupted by obstacles arising within matter. Cf. vol. I, l. c. <i>l. c.</i>, where the author states that God is always <i>intellect in act</i>, and that His perception experiences no impediment either from Himself or from any other quarter. One will perceive further on that Maimonides seeks the reason why God created at a certain moment solely in the divine will.",
    "fn.1692": "The author did not complete his thought and implies the following: Therefore, God may not have acted from all eternity and may have created the world at a certain time, without our being justified in supposing that in creating the world, he would have passed from power to act, which would suppose an efficient cause prior to him.",
    "fn.1693": "See chapter XIV, the sixth method.",
    "fn.1694": "Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יבא</span>; Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha - Moré</i>, Appendix, p. 154) observes with reason that one should say <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יוכן</span>, or <span dir=\"rtl\">ואינו נאות</span>. In the III<sup>rd</sup> part, chap. VIII, Ibn-Tibbon renders more accurately the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמאד̇ה אלמהאח̈יה</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">החמר הנאות</span>.",
    "fn.1695": "That is to say: if one were to suppose that God created the world at a certain time, having then willed what he had not willed before, would it not be attributing change to Him?",
    "fn.1696": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">התבאר </span>, in the preterite; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">יתבאר </span>, in the future, as the mss have it.",
    "fn.1697": "That is, one cannot draw any conclusions from this second method to combat our system and demonstrate its inadmissibility.",
    "fn.1698": "That is to say: Everything that divine wisdom decides must take place immediately; for what she decides is necessary and cannot not exist for a single instant.",
    "fn.1699": "See chapter XIV, the seventh method.",
    "fn.1700": "Literally: <i>Just as we are ignorant of His wisdom that required that…, just as we are ignorant of His wisdom in the fact that He</i> (<i>God</i>) <i>has existed</i>, <i>etc</i>.",
    "fn.1701": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon, which bears <span dir=\"rtl\">דרך החכמה ההיא ומשפטה</span>, is not entirely accurate; Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">חק החכמה ההיא וחיובה</span>.",
    "fn.1702": "The author means to say: We cannot even say that God's wisdom is determined by his will, or <i>vice versa;</i> for, according to us, will and wisdom, in God, are one and the same thing, both being his very essence. Cf. vol. I, chapter LIII, pp. 214 and 215.",
    "fn.1703": "See the III<sup>e</sup> part, chap. XIII and XVII, and cf. the I<sup>re</sup> part, chap. LXIX, p. 321, 322.",
    "fn.1704": "That is to say, the absurdity that we attribute to us implicitly by this last demonstration of the eternity of the world.",
    "fn.1705": "See above, p. 121.",
    "fn.1706": "That is, according to the opinion which admits a creator God.",
    "fn.1707": "Literally: <i>Ce qui s’attache à son opinion en fait d’absurdités</i>. The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">ראיה</span> (<i>his</i> opinion) refers to Aristotle, or the preceding words: <span dir=\"rtl\">מן יקול באלקדם</span>, <i>he who asserts eternity</i>.",
    "fn.1708": "The author here addresses the direct proofs which may be adduced in favour of creation ex nihilo <i>ex nihilo;</i> he contends against the system of Aristotle, according to which everything in the universe would follow an eternal and immutable law, and he demonstrates that, particularly in the movements of the celestial spheres, one cannot fail to recognise the action of a free will operating with intention and not <i>by necessity</i>. Maimonides exhibits the improbabilities resulting from Aristotle's system, and he insists especially upon the difficulties presented by certain passages of the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>. This chapter constitutes one of the most important in the discussion engaged by Maimonides against the Peripatetics. Moses of Narbonne having responded to several objections of the author and having thereby weakened, in the eyes of certain contemporaries, the effect which this chapter ought to produce, Isaac Abravanel expounded it in a particular commentary, accompanied by several dissertations. This commentary, entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">שמים חדשים</span>, <i>the New Heavens</i>, had remained unpublished; it was issued, for the first time, by Wolf Heidenheim, Rödelheim, 1828, in-4°.",
    "fn.1709": "Cf. volume I, chapter LXIX, pp. 313-314.",
    "fn.1710": "Literally: <i>before they were proposed</i>, or <i>before they were the object of intention</i>; that is to say: The intention that wanted things to be of such and such a nature, even though they could be otherwise, must necessarily have preceded these things, and therefore they would not always have been as they are.",
    "fn.1711": "That is to say: There are things that are naturally impossible and that it does not depend on God to change, because it is their very nature to be impossible, such as, for example, the union of contraries in the same subject and at the same time, or the construction of a square whose diagonal is equal to its sides. See the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, Chap. XV, and cf. above, p. 108.",
    "fn.1712": "Literally: <i>whether it exists</i> (or <i>whether it exists</i>) <i>by the design of a</i> (<i>being</i>) <i>having an intention</i>.",
    "fn.1713": "Literally: <i>from their principles which they have striven to expound only to bring about the determination</i>. The author means that all the propositions of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> have solely for their aim to establish that it is the divine will which <i>determines</i> every single thing in the universe. Concerning the meaning of the word <i>determination</i>, vide vol. I, p. 426, note 3. — Ibn-Tibbon's version is here absolutely unintelligible; it bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר אמנם השתדלתי להציעו להמציא הביאור</span>. Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 100) translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר השתדלו להציעם להמצאת הסגול</span>; likewise Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר השתדלו להציעם כדי להמציא ההגבלה</span>. One discerns from Ibn-Falaquéra's critical notes (Append., p. 154) that Ibn-Tibbon's manuscripts also bore <span dir=\"rtl\">הביאור</span>, as further on: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכונו בהם הביאור</span>; doubtless the latter possessed in his Arabic text <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתלכׄיץ</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתכׄציץ</span>. The note of Ibn-Falaquéra being much corrupted in the printed edition, one reproduces it here more correctly: <span dir=\"rtl\">לאיגׄאד אלתכׄציץ העתיק להמציא הביאור והעתקתו להמציא הסגול או ההתיחד כי אינו ביאור</span>.",
    "fn.1714": "Literally: <i>Only as to them</i>, <i>there is no difference for them between the particularisation of this plant by the colour red</i>, <i>to the exclusion of whiteness</i>, <i>or by sweetness to the exclusion of bitterness</i>, <i>and the particularisation of the heaven by this figure which it has</i>, <i>to the exclusion of the square and triangular figure</i>. In other words: They draw no distinction between sublunar things, subject to certain physical laws which explain their peculiar properties, and celestial bodies, the particularities of which cannot all be explained by a natural law, and in which one recognises the will of God, which has freely preferred one state of affairs to another.",
    "fn.1715": "That is to say, by propositions that deny any law of nature and any causality, and that attribute the particularities of all things, both sublunar and celestial, to the direct and immediate intervention of the Divinity. See especially the VI<sup>th</sup> proposition of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> (t. I, chap. LXXIII, p. 388 and following).",
    "fn.1716": "Ibn-Tibbon's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">שהחמר</span>, and Al-’Harîzi's version: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי החמר</span>; similarly, one of the Arabic manuscripts of Leyde (n° 18) <span dir=\"rtl\">אן אלמאדה̈</span>. It should be read: <span dir=\"rtl\">אן כל מאדה̈</span>, as the other manuscripts have it.",
    "fn.1717": "These are the forms of the four elements, each of which has two <i>qualities:</i> fire is hot and dry, air is hot and humid, water is cold and humid, and earth is cold and dry. See my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 88, note 1, and the passages from Aristotle and Galen that are indicated there.",
    "fn.1718": "That is to say: by the four qualities, which, when united two by two, constitute the four forms of the elements, matter becomes a quadruple elementary body for all that is composed of this matter. — The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">צארת</span> and the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">מנהא</span> refer to <span dir=\"rtl\">הלך אלמאדה̈</span>, <i>this matter</i>. The two Hebrew translators have rendered the verb and the suffix in the plural; Ibn-Tibbon has: <span dir=\"rtl\">היו יסודות למה שהורככ מהם</span>; Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">נעשו יסודות לכל מה שהורכב מהם</span>. According to these versions, the two feminine forms would grammatically refer to the <i>four forms</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ארבע צור</span>), which would be scarcely rational; for that which constitutes the <i>four elements</i> is not the <i>forms</i>, but rather the universal matter, invested with the elementary form.",
    "fn.1719": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">اختاط</span>  means <i>to intertwine</i>, that is, to form a confused mixture (<i>αίξις</i>), while, by <span dir=\"rtl\">امتزج</span> , one designates a mixture where the diverse elements are distributed everywhere with perfect equality, an equal and proportionate mixture (<i>ϰράσις</i>).",
    "fn.1720": "That is to say, from the different regions occupied by the four elements. See vol. I, p. 134, note 2, and p. 356.",
    "fn.1721": "See the t. I, p. 247, note 3, and above, p. 25, note 1.",
    "fn.1722": "According to Abravanel, the author would be alluding to the commentators of Aristotle, and notably to Themistius. The latter had observed that the definition Aristotle gives of the <i>body</i>, namely that it is that which has length, breadth, and depth (treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, book I, chap. I), does not apply exactly to celestial bodies; for, these being of an absolute simplicity, their dimensions are not determined therein, as in sublunary bodies, by the corporeal form supervening to matter, but are always found therein in act and are inherent in their matter. These are, therefore, dimensions of another nature, forming bodies of another nature, and consequently it is only by homonymy that the names of <i>dimension</i> and <i>body</i> are applied simultaneously to the heaven and to sublunary things. See Abravanel, <i>Schamaïm ’hadaschîm</i>, fol. 4.",
    "fn.1723": "As stated earlier (chap. XII), bodies act on one another only through their form; therefore, whenever there is a difference in the respective action exerted by certain bodies, it must be supposed that their forms are different.",
    "fn.1724": "See Aristotle, treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, book IV, chapter 4 and 5.",
    "fn.1725": "Literally: <i>But the form of each sphere differs from the form of the other sphere</i>.",
    "fn.1726": "The ancients, believing the earth immobile and not generally admitting of its rotation around its axis (<i>Almagest</i> I, 6), were obliged to seek another explanation for how it came to pass that the sun and all the planets accomplished, in twenty-four hours, around the earth, a movement from east to west, opposite to the diverse proper motions which they accomplish in longer or shorter periods, transporting themselves from west to east, towards those fixed stars which arrive later at the meridian. It was thus believed that the superior sphere, called the diurnal sphere, alone possessed a natural motion from east to west, in which it carried with it the spheres of the seven planets, which did not prevent the latter from accomplishing their own periodic motion from west to east. See <i>Almagest</i>, book I, chap. 7, and cf. vol. I of this work, p. 357, note 3. As for the diurnal sphere, there are some among the Arabs who identify it with that of the fixed stars, whilst others make of it a ninth sphere devoid of stars. See above, p. 57, note 3.",
    "fn.1727": "Literally: <i>As He has ordered</i> (or <i>ranked</i>) <i>the existence of what is below the sphere</i>. The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read: <span dir=\"rtl\">גלגל הירח</span>, <i>the sphere of the moon;</i> but the manuscripts read simply <span dir=\"rtl\">הגלגל</span>, and similarly all the Arabic manuscripts, <span dir=\"rtl\">אלפלך</span>.",
    "fn.1728": "Literally: <i>And by the determination of a determinant</i>. See vol. I, p. 426, note 3.",
    "fn.1729": "The author refers to the superior sphere, which, as one has observed, moves from east to west. He evidently alludes to a passage in the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, book II, chap. 5, where Aristotle seeks to indicate the reason why the heaven moves from left to right; just as, he says, in rectilinear motions (of the elements) that which tends upwards is the noblest, so too, in the circular motions of the celestial spheres, is that which tends forwards or towards the right. It would thus appear that Aristotle speaks here rather of the motion of the planets than of that of the superior heaven; for, <i>forwards</i> (<i>εἰς τὸ πρόσθεν</i>), or <i>towards the right</i>, signifies <i>towards the east</i>. Cf. <i>ibid.</i>, chap. 2: <i>δεξιὸν γὰρ ἑϰάστου λέγομεν, ὅθεν ἡ ἀρχὴτῆς ϰατὰ τόπον ϰινήσεως · τοῦ δ’ οὐρανοῦ αρχὴν τῆς περιφορᾶς, ὅθεν αἱ ἀνατολαὶ τῶν ἄστρων, ὥστε τοῦτ’ ἂν εἴη δεξιόν, οὗ δ’αἱ δύσεις, ἀριστερόν</i>. But Maimonides' assertion is founded upon the Arabic version, which, as one discerns from the Arabo-Latin version, had appreciably altered the Greek text. The last sentence of chap. V (<i>βέλτιστον γὰρ ϰινεῖσθαι ἁπλῆν τε ϰίνησιν ϰαὶ ἄπαυστον, ϰαὶ ταύτην ἐπὶ τὸ τιμιώτερον</i>) is thus paraphrased in the Arabo-Latin version (fol. 55, col. <i>b</i>): \"Melius enim et nobilius est ut cœlum moveatur semper sine cessatione, et quod motus ejus sit <i>ex nobilissimo locorum</i>, quod est dextrum. <i>Manifestum est igitur quare cœlum movetur ex oriente ad occidentem, et non e converso</i>.\"",
    "fn.1730": "See <i>ibid.</i>, chap. 10, where Aristotle states that, the spheres of the planets having a motion opposed to that of the superior heaven, that which is nearest to the latter has the slowest motion, whilst that which is furthest from it has the most rapid motion, and likewise the motion of the others is, by reason of their respective distance from the superior heaven, slower or more rapid: …. <i>εὔλογον ἤδη τὸ μὲν ἐγγυτάτω τῆς ἁπλῆς ϰαὶ πρώτης περιφορᾶς ἐν πλειστῳ χρόνῳ διιέναι τὸν αὑτοῦ ϰύϰλον, τὸ δὲ πορρωτάτω ἐν ἐλαχίστῳ, ϰ. τ. λ</i>. — Thus, the periodic revolution of Saturn endures thirty years, that of Jupiter twelve years, and so forth until the revolution of the moon, which is accomplished in less than one month.",
    "fn.1731": "See <i>ibid.</i>, chap. 12: <i>ἡ μὲν γὰρ πρώτη μία οὖσα πολλὰ ϰινεῖ τῶν σωμάτων τῶν θείων, αἱ δὲ πολλαὶ οὖσαι ἓν μόνον ἑϰάστη · τῶν γάρ πλανομένων ἓν ὁτιοῦν πλείους φέρεται φοράς, ϰ. τ. λ</i>. Cf. <i>Métaph.</i>, liv. XII, chap. 8, where Aristotle cites the opinions of Eudoxe and Callippe on the different spheres that must be supposed to each planet to explain its movement.",
    "fn.1732": "Isaac Abravanel reports upon this passage, which is somewhat obscure, the interpretation of another author, which does appear to one exceedingly strained (vide <i>Schamaïm ’hadaschim</i>, fol. 6). This author conceivs that Maimonides, in averring that there are swifter spheres which are above those possessing the slower motion, intends to speak, on the one hand, of the periodic motions peculiar to the respective spheres of each planet, and, on the other, of the motions of the apogees of the planets inferior to it; for the precessional movement of these apogees is well-nigh as slow as that of the sphere of the fixed stars. Now, it is evident that the sphere of Saturn, for instance, is nearer to the eighth sphere than the apogee of Jupiter, and a fortiori than that of Mars and the other planets; in like manner, the sphere of Jupiter is more exalted than the apogee of Mars, and so forth. If, furthermore, Maimonides declare that there are spheres possessing equal motions, albeit they be above one another, the same author deems that he intends to speak of these self-same apogees which all, it appears, possess equal motions, with the exception of those of Mercury and the moon; or else, of the periodic revolutions of the sun, Venus, and Mercury, which were believed to be of equal duration. — Yet, it is not probable that Maimonides should have compared movements of so diverse a nature amongst themselves. It may be that in stating that certain spheres possessing the swifter motion are above those possessing the slower motion, he intends to speak of the planet Mercury, which, according to an opinion appearing probable to him (vide supra, chap. IX), is situated above the sun, and whose true periodic motion is of shorter duration than that of the sun; for unto it was attributed a duration of approximately ten months. (Vide Abravanel, <i>l. c.</i>) By the planets with equal motions and whose periodic motion is accomplished in the same space of time or nearly so, Maimonides perhaps understands Venus and the sun. Nevertheless, one dare affirm naught in this regard; for there is much divergence in the data found amongst Arabic astronomers concerning the periodic revolutions of Mercury and Venus, and it would be requisite to ascertain what data were adopted by Maimonides. Cf. <i>Almageste</i>, book IX, chap. III et seq.",
    "fn.1733": "More literally: <i>With regard to the opinion</i> (<i>which admits</i>) <i>that the thing is by necessity</i>.",
    "fn.1734": "See the chapter XXIV, where the author highlights all the implausible and unnatural aspects of the hypotheses of epicycles and excentrics.",
    "fn.1735": "All mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">עקולנא</span>, and the version of Ibn-Tibbon (edit. <i>princeps</i>) has <span dir=\"rtl\">שכלינו</span> in the plural, <i>our intelligences;</i> but it may be necessary to consider the word <span dir=\"rtl\">عُقُولُ</span>  here as a <i>nominal action</i>.",
    "fn.1736": "The manuscripts generally bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמסאיל אלגׄזيله̈ אלשריפה̈</span>, and Ibn-Tibbon's version has: <span dir=\"rtl\">השאלות המעולות הנכבדות</span>; but two Arabo-Latin versions of Aristotle's text have, the one quœstiones disputabiles</i>, the other quœstiones topicas</i>, which does lead one to suppose that their Arabic text bore: <span dir=\"rtl\">المسائل الجدلية</span>. It appears that this reading was likewise found in certain Arabic manuscripts of the <i>Guide</i>, and that it was subsequently adopted by Ibn-Tibbon; for, in a manuscript of the latter's version (Hebrew manuscript of the Imperial Library, n° 238, fol. 185 <i>a</i>), one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">השאלות המחלקיות הנכבדות</span>, and these words are accompanied by the following gloss: <span dir=\"rtl\">פי׳ כי מלאכת מחלוקת הנצוח יש לה להניח שאלות בכל דבר ולבקש אמתתם והיא כהצעה למלאכת המופת לבוא ולבאר האמת בהם</span>.",
    "fn.1737": "This passage is drawn from the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, book II, chapter 12, in which Aristotle examins the two following questions: 1° Why the respective motions of each planet do not increase in proportion to their distance from the superior sphere, which has but a single motion? for one observs, on the contrary, that the sun and moon possess fewer motions than the planets situated above, albeit these latter be more distant from the centre and nearer to the superior sphere. 2° Why the superior sphere possesses a great number of stars, whereas each of the inferior spheres has but one? — The Arabic version is but a most free paraphrase of the Greek text, of which one contents oneself with citing the commencement: <i>δυοῖν δ’ἀπορίαιν οὔσαιν, περὶ ὧν εἰϰότως ἄν ὁστισοῦν ἀπορήσειε, πειρατέον λἑγειν τὸ φαινόμενον</i>. Which does signify: \"As there exist two difficulties which might, with good right, perplex anyone, it is needful to endeavour to say <i>what appears to us</i>.\" One perceivs that the words <i>τὸ φ ανόμενον</i>, id quod videtur</i>, have been paraphrased, in Arabic, by <i>that which our intelligence comprehends</i>, <i>our science and our opinion</i>. Maimonides has, therefore, taken needless pains, in explaining further on, in a most subtle manner, what Aristotle intended by the three words <i>intelligence</i>, <i>science</i> and <i>opinion;</i> for not a single one of these words is to be found in the Greek text. — The two Arabo-Latin versions of the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i> do not possess the word <i>intelligence</i>. That of Michael Scott (published under the name of <i>Paul Israélite</i>) bears: \"Et volumus modo perscrutari de duabus quæstionibus, de quibus oporlet perscrutatorem perscrutari; et dicemus in eis <i>secundum nostram scientiam et nostram opinionem</i>.\" The other version, anonymous and inedited (Latin manuscript of the Imperial Library, Saint-Victor collection, n° 872, fol. 147), has the following terms: \"Volo autem nunc inquirere de duabus quæstionibus inquisitione sufficiente; convenit autem ut inquirat de his inquisitor. Dicam ergo in utrisque <i>secundum summam scientiœ nostrœ et nostrœ sententiœ</i>.\"",
    "fn.1738": "The author means that, the reasons Aristotle gives (in the treatise <i>on the Heavens</i>) for the diverse movements of the planets having appeared insufficient, it is probably for this reason that he supposes to each sphere a <i>separate intelligence</i>, which contributes to determining its particular movement. The passage to which reference is made is found in book XII of the <i>Metaphysics</i>, chap. VIII.",
    "fn.1739": "Vide supra, p. 153, note 1. Concerning the reason why the spheres possess a slower motion in proportion as they approach more closely to the superior (diurnal) sphere, Aristotle expresses himself in these terms (<i>De Caelo</i>, II, 10): <i>τὸ μὲν γὰρ ἐγγυτάτω μάλιστα ϰρατεῖται, τὸ δὲ πορρωτάτω πάντων ἥϰιστα διὰ τὴν ἀπόστασιν</i>. The import is: that the spheres most approximate to the diurnal motion, which proceeds from orient to occident, are subject to the greatest influence of this motion, insomuch that their contrary motion from occident to orient is more feeble; and, conversely, those which are most remote from the diurnal motion are less impeded in their opposing motion, which, consequently, is more potent and more swift. — This reasoning, indeed, appears rational (<i>εὔλογον</i>), as Aristotle affirms. If Maimonides deems it strange and astonishing, it is doubtless because the tardiness or swiftness of the <i>periodic</i> motion of the spheres (from occident to orient) is not proportionate to their respective distance from the superior sphere, or else because, according to him, there is one sphere more swift than another, which nevertheless is situated above the latter. The term <span dir=\"rtl\">מסתגרבה̈</span>, <i>strange</i>, is not rendered in the Hebrew version of Ibn-Tibbon, nor in that of Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.1740": "See above p. 154, and ibid., note 1.",
    "fn.1741": "According to Abravanel (<i>loco citato</i>, fol. 8 <i>a</i>), Maimonides alludes to the retrograde motion of the nodes of the planets, which proceeds from orient to occident. Indeed, it is scarcely possible to explain this passage otherwise; for none of the spheres of the planets possesses a natural motion from orient to occident. As spheres were imagined for each motion, so were they also attributed to the retrograde motion of the nodes. Thus, the Arabs assign to the nodes of the moon a sphere which they denominate <span dir=\"rtl\">فلك الجوزهر</span>, a term rendered by Jewish authors as <span dir=\"rtl\">גלגל התנין</span> (vide <i>Yesôd ’olâm</i>, book III, chap. VIII). Now, the motion of the nodes of the planets situated above the moon is of an extreme tardiness and almost imperceptible, in comparison with the motion of the moon's nodes, which, according to Maimonides, traverse in one year 18°, 44′, 42″ (vide Abridgement of the Talmud, treatise <i>Kiddousch ha-hodesch</i>, chap. XVI, § 2). However, according to the principles established by Aristotle, the spheres moving from orient to occident ought to possess a more rapid motion, in proportion as they are more approximate to the diurnal sphere and more subject to its influence. Such appears to be the import of Maimonides' objection, however subtle it may seem. Concerning the nodes and their movements, confer Riccioli, <i>Almagestum novum</i>, vol. I, part I, p. 502.",
    "fn.1742": "That is to say: The diversity observed in the movement of the spheres could have been reduced to a physical cause, just as one can explain, from a physical perspective, why the four elements occupy different positions, some towards the center, others towards the circumference.",
    "fn.1743": "See above p. 146, note 3.",
    "fn.1744": "See above, chapter VIII, p. 78, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 4.",
    "fn.1745": "That is, the matter of the stars and that of the spheres.",
    "fn.1746": "Ibn-Falaquéra (Moré ha-Moré, p. 102) observes, with reason, that Maimonides improperly uses the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ikhtilaf</span> (which means <i>diversity</i> or <i>variety</i>), and that one should say <span dir=\"rtl\">faraq</span>, as in the text of Abu-Nasr.",
    "fn.1747": "The author means that it would be quite astonishing if the star were fixed in its sphere due to a physical and necessary law, and that this cannot be explained except by the will of the Creator acting freely and with a certain intention.",
    "fn.1748": "Literally: <i>No surprise accompanies this opinion</i>.",
    "fn.1749": "The Talmud reports that Abraham possessed great astronomical knowledge, and that all the kings of the East and West came to consult him. See Talmud of Babylon, <i>Baba-Bathra</i>, fol. 16 <i>b; Yoma</i>, fol. 28 <i>b;</i> cf. Josephus, <i>Antiquities</i>, book I, chapter 8, § 2.",
    "fn.1750": "The author has here committed an error of memory; the words <span dir=\"rtl\">עושה השמים</span> are found nowhere in Jeremiah. One has probably thought of that passage in Jeremiah (32, 17): <i>O Lord God! behold, thou hast made the heaven, etc.;</i> or of that other passage (X, 12; LI, 15): <span dir=\"rtl\">עושה ארץ בכחו וכו׳</span>, <i>He that has made the earth by his power</i>, <i>etc</i>.",
    "fn.1751": "See vol. I, chap. LXX, p. 324.",
    "fn.1752": "See above, chap. IV, p. 54-56.",
    "fn.1753": "Cf. above, p. 157, note 2.",
    "fn.1754": "See above p. 152, note 3, and p. 153, n. 1.",
    "fn.1755": "More literally: <i>Grâce à quoi l’une se distingue-t-elle par une</i> (<i>certaine</i>) <i>nature à l’exclusion de la nature de l’autre</i>.",
    "fn.1756": "Literally: <i>Que celui qui l’a particularisé</i> (or <i>déterminé</i>) <i>n’a jamais cessé</i> (<i>d’agir</i>) <i>ainsi</i>.",
    "fn.1757": "That is to say, the opinion which attributes everything to an acting being with intention and will, and not to a blind necessity.",
    "fn.1758": "On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אבחריה</span>, see the t. I, p. 300, note 2. It corresponds here to the Greek words <i>ἐπὶ πολύ</i>, which Aristotle uses in this demonstration. See <i>Phys.</i>, book II, at the beginning of chap. V.",
    "fn.1759": "That is to say, most frequently, individuals of the same species have the same figures and contours, with rare exceptions, and similarly, certain actions emanate from each species that rarely fail to appear.",
    "fn.1760": "One cannot see to what the masculine suffix of the words <span dir=\"rtl\">גּ̇ואי̈אחה</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">כלה</span> refers; one must infer <span dir=\"rtl\">אלעאלס</span>, <i>the world.</i>",
    "fn.1761": "The Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">תלקא אנפסהא</span> correspond, in the Greek text, to <i>τὸ αὐτόματον</i>, and ought to be considered, in some wise, as a compound substantive, the literal translation of which would be: the <i>sua sponte, spontaneity.</i> Confer supra, p. 17, at the close of the XVII<sup>th</sup> proposition, where the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מן הלקאיה</span> correspond to the Greek words <i>ὑφ’ ἑαυτοῦ</i>. — The Arabic translation of this passage from Aristotle is almost literal, saving a few slight variants, which one also discovers in the Arabo-Latin version. Vide <i>Physica</i>, book II, chap. 4: <i>Ἐισἰ δε τινες οὶ ϰαὶ τοὐρανοῦ τοῦδε ϰαὶ ϰοσμιϰῶν πάντων αἰτιῶνται τὸ αὐτόματον, ϰ. τ.</i>).",
    "fn.1762": "Maimonides, when questioned by Samuel ibn-Tibbon concerning the precise import of these words of Aristotle, furnished him, in the letter already cited (vide supra, pp. 21 and 24), with the following explanation: <span dir=\"rtl\">ענינו כי כל הגופים הנראים ר\"ל\nהמושגים בהרגשת הראות מהם על דרך משל הארץ והמים והזהב והכסף\nוזולתם מזה הסוג ומהם השמים והכוכבים ואלה כלם אמר בהם בלבד\nנראים אבל השמים והכוכבים בלבד סגולה משאר הגופים הנראים\nקוראים אותם הפילוסופים הגופים האלהיים וכן יאמרו על הגלגל\nהגוף האלהי ענינו כי הוא הגוף אשר הוא קים בקיום הנצחי לפי מחשבתם</span> \"The import is: All visible bodies, that is to say, those perceived by the sense of sight, such as, for instance, the earth, water, gold, silver, and other like things, are denominated merely <i>visible</i>, and thereto one includs also the heaven and the stars; but these latter alone, amongst all visible bodies, are denominated by the philosophers, par excellence, <i>the divine bodies.</i> And likewise they term the celestial sphere <i>the divine body</i>, intending thereby that it is, according to their opinion, the body which is stable as God Himself.\"",
    "fn.1763": "Literally: <i>that they are by the design of a </i>being<i> </i>acting with intention and by the will of a <i>willing</i>.",
    "fn.1764": "The meaning of this passage is as follows: Aristotle, whilst considering the existence of the world as a <i>necessary</i> thing, does not, on that account, believe the world to be the work of blind fatality, nor to have issued from a cause which acts without consciousness of its own operation, as a body which casts a shadow; but he believs, on the contrary, that God acts with full consciousness and is the efficient cause of the world, even as the intellect is the efficient cause of the intelligible, which, as <i>intelligible</i>, is necessarily thought and comprehended by the intellect.",
    "fn.1765": "Cf. <i>Métaph.</i>, XII, 7: <i>ἐπεἰ καἰ ἡ ἡδονὴ ἐνέργεια τούτου, ϰ. τ. λ.</i>",
    "fn.1766": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">ולזה לא יאמר לזה כונה</span>, ms. <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יאמר וכו׳</span>. Al-’Harîzi has better rendered this passage: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואפילו אם יאמר כי הוא רוצה מה שיתחייב ממנו ···· לא יאמר לזה כונה ♦</span>",
    "fn.1767": "That is, those who, while admitting the eternity of the world, maintain that there is from God <i>purpose</i> and <i>determination</i>. See at the end of the preceding chapter.",
    "fn.1768": "Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi have: <span dir=\"rtl\">two reasons</span>, <i>two causes;</i> this is because instead of the two words <span dir=\"rtl\">סבב אן</span>, they read <span dir=\"rtl\">סבבאן</span>, in the dual, which would, however, be an error, as the genitive <span dir=\"rtl\">סבבין</span> would be required. One of our mss. has, in fact, this latter form, but this is merely a supposed correction by the scribe.",
    "fn.1769": "That is, in any agent that acts in the manner in which we act.",
    "fn.1770": "That is to say: Just as its action could not have begun in time, so its will, purpose, etc., necessarily existed from all eternity.",
    "fn.1771": "See above chapter XIII, p. 112.",
    "fn.1772": "That is to say: they may have thought it more fitting for God to be called efficient or agent, and they may have wanted to avoid the unpleasant expressions of first cause and necessity. (Cf. the t. I, at the beginning of chap. LXIX).",
    "fn.1773": "That is, the intention or plan that we attribute to God. Ibn-Tibbon added the words <span dir=\"rtl\">היטם יתעלה </span> for greater clarity.",
    "fn.1774": "Literally: If this cause is the reason for this diversity and if this necessarily results from its existence.",
    "fn.1775": "The participle <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמבצע</span> , which relates to <span dir=\"rtl\">אלפאעל</span>, <i>the efficient</i>, should be pronounced in the active form; the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have the passive participle <span dir=\"rtl\">המיוחדת</span>, but the manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">המיחרת</span>.",
    "fn.1776": "See above, chap. IV, where the author discussed the order of the Intelligences and the spheres, attributing Aristotle's theories to the Arab peripatetics.",
    "fn.1777": "That is to say, from the material of sublunar things, which emanates from the spheres, which in turn emanate from the Intelligences.",
    "fn.1778": "Literally: <i>And none of these things precedes the other and exists, according to him</i> (Aristotle), <i>without her</i>.",
    "fn.1779": "See above, p. 148, notes 1 and 2.",
    "fn.1780": "Ibn-Tibhon, not having been able to find Hebrew words to designate all these secondary qualities <i>secondaries</i>, has put for the two first, <span dir=\"rtl\">the rough and its opposite</span>, <i>the smooth and its opposite</i>, and for the two last, <span dir=\"rtl\">the spongy and its opposite</span>, <i>the spongy quality and its opposite.</i> Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">the thick and the smooth and the hard and the soft and the heavy and its opposite</span>.",
    "fn.1781": "The author intends to convey that, although it is evident that both species of qualities exist simultaneously, and that the primary qualities do not temporally precede the secondary qualities, it is nevertheless affirmed that the latter are emanated from the former, as the effect emanates from its cause. Ibn-Tibbon adds, in his version, these explanatory words: <span dir=\"rtl\">שאין נופל בזה ענין החרוש אך ענין הסבה</span>. “And it does not concern herein the idea of <i>birth</i>, but the idea of <i>cause</i>.”",
    "fn.1782": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויאמר ארסטו</span>; one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">יאמר</span>, without the <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span> conjunction, as the mss. of Al-’Harîzi have: <span dir=\"rtl\">על זה הדמיון בעצמו אמר ארסטו</span>.",
    "fn.1783": "We must add, in the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">או</span>, which is found in the manuscripts. In the following phrase: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי בלגו וכן׳</span>, we must delete the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כי</span>, which the manuscripts do not have, but is also found in the version of Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.1784": "This proposition, which Maimonides borrowed from Ibn-Sīnā, has not been enunciated by Aristotle; yet one does find in the writings of the latter several passages which may have given rise to it. Thus, for example, Aristotle says in the <i>Physics</i> (Book VIII, Chap. VI, end) that that which is unmoved (the prime mover), remaining simple and ever the same, can produce but one simple motion (<i>τὸ ὃ’ἀϰίνητον, ὥσπερ εἴρηται, ἅτε ἁπλῶς ϰαὶ ὡσαύτως ϰαὶ ἐν τῷ αὐτῷ διαμένον, μίαν ϰαὶ ἁπλῆν ϰινήσει ϰίνησιν</i>). In the <i>Metaphysics</i> (Book XII, Chap. VIII, towards the end), it is stated that the prime unmoved mover being <i>one</i> both in idea and in number, it follows that that which is moved always and continuously is likewise <i>one;</i> and that, consequently, there is but one heaven (<i>ἓν ἄρα ϰαὶ λόγῳ ϰαὶ ἀριθμῷ τὸ πρῶτον ϰινοῦν ἀϰίνητον ὄν · ϰαὶ τὸ ϰινούμενον ἄρα ἀεὶ ϰαὶ συνεχῶς ἓν μόνον · εἷς ἄρα οὐρανὸς μόνος</i>). Herein, as elsewhere, Maimonides does attribute to Aristotle a theory of Ibn-Sīnā, who affirms that the first cause, being simple and absolute unity, could have directly emanated from itself but one simple intelligence; Ibn-Rushd observs that it is by error that this proposition has been ascribed to Aristotle, one having misapprehended the sense which that philosopher attached to the idea of <i>unity</i>, when he presents the universe as a unity, or an organic whole, emanated from a first and unique cause. Cf., upon this subject, my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 360 et seq. — The author will show in this chapter that the entire edifice of the successive emanation of Intelligences and spheres, according to the theory of Aristotle (or rather of Ibn-Sīnā), suffices not to explain the multiplicity and diversity which reign in the world, but that all difficulties vanish, as soon as one admitts a creating God, who has created the world by His free will.",
    "fn.1785": "There is herein an apparent contradiction with that which the author has stated concerning fire in Chap. LIII of the First Part (pp. 207-208), in which he cits the example of fire to demonstrate that a single simple cause may produce effects seemingly diverse, whilst here he does expressly state that fire is in itself a compound of two simple things. But all the effects of fire which the author enumerates in the passage in question, he does, in reality, attribute to one of the two simple qualities of fire, namely, to heat.",
    "fn.1786": "This proposition and the two following ones are not borrowed from Aristotle; the author states them as axioms, against which no objection can be raised.",
    "fn.1787": "As we have already observed, the author attributes to Aristotle the theory of the emanation of the Intelligences one from another, which belongs to Al-Farabi and Ibn-Sina. Cf. above p. 51, note 4, and my <i>Mélanges, etc.</i>, p. 331 and 360.",
    "fn.1788": "The editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon read <span dir=\"rtl\">הראשון</span>, which is merely a printing error; the manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">האחרון</span>.",
    "fn.1789": "According to the theory of Ibn-Sīnā, each intelligence is in some wise <i>compounded</i>, inasmuch as, on the one hand, it does think itself, and, on the other hand, it does think its cause, or the superior Intelligence, which serves it as a form and of which it is in some wise the substratum. From the first of these two thoughts emanats the sphere, and from the second emanats the inferior Intelligence. Cf. above, towards the end of Chap. IV, p. 60 et seq.",
    "fn.1790": "See above, p. 159-160.",
    "fn.1791": "That is to say: It would no longer suffice to consider each Intelligence as composed of two simple elements or two kinds of <i>thoughts</i>, but rather one of these two elements would itself have to be composed in order to serve as a cause for the two species of matter and form that are in the sphere and the star.",
    "fn.1792": "Literally: <i>And this</i>, <i>if the matter of all the stars were a single one;</i> that is, the hypothesis of a <i>composite element</i>, which would exist in the separate Intelligences and from which would be emanated the matter of the spheres and that of the stars, this very hypothesis, I say, would not suffice even if one were to admit that the matter of all the stars is a single and the same one.",
    "fn.1793": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אללטכׄיה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(اللطخية)</span> does designate, according to Maimonides (in his letter to R. Samuel ibn-Tibbon), “the stars which possess no lustre, which one does not well distinguish, even in the darkness of night, and whose appearance differs but little from that of the firmament” <span dir=\"rtl\">הכוכבים שאין להם זהר ואפילו באישון לילה ואפלה אינם נכרים ומראהם משונה מעט מן מראה הרקיע·</span> Al-Ḥarīzī renders this word by <span dir=\"rtl\">הכוכבים המעוננים</span>, <i>the nebulous stars.</i>",
    "fn.1794": "The author intends to convey that it would moreover be necessary to posit, within the Intelligences, separate elements, as efficient causes of the diverse matters, and other elements, as causes of the forms. Thus, for example, the intelligence which presids over the sphere of the fixed stars must needs contain a <i>compound element</i> to produce the matter and form of that sphere; a second to produce the matter and form of the brilliant stars; a third to produce the matter and form of the nebulous bodies; and lastly, a <i>simple element</i> to produce the Intelligence which is beneath it, or that of the sphere of Saturn.",
    "fn.1795": "Literally: <i>They do not preserve the regularity of the order.</i>",
    "fn.1796": "See above, p. 153-154.",
    "fn.1797": "In other terms: How comes it that the celestial bodies, possessing one and the same matter, do not successively receive form one from another, as occurs in the elements and generally in sublunary things, which one observes constantly serving mutually as substratum and form to one another, traversing the various degrees of individuality, species, and genus, because prime matter, being one, is apt to receive all forms?",
    "fn.1798": "For it is from the form of the movement, that is, the circular movement common to all spheres, that one has concluded that their matter is one.",
    "fn.1799": "Literally: <i>so that the aptitude is not destroyed</i>, that is to say: So that one is not forced to deny that the matter, which one has supposed to be <i>one</i>, is apt to receive all forms and all accidents. Ibn Tibbon has rendered the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאסתיהאל</span> by the following paraphrase: <span dir=\"rtl\">the material is worthy of all forms and all accidents</span>.",
    "fn.1800": "In several editions of Ibn-Tibbon's translation, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">it will not be acquired</span> are missing after the word <span dir=\"rtl\">which</span>, rendering the sentence unintelligible.",
    "fn.1801": "Literally: <i>by the will of a willing one.</i>",
    "fn.1802": "More literally: <i>namely that God… would exist in relation to all beings</i> (<i>in such a wise</i>) <i>as to innovate nothing.</i> The Arabic phrase is somewhat obscure, and the Arabic manuscripts offer one certain variants. One of the Leyden manuscripts (No. 18) bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">והי כון אלאלאה אלדׄי יקר כל עאקל בכמאלה בגׄמיע אנואע אלכמאלאת מע כל אלמוגׄודאת לא יבדי שיא</span>. Ibn-Tibbon's version accords with this reading, which omits the words <span dir=\"rtl\">צאר וגׄודה</span> and replaces the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">ענד</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">מע</span>; but the translator has supplied the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בענין</span>, <i>de manière.</i> Al-’Harîzi has translated somewhat freely: …. <span dir=\"rtl\">והיא היות האלוה ית׳ לא יוכל לשגות דבר בכל הנמצאים</span>.",
    "fn.1803": "That is to say: if one were to suppose that order and symmetry could be disrupted in the smallest details of the universe, it would be, in a sense, attributing imperfection to God.",
    "fn.1804": "On the meaning of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמתעצבין</span>, see vol. I, p. 438, n. 2. Al-’Harîzi translates this word as <span dir=\"rtl\">המקנאים</span>, <i>men consumed by jealousy</i>, <i>passionate men</i>; Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, mss.) as <span dir=\"rtl\">המתגברים</span>, <i>men who are violent</i>.",
    "fn.1805": "Here is how Maimonides himself, in his letter to Ibn-Tibbon, explains this passage: <span dir=\"rtl\">or one who preceded him with mistaken opinions and he wants to repel the difficulties that contradict his mistaken opinions</span>, <i>or who has adopted mistaken opinions in advance and who wants to repel the objections that overturn his mistaken opinions.</i>",
    "fn.1806": "Literally: <i>and the revelations of evils</i>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולהרבות המחלוקות</span>, <i>and</i> (<i>which serve</i>) <i>to multiply disputes;</i> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשרור</span>, he read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשדׄודׄ</span>.",
    "fn.1807": "Lit.: <i>in order to have</i> <small>TIED</small> <i>the doubts.</i> The expression <span dir=\"rtl\">‘uqda al-shukuk</span>, <i>tying the doubts or difficulties</i>, is opposed to <span dir=\"rtl\">hul al-shukuk</span>, <i>untying</i> or <i>resolving the difficulties.</i>",
    "fn.1808": "Literally: the two sides of the contradiction in this thing.",
    "fn.1809": "Cf. above, chap. XV, p. 122.",
    "fn.1810": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version generally carry <span dir=\"rtl\">אראנו</span>; one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">הראינו</span>, as in the manuscripts and the edition <i>princeps.</i>",
    "fn.1811": "Literally: <i>but rather the importance of their absurdity and of the opposition which being offers to them.</i> The author has expressed himself in a manner too concise and indeed somewhat illogical; for it is not the doubts which are more or less absurd and in contradiction with the nature of being, but the two opposite hypotheses, the objects of the doubts. The purport of this phrase is, in fine, as follows: when one compares the two opposing hypotheses, to examine which of the two appears more doubtful, it is not a matter of ascertaining on which side lies the greater number of doubts, but rather of weighing the improbabilities which would result from each of the two hypotheses, and of discerning which of the two is more contrary to the laws of nature.",
    "fn.1812": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version add here the words <span dir=\"rtl\">על אמתתם</span>, which are not found in the manuscripts.",
    "fn.1813": "Literally: <i>so that you may know the doubts in their reality.</i>",
    "fn.1814": "Literally: <i>for this opinion contains the overthrow of the law's foundation, and a heresy in respect of God.</i> By the words <i>this opinion</i>, the author designates the opinion of the world's eternity, albeit he has not expressly spoken thereof in that which precedes. Concerning the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אפתיאת</span>, which one here translates as <i>heresy</i>, see one's <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe,</i> p. 269, n. 3, and Cf. the III<sup>rd</sup> part of the <i>Guide</i>, at the commencement of chapter XVI.",
    "fn.1815": "Literally: <i>be therefore always suspicious in your spirit about this.</i> The word <span dir=\"rtl\">דׄהנך</span>, <i>your spirit</i>, is here an accusative absolute, or <i>circumstantial term</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(ذِهْنِكَ)</span> . See Silvestre de Sacy, <i>Grammaire arabe</i> (2<sup>nd</sup> edition), vol. II, n° 330.",
    "fn.1816": "The two prophets here spoken of are Abraham and Moses. The dual is evinced by the pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">המא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(ق)</span> ; but the manuscripts present the incorrect form <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄין</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(الَّذِينَ)</span> for <span dir=\"rtl\">الآذان</span> . Leyden manuscript No. 18 has <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄין הם</span> in the plural, and this reading has been likewise followed by Al-’Harîzi and Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 112), who perceived a plural in <span dir=\"rtl\">אלבנייק</span> ; the former has <span dir=\"rtl\">ומקבל דעת הנביאים</span>, and the latter <span dir=\"rtl\">ומקבל מהנביאים</span>.",
    "fn.1817": "The author uses here the words spoken one day by R. Io'hanan ben-Zaccaï, in a discussion he had with a Sadducee. See <i>Meghillath tâ'anith</i>, chapter V, and Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Bâba bathra</i>, fol. 115 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.1818": "That is, idolaters; see vol. I, p. 280, note 2.",
    "fn.1819": "See above, chap. <small>XIX</small>, p. 155, and <i>ibid</i>., note 2.",
    "fn.1820": "Literally: <i>that man has encompassed by science the order, etc.</i>",
    "fn.1821": "Throughout this entire chapter, Maimonides addresses himself particularly to the disciple for whom he originally composed the <i>Guide</i> (see the commencement of the I<sup>st</sup> part). One knows from other documents that this disciple, an emigrant from the Maghreb who later settled in Aleppo, passed but a brief space of time in Maimonides' company, established as he was in Old Cairo, and that this time was devoted to astronomical studies. Cf. one's <i>Notice sur Joseph ben-Iehouda</i>, in the <i>Journal Asiatique</i>, July 1842, pp. 14 and 34.",
    "fn.1822": "Literally: <i>it is that, with regard to the regularity of movements and the conformity of the path of the stars with what is seen, everything follows two principles</i>.",
    "fn.1823": "The ancient astronomers, in order to account for the apparent inequalities in the movements of the planets, posited them to move, at one time, in eccentrics – that is to say, in spheres whose centre deviates from that of the zodiac or of the Earth; at another time, in epicycles – that is, in small secondary spheres, borne by the great concentric or eccentric spheres, the centre of which is supposed to move upon the surface of the great sphere on the circumference of a great circle, termed the <i>deferent</i> (Vide <i>Almagest</i>, Book VI, Chapter 3). These hypotheses, purely geometrical, most ingenious, and at the same time exceedingly complicated, remained throughout the Middle Ages one of the cornerstones of astronomical science. They were rendered superfluous by the discovery of the elliptical orbits of the planets and by that of the laws of attraction. From the commencement of the twelfth century, the Arabian philosophers were disquieted by the improbability of Ptolemy's hypotheses and their lack of conformity with the physical principles and theories of motion expounded by Aristotle. Attempts were made to substitute other hypotheses; however, one did not succeed in elaborating a system capable of contending with that of Ptolemy (Vide my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, pp. 412, 430, 520 et seq.). These attempts met with no success among astronomers, and Maimonides himself, who here assails Ptolemy's hypotheses from a philosophical standpoint, hesitates not, in his treatise <i>Kiddousch ha-’hodesch</i>, or <i>On the Fixation of the New Moons</i>, to admit them in their entirety and to take them as the bases for his astronomical calculations.",
    "fn.1824": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">באלגׄמלהֹׁ</span> refers, as an adverb, to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄארגׄא ען אלקיאם</span>, and it is erroneously that the two Hebrew translators have referred it to what follows, by adding a copulative <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>. Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וםוף דבר חולק וכו׳</span>, and Al-Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ועל דרך כלל הוא הפך</span>. Only one of our MSS. (that of Leyden, no. 18) has <span dir=\"rtl\">ובאלגׄמלהֹׁ</span>, with the copulative <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>.",
    "fn.1825": "For the sun, Ptolemy is limited to the hypothesis of an excentric sphere. Cf. above, p. 93, n. 4.",
    "fn.1826": "That is to say: we have established that all parts of the sky, although perpetually in motion, never change their position, and that the different spheres, in turning on themselves, do not transport themselves from one place to another.",
    "fn.1827": "Cf. vol. 1, chap. LXXII, p. 359.",
    "fn.1828": "Cf. above, p. 82, where the author states that he studied with one of the disciples of Abou-Becr Ibn-al-Çayeg.",
    "fn.1829": "That is to say: one would content oneself, if need be, with their reasonings, were it truly established that the centre of the eccentric is always within the lunar sphere and that it is situated in the sphere of fire or in that of air, albeit one might object that, even under this hypothesis, it would still not constitute a movement around something fixed. — In order to comprehend what the author here states regarding movement <i>around a point in fire or in air</i>, one must recall Aristotle's theories concerning the position of the four elements within the lunar sphere, and their differing regions. Vide Vol. I, p. 134, n. 2, and p. 359, n. 1.",
    "fn.1830": "The author intends to convey that the Arabian astronomers have fixed the distances between the centres of the eccentrics and the centre of the zodiac or of the Earth, and likewise the distances of the planets and their magnitudes, taking for their unit the radius or semi-diameter of the Earth. This is, indeed, what Albatâni, or Albategnius (d. 929), accomplished; see his treatise on astronomy, published in Latin under the title <i>De Scientia stellarum</i>, Chapter 50, and Cf. Delambre, <i>Histoire de l’astronomie du moyen âge</i>, p. 50. One shall observe further on that, according to our author, it is pre-eminently Al-Kabîci, or Alkabitius, who furnished the complete demonstration thereof.",
    "fn.1831": "Literally: <i>the excentric point of the world, around which the sun turns</i>.",
    "fn.1832": "It is necessary to erase, in Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חמה</span>.",
    "fn.1833": "That is to say: it is at the same distance from the center of the world as the center of the excentric of Mars.",
    "fn.1834": "All mss. have, here and in the following phrase, <span dir=\"rtl\">פלך</span>, in the singular; the dual <span dir=\"rtl\">פלבי</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(فَلَكی)</span> would be more correct. Ibn-Tibbon has, the first time, <span dir=\"rtl\">גלגל</span>, and the second time <span dir=\"rtl\">גלגלי</span>, in the plural. Cf. above, p. 80, n. 5.",
    "fn.1835": "On the form of the imperative <span dir=\"rtl\">ארי</span>, See vol. I, p. 19, n. 2.",
    "fn.1836": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ומרכזהמא</span>, several manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">ומרבזאהמא</span>, or <span dir=\"rtl\">ומרכזיהמא</span>, in the duel; but all manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">מכׄתלף</span>, in the singular.",
    "fn.1837": "This proposition is somewhat obscure. The following, as it appears to one, is its purport: The celestial spheres being all precisely nested one within another, without any void existing between them (vide Vol. I, pp. 356-357), it necessarily follows that, of two spheres possessing different centres, the superior, or the greater, shall form on one side upon the lesser an thick arch, whilst, on the other sides, it shall form around the small inner sphere an envelope whose thickness shall progressively diminish, in proportion to the distance between the two centres. It is manifest then that, whenever the great sphere moves around an axis other than that which passes through the two centres, it shall invariably draw the smaller sphere along by its motion. But, if the movement of the great sphere occurs around the axis which passes through the two centres, it shall not be impeded by the inner sphere, which may ever remain in the same position; so that the great sphere may roll around the surface of the smaller, without drawing it along by its movement. — Ibn-Tibbon has expressed himself inaccurately, by stating: <span dir=\"rtl\">הקוטר העובר בין שני המרכזים</span>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בין</span>, one ought to have said <span dir=\"rtl\">על</span>.",
    "fn.1838": "For, if there were a sufficient void within the interior of the great sphere, its movement, regardless of the axis around which it moved, would no longer be hindered by the small one, which would therefore no longer be forced to move with it.",
    "fn.1839": "That is, from the hypothesis of eccentric spheres, which supposes the existence of spheres with different centers nested one within the other.",
    "fn.1840": "This hypothesis permits one to posit an interval between the two spheres, which, no longer being chained one to the other, remain free and independent in their respective movements. The void is supposed to be filled by spherical bodies which do not partake of the life and movements of the celestial sphere, and which vary in form according to the void they are to fill. Rabbi Levi ben-Gerson, who adopts this hypothesis, terms such a body: <span dir=\"rtl\">גשם בלתי שומר תמונתו</span>. Vide <i>Mil’hamoth Adonaï</i>, Book V, Second Part, Chapter 2.",
    "fn.1841": "This is the famous Arab astronomer Thâbit-ben-Korra (commonly known as Thébith), a Sabian from 'Hârran, who died in 901. See on this astronomer, d'Herbelot, <i>Bibliothèque orientale</i>, in-folio edition, p. 1015; Casiri, <i>Biblioth. arab. hisp.</i>, vol. I, p. 386 and following. Maimonides also cites him in the third<sup>e</sup> part of this work, chapter XIV, where he attributes to him the same theory of intermediate bodies.",
    "fn.1842": "The author intends to speak of the deviations of these two planets in latitude. The theory to which allusion is made is expounded in the <i>Almagest</i>, book XIII, chap. I, et seq. Cf. Al-Farghâni, <i>Elementa astronomica</i>, chap. XVIII. The Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">ميل</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">انحراف</span> correspond to the Greek words <i>ἔγϰλισις</i> and <i>λόξωσις</i>; Delambre opines that the former of these two words denots the inclination of the eccentric to the zodiac, and the latter, the inclination of the epicycle to the eccentric. See the notes on the <i>Almagest</i>, edit. of Abbé Halma, vol. II, p. 25. The Hebrew version of Ibn-Tibbon renders not the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אנחראף</span>. Al-Harizi has <span dir=\"rtl\">הנטיה והיציאה</span>.",
    "fn.1843": "Delambre (l. c.) expresses himself almost in the same sense on the difficulty of this theory: ‘This entire chapter, he says, is difficult to understand, impossible to retain. One cannot form a precise idea of this entire theory except by examining the tables where it is contained. This remark applies more or less to everything that follows, up to the tables.’",
    "fn.1844": "Literally: <i>a manifesté l’impuissance en cela;</i> that is to say: it has declared that man is incapable of forming a just idea of it.",
    "fn.1845": "This passage, drawn by Maimonides from the Arabic version of the <i>Almagest</i> (book XIII, chap. 2), differs somewhat from the Greek text, which bears: Κ<i>αὶ μηδεὶς τὰς τοιαύτας τῶν ὑποθέσεων ἐργώδεις νομισάτω, σκοπῶν τὸ τῶν παρ’ ἡμῖν ἐπιτεχνημάτων κατασκελές · Οὐ γὰρ προσήκει παραϐάλλειν τὰ ἀνθρώπινα τοῖς θείοις</i>. «Let no man deem such hypotheses difficult (to admit), considering the rigidity of the contrivances employed by us; for it is not fitting to compare human things with divine things.»",
    "fn.1846": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא מא דׄברת לך</span>, are missing in the ms. of Leyden, n. 18, and the two Hebrew translators have also omitted them, although they are necessary for the sense of the phrase.",
    "fn.1847": "Cf. above, p. 187, n. 1, and the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. XIV. One finds nowhere any information concerning the astronomer Al-Kabîci, nor concerning his treatise <i>On Distances</i>, neither is it known in what wise he demonstrated the distances between the centres of the eccentrics and the centre of the zodiac, taking for unity the semi-diameter or the radius of the earth. The astronomer in question is doubtless the same whom the scholastics oft cite under the patronymic appellation of <i>Alkabitius</i>, and who, according to Albertus Magnus, was named <i>Abdilazil</i>, or more accurately <i>’Abd-al-’Azîz</i>. See <i>Speculum astronomiœ</i>, chap. V and XI (Opp. vol. V, p. 659 and 663). The Imperial Library possesses several MSS. containing an <i>Introduction to Astrology</i>, by Alkabitius, which beginns with these words: Postulata a Domino prolixitate vitae Ceyfaddaula, id est gladii regni (MS. from the Sorbonne collection, n. 976). From this, one may conclude that our astronomer lived at the court of Seif-ed-Daula, ’Ali-ben-’Hamdân, at Aleppo, and consequently, that he flourished in the first half of the X<sup>th</sup> century of the Christian era. Cf. the <i>Bibliographical Dictionary</i> of ’Hadji-Khalfa, edit. of Mr. Flügel, vol. V, p. 473. The two works indicated by the Arabic bibliographer under n<sup>os</sup> 11,681 and 11,682 appear to one to be identical. The former, which was dedicated to Seif-ed-Daula, is anonymous; the latter is attributed to ’Abd-al-’Azîz ben-’Othmân al-Kabîci. Both bear the title of <i>Introduction to Astrology</i>. — As for the name <span dir=\"rtl\">القبيصي</span>, one may pronounce it <i>Al Kabîci</i> or <i>Al-Kobéici</i>; the version of Ibn-Tibbon might justify this latter pronunciation, for it bears <span dir=\"rtl\">הקבאיצי</span>.",
    "fn.1848": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">כי לפי דעתו</span>, must be omitted in the version of Ibn-Tibbon, which also contains them in the mss., but they are contrary to the Arabic phrase's construction.",
    "fn.1849": "That is, by means of the excentric or by means of the epicycle. All the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אצלין</span>, without the article, and similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">באחד משיני שרשים</span>; Al-Harizi <span dir=\"rtl\">העיקרים</span>, with the article.",
    "fn.1850": "The author here alludes to an ingenious observation by Ptolemy (<i>Almagest</i>, book V, chap. 5), relative to the oscillatory motion of the line of apsides or of the diameter of the moon’s epicycle, which forms a corollary to the two inequalities of <i>excentricity</i> and <i>evection</i>. See my Notes on the discoveries attributed to the Arabs relative to the inequalities of the moon’s motion (<i>Proceedings of the Sittings of the Academy of Sciences</i>, vol. XVI, p. 1444 et seq., and vol. XVII, p. 76 et seq.), and the memoir by Mr. Biot in the <i>Journal des Savants</i>, October 1843, p. 623 et seq.",
    "fn.1851": "That is to say, the moment in which the eclipse beginns, and the extent of the darkened surface. The masculine suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">מקאדירה</span> (Ibn-Tibbon <span dir=\"rtl\">ושעורו</span>), <i>its measures</i>, referrs to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אטׄלאמהא</span>, <i>their obscuration;</i> some MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ומקאדירהא</span>, <i>and their measures</i>, the suffix referring to the eclipses, and likewise, the version of Al-Harizi and some MSS. of that of Ibn-Tibbon have <span dir=\"rtl\">ושעורם</span>.",
    "fn.1852": "See Ptolemy, <i>Almageste</i>, book XII, chapter I and following, and Cf. above, p. 86, n. 2.",
    "fn.1853": "That is to say: the astronomer, as such, is not concerned with knowing whether his hypotheses can be admitted or not, from a philosophical point of view, but only whether they suffice to explain the phenomena. Cf. above, chap. XI, p. 92, 93.",
    "fn.1854": "Ibn-al-Çayeg meant that Aristotle may have known the hypothesis of eccentricity, but believed it to be unnecessary, because he thought that one could also find the location of the sun using homocentric spheres, by assuming a certain inclination. Cf. above, p. 57 note 1.",
    "fn.1855": "See above, chap. <small>XXII</small>, p. 179.",
    "fn.1856": "Literally: <i>man does not grasp anything, except this small dose of mathematics</i>.",
    "fn.1857": "The masculine verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">בעד</span>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">עלא</span>, relate to <i>the Heavens; the </i>substantive <span dir=\"rtl\">אלםמא</span> is of the common gender, and the author most often puts the adjectives and verbs related to it in the feminine.",
    "fn.1858": "The reading of this passage is uniform in all the manuscripts, save that the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אנה דלנא</span> are wanting in one of the Leyden manuscripts (no. 18). Al-Ilarîzi's version perfectly accords with the reading of our Arabic manuscripts; it bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">והראיה הכוללת המצואה מהם על מניעם הוא דבר אשר לא יגיעו דעות בני אדם אליו</span>. Ibn-Tibbon appends after <span dir=\"rtl\">מניעם</span> the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל שאר ענינם</span>, which essentially alters the sense of this phrase, and which would be rendered thus: “The general proof one may derive therefrom is that it proves to us (the existence of) its prime mover; <i>but the remainder of that which concerns it</i> (that is to say, the heaven) is a matter to the knowledge of which human intellects could not attain.” Ibn-Tibbon's reading appears justified by other passages in this treatise, in which the author expressly states that Heaven generally proves to us the existence of a prime mover, albeit one cannot render an exact account of the laws of motion. See, for instance, Part I, chap. IX, and above, chap. II and chap. XVIII, p. 144.",
    "fn.1859": "Literally: <i>the extreme point</i> (that is, the strongest proof) <i>of my preference for the search for truth, it is that I have manifested and declared my perplexity in these matters</i>. In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, one must write: <span dir=\"rtl\">I have explained and declared my perplexity in these matters</span>, as in the edition <i>princeps</i>.",
    "fn.1860": "Literally: '<i>The gates of allegorical interpretation would not, moreover, be closed before us, nor inaccessible to us, concerning the newness of the world</i>.' The author signifies that the texts relating to <i>the creation</i> of the world might be allegorically interpreted, as must be those from which it appears to result that God is a corporeal being. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">تأويل</span> designates <i>the allegorical interpretation</i> of the sacred text, whilst the explanation of the literal sense is designated by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">تفسير</span> .",
    "fn.1861": "More literally: <i>and it is necessary to interpret all that a demonstration refutes in its literal sense and that one knows to have need of interpretation</i>.",
    "fn.1862": "Literally: <i>by other modes of preference;</i> that is, by motivating the preference one would give to this contrary opinion in different ways.",
    "fn.1863": "On the meaning of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">عطر</span>, cf. vol. I, p. 115, note 1. —All the Arabic mss. have the infinitive <span dir=\"rtl\">תעטיל</span>, although the two preceding verbs are in the participle. The two Hebrew versions have the participle <span dir=\"rtl\">ומבטלת</span>.",
    "fn.1864": "By the word <span dir=\"rtl\">باطن</span>, <i>interior</i> (in Hebrew <span dir=\"rtl\">תוך</span>), the Arabs designate the allegorical sense of the words of the Qur’an, as opposed to <span dir=\"rtl\">ظاهر</span>, <i>exterior</i>, which designates the literal sense. Hence derives the name of <i>Bâtenis</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">الباطنية</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">اهل الباطن</span>), that is to say, adherents of the allegorical sense, a mystical Muslim sect which formed under the influence of Neo-Platonic doctrines and which bears much analogy to the Jewish <i>kabbalists</i>. See concerning this sect, its names, and its various branches, Schahrestâni, <i>Histoire des sectes religieuses et philosophiques</i>, Arabic text published by Mr. Cureton, p. 147 et seq. (German translation by Mr. Haarbrucker, vol. I, p. 221 et seq.).",
    "fn.1865": "See above, chapter XIII, p. 107 and following.",
    "fn.1866": "Cf. above, p. 109, note 1.",
    "fn.1867": "That is, in the sense of this opinion of Plato.",
    "fn.1868": "Most mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">and he found</span> in the active form, and similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">and he found for him many fancies</span>. We have preferred to write <span dir=\"rtl\">and he was found</span>, in the passive, the reading that we found only in one of our mss., but which is confirmed by the version of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">and he was found for him and so on</span>.",
    "fn.1869": "The text unnecessarily repeats the words: <span dir=\"rtl\">הדׄא אלראי אלאכׄר</span> <i>to this other opinion;</i> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאכׄר</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">איצׄא</span>, and similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">גם כן</span>.",
    "fn.1870": "That is to say: why did he not make it so, by a natural sentiment, that we would be inclined to do what he ordered and abstain from what he forbade, if it is true that he had only the intention that we do such a thing and abstain from such another?",
    "fn.1871": "Most mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">צורה</span>, in the plural <i>ses formes</i>, and similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">צורותיו</span>; I<sup>e</sup> ms. of Leyden, no. 18, has <span dir=\"rtl\">צורתה</span> in the singular, a reading adopted by Ibn-Tibbon.",
    "fn.1872": "Literally: <i>….. the denial and negation of all external words of the law about which there can be no doubt for an intelligent man that they are</i> (<i>taken</i>) <i>in these external</i> (or <i>literal</i>) acceptations. On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ظاهر</span> , in the plural feminine <span dir=\"rtl\">ظواهر</span> , cf. above, p. 197, note 2.",
    "fn.1873": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">על הקדמות</span> are missing in most editions of Ibn-Tibbon's translation, but are found in the <i>princeps</i> edition.",
    "fn.1874": "See the <i>Pirké R. Eli’ézer</i>, ch. III. The citation of Maïmonide is a bit abridged.",
    "fn.1875": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon reads <span dir=\"rtl\">רחוק</span>; one should have said <span dir=\"rtl\">מגונה</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">זאת דבה גדולה ורעה</span>.",
    "fn.1876": "The author means that R. Eliezer, in this case, would have affirmed the eternity of the prime matter, which he would have allusively designated by the <i>light of his garment</i> and by <i>the snow under the throne of glory.</i>",
    "fn.1877": "Literally: <i>If it were only according to the opinion of Plato;</i> that is to say: he would have affirmed the eternity of matter, although in a sense that is not as contrary to religion as the opinion of Aristotle. See above, chapter XIII, <i>second opinion.</i>",
    "fn.1878": "The ancient rabbis enumerate seven things created before the creation of the world, amongst which is found <i>the throne of glory</i>. See <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. 1 (fol. 1, col. 2); Babylonian Talmud, <i>Pesa’hîm</i>, fol. 54 <i>a; Nedarîm</i>, fol. 39 <i>b.</i> Cf. the <i>Khozari</i>, book III, § 73, and the ’<i>Akédâ</i>, chap. 101.",
    "fn.1879": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תאויל</span>, see above, p. 196, note 1. Ibn-Tibbon uses the Hebrew word <span dir=\"rtl\">פירוש</span> in the same sense, as observed by Ibn-Falaquéra in the Appendix to <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, at our passage (p. 154):",
    "fn.1880": "See the First<sup> part</sup> of this work, chap. IX.",
    "fn.1881": "Ibn-Tibbon's version lacks clarity here, owing to its too great literalness; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אמונתו</span> is the complement of <span dir=\"rtl\">יבלבל</span>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">היודע</span>, an adjective of <span dir=\"rtl\">בעל הדת</span>, bears the sense of <i>learned</i> (or 'savant'). The Hebrew phrase ought to be construed in this manner: <span dir=\"rtl\">יבלבל מאד מאד אמונת בעל הדת היודע</span>· The author signifies that this passage places in great perplexity him who is at once an orthodox theologian and a man of science. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ועל דרך כלל הוא דבר משבש דעת המאמין בתורה וישחית לאיש החכם אמונתו מאד מאד</span>.",
    "fn.1882": "According to Moïse de Narbonne, the author wants to say that, by this expression, Rabbi Eliézer undoubtedly designates a pure and brilliant matter, and not an attribute of God.",
    "fn.1883": "Cf. my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 144-145, where I have reported a curious explanation of this passage, attributed to Emperor Frederick II.",
    "fn.1884": "For the understanding of this passage, see the I<sup>st</sup> part of this work, chap. XXVIII, where the author goes into lengthy details on this subject.",
    "fn.1885": "See Voyage of Beréschîth Rabbâ, sect. 12 (fol. 11, col. 1). Cf. Babylonian Talmud, tractate Yômâ, fol. 54 b.",
    "fn.1886": "The author insists upon this subject, because there exists in this regard a divergence of opinions, not only amongst the doctors (see <i>l. c.</i>), but likewise amongst the philosophers; for the Platonists, and notably the Alexandrians, admitted a single matter which, of extreme subtlety at its origin, proceeded to condense itself successively more and more. Amongst the Jewish philosophers, it is Ibn-Gebirol who professes this opinion, in his <i>Source de vie</i>.",
    "fn.1887": "Literally: <i>but its destruction, after having been born and formed, is not</i>, <i>according to us</i>, <i>etc</i>.",
    "fn.1888": "Literally: <i>That this should not oblige us</i>, or <i>that this should not follow for us</i>; that is, that this reasoning could not be applied to the opinion we have maintained.",
    "fn.1889": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, <span dir=\"rtl\">the natural</span> is a printing error; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">natural</span>, without the article.",
    "fn.1890": "That is, it did not exist in this form that it now has; for all sublunar things are born from one another, and all are born from the mixture of the elements. — In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">נמצא</span> is missing here, after <span dir=\"rtl\">תדבר</span>, which is found in the manuscripts.",
    "fn.1891": "That is, it does not preserve perpétually the form it has now; for things <i>nées</i> eventually lose their forms and return to their original elements.",
    "fn.1892": "One must read, in the version of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">או בגזרת חכמתו</span>; the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">או</span> is missing in most editions, but is found in the <i>princeps</i> edition.",
    "fn.1893": "See what has been said about <i>the throne of glory</i> in the previous chapter. — All manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">בל אלנץ בתאבידה</span>, an elliptical phrase, in which one must understand the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קאל</span>. Ibn-Tibbon has supplemented this ellipse by translating: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל הכתוביס אומריס בנצחותו</span>.",
    "fn.1894": "It has already been observed elsewhere that our author attributes immortality only to the souls of the righteous, or of superior men, that is to say, to those which in this life have attained to the degree of <i>the acquired intellect</i>, whilst the souls of the impious, or those which have not sought to perfect themselves here below through virtue or science, are vowed to destruction. See vol. I, p. 328, note 4. And cf. the <i>Mischné-Torâ</i>, book I, treatise <i>Teschoubâ</i> (of penitence), chap. VIII, §§ 1-3.",
    "fn.1895": "More literally: <i>As those whose faith is widespread believe</i> <i>about the people of paradise</i>. The author is obviously alluding to the Muslim fables about the delights of paradise. — Cf. on this passage, Maimonides, <i>Commentary on the Mishnah</i>, 4<sup>th</sup> part, introduction to the X<sup>th</sup> (XI<sup>th</sup>) chapter of the treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>.",
    "fn.1896": "Most mss. have: <span dir=\"rtl\">מן טׄאהר</span>; some have: <span dir=\"rtl\">מן טׄאהר נץ</span>. The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read: <span dir=\"rtl\">מפשוטו כל דבר</span>, one should read: <span dir=\"rtl\">מפשוטו של דבר</span>.",
    "fn.1897": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יִשָּׂאח' (يُشَاحّ)</span> is here the passive aorist of the third form of the root <span dir=\"rtl\">شيخ</span>, bearing the sense of <i>to reckon strictly with someone</i>, <i>to be parsimonious or rigorous</i>, <i>to yield nothing to someone</i>. It is in this same sense that the Talmudists employ the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">הקפיד</span>, and by this verb Maimonides himself, in his letter to Ibn-Tibbon, translates our Arabic verb: <span dir=\"rtl\">סאלתני כיף יתרגׄם פלא ישאחח תתרגׄס אין בזה הקפדה</span>. —In most manuscripts, the verb in question is written <span dir=\"rtl\">ישאחח</span>, probably to render the root more readily discernible. In the introduction to the <i>Kitâb al-luma’</i>, the Oxford manuscript likewise presents <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמשאחחה̈</span>, for <span dir=\"rtl\">المشاحة</span>. Vide <i>my Notice sur Abou’l-Walid</i>, p. 133 (<i>Journal asiatique</i>, Nov.-Dec. 1850, p.355).",
    "fn.1898": "Ibn-Tibbon's version here appends the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ויציאה מעקרי הדת</span>, the equivalent of which is not to be found in any of our Arabic manuscripts, nor in the version of Al-’Harîi. This addition may proceed from an observation that Maimonides, in his letter, addressed to the translator, concerning the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">نشز</span>, which signifies, according to him, <i>to depart from the rank</i> or <i>from the line</i>, <i>to deviate from an opinion</i>, and which he stated he had employed here in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">יציאה מעקרי הדת</span>, <i>to depart</i> (to deviate) <i>from the principles of religion:</i>",
    "fn.1899": "Most mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄב</span>; one of the Leiden mss. (no. 18) has <span dir=\"rtl\">נגׄד</span>, and similarly Al-’Harîsi: <span dir=\"rtl\">כמו שנמצא</span>, <i>comme nous le trouvons</i>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon combines the two readings <span dir=\"rtl\">כמו שנמצא שחייבוהו</span>; Ibn-Tibbon probably placed one of the two verbs in the margin, which the copyists later combined.",
    "fn.1900": "See <i>Wayyikra Rabbah</i>, sect. 28 (fol. 168, col. 4); <i>Midrash-Koheleth</i>, at chap. I, v. 3. The citation of Maimonides, as often happens, differs a little from our editions of the <i>Midrashim</i>, which read: <span dir=\"rtl\">they found in him things that tend towards heresy</span>.",
    "fn.1901": "Literally: <i>Toward foreign opinions from the opinions of the law</i>.",
    "fn.1902": "That is to say: those who have not been able to distinguish eternity from the world of its perpetual duration have had to explain these words of Ecclesiastes in the sense that the earth will have the duration that was predetermined for it by the Creator.",
    "fn.1903": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the verse cited here is: <span dir=\"rtl\">י״י מלך עולם ועד</span> (Ps. 10, 16).",
    "fn.1904": "See, above, chap. X and <i>passim</i>.",
    "fn.1905": "That is, the heavens and their laws.",
    "fn.1906": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version carry <span dir=\"rtl\">כי אי אפשר המצא דבר מביא לשנוים</span>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">המצא דבר</span> are not found in the mss., and were only added for greater clarity.",
    "fn.1907": "Literally: <i>It is also as if he had desired to assign an aim to that which exists, or to excuse that which is altered, by saying,</i> etc.; that is to say: it would appear that Solomon, by the final words of this verse, intended to indicate the purpose God had in creating the world, or else to justify the change that the laws of nature seem to undergo, at certain epochs, through the intervention of miracles. — The words <span dir=\"rtl\">תכלית כונה</span>, in Ibn-Tibbon's version, constitute a double translation of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">גאיה̈</span>; one must therefore expunge one of the two words.",
    "fn.1908": "Cf. on this expression, applied to Moses, vol. I, p. 216, note 2.",
    "fn.1909": "Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">על נתיצת עם</span>; Al-’Harîzi says more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">על עקירת מלכות</span>, because the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">دولة</span> , which Ibn-Tibbon often translates by <span dir=\"rtl\">עם</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">אומה</span>, <i>nation,</i> means <i>dynasty, empire.</i>",
    "fn.1910": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">كور</span>, in its first and second forms, signifies <i>to wrap the head</i> (with a turban). In the Koran, <span dir=\"rtl\">كُوِّرَتْ</span> is spoken of the sun (chap. LXXXI, v. 1): <span dir=\"rtl\">إذَا الشَّمْسُ كُوِّرَتْ</span>. Commentators are not in accord regarding the sense of this passive verb; it is explained by <i>to be obscured</i> or <i>enveloped, to set, to vanish.</i> Vide the Commentary on the <i>Séances de Hariri,</i> p.313. Maimonides attaches thereto the idea of <i>overthrow</i>, <i>destruction</i>, as he himself states in his letter to R. Samuel Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלסמא כורת באלכאף ומעני אלתכויר אבארה̈ אלסמא</span> \"One must read <span dir=\"rtl\">כורת</span>, with a <i>câf.</i> This verb is spoken of the <i>destruction of the heavens.</i>\"",
    "fn.1911": "That is to say: it has been turned upside down.",
    "fn.1912": "Saadia translates in the same sense: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואדׄ יבער אללה האולא אלנאס</span>, <i>and God will remove from men</i> (the Israelites).",
    "fn.1913": "Literally: <i>I will show you the truth and the demonstration;</i> that is to say: I will demonstrate to you by examples that what I am saying is true.",
    "fn.1914": "Ibn-Tibbon has: <span dir=\"rtl\">והשמים נהפכים עליו</span>; perhaps he read, in his Arabic text, <span dir=\"rtl\">מנקלבה̈</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מנטבקה̈</span>.",
    "fn.1915": "The ms. of Paris (n° 237) reads מתממא, in the end.",
    "fn.1916": "Similarly Saadia: <span dir=\"rtl\">פיכׄזא אלקמ ריון ויכׄיב אלשמסיון</span>.",
    "fn.1917": "See vol. I, p. 111, note 2, and p. 355, note 1.",
    "fn.1918": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">اقبال</span>, fourth form of the root <span dir=\"rtl\">قول</span>, or <span dir=\"rtl\">قيل</span> (Vide the Commentary on the <i>Séances de Hariri</i>, p. 6), signifies <i>to rescind a contract, to release someone from an obligation</i>, <i>to pardon him;</i> hence, <span dir=\"rtl\">أقال عثرته</span>, <i>he raised him from his fall,</i> both literally and figuratively. Maimonides, in his letter, had counselled Ibn-Tibbon to translate the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אקאלה̈ עתׄרתהם</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">רפוי מחצתם</span>, <i>the healing of their wound</i>, doubtless by allusion to the words of Isaiah, <span dir=\"rtl\">ומחץ מכתו ירפא</span>. Doubtless Ibn-Tibbon found this translation too liberal; he translates more literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">הָקים כשלונם</span>. Maimonides employs the same expression in the third part, chap. XVI: <span dir=\"rtl\">ועתׄרוא עתׄרה̈ לא אקאלה̈ להם מנהא</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">כשלו כשלון אין תקומה להם ממנו</span>, literally, <i>and they made a false step</i> (or <i>a fall</i>) <i>from which they could not recover.</i>",
    "fn.1919": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">וסקוט בכׄותהם וכׄמול חטׄוטׄ עטׄמאיהם</span> are rendered, in Ibn-Tibbon's version, by <span dir=\"rtl\">והשפלם ושחות גדוליהם</span>. One observes that this translator has neglected the words <span dir=\"rtl\">בכׄות</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">חטׄוטׄ</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates more precisely: <span dir=\"rtl\">ורדת מזלם ושפלות גדולת נכבריהם</span>. Ibn-Falaquéra, in his critical observations upon Ibn-Tibbon’s version (Appendix <i>of the Moré-ha-moré</i>, p. 149), has already remarked upon the omission of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חטׄוטׄ</span>, which, says he, bears here the sense of <i>dignities, honours</i> (cf. Vol. I, p. 52, note 2); he does at the same time observe that <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄמול</span> here signifies <i>disappearance</i>, <i>evanescence:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">וזו המלה (חטֿ) היא בפרק כ\"ט מהחלק השני ובמול חטֿוטֿ\nעצמיותיהם העתיק ושחוה גדוליהם ולא העתיק זו המלה והעתקתו\nוההתעלם מעלות גדוליהם כי במול ההעלם הפך הראות ומלת במול\nשהוא ההעלם הורה על ענין שהוא יתעלם הכבוד והמעלה</span> .",
    "fn.1920": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אגיא</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">إِغْيَاءُ</span> , accusative <span dir=\"rtl\">أغنياء</span> ) is the nomen actionis of the II<sup>e</sup> form of the root <span dir=\"rtl\">غّ</span> and is employed in the sense of <i>hyperbole</i> (derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">غاية</span> , <i>extremity</i>). This signification of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">أغياء</span> is not indicated in the dictionaries; but Maimonides employs this word in several passages of this treatise: for example, hereafter, chapter XLVII: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאסתעאראת ואלאגֿאזאת</span> , <i>the metaphors and the hyperboles.</i> One employs also in the selfsame sense the word <span dir=\"rtl\">تغاي</span> , nomen actionis of the VI<sup>e</sup> form. See mine own edition of the <i>Commentary of R. Tan’houm upon ’Habakkuk</i>, p. 33, and note 13 <i>b,</i> p. 98.",
    "fn.1921": "Ibn-Tibbon translated the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחארהם</span> inexactely by <span dir=\"rtl\">ועניניהם</span>; Al-’Harîzi had <span dir=\"rtl\">ואותותיהם</span>. Both of them omitted to translate <span dir=\"rtl\">אלטׄאהרהׄ</span>, which is also missing in ms. n° 18 of Leyde.",
    "fn.1922": "All the Arabic manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">ותנסי תלך</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi translate as <span dir=\"rtl\">וישכחו הראשונים</span>.",
    "fn.1923": "Here, in Ibn-Tibbon's version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וימלאו את הארץ</span>, which are not expressed in the Arabic text nor in Al-’Harîzi's version, must be erased.",
    "fn.1924": "In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ממה שהגיע אליו</span>, one must read: <span dir=\"rtl\">מכל מי שהיו לו</span>, as the mss. have it. — The sentence having been interrupted here by a parenthesis, the author repeats it a second time.",
    "fn.1925": "Literally: <i>Measure it</i> (or <i>compare it</i>) <i>with what I have cited.</i>",
    "fn.1926": "This phrase is missing in the editions of the version of Ihn-Tibbon; we must add, according to the manuscripts: <span dir=\"rtl\">For Joel already said this about the day of the Lord's coming upon them</span>.",
    "fn.1927": "By these last words, the author, as he himself will say, indicates that it is possible that a temporary change may sometimes occur due to a miracle; but the laws of nature will never be modified in a definitive manner.",
    "fn.1928": "See Talmud of Babylon, <i>Rôsch ha-schanâ</i>, fol. 31 <i>a; Synhédrin,</i> fol. 97 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.1929": "The measurement of time by <i>a millennium</i> proves that, according to the author of this Talmudic passage, movement and time will exist. Therefore, something of the <i>Creation</i> will remain; for, in the orthodox system, movement and time cannot be eternal, and must necessarily be <i>created</i>, as maintained by Maimonides against Aristotle (see above, chap. XIII, and below, chap. XXX, p. 231 et seq.).",
    "fn.1930": "That is, in the system of those who say that the world, after having run a certain cycle, returns to its first state of chaos, so that God creates worlds to destroy them after a certain time: <span dir=\"rtl\">בורא עולמות ומחריבן</span>. See <i>Beréschîth-Rabbâ</i>, sect. 3 (fol. 3, col. 3), and cf. below, p. 233, and <i>ibd</i>., notes 1 and 2.",
    "fn.1931": "That is, the one who does not take these words as a metaphor, but rather believes that it is a real renewal in nature, must suppose that this renewal was foreseen, and, so to speak, set aside from the moment of creation.",
    "fn.1932": "The author appears to have had in view the following passage from the <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. I (fol. 2, col. 1): <span dir=\"rtl\">אפילו אותן שכתוב בהן כי הנני בורא שמים חדשים כבר הן ברואים מששת ימי בראשית הה״ד כי כאשר השמים החדשים והארץ החדשה חדשה אין כתיב כאן אלא החדשה</span>. One observes that there is no mention here of the participle <span dir=\"rtl\">עומדיס</span> nor of the verse from Ecclesiastes; it would appear that Maimonides had before him another redaction of this passage. See the commentary <i>Yephé toar</i> upon the <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. I, at the end of § 18.",
    "fn.1933": "Phrase borrowed from the Talmud, 'Abôdâ-Zarâ, fol. 54 b.",
    "fn.1934": "Literally: <i>This subject is</i> (or <i>means</i>) <i>that they believe in miracles</i>, etc. That is to say: the thing they wanted to indicate by the passage in question is their way of thinking about miracles.",
    "fn.1935": "On this opinion, which the author also discusses in his Commentary on the <i>Mischnâ</i>, see vol. I, p. 296, note 1.",
    "fn.1936": "Literally: <i>The time when it must advance what it advances;</i> that is to say, where it must proclaim the arrival of such a miracle.",
    "fn.1937": "Literally: <i>S’il en est comme tu le vois</i> (<i>here</i>), <i>this indicates the greatness of the one who said it, and</i> (<i>shows</i>) <i>that he found it extremely difficult, etc.</i>",
    "fn.1938": "The author uses here the Hebrew words <span dir=\"rtl\">the work of creation</span>, so used by the Talmudists to designate the act of creation and the relation that is made of it in the first <sup>chapter</sup> of the <i>Genesis</i>, beginning with the word <i>Bereshith.</i> Cf. Vol. I, p. 9, note 2, and p. 349, note 2.",
    "fn.1939": "The text says: <i>After it has thus been fixed.</i> The feminine verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אסתקירה</span> may relate to <span dir=\"rtl\">טביעה̈</span>, <i>nature, physical disposition</i>, or to <span dir=\"rtl\">משיה̈</span>, <i>will;</i> one believs that, in the author's mind, the verb relats to both matters at once, and Ibn-Tibbon, indeed, has rendered the verb in the plural (<span dir=\"rtl\">שהונחו</span>). One must read, in the Hebrew version, <span dir=\"rtl\">יתחדש רצון אחֵר אחַר שהונחו כן</span>. The copyists have neglected one of the two <span dir=\"rtl\">אחר</span>, which they deemed a superfluous repetition.",
    "fn.1940": "The author expresses himself in a less correct manner, saying literally: <i>This water was particularly to be divided;</i> he implies the <i>water in which the Egyptians were submerged</i>.",
    "fn.1941": "That is to say, in the passage of the <i>Midrash</i>, indicated above. See <i>Bereshith Rabbah</i>, section 5 (fol. 4, col. 3).",
    "fn.1942": "Cf. above, p. 182, note 1.",
    "fn.1943": "See above, p. 225, note 2, and cf. chap. II, p. 50.",
    "fn.1944": "All manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">טׄן</span> (with <i>ts</i>); but one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄן</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ضمن</span> to be avaricious, to be reserved), and it is in this sense that Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi have translated: the former has: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא היו מסתירים</span>, the latter: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא מנעוהו</span>. Cf. Vol. I, p. 67, note 2.",
    "fn.1945": "See the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תעטיל</span>, vol. I, p. 115, note 1.",
    "fn.1946": "Ibn-Tibbon translates <span dir=\"rtl\">הנטיה מן המכמה</span>, <i>by deviating from science.</i> Ibn-Falaquéra (Appendix of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, pp. 154-155) does observe that the translator doubtless read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתעדי</span> (with a <i>daleth</i>), instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתערי</span> (with <i>rêsch</i>); this latter word, he adds, should be translated by <span dir=\"rtl\">העֵרום</span>, <i>the denudation</i>, and the sense is <span dir=\"rtl\">ריקם מהחכמות</span>, <i>devoid of sciences.</i>",
    "fn.1947": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">לכן כל</span>, some manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">לכל</span>, a word which would depend upon <span dir=\"rtl\">פלאזם</span>, so that one should translate: <i>This is a duty for whosoever shall have acquired some knowledge thereof.</i> Al-’Harîzi has translated in this latter sense: <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא ראוי לכל מי שידע דבר זה</span>: Ibn-Tibbon has followed the reading which one has adopted in the present text: <span dir=\"rtl\">הוא ראוי אבל כל מי שידע מזה דבר</span>.",
    "fn.1948": "See, for example, the Commentary on the treatise '<i>Haghigâ</i>, chapter II, § 1.'",
    "fn.1949": "Allusion to a passage from Proverbs (XXV, 2), which the ancient rabbis apply to the mysteries contained in the I<sup>st</sup> chapter of Genesis. See <i>Beréschîth Rabba</i>, sect. 9, at the beginning.",
    "fn.1950": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">نوح</span> signifies properly <i>to make visible</i>. Ibn-Tibbon translates (ms.): <span dir=\"rtl\">but the revelation is withheld, however, a little will be revealed from them</span>. Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">but the manifest is withheld and forbidden, and therefore one must hint at the beginning of words</span>.",
    "fn.1951": "In Ibn-Tibbon's version, one should read: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא שהם מעורבות כדברי אחרים ובדברים אחרים</span>, as the mss. and the edition <i>princeps</i> have it, in effect. Similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל הם מתערבים בדברי אחרים ובמאמרים אחרים</span>.",
    "fn.1952": "On the words מתׄאלהא אלאול, cf. vol. I, p. 75, note 1.",
    "fn.1953": "According to the commentators, the tree <span dir=\"rtl\">שָקֵד</span>, <i>amandier</i>, takes its name from the root <span dir=\"rtl\">שקד</span>, <i>se hâter</i>, because it blossoms more quickly than other trees. See Raschi and Kim'hi on the book of Jeremiah, chapter 1, verse 12.",
    "fn.1954": "See, below, chapter XLIII.",
    "fn.1955": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">השמל</span> is explained by the Rabbins in divers manners: some assert that the <span dir=\"rtl\">חשמלים</span> are <span dir=\"rtl\">חיות אש ממללות</span>, <i>animals of fire which speak;</i> others affirm that the word comes from <span dir=\"rtl\">חשה</span>, <i>to be silent</i>, and from <span dir=\"rtl\">מלל</span>, <i>to speak</i>, forasmuch as these celestial beings at one time are silent, at another speak; yet others derive the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חשמל</span> from <span dir=\"rtl\">חוש</span>, <i>to hasten</i>, and from <span dir=\"rtl\">מול</span>, <i>to cut</i>, <i>to cease</i>, <i>to stop.</i> Consult the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. VII. In the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עגל</span>, <i>calf</i>, there is an allusion to <span dir=\"rtl\">עגול</span>, <i>round;</i> for the feet of the ’<i>Hayoth</i>, or celestial animals, are <i>rounded.</i> Maimonides does not declare himself upon the allusions which he discovers in the words <span dir=\"rtl\">נחשת</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">קלל</span>; the commentators of the <i>Guide</i> are of opinion that, in <span dir=\"rtl\">נחשת</span>, allusion is made to <span dir=\"rtl\">השחתה</span>, <i>corruption</i> (that is to say, to that which is exempt from corruption), and, in <span dir=\"rtl\">קלל</span>, to <span dir=\"rtl\">קל</span>, <i>light, rapid.</i> Cf., above, chap. X (p. 91), and, further on, chap. XLIII. — After <span dir=\"rtl\">נחשת קלל</span>, the version of Ibn-Tibbon adds the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וזולת זה</span>, and, likewise, Al-’Harîzi, <span dir=\"rtl\">וזולתו</span>. The Leyden MS., no. 18, has indeed <span dir=\"rtl\">וגיר דׄלך</span>.",
    "fn.1956": "In the <i>copper mountains</i> of the vision of Zechariah, Raschi sees an allusion to the power of the four <i>dynasties</i> represented by the four chariots. See Raschi on Zechariah, chap. 6, v. 1. On the allusions that Maimonides finds in the vision of Zechariah, see above, p. 91, note 1.",
    "fn.1957": "The author intends that <i>the principle</i>, as such, does not temporally precede the thing of which it is the principle; thus, in the examples which the author is about to adduce, the heart cannot be denominated the <i>principle</i> of the animal save at such time as the latter is completely formed and has attained to life; and likewise the element cannot be termed the element of a thing save at such time as that thing exists. The principle, either exists <i>in</i> the thing and constituts a part thereof, or exists only simultaneously <i>with</i> the thing without constituting a part thereof; neither of the two examples applies to this latter case, and some commentators supply this omission by adducing the example of the rising of the sun, which is the principle and the cause of day, albeit there exists between the two no other relation than that of simultaneity. — According to Moses of Narbonne, the words <i>although it does not temporally precede it</i> would refer solely to the second case, or that of simultaneity; and to this same case would apply the example of the <i>heart</i>, which does not temporally precede the animal life of which it is the principle. The second example, according to him, would apply to the principle which is <i>in</i> the thing to which it serves as principle <i>and which temporally preceds it;</i> for prime matter, or privation, and the foundations of an edifice temporally precede the thing to which they serve as principle. Here follow the exact words of Moses of Narbonne, according to the MSS.: <span dir=\"rtl\">ירצה וזה שההתחלה אשר בעצם לא במקרה כמו ההעדר נמצאת כמה היא לו התחלה ר״ל שהיא חלק ממנו ותקדם לו בזמן או עמו אע״פ שלא תקדס לו בזמן כמו שיאמר שהלכ התחלת החי וזה משל על ההתחלה הנמצאת עם מה שהיא לו התחלה אשר לא תקדם בזמן כי מיד שנוצר הלב רוח חיים כקרבו כמו שיאמר מהלב התחלת החיים והיסוד התחלת מה שהוא לו יסוד וזה משל על ההתחלה הנמצאת במה שיש לו התחלה ותקדם לו בזמן כמו ההיולי הראשון ויסוד הבנין אם טבעי ואם מלאכותי</span>.",
    "fn.1958": "Ibn-Tibbon renders: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל הראשון אמנם יאמר על הקודם בזמן לבד</span>. Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל הראשון הוא אמור על הקדמון בזמן בלכד</span>. Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>l. c</i>., p. 155) does observe with reason that these two translations are inaccurate; for one may understand them in this sense, that the word <i>first</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(أوّل)</span> is spoken <i>only</i> of that which is anterior in time (<span dir=\"rtl\">כי הראשון לא יאמר אלא על הקודם בזמן לבד</span>), whereas the author has but now stated that this word is sometimes synonymous with <span dir=\"rtl\">مبدأ</span> . Indeed, the two translators have neglected the particle <span dir=\"rtl\">قل</span> (in <span dir=\"rtl\">פקד יקאל</span>), which signifies <i>sometimes</i>, and Ibn-Falaquéra renders: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואמנס הראשון לפעמ׳ם יאמר על הקודם כזמן לכד</span>.",
    "fn.1959": "That is, the head, by its position, is the beginning of the animal; for the true principle of animal life is the heart.",
    "fn.1960": "See above, chap. XIII, p. 105.",
    "fn.1961": "The text says: <i>the</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">ב</span> <i>has the meaning of</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">ف</span> <i>in</i>, which Al-Ḥarîzi has rendered precisely by <span dir=\"rtl\">והבית כמו פי בלשון ערב</span>. Ibn-Tibbon has substituted: <span dir=\"rtl\">והכית כבית כלי</span>, 'the <small>BETH</small> is (employed) as the <small>BETH</small> of a <i>vessel</i> (or of a <i>container</i>),' that is to say, as a preposition indicating a circumstantial relation of time or place, in Arabic <span dir=\"rtl\">ظَرْף</span>. Cf. the <i>Sépher ha-rikmâ</i>, chap. VI, p. 31: <span dir=\"rtl\">והבית ⋯ לענין כלי או מחזיק</span>. In some mss. of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one does read: <span dir=\"rtl\">והבית כבית ביום</span>; which does signify that the <i>bêth</i> is a preposition of time, as in <span dir=\"rtl\">ב׳וס</span>.",
    "fn.1962": "The <i>principal word</i>, as the author has just said, does not imply a <i>temporal beginning</i>.",
    "fn.1963": "The text says: <i>because they have found</i>. The editions of Ibn-Tibbon have <span dir=\"rtl\">הוא מוצאם</span>, and some mss., <span dir=\"rtl\">באשר מצאו</span>; Al-’Harîzi says more exactly <span dir=\"rtl\">מפני שמצאו</span>.",
    "fn.1964": "Vide <i>Beréschith rabbâ</i>, sect. 3 (fol. 3, col. 3). It is to be remarked that here, as elsewhere, Maimonides does not precisely reproduce the text of our editions of the <i>Midrasch</i>, according to which the opinion of the two doctors is founded upon the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ויהי ערב</span>, '<i>and it was evening</i>' (Genesis, 1, 5). These words not being preceded by the creative utterance <span dir=\"rtl\">יהי ערכ</span>, '<i>let there be evening</i>,' they believed to discern therein an allusion to the pre-existence of the <i>order of time</i>.",
    "fn.1965": "That is to say, inasmuch as one speaks of a <i>first day;</i> for the biblical text does not bear <span dir=\"rtl\">יום ראשון</span>, but <span dir=\"rtl\">יום אחד</span>, '<i>one day</i>.' In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, there has been inserted, indeed, <span dir=\"rtl\">יום אחד</span>; but the mss. of this version, as well as those of the Arabic text and of Al-Ḥarîzi's version, bear <span dir=\"rtl\">יום ראשון</span>.",
    "fn.1966": "It has been observed (above, p. 222) that, according to the Talmudists, each world has a duration of six thousand years, followed by a seventh millennium of chaos, after which, in the opinion of R. Abbahou, a new world is created.—Leon Hebreo attributes this opinion to that of Plato, who proclaims the eternity of chaos. See <i>Dialoghi di amore</i>, ed. of Venice, 1572, fol. 151, and cf. above, p. 109, note 3.",
    "fn.1967": "R. Judah confines himself to establishing the eternity of time; R. Abbahou does moreover add thereto this other idea of worlds successively created and destroyed, that is to say, of diverse attempts at creation which God would have destroyed because they corresponded not to the ideal He had held in view, as R. Abbahou does expressly state in another passage of the <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i> (sect. 9, at the commencement): <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר דין הניין לי יתהון לא הניין לי</span>. It is therefore this idea, so little worthy of the divine omnipotence, that Maimonides finds more blameworthy than the opinion of R. Judah, which conforms to the Peripatetic doctrine. — It is in this sense that our passage is explained in the unpublished commentary of Moses of Salerno (Heb. ms., no. 238, of the Imp. Libr., fol. 220 <i>b</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר משה הקטן בדין גנה הרב רבנו המאמר הזה האחרון מן הראשון וכי היה הקב״ה בונה עולמות שלו ולא היו עולים בידו כתקונן ולזה היה מחריבן פאדם המתחיל במלאכה ומתקלקלת בידו ומרוב כעס מחריבן לא מחכמה יצאו הדברים</span>.",
    "fn.1968": "This solution, as we will see, consists in the supposition that all things in the sky and on the earth were created, at least in germ, from the very first moment of creation, and only needed to develop and organize themselves successively.",
    "fn.1969": "That is to say, unless these two doctors were content with the aforementioned solution, and wished to simply uphold the eternity of time, and, consequently, the eternity of the world.",
    "fn.1970": "See above, chap. XXVI.",
    "fn.1971": "According to this, one ought thus to translate the first verse of Genesis: 'In the beginning God created <i>with</i> the heaven and <i>with</i> the earth.' Grammar does not permit one to take this explanation seriously; for the particle <span dir=\"rtl\">את</span> is here evidently the sign of the direct object. This is one of those subtle interpretations which, alongside the literal sense, serve as a <i>point of support</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אסמכתא</span>) for some doctrine or other which the Rabbins sought to attach in an ingenious manner to the biblical text.",
    "fn.1972": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise '<i>Haghîgâ</i>, fol. 12 <i>a</i>; <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. 1, at the end.",
    "fn.1973": "All the mss. we have been able to consult have <span dir=\"rtl\">והבעת</span>; it seemed to us more correct to write <span dir=\"rtl\">ותבאינת</span>, in the VI<sup>e</sup> form.",
    "fn.1974": "Literally: <i>De sorte qu'ils ont comparé cela à un laboureur etc</i>.",
    "fn.1975": "Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>’Haghîgâ</i>, fol. 12 <i>a</i>. The author appears to have committed an error of memory in stating that this passage is found in the <i>Beréschîth rabbâ;</i> in our editions of the <i>Midrasch</i>, one finds but the commencement of the Talmudic passage: <span dir=\"rtl\">אור שברא הקב״ה ביום ראשון אדם צופה ומביט בו מסוף העולם ועד סופו וכוי</span>. Vide <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. 11 and 12 (fol. 9, col. 2, and fol. 10, col. 3).",
    "fn.1976": "That is, one understands all of them under the word <i>terre</i> from the first verse. The author means that the enumeration of the four elements in the second verse (cf. the following paragraph) proves that the word <i>terre</i> from the first verse includes all four; and, to prove more clearly that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ארץ</span> specifically refers to the element of the earth, he cites the tenth verse again.",
    "fn.1977": "The author has expressed himself in an embarrassed and somewhat obscure manner; the sense is: one aims to take the name in its particular and restricted acceptation, and to distinguish it from the other general acceptation, the name having both senses at once. Thus, for example, in verse 1, the word <i>earth</i> signifies both the sublunar world in general, and the element of earth in particular; whereas, in verses 2 and 10, this element is separated from the aggregate of the four elements, likewise designated by the word <i>earth</i>.",
    "fn.1978": "That is to say, by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הארץ</span> of verse 1. — In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, there is here a transposition; the reading of the mss. is conformable to the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי היסודות הארכעה נזכרו תחלה אמר השמים אשר אמרנו ששם ארץ הראשון יורה עליהם</span>. That which occasioned the transposition is doubtless the ambiguity present in the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עליהם</span> (as also in the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">עליהא</span>), which referrs to the elements; to avoid this ambiguity, Al-Ḥarîzi has substituted for <span dir=\"rtl\">עליהם</span> the words <span dir=\"rtl\">על ארבעתם</span>.",
    "fn.1979": "In the words <span dir=\"rtl\">רוח אלהים</span>, which are generally translated as the <i>esprit de Dieu</i>, the author sees the element of the air Cf. the t. I, p. 144, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 4.",
    "fn.1980": "According to our author, the parallelism indicates that <span dir=\"rtl\">חשך</span> (darkness), in this last verse, has the same meaning as <span dir=\"rtl\">אש</span> (fire).",
    "fn.1981": "Ibn-Sinâ expresses himself thus, when speaking of the sphere of elementary fire: <span dir=\"rtl\">واما هي النار فانها طبقة واحدة ولا ضوء لها بل هي</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">كالهواء المشفّ الذي لا لون له</span> ‘Fire forms but a single layer; it has no light, but is as the diaphanous air, which has no colour.’ Vide Schahrestâni, <i>Histoire des sectes religieuses et philosophiques</i>, p. 410 (Germ. transl., vol. II, p. 305). —Herein is treated of the <i>diaphanous in potentiality</i>, which may even be darkness, whilst the <i>diaphanous in actuality</i> is inseparable from light. Cf. Aristotle, treatise <i>On the Soul</i>, book II, chap. 7: <i>Φῶς δέ ἐστιν ἡ τούτου ἐνέργεια τοῦ διαφανοῦς ᾗ διαφανές · δυνάμει δἑ ἐν ᾧ τοῦτ ἐστί, ϰαί τὸ σϰὀτος</i>.",
    "fn.1982": "It is obviously necessary to read <span dir=\"rtl\">בתכׄציצה</span>, as the ms. of Leyde, n° 18, does, although most of the mss. carry <span dir=\"rtl\">תכציצה</span> without <span dir=\"rtl\">ב</span>.",
    "fn.1983": "This explanation of Maimonides is cited by Saint Thomas: «Rabbi Moyses ….. ignem significatum esse dixit per tenebras, eo quod ignis in propria sphæra non luceat, et situs ejus declaratur in hoc quod dicitur <i>super faciem abyssi</i>.» Vide <i>Quœstiones disputatœ</i>, de Creatione, Quæst. IV, art. 1 (Lyons edition, fol. 25 <i>d</i>). The author of the <i>Zohar</i> likewise makes allusion thereto; vide my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 278.",
    "fn.1984": "That is to say, in verse 2, in the words <i>and the spirit of God</i> (or the <i>air</i>) <i>moved upon the face of the waters</i>. Here, as has been observed, the word <i>water</i> denots the <i>elementary water</i> or the sphere of the element of water, and the form with which this water was invested, is <i>the elementary form</i>.",
    "fn.1985": "That is to say, terrestrial water, or that of the seas, rivers, etc.",
    "fn.1986": "By the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלהוא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(الهواء)</span> , one must understand the atmosphere here, and not the sphere of the air element, which is above the water element.",
    "fn.1987": "See <i>Bereshith Rabbah</i>, section 4, at the beginning (folio 3, column 3).",
    "fn.1988": "Literally: <i>Is also</i> (<i>to be explained</i>) <i>according to what I have expounded unto thee</i>. The author intends to speak of the observation he has made concerning the expression <i>and God called such a thing by such a name</i>. Vide above, p. 236, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1. The passage which one has placed between [ ] is a <i>note</i> which must be separated from the rest of the paragraph, which treats of <i>water</i>.",
    "fn.1989": "Cf. above, p. 78, note 4; and p. 159-160. — All mss. read <span dir=\"rtl\">מרכוזאת</span>, it would be more correct to write <span dir=\"rtl\">מרכוזה̈</span>.",
    "fn.1990": "If the stars were prominent and were not fixed in the vault of the sphere itself, it would necessarily be the case that there was a void between the different spheres.",
    "fn.1991": "By this third part, the author appears to mean the sphere of the elementary water.",
    "fn.1992": "The author intends to say, as it seems unto one, that this subject has been treated, in the account of the Creation, in a manner more enigmatic than the rest of that account. Rabbi Samuel Ibn-Tibbon calls the passage which treats of the separation of the waters: <span dir=\"rtl\">חדר אפל מאד וסוד עמוק סתום וחתום</span>, <i>a chamber most obscure and a profound mystery, sealed and closed</i>. Vide his treatise <i>Yikkawou ha-maïm</i>, chap. 20 (Presburg edition, 1837, p. 137).",
    "fn.1993": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise '<i>Haghîgâ</i>, fol. 14 <i>b;</i> cf. the t. I of this work, p. 110, note 5.",
    "fn.1994": "This is to explain one enigma by another; for the author does not, according to one's understanding, reveal the import of R. ’Akiba's obscure pronouncements, which have proved the very crux of interpreters. This entire paragraph, generally speaking, is exceeding obscure, and it would seem Maimonides intended strictly to conform to Talmudic injunctions by expressing himself but by insinuation concerning the <i>Ma’asé Beréschîth</i> (vide the Introduction to this work, Vol. I, p. 10). It appears that our author, combining the words of Genesis with Peripatetic theories, admitted, between the moon's orbit and our atmosphere, the existence of the three elements of fire, air, and water, forming spheres which encompass our globe. These spheres contain the sublunar prime matter clothed in elementary forms; these are but elements <i>in potentiality</i>, which, in our atmosphere, become elements <i>in actuality</i>. In the third sphere, or water, a separation was formed, called the <i>firmament</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רקיע</span>); the upper part remained water as an <i>element</i> in its entire abstraction, whilst the inferior part became water properly so called, which fills the earth's cavities. Thus, according to our author, it would be the superior water, the humid and cold element, which R. ’Akiba designated by the words <i>pure marble</i>, and he would have intended to signify that one must carefully refrain from discerning therein water akin to that found here below. Such appears to one to be the import of this entire obscure paragraph. Nevertheless, according to Moses of Narbonne and sundry other commentators, the inferior water, even the water of the seas, would itself constitute the <i>element</i> of water; whilst by the superior water, Maimonides is supposed to understand the middle stratum of the air. According to the Arabian Peripatetics, the air is divided into three strata: the first, nigh unto the element of fire, is hot and dry, and gives birth to divers meteors, such as comets, shooting stars, lightning, and the like; the second, that of the middle, is cold and humid, and the vapours ascending thereto redescend in the form of rain, snow, or hail; the third, nigh unto the earth, is warmed by the reverberation of the sun's rays. According to the commentators, it is this latter stratum of air which Maimonides would have regarded as the <i>separation</i>, or the firmament, which divids the inferior water, or that of the seas (which itself is elementary water), from the superior water, or the second stratum of air, which is but <i>water in potentiality</i>. It would therefore be of this latter that R. ’Akiba intended to speak when affirming that one must not call it <i>water</i>. Vide the commentaries of Moses of Narbonne and Schem-Tob, on our passage, and the commentary of Isaac Abravanel on the Pentateuch, at verse 6 of the first chapter of Genesis, fourth opinion.",
    "fn.1995": "Literally: <i>All that people have said concerning each thing thereof</i>. The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אנשי החכמה</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">האנשים</span>, a reading which the MSS. possess. The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">מנהא</span> referrs to the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאתֿאר (الآثار)</span> (<i>the signs, the meteors</i>), which here denots Aristotle's <i>Meteorology</i>, called by the Arabians: <span dir=\"rtl\">كتاب في الآثار العلوية</span> .",
    "fn.1996": "The formula <i>el Dieu vit que c’était bien</i>, by which the author of Genesis concludes the account of each period of creation, is not found in the second day, because, say the rabbis, the work of the water was not completed until the third day. Therefore, this formula is found twice in the account of the third day (verses 10 and 12).",
    "fn.1997": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">דרש</span> refers, among rabbis, to scholastic or allegorical interpretation, in contrast to literal explanation, known as <span dir=\"rtl\">פשט</span>.",
    "fn.1998": "See <i>Beréschîth rabba</i>, sect. 3 (fol. 4, col. 1): <span dir=\"rtl\">Rabbi Shmuel said, because the work of the waters was not completed, therefore it is written in the third chapter, that it is good to do the work of the waters twice, and one for the work of the day</span>.",
    "fn.1999": "Literally: <i>De l’une des choses créées de l’être</i> (or <i>du monde</i>). Instead of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הנמצאות</span>, which the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have, the mss. have, more accurately, <span dir=\"rtl\">המציאות</span>.",
    "fn.2000": "Word for word: <i>By a rough examination</i> (or <i>in general</i>).",
    "fn.2001": "That is to say: For one who knows not the speculative doctrines of physical science, there exists, between us and the inferior part of heaven (or the moon's orbit), no other body than the elements, and one comprehends not that there might be, beyond water properly so called, another water above the atmosphere; for that which has been said of <i>elementary</i> water, or of <i>water in potentiality</i>, is utterly unknown to him.",
    "fn.2002": "The author has expressed himself in a manner that is not entirely accurate; for he clearly means to say that if someone were to imagine that, by the <i>raki’a</i>, or firmament, one should understand the true sky, or the celestial sphere, then, according to the biblical theory, there would be water above the celestial sphere.",
    "fn.2003": "Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">from the sealed secrets</span> (from the mysteries <i>sealed</i>); he read: <span dir=\"rtl\">the sealed one</span> with a <i>dot</i> punctuated <span dir=\"rtl\">(the sealed one)</span> , but all our mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">the hidden one</span> without a point <span dir=\"rtl\">(the hidden one)</span> , and similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">from the hidden mysteries</span> (from the mysteries <i>hidden</i>).",
    "fn.2004": "That is, the separation of the waters by means of the firmament.",
    "fn.2005": "That is to say: The creation of the <i>raki'a</i>, or firmament, did not aim at prolonging the duration of the entire universe; for the world could have existed and perpetuated itself without the firmament and the separation of the waters.",
    "fn.2006": "Literally: <i>The doctors have already explained that the herbs and trees that God caused to grow from the earth, He caused them to grow only after having caused rain to fall upon it</i>. — See <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. 13 (fol. 11, col. 2): <span dir=\"rtl\">אלו ואלו לא צמחו עד שירדו עליהם גשמים</span>.",
    "fn.2007": "See above, chap. X, p. 89.",
    "fn.2008": "Cf. vol. I, p. 362, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.2009": "Vide Aristotle, <i>Meteorolog</i>., Book II, Chap. 4: <i>Ἒστι γὰρ δύο εἴδη τῆς αναθυμιάσεως, ὣς φαμεν, ἡ μὲν ὑγρὰ ἡ δὲ ξηρά · ϰαλεῖται δ’ἡ μὲν ἀτμίς, ἡ ὅὲ τὸ μὲν ὅλον ἀνώνυμος, τῷ δ’ ἐπὶ μέρους ἀνάγϰη χρωμένους ϰαθόλου προσαγορεὑειν αὐτἠν οἷον ϰαπνὀν</i>. <i>Ibid.</i>, Book 3, Chap. 6: Δ<i>ύο μὲν γὰρ αἱ ἀναθυμιἁσεις, ἡ μὲν ἀτμιδώδης ἡ δὲ ϰαπνώδης, ὣς φαμεν, εἰσίν</i>. Cf. Ibn-Sînâ, in the work of Schahrestâni, p. 410 et seq. (Ger. transl., Vol. II, p. 306 et seq.). — The dual <span dir=\"rtl\">אלבכׄאראן</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(البخاران)</span> here denots both species of <i>exhalations</i> (<i>ἀναθυμιἁσεις</i>), of which the one (<i>ἀτμίς</i>, <i>vapour</i>) is particularly designated in Arabic by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">بخار</span>, and the other (<i>ϰαπνός</i>, <i>fume</i>) by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">دخان</span> .",
    "fn.2010": "Cf. vol. I, p. 360 and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.2011": "The text reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואן אלטׄלאם</span>, <i>and that the tenebres;</i> the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">ואן</span>, <i>and that</i>, is attached to the beginning of this passage <span dir=\"rtl\">וקד עלמת⋯אן</span>, <i>you know that</i>, etc.",
    "fn.2012": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have: <span dir=\"rtl\">a matter that passes over him, a new thing is formed over him</span>, which is a double translation of the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">târek 'alayha</span>. The mss. have only: <span dir=\"rtl\">a matter that passes over him</span>, and 'Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">and the light of a word is formed over him</span>.",
    "fn.2013": "The author means that the Scripture also speaks of everything that has just been said; after the celestial sphere, mentioned in the first verse of Genesis, we speak of the elements, of light and darkness, of exhalations (II, 6), of plants, of animals, and finally of man.",
    "fn.2014": "See Babylonian Talmud, Rosch-ha-schanâ, fol. 11 a; ’Hullîn, fol. 60 a.",
    "fn.2015": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">לדעתן</span>, which our author applies to the <i>forme</i>, refers to rational beings; all creatures, say the rabbis, emerged from the Creator's hand, not in a germinal state, but in their most developed and perfect state.",
    "fn.2016": "The text says: <i>Avec ses plus beaux accidents;</i> that is, the beings, at the moment of creation, possessed externally all the beautiful accidental qualities of which they were susceptible.",
    "fn.2017": "Literally: <i>According to what one has said, that there was not yet</i>, <i>etc.</i> That is to say: until the close of the sixth day, the nature of things was not yet established by immutable laws; there is consequently naught offensive in the accounts of the II<sup>nd</sup> chapter of Genesis, once it is admitted that all transpired within the compass of the sixth day. Compare that which the author has stated above (p. 235) concerning the light, citing the instance of the husbandman and the sowing.",
    "fn.2018": "Literally: <i>And I shall likewise cause thee to note certain matters, even as they themselves</i> (them) <i>have caused us to note</i>. The author intends to signify that he will proceed after the manner of the doctors, merely drawing the reader's attention to particular points, without expanding upon his own notions. — Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">נבהונא</span>, the two Leyden MSS. present <span dir=\"rtl\">נבהוא</span>, without the suffix; likewise do the two Hebrew versions: <span dir=\"rtl\">כמו שהעירו</span>.",
    "fn.2019": "The two Hebrew translators have taken the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא אבסטהא</span> in the sense of <i>je ne les rendrai pas simples</i>, that is, I will not make their meaning clear and simple. Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא אשימם פשוטים</span>; Al-’Harîsi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא אפרש פשטם</span>.",
    "fn.2020": "By the Hebrew words <span dir=\"rtl\">מגלה םוד</span>, the author alludes to a passage in Proverbs, XI, 13.",
    "fn.2021": "The author has in view a passage from the <i>Beréschîth Rabbâ</i>, sect. 8, at its commencement (fol. 6, col. 2), where it is stated that Adam was created simultaneously man and woman, and that he possessed two faces (<span dir=\"rtl\">דיו פרצופין</span>), turned in two directions; cf. Babylonian Talmud, <i>’Eroubin</i>, fol. 18 <i>a</i>. This passage calls to mind the fable of the <i>Androgyne</i>, in Plato's <i>Symposium</i> (p. 189).",
    "fn.2022": "Ibn-Tibbon renders <span dir=\"rtl\">והובא אליו</span>; Al-’Harîzi renders more accurately <span dir=\"rtl\">והקביל אותו בה</span>: the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וקובל בה</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(وقُوبِلَ بِه)</span> signify literally: <i>and it</i> (this half) <i>was placed opposite to him</i>. Ibn-Falaquéra has already observed that the author alludes to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">עזר כנגדו</span>, <i>an help meet for him</i> (Genesis, 2, 18, 20). Vide <i>Moré ha-Morè</i>, Appendix, p. 155.",
    "fn.2023": "The translation of Ibn-Tibbon, אהד מחלקיו (one of its parts), is inaccurate; see Ibn-Falaquéra, l. c.",
    "fn.2024": "The author intends to convey: It is manifest that this narrative contains a certain philosophical idea; he contents himself with indicating it, but deems it not fitting to expound it clearly. According to the commentators, the author perceived in this narrative an allusion to the union of matter and form, which, in one's thought, are two distinct entities, but which, in reality, are ever conjoined, and which the creative word called forth from naught at the same instant. The author appears to allude to the same idea in chapter VI of the I<sup>st</sup> part, stating that the word <small>ISCHA</small> (woman) has been employed metaphorically for any entity destined and prepared to join itself to another, which signifies the <i>prime matter</i> destined to receive the <i>form</i>, or the <i>male</i> (<small>ISCH</small>), and which one separats in one's thought.",
    "fn.2025": "See <i>Pirkè rabbi-’Eliézer</i>, chapter XIII.",
    "fn.2026": "See <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, section 56 (fol. 49, col. 4).",
    "fn.2027": "Here again, the author does not pronounce upon the philosophical idea which he believs to be hidden beneath this narrative and indicated by the names therein employed. According to the commentators, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">נָחָש</span>, <i>serpent</i>, would signify the imaginative faculty and be in relation to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">נַחַש</span> which denots <i>divination</i>, in which imagination plays a considerable part; the name Sammaël would derive from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">סמא</span>, <i>to blind</i>, and would signify the appetitive faculty, or concupiscence, which blinds man; lastly, God who laughs at the camel (serpent) and its rider, represents intelligence.",
    "fn.2028": "One must recall that God spake unto the serpent: <i>And I will put enmity between thee and the woman</i>, <i>between thy seed and her seed</i> (Genesis, 3, 15). — Even as one has just stated, the serpent represents the imaginative faculty; the author intends here to indicate, it would seem, this allegorical sense: That imagination does not directly affect the intellect, represented by Adam, and that it disturbs the latter only through an intermediary, which is either matter or the sensible faculty, represented by Eve.",
    "fn.2029": "That is, by striking it on the head.",
    "fn.2030": "The commentators afford but unsatisfactory explanations concerning the allegory which the author may have had in view in this latter passage; the most plausible appears to one to be that of Schem-Tob, who expresses himself nearly thus: The seed of the woman, or the human being, by its rational and speculative faculty, or by its intelligence, does prevail over imagination, which has its seat in <i>the head</i> and destroys its phantoms. But oft-times the imaginative faculty and the passions arising therefrom strike man <i>on the heel</i>, that is to say, they hinder him from progressing, from developing his intellectual faculties, and from attaining to the conception of <i>intelligible</i> matters.",
    "fn.2031": "More literally: <i>Among the wondrous passages, etc., is that which they say</i>. All manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">והו קולהם</span>, and similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא אמרם</span>; these words being the <i>consequent</i> of the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ומן אלאקאויל</span>, it would have been more correct to write <span dir=\"rtl\">הו</span>, without the conjunctive <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>. It is doubtless the author himself who wrote <span dir=\"rtl\">והו</span>, by inadvertence. — As for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פהמת</span>, Ibn-Tibbon regarded it as a passive verb <span dir=\"rtl\">(فُهِمَتْ)</span>, whose subject is <span dir=\"rtl\">פצול</span>, and translated it as <span dir=\"rtl\">וכשיובנו</span>; but the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תעגׄב</span> which follows shows with evidence that one must pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">فَهِمْتَ</span>, an active verb, of which <span dir=\"rtl\">פצול</span> is the direct object.",
    "fn.2032": "See Talmud of Babylon, <i>Schabbâth</i>, fol. 146 <i>a; Yebamôth</i>, fol. 103 <i>b</i>. The meaning of the passage, according to Maimonides, appears to be as follows: The imaginative faculty, by arousing passions, stains man with impurity; the Israelites, by receiving a moral law that subdued their passions, purified themselves of this impurity, from which pagans remained forever stained.",
    "fn.2033": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תדבר</span> should be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">تَدَبُّر</span>, the imperative of the V<sup>e</sup> form, and it was a mistake for Ibn-Tibbon to have translated it as <span dir=\"rtl\">והנהיג</span> (manuscripts <span dir=\"rtl\">והנהיגו</span>). Al-’Harîzi rendered it more accurately as <span dir=\"rtl\">והעיר מחשבתך לזה</span>.",
    "fn.2034": "See <i>Beréschîth rabbà</i>, sect. 15 (fol. 13, col. 2); Talmud of Jerusalem, <i>Berakhôth</i>, chap. I, and the commentary <i>Yephé march</i>, <i>ibid.</i>, § 4.",
    "fn.2035": "Most Arabic manuscripts of the <i>Guide</i> and the version by Al-’Harîzi have <span dir=\"rtl\">כוורתו</span>; but our editions of the <i>Midrash</i> and of the Jerusalem Talmud have <span dir=\"rtl\">קורתו</span>, and this reading is also found in an ancient manuscript of the <i>Bereshith Rabbah</i> which the Imperial Library possesses. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">קורה</span>, which signifies <i>beam</i>, is here employed in the sense of <i>tree trunk</i>.",
    "fn.2036": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ההשאלה</span>, which the Ibn-Tibbon version has here, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">ההשלמה</span> as the manuscripts of this version and that of Al-’Harîzi do.",
    "fn.2037": "Here again, one can but dimly perceive the allegorical sense which the author discovered in the words of the doctors. What is most probable is that he saw in the <i>tree of thy life</i> the science which is the true life of the human soul. This science embraces an extent of five hundred years' journey, that is to say, it extends over all that exists beneath the sphere of the moon; for, according to the Rabbins, there are five hundred years' journey from the earth to heaven or to the last of the celestial spheres: <span dir=\"rtl\">מן הארץ עד לרקיע מהלך חמש מאות שנה</span> (Babylonian Talmud, ’<i>Haghigah</i>, fol. 13 <i>b</i>; cf. the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. XIV). These sublunar things, which alone are completely accessible to human science, are designated by the <i>trunk</i> of the tree; its branches, which extend far beyond the sphere of the moon, represent the science of the celestial spheres and metaphysics, of which man can acquire but a more or less imperfect knowledge. Cf. the commentaries of Ephodi and Shem-Tob.",
    "fn.2038": "See Voyage of <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, section 15, at the end, where, after reporting the opinions of several doctors on the species to which the <i>tree of knowledge</i> belonged, the opinion of R. Josua ben-Levi is cited, who said that the tree of knowledge should never be designated with precision, so that no man might know the fruit that had led to sin.",
    "fn.2039": "To comprehend this passage, one must recall the distinction which the author established, in chapter II of the I<sup>st</sup> part, between the knowledge of truth and falsehood and that of good and evil. The intellect, by which man knows truth and falsehood, was given to man from the moment of creation, and it is this which rendered him similar to God; but the knowledge of that which is beautiful or ugly, good or evil, is but a consequence of man's sin and the loss of his state of innocence. According to the author, therefore, the passage from the <i>Midrash</i> which has just been cited signifies that it is not God who directly reveals to man, by giving him intellect, the knowledge of that which is beautiful or ugly, becoming or unseemly, and that the objects of this knowledge would not exist for him, had he not sinned and were he not led astray by his desires and evil inclinations.",
    "fn.2040": "See <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, section 16 (fol. 14, col. 1).",
    "fn.2041": "Ibn-Tibbon (ms.) did not say this is the language <span dir=\"rtl\">, which is a misinterpretation; for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">al-nats </span> here refers to the biblical verse. Al-’Harîzi says more accurately <span dir=\"rtl\">this is not the verse to be elevated and so on </span>.",
    "fn.2042": "That is, in a moral situation that elevated him above all beings on earth, and it is this situation that is allusively designated by the words <i>garden of Eden</i>.",
    "fn.2043": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אמהל</span> is in the passive <span dir=\"rtl\">أَمْهِلْ</span>. Ibn-Tibbon's translation, <span dir=\"rtl\">שהאריך</span> (for <span dir=\"rtl\">שהאריך אף</span>), is not entirely accurate; some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">שנמחל</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואם האריכו חיי המתגבר והרוצח</span>.",
    "fn.2044": "The author confines himself to inviting the disciple's meditation upon the symbolic meaning of the names Cain, Abel, and Seth, and upon the allegories contained within the biblical narrative. The author's silence concerning his veritable thought has given rise to diverse explanations; commentators generally concur in viewing the three sons of Adam as symbols of distinct faculties of the rational soul. Cain represents the faculty of <i>practical arts</i>, necessary for the body's preservation, and of which agriculture is one of the principal; the name <i>Cain</i>, which the biblical text relates to the verb <i>kanâ</i> (to acquire), signifies <i>acquisition</i>, <i>possession</i>. Abel represents <i>reflection</i>, which judges the expediency of actions from a moral perspective, and which determines the regimen of the individual and of societies, as represented by the image of the shepherd. The name <i>Abel</i>, which signifies <i>vanity</i>, indicates that the faculty of reflection, albeit superior to that of the practical arts, is a thing vain and perishable; for that which remains of man after death is the sole <i>intellect</i>, represented by <i>Seth</i>, who alone, amongst Adam's sons, resembled his father created in the image of God, as the author has observed in the I<sup>st</sup> part of this work, chap. VII. If Cain slays Abel <i>in the field</i>, this signifies that man who lives outside of social customs and laws possesses not exact notions of that which is just or unjust, and gives himself over to violence. Consult the commentaries of Moses of Narbonne and Ephôdi; and cf. Maimonides' passage on the rational part of the soul, cited in vol. I of this work, p. 210, note 1. Consult also, regarding our passage, the commentary of Isaac Abravanel on the Pentateuch, Genesis, chap. 4, verses 1-8.",
    "fn.2045": "That is to say, that words are a thing of pure convention, and that they have not taken their origin in the nature itself of the things that they serve to designate. This is what results, according to the author, from the passage in question, where one attributes the denomination of all beings to the sole <i>will</i> of Adam, who invents names at random [[t:2]].",
    "fn.2046": "The author appears to allude to a passage in the <i>Midrash</i>, where it is stated that Adam's wisdom, that is to say, his knowledge of the things of nature, was superior to that of the angels; for these latter were ignorant of the nature of sublunar things and knew not how to call them by their names, whereas Adam knew how to give to each animal the name naturally befitting it and characterising its being: <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר לזה נאה לקרותו שור ולזה ארי ולזה סוס ולזה חמור ולזה גמל ולזה נשר וכן כלם שנאמר ויקרא האדם שמות לכל הבהמה וגו׳</span>. Consult the <i>Midrash Tanhuma</i>, book of Numbers, section <span dir=\"rtl\">הקת</span> (Verona edition, fol. 77, col. 1); <i>Bereshith Rabba</i>, sect. 17 (fol. 14, col. 3). The words <span dir=\"rtl\">לזה נאה לקרותו וכו׳</span>, <i>this one it is fitting to call</i>, <i>etc.</i>, are explained by Isaac Abravanel in these terms: <span dir=\"rtl\">לפי שלא היו אותם השמות מוסכמים כפי הרצון כלבד אבל מונחים ונגזרים באופן נאות לטבעיהם ולצורותיהם</span> \"Because these names were not merely conventional and arbitrary, but rather established and derived in such a manner as to correspond to the nature of the objects and their forms.\" Consult Abravanel's dissertation on the wisdom of Solomon, chap. 2, in his commentary on the book of Kings, chap. <small>III</small>, v. 6 et seq. (Commentarius in prophetas priores, Lipsiæ, 1686, fol. 210, col. 3). This opinion of the ancient rabbis, which is here contended against by Maimonides, is likewise adopted by R. Judah ha-Levi, in his <i>Kuzari</i>, book IV, § 25 (Buxtorf's edition, p. 303).",
    "fn.2047": "By <i>heaven</i>, we must understand here the entirety of the universe.",
    "fn.2048": "See Vol. I, p. 3, note 2.",
    "fn.2049": "The author means that this verb is never used when it comes to the creation of the sky and the earth, or the entire universe; because, when speaking of the earth alone, one also says <span dir=\"rtl\">יוצר ארץ</span> (Isaiah, <small>XLV</small>, 18), which the author probably interpreted as <span dir=\"rtl\">יוצר הרים</span>.",
    "fn.2050": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">his creations</span>, <i>to their creatures</i>, which is obviously a mistake; the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">their forms</span>; but one must read, according to the Arabic, <span dir=\"rtl\">their forms</span>, <i>to their forms</i>, where the suffix refers to heaven and earth.",
    "fn.2051": "All manuscripts possess <span dir=\"rtl\">ולמא</span> with <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>, and this word consequently commencs a new sentence. In several manuscripts of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">והאדון</span> is followed by <span dir=\"rtl\">כאשר</span>, which is wanting in the editions; but one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">וכאשר</span>. That which occasioned the error is that it was not perceived that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">והאדון</span> forms a second biblical citation.",
    "fn.2052": "Literally: <i>And this inclines towards the belief in the eternity of a certain matter</i>. The author intends to convey: as there exists a correlation between the master and the possession, and as the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קונה</span>, <i>possessor</i>, when applied to the creator, appears to imply the pre-existence of matter, one has generally employed, to designate the act of creation, the verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">ברא</span> (to create) and <span dir=\"rtl\">עשה</span> (to make), which comprise the idea of <i>producing from naught</i>.",
    "fn.2053": "Cf. the t. I, chap. II, p. 37, and above, chap. VI, p. 66.",
    "fn.2054": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">life</span> , cf. vol. I, p. 52, note 2.",
    "fn.2055": "That is to say, of that of the heaven and of the universe. The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">וחטׄהא</span> is rendered, as are the preceding pronouns, in the feminine singular, and not in the dual, inasmuch as the two things were confounded in the author's thought, so that he has had regard solely to the feminine word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלסמא</span>, <i>the heaven</i>, as one perceivs at the end of this sentence, where it is stated word for word: <i>For it is He who is God and not she</i>, <i>that is to say</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">אלסמא</span> (the heaven).",
    "fn.2056": "Literally: <i>These measures</i>, or <i>these quantities here</i>.",
    "fn.2057": "That is to say: they suffice for the intelligent and educated reader that the author had in view.",
    "fn.2058": "That is to say: By all that has been previously stated concerning the importance of the dogma of creation, one must already have apprehended, by one’s own understanding, why the law of the Sabbath has been so frequently reiterated in the Pentateuch, and why he who transgresses it is punished with the most grievous penalty, that of stoning, a punishment which was, in sooth, once applied by the legislator himself, as reported in the Book of Numbers, chapter xv, vv. 32-36. — This chapter concerning the Sabbath is connected, as a natural complement, with all that the author has set forth regarding <i>Creation</i>, which is symbolically represented by the solemn repose of the seventh day of the week.",
    "fn.2059": "The author intends to convey that, in the Decalogue, the law of the Sabbath, which constitutes the fourth commandment, occupies the third rank after those commandments pertaining to the existence and unity of God; for the third commandment, or the prohibition against <i>uttering the name of the Eternal in vain</i>, is connected to the first two commandments and does not proclaim a new fundamental principle, whereas the law of the Sabbath does proclaim the dogma of Creation. Thus it is that the author has stated above (chap. XIII, p. 107), when speaking of <i>Creation</i>, that it is the <i>second principle</i> after that of the unity of God.",
    "fn.2060": "The author seems to be alluding to conversations he had with his disciple on the motives that can be supposed for certain precepts related to religious practices. See what he says on this matter in the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, chap. XXVII and <i>passim</i>.",
    "fn.2061": "Literally: <i>When all men would remain idle</i>.",
    "fn.2062": "It is curious that the Arabic text and the Hebrew version of Ibn-Tibbon both have here, in almost all mss. as in the editions, <span dir=\"rtl\">במצרים</span>, while the biblical text has <span dir=\"rtl\">בארץ מצרים</span>. The author probably made a memory error, thinking of two other verses from Deuteronomy (XVI, 12; XXIV, 18).",
    "fn.2063": "That is to say: The two causes indicated in the two Decalogues are justified by the consequences that will be discussed.",
    "fn.2064": "Literally: <i>a general benefit for the confirmation of speculative opinion and for the well-being of the corporeal state</i>. Most manuscripts bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">פי צחיח אלראי אלנטׄרי</span>. And it is likewise this reading which Ibn-Tibbon appears to express, who has: <span dir=\"rtl\">בדעת האמתי העיוני</span>; but the construction obliges one to consider the word <span dir=\"rtl\">צחיח</span> here as a noun of action, in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">תצהיח</span>. The Leyden manuscript (no. 18) does indeed bear <span dir=\"rtl\">פי תצחיח</span>, and Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">בקיום דעת העיון</span>.",
    "fn.2065": "One believes, with Isaac Abravanel, that the author perceived a certain relation between the three opinions on prophecy and the three opinions which he has reported above (chap. XIII) concerning the origin of the world; for it could not be supposed that he intended merely to speak of the <i>triplicity</i> of opinions in both subjects, which is but a wholly accidental matter and would not have merited the author’s insistence. According to Abravanel, the first opinion on prophecy, that of the common multitude of believers, is analogous to the first opinion on the origin of the world, namely, that which holds everything to emanate from the sole will of God, without admitting any species of primary matter or of pre-existent <i>substratum</i> capable of receiving form. The second opinion on prophecy corresponds to the third on the origin of the world, or to that of the Peripatetics. These latter, admitting naught of the supernatural, discern in prophecy but the development and <i>entelechy</i> of a faculty which the entire human species possesses in potency; just as, according to them, there exists in the universe a pre-existent matter which, from all eternity, has received its form. Finally, the third opinion, which attributes prophecy to a pre-existent faculty developing by divine will, is analogous to that professed by Plato concerning the origin of the world, and according to which the world, having emerged from eternal chaos, possessed a temporal beginning and was <i>ordered</i> by the free will of God. Vide the commentary of Isaac Abravanel on various parts of the <i>Moré Néboukhim</i>, published by Mr. J. Landau, II<sup>nd</sup> fascicle (Prague, 1832, in-4°), fol. 20; cf. Isaac Arama, ’<i>Akédâ</i>, chap. 35.",
    "fn.2066": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version add the word <span dir=\"rtl\">in prophecy</span>. According to this reading, one should translate: <i>The opinions that those who believe in God profess about prophecy</i>; but all the mss. of the Arabic text have only <span dir=\"rtl\">I saw the creation of the Lord</span>, and similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">the opinions of those who believe in the Creator</span>.",
    "fn.2067": "Ibn-Tibbon has: <span dir=\"rtl\">המון הפתאים</span>, and Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">המון הסכלים</span>, that is to say, <i>the multitude of the ignorant;</i> but, had the author intended to speak of the <i>ignorants</i> in general, he would have said <span dir=\"rtl\">الجهال</span>. By the word <span dir=\"rtl\">الجاهلية</span>, the author designates, in accordance with the usage of Arabic writers, the state of peoples who have not been instructed by divine revelation, or the era of paganism.",
    "fn.2068": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon reads <span dir=\"rtl\">וקצת עמי אנשי תורתנו</span> (in some mss., <span dir=\"rtl\">וקצת המון מאנשי תורתנו</span>); he has read: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובעץׄ עואם אהל שריעתנא</span>, as indeed the ms. of Leiden, no. 18 does.",
    "fn.2069": "The author intends to refer to the Arabic Peripatetics, who consider the gift of prophecy as the highest degree of development of the rational and moral faculties of the soul, a degree to which man attains less by study than by the purification of the soul, detaching oneself completely from the things of this world and thus preparing oneself for the most intimate union with the active intellect, which actuates all the faculties that our soul possesses in potency. Vide Ibn-Sînâ, in the analysis of Schahrestâni, pp. 428-429 (Germ. transl., vol. II, pp. 331-332), and cf. one’s <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, pp. 364-365.",
    "fn.2070": "Cf. the first part, chapter XXXIV, second cause (t. I, p. 119).",
    "fn.2071": "Literally: …, <i>as it is with every perfection whose existence is</i> <small>POSSIBLE</small> <i>in a certain species; for the existence of this perfection could not extend to its extreme and end in each etc</i>.",
    "fn.2072": "For, as the author says elsewhere, what is <i>possible</i> for the species cannot not necessarily occur. See above, p. 39, and <i>ibid.</i> note 2.",
    "fn.2073": "More literally: <i>And if this perfection is a thing that needs, in order to be realized, something that would make it go out</i> (or <i>put it into action</i>), <i>there must be something that would make it go out</i>.",
    "fn.2074": "The opinion which the author is about to set forth, and which he considers as the orthodox opinion, is far from being that of the principal Jewish theologians, who believe generally that the lofty faculties possessed by the prophets proceed from the will of God, and not from a certain innate nature, <span dir=\"rtl\">מצד הבורא ולא מצד עצמם</span>, as Saadia states (<i>Book of Beliefs and Opinions</i>, book III, chap. 4, end). Hence, the opinion of Maimonides, who attributes too great a share to the intellectual faculties of man in prophecy, and who allows divine will to intervene only as an impediment, has not failed to encounter numerous opponents. See Joseph Albo, <i>’Ikkarîm</i>, book III, chap. 8; Isaac Arama, <i>’Akedâ</i>, chap. 35 (Presburg edition, octavo, vol. II, fol. 10 <i>b</i> et seq.), and the postscript of Abravanel to his commentary on this chapter, <i>loco citato</i>, fol. 22 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.2075": "Literally: <i>and this is</i>, <i>in my opinion</i>, <i>similar to all miracles and following the same order</i>. That is to say: If a man, despite fulfilling the necessary conditions for prophecy, does not become a prophet, there is, as in all miracles, a genuine interruption of the laws of nature, which must be attributed to divine will.",
    "fn.2076": "The two Hebrew versions have <span dir=\"rtl\">בעניין אלישע</span>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ענד קצדה</span>, the two translators read <span dir=\"rtl\">ענד קצה̈</span>, a reading that is indeed found in the Leiden ms., no. 18, but is incorrect.",
    "fn.2077": "See Babylonian Talmud, <i>Shabbath</i>, fol. 92 <i>a</i>, and <i>Nedarim</i>, fol. 38 <i>a</i>, where the editions read: <span dir=\"rtl\">אין הקב״ה משרה שכינתו אלא וכו׳</span>. The author here takes the words <i>strong</i> and <i>rich</i> in the moral sense ascribed to them by Ben-Zôma: the <i>strong</i> is he who knows how to subdue his passions; the <i>rich</i>, he who is content with that which he possesses. See <i>Mishnah</i>, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Aboth</i>, chap. IV, § 1, and Maimonides' Introduction to this same treatise, entitled <i>Eight Chapters</i>, chap. VII.",
    "fn.2078": "See the Introduction of Maimonides to the I<sup>re</sup> part of the <i>Mischnâ</i>, or <i>Séder</i> Z<i>era’îm</i> (Pococke, <i>Porta Mosis</i>, p. 18 and following).",
    "fn.2079": "That is, in the <i>Mischné-Tôrâ</i>, or Talmudic Abridgment, book I, tractate <i>Yésodé ha-Tôrâ</i>, chapter VII.",
    "fn.2080": "The subject of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">וראצׄה</span> and of the two following verbs is necessarily Jeremiah, and it would have been more correct to write <span dir=\"rtl\">פראצׄה</span>; but the reading which one has adopted is that of all the mss. Ibn-Tibbon’s version renders but two of the three verbs; the editions have <span dir=\"rtl\">והכינו ולמדו</span>, and the mss. <span dir=\"rtl\">ולמדו והכינו</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">והרגילו ולמדו והכין נפשו</span>.",
    "fn.2081": "According to the rabbinical commentaries, the word <i>greatnesses</i>, in the words of Jeremiah, denotes the <i>prophecy</i> which Baruch coveted and for which he had prepared himself. It would therefore result from this passage that, by divine will, the gift of prophecy may be denied even to those who are worthy of it by their natural qualities and who have worthily prepared themselves for it. Nevertheless, the author adds, it might be permissible to discern in Jeremiah’s words an express declaration that Baruch was not sufficiently prepared, and that it was on his part too great an ambition to desire to be a prophet, so that the passage from Jeremiah could not serve as proof for the thesis which has just been advanced. — The commentators express astonishment that the author should thus refute himself the sole biblical proof he has adduced in favour of his thesis, and they perceive therein an implicit admission that he preferred to embrace the opinion of the philosophers without any reservation. But the author states expressly that those who might wish to interpret the passage from the book of Jeremiah and that from Lamentations (II, 9) in the sense that natural aptitude and preparation alone suffice to form the prophet, would discover many other passages, both in Holy Writ and in the writings of the doctors, which would prove that, by divine will, the gift of prophecy may be denied even to him who is perfectly prepared for it. Abravanel cites the example of the seventy elders, <i>who prophesied when the spirit rested upon them</i>, <i>but who did not continue</i> (Numbers, 11, 35), which proves that they ceased to prophesy as soon as the divine will impeded them: similarly, the prophets Hosea (XII, 11) and Amos (3, 8) evidently make prophecy dependent upon the word of God or His will. See Abravanel, <i>loco citato</i>, fol. 20 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.2082": "That is to say, because the afflictions of exile did not afford them the leisure requisite for preparation; for it is necessary that the prophet should possess a tranquil mind, free from all preoccupation. See Babylonian Talmud, <i>Shabbath</i>, fol. 30 <i>b:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">אין השכינה שורה לא מתוך עצבות ולא מתוך עצלות</span>; cf. Maimonides, <i>Yesodé ha-Tôrâ</i>, chap. VII, § 4, and infra, chap. XXXVI (p. 287).",
    "fn.2083": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ינבי</span> is evidently active <span dir=\"rtl\">يَنْبِي</span> with the unspoken word <span dir=\"rtl\">אללה</span> as its subject and <span dir=\"rtl\">אברהם</span> as its object, and it is erroneously that the two Hebrew translators have rendered it as a neuter or passive verb, one by <span dir=\"rtl\">שינׇּבא</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">הנבא</span>, the other by <span dir=\"rtl\">שיתנבא</span>; one has therefore written <span dir=\"rtl\">חמארא</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄפדעא</span> in the accusative, although the mss. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">חמאר</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄפדע</span>, without <span dir=\"rtl\">א</span>.",
    "fn.2084": "That is to say: the divine power grants the gift of prophecy only when it has become possible through a good preparation. All the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">קדרה̈ אלאלאה</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon rendered as <span dir=\"rtl\">גזרת השם ית׳</span>, taking <span dir=\"rtl\">קדרה̈</span> in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">קדר</span>; Al-’Harîzi has: <span dir=\"rtl\">כה האלוהי</span>.",
    "fn.2085": "The author means that one should not conclude from this passage that certain men are predestined to prophecy by the sole will of God, and without the need for intellectual or moral preparation.",
    "fn.2086": "Jeremiah says: <i>I cannot speak, for I am a youth</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">נער</span>; from which it might be inferred that, albeit an inexperienced youth, he could be entrusted with a prophetic mission, and that he required no preparatory study. To answer this objection, the author cits sundry examples which prove that the Hebrew word <small>NA’AR</small> does also sometimes designate a man of mature age. It does indeed designate the <i>servant</i>, of whatsoever age, as the Greek word <i>πϰῖς</i> and the Latin word <i>puer</i> boy; but this sense cannot apply to the passage in Jeremiah, and the author’s explanation is evidently forced.",
    "fn.2087": "The author appears to be alluding to the words of the chief cupbearer of Pharaoh (Genesis, 41, 12), who designates Joseph as <small>NA’AR</small>, or <i>young man</i>, although he was then nearly thirty years old (cf. <i>ibid.</i>, v. 46).",
    "fn.2088": "All mss. read: <span dir=\"rtl\">אחד ותׄמאנין</span>; we have written more correctly <span dir=\"rtl\">אחדי</span> in the feminine, because one must understand <span dir=\"rtl\">סנה̈</span>. Further on, the mss. read incorrectly <span dir=\"rtl\">ארבעה̈ עשר</span>, for <span dir=\"rtl\">ארבע עשרה̈</span>; similarly <span dir=\"rtl\">מאיה̈ ועשרה̈</span> for <span dir=\"rtl\">מאיה̈ ועשר</span>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">סבעה̈</span> for <span dir=\"rtl\">סבע</span>.",
    "fn.2089": "The editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have <span dir=\"rtl\">כל מגיד בעולם</span>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בעולם</span>, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">בנעלם</span> as the mss. have it. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">כל מודיע העתידות</span>.",
    "fn.2090": "It appears evident to one that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">רויה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(رؤية)</span> must here be taken in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">رؤيا</span>, <i>dream</i>, and it is erroneously that Ibn-Tibbon has rendered this word by <span dir=\"rtl\">מחשבה</span>, <i>thought.</i> The <i>true dream</i> is that which Aristotle calls <i>εὐθυονειρία</i>. Vide one's <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 95, note 1.",
    "fn.2091": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מחיצה</span>, which properly signifies <i>partition, separating wall, compartment</i>, is here employed figuratively to designate the various degrees of perception. Vide <i>Mekhiltha</i> or Rabb. Comment. upon Exodus, at chap. 19, verse 24 (Venice edit., fol. 25, col. 1), and the commentary of <i>Raschi</i> on the same passage of Exodus; cf. Abravanel, commentary on Exodus, chap. 19, 13<sup>th</sup> question.",
    "fn.2092": "That is to say: The people heard not distinctly, and in their totality, all the divine words which reached the ear of Moses. — In Ibn-Tibbon’s version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄמיע</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">הו כלה</span> have not been reproduced; nevertheless, the former of these words is rendered in the mss. and in the commentaries, which have: <span dir=\"rtl\">כל המגיע למשה</span>. Certain mss. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">קול המגיע</span>, which is an error. Al-’Harîzi translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא היו כל הדברים המגיעים למשה מגיעים לכל ישראל</span>.",
    "fn.2093": "See <i>Mekhilthâ</i>, on the verse <span dir=\"rtl\">וידבר אלהים</span> (Exodus, 20, 1): <span dir=\"rtl\">מלמד שאמר המקום עשרת הדברות בדבור אחד חזר ופרשן דבור דבור בפני עצמו</span>.",
    "fn.2094": "Literally: <i>non the distinction of discourse</i>.",
    "fn.2095": "That is to say: the first two commandments, the one commencing with the words <i>I am the Lord thy God</i>, and the other with the words <i>thou shalt have no other gods</i>, all the Hebrews present before Mount Sinai heard them pronounced by God Himself. Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Maccoth</i>, fol. 24 <i>a; Midrash of the Canticle</i>, or <i>Shir ha-Shirim Rabbah</i>, fol. 3, col. 2. — According to this passage, therefore, the first two commandments would constitute an exception to the principle which the author has just laid down, inasmuch as the entire people heard directly every word from the voice of God, without needing the mediation of Moses.",
    "fn.2096": "That is to say: these are perfectly demonstrable philosophical principles, and for which one does not need a prophetic revelation.",
    "fn.2097": "This is, according to the author, the meaning of this passage from Deuteronomy: you have been shown the way, <i>to know</i>, that is to <i>recognize</i>, by the sole path of intelligence and science, that God exists and that there is no other God outside of Him.",
    "fn.2098": "The other eight commandments concern matters which fall not within the domain of the intellect, and which could not be the object of a <i>demonstrative syllogism;</i> they pertain to virtues and vices, good and evil, which are within the sphere of <i>probable opinions</i>, or else they are matters purely <i>traditional</i>, as, for example, the fourth commandment concerning the Sabbath. Upon the sense of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמשהוראת</span>, vide vol. I, p. 39, note 1.",
    "fn.2099": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">استقل</span> has here the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">أجمل</span> , <i>support, comport, </i>admit, permit; the author means that this is the opinion that, according to the texts, is admissible.",
    "fn.2100": "That is to say: a single prolonged sound, without any interval. Vide <i>Mekhiltha, l. c.</i> (cf. <i>Yalkut</i>, vol. I, no. 285): <span dir=\"rtl\">אח כל הדברים מלמד שאמר המקום עשרת הדברות בדבור אחד מה שאי אפשר לבשר ודם לומר כן</span>. Cf. <i>Midrash Tanhuma</i>, section <span dir=\"rtl\">יתרו</span> (fol. 51, col. 3): <span dir=\"rtl\">אמרו ז״ל שעשרת הדברות כלן בקול אחד יצאו מפי הגבורה וכו׳</span>. One apprehends that in the Arabic text (<span dir=\"rtl\">קול ואחד</span>), <span dir=\"rtl\">קול</span> must be considered as a Hebrew word signifying <i>voice, sound</i>. It is likewise in this sense that it has been taken by Ibn-Tibbon, who has: <span dir=\"rtl\">קול אחד</span>, whilst Al-’Harîzi translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">דבור אחד</span>. Immediately thereafter, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">והו אלקול</span> are rendered in Ibn-Tibbon’s version by <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא המאמר</span>, and in that of Al-’Harîzi by <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא הדבור</span>. However, in Ephodi’s commentary, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">וזהו הקול</span>.",
    "fn.2101": "The author therefore deems admissible the opinion of certain doctors, who maintain that all the Israelites heard the first two commandments proclaimed by the divine voice itself. But, according to the principle which one has laid down at the commencement of the chapter, one conceives that Moses alone heard them distinctly, whilst for the people it was but a confused sound of voice; which signifies that, even of these purely intelligible and perfectly demonstrable matters, Moses possessed a clearer and more profound understanding than all the other Israelites. If one explains in this sense that which has been stated concerning the first two commandments, it is necessary to suppose that, as regards the other eight commandments, the people no longer heard even the confused sound which they had at first perceived, and that these commandments were simply reported to them by Moses, in the name of God, like all the other commandments of the divine law. But, whether one admits that the people heard the whole Decalogue directly from the divine voice, or that they heard only the first two commandments, that which the author desires to establish above all is that the people heard this divine voice but confusedly, and that the words did not distinctly strike their ear.",
    "fn.2102": "Literally: <i>The doctors have mentioned this</i>, <i>supporting it, etc.</i> The author intends to speak of that prolonged and confused sound of voice which the Israelites heard during the proclamation, whether of the entire Decalogue or of the first two commandments only. Cf. <i>Mekhîltha, l. c.</i>, <i>Yalkout</i>, vol. I, no. 285, and vol. II, no. 783: <span dir=\"rtl\">שלא כמדת הקדוש ברוך הוא מדת בשר ודם בשר ודם אינו משמיע ב׳ דברים כאחד אבל מי שאמר והיה העולם אמר עשרת הדברות בדבור אחד שנאמר אחת דבר אלהים וידבר אלהים את כל הדברים האלה לאמר.</span>",
    "fn.2103": "Here, the two Hebrew translators regarded <span dir=\"rtl\">קול</span> as an Arabic word and rendered it, one by <span dir=\"rtl\">מאמר</span>, the other by <span dir=\"rtl\">דבור</span>; but it results from the whole that the subject is consistently the <i>divine voice</i> heard by all the Israelites, which inspired them with so much terror. After the proclamation of the commandments, says the author, they no longer heard this divine voice, but only the voice of the thunder. In the passage of the <i>Midrasch</i> which the author adduces (<i>Schîr ha-Schirim rabbâ, l. c.</i>), it is stated that, according to R. Joshua, son of Levi, the Israelites heard proclaimed, <i>from the mouth of God</i>, only the first two commandments, but that, according to the other doctors, they heard the entire Decalogue; all, therefore, concur in asserting that by <i>the voices</i> in question before and after the Decalogue, one must not understand <i>the divine voice</i>, but the reverberation of trumpets and of thunder. — By <span dir=\"rtl\">מדרש חזית</span>, one designates the <i>Midrasch</i> of the Song of Songs, which commences with the citation of the verse <span dir=\"rtl\">חזית איש מהיר במלאכתו</span> (Prov. 22, 29).",
    "fn.2104": "The author is speaking here in the sense of those who say that the Israelites themselves only directly understood the two first commandments, an opinion that the author deemed admissible and which, in the end, appears to be adopted. See above, p. 270, note 4.",
    "fn.2105": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אעני תלך אלאצואת</span> have not been rendered in the versions of Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi, and are not found in the ms. of Leyde, no. 18.",
    "fn.2106": "Literally: <i>voyait.</i> Cf. vol. I, chap. XLVI, p. 161.",
    "fn.2107": "See vol. I, chapter LXV, p. 290, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.2108": "By the Hebrew words <span dir=\"rtl\">יצאה נשמתן בשמעו</span>, the author alludes to a passage from the <i>Midrasch</i> of the Song of Songs, chapter V, verse 6 (fol. 19, col. 4): <span dir=\"rtl\">נפשי יצאה בדברו מקול דבורו הראשון שאמר אנכי ה׳ אלהיך</span>. Cf. <i>Yalkout</i>, at the same verse of the Song of Songs (t. II, n° 988, fol. 179, col. 4): <span dir=\"rtl\">בדבור ראשון יצאה נשמתן של ישראל</span>.",
    "fn.2109": "Literally: <i>The whole which one has separated</i>. The author intends to convey that Onkelos, by his method of translation, has brought forth the distinction which must be established in this perception, common to Moses and to the other Israelites, by indicating that Moses perceived the divine word distinctly and directly through his intellect, which reproduced it with clarity, whereas for the other Israelites it was in some sort veiled; for these perceived it but confusedly, and it could not so vividly strike their intelligence, which was perturbed by the senses. — According to R. Moses ben-Na’hman (Commentary on the Pentateuch, Exodus, 20, 16), Maimonides’ observation concerning Onkelos would be but faintly founded; one cites in this regard several passages from Onkelos’ version, where the communication of the divine word to the Israelites is expressed by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מלל י״י</span> (Cf. Exodus, 20, 19; Deuteronomy, V, 4, 19 and 21), and another passage where the communication made to Moses himself is expressed by the periphrasis <span dir=\"rtl\">מן קדם י״י</span> (Cf. Exodus, 19, 19). — Abravanel, in justification of Maimonides, observes that, in the first four passages, the Hebrew text itself sufficiently indicates that the word was communicated by an intermediary designated by the words <i>heaven</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מן השמים</span>) and <i>fire</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מתוך האש</span>), and that, consequently, Onkelos was not obliged to employ a periphrasis; as for the last passage, it speaks of the divine voice heard simultaneously by Moses and by the Hebrew people, and it is for this reason that Onkelos deemed it necessary to employ the periphrasis <span dir=\"rtl\">מן קדם י״י</span> here. Cf. Abravanel’s Commentary on our chapter, fol. 26 <i>b</i> and 27 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.2110": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate Meghîllâ, fol. 3 a.",
    "fn.2111": "Ibn-Tibbon's version reads <span dir=\"rtl\">החכמים שבישראל</span>, <i>the wisest in Israel</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">החכמים</span> should be considered a superlative, by which the translator aimed to render the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">באטלאק</span>, <i>in the absolute sense</i> or <i>par excellence</i>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר הס חכמי ישראל במלה מוחלטת</span>.",
    "fn.2112": "More literally: <i>in a measure greater than what they</i> (<i>the doctors</i>) <i>said</i>.",
    "fn.2113": "Literally: <i>the truth of this perception and what was its state, that is what is very occult for us</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄפי</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(خَفِي)</span> should be considered as a neuter adjective: <i>something occult</i>.",
    "fn.2114": "The author intends to convey, not that the <i>messenger</i>, or the angel, of whom there is question in the passage from Exodus, is himself the <i>prophét</i>e of whom Deuteronomy speaks, but that it designates the <i>active intellect</i> which inspires the prophet. It is evidently a consequence of what the author has stated above (ch. VII, p. 76) that, in the passage from Exodus, one must understand by <i>angel</i> that one of the <i>separated</i> intelligences, or of the superior forces, through which God communicates with the prophet.",
    "fn.2115": "That is to say: this proves that it is a matter of the superior power in relation to the prophet, and not of an angel who would have marched at the head of the people and who would have revealed himself to him.",
    "fn.2116": "Cf. the first <sup>part</sup> of this work, chap. LXIV (vol. I, p. 286).",
    "fn.2117": "Literally, <i>to inform them that etc</i>. The conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">أنَ‏</span> , <i>that</i>, serves here to introduce direct discourse. Cf. vol. I, p. 283, note 4. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אעלאמא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(أَعْلَامًا)</span> , the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אעלאם</span>; this infinitive, I think, should be taken adverbially and put in the accusative.",
    "fn.2118": "In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">מעמד</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">במעמד</span>.",
    "fn.2119": "Voy. Exode, chap. 40, verset 38; Nombres, chap. 9, versets 15 et 16.",
    "fn.2120": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">محمد</span> signifies <i>to extend</i>, <i>to deploy</i>, <i>to prepare</i>. The sense is: He shall render unto you accessible the land which ye must conquer. The editions of the Ibn-Tibbon version have <span dir=\"rtl\">וישקיט</span>, <i>he shall pacify;</i> in some MSS. one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">ויזמן</span>, <i>he shall prepare</i>. Al-’Harîzi has: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויושיבכם על הארץ</span>.",
    "fn.2121": "These words pertain yet to that which would be needful for the taking possession of the conquered land, whilst the ensuing words relate generally unto the rules of conduct which they were bound to observe thereafter. The conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">ف</span>, in <span dir=\"rtl\">פיעלמכם</span>, does indicate that this verb denots a new action, a consequence or sequel of the preceding. In the Ibn-Tibbon version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וילמדכם מה שצריך לעשותו</span> may appear an unneeded repetition of <span dir=\"rtl\">ויודיעכם מה שתעשוהו</span>.",
    "fn.2122": "That is to say: by the elucidation of the passage <i>Behold, I send an angel before thee</i>, applied unto the prophets who should come after Moses, one has made known the principle which the author has expounded elsewhere and of which the ensuing chapter treats, namely, that the other prophets, both before and after Moses, received divine inspiration by an angel, that is, by some <i>intermediate</i> agency, such as the active intellect, or merely the imagination. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אעטי</span> ought to be considered, one believs, as a passive preterite <span dir=\"rtl\">(أَعْطَىٰ)</span>; as it does precede the subject <span dir=\"rtl\">אלואעדה̈</span>, it may have been rendered in the masculine, albeit this subject be feminine; likewise, according to the Ibn-Tibbon version, the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">נתן</span> must be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">נתּן</span>, which is indicated in some MSS. by the <i>full</i> writing: <span dir=\"rtl\">ניתן</span>. In several of our Arabic MSS. one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">אעטא</span>, which ought to be considered as a <i>noun of action</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">إِعْطَاء</span>, so that it were needful to translate into Hebrew: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובזה גם כן נתינת היסוד</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">איצׄא</span>, <i>also</i>, is not found in all MSS. and has not been rendered in the two Hebrew verses.",
    "fn.2123": "That is to say, in a popular language, accessible to everyone, like that of the Talmudic works of our author.",
    "fn.2124": "Vide the Commentary on the <i>Mishnah</i>, Introduction to the X<sup>th</sup> (XI<sup>th</sup>) chapter of the treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, seventh article of faith (Pococke, <i>Porta Mosis</i>, p. 169-173); <i>Mishneh Torah</i> or Compendium of the Talmud, treatise <i>Yesodei ha-Torah</i>, chap. VII, § 6. The author therein signals the following differences: 1° God spake unto all the prophets generally only through an <i>intermediary</i>, whereas He spake unto Moses without any intermediary. 2° All the prophets received their divine revelations only in dreams or nocturnal visions, or in a state of slumber, in which the senses ceased to function; but Moses received his inspirations in the waking state and in full possession of all his faculties. 3° All the prophets experienced, during their vision, a convulsive trembling and an extreme disquiet; Moses was ever in a perfect calm. 4° All the prophets, though perfectly prepared for divine inspiration, were inspired only at certain epochs, by a particular grace of the divine will; Moses possessed the privilege of being able spontaneously and at any hour to invoke divine inspiration.",
    "fn.2125": "From one point of view, one may consider the name of <i>prophet</i> as a <i>common</i> noun, suiting Moses as it does all other prophets; but, from another point of view, one may consider it as a <i>homonymous</i> noun, since there is an essential and sharply defined difference between Moses and the other prophets. It is therefore what one terms an <i>ambiguous</i> or <i>amphibological</i> noun. Vide Vol. I of this work, p. 6, note 3, and p. 229.",
    "fn.2126": "For example, Noé, Sem, and Eber.",
    "fn.2127": "The author means that, in the passage he is about to cite, the matter is simply stated as a fact, and not presented as a theory.",
    "fn.2128": "That is to say: and all the more so must the perception of Moses have essentially differed from that of those who, among the pagan nations, were considered prophets, such as Bileam.",
    "fn.2129": "Ibn-Tibbon's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">how strange they are</span>, or, according to some mss., <span dir=\"rtl\">one would be astonished at them</span>, <i>and be astonished at them;</i> this translation is inaccurate, as Ibn-Falaquéra has already observed. See the Appendix to <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 155: <span dir=\"rtl\">the ancient version has יםתפהם ענהא העתיק יתמה מהם והעתקתו יבקש ידיעתם והחריזי העתיק ישאל בעדם.</span>",
    "fn.2130": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">על צד ההגדה</span> are missing, which can be found in some manuscripts.",
    "fn.2131": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">The lover and the hater who are pleased with him and who dispute with him</span>; this is a double translation of the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">Al-mu'awwil lahu and Al-mukhali' 'alayhi</span>.",
    "fn.2132": "Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi only translated one of the two synonymous verbs: <span dir=\"rtl\">בי קשר הנה שני עניינים יחד</span>.",
    "fn.2133": "The ms. of Leiden, no. 18, reads <span dir=\"rtl\">בעדה</span>, <i>after him</i>; the same Al-’Harîzi, <span dir=\"rtl\">after him</span>.",
    "fn.2134": "See I Kings, chap. 18, verses 19-39.",
    "fn.2135": "The author does not clearly express his entire thought. According to the commentators, he does indicate by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ענדהם</span>, <i>for them</i>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">הנאך</span>, <i>there</i> (that is to say, in those regions), that the miracle was purely local and that one ought not to conceive of a true retardation in the movement of the solar sphere; for such a miracle would have caused a universal catastrophe. Indeed, as the author does expressly state elsewhere (Part I<sup>st</sup>, chap. LXXII, p. 362): “Even as, when the heart ceass for but a single instant, the individual dies, and his movements and faculties cease, so likewise, were the celestial spheres to halt, it would be the death of the entire universe and the annihilation of all that is therein.” — Maimonides does therefore seem to insinuate here that, at the time of the battle of Gibeon, the day had appeared to the Israelites to prolong itself beyond its ordinary limits, on account of the great things which had been accomplished; or else that, by a miracle, God had caused an independent light to appear, distinct from that of the sun, so that it might have been said that the sun itself had stood still.",
    "fn.2136": "Literally: <i>for the singularity of this perception is like the singularity of these acts</i>.",
    "fn.2137": "Literally: <i>all this after this degree</i> (of which it has been spoken).",
    "fn.2138": "Literally: <i>the reality of prophecy and its quiddity</i>.",
    "fn.2139": "Ibn-Tibbon translated the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מאדה̈</span>, <i>matière</i>, as <span dir=\"rtl\">ליחה</span>, <i>humeur</i>; he did the same in several other passages. See, for example, chapter LXXII of the first part, Arabic text, fol. 102 <sup>a</sup>, and French translation, p. 368.",
    "fn.2140": "Ibn-Tibbon did not translate the words <span dir=\"rtl\">יגׄבר פאיתה או</span>; Al-’Harîzi’s version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">וזה הדבר לא יתכן להשיג מה שאבד ממנו או למלאות חםרונו</span>.",
    "fn.2141": "Literally: <i>of its measure</i>, that is, of its volume too large or too small.",
    "fn.2142": "Literally: <i>there is no artifice for this</i>.",
    "fn.2143": "Cf. Ibn-Sînâ, in the summary of Schahrestâni, p. 416 (all. trans., t. II, p. 314), where it is said of the imaginative faculty, that it retains the memory of what the <i>common sense</i> has received from the (five) senses, and that it preserves it when the sensible things have disappeared <span dir=\"rtl\">تحفظ ما قبله (لحسن)</span>  <span dir=\"rtl\">(the shared from the senses and it remains there after the disappearance of the sensibles</span>",
    "fn.2144": "The text reads: <i>and that his activity etc.;</i> the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">ואן</span>, <i>and that</i>, is attached to the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">עלמת</span> (you know, you understand) which is found at the beginning of the sentence.",
    "fn.2145": "See above, p. 267, note 2.",
    "fn.2146": "That is to say: the inspiration in question is of the same kind in dreams and in prophecy, and it differs only in degree of intensity, being much stronger in prophecy than in dreams. — The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יכׄתלף</span>, which is in the masculine singular, refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">פיץׄ</span>, <i>inspiration</i>, and it is erroneously that Ibn-Tibbon rendered it by the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">יתחלפו</span>.",
    "fn.2147": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate Berakhôth, fol. 57 b.",
    "fn.2148": "See <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect.17 (f.14, col.4), and sect.44 (f.39, col.3).",
    "fn.2149": "Literally: <i>before its perfection and before it has matured</i>.",
    "fn.2150": "Some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">אבתדאה</span>, others <span dir=\"rtl\">אבתדא</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">אבתדי</span>. Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר יראהו</span> (the thing <i>which it sees</i>)<i>;</i> he has therefore read, in his Arabic copy, <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי תראה</span>, with the omission of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מנהא</span>, a reading found in the Leyden manuscript, No. 18. In the sole manuscript of Al-’Harîzi’s version, this passage is wanting. One adopts the reading <span dir=\"rtl\">אבתדאה</span>, which one considers a noun of action with a suffix, pronouncing <span dir=\"rtl\">ابتدأوه</span>  (<i>its beginning</i> or <i>its origin</i>).",
    "fn.2151": "Literally: <i>that, if there were a human individual etc.</i> One has felt obliged to modify slightly, in one’s translation, the initial words of this sentence, which commences a lengthy hypothetical period enumerating all the physical and moral qualities requisite for prophetic inspiration, and of which the grammatical complement only begins with the words: <span dir=\"rtl\">פאלשכׄץ אלדׄי הדׄה צפתה</span> (fol. 79 <i>b</i>, line 10). If there were a human individual, says the author, who possessed all the qualities about to be enumerated, this individual, impelled by the action of his perfect imaginative faculty and calling forth through his speculative perfection the inspiration of the active intellect, would doubtless perceive divine, extraordinary things, etc. (hereafter, p. 286). — It was all the more necessary, for clarity, to divide this period, as the author himself had lost the thread and interrupted himself with a brief digression on a passage from Aristotle’s <i>Ethics</i>.",
    "fn.2152": "That is, so that all the faculties he possesses <i>en puissance</i> could develop and pass into reality or the <i>acte</i>.",
    "fn.2153": "One must remember that, according to Aristotle, virtue consists in avoiding extremes and in knowing how to hold in one's inclinations a reasonable middle ground, equally distant from too much and too little. See <i>Éthique à Nicomaque</i>, book II, chapters 5 and 6.",
    "fn.2154": "Word for word: <i>that his thought was idle and his desire unoccupied with regard to animal things</i>.",
    "fn.2155": "See <i>Nicomachean Ethics</i>, book III, chapter 13, where Aristotle says, speaking of the sense of touch: Κ<i>aὶ δόξειεν ἂν διϰαίως ἐπονείδιστος εἶναι, ὅτι οὐχ ᾗ ἄνθρωποί ἐσαεν ὑπάρχει, ἀλλ’ ᾗ ζῷα</i>.",
    "fn.2156": "The mss. generally have the form <i>vulgar</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">Yithnabua</span>, some of them <span dir=\"rtl\">Yithnabun</span>; we have written more correctly <span dir=\"rtl\">Yihnebaoq</span>.",
    "fn.2157": "Literally: <i>not true</i>, or <i>not real</i>; that is to say, ambitions that aim at vain things, devoid of any true value.",
    "fn.2158": "Literally: <i>only for this</i>, that is, for the sole pleasure of receiving honors and being respected and obeyed. According to Abravanel (<i>l. c.</i>, fol. 38 <i>a</i>), the author would allude to the ambitious views of the prophet Muhammad.",
    "fn.2159": "One considers this sentence a parenthetical remark, by which the author more clearly explains what he intended to indicate by the words <i>solely for that;</i> he states, therefore, that the truly wise man, far from seeking a puerile satisfaction in the vain homages of the multitude, ought to value the respect and submission that men may evince towards him only inasmuch as these men are in a position to be useful or detrimental to him.",
    "fn.2160": "It seems to me that by <i>solitary</i> the author means here the sage, who isolates himself from the society of men, in order to find himself outside the reach of their vices and not be disturbed in his meditations; it is the sage of whom Ibn-Bâdja speaks in his treatise <i>the Regime of the Solitary</i>. See my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 388 and following.",
    "fn.2161": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate Schabbath, fol. 30 b.",
    "fn.2162": "See <i>Pirké Rabbi Eli’ézer</i>, chap. XXXVIII, and cf. Maimonides, <i>Huit chapitres</i>, or Introduction to the treatise <i>Aboth</i>, chap. VII.",
    "fn.2163": "<span dir=\"rtl\">נובה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(نُوبَة)</span> signifies an <i>unfortunate mishap</i>. Ibn-Tibbon’s translation, which has <span dir=\"rtl\">תלונת</span>, <i>murmur</i>, is not exact. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אחר נבואת המרגלים</span> (after the <i>prophecy</i> of the explorers), which is a non-sense; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">נובה̈</span>, he read <span dir=\"rtl\">נבוה̈</span>, a reading indeed found in the Leyden manuscript, No. 18.",
    "fn.2164": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>Ta’anith</i>, fol. 30 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.2165": "The author, having incidentally adduced the example of Moses, which confirms, as a general thesis, the dictum of the doctors he has just cited, deemed it necessary to add that this example applies not, as does that of Jacob, to the reflection he has just made concerning the materiality of the imaginative faculty, inasmuch as the latter played no part whatsoever in the prophecy of Moses. According to Éphôdi, the author would have wished to intimate here, without venturing to state it clearly, that Moses himself required, to a certain extent, the imaginative faculty, for the prediction of the future. Abravanel (<i>l. c.</i>, fol. 35 <i>a</i>) considers this opinion of Éphôdi a veritable heresy.",
    "fn.2166": "See the passages indicated above, p. 277, note 2.",
    "fn.2167": "Literally: <i>and this could not continue;</i> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דׄלך</span>, <i>this, that thing</i>, refers to the prophecy. Al-’Harîzi translates literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">and this was not customary</span>; Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">and they did not persist in it</span>, <i>and it did not continue for them</i>.",
    "fn.2168": "Literally: <i>what dejection or</i> (<i>what</i>)<i> sorrow can there exist for a man, in any circumstance whatsoever</i> (<i>that may be</i>)<i> more grave etc.</i> — Ibn-Tibbon has rendered this passage inexactly, having mistaken the sense of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אי</span>, which he pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">أَيّ</span>, and which he rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">כלומר</span>, <i>namely</i>, whereas one must pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">أي</span>; this error led him to append a conjunctive <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span> to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אשד</span> and to translate <span dir=\"rtl\">ויותר חזק</span>. The sole Leyden manuscript, No. 18, has the reading <span dir=\"rtl\">ואשד</span>, which may well be but a supposed correction made after Ibn-Tibbon’s version. Al-’Harîzi comprehended not this passage any better: he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי הוא עצכות או עצלות אשר ימצא אדם בעניניו וכל שכן בהיותו עבד וכו׳</span>.",
    "fn.2169": "The Hebrew words <span dir=\"rtl\">and there is no power in your hand</span> (literally: <i>without it being in the power of your hand</i>) are borrowed from Deuteronomy, chap. XXVIII, verse 32. In the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">your hand</span> is a printing error; the manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">your hand</span>.",
    "fn.2170": "That is, the instrument of prophecy, which is the free imaginative force unencumbered by any concern.",
    "fn.2171": "By the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פיץׄ</span>, <i>épanchement, émanation</i>, the author here means the influence that the <i>intellect actif</i> exerts on man's faculties.",
    "fn.2172": "Literally: <i>C’est qu’il en arrive parfois quelque chose à un individu, de manière que la mesure de ce quelque chose qui lui arrive est assez pour le perfectionner, pas autre chose</i>.",
    "fn.2173": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">שלימותו</span>, which most editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have, one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">השלמתו</span>, as the manuscripts and the edition <i>princeps</i> have. Al-’Harîzi's version is clearer here; it reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">שיעור יותר ממה שיהיה שלם ויוכל להשלים בו לזולתו</span>.",
    "fn.2174": "One has followed the reading offered by most manuscripts, which bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">קדר יכון מדבר <span dir=\"rtl\">(مُدَبَّرًا)</span> בגירה</span>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">כשיעור שיהיה מנהיג בו עצמו לא זולתו</span>; likewise, Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">כפי מה שינהיג בו לנפשו לא לזולתו</span>, <i>as much as is requisite to govern oneself, and not others</i>. The two translators have read: <span dir=\"rtl\">קדר יכון מדברא בה נפםה לא גירה</span>, a reading indeed found in the two Leyden manuscripts, and which is more conformable to the author's expression above, ch. XI (pp. 95-96). According to the reading one has adopted, the sense is: <i>whereas there are other beings whose entire perfection consists in being constituted in such wise as to receive the influence and direction of the beings superior to them</i>. Herein one speaks of the inferior beings governed by the superior beings, whose forces are communicated to them. See above, ch. X and XI, and the I<sup>st</sup> part of this work, ch. LXXII (vol. 1, p. 361).",
    "fn.2175": "Literally: <i>on account of the scarcity</i> paucitatis <i>of the thing which emanates;</i> that is to say, because the <i>active intellect</i> does not emanate sufficiently. It goes without saying that this insufficiency cannot be attributed to the active intellect itself, which is always in act and always acts with the same force; but the matter upon which it acts (or the <i>hylice</i> intellect of man) may sometimes not be apt to receive the form, so that the action of the <i>active intellect</i>, which imparts the form, appears to be arrested or weakened (see above, p. 139, and vol. I, p. 311, note 4). According to Schem-Tob and Abravanel, one must understand by <i>the thing which emanates</i> the rational faculty, which spreads upon the imaginative faculty that which it has received from the <i>active intellect</i> and which, according as it is more or less strong, acts more or less upon the imaginative faculty. Abravanel, who could but read the version of Ibn-Tibbon, deems it necessary to refer the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ממנו</span> (in <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא ישפע דבר ממנו</span>) to the rational faculty; but the Arabic text, which has <span dir=\"rtl\">מנה</span>, in the masculine, does not admit of this explanation.",
    "fn.2176": "In the ms. of Leiden, no. 18, one has omitted the words <span dir=\"rtl\">as I was between and I was in</span>  <span dir=\"rtl\">the one who was in the realm of the philosophers and as this is the mixture</span> , and the two Hebrew translators have also passed them over. The author wants to speak of what, in the previous chapter, he said of the faculty of imagination and the divine emanation spreading out first on the rational faculty and then on the imaginative faculty.",
    "fn.2177": "Some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">וואצׄעוא</span>, with the <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span> copulative; according to this reading, it would be necessary to translate: <i>This is the class of statesmen, legislators, etc.</i>",
    "fn.2178": "Literally: <i>much reciprocal superiority;</i> that is to say, that the individuals belonging to each of these categories differ greatly amongst themselves by the superiority they possess one over another. The version of Ibn-Tibbon, which bears <span dir=\"rtl\">יתרון רב מאד</span>, lacks clarity; the sense is: <span dir=\"rtl\">יש יתרון רב מאד לבני אדם זה על זה</span>. At the commencement of this chapter, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ותתפאצׄל עקולנא</span> have been more accurately rendered by: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויהיה יתרון שכלינו זה על זה</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וידוע כי כל מין משלשת המינים האלה יש יתרון גדול לזה על זה</span>; according to this translation, it would be the three categories themselves that would be declared to possess a superiority one over another, which is a misconception.",
    "fn.2179": "Literally: <i>or, in quantity</i> (<i>sufficient</i>)<i> to perfect it</i> (i.e., the individual to whom it arrives), <i>nothing else, or, in such quantity that there remains</i> (to the individual), <i>after its perfection, enough to perfect others</i>.",
    "fn.2180": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">השכלי</span>, one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">הדברי</span>, as the mss. have it.",
    "fn.2181": "Ibn-Tibbon did not express the demonstrative pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">הדׄא</span>; Al-’Harîzi's version reads <span dir=\"rtl\">זה השכל</span>.",
    "fn.2182": "Literally: <i>and to extend from the one who receives this effusion to another who receives it after him</i>. The verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">הַמַּהֵר, הַפִּיץ</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">תנתהי</span> are in the feminine, having as subject <span dir=\"rtl\">טביעה̈</span>, <i>nature</i>.",
    "fn.2183": "Literally: <i>to an individual that this effusion cannot surpass</i>.",
    "fn.2184": "See above, ch. XI (p. 96), the example of the rich man. Ibn-Tibbon's version simply reads <span dir=\"rtl\">as we have explained </span>; but the Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">matla </span> means <i>to make a comparison </i>.",
    "fn.2185": "Literally: <i>if it were to be injured in its body</i>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">himself</span>, for <span dir=\"rtl\">as his body</span>.",
    "f1": "val1",
    "f2": "val2",
    "f3": "val3",
    "fn.2187": "The two Hebrew translators omitted translating the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">צרח</span>, which is also missing in the ms. of Leiden, no. 18.",
    "fn.2188": "Ibn-Tibbon, who has <span dir=\"rtl\">לםתום</span>, appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">יכׄתם</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(يختم)</span> , with a <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄ</span> punctuated text; according to him, it would be necessary to translate: <i>he wanted to close his prophetic mission</i>.",
    "fn.2189": "That is to say: a certain faculty of the soul which imparts boldness, and which forms part of the motive faculties. In our editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must efface the <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span> of <span dir=\"rtl\">וגבורה</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">גבודה</span> is moreover not well chosen to render the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">إقدام</span>, which signifies <i>boldness, courage</i>. Ibn-Falaquéra, here blaming the employment of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">גבורה</span>, proposes to translate it as <span dir=\"rtl\">כח קדימה</span>, and expresses himself thus concerning the faculty designated by this name: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכה הקדימה הוא שיקדים האדם וילך להלחם עם האריה או הדומה ולהכנם במקום םכנה ולא יפחד</span> 'This faculty is that by virtue of which man advances boldly (<span dir=\"rtl\">יקדים</span>) to combat the lion or other similar creature, or to proceed without fear into a place of peril.' See Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 155. Al-’Harîzi calls this faculty <span dir=\"rtl\">כה אמצה</span>.",
    "fn.2190": "Cf. vol. I of this work, p. 367, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 5.",
    "fn.2191": "According to Moses of Narbonne and Schem-Tob, the author, by the words <i>with a certain way of thinking</i>, means that one must also join a certain way of seeing to the natural predisposition; according to what one believes, for example, that with death everything ends for man, or that a heroic death places man in the rank of superior beings, one will neglect this natural disposition, or one will exercise it.",
    "fn.2192": "Both Hebrew versions have אלו הכחות (these faculties); but all Arabic manuscripts have the singular אלקוה̈.",
    "fn.2193": "Word for word: <i>so that you find in your soul</i>.",
    "fn.2194": "Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">מי שדמיונו ומשערו וכו׳</span>. According to Ibn-Falaquéra, the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">حدס</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">شعور</span> designate sagacity of mind, or the faculty of suddenly divining the unknown from the known (<span dir=\"rtl\">וענינם אצל חכמי המחקר זכות השכל שמבין הדבר הנםתר מתוך הדבר הנגלה במהירות ובזמן קצר מאד</span>]). He censures, as a grave error, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דמיון</span>, <i>imagination</i>, employed herein by Ibn-Tibbon. See in the Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Morè</i>, p. 156, the note on ch. XLV.",
    "fn.2195": "Literally: <i>that he cannot almost imagine that a thing is, without it being</i> (<i>actually</i>)<i> as he had imagined it, or that it is in part</i>.",
    "fn.2196": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">קראין</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">قرأتن</span>, plural of <span dir=\"rtl\">قريظة</span>) signifies <i>conjectures, combined circumstances</i>. Ibn-Tibbon renders this word simply by <span dir=\"rtl\">ענינים</span>, and Al-’Harîzi by <span dir=\"rtl\">ענינים דבקים</span>. According to Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>l. c.</i>, p. 156), this word would be employed in the sense of <i>premises;</i> for, says he, the premises, <i>united together</i>, give rise to the conclusion. But, if it be true that the author says, immediately thereafter, <span dir=\"rtl\">תלך אלמקדמאת</span>, <i>these premises</i>, it does not thence follow that he employs the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קראין</span> in the sense of <i>premises;</i> he means only that the mind does swiftly traverse all the circumstances which have presented themselves at divers moments, and which, to one's apprehension, link themselves mutually in the twinkling of an eye and serve as premises, whence one draws conclusions.",
    "fn.2197": "Literally: <i>that it is in a moment</i>.",
    "fn.2198": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ינדׄר</span>, the ms. of Leyde, n° 221, carries <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄבר</span>; similarly, Ibn-Tibbon <span dir=\"rtl\">יגידו</span>, <i>annoncent</i>.",
    "fn.2199": "Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">שהתגבר</span>; but this translation is precisely criticized by Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>l. c.</i>, p. 155-156), who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">והיא קדימת האיש הנפרד</span>.",
    "fn.2200": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ירתע</span> is the apocopated future of the VIII<sup>e</sup> form of the root <span dir=\"rtl\">(يَرْتَعْ) روع</span> .",
    "fn.2201": "That is to say: audacity is indispensable to all prophets, although they possess it to varying degrees.",
    "fn.2202": "The sense is: Just as prophets predict things that it is not given to every man to foresee by the sole faculty of divination that we all possess to a certain point, so too they have speculative knowledge to which the common man cannot rise by the sole power of philosophical speculation.",
    "fn.2203": "Literally: <i>the same effusion</i>, that is to say, that of the active intellect.",
    "fn.2204": "Word for word: <i>of the things of a real being;</i> that is, the things as they are in all their reality.",
    "fn.2205": "Literally: <i>whoever prefers justice</i> (or <i>impartiality</i>) <i>for himself</i>.",
    "fn.2206": "After seeking to establish, by simple analogy, that the rational faculty must be, like the imaginative faculty, much stronger in prophets than in all other men, the author wants to show, by a more direct proof, that what he has said applies even better to the rational faculty than to the imaginative faculty.",
    "fn.2207": "See the previous chapter, p. 291. — Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">Al-Dawîn</span>, the mss. generally have <span dir=\"rtl\">Al-Dawî</span>, which is incorrect.",
    "fn.2208": "Literally: <i>and whose traces have remained imprinted in their chimeras</i>.",
    "fn.2209": "It is probably due to distraction that Ibn-Tibbon translated here by <span dir=\"rtl\">מקומות</span> (places, locations), the word that he himself rendered a little higher by <span dir=\"rtl\">רשומים</span> (traces), as observed by Ibn-Falaquéra (Appendix to <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 156).",
    "fn.2210": "Literally: <i>and they appeared to them in such a manner that they thought them to be something new</i>.",
    "fn.2211": "The author means that it is even possible that the predictions of these pretended prophets are not always based on pure fancies, but on vague reminiscences that, without their knowledge, affect their imagination, to which they present themselves as a sudden revelation.",
    "fn.2212": "Literally: <i>it is one of the places which lead one astray and cause one to perish; and how many have perished thereby of those who laid claim to discernment?</i> The author would speak of those sudden inspirations, by virtue of which certain men do deem themselves able to arrogate the gift of prophecy, and which are naught else but reminiscences of ancient studies and of certain ideas most simple and most rational. — It is erroneously that Ibn-Tibbon has rendered the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמהלכה̈</span> (which cause to <i>perish</i>) by <span dir=\"rtl\">הממיתים</span> (which cause to <i>die</i>), and likewise <span dir=\"rtl\">וכם הלך</span> (and how many <i>have perished</i>), by <span dir=\"rtl\">וכמה מתו</span> (and how many <i>have died</i>)<i>;</i> the Arabic term here denots a <i>moral perdition</i>. Al-’Harîzi has the words <span dir=\"rtl\">המאבידות</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">אבדו</span>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄין ירידון אלתמייז</span> (word for word: <i>those who desire discernment</i>) admit of two different interpretations; one may translate: <i>those who lay claim to discernment</i> or <i>to sound judgment</i>, and that is the sense adopted by Ibn-Tibbon, who has <span dir=\"rtl\">המחזיקים עצמם כחכמים</span>, <i>who take themselves for wise men;</i> or else one may understand by these words: <i>those who seek to discern well</i>, a sense adopted by Al-’Harîzi, who has <span dir=\"rtl\">רודפי האמת</span>, <i>who pursue truth</i>.",
    "fn.2213": "The text contains <span dir=\"rtl\">ומן אגׄל הדׄא</span>, <i>c’est pourquoi</i>, words that relate to what the author said about the reminiscences that mix with the ghosts of the imagination.",
    "fn.2214": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ענד פיץׄ</span>, the ms. of Leyden, n° 18, has <span dir=\"rtl\">מן פיץׄ</span>, and this is the reading adopted by the two Hebrew translators; Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">באמצעות שפע</span>, and Al-’Harîzi, <span dir=\"rtl\">משפע</span>.",
    "fn.2215": "Maimonides, following the example of the Chaldaic version, considers the word <span dir=\"rtl\">נביא</span> here as a noun in the sense of <i>prophet</i>, although it would be more natural to see it as a verb, <i>hiph'il</i> of <span dir=\"rtl\">בוא</span>.",
    "fn.2216": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ממן עלמנאה</span>, the ms. of Leyde, n° 18, reads <span dir=\"rtl\">ממן תקדם</span>, <i>of those who have preceded.</i> This reading was followed by the two Hebrew translators; Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">ממי שקדם</span>, and Al-’Harîzi, <span dir=\"rtl\">מן הקדומים</span>.",
    "fn.2217": "The text says: <span dir=\"rtl\">אן אללה</span>, <small>QUE</small> <i>God.</i> We know that the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">אן</span> sometimes serves to introduce direct discourse; cf. vol. I of this work, p. 283, note 4.",
    "fn.2218": "Only one of our manuscripts, the n° 18 of Leyde, has אלוהי (with the article), the same as the two Hebrew versions הנבואה.",
    "fn.2219": "See the I<sup>st</sup> part, ch. LXIII, p. 281-282.",
    "fn.2220": "The author has in view these words of the same verse: <i>so that he may prescribe to his sons and his house after him to observe the way of the Eternal, etc.</i>, where it is said clearly that he limited himself to instructing his own in what they should do to please God, without saying that God had sent him to proclaim such and such a command [[t:2]].",
    "fn.2221": "See, for example, <i>Beréschîth rabba,</i> sect. 43 (fol. 55, col. 2, and 56, col. 1), where mention is made of the school of Shem and Eber. One cannot affirm whether, in our <i>Midraschîm,</i> there is mention of a <i>tribunal</i> or a <i>school of Methuselah;</i> but here and there is mention of the great piety of this patriarch and of his profound learning. See, e.g., the <i>Midrasch Abkhir</i>, cited in the <i>Yalkout</i>, vol. I, no. 42.",
    "fn.2222": "That is to say: to the descendants of such an one, to such a tribe, or to such a people.",
    "fn.2223": "Literally: <i>who stand upright</i> (or <i>standing</i>) <i>to follow</i> her, which Ibn-Tibbon rendered as <span dir=\"rtl\">מי שהתישר להמשך אחריה</span>. Al-’Harîzi translated more freely: <span dir=\"rtl\">and all who pursue her and walk in her ways</span>.",
    "fn.2224": "That is to say: this law must always be followed, and it will never be replaced by another; for, as the Scripture says, it does not need to be sought in the sky, nor beyond the seas; it is in our mouth, in our heart, and it perfectly adapts to human nature.",
    "fn.2225": "The author here plays upon the double meaning of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">معتدل</span>, which denots that which is <i>equal</i>, <i>in equilibrium</i>, <i>well-proportioned,</i> and, figuratively, that which is <i>equitable</i> or <i>just;</i> it corresponds to the Latin words œquus and œqualis, which are likewise employed in both their literal and figurative senses, whereas the Hebrew word <span dir=\"rtl\">צדיק</span> is used only in its figurative sense. The wordplay in question here can scarcely be rendered into French; but the words <i>equal</i> and <i>equitable</i> share the same etymology.",
    "fn.2226": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">الرهبانية</span>, which signifies <i>the life of an anchorite</i>, is rendered in the <i>version of Ibn-Tibbon by this periphrasis:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">עבודת המתבודד בהרים הפורש עצמו מן הבשר והיין ודבריס רביס מצרכי הגוף</span>, <i>the worship of him who dwells solitarily in the mountains, and who abstains from flesh, from wine, and from many other things needful for the body;</i> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">السياحة</span> is rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">טלטול לעבודה</span>, <i>Wandering life whose object is worship.</i> The version of Al-’Harîzi does not render these two words. — In lieu of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלרהכאניה̈</span>, the Leiden manuscript, No. 18, has <span dir=\"rtl\">אלבראהמה̈</span>, <i>Brahmanism.</i> A manuscript of Ibn-Tibbon's version (Imp. Library, ancient collection, No. 238, fol. 235 <i>b</i>) presents the singular reading <span dir=\"rtl\">במופתיות</span>, which would lead one to suppose that <span dir=\"rtl\">אלברהאניה̈</span> (instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלרהבאניה̈</span>) was read in the Arabic text, a reading which here offers no sense.",
    "fn.2227": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלסאלפה̈</span>, the ms. of Leiden, no. 18, has <span dir=\"rtl\">אלגׄאהליה̈</span>; similarly, Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">הסנליות</span>, and Al-’Harîzi, <span dir=\"rtl\">הסכלוׄת</span>, which should be understood here in the sense of <i>pagans.</i> Cf. above, p. 260, note 2.",
    "fn.2228": "See the third part of this work, ch. XXXI and following. — In Ibn-Tibbon's version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">עלת טעמי</span> are a double translation of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תעליל</span>; Al-’Harîzi has: <span dir=\"rtl\">בטעמי המצות</span>.",
    "fn.2229": "That is to say, in their just proportion holding the middle ground between too much and too little. Cf. above, p. 285, note 1. — The suffix, in <span dir=\"rtl\">אעתדאלהא וחבמתהא</span>, clearly refers to the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשראיע</span> (Hebrew <span dir=\"rtl\">המצות</span>), and Ibn-Tibbon was wrong to put the suffix in the singular: <span dir=\"rtl\">שוויה וחכמתה</span>.",
    "fn.2230": "These words would signify, according to the author: have I been harsh towards Israel, by my commandments, like unto a desert and a land of deep darkness, in which the traveller does encounter difficulties at every step? Cf. the commentary of Abravanel upon this verse of Jeremiah: <span dir=\"rtl\">ראו דבר י״י הביטו מצותיו ותורותיו אם יש בהם מעמם וטורח לבעלי התורה</span>, '<i>behold the word of the Eternal,</i>' that is to say, behold whether there be in His commandments and in His laws a burden and a weariness for those who possess the law.",
    "fn.2231": "Literally: <i>and that the punishment strikes him who exercises it.</i>",
    "fn.2232": "In Ibn-Tibbon’s version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">גנות רוע</span> are a double translation of the Arabic word; Al-’Harîzi has: <span dir=\"rtl\">כפי נבלות מדותיו</span>.",
    "fn.2233": "Literally: <i>but it</i> (the law) <i>must be considered by reason of the perfect man, the aim of the law being that all men should be</i> (<i>as</i>) <i>that man</i>. The Arabic MSS. bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">אן יכון אלנאס כלהס דׄלך אלאנסאן</span>; the sense is: that all men should be identically that man, i.e., <i>should perfectly resemble him.</i> The two Hebrew versions have <span dir=\"rtl\">כמו האיש ההוא</span>. — The most ancient of the Oxford MSS. (Uri, No. 359, cf. Vol. I, p. 462) presents this singular reading: <span dir=\"rtl\">אן יכון אלנאס כלחס דׄלך אלזמאן כאמלין</span>, '<i>that all men should be perfect in that time.</i>'",
    "fn.2234": "See Aristotle's <i>Politique</i>, book I, chapter 1: <i>ἄνθρωπος φύσει πολιτιϰὸν ζῶον</i>. Cf. the III<sup>e</sup> part of this work, chapter XXVII.",
    "fn.2235": "Literally: <i>whether it be joined</i> (with its similars).",
    "fn.2236": "We observe in the overall beings sublunary a progression from the least perfect to the most perfect. In addition to the four elements, from which all beings are composed, plants have the vegetative soul, to which, in animals, is joined the vital soul; man alone possesses the rational soul and is thus the most composed and the most perfect of all sublunary beings.",
    "fn.2237": "Literally: <i>that one does scarce find two individuals who agree, in anywise, in one of the species of morals, save as one finds their exterior countenances to agree</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא מא</span>, the Leyden MS., No. 18, bears <span dir=\"rtl\">כמא לא</span>, and similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">כמו שלא תראה</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא כשתמצא</span>.",
    "fn.2238": "Literally: <i>is brought near or tends to be brought near.</i>",
    "fn.2239": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">לחיים</span> , <i>until the limit</i>, <i>at the point</i>, the Leiden manuscript (no. 18) has the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלי חד</span>, which have the same meaning; the version of Ibn-Tibbon has simply <span dir=\"rtl\">עד</span>, <i>until</i>; similarly, Ibn-Falaquéra, <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, p. 116.",
    "fn.2240": "That is, in most of the moral qualities of the soul.",
    "fn.2241": "Literally: <i>as its nature</i> (that of the human species) <i>requires that there be this variation in its individuals.</i>",
    "fn.2242": "Instead of the first <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמפרט</span>, which should be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">المفرط</span>, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמקצר</span>.",
    "fn.2243": "Literally: <i>there is yet an entrance into the natural thing.</i>",
    "fn.2244": "That is to say, the prophet and the legislator. Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">מה שצוה הנביא ההוא</span>, '<i>that which that prophet has prescribed;</i>' similarly Al-’Harîzi, <span dir=\"rtl\">במה שצוה הנביא</span>, and Ibn-Falaquera (<i>l. c.</i>), <span dir=\"rtl\">מה שאמר אותו הנביא</span>. This translation is justified by but one of our MSS., No. 18 of Leyden, which bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">בעמל מא גׄא בה אלנביא</span>.",
    "fn.2245": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ותרך</span>, the Leyden MS., No. 18, has <span dir=\"rtl\">ותרבה</span> with a suffix. Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">והנּיח</span>, without a suffix. At the commencement of the sentence, Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">ויהיה</span>, whereas all the Arabic MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">יכון</span>, without the copulative <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>; <span dir=\"rtl\">הקצתו</span> in Ibn-Tibbon’s editions is a misprint for <span dir=\"rtl\">הקצת</span>.",
    "fn.2246": "In the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">from the Lord</span> are missing here; the mss. read: <span dir=\"rtl\">that which did not come through prophecy from the Lord, a complete day</span>. The translator has used the word <span dir=\"rtl\">day</span> as an adverb, in the sense of <i>never</i>, imitating the Arabic adverb <span dir=\"rtl\">yesterday</span>.",
    "fn.2247": "See I Kings, ch. 22, <small>V</small>. 11 and 24.",
    "fn.2248": "See Jeremiah, chap. 28, <small>V</small>. 1 et seq. — This example, at first glance, does not appear well chosen, inasmuch as Hanania, as well as Zedekiah, is presented as a false prophet, and his words are expressly gainsaid by Jeremiah. Ibn-Caspi, in his commentary, entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">עמודי כסף</span>, raises this difficulty in these terms: <span dir=\"rtl\">תמה איך יאמר הר״מ זה כי עליו כתוב כמו על צרקיה כי סרה דבר ובכלל לא מצאתי הבדל בין צדקיה וחנניה</span>. But, in establishing here a distinction between Zedekiah and Hanania, Maimonides did but follow the Talmud, according to which the former had predicted <i>things he had never heard</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מתנבא מה שלא שמע</span>), whilst the latter had predicted <i>that which had not been said unto him</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מתנבא מה שלא נאמר לו</span>), i.e., he had merely reproduced a prophecy of Jeremiah, the sense of which he had falsified or misunderstood. According to the Jerusalem Talmud (treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, last chapter), Hanania, in predicting that, <i>in two years</i>, God would break the yoke of the king of Babylon, believed himself to be in accord with Jeremiah, who predicted <i>seventy years of captivity</i> (Jeremiah, 29, 10), which, in Hanania’s opinion, had commenced during the reign of Manasseh. According to the Babylonian Talmud (same treatise, fol. 89 <i>a</i>), the words of Hanania: '<i>I will break the yoke of the king of Babylon,</i>' were but a reproduction of the words of Jeremiah (49, 35): '<i>behold,</i> <i>I will break the bow of Elam;</i>' since, Hanania said to himself, the Elamites, who were but the auxiliaries of Babylon, were to receive celestial chastisement, so much the more reason the Chaldeans themselves.",
    "fn.2249": "That is to say, the regimes or governments founded by a mere legislator. — Several editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version bear <span dir=\"rtl\">המונחים לא המושמים</span>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span>, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">ל״א</span>, an abbreviation for <span dir=\"rtl\">לשון אחר</span>, for the words <span dir=\"rtl\">המונחים</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">המושמים</span> are two distinct translations of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמוצׄועה̈</span>. Cf. Vol. I, p. 411, note 2.",
    "fn.2250": "Literally: <i>that these are laws which they have established by means of their reflections.</i>",
    "fn.2251": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תחתאגׄ</span> is the 2<sup>e</sup> person of the masculine, the ms. of Leyden, n° 18, has <span dir=\"rtl\">יחהאגׄ</span>, in the 3<sup>e</sup> person, and similarly Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">לא יצטרך</span>.",
    "fn.2252": "That is to say, borrowings from the prophets, but which were wanted to be passed off as original works. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מנתחלח̈</span>, which Al-’Harizi renders by <span dir=\"rtl\">borrowings from someone else</span>, was thus paraphrased by Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">that they would say they said from their heart and took from someone else</span>. Cf. the t 1, p. 419, note 1.",
    "fn.2253": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, <span dir=\"rtl\">השגה</span> is a printing error for <span dir=\"rtl\">השגחה</span>.",
    "fn.2254": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מטׄנונח̈</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon rendered by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ראה אותו</span>, is missing in the ms. of Leiden, no. 18, and was not rendered in the version of Al-’Harîzi, which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ושישיגו שום הצלחה בעצת המנהיג ההוא</span>.",
    "fn.2255": "See above, p. 291.",
    "fn.2256": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">מעויניס</span>; one should read, according to the manuscripts, <span dir=\"rtl\">מעייניס</span>.",
    "fn.2257": "In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">חעקב</span> is rendered by the two verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">ילחקור ולדעת</span>; the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ולחקור ולבדוק</span>, which is a double translation of the Arabic verb. The version of Al-’Harîzi carries <span dir=\"rtl\">ולדקדק על פעליו</span>.",
    "fn.2258": "See above, p. 285, note 3.",
    "fn.2259": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">פצׄח</span>, which signifies <i>to confound</i>, <i>to put publicly to shame</i>, has been rendered, in Ibn-Tibbon's version, by <span dir=\"rtl\">עבר ופרסם</span>. Al-’Harîzi does translate more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">וע״כ גלה בה הבורא חרפת כל מתנחלי הנבואה</span>. — After the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כֹּל מַדָּע</span> , <i>whosoever arrogated unto himself</i>, one must needs understand <i>the gift of prophecy;</i> the two Hebrew translators have supplied this ellipsis. The sense of this sentence is: that God has confounded the false prophets by unmasking their hypocrisy and by making it publicly manifest that they were debauched men who committed adultery.",
    "fn.2260": "Literally: <i>Do you not see Sidkia etc.?…, how they usurped the prophecy, and the men followed etc.?</i>",
    "fn.2261": "This is what the author appears to find in the words of Jeremiah, cited below: <i>and that they had spoken words of falsehood that I had not commanded them.</i> The falsehood consisted, according to the author, in their attributing words spoken by other prophets to themselves.",
    "fn.2262": "That is to say: the intention of what was said in the last paragraph, in order to make the distinction between the true prophet and the plagiarist.",
    "fn.2263": "The author means that what Scripture calls <span dir=\"rtl\">מראׇה</span>, the theologians explain by <span dir=\"rtl\">מראֵה הנבואה</span>, <i>that which is seen in the state of prophecy, prophetic vision</i>.",
    "fn.2264": "See II Kings, 3, 15; Ezekiel, 1, 3; III, 22; XXXVII, 1; XL, 1.",
    "fn.2265": "A word derived from the verb ’<small>HAZA</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">הזה</span>), <i>see</i>. See Genesis 15:1; Numbers 24:4 and 16.",
    "fn.2266": "See above, at the beginning of chap. XXXVII.",
    "fn.2267": "See <i>Bereshith rabbah</i>, section 63 (fol. 55, col. 2), where R. Levi says, in the name of R. Aba, that God spoke to Rebecca through the intermediary of an angel.",
    "fn.2268": "Literally: <i>The relation</i> (or <i>the statement</i>) <i>of the word addressed to the prophets, according to the expression that appears in the prophetic books, is made in four ways</i>.",
    "fn.2269": "In none of our manuscripts is the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יצרח</span> preceded by the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">אן</span>; and similarly, Ibn-Tibbon simply has <span dir=\"rtl\">יגלה</span>. Al-’Harîzi's version has <span dir=\"rtl\">שיאמר הנביא</span>.",
    "fn.2270": "The two Hebrew translators omitted the imperative <span dir=\"rtl\">אפעל</span>, due to its synonymy with <span dir=\"rtl\">אצנע</span>.",
    "fn.2271": "In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, even in the mss., one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">והשודש</span>, which is obviously a scribe's error, for <span dir=\"rtl\">והושרש</span>.",
    "fn.2272": "In the last three examples, as in those of the second mode, the word <small>ÉLOHIM</small> (God) does signify, according to our author, an <i>angel</i> (vide vol. I, chap. II, p. 37, and above, chap. VI, p. 66), and it is to be remarked that, for the third and fourth modes, the author cits only examples in which God is designated by the <i>tetragrammaton</i> name. Concerning the last two examples, one must recall that, if it is not expressly stated therein that Bileam's vision occurred in a dream, this does result from verse 8, where Bileam says unto Balak's ambassadors: <i>Lodge here this night, and I will bring you word again</i>. — The second and third examples are wanting in Ibn-Tibbon's version.",
    "fn.2273": "If the author cites the example of Gideon here, it must be understood in the sense that the word of the Eternal was addressed to a prophet who spoke to Gideon; for Gideon himself was not a prophet, as the author declares later, chap. XLVI.",
    "fn.2274": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">והנה דבר י״י אליו</span> are also found in chapter XV of Genesis, verse 4; but the author could not have had this passage in view, which is connected with verse 1 of the same chapter, and therefore belongs to the third mode.",
    "fn.2275": "Instead of the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וכדׄלך פי לבן בחלום הלילה</span>, which the Arabic manuscripts generally contain, but which have been suppressed in the Leyden manuscript, no. 18, and in Al-’Harîzi's version, Ibn-Tibbon does render textually the passage from Genesis, chap. 31, <small>v.</small> 24: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויבא אלהים אל לבן האדמי בחלום הלילה</span>. In general, the construction of this sentence is somewhat illogical, and one has been obliged to supply in one's translation the words: <i>that God appeared unto him in a dream</i>, which are understood.",
    "fn.2276": "The editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have <span dir=\"rtl\">Vekhol zeh</span>, which is only a printing error for <span dir=\"rtl\">Vilzeh</span>, the reading of the mss.",
    "fn.2277": "Literally: <i>without its being that such-and-such a one;</i> that is to say, without the divine word having been addressed directly to him. — Most manuscripts bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולים יכון דׄלך אלפלוני ולא אתאה</span>, and it is this reading that one has adopted. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלפלוני</span>, the Leyden manuscript, no. 18, bears <span dir=\"rtl\">לפלוני</span>, a reading likewise found in Ibn-Tibbon's version. The manuscript of the Imperial Library (anc. Heb. fonds, no. 237) bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולים יכון דׄלך אלפלוני אתאה</span>, suppressing the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא</span>, which all other Arabic manuscripts, as well as both Hebrew versions, retain. The Leyden manuscript, no. 221, bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולם יכן דׄלך אלפלוני נביא</span>, 'without that such-and-such a one were <i>a prophet</i>.' Al-’Harîzi does translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא ידבר עם פלוני ולא באה נבואה אליו מעולם</span>. These diverse variants arise solely from the singularity which the primitive reading presents at first glance; for the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולים יכון דׄלך אלפלוני</span> may be translated: <i>without that such-and-such a one were</i> (or <i>existed</i>).",
    "fn.2278": "Voy. <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. 63 (fol. 55, col. 2). Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">עבר</span>, the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have, like the editions of the <i>Midrasch</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">שם ועבר</span>,",
    "fn.2279": "That is to say: it was Eber who replied to Rebecca in the name of the Eternal.",
    "fn.2280": "See above, p. 314, note 2.",
    "fn.2281": "See the following chapter.",
    "fn.2282": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">סאיר</span> is here employed in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">כל</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄמיע</span>, <i>totality</i>, <i>all</i>, and it is in the same sense that one must likewise sometimes take the word <span dir=\"rtl\">שאר</span> in the Hebrew versions. — The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have here <span dir=\"rtl\">בשאד</span> (with <i>câph</i>)<i>;</i> one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">בשאד</span> (with <i>beth</i>).",
    "fn.2283": "That is to say, a state where the imaginative faculty takes precedence over sensory perception.",
    "fn.2284": "For example, the <i>three men</i> that Abraham saw (Genesis, 18, 2), and the <i>man</i> that Joshua saw (Joshua, 5, 13).",
    "fn.2285": "On these different degrees of prophecy, see below, ch. XLV.",
    "fn.2286": "That is to say, this principle that, in all the appearances reported in one of the four ways in which it has just been spoken, it is a matter of a vision or a dream.",
    "fn.2287": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">ואמרו מאמר אליהם</span>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מאמר</span> is merely a printing error, for <span dir=\"rtl\">ונאמר</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ודברו עמו והשיב להם</span>.",
    "fn.2288": "See <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. 48 (fol. 42, col. 4). — This passage is exceedingly obscure; for one comprehends not how the author has discovered all that he here setts forth in the few words of R. ’Hayya, which appear to have no other aim than to expound the use of the singular in verse 3, whilst in the subsequent verses Abraham addresses the three angels conjointly and ever speaks in the plural. R. ’Hayya therefore says that Abraham addressed himself to the chief amongst them, who was Michael. In order to resolve the difficulty, Abravanel (Commentary upon the <i>Moré</i>, II, fol. 40 <i>a</i>) is of the opinion that one must revert to the two views advanced by the doctors concerning the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אדני</span>, <i>Lord</i>, employed in verse 3; according to some, this word replacs the <i>tetragrammaton</i> of verse 1 and denots God Himself; according to others, it denots one of the <i>three men</i> of whom verse 2 speaks. According to the first opinion, the vision, in which Abraham represented to his mind God Himself, or the <i>Supreme Cause</i>, would have been interrupted by the apparition of the <i>three men</i>, and Abraham, distracted by this apparition, would have besought God not to depart from him, that is to say, he would have striven not to suffer interruption in his contemplation of the Divinity. According to the second opinion, the apparition of the three men would be identical with that of God, of which verse 1 speaks; so that verse 2 and those following would but elucidate in detail the nature of the divine manifestation of verse 1. Now, R. ’Hayya, evidently professing the second opinion, it follows that, according to him, all the particulars recounted from verse 2 onwards constitute a prophetic vision, of the same character as the apparition of God in verse 1, which would merely summarise the sum of the details that succeed. — Doubtless, this is to expound with excessive subtlety the plain words of R. ’Hayya; yet it is consonant with the manner in which Maimonides, in general, interprets the oft-times artless sayings of the ancient doctors.",
    "fn.2289": "Literally: <i>for it is a mystery amongst mysteries</i>. Ibn-Tibbon’s version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">בםוד מן הםודות</span>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בםוד</span> (mss. <span dir=\"rtl\">כםור</span>), one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">בי הוא םוד</span>, as in Al-’Harîzi’s version. — The mystery of which the author desirs to speak is that all the particulars recounted in Chapter XVIII of Genesis, the repast made by the <i>three men</i>, the words which they addressed to Sarah, et cetera, did not truly come to pass, but transpired solely within Abraham’s imagination, that is to say, within a prophetic vision. The like is true, as the author is about to relate, of the angel’s struggle with Jacob, of what befell Balaam with his ass, of the angel who appeared unto Joshua before Jericho, and of many other biblical narratives, which ought to be regarded as prophetic visions. This opinion, as one may well surmise, has encountered, amongst Jewish theologians, numerous adversaries. See notably the polemic of R. Moses ben-Na’hman, in his Commentary upon Genesis, in Chapter XVIII.",
    "fn.2290": "The author means that in verse 25, where it is said: <i>Jacob being left alone, a man wrestled with him</i>, one must see, in the <i>man who wrestled</i>, one of the <i>angles of God</i> mentioned in verse 2.",
    "fn.2291": "One must here recall that, according to the ancient Jewish doctors, the gift of prophecy was likewise vouchsafed unto certain pagan sages, whose mission it was to foretell the destiny of the people of Israel. See that which is said, concerning Balaam, in the <i>Midrasch</i> of Leviticus or <i>Wayyikra rabbà</i>, sect. 1 (fol. 147, col. 1), and in the <i>Midrasch</i> of Canticles, or <i>Schîr ha-schîrîm rabbâ</i>, in Chapter II, <small>V</small>. 3 (fol. 9, col. 4).",
    "fn.2292": "Which, as the author says at the beginning of this chapter, indicates a dream, or a prophetic vision.",
    "fn.2293": "This citation is not textual; for the author appears to have in view the following passage from the <i>Wayyikra rabbâ</i>, sect. 1 (fol. 146, col. 1): <span dir=\"rtl\">וכי מלאך היה והלא פינחס היה ולמה קורא אותו מלאך אלא אמר ר׳ סימון פינחס בשעה שהיתה רוח הקדש שורה עליו פניו בוערות כלפידים.</span>",
    "fn.2294": "See above, ch. VI, p. 68, and cf. <i>Wayyikra rabbâ</i> (<i>l. c.</i>).",
    "fn.2295": "See above, chapter XXXII, third opinion.",
    "fn.2296": "The author wishes to explain here what one should understand by the angel who appeared to Hagar (Genesis, 16, 7 and following; XXI, 17), and by the one that Manoah and his wife saw (Judges, 22, 3 and 11).",
    "fn.2297": "The author intends here to indicate that the celestial voice of which frequent mention is made in the writings of the ancient rabbis (cf. Gospel of Matthew, III, 17), and which they term <span dir=\"rtl\">בת קול</span>, <i>daughter of voice, sound of voice, echo</i>, is naught else but the product of an exalted imagination, by which certain persons, possessing none of the requisite qualities for prophetic visions, believe they hear words addressed unto them from heaven. Oft-times even these persons believe they discern celestial apparitions, yet these are but phantoms of their imagination; and such was that which befell Hagar, as likewise Manoah and his wife.",
    "fn.2298": "That is, the homonymy of the word <i>malakh</i>, which is used in various acceptations, has led people to believe that, in what is said of Hagar, as well as of Manoah and his wife, it is really a matter of the appearance of an angel.",
    "fn.2299": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ירפע</span> (with <i>rêsch</i>), several manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">ידפע</span> (with <i>daleth</i>); likewise Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">העקר הדוחה</span>, and Al-’Harîzi has <span dir=\"rtl\">העקר אשר ידחה</span>, <i>the principle which repells</i> (or <i>refuts</i>). The author intends to convey that it is precisely the homonymy of the word <small>MALAKH</small>, posited as a principle, which serves to remove difficulties and to elucidate doubtful passages.",
    "fn.2300": "The author observes, in conclusion, that the same expression is employed concerning Hagar as concerning Joseph. According to the <i>Midrashim</i>, or allegorical interpretations, the man who met Joseph was an angel; but one could not intend to attribute to this young child the sublime perceptions of the prophets, and it is clear that only an apparition presented by his overexcited imagination was spoken of. It would, therefore, be the same for Hagar, concerning whom the Scriptural text employs the same terms.",
    "fn.2301": "Literally: <i>that the prophets sometimes prophesy by parables</i>. See Abrégé du Talmud, treatise <i>Yesôdé ha-Tôrâ</i>, ch.VII, § 3.—The Arabic mss. generally have <span dir=\"rtl\">our works</span>, in the plural, <i>our works</i>; the Hebrew versions have, one <span dir=\"rtl\">our company</span>, the other <span dir=\"rtl\">our book</span>, in the singular.",
    "fn.2302": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must erase the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כי</span>, and read only <span dir=\"rtl\">כמו</span>, as the mss. and also Al-’Harîzi have it.",
    "fn.2303": "See Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Berakhôth</i>, fol. 55 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.2304": "Zachariah, after presenting, in chapters I-III, several parables, recounts in chapter IV a new vision he had and which is presented by the parable of the seven-branched candlestick; then the meaning of this parable is explained to him by the angel in this same vision, first the general meaning and then the details (chapter IV, <small>V</small>. 6-14).",
    "fn.2305": "That is to say: it is clear that here it is a question of a <i>vision</i>, designated by the word ’<small>HAZÔN</small>.’",
    "fn.2306": "The author means to say that the word ’<small>HAZÔN</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חזון</span>) cannot indicate a third way of prophetic inspiration, and it is necessarily synonymous with <small>MAREA</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מראה</span>) and with <small>MA</small>’<small>HAZÉ</small> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מחזה</span>).",
    "fn.2307": "See below, ch. XLV.",
    "fn.2308": "See Zechariah, ch. 11, <small>V</small>. 7 and following. The meaning of this parable was not explained to the prophet in his vision, as was that of the <i>candlestick</i>, mentioned earlier. Our author attempts to indicate its meaning later on.",
    "fn.2309": "Almost all MSS. bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי ראה</span>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ראה</span> should be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">رَأَى</span>; it would have been more correct to say <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתי ראהא</span>, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חומה</span> being feminine; but perhaps the author had in mind the masculine word <span dir=\"rtl\">אנך</span>, or a masculine Arabic word such as <span dir=\"rtl\">حائط</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">تمور</span>, 'wall'. The MS. of the Hebrew supplement of the Imperial Library, No. 63, written by the hand of R. Saadia Ibn Danan, bears <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתי ראה</span>.",
    "fn.2310": "In other words: the prophets sometimes see things that do not represent a subject in and of themselves, but whose name alone recalls, through etymology, a certain idea expressed by a similar name, so that the entire vision is based on a play on words, as the author will explain with some examples.",
    "fn.2311": "That is to say, to play upon the double meaning of the root <span dir=\"rtl\">שקד</span>, whence are derived at once the word which signifies <i>amandier</i>, and that which signifies <i>vigilant, attentif</i>, so that the vision of <i>l’amandier</i> indicates these words of God: <i>car je suis vigilant pour accomplir ma parole</i>. Cf. above, chap. XXIX, p. 229, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.2312": "That is to say, when the prophet, in order to allude to a certain subject, uses a name that, by itself, has a completely different meaning, but whose letters transposed give the name of the subject in question.",
    "fn.2313": "Several mss. have the incorrect forms <span dir=\"rtl\">אלעציאן</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">אלעצאיין</span>; others have the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">אלעזי</span> , and similarly Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">בלקחו המקלות</span>, while Al-’Harîzi has <span dir=\"rtl\">בקחתו שני מקלות</span>.",
    "fn.2314": "See Zachariah, ch. 11, <small>V</small>. 7; as we shall see, the author, in citing this example, has in view the word ’<small>HÔBELÎM</small>.'",
    "fn.2315": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מסחלדה</span>, the Leiden ms. no. 18 has <span dir=\"rtl\">מסרורה̈</span>, which has the same meaning. The latter word could have been changed to <span dir=\"rtl\">מסדדה̈</span>, which explains why Ibn-Tibbon's version has <span dir=\"rtl\">מיושרת</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates <span dir=\"rtl\">מאושרת בה</span>.",
    "fn.2316": "One supposes that Maimonides adopts for the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">האמיר</span> the meaning attributed to it by Ibn-Djanah, in his dictionary: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומענאה ענדי אלעלא ואלתרפיע ולדׄלך קיל ללגצן אלעאלי אמיר פמעני האמירך רפער ואעלאך</span> \"It has, according to me, the sense of <i>élévation</i> and of <i>exaltation;</i> this is why the upper branch is called <span dir=\"rtl\">אָמִיר</span> (Isaiah, 17, 6). The meaning of <span dir=\"rtl\">האמירך</span> is, therefore: he has <i>exalté</i> thee and <i>élevé</i> thee.\" Cf. the Dictionary of David Kimhi.",
    "fn.2317": "We translate the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">Vitqatsar nafshi</span> in the sense indicated by the author himself, in chapter XLI of the first <sup>part</sup>.",
    "fn.2318": "That is, according to the method of allusions made by etymology or homonymy of the names borne by the subjects represented in the parables.",
    "fn.2319": "That is to say, in Ezekiel's vision. All Arabic MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">וקלל</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">ועגל</span> with the copulative <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>, which proves that the author's observation applies not to the expressions <span dir=\"rtl\">נחשת קלל</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">רגל עגל</span> (Ezek., 1, 7), as the version of Ibn-Tibbon might lead one to believe, but to each of the four words separately. Cf. above, p. 229, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 4.",
    "fn.2320": "One has had, for clarity, to invert the order of words in this last phrase, of which the literal translation is as follows: <i>Et dans</i> (<i>certains</i>) <i>endroits, il y a d’autres</i> (<i>mots</i>) <i>que ceux-là, qui, si tu les poursuis par ton esprit dans chaque endroit, te deviendront clairs par l’intention du discours, après cette observation</i>. The author means that, besides the words of Ezekiel just cited, one finds here and there others, the sense of which, after the observation made above, may be easily divined from the ensemble of each passage, if one applies one's mind well to it. — It is necessary to add, after <span dir=\"rtl\">בדׄהנך</span>, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי כל מוצׄע</span>, which are found in most MSS. and which the two Hebrew versions render by <span dir=\"rtl\">בבל מקום</span>; these words are lacking in some MSS. and have been omitted in our Arabic text.",
    "fn.2321": "The text says simply: <span dir=\"rtl\">ירי</span>, 'he sees' <i>il voit;</i> but the sense is necessarily: 'he believes he sees' <i>il croit voir</i>, or 'it seems to him he sees' <i>il lui semble voir,</i> and even this can occur not in a 'vision' <i>vision</i> properly so-called, but only in a dream. See above, p. 314, and hereafter, chap. XLV, seventh degree, and <i>ibid.,</i> XI<sup>e</sup> degree, p. 344, note 3.",
    "fn.2322": "This passage was omitted in the version of Ibn-Tibbon; but it is found in all the mss. of the Arabic text and in the version of Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.2323": "In this citation, the biblical passage has been transposed, no doubt due to a memory error. We have reproduced the citation as it is found in all the Arabic mss. and in the two Hebrew versions.",
    "fn.2324": "The expression <span dir=\"rtl\">יד י״י</span>, <i>la main de l’Éternel,</i> proves that it is a prophetic vision. See above, at the beginning of ch. XLI.",
    "fn.2325": "The author desires to justify his citation of the passage from Ezekiel in support of this 5<sup>e</sup> category, where the prophet hears a voice without seeing any figure. He says, therefore, that one must not believe that what spoke to Ezekiel was that sublime apparition of which he speaks in chap. I, v. 26, and whose surroundings he describes with such detail; it was, on the contrary, an invisible being who spoke to him to give him the mission expounded in chap. II, v. 3 et seq.",
    "fn.2326": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">صاعقة</span> designates the <i>fire from heaven</i>, or the <i>lightning;</i> in two passages of vol. I<sup>st</sup>, p. 220 and p. 369, this word has been inaccurately translated by <i>storm;</i> Ibn-Tibbon rendered it in the three passages by <span dir=\"rtl\">זועה</span>, which is not exact; in chapter XII of the III<sup>rd</sup> part, he has more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">הברקים העצומים</span>. According to Al-Kazwîni's definition, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">صاعقة</span> denotes an igneous meteor: it is a fire which falls from heaven, which burns all bodies it encounters, and which penetrats the earth and the hardest rocks. See Al-Kazwîni, <i>’Adjâyib al-makhloukât</i>, published by Mr. Wüstenfeld (Gœtting, 1849, in-4°), p. 91.",
    "fn.2327": "Ibn-Tibbon, perhaps to avoid unnecessary repetition, but simply <span dir=\"rtl\">and he said</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates more literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">and he said for this reason</span>.",
    "fn.2328": "Ibn-Tibbon's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">או יהיה אמרו</span>; Al-’Harîzi's version has more accurately <span dir=\"rtl\">ומה שאמר</span>.",
    "fn.2329": "The translation of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">ולזה לא ידע</span>, is inaccurate; it should be translated as <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא ירע גם כן</span>.",
    "fn.2330": "Abravanel (Commentary on the <i>Moré,</i> II, fol. 42 <i>b</i>) subtilizes upon the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מעלות</span> which several MSS. of Ibn-Tibbon's version possess here, and which, according to him, would have been employed here with intention, because the degrees are enumerated by the author in an <i>ascending</i> progression; but Abravanel's observation can adapt neither to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מראתב</span>, which the Arabic text has, nor to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מדרגות</span> which the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version possess.",
    "fn.2331": "That is, according to philosophical speculation and religious law.",
    "fn.2332": "In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">in the books of prophecy, the prophet </span>, as the mss. have it; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">prophecy </span> is missing in all editions.",
    "fn.2333": "Literally: for, <i>as for these degrees which I am about to enumerate</i>, <i>it may be that the revelation of such a prophet comes unto him partly under a certain form among them;</i> that is to say, under the form of one of these degrees. Ibn-Tibbon supplies the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אחת</span>, by rendering the words <span dir=\"rtl\">בחסב צורה̈ מנהא</span>, which all Arabic MSS. possess, by <span dir=\"rtl\">לפי צורת אחת מהם</span>.",
    "fn.2334": "According to Abravanel (<i>loc. cit.</i>), the author doubtless alludes to Samuel and Jeremiah, who, both the one and the other, after having spoken and written under <i>prophetic inspiration</i>, did also leave writings which have been classified amongst the <i>Hagiographa</i>, to which one attributs but an inferior degree of inspiration, termed <i>holy spirit</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רוח הקדש</span>). It was under this inspiration that Samuel did write the book of <i>Ruth</i> and Jeremiah his <i>Lamentations.</i>",
    "fn.2335": "This phrase is under the influence of the particle <span dir=\"rtl\">קד</span>, <i>sometimes, often</i>, placed after <span dir=\"rtl\">בל</span>, and it would have been more logical to place after <span dir=\"rtl\">כדׄלך</span>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon does not render this nuance.",
    "fn.2336": "The author has not expressed himself here with the desired precision; for he clearly means: <i>and then be deprived</i>, <i>forever</i>, <i>of the gift of prophecy</i>.",
    "fn.2337": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">סאיר</span> has the meaning of <i>tous;</i> cf. above, p. 318, note 5.",
    "fn.2338": "The author has diverted these words from their true meaning, which is as follows: <i>to fulfill the word of the Lord</i> (<i>announced</i>) <i>by the mouth of Jeremiah.</i>",
    "fn.2339": "Literally: <i>After having preceded this preliminary observation and given it as a preparation.</i>",
    "fn.2340": "The author will enumerate eleven degrees, of which the first two are merely a preparation for prophecy and characterize the <i>Holy Spirit;</i> the next five degrees belong to the <i>prophetic dream,</i> and the last four to the <i>prophetic vision.</i>",
    "fn.2341": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">דׄי קדר</span>, which are found in all the mss. of the Arabic text, have been omitted in the two Hebrew versions.",
    "fn.2342": "Voy. Juges, ch. 14, v. 6 et 19; I Samuel, ch. X, v. 6; ch. XVI, v. 13, et <i>passim.</i> The expression <span dir=\"rtl\">צלח על</span> means literally <i>to melt upon, to invade, to come upon.</i>",
    "fn.2343": "See Judges, ch. 6, v. 34; I Chronicles, ch. 12, v. 18; II Chronicles, ch. 24, v. 20.",
    "fn.2344": "See Numbers, ch. 11, v. 25 and 26; Isaiah, ch. 11, v. 2.",
    "fn.2345": "See Judges, ch. 2, v. 18; I Samuel, ch. III, v. 19; ch. XVIII, v. 12.",
    "fn.2346": "The text says: <i>and others</i> <small>NAMES</small>; as we have already observed (t. I, p. 6, note 1), the author understands by <i>name</i>, not only substantives and adjectives, but also infinitives of verbs.",
    "fn.2347": "All manuscripts of the Arabic text have the Hebrew word <span dir=\"rtl\">משיחי</span>, <i>oints</i>, i.e. <i>rois</i>; Ibn-Tibbon's version substituted the word <span dir=\"rtl\">יועצי</span>, <i>conseillers</i>, and Al-’Harîzi's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">שרי</span>, <i>princes</i>.",
    "fn.2348": "Amasa, although he was neither a judge nor a king, has been cited here because he saved <i>a great virtuous man</i>, King David.",
    "fn.2349": "Literally: <i>the boundary of men.</i>",
    "fn.2350": "See Exodus, ch. 2, v. 11 to 13.",
    "fn.2351": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">חתי אנה בעד</span>, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">חתי בעד</span>, omitting the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אנה</span>; this reading is expressed by the version of Al-’Harîzi, which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">and this power was still great in him, even at the moment of his fear and flight</span>.",
    "fn.2352": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">והגיעו</span>, which is an error; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">בהגיעו</span>, as some mss. and Ibn-Tibbon's version also have. All our Arabic mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ענד וצולה</span>.",
    "fn.2353": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">לה קדר עטׄים</span>, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">לה עטם</span>. Ibn-Tibbon translates succinctly: <span dir=\"rtl\">in the one who did good, it is very great</span>; Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">in the one who does good, there is a great excellence</span>.",
    "fn.2354": "The author uses here the Hebrew action noun <span dir=\"rtl\">הצלחה</span>, by allusion to the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">הצליח</span>, <i>prospérer,</i> of which the Scripture makes use in speaking of the services of Joseph in the house of Potiphar. See Genesis, ch. 39, <i>v.</i> 2.",
    "fn.2355": "All the Arabic mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">בתסביח</span> in the singular; Ibn-Tibbon has the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בתשבחות</span>, which, in rabbinic Hebrew, is used mostly in the plural.",
    "fn.2356": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Meghillah</i>, fol. 7 <i>a.</i> The author means that what is said explicitly about the book of Esther also applies to all other hagiographers.",
    "fn.2357": "It is known that this is the name of the sacred lot that the high priest consulted and which was placed in the <i>pectoral </i>. See, on this ancient Hebrew oracle, my History of the <i>Palestine </i>, p. 176.",
    "fn.2358": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Yôma, </i> fol. 73 <i>b: </i> <span dir=\"rtl\">Every priest who does not speak in the spirit of holiness and Shekhinah, a line is drawn over him, and one does not ask him </span>.",
    "fn.2359": "Ahia, or A’hiyya, was a contemporary prophet of Solomon. See 1 Kings, ch. 11, v. 29.",
    "fn.2360": "That is to say: they were inspired only by the <i>Holy Spirit</i> and had no true prophetic <i>inspiration</i>.",
    "fn.2361": "See above, chapter XLI, p. 318.",
    "fn.2362": "The author is referring to Abraham and Israel, or Jacob.",
    "fn.2363": "Literally: <i>solvere, dimittere sermonem</i>, that is to say, to speak in an absolute sense. Ibn-Tibbon translates the words <span dir=\"rtl\">יטלק אלקול</span> too literally as <span dir=\"rtl\">מתיר המאמר</span>, rather than saying <span dir=\"rtl\">אומר במוחלט</span>.",
    "fn.2364": "Almost all manuscripts have the name of action <span dir=\"rtl\">אגׄמאע</span>; literally, <i>the convention of the nation</i> (<i>it was</i>) <i>to arrange etc.</i> Ibn-Tibbon has the preterite <span dir=\"rtl\">הסכימה</span>. One of the Leiden manuscripts (no. 221) has: <span dir=\"rtl\">אגׄמע אלעלמא</span>, <i>the scholars have agreed</i>.",
    "fn.2365": "The Arabic mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ראי</span>, a verb that must be considered as a preterite passive and pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">رأى</span> or better <span dir=\"rtl\">ربي</span> ; that is, <i>although…. an angel had been seen, etc.</i> Ibn-Tibbon substituted the plural of the preterite active: <span dir=\"rtl\">א״ע״פ שראו</span>. Al-’Harîzi has <span dir=\"rtl\">שראה</span>, in the singular active, which is incorrect.",
    "fn.2366": "See above, chapter XLIII, p. 325.",
    "fn.2367": "See above, chapter XLIV, p. 332.",
    "fn.2368": "See above, chapter XLIV, p. 330.",
    "fn.2369": "See <i>ibid.,</i> p. 330.",
    "fn.2370": "See above, chap. XLI, p. 314 and 315, chap. XLII, p. 320, chap. XLIV, p. 330, and <i>ibid.,</i> note 1.",
    "fn.2371": "We have reproduced the reading found in almost all the mss., where these two verses are cited inaccurately; it is the author himself, it seems, who made an error of memory in writing <span dir=\"rtl\">ראיתי</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואראה</span>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">ויאמר</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואשמע את קול אדני אמר</span>.",
    "fn.2372": "See Genesis, chapter 15, verses 9 and 10.",
    "fn.2373": "Literally: <i>for these parables were in a vision</i> (<i>which took place</i>) <i>during the day.</i> The word <span dir=\"rtl\">במראה</span> is missing in some Arabic mss., as well as in the version of Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.2374": "That is to say, as the biblical text itself has declared, by stating: <i>the sun was about to set</i> (Genesis, 15, 12), whence it follows that it was still day. If, in an earlier passage (v. 5), God bidds Abraham to count the stars, one must not conclude therefrom that it was night; for one must not forget that it concerns not reality here, but a <i>vision,</i> in which it might appear to Abraham that it was night and that the stars were visible. This vision is composed of two acts, of which the second, marked by the profound slumber of the <i>seer</i>, constituts a <i>parabolic</i> vision which forms the eighth degree, and of which the first, as one shall presently see, constituts a superior degree, without parable, which is the ninth. See above, ch. XLI, p. 314, and cf. Abravanel's Commentary on Genesis, ch. 15, at the commencement.",
    "fn.2375": "See above, chapter XLII, pages 320-322.",
    "fn.2376": "Literally: <i>the highest of the degrees of the prophets, of whose books</i> (<i>holy</i>) <i>attest to the state.</i>",
    "fn.2377": "Literally: <i>after having established that which has been established concerning the perfection of the rational qualities of the individual according to what speculation results in</i>, <i>and after having decreed an exception for Moses, our master.</i> The author intends to say that this eleventh degree is the highest degree to which a prophet can attain, but that for this degree, as for all others, one must first and foremost presuppose in the person of the prophet natural dispositions cultivated by speculation and by the exercise of moral qualities, as has been established afore (see ch. XXXII, third opinion); moreover, the author adds, one must well know that by stating this to be the highest degree attained by prophets, he intends to establish an exception for Moses, who is comprised in none of the categories just enumerated and who forms a category apart (see above, ch. XXXV). For the words <span dir=\"rtl\">תקריר מא תקרר</span>, the Leyden MS., no. 221, bears in the margin the variant <span dir=\"rtl\">הקדים מא תקדם</span>; it is this latter reading which Al-’Harîzi's version appears to express, bearing <span dir=\"rtl\">אהר הצעת מה שראוי להציע</span>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ובעד אלאסתתׄנא</span>, which signify <i>and after having excepted</i> or <i>established an exception</i>, are rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version by <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחר ההתנות</span>, <i>and after having laid down a condition.</i> Al-’Harîzi renders more exactly: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחרי שנוציא מן התנאי משה רבנו</span>.",
    "fn.2378": "Literally: <i>the prophet believes that God speaks to him.</i> The author asks if one should not establish, for the <i>vision</i>, an even higher degree, which would be analogous to the seventh degree, related to the <i>dream</i>; and he responds in a negative manner.",
    "fn.2379": "All manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">קוה̈ פעל אלמתכׄילה̈</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon renders as <span dir=\"rtl\">פעל הכח המדמה</span>, and Al-’Harîzi, as <span dir=\"rtl\">יכולת הכח המחשבית</span>; there is in these versions a slight inaccuracy, and one ought to have said: <span dir=\"rtl\">כח פעל המדמה</span>. — The author intends to convey that the imaginative faculty, as it operates in <i>vision</i>, does not extend so far as to make the prophet believe that the Divinity itself addresses him, whereas in the <i>dream</i>, in which the imagination is perturbed by all manner of phantoms, the prophet may perchance believe that it is God Himself who speaks unto him.",
    "fn.2380": "Literally: <i>in other prophets.</i> The author means: in prophets other than Moses, because the latter saw God <i>face to face</i> and God spoke to him <i>mouth to mouth</i>, which means that God manifested himself clearly to his intelligence, without the intermediary of the imagination.",
    "fn.2381": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אחזאל</span> , <i>union,</i> cf. the t. I, p. 37, note 1.",
    "fn.2382": "The Arabic text says <span dir=\"rtl\">אפתעאל</span>, a term that designates the VIII<sup>e</sup> form of Arabic verbs, and that Arab rabbis improperly employ to designate the <i>hithpa’el</i> form of Hebrew verbs. See my edition of the <i>Commentary of R. Tan’houm of Jerusalem, on the book of ’Habakkouk</i> (in the t. XII of the Bible of M. S. Cahen), p. 94, note 3.",
    "fn.2383": "The author means that this verb indicates that it is a matter of intellectual perception, which, although under the action of the imaginative faculty, still retains enough clarity and is not dominated by the phantoms of a confused imagination.",
    "fn.2384": "Literally: <i>there is the audition of a discourse from God.</i> Ibn-Tibbon translated the words <span dir=\"rtl\">סמאע כלאם</span> literally by <span dir=\"rtl\">שֵׁמע דבר</span>; the editors substituted the noun <span dir=\"rtl\">שמע</span> for the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ישמע</span>.",
    "fn.2385": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">חדס</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(حَدُس)</span> denotes an <i>opinion founded upon a mere conjecture</i>, and it is with good cause that Ibn-Falaquéra has here criticised Ibn-Tibbon’s version, which bears <span dir=\"rtl\">על צד המחשבה</span>. According to Ibn-Falaquéra, one ought to translate <span dir=\"rtl\">על צד האומד</span>. Vide Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Moré,</i> p. 156, and confer above, p. 296, note 1.",
    "fn.2386": "In other words: it is upon the hypothesis that the prophet imagines it to be God Himself who addresses him. — This passage, which is somewhat obscure, particularly in the Hebrew version, has perplexed the commentators. Herein lies its manifest import: The author, having declared it improbable that the prophet, in a <i>vision</i>, could believe himself to hear the word of God, anticipates an objection which might be raised against him, founded upon certain biblical passages, where doubtless there is mention of the <i>divine word</i> addressed to the prophet in a <i>vision</i>, as for instance, Genesis, ch. 15, v. 1 and 4. He replies that, according to his purely conjectural opinion, these passages concern only the divine word addressed to the prophet through the medium of an unknown <i>voice</i>, a <i>personage</i>, or an <i>angel</i> (which constitutes the IX<sup>th</sup>, X<sup>th</sup>, and XI<sup>th</sup> degrees), whereas in the passage from Numbers, 12, 6, it concerns the immediate word of God which the prophet believes himself to hear, a phenomenon which can occur only in the dream. Nevertheless, the author subsequently continues, it were permissible likewise to assign a more general sense to the passage from Numbers; for it may be that this passage signifies that in <i>vision</i> one never hears any word whatsoever, and that one merely perceives <i>parables;</i> so that it should be admitted that, wheresoever <i>words</i> are mentioned in a <i>vision</i>, it refers to a revelation which, having commenced with a vision, concludes with a <i>dream.</i> In this case, the author concludes, there would be but eight degrees of prophecy; for the diverse degrees of vision would be reduced to a single one, namely the eighth, in which the divine intellect acts upon the prophet, in the waking state, by means of parabolic visions.",
    "fn.2387": "The author means that the literal sense (<span dir=\"rtl\">אלטׄאהר</span>) of the passage in Numbers supports the interpretation he has given, namely that one excludes from the vision only the immediate word of God that the prophet can believe he hears in the <i>songe.</i>",
    "fn.2388": "See above, chapter XLI, p. 314.",
    "fn.2389": "See <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>, sect. 17 (fol. 14, col. 4), and sect. 44 (fol. 39, col. 2 and 3).",
    "fn.2390": "Word for word: <i>or intelligible unions</i>, that is, revelations resulting from the union of the universal active intellect with the prophet's intelligence. — Ibn-Tibbon renders the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלצאלאת</span> , <i>unions</i>, with <span dir=\"rtl\">השגות</span>, <i>perceptions</i>, which is incorrect.",
    "fn.2391": "All mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">תׄמאן</span>; but, in the constructed state, it is more correct to write <span dir=\"rtl\">תׄמאני</span>.",
    "fn.2392": "That is to say: even if, in the narration of the vision, mention were made of a <i>personage</i> who had spoken to the prophet, which, according to what has been previously stated, would not yet constitute the most exalted degree of prophecy, but only the penultimate degree (the X<sup>th</sup>). The author means that, according to this latter interpretation, the vision would form but one single general degree, that of <i>parabolic revelation</i>, in which one would no longer have other gradations to establish, as has been done for the dream; for, if in the parabolic vision one sometimes speaks also of words addressed to the prophet by a personage or an angel, this can be only by a simple allegory, or to indicate that the <i>vision</i> has finally become a <i>dream.</i>",
    "fn.2393": "We consider this observation superfluous; for it seems to us that the author has already sufficiently explained himself above, in saying that it is only in the dream that the prophet can believe to hear the direct word of God. Cf. p. 344, note 3.",
    "fn.2394": "That is to say, by the direct influence of the universal active intellect, which communicated itself to the intelligence of Moses, without the imagination having any part in it.",
    "fn.2395": "See <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, or the Talmudic Abridgment, the treatise <i>Yésôdé ha-Torâ,</i> ch. VII, § 6, and cf. above, ch. XXXV (p. 277, note 2).",
    "fn.2396": "In other words: By examining a single individual of any species whatsoever, and by discerning that which constitutes its essential form or <i>quiddity</i>, one concludes, by induction, that all individuals of the same species possess the same quiddity. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">צורה̈</span> here denotes <i>a specific form</i>, and it is erroneously that Ibn-Tibbon has rendered it by <span dir=\"rtl\">תכונה</span>, <i>constitution</i>. Al-’Harîzi’s version bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויוָּדע בי זאת צורת בל איש ממנו</span>.",
    "fn.2397": "Word for word: <i>from one form among the forms of the prophets' accounts</i>. The author intends to convey, as it appears to one, that it suffices to have recognised the form of a certain part of the prophet's account to draw a conclusion concerning all parts of that account; if one part of the vision related by the prophet reveals itself as a parable, then all the remainder shall likewise possess a parabolic sense. The sense which one attributes to our passage seems to emerge from the following example of a dream in which one believes oneself to undertake a journey, and in which one beholds all manner of imaginary details. Each of the examples which the author shall presently cite from Ezekiel and other prophets is recognised as a vision by one of the expressions designating prophetic vision, and thenceforth all details reported by the prophet form part of this selfsame vision.",
    "fn.2398": "Literally: <i>and it was of his state and of his thing</i> (or <i>condition</i>) <i>in such and such a manner</i>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">כית וכית</span> are found in but one of the manuscripts which one has been able to consult, and there they were added by a more recent hand. Nevertheless, the sense of the phrase demands these words, or another similar expression. The version of Al-’Harîzi appears to express the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כית וכית</span>; it reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">והיה עניינו כך וכך</span>. Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">והיה מעניינו מה שהיה</span>, which would lead one to suppose that his Arabic exemplar had <span dir=\"rtl\">מא כאן</span>. The two translators have rendered these two words <span dir=\"rtl\">חאל</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר</span>, which are nigh synonyms, by the single word <span dir=\"rtl\">עניין</span>. Maimonides had perchance written: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכאן מן האלה ומן אמרה כדׄלך</span>; the copyists may have omitted the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כדׄלך</span>, because this word was repeated immediately after …. <span dir=\"rtl\">כדׄלך הדׄה אלאמתׄאל</span>.",
    "fn.2399": "Literally: <i>those that are seen, or that are made, in the prophetic vision, etc.</i>",
    "fn.2400": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">in that which he commands</span>, which is inaccurate. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">and all that the parable of that act imposes from deed and etc.</span>; here the Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">to impose</span> is rendered exactly by <span dir=\"rtl\">to impose</span>; but the words <span dir=\"rtl\">and all that</span> are an inaccurate translation of the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">by a thing</span>.",
    "fn.2401": "The author means that all the details recounted in a prophetic vision, as well as all the acts attributed to the prophet, are part of the vision and should not be considered as real facts.",
    "fn.2402": "That is to say: some of these details are recounted simply and in an absolute manner, without it being expressly stated that they appertain to the vision, so that the vulgar apprehend them as real facts. — Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בעצׄהא</span>, the Leyden manuscript, no. 18, has <span dir=\"rtl\">בעדה</span>, a reading of scant plausibility, but one which has been reproduced by Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובא זכרון זה בספרי הנבואה אחר זה במאמר מוחלט</span>. Instead of the adverb <span dir=\"rtl\">מטלקא</span> (manuscripts <span dir=\"rtl\">מטלק</span>), several manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">מטלקה̈</span>, an adjective which refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">בעצׄהא</span>; likewise Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">קצתם מוחלטים</span>.",
    "fn.2403": "For, as the author has stated above (chap. XLI, p. 314): “Whenever a passage (of Scripture) says of someone that an angel spake unto him, or that the word of God was addressed unto him, this could not have occurred otherwise than in a dream or in a prophetic vision.” The author here makes allusion to several passages where one finds the formula <span dir=\"rtl\">ויאמר י״י אלי</span>, <i>and the Lord said unto me,</i> and where either actions, or displacements, or conversations with God are attributed unto the prophet. See, for example: Jeremiah, chap. 1, vv. 7, 9, 12 and 14; chap. XIII, v. 6 et seq.; Ezekiel, chap. XLIV, v. 1 et seq.; Zechariah, chap. 11, v. 13 et seq.",
    "fn.2404": "That is, these are real facts, accomplished by the prophet while his senses functioned and manifested their full activity.",
    "fn.2405": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קאל</span> is a pleonasm that unnecessarily repeats the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ודׄכר</span>. Ibn-Tibbon did not translate the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קאל</span>.",
    "fn.2406": "Most of our manuscripts read: <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄחכה̈ ללספהא וסכׄריה̈</span>, and it is this reading which the version of Al-’Harîzi expresses, bearing: <span dir=\"rtl\">לעג לרקים והתול</span>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄחכּה̈</span>, <i>derision</i>, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">הָדָא</span>, <i>a target for scorn</i>, a reading which Ibn-Falaquéra also had, who does criticise Ibn-Tibbon for having placed in his version: <span dir=\"rtl\">דומים לשוטים ולשכורים</span> (see Appendix of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 156). One does not comprehend whence Ibn-Tibbon derived the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דומים</span>, <i>similar</i>. As for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ולשכורים</span>, Ibn-Falaquéra does observe that instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">סכׄריה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(سُخْرِيَّة)</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">סכׄרה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(سخرة)</span>, which signifies <i>derision</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לעג וקלם</span>), Ibn-Tibbon has read <span dir=\"rtl\">סכרה̈</span>, with a <span dir=\"rtl\">כ</span> without a point, that is to say <span dir=\"rtl\">سكرة</span>, the plural of <span dir=\"rtl\">شكر</span>, <i>a drunken man;</i> but, in this case, the construction demanded <span dir=\"rtl\">וללסכרה̈</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלסכרה̈</span>, with the article.",
    "fn.2407": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, these latter words are twice translated: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויצום לעשות מעשה השוטים ויצום לעשות מעשה השגעון</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלכׄרק</span> is considered once as a plural (<span dir=\"rtl\">خرق</span>, plural of <span dir=\"rtl\">أُخْرَقُ</span>) and once as a singular in the sense of <i>stupidity, madness;</i> the manuscripts have only the 2<sup>nd</sup> version, <span dir=\"rtl\">מעשה השגעון</span>, and likewise Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.2408": "The author doubtless refers to the last passage from Ezekiel which he has just cited (chap. V, verse 1), in which the prophet is commanded to pass a razor over his head and his beard. In so doing, Ezekiel, who was a priest (<i>ibid.</i>, chap. I, v. 3), would have twice violated the Mosaic law; for, not only is it forbidden to any Israelite to cut the corners of his hair and to shave the corners of his beard (Leviticus, XIX, 27), but this prohibition is moreover specifically reiterated for the priests (<i>ibid.</i>, XXI, 5). — All Arabic manuscripts of the <i>Guide</i>, as well as those of Ibn-Tibbon's version, read: <span dir=\"rtl\">או פאת ראש</span>, and it is erroneously that, in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version and in Al-’Harîzi's manuscript, one finds <span dir=\"rtl\">ופאת ראש</span>.",
    "fn.2409": "In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מראה נבואה</span>, one should read, according to the Arabic text, <span dir=\"rtl\">במראה הנבואה</span>.",
    "fn.2410": "See Jeremiah, chapter 13, verses 4-7.",
    "fn.2411": "Literally: <i>Toutes ces paraboles</i> (<i>furent</i>) <i>in the prophetic vision</i>.",
    "fn.2412": "Literally: <i>the matter no longer remains veiled, in such wise</i> (<i>as to lead one to suppose</i>) <i>that aught of it possessed reality</i>. This phrase refers not merely to the example drawn from Hosea, but likewise to the preceding examples. The author means to state: once it is clear that the totality of these visions bears a parabolic sense and that they do not concern real events, there remains no ground for doubt regarding any detail, nor for supposing that a single one of these particular events actually transpired; unless, the author continues, one be utterly incapable of comprehending prophetic visions, such that they should be unto one <i>a sealed book</i>, as says Isaiah.",
    "fn.2413": "Literally: <i>save when it should have been said of us;</i> that is to say, one could not fall into such like errors, unless these words of Isaiah apply to one: <i>And every vision etc.</i>—One has adopted the reading of the great majority of manuscripts, which bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא ענד מא קיל פינא</span>; Ibn-Tibbon has: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא כשנאמר בהם</span>; he has therefore read <span dir=\"rtl\">פיהם</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">פינא</span>. The Leyden manuscript, no. 18, has <span dir=\"rtl\">פיה</span> in the singular; but this reading, like that of Ibn-Tibbon, appears to one to present more difficulties than the reading here adopted. The manuscript of the Hebrew supplement to the Imperial Library, no. 63, written by the hand of R. Saadia ibn-Danan, bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא ענד מן קיל פיה</span>, <i>save for him of whom it has been said;</i> that is to say, there can be no doubt in this regard save for those of whom Isaiah spoke in the cited passage. This reading, in truth, offers a simpler sense; but it is precisely on account of this that it appears suspect to one, and that one can perceive in it naught but an arbitrary emendation of the primitive text. In the manuscript of Al-’Harîzi's version, this entire phrase is wanting.",
    "fn.2414": "See Judges, chapter 6, v. 21, 37 and following.",
    "fn.2415": "Literally: <i>and how then</i> (<i>could he have reached</i>) <i>the rank of miracles?</i>",
    "fn.2416": "The too literal version of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">שישיג בשופטי ישראל</span>, is not clear enough, and the editors have completely altered the sense, writing <span dir=\"rtl\">כשופטי</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בשופטי</span>. Al-’Harîzi has better translated: <span dir=\"rtl\">ותכלית מעלתו שהיה נמנה בשופטי ישראל</span>.",
    "fn.2417": "See Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Rôsch ha-Schanâ</i>, fol. 25 <i>a</i>, <i>b</i>, where it is stated that, in a verse of the First Book of Samuel, chap. XII, verse 11, three men <i>of the least importance</i> in the world (<span dir=\"rtl\">קלי עולם</span>), Jerubbaal (Gideon), Bedan (Samson), and Jephthah, are contrasted with the three men <i>of the greatest importance</i> in the world (<span dir=\"rtl\">חמורי עולם</span>), who are Moses, Aaron, and Samuel, mentioned together in Psalm XCIX, v. 6.",
    "fn.2418": "The author is likely alluding to what he said in the previous chapter (1<sup>st</sup> degree), about the judges of Israel, whom he places in a much lower rank than that of the true prophets.",
    "fn.2419": "See above, chapter XLI, page 317.",
    "fn.2420": "The example of Zechariah that the author is about to cite follows what the author has said about the details reported in certain visions of the prophets Ezekiel, Isaiah, Jeremiah, and Hosea; for the passage related to Gideon is only an observation that the author has intercalated here in passing, because Gideon also, in a kind of vision or dream, believed to see certain particular facts that only took place in his imagination.",
    "fn.2421": "According to the author, it is not God who is presented here under the image of the shepherd, as most commentators believe, but rather the prophet Zechariah, who, in a prophetic vision, takes the two reins and performs the symbolic actions mentioned later.",
    "fn.2422": "The editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have: <span dir=\"rtl\">שהוא עשה כן</span>; one should read, according to the manuscripts, <span dir=\"rtl\">שהוא עשה בו</span>.",
    "fn.2423": "That is to say, it forms part of the overall narrative that has a parabolic meaning.",
    "fn.2424": "Word for word: <i>que la plupart de la prophétie des prophètes</i> (<i>se fait</i>) <i>par des paraboles</i>. Ibn-Tibbon has: <span dir=\"rtl\">שרוב נבואות</span>, ‘that the majority <i>of the prophecies</i>’. Al-’Harîzi has more exactly <span dir=\"rtl\">נבואת</span> in the singular.",
    "fn.2425": "Regarding the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אגיא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">أغياء</span>, a noun of action from the IV<sup>th</sup> form of the root <span dir=\"rtl\">غزة</span>, see above, page 217, note 1. Ibn-Tibbon's version has, for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלאגיאאת</span>, the two words <span dir=\"rtl\">וההפלגות והגוזמות</span>; his Arabic manuscript had perchance additionally the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלמבאלגאת</span>, even as one reads further on: <span dir=\"rtl\">אגיא ומבאלגה̈</span>.",
    "fn.2426": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי תחריר</span> properly signify <i>in an exact manner;</i> that is to say: should one adhere to the exact sense of the terms, perceiving therein naught of the hyperbolic. In Ibn-Tibbon's version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כמשמעם מדוקדקים</span> are a double translation of the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי תחריר</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכשיובן על השיעור</span>, as though he had read in his Arabic text <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי תקדיר</span>; this reading is indeed found in one of our manuscripts, but it offers no plausible sense.",
    "fn.2427": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate ’<i>Hullîn</i>, fol. 90 <i>b;</i> tractate <i>Tamîd</i>, chap. II, § 1.",
    "fn.2428": "And, consequently, the cubit of ’Og was greater than an ordinary cubit.",
    "fn.2429": "Instead of the dual <span dir=\"rtl\">מהלי</span> , several MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">מתׁל</span> in the singular; likewise Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">כמו אורך איש</span>, <small>AS</small> <i>the length of a person etc.</i> But this is a very grave error, and the version of Ibn-Tibbon, which has <span dir=\"rtl\">כפל</span>, <i>the double</i>, does confirm the reading <span dir=\"rtl\">מהלי</span> (for which some MSS. incorrectly have <span dir=\"rtl\">מלא</span> ). The author intends to say that the text of Deuteronomy, by assigning to the bed of ’Og a length of <i>nine cubits</i>, does fix the stature of ’Og at six cubits, which is double the stature of an ordinary man. — The ancient Hebrew cubit was of approximately 525 millimetres (vide my History of <i>Palestine</i>, p. 397), and the length which the author here attributs to the ordinary stature would be 1 metre 575 millimetres; consequently, the stature of ’Og would have been 3.15 metres.",
    "fn.2430": "All the Arabic mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">enough</span>, and similarly Al-’Harîzi, <span dir=\"rtl\">or a little more</span>. Ibn-Tibbon has, by error: <span dir=\"rtl\">or a little less</span>, <i>or a little less</i>. In one ms. one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">or a little more or less</span>, <i>a little more or less</i>.",
    "fn.2431": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">indication</span>, which means here <i>exact indication, in full letters</i>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">fixation</span> (with <i>daleth</i>), <i>fixation, determination</i>. Ibn-Tibbon translated in this sense: <span dir=\"rtl\">the measure of the days of those people</span>; Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">the interest of the length of the days of the ancient people</span>.",
    "fn.2432": "Literally: <i>or by the voice of the miracle and proceeding in the manner of this one.</i>",
    "fn.2433": "The author means that the Chaldean paraphrase of Jonathan, in the passage of Isaiah, is similar to that given by Onkelos in the passage of Deuteronomy (XXXII, 14): <span dir=\"rtl\">יהב להון בזת מלכיהון ושליטיהון וכו׳</span>, <i>He gave them the spoil of their kings and their rulers, etc.</i>",
    "fn.2434": "Literally: <i>by this consideration also, you will be saved from the imagination of a being that God has not produced;</i> that is to say: by penetrating well into all that has been said, you will no longer be exposed to imagining the existence of these extraordinary beings that have never existed in the real world.",
    "fn.2435": "See the I<sup>st</sup> part, chap LV, and <i>passim.</i>",
    "fn.2436": "This sentence is elliptical; its import is as follows: if one were unwilling to admit that which I have stated, then one would possess no other means, for escaping those false notions of which I have just spoken, than to believe that the words of the prophets are mendacious.—In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">או יחשוב</span> (or <span dir=\"rtl\">יחָשב</span>), one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">או החשוב</span>, as has Al-’Harîzi; all the Arabic MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">תטׄן</span>, in the second person.",
    "fn.2437": "Ibn-Tibbon translated <span dir=\"rtl\">העלם</span>, which is incorrect. Al-’Harîzi translated more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">כשתתרשל במה שעוררנוך</span>.",
    "fn.2438": "Literally: <i>the will of God and His choice</i> or <i>free will</i>. Cf. the First <sup>part</sup>, chapter LXIX.",
    "fn.2439": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי אקאויל</span> are wanting in some MSS., where one does read <span dir=\"rtl\">תחדׄף אלאנביא</span>. According to this reading, <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאנביא</span> would be the subject of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תחדׄף</span>, which would be in the active voice <span dir=\"rtl\">(تُحْذَفُ)</span> , so that one would needs translate: <i>the prophets do sometimes omit;</i> but this would not accord with the two following passive verbs: <span dir=\"rtl\">بسم</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">ויקאל</span>. Nevertheless, this reading has been adopted by Al-’Harîzi and by Ibn-Falaquéra; the former does translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">כן ידחו הנביאים לפעמים הסבות ההם האמצעיות ומיחסים זה הפעל לבורא ויאמרו וגו׳</span>; the latter (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 117): <span dir=\"rtl\">ומפני זה יחסרו הנביאים אותן הסבות האמצעיות וייוחס לאלוה ית׳ אותו הפעל האישי ויאָמר וכו׳</span>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon does express the words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי אקאויל</span>; but the construction of the sentence is therein somewhat modified.",
    "fn.2440": "By <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמחקקין</span>, <i>those who establish the truth</i>, the author appears to designate here the true philosophers, in opposition to the <i>Motécallemîn,</i> who see in God the <i>immediate cause</i> of each particular fact.",
    "fn.2441": "On the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">אהל שריעה̈</span>, cf. the t. I, p. 68, note 3.",
    "fn.2442": "The author has here in view the exposition of <i>causes</i>, given by Aristotle, <i>Phys.,</i> bk. II, chaps. 3-6. By <span dir=\"rtl\">ذاتية</span> , the author understands that which is cause in itself (<i>ϰαθ’ ἁυτό</i>), or <i>essentially</i> and by its nature; by <span dir=\"rtl\">اختيارية</span> , <i>arbitrary</i>, he understands those causes residing in a moral <i>choice</i> (<i>προαιρεσις</i>), in a deliberate intention (<i>διάνοια</i>); the word <span dir=\"rtl\">عرضية</span> designats <i>accidental</i> causes (<i>ϰατὰ συμβεβηϰός</i>), and the word <span dir=\"rtl\">اتفاقية</span> , causes due to chance (<i>ἀπὸ τύχης</i>).Cf. <i>Metaphysics,</i> bk. V, chap. 30; bk. XI, chap. 8. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אחפאקיה</span> is not rendered in the version of Ibn-Tibbon; Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>l. c.</i>) does translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">או מקריות הזדמניות</span>.",
    "fn.2443": "The author appears to allude to that which Aristotle does term <i>τὸ αὐτόματον</i>, <i>that which comes to pass of itself, the spontaneous</i>, and which he seems to reduce to the acts of animals and the phenomena of inanimate things, which are not the product of a will endowed with free agency: <i>τὸ δ’ αὐτόματον ϰαί τοῖς ἂλλοις ζώοις ϰαί πολλοῖς τῶν ἀψύχων, ϰ. τ. λ. Phys.</i>, bk. II, chap. 6. Elsewhere Aristotle does state that one may attribute <i>spontaneity</i> to children and animals, but not <i>moral choice</i> or <i>intention:</i> <i>τοῦ μὲν γὰρ ἑϰουσίου ϰαί παῖδες ϰαί τἂλλα ζῷα ϰοινωνεῖ, προαιρέσεως δ’οὔ</i>. <i>Nicomachean Ethics,</i> bk. III, chap. 4.",
    "fn.2444": "Ibn-Tibbon has two verbs here: <span dir=\"rtl\">כפי מה שהונח והתישב</span>, and similarly Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>l. c.</i>); the Arabic mss. have only the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קדר</span>.",
    "fn.2445": "The author, after having spoken of God's intervention in the will of the irrational animal, in free will, and in the course of natural things or in the <i>essential causes</i>, adds this parenthesis to make it understood that it necessarily follows from what has just been stated that chance also is a cause which must be referred to God; for chance (<i>τὑχη</i>), according to Aristotle's definition, without being itself the end, is always in relation to an end of nature or to the intention and free choice (<i>προαίρεσις</i>) of a rational being (cf. <i>Phys.</i>, bk. II, chap. 5: <i>ἒστι δ’ἓνεϰά του ὃσα τε ἀπὸ διανοίας ἂν πραχθείη ϰαί ὃσα ἀπὸ φύσεως · τὰ δὴ τοιαῦτα ὃταν ϰατὰ συμβεβηϰὸς γένηται, ἀπὸ τύχης φάμεν εἶναι, ϰ. τ. λ</i>. Cf. <i>Metaphys.</i>, bk. XI, chap. 8, towards the end). One may therefore say that chance is an excess, or an accessory, of the end aimed at either by nature or by the intention of a rational being (free will); most often, chance partakes of this end, as it may also partake of the animal will, this will being capable of accidentally becoming the cause of an effect which it would not have had as its purpose.",
    "fn.2446": "Literally: <i>as of the war of a people who dominate over one </i> (<i>another</i>) <i>people</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">סלטוא</span>, one of our mss. has <span dir=\"rtl\">טׄלטוא</span>.",
    "fn.2447": "This is incorrect, as Ibn-Caspi observes in his commentary ’<i>Ammoudé Keseph;</i> for the words cited apply to the army of the Medes and the Persians, called to destroy the Babylonian empire.",
    "fn.2448": "Ibn-Tibbon's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">when he had entangled his name with his livelihood</span>, <i>when God caused him to have his sustenance;</i> one sees that Ibn-Tibbon did not have, in his Arabic text, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">a woman</span>, <i>and instead of </i>eight<span dir=\"rtl\"> , he pronounced </span>his superiority<span dir=\"rtl\"> .",
    "fn.2449": "It would seem that this passage would be better placed among the examples of <i>chance;</i> for the outcome of Joseph's sale was altogether different from that which his brothers had intended to obtain; and indeed, the author cites further on, among the examples of <i>chance</i>, verse 7, which relates to the same fact. Some commentators have endeavoured to explain this species of contradiction by drawing a subtle distinction between verse 7 and verse 8 (see the commentaries of Ibn-Caspi and Ephodi). It appears that the author wishes to convey that Joseph, after having attributed to God, in verse 7, the accidental result of the act emanating from his brothers' free will, retracts in verse 8, stating that a result of such high importance could not be purely <i>accidental,</i> and that it was necessarily God Himself who guided the free will of the sons of Jacob, so as to cause them to accomplish, unknowingly, a great act which was within the plan of His divine providence. Cf. Abravanel's commentary on Genesis, chap. 45, on the two verses in question.",
    "fn.2450": "In some MSS., this phrase presents slight variants which render it rather obscure: <span dir=\"rtl\">באלכן מעני דלך קיאם בין עלי מא שאבה הדה</span>  <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאיצ'אפה מן איצ'א אלקול פקט</span> . Al-Ḥarîzi adopted this rendition, which he has rendered somewhat unintelligibly: <span dir=\"rtl\">אך ענין זה הסברא גלויה לו לזה הבנין מן המאמר בלבד</span> (read <span dir=\"rtl\">על מה שיזדמן</span> (?<span dir=\"rtl\">שידמה</span>. Some MSS. of Ibn-Tibbon's version reproduce the same reading: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא ענינו היקש מבואר על מה שדומה לזאת המדה עוד מן האמירה לבד</span>. Other MSS. of this version, as well as the editions, confirm the reading one has adopted; but it is necessary to efface, in the editions, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הבאים</span>, which is superfluous and not found in the MSS.",
    "fn.2451": "The mission of Eliezer had the purpose of going to find a woman for Isaac in the native land of Abraham, without it being directly a question of Rebecca; it was by chance that this one presented herself first to the choice of Eliezer.",
    "fn.2452": "Here again, we attribute to God a chain of fortuitous circumstances, which necessitated David's departure.",
    "fn.2453": "That is to say: causes <i>natural</i>, proceeding from the laws of nature.",
    "fn.2454": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon carries: <span dir=\"rtl\">והנהיגהו</span>; that of Al-’Harîzi has: <span dir=\"rtl\">והבינהו</span>, which is preferable; cf. above, p. 250, note 3. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ותדברה</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ותדׄכרה</span>, <i>and remember.</i>",
    "fn.2455": "Word for word: <i>and the reality of the thing will be revealed to you in such a passage as might suggest a deviation from the truth.</i>.",
    "fn.2456": "Literally: <i>this is the term of that to which the discourse has led me</i> or <i>brought me</i>. —Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אנתהי בי אלקול</span>, some MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אנתהי באלקול</span>; some others have <span dir=\"rtl\">בה</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בי</span>. —In several editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version one reads, by a typographical error: <span dir=\"rtl\">אל המאמר או</span>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">או</span>, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">בן</span>, as in the <i>princeps</i> edition.",
    "fn.2457": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי הדׄה אלמקאלה̈</span>, <i>in this treatise</i>, which are found in all Arabic MSS., have not been rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version. The author perhaps wished to imply by these words that he purposed to return to this subject elsewhere; one knows indeed that he had commenced the composition of a particular work <i>On Prophecy</i>. See Vol. I of that work, p. 15.",
    "fn.2458": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מקדמה̈</span> cannot be translated here as either <i>Introduction</i> or <i>Preface</i>; for the passage that follows does not serve as an Introduction to this third part of the <i>Guide</i>, but contains only some observations that the author deemed necessary to precede the seven first chapters, containing the explanation of the <i>Ma’asé mercabâ</i> or the vision of Ezekiel.",
    "fn.2459": "Cf. t. I, p. 9, note 2, and t. II, p. 50.",
    "fn.2460": "That is to say: addressing intelligent readers for whom I composed this work and who will be able to understand the half-explanations. The version of Ibn-Tibbon reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">כפי מה שחובר לו זה המאמר</span>, which is not very clear. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מה</span>, one should write <span dir=\"rtl\">מי</span>, as one finds in some manuscripts.",
    "fn.2461": "See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Pesaḥim</i>, fol. 119 <i>a</i>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולמכםה עתיק</span> probably signify: <i>and in order to possess luxurious garments;</i> the Talmud, as one has just seen, diverts these words from their natural sense, in order to perceive therein an allusion to those who veil the profound mysteries of the <i>Maʿaseh Merkabah</i>, revealed by God to His chosen ones. The words <i>the Ancient of Days</i> designate <i>God</i>, by allusion to the words of Daniel, chap. 8, verses 9 and 13. —Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">שגלן</span>, <i>which He revealed</i>, our editions of the Talmud bear <span dir=\"rtl\">שכםה</span>, <i>which He covered;</i> this variant alters nothing of the substance of the interpretation and the use Maïmonides makes of it.",
    "fn.2462": "Some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">בייא</span> , in the first person; similarly Al-’Harîzi and the mss. of the version of Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">בארנו</span>. It would be necessary to translate from this: <i>We have already explained</i>.",
    "fn.2463": "According to the Ibn-Tibbon version, one should translate: 'Even what is understood by him whom God has endowed with intelligence.' Al-’Harîzi translates more literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">אפילו השיעור אשר התבאר ממנו למי שנפתח לו השער מדעתו</span>.",
    "fn.2464": "See what the author has said on this subject in the first part of this work, chapter <small>XXXIV</small> (t. I, p. 127 and following).",
    "fn.2465": "Word for word: <i>ne little, nor much</i>.",
    "fn.2466": "Literally: <i>for it had not ceased to be transmitted from one chief</i> (<i>of a school</i>) <i>to another</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">צדר</span> here bears the meaning of <i>principal, chief</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's version, <span dir=\"rtl\">לא נעדר מקבל מפי מקבל</span>, is at once obscure and incomplete. Al-’Harîzi, taking the word <span dir=\"rtl\">צדר</span> here in the sense of <i>breast</i>, <i>heart</i>, translates thus: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי מאז ועד עתה לא חדל להיות מקובל מפה אל פה וחקוק מלב אל לב</span>.",
    "fn.2467": "The author here evidently distinguishes two parts of the <i>Ma’asé mercabâ</i>, one of which he understood <i>approximately</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">עםאה</span>) and the other of which appeared to him of indubitable clarity. This distinction is not sufficiently apparent from Ibn-Tibbon's version, which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">להעירך על מה שאפשר שנודע לי והתבאר אלי בלי םפק במה שהבינותיו מזה</span>. Al-’Harîzi has well rendered all the verbs of this phrase; but the nuance so essential of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עםאה</span>, <i>approximately</i>, has vanished in his version, which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">להעיר אותך על מה שנראה לי והתבאר לי ונודע לי בלי םפק ממה שהבינותי ממנו</span>.",
    "fn.2468": "Ibn-Tibbon adds, for clarity, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">המוריש</span>; he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">או קנאת המוריש ביורש על ירושתו</span>, <i>or jealousy of the testator against the heir for his inheritance</i>.",
    "fn.2469": "Literally: <i>joint to what is required by opinion;</i> that is to say, without mentioning the reserve imposed upon me by my own judgment.",
    "fn.2470": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">דׄא חדם ותכׄמין</span> are rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version by <span dir=\"rtl\">בעל םברא</span>; Ibn-Falaquéra observes that it would be more exact to translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">בעל עומד ומשער</span>. See the Appendix of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 156, the note pertaining to chap. <small>XLV</small> of the II<sup>nd</sup> part; and cf. volume II, p. 296, note 1, and p. 345, note 6.",
    "fn.2471": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תלקנת</span>, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">تَلَقَّيْتَ</span> , which has almost the same meaning.",
    "fn.2472": "That is to say: this important subject is as the great stake of a tent from which all the hanging is suspended, or as a column which supports the entire roof of an edifice. — In the Arabic text, the words <i>a stake etc.</i> are written in Hebrew, which might lead one to believe they are drawn from a Talmudic passage relating to the <i>Ma’asé mercabâ;</i> but one has sought in vain for such a passage, and it must be supposed that the author has simply employed here a rabbinic locution much used in his time and which served him to characterise the high importance of Ezekiel's vision. This is also the opinion of M. le grand rabbin Klein, whom one consulted in this regard and who indicated to one a passage from the Jerusalem Talmud, treatise <i>Berakhôth</i>, chap. IV (fol. 19 <i>a</i>), where, concerning the elevation of R. Éliézer ben-Azaria to the dignity of <i>nâsi</i>, it is stated: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשרי אדם שיש לו יתד להתלות בה</span>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ועמוד שהכל נשען עליו</span> are analogous to these words from the Book of Judges (16, 26): <span dir=\"rtl\">העמדים אשר הבית נכון עליהם</span>.",
    "fn.2474": "It is thus, as Abravanel observes (<i>l. c.</i>, fol. 53 <i>b</i>), that Judah is called a <i>young lion</i> (Genesis, 49, 9), and Joseph a <i>bull</i> (Deuteronomy, XXXIII, 17; Genesis, 49, 6).",
    "fn.2475": "The Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אבהמת</span> is inaccurately rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version as <span dir=\"rtl\">שלא נזכרו</span>, <i>which had not been mentioned</i>. Ibn-Falaquéra correctly observes that one should say: <span dir=\"rtl\">דברים שנעלמו</span>. See the Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 156.",
    "fn.2476": "Id est: each of the <i>cherubim</i>, for the beings who in the first vision are designated <i>‘hayyoth</i> (animals) are termed <i>cherubim</i> in the second vision. Nevertheless, it is evidently in error that Maimonides referrs verse 14 of Chap. X to the <i>cherubim</i>, or, which is the selfsame, to the <i>‘hayyoth;</i> for it appears from the whole discourse that in the verse in question the four faces are ascribed to the <i>wheels</i> (<i>ophannim</i>), and not to the <i>cherubim</i>. See Abravanel’s commentary on Ezekiel, Chap. I, <i>v.</i> 5, and Chap. X, <i>v.</i> 14.",
    "fn.2477": "The author appears to be alluding to a passage in the Talmud, tractate <i>’Haghigâ</i>, fol. 13 <i>b</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">What does </span>Keroub<i> mean?</i> Rabbi Abahou says <i>like a child</i> (<small>KE-RABYA</small>), because in Babylon they call the child <small>RABYA</small>.",
    "fn.2478": "That is: since the first two are human faces, the last two are also.",
    "fn.2479": "All manuscripts bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואנמא חדׄף פני שור</span>; the author speaks of the omission, made in the second vision, of the <i>ox-face</i> and its replacement by the <i>cherub-face</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">חדׄף</span>, Ibn-Tibbon appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">חרף</span>, for he renders (manuscripts and editio princeps): <span dir=\"rtl\">ואמנס מלת פני שור</span>; the editors, having found this version somewhat obscure, have added before <span dir=\"rtl\">מלת</span> the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר</span>. Yet the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">חרף</span> is employed solely in the sense of a letter or particle, and could not have been used herein to designate the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">פני שור</span>. Al-Ḥarīzī renders more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואולם עזב לזכור פני שור</span>.",
    "fn.2480": "See Vol. II, Chap. <small>XLIII</small>, p. 327, where the author states that prophets, in naming an object that has appeared to them in a vision, do sometimes allude to another object bearing an analogous name. The author does not specify of what etymology he wishes to speak here. Perchance he means that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">שור</span> (ox) has been replaced by <i>cherub</i>, signifying a <i>young man</i>, to indicate the notion of <i>contemplation</i> contained within the Hebrew root <span dir=\"rtl\">שור</span>, which can be applied only to a human being. Cf. Ephodi’s commentary.",
    "fn.2481": "Literally: <i>this by which we began the explanation</i>; that is, what we deemed necessary to explain beforehand in order to enter into the details of Ezekiel's vision. Ibn-Tibbon's version, <span dir=\"rtl\">מה שהחלונו לבארן</span>, is not accurate; one should translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">מה שהחלונו בביאורו</span>.",
    "fn.2482": "The four <i>‘hayyoth</i> represent, according to Maimonides, the four spheres, as one has stated afore, p. 7, note 1. One has observed the significance of their <i>human figure;</i> the symbol of the <i>four wings</i> has been elucidated elsewhere, and it has been seen that, according to our author, they represent the four causes of the spheres’ motion. See Vol. I, p. 179, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2; Vol. II, p. 88, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1.",
    "fn.2483": "The <i>two hands</i> represent the <i>double action</i> of the forces emanating from the celestial spheres and of which the author has spoken in Chap. X of Part <sup>II</sup> (Vol. II, p. 89); this double action consists in giving birth to all things, and in preserving that which is born. In the passage one has just indicated, the author likewise compars this action to that of art or of man’s artistic faculty <span dir=\"rtl\">(أغنية)</span>.",
    "fn.2484": "The feet or legs without joints indicate, according to the author, the perpetual motion of the spheres, for he who wishes to sit down to rest from his walk needs joints.",
    "fn.2485": "See <i>Beréschîth rabbâ</i>. section 65 (folio 58, column 3): <span dir=\"rtl\">It is said by Rabbi Shmuel: There is no ascension to above, as it is said, and their feet are straight, they have no leaps</span>.",
    "fn.2486": "The author sees in these words an allusion to the circular movement of the spheres; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עגל</span>, <i>veau</i>, recalls the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עגול</span>, <i>round</i>. Cf. t. II, at the end of the chapters <small>XXIX</small> and <small>XLIII</small>.",
    "fn.2487": "Most mss. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">בינהמא</span>, in the duel, which means: between the <i>’hayyôth</i>, taken two by two.",
    "fn.2488": "One must recall what the author says elsewhere about the celestial spheres, which are closely fitted into one another without leaving any void. See t. I, ch. <small>LXXII</small>, p. 357.",
    "fn.2489": "All manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">מע כונהם מתלאזקין</span>, which is incorrect, for grammatical rules demanded <span dir=\"rtl\">מע כונהא מתלאזקה̈</span>. One believs that one must pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">מחלאוקין</span>, in the masculine dual, and that the author, forgetting the <i>‘hayyoth</i>, had in mind the spheres which they represent, for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">فلك</span>, <i>sphere</i>, is of the masculine gender; he here considers the spheres two by two, attached one to another. One has therefore deemed it proper, to obtain an entirely correct reading, to write <span dir=\"rtl\">כונהמא</span>, with the dual suffix, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">כונהם</span> which the manuscripts present.",
    "fn.2490": "One has already seen that the faces and the wings represent, according to the author, the causes of the movement of the spheres. Among these causes, three are inherent in each sphere, namely: its sphericity, its soul, and its intellect; the fourth is the superior Intelligence, the object of desire of each sphere. This fourth cause is an Intelligence <i>separate</i> from the sphere, and the various separate Intelligences are likewise distinct among themselves, in that they are the causes and effects of one another. (Vide Vol. II, Introduction, sixteenth proposition, and <i>ibid.</i>, p. 31, note 2.) Wherefore the prophet says that the faces and the wings were separate <i>from above</i>, which signifies that the superior causes of movement are separate from their respective spheres and distinct among themselves. Such appears to be the meaning of the obscure words of Maimonides; but he affords us no indication as to the manner in which he interprets the remainder of verse 11: <span dir=\"rtl\">לאיש שתים חוברות איש ושתים מכסות את גויתיהנה</span>. Perhaps one ought, according to Maimonides, to translate these words thus: <i>two</i> (<i>wings</i>) <i>were attached to each</i> (<i>of the ’hayyôth</i>) <i>and two covered their bodies</i>, that is to say, two of the motive causes, namely sphericity and the soul, are inherent in each sphere, whilst the two others, as it were, hover above it; for the separate Intelligence, the object of the sphere's desire, is found outside of it, and the intellect which conceivs this desired object is in an intimate relation with it.",
    "fn.2491": "This last phrase, found in several Arabic manuscripts, was omitted in both Hebrew versions as unnecessary repetition.",
    "fn.2492": "The author is alluding to the spheres themselves and to the stars they contain; the former have a pure and transparent matter, but which does not give light; the latter are luminous without being transparent. See t. II, chap. <small>XIX</small>, p. 159-160.",
    "fn.2493": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄוהרהא</span>, Ibn-Tibbon appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">ווגׄוההא</span>, because his version has <span dir=\"rtl\">ופניהם</span>, <i>of their faces;</i> Al-’Harîzi has <span dir=\"rtl\">ועצמם</span>, <i>of their substance</i>.",
    "fn.2494": "See t. I, chap. <small>LXXII</small>, p. 357, where the author says of the spheres: 'They all have an equal circular motion, and in none of them is there either precipitation or retardation; I mean that none of these spheres moves sometimes quickly and sometimes slowly, but that each, for its speed and manner of motion, remains subject to its natural law.'",
    "fn.2495": "The author alludes to the desire of each sphere to approach its proximate cause, or the separate Intelligence that presides over it, and determines its movement.",
    "fn.2496": "The author intends to indicate, so it seems to one, that some doubts may yet remain regarding the sense he attributs to the word <i>face</i>, and that it is not indubitably proven that it concerns the desire which draws each sphere towards its separate Intelligence. Indeed, the word <i>face</i> is equivocal, inasmuch as the prophet attributs to each <i>’hayyâ</i> four faces. According to Shem-Tob, the author does here allude to the objection he has made against the philosophers, in Chap. <small>XIX</small> of the Second Part (p. 162), by stating that one cannot attribute a spatial position to the separate Intelligences and that one cannot say they are in the East or in the West.",
    "fn.2497": "In any case, says the author, it is certain that the movement of the spheres is here in question, which is effected in diverse senses, for some direct themselves from West to East and others from East to West. Vide Vol. I, p. 357, and Vol. II, p. 151 and p. 158. The author cannot here have intended to allude to the superior sphere, denominated the <i>diurnal sphere</i>, and which movs from East to West; for, according to him, the diurnal sphere is without stars and counts not among the four spheres represented by the four <i>’hayyôth</i>. These latter, as one has already seen, p. 7, note 1, are: the sphere of the moon, that of the sun, that which embraces the spheres of the five planets, and finally that of the fixed stars. The proper movement of all these spheres takes place from West to East; if therefore the author does here allude to an opposing movement from East to West, he can only have had in view the spheres of the nodes of the planets, as one has observed in Chap. <small>XIX</small> of the Second Part, p. 158, note 2.",
    "fn.2498": "One believs that the author does here again speak of the two opposing movements of the spheres, namely their periodic movement from West to East and the diurnal movement from East to West which is imparted to them by the superior sphere, or the diurnal sphere. Vide Vol. II, p. 151, note 3. — Moses of Narbonne and Shem-Tob, deceived by Ibn-Tibbon's translation, which bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">והן חוזרות חלילה</span>, <i>and they turned circularly</i>, have supposed that it does here concern the <i>circular</i> movement, by which the spheres always return to their point of departure; but were such the sense, the comparison with the movement of lightning would be ill-chosen. The Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">והי ראגׄעה̈ עלי אעקאבהא</span> signify: <i>and they returned upon their footsteps</i>, and it is in this sense that Al-Ḥarīzī does translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">והם חוזרות על עקבותיהם במרוצה</span>.",
    "fn.2499": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בריא חדא</span>, our editions of the Targum bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">בריָתא כחדא</span>, so that one should translate: <i>and the creatures</i> (the <i>’hayyôth</i>) <i>returned together, etc</i>. Some manuscripts of the Targum have <span dir=\"rtl\">בריְתא חדא</span>, and others <span dir=\"rtl\">ברית חדא</span>; in Abravanel’s commentary upon this verse, the citation from the Targum is in conformity with that of Maimonides. The editions of the Targum furthermore add after <span dir=\"rtl\">ומקפן</span> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ומכסן</span>, which is not found in the manuscripts.",
    "fn.2500": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ליסת חרכה̈</span>, the Leyden MS., No. 18, exhibits <span dir=\"rtl\">ליסת תחרכהא</span> (that is to say: <span dir=\"rtl\">تحرّكها</span>), and this, perhaps, was the reading adopted by Ibn-Tibbon, who renders it <span dir=\"rtl\">אין תנועתה מעצמה</span>. Other MSS. read <span dir=\"rtl\">ליסת תחרכה</span>, which is still less correct. The version by Al-Ḥarîzî, <span dir=\"rtl\">איננה תנועה מעצמה</span>, conforms to the reading one has adopted.",
    "fn.2501": "Literally: <i>that to the side towards which it was the divine intention for the ḥayyā to move, towards that same side it accomplished etc.</i> The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלי תלך אלגׄהה̈</span>, <i>towards that same side</i>, are not rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version. That by Al-Ḥarîzî reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">אל הפאה ההיא התנועע בתנועה קלה שהיא רצוא ושוב</span>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">התנועע</span>, which appears here in one's editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version and that by Al-Ḥarîzî, one should write <span dir=\"rtl\">תתנועע</span>, as indeed it is found in the MSS. of Ibn-Tibbon and in the editio princeps.",
    "fn.2502": "See vol. I, chap. XL, p. 144.",
    "fn.2503": "Literally: <i>He says then: the side towards which it was the divine intention for the ḥayyā to move, towards that same side the ḥayyā ran.</i> The author would have expressed himself in a manner at once simpler and more correct, had he, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">פפי תלך אלגׄהה̈ תגׄרי אלחיה אליהא</span>, said <span dir=\"rtl\">פאלי תלך אלגׄהה̈ תגׄרי אלחיה</span>. Ibn-Tibbon has reproduced this species of pleonasm, by saying: <span dir=\"rtl\">בצד ההוא תרוץ החיה אליו</span>. Al-Ḥarîzî has: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי הפיאה אשר ירצה החפץ האלוהי ללכת החיה אליה שם הוא חפץ החיה ללכת</span>. — According to the commentators, the author here refers to <i>desire</i>, which attracts each sphere towards its respective Intelligence, the proximate cause of its motion. It is also in this sense that Abravanel understands the words of Maimonides, and he objects that a separated Intelligence has no determined place, so that one might say it causes the spheres to move towards it. Vide Abravanel, Commentary on Ezekiel, chap. I, <i>v</i>. 12: <span dir=\"rtl\">וגם אמרו אל אשר יהיה שמה הרוח ללכת ילכו שהרב מפרש אותו על הגלגל שיתנועע לצד מניעו יקשה לו מאד שהנבדל אין לו מקום מיוחד ויחסו לכל המקומות הוא אחד ולמה אם כן התחלפו תנועות הגלגלים</span>. But this is precisely the objection that Maimonides himself made to the hypothesis of the philosophers, in order to demonstrate that the diverse movements of the celestial spheres are determined only by the free will of the Creator acting with intention. Vide Vol. II, chap. <small>XIX</small>, p. 162. Evidently, it is this same divine intention that, according to Maimonides, Ezekiel would here have designated by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">רוח</span>, <i>spirit, intention</i>.",
    "fn.2504": "More literally: <i>But as the words etc. might have this literal meaning, that at one time God will, in the future, desire that it should move towards a certain side, and the ḥayyā will go to that side, and at another time He will desire that it should move towards another side, opposed to the former, and it will move…</i> At the commencement of this phrase, Ibn-Tibbon has somewhat modified the construction. Al-Ḥarîzî translates literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומפני שהיה מה שאמר אל אשר יהיה שמה הרוח ללכת ילכו שילכו לפאה אחת</span> (<i>read</i> <span dir=\"ltr\">בעתיד</span>) <span dir=\"rtl\">יורה פשט זה המאמר כי פעם ירצה בעת ותלך החיה לפאה ההיא וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.2505": "The author is referring to the sublunar material or the elementary body that forms the four elements, each of which has a <i>specific</i> particular form. Cf. t. I, chap. <small>LXXII</small>, p. 358-359; t. II, chap. <small>XIX</small>, p. 148, and <i>ibid</i>., notes 1 and 2.",
    "fn.2506": "For the elements are inert bodies that have no movement of their own, like animals, and are moved by the celestial sphere. See below, p. 21, note 4.",
    "fn.2507": "See Ezekiel, chapter I, <i>v</i>. 18; the author will soon say what he means by the <i>eyes</i>.",
    "fn.2508": "Name of a precious stone by which, according to the author, Ezekiel indicates the raw material. See further on, chap. IV, towards the end.",
    "fn.2509": "That is to say, that the four faces attributed to the sublunar prime matter represent the four elements. If he calls them <small>OPHANNÎM</small>, or <i>wheels</i>, one must recall that the elements likewise form spheres placed one within another. Vide Vol. I, chap. <small>XXXVI</small>, p. 134, note 2, and infra, chap. III, where the author explains this verse: <i>the</i> <small>OPHANNÎM</small>, <i>I heard them called</i> <small>GALGAL</small> (Ezekiel, X, 13).",
    "fn.2510": "That is, the bodily form common to the four elements.",
    "fn.2511": "The author alludes to the mixture of elements, which, as he says elsewhere, act on one another and receive impressions from one another. See t. I, chap. <small>LXXII</small>, p. 360.",
    "fn.2512": "See above, p. 12, note 1. — The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מורכב</span> and a little later <span dir=\"rtl\">שקצתם מחובר בקצתם</span>, which gives a contradiction. One must put <span dir=\"rtl\">מחובר</span> in the first place and <span dir=\"rtl\">מורכב</span> in the second, as the mss. indeed have.",
    "fn.2513": "In the Arabic phrase, the suffix of <span dir=\"rtl\">אנהא</span> can refer only to the <i>ophannîm</i>, and Ibn-Tibbon's version <span dir=\"rtl\">שהוא מלא עינים</span> is inexact. That by Al-Ḥarîzî reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי הם מלאות עינים</span>. As for the meaning, the commentators are of opinion that Maimonides alludes to the numerous living creatures composed of the elements, and which all possess eyes. Indeed, it were difficult to discover another meaning in the words of Maimonides, and it is with reason that Abravanel raises an objection against so improbable an interpretation of the prophet's words (Commentary on Ezekiel, I, 18):",
    "fn.2514": "That is to say, that the elements contain a multitude of <i>images</i> of all manner of creatures. The commentators, misled by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דמיונות</span> which Ibn-Tibbon's version here employs, have explained it in the sense of <i>imagination</i>, 'for (says Shem-Ṭob) from the elements are born animals which possess <i>imagination</i>' <span dir=\"rtl\">כי מהיסודות יתחדשו בעלי חיים שיש להם דמיון</span>. This explanation is not only too recondite, but it can in no wise apply to the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אמתׄלה̈</span> (plural of <span dir=\"rtl\">מתׄאל</span>), which never bears the meaning of <i>imagination;</i> and moreover, it does not accord with the example that Maimonides is about to cite from the Talmud.",
    "fn.2515": "Literally: <i>as one finds that the ancients of the language express themselves</i>. Ibn-Tibbon has simply: <span dir=\"rtl\">קדמונינו</span>; Al-’Harîzi does translate more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">כמו שיאמרו זקני הלשון</span>. — The expressions here cited by Maimonides are found in the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Baba Kamma</i>, fols. 65 and 66, in which the restitution of stolen articles is discussed, of which the <i>principal</i> must be restored <i>similar to that which was stolen</i>, that is to say, according to the value it held at the moment of the theft; but the fine, that is, the double (Exodus, 22, 3, 6), the quadruple, or the quintuple (<i>Ibid</i>., XXI, 37), is paid according to the value the stolen articles possess at the moment of judgment.",
    "fn.2516": "That is, the word <i>yeux</i> has here the sense of circumstance or quality, for the elements contain many things of different qualities.",
    "fn.2517": "This passage is drawn from the Second Book of Samuel, chap. <small>XVI</small>, verse 12, where the <i>kethib</i> bears <span dir=\"rtl\">בעוני</span> (for <span dir=\"rtl\">בְעָנְיִי</span>), <i>my affliction</i>, and the <i>keri</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">בעיני</span>, <i>mine eye</i>, which Maimonides does explain by <i>my state</i>. Cf. the commentary of David Kim’hi upon this verse:",
    "fn.2518": "The author is alluding to the movements that elements make to return to their natural place, when any cause has removed them from it; this movement is made in a straight line. See t. I, chap. <small>LXXII</small>, p. 358-359.",
    "fn.2519": "That is, the <i>ophannîm</i> or the four elements were each directed towards their natural place, the earth towards the center of the world, fire towards the circumference, water and air towards intermediate places between the center and the circumference.",
    "fn.2520": "The particle <span dir=\"rtl\">but </span>, <i>however, to the contrary </i>, was not rendered by Ibn-Tibbon, who simply said: <span dir=\"rtl\">and there is no movement for them, etc. </span>.",
    "fn.2521": "It is necessary to recall what the author says elsewhere in describing the elements (vol. I, p. 358): “These are inanimate bodies which possess neither life nor perception, and which do not move by themselves, but remain at rest in their natural places.” The motion which the elements make to depart from their natural place is never spontaneous, but is purely <i>accidental</i>, and is impressed upon them by an exterior force, as, for instance, the upward movement of earth and water, and the downward movement of fire and air. Likewise, the circular motion made by the spheres of the elements, and which produces the mixture of the elements, has for its cause the movement of the celestial sphere. Cf. vol. II, chap. <small>XIX</small>, p. 148.",
    "fn.2522": "For every movement in the sublunar world, one can give as cause the movement of the celestial sphere. See vol. I, p. 361 and following; vol. II, p. 30.",
    "fn.2523": "One cannot affirm how Maimonides, within his system, understood the words <i>and when they stood still, these stood still</i>, for the movement of the <i>’hayyôth</i>, or of the celestial spheres, never ceass. According to certain commentators, Maimonides doubtless understood the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">עמד</span> here in the sense of <i>to endure, to subsist</i> (cf. vol. I, chap. <small>XIII</small>, p. 63); that is to say, the <i>ophannîm</i> do <i>subsist</i> only through the duration of the <i>’hayyôth</i>. According to others, he might have alluded to the apparent <i>station</i> of the planets (vide vol. II, chap. <small>X</small>, p. 86, note 2), during which the forces of the stars would cease to act upon the elements. Cf. Samuel ibn-Tibbon, <i>Yikkawou ha-maïm</i>, chap. <small>X</small>, p. 53.",
    "fn.2524": "That is to say: the prophet reprises the description of what he had only indicated briefly in verse 4.",
    "fn.2525": "The author leavs one to surmise what he intends by these differing images. According to Ephôdi and Schem-Tob, the <i>firmament</i> would represent the ninth sphere, devoid of stars, or that of diurnal motion, which accords right well with the conceptions of Maimonides. The throne would represent the convexity or the surface of this same sphere (cf. Samuel ibn-Tibbon, loc. cit., p. 48), and the human figure hovering above would represent the intelligence of that sphere, which is the first of the separated Intelligences. Further on, the author declars that one must beware of discerning in the human figure God Himself, and that it could represent only a created being, namely the first of the Intelligences. It is true that in chap. <small>LXX</small> of the First Part, the author depicts the Divinity itself as enthroned upon the heaven ’<i>Arabôth</i>. But this contradiction is merely apparent, as one has already observed elsewhere. Vide vol. I, p. 28, note 1. — According to Moses of Narbonne, one must perceive in the throne a superior sphere, above the diurnal sphere, which bears the name of <span dir=\"rtl\">שמי ההצלחה</span>, <i>heaven of beatitude;</i> but this is a notion of the Kabbalists, which one could not attribute to Maimonides.",
    "fn.2526": "That is, the spheres are the beings entrusted by the creator to exercise a certain action on the sublunar world. See the definition the author gives of the word <i>ange</i> in chap. <small>VI</small> of the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work.",
    "fn.2527": "The cherubim represent, like the <i>’hayyôth</i>, the celestial spheres. This verse, according to Maimonides, indicates, like verses 19-21 of chapter I, that the movement of the elements depends on that of the spheres. See above, p. 21, and <i>ibid.</i>, notes 4 and 5.",
    "fn.2528": "See above, page 18, note 5.",
    "fn.2529": "That is, the <i>living ones</i>, or the celestial spheres. According to the author, Ezekiel would allude, in the passage cited, to the circular movement that the elements make <i>necessarily</i> in following the movement of the celestial sphere, designated here by the word <i>head</i>, a thing that the prophet did not sufficiently explain in the first vision. See above, p. 21, note 4.",
    "fn.2530": "In the first vision (chap. I, verse 18), the prophet had declared: <span dir=\"rtl\">וגבותם מלאות עינים</span>, <i>their backs</i>, or, according to our author, <i>their</i> <small>BODIES</small>, <i>were full of eyes</i>; whereas here he does attribute the eyes to all parts of the <i>ophannîm</i>, to their flesh, to their hands, to their wings, signifying that not only the bodies of the elements, but all parts of these bodies, their mixtures and their transformations, are full of <i>eyes.</i> Concerning the meaning of the word <i>eyes</i>, the author has explained himself in the preceding chapter. Vide p. 19 and notes 3 et seq.",
    "fn.2531": "The author appears to mean that in the first vision the prophet speaks only of the elementary body in general, while here he alludes to the mixture of elements and their various transformations.",
    "fn.2532": "See above, page 18, note 2.",
    "fn.2533": "That is to say: each of the four elements is under the special influence of one of the four spheres. See vol. II, chap. <small>X</small>, p. 86-87.",
    "fn.2534": "All the spheres together form only one substance, called the <i>fifth body.</i> See vol. I, chap. <small>LXXII</small>, p. 356-357, and cf. above, p. 18, note 6.",
    "fn.2535": "I read <span dir=\"rtl\">רֹבַּב</span> in the active form, whose subject is Ezekiel, as in <span dir=\"rtl\">סמא</span>. Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">כמר שנזכר</span>, in the passive.",
    "fn.2536": "That is to say: because the elements form also spheres attached to one another, and they all have the <i>form corporelle.</i> See t. I, p. 134, note 2, and above, p. 18, note 2.",
    "fn.2537": "All Arabic mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתצריח בקולה</span>, and similarly the version of Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">מה שהודיע באמרו</span>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon in our editions reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">עניין מאמר הנביא מבואר</span>; one must erase the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עניין</span>, which is not found in the mss.",
    "fn.2538": "According to the author, Jonathan would have intended to signify by this translation that the <i>ophannîm</i>, which he considers to be the celestial spheres, are beneath the highest part of heaven, or the abode of the <i>’hayyôth</i>, which would represent the separate Intelligences. Abravanel, who professes the same opinion, thus explains the words <i>an ophan upon the earth:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">רוצה לומר שראה עוד מעניני החיות שהם השכלים הנבדלים שהיה אופן אחד בארץ כלומר למטה מן החיות וכמו שתרגם יונתן בזה מלרע לרום שמיא והאופן האחד ההוא הוא רמז לכדור השמימי הכולל</span> .<span dir=\"rtl\">שהיה אצל החיות מתנועע מרוחם וכחם</span>",
    "fn.2539": "Literally: <i>But it seems to me that the matter is only as follows.</i> Most manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא הכדׄא</span>, and likewise Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא כך</span>. In some Arabic manuscripts <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא</span> is omitted, and likewise in Al-’Harîzi’s version, which bears <span dir=\"rtl\">וכמדומה לי כי אין הדבר כך</span>, <i>but it seems to me that it is not thus.</i> But if the word <i>thus</i> referred to Jonathan’s opinion, it would have been necessary to say in Arabic <span dir=\"rtl\">כדׄלך</span>, and not <span dir=\"rtl\">הכדׄא</span>.",
    "fn.2540": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">לכונהא ללתדויר</span> signify: <i>because it inclines towards the round form.</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">לכונהא</span> here bears the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">למילהא</span>, or else one must understand <span dir=\"rtl\">מאילה̈</span> to be implied. It is in this sense that Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">מפני שהוא נוטה לעגול</span>. Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">להיותה עגולה</span>, as if, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ללתדויר</span>, he had read <span dir=\"rtl\">מנורה</span>. Some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">ללדור</span>.",
    "fn.2541": "The editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have: …<span dir=\"rtl\">because every sphere and what is called and so on</span>; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">and because</span> … <span dir=\"rtl\">what is called</span>, as the mss. do and as the Arabic text and the sense require.",
    "fn.2542": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>Schabbâth</i>, fol. 151 <i>b.</i>.",
    "fn.2543": "For the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלבכרה̈</span>, <i>the pulley</i>, Ibn-Tibbon has put <span dir=\"rtl\">הגלגלים הקטנים של עץ והגדולים</span>, <i>the small and the large wooden wheels</i>, which does not offer a very clear sense; better Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">העגול אשר יסוב עלין חבל הדלי הנקרא בלשון ערב בכרה̈</span>, <i>the wheel upon which the rope of the bucket turns</i>, <i>and which is called in Arabic</i> <small>BACRA</small>. It is in this sense that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">גלגל</span> is employed in Ecclesiastes, chapter 12, verse 6: <span dir=\"rtl\">ונרץ הגלגל אל הבור</span>, <i>and the wheel broken at the cistern;</i> likewise, in the Midrash <i>Wayyikra rabba</i>, section 34 (fol. 174, col. 2): <span dir=\"rtl\">אהן עלמא מדמי לגלגל דאנטילא וגו׳</span>, <i>this world is like unto the wheel of the bucket</i>, <i>etc.</i>",
    "fn.2544": "According to Maimonides, the passage from Ezekiel signifies naught else save that the <i>ophannîm</i> or the elements likewise form spheres (vide supra, p. 18, note 5, and chapter III), and one ought not, with Jonathan ben Uziel, to perceive in the <i>ophannîm</i> the celestial spheres.— The editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version have here <span dir=\"rtl\">לא שהם השמים</span>; the manuscripts of this version bear, in conformity with the Arabic text, <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא שהם גלגלים</span>. — Immediately thereafter, the abbreviation <span dir=\"rtl\">א״כ</span>, for <span dir=\"rtl\">אס כן</span>, is likewise an error; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל</span>, as the manuscripts have it.",
    "fn.2545": "To comprehend this passage, one must recall what the author has said elsewhere concerning the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">במעשה לבנת הספיר</span>, <i>as a work of the whiteness of sapphire</i>, in which he perceivs an allusion to the sublunar prime matter. Vide vol. I, chap. <small>XXVIII</small>, p. 94 et seq.; vol. II, chap. <small>XXVI</small>, p. 202. He discovers this same allusion in the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כעין אבן תרשיש</span>, <i>as the colour of a Tarschisch stone</i>, which, according to him, likewise designate the sublunar matter or that of the elements, and it appears manifest to him that the <i>ophannîm</i> designate the elements. To corroborate this explanation, he observs that Jonathan, in his paraphrase of Ezekiel (I, 16, and X, 9), employs the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אבן טבא</span>, <i>precious stone</i>, the same as Onkelos employs in his paraphrase of the Pentateuch (Exodus, 24, 10), and which, according to our author, designate the sublunar matter. One does readily perceive all that is vicious in this reasoning, inasmuch as, according to Maimonides, Jonathan does discern in the <i>ophannîm</i> the celestial spheres, and consequently, he could not by the words <i>precious stone</i> have intended to designate the sublunar matter. This observation by Maimonides does afford but too much occasion for criticism, and Abravanel failed not to expose its feebleness. Vide Abravanel’s commentary on Ezekiel, chapter I, verse 16, and his <i>Commentary upon various parts of the Moreh Nevukhim</i>, II<sup>nd</sup> fasciculus, fol. 50 <i>a</i>, fourteenth objection.",
    "fn.2546": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תאוילי</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">תאויל</span> (for the accus. <span dir=\"rtl\">ר.אוילא</span>). The same variant exists in the mss. of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, which have, some <span dir=\"rtl\">פירוש</span>, others <span dir=\"rtl\">פירושי</span>. Al-’Harîzi has <span dir=\"rtl\">ובכל זאת איני מכריע הדין</span>.",
    "fn.2547": "Literally: <i>in which one of the two interpretations exists that is in accordance with what was intended.</i>",
    "fn.2548": "The Arabic text, in all mss., has the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא מראות</span>, which have been omitted in the two Hebrew versions.",
    "fn.2549": "That is to say: he begins the account of each of these three perceptions with the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וארא</span>, <i>and I saw</i>, in order to separate them from one another.",
    "fn.2550": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">העניין</span>, <i>of the subject;</i> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאדם</span>, Ibn-Tibbon had the reading <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאמר</span>, which we find in one of our Arabic mss.",
    "fn.2551": "The author will cite further on, in chapter VII, several explanations of the word <i>’haschmal</i>.— One might object that the author's observation is not accurate with regard to the third perception, which would be introduced by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וארא</span>, <i>I saw</i>, from verse 27; for the author himself stated at the end of chapter II that the third perception is already indicated in verses 22 and 26. Abravanel, in his commentary on Ezekiel (I, 4), criticises Maimonides' observation in other respects.",
    "fn.2552": "That is to say, Rabbi Judah the holy, who is considered the author of the <i>Mischnah.</i>",
    "fn.2553": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>’Haghigâ</i>, fol. 13 <i>a.</i>.",
    "fn.2554": "It follows from the entirety of this <i>beraïtha</i>, which is linked to the words of the Mishnah <span dir=\"rtl\">אין דורשין וגו׳</span> (<i>’Haghigâ</i>, II, 1), that one must infer in this phrase the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דורשין</span> or a similar word.",
    "fn.2555": "Our editions of the Talmud, as well as the version by Ibn-Tibbon, simply bear <span dir=\"rtl\">רבי</span>, that is to say, the <i>rabbi</i> par excellence, which designates Rabbi Judah the Saint; but all our Arabic MSS., as well as the version by Al-’Harîzi, bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ר׳ מאיר</span>. This latter reading, which, according to the commentator Shem-Tov, was also found in certain MSS. of the Talmud, is doubtless the true one, and, by adopting it, one makes the difficulties indicated by Shem-Tov disappear.",
    "fn.2556": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מן וארא ועד חשמל</span> (that is, <i>from</i> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וארא</span> of verse 4 <i>to</i> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חשמל</span> of verse 27), our editions of the Talmud simply <span dir=\"rtl\">עד וארא</span>. According to this reading, it is the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וארא</span> of verse 27, which does not change the meaning.",
    "fn.2557": "The general meaning of this passage, according to Maimonides, appears to be as follows: According to Rabbi Meir, it is permissible to explain the entire first chapter of Ezekiel up to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וארא</span> of verse 27, that is to say, up to and including verse 26; according to Rabbi Isaac, one may also explain the first words of verse 27, that is, the meaning of the word <i>’haschmal.</i> From that point until the end of chapter I, it is only permitted to teach the disciple the elementary principles. According to others, the prohibition against giving a clear explanation extends to the entire first chapter, even to verses 4 to 26, of which only the elementary principles may be taught. As for verses 27 and 28, only the sage of superior intellect may study them. It is evidently this opinion which Maimonides attributes to Rabbi Judah the Saint, probably because the words of the Mishnah are reproduced in this passage: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא א״כ היה חכם ומבין מדעתו</span>, 'unless one be a wise man, understanding by his own intellect.'",
    "fn.2558": "As has been seen above, p. 23, note 2, the human figure represents, according to the commentators, the first of the separated Intelligences or that of the ninth sphere; this Intelligence, although emanating from the absolutely simple Being, is nevertheless compound in that it perceives simultaneously the first cause, or God, and itself. It would therefore be this twofold perception which the prophet would have indicated by the <i>divided</i> human figure. The theory to which, according to the commentators, Maimonides would allude, belongs to Ibn-Sīnā. Cf. my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 360.",
    "fn.2559": "That is, because the <i>’hayyôth</i> are the cause of the movement of the <i>ophannîm</i>, and, as such, are prior to them. Cf. above, p. 21, and <i>ibid.</i>, notes 4 and 5.",
    "fn.2560": "According to the commentators, Maimonides would have indicated by these latter words that the perception of the <i>’hayyôth</i>, or the study relating to the celestial spheres and the stars, being within the domain of the mathematical sciences, must in the order of studies precede the perception of the <i>ophannîm</i> (elements), or the study of physics. Cf. Vol. I, chap. <small>XXXIV</small>, p. 123: 'It is therefore necessary that one who wishes to attain human perception should instruct himself in logic, then gradually in mathematics, then in the physical sciences, and after that in metaphysics.' Cf. <i>ibidem</i>, p. 13, note 2, and <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 357. — Abravanel rightly observes that, admitting the explanation Maimonides gives of the whole of Ezekiel's vision, it would have been far more rational to begin with the <i>ophannîm</i> or elements, and to ascend from thence to the <i>’hayyôth</i> or celestial spheres, in order to arrive subsequently at the Intelligences of the spheres, or else to follow the inverse order. Cf. Abravanel's second preface to the book of Ezekiel, second method, fourth objection.",
    "fn.2561": "That is to say, that of the <i>’hayyôth</i> and the <i>ophannîm</i>, or the studies of astronomy and physics.",
    "fn.2562": "All our mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ראוא</span>, in the plural; we have written more correctly <span dir=\"rtl\">ראיא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(رَأْيًا)</span> , in the dual.",
    "fn.2563": "For the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אגׄנאדה</span>, Ibn-Tibbon's version has the two words <span dir=\"rtl\">רגליו וחיילותיו</span>, <i>his infantry and his troops</i>. However, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">רגליו</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">דגליו</span> (with <i>daleth</i>), <i>his flags</i>, and this reading is preferable.",
    "fn.2564": "Almost all manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">and the righteous</span>, with the <span dir=\"rtl\">and</span> copulative; a single manuscript (Bibliothèque impériale, ancient Hebrew fund, n° 230) has <span dir=\"rtl\">the righteous</span>, without <span dir=\"rtl\">and</span>, and this reading is also that of Ibn-Tibbon, who has <span dir=\"rtl\">do his word and his commandments</span>.",
    "fn.2565": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>’Haghigâ</i>, fol. 13 <i>b.</i>.",
    "fn.2566": "All our mss. have: <span dir=\"rtl\">והדׄא אלנץ ימכן אן יתאול לקאילה</span>, literally: <i>it may be that this text is interpreted by the one who said it</i>, <i>etc.</i> In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לקאילה</span> is not expressed.",
    "fn.2567": "The author offers two interpretations of the Talmudic passage. According to one, the two prophets were equally perfect; but the contemporaries of Ezekiel, living in the Babylonian exile, were less capable of understanding divine matters than the contemporaries of Isaiah. According to the second interpretation, the difference lay in the prophets themselves; Isaiah, being more cultivated and more perfect, was in no wise disturbed by all that he saw and recounted it briefly and with simplicity, whereas the words of Ezekiel reveal all the astonishment and trouble which his vision caused him.",
    "fn.2568": "Cf. Ezekiel, chap. I, verses 1 to 3, where the date and place of the vision are fixed with the utmost precision. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קיד</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(قيد)</span> signifies <i>to bind closely</i>, <i>to chain</i>, <i>to fix the reading of a word by vowels</i>, <i>to specify.</i> Ibn-Tibbon appears to have taken the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תקייד</span> in the sense of <i>to attach;</i> the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וקשרה במקום</span> signify <i>and that one attaches it to a place</i>, whereas the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">ותקייד אלמוצׄע</span> signify: <i>and the determination</i> or <i>precision of the place.</i> The suffixes in <span dir=\"rtl\">קשרו</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">וקשרה</span> were added by Ibn-Tibbon and are not found in the Arabic. Al-’Harîzi translates: .<span dir=\"rtl\">טעם כתיבת עניין המרכבה בשנה אשר נראתה בה ובחדש וביום ובמקום</span>",
    "fn.2569": "Ibn-Tibbon’s editions present <span dir=\"rtl\">אין עניין לו</span>, <i>which has no meaning;</i> the manuscripts, in conformity with the Arabic, have: <span dir=\"rtl\">אין עניין בו</span>, <i>in which there is no idea.</i> — Maimonides leavs one to conjecture what idea, according to him, this fixation of date and place does contain. The commentators, giving free rein to their imagination, have supplied, with greater or lesser success, the author’s silence, and it is with reason that Abravanel (Commentary on Ezekiel, I, 1-3) perceivs in their interpretations naught but vain trivialities (<span dir=\"rtl\">כולם הבל המה מעשה תעתועים</span>). One shall content oneself with indicating the explanation of Moses of Narbonne, who, if he has not divined Maimonides’ thought, has at least furnished a sufficiently plausible reason. According to him, the prophet would have wished to indicate that his narrative concerns not certain phenomena which were actually visible in the heavens, but rather things which transpired within his soul, that is to say, a prophetic vision, the fruit of an exalted imagination. The precision of the day and place was intended to demonstrate unto all that the entire narrative is but imaginary, no other than Ezekiel having beheld these celestial phenomena on the day and in the place indicated by him. In sum, one cannot assert what mystery Maimonides sought in the precise indications of the prophet, who probably had no other aim than to record the place and epoch in which he felt himself called to his divine mission.",
    "fn.2570": "The words <i>the heavens opened</i>, says the author, are the key to the entire narrative of Ezekiel; for these words, obviously metaphorical (cf. t. II, p. 359-360), and indicating the emanation of the divine spirit, show that, in the entire narrative, it is a matter of a prophetic vision, and not of things perceivable by the senses.",
    "fn.2571": "Cf. t. II, at the beginning of chapter <small>XLI</small>.",
    "fn.2572": "Literally: <i>but</i> (<i>he expresses himself</i>), <i>by a simple enunciation, upon a form of real existence as it is.</i> — By this acute observation, the author intends to indicate that the prophet, when speaking of the celestial spheres and of that which is above (vide supra, p. 23, note 2), employs the word <i>resemblance</i>, to imply that these are things which human intelligence grasps not in their entire reality, whereas it may possess a clear and certain knowledge of the <i>ophannîm</i>, or of the elements, and of all sublunar things. Cf. vol. II, chap. <small>XXII</small>, p. 179, and chap. <small>XXIV</small>, p. 194, where the author insists upon the uncertainty of human knowledge pertaining to the superior world, the perfect understanding of which belongs to God alone.",
    "fn.2573": "The author purports that this passage, in which the <i>ophannîm</i> are mentioned, is not constructed in like manner as the passages cited heretofore. There, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דמות</span>, <i>resemblance</i>, closely linked to the word designating the object of the vision, indicats that it concerns but an <i>appearance</i> possessing more or less probability; here, the word <i>resemblance</i> is employed in the sense of <i>form</i> and denots a truly perceptible form, that is to say, the corporeal form. Cf. supra, p. 18, note 6.",
    "fn.2574": "That is: in the second vision, chapter X, the prophet expresses himself in a way that confirms the observation made by the author on the use of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דמות</span>, <i>ressemblance</i>.",
    "fn.2575": "The author intends that in chap. X, where the prophet commencs by speaking of the heaven, or of the superior sphere, to subsequently enumerate in detail all that it contains in the way of spheres and elements, he speaks of this heaven in an absolute manner and without employing the word <i>resemblance.</i> As shall be seen, the author concluds therefrom that the prophet employs the word <i>resemblance</i> when he wishes to speak of things of which the knowledge is uncertain and concerning which doubts exist.",
    "fn.2576": "That is to say, in chapter I, <i>v.</i> 22, where he says: <span dir=\"rtl\">ודמות על ראשי החיה רקיע</span>, <i>and above the</i> <i>’hayyâ there was the resemblance of a firmament.</i>",
    "fn.2577": "That is to say: the manner in which the prophet expresses himself here concerning the firmament and the throne indicats that he had attained unto a clear knowledge of the firmament, or of the diurnal sphere, which left him no further doubt; but that which is above this sphere ever remained enveloped in clouds for him. Wherefore he employs the word <i>resemblance</i> no longer here, when speaking of the firmament; but he continus to employ it when speaking of the throne, a term by which he here summariss all that is above the celestial spheres, such as the Intelligences and God.",
    "fn.2578": "That is to say, to the hands. Some manuscripts present <span dir=\"rtl\">ורתבה̈ דׄלך</span>, <i>the rank</i> or <i>the place thereof</i> (that is to say <i>of the hands</i>); others present <span dir=\"rtl\">ורתבת דׄלך</span>, which signifies word for word: <i>and that has been ranked</i> or <i>placed;</i> this latter reading is less correct, for the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">وَرَقَبَتُ</span> would require a feminine subject <span dir=\"rtl\">تلك</span>. One of our manuscripts bears <span dir=\"rtl\">ורתבה תחת כנפיהם</span>, without the pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">דׄלך</span>. According to this reading, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ורתבה</span> would be an active verb with a suffix; but the suffix can refer only to the <i>hands</i>, and it would require <span dir=\"rtl\">ורתבהא</span>. It is perchance this reading that Ibn-Tibbon possessed, who translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">וסדרם תחת כנפיהם</span>. Another manuscript (that of the Heb. suppl., No. 63) has simply <span dir=\"rtl\">ואן דׄלך תחת כנפיהם</span>, a reading reproduced by Al-’Harîzi, who translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכי זה היה תחת כנפיהם</span>.",
    "fn.2579": "The mystery to which Maimonides alludes in this paragraph doubtless appears to be as follows: in the first vision, the prophet beholds simultaneously the wings and the hands, that is to say, the causes of the motion of the spheres and the effects produced by this motion upon the sublunar world (vide supra, p. 11, notes 1 and 2); in the second vision, in which the prophet informs one that the <i>’hayyôth</i> are cherubim or angels, that is to say, beings which act by their forces upon the sublunar world (cf. supra, p. 24, note 1), he himself beholds the hands emerge <i>beneath the wings</i>, indicating thereby, more clearly than he had done aforetime, that the wings allegorically represent the causes of motion, whilst the hands represent the forces emanating from the spheres, and which diffuse themselves over the sublunar world to bring forth all that is born and to preserve the same.",
    "fn.2580": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בקולה אופנים</span>, several manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">בקול האופנים</span>, the reading adopted by the two Hebrew translators; Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">איך באר במאמר האופנים לעמתם</span>. Al-’Harîzi has <span dir=\"rtl\">איך באר האופנים לעמתם</span>. The reading we have adopted, based on two of the best manuscripts, makes the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לעמתם</span> stand out more, on which the author insists particularly.",
    "fn.2581": "The author, as it appears, desires to draw attention to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לעמתם</span>, <i>over against them</i> (the cherubim), employed in the second vision, chapter X, verse 19. The prophet, he states, reiterates this notion that the <i>ophannîm</i> moved <i>over against</i> the cherubim or the <i>’hayyôth</i>, albeit he attributes here to the <i>ophannîm</i>, no more than in the first vision, any figure of a living being possessing within itself the principle of its motion (cf. supra, p. 18, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2), and he further indicates clearly thereby that the movement of the elements is due solely to that of the spheres. Vide supra, p. 21, and <i>ibid.</i>, notes 4 and 5.",
    "fn.2582": "Literally: <i>its reality and its quiddity.</i> Ibn-Tibbon omitted in his version the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ומאהיתהא</span>, <i>and its quiddity</i>, as Ibn-Falaquera has already observed (Append. to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 157), who believes that this word is necessary for comprehending Maimonides' intention. The version of Al-’Harizi bears <span dir=\"rtl\">מהות הקשת ואמתתה ידועה</span>; here, the word <i>quiddity</i> is expressed, but not the word <i>matter.</i>",
    "fn.2583": "This obscure passage has been explained by Moses of Narbonne, and, after him, by Ephodi and Shem-Tob, in a manner far too recondite, and one deems it improbable that these commentators have divined the thought of Maimonides. According to them, the author would have signified that Ezekiel here compares the disposition of the prophet's soul to receive the inspiration emanating from the <i>glory of the Eternal</i>, or from the Separated Intelligence, to the disposition of a rain-laden cloud to receive the impression of the sun's rays, which produce the rainbow. This is what the author would have intended to indicate by speaking of an extraordinary or very remarkable allegory and by adding the words: <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא בלא ספק בכח נבואה</span>, as the version of Ibn-Tibbon expresses it. But evidently, the author intends by these words that the comparison in question could only have issued from the imagination of a prophet, fortified by inspiration. Furthermore, as Abravanel observes (Commentary on Ezekiel, I, 28), Ezekiel's sole aim is to delineate the vision he had beheld, and this allegory concerning prophetic inspiration would here constitute an entirely misplaced digression. Moreover, the <i>gloire de l’Éternel</i> here indicates somewhat objective which the prophet believed himself to behold, and cannot be an image to designate prophetic inspiration. Finally, one would not perceive, according to this explanation, what the author intended by these words: <i>one knows the matter of the rainbow, its reality and its quiddity;</i> these words, assuredly, must possess their proper import. —One does not pretend to have divined the true sense of this passage; but one shall propose the following explanation, which appears to one at least simpler than that of the commentators. According to Maimonides, the splendour which environed the <i>human figure</i> (vide supra, p. 23, note 2) appeared unto the prophet as the rainbow, in which one perceives but the <i>reverberation</i> of light, refracted and disturbed by humidity. One knows the matter or substratum of the rainbow, which is the rain-cloud. One knows equally its quiddity or form, which is the light. But one does not comprehend the rainbow as it appears to one, that is to say, the colours of which it is composed; for, according to Ibn-Sinâ, one knows not its causes (vide my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 317, and Shahrestâni, p. 413). In like manner, one can perceive but a faint reflection of the splendour which environs the first of the Intelligences, represented by the <i>human figure</i>, and it is this reflection that the prophet represents as the <i>likeness</i> of the glory of the Eternal (that is to say, according to the explanation which the author has given higher up of the word <i>likeness</i>, as something vague and doubtful). This comparison appears to Maimonides so extraordinary that he can discern therein but the product of prophetic inspiration.",
    "fn.2584": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">שעל</span> (for <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר על</span>) is found in all the mss. of the original Arabic. The author, reproducing, before and after the relative pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">ש</span>, the textual words of Ezekiel, has allowed the Hebrew pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">ש</span> to slip into his discourse instead of the Arabic pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי</span>.",
    "fn.2585": "See Ezekiel, chap. I, <i>v.</i> 27, and chap. VIII, <i>v.</i> 2. We have already stated earlier, p. 32, note 2, what meaning, according to commentators, Maïmonide gives to the allegory of the human figure divided into two parts.",
    "fn.2586": "This explanation and the succeeding one are drawn from the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>’Haghigâ</i>, fol. 13 <i>a</i> and <i>b.</i> The first explanation is wanting in our editions of the Talmud; but in the Dictionary of David Kimchi, under the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חשמל</span>, where the entire Talmudic passage is reproduced, the quotation commences with these words: <span dir=\"rtl\">מאי חשמל חש מל</span>. Maimonides' aim here is not to investigate the true sense of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חשמל</span>, which is doubtful, but merely to demonstrate that the ancient Rabbins discerned therein a compound word, encompassing two distinct ideas; for in chap. I, <i>v.</i> 27, the whole ensemble of the figure, both its upper and lower parts, appears to be designated by the word <i>’haschmal</i>. Cf. vol. II, chap. <small>XXIX</small>, p. 229, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 4.",
    "fn.2587": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">יתעלה מכל המרכבה</span>, which is merely a typographical error; the mss. have correctly <span dir=\"rtl\">הרכבה</span>.",
    "fn.2588": "See, for example, t. I, chap. <small>LIV</small> (p. 216 and following), and chap. <small>LXIV</small> (p. 286), where the author explains that man cannot perceive the divine essence, and that by the glory of God <i>manifested</i> one must understand a <i>created light</i>, or the manifestation of God through his works.",
    "fn.2589": "On the meaning of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">רכב</span>, <i>to mount, to ride</i>, applied to God, as well as the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מרכבה</span>, <i>mount</i>, <i>chariot</i>, see vol. I, chap. <small>LXX</small>. The author means that all the perceptions of the prophets were limited to the <i>glory</i> of God manifesting itself in the universe, of which it is the primary mover.",
    "fn.2590": "The manuscripts present, some <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄממת</span>, others <span dir=\"rtl\">תממת</span>; one has adopted the former of these two readings, confirmed by Al-’Harîzi and Ibn-Falaquéra (Append. of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 157), who, both alike, translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">כשתקבץ</span>, <i>if thou dost resemble.</i> The other reading is that of Ibn-Tibbon, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">שאם תשלים</span>, <i>if thou dost complete.</i> For the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄאת</span>, Ibn-Tibbon presents <span dir=\"rtl\">יבא לך</span>, and Al-’Harîzi <span dir=\"rtl\">יתקבץ לד</span>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לד</span> is not to be found in any of our Arabic manuscripts.",
    "fn.2591": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">قم</span>, in its first <sup>re</sup> and fifth <sup>e</sup> forms, signifies: <i>to rush headlong, to engage rashly in an affair.</i> The words <span dir=\"rtl\">בל קד תקחמת בתׄירא</span> do not appear to one to have been rendered with exactitude by the two Hebrew translators; Ibn-Tibbon’s version states: <span dir=\"rtl\">וגם לחצתי מאד ודחקתי</span>; that of Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ודחקתי נפשי מאד</span>.",
    "fn.2592": "That is to say, the form entirely abstract from the matter which serves as its <i>substratum</i>. The author, as one shall presently observe, principally intends to speak of the <i>specific</i> form, which constitutes the veritable quiddity of all that nature does produce. This form, considered in itself, is imperishable; for the genera and species abide, whilst the individuals alone perish. — At the close of the sentence, Al’-Harîzi has referred the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">ילחקהא</span>, as also the pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">הי</span>, to the <i>form;</i> he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא ישיגנה הפסד אבל היא קיימת</span>. This translation may accord with the author’s intent; but it is contrary to the construction of the Arabic sentence. Ibn-Tibbon translates more exactly: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל מצד הצורה ובבחינת עצם הצורה לא ישיגם הפסד רק הם עומדים</span>. —After having concluded the exposition of Ezekiel’s vision, a subject which still appertains to the second <sup>e</sup> part of this work, the author will proceed to set forth his theory concerning evil and Providence, to which the questions treated in this chapter and in the subsequent one serve as preliminaries.",
    "fn.2593": "For a deeper understanding of this chapter, see chapter XVII of volume I of this work, p. 69.",
    "fn.2594": "Cf. ibid., Introduction, p. 20 and following.",
    "fn.2595": "Cf. <i>ibid.</i>, p. 68, and note 4.",
    "fn.2596": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">יותי</span> (aorist passive of the fourth <sup>e</sup> form of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">أبي</span>, <i>to bring forth</i>, <i>to cause to come</i>), the Hebrew suppl. ms., no. 63, presents <span dir=\"rtl\">יותׁר</span>; likewise Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">במה שצריך לבחור בו</span> “that which one ought to prefer.” The version of Al-’Harîzi, <span dir=\"rtl\">במה שראוי להגביר בו</span>, does appear to express the same reading.",
    "fn.2597": "That is, the various forms that matter receives.",
    "fn.2598": "See the I<sup>st</sup> part of this work, chapter I<sup>er</sup>.",
    "fn.2599": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלכדרה̈</span> is not rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version; Al-’Harîzi's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">בזה החמר אשר מן העפר העכור האפל</span>.",
    "fn.2600": "Ibn-Tibbon did not translate the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וחכמא</span>; Al-’Harîzi renders it as <span dir=\"rtl\">וממשלה ודין ושלטו</span>.",
    "fn.2601": "That is, with the active intellect, source of all forms. See vol. II, chap. <small>IV</small>, p. 57 and following.",
    "fn.2602": "Ibn-Tibbon adds the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ותאוותיו</span>, <i>and his concupiscences</i> ; this word is not expressed in any of our Arabic mss. or in Al-’Harîzi's version, which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכשימשכו אותם מעוררי החמר לטנופם וחרפתם הגלויה</span>. In the latter version, the plural suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">לטנופם וחרפתם</span> is incorrect; for the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">לקדׄארתהא ועארהא</span> refers to the material.",
    "fn.2603": "See volume II, page 283, note 3.",
    "fn.2604": "Ibn-Tibbon's version reads <span dir=\"rtl\">כי צריך למשכיל</span>, and similarly Al-’Haiîzi's version <span dir=\"rtl\">ויצטרך המשכיל</span>, <i>it is therefore necessary for the intelligent man to restrict etc.</i>; two of our Arabic mss. also have <span dir=\"rtl\">ינבגי ללעאקל</span>; but the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ללעאקל</span> is missing in most mss.",
    "fn.2605": "Ibn-Tibbon translates <span dir=\"rtl\">ולהשמר מהם</span>, <i>s’en garder;</i> Al-’Harîzi has more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולהסתר</span>.",
    "fn.2606": "Literally: <i>qu’on n’y fasse pas tomber le discours et qu’on n’étende pas la parole là-dessus.</i>",
    "fn.2607": "Ibn-Tibbon translates simply <span dir=\"rtl\">those who are different from the name</span>; but the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">al-mughibun</span> means: <i>those who are veiled</i>, that is, those who have a veil over their eyes so as not to see God. Al-’Harîzi translates freely: <span dir=\"rtl\">those who will not see the face of God</span>.",
    "fn.2608": "Literally: <i>in the opposite of what was intended with them;</i> that is, the intention that God had towards men in creating them.",
    "fn.2609": "Cf. vol. II, chap. <small>XXXVI</small>, p. 281-282.",
    "fn.2610": "More literally: <i>it is for this reason</i> (or <i>to this end</i>) <i>that Solomon did preach all these moral aphorisms, both he and others.</i>—Al-’Harîzi translates literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">ועל כן ייםר שלמה בכל אלה המוםרים הוא וזולתו</span>. Ibn-Tibbon presents <span dir=\"rtl\">המוםרים ההם כלם</span>, without the prefix <span dir=\"rtl\">ב</span>; in several Arabic manuscripts, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">תלך</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בתלך</span>.",
    "fn.2611": "The editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומזאת התורה והזהרה</span>, an incorrect and little intelligible reading; the mss. have, in accordance with the Arabic text, <span dir=\"rtl\">ומצות התורה והזהרותיה</span>. Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">וצוויי התורה ואיסוריה</span>.",
    "fn.2612": "Literally: <i>and that he places degrees in his soul;</i> that is, that he does not allow these material demands to reach him until certain degrees. In Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לנפשו</span> (for <span dir=\"rtl\">פי נפםה</span>) is inaccurate. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וישים להם מעלות בנפשו</span>.",
    "fn.2613": "See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Pesa’hîm</i>, fol. 49 <i>a</i>, where it is stated that it is not permitted unto the disciples of the sages to partake of a feast not consecrated to a religious act (<span dir=\"rtl\">סעודה שאינה של מצוה</span>); that he who partakes of a profane feast shall ultimately, according to the prophet Amos (6, 4-7), go into exile, and that he who multiplies feasts in all places does destroy his house, render his wife a widow and his children orphans, forget that which he has learned, draw unto himself many quarrels, cause himself to be disobeyed, profane the name of God, that of his preceptor and that of his father, and bring upon himself, his children, and his grandchildren, an ill repute in perpetuity.",
    "fn.2614": "See <i>ibid</i>., the treatise ’<i>Hullîn</i>, fol. 7 <i>b:</i> ‘It is reported of Rabbi Pine’has ben Iaïr that he never broke bread that was not his, and that from the day he arrived at reason, he no longer enjoyed his father’s meal.’",
    "fn.2615": "That is, Rabbi Judah the saint. According to the Talmud account, l.c., Rabbi Pinehas initially accepted the invitation of the holy doctor, but then sought various excuses to avoid it.",
    "fn.2616": "That is to say, in drink, as in food, one must confine oneself to what is indispensable and abstain from the superfluous. — Regarding the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשראב</span>, <i>the drink</i>, Ibn-Tibbon has rendered it by <span dir=\"rtl\">היין</span>, <i>the wine;</i> in certain Arabic dialects, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">شراب</span> is indeed employed in the sense of <i>wine;</i> but in this place, the word evidently possesses a more general signification. As for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלקצד</span>, <i>the goal</i>, which is found in all our manuscripts, Al-’Harîzi appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלקדר</span>, <i>the measure;</i> for he translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">ודין המשקה כדין המאכל צריך להיות בשיעור</span>, <i>the drink</i>, <i>like the food</i>, <i>must be taken with measure</i>.",
    "fn.2617": "Literally: <i>it is a thing</i> <small>PROBABLE</small> (<i>ἔνδοξον</i>), <i>and not</i> <small>INTELLIGIBLE</small> (<i>νοητόν</i>). See volume I, page 39, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1.",
    "fn.2618": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have: <span dir=\"rtl\">attributed to the intellect</span>, which offers no sense; one must read: <span dir=\"rtl\">distant from the intellect</span>, as the mss have it.",
    "fn.2619": "See this commentary, chapter I, § 5, and <i>passim</i>; and the <i>Eight Chapters</i> that serve as its Introduction, chapter IV, towards the end.",
    "fn.2620": "See <i>Wayyikra rabba</i>, section 24 (fol. 165, col. 3); Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>Berakhôth</i>, fol. 10 <i>b.</i>.",
    "fn.2621": "Literally: <i>may no seed issue from him before Reuben.</i> See <i>Beréschîth rabba</i>, sect. 98 (fol. 84, col. 4), and sect. 99 (fol. 87, col. 2). Cf. <i>Yalkout</i>, tome I, n° 157.",
    "fn.2622": "See the Babylonian Talmud, treatise Yômâ, fol. 29a. The Talmud simply means that lustful thoughts excite us more than the pleasure itself; it compares this excitement to that caused by the smell of roasted meat. Maimonides, as was his habit, diverts the Talmudic passage from its literal sense and, through an ingenious interpretation, gives it a purely moral sense.",
    "fn.2623": "Instead of the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתאבעה̈ להא</span>, <i>which belong to Him</i>, the version of Ibn-Tibbon reads <span dir=\"rtl\">וכל כחותיה</span>, <i>and all His forces</i>; the version of Al-’Harîzi is in accordance with the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי זאת הצורה האנושית וכל כחותיה הבאים ממנה אין ראוי להשתמש וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.2624": "The two Hebrew versions read: <span dir=\"rtl\">to descend to attain the low.</span> This translation is inaccurate; the two translators have erred in the sense of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">دَرُّكَ</span> . See the tome I, p. 188, note 5.",
    "fn.2625": "Consult, amongst other sources, the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Kéthubôth</i>, fol. 8 <i>b:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">כל המנבל פיו ומוציא דבר נבל מפיו אפילו נחתם לו גזר דינו של שבעים שנה לטוכה נהפך עליו לרעה</span>, ‘whosoever defils his mouth and utters an obscene word, even if his decree of destiny for seventy years of felicity had been sealed (by God), it shall be turned for him into misfortune.’ Cf. treatise <i>Schabbâth</i>, fol. 33 <i>a.</i>",
    "fn.2626": "All the Arabic mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\"><span dir=\"rtl\">ומיז בהא</span>  בהא</span>, <i>and by which it has been distinguished.</i> Ibn-Tibbon completes the sense, translating: <span dir=\"rtl\">to distinguish it from other living beings</span>, <i>in order to distinguish it from other animals.</i>",
    "fn.2627": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon reads <span dir=\"rtl\">ודבריהם</span>, and that of Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ועניניהם</span>; both are incorrect, for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אכׄבאר</span> has here evidently the sense of <i>narrations</i> or of <i>contes.</i>",
    "fn.2628": "Of six manuscripts which one has consulted, only two bear the reading <span dir=\"rtl\">לגו</span>, which one has deemed it proper to adopt. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">أبو</span> signifies <i>an ill-considered utterance</i>, <i>a word spoken at random</i>, <i>a vain word</i>, and it is this meaning which appears best suited to our passage. Three manuscripts exhibit <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄלו</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(جَدُّو)</span>, a word signifying <i>lustre, polish, splendour;</i> and it is perhaps this reading that Al-’Harîzi expresses by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">גאוה</span>, <i>pride;</i> he translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואל תחשוב כי זה היה לרב גאותנו או טעותנו</span>. A single manuscript bears <span dir=\"rtl\">גלו</span>, which one may pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">غدو</span>, <i>exaggeration, hyperbole;</i> it is this reading that Ibn-Tibbon expresses, who translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא תחשוב שהוא הפלגה ממנו וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.2629": "The mss. of Ibn-Tibbon's version have, in accordance with the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">and not for seed, nor for urine, nor for excrement</span>. In the editions, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">and not for urine</span> have been omitted, and for <span dir=\"rtl\">excrement</span>, one has put <span dir=\"rtl\">exit</span>. This variant has led Buxtorf astray, who translates: 'neque seminis, <i>nor ejaculation of that sort</i>', a translation followed by M. Scheyer.",
    "fn.2630": "The author speaks here of the Talmudic language, where the word <span dir=\"rtl\">גיד</span>, <i>nerf</i>, is used to designate the male member; in the biblical books the word <span dir=\"rtl\">גיד</span> is never used in this sense.",
    "fn.2631": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">שפכה</span>, which is found in Deuteronomy, chapter XXIII, <i>v.</i> 2, comes from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">שפך</span>, <i>to pour</i>, and means: 'The member by which urine or semen is discharged'.",
    "fn.2632": "The author associates the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קבָה</span>, with the suffix <span dir=\"rtl\">קֳבָתָהּ</span> (Numbers, 25, 8), <i>anus, vulva</i>, with <span dir=\"rtl\">קֵבָה</span>, <i>estomac</i> (Deuteronomy, XVIII, 3); the two words appear to come from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">נקב</span>, <i>perforer</i>, <i>faire une excavation.</i>",
    "fn.2633": "The author omitts the proper terms which serve to designate excrement and urine, and which the Bible has preserved for us in the <i>kethîb</i>, or the written reading; therein are excrements designated by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חראים</span>, and urine by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">שינים</span>, and it is by reason of decorum that in the <i>keri</i>, or the reading, those words indicated by the author are substituted for these. Were it indeed true that no account should be taken of the <i>kethîb</i>, one might inquire why the author, immediately thereafter, deems it necessary to justify the employment of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">שגל</span>, designating the act of cohabitation, seeing that this verb likewise appears solely in the <i>kethîb</i>, and that in the <i>keri</i> the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">שכב</span> is substituted for it. In general, the observations put forth by the author concerning the appellation of a <i>holy</i> tongue may give rise to criticism, and indeed Rabbi Moses ben Na’hmân has already criticised them with justice in the commentary upon Exodus, chapter 30, verse 13.",
    "fn.2634": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי אלמכתוב</span> signify <i>according to what is written</i>, that is to say, according to the written reading, which the Masoretes designate as <i>kethîb;</i> for in the reading, one pronouncs <span dir=\"rtl\">ישכבנה</span>. Ibn-Tibbon would have done well to employ here the Chaldaic term <span dir=\"rtl\">כתיב</span>, consecrated by the Masora, the Hebrew form <span dir=\"rtl\">הכתוב</span> being liable to engender a misunderstanding; indeed, M. Scheyer translats the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לפי הכתוב</span> as <i>in der Schrift</i> (in Holy Scripture). Al-’Harîzi translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">על פי שנכתב</span>.",
    "fn.2635": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">in general the chapter</span>, is not quite exact; Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">for the most part this chapter</span>.",
    "fn.2636": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">חגׄאב</span> signifies <i>that which intercepts</i> (<i>the sight</i>), <i>an obstacle</i>, <i>a veil.</i> Ibn-Tibbon renders it by two terms: <span dir=\"rtl\">מחיצה גדולה ומסך מונע</span> (Cf. <i>Eight Chapters</i>, or Introduction to the treatise <i>Abôth</i>, chap. VII, where Ibn-Tibbon renders the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">חגׄאב</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">מחיצה</span>); the notion of <i>hindrance</i>, <span dir=\"rtl\">מונע</span>, is expressed in the Arabic original by the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">ען</span>; Al-’Harîzi translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">החמר הוא מסן גדול לבל נשיג השכל הנבדל</span>.",
    "fn.2637": "The Arabic text has only the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמפארק</span>, <i>which is separated</i>, <i>τò ϰεχωρισμένον</i>, a term that designates God and other spiritual substances. See vol. II, p. 31, note 2.",
    "fn.2638": "That is, the one that has been called <i>ether</i> or <i>the fifth body.</i> See vol. II, page 25, note 1. The author means that even the spheres and stars, which have a very subtle matter, are prevented by this from perceiving the <i>separate intelligences</i> in their full reality.",
    "fn.2639": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read: <span dir=\"rtl\">one of the opinions</span>; one must write: <span dir=\"rtl\">one of the intellects</span>, as the mss of this version generally have it.",
    "fn.2640": "The author, with these words, alludes to God's revelation on Mount Sinai. See Exodus, chapter 19, <i>v.</i> 9; Deuteronomy, chapter IV, <i>v.</i> 11.",
    "fn.2641": "See the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chapter <small>XXXIII</small>.",
    "fn.2642": "For the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלבאהר</span>, <i>resplendent</i>, Ibn-Tibbon has the two words <span dir=\"rtl\">הגדול החזק</span>, <i>great and strong;</i> Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately <span dir=\"rtl\">אור בהיר</span>.",
    "fn.2643": "Literally: <i>by the effusion of which everything dark becomes luminous.</i> Instead of these last words, one reads in some mss.: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי פיצׄה איצׄא מניר</span>, <i>of which the effusion is also luminous</i> or <i>resplendent.</i> The version of Ibn-Tibbon confirms the reading that we have adopted. In that of Al-’Harîzi these words are omitted.",
    "fn.2644": "That is to say, the negation or absence of positive qualities. See vol. I, chap. <small>LXXIII</small>, seventh proposition of Motécallemîn (p. 395 and following). On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מלכה̈</span>, <i>capacity</i>, see <i>ibidem</i>, p. 195, notes 1 and 2.",
    "fn.2645": "That is, as two positive qualities <i>positives</i>, opposed to each other, and of which one is not simply the <i>négation</i> of the other.",
    "fn.2646": "See Voyage, vol. I, p. 396, and ibid., note 2.",
    "fn.2647": "That is to say: As <i>privations</i> are for them positive qualities, and as they recognise no other non-being save absolute non-being, they were enabled to declare, in an absolute sense, that non-being requirs no agent; for <i>non-being</i>, as they apprehend it, is nihility, or that which has never existed and shall never exist, and it is solely the creative act which stands in need of an agent.",
    "fn.2648": "The author means that, according to philosophers as well, one can say that privations in general do not require an agent, although on the other hand, what causes a certain <i>capacity</i>, or positive quality, to cease can be considered as the true agent of the <i>privation</i>, as will be explained later.",
    "fn.2649": "That is to say: their proposition that asserts that non-being, or privation, does not need an agent, does not prevent them from saying that it is God who makes someone blind or deaf; for, according to their principle, blindness and deafness are not the <i>privations</i> or negations of sight or hearing, but positive <i>qualities</i> that God creates in man.",
    "fn.2650": "See t. II, Introduction, end of the 18<sup>th</sup> proposition.",
    "fn.2651": "See Aristotle's <i>Physics</i>, Book VIII, end of Chapter 4 (translation by M. Barth. Saint-Hilaire, vol. II, p. 489): «To set in motion the obstacle which opposes the act and hinders it, is still to move, at least in a certain manner, and in another sense it is not precisely to move. For example, if one removes the column which supports something, or if one takes away a stone which is upon a wineskin in the water, it is still to move indirectly (or <i>accidentally</i>, <i>ϰατὰ συμϐεϐηϰός</i>), just as the ball which is returned is set in motion, not by the wall, but by the player who has cast it.»",
    "fn.2652": "The Ibn-Tibbon edition has the word עניין here, meaning 'thing'; it should be written קניין, meaning 'capacity', as the mss have.",
    "fn.2653": "The author means that, if one seems to attribute to divine action the <i>privations</i>, such as mutism, deafness, and blindness, one should see this only as a figurative expression, which means that God, by an indirect action <i>indirectly</i>, causes the <i>capacities</i> of speaking, hearing, or seeing to cease.",
    "fn.2654": "One might also, says the author, interpret this passage in the sense of a <i>direct</i> action, understanding by this action the creation of a matter which would not be apt to serve as a substratum for the <i>capacities</i> of speech, hearing, or sight; for, if one produces a matter not susceptible of a certain capacity, it may be said of him that he has effected the privation of that same capacity.",
    "fn.2655": "Literally: <i>And one can only say that he has caused the privation by accident or indirectly.</i>",
    "fn.2656": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">by essence</span>, <i>essentially</i>, or <i>essentially</i>, is opposed to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">accidentally</span>, of the previous phrase, and which corresponds to the term of Aristotle (<i>according to the conjunction</i>). For greater clarity, we have used the words <i>directly</i> and <i>indirectly.</i>",
    "fn.2657": "We have slightly modified the construction of this sentence; the text says: ‘… is necessarily an existing thing, whatever action it may be; for its action (i.e., that of the agent) can only be related to something existing.’",
    "fn.2658": "That is to say, that evil possesses no real existence apart from things. See <i>Metaphysics</i>, Book IX, Chapter 9, where Aristotle, speaking of <i>potency</i> and <i>act</i>, says that the latter is superior to the highest <i>potency.</i> Potency compriss the opposites simultaneously, for one and the same thing may hold in potency health and sickness, repose and motion; but opposed <i>acts</i> cannot subsist simultaneously, for one cannot at once possess health and be sick, and consequently, one of the two is the good. As concerns evils, <i>the end</i> (<i>τελος</i>) and the <i>act</i> are necessarily worse than the potency, inasmuch as the latter compriss simultaneously the opposite or the good. «Whence it follows, says Aristotle, <i>that evil subsists not apart from things</i>, for evil is by its nature posterior to potency. Thus, therefore, in primitive and eternal things, there is neither evil, nor defect, nor aught corrupted, for corruption also forms part of evils.» Δ<i>ῆλον ἄρα ὄτι οὐϰ ἔστι τò ϰαϰòν παρὰ τἀ πράγματα · ὔστερον γἁρ τῇ φύσει τὸ ϰαϰὸν τῆς δυνάμεως · οὐϰ ἄρα οὐδ’ ἐν τοῖς ἐξ ἀρχῆς ϰαὶ τοῖς ἀίδίοις οὐθέν ἐστιν οὔτε ϰαϰòν οὔτε ἁμάρτημα οὔτε διεφθαρμένον · ϰαὶ γἁρ ἡ διαφθορὰ τῶν ϰαϰῶν ἐστίν</i>.",
    "fn.2659": "Literally: <i>that it never lies.</i> Ibn-Tibbon has referred the suffix of <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄדתהא</span> to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄזאיאת</span>, <i>details;</i> he renders: <span dir=\"rtl\">תמצאם שלא יחסר מהם לאחד</span>, thou <i>shalt find that not a single one of them is lacking.</i> Al-’Harîzi renders in the same sense, though more freely: <span dir=\"rtl\">תמצא כי לא ישקרו</span>, <i>thou shalt find that they lie not.</i> It appears more rational unto us, and more conformable to the turn of the phrase, to refer the suffix to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלקצׄיה̈</span>, <i>the thesis.</i>",
    "fn.2660": "That is to say, a certain equilibrium in the humours and throughout the entire constitution of the body, an equilibrium which is sometimes disordered by excesses. See Galen, <i>Commentary</i> <i>ad Hippocratis aphorismos</i>, II, 4: <i>τῆς γὰρ ὑγίεὶας συμμετρίας οὔσης, ϰ. τ. λ</i>. <i>Commentary II in Hippocratis l. I Epidemiorum</i> (Kühn's edition, vol. XVII, 1<sup>st</sup> part., p. 97): <i>τῆς γὰρ ὑγιείας ἐϰ συμμετρίας γινομένης τῶν τεττάρων στοιχείων</i>. <i>De Humoribus</i>, toward the close (vol. XIX, p. 491): <i>ἔοιϰε δἐ τὴν ὑγίειαν χαραϰτηριζεσθαι τῇ τούτων ἰσότητι τε ϰαὶ συμμετρότητι</i>. Cf. Aristotle, <i>Problemata</i>, I, 3: <i>ἡ δε ὑγίεία ἰσότης</i>.",
    "fn.2661": "That is to say, that <i>symmetry</i> is a term that indicates a <i>relation</i>; for what is said to be <i>symmetrical</i>, or in balance, is so only in relation to something else. Consequently, the disease, which is nothing other than the lack of symmetry or proportion in the constitution, is not something positive, directly created by God.",
    "fn.2662": "See vol. I, chap. <small>XVII</small>, p. 69, and above, at the beginning of chap. <small>VIII</small>.",
    "fn.2663": "That is, the sublunar matter, which is the seat of birth and corruption. In celestial bodies, which have a different matter, there is nothing perishable.",
    "fn.2664": "As the author has said in several places, matter never ceases to strip itself of a form in order to take on another. Thus, there is a perpetual birth (<i>γένεσις</i>) in matter, with individual forms <i>succeeding</i> one another without interruption.",
    "fn.2665": "See <i>Beréschith rabbâ</i>, section 9 (fol. 7, col. 3). Rabbi Méir, intending doubtless to link to this passage a moral reflection upon death, which conducts man to the future life, appears to play upon the assonance of the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מאד</span>, <small>MEOD</small> (much, very), and <span dir=\"rtl\">מות</span>, <small>MAWS</small> or <small>MÔTH</small> (death). He was doubtless far removed from the thought ascribed to him here by Maimonides.",
    "fn.2666": "See <i>Beréchîth rabbâ</i>, sect. 51 (fol. 45, col. 4): <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר ר׳ חנינא אין דבר רע יורד מלמעלה</span>.",
    "fn.2667": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <i>great</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אלעטׄימה̈</span>) is missing; the mss. of this version have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלו הרעות הגדולות</span>.",
    "fn.2668": "The text reads: «each individual.… <i>does unto himself</i> and unto others evils etc.» It is observed that the construction is irregular and that there is here a species of <i>anacoluthon;</i> for the subject, <i>the parties</i>, remains without a verb. To render the phrase more regular, one should say: «likewise, <i>as concerns</i> the parties among men, each individual, according to the measure of his ignorance, does unto himself etc.» One has somewhat modified the construction of the phrase, by rendering the words <i>the parties</i> the subject of the verb <i>inflict upon themselves</i>.",
    "fn.2669": "Literally: <i>the afflictions</i> (which are) <i>serious in relation to the individuals of the species</i>.",
    "fn.2670": "That is, to which the specific form of man serves as a substratum, just as the eye serves as a substratum to the faculty of vision.",
    "fn.2671": "Ibn-Tibbon has the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חידות</span> here, which means <i>énigmes</i> or <i>èpigrammes</i>; but the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">خطب</span> should be rendered in Hebrew as <span dir=\"rtl\">מליצות</span>.",
    "fn.2672": "Literally: <i>in the time</i> or <i>the age</i>. The Arabian orators and poets to whom the author here alludes do often employ the word <span dir=\"rtl\">زمان</span>, <i>time</i>, to designate the finite period which man passes upon earth, the terrestrial life, the vicissitudes and fortune; and they do oft speak of the perfidy and illusions of time. The Hebrew poets of the Middle Ages employ in the same sense the word <span dir=\"rtl\">זמן</span>; one shall recall but this well-known verse of Ibn-Gebirol: <span dir=\"rtl\">זמן בוגד אסרני בפידו</span> \"Time (or <i>fortune</i>) <i>perfidious has enchained me by its misfortune</i>.\"",
    "fn.2673": "It is here a question of the celebrated physician known in the Middle Ages under the name of <i>Rhazes;</i> his Arabian name was: Abou-Becr Mo’hammed ben-Zacariyya <i>al-Râzi;</i> he was one of the principal physicians to the Abbasid Caliph Al-Moktadir, rendered himself known by a prodigious number of writings on medicine, mathematics, and philosophy, and died in the year 320 of the Hegira (932) or, according to others, in 311 (923). Concerning his life and writings, see d’Herbelot, <i>Bibliothèque orientale</i>, fol. ed., p. 713; Casiri, <i>Biblioth. arab. hisp.</i>, vol. I, p. 262 et seq.; Wüstenfeld, <i>Geschichte der Arabischen Ærzte</i>, p. 40 et seq. His worth as a philosopher was not of great moment, and his philosophical writings are scarcely ever cited as authority. It is perceived, from our passage, that Maimonides held the work of theology or metaphysics composed by Al-Râzi in very low esteem. In his letter to Rabbi Samuel Ibn-Tibbon, our author likewise speaks with much disdain of this work: <span dir=\"rtl\">וספר חכמה אלהית שחבר אלראזי אין בו תועלת לפי שאלראזי היה רופא בלבד</span> \"The book of metaphysics composed by Al-Râzi has no utility, for Al-Râzi was but a physician.\" See <i>Lettres de Maïmonide</i>, Amsterdam ed., fol. 14 <i>b</i>. — A Spanish Muslim author, the Kadhi Çâ’id, cited by Ibn-Abi-Océibi’a, pronouncs upon Al-Râzi the same judgment as Maimonides. Thus does he express himself concerning this physician:",
    "fn.2674": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">رنانة</span>  means: total absence or paralysis of a limb. See the Arabic commentary by Silv. de Sacy on the sessions of Hariri, p. 273, at the bottom: <span dir=\"rtl\">والزمانة عدم بعض الاعضاء وتعطيل القُوى</span> . Ibn-Tibbon has accurately rendered this word by <span dir=\"rtl\">בטול האבריס</span>; Al-’Harîzi has translated it more vaguely by <span dir=\"rtl\">החלייס</span>.",
    "fn.2675": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">מגמילות חסדי י״י למציאותו וטובתו המבוארת</span>; but several MSS. which one has consulted lack the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וטובתו</span>. It is manifest that Ibn-Tibbon had at first erred concerning the sense of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄודה</span>, which he pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">وُجُودَةٌ</span>. Having then perceived that the initial <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span> of this word was copulative and that one ought to pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">וُجُודِهِ</span>, he replaced the word <span dir=\"rtl\">למציאותו</span> with <span dir=\"rtl\">וטובתו</span>. The copyists, as one has already observed in many other passages, reproduced both the error and the correction. Al-’Harîzi translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">…כי הבורא טוב ומטיב וטובתו ידועה על בני אדם</span>",
    "fn.2676": "That is, in relation to the Intelligences, celestial spheres, elements, and species of animals, which are the only immutable things and are not subject to what the common people call evil.",
    "fn.2677": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">וכל מא גׄא ··· קדרה</span> (literally: <i>and all that in the texts of the prophetic books is found concerning this important subject, of great utility etc.</i>) are connected to the preceding Biblical citations. In Ibn-Tibbon’s version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">גדול התועלת</span> may be considered as an attribute of the subject <span dir=\"rtl\">וכל מה שבא</span>, so that one might translate: \"<i>is</i> of great utility,\" although in such a case it would have been better to say: <span dir=\"rtl\">הוא גדול התועלת</span>; but in the Arabic text, which bears <span dir=\"rtl\">אלעטׄים אלפאידה̈</span>, with the article, these words are recognized as a simple appositive. Al-’Harîzi has avoided all equivocation by translating: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר תועלתו גדולה</span>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יגלט</span> commence a new sentence, and it is inappropriately that Ibn-Tibbon has connected them to the preceding by translating: <span dir=\"rtl\">ושלא יטעה</span>; one ought to have said <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יטעה</span>.",
    "fn.2678": "That is to say: the Intelligences of the spheres, or the angels, are of an essence so different from that of man, that one cannot establish any proportion between these two species of beings.— Instead of the words <span dir=\"rtl\">איך יהיה ערך על דרך האמת</span>, which are read in the editions of the translation of Ibn-Tibbon, the mss. have: <span dir=\"rtl\">אין ערך על האמת</span>.",
    "fn.2679": "Literally: <i>the noblest of that which has</i> <small>BECOME</small> or <small>BEEN BORN</small>. Ibn-Tibbon’s version has neglected the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">חבון</span>, which denots <i>birth</i> or <i>contingency</i> (<i>γένεσις</i>); the exact translation would be: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואמנם האדם הוא נכבד מבל מה שנתהוה וזה בעולמינו התחתון</span>. In the same version, one must repeat after <span dir=\"rtl\">ר״ל</span> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">נכבד</span>, which is found in the MSS. and has been omitted in the editions.",
    "fn.2680": "We have translated this difficult verse in the sense that seems to be attributed to Maimonides and which responds to the idea he has just expressed on the evils that afflict human individuals.",
    "fn.2681": "The text bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">פי אצל אלגׄבלה̈</span>, <i>in the primitive formation</i>, which ’Harîzi has exactly rendered as <span dir=\"rtl\">בעיקר הבריאה</span>. Ibn-Tibbon’s version (even in the MSS.) bears <span dir=\"rtl\">בכלל היצירה</span>, which can only be a simple <i>lapsus;</i> for elsewhere Ibn-Tibbon renders the words in question as <span dir=\"rtl\">בעקר הבריאה</span>. See, for example, Part II<sup>e</sup>, chapter <small>XXXVI</small>: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי כשיהיה איש מן האנשים עצם מוחו בעקר בריאתו על תכלית שוויו.</span>",
    "fn.2682": "Ibn-Tibbon's version has <span dir=\"rtl\">מאויר הנפםד</span>; one should write, according to the mss., <span dir=\"rtl\">כהפםד האויר</span>, as also the version of Al-’Harîzi does.",
    "fn.2683": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">بدو أعمق</span> , plural of <span dir=\"rtl\">صاعقة</span> , see vol. II, p. 331, note 3.",
    "fn.2684": "On the meaning of the word خسوف (plur. of خسف), see the t. I, p. 369, note 1. Al-’Harîzi, confusing the written form of <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄםוף</span> (with a <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄ</span> punctuated) with <span dir=\"rtl\">כםוף</span> (كُسوف), awkwardly translated: <span dir=\"rtl\">לקות המאורות</span>, <i>the eclipses</i>.",
    "fn.2685": "That is to say, that particular forms should succeed one another in matter, so that the latter, in order to assume one form, should divest itself of another; for in this consist the generation and corruption of things. See vol. I, chap. <small>XVII</small>, p. 60, and <i>passim</i>, and above, at the commencement of chap. <small>VIII</small>, and at the end of chap. <small>X</small>.",
    "fn.2686": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must erase the word <span dir=\"rtl\">the present</span> preceding <span dir=\"rtl\">it is drawn</span> which is not found in the mss.",
    "fn.2687": "This somewhat obscure passage has not been, one apprehends, entirely understood by Ephodi, the sole commentator who occupies himself therewith, nor by Buxtorf, who has reproduced his explication. The translation of Mr. Scheyer likewise leavs much to be desired. The following appears to one to be the simplest sense of this passage: Whosoever, being yet of flesh and bone, that is to say, a mere mortal, nevertheless desirs to be shielded from external impressions which produce ills, that one, I aver, seeks to unite within himself two contrary things; for he would fain be at once a mortal, subject to impressions, and a being unimpressible, like unto the celestial bodies. Evidently, were he like unto the latter, shielded from every external impression, he would not be a being subject to <i>contingency;</i> on the contrary, he would be, like those celestial bodies, which are neither <i>generated</i> nor perishable, and like them, he would be unique of his species and would not be simply one of the individuals of a single species.",
    "fn.2688": "The author here intends the work of Galen, which bears in the Arabic version the title of <span dir=\"rtl\">كتاب منافع الاعضاء</span> , <i>Book of the Uses of the Members</i>. This is the treatise which in the Greek original is entitled: Π<i>εpὶ χρείας τῶν ἐν ἀνθρώπου σώματι μορίων</i> (Concerning the use of parts of the human body). In Book III, chap. 10 (Kühn's edition, vol. III, p. 238), one reads the following passage: Σ<i>ϰόπει γάρ μοι τὴν ὕλην, ἐξ ἧς ἕϰαστον ἐγένετο, ϰαὶ μὴ μάτην ἐλπίσῃς, ἐϰ ϰαταμηνίου ϰαὶ σπέρματος ἀθάνατον δύνασθαι συστῆναι ζῶον, ἢ ἀπαθὲς, ἢ ἀειϰίνητον, ἢ λαμπρòν οὕτω ϰαὶ ϰαλòν ὡς ἥλιον</i>.",
    "fn.2689": "That is to say, the evil of <i>mortality</i>. Ibn-Tibbon, one apprehends, has not rightly apprehended the sense of this passage. He renders it thus: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואי אפשר לזה המין מבלתי רע שימצא לו</span>, \"it is therefore impossible that this species (that is to say, the human species) should not be subject to evil.\" The Arabic text does not admit of this sense, for it would have been necessary to say: <span dir=\"rtl\">פלא בד להדׄא אלנוע מן שר יוגׄד לה</span>. Al-’Harîzi appears to have better apprehended the sense; he renders it: <span dir=\"rtl\">אם כן אי אפשר שלא ימצא זה המין ממיני ההזק</span>.",
    "fn.2690": "The author is referring to the evils that strike man insofar as he is a mortal being, and which prevent him from reaching the natural term of his existence; and he notes that the causes of accidental death are relatively very rare.",
    "fn.2691": "Word for word: <i>it is born infirm only by anomaly</i>. In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">על דרך פלא ועל צד זרות</span> appear to be a double translation of the Arabic adverb <span dir=\"rtl\">שאדׄא</span>.",
    "fn.2692": "All the Arabic mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ללמטׄלום</span>, <i>the one who is oppressed</i>; it is evident that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לעשות</span>, in the version of Ibn-Tibbon, is an ancient copying error, and that one should write <span dir=\"rtl\">לעשׁוּק</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">but a man who is oppressed has no power to repel them</span>.",
    "fn.2693": "Concerning the sense of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אחרי</span> , see vol. I, p. 300, note 2. Ibn-Tibbon here renders it by the adverb <span dir=\"rtl\">מאד</span>, which is not exact; perchance one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">מאדי</span>, an adjective formed by Ibn-Tibbon to render the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אכתׄרי</span>. See, for example, Part II, at the commencement of chap. <small>XX</small>: <span dir=\"rtl\">אינם תמידיים ולא מאודיים</span>.",
    "fn.2694": "Ibn-Tibbon, who translates <span dir=\"rtl\">and this is the majority</span>, may have read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאכתׄר</span>, as some mss. indeed do; but most mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאכתׄרי</span>, a form of which we spoke in the preceding note.",
    "fn.2695": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ילאם אלמצאב עליה באלחקיقه̈</span>, a manuscript from the Imperial Library, supplement No. 63, has <span dir=\"rtl\">ילאם אלמצאב עלי מא יגׄתניה</span>; according to this reading, one ought to translate: <i>those who are afflicted thereby deserve to be blamed for the sin which they have committed</i>. It is this reading which Al-’Harîzi appears to render, for he translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא שראוי להאשים מי שבאתהו התלאה מפני שגרם אותה לעצמו</span>.",
    "fn.2696": "We have translated the last words of the verse according to the sense that Maimonides attributes to them, and which he makes known by using the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאפכאר</span>, <i>thoughts</i>.",
    "fn.2697": "Maimonides diverts these words from their true meaning, and understands them in the sense that man is, by nature, the creator of his own sufferings.",
    "fn.2698": "Literally: <i>all kinds of diseases and bodily and mental afflictions</i>.",
    "fn.2699": "One must recall that not only the vital soul, but also the rational faculty and the <i>hylic intellect</i>, are considered by our author as inherent forms of the body and perishable. See vol. I, p. 146, and p. 328, note 1.",
    "fn.2700": "See Vol. II, chap. <small>XXXVI</small>, pp. 281-282, and p. 284; <i>Huit Chapitres</i>, at the beginning of chap. <small>VIII</small>. Cf. Aristotle, <i>Treatise on the Soul</i>, Book I, chap. <small>I</small><sup>er</sup> (§ 11): For it is also necessary to consider that all the affections of the soul are with the body, etc.</i>",
    "fn.2701": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">مكة</span> , which corresponds to Aristotle’s word <i>ἕξις</i>, designats, as one has stated elsewhere, a disposition become durable and firm, and may be rendered at times by capacity and at times by habit. See vol. I, p. 195, notes 1 and 2. Ibn-Tibbon, who ordinarily renders it by <span dir=\"rtl\">קניין</span>, here renders it by <span dir=\"rtl\">טבע חזק</span>, <i>a firm natural disposition</i>. Al-’Harîzi’s version has: <span dir=\"rtl\">ותשיג קניה בתשוקתה למה שאין לו צורך בן</span>, a translation which one can scarcely comprehend without an understanding of the Arabic text.",
    "fn.2702": "According to Ibn-Tibbon’s version, one ought to translate: <i>or of all that which it is possible to find;</i> but the words <span dir=\"rtl\">או מכל</span> appearing in the editions are doubtless faulty. The manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">ומכל מה</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">מכל מה</span>, and Al-’Harîzi’s version <span dir=\"rtl\">בכל מה</span>; but most of the Arabic manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">كل מא</span>. The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄודה</span> referrs to each of the two words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלזמרד ואליאקות</span>; it would have been more regular to write <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄודהמא</span>.",
    "fn.2703": "Allusion to the words of Jacob, <i>Genesis</i>, chapter <small>XXVIII</small>, verse 20.",
    "fn.2704": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">حَوَاصِلُ</span>, plural of <span dir=\"rtl\">حاصل</span>, signifies <i>that which one has obtained</i> or <i>gained, that which one possesses</i> (Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">הקניניס</span>). In lieu of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלחואצל</span>, certain manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלאחואל</span>; this variant, which offers no very precise sense, has been reproduced in the version of Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויכלו הכחות ויאבדו הענינים</span>.",
    "fn.2705": "Word for word: <i>one must consider our conditions with regard to finding;</i> that is to say: one must consider the greater or lesser ease or difficulty which one has in finding the things one desirs to possess. It is manifest, by that which follows, that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלוגׄוד</span> is not here employed in its philosophical sense of <i>being</i> or of <i>universe</i>, but in its primitive sense of <i>finding;</i> it is doubtless in the same sense that one must here take the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מציאות</span> in Ibn-Tibbon’s version, where one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">ענינינו</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ענינו</span>, albeit this latter reading is also found in the manuscripts. The manuscript of Saadia ibn-Danan (Suppl. hébr., n° 63) has here a variant which deservs to be noted, inasmuch as it has been reproduced by Al-’Harîzi; therein one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">פי אלוגׄוד</span> (read <span dir=\"rtl\">האן</span> ) <span dir=\"rtl\">וינבגי אן יעתבר אעתבאר תׄאני</span>; the version of Al-’Harîzi bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">וצדיך שנתבונן התבוננות שניה במציאות</span>.",
    "fn.2706": "One finds these same reflections, almost in the same terms, in a passage from Ba’hya, <i>Devoirs des cœurs</i>, book II, at the end of chap. 5, which Maïmonide appears to have had before his eyes.",
    "fn.2707": "All mss. read: <span dir=\"rtl\">אמא אלמא פקד יבקי אליום ואליומין</span>, <i>as for water, he</i> (man) <i>can survive for a day or two</i>. It is necessary to imply <i>without it</i>, or <i>without drinking it</i>, and Ibn-Tibbon supplemented this ellipsis by adding the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בלעדיו</span>; similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">יתכן לעמוד בלתו</span>.",
    "fn.2708": "We had to suppress, in the translation, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יגתדׄי</span>, to avoid the pleonasm: <span dir=\"rtl\">דון גדׄא</span>.",
    "fn.2709": "Our manuscripts generally bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ללתטבב</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">ללטבב</span>, and this reading is confirmed by the version of Ibn-Tibbon, which has <span dir=\"rtl\">לרפואה</span>. Ibn-Falaquéra read <span dir=\"rtl\">ליטיב</span>, which he renders by <span dir=\"rtl\">להתבשם</span>, <i>to perfume oneself</i>. See Appendix of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 157. Al-’Harîzi appears to have had the same reading; but he here takes the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">طالب</span> in the sense of <i>to enjoy, to amuse oneself</i>, and he renders: <span dir=\"rtl\">להנאה ולתענוג</span>, <i>for pleasure and enjoyment</i>. But the reading of our manuscripts, confirmed by Ibn-Tibbon, presents no difficulty whatsoever; for the four substances in question were indeed employed as remedies, whether true or imaginary, against certain maladies. See, in the Dictionary of Simple Medicaments by Ibn-Béitar, translated into German by M. Jos. de Sontheimer (Stuttgart, 1840, 2 vol. gr. in-8°), the articles <i>Moschus</i>, tome II, p. 515; <i>Ambra</i>, tome II, p. 210; <i>Smaragdus</i>, tome I, p. 537, and <i>Hyacinthus</i>, tome II, p. 591-592. Maimonides himself, in a treatise composed, by order of the Kâdhi al-Fadhel, concerning the treatment of venomous bites and of those who have taken poison (book I, chap. 3), mentions the powder of <i>emerald</i> as one of the most efficacious antidotes. See manuscript n° 411 of the ancient collection of the Imperial Library, fol. 130 <i>b</i>. This manuscript contains the Arabic original in Hebrew characters.",
    "fn.2710": "Ibn-Tibbon renders: <span dir=\"rtl\">זהו פרסום גמילות חסדי הש״י למציאותו</span>. One observs that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄודה</span>, which one supposs should be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">وَجُودِهِ</span>, Ibn-Tibbon pronounced it <span dir=\"rtl\">וُجُודَهُ</span>; but, had the author intended to speak of God’s bounty towards the <i>universe</i>, he would have said <span dir=\"rtl\">עֲלֵי וְגָ֞דָה</span>; for the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">أفضل</span> requirs the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">علي</span>. Cf. above, p. 68, note 1. — One shall further remark that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תבייו</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">הבו</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon renders by <span dir=\"rtl\">פרסום</span>, is wanting in several manuscripts, in which it is replaced by the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">מן</span>; likewise Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">וזהו מחסדי הבורא וטובתו</span>.",
    "fn.2711": "All Arabic manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">among them</span>, and similarly the versions of Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi, <span dir=\"rtl\">among them</span>, <i>among them</i>; the plural suffix irregularly refers to the preceding word <span dir=\"rtl\">life</span>, which is often used as a collective, although here it is clearly singular.",
    "fn.2712": "Literally: <i>the natural birth and corruption;</i> that is, the natural laws governing beings subject to birth and corruption.",
    "fn.2713": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read: <span dir=\"rtl\">מכל שאר</span>; manuscripts have more correctly: <span dir=\"rtl\">משאר</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">שאר</span>, as well as the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">סאיר</span>, have here the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">כל</span>. Cf. Volume II of this work, p. 318, note 5, and p. 334, note 5.",
    "fn.2714": "The author alludes to the three kinds of faculties admitted by ancient physicians. See vol. I, p. 355, note 1.",
    "fn.2715": "For the understanding of this passage, see vol. I, chap. LXXII, pp. 364-365. On the meaning of the word תפאצׄל, see ibid., p. 365, note 3.",
    "fn.2716": "That is to say: That which causs there to be no essential difference between individuals is the nature inherent in the general matter of the species, in which all individuals of one and the same species participate equally. In <span dir=\"rtl\">מא קצר בה</span>, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בה</span> has a neutral sense; one would better understand <span dir=\"rtl\">בהא</span>, in the feminine, referring to <span dir=\"rtl\">טביעה̈</span>, <i>the nature;</i> but all manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">בה</span>. For the first <span dir=\"rtl\">שכׄץ</span>, most manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">שכׄצא</span>, in the accusative; but <span dir=\"rtl\">קצד</span> is evidently a passive verb <span dir=\"rtl\">(قَصَدَ)</span> of which <span dir=\"rtl\">שכׄץ</span> is the subject. As for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מא</span>, Ibn-Tibbon does rightly take it in the negative sense by rendering: <span dir=\"rtl\">ל א כוון בו איש מבלתי איש</span>. Al-’Harîzi, neglecting the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דון</span>, renders: <span dir=\"rtl\">ממה שהיתה הכוונה בו לאיש ואיש</span>.",
    "fn.2717": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">نافجة</span>  (plur. <span dir=\"rtl\">فواج</span> ), derived from the Persian <span dir=\"rtl\">نافة</span> , designates the kind of bladder or pouch that the musk animal has near the navel and in which the musk liquid seeps.",
    "fn.2718": "That is, his person does not lose any value. Ibn-Tibbon translates inaccurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא זה ··· חסר דבר מחויב</span>, 'and he who etc. <i>does not lack a necessary thing</i>.'",
    "fn.2719": "Literally: <i>according to its gradation</i>, that is, making things more or less accessible to each individual, according to their greater or lesser necessity.",
    "fn.2720": "This is how the author sometimes designates Moses, when the latter proclaims not a fact that has been revealed to him, but a high philosophical truth. Cf. the t. I, at the beginning of the chapter LIV, p. 216, note 2. The editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have here <span dir=\"rtl\">אדון הנביאים</span>; but the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אדון החכמים</span>.",
    "fn.2721": "See volume I, page 363, note 5.",
    "fn.2722": "Ibn-Tibbon neglected the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אדׄהאן</span>, <i>the minds</i>, and he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">הרבה נבוכו השלמים</span>; the editors, taking <span dir=\"rtl\">הרבה</span>, <i>often</i>, in the sense of <i>much</i>, deemed it necessary to add the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">מן</span>, which is not found in the MSS. The version of Al-’Harîzi has, conformably to the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">דעות השלמים</span>; likewise Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 119): <span dir=\"rtl\">הרבה נבוכו שכלי השלמים</span>. — The author in this chapter addresses the question of the ultimate end of the universe, with a view to demonstrate how grave is the error of those who consider man as the aim of creation, and attribute to the latter an importance which it is far from possessing.",
    "fn.2723": "Word for word: <i>this question falls;</i> that is to say, the question of the ultimate end of the universe is insoluble for philosophers, proponents of the eternity of the world, as it is for believers who admit of creation <i>ex nihilo</i> out of nothing. As the author will state further on, the true final cause resides in <i>God;</i> according to the philosophers, it is divine <i>wisdom</i>, and according to believers, it is divine <i>will</i>.",
    "fn.2724": "Cf. tome II, chap. XX, p. 167: ‘The idea of design and that of determination apply only to a thing that does not yet exist, and which may exist or not exist as projected or determined.’",
    "fn.2725": "See the Introduction to the II<sup>nd</sup> part, XX<sup>th</sup> proposition.",
    "fn.2726": "This third proposition necessarily follows from the two former, which serve as its premises. The final cause presupposes an agent acting with intention; that which is made with intention had no prior existence. Consequently, that which has always existed is in no wise a thing made with intention and possesses no final cause. Thus, as the author will presently set forth, one cannot inquire what is the final cause of God's existence, and according to the Peripatetics, who admit the eternity of the world, one cannot assign to it any other final cause than <i>divine wisdom</i>. As has been set forth elsewhere (Part I, chap. <small>LXIX</small>), God is at once the efficient cause, the formal cause, and the final cause of the universe.",
    "fn.2727": "The Arabic text bears, in most of the MSS., (read <span dir=\"ltr\">דׄי</span>) <span dir=\"rtl\">בקצד דׄו עקל</span>. But the version of Ibn-Tibbon and the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 119, bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">בכוונת התחלת שכל</span>, <i>by the intention of an intelligent principle</i>. Al-’Harîzi employs nearly the same terms, which one finds only in a single Arabic MS. (Suppl. hébr. No. 63), which bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">בקצד מבדא עקלי</span>.",
    "fn.2728": "According to the opinion of Aristotle, states the author, all ultimately emanates from eternal and immutable laws, established by divine wisdom. God, as is stated elsewhere, is the ultimate end, the end of ends, and consequently one could not seek another ultimate end of the universe. Vide vol. I, chap. <small>LXIX</small>, pp. 321-322. It is of this ultimate end that the present discussion treats, and not of the particular final aim which one seeks in various beings, as will presently be stated.",
    "fn.2729": "Vide, for example, <i>Phys.</i>, Book II, chap. <small>VIII</small>, where Aristotle demonstrates, by the regularity of natural phenomena, that everything in nature has a certain end, and that nature, obeying eternal laws, is not the effect of chance: Ἕ<i>στιν ἀρα τὸ ἕνεϰά του ἐν τοῖς φύσει γινομένοις ϰαὶ οὖσιν, ϰ. τ. λ.</i> End of the chapter: <i>Ὅτι μὲν οὖν αἰτία, ἡ φύσις, ϰαὶ οὕτως ως ἕνεϰά του, φανερόν</i>.",
    "fn.2730": "The why, or the final cause, is the good, and ought to be regarded as that which is best in things: Τ<i>ὸ γὰρ οὗ ἕνεϰα βέλτιστου ϰαὶ τέλος τῶν ἄλλων ἐθέλει εἶναι</i>. <i>Physics</i>, Book II, chap. <small>III</small>. Cf. <i>Metaphysics</i>, Book V, chap. <small>II</small>. — It follows from the manner in which this sentence is phrased in our text that, according to Maimonides, Aristotle himself would have stated that the final cause is unknown in most <i>species</i>. But one does not find that Aristotle has anywhere pronounced himself in this sense, and moreover, the author himself states further on that, according to Aristotle, the <i>end</i> of species resides in generation and corruption. This difficulty vanishes if, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואן הדׄא</span>, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">והדׄא</span>, as several MSS. indeed have; this variant has also been adopted by Ibn-Tibbon, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וזאת הםבה התכליית … תעלם ברוב המינים</span>. In accordance with this, one must omit the conjunction <i>that</i> in our translation and render: 'but this final cause, etc.', so that Maimonides would here express his own opinion, or that of Ibn-Sînâ, and not that of Aristotle.",
    "fn.2731": "See vol. II of this work, p. 119, note 4.",
    "fn.2732": "See Politics, book I, chapter VIII: Ὥ<i>στε ὁμοίως δῆλον ὅτι ϰαὶ γενομένοις οἰητέον τὰ τε φυτὰ τῶν ζώων ἕνεϰεν εἶναι, ϰαὶ τὰ ἄλλα ζῶα τῶν ἀνθρώπων χάριν</i>. Treatise on Plants, book I, chapter II: Κ<i>αὶ τὸ φὺτον οὐϰ ἐδημιουργἠθη εἰ μὴ διὰ τὸ ζῴον</i>.",
    "fn.2733": "See <i>Parts of Animals</i>, book I, chap. 5: Ἐ<i>πεὶ δὲ τὸ μὲν ὄργανον πᾶν ἕνεϰά του, τῶν δὲ τοῦ σώματος μορίων ἕϰαστον ἕνεϰά του, ϰ. τ. λ.</i> In general, the treatise on Parts of Animals contains details on the purpose and destination of each of the organs of the animal body.",
    "fn.2734": "Philosophers, recognizing a final cause in every physical thing, were forced to ascend the series of causes and thus arrive at a final end, which is the intelligent or divine principle, the absolute cause of all these intermediate causes, each existing for the sake of another. See Metaphysics, book II, chapter 2, and book XII, chapter 7.",
    "fn.2735": "That is to say, in order for there to be <i>design</i>, it must be a thing called into existence after not having existed. Cf. vol. II, at the beginning of chapter <small>XIX</small>, p. 145.",
    "fn.2736": "Vide <i>Physics</i>, Book II, Chapter <small>VII</small>: Ἔ<i>ρχεται δὲ τὰ τρία εὶς τὸ ἓν πολλἁϰις • τὸ μὲν γὰρ τί ἐστι ϰαι̂ τὸ οὗ ἕνεϰα ἕν ἐστι, τὸ δ’ὅθεν ἡ ϰίνησις πρῶτον τῷ εἴδει ταὐτὸ τούτοις • ἄνθρωπος γὰρ ἄνθρωπον γεννᾷ</i>. «Oft-times do three of these causes reduce themselves to one. Thus the essence and the final cause are united; and moreover, the cause whence the initial motion proceeds is <i>specifically</i> confounded with these two: as, for instance, man engenders man.» (Trans. by Mr. Saint-Hilaire.)",
    "fn.2737": "That is to say: it is only the proximate final cause <i>prochaine</i>.",
    "fn.2738": "All the manuscripts of the Arabic text bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי לא בד מנה</span>. The pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי</span> and the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">מנה</span> necessarily refer to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דואם</span>, <i>permanence</i>. According to Ibn-Tibbon's version, which bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר אי אפשר מבלעדיהם</span>, one should translate: <i>which are indispensable</i>, that is to say: generation and corruption. Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 120) reproduces Ibn-Tibbon's version, which is in no wise conformable to the Arabic text.",
    "fn.2739": "The text of this passage is somewhat obscure. The author intends to convey that, according to Aristotle, the final aim of each species of being is to attain to the production of that which is most perfect within the species; and this final aim is achieved by means of generation and corruption, which perpetuate themselves, individual forms continuously succeeding one another, until matter is invested with the most perfect form. One shall presently observe that the author's reasoning aims to prove this: the final aim of species being to arrive, through a series of generations and corruptions, at that which is most perfect in each species, and thence at the most perfect species, which is the human species, there is no further occasion, once this species has been reached, to seek another final aim within the world, and consequently one cannot seek the final aim of the universe as a whole. This latter final aim can only be sought in a principle external to the universe; this principle is God (<i>ὅ τε γἀρ θεὸϛ δοϰεῖ τῶν αἰτίοιν πᾶσιν εἶναι ϰαὶ ἀρχή τιϛ</i>`.` <i>Metaph.</i>, I, 2). According to the philosopher, it is <i>divine wisdom</i>; according to the believer, it is <i>divine will</i>.",
    "fn.2740": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">יטלב בה</span>, literally: <i>by which one seeks</i>, have been translated in Ibn-Tibbon's version as <span dir=\"rtl\">שיקוה להמצא בה</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">that which is sought in it from the perfection</span>.",
    "fn.2741": "The author alludes to the influence that the stars would have on all parts of the sublunar world, of which man, according to this opinion, is the most perfect being and the final cause. Cf. the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chap. <small>X</small>.",
    "fn.2742": "That is to say: I have created heaven and earth, with the laws that govern them, solely for the sake of My covenant with Israel and the law I have given to this people. One has translated this verse in the sense evidently attached to it by our author, and which is indicated in a passage of the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Pesa’hîm</i>, fol. 68 <i>b</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">אילמלא תורה לא נתקיימו שמים וארץ</span>; Rashi, in his commentary on Jeremiah, mentions this Talmudic explanation, observing with good reason that it does not accord with the ensuing discourse of Jeremiah.",
    "fn.2743": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">סאיר</span> has here the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">כל</span>. Cf. volume II, p. 318, note 5, and p. 334, note 5.",
    "fn.2744": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">הדׄה אלגאיה̈ אלתי הי וגׄוד אלאנסאן</span> appear to one to commence the interrogative sentence and to form, with that which follows, a species of anacoluthon. The author would have expressed himself more regularly by saying: <span dir=\"rtl\">אדׄא כאנת הדׄה אלגאיה̈ וגׁוד אלאנסאן</span>, and it is in this sense that one has translated. Ibn-Tibbon, conjoining these words with what precedes, has added a few explanatory words; he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא שיאמר למי שיאמין זה שהכל מפני זאת התכלית כלומר מציאות האדם</span>. Al-’Harîzi has suppressed, after <span dir=\"rtl\">הדׄא</span>, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הדׄה</span>, which he deemed superfluous; he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">למי שמאמין בזאת התכלית שהיא מציאות האדם</span>. The difficulty which one has pointed out has been the cause of certain variants. In some manuscripts, one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">למעתקד הדׄא להדׄה אלגאיה̈ וגו׳</span>; the Leiden manuscript, No. 221, bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">למעתקד הדׄה גאיתה אלגאיה̈ אלתי הי ובו׳</span>, a reading entirely corrupted. Most manuscripts, and the best among them, present the reading which one has adopted.",
    "fn.2745": "That is, it was possible that man was created immediately and directly, without any other creation preceding him.",
    "fn.2746": "All the Arabic manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">פאידתה</span> with a suffix, <i>its utility</i>, and likewise the manuscripts of Ibn-Tibbon's version: <span dir=\"rtl\">תועלתו</span>; the suffix refers to God, and the sense is: what was the utility that God sought in all those things? It is therefore erroneously that the suffix <span dir=\"rtl\">ן</span> has been suppressed in the editions; which Al-’Harîzi also did, translating: <span dir=\"rtl\">אס כן מה התועלת באלה הנבראים וגו׳</span>, <i>what utility, then, was there in all these created things?</i>",
    "fn.2747": "That is to say: all things have been created by the inscrutable will of God, which is determined by itself alone and acts not with regard to anything outside of itself. Cf. Volume I, chapter <small>LXIX</small>, p. 321-322; Volume II, chapter <small>XVIII</small>, 2<sup>e</sup> method, p. 141.",
    "fn.2748": "These words are taken from the ritual for the day of expiations, the prayer of <i>Ne’ilah</i> or of Consecration.",
    "fn.2749": "That is to say: if it be true that all things exist solely for the benefit of man, and that for the existence of the latter, no other final cause can be adduced save the will of God. The author intends to demonstrate that those who profess this opinion are caught in a vicious circle; for, their belief in the novelty of the world compelling them to deny that all things were successively brought forth by an immutable chain of laws, one might ever put to them that question already posed above: To what end served the creation of all these things, which were not themselves the final aim, and which would have been created solely for the benefit of man, who could exist without them?",
    "fn.2750": "Literally: <i>that it would have been possible for Him</i> (God) <i>to create the inverse of this being</i> (or <i>universe</i>), <i>in its causes and its effects</i>. The author intends to convey that, according to the belief which admits of creation ex nihilo, nothing being produced by a necessary and immutable law, God could have fashioned the entire universe otherwise than it is; in the various parts of creation, which are the causes and effects one of another, the causes could have been the effects, and the effects the causes.",
    "fn.2751": "It is with the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תלזס</span> that the complement of the sentence commences, and it is by error that the editors of Ibn-Tibbon's version have rendered <span dir=\"rtl\">ותתחייב</span> with a copulative <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תתחייב</span>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תלזם</span>, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">תלזמה</span> with the suffix <span dir=\"rtl\">ה</span>, which, in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, is rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">לן</span>. Al-Ḥarîzi, who likewise read <span dir=\"rtl\">תלזמה</span> and knew not what to make of the suffix <span dir=\"rtl\">ה</span>, conceived the singular idea of supplying Aristotle; he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">יתחייב ארסטו דבה גדולה</span>, which constitutes a grave mistranslation.",
    "fn.2752": "It is worth recalling that by the <i>angles</i>, the author understands the Intelligences of the spheres.",
    "fn.2753": "See the first part of this work, chap. <small>LIII</small> and following.",
    "fn.2754": "See <i>Ibid.</i>, chapter <small>LXIV</small>, pp. 287-288.",
    "fn.2755": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלי פעלה</span>, some manuscripts read: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלי פעלי</span>; based on this, we should translate: <i>all that is attributed to my action;</i> and this is how Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">כל מה שיוחס לפעלתי</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates according to our reading: <span dir=\"rtl\">כל מה שנסמך אלי פעולתו</span>.",
    "fn.2756": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">תורה</span>, which is usually translated as <i>doctrine</i> or <i>Law</i>, comes from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">הורה</span>, <i>to indicate</i> (the path), <i>to guide</i>.",
    "fn.2757": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">menahah</span>, <i>one of them</i>, refers to the works of creation indicated by the <span dir=\"rtl\">account of the beginning</span>.",
    "fn.2758": "See at the beginning of chapter <small>XXVI</small> of the I<sup>re</sup> part (p. 88, note 1), and <i>passim</i>.",
    "fn.2759": "See tome II, chap. XXX[0]XXX[1], p. 243.",
    "fn.2760": "The words  are rendered in the version of Ibn-Tibbon as  ; Al-’Harîzi translates: . On the meaning of the Arabic verb , see t. I, p. 77, n. 5.",
    "fn.2761": "That is to say: one must not believe that by these words it was meant that the stars were created in order to shine on the earth. As the author said elsewhere, it would be absurd to admit that what is higher, more perfect, and more noble exists for the benefit of what is inferior to it. See vol. II, chap. <small>XI</small>, p. 95.",
    "fn.2762": "See above, page 85, note 3.",
    "fn.2763": "See the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chapter <small>X</small>.",
    "fn.2764": "That is, of little value. Ibn-Tibbon renders the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קדרנא</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">our grade</span> (or, according to the manuscripts, by <span dir=\"rtl\">our excellence</span>). Ibn-Falaquéra, <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, p. 121, translates it more accurately by <span dir=\"rtl\">our burden</span> and Al-’Harîzi by <span dir=\"rtl\">our weight</span>.",
    "fn.2765": "See vol. II, p. 18, <small>XIX</small><sup>e</sup> proposition, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 3.",
    "fn.2766": "The Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אעוגׄאגׄ</span> (which Ibn-Tibbon renders here by the two words <span dir=\"rtl\">ascending and curved</span>) properly means <i>curvature, state of what is tortuous</i>, and this is the sense that the author attributes to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">iniquity</span>, which figuratively means <i>iniquity</i>.",
    "fn.2767": "One has scarcely need to observe that the word <i>privation</i> is employed here in the Aristotelian sense of the term <i>στέρησιϛ</i>. It is said of that which is <i>material</i>, that it is associated with privation, for abstract matter is necessarily <i>deprived</i> of all form. Cf. Vol. I, Chap. <small>XVII</small>, p. 69, and above, towards the end of Chap. <small>X</small>. — Ibn-Tibbon's version, which renders <span dir=\"rtl\">התפשטות ההעדר</span>, has been justly censured by Ibn-Falaquéra (Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 157), who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">התחבר ההעדר</span>, and it is in this sense that Ibn-Tibbon himself rendered the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">מקארנה̈</span> in the two passages previously cited. But one believes that both were mistaken in reading here <span dir=\"rtl\">מקארנה̈</span>, with the feminine <span dir=\"rtl\">ה̈</span>, as a noun of action; for, although several manuscripts bear the <span dir=\"rtl\">ה̈</span> punctuated, one deems that it must be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">מקארנה</span>, as a participle, accompanied by the masculine suffix, which refers to the preceding <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי</span>, such that <span dir=\"rtl\">מקארנה</span> is parallel to <span dir=\"rtl\">מִכְלָאָה</span>. Thus did Al-Ḥarîzi understand it, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ד״ל שהוא דבק באפיסה</span>.",
    "fn.2768": "Some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">לם</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ולם</span>, such that the complement of the sentence would commence with the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לם יגלט</span>; but in that case, it were necessary to say <span dir=\"rtl\">לא יגלט</span> and to write a little further on <span dir=\"rtl\">ואסתראח</span> with a copulative <span dir=\"rtl\">ן</span>. The reading <span dir=\"rtl\">ולס יגלט</span> is therefore more correct, and indeed the two Hebrew versions have <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יטעה בה</span> (in the editions, <span dir=\"rtl\">בו</span> is a printer's error).",
    "fn.2769": "In the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, a few words are missing here; in the mss. one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">in order to seek the ultimate purpose for what lacks that purpose</span>.",
    "fn.2770": "Vide Vol. II of this work, p. 187, note 1. One would add that Ptolemy had already measured the distances of the moon and the sun from the earth, taking the earth's radius as the unit. Vide <i>Almagest</i>, Book V, Chaps. <small>XIII</small> and <small>XV</small>. The Arabian astronomers measured in like manner the distances of all the planets and of the sphere of the fixed stars. Vide Mahometis Albatenii, De Scientia stellarum, Cap. <small>L</small>, and Al-Farghàni, Elementa astronomica, Cap. <small>XXI</small> (Golius's Edition), pp. 81-82.",
    "fn.2771": "By reckoning the year at 365 1/4 days and the day's journey at 40 miles, one finds for 8,700 years 127,107,000 miles. These miles, as the author states, are those of the <i>Law</i>, that is to say, of the Talmudic tradition, and each comprises 2,000 common cubits, or, as the text expresses it: <i>according to the cubit of usage</i>. According to the Arabs, who assign to the mile 4,000 cubits, or approximately double the legal mile of the Jews, the distance here indicated by Maimonides would be 63,553,500 miles. According to Al-Farghâni (<i>l. c.</i>, chap. <small>XXI</small>, p. 82), the distance from the earth to the summit of Saturn, or to the sphere of the fixed stars, would be 20,110 terrestrial radii (the radius being 3,250 miles, <i>ibid.</i>, chap. <small>VIII</small>, p. 31), or 65,357,500 miles. The difference, relatively inconsiderable, between Maimonides' figure and that of Al-Farghâni, will surprise no one, when one considers a calculation so vague, to say nothing of the variation in cubits and miles in different countries and at different epochs.",
    "fn.2772": "The Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">مقعر</span> is here rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קבוב</span>, which one believes to be a typographical error for <span dir=\"rtl\">נבוב</span>, a Biblical word signifying <i>hollow, concave;</i> the manuscripts here have the synonym <span dir=\"rtl\">חלל</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon likewise employs in chap. <small>XXIV</small> of the II<sup>nd</sup> part. Further on, the manuscripts of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">קרקעית שבתי</span>, where the editions have <span dir=\"rtl\">קבוב שבתי</span> (in Arabic <span dir=\"rtl\">הצׄיץׄ זחל</span>).",
    "fn.2773": "All Arabic mss. have the word <span dir=\"rtl\">רסאיל</span> in the plural; Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">אגרת</span> in the singular. In chapter <small>XXIV</small> of the II<sup>e</sup> part (volume II, p. 191), the author cites a treatise on <i>Distances</i> composed by Al-Kabici; here, he may be alluding to several works dealing with the same subject.",
    "fn.2774": "See Voyage, on this hypothesis, vol. II, p. 189-190.",
    "fn.2775": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בעצׄ</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">בעד</span>, <i>distance</i>, so that it would be necessary to translate: 'as one can conclude from the measurement <i>of the distance</i> of its stars.' Although this reading was adopted by Ibn-Tibbon, Al-’Harîzi, and Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 121), we believe that the scientific meaning of this passage makes it unacceptable.",
    "fn.2776": "According to the calculation established by Al-Batâni or Albategnius, the largest of the fixed stars would possess a volume that would contain approximately one hundred and two times that of the earth. Vide <i>De Scientia stellarum</i>, chap. L, p. 199. According to Al-Farghâni, the volume of each star of the first magnitude would contain one hundred and seven times that of the earth. Vide <i>Elementa astronomica</i>, cap. <small>XXII</small> (Golius's edition), p. 84. Ibn-Gebirol likewise states in the <i>Kéther Malkhouth</i>, when speaking of the stars of the eighth sphere: <span dir=\"rtl\">וגוף כל ככב מהם מאה ושבע פעמים כגוף הארץ</span>.",
    "fn.2777": "See tome I, p. 357, note 3, and tome II, p. 151, note 3. — Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">המקיף בכּׂל התנועה היומית</span>. Ibn-Falaquéra, <i>l. c.</i>, translates more accurately and with greater clarity: <span dir=\"rtl\">המסובב הכל</span>, <i>who makes everything move</i>. Similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">המגלגל את כלם בתנועת יום אחד</span>.",
    "fn.2778": "The Arabic text reads <span dir=\"rtl\">לא גוא להא</span> , <i>has no part;</i> that is to say: if the earth is so exceedingly small relatively to the sphere of the fixed stars, of which it forms the centre, that one could not specify in what proportion it stands to this sphere. Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 122) translates literally <span dir=\"rtl\">אין חלק לה</span>. Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi translate according to the sense <span dir=\"rtl\">אין שיעור לה</span>.",
    "fn.2779": "All the Arabic manuscripts read: <span dir=\"rtl\">בחסב נטׄר מא</span>, <i>according to a certain speculation</i> or <i>way of viewing</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's version has: <span dir=\"rtl\">לפי עיוננו</span>, “according to <i>our</i> way of viewing”; that of Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">לפי עיונו</span>, which is perhaps but a copyist's error. — The author doubtless means that from a certain point of view, that is to say, if one considers the trifling value of a needle, it might appear absurd and a matter of poor economy to employ costly instruments for manufacturing a needle; but that nevertheless, this is not an absurdity in the absolute sense, for great instruments can sometimes serve to produce a small tool of great necessity.",
    "fn.2780": "The author intends to convey that the difficulty raised by this objection may serve as an argument in favour of the <i>novelty</i> of the world; for it vanishes, like so many other difficulties encountered within the system of the <i>eternity</i> of the world (vide chaps. <small>XIX</small> and <small>XXII</small> of the II<sup>nd</sup> part), as soon as one admits a creating God producing all things by His free will, the mysteries of which are inaccessible to us.",
    "fn.2781": "The author expresses himself here in an elliptical manner; he means that, apart from the purpose he has just indicated, he had another, that of justifying the doctors against the criticisms they are the object of from certain men who have only superficial knowledge in astronomy.",
    "fn.2782": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אסתגיא</span>, <i>to tax as exaggeration</i>, several manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">אסתבעאד</span>, <i>to declare as implausible;</i> we have preferred the first reading, which is that expressed by Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">שהיה חושב לגוזמא</span>.",
    "fn.2783": "See Talmud of Jerusalem, tractate <i>Berakhôth</i>, chap. I, § 1: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכשם שבין הארץ לרקיע מהלך ת״ק שנה כך בין רקיע לרקיע מהלך ת״ק שנה ועוביו מהלך ת״ק שנה</span>.",
    "fn.2784": "That is, as the doctors count seven spheres, which are those of the planets.",
    "fn.2785": "That is to say: spherical bodies without a star, which, according to Thabit's hypothesis, exist between each pair of spheres.",
    "fn.2786": "Literally: <i>on the contrary, each time it is possible to interpret the words of a person in such a manner as to bring them into accord with the entity whose reality has been demonstrated, this is what is most worthy and most fitting for one who possesses a noble character and loves to be just</i>. — The two superlatives <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאולי ואלאחק</span> are rendered, in Ibn-Tibbon's version, by a single one, <span dir=\"rtl\">יותר ראוי</span>, to which is conjoined the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לעשותו</span>, which stands at the end of the phrase and possesses no equivalent in the Arabic text. For <span dir=\"rtl\">אלפאצׄל אלטבאע</span>, <i>one who possesses a noble character</i>, Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">באדם המעולה</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">לתמימי הטבע</span>.",
    "fn.2787": "That is to say, there are things which, by their very nature, are of an absolute impossibility, and of which it would be absurd to admit the possibility; wherefore one could not attribute to the divine omnipotence itself, which has made them thus, the power to change them. All the examples which the author is about to cite may be referred to the <i>principle of contradiction</i>, placed by Aristotle at the head of his logic. See the treatise <i>l’Herméneia</i> or On Interpretation, chap. <small>VII</small> et seq.; <i>Metaphysics</i>, book IV(Γ), chap. <small>III</small>. The fourth book of the Metaphysics is devoted in great part to the development of this grand principle. — The author seeks in this chapter to determine precisely the nature of the possible and the impossible; this determination, as one shall see, is necessary unto him for the development of his ideas on divine omniscience and on Providence. He demonstrates that whilst certain things are of an impossibility evident to all, there are others where the criterion of the possible and the impossible eludes us, even as certain impossibilities demonstrated by the mathematical sciences could not be comprehended by those who are not well-versed in these sciences.",
    "fn.2788": "On the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">אהל אלנטׄר</span>, see vol. I, p. 184, note 3.",
    "fn.2789": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must substitute the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עליו</span> with the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לשנותו</span>, as the manuscripts have it.",
    "fn.2790": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">الاعيان</span>, signifying the <i>chiefs</i> or the <i>principals</i>, is here evidently employed by Maimonides to designate the two principal constituents of the body, namely, substance and accidents. Nevertheless, according to the <i>Kitâb al-Ta’rifât</i>, this word would particularly designate substances, to the exclusion of accidents. Here follows what is read therein, according to the translation of Silvestre de Sacy (Notices et Extraits des mss., t. X, p. 64-65): “<i>A’yân</i>, that is to say, substances. These are the things which subsist by themselves. When one says, <i>which subsist by themselves</i>, it signifies that they occupy space by themselves, without their existence in space depending upon the concomitant existence of another thing. This is contrary to accidents, whose existence in space depends upon the concomitant existence of the substance which serves as their support, that is to say, which is the bond whereby they are sustained.” This definition is in substance the same as that afforded, with greater development, by the great Arabic dictionary of technical terms published at Calcutta. See B<small>IBLIOTHECA</small> I<small>NDICA</small>, <i>A Dictionary of the technical terms used in the sciences of the Musulmans</i>, p. 1073.",
    "fn.2791": "See vol. I of this work, chap. <small>LXXIII</small>, p. 391, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1. Some Jewish doctors of the Karaite sect have also professed this doctrine of the <i>accident without substrate</i>. See Ahron ben-Élie, <span dir=\"rtl\">עץ חיים</span>, edition of Leipzig, chap. <small>IV</small> (p. 16), and chap. <small>XI</small> (p. 32).",
    "fn.2792": "Considering the future end of the world as a religious dogma, they imagined this hypothesis of the <i>accidental destruction</i>, of which the author speaks in the place cited in the previous note.",
    "fn.2793": "It is evident that the author is making an allusion here to the creation of the world <i>ex nihilo</i> accepted by believers and denied by philosophers.",
    "fn.2794": "It is demonstrated that every solid angle is comprised under plane angles that are smaller than four right angles. See the <i>Éléments d’Euclide</i>, book XI, proposition 21.",
    "fn.2795": "The author here expresses the uncertainty which obtains in many cases concerning that which is possible or absolutely impossible, and he inquirs whether the criterion reside solely in the intelligence, or if it also dwell in the imagination. —The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פיא לית שערי</span> are ever inaccurately rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version by <span dir=\"rtl\">ואני תמה</span>, <i>I marvel</i>. Al-’Harîzi renders more exactly: <span dir=\"rtl\">מי יתן ידעתי</span>; similarly Ibn-Falaquéra: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומי יתן אדע</span>. See <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 125, and cf. Appendix, p. 153.",
    "fn.2796": "That is to say: is there something that can put an end to the vagueness and uncertainty, and does each man possess the criterion of the possible and the impossible?",
    "fn.2797": "Ibn-Tibbon has omitted from his version the words: <span dir=\"rtl\">Pikol anah yamkhen</span>. Al-’Harîzi's version is more accurate here: <span dir=\"rtl\">in a matter whose existence is possible to know and he says it is possible in its nature</span>. Ibn-Falaquéra, <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, p. 125, translates similarly: <span dir=\"rtl\">in a matter that is possible to exist in his opinion and he says it is possible in its nature</span>.",
    "fn.2798": "Word for word: <i>while the adversary says</i>. This adversary who makes objections to him is, or another person (<span dir=\"rtl\">ינאזע אלשכץ גירה</span>), or his own soul (<span dir=\"rtl\">או תנאזעה נפסה</span>), that is, himself.",
    "fn.2799": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon is still somewhat abridged here. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">Those places of inquiry will be scrutinized in detail</span>. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">استقصى</span>  means: <i>to go far</i>, <i>to penetrate deeply into something</i>, <i>to delve into a question</i>.",
    "fn.2800": "Ibn-Tibbon has here, we do not know why, rendered the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לאזמה̈</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">they are eager for their nature</span>; Al-’Harîzi and Ibn-Falaquéra, <i>l. c.</i>, have: <span dir=\"rtl\">they are committed</span>.",
    "fn.2801": "More literally: <i>which one might suppose to belong to either of the two categories</i>. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">فرض</span> signifies <i>to posit</i>, <i>to suppose</i>, and denots something hypothetical. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">שיונחו</span>, found in Ibn-Tibbon and Ibn-Falaquéra, contains the same idea. — Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תפרץׄ</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(تُفْرَضُ)</span>, some MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">תערץׄ</span>, a reading which offers no truly plausible sense.",
    "fn.2802": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">الفئات</span> , <small>VIII</small><sup>e</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">قالت</span> , means <i>to place oneself above something, to insist on one's own opinion, to profess paradoxical</i> or <i>heretical</i> opinions. Cf. my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 269, note 3.",
    "fn.2803": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">إقالة</span>, see vol. II, p. 215, note 1.",
    "fn.2804": "Literally: <i>this division is also necessary and true</i>.",
    "fn.2805": "The Arabic text has irregularly in all our mss. <span dir=\"rtl\">ממתנע</span>, in the singular, instead of the dual <span dir=\"rtl\">ממתנעאו</span>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon has the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">נמנעים</span>.",
    "fn.2806": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read: <span dir=\"rtl\">והערתם ותראה שזה מקום טעותם</span>; the mss. have, in accordance with the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">והערתי שזה מקום טעותם</span>.",
    "fn.2807": "The Arabic text has: <span dir=\"rtl\">פי אלתדביר</span>, and the two Hebrew versions <span dir=\"rtl\">בהנהגה</span>, which signifies <i>of the Government</i> or <i>of the Regime</i> (divine). According to M. Scheyer (p. 88, note 2), one might here be speaking of the treatise by Alexander known under the title of <i>περὶ ἑιμαρμένης</i>, <i>On Destiny</i> or On Fatality; but one finds not in this treatise the considerations to which allusion is made, and which, according to Maimonides, would have been extensively developed by Alexander. In § <small>XXX</small>, where Alexander speaks of divine <i>prescience</i>, he has in view naught but to combat the error of those who believe that this prescience does shackle our liberty of action; yet he enters into none of the considerations of which Maimonides here speaks. In the Arabic lists of Alexander’s works furnished by Al-Kifti (Casiri, t. I, p. 243 et seq.) and by Ibn-Abi-Océibi’a, one finds no writing entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">في التدبير</span>. Perchance this title does designate the same work which in the Arabic lists is mentioned under the title of <span dir=\"rtl\">كتاب العناية</span>, <i>Book of Providence</i>, probably the same which in Greek was entitled <i>περἰ προνοίας</i> (cf. Wenrich, De auctorum grœcorum etc., p. 277). — In general, it is difficult, if not impossible, to verify the citations which Maimonides makes from Alexander, whose works are for the most part lost or unpublished.",
    "fn.2808": "The author means that philosophers, while constantly repeating that the evils afflicting individuals are their own work, or must be attributed to the particular condition of the individual matter, seem to forget this same theory when judging the being in general from the point of view of the individual condition of men, and when they argue from this individual condition to deny divine Providence.",
    "fn.2809": "See above, chap. XII.",
    "fn.2810": "The base the author is referring to is the individual condition of men, taken as a starting point when reasoning about divine Providence.",
    "fn.2811": "That is to say: they attempted to eliminate the great difficulty that often arises from the individual conditions of men, in that we see the virtuous man afflicted with great evils, while the wicked man finds himself in a state of happiness. In order to eliminate this incompatibility with divine justice, they saw no other means than to deny Providence, or the direct intervention of God in human affairs.",
    "fn.2812": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, one must erase after <span dir=\"rtl\">הדבר בכח</span> the words <span dir=\"rtl\">דבר אחד</span>, and write at the end of the phrase <span dir=\"rtl\">אחד</span> (with <span dir=\"rtl\">ד</span>) instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אחר</span> (with <span dir=\"rtl\">ר</span>). The manuscripts read: <span dir=\"rtl\">והשנית חיות ידיעת הדבר בכח וידיעת היותו בפעל דבר אחד</span>. — Things, before existing in act, existed in potency; if, therefore, the adversaries argue, God knew things before they existed in act, then potency and act would be confounded in the divine knowledge.",
    "fn.2813": "More literally: <i>the opinions clashed amongst them;</i> that is to say: they successively rejected one another’s opinions. (The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تراجم</span> signifies primitively: <i>to cast stones mutually</i>). Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תראגׄמת</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(تراجعت)</span>, the manuscript of Saadia ibn-Danan (Suppl. hébr. n° 63) has <span dir=\"rtl\">תזאחמת</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(تزاحمت)</span>, <i>they crowded and pushed one another back;</i> the two verbs resemble one another in Arabic script, but cannot be confounded in Hebrew script. The same expression and the same variant are found further on, in chapter <small>XXII</small> (fol. 45 <i>b</i>, line 6, of the Arabic text). Ibn-Tibbon translates in these two passages: <span dir=\"rtl\">הפליגו לחשוב רע</span>, <i>they went very far in their evil opinions;</i> Al-’Harîzi’s version renders it here as: <span dir=\"ltr\">המחשבות בזה (l. וכבר נרסקו (?נדחקו והתרוצצו</span>, and further on: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר התלכדו בהס המחשבות בזה הענין</span>.",
    "fn.2814": "See the following chapter for these questions, and cf. <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 319 and p. 362.",
    "fn.2815": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הענין</span> is a typographical error; the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">העיון</span>, which is in accordance with the Arabic text.—It is also necessary to erase in the editions, after <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא היו הורסים</span>, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אליו</span>.",
    "fn.2816": "Ibn-Tibbon's version has omitted the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אָבַר פִּי חֵל</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחר כן אתיר הספיקות</span>.",
    "fn.2817": "Word for word: <i>from the sky to what is outside of it</i>. To these last words, Ibn-Tibbon substituted <span dir=\"rtl\">and what is within them</span>, <i>and what is within them (the skies); Al-’Harîzi had <span dir=\"rtl\">down to the bottom of the earth</span>, <i>down to the bottom of the earth</i>.",
    "fn.2818": "Literally: <i>it has occurred by chance and according to how it has been arranged</i>, that is, according to the natural dispositions as a result of which things occur one after the other. Ibn-Tibbon translates inaccurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">and as it has happened</span>, <i>and as it has come to pass</i>.",
    "fn.2819": "Cf. vol. I, chap. <small>LXXIII</small>, 1<sup>st</sup> proposition, p. 377.",
    "fn.2820": "See Physica, book II, chap. V and VI; Metaphysica, book XI, chap. VIII. Cf. vol. II, p. 363, note 2.",
    "fn.2821": "See vol. II, chap. <small>XX</small> (p. 164 et suiv.).",
    "fn.2822": "In the sense of Aristotle’s doctrine, there can be no question of a <i>Providence</i> such as one understands it. That which Maimonides here terms Providence, when speaking of Aristotle, can be naught but the eternal law of the universe, of which God is the Prime Mover. This law is absolute and immutable for all that pertains to the celestial spheres, in which nothing occurs by chance or irregularly; whereas, in sublunary matters, there are many effects of chance, and stability exists only for that which is under the direct influence of the celestial spheres, such as the elements and the <i>species</i> of plants and animals. For this reason, Alexander of Aphrodisias rightly stated that, according to Aristotle, divine Providence (<i>πρόνοια</i>) ceases at the sphere of the moon. If, in the small treatise <i>On the World</i> (chap. 6), opinions almost identical with those Maimonides later proclaims in the name of religion are expressed regarding the action of divine Providence in nature, one must recall that the treatise in question is generally considered apocryphal. The Arabs were unacquainted with it, and the opinions Maimonides attributes to Aristotle are entirely consistent with those resulting from the general spirit of Aristotelian philosophy and which are expounded notably in the <i>Physics</i> (book II, chapters <small>III</small> to <small>VI</small>). Nevertheless, in a passage of the <i>Ethics</i>, Aristotle appears to admit that virtuous men enjoy the particular protection of the Divinity. Vide infra, p. 135, note 1.",
    "fn.2823": "Already the Platonist Atticus, of the II<sup>e</sup> century, had reproached Aristotle for denying divine Providence with regard to sublunar things and man. See Eusèbe, <i>Prœparat. evangel.</i>, book XV, chapter 5 and 12.",
    "fn.2824": "On the influence that celestial spheres exert on sublunar things, see the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chapter <small>X</small>.",
    "fn.2825": "That is, in order to receive the faculty of vegetating, or the vegetative soul. On Aristotle's theory regarding the faculties of the soul and their gradation, cf. vol. I, p. 304, note. On the successive transformations of the prime matter, see <i>ibid.</i>, p. 360.",
    "fn.2826": "Cf. vol. I, p. 367, and ibid., note 5.",
    "fn.2827": "The suffix, in the words <span dir=\"rtl\">שכׄצה</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">נועה</span>, refers grammatically to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ומא</span>, which begins the sentence. The literal translation of this sentence would be: <i>that which is yet purer of it</i> (i.e., the purer portion of matter), <i>so as to receive the form of intelligence, is endowed with another force, by which it governs, thinks, and reflects upon that by which the duration of</i> <small>ITS</small> <i>individual and the preservation of</i> <small>ITS</small> <i>species</i> (i.e., of the individual and species formed from this portion of matter) might become possible, <i>by reason of the perfection of that individual</i>.",
    "fn.2828": "All Arab mss. carry <span dir=\"rtl\">פי סאיר אשכׄאץ</span>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">סאיר</span>, which has here the sense of <i>all</i> (cf. t. II, p. 318, note 5), was omitted in Ibn-Tibbon's version. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל שאר התנועות שיש בשאר אישי המין</span>.",
    "fn.2829": "Ibn-Tibbon renders the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">جدار</span> inexactly by <span dir=\"rtl\">תל של אבנים</span>, <i>a pile of stones;</i> this word, like the Hebrew <span dir=\"rtl\">גדר</span>, means: <i>fence, wall</i>. Al-’Harîzi's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ותפיל אבן מקיר</span>.",
    "fn.2830": "In our edition, <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמא</span> was inadvertently printed, with the article; the manuscripts generally read <span dir=\"rtl\">מא</span>, that is to say <span dir=\"rtl\">العلم</span>. The manuscripts of Ibn-Tibbon’s version likewise read <span dir=\"rtl\">ויסער מים</span>; in the editions, <span dir=\"rtl\">מים</span> has been changed to <span dir=\"rtl\">הים</span>. One of the Leyden manuscripts (n° 18) reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ותמוגׄ מא בחר</span>; similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ותמוגג מימי הים</span>.",
    "fn.2831": "An allusion to an event recounted in the First Book of Kings, chapter <small>XIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 24. — The Aristotelian theory of chance, which the author here summarises, is expounded in the <i>Physics</i>, book II, chapters 5 and 6. The author has particularly had in view that which Aristotle terms the <i>spontaneous</i> or the <i>fortuitous</i> (<i>τὸ αὐτόματον</i>, chap. 6) and which he distinguishes from chance (<i>τύχη</i>) properly so called, the latter always being in relation to a natural purpose or to the intention and free choice of a rational being. Cf. vol. II, p. 362, note 4, and p. 363, note 2.",
    "fn.2832": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי אלשאדׄ</span>, which signify: <i>in a singular case</i> or <i>rare</i>, have been paraphrased in Ibn-Tibbon's version by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">at distant times and through a foreign path</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">and it did not emerge from its boundary except through a great innovation</span>.",
    "fn.2833": "All the Arabic mss. read: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלחיואן ואלאנסאן</span>; the version of Ibn-Tibbon substitutes: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובעלי חיים מדברים ושאינם מדברים</span>, <i>i.e., rational or irrational animals</i>.",
    "fn.2834": "Word for word: neither partial, nor universal.",
    "fn.2835": "That is to say: that which is subject to a rule or a government is necessarily known by him who governs it. — All the Arabic manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">כל מא</span>, <i>all that</i>, and one believes that the verbs <span dir=\"rtl\">ידבר</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">עלם</span> ought to be pronounced in the passive voice. Ibn-Tibbon renders the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כל מא</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">כל מי</span>, <i>whosoever</i>, considering these two verbs as active forms; likewise Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">כל המנהיג דבר הוא יודע אותו</span>, <i>whosoever governs a thing knows it</i>.",
    "fn.2836": "On the <i>Asch’ariyya</i>, or Ascharites, see vol. I, p. 338, note 1.",
    "fn.2837": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יהודה</span> is not expressed in the version of Ibn-Tibbon, nor in that of Al-’Harîzi, which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי כי רצה ית׳ לשלוח שלוחים ולצוות ולהזהיר ולהפחיד ולהבטיח</span>.",
    "fn.2838": "For this exposition of the doctrine of the Ascharites, see Pococke, <i>Specimen hist. arab.</i>, p. 245 and following, and vol. I, p. 338, note 1, and p. 186, note 1; see also <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 324 and following.",
    "fn.2839": "This opinion is that of the Kadrite sect, which professed the doctrine of <i>free will</i> in the most absolute manner. See <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 310, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1.",
    "fn.2840": "The Muʿtazilites adopted the doctrine of free will professed by the Qadarites. See concerning this sect, Pococke, Specimen hist. arab., p. 211 et seq., p. 240 et seq.; Shahrestani, p. 29 et seq. (German transl., vol. I, p. 41 et seq.); Mélanges etc., p. 311.—As the author here observes, the power of man, according to the Muʿtazilites, is not absolute; that is to say, he does not possess in an absolute sense the freedom to act in conformity with his will; for it is necessary that, at the moment of acting, God should create in him the faculty of action. See Volume I of this work, Chap. <small>LXXIII</small>, p. 394, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.2841": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">יענוש</span>, found in almost all editions of Ibn-Tibbon, is an error; one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">יענו</span>, as in the manuscripts and the <i>princeps</i> edition. Al-’Harîzi translates more literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">והיתה תשובתם</span>.",
    "fn.2842": "Word for word: <i>ils se sont chargés</i> (<i>du fardeau</i>) <i>of the absurdity in saying etc</i>.",
    "fn.2843": "Certain Muʿtazilites maintained indeed that animals, even down to the vilest insects, if they have suffered, are entitled to compensation; and God, who is absolute justice, will grant them this compensation, by causing them to be born anew and to enjoy these benefactions. See Ahron ben-Élie, <span dir=\"rtl\">עץ חיים</span>, Leipzig edition, Chap. <small>LXXXIX</small>, p. 135: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואמרו שעתיד השם ית׳ שימציאם כמו שהיו וינהלם אל מקום דשאים ומעינות</span>.",
    "fn.2844": "One must remember that, according to the Asharites, who deny all causality, each human action is a new accident created by God, and that, according to the Mu'tazilites themselves, at least at the moment of acting, God must create in the man the <i>faculty of acting</i>, although the action emanates from his free will. See vol. I, p. 394.",
    "fn.2845": "In Ibn-Tibbon's version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ובאנשי תורתינו</span> are an addition of the translator, who, on the other hand, has suppressed the words <span dir=\"rtl\">בחמד אללה</span>, <i>Dieu merci</i>, which all the Arabic manuscripts have.",
    "fn.2846": "It is almost in the same terms that Shahrestani expresses himself concerning the opinion of the Ashʿarites: “God is the absolute Master in His creation, in which He does what He will and where He ruls according to His good pleasure; if He should cause all creatures to enter paradise, it would in no wise be an injustice, and if He should cause them to enter the fire of hell, there would be no iniquity therein; for injustice consists in arbitrarily disposing of that of which one has not the right to dispose, or in placing a thing where it ought not to be. But God is the absolute Master, and one cannot imagine on His part any injustice, nor attribute to Him any iniquity.” See Shahrestani, p. 73 (German transl., vol. I, p. 110), and cf. Pococke, Specimen hist. arab., p. 252.",
    "fn.2847": "That is, the being who, without having deserved it, has suffered in this world; some mss. have here the words <span dir=\"rtl\">in this world</span>, <i>in this world</i>, which are necessarily implied; similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">and as for him who has been punished in this world</span>.",
    "fn.2848": "Literally: <i>that all these human circumstances are according to merit</i>.",
    "fn.2849": "See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Shabbat</i>, fol. 55 <i>a</i>, where biblical passages are also cited in support, and cf. hereinafter at the commencement of Chap. <small>XXIV</small>.—The commentator Shem-Tov observes with good reason that this opinion is refuted in the same passage by the Talmud itself, and that it here concerns a popular doctrine taught to the vulgar, but which the Talmudists did not pretend to offer as an incontestable truth. Indeed, neither Holy Scripture nor the Doctors pronounce themselves in this respect in an absolute manner. Many biblical passages tend to establish that, whilst it is true that the absolute justice of God cannot in any wise be called into question, it does often present problems insoluble to our intelligence. One must be convinced of this truth, even when the facts would seem to contradict it; one must not discern in a man's felicity a proof of his piety, nor in his misfortune a proof of his impiety. This is, above all, what the Book of Job tends to establish.",
    "fn.2850": "Voy. <i>Mischna</i>, III<sup>e</sup> part<i>, treatise </i>Sotah[[t:6]], chap. I, § 5.",
    "fn.2851": "Ibn-Tibbon’s version bears <span dir=\"rtl\">וממעשה הכבוד והיושר</span>, a reading which is also found in the manuscripts of this version; this is doubtless but an error of the copyists, and it is probable that Ibn-Tibbon rendered the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלבר</span> by the analogous Hebrew word <span dir=\"rtl\">בּרׁ</span>, which bears the same meaning (e.g., <span dir=\"rtl\">כברׁ ידי</span>, Ps. 18, 21 and 25), and that he wrote <span dir=\"rtl\">ממעשה הברׁ</span>. Al-Ḥarizi omitted this word, and has merely <span dir=\"rtl\">ממעשה היושר</span>.",
    "fn.2852": "The author intends to convey that moral sentiment prescribes good actions and repels evil ones, and that man is rewarded for the former and punished for the latter, even when they have not been the object of a special recommendation on the part of a prophet. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלפטרה̈</span>, which one has here rendered by <i>natural sentiment</i>, designates in general <i>that which is innate in man</i>, his nature, his character. Ibn-Tibbon has improperly rendered it by <span dir=\"rtl\">שכל</span>, <i>intelligence;</i> Al-Ḥarizi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי הוא מוזהר בבריאתו וטבעו</span>.",
    "fn.2853": "See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Bava Kamma</i>, fol. 38 <i>b;</i> treatise <i>Pesahim</i>, fol. 118 <i>a</i>. Cf. <i>Yalqut</i>, Vol. I, No. 187. — All the Arabic and Hebrew manuscripts of the <i>Guide</i> bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">זכות כל בריה</span>; the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version bear, like the editions of the Talmud, <span dir=\"rtl\">שכר כל בריה</span>.",
    "fn.2854": "See <i>Beréschîth rabba</i>, section 67 (folio 59, column 3), and cf. Talmud, treatise <i>Bâba Kamma</i>, folio 50 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.2855": "That is to say, the Israelite who practises the moral duties prescribed to him by the Law is not comparable to the Gentile who practises these same duties without their having been prescribed to him. See Babylonian Talmud, treatises <i>Kiddouschîn</i>, fol. 31 <i>a; Bâba Kamma</i>, fol. 87 <i>a;</i> and ’<i>Abôdâ Zara</i>, fol. 3 <i>a</i>. The terms in which Maimonides cites this Talmudic dictum would appear to indicate that the Gentile who performs a moral duty is superior to the Israelite to whom this duty is prescribed by the Law. Nevertheless, the text of the Talmud states: <span dir=\"rtl\">גדול מצווה ועושה ממי שאינו מצווה ועושה</span> “He who performs a duty that is prescribed to him is greater than he who performs it without its having been prescribed to him”; that is to say, the Israelite, who in practising virtue thereby fulfils a religious duty, receives a greater reward than he who fulfils but a mere moral duty.",
    "fn.2856": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>Berakhôth</i>, fol. 5 <i>a</i>, where Raschi explains the words <span dir=\"rtl\">יסורין של אהבה</span>, <i>châtiments d’amour</i>, in the sense that God chastises man sometimes in this world without him having committed any sin, in order to grant him in the future world a reward above his merits.",
    "fn.2857": "The Talmud, however (<i>l. c.</i>), attributes it to the verse from Proverbs (III, 12): ‘The Lord chastises him whom he loves.’",
    "fn.2858": "See hereafter, chap. <small>XXIV</small>, where the author will explain in what sense one must understand the passages which seem to state that God <i>tests</i> man. Therein will he also respond to an objection which one might draw from Deuteronomy, chap. <small>VIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 16, where it is stated that God tested the Hebrew people in the wilderness, <i>in order to do them good later</i>.",
    "fn.2859": "Among the small number of <i>Geonim</i> whose writings have reached us, one knows of none who professed this singular doctrine; in the writings of Saadia, no trace thereof is to be found. Yet it was, it would appear, quite prevalent among the doctors of the Karaite sect, who followed in all respects the doctrines of the Mo’tazales. Joseph ha-Roëh, called in Arabic Abou-Ya’koub al-Bacîr, expounded the doctrine of <i>compensation</i> in his dogmatic treatise entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">ספר נעימות</span> (cf. concerning this work, <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, pp. 476-477). Chapter <small>XXIII</small> of this work is entitled: <span dir=\"rtl\">בתמור ואשר ידבק בו</span>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תמור</span> is the consecrated term by which the Karaite translators rendered the term <span dir=\"rtl\">عوض</span> , <i>compensation</i>. One cites the beginning of this chapter: <span dir=\"rtl\">דע כי בארנו כי הקדמון ית׳ לא יבחר הרע וכי הוא לא ימנע החוב ואם יכאיב החיות והבהמות והטפים והם אינם מחוייבים כאשר נבאר בלא ספק מלתת להם תמור שיוציא עמו הכאב מלהיותו חמס</span>. “It is to be known, as one has set forth (chap. 22), that God delights not in evil and that He refuses not that which is due. If He causes wild beasts, domestic animals, and children to suffer, without these being culpable, as one shall set forth, He must doubtless grant them a compensation, by means of which He causes the suffering not to be an injustice.” The same subject is treated in chap. <small>XXVII</small> of the <span dir=\"rtl\">מחכימת פתי</span>, an extract from the preceding work. These two works are now to be found in the Imperial Library, ms. of the Hebrew supplement, no. 127. Aaron ben Elijah (<span dir=\"rtl\">עץ חיים</span>, p. 127) likewise states that great doctors in Israel have adopted this doctrine which reason reproves: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלו דברים לא ישוערו בשכל וגדולי חכמי ישראל נטו בזה</span>. Here, as elsewhere, some Rabbinite doctors among the <i>Geonim</i> have followed the example of the Karaites. Cf. vol. I, chap. <small>LXXI</small>, pp. 336-337.",
    "fn.2860": "That is, divine Providence exists only for beings that are most directly under the influence of the divine breath. On what is understood by the term <i>êpanchement</i>, see vol. II, chap. <small>XII</small>, p. 101-102.",
    "fn.2861": "The majority of our MSS. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ואמא אן גרק</span>, such that one ought to consider the word <span dir=\"rtl\">גרק</span> as a preterite and pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">إِن غَرِقَ</span>, and indeed it is in this sense that Ibn-Tibbon translated: <span dir=\"rtl\">אמנם אם טבעה הספינה</span>, <i>if the ship has been submerged</i>. However, the noun of action <span dir=\"rtl\">وأحرار</span>, which comes immediately after, proves that <span dir=\"rtl\">غرق</span> must likewise be pronounced as a noun of action. One therefore believes it necessary to adopt the reading <span dir=\"rtl\">אן גרק</span> (without <span dir=\"rtl\">ואמא</span>) which some MSS. possess, and to consider <span dir=\"rtl\">אן</span> as an adverb in the sense of <i>certainly:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">إِنَّ غَرَقَ السَّفِينَةِ</span> . Al-’Harîzi translates exactly: <span dir=\"rtl\">אד טביעות הספינה וכל מה שיש בה ונפילה הקיר על אנשי הבית</span>.",
    "fn.2862": "As one observes, Maimonides acknowledges particular Providence solely with regard to individuals of the human species, and even therein he imposes a restriction, stating further on, \"that only he unto whom something of the <i>emanation</i> (of the divine intellect) attaches itself, shall partake of Providence in proportion to his participation in the intellect.\" See also further on, chap. <small>LI</small>, and cf. Levi ben-Gerson, <i>Wars of the Lord</i>, book IV, chap. 7. This doctrine was necessarily destined to displease the orthodox rabbis; Christian theologians were likewise moved by it, and Saint Thomas deemed it incumbent upon him to refute it in his <i>Summa Theologica</i>, first part, quest. <small>XXII</small>, art. 2: \"Some, however, posited that only incorruptible things are subject to Providence, but corruptible things not according to individuals, but according to species; for thus they are incorruptible..... From the generality of corruptible things, however, <i>Rabbi Moyses</i> excepted men, on account of the splendour of the intellect in which they participate. In other corruptible individuals, he followed the opinion of others; but it is necessary that all things be subject to divine Providence, not only in general, but also in particular. Which is thus evident, etc.\" Nevertheless, Saint Jerome had already professed an opinion on Providence similar to that of Maimonides. See his commentary on Habakkuk, 1, 14 (<i>S. Hieronymi opera</i>, ed. Martianay, vol. III, col. 1600): «Moreover, it is absurd to reduce the majesty of God to this, that He should know at every moment how many gnats are born, or how many die, what is the multitude of bugs and fleas and flies on earth, how many fish swim in the water, and which of the smaller ones should yield as prey to the larger. Let us not be such foolish flatterers of God, that while we draw down His power even to the lowest things, we are injurious to ourselves, by saying that His providence is the same for rational and irrational beings.»",
    "fn.2863": "Most of the Arabic and Hebrew MSS. cite this last verse inaccurately; the author himself, by an error of memory, appears to have confounded several verses of Leviticus (chap. <small>XX</small>, verses 3, 5, 6). The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">ונתתי פני באיש ההוא</span>. Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ונתתי את פני באיש ההוא</span>. The citation, as one has written it, conforms to verse 6 and is found in one of the Leyden MSS. (No. 18).",
    "fn.2864": "That is, divine Providence extends to every human individual. All mss. of the Arabic text have only <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי טאענאיה טאשנציה</span>. These words were paraphrased by Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">שההשגחה האישית נמצאת בבני אדם</span>. Al-’Harîzi expresses himself in the same way: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי השמירה דבקה בכל איש מבני אדם</span>.",
    "fn.2865": "That is, Providence has only the <i>espèce</i>, but not the <i>individus</i>.",
    "fn.2866": "That is, the opinion according to which the <i>individuals</i> among irrational animals are deprived of the care of Providence.",
    "fn.2867": "See Talmud of Babylon, <i>Schabbath</i>, f. 107 <i>b</i>, and <i>Abôdâ Zara</i>, f. 3 <i>b.</i>",
    "fn.2868": "The Arabic MSS. generally have <span dir=\"rtl\">אפצׄאלה</span>; Ibn-Tibbon's version renders <span dir=\"rtl\">פעולותיו</span>, <i>his actions</i>. Ibn-Falaquera (<i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, Appendix, p. 157) has already observed that it would be more fitting to say <span dir=\"rtl\">הטבתו</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">פעולותיו</span>, and he adds that Ibn-Tibbon perhaps had in his Arabic text <span dir=\"rtl\">אפעאלה</span>, a reading which one does indeed find in a MS. of the Imperial Library (ancien fonds hébreu, No. 229).",
    "fn.2869": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>Bâba Mezî’a</i>, fol. 32 <i>b</i>. Cf. <i>Schabbath</i>, fol. 154 <i>b</i>. The author means that it would seem to result from this passage that God takes care not only of the species of animals, but also of each individual, since one cannot torment the species, but only the individual.",
    "fn.2870": "One cannot say where the author observed that the ancient doctors, in declaring it forbidden by the Law to torment animals, rely upon the passage from Numbers. In the Talmudic passages one has indicated, the doctors who profess this opinion invoke a verse from Exodus, chap. <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 5, and another from Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXII</small>, <i>v.</i> 4, in which it is commanded to relieve animals, even those of an enemy, that succumb under their burden.",
    "fn.2871": "That is to say, man alone, who partakes more or less of the divine intellect, shall likewise be more or less the object of divine Providence. Aristotle himself is not disinclined to admit Providence in the sense expounded by Maimonides. In a passage from the <i>Ethics</i>, Aristotle expresses himself in these terms: \"He who acts according to intellect and dedicates himself to its service appears to be in the best condition and most beloved by the Divinity; for if it be true, as it seems, that the gods have some care for human affairs, it is rational that they should rejoice in that which is best and most analogous to their nature, namely, intellect, and that they should reward those who love and honour this thing and who, taking care of what they possess most dear, do what is just and honourable… The wise man consequently shall be the most felicitous.\" See <i>Nicomachean Ethics</i>, book X, end of chap. <small>IX</small>: Ὁ <i>δὲ ϰατὰ νοῦν ἐνεργῶν ϰαὶ τουτον θεραπεύων ϰαὶ διαϰείμενος ἄριστα ϰαὶ θεοφιλέστατος ἔοιϰεν εἶναι, ϰ. τ. λ.</i>",
    "fn.2872": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמעקול</span>, which we have rendered here as <i>reason</i>, properly means the <i>intelligible</i> (<i>τò νοητóν</i>) or <i>that which is conceived by the intelligence</i>.",
    "fn.2873": "Ibn-Tibbon adds the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ושגעון</span>, <i>and to madness;</i> the Arabic text has only the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאכׄתלאט</span>, <i>confusion</i>, and similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">והתוספת הביא ערבוב גמור</span>.",
    "fn.2874": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">مكابرة</span>, see tome I, p. 352, note 2. — The author is speaking here of the Ascharites and the Mo’tazales who went too far in maintaining, against the evident testimony of reason and the senses, that divine Providence extends over each being in particular, even the most insignificant insect.",
    "fn.2875": "The root <span dir=\"rtl\">فرط</span> in its fourth form bears the meaning of <i>to exceed measure, to exaggerate, to do too much</i>. The second form has the contrary meaning: <i>to do too little, to use too much circumspection or negligence</i>. Ibn-Tibbon here renders the noun of action <span dir=\"rtl\">تفريط</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">קצור וחסרון</span>. Cf. vol. II, Arabic text, fol. 84 <i>b;</i> French transl., p. 303. — The author here intends to speak of Aristotle, who grants too little to divine Providence and confines it within limits too narrow.",
    "fn.2876": "That is, the opinion of Aristotle reverses all moral order in human society, placing the human individual, like the beast, outside the care of Providence.—Ibn-Tibbon omitted the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄוד</span> in his translation; Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">והפסד סדר מציאות האדם</span>.",
    "fn.2877": "One has already observed elsewhere that the question debated between the nominalists and the realists likewise occupied the Arab thinkers, and that Maimonides, as a true Peripatetic, declares himself without reserve in favour of nominalism. See Volume I, p. 185, note 2. — Maimonides aims to demonstrate in this chapter that, the human species having real existence only through the individuals which compose it, Providence, which depends upon the divine intelligence poured forth upon mankind, must necessarily extend to all human individuals.",
    "fn.2878": "That is to say, the more an individual will be apt to receive the emanation of divine intelligence, whether the matter will be better disposed for this or because he will have prepared himself through studies and pious practices. Cf. the second tome, chapter <small>XXXII</small>, 2<sup>nd</sup> and 3<sup>rd</sup> opinion on prophecy; chapter <small>XXXVI</small>, p. 284-286.",
    "fn.2879": "Literally: <i>that the Providence of God will be very great towards the prophets and in accordance with their degrees in prophecy</i>. On these <i>degrees</i>, see vol. II, chap. <small>XLV</small>.",
    "fn.2880": "We cannot say in what sense Maimonides intends the word <span dir=\"rtl\">to seem</span> which is generally translated: <i>to perish</i>. We adopt the opinion of Raschi, who says that the verb should be taken in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">silence</span>, <i>silence</i>.",
    "fn.2881": "See what the author says about the severity recommended towards certain idolatrous cities, vol. I, chap. <small>LIV</small>, p. 221-222.",
    "fn.2882": "Literally: <i>and its edifice</i> (rests) <i>thereupon</i>, <i>I mean on what the Providence etc.</i> All the Arabic mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ועליה</span>, with the masculine suffix, referring to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלגרץׄ</span>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">ועליה</span>, with the feminine suffix, referring to <span dir=\"rtl\">פנה</span>.",
    "fn.2883": "That is, those who know how to successively pass their souls through all the degrees of moral qualities or virtues, in order to attain the supreme virtue.",
    "fn.2884": "Shem-Tob expresses astonishment that Maimonides here cites Al-Farabi instead of Aristotle himself, who, in a passage of the <i>Nicomachean Ethics</i> (see above, p. 135, note 1), states nearly the same thing. The same observation had already been made by Joseph, the father of Shem-Tob, who, in his commentary upon the <i>Ethics</i>, expressly states in reference to this passage that the absolute negation of Providence was professed by Alexander, but not by Aristotle, and that the latter, on the contrary, professes nearly the same opinion as Maimonides: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובכלל הדעה החמישי אשר יחסו ר״מ ז״ל לעצמו בפרק י״ח ח״ג עם מה שביאר בפרק נ״א ח״ג מסכים עם מה שאמרו ארסטו הנה</span>. See Hebrew manuscript of the Imperial Library, Oratoire fund, No. 121, fol. 366 <i>a</i> and <i>b.</i> — Cf. above, p. 116, note 1.",
    "fn.2885": "See above, p. 137, p. 1.",
    "fn.2886": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ותטאבק</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(وتُطابِقُ)</span> refers, like <span dir=\"rtl\">תסלם</span>, to the subject <span dir=\"rtl\">קואעד</span>. Word for word, this phrase should be translated thus: <i>And by it</i> (by this chapter) <i>all the fundamental principles of the Law shall be sound and secure for thee, and they shall conform for thee to speculative and philosophical opinions;</i> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ארא</span> is the object of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תטאבק</span>, and one ought to write more correctly <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא</span>, in the accusative case. It is in this sense that Ibn-Tibbon translated <span dir=\"rtl\">ויאותו לך לדעות עיוניות פילוסופיות</span>, <small>where</small> the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ויאותו</span> refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">פנות התורה</span>. Ibn-Falaquera, <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, p. 130, considering the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ארא</span> as the subject of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ותטאבק</span>, translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויהיו נאותות לך דעות עיוניות פילוסופיות</span>, “and the speculative and philosophical opinions shall appear suitable to thee.” Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ותטאבק</span>, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">ויטאבק</span>, in the masculine, referring to <span dir=\"rtl\">אלפצל</span>; according to this reading, one should translate: <i>and it</i> (this chapter) <i>shall appear conformable to the opinions etc.</i>",
    "fn.2887": "See volume I, page 128, note 3.",
    "fn.2888": "Certain thinkers, the author states, have concluded from the lack of regularity, good order, and justice which one often observes in the conditions of men, that God does not watch over human destinies (see above, chap. <small>XVI</small>]). But they have not reflected that these conditions do not always arise naturally and spontaneously, and that they are most often the work of man, endowed with free will and reflection; one cannot, therefore, perceive therein proof against the absolute justice and omniscience of God.",
    "fn.2889": "That is to say, the opposition of the wicked who seek to thwart his efforts for good. — Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלגיר</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(الغير)</span>, 'of another,' one of our manuscripts bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלגׄור</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(الجور)</span>, 'of tyranny.' Although this latter reading is perhaps preferable, one has deemed it proper to write <span dir=\"rtl\">אלגיר</span>, as almost all manuscripts have it, and as it was also in Ibn-Tibbon’s manuscript, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">להתקומם זולתו לו</span>. Al-’Harizi has omitted the words <span dir=\"rtl\">למקאומה̈ אלגיר לה</span>; his version bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי כוונתו לעשות טוב ומה שיסבל בו מן היגיעה אינו מועיל</span>.",
    "fn.2890": "That is to say, from the pernicious opinion that concludes from the happiness of the impious that God ignores human affairs.",
    "fn.2891": "Literally: <i>et de ce qu'elle avait nécessairement produit en fait d'injustice et de violence des hommes les uns contre les autres.</i> The version of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">ומה שחייב והביא בני אדם לחטוא וג׳</span>, lacks both clarity and accuracy.",
    "fn.2892": "The two humours here in question are the <i>vitreous</i> humour and the <i>aqueous</i> humour; by the two membranes, the author appears to designate the <i>choroid</i> and the <i>transparent cornea.</i> It is scarcely necessary to add that, by the <i>opening</i>, the author understands the pupil. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ודונהא</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon translates as <span dir=\"rtl\">ותחתיה</span>, signifies not here 'below it,' but 'outside of it,' or 'furthermore.'",
    "fn.2893": "Word for word: <i>who have the good organization that one knows</i>.",
    "fn.2894": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">تدبير</span>, which we believe should be translated here as <i>faculté organisatrice</i>, properly means <i>governance</i>, <i>regime</i>, <i>direction</i>. Cf. what is said elsewhere about the <i>faculté directrice</i> (tome I, p. 363, and <i>ibid</i>., note 5).",
    "fn.2895": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתדביר אלמהני</span> are rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version as <span dir=\"rtl\">ההנהגה הדומה למלאכת מחשבת</span>. This paraphrase is criticized by Ibn-Falaquéra, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ההנהגה האומנית</span> (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, Appendix, p. 157). On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">المهنى</span> , cf. Volume II, p. 89, note 2.",
    "fn.2896": "That is to say, presumptuous attacks directed against divine omniscience, as David says: <i>And they say, The LORD ses not, neither does the God of Jacob regard it.</i> The author's aim, in this somewhat intricate and rather obscure sentence, is to comment upon the words of the Psalmist which are to follow. According to him, the sacred poet intends that the arrogance of the impious, who express doubts so grave concerning divine omniscience, has no other source than the defectiveness of our perception, and that God, who has bestowed upon us intelligence, knows this defectiveness against which it will needs contend; one ought not, therefore, to take any account of these arrogant attacks which possess no foundation in serious reasoning and which emanate solely from our incapacity to rightly comprehend divine matters. — The obscurity of this sentence has given rise to several variants; we here present the reading of MS. No. 63 of the Hebrew Supplement of the Bibliothèque impériale, in agreement with MS. No. 18 of Leyden, saving some slight differences which one placs within parentheses: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואן אללה עז וגׄל אלדׄי והבנא הדׄא אלעקל אלדׄי בה נדרך ומן אגׄל קצורה ען אדראך חקיקתה תעאלי חדתׄת לה הדׄה עלם תעאלי הדׄה</span> (L. <span dir=\"rtl\">אלעטׄימה̈ וקד</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">קד</span> (L. <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשבהח̈</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אלשבהאת אלקצור מנא ואן פכרתהם הדׄה אלמקצרח̈ לא תלתפת אלי מא אוגׄבתה מן אלתהאפת</span>. The version of Al-’Harîzi tracs this same reading: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכי הבורא ית׳ אשר נתן לנו השכל להשיג בו כל דבר ומפני זה השבוש הגדול</span> (sic) <span dir=\"rtl\">קיצורו להשיג אמתת האל ית׳ אירע לנו וכבר ידע זה הבורא קיצור יכלתנו וכי מחשבתם זאת הקצרה לא תשית לב אל הסרה אשר דברה על הבורא</span>. The reading which one has adopted is entirely in agreement with the version of Ibn-Tibbon. In one's translation, one has suppressed the two <span dir=\"rtl\">ואן</span>, to render the sentence somewhat less encumbered.",
    "fn.2897": "That is to say, they compared God to a statue devoid of feeling. We have vainly sought this passage in the <i>Midrashim</i> that are available to us. Perhaps it is taken from a Midrash that no longer exists. It is also cited by David Kimchi in his commentary on II<sup> Kings</sup> and in his Dictionary, under the root <span dir=\"rtl\">חפה</span>.",
    "fn.2898": "To suppose that God could know now what he did not know before, would be to attribute to him not absolute perfection, but perfectibility, and to believe that he could pass from <i>power</i> to action; that is why one must admit that the knowledge of God is absolutely perfect, and that there can be no change in it. See tome I, chapter <small>LV</small>, p. 225.",
    "fn.2899": "The science of God, being identical with his essence, must be <i>one</i> like the latter and cannot be multiple; this is what those who admit in God, in addition to science, various other <i>essential attributes</i> cannot contest. See vol. I, chap. <small>L</small> and <small>LIII</small>.",
    "fn.2900": "That is, we believers who appear to contradict these two propositions by admitting that God knows individual things, which are and multiple and accidental.",
    "fn.2901": "The author here addresses to believers the following objection: From that which one has just stated, two theses rejected by philosophers would ensue: 1° that divine science has for its object non-being, inasmuch as it cogniss that which does not yet exist; 2° that it embraces the infinite, inasmuch as individuals who do not yet exist, but who shall exist in the future, are infinite. — All the Arabic manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאעדאם</span>, in the plural, <i>the non-beings</i>, the non-existent things. Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">ההעדר</span>, in the singular, which is inexact.",
    "fn.2902": "In other words: what divine knowledge cannot have as its object is absolute non-being, which never exists, just as our human knowledge cannot have as its object what presently has no existence.",
    "fn.2903": "That is to say, certain thinkers, to escape the difficulty which has just been indicated, have contended that divine science has truly for its direct object only the <i>species</i>, which are <i>finite</i>, but that by knowing the species, it thereby indirectly knows all individuals, past, present, and future, contained within each species. — The words <span dir=\"rtl\">במעני מא</span>, <i>in a certain sense</i>, refer, in one's opinion, to the entire complex of the sentence, and not solely, as one might believe, to the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ויסתרסל</span>; one does believe that the author alludes to those of the Motécallemin who, in a certain sense, recognised in the <i>species</i>, as in all <i>universals</i>, a real existence outside of the understanding. See Vol. I, p. 185, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.2904": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מתשרע</span>, which we translate as <i>theologians</i>, designates, like <span dir=\"rtl\">אהל אלשריעה̈</span>, the doctors of the three monotheistic religions. Cf. vol. I, p. 68, note 3.",
    "fn.2905": "That is, it only knows what pertains to the higher world, and, in the sublunar world, its science encompasses genres and species, but not individuals. Cf. <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 319.",
    "fn.2906": "The author shows how one has arrived successively at summarising the entire science of God within the science which He possesses of His own essence. In sum, the author distinguishes three opinions concerning divine science: 1° that which admitts that God embraces by a <i>single</i> science both varied things and those which daily arise, so that there is in His science neither multiplicity nor change; but according to this opinion, which is that of believers, the science of God would embrace the infinite and non-being. To avoid this difficulty, it has been maintained 2° that divine science extends directly only to stable things, that is to say, to individuals of the superior world and to the genera and species of the sublunary world. But others have with reason objected that this opinion would not exclude the multiplicity of divine science, and they have perforce arrived at maintaining 3° that God knows only His own essence, and that it is in the contemplation of Himself that He contemplats those things of which He is the prime cause. One finds more ample details concerning these different opinions in Averrhoës' <i>Destruction of the Destruction</i>, disputatio XIII.",
    "fn.2907": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, there is a grave error here; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מיוחד</span> one must write <span dir=\"rtl\">מחויב</span>, as the mss. of this version have it.",
    "fn.2908": "Literally: <i>On the contrary,</i> <i>this same incapacity of our intelligences to comprehend His essence is also the incapacity to comprehend the knowledge of things as they are.</i> — All our Arabic MSS. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">כיף הי</span>, and the pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">הי</span> can refer only to <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאשיא</span>, <i>the things;</i> so that it would be necessary to say in Hebrew <span dir=\"rtl\">איך הם</span>. Nevertheless, the editions and MSS. of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">איך היא</span>, and here the pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">היא</span> can refer only to <span dir=\"rtl\">ידיעתו</span>, <i>His knowledge.</i> It is doubtless the Arabic pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">הי</span> which occasioned the translator's inadvertent error. It is by a similar inadvertence that, a little further on, Ibn-Tibbon rendered in the feminine all the pronouns and verbs referring to the masculine word <span dir=\"rtl\">עצם</span>, a translation of the feminine word <span dir=\"rtl\">ذات</span> , <i>essence.</i>",
    "fn.2909": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">שהמציאה</span>, while the manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">שהמציא</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately <span dir=\"rtl\">מכל מה שיִּמָּצא</span>, since the Arabic verb must be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">يوجدُ</span> in the aorist passive.",
    "fn.2910": "That is to say, as one had imagined that the science of God and our own, having the same name, also had the same character, it has resulted that one has not been able to reconcile the multiplicity and variability of the objects of science with the absolute unity and immutability of God.",
    "fn.2911": "Thus, for example, the man having free will can choose the right path or the wrong one; God knows in advance which of the two paths he will choose, but this does not prevent him from preserving his freedom to choose.",
    "fn.2912": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">مريّة</span> appears to be a contraction of <span dir=\"rtl\">مرئية</span>, <i>which seems</i>, <i>in opinion, a dubious thing.</i> Ibn-Tibbon did not render this word due to its synonymy with <span dir=\"rtl\">شاي</span>, and he replaced it with <span dir=\"rtl\">כלל</span>, <i>not at all.</i>",
    "fn.2913": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולזה אמר</span>; manuscripts conform to the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולולי זה לא אמר</span>. — The two passages from Deuteronomy cited here demonstrate that the Law speaks of God in a dubitative manner and that divine prescience does not exclude the idea of possibility.",
    "fn.2914": "It might seem, the author says, that the science of future things is only a science of what is <i>in potentiality</i>, while that of past or present things is a science of what is <i>in actuality</i>, and that, consequently, the latter is something more than the former, so that there would be a change of science here.",
    "fn.2915": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">خلص_</span> , which properly means <i>to render pure</i>, appears to have here the sense of <i>to render something independent of another, to choose, to decide on something.</i> In the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תתברר</span> is a printing error; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">תברר</span>, in the active form, as the mss. have it.",
    "fn.2916": "As the author has already stated above, although God knows beforehand which of two things, both possible, shall come into existence, this does not prevent the two things from retaining the nature of the possible. But as soon as one knows with certainty that such a thing shall come to pass, this thing can no longer be in the category of the <i>possible;</i> for it must needs be <i>necessary</i> in order that one may be certain beforehand that it will be realised. There is, therefore, here yet another difference between the science of God and that of one's own.",
    "fn.2917": "One must recall that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">عناية</span> comes from the root <span dir=\"rtl\">عني</span> , which, in its I<sup>st</sup> and VIII<sup>th</sup> forms <span dir=\"rtl\">أعني</span> , signifies <i>to take care</i> or <i>heed of a thing</i>, <i>to be solicitous;</i> the substantive <span dir=\"rtl\">عناية</span> is therefore applicable, like the Greek word <i>πρόνοια</i>, and the Latin word providentia, as much to Divine Providence as to human foresight, and it is in this more extended sense that one here employs the French word <i>providence</i>, for want of discovering another word which exactly renders the Arabic term.",
    "fn.2918": "All Arabic mss. have: <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄאת אלאשכאלאת וחדתׁת אלשכוך אלמדׄכורה̈</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">יבואו השיבושים וירבו הספיקות הנזכרות</span>. Ibn-Tibbon, who usually takes the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אשבאל</span> as a synonym for <span dir=\"rtl\">שך</span>, <i>doubt</i>, has only: <span dir=\"rtl\">יבואו הספיקות הנזכרות</span>.",
    "fn.2919": "That is to say, if it has been executed as the artist's science had conceived it beforehand. — Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אן צנע</span>, three MSS. of the Bodleian Library bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">אנצנע</span>, so that one should translate: <i>indeed,</i> <i>the work has been executed conformably to the artist's science; he, therefore, etc.</i> One has followed the reading of most MSS., which is also that of the two translators; Ibn-Tibbon has: <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא שהדבר העשוי אם נעשה נאות לידיעת עושהו</span>. Al-Ḥarīzī has: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי הדבר העשוי כשיֵעָשה על כוונת דעת עושהו</span>.",
    "fn.2920": "That is to say, he has but realised a work which existed in his idea and of which he had perfect knowledge beforehand. The Arabic MSS. offer several variants in this phrase. The reading which one has adopted is that of most MSS., save the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חאבעא</span> which is written <span dir=\"rtl\">תאבע</span>; this reading signifies word for word: <i>then his artist has done the thing which he has done only by following his science.</i> The Leyden MS. (No. 221) bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">אנמא צנעה מא צנעה תאבעה לעלמה</span> (i.e. <span dir=\"rtl\">صَنْعَةٌ مَا صَنْعَةٌ تَابِعَةٌ</span> ). It is this reading which the two translators appear to express; Ibn-Tibbon's version bears (in the MSS.): <span dir=\"rtl\">יהיה מעשה עושהו אם כן למה שעשה נמשך לידיעתו</span>, <small>where</small> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">נמשך</span> is the adjective of <span dir=\"rtl\">מעשה</span>; Al-Ḥarīzī translates: (l. <span dir=\"rtl\">לא עשהו אלא במעשה נמשך אחרי מדע</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מדעו</span>.",
    "fn.2921": "That is to say, he has drawn from the work itself the knowledge he possesses; the work thus acts upon him and produces the knowledge, whereas in the artist, the knowledge produces the work. In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, <span dir=\"rtl\">וידיעתו</span> is an error; one must read, according to the manuscripts, <span dir=\"rtl\">וידעוהו</span>.",
    "fn.2922": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">בנדקה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">بندقة</span> signifies <i>globule</i> or <i>hazelnut</i>. This word doubtless designates here small balls of the size of a hazelnut; it is in the same sense that Ibn-Tibbon has here employed the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לוזה</span>. Al-’Harîzi has: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובל אבן נופלת</span>, <i>each stone that falls</i>. — One has translated literally what the author says of this machine, evidently an improved <i>clepsydra</i>, such as the Arabs of the Middle Ages possessed; but one must confess one cannot furnish exact details concerning this machine, which moreover would require to be explained by means of a drawing. The balls which descended, drawing along the threads to which they were attached, doubtless struck the hours by falling upon a metallic plate. One is assured, at least, that a similar mechanism existed in the clepsydra which the Caliph Haroun-al-Raschid sent to Charlemagne with other precious objects: «... and also a clock wonderfully constructed from orichalcum by mechanical art: in which the course of twelve hours was turned to a clepsydra, with as many bronze balls, which fell at the completion of the hours and by their fall caused the cymbal beneath them to ring.» See <i>Annales Francorum</i>, ad an. 806 (ap. Bouquet, <i>Recueil des Historiens des Gaules</i>, Paris, in-fol., 1744, t. V, p. 56).",
    "fn.2923": "These are the three species of beings of which, according to the author, the universe is composed (see volume II, chapter <small>X</small>, p. 91): the <i>separated</i> beings are the Intelligences of the spheres (cf. <i>ibid.</i>, p. 31, note 2); the material and permanent individuals are the spheres and the stars, which possess an ethereal matter; finally, the individually variable material beings are the sublunar beings whose individuals perish, but whose genera and species are immutable.",
    "fn.2924": "Literally: <i>that is why there is for God neither multiplicity of sciences, nor renewal or change of science</i>.",
    "fn.2925": "It is in the same manner that Ibn-Roschd expresses himself upon the difference existing between divine science and human science. See <i>Destruction de la Destruction</i>, at the end of disputation <small>XIII</small>; one quots the Hebrew version: <span dir=\"rtl\">וזה שקר אצל הפילוסופים שיהיה מדעו על הקש מדענו לפי שמדענו עלול מהנמצאות ומדעו עלה להם</span> ···· <span dir=\"rtl\">ובכלל הנה כבר קדם שהענין במדע הראשון מקביל הענין במדע האדם ר״ל שמדעו הוא הפועל לנמצאות לא שתהיינה הנמצאות פועלות מדעו ית׳</span> «It is false, according to the philosophers, that the science of God is analogous to our science, for our science is <i>caused</i> by beings, whilst divine science is their <i>cause</i>…. In sum, as one has already stated, the science of the first being possesses a sense directly opposite to that of the science of man; one means that it is divine science which is the efficient cause of beings, and that it is not beings which are the efficient causes of divine science.» — But, from the viewpoint of the philosophers, there remains herein a difficulty, in that divine science, being absolute unity, could not be placed in relation with the multiple. Ibn-Sînâ has pretended to resolve this difficulty by diverse hypotheses, rejected by Ibn-Roschd, who, for his part, refuss to admit that divine science extends over particular and accidental things. (See one's <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, p. 360-362). Maimonides wisely pauss before this insoluble problem, and confins himself to establishing that one is incapable of forming an idea of divine science, identical with the very essence of God, which is inaccessible to one's intelligences.",
    "fn.2926": "That is, so that our essence may be the divine essence and our perception the divine perception.—In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">השתכלנו</span> is a printing error; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">השתדלנו</span>.",
    "fn.2927": "That is, to understand in what way God perceives things.",
    "fn.2928": "Literally: <i>by which one can have this perception</i>. The sense is: If we could understand in what manner God perceives things, then we would possess ourselves this divine intelligence by which God has this kind of perception.",
    "fn.2929": "Literally: <i>the problem of the knowledge that God has of what is outside of him</i>.",
    "fn.2930": "The author calls this story <i>strange</i> and <i>amazing</i>, because it presents a pious man condemned, without any apparent reason, to the most terrible sufferings, and it seems incomprehensible to the superficial reader who does not penetrate its mystery. — The two Hebrew versions only have the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הנפלא</span> for the two adjectives.",
    "fn.2931": "See Talmud of Babylon, <i>Baba Bathra</i>, fol. 15 <i>a</i>, where the different opinions on the various epochs assigned by other doctors to Job are also found.",
    "fn.2932": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">לה</span>, <i>to him</i>, which all the Arabic mss. have, can only be grammatically related to Job.",
    "fn.2933": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי מחל קציהה אלמנורה</span> signify literally: <i>de tali ejus casu qui existit;</i> the author means that his true or false story, which exists in any case before us, has troubled the minds of readers. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">קצׄיתה</span>, <i>son aventure</i> (casus, eventus), several mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">קצתה</span>, <i>son histoire</i>.",
    "fn.2934": "The author is speaking of the doubts that, for the reason he is about to say, have been expressed regarding the omniscience of God and the Providence. See above, chap. <small>XVI</small>.",
    "fn.2935": "These final words are borrowed from the Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>’Haghîgâ</i>, fol. 13 <i>a</i>, where they relate to Ezekiel's vision.",
    "fn.2936": "Word for word: <i>truths which have no term after them</i>, that is to say, which are themselves the ultimate term of truth. In Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must read, according to the MSS., <span dir=\"rtl\">אמתות</span>, <i>truths</i>, instead of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תעלומות</span>, <i>mysteries</i>, which the editions present; Al-’Harîzi translats literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">ונודע מהם אמתות שאין תכלית אחריהם</span>.",
    "fn.2937": "That is, according to the commentator Schem-Tob: what I can say without clearly pronouncing on mysteries that it is not permitted to reveal.",
    "fn.2938": "On the meaning of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">הדבר</span>, see tome II, p. 250, note 3. In Ibn-Tibbon's version, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">by this counsel</span>, the mss. have, in accordance with the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">by this parable</span>; similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">return to your mind to this parable</span>.",
    "fn.2939": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have the plural, <i>which are the true opinions;</i> the manuscripts, however, conform to the Arabic: <span dir=\"rtl\">and see what is the true opinion</span>. On the imperative <span dir=\"rtl\">arí</span>, an incorrect form, see vol. I, p. 19, note 2.",
    "fn.2940": "Word for word: <i>in their multitude and in their troops</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄמארהם</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(جَمَارُهُم)</span> , <i>in universitate eorum</i>, several mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">גמארהם</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(غَمَارِهِم)</span> ; on the latter word, which may be preferable, see tome I, p. 223, note 3.",
    "fn.2941": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon deviates slightly from the text; it reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">שמציאות הכל על יתם אחד ועל ערך אחד</span> (mss. <span dir=\"rtl\">שאז היה נראה</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">דומה</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">פכאן</span>, Ibn-Tibbon appears to have pronounced it <span dir=\"rtl\">فَكَأَنَّ</span> . Al-’Harîzi translates more literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי אז יהיה מציאות הכל על דרך אחד</span>.",
    "fn.2942": "The author here expresses himself in veiled terms, as he has done in his explanation of the <i>Ma’asé Beréschith</i> and the <i>Ma’asé Mercâbâ</i>. He alludes to the idea which he has developed higher up (chap. x), namely, that whatsoever is emanated <i>directly</i> from the will of the Creator is <i>good</i>, and that God is never the direct author of evil. It is only accidentally and indirectly that evil can be attributed to divine action; matter in itself, created by God, is not an evil, but it becoms the source of evil by the <i>privation</i> inherent in it, which is the cause of <i>corruption</i> (<i>φθορά</i>). In the prologue of the Book of Job, one must understand, by the <i>sons of God</i>, the good which God had directly in view in creation, that is to say, the Intelligences and the spheres, as well as the <i>forms</i> emanated from them, which are the causes of the <i>generation</i> (<i>γένοσις</i>) and the preservation of beings. Satan, on the contrary, represents <i>privation</i>, the accidental source of evil.",
    "fn.2943": "That is, evil, which arises from privation, exists only on the <i>earth</i>, or in the <i>sublunar world</i> (cf. tome II, chap. <small>XXX</small>, p. 235); for the superior matter is not accompanied by privation, and nothing in the superior world is corruptible.",
    "fn.2944": "That is, the sublunar matter and the <i>privation</i> that accompanied it were the cause of the accidental misfortunes that befell Job, and in which Job and his friends believed to see an evil coming <i>directly</i> from God.",
    "fn.2945": "Verbatim: <i>after having caused this implication to be understood;</i> that is to say, after having indicated, by the allegory of Satan, the true idea of <i>evil</i>. Ibn-Tibbon, who does translate these words by <span dir=\"rtl\">וכאשר יִשׁב זה הענין</span> (that is to say, after having <i>established</i> this subject), appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">קרר</span> with <span dir=\"rtl\">ר</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">קיר</span> with <span dir=\"rtl\">ד</span>, as is found in all manuscripts; Al-’Harîzi does translate literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכאשר שיער זה השיעור</span>.",
    "fn.2946": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אהל אלנטׄר</span>, <i>the thinkers</i>, Ibn-Tibbon has simply <span dir=\"rtl\">העיון</span>; it should be <span dir=\"rtl\">בעלי העיון</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלקצׄיה̈</span>, which signifies <i>proposition, judgment</i>, is here employed in the sense of <i>narrative</i>, <i>subject</i>, even as the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלקצה̈</span>; hence the manuscripts do have, in this chapter, now the one, now the other of these two words (vide supra, p. 160, note 1). Ibn-Tibbon (in the manuscripts) has here <span dir=\"rtl\">בזאת הגזירה</span>. The editions have substituted for these two words the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בן</span>, referring to <span dir=\"rtl\">זה הענין</span>.",
    "fn.2947": "Cf. above, p. 113, note 2.",
    "fn.2948": "Literally: <i>Subsequently</i> (<i>it must be observed</i>) <i>that his misfortunes have been graduated</i>, <i>etc.</i> Instead of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">דרגׄ</span>, <i>to graduate</i>, one of our manuscripts (suppl. hébreu, no. 63) has <span dir=\"rtl\">דׄבר</span>; likewise the two Hebrew versions: that of Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">ועוד שנזכרו מקריו</span>; that of Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחר כן זכר תלאותיו</span>.",
    "fn.2949": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ירהאע</span>, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">ירהדע</span>. Our editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version appear to render both verbs at once: <span dir=\"rtl\">מי שלא יבהל ולא יסוב לבבו</span>. In some manuscripts of this version and in the commentary of Schem-Tob, one does read <span dir=\"rtl\">מי שלא יִתּר לבבך</span> (cf. Job, 37, 1). The version of Al-’Harîzi bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">מי שלא יחרד לאיבוד ממונו</span>.",
    "fn.2950": "Literally: <i>without despairing</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's version renders the word <span dir=\"rtl\">יהלע</span> with two verbs: <span dir=\"rtl\">and will not despair and will not be terrified</span>; Al-’Harîzi's version has <span dir=\"rtl\">and will not stumble and will not be frightened</span>.",
    "fn.2951": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's translation, the infinitive <span dir=\"rtl\">להרגיש</span> is a printing error; it should be read as <span dir=\"rtl\">למרגיש</span>, in the participle form, as found in the manuscripts.",
    "fn.2952": "The author means that one must needs apprehend the profound idea concealed beneath the word <span dir=\"rtl\">להתיצב</span>, <i>to present oneself</i>, and the reason why this expression was omitted on the first occasion. Even as Maimonides himself does intimate in that which follows, Satan, who represents <i>privation</i> (<i>στέρησις</i>), may be considered, up to a certain point, as a direct aim of creation, inasmuch as birth and corruption, which the Creator purposed in the sublunar world, do occur solely as a consequence of privation, which is inherent in matter and consequently plays a significant part in the affairs of this lower world (cf. vol. I, chap. <small>XVII</small>, p. 69). Satan, or the <i>privation</i> upon which <i>evil</i> depends, has therefore likewise, in some measure, the right <i>to present himself before the Eternal;</i> but he possesses it in a lesser degree than the superior beings who are pure good. (Vide supra, p. 162, notes 1, 2, 3). Wherefore, for the <i>sons of God</i>, the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">להתיצב</span>, <i>to present oneself</i>, is employed twice, whereas for Satan it is employed but once.",
    "fn.2953": "That is to say, all the ideas that the author finds hidden in the different passages he has cited.",
    "fn.2954": "Verbatim: <i>subjected to His command in that which He willed;</i> that is to say, that the beings here designated by the words <i>sons of God</i> and <i>Satan</i> perforce obeyed the eternal laws emanating from the divine will. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">سخر</span> signifies <i>to subject someone to forced service</i>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מוכרחים</span>, employed by Ibn-Tibbon for the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">مسخرين</span>, does perhaps express this idea more energetically than the word <span dir=\"rtl\">משועבדים</span>, which Ibn-Falaquéra wishes (Append. to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 157), and which is here employed by Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.2955": "The Arabic text simply reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">from the voice of Zechariah</span>, <i>from the discourse of Zechariah</i>, or, <i>according to the words of Zechariah</i>. Ibn-Tibbon added for greater clarity the words <span dir=\"rtl\">understand this</span>; similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">and know this from the utterance of Zechariah</span>.",
    "fn.2956": "In the verse from Zechariah, the four winds also represent the superior beings, designated in the book of Job by the words <i>sons of God</i>. See volume II, chapter <small>X</small>, page 91, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1.",
    "fn.2957": "That is to say: the superior beings, sole representatives of absolute good, are stable and durable, not being subject to birth and corruption; but Satan also occupies a certain rank in the sublunar world, as one has observed above, p. 165, n. 1.—In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must efface after <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל</span> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">יחם</span>, which is not found in the MSS. and which was added by the editors to justify the adjectives <span dir=\"rtl\">קיים</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">מתמיד</span>, which are in the singular; but it is only through inadvertence that Ibn-Tibbon employed the singular, imitating the Arabic forms <span dir=\"rtl\">אתׄבת ואדום</span>, which may also be used for the plural. He excuss himself in his preface for the numerous errors of this nature. Al-’Harîzi's version has here more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל בני האלהים הם קיימים ועומדים</span>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">חממא</span> have been rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version by <span dir=\"rtl\">חלק אחד</span>: 'he too has a certain share'; this translation has been justly criticised by Ibn-Falaquéra. Vide Append. to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 149: <span dir=\"rtl\">וידוע כי אין לשטן חלק אחד במציאות אלא שיש לו מעלה מה במציאות כי יש לו ממשלת הארעיות והוא משוטט בארץ</span>. Cf. also Vol. I, p. 52, note 2.",
    "fn.2958": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">חיל</span> is the passive of <span dir=\"rtl\">חאל</span> (rad. <span dir=\"rtl\">حمول</span> ), which means among other things <i>to establish a separation</i>. Ibn-Tibbon adds the following explanatory words: <span dir=\"rtl\">meaning that he was not given permission over it</span>.",
    "fn.2959": "See vol. I, chap. <small>XLI</small>, p. 146, note 2.",
    "fn.2960": "For the acquired intellect, which is always in act and is the only immortal one, has nothing that is in potency, and therefore Satan, who represents privation, is completely foreign to it and has no hold on it. See vol. I, l.c., and cf. ibid., chap. LXX, p. 328, note 4.",
    "fn.2961": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>Bâbâ bathra</i>, fol. 16 <i>a</i>. This is the passage to which the author alluded earlier by saying: 'I will report the words of the doctors who have drawn my attention to everything I have been able to understand of this important parable.' — On the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">יצר הרע</span>, <i>le mauvais penchant</i>, cf. vol. II, p. 103, note 2.",
    "fn.2962": "According to everything that has been said above, this Talmudic passage would signify that the matter accompanied by the <i>privation</i>, principle of evil, produces the bad inclinations that lead man to his downfall.",
    "fn.2963": "See the <i>Baraïtha</i>, as reported in <i>Bâba bathra</i>, <i>l. c.</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">תנא יורד ומתעה וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.2964": "Literally: <i>il</i> (<i>Dieu</i>) <i>ne lui montra que pour lui indiquer une idée etc</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מעבר</span> is wanting in some MSS., and it is also omitted in Al-’Harîzi's version, which bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי הראהו זה להורות על זה הענין בעצמו וגו׳</span>. In Ibn-Tibbon's version, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">והענין</span>, some MSS. have more accurately <span dir=\"rtl\">הענין</span> without the copulative <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span>. As to the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אוראה</span>, it is an incorrect form for <span dir=\"rtl\">אראה</span>. Cf. Vol. I, p. 97, note 4.",
    "fn.2965": "According to the Talmud, treatise <i>Synhedrin</i>, fol. 91 <i>a</i>, one might here allude to the sin committed by the descendants of Joshua in marrying foreign women. Vide Ezra, chap. <small>X</small>, <i>v</i>. 18, and cf. the Chaldaic version to the book of Zechariah, 3, 3, where the words 'Joshua wore soiled garments' are thus paraphrased: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויהושע הוו ליה בנין דנסבין להון נשין דלא כשרן לכהונתא</span>, 'Joshua had sons who took wives improper for the priesthood'.",
    "fn.2966": "For, as we have seen, Satan is only in relation to the sublunar world.",
    "fn.2967": "Ibn-Tibbon's version bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויורנו בדרך הטעות והשניגה</span>, 'and which <i>leads us</i> into the path of error and straying.' Ibn-Tibbon has here committed a grave error; for all the Arabic MSS. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">ויובק</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(وَيُوبِقُ)</span>, the IVth form of <span dir=\"rtl\">ובק</span>, which signifies 'to perish', 'to be lost'. Al-’Harîzi translats more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויכשיל בדרכי הטעות</span>.",
    "fn.2968": "That is to say, the idea that the author associates with the symbolic being called Satan.",
    "fn.2969": "Vide Mischnâ, Ist part, treatise <i>Berakhôth</i>, chap. <small>IX</small>, § 5, where the words <span dir=\"rtl\">בכל לבבך</span>, 'with <i>ALL</i> <small>thy heart</small>' (Deuteronomy, VI, 5), are explained by <span dir=\"rtl\">בשני יצריך</span>, 'with thy two inclinations', which the doctors understand in this sense, that one must thank God even for the evil which befalls one and of which one's evil nature is often the sole cause, and that one must praise Him even in moments of sadness and irritation: <span dir=\"rtl\">חייב אדם לברך על הרעה כשם שמברך על הטובה שנאמר ואהבת את י״י אלהיך בכל לבבך וגו׳ בכל לבבך בשני יצריך ביצר הטוב וביצר הרע</span>. cf. Babylonian Talmud, same treatise, fol. 61 <i>a:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">שני יצרים ברא הקב״ה וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.2970": "Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Synhedrin</i>, fol. 91 <i>b</i>, in which is reported a conversation between Rabbi Iehouda ha-Nasi and Antoninus upon the question whether the <i>evil inclination</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">יצר הרע</span>) is born at the moment of the foetus's formation, or at the moment when the child is brought forth; Antoninus pronouncs himself in the latter sense, and Rabbi adds that, indeed, one may cite in support of this opinion the verse from Genesis (4, 7): <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר רבי דבר זה למדני אנטונינוס ומקרא מסייעו שנאמר לפתח חטאת רובץ</span>. Cf. <i>Beréschith rabba</i>, section <small>XXXIV</small> (fol. 30, col. 1). The good inclination (<span dir=\"rtl\">יצר טוב</span>), according to the Rabbins, develops itself only at the age of thirteen years. Vide the <i>Midrasch Kohéleth</i>, at chap. <small>IX</small>, <i>v</i>. 14: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולמה הוא קורא ליצר הרע גדול שהוא גדול מיצר טוב שלש עשרה שנה</span>.",
    "fn.2971": "See the interpretation of this verse from Ecclesiastes in the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Nedarîm</i>, fol. 32 <i>b</i>, and compare the Chaldaic paraphrase of the same verse.—The Arabic manuscripts and those of Ibn-Tibbon's version, as also the editions, generally present <span dir=\"rtl\">ילד מסכן</span> here, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">איש מסכן</span>; and it was doubtless the author himself who wrote <span dir=\"rtl\">ילד</span> inadvertently, when contemplating another verse from Ecclesiastes: <span dir=\"rtl\">טוב ילד מסכן וחכם</span> (chap. IV, <i>v.</i> 12).",
    "fn.2972": "The author means that the good inclination and the evil inclination, both derived from the faculties of the soul, can be called <i>anges;</i> for this term designates, among other things, all physical forces and all faculties of the soul. See vol. II, chap. <small>VI</small>, p. 70 and following.",
    "fn.2973": "Literally: <i>this matter so generally known in the words of the doctors</i>, <i>namely that every man etc.</i> See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>’Haghigâ</i>, fol. 16 <i>a</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">שני מלאכי השרה מלוין אותו וגוי</span>. Compare treatise <i>Berakhôth</i>, fol. 60 <i>b:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">הנכנס לבית הכסא וגו׳</span>, and <i>ibid.</i> Raschi's commentary.",
    "fn.2974": "See the treatise <i>Schabbâth</i>, fol. 119 <i>b:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">ב׳ מלאכי השרת מלוין לו לאדם בע״ש מבית הכנסת לביתו אחד טוב ואחד רע</span>.",
    "fn.2975": "That is, according to the word of R. Siméon, son of Lakisch, cited above.",
    "fn.2976": "Literally: <i>I think not otherwise</i>, <i>save that I have explained and elucidated the history of Job even unto its term and its end</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's version, which too servilely reproduces the turn of the Arabic phrase, may not appear sufficiently clear; Al-’Harîzi has rendered this phrase with greater clarity: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכמדומה לי כי כבר פרשתי בזה מה שיש בידי וביארתי עניין איוב עד תכליתו וסופו</span>. The author intends to convey that he believes himself to have done all that is possible to explain the allegory contained in the historical prologue of the book of Job.",
    "fn.2977": "Literally: <i>to the words outside of this</i>; i.e., to what each of them has said outside of the passages that will be cited as proofs of their opinions.",
    "fn.2978": "The author intends to convey that, in the discourses of Job and his friends, there are characteristic passages in which their respective opinions are delineated, forming the essence of the allegory they have in view; the remainder possesses no significance for the allegory in itself and for the philosophical idea which it encloses, serving only to complete the portrayal from the perspective of poetic art. This is what the author intends to convey by the words <i>necessitated by the order</i> (or the ensemble) <i>of the discourse</i>. He refers to the Introduction of his work, where he stated that at times the entire allegory reveals the whole of the subject represented, but that alongside it are found passages which add naught to the represented subject, and which serve solely for the embellishment of the allegory and the symmetry of the discourse. See vol. I, p. 19 et seq.",
    "fn.2979": "Word for word: <i>as soon as she arrived;</i> that is, the story. The feminine verb <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄרת</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(جَرَت)</span> which all mss. have cannot be related to anything other than the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קצה̈</span>, <i>story</i>.",
    "fn.2980": "That is, Job himself, in his complaints, does not go so far as to say that God had no knowledge of what had happened to him, nor that God was unjust towards him; he restricts himself to protesting his innocence, and to maintaining that he had not deserved this severe punishment.",
    "fn.2981": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">יצׄען</span>, which I believe to be the energetic future <span dir=\"rtl\">يَضَعَنَّ</span> , of <span dir=\"rtl\">to place filaments</span> in the sense of <i>to humble oneself</i>, several mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ידען</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(يَدْعَن)</span> , of <span dir=\"rtl\">to submit</span> , <i>to obey</i>, <i>to submit</i>. This last reading may be preferable.",
    "fn.2982": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ובבניה</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(ويكيبيديا)</span> , some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">ובביתה</span>, a variant that can be explained by a copy in Arabic characters; similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובביתו</span>. Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויחולו עליו ועל בני ביתו וזרעו</span>.",
    "fn.2983": "Literally: <i>its fracture shall necessarily be joined</i> or <i>reset</i>. Concerning the locution <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄבר צדעה</span>, compare the Arabic text further on, chap. <small>XXXVI</small> (fol. 77 <i>b</i>, before last line), chap. <small>XL</small> (fol. 87 <i>b</i>, l. 4), chap. <small>XLI</small> (fol. 91 <i>a</i>, l. 19), chap. <small>XLIX</small> (fol. 114 <i>a</i>, l. 14), chap. <small>LIII</small> (fol. 131 <i>a</i>, last line).",
    "fn.2984": "Word for word: <i>by contempt for the human species and by disdain for it</i>. Ibn-Tibbon omitted in his version the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וּזְאטְרָא לָהּ</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">on account of the human being's low value and his being as nothing in the eyes of the Creator</span>.",
    "fn.2985": "It is clear that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">שוׄט</span>, which means <i>fléau</i>, is taken by Maimonides in the sense of <i>flot</i>, <i>torrent</i>, which does not emerge from Ibn-Tibbon's version, which reproduces the word <span dir=\"rtl\">שוט</span> from the text of Job. Al-’Harîzi translates better: <span dir=\"rtl\">אם יבא גשם שוטף</span>.",
    "fn.2986": "The Arabic phrase is exceedingly concise; its purport is as follows: Job declares that one must not hope for any compensation after death, and that consequently, if one beholds the virtuous man overwhelmed by sufferings, whilst the impious enjoys perfect felicity, one can offer no solution to this problem; and nothing further remains for one to say, save that there is abandonment on the part of God, that is to say, that God concerns Himself not with individual humans and abandons them to their fate.—Ibn-Tibbon, having imperfectly apprehended the sense of this phrase, thus paraphrased it: <span dir=\"rtl\">אם כן לא נשארה תוחלת אלא שזה עזיבה ושכחה</span> 'there remains therefore no hope, but there is here abandonment and forgetfulness.' Al-’Harîzi translates more exactly: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויצא מדברו כי זה התרשלות מהבורא</span>, 'it results therefore from his words that there is here carelessness on the part of the creator.'",
    "fn.2987": "Literally: <i>how He neglected not the primitive work of the formation</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">كون</span> , <i>γένεσις</i>) <i>of the human individual and his creation, and yet neglected to govern him</i>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אצל צנע כון</span> ought to be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">أَصْلُ صُنْعِ كَوْنٍ</span> , <i>the principle of the work of formation</i>, that is to say, the primitive work of formation. By these words, the author alludes to the description given by Job of the formation of the foetus (X, 10 et seq.). The word <span dir=\"rtl\">וכׄלקה</span> has been considered by Ibn-Tibbon as a verb <span dir=\"rtl\">(وَخَلَقَهُ)</span> , and he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וברא אותו</span>; but in such a case it would have been more correct to say <span dir=\"rtl\">فَلَقَةُ</span> . One considers this word as a noun of action <span dir=\"rtl\">وَخَلَقَهُ</span> , in the accusative case, like <span dir=\"rtl\">أصل</span> . It is in the same sense that Al-’Harîzi translates this word, although his translation is, moreover, very confused: <span dir=\"rtl\">והחל לתמוה איך יתכן להתרשל האומן אשר כיון נוף הארס וברייתו ויתרשל בשמירתו</span>.",
    "fn.2988": "All our Arabic MSS. have only: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמעתקדה̈ פי אלענאיה̈</span>, <i>believed</i> or <i>admitted concerning Providence</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">אחת מן הדעות הנאמרות בהשגחה ואשר האמינוה קצת המעיינים</span>, \"one of the opinions stated concerning Providence and which certain thinkers have admitted.\" Al-’Harîzi has: <span dir=\"rtl\">זאת היא אחת מן הדעות והאמונות אשר להאמינם בשמירת הבורא</span>. One perceives that he had the same reading as ourselves; but his translation lacks exactitude. The author alludes to the opinion professed by Aristotle and other philosophers. See above, chap. <small>XVII</small>, 2<sup>nd</sup> opinion.",
    "fn.2989": "Properly: <i>maladive;</i> the two Hebrew translators have: <span dir=\"rtl\">בתכלית הרע</span>.",
    "fn.2990": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Bâba bathra</i>, fol. 16 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.2991": "<span dir=\"rtl\">עפרא בפומיה</span>, <i>poussière sur sa bouche</i>, is a proverbial expression meaning: his mouth deserves to have dust thrown into it to shut it up, or to soil it.",
    "fn.2992": "Another proverbial expression, meaning: to turn everything upside down, to profess subversive opinions.",
    "fn.2993": "Ibn-Tibbon adds <span dir=\"rtl\">and his friends</span>, <i>and to his friends</i>, which is not found in the Arabic text or in Al-’Harîzi's version; and indeed, in the text of the book of Job, <small>XLII</small>, 7, God's words are addressed only to Eliphaz.",
    "fn.2994": "That is to say, in order to justify this address which God directs to Eliphaz. In Ibn-Tibbon's version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">על זה מן הדברים ההם</span> constitute a double translation of the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">ען דׄלך</span>; the MSS. do not contain <span dir=\"rtl\">על זה</span>. Al-’Harîzi's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">כדי לנקותו מזה החטא</span>, which is a pure misinterpretation, for the author does not intend to speak of Job's justification.",
    "fn.2995": "Talmud, <i>ibidem</i>, fol. 16 <i>b.</i> According to the Talmud, the words that God addresses to Eliphaz would have this sense, that Job, overwhelmed by pain, could not be held responsible for the complaints he uttered, while his friends were guilty for having pretended <i>to justify</i> God. Job at least left the question intact; while his friends pretended to resolve it by erroneous arguments.",
    "fn.2996": "That is to say, what the doctors aver in explanation of the words addressed by God to Eliphaz does not well conform to the entirety of the parable of the Book of Job. Indeed, according to Maimonides, the character of Job holds within this parable a well-defined philosophical role; he does not merely utter complaints extorted by pain, but he professes concerning Providence a well-considered opinion which is reprehensible from the religious point of view. The author therefore seeks to explain, in a manner differing from that of the doctors, the meaning of these words: \"for ye have not spoken of me aright, as my servant Job.\"",
    "fn.2997": "Word for word: <i>the cause of this;</i> that is, the cause of the discourse addressed by God to Eliphaz.",
    "fn.2998": "The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">ערפה</span> refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאלאה</span>, <i>God</i>. Some MSS. have incorrectly <span dir=\"rtl\">ערפהא</span> with the feminine suffix, and likewise the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">שידעה</span>, whereas the MSS. of this version have <span dir=\"rtl\">שידעהו</span>. Ibn-Tibbon adds in this sentence the words <span dir=\"rtl\">בלא ספק</span>, <i>without doubt</i>, the equivalent of which one finds in none of the Arabic MSS. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ממנו</span>, at the end of the sentence, is a mere typographical error, and is not found in the MSS. Al-’Harîzi translates accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">שהיא מזומנת לכל מי שידעהו</span>.",
    "fn.2999": "That is to say, for having attached myself to the material things of human life and for having formed thereon my judgment. Moses of Narbonne and other commentators rightly opine that Maimonides understands these words of Job in a moral sense, and that the words <i>dust</i> and <i>ashes</i> here signify nature; the same applies to that passage in the prologue: <i>and he sat down among the ashes</i>. This explanation accords perfectly with the allegorical sense which Maimonides, in the preceding chapter, attributes to the entire prologue. Cf. Samuel ibn-Tibbon, treatise <i>Yikkawou ha-maïm</i>, chap. 25, p. 101.",
    "fn.3000": "This opinion is the one that the author presented earlier as the generally accepted orthodox opinion by the prophets and the doctors of Israel. See ch. <small>XVII</small>, 5<sup>th</sup> opinion.",
    "fn.3001": "In Ibn-Tibbon's version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">שאתה חושב אותו ונשען עלין</span> are a double translation of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תעתמרה</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי זה אשר היית סומך עליו</span>.",
    "fn.3002": "Word for word: <i>to turn in a circle towards this goal</i>, an expression that Ibn-Tibbon weakened by translating <span dir=\"rtl\">מלכת בזה הדרך</span>. Al-’Harîzi translated more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא חדל אליפז לסבב סביב זה הענין</span>.",
    "fn.3003": "This opinion, as we have seen in chap. <small>XVII</small>, p. 128 and <i>ib.</i>, n. 4, is that of the Mo’tazales and certain Jewish doctors among the Karaïtes and the <i>gueônim</i>.",
    "fn.3004": "The verbal form <span dir=\"rtl\">to be compensated</span> has here clearly the passive or neutral sense, and it would have been more regular to say <span dir=\"rtl\">to be repelled</span>; perhaps one should pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">to be compensated</span> , for <span dir=\"rtl\">to be compensated</span>.",
    "fn.3005": "In all Arabic mss. one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא למא פעל הדׄא</span>, <i>why he has done such</i> (<i>other</i>) <i>thing</i>. Ibn-Tibbon translates inaccurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולמה לא עשה זה</span>; the version of Al-’Harîzi contains the same inaccuracy.",
    "fn.3006": "Ibn-Tibbon renders the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עטׄמתה</span> inexactly as <span dir=\"rtl\">עצמותו</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי תעצומו ואמתתו יחייבו וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.3007": "One recognizes in this opinion that of the Ascharites. See chap. <small>XVII</small>, 3<sup>d</sup> opinion.",
    "fn.3008": "It is difficult to ascertain in what sense Maimonides interprets the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כי כפלים לתושיה</span>, which have been so diversely expounded by ancient and modern commentators, and of which the most natural meaning appears to be this: for it is infinitely greater than thy wisdom and thy commonplace reasoning. The ancient Jewish commentators generally take the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תושיה</span> in the sense of <i>Torah</i>, <i>doctrine</i> or <i>instruction</i>, and it is in this sense that one has translated it.",
    "fn.3009": "Literally: <i>vois et réfléchis comment a été établie cette histoire qui a troublé les hommes et les a conduits aux opinions que nous avons précédemment exposées sur la Providence de Dieu à l’égard des créatures</i>.",
    "fn.3010": "Word for word: <i>one mentions all that the division required</i> or <i>the classification;</i> that is to say, one surveys the various opinions which may exist concerning this question of Providence. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ודׄכר</span> which Ibn-Tibbon considered a noun of action <span dir=\"rtl\">(وَذُكِرَ)</span> and which he translated as <span dir=\"rtl\">וזכרון</span>, ought to be pronounced as a passive preterite <span dir=\"rtl\">(وذكّر)</span> .",
    "fn.3011": "Ibn-Tibbon suppressed in his version the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלעלם</span>, <i>and science;</i> according to Ibn-Falaquéra, he would have done so intentionally, because it is stated in the preceding chapter (p. 163) that Job was not a man of <i>science</i>. See Append. of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 157: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויש מקשים ממה שנאמר בחשיבות ובחכמה כי למעלה שלל ממנו</span> (read <span dir=\"rtl\">בזה הפרק</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בפרק כ״ג החכמה והסיר מלת חכמה מההעתקה והיא מצואה בערבי אלפצׄל ואלעלם</span>. Ibn-Falaquéra endeavours to justify Maimonides concerning this apparent contradiction, by stating that in the preceding chapter one refers to <i>speculative science</i> which Job did not possess, whereas here it concerns solely <i>practical wisdom</i> and morality. This subtle distinction appears superfluous to one; for in the present passage, it concerns not only Job, but also and pre-eminently his friends and interlocutors, who are presented as men possessing real science. Al-’Harîzi did not pause upon the difficulty indicated by Ibn-Falaquéra; he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וסמכו הכל לאחד מגדולי הדור חכם וחסיד</span>.",
    "fn.3012": "That is, to justify the intervention of this new interlocutor, when the debate is so exhausted that Bildad no longer knows what to say except repeat some commonplaces (ch. xxv) and that Sophar is entirely reduced to silence.",
    "fn.3013": "That is to say, to Job and his three friends. The manuscripts generally read: <span dir=\"rtl\">פצׄל ענדהם</span>; likewise Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">שובח אצלם</span>, and Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">נכבד בעיניהם</span>. The Leyden manuscript, No. 221, has <span dir=\"rtl\">פצׄל עליהם</span>, and it is in this sense that one has translated. One of the manuscripts of the Imperial Library (old collection, No. 230) bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">פצל ענהם</span>; according to this reading, which is perhaps preferable, one should translate: <i>wherefore he is separated</i> or <i>distinguished from them</i>.",
    "fn.3014": "The Arabic verb  is rendered in the version of Ibn-Tibbon by the two verbs  and ; the first of these two verbs, which should be pronounced , comes from  (I Sam., 3, 13).",
    "fn.3015": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">فَزِدْ</span> signifies <i>to have the mind enfeebled by old age</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">الفند وهو الضعف فى الرأى من الهرم</span> Comment. on Hariri, p. 133), and in the second form <span dir=\"rtl\">فنّد</span> <i>to say that someone raves by reason of old age</i>. For this reason Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכן אמר על שלשת רעיו שנפסד דעתם על רוב זקנתם</span>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">פנד</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">فند</span>, Ibn-Falaquéra read <span dir=\"rtl\">קיד</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(قيد)</span>, and he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">קבץ דעות חכיריו</span>. Vide Appendix of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 157. This variant is explained by a copy written in Arabic characters. Al-’Harîzi had perhaps the same variant; he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכן זכר סברות שלשת רעיו</span>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי אלענאيه̈</span>, <i>concerning Providence</i>, are wanting in some manuscripts, and have been expressed neither by Ibn-Tibbon, nor by Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.3016": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תוניב</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">תוביךׄ</span>, which has the same meaning; Ibn-Tibbon has two words here as well: <span dir=\"rtl\">הכהוי והגערה</span>. Cf. p. 181, n. 3.",
    "fn.3017": "That is to say, that God derives no advantage from the virtue of men, and that their sins reach Him not. Vide Job, chap. <small>XXXV</small>, <i>vv.</i> 6 and 7: <i>If thou sinnest,</i> <i>what harm dost thou unto Him? If thy crimes be numerous, what evil does He receive thereby? If thou art virtuous, what dost thou give unto Him, or what does He receive from thy hand?</i> The words <span dir=\"rtl\">לא יבאלי</span> properly signify: <i>he pays no attention</i> or <i>he is not preoccupied;</i> but this is not what the author intends to convey, for Elihu, on the contrary, admitted that God is preoccupied with human actions, that He recompenses the pious man and punishes the sinner. It is therefore with intention, one believes, that Ibn-Tibbon translates these words by <span dir=\"rtl\">לא ירגיש</span>, <i>he feels not</i> or <i>he perceives not</i>, that is to say, that the good or evil wrought by man affects not the divine essence. Ibn-Falaquéra, finding this expression too material (<span dir=\"rtl\">וליחס לו יה׳ הרגשה אינו נכון</span>, loc. cit.), prefers to translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא ישית לב</span>; but these words possess the disadvantage of not precisely rendering the author’s thought, no more than do the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לא יחוש</span> employed by Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.3018": "That is, if, by the effect of divine Providence, any force of nature comes to his aid. We have already seen that the word <i>angel</i> often designates forces emanating from celestial spheres and all physical forces. See vol. II, chap. <small>VI</small>, p. 70 and following.",
    "fn.3019": "Literally: <i>his fall will be raised</i>, or better <i>he will be raised from his fall</i>. On this expression, see vol. II, p. 215, note 1.",
    "fn.3020": "That is, nature can conquer disease and save man two or three times; but he is mortal and will ultimately succumb.",
    "fn.3021": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">כמאל</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">חאל</span>, and similarly Ibn-Tibbon <span dir=\"rtl\">אל ענין הבריאות</span>, <i>to the state of health</i>.",
    "fn.3022": "In Ibn-Tibbon’s version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כונה אפתתח</span> are inaccurately rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">מה שאמר</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">והוסיף כי החל קודם זה הענין לספר איכות הנבואה</span>.—By bringing forth, in Elihu’s discourses, the two points which have just been mentioned, namely, the manner in which God often savs man from imminent evil and the manner in which prophetic inspiration comes unto man, the author appears to indicate that Elihu’s opinion accords with his own, according to which the human individual is solely the object of the particular providence of the Divinity. Vide above, chap. <small>XVII</small>, p. 129 et seq., where the author establish-eth that man alone, who partakes more or less of the effluence of divine intelligence, partakes in the same measure of divine Providence.",
    "fn.3023": "That is, his opinion regarding divine Providence, which, according to the author, follows from the passages he has just cited.",
    "fn.3024": "One perceives not clearly the connection of these latter passages with that which precedes, and, in general, the author expresses himself upon the opinion he attributs to Elihu in a very obscure manner. Here, it appears to one, he attributs to Elihu the opinion which he has developed earlier (chap. <small>XII</small>) concerning the various evils that afflict men, and which would seem to contradict what has been said regarding Providence watching over human individuals; he shows that Elihu thinks, as he himself does, that these evils, inherent in matter or in human nature, befall the individual but in an exceptional manner and are a good for humanity as a whole. By the examples of pestilence and war, the author alludes, one believes, to the first and second species of evils of which he has spoken in chap. <small>XII</small>. As a final solution to the problem, as one shall presently observe, the author establishes that one cannot form a just idea of divine Providence, of divine governance, and of divine science; for one judges of these by human governance, whereas it is merely by simple homonymy that <i>governance, science</i>, and many other matters are predicated at once of God and of us, albeit the two things are totally and essentially different.",
    "fn.3025": "That is, in the <i>théophanie</i> that concludes the book of Job (chapters <small>XXXVIII</small> to <small>XLI</small>).",
    "fn.3026": "According to the author, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לויתן</span>, derived from the root <span dir=\"rtl\">לוה</span>, <i>joindre</i>, <i>unir</i>, would signify an imaginary monster, <i>réunissant</i> the most diverse animal forms.",
    "fn.3027": "That is, in the sublunar world, where everything is born and perishes in turn.",
    "fn.3028": "That is, the word providence, <i>providentia</i>, applied to God, does not have the same meaning as when it is applied to our <i>prévoyance</i> and the care we take of something. See above, p. 154, note 1.",
    "fn.3029": "On the manner in which the author interprets this passage, see above, p. 177, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1.",
    "fn.3030": "Voy. Talmud de Babylone, treatise <i>Schabbath</i>, fol. 88 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.3031": "Literally: <i>And it is excepted only what occurs in the order</i> or <i>the entirety of the discourse and in the sequel of the allegory</i>. See at the end of the preceding chapter, p. 171, note 2.",
    "fn.3032": "The author explains in this chapter the biblical passages that seem to say that God, by the evils he inflicts on man, sometimes has the purpose of putting him to the <i>test</i>.",
    "fn.3033": "The six passages, as will be seen, are: Genesis, chapter <small>XXII</small>, <i>v</i>. 1; Exodus, chapter <small>XVI</small>, <i>v</i>. 4; chapter <small>XX</small>, <i>v</i>. 17; Deuteronomy, chapter <small>VIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 2 and 16; chapter <small>XIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 4.",
    "fn.3034": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">מא יוהם טׄאהרה</span> signify <i>whose literal sense might lead one to believe</i> or <i>to suspect</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's version, <span dir=\"rtl\">מה שיַראה פשוטו</span>, lacks exactitude; Al-’Harîzi’s version is here somewhat unintelligible, bearing: <span dir=\"rtl\">פשט שיספק לנו זה הענין</span>. — The passage to which the author alludes, and which he will explain anon, is that of Deuteronomy, ch. <small>VIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 16, where one reads the words <span dir=\"rtl\">להיטיבך באחריתך</span>, <i>to do thee good in thy latter end</i>.",
    "fn.3035": "See above, chap. <small>XVII</small>, p. 126-128, where the author, after citing this passage and a few others similar to it, also speaks of those who admit the <i>punishments of love</i>, or the pains inflicted on man, not because he had sinned beforehand, but so that his future reward might be all the greater.",
    "fn.3036": "Ibn-Tibbon has here omitted the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קדר</span>, <i>measure, value, degree;</i> immediately thereafter, he renders the same word by <span dir=\"rtl\">יכולת</span>, whereas it ought to be translated by <span dir=\"rtl\">שיעור</span>. Al-’Harîzi translats most inexactly: <span dir=\"rtl\">כדי שתחזק אמונת האיש ההוא או האומה ותכלית צדקתו</span>. As for the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יעלם</span>, it must be pronounced in the passive <span dir=\"rtl\">(يَعْلَمُ)</span>. Ibn-Tibbon translats it actively, adding the subject <span dir=\"rtl\">עד שידע השם ית׳</span>.",
    "fn.3037": "That is to say, it is very difficult to interpret the six passages in the sense that has just been indicated; for this would imply that God needs an inquiry to know the truth, and that His knowledge can undergo a change.",
    "fn.3038": "Literally: <i>et d’eux deux</i>, that is, Abraham and Isaac. Here experience and inquiry could obviously serve only God Himself.",
    "fn.3039": "The text reads: <i>and it was said to him</i>, that is, to Abraham. The mss. of the version of Ibn-Tibbon also have <span dir=\"rtl\">ונאמר לו</span>, while the editions incorrectly have <span dir=\"rtl\">ונאמר להם</span>.",
    "fn.3040": "That is to say, the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">לדעת</span>, <i>to know</i>, does not signify “that <i>God</i> may know it,” but “that <i>one</i> may know,” as in the verse of Exodus, <small>XXXI</small>, 13. — Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בל דׄלך</span>, several MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">בדׄלך</span>, and likewise Ibn-Tibbon (MSS.): <span dir=\"rtl\">בי זה הוא כבר ידעו כאמרו וגו׳</span>; but the sense here evidently demands the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">בל</span>, <i>but</i>.",
    "fn.3041": "That is, in the passage of Exodus.",
    "fn.3042": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">إيهام</span> (a noun of action of the IV<sup>th</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">وهم</span>) denots a manner of speaking or acting, the object of which is to cause that to be admitted as true which is but an <i>insinuation</i>. Ibn-Tibbon simply has <span dir=\"rtl\">אותותיו</span>, <i>his signs</i>, referring to the passage in Deuteronomy: <span dir=\"rtl\">ונתן אליך אות או מופת</span>.",
    "fn.3043": "That is to say: the Gentiles, perceiving the unshaken faith of the Hebrews, shall take it for a model and shall possess firm convictions against which all the pretended miracles wrought by false prophets shall prove futile. — The words <span dir=\"rtl\">לְמִתְחַתֵּר בְּמַעְגָּל</span>, which one has translated: <i>to the worker of miracles</i>, signify properly: <i>to him who strivs by miracle</i>, that is to say, who seeks to convince by miracle. <span dir=\"rtl\">מתחד</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(مُتَّحِد)</span> is the participle of the V<sup>th</sup> form of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">خَدِيَ</span>, which the Dictionaries explain by pugnavit, certavit in aliqua re peragenda. Ibn-Tibbon translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">ל עשיית מופת</span>, and Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">למפתה במעשה נס</span>; Ibn-Falaquéra (Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 157) with good reason censurs these two translations, and proposs to render: <span dir=\"rtl\">ל מתגבר במופת</span>, <i>to him who seeks to prevail by miracle</i>.",
    "fn.3044": "Literally: <i>it is a call</i> or <i>an invitation to impossible things;</i> that is to say: the false prophet, by proclaiming the existence of several gods, would invite belief in matters whose impossibility is demonstrated; all his miracles cannot make falsehood truth, for a miracle can serve but to confirm a thing possible. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">דעא</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon takes for a noun of action <span dir=\"rtl\">(دُعَاءُ)</span> and which he translats by <span dir=\"rtl\">קריאה</span>, has been translated by Al-’Harîzi in the sense of the preterite <span dir=\"rtl\">(دعا)</span>: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי הוא קורא להאמין בנמנע</span>. Perchance Al-’Harîzi had <span dir=\"rtl\">ידעו</span>, in the aorist, as indeed one of our MSS. possesses it. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתחדי</span>, which one has rendered by <i>to lean upon</i>, signifies properly <i>to overcome, to prevail</i>. See the preceding note.",
    "fn.3045": "See treatise <i>Yesodé ha-Tôrâ</i>, chapter <small>VIII</small>.",
    "fn.3046": "Word for word: <i>and may your feet not slip</i>.",
    "fn.3047": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">כמא זעמתם</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon did not translate, seem to refer to this passage from Exodus (20, 10): <i>Speak, you, with us, and we will listen, and let God not speak with us, lest we die</i>.",
    "fn.3048": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">إغيام</span>, see tome I, p. 159, note 3, and tome II, p. 217, note 1.",
    "fn.3049": "Literally: <i>to wit, that a man be sterile</i>. All the MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">רגׄלא עקימא</span>, both words in the accusative; one believs that it must be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">רَجُلًا عَقِيمًا</span>, considering the first of these two words as the <i>subject</i> of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">يكون</span> and the second as the <i>enunciative</i> or <i>predicate</i>.",
    "fn.3050": "In Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בו</span> should be changed to <span dir=\"rtl\">בזה</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates <span dir=\"rtl\">ויבז בעיניו זה הבן החמוד</span>.",
    "fn.3051": "That is to say: one must not explain Abraham's action by any other circumstance whatsoever that might have guided him, nor believe that he was under the sway of any impression whatsoever, as, for instance, fear. — The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תדעי</span>, which may be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">تُدْعَى</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">تُدَّعِي</span> (the passive of the first or of the eighth form), signifies in the former case <i>to invoke, to call</i>, and in the latter case <i>to pretend, to presume;</i> some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">תרעי</span> (with <span dir=\"rtl\">ר</span>) or <span dir=\"rtl\">יראעי</span>, and, according to these readings, one should translate the verb as <i>to consider, to have regard for</i>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יותׄר אנפעאל בוגׄה</span> signify literally: <i>nor prefer in any wise an impression</i>; that is to say, nor prefer to explain Abraham's action by an impression he might have received, as, for instance, by a feeling of fear. Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא לעורר הפעלות כלל</span>, which is inexact. Ibn-Falaquéra rightly observes that Ibn-Tibbon appears to have confounded <span dir=\"rtl\">יותׄר</span> with <span dir=\"rtl\">יהי</span>, aorist of <span dir=\"rtl\">אתׄאר</span> (the fourth form of the root <span dir=\"rtl\">ثار</span>), <i>to excite</i>. Vide Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 157.",
    "fn.3052": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תעין</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(تعين)</span>  means here <i>to be prescribed, to be incumbent as a duty</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's translation, <span dir=\"rtl\">כדי שיתפרסם לאדם מה ראוי לעשותו</span>, does not seem accurate to me. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא לתורה פשוטה הראויה לבני אדם</span>; it seems he has better grasped the sense, although he expresses himself with less clarity.",
    "fn.3053": "Allusion to the second verse of chapter <small>XXII</small> of Genesis.",
    "fn.3054": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">או שבהה̈</span> were not translated by Ibn-Tibbon.",
    "fn.3055": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלעטׄים כׄטרה</span> literally mean: <i>of great importance;</i> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄטרה</span> has not been translated by Ibn-Tibbon, nor by Al-’Harîzi; the latter translates:<span dir=\"rtl\">and his soul was not drawn to do this great deed on account of a doubtful matter</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מספק</span> is Hebrew, <i>from doubt</i>.",
    "fn.3056": "All the mss. have , and similarly Al-’Harîzi has ; Ibn-Tibbon translates inaccurately . The author means that alongside Abraham, his only legitimate son, was the most suitable to figure in this story.",
    "fn.3057": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולקיים האמונה</span>; the manuscripts, however, conform to the Arabic text, <span dir=\"rtl\">ולקיים הנבואה</span>.",
    "fn.3058": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הנחלקות</span> is a printing error; it should be read <span dir=\"rtl\">הנלקחות</span>.",
    "fn.3059": "That is to say, the sixth article of faith that establishes the truth of prophecy. Ibn-Tibbon's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">פנת אמונת הנבואה</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">עיקר אמתת הנבואה</span>. It is likely that Ibn-Tibbon also wrote <span dir=\"rtl\">אמתת</span>, which was changed to <span dir=\"rtl\">אמונת</span>.",
    "fn.3060": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אמרא</span>, which we pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">أَمْرًا</span>  (a <i>thing</i>)<italic/>, has been rendered in the version of Ibn-Tibbon by <span dir=\"rtl\">אדם</span> (a <i>man</i>)<italic/>, that is <span dir=\"rtl\">(مُرّ)</span> . Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ירצה לנסות דבר ולבחון אותו</span>.",
    "fn.3061": "Ibn-Tibbon rendered the text to the second person <span dir=\"rtl\">at great trouble</span> to the second person; but the Arabic verb must be pronounced in the first person <span dir=\"rtl\">(I have toiled)</span>. Al-’Harîzi translated exactly <span dir=\"rtl\">I have been wearied</span>.",
    "fn.3062": "In the <i>Kitâb al-Ta’rifât</i>, it is stated: “<i>’Abath</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">عبث</span> signifies to occupy oneself with a thing of which one is unaware of any utility, or to act without a true purpose.” See Freytag’s Dictionary for this word.",
    "fn.3063": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">כל מן תבע</span>, some mss. have: <span dir=\"rtl\">בל כל מתבע</span>; it is this reading that seems to be expressed by Ibn-Tibbon, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וגם לפי דעת כל נמשך אחר תורת מרע״ה</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates in the same sense: <span dir=\"rtl\">לפי דעתנו אנחנו וכל מאמין תורת מ״ר</span>.",
    "fn.3064": "See vol. II, p. 119, note 4, and above, chap. <small>XIII</small>, p. 85.",
    "fn.3065": "The author alludes here to certain Motécallemin, and particularly to the Ascharites, who deny all causality and make everything depend on the sole will of God. See the t. I, chap. <small>LXXIII</small>, 6<sup>th proposition, p. 389-395; and <i>Mélanges etc.</i>, p. 326 and p. 378-379.",
    "fn.3066": "Here again, the author alludes to the Ascharites, who deny all <i>causalité</i>, both in the universe as a whole and in the smallest details of creation.",
    "fn.3067": "See Vol. I, p. 111, note 2.",
    "fn.3068": "Literally: <i>that which His wisdom demands should be in such a manner</i>. The meaning is: God wills not all that is possible in itself and by the nature of things, but divine wisdom preferrs certain possibilities to others, and wills that the possible should realise itself in this or that manner. One must recall what the author has stated above, chapter <small>XX</small>, p. 152, namely, that divine prescience does not remove the possible from its nature as <i>possible</i>. Consequently, those things which by their nature are <i>possible</i> and which may in reality either be or not be, are realised in a certain manner, whether by that which divine wisdom demands, or by the free will of man.",
    "fn.3069": "Literally: <i>as for the work wholly good which God wills to do, no impediment can intervene between it and Him, and nought can hinder it</i>. In the words <span dir=\"rtl\">בינה ובינה</span>, the former suffix referrs to the <i>work</i> and the latter to <i>God</i>. The manuscripts of Ibn-Tibbon's version exhibit, in conformity with the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא יבדיל בינו ובינו מונע</span>; in the printed editions, <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span> has been awkwardly changed to <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא</span>, and for greater clarity, <span dir=\"rtl\">בינו ובינו</span> has been explained by <span dir=\"rtl\">בינו ובין הפעל ההוא</span>.",
    "fn.3070": "Instead of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מתכררא</span>, one of our manuscripts has <span dir=\"rtl\">מדׄכורא</span>, and similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">נזכר</span>.",
    "fn.3071": "See <i>Beréschith rabbâ</i>, section 9, at the beginning; <i>midrasch Kohéleth</i>, fol. 67, col. 1: <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר ר׳ תנחומא בעונתו נברא העולם לא היה העולם ראוי להבראות קודם לכן</span>. \"Rabbi Tan’houma said: the world was created <i>in its time</i>, that is, it was not fitting for the world to be created before then.\"",
    "fn.3072": "All our manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">אצלה</span> with the masculine singular suffix, which can refer only to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מעטׄם</span>. One has preferred to write <span dir=\"rtl\">אצלהא</span>, with the feminine suffix referring to the collective <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאוהאם</span>. Ibn-Tibbon's version likewise bears <span dir=\"rtl\">שרשם</span>, with the plural suffix referring to <span dir=\"rtl\">הספיקות</span>; nevertheless, some manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">שרשו</span>, and similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ודע בי רוב השניאות ⋯ הוא מפני טעות ונוי</span>.",
    "fn.3073": "Cf. what the author said earlier about the final cause of the universe and its parts, chap. XIII, p. 92 and following.",
    "fn.3074": "If the works of God, they said, emanated from divine wisdom, the world would necessarily be eternal, like the eternal nature of divine wisdom itself.",
    "fn.3075": "That is, according to both opinions, one must acknowledge that the entirety of the universe and all its details have a certain purpose, in conformity with divine wisdom, and that God has not done anything in vain.",
    "fn.3076": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בינאה</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">בינהא</span>; although this reading offers no sense, it has been reproduced by Ibn-Tibbon, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא במה שביניהם</span>.",
    "fn.3077": "Word for word: <i>and we do not recognize in it the mode of wisdom</i>. All our mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">פיה</span> with the masculine singular suffix, which can only be grammatically related to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בעצׄהא</span>. Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi have <span dir=\"rtl\">בהם</span>, as if they had read in the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">פיהא</span>.",
    "fn.3078": "See Deuteronome, XXII, 11; Exode, 23, 19; Lévitique, XVI, 10 and 21.",
    "fn.3079": "That is to say, Satan, or the spirit of doubt and contradiction, critiqus these regulations as useless, and the Gentiles make them an object of pleasantry and employ them to impugn the divinity of the Law. The author has in view a passage from the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Yomâ</i>, fol. 67 <i>b</i>, albeit the terms are not precisely identical. Cf. the <i>Eight Chapters</i>, serving as an introduction to the commentary on the treatise <i>Aboth</i>, at the close of chapter 6.",
    "fn.3080": "All Arab mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">מתׄל תלך</span>, <i>like those</i>, that is, as obvious as it is for the prohibitions of killing and stealing. Ibn-Tibbon rendered the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מתׄל תלך</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">במו שהתבאר בנזכרים</span>.",
    "fn.3081": "Literally: <i>the foreskin;</i> we call thus the fruits borne by the tree during the first three years. See Leviticus, chapter <small>XIX</small>, <i>verse</i> 23.",
    "fn.3082": "That is, the planting of heterogeneous plants in the midst of vines. See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXII</small>, <i>v</i>. 9, and further on, chapter <small>XXXVII</small>.",
    "fn.3083": "The author intends that the doctors explain the verse from Deuteronomy by an ellipsis, by understanding, before <span dir=\"rtl\">מכם</span>, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ואם רק הוא</span>; so that the meaning of the verse would be thus: the law is not a vain thing, and if it appears vain unto you, that comes from yourselves. Vide Jerusalem Talmud, treatise <i>Péâ</i>, chap. I (<i>Yephé mareh, ibid.</i>, § 2); treatise <i>Kethoubôth</i>, chap. <small>VIII</small>, at the close.",
    "fn.3084": "Vide the <i>Midrash</i> on Ecclesiastes, at chapter <small>VII</small>, <i>v</i>. 23 (fol. 76, col 4): <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר שלמה על בל אלה עמדתי ופשפשתי ופרשה של פרה אדומה חקרתי ביון שהייתי יגע בה ודורש וחוקר בה אמרתי אחכמה והיא רחוקה ממני</span> \"Solomon said: I have tarried to seek out all this; I have scrutinised the chapter of the red heifer. But after having wearied myself in examining and scrutinising it, I said unto myself: Shall I possess wisdom? It is far from me.\"",
    "fn.3085": "These three commandments concern kings; it is forbidden unto them 1° to possess many horses, for the reason that a fondness for horses might lead to relations with Egypt; 2° to possess many wives, because a fondness for women might divert them from their duties; 3° to amass much gold and silver, because the possession of great riches might render them proud. Vide Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XVII</small>, <i>v</i>. 16 and 17. It is true that the reason for the 3<sup>rd</sup> prohibition is not indicated in verse 17; but the author appears to find it in verse 20, where it is stated: <i>that his heart be not lifted up above his brethren, etc.</i> Vide what the author states concerning these <i>three</i> commandments, at the close of his <i>Sépher Miçwoth</i>. In the Talmud, tractate <i>Synhedrin</i>, fol. 21 <i>b</i>, mention is made only of the first two prohibitions <span dir=\"rtl\">שתי מקראות</span> which Solomon transgressed, asserting that he would well know how to avoid relations with Egypt and the seduction of women. Doubtless it is this Talmudic text which moved the editors of Ibn-Tibbon's version to alter the word <span dir=\"rtl\">שלש</span> into <span dir=\"rtl\">שתי</span> (cf. the commentary of Ephodi). Nevertheless, the MSS. of this version and the edition <i>princeps</i> bear <span dir=\"rtl\">שלש מצות</span>, <i>three</i> commandments, conformably to the Arabic text, and likewise the version of Al-’Harizi.",
    "fn.3086": "That is to say, God wanted to impose certain religious duties on man solely in order to prescribe duties to him, and without the prescribed thing having any purpose in itself.",
    "fn.3087": "That is, to inspire in them for God feelings of submission and passive obedience; or, as the author appears to indicate further on, to inspire in them feelings of compassion for animals, by prescribing to them that they slaughter them in a manner that would cause them less suffering.",
    "fn.3088": "The text of the Psalms has <i>purified, pure;</i> but it is known that certain rabbis, when it concerns substantiating their pronouncements with biblical texts, do not pride themselves on grammatical exactitude. The passage in question is found in <i>Bereshith Rabbah</i>, sect. 44, at the commencement (fol. 38, col. 3); cf. <i>Midrash Tanhuma</i>, section <span dir=\"rtl\">שמיני</span> (Verona edit., fol. 53, col. 4): <span dir=\"rtl\">וכי מה אכפת לו להק״בה בין ששוחט הבהמה ואוכל או נוחר ואוכל וגו׳</span>. The citation of the passage from <i>Bereshith Rabbah</i> presents some slight variants. The editions of the <i>Midrash</i> have: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכי מה אכפת לו להק״בה למי ששוחט מן הצואר או מי ששוחט מן העורף</span>. All our Arabic MSS. bear … <span dir=\"rtl\">מי שהוא שוחט מן הצואר ומי שהוא</span>. The reading which one has adopted (<span dir=\"rtl\">בין מי שהוא … למי שהוא …</span>) is that of Ibn-Tibbon, which possesses the advantage of being more conformable to the manner in which the author reproduces the words in question further on.",
    "fn.3089": "More literally: <i>in such wise that one departs not from the general tenor of their pronouncement;</i> that is to say, from the principle which they have proclaimed elsewhere throughout, namely, that all commandments possess a reason. Instead of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כלום</span>, which most editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version present here, the MSS. and the edition <i>princeps</i> bear <span dir=\"rtl\">בלם</span>, conformably to the Arabic text, which has <span dir=\"rtl\">אגׄמע</span>.",
    "fn.3090": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄמלה̈ אלמצות</span>, <i>the totality</i> or <i>the ensemble of the commandments</i>, lack precision; but one perceives from the examples which the author is about to cite that he intends to establish a distinction between those commandments which contain a general disposition and those pertaining to specific details. Ibn-Tibbon deemed it better to bring forth the author's thought by rendering <span dir=\"rtl\">המצוה</span> in the singular, and according to him, one ought to translate it as: <i>the generality of the commandment</i>.",
    "fn.3091": "See above note 1 of the previous page.",
    "fn.3092": "See further, chapter <small>XLVIII</small>.",
    "fn.3093": "See the traditional dispositions concerning the manner of slaughtering animals, in the <i>Mishnah</i>, V<sup>th</sup> part, tractate <i>Hullin</i>, chap. <small>II</small>. — The Arabic casuists distinguish two methods of slaughtering animals; one is designated by the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">דׄבח</span>, signifying <i>to cut the throat in the upper part;</i> the other by <span dir=\"rtl\">נחר</span>, signifying <i>to pierce the lower part of the neck</i>, in the hollow, near the <i>sternum</i>. Vide <i>The Hedaya or Guide, a commentary on the musulmans laws, translated</i> by Hamilton, vol. IV, p. 72: «The most eligible method of slaying a camel is by <i>na’hr</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(نحن)</span> , that is spearing it in the hollow of the throat, near the breast-bone, etc.» Doubtless it is in the same sense that one must understand the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">נחר</span>, in the Mishnah, loco citato </i>, <small>V</small>, § 3, <small>VI</small>, § 2, and Talmud, ibidem </i> f. 17 <i>a</i>; at the very least, one could not admit the explanation of Obadiah of Bertinoro, according to which <span dir=\"rtl\">נחר</span> would signify to thrust the knife into the nostrils (<span dir=\"rtl\">הנוחר שתוחב הסכין בנחיריו וחותך</span>).",
    "fn.3094": "See above, p.206, note 2.",
    "fn.3095": "The author intends to convey that he has cited this example solely because it appears from the terms employed by the doctors that these did deem the particular regulations concerning the <i>sche’hita</i>, or the manner of slaughtering animals, to be provisions for which one could assign no reason. As for himself, he adds, he does acknowledge a moral aim in these regulations; for the legislator sought to prescribe that animals should be put to death in such wise as to cause them the least possible suffering, and to indicate the means of achieving this result.",
    "fn.3096": "See further on, chapter <small>XLVI</small>.",
    "fn.3097": "That is to say, in accordance with the free will, man can do it in one way or another, and without his action being determined by any purpose whatsoever.",
    "fn.3098": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ָבזט</span>, which the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have here, offers no meaning; the manuscripts of this version, like those of the Arabic text, bear <span dir=\"rtl\">מוטא</span>.",
    "fn.3099": "See t. I, chap. XXXI[0]XXXI[1], p. 105.",
    "fn.3100": "Cf. <i>ibid.</i>, chapter <small>XXXIII</small>.",
    "fn.3101": "Ibn-Tibbon's version deviates slightly from the Arabic text. Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">and it shall not be permitted to any man among men to do his will, and according to what his strength can achieve</span>.",
    "fn.3102": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">קסר</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">יקר</span>, <i>which he persists in doing</i>, <i>which he constantly does.</i> Ibn-Tibbon simply <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל יעשה</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates: (i. e. <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל ישתמש בה</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ביכלתו</span>.",
    "fn.3103": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בהכרח</span>, which is read in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">בחברה</span>, as the mss. of this version have it. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">מדות טובות מועילות בחברתם</span>.",
    "fn.3104": "Literally: <i>that he be in good health and in the best of his bodily conditions.</i>",
    "fn.3105": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">(كَن) دَا</span>  means <i>what serves to cover</i>, <i>garment, shelter</i>, <i>habitation.</i> Ibn-Tibbon translates this word by <span dir=\"rtl\">דירה</span>, <i>habitation;</i> Al-’Harîzi by <span dir=\"rtl\">מחסה</span>, <i>shelter.</i>",
    "fn.3106": "See vol. II, chap. <small>XL</small>, p. 306, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.3107": "See vol. I, p. 306, 307, note. — In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ר״ל שיהיה לו שכל בפעל</span> have been omitted, which are found in the mss. of this version and in that of Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.3108": "Literally: <i>that man may receive something intelligible;</i> that is, something belonging to the realm of the intelligible, pure thoughts or ideas.",
    "fn.3109": "See volume II, chapter <small>XXXIX</small>, p. 301 and following.",
    "fn.3110": "All the Arabic mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשריעה̈</span> in the singular; Ibn-Tibbon's version has <span dir=\"rtl\">אלו התורות בלם</span> in the plural, and similarly that of Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלה המצות</span>.",
    "fn.3111": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate Kiddouschin, fol. 39 b; 'Hullin, fol. 142 a. In our editions of the Talmud, the reading differs a little from the citation of Maimonides; this is in accordance with the reading of the Yalkout, tome I, n° 930, towards the end.",
    "fn.3112": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ותחדיד</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ותחריר</span> (with <span dir=\"rtl\">ר</span>), which does not make a difference for the meaning. The first lesson is confirmed by the two translators. Ibn-Tibbon renders the action name <span dir=\"rtl\">תחדיד</span> by the participle <span dir=\"rtl\">מוגבלות</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">בהפרש והגבלה</span>.",
    "fn.3113": "As the author has explained elsewhere, one cannot approach metaphysics with fruitfulness without first undergoing many preparatory studies. See vol. I, chap. <small>XXXIV</small>, pp. 121-124.",
    "fn.3114": "The translation by Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">ולזה ראוי שייראו ויפחדו ממרות בו</span>, is not entirely accurate; Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">וע״כ יצטרך ליפחוד ממנו ולירוא ולהשמר מן הפשע</span>.",
    "fn.3115": "The author is referring here to speculative truths, related to created things and which are the object of physical sciences.",
    "fn.3116": "By <i>those</i>, the author means: the ideas that are the ultimate end.",
    "fn.3117": "See Deuteronomy, ch. <small>XI</small>, <i>v.</i> 13 and 22; ch. <small>XIX</small>, <i>v.</i> 9; ch. <small>XXX</small>, <i>v.</i> 6, 16, 20.",
    "fn.3118": "That is to say: the doctors have likewise observed that it is in the contemplation of creation that man derivs his true love of God. See what the author says (after the <i>Siphri</i>, upon Deuteronomy, chap. <small>VI</small>, <i>v.</i> 5) in his <i>Mischné Torâ</i> or Abridgement of the Talmud, book I, treatise <i>Yesodé ha-torâ</i>, chap. II, § 2 et seq.",
    "fn.3119": "In the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">להאמין</span>, required by the sense, has been added; the mss. carry, in accordance with the Arabic text, <span dir=\"rtl\">למה נצטוינו שהשם אחד</span>.",
    "fn.3120": "See p. 206, note 1.",
    "fn.3121": "See Deuteronomy, <small>XXII</small>. 11; Leviticus, <small>XIX</small>, 19; Exodus, <small>XXIII</small>, 19.",
    "fn.3122": "See Leviticus, <small>XVII</small>, 13; Deuteronomy, <small>XXI</small>, 1 to 9; Exodus, <small>XIII</small>, 13.",
    "fn.3124": "This manner of considering paganism in general is founded upon Holy Scripture, which speaks solely of the pagans of Western Asia, presenting them generally as devoted to the worship of the stars, symbolically represented by idols. Cf. what our author states in his commentary upon the Mishnah, treatise, <i>Aboda Zarâ</i>, ch. <small>IV</small>, § 7, and <i>passim</i>, and in his <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, I<sup>st</sup> part, treatise <i>on Idolatry</i>, chap. <small>I</small>. It is in the same vein that the Arab Christian historian Aboul-Faradj, in several passages of his <i>History of the Dynasties</i>, expresses himself concerning the cult of the Sabeans, a name by which, like Maimonides, he designates all pagan peoples. Vide Chwolson, <i>l. c.</i>, p. 254-55.",
    "fn.3125": "The author likely refers to the purported historical accounts found in the <i>Agriculture Nabatéenne</i>, of which he speaks later, p. 231.",
    "fn.3126": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מנהא</span> (<i>by them</i>, i.e. by these documents) is missing in several mss., and the two Hebrew versions do not reproduce it.",
    "fn.3127": "All our mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאלאה</span> in the singular; similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">your faith in the stars that they are gods</span>. In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">the gods</span> is an error; the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">the goddess</span>.",
    "fn.3128": "Cf. Schahrestani, p. 245 (trad. all., t. II, p. 68).",
    "fn.3129": "All the Arabic manuscripts have: <span dir=\"rtl\">סאיר אלסבעה̈ כואכב</span>; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">םאיר</span> here bears the meaning of <i>all</i> (Cf. vol. II, p. 318, note 5, and p. 334, note 5). Al-’Harîzi translates exactly: <span dir=\"rtl\">שאר שבעת הכוכבים</span>. Ibn-Tibbon, having failed to apprehend the sense of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">סאיר</span> here, translated: <span dir=\"rtl\">ששאר הכוכבים החמשה</span> 'the other <i>five</i> planets'; but perhaps this is merely an awkward correction by the copyists.",
    "fn.3130": "Coutha is, according to Arab geographers, a city situated in Babylonian Irak, to the south of Baghdad and near the canal <i>Nahr-Malca</i>, between the Euphrates and the Tigris. Some modern authors identify this Coutha of the Arab geographers with the district of the same name mentioned in the Bible (II Kings, <small>XVII</small>, 24). Vide Rosenmüller, <i>Biblische Alterthumskunde</i>, vol. I, 2<sup>nd</sup> part, pp. 29 and 74; Winer, <i>Bibl. Realwörterbuch</i>, vol. I, sub verbo <i>Cutha.</i> The Babylonian Talmud (<i>Baba Bathra</i>, fol. 91 <i>a</i>) appears to identify <span dir=\"rtl\">כותא</span> with Ur-Kasdim, where, according to Genesis, Abraham spent his youth, and which is considered the place of his birth.",
    "fn.3131": "All the Arabic manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">לטרף אלשאם</span>, <i>towards the region of Syria</i>, whilst Ibn-Tibbon’s version reads <span dir=\"rtl\">לקצה המזרח</span>, <i>to the extremity of the East;</i> Al-’Harîzi translates more exactly <span dir=\"rtl\">ויגרש אותו לקצה ארץ כנען</span>, <i>he expelled him towards the extremity of the land of Canaan.</i> — One discerns in this narrative an echo of the Jewish traditions which explain Abraham’s emigration (Genesis, <small>XI</small>, 31) by the persecutions he was compelled to endure in his native land. Josephus merely states that the Chaldeans and other peoples of Mesopotamia having risen against him, he deemed it prudent to emigrate (<i>Antiquities</i>, I, 7, § 1). According to the Rabbinical traditions, Nimrod caused him to be cast into a furnace, from which he was miraculously saved (vide one’s work <i>Palestine</i>, p. 102 <i>b</i>]). This tradition has likewise found a place among some Church Fathers and in the Koran, and Muslim authors have embellished it with numerous details of their own imagination. Concerning these diverse traditions, one may consult B. Beer, <i>Leben Abrahams</i>, <i>nach Auffassung der jüdischen Sage</i>, chap. II. Regarding the Muslim traditions in particular, vide d’Herbelot, <i>Bibliothèque orientale</i>, under the article <i>Abraham;</i> Hyde, <i>De religione veterum Persarum</i>, p. 27 and following (2<sup>nd</sup> edition); Reinaud, <i>Monuments arabes</i>, <i>persans et turcs</i>, vol. I, p. 144 and following.",
    "fn.3132": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">הכדׄא הכוא</span> were not translated by Ibn-Tibbon.",
    "fn.3133": "The author will provide more detailed information on this famous work later. See p. 231, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.3134": "The Arabic text reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">אהצארנא</span>, <i>our monuments</i>, which can refer to both the Holy Scripture and rabbinic traditions; the version of Ibn-Tibbon reads <span dir=\"rtl\">ספרינו</span>. Al-’Harîzi appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">ולם ידׄכרוא אתׄארה אלצאדקה̈</span>; for he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא זכרו אותותיו הנאמנות</span>, which does not offer a suitable meaning.",
    "fn.3135": "Literally: <i>and that he preferred truth to his honor.</i> Ibn-Tibbon translates inaccurately <span dir=\"rtl\">וכן הדין לעשות לכבודו</span>, <i>and it is just to act thus for his glory</i>, that is, for the glory of God; Al-’Harîzi omitted this passage.",
    "fn.3136": "Word for word: and the outcome (or the result) thereof has been what we see today.",
    "fn.3137": "Literally: <i>with the exception of the remnants of that vanished sect</i> or <i>lost.</i> The author intends to speak of the adherents of <i>Sabianism</i>, a word which, for him, designates paganism in all its varied forms. Concerning the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מלה̈</span>, cf. vol. I, p. 340, note 2. The participle <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמחמדה</span>, or, as some manuscripts have it, <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמדמרה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(الْمُدَمِّرَة)</span>, signifies <i>lost</i>, <i>vanished</i>, <i>extinct</i>, and it is erroneously that Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">השפלה</span>, <i>vile.</i> But one of our manuscripts has <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמדׄכורה̈</span>, and this reading is reproduced by Al-’Harîzi, who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא שארית האומה ההיא הנזכרת</span>.",
    "fn.3138": "On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אגיא</span>, see vol. II, p. 217, note 1.",
    "fn.3139": "In other words: the speculation of the earliest pagan philosophers could only have risen to a kind of pantheism, such as that of the Ionian school. Cf. Vol. I, chap. <small>LXX, </small>p. 325.",
    "fn.3140": "On this philosopher, also known as Ibn-Badja, see my Mélanges etc., p. 383 and following. His commentary on the Acroasis, or Physics of Aristotle, which is also cited by Ibn-Abi Océibi'a (see ibid., p. 386), has not come down to us.",
    "fn.3141": "This is a necessary consequence of their belief in the eternity of the world. Cf. <i>Khozari</i>, l. I, § 1.",
    "fn.3142": "According to the <i>Nabatæan Agriculture</i>, Adami or Adam, a disciple of an ancient sage named Yanbouschâd (vide <i>Khozari</i>, lib. I, § 61), is said to have penned a thousand folios, in which he surveyed such plants as flourish in one country but fail in another, and detailed their virtues and their properties, whether useful or deleterious. To him likewise was attributed a momentous work concerning the nature of soils, their various savours, their qualities, and their productions. Vide Quatremère, <i>Mémoire sur les Nabatéens</i>, in the <i>Journal Asiatique</i>, March 1835, p. 228. Among other works, there is attributed to him one entitled <i>Livre des mystères de la Lune</i>, which treated of the artificial generation of plants. Vide Chwolson, <i>Ueber die Ueberreste der altbabylonischen Literatur in arabischen Uebersetzungen</i>, in vol. VIII of the Mémoires présentés à l’Académie de St-Pétersbourg par divers savants (separate impression; 1859, p. 166). Cf. hereinafter, p. 233, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.3143": "Vide Quatremère, <i>loc. cit.</i>, p. 229, where it is stated, according to the <i>Nabatæan Agriculture</i>, that Noah was reputed author of a great work inspired in him by the moon, and in which is likewise mentioned a letter which he penned to an ancient Canaanite sage, exhorting him to forsake the worship of the planets and to adore the sole eternal God. Cf. the particulars which M. Chwolson (<i>loc.</i> <i>cit.</i>, pp. 142 and 176) provides, from the same source, concerning the sage named <i>Anou’ha</i>, who is none other than Noah.",
    "fn.3144": "By the name of <i>Seth</i>, the author doubtless designates that personage who, in the <i>Nabatæan Agriculture</i>, is oft mentioned under the appellation of <i>Ischîta</i>, son of Adâmi, and who is held to be the founder or propagator of astrolatry and of all the superstitions pertaining thereto. Vide Chwolson, <i>loc. cit.</i>, p. 27. — M. Quatremère, following the example of Maimonides and Judah Hallevi, author of the book <i>Khozari</i>, rightly discerns in the names of <i>Adâmi</i>, <i>Ischîta</i>, <i>Anou’ha</i> and <i>Ibrahim el-Kana’ani</i>, the Biblical names of Adam, Seth, Noah, and Abraham (vide the <i>Journal Asiatique</i>, <i>loc. cit.</i>, and the <i>Journal des Savants</i>, March 1857, p. 147), and it is in vain that M. Chwolson (<i>loc. cit.</i>, pp. 43-44), in favour of his untenable system, disputes this identification.",
    "fn.3145": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואנהם</span> (for which several MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">והם</span>), one believes it ought to be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">وإذنهم</span>, and certainly. The two Hebrew translators read <span dir=\"rtl\">ואנהם</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon renders <span dir=\"rtl\">ושהיו</span>, and Al-’Harîzi <span dir=\"rtl\">וכי היו</span>. But this does not connect well with the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מורים על</span>) <span dir=\"rtl\">תדל עלי</span>).",
    "fn.3147": "All the Arabic mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">טולהא קאמה̈</span>, <i>whose length was a man's stature</i>. The translation of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">כקומת אדם</span>, is ambiguous, and modern translators, such as Buxtorf and Scheier, have thought that it referred to Adam's stature. Al-’Harîzi translates with greater precision: <span dir=\"rtl\">קומה אחת</span>.",
    "fn.3148": "The author means that one should be astonished that the Sabians, who, like the philosophers, admitted the eternity of the world, and who, consequently, must have believed that everything in nature was subject to an eternal and immutable law, could nonetheless believe in so many things that are in manifest opposition to the laws of nature.",
    "fn.3149": "All our mss. have: <span dir=\"rtl\">הו אלאלאה</span>; the masculine singular pronoun agrees, by a kind of attraction, with the following word. Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi have <span dir=\"rtl\">הם האלוה</span>, as if they had read in their Arabic text <span dir=\"rtl\">הי</span>, which would be more natural.",
    "fn.3150": "That is to say, when Abraham emerged from childhood. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">עמודו של עולם</span>, <i>pillar of the world</i>, form an epithet frequently bestowed upon Abraham, who was the first to combat idolatry and proclaim the existence of the Creator. The author employs this epithet in the <i>Mischné Tora</i>, treatise on <i>Idolatry</i>, ch. I, § 2. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">نشأ</span> , signifying <i>to grow, to wax great</i>, has been inaccurately rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version by <span dir=\"rtl\">יצא</span> and in Al-’Harîzi's by <span dir=\"rtl\">נולד</span>.",
    "fn.3151": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">مُفَارِقُ</span>, see vol. II, p. 31, note 2.",
    "fn.3152": "See Genesis, <small>XXI</small>, 33, and vol. I, p. 3, note 2.",
    "fn.3153": "Cf. the description of the temples of the Sabians by Schems ed-Din Dimeschki, in the work <i>The Sabians</i> by M. Chwolson, vol. II, p. 380 and following, and particularly that of the temples of the sun and the moon, p. 390 and p. 396.",
    "fn.3154": "That is to say, they assigned to each planet one of the seven metals and of the seven climes, attributing to each planet an influence over one of the climes, and, like the alchemists of the Middle Ages, a participation in the formation of metals. Oriental writers enumerate seven metals, which are: gold, silver, copper, lead, iron, tin, and a 7<sup>th</sup> called <i>Khar-sîni</i> (Chinese iron). Cf. Kazwini, in M. Silvestre de Sacy’s <i>Chrestomathie arabe</i>, vol. III, p. 390. According to the Sabians, gold is attributed to the sun, silver to the moon, and so forth. Cf. Dimeschki, <i>l. c.</i>, p. 411. As for the seven climes, one has already stated that Yakout indicates their relations with the planets according to the Persians and the Greeks. According to the Persians, the respective order of the planets presiding over each clime conforms to the natural order of the planets, commencing with the last; thus Saturn presides over the 1<sup>st</sup> clime, Jupiter over the 2<sup>nd</sup>, Mars over the 3<sup>rd</sup>, the sun over the 4<sup>th</sup>, Venus over the 5<sup>th</sup>, Mercury over the 6<sup>th</sup>, the moon over the 7<sup>th</sup>. According to the Greeks, the order is as follows: Saturn, sun, Mercury, Jupiter, Venus, moon, Mars. Dimeschki, in his description of the temples consecrated to the planets (<i>l. c.</i>, p. 382 et seq.), follows the order adopted by the Persians; likewise Ibn-Ezra, in his astrological work <i>Réschith ’Hokhma</i>, ch. <small>IV</small>, when speaking of the influence exerted by each planet upon sublunary things. If one’s explanation of the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אקלים אלשמס</span> (p. 224, note 1) be exact, the author of the <i>Agriculture Nabatéenne</i> would have followed the order adopted by the Greeks.",
    "fn.3155": "Here, in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וידברו הצלמים ההם</span> are missing, which are found in the mss. of this version. After the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ותוחי ללנאם</span>, <i>inspiraient les hommes</i>, Ibn-Tibbon has reasonably omitted the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אעני אלאצנאם</span>, <i>je veux parler de ces statues</i>, which are found in the Arabic text, but are superfluous.",
    "fn.3156": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ופעל בהא</span> are found in all our mss., except the ms. of Leyden, no. 221. They are reproduced in the version of Ibn-Tibbon, which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">and he did it to him and with him</span>. Pococke, who, in his <i>Specimen Hist</i>. <i>Ar</i>., p. 142, published the Arabic text of the entire paragraph, omitted the two words in question.",
    "fn.3157": "Concerning the word <span dir=\"rtl\">דוחאניה̈</span>, cf. vol. I, p. 281, note 2. Regarding this paragraph in general, cf. Schahrestâni, p. 244 et seq. (German trans., vol. II, p. 66 et seq.), and Pococke, <i>l. c.</i>, p. 139 et seq. The latter emphasises (p. 143) that, according to Schahrestâni, the Sabians perceived in the spiritual forces of the stars only <i>intermediate</i> beings, above whom is the Lord of lords or the Supreme God, which is contrary to the idea that Maimonides presents concerning the religion of the Sabians; but one apprehends that Schahrestâni was led into error by the writings of some Sabians of Harran, who had intermingled the beliefs of the ancient Chaldean pagans with the philosophical doctrines of the Neoplatonists.",
    "fn.3158": "That is, as spoken of in our sacred books. See 1 Kings, chap. <small>XVIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 19. The author, in saying <i>tels furent</i>, refers to the mode of inspiration he has just discussed, and the sense is: in this manner were the prophets of Baal etc. inspired.",
    "fn.3159": "By the Hebrew words <span dir=\"rtl\">עזבו את י״י</span>, which are found in the Arabic text here, allusion is made to a passage from Isaiah, chap. 1, <i>v.</i> 4.",
    "fn.3160": "The author reproduces here in Hebrew the words from Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XVIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 10 and 11, which designate various species of diviners, magicians, and necromancers. It is not the place to cite the different opinions on the etymology and the true meaning of each of these words, and we have been content to substitute French words of an approximate sense.",
    "fn.3161": "See the I<sup>st</sup> part of the <i>Mischné Tora</i>, tractate <i>Aboda Zara</i> (on idolatry), chapter I, § 3.",
    "fn.3162": "Literally: was inspired. The word נבי (nabi) that all mss. have must be pronounced as the preterite passive: نبي (nabi).",
    "fn.3163": "Cf. Exodus, <small>XXXIV</small>, 13; Deuteronomy, <small>VII</small>, 5. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">מזבחותיהם תתצון</span>, as they are cited in all the Arabic MSS., as well as in the versions of Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi, are found only in a passage of the book of Judges, chap. <small>II</small>, <i>v.</i> 2; in the passage from Exodus, one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">את מזבחתׄם</span>, and in that from Deuteronomy <span dir=\"rtl\">תתצו</span>. The author has confounded these different verses in his memory. Cf. hereafter, p. 243, note 1.",
    "fn.3164": "It is known that the worshippers of <i>Moloch</i> would burn their children or pass them through fire in honor of this God. See; <i>Palestine</i>, p. 90 <i>a</i> and 91 <i>b.</i>",
    "fn.3165": "See above, chapter <small>XXVII</small>.",
    "fn.3166": "One has reproduced this citation precisely as it appears in all the MSS. of the Arabic text, as well as in the MSS. and editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version. The author has here confounded two Biblical passages: in the one, one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי אחרי התהו אשר לא יועילו ולא יצילו</span> (I Sam., <small>XII</small>, 21); in the other: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחרי לא יועילו הלכו</span> (Jeremiah, <small>II</small>, 8). Al-’Harîzi has corrected the citation by restoring the passage from Jeremiah.",
    "fn.3167": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ספורים</span>, in the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, is a printing error; the mss. of this version have <span dir=\"rtl\">הספרים</span>.",
    "fn.3168": "Abou-Bekr A’hmed ben ’Ali Ibn-Wa’hschiyya, descended from a Nabataean or Chaldean family which had embraced Islamism, published, in 291 A.H. (904 A.D.), a voluminous work entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">الفلاحة النبطية</span> , the <i>Nabataean Agriculture</i>, which he asserted to have translated from the Chaldean, and to which he ascribed as author an ancient Chaldean sage named Kothâmi. This latter cites many more ancient authors, at the head of whom one observs Dewanai, Çaghrith, and Yanbouschad; this last, the preceptor of Adâmi or Adam (cf. the <i>Khozari</i>, bk. I, § 61). The work in question contains, alongside many rational agronomical theories, a multitude of absurd fables and alleged historical data concerning the Canaanites, Chaldeans, and Assyrians. This work, which was at first known to European scholars solely through the citations of Maimonides, has, of late, been the subject of a more serious examination. M. Étienne Quatremère, who had at his disposal only the 2<sup>nd</sup> and 3<sup>rd</sup> parts of the work (Arabic MS. of the Imperial Library, No. 913), placs Kothâmi's floruit as far back as the commencement of the VI<sup>th</sup> century before the Christian era. Vide <i>Mémoire sur les Nabatéens</i>, in the <i>Journal Asiatique</i>, March 1835, p. 231 et seq. M. Chwolson, who had before him the MSS. of Leyden and Saint Petersburg, containing the entire work divided into nine parts, arrived at a result far more astonishing; according to him, the composition of the <i>Nabataean Agriculture</i> by Kothâmi would date back at least to the XIV<sup>th</sup> century before the Christian era. Vide the memoir cited above, p. 222, note 5. It has not been difficult, for authors more accustomed than M. Chwolson to handle historical criticism, to demonstrate all that is exorbitant in his thesis. Already Spencer had opined that this work and the Sabian writings in general must belong to that pseudepigraphic literature which attained such great development in the first centuries of the Christian era:",
    "fn.3169": "See tome I, p. 281, note 1.",
    "fn.3170": "By the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אדם הראשון</span>, Maimonides designats the ancient sage who, in the <i>Nabataean Agriculture</i>, is called sometimes <i>Adam</i>, sometimes <i>Adami</i>, and whom he rightly identifies with the Adam of Holy Scripture. Ibn-Wa’hschiyya, imbued with Muslim traditions and forgetting his rôle as translator of ancient Chaldean books, also sometimes adds to Adam the epithet of <span dir=\"rtl\">أبونا</span> , <i>our father</i>, or of <span dir=\"rtl\">أبو البشر</span> , <i>father of humanity.</i> Vide the aforesaid memoir of M. Chwolson, p. 24, note 33, and p. 174. According to the <i>Nabataean Agriculture</i>, Adam is the father of the Chaldeans, but not of the Assyrians (<i>ibid.</i>, p. 44, note 81, and Gutschmidt, in the place cited, p. 33); if he be called <i>father of humanity</i>, it is, says M. Chwolson (p. 174), because by his doctrines and by his writings he had become the benefactor of humanity.",
    "fn.3171": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">معرض</span>, cf. vol. II, p. 127, note 4.",
    "fn.3172": "The author means that when recounting these fables, one does not at all appear to be telling something extraordinary, and one seems to be simply exposing what is remarkable in the nature of plants.",
    "fn.3173": "That is, by employing methods drawn from the study of natural sciences.",
    "fn.2186": "All mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">יקרא</span>  and <span dir=\"rtl\">יםכנוא</span> in the subjunctive mood; the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">أن</span> is to be understood. See Silvestre de Sacy, <i>grammaire arabe</i>, (2<sup>e</sup> edition), vol. II, n° 64.",
    "fn.3174": "The Biblical word <span dir=\"rtl\">אֲשֵרָה</span> has been at times translated as <i>sacred grove</i>, and at other times considered as synonymous with <i>Astarte;</i> the author generally applies it to plantations made in honour of divinities. Cf. Spencer, On the Laws of Hebrew Rites, bk. II, chap. 16 (Cambridge ed., p. 396 et seq.).—The literal translation of this passage is as follows: <i>one of the fables of this book</i>, <i>is that the shrub of the althaea, which is one of the Asheroth that they made, as I have made known unto thee, that this shrub was, it is said, etc.</i> The word <span dir=\"rtl\">خطمي</span>  or <span dir=\"rtl\">خطمية</span>  is the name of a malvaceous plant, the <i>althaea</i> or <i>marshmallow.</i> In the Mischna (<i>Kilaïm</i>, chap. I, § 8), it is, according to some commentators, designated by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חלמית</span>.",
    "fn.3176": "M. Chwolson incidentally mentions this fable that he found in the Leyden manuscript, no. 303 <i>a</i>, p. 102 and following. See <i>Ueber Tammuz, l. c.</i>, p. 165, note 3.",
    "fn.3177": "See the book of Daniel, chap. <small>II</small>, <i>v.</i> 12, 14, 18, 24, 48; chap. <small>IV</small>, <i>v.</i> 3; chap. <small>V</small>, <i>v.</i> 7.",
    "fn.3178": "According to Moses of Narbonne, the author does here allude to the numerous superstitions which reigned in his time, such as the belief in the efficacy of amulets and imaginary holy names, in the existence of mischievous genii, et cetera. Cf. Vol. I, chap. <small>LXI</small>, p. 271, and chap. <small>LXII</small>, pp. 278-79. — The sense of our sentence is this: if the belief in the existence of God were not now so generally diffused,— which hinders one from falling into polytheism and idolatry, one would perhaps be no more enlightened than the ancient pagans, judging by the numerous superstitions which still prevail amongst us.",
    "fn.3179": "It is said that the king had his bones ground in a mill and that he scattered the powder in the wind. See the <i>Kitâb al-Fihrist</i>, ap. Chwolson, <i>Die Ssabier</i>, tome II, p. 27.",
    "fn.3180": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">פוקף</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">פוקע</span>, <i>tomba;</i> similarly Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi <span dir=\"rtl\">ונפל</span>.",
    "fn.3186": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">חכוא</span>, one of our mss. has <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמוא</span>, and similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">כמו שגזרו</span>, <i>as they judge</i>.",
    "fn.3187": "The author is addressing his disciple Joseph ben-Iehouda here, to whom he dedicated this work. Cf. vol. I, at the end of chap. <small>LXVIII</small> (p. 312, note 3); vol. II, at the beginning of chap. <small>XXIV</small>.",
    "fn.3175": "The word <i>Chaldean</i> is here employed in the sense of <i>magician.</i> In some MSS., the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ללכלדאניין</span> is replaced by <span dir=\"rtl\">ללרוחאניין</span>, a word which designats men inspired by the spirits presiding over the stars, the elements, the plants, et cetera. The version of Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">לכלדיים</span>, a word which, in the editions, has been corrupted into <span dir=\"rtl\">לכל הדיינים</span>; the version of Al-’Harîzi has <span dir=\"rtl\">אל הרוחניים</span>.",
    "fn.3181": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must add after <span dir=\"rtl\">ויספדו עליו</span> the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הנשים</span> which is missing in the editions, but is found in the mss. At the end of the sentence, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">בני אדם</span> which are also in the mss. must be erased.",
    "fn.3182": "The legend of Tammouz, which the author here reports in abridgement, is drawn from the second part of the <i>Nabatæan Agriculture</i> (Arabic manuscript of the Imperial Library, no. 913, fol. 8 and 9), where one finds extensive details concerning the death and mourning of Tammouz, as well as of Yanbouschad, who met with a similar end. All this passage has been recently published in Arabic, with a German translation, by M. Chwolson, in his Memoir on Tammouz (<i>Ueber Tammouz, l. c.</i>, p. 129 et seq.). One reads therein, among other things, that all the legends relative to Tammouz were gathered in a particular collection, and that the Babylonians recited them in the temples with tears and lamentations. It is moreover stated therein that Tammouz bestowed his name upon one of the Babylonian months (July) and that all the other months likewise derived their names from certain sages of high antiquity (cf. Makrizi, ap. Chwolson, <i>Die Ssabier</i>, vol. II, p. 606). The prophet Ezekiel (VIII, 14) alludes to the mourning of Tammouz, celebrated by women. It appears to result from this passage that Tammouz is the name of a god, and it is perchance not without reason that S. Jerome has already identified him with <i>Adonis</i>, lamented by women on the anniversary of his cruel death. The Syrian lexicographer Bar-Bahloul, under the word <i>Tammouz</i>, recounts the legend of Adonis, whom he consequently identifies with the god Tammouz, who gave his name to one of the months of the Syrians. See Castell's Syriac Dictionary, published by J. D. Michaelis, p. 964. The identity of Tammouz and Adonis has been generally admitted by modern scholars, although the legend of Adonis differs in a most notable manner from that which the <i>Nabatæan Agriculture</i> relats concerning Tammouz. It is especially by relying upon the authority of this latter work that M. Chwolson, in his Memoir on Tammouz, felt bound to contest the identity of the former with the Adonis of the Phoenicians and the Greeks; but one has already seen that this authority is of little weight. One has not here to enter into details on these subjects, and one is content to refer to the critical observations of M. Alfred de Gutschmidt, <i>l. c.</i>, p. 52-53.",
    "fn.3188": "Maimonides, like other Arab authors, does sometimes make of the <i>Kasdim</i> and the <i>Chaldeans</i> two distinct peoples, albeit these two names designate one and the same people. Cf. hereinafter, at the commencement of chap. <small>XXXVII</small>, and Dimeschki, ap. Chwolson, <i>Die Ssabier</i>, vol. II, p. 414. — In Ibn-Tibbon’s version, there is here a transposition; it reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">שגעונות הכשדיים והכלדיים והבלי הצאבה</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">הבלי</span> is wanting in the editions.",
    "fn.3189": "The orthography of this word varies greatly in the manuscripts, and its etymology is uncertain. The work exists in the Bodleian Library, and it is stated, after the title, that Aristotle composed this work for Alexander, when the latter desired to depart from Greece to proceed unto Persia. See Uri’s <i>Catalogue</i> (Arabic manuscript), p. 126, no. 515. Aboul-Kâsim Moslima al-Madjriti, an Arab-Spanish author of the X<sup>th</sup> century, gives in his work entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">غاية الحكيم</span>, <i>the final aim of the scholar</i> (Casiri, vol. I, p. 378), several extracts from the book <i>Istimakhis.</i> M. Steinschneider, in his Notice on a Hebrew version of Al-Madjriti’s treatise, informs one that the <i>Istimakhis</i> is a book of magic, and he supposs that this word is corrupted from the Greek <i>στοιχειωματιϰος, astrologer, who casts horoscopes.</i> See <i>Pseudepigraphische Literatur</i>, p. 37, in the Collection entitled <i>Wissenschaftliche Blätter aus der Veitel-Heine-Ephraim’schen Anstalt</i>, Berlin, 1862, large in-8°.",
    "fn.3192": "One believes, with Mr. Chwolson (<i>Die Ssabier</i>, vol. I, p. 715), that the author intends to speak of the book on genethlialogy attributed to the Babylonian Tenkelouscha, which bears the title <span dir=\"rtl\">في صور درج الفلك</span>. In his memoir on ancient Babylonian literature (<i>Ueber die Ueberreste etc.</i>, p. 150 et seq.), Mr. Chwolson has furnished an analysis of Tenkelouscha’s book, which he places in the I<sup>st</sup> century of the Christian era. The Arabs cite, beside Tenkelouscha, another astrologer named Tinkérous, author of a Book of Genethlialogy according to the Degrees of the Celestial Sphere (see Flügel, in the <i>Zeitschrift der D. M. G.</i>, vol. XIII, p. 628); the two names, it is perhaps, indicate but one and the same person. Already Saumaise (<i>de Annis climactericis et antiqua astrologia</i>, preface, 3<sup>rd</sup> folio) identifies Tenkelouscha with Teucer or Teukros the Babylonian (Τ<i>εῦϰρος βαϐυλώνιος</i>), who figures as an astrologer amongst the Greeks of the late epoch and who lived before the III<sup>rd</sup> century of the Christian era. See Ewald, in the <i>Göttinger gelehrte Anzeigen</i>, year 1859, p. 1141, and the details furnished concerning Tenkelouscha and Teukros the Babylonian by Mr. Gutschmidt, in his memoir on Nabataean Agriculture (<i>Zeitschrift der D. M. G.</i>, vol. XV, p. 82 et seq., and p. 104 et seq.). Cf. also Renan, <i>Mémoires de l’Académie des Inscriptions et Belles-Lettres</i>, vol. XXIV, I<sup>st</sup> part, p. 186 et seq.",
    "fn.3199": "See the <i>Siphri</i>, at the passage indicated in the book of Numbers, and cf. Babylonian Talmud, <i>Horayôth</i>, fol. 8 <i>a, Kiddouschin</i>, fol. 40 <i>a; </i>Maimonides, treatise on the <i>Idolâtrie</i>, chap. II, § 4.",
    "fn.3200": "Literally: <i>only on condition that ye worship the sun and the planets</i>, <i>and that if ye provoked them etc.</i> The author introduces, at the end of the sentence, the direct discourse of the speakers. This species of <i>anacoluthon</i> is not uncommon in Arabic. Cf. vol. I, p. 283, note 4.— Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi have rendered the 3rd person, <span dir=\"rtl\">כשיעבדו</span>.",
    "fn.3201": "All our Arabic manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמשתרי</span>, <i>Jupiter</i>, and similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">כוכב צדק</span>, whilst Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">מאדים</span>, <i>Mars</i>. Perhaps it is with intention that Ibn-Tibbon substituted the planet <i>Mars</i>, to which astrologers attribute all ill influence, whereas <i>Jupiter</i> ever prognosticates happiness and is termed the <i>great fortune</i>. Cf. Reinaud, <i>Monuments arabes</i>, <i>persans et turcs</i>, vol. II, p. 371 and following.",
    "fn.3202": "All our mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">והן</span> in the masculine; this pronoun is agreed with the word <span dir=\"rtl\">רצא</span> that follows, and it must be considered as neuter: <i>and that is their pleasure.</i>",
    "fn.3214": "The words which the Law <i>enjoins</i> to be pronounced are, for instance, those prescribed for the offering of the first-fruits and of the tithe (Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXVI</small>, <i>v.</i> 5-10 and 13-15); on the other hand, the Law <i>forbids</i>, for example, the vain pronunciation of the Lord's name (Exodus, ch. <small>XX</small>, <i>v.</i> 7), the pronunciation of the names of false gods (<i>ibid.</i>, chap. <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 13), the uttering of a false oath (Leviticus, chap. <small>XIX</small>, <i>v.</i> 12), the slandering of others (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 16), etc. — The author here observes that <i>words</i>, while indeed forming a fourth class of commandments (vide the <i>Sépher Miçwôth</i>, Introduction, 9<sup>th</sup> principle), do not here require particular substantiation; for, in respect of their underlying reasons, the commandments pertaining to <i>words</i> belong to one of the three classes herein enumerated.",
    "fn.3215": "Literally: <i>in the gradual development of the conditions of each individual as a whole, one after the other</i>. The author, as will be seen later, wants to speak here of the successive developments of the animal body and the way it was provided for its nourishment for each phase of its development.",
    "fn.3216": "Literally: <i>having escaped from the extremity of the muscle</i>, that is to say, having extended beyond the extremity of the muscle. The reading which one has adopted is that of all the Arabic MSS.; Ibn-Tibbon’s version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">אחר כן יצא העורק מקצה העצב</span>, <i>the muscle having issued from the extremity of the nerve</i>, which offers no suitable sense. Al-’Harîzi’s version reads, in conformity with the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">אחר כן נברר ונתמלט הגיד העצבי מקצת הגיד הנקראת עצׄלה̈</span>.",
    "fn.3217": "On this entire passage, cf. Galen, <i>De usu partium etc.</i>, lib. 1, cap. 17, lib. II, cap. 3, and <i>passim; De motu musculorum</i>, lib. I, cap. 1 and following; <i>Canon</i> of Ibn-Sînâ, Arabic text, I<sup>re</sup> part, p. 19, lines 8 and following.",
    "fn.3218": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">באלעטׄם</span>, <i>at the bone</i>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">באלעצׄו</span>, <i>at the member</i>; similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">and the cord would become attached to the limb</span>.",
    "fn.3219": "See above, chapter <small>XII</small>, p. 72, note 2.",
    "fn.3220": "In the original, this phrase and the following one form a parenthesis, and the complement of the period begins only with the words: <i>La sagesse divine … ne jugea pas convenable etc.</i>.",
    "fn.3221": "In the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לשמש ולירח</span> are superfluous; these words are not found in the mss. of this version.",
    "fn.3222": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version incorrectly read: <span dir=\"rtl\">והגיע התחבולה בזאת הערמה האלהית</span>; one should read, according to the mss.: <span dir=\"rtl\">והגיע בזאת התחבולה האלהית</span>.",
    "fn.3223": "In Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">באמונתנו</span>, as the mss. have it; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">באומתנו</span> of the editions is simply a printing error.",
    "fn.3224": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s translation, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אזהרות</span> is missing here, which is found in the manuscripts.",
    "fn.3225": "All Ar. mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄואבך</span>, <i>ta réponse</i>, and similarly Al-’Harîzi <span dir=\"rtl\">תשובתך</span>; Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">תשובתי</span>, <i>ma réponse</i>.",
    "fn.3226": "[[fn:1]]All Arabic manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">al-Ghazali</span>; Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">ha-sharé</span>, and Al-’Harîzi <span dir=\"rtl\">ha-khoné</span>. I believe the correct pronunciation is <span dir=\"rtl\">al-Jadá</span>, derived from the root <span dir=\"rtl\">j-b-l</span>, meaning <i>diligens fuit, intendit</i>.",
    "fn.3227": "Allusion to the book of Numbers, chapter <small>XIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 28.",
    "fn.3228": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלחביר</span> , <i>the life of the desert</i>, some MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלבר</span> , <i>fatigue</i> or <i>misery</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">שעתׄ</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(شعث)</span> properly signifies: <i>disorder, dirtiness</i>, <i>lack of care;</i> in the dictionaries, the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">شَعِثَ</span> is rendered by disgregatus fuit, capillum dispersum et pulvere inquinatum habuit. Ibn-Tibbon has paraphrased the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ושעתׄ אלגׄסם</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">ומיעוט הנאות הגוף מרחיצה וכיוצא בהם</span>. Al-’Harîzi’s version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי רוב היגיעה ומיעוט הרחיצה והסיכה יעורר אומץ הלב</span>.",
    "fn.3229": "That is, the laws relating to ceremonial practices, and particularly to sacrifices. Ibn-Tibbon translated the word <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄמלה̈</span>, <i>ensemble</i>, <i>classe</i>, by <span dir=\"rtl\">part</span>, <i>portion</i>.",
    "fn.3230": "That is to say, concerning the rewards promised unto those who observe the law, and the punishments wherewith transgressors are threatened. Al-’Harîzi translates literally, suppressing only <span dir=\"rtl\">תפציל</span>, <i>detail:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">בסבת ההפחדה וההבטחה על כל התורה</span>. Ibn-Tibbon, doubtless finding the Arabic text overly obscure, has paraphrased it in these terms: <span dir=\"rtl\">על סבת היעדים הטובים אשר יעד על שמירת המצות והיעדיס הרעים אשר יעד על העבירות</span>.",
    "fn.3231": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אעמאלהא</span>, <i>ses pratiques</i>, Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">ככל הכתוב בה</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולעשות מעשיה</span>.",
    "fn.3232": "The Arabic text says: <i>to conceive this always;</i> that is to say, to comprehend that such has truly been the divine intention. Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">לקבלה ולעשותה תמיד</span>, <i>to conceive and to practice it always</i> (where the pronoun <i>it</i> refers to the <i>Law</i>), and likewise Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">לקבל אותה ולעשותה תמיד</span>; but, were such the meaning, the author would have said: <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי קבולהא ואלעמל בהא</span>, and not: <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי קבול דׄלך ואלעמל בה</span>.",
    "fn.3245": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי הרעהם ללקראבין</span> have been thus paraphrased by Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">על רוב השתדלותם והתחזקם להביא הקרבנות</span>.—The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">הרע</span> signifies <i>to walk rapidly</i>, <i>to hasten</i>. The Leyden manuscript, no. 18, bears in the margin the following gloss: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלהרע אלגׄרי אלי אלשי ואלמסארעה̈ אליה</span>. «<span dir=\"rtl\">הסרע</span> signifies to run towards a thing and to proceed to it in haste.»",
    "fn.3246": "The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">לדׄאתה</span> refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">באלמקצוד</span>; Ibn-Tibbon, who read <span dir=\"rtl\">לעצ</span> with the plural suffix, appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">לדׄאתהא</span>. Al-’Harîzi attributes the suffix to <i>God;</i> he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי אינם כונה חזקה לעצמו ית׳</span>.",
    "fn.3261": "The ancient rabbis apply this passage from the <i>Song of Solomon</i> to the obedience that the community of Israel promised to its divine husband at the revelation on Mount Sinai. See the <i>Midrash on the Song of Solomon</i> at this passage.",
    "fn.3262": "See below, chapter <small>XLIX</small>.",
    "fn.3263": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">וקאל וקדשתם וגו׳</span> form part of the antecedent of the sentence, of which the words <span dir=\"rtl\">קאל אל תגשו וגו׳</span> constitute the complement. Ibn-Tibbon’s version, which has <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר וקדשתם</span> and subsequently <span dir=\"rtl\">ואמר אל תגשו</span>, is inexact, and this inexactitude also exists in some Arabic manuscripts, which have the first time <span dir=\"rtl\">קאל</span>, and the second time <span dir=\"rtl\">וקאל</span>. The two Leyden manuscripts have twice <span dir=\"rtl\">וקאל</span>; according to this reading, the complement would commence only at the words <span dir=\"rtl\">פקד צרח</span>.",
    "fn.3272": "These last words are taken from Ecclesiastes, chap. <small>XII</small>, <i>v.</i> 11. In several Arab mss., as well as in the Hebrew versions, one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכולם נתנו מרועה אחד</span>, in accordance with a passage from the Babylonian Talmud, tractate ’<i>Haghighâ</i>, fol. 3 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.3273": "That is to say: it would have been impossible that the revealed Law and the laws of nature had as their purpose the good of each individual in particular; for all is calculated for the good of the human species in general.",
    "fn.3274": "See above, chapter <small>XV</small>.",
    "fn.3275": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מקידה̈</span>, the ms. n° 63 of the supplementary Hebrew text has <span dir=\"rtl\">מפידה̈</span>; this latter reading has been reproduced by Al-’Harîzi, who has <span dir=\"rtl\">מועילות</span>. On the meaning of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">קיד</span>, see above, p. 35, note 4.",
    "fn.3276": "That is to say: the legal regime would no longer possess a fixed and absolute principle, but would be something relative which would vary according to individuals and circumstances. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">נתת דבריך לשיעורין</span> are a Talmudic locution indicating that legal provisions, interpreted in a certain manner, would lack a general principle, and would become something relative, which the Talmud declares inadmissible. See, for example, Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Schabbâth</i>, fol. 35 <i>b; ’Hullin</i>, fol. 9 <i>a</i>, and cf. further on, chap. <small>XLIX</small>, what the author states concerning circumcision, fixed on the eighth day after birth.",
    "fn.3288": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon reads <span dir=\"rtl\">בספר עבודה</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">בקצת ספר עבודה</span>.",
    "fn.3289": "See above, chap. XXXII[0]XXXII[1], p. 251.",
    "fn.3290": "Literally: <i>the aim of all this is to destroy the avidity and the propensity one experiences for seeking out that which is sweetest, and for adopting as the ultimate goal the passion for eating and drinking</i>. Concerning the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הטוב</span>, \"propensity,\" vide above, p. 261, note 1. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אתכׄאדׄ</span>, \"to adopt,\" some manuscripts incorrectly have: <span dir=\"rtl\">אכתׄאר</span>, \"to multiply,\" which has given rise to Al-’Harîzi’s translation: <span dir=\"rtl\">והרבות תאות האכילה</span>, in which the words of the Arabic text <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלשראב גאיה̈</span> are suppressed.",
    "fn.3307": "That is, for sins committed through error. On these different types of sacrifices, see my <i>Reflections on the cult of the ancient Hebrews</i> (Bible of M. Cahen, vol. IV), p. 34 and following; <i>Palestine</i>, p. 160 and following.",
    "fn.3308": "That is, everything that has been enumerated in this chapter.",
    "fn.3309": "See above, chapter <small>XXIX</small>, p. 228, note 4.",
    "fn.3310": "See above, p. 239, note 2.",
    "fn.3311": "More literally: <i>but logic does not judge and reason cannot believe that these practices.… produce anything</i>.",
    "fn.3312": "In the editions of the Ibn-Tibbon version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">או הדלוג על רגל אחת</span> are missing here, which are found in the mss. of this version.",
    "fn.3313": "The Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">ותד</span>, <i>nail</i>, is sometimes used to designate the cardinal points. See Saumaise, <i>De annis climactericis</i> (Leyde, 1648), p. 440, and the addition to this page at the end of the volume. The rabbis use the word <span dir=\"rtl\">יתד</span> in the same sense.",
    "fn.3328": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>Schabbath</i>, fol. 67 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3329": "For, as Raschi's gloss states, its excessive sap is the reason why its fruits fall. See the <i>Additions and Rectifications</i>.",
    "fn.3330": "Literally: <i>that the reasoning would not require</i>. In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הטבעי</span> has been added, which is not found in the manuscripts of this version.",
    "fn.3331": "See Mischnâ, V<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>’Hullin</i>, chap. <small>IV</small>, § 7. The commentators say that magicians had the custom of burying the fetus in a crossroads, believing thereby to preserve the animal from a new abortion.",
    "fn.3332": "In the mss. of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, one reads here, in accordance with the Arabic text, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ועל זה תעשה היקש</span>, which are missing in the editions. Al-’Harîzi has <span dir=\"rtl\">ועל זה תהיה סובר</span>.",
    "fn.3333": "Vide Mishnah, treatise <i>Schabbath</i>, chap. <small>VI</small>, § 10, and Babylonian Talmud, same treatise, fol. 77 <i>a</i>. It was believed, by superstition, that the nail which had been used for a hanging might serve as a remedy against tertian fever or against a swelling. The tooth of a live fox was considered a remedy to rouse a man from a profound slumber; the tooth of a dead fox, conversely, was reputed to be a remedy against insomnia. Wherefore the Talmudists permitted one to go forth with these objects on the Sabbath day.",
    "fn.3341": "See the <i>Sépher miçwoth, l. c.</i>, n° 25, and <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book I, tractate <i>Aboda Zarâ</i>, chapter <small>VII</small>, § 2. Cf. Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Maccôth</i>, fol. 22 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3342": "Allusion to circumcision often called <i>Alliance</i> (cf. Talmud, tractate <i>Nedarîm</i>, fol. 32 <i>a</i>), to the <i>blood</i> of the Alliance (Exodus, <small>XXIV</small>, 8), to the <i>words</i> of the Alliance (Deuteronomy, <small>XXVIII</small>, 69, and <small>XXIX</small>, 8).",
    "fn.3343": "Allusion to the passage where the sky and the earth are invoked as witnesses to the threats pronounced against Israel. See Deuteronomy, <small>IV</small>, 26, and <small>XXXI</small>, 28.",
    "fn.3344": "Voy., for example, Deutéronome, <small>XXIX</small>, 18 to 20, and the passages already cited by the author (above, p. 281).",
    "fn.3345": "Word for word: <i>and to move away from it, to another side</i>. All our mss. read: <span dir=\"rtl\">to the land of the desert</span>; the version of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">to the very end</span>, would correspond, in Arabic, to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">to the land, the desert, the wilderness</span>.",
    "fn.3359": "Vide Leviticus, chap. <small>XIX</small>, <i>v.</i> 24. The term <span dir=\"rtl\">נטע רבעי</span> designates, amongst the Talmudists, the fruit of a <i>plantation in the fourth year;</i> vide Mischnah, I<sup>st</sup> part, treatise <i>Ma’aser Schêni</i>, chap. <small>V</small>, § 4 et seq. In the verse which one has just indicated, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">קדש הלולים לי״י</span>, <i>an object sacred for praise unto the Eternal</i>, are explained by the rabbis in this sense, that the fruit of the fourth year must be consumed, like the second tithe, in the place where the central sanctuary is situated.",
    "fn.3360": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ערלה</span>, <i>foreskin</i>, see p. 204, note 4.",
    "fn.3361": "Literally: <i>who follows the course of talismans</i>, that is to say, who belongs to the genre of talismans; for this procedure is related to the constellations and invokes their influence. Cf. vol. I, p. 281, note 1.",
    "fn.3362": "The author alludes to a passage in the Babylonian Talmud (treatise <i>Berakoth</i>, fol. 10 <i>b</i>, treatise <i>Pesa’hîm</i>, fol. 56 <i>a</i>), where mention is made of several measures taken by King Hezekiah and approved by the Sages, notably the suppression of a certain Book of Medicines. Elsewhere, Maimonides speaks more explicitly of this book, which, according to him, contained talismanic remedies. See the Commentary on the Mishnah, II<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Pesa’hîm</i>, chap. <small>IV</small>, § 9, where the author also contends against the opinion of some commentators, who assert that this book was suppressed because too great a confidence was reposed in it, and the succour of Providence was no longer invoked. See Rashi’s gloss on the two Talmudic passages, and Kimchi’s commentary on the II<sup>e</sup> book of Kings, chap. <small>XX</small>, <i>v.</i> 4, where the book in question is attributed to Solomon. Cf. Josephus, <i>Antiquities</i>, book VIII, chap. 2, § 5.",
    "fn.3363": "This peculiar method is likewise indicated by Ibn-al-Awam, drawing upon Ibn-Wa’hschiyya. See the <i>Book of Agriculture by Ibn-al-Awam</i>, translated from the Arabic by M. Clément-Mullet, vol. I (Paris, 1864), p. 464. In a note, p. 467, the learned translator expresses himself in these terms: “The quotation from Ibn-al-Awam is not complete, yet it is more extensive than that of Maimonides. One has found it, one believes, complete in the ms. of the Imperial Library, no. 884, fol. 82 r°, where it is given under the rubric of Ibn-Wahschiah, that is to say, according to the Nabatæan Agriculture. One deems it of interest to one’s readers to reproduce this passage as given in the ms.; but on account of the details it contains, it has appeared meet to render it in Latin. “Ibn-Wahschiah said: Whosoever wishes to graft a tree into another, let him approach a beautiful virgin of exquisite comeliness. Let him lead her by the hand, and command her to stand next to the tree which he intends to graft. And when the grafting branch is cut off and brought to the tree to be grafted, with the virgin always standing nearby, let him open the fissure. Then, stripping the virgin of her toga, and casting off his own garment, at the same moment he shall lie with the woman standing upright; so that the grafting of the branch and the venereal act coincide, and let him also take care that the emission of semen occurs at the very end of the grafting, and let him not leave the virgin until the grafting is completed. If she should become pregnant, they say the tree will obtain the sweet smell and excellent taste of the foreign branch in their entirety; if not, then but little of these. In the same manner, one should act who has attempted to graft a pear onto a citron, so as to obtain the excellent colour and taste of the citron. The virgin must be of willing spirit, assenting, and compelled by no force.”",
    "fn.3364": "That is, the benefits one hoped to obtain from grafting.—In the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חתועלות</span> is obviously a mistake; several mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">התוחלת</span>, which corresponds better to the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלטמע</span>, <i>desire, concupiscence</i>. The version of Al-’Harîzi reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">עם התאוה בתועלות ההם</span>.",
    "fn.3365": "According to rabbinic tradition, as we will see, the defense of grafting is included in these words of Leviticus, chapter <small>XIX</small>, <i>v.</i> 19: <i>you shall not sow your field with diverse kinds</i>. See Maimonides, <i>Sépher Miçwôth</i>, negative precepts, n<sup>os</sup> 215 and 216.",
    "fn.3366": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">מסבות</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מסברת</span>, which is merely a printing error.",
    "fn.3367": "That is, due to the abominable practices which, among pagans, took place for grafting.",
    "fn.3368": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">When you shall have perused this explanation of the commandment</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">When you shall have pondered what was said in the Talmud about the nature of the commandment</span>. On the word <span dir=\"rtl\">pikah</span>, see t. I, p. 7, note 1.",
    "fn.3369": "By the term <span dir=\"rtl\">לוקין</span> (<i>vapulant</i>), the Talmudists designate those who are liable to the punishment of lashes (<span dir=\"rtl\">מלקות</span>), for having transgressed the legal defenses of certain categories exposed in the Mischnâ, treatise <i>Maccoth</i>, chap. <small>III</small>.",
    "fn.3370": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Kiddouschin</i>, fol. 39 <i>a;</i> and Maimonides, <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book VII, tractate <i>Kilaïm</i>, chapter <small>I</small>, §§ 1 and 5.",
    "fn.3371": "See Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXII</small>, <i>v.</i> 9; Mishnah, I<sup>re</sup> part, treatise <i>Kilaïm</i>, chap. <small>VIII</small>, § 1; Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Kiddoushin, l. c.</i>; Maimonides, <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book V, treatise <i>Maakhaloth asourôth</i>, chap. 10, § 6. In the passage of Deuteronomy, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">פן תקדש</span>, <i>lest</i> (<i>the whole</i>) <i>be prohibited</i>, are explained by some doctors, by playing upon the words, as <span dir=\"rtl\">פן תוקד אש</span>, that is to say, “lest the whole be condemned to be consumed by fire, like all that which it is forbidden to employ for any purpose whatsoever.” See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Kiddoushin</i>, fol. 56 <i>b;</i> Maimonides, <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book VII, treatise <i>Kilaim</i>, chap. <small>V</small>, § 7. Cf. Mishnah, treatise <i>Temourâ</i>, chap. <small>VII</small>, § 5.",
    "fn.3372": "See above, p. 283, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 5.",
    "fn.3373": "By the word <span dir=\"rtl\">درأى</span> (pl. of <span dir=\"rtl\">دُرِّيٌّ</span>), one generally designates <i>scintillating stars</i>, which are distinguished from others by their great brilliance. Here, it is evidently a matter of the five planets, apart from the sun and moon. Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">לחמשת הככבים המשרתים מלבד שני המאורים</span>. The same word is also employed at the commencement of the book <i>Khozari</i>, in the sense of <i>planet:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">קוי אלאפלאך ואלדרארי ואלברוגׄ</span>, <i>the forces of the spheres, of the planets, and of the signs of the zodiac;</i> the translation by R. Judah ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">כחות הגלגלים והמזלות והחיילים</span>, is not of rigorous exactitude.",
    "fn.3374": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ללכׄמסה̈</span>, one of our manuscripts (ancient Hebrew fund, number 230) has <span dir=\"rtl\">ללסבעה̈</span>; Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">to the seven servants who are included in both of them</span>.",
    "fn.3375": "According to this doctor, for there to be <span dir=\"rtl\">כלאי הכרם</span>, or <i>mixture of vine</i>, one must have cast, with the vine-seed, two other species of seeds, making in total three species. See Babylonian Talmud, treatises <i>Berakhoth</i>, fol. 22 <i>a; Kiddoushin</i>, fol. 39 <i>a; ’Hullîn</i>, fol. 82 <i>b</i> and 136 <i>b</i>. Maimonides himself admits this opinion, as a doctrinal decision, in his <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book VII, treatise <i>Kilaïm</i>, chap. <small>V</small>, § 2; but he finds it astonishing, inasmuch as it is not expressly indicated in any text of the Pentateuch. He believes, therefore, as he is about to state, that Rabbi Yoschia had discovered in some pagan book the custom of mingling vine-seed with that of <i>two</i> other species of plants.",
    "fn.3376": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">כוחין</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">פי חד</span>, which is the same thing. Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">בענין</span> and Al-’Harîzi <span dir=\"rtl\">בדרד</span>. The exact translation would be <span dir=\"rtl\">בגבול</span>, <i>within such a limit</i> or <i>extent.</i>",
    "fn.3377": "That is, in the various classes to which they appear to belong, if one only considers the material acts they prescribe.",
    "fn.3378": "The editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have here <span dir=\"rtl\">Bepartem</span>, which is a simple printing error; the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">Sepharneum</span>.",
    "fn.3379": "See above, p. 269, note 2.",
    "fn.3380": "Ibn-Tibbon's version adds the following words here: <span dir=\"rtl\">like a widow and an orphan and the like</span>, <i>like a widow</i>, <i>like an orphan</i>, <i>etc</i>., which are found neither in the original nor in Al-’Harîzi's version.",
    "fn.3381": "Ibn-Tibbon's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא בזריעה</span>, <i>ni of sowing;</i> Al-’Harîzi's version has, in accordance with the Arabic text, <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא בקצירה</span>.",
    "fn.3382": "See Deuteronomy, chap. XIV, v. 22-29; Mishnah, I part, tractate Ma’aser schéni, chap. I to III. Cf. Palestine, p. 172 b, and ibid., note 2.",
    "fn.3383": "Literally: <i>to have it obtained</i>, that is to say, for the poor. The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">to give</span>, <i>to give;</i> the mss. and Schem-Tob's commentary have <span dir=\"rtl\">to set aside</span>.",
    "fn.3384": "I consider the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאגׄתמאע</span> to be the subject of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יוגׄב</span>, so that one must pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">الاجتماعُ</span> . Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויביא להתקבץ במקום אחד שתתחזק וגו׳</span>, which does not offer a suitable meaning; one should translate: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויביא התתקבץ במקום א׳ להתחזק</span>.",
    "fn.3385": "See above, p. 291, note 1.",
    "fn.3386": "Literally: <i>as to the fruit of the fourth year</i>, <i>albeit it bears</i><i>an odour of idolatry</i>, <i>because it pertains to the first products</i>, <i>etc.</i> It has been observed above that the precept concerning the consumption of the fruits of the fourth year in the central sanctuary, according to Maimonides, is connected with an idolatrous custom, relating to the use of the first products of trees called <span dir=\"rtl\">ערלה</span>, <i>foreskin.</i>",
    "fn.3387": "Regarding the offering from the produce of the land termed <span dir=\"rtl\">תרומה</span>, which is designated for the priests, vide Deuteronomy, <small>XVIII</small>, 4. For that of the dough, styled <span dir=\"rtl\">חלה</span>, <i>cake</i>, Numbers, <small>XV</small>, 20. As to the first-fruits, or that which ripens first each year, <span dir=\"rtl\">בכורים</span>, Exodus, <small>XXIII</small>, 19; <small>XXXIV</small>, 26; Deuteronomy, <small>XXVI</small>, 2. Respecting the first of the fleece, <span dir=\"rtl\">ראשית הגז</span>, Deuteronomy, <small>XVIII</small>, 4.",
    "fn.3403": "Word for word: <i>and with even greater reason for that which is outside of it;</i> that is to say, and with even greater reason shall the slave be freed for another, more grievous injury. The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">סואחא</span>, or the pronoun <i>it</i>, refers grammatically to the <i>tooth.</i> The version of Ibn-Tibbon (MS.) reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכל שכן על זולתה</span>, with the addition of the explanatory words <span dir=\"rtl\">מן האברים</span>.",
    "fn.3404": "Vide <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book XI, treatise <i>Rocéa’h</i> (on Homicide), chap. 11, §14, in which Maimonides expounds the passage from Exodus, chap. <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 20 and 21, in the following manner: Should the master have employed a <i>rod</i>, that is, the ordinary instrument of correction, he shall be declared guiltless in the event that the slave survived for one or two days; but, if the slave perishes forthwith, the master shall suffer the penalty of death. If the master, on the contrary, has availed himself of a deadly weapon, the capital penalty shall ever be inflicted upon him, even though the slave should have died but long thereafter.",
    "fn.3420": "On the talmudic terms <span dir=\"rtl\">תם</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">מועד</span>, cf. <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, <i>l. c.</i>, chap. <small>I</small>, § 4, and cf. Exodus, chap. <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 29: <span dir=\"rtl\">והועד בבעליו</span>.",
    "fn.3421": "See Exodus, chapter <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 35-36, and the Commentaries; cf. Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Baba Kamma</i>, fol. 26 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3183": "One shall perchance remain for ever ignorant as to what, on these diverse points, were the traditions reported by the <i>Nabatæan Agriculture.</i> M. Chwolson informs one that in the sole complete manuscript of the Leyden Library, no. 303, there are wanting at the end of the 1<sup>st</sup> volume 40 leaves, which were misplaced at Leyden and which contained precisely the traditions in question; in the book of the Babylonian <i>Tenkeluscha</i>, says the same author, there is incidental mention of the <i>tree of life</i>, guarded by two angels. See <i>Ueber die Ueberreste der Altbabylonischen Literatur</i>, p. 34, note 58, and p. 181. — Be this as it may, it is evident, as Maimonides himself will presently state, that the pretended translator of the <i>Nabatæan Agriculture</i> has here reproduced the biblical traditions, which he has amplified after his own fashion, probably with the aid of Muslim traditions. Cf. d’Herbelot, <i>Bibliothèque orientale</i>, under the article <i>Adam.</i>",
    "fn.3184": "The author, in employing the word <span dir=\"rtl\">الميل</span>, the <i>communions</i> or <i>religious sects</i>, appears to designate those nations which have adopted the holy Scripture of the Jews, and thereby to insinuate that the <i>Nabatæan Agriculture</i> does not date beyond the times of Christianity, and perhaps not even beyond those of Islamism. The two Hebrew translators render rather inexactly the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">מלל</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">אומות</span>, which corresponds more precisely to <span dir=\"rtl\">أُمَمُ</span>.",
    "fn.3185": "All our mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">עמלוא</span>, without the <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span> copulative, and this verb must be considered as a complement to the words <span dir=\"rtl\">למא שהרת אלתורה</span>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon contains <span dir=\"rtl\">ועשו</span> with the <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span> conjunctive, and similarly that of Al-’Harîzi <span dir=\"rtl\">וחברו</span>, which is incorrect.",
    "fn.3190": "Tomtom is, according to the Arabs, an Indian author of whom various works on magic, translated into Arabic, are cited. See Hadji-Khalfa, volume I, p. 194 (no. 251); vol. II, p. 288 (no. 2974); vol. III, p. 54 (no. 4475); D’Herbelot, <i>Bibliothèque orientale</i>, p. 1031 <i>a</i>; Chwolson, <i>Die Ssabier</i>, vol. I, p. 712. The passage from Ibn-Khaldoun’s <i>Prolégomènes</i>, cited by Mr. Chwolson, is found in the III<sup>rd</sup> part, p. 125, of Mr. Quatremère’s edition. Further on, Maimonides cites Tomtom’s book three more times: in chapter <small>XXXVII</small>, concerning the women’s garments donned by men when standing before the planet Venus, and the men’s armour worn by women when standing before the planet Mars; in chapter <small>XLI</small>, concerning the blood drunk by pagans in certain idolatrous rites; and in chapter <small>XLVI</small>, concerning the lions, bears, and other ferocious beasts which pagans offered in sacrifice to their gods.",
    "fn.3191": "One has nowhere found the slightest information regarding this book, and one is even uncertain of the pronunciation of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">المسرب</span>; nevertheless, the reading <span dir=\"rtl\">אלסרב</span> is guaranteed by seven Arabic MSS. and by an equal number of MSS. of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, which have <span dir=\"rtl\">ספר הסרב</span>. In a citation from Saad ben-Mansour (ap. Steinschneider, <i>l. c</i>., p. 83, note 4), one likewise reads <span dir=\"rtl\">אלסרב</span>. A single MS. of Ibn-Tibbon’s version (Oratory collection, no. 46) bears <span dir=\"rtl\">השרב</span>, as do the editions of this version; this variant is of no import whatsoever. In two Arabic MSS. (Suppl. Hebr., no. 63, and Leyden MS., no. 18), one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">לאסרגׄ</span>, a word which ought to be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">الشرج</span> (pl. of <span dir=\"rtl\">السراج</span>), for Al-’Harîzi, who had the same reading, renders it as <span dir=\"rtl\">םפר הנרות</span>, <i>book of lamps.</i> One also finds traces of this reading in two MSS. of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, one of which (Suppl. Hebr., no. 26) bears <span dir=\"rtl\">ספר הסרגי</span>, and the other (Orat., no. 47) has <span dir=\"rtl\">ספר הנר</span>. This latter reading is the only one which corresponds to <span dir=\"rtl\">כתאב אלסראגׄ</span>, the title of a work on alchemy and magic by Ya’hya al-Barmeki, cited by Hadji-Khalfa (vol. III, p. 588, no. 7074), and in which Mr. Chwolson believes he recognises the work here designated by Maimonides (see <i>Die Ssabier</i>, vol. I, pp. 713-14); but this isolated reading cannot prevail against that of the majority of MSS., which one has deemed it proper to adopt. Finally, the reading <span dir=\"rtl\">אלסרס</span>, which is found only in an imperfect MS. in the Leyden Library (no. 221), is evidently corrupt.",
    "fn.3193": "The Arabs speak of three ancient sages named <i>Hermes</i>, the first of whom, called Hermes al-Harâmisa (the Hermes of the Hermeses), is identified with the <i>Enoch</i> of the Bible, whom the Arabs call <i>ldrîs.</i> This is the Hermes <i>Trismegistus</i> of the Greeks, in whose name, during the first centuries of the Christian era, several works of astrology, magic, and alchemy were forged. The pseudo-Hermetic writings were translated into Arabic. The work here indicated by Maimonides is perhaps that which Hadji-Khalfa (vol. V, p. 247, no. 10877) cites under the title of <span dir=\"rtl\">كنز الاسرار</span>, <i>The Treasury of Secrets</i>. Regarding the Arabian fables related to Hermes and the books attributed to him, see D’Herbelot, <i>Bibliothèque orientale</i>, art. <i>Hermes</i>, and Casiri, <i>Biblioth. arab. hisp</i>., vol. I, pp. 372, 374-76.",
    "fn.3194": "One lacks precise information concerning Isaac the Sabian and his works; this author was doubtless from Harrân. The Sabians of Harrân count several authors named Ibrahim, whose given name was <i>Abou-Is’hâk;</i> but amongst their works enumerated by Al-Kifti in the <i>Tarîkh al-’hocamâ</i>, one does not encounter those of which Maimonides here speaks. M. Chwolson supposes that Maimonides intended to speak of certain works by Senân ben-Thabit ben-Korra, dedicated to a certain <i>Abou-Is’hâk</i> Ibrahim ben-Helâl (vide <i>Die Ssabier</i>, vol. II, preface, p. v, note 17); but such an error, on the part of Maimonides, is hardly probable.",
    "fn.3195": "By <i>intelligible</i> forms, one appears to understand here the <i>hypostases</i> of the Neoplatonists, otherwise termed the simple or intelligible substances (Cf. Ibn-Gebirol, <i>Source of Life</i>, III, 15 and passim); and one believes with M. Chwolson (<i>Die Ssabier</i>, vol. II, p. 727) that Maimonides here has in view the Sabians of Harrân, amongst whom Neoplatonic ideas were widespread, and who, as Massoudi tells one (vide <i>ibid.</i>, p. 367), possessed temples consecrated to the intelligible substances, or to the hypostases.",
    "fn.3196": "See above, p. 234, note 4.",
    "fn.3197": "According to the author, by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מצבות</span>, one understands stones that were erected in honour of certain deities, and near which one assembled to render them cultic worship. Vide Maimonides, treatise on <i>Idolatry</i>, chap. <small>VI</small>, § 6; <i>Sépher Miçwôth</i>, negative precepts, no. 11. Cf. Genesis, chap. <small>XXVIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 18. Sanchoniathon speaks of these stones which the Phoenicians termed Bœtylia (<span dir=\"rtl\">בית אל</span>]). Vide Eusebius, Prœparat. evang., l. I, ch. 10.",
    "fn.3198": "All manuscripts possess <span dir=\"rtl\">מזבחותיהם תתצון</span>; but in no verse of the Pentateuch are these two words combined together, and they are found only in the Book of Judges, chap. 2, <i>v.</i> 2. One has written <span dir=\"rtl\">תתצן</span>, according to the verse from Deuteronomy (<small>VII</small>, 5), which the author had in view and which he confounded with a verse from Exodus (<small>XXXIV</small>, 13). Cf. above, p. 229, note 3. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תגדעון</span>, some Arabic manuscripts, as well as the two Hebrew versions, possess <span dir=\"rtl\">תשרפון באש</span>, according to Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XII</small>, <i>v</i>. 3, which is cited thereafter.",
    "fn.3203": "See Varron, <i>De re rustica</i>, II, 5: 'Here is the companion of men in rustic work and the minister of Ceres. The ancients wished to abstain from this hand so much that they would have their head cut off if anyone had killed it.' Columelle, l. <small>VI</small>, præfat.: '... What then is the most laborious companion of man in agriculture, whose reverence was so great among the ancients that it was as capital a crime to kill an ox as a citizen.' See also further on, at the beginning of chap. <small>XLVI</small>.",
    "fn.3204": "Literally: <i>because they unite together strength and good disposition for man in agriculture</i>. In most editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, this passage is much corrupted; the princeps edition bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">רצון בני אדם</span> <span dir=\"ltr\">(lis. אחר)</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">מפני שקבצו הכח וטוב ההמשך ואחר בעבדה האדמה</span>. The manuscripts have: <span dir=\"rtl\">מפני שקבצו הכח והמשכם לרצון בני אדם וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.3205": "Ibn-Tibbon, in order to reproduce the expressions of a verse from Leviticus (chapter <small>XXVI</small>, <i>v.</i> 4), has thus paraphrased the last words: <span dir=\"rtl\">ונתינת הארץ יבולה ועץ השדה פריו לשבע</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">וירבו התבואות ויפרו האילנות</span>.",
    "fn.3206": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלאצנאם</span>, some manuscripts have: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלאגׄסאם</span>; this reading is reproduced in the two Hebrew versions, which have <span dir=\"rtl\">והגופות</span>, <i>and these bodies.</i>",
    "fn.3207": "Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויבאו המקרים הרעים לעתים</span>, which is not very clear. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אחואל</span> refers here to <i>les circonstances extérieures, les relations sociales</i>. Cf. above, chapter <small>XXVII</small> (p. 213): <span dir=\"rtl\">אחואל אלנאס בעצׄהם מע בעץׄ</span>, <i>les relations mutuelles des hommes</i>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויַרבו ההזק בבני אדם</span>.",
    "fn.3208": "Voy. Deutéronome, chap. <small>XXVIII</small>; these last words are borrowed from the <i>v.</i> 69, which concludes the chapter.",
    "fn.3209": "Literally: <i>afin qu'on les adorât</i>. The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version generally read: <span dir=\"rtl\">עד שיעזבום</span>; the mss. and the first edition <i>princeps</i> have correctly <span dir=\"rtl\">עד שיעבדום</span>.",
    "fn.3210": "Cf. above, chapter <small>XXVI</small>.",
    "fn.3211": "Cf. above, chapter <small>XXVII</small>, p. 213-214.",
    "fn.3212": "See above, <i>l. c.</i>, p. 203-204, what the author says about the regulations called <span dir=\"rtl\">חקִּים</span>.",
    "fn.3213": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר</span>, several manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאמר</span>, with the article. Al-’Harîzi took this word in the sense of <i>precept</i>, <i>commandment</i>, and translated <span dir=\"rtl\">וכשתהיה המׂצוה</span>. Ibn-Tibbon appears to have taken <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר</span> for an accusative <span dir=\"rtl\">(אמרא)</span>; he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואם יהיה ענין שלא יודע וגו׳</span>, <i>but if it be a matter for which one knows no motive.</i>",
    "fn.3233": "One has written <span dir=\"rtl\">פאן</span>, albeit this reading is found in but one of our MSS. (suppl. n° 63); the other MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">כאן</span> (i.e. <span dir=\"rtl\">كان</span> ), <i>as if it were there still etc.</i> Ibn-Tibbon’s version, <span dir=\"rtl\">כי זאת גם כן תחבולה וגו׳</span>, favours the reading one has adopted; likewise, that of Al-’Harîzi, which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי גם הם תחבולה וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.3234": "In Ibn-Tibbon's translation, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אתיאן</span> was incorrectly rendered as <span dir=\"rtl\">רצון</span>. Al-’Harîzi translated it more accurately as <span dir=\"rtl\">עשיית המצות אשר חפץ</span>.",
    "fn.3235": "The author means: although any individual being whatsoever may, by a miracle, change its nature.—The editions of the Ibn-Tibbon translation add, after <span dir=\"rtl\">שינוי טבע</span>, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ישנה השם</span>, which are superfluous and do not appear in the manuscripts.",
    "fn.3236": "As it is God who speaks here, it follows that His unchangeable will has formed the human heart in such a way that its nature can never be changed by a miracle, and that the will of man can alone overcome this nature.",
    "fn.3237": "Literally: <i>and because of this there are</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">גׄא</span>) <i>precepts etc.</i>; that is to say: as God grants unto man full liberty and as man may overcome the inclinations of his heart, he alone is responsible for his deeds, and because of this he may be rewarded or punished. — For the Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄא</span>, the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version have <span dir=\"rtl\">באר</span>; one must write <span dir=\"rtl\">באה</span>, as the MSS. have it.",
    "fn.3238": "Literally: <i>with his proofs</i>. In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, one must write <span dir=\"rtl\">במופתיה</span>, as the mss. indeed have; the editions carry incorrectly <span dir=\"rtl\">במופתים</span>.",
    "fn.3239": "Ibn-Tibbon rendered the two words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשרעיה̈ אלתוראתיה̈</span>, adjectives of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלקואעד</span>, with the single word <span dir=\"rtl\">התוריות</span>; similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">לפי עקרי התורה</span>.",
    "fn.3240": "That is, if man were to observe the divine commandments only because the divine will had each time disposed human nature to conform to them, then the mission of the prophets and legislation would be unnecessary, since man would naturally be disposed to do what is prescribed in the laws.",
    "fn.3241": "Most MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄעלה</span>; this verb, possessing a suffix, is necessarily active, which compels one to write <span dir=\"rtl\">ביחא ואחדא</span> in the accusative, and to consider likewise the preceding verb <span dir=\"rtl\">חרם</span> as an active verb whose implied subject is <i>God</i>. Ibn-Tibbon, having probably read <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄעל</span>, without a suffix, renders both verbs in the passive voice: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל נאסר כל זה עלינו והושם בית אחד</span>. In two MSS. one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄעלת</span>, in the feminine passive; but in this sentence there is no feminine subject to which this verb might refer. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל אסור לנו כל זה וצוה לבנות בית אחד</span>.",
    "fn.3242": "Fringes attached to the sides of garments. See Numbers, chap. <small>XV</small>, <i>v.</i> 38.",
    "fn.3243": "Inscriptions on the doorposts (<i>mezouzoth</i>) of houses. See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>VI</small>, <i>verse</i> 9; chapter <small>XI</small>, <i>verse</i> 20.",
    "fn.3244": "Phylacteries to be attached to the arm and the forehead. See Exodus, ch. <small>XIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 9 and 16; Deuteronomy, chap. <small>VI</small>, <i>v.</i> 8; chap. <small>XI</small>, <i>v.</i> 18.",
    "fn.3247": "Literally: you have attached yourselves to what has been done in her favor; that is, to the practices that were only prescribed to achieve this end. The editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon generally have בעבודה with ד; one must read בעבורה.",
    "fn.3248": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">וֲ֮אטיו אדיוה הָהיו</span>, found in the original Arabic and Ibn-Tibbon's version, are inaccurate; the text of Jeremiah reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">וֲ֮אטיו הְוהוה הָהיו הָהיו הָהיו אטיו הָהיו</span>.",
    "fn.3249": "That is, one must not consider as <i>sacrifice</i> the first <i>passover lamb</i> made by the Hebrews before their departure from Egypt.",
    "fn.3250": "See further on, chapter <small>XLVI</small>.",
    "fn.3251": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Schabbath</i>, fol. 87 <i>b</i>; <i>Synhédrin</i>, fol. 56 <i>b.</i> — The Chaldean verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אִפְּקוד</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">איפקוד</span> is an irregular passive form for <span dir=\"rtl\">אתפקד</span>.",
    "fn.3252": "Word for word: <i>faith in true opinions, and that is the novelty of the world.</i>",
    "fn.3253": "See the second <sup>e</sup> part of this work, chapter <small>XXXI</small>.",
    "fn.3254": "Word for word: <i>the most frequent passion of the multitude is their unconstrainedness</i> or <i>their being carried away</i>. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תסיב</span> is the fifth form of <span dir=\"rtl\">סאב</span>, which signifies <i>to walk freely without restraint</i> (speaking of animals), <i>to allow oneself to be unrestrained</i> or <i>to be carried away</i>. Most editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version have here <span dir=\"rtl\">ושלותם</span>, which is merely a misprint for <span dir=\"rtl\">ושלוחם</span>, as correctly rendered by the manuscripts and the <i>editio princeps;</i> cf. further on the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלתחסיב פי אלמאכל</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">והשלוח במאכלים</span>. The primitive verb <span dir=\"rtl\">סיאב</span> is found at the end of chapter <small>XXIII</small> of the first part (fol. 28 <i>a</i>, l. 3), in the sense of <i>to be abandoned</i> or <i>left to one’s own devices</i>, and Ibn-Tibbon translates it by <span dir=\"rtl\">הופקר</span>, in conformity with the translation Maimonides himself had given of it in his Letter.",
    "fn.3255": "On these two species of perfections, see above, ch. <small>XXVII</small>, p. 211.",
    "fn.3256": "Ibn-Tibbon deviates here from his usual literalness, translating: <span dir=\"rtl\">קודם זמנו הטבעי</span>; similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">לפני בוא עתו הטבעי</span>, <i>before his natural time had come</i>. The literal translation would be: <span dir=\"rtl\">קודם שיחייב לו זה ענינו הטבעי</span>.",
    "fn.3257": "The term <span dir=\"rtl\">خطبة</span> has the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">أمرة</span> , <i>res, negotium ejus;</i> one of our manuscripts reads <span dir=\"rtl\">כׄטאה</span>, <i>his sin</i>, and it may be that this was the reading of Al-’Harîzi, who translates <span dir=\"rtl\">בטרם שתגדל רעתו</span>.",
    "fn.3258": "Cf. further on, chapter <small>XLI</small>, where it is stated that the punishment of the rebellious son is preventive; for he would necessarily come to commit murders (<span dir=\"rtl\">פאנה סוף יקתל צׄרורה̈</span>, Arabic text, fol. 90 <i>b</i>, l.7 from below). In the Mishnah (fourth part, treatise <i>Synhedrin</i>, chapter <small>VIII</small>, § 5), one reads likewise: <span dir=\"rtl\">בן סורר ומורה נדון על שם סופו</span>.",
    "fn.3259": "The Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تأتي</span>  (VI<sup>th</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">أتى</span> ) means <i>to be supple</i>, <i>docile</i>, <i>condescending</i> ; it has been paraphrased by Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">and a man should be gentle with his companions</span>. Similarly for <span dir=\"rtl\">גלטׄה̈</span>, <i>grossness</i> , Ibn-Tibbon has put: <span dir=\"rtl\">not to listen to his companions</span>.",
    "fn.3260": "<span dir=\"rtl\">מניב</span> is the participle of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אנאב</span> (fourth form), which signifies: annuit rei. Ibn-Tibbon’s version has <span dir=\"rtl\">ונשמע אליהם לחפצם</span>. In several manuscripts, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מניב</span> is suppressed; the Leyden manuscript, no. 18, has a second time <span dir=\"rtl\">מגׄיב</span>. For <span dir=\"rtl\">מתאתיא</span>, the Leyden manuscript, no. 221, has <span dir=\"rtl\">מתאדב</span>, <i>well-bred</i>. Al-’Harîzi replaces the four terms by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">נשמע ונמשך לכל חפץ</span>.",
    "fn.3264": "That is to say, the sanctity mentioned in the verse of Leviticus is, according to the <i>Siphra</i>, that which is acquired by observing the divine commandments; hence it follows that the law had the purpose of sanctity.",
    "fn.3265": "For the second <span dir=\"rtl\">Almangehah</span>, the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have <span dir=\"rtl\">Hemegunoth</span>; the mss. carry <span dir=\"rtl\">Hemetmeoth</span>.",
    "fn.3266": "We translate this passage according to the sense attributed to it by Maimonides here, but which applies with difficulty to the words and which was certainly far from the prophet's thought; for, according to most commentators, it is here a question of idolaters who purify themselves to enter the gardens consecrated to the divinities and to adore a statue that is in the middle.",
    "fn.3267": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">משהורה̈ אלנקא ואלטהארה̈</span> have not been translated in the version of Ibn-Tibbon. Al-’Harîzi has also abridged the phrase, translating: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי הם מבחוץ נקיים וטהורים</span>.",
    "fn.3268": "This sentence is somewhat obscure; its sense appears to be as follows: The Law being a divine thing, it might seem that it ought to have provided for the absolute good, both of humanity in general and of each individual in particular. But, if one considers nature, which likewise proceeds from God, one will find that it too comprehends all the general advantages which the Law had in view, and yet that there sometimes result from it damages to individuals. Shem-Ṭob cites the following examples: In the legal order, the condemnation of the guilty is a good for society, and yet it may sometimes happen that innocent persons are condemned in consequence of false testimony. In the natural order, rain, necessary for vegetation, is a benefit to humanity, and yet, when too abundant, it can cause severe calamities. One perceives consequently that, both in the legal and in the natural order, God has provided for the general good of society, without regard to individuals who, by exception, are sometimes the victims of the legal or the natural order. This is an incontestable fact which one is not called upon to explain, and which must be attributed to the impenetrable will of the Divinity. — The grammatical construction of this sentence further increases its obscurity; thus the suffix of <span dir=\"rtl\">פיהא</span> evidently refers to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשריעה̈</span> which stands at the beginning of the sentence, whilst the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄמנהא</span> refers to the plural: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאמור אלטביעיה̈</span>; the natural construction would therefore be thus: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתי פי צׄמנהא תלך</span> (i.e. <span dir=\"ltr\">פי אלשריעה̈</span>) <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמנאפע אלעאמה̈ אלמוגׄודה̈ פיהא</span>, and in Hebrew: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר יש בכללם התועלות ההם הכוללות הנמצאות בה</span>. Ibn-Tibbon rendered the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פיהא</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">בהם</span>, whereas it should have been rendered by <span dir=\"rtl\">בה</span>, that is to say <span dir=\"rtl\">בתורה</span>. Al-’Harîzi suppressed the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פיהא</span>, which he probably deemed superfluous; he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי התורה היא ענין אלהי כי יבחנו אותה בדברים הטבעיים אשר התועלות ההם הכוללות המצואות בכללם יתחייב בהם היזק אנשים</span>.",
    "fn.3269": "That is to say, by establishing a comparison between the Law revealed by God and the laws of nature which also come from God.",
    "fn.3270": "The more regular construction of this sentence would be as follows: <span dir=\"rtl\">לאן אלצור אלטביעיה̈ אלנועיה̈ לא יחצל ענהא פי כל שכׄץ ושכׄץ כל מא ילזם</span>; literally: <i>for as concerns specific physical forms, there does not result from them, in each individual, all that is necessary</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">לא יחצל</span>, two of our MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">לא תחצל</span>, omitting <span dir=\"rtl\">ענהא</span>; according to this reading, one would have to pronounce <span dir=\"rtl\">تحصيل</span>, considering the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלצור אלטביעיה̈</span> as the subject of this active verb.",
    "fn.3271": "That is to say: both natural things and the dispositions of the Law emanate from God. Ibn-Tibbon’s version has <span dir=\"rtl\">ופעולה אחת</span>, <i>and of a single action;</i> but all our Arabic MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ופאעל ואחד</span>, <i>and of a single agent</i> or <i>efficient;</i> and likewise, Al-’Harîzi’s version has <span dir=\"rtl\">ופועל אחד</span>.",
    "fn.3277": "That is, the detailed exposition of the reasons for all the Mosaic laws.",
    "fn.3278": "It will be observed that the following classification sometimes differs, in details, from that which served as the basis for the division into <i>fourteen</i> books of Maimonides’ great work, entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">משנה תורה</span>, <i>Repetition of the Law</i>, and which is also called <span dir=\"rtl\">יד חזקה</span>, <i>Strong Hand</i> (the word <span dir=\"rtl\">יד</span> having the numerical value of 14). In that code, it was a question of placing all homogeneous subjects within the same division, and it was necessary, for instance, to classify fasts under the division of <i>solemn times;</i> whereas here, the classification has solely for its basis the homogeneity of the motives which the author posited for the different laws of the same class.",
    "fn.3279": "See above, chapter <small>XXVI</small>, p. 204, and <i>ibid.</i>, notes 1, 4, 5.",
    "fn.3280": "That is, the vows that are susceptible to redemption and those called <span dir=\"rtl\">חרם</span>, <i>interdit</i> or <i>anathème</i>, and which cannot be redeemed. See Leviticus, chapter <small>XXVII</small>, and <i>Mischné Tora</i>, book VI, last treatise.",
    "fn.3281": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">قصاص</span> signifies <i>talion</i> or <i>public vengeance</i>. Ibn-Tibbon here inaccurately renders the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלקצאצאת</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">דיני ממונות</span>, and subsequently by <span dir=\"rtl\">דיני התשלומין</span>. The Hebrew translation of this word is <span dir=\"rtl\">עונשין</span>, as Al-’Harîzi has it. Further on, at the commencement of chap. <small>XLI</small>, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">הי אלקצאצאת</span> are rendered in Ibn-Tibbon’s version by <span dir=\"rtl\">הם לקחת הדין מן החוטא</span>.",
    "fn.3282": "Word for word: <i>it is not like the weakness of mind of one who pretends;</i> that is, it is not like the weak-minded claim.",
    "fn.3283": "Loans are also mentioned in the IV<sup>th</sup> class; but there they are discussed from the point of view of charity and beneficence, whereas here it is from the point of view of civil law.",
    "fn.3314": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">נצבה̈</span>, <i>situation, place</i>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">נסבה̈</span>, <i>rapport</i>, and similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">על ערך כך</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">במערכת כך</span>.",
    "fn.3315": "The text says word for word: <i>et l’achèvement de cette longue opération;</i> the author means that one lists many other acts that these women must accomplish and that it would be too long to list.",
    "fn.3316": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have מסופקות (mispoked); according to the mss. and conforming to the Arabic text, one must read מפוסקות (mipoked).",
    "fn.3317": "See above, p. 226, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 6.",
    "fn.3318": "In some Arabic mss., one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתי בפעלהא ינפעל להם אלסחר</span>, <i>by the exercise of which magic is performed;</i> and this reading has been followed by the two Hebrew translators; Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר בעשותם אותם יעשה להם הכישוף</span>; Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר בעשייתם יעשה הכישוף</span>.",
    "fn.3319": "Here follows the literal translation of this somewhat lengthy passage, which one has found it necessary to abridge and certain expressions of which have been modified: <i>Since the tendency of the entire Law and the pole upon which it revolves was to cause idolatry to cease, to efface its trace, and</i> (<i>to ensure</i>) <i>that one should not believe of any star whatsoever that it might be harmful or useful in any of the circumstances which pertain to human individuals — for it was this opinion which led to the worship of the stars — it was necessarily incumbent that the magician should be put to death</i>.",
    "fn.3320": "Literally: <i>like the magicians claimed that their magic was effective and that they could, by these operations, expel, etc.</i> The complement of this phrase and the following ones begins with the words <span dir=\"rtl\">פלאגעל הרה טאמור</span>  <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמשהורה חיוניה</span> , <i>on account of these opinions that were then generally prevalent</i>.",
    "fn.3321": "The expression <span dir=\"rtl\">derakhim ha-amorî</span>, <i>ways</i> or <i>customs of the Amorrhéens</i>, is very common among the rabbis, to generally designate pagan superstitions. See, for example, the Mishnah, II<sup>nd</sup> part, treatise <i>Shabbâth</i>, chap. <small>VI</small>, § 10.",
    "fn.3322": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">דברי הברית</span>, <i>paroles de l’Alliance</i>, are borrowed from Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXVIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 69, where they particularly designate the curses contained in this chapter.",
    "fn.3323": "The text reads: <i>vous croyez</i>, and the sentence continues in the form of direct speech (<span dir=\"rtl\">תבעד ענכם</span> ··· <span dir=\"rtl\">בכם</span>). Cf. t. I, p. 283, note 4; t. II, note 2.",
    "fn.3358": "See above, chapter <small>XXXIV</small>.",
    "fn.3388": "See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XVIII</small>, verse <i>3</i>.",
    "fn.3389": "See <i>ibid.</i>, chapter <small>XXVI</small>, <i>v</i>. 3 to 10.",
    "fn.3390": "See Mishnah, I <sup>part</sup>, tractate <i>Bikkurim</i>, chap. <small>III</small>, § 4. — Ibn-Tibbon's version, <span dir=\"rtl\">he takes a basket on his shoulders</span>, is not entirely in accordance with the Arabic text.",
    "fn.3391": "Literally: <i>thus as regards the reward and the punishment;</i> that is, the events that took place in Egypt confirm the truth of the prophecy and establish that there is a providential retribution that rewards the oppressed and punishes the oppressor.",
    "fn.3392": "That is to say: the reason wherefore the consecration of the first-born of these diverse species is particularly prescribed. Vide Numbers, chap. <small>XVIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 17, and confer Exodus, chap. <small>XIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 13. The Rabbins deduce from the verse in Exodus, in which specific mention is made of the first-born of the <i>ass</i>, that this species alone is to be understood by the words <i>unclean animal</i> in the verse in Numbers. Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Bekhorôth</i>, fol. 5 <i>b;</i> Maimonides, <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book VIII, treatise <i>Biccourîm</i>, chap. <small>XII</small>, §3.",
    "fn.3393": "In most mss., and in Ibn-Tibbon's version, one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">רועי צאן היו עבדיך</span>, an inaccurate citation instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">רעה צאן עבדיך</span>. The author may have confused in his memory the passage he had in view with the verse 34 of chap. <small>XLVI</small>, where one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">אנשי מקנה היו עבדיד</span>.",
    "fn.3394": "See Numbers, chap. <small>XXXI</small>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">גזוה̈</span>, <i>expédition</i>, was inaccurately rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version as <span dir=\"rtl\">שלל</span>, <i>butin.</i>",
    "fn.3395": "Voy. Mischnâ, V<sup>e</sup> partie, treatise <i>Bekhorôth</i>, chapter <small>I</small>, § 7.",
    "fn.3396": "Literally: <i>that the earth gives abundant products and is strengthened by fallow land</i>.",
    "fn.3397": "Word for word: <i>with regard to which there can be no absolute sale</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">בתאת</span> should be considered as an adjective of <span dir=\"rtl\">ביע</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(sale)</span> and should be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">بتات</span>; it means <i>what is irrevocable</i>, <i>absolute</i>.",
    "fn.3398": "Ibn-Tibbon omitted from his version the words: <span dir=\"rtl\">וינהגע גלתה לא גיר</span> , <i>and that he might consume its produce</i>, <i>nothing else.</i> Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויהיה לו פירותיו ולא לזולתו</span>; one should have said more precisely: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא זולתס</span>.",
    "fn.3399": "See below, chapter <small>XLIX</small>.",
    "fn.3400": "Literally: <i>who arise among men in the states.</i>",
    "fn.3448": "According to Rabbinic tradition, here designated by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">فقة</span> (cf. volume I, p. 7, note 1), the passages of the Pentateuch concerning the law of retaliation ought not to be taken literally, and the legislator doubtless intended to speak only of pecuniary compensation. According to Josephus (<i>Antiq.</i>, IV, 8, 35), it rested at least with the injured party to content himself with monetary indemnity. The Rabbins adduce several proofs in favour of this interpretation, and they observe, amongst other points, that were the biblical text to be taken literally, the punishment in many cases would be disproportionate to the crime committed, for the operation might occasion the death of the culprit: <span dir=\"rtl\">עין תחת עין ולא נפש ועין תחת עין</span>, «an eye for an eye, and not the eye and the life for an eye.» Consult the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Baba Kamma</i>, fol. 84 <i>a.</i> Maimonides, in the <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, pronounces himself in the same sense. Consult the XI<sup>th</sup> book, treatise <i>Hovel u-mazzîk</i> (concerning him who renders himself guilty of injuries), chap. I, §§ 2 and 3. One has, therefore, here an evident proof that Maimonides, in the present work, follows his personal opinion, without concern for Rabbinic decisions. Cf. volume II of this work, p. 376, in the addition to note 3 of p. 352. Nevertheless, it is indeed difficult to wholly absolve our author from the reproach of self-contradiction; in his Introduction to the <i>Commentary</i> on the Mishnah, where he lays down general principles and where he appears to speak in his own name, he expressly states that a pretended prophet who should assail the traditional explanation of the texts, and who should say, for instance, that the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וקצותה את כפה</span>, «<i>thou shalt cut off her hand</i>» (Deuteronomy, <small>XXV</small>, 12), are to be understood literally, and not in the sense of a pecuniary penalty, would thereby demonstrate himself to be a false prophet and would be put to death.",
    "fn.3529": "Literally: <i>The law has therefore respected the inviolability of exposure through cohabitation</i>, <i>albeit this latter should have occurred through a certain sin</i>. Most MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">דעת</span> with <span dir=\"rtl\">ד</span>; according to this, one should translate: <i>the Law has therefore</i> <small>PROCLAIMED</small> <i>the inviolability etc.</i> One has written <span dir=\"rtl\">רעת</span> with <span dir=\"rtl\">ר</span>, a reading found in some of our MSS. and adopted by the two Hebrew translators (<span dir=\"rtl\">שמרה התורה</span>); one here takes the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">רעת</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(رَغَثَ)</span> in the sense of the III<sup>rd</sup> form <span dir=\"rtl\">(راعت)</span> . The words <span dir=\"rtl\">חרמה̈ כשפה̈</span> present some obscurity; in one MS. one reads <span dir=\"rtl\">חרמת כשפה̈</span>; in another <span dir=\"rtl\">חרמה̈ כשפת</span>. These words have visibly embarrassed the two Hebrew translators, who have not been able to render a precise translation thereof. Ibn-Tibbon renders <span dir=\"rtl\">חרמה̈ כשפה̈ אלגׄמאע</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">קרבת המשגל</span>, and Al-’Harîzi by <span dir=\"rtl\">ענין גלוי הבעילה</span>. In one of the Oxford MSS. (Hunt. 162), one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">כ׳יטבה אלנכאח</span> , instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">כשפה̈ אלגׄמאע</span>; indeed, the noun of action <span dir=\"rtl\">كشف</span> is more commonly used than <span dir=\"rtl\">كشفة</span> . The sense is: the Law has commanded the warrior to respect the captive woman whom he has abused, notwithstanding that the relations he has had with her were a sin, cohabitation with a pagan woman being prohibited by the law.",
    "fn.3530": "Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">באלה המצות</span>, in the plural; Al-’Harîzi has, in accordance with the Arabic text, <span dir=\"rtl\">בזאת המצוה</span>.",
    "fn.3531": "Literally: <i>and that one does not deviate from mutual aid useful to both parties</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's version deviates slightly from the literal sense; it reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ושיעזרו העוסקים זה את זה להועיל כל אחד את חבירו</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">ושלא יהיו נוטים בו מן העזר והסיוע לשני בעלי העסק</span>.",
    "fn.3532": "See Leviticus, chap. <small>XXV</small>, <i>v.</i> 14-17, and Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Baba Meci’a</i>, fol. 51 <i>a</i>. Should the fraud to the detriment of the buyer or the seller exceed the sixth part of the object's total value, the transaction is declared null by the traditional law. See Talmud, <i>l. c.</i>, and <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book XII, treatise <i>Mekhirâ</i> (concerning sales), chap. <small>XII</small>, §§ 2 et seq., and chap. <small>XIV</small>, § 1.",
    "fn.3533": "See Mischnâ, IVth part, tractate Baba Meci’a, chap. IV.10; Mischné Torâ, l.c., chap. XIV.12 and following. On the conditions the author speaks of here, see in general the entire tractate Mekhirâ (of Sales).",
    "fn.3348": "According to Maimonides, the cult of Moloch consisted not in burning children, but merely in causing them to pass between two fires, as a ceremony of lustration; vide his commentary on the Mischnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Synhedrin</i>, chap. <small>VII</small>, § 7, and <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book I, treatise on <i>Idolatry</i>, chap. <small>VI</small>, § 3. The interpretation of Maimonides is in accord with that of the Talmud, treatise <i>Synhedrin</i>, fol. 64 <i>b</i>. Nevertheless, there are rabbis who opine that the children were burnt by being cast into the arms of a bronze statue reddened by fire. Vide <i>Yalkout</i>, on Jeremiah, <small>VII</small>, 31 (vol. II, No. 277), and cf. <i>Palestine</i>, p. 91.",
    "fn.3349": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אנפעאלהן</span>, which is here in the Arabic text, is inexact in Ibn-Tibbon's version, translated as <span dir=\"rtl\">האמין הנשים לכל דבר</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates more exactly: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומהירות היותם נפעלות לכל דבר</span>.",
    "fn.3350": "Literally: <i>this thing which ye do, in order that, by this act, the children may live, etc.</i> Concerning the use of the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">אן</span> with the direct phrase, cf. supra, p. 281, note 3. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תפעלונה</span>, some MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">יפעלונה</span>, in the 3<sup>rd</sup> person. Similarly, Ibn-Tibbon (in the MSS.) has <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר יעשוהו</span>, and Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר יעשן</span>.",
    "fn.3351": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ושמתי אני</span>, several manuscripts have incorrectly written <span dir=\"rtl\">ונתתי אני</span>; Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi have <span dir=\"rtl\">ונתתי</span>. Cf. above, p. 132, note 1.",
    "fn.3352": "Literally: <i>Regarde par conséquent la malice de l'auteur de cette opinion, et comment il l'a perpétuée par cette chimère;</i> that is, by making people believe that this superstitious practice is necessary for the conservation of the race.",
    "fn.3353": "That is to say: they have prescribed other superstitious observances, under the pretext that they contributed to the conservation of goods.",
    "fn.3354": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read: <span dir=\"rtl\">שיהיה אילן אחד נעבד</span>; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">לנעבד</span> as several mss. do, in accordance with the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">ללמעבוד</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">שיהיה אילן אחד לזה הפסל הנעבד</span>.",
    "fn.3355": "See above, p. 234, note 4.",
    "fn.3356": "The Biblical text (Leviticus, <small>XIX</small>, 23) does not precisely state that that which the tree produces in the first three years should be burnt. But, according to tradition, it is commanded to destroy this produce entirely, and it is forbidden to employ it for any use whatsoever. Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatises <i>Pesachim</i>, fol. 22 <i>b; </i>Baba Kamma</i>, fol. 101 <i>a</i>. In the Mischnah (V<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Temourâ</i>, chap. <small>VII</small>, § 5), this produce, denominated <span dir=\"rtl\">ערלה</span>, is reckoned among the things which must be burnt.",
    "fn.3357": "Cf. Mischna, I<sup>st</sup> part<i>, treatise </i>Shebiith<small>, chap. </small>II[[t:6]], § 6.",
    "fn.3574": "These are the three first treatises of the eighth book of the <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, entitled ’<i>Abôda</i> (of the cult). The entire book has been translated into Latin by Louis de Compiègne de Veil and published under the title: <i>De Cultu divino, tractatus IX continens</i>, Paris, in-4°, 1688.",
    "fn.3575": "Cf. Ezekiel, chap. XX[40], where the mountain of the Temple is called הר מרום ישראל, the high mountain of Israel[7]. According to a well-known Jewish tradition, the temple was situated on the highest point of Palestine. Cf. the commentary of David Kim'hi on the passage of Ezekiel.",
    "fn.3576": "One knows that the Arabs thus name the direction towards which it is prescribed for them to turn during prayer. Muhammad, in imitation of the Jews, had at first designated the Temple of Jerusalem as the <i>Kiblâ</i>; but later he designated that of Mecca. See d’Herbelot, <i>Bibliothèque orientale</i>, in-folio edition, p. 952. The author here employs this word in the same sense; the <i>Kiblâ</i> of the Jews was the Holy of Holies. See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Berakhôth</i>, fol. 30 <i>a</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">היה עומד בבית המקדש יכוין את לבו כנגד בית קדשי הקדשים</span>. The author proceeds to inform us why this part of the Temple of Jerusalem was situated to the West. Ibn-Tibbon, omitting the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וכׄצץ אלקבלה̈</span>, has briefly rendered <span dir=\"rtl\">ויחד המערב</span>. Al-’Harîzi, seeking to render the idea of the word <i>Kiblah</i>, translated: <span dir=\"rtl\">ושׄם כונת תפלתו מוגבלת לפאת מערב</span>.",
    "fn.3577": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>Baba Bathra</i>, fol. 25 <i>a</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">Rabbi Abahu says, the Shekhinah is in the West</span>.",
    "fn.3578": "I have vainly sought a passage of this sort in the Guemarâ of <i>Yômâ</i>, in both the Babylonian and Jerusalem versions. See the <i>Additions and rectifications</i> at the end of this volume.",
    "fn.3579": "Cf. Tacite, Hist., book III, chap. 24: 'and orientem solem (ita in Syria mos est) tertiani salutavere.' Lipsius, in his Notes, cites passages from several ancient authors that prove the practice of turning towards the east during prayer existed among the Greeks, Romans, and many other peoples.",
    "fn.3580": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">רדה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(ردة)</span> , <i>defection</i>, <i>apostasy</i>, has not been translated by Ibn-Tibbon or by Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.3581": "The author is referring to the paraphrase of Onkelos on the verse 14 of chapter XXII of the book of Genesis.",
    "fn.3515": "See Joshua, chap. <small>XXII</small>, <i>v</i>. 12. The author made a mistake in memory here; in the passage of Joshua to which he refers, one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויקהלו כל עדת בני ישראל שלה לעלות עליהם לצבא</span>, <i>and all the assembly of the children of Israel gathered to go up against them.</i>",
    "fn.3516": "See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXV</small>, <i>v.</i> 19, and <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book <small>XIV</small>, treatise on <i>Rois and Guerres</i>, chapter <small>I</small>, §§ 1 and 2.",
    "fn.3517": "Literally: <i>toward the sons of such a one;</i> we know that this is the manner in which the Arabs designate tribes.",
    "fn.3518": "See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 13 and 14, and <i>Mishneh Torah, l.c.</i>, chapter <small>VI</small>, sections 14 and 15.",
    "fn.3519": "See above, chap. XXXIII[0]XXXIII[1], p. 264.",
    "fn.3520": "The editions of the Ibn-Tibbon version have <span dir=\"rtl\">ובזאת הכונה</span>; one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">ובזאת המצוה</span>, as the mss. of this version do.",
    "fn.3521": "The author refers to Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 11 and 12; Leviticus, chapter <small>XV</small>, <i>v.</i> 16. The Hebrew terms used by the author are taken from other passages (Leviticus, <small>XIV</small>, 8; Numbers, <small>XIX</small>, 7) that do not relate to this subject.",
    "fn.3522": "The author doubtless intends to convey that he concerns himself here solely with the rational explanation of the biblical texts, without regard to the distinctions established by traditional law, concerning legal impurities, between the three enclosures denominated: camp of the Israelites (<span dir=\"rtl\">צחנה ישראל</span>), camp of the Levites (<span dir=\"rtl\">מחנה לויה</span>), and camp of the divine majesty (<span dir=\"rtl\">מחנה שכינה</span>). See the <i>Sifre</i> on the passage of Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 11; Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Pesachim</i>, fol. 68 <i>a; Mishneh Torah</i>, book VIII, treatise <i>Bi'at ha-Mikdash</i> (concerning entrance into the sanctuary), chap. <small>III</small>, § 8. Cf. Commentary on the Mishnah, VI<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Kelim</i>, chap. <small>I</small>, § 8.",
    "fn.3523": "Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 10-14.",
    "fn.3524": "That is to say: The law only wanted to impose certain rules of conduct on the soldiers with regard to the captive, in order to curb their unbridled passions. See Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Kiddouschîn</i>, fol. 21 <i>b.</i>.",
    "fn.3525": "The Hebrew words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא ילהצנה במלחמה</span> are taken from the Talmud, <i>l. c.</i>, fol. 22 <i>a</i>. Cf. <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book XIV, treatise on <i>Kings and Wars</i>, chapter <small>VIII</small>, §§ 2-9.",
    "fn.3526": "That is, she leaves it entirely free to indulge in all the demonstrations of her sadness.",
    "fn.3527": "Cf. Mishnaic Torâh, l. c</i>., §§ 2 and 5.",
    "fn.3528": "Ibn-Tibbon's version reads <span dir=\"rtl\">עד הזמן ההוא</span>; Al-’Harîzi's version is more accurate: <span dir=\"rtl\">כל הזמן ההוא</span>.",
    "fn.3654": "Vide Leviticus, chap. <small>XXVII</small>, <i>v</i>. 13, 15, 19, 27 and 31; Mischnâ, V<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>’Arakhin</i>, chap. <small>III</small>, §2, and chap. <small>VII</small>, §2; Maimonides, <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book VI, treatise ’<i>Arakhin</i>, chap. <small>IV</small>, §5, and especially chap. <small>V</small>, §3.—According to the traditional law, it is one fourth of the value which is added, so that the sum added forms one fifth of the total price of redemption.",
    "fn.3655": "See, for example, the Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Synhédrin</i>, fol. 10 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3656": "All mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">יתחרי</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">يَتَحَرَّى</span> , V<sup>e</sup> form of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">حمى</span> , meaning <i>to inquire carefully about something</i>. Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi have used in their versions the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ידקדק</span>; they seem to have read in their Arabic text <span dir=\"rtl\">יתחרר</span>.",
    "fn.3657": "Word for word: <i>he will not take much trouble to present it;</i> that is, to show it to others.",
    "fn.3658": "Word for word: <i>we called for help against him t’augmentation;</i> that is, the treasury of the sanctuary was covered, by the augmentation, against the bad faith of the owner of the consecrated thing. The translation of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">they were obliged to add</span>, is inaccurate.",
    "fn.3659": "The author intends, as it seems to one, that, in case the proprietor should refuse to add the fifth part, the consecrated object might be sold by the priests to another person for the true price. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">מן גירה</span> ought here to be translated <i>to another</i>, for the indirect object of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">باع</span>, <i>to sell</i>, is expressed in Arabic by the accusative or by <span dir=\"rtl\">من</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">נ</span>; thus, to say <i>he sold him the thing</i>, one would put in Arabic <span dir=\"rtl\">باعة المشى</span>, or else <span dir=\"rtl\">باع الشئ منه</span>. Vide Sylvestre de Sacy's Commentary on the <i>Séances de Hariri</i>, p. 354.",
    "fn.3660": "Word for word: <i>and by what means one sought to approach him, or to insinuate oneself near him</i>. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תקרב</span> is here the passive of the V<sup>e</sup> form and must be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">تَقْرُبُ</span>.",
    "fn.3689": "Vide Leviticus, chap. <small>V</small>, <i>v</i>. 15, 18 and 25; chap. <small>XIV</small>, <i>v</i>. 12-13; chap. <small>XIX</small>, <i>v</i>. 21-22; Numbers, chap. <small>VI</small>, <i>v</i>. 12. On the differences between the <i>’Hattath</i> and the <i>Ascham</i>, vide one’s <i>Réflexions sur le culte des anciens Hébreux</i>, p. 34-35; <i>Palestine</i>, p. 160 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.3690": "The author intends that, for the burnt offering which supposs no sin and which is oft a voluntary sacrifice offered unto God, the male was preferred. Vide Leviticus, chap. <small>I</small>, <i>v</i>. 3 and 10; for birds alone, the sex is not distinguished (<i>ibid., v</i>. 14), and one may as well offer a female as a male for a burnt offering. Cf. Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Mena’hoth</i>, fol. 25 <i>a; Temourâ</i>, fol. 14 <i>a;</i> Maimonides, <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book VIII, treatise <i>Issouré Mizbea’h</i>, chap. <small>III</small>, §1.",
    "fn.3691": "Literally: <i>because it is likewise a suspicion of sin;</i> that is to say, because this latter offering has for its motive a suspicion of sin. The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">לאנהא</span> relats solely to <span dir=\"rtl\">מנחת סוטה</span>, whereas the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">תקריבהא</span> relats to both offerings. Vide Leviticus, ch. <small>V</small>, <i>v</i>. 11, and Numbers, ch. V, <i>v</i>. 15. Cf. one’s <i>Réflexions, etc.</i>, p. 39, and <i>Palestine</i>, p. 163 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.3692": "Word for word: <i>it is as if she had been moved to repentance</i>, and (as if) <i>one were saying to her</i>. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">חרך</span> must be read obviously in the passive <span dir=\"rtl\">(خُبْرِكَ)</span> ; Ibn-Tibbon's translation, <span dir=\"rtl\">as if he were rousing her</span>, seems to me inexact; similarly Al-’Harîzi's translation, which has <span dir=\"rtl\">as if he were awakening</span>.",
    "fn.3693": "Ibn-Tibbon's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">these laws have already been drawn up in their entirety in order</span>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">laws</span>, the mss. have more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">portions</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">and behold, all these details have been drawn up in order</span>.",
    "fn.3694": "See note 2 on the previous page.",
    "fn.3695": "See <i>Yalkout</i>, on Leviticus, chap. <small>IX</small>, <i>v</i>. 2, n° 521 (based on the <i>Tôrath Kohanim</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">יש בידכם בתחלה וישחטו שעיר עזים יבא שעיר ויכפר על מעשה שעיר</span>.",
    "fn.3696": "Vide <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book I, treatise <i>De’ôth</i>, chap. <small>II</small>, §2, and Commentary on the Mishnah, <i>Eight Chapters</i>, serving as an Introduction to the treatise <i>Abôth</i>, chap. <small>IV</small>, in which the author cits as an example the vice of <i>parsimony</i> or avarice, which must be cured by the opposing vice of <i>prodigality</i>, in order to enable the parsimonious to acquire the intermediate virtue of <i>generosity</i> (vide Pococke, <i>Porta Mosis</i>, p. 198-199). Cf. vol. II, p. 285, note 1.",
    "fn.3582": "See Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XII</small>, <i>v.</i> 11, 14, 18, 21, 26; chap. <small>XV</small>, <i>v.</i> 20; chap. <small>XVI</small>, <i>v.</i> 6; chap. <small>XVII</small>, <i>v.</i> 10. In all these passages, the place of the future sanctuary is designated by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר יבחר י״י</span>, <i>which God shall choose</i>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אל המקום</span>, which are found in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon and the version of Al-Ḥarîzi, appear in but one of our Arabic manuscripts and refer only to two passages (Deuter., <small>XII</small>, 26; <small>XVI</small>, 6), whereas the author speaks in general of all the passages one has just cited.",
    "fn.3583": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">he was commanded to build</span>; the mss. of this version and Schem-Tob's commentary carry, in accordance with the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">until it is the command to unite</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates in the same sense: <span dir=\"rtl\">so that it would be the desire of one man</span>.",
    "fn.3584": "See <i>Mishnah Torah</i>, book XIV, treatise <i>on Kings and Wars</i>, chapter <small>I</small>, sections 1 and 2. Cf. Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, fol. 20 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.3585": "See above, p. 226.",
    "fn.3586": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read <span dir=\"rtl\">או לגלגל אחד</span>; the manuscripts, in conformity with the Arabic text, have: <span dir=\"rtl\">או לחלק מגלגל</span>, <i>or to a portion of a sphere</i>. The author, it appears to one, intends to indicate by these words that the stars, or the luminous bodies of the planets, are material beings like the sphere of which they form a part, and that they are not purely spiritual beings, external to the spheres, like the <i>Separate Intelligences</i>, of which mention shall be made a little further on.",
    "fn.3587": "We note here in passing that Maimonides himself takes the word <span dir=\"rtl\">לבת</span> in the sense of <i>midst</i>. See t. I, chap. <small>XXXIX</small>, p. 142. It was by accident that in t. II, ch. <small>VI</small>, p. 73, we reproduced the ordinary translation: ‘in a <i>flame</i> of fire.’",
    "fn.3588": "See above, chapter <small>XXIX</small>, p. 234, note 4.",
    "fn.3589": "See ibid., p. 228.",
    "fn.3590": "See Vol. II, p. 31, note 2.",
    "fn.3591": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">וגׄודה</span>, <i>son être</i>, <i>son existence</i>, the version of Ibn-Tibbon incorrectly translates <span dir=\"rtl\">טובו ואורו</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">והם אשר נאצל עליהם מציאותו ית׳</span>.",
    "fn.3592": "See t. I, chap. <small>XLIX</small>; t. II, chap. <small>VI</small>, where the author explains that by the <i>anges</i> he means the <i>Intelligences separated</i>.",
    "fn.3786": "See Mischnâ, I<sup>st</sup> part, treatise <i>Berakhôth</i>, chap. <small>V</small>, § 3, where it is stated that one ought to silence him who, in his prayer, speaks of the mercy God has shown towards the bird's nest; Maimonides, in his <i>Mischné Torâ</i> (book II, treatise of Prayer, chap. <small>IX</small>, § 7), expresses himself in the same sense as the Mischnâ, and contrary to that which he states in our present passage, in which he manifests his personal opinion. Cf. t. II, p. 376 (end of the addition to p. 352, note 3), and above, ch. <small>XLI</small>, p. 313, note 1, and chap. <small>XLVI</small>, p. 372, note 2.",
    "fn.3787": "That is, the opinion that admits divine laws have rational motives and emanate from divine wisdom. See above, chap. <small>XXVI</small> and <small>XXXI</small>.",
    "fn.3788": "See above, chapter <small>XLVI</small>, p. 373.",
    "fn.3789": "See Numbers, chapter <small>XXX</small>, <i>verse</i> 3-17.",
    "fn.3790": "That is, they serve as a guarantee for abstinence; for man, fearing to give in to intemperance, often secures himself by a vow. See Mishnah, IVth part, treatise Aboth, chapter III, section 13.",
    "fn.3791": "Literally: <i>for all that is related to him</i>. The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read: <span dir=\"rtl\">בכל מה שיש לו בו נזק או תועלת</span>. The mss. have, in accordance with the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">בכל מה שנתלה בו</span>. The sense is: the thing remains abandoned to the head of the family, who can approve the vow of his wife or daughter, or cancel it.",
    "fn.3792": "See Numbers, chap. <small>XXX</small>, <i>v.</i> 17. The Rabbins deduce, from the words <span dir=\"rtl\">בנעוריה בית אביה</span>, <i>in her youth, in her father’s house</i>, that the father is empowered to annul his daughter’s vows solely until such age as she becomes nubile (<span dir=\"rtl\">בוגרת</span>), that is to say, until the age of twelve and a half years. See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Nedarîm</i>, fol. 70 <i>a</i>, <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.3793": "See Num. chap. <small>VI</small>, <i>v.</i> 2-4.",
    "fn.3794": "Literally: <i>who has caused the death of the ancients and the moderns</i>. The Hebrew word <span dir=\"rtl\">רבים</span> that follows, and which is also found in the Arabic text, should be joined to the following words, and in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the preposition <span dir=\"rtl\">מן</span> before <span dir=\"rtl\">הראשונים</span>, which is not found in the mss., should be erased.",
    "fn.3795": "These words that the author has written in Hebrew are an imitation of a passage from Proverbs, chap. <small>VII</small>, <i>v.</i> 26, which relates to the debauched and adulterous woman.",
    "fn.3796": "The author clearly thought of the passage in Numbers, chap. <small>VI</small>, <i>v.</i> 4; but instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">יֵעָשה</span>, he wrote <span dir=\"rtl\">יֵצֵא</span>, from a passage in the Judges, chap. <small>XIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 14. Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi have restored the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יעשה</span> of Numbers.",
    "fn.3797": "See Numbers, chapter <small>VI</small>, <i>verse</i>. 7.",
    "fn.3798": "Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">מן היין</span>. He may have had <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשראב</span> in his Arabic text instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשרב</span>.",
    "fn.3799": "See above, chapter <small>XXXV</small>, p. 273.",
    "fn.3800": "The passage the author had in mind is found, not in the 9th, but in the 8th book of the Ethics to Nicomachus, at the beginning of chap. 1: ἔστι γὰρ φιλία ἀρετή τις ἢ μετ’ ἀρετῆς, ἔτι δ’ἀναγϰαιοτάτη εἰς τὸν βίον[7]. Cf. above, p. 343, note 2.",
    "fn.3801": "Vide Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 18. — The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version bear <span dir=\"rtl\">למה שיש בהתרה וגו׳</span>, <i>on account of what there might be in her admission, etc.;</i> that is to say, in the admission of the <span dir=\"rtl\">קדשה</span>, or <i>prostitute</i>. The MSS. have, more conformably to the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">למה שיש בה וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.3802": "The first <span dir=\"rtl\">עצבה</span> should be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">عَصَبَةُ</span>, <i>family;</i> the second is the plural of this same word with a suffix, that is to say <span dir=\"rtl\">عَصَبَةٌ</span>, <i>her relations</i>. Ibn-Tibbon's translation, <span dir=\"rtl\">לא ידע לעצמו קרוב</span>, is inexact. Al-’Harîzi renders it better: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא יִוָּדע לו יחס</span>.",
    "fn.3803": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">זיגׄה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(زيجة)</span> , which the author employs several times in this chapter and which is not found in Arabic dictionaries, means <i>wedding</i>, like the Talmudic word <span dir=\"rtl\">זיגה</span>.",
    "fn.3804": "Literally: <i>and he would say: it is a wife;</i> the translation by Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">שאאו אהודו</span>, is not literal.",
    "fn.3805": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">עקד</span> has not been rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version.",
    "fn.3806": "Word for word: <i>she would seek a negligence</i>; that is to say, she would wait for a moment of negligence on the part of her husband; the translation of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">היתה האשה מחזרת אחר פשיעה</span>, is inaccurate.",
    "fn.3807": "See Numbers, chapter <small>V</small>, <i>v.</i> 11-31, and cf. above, chapter <small>XLVII</small>, p. 394, note 2.",
    "fn.3808": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ותחתאט גאיה̈ אלאחתיאט</span>, synonyms of the preceding words, have been omitted in the version of Ibn-Tibbon.",
    "fn.3284": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאיאם אלמחטורה</span> signify literally: dies vetiti, prohibiti (a labore). Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמחטׄורה̈</span> (in certain manuscripts, with an incorrect orthography, <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמחצׄורה̈</span>), mss. No. 18 of Leyden and No. 63 of the Hebrew supplement of the Imperial Library present <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמחדודה̈</span>, and it is this latter reading which Ibn-Tibbon would appear to have followed, as he translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">הימים הידועים</span>, \"the determined days.\" The version of Al-’Harîzi has <span dir=\"rtl\">הימים הספורים</span>, \"the numbered days;\" he evidently possessed the faulty reading <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמחצורה̈</span> (the <span dir=\"rtl\">צ</span> without a point). Cf. further on, chap. <small>XLI</small>, <span dir=\"rtl\">בעץׄ מחטׄוראתהא</span> (fol. 91 <i>a</i> of the Arabic text, line 3 from the bottom).",
    "fn.3285": "The editions of the Ibn-Tibbon translation add here the words <span dir=\"rtl\">והם אשר ספרנום בספר זמנים</span>. These words, which would indeed be in their place here, do not, however, appear in the manuscripts of the Ibn-Tibbon translation or in that of Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.3286": "All our Arabic mss. have simply <span dir=\"rtl\">ראי</span>, <i>opinion, idée</i>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">דעת אמיתית</span>, and similarly that of Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">סברא נכונה</span>.",
    "fn.3287": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלעאמה̈</span>, <i>général</i>, refers to the cult ceremonies imposed on all Israelites, excluding those that concern only priests and Levites, which will be discussed later.",
    "fn.3941": "Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואחר שהעניין כן</span>, “<i>since</i> it is so.” Ibn-Falaquera observes with reason that it were necessary to translate <span dir=\"rtl\">ואם היה הדבר כן</span>, <i>if it were so;</i> for the author affirms nothing, and it is here but a simple hypothesis. The idea of <i>since</i>, he adds, would be expressed in Arabic by <span dir=\"rtl\">وكما</span> , and not by <span dir=\"rtl\">וأن</span> . Vide Append. of the <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, pp. 147 and 158.",
    "fn.3457": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ויחתאט</span> was not translated by Ibn-Tibbon. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ויסהעד לה</span> mean literally: <i>and to prepare against him</i> (to defend oneself).",
    "fn.3458": "The author, before entering into the details of criminal penalties, poses in this <i>Observation</i> some general principles that, according to him, guided the legislator. On the use of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מקדמה̈</span>, cf. above, p. 3, note 1.",
    "fn.3459": "Word for word: <i>but for that which does rarely occur, the weaker penalty, joined to the rarity of the case, suffices to prevent it</i>. The words <i>joined to the rarity of the case</i> constitute a troublesome repetition. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פיסיר אלעקאב</span>, which form the subject of the participle <span dir=\"rtl\">באף</span>, are rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version by <span dir=\"rtl\">במעט מן העונש</span>; the prefix <span dir=\"rtl\">ב</span> in <span dir=\"rtl\">במעט</span> ought to be suppressed, albeit it is found also in the manuscripts.",
    "fn.3460": "That is to say, if one does classify crimes or sins according to the penalty they entail, one shall find that they are of four categories, of which three entail penalties of greater or lesser severity, and of which the fourth comprehends light sins which entail no judicial penalty. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">תרתיב אלקצאץ</span> signify literally: <i>the classification of the penalty;</i> but it is evident that the author intends to speak of the classification of crimes or sins according to the penalty, inasmuch as he includs in this classification a category of sins without penalty.",
    "fn.3461": "The rabbis, hearing the <i>retranchement</i>, about to be mentioned, in the sense of premature but natural death, call the capital punishment inflicted by men: <span dir=\"rtl\">מיתת בית דין</span>, <i>mort par le tribunal.</i> In what follows, we translate this term more simply by <i>peine de mort</i> or <i>peine capitale.</i>",
    "fn.3996": "All manuscripts have distinctly: <span dir=\"rtl\">מן חצל</span> , and this reading is confirmed by Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 139), who translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">he attains</span>. The construction of the sentence required more regularly: <span dir=\"rtl\">אַךְ חצל</span> .",
    "fn.3431": "See Deuteronomy, chapter XXII</small>, <i>v</i>. 1 to 3.",
    "fn.3432": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי צלאח אלאחואל</span> literally mean: <i>for the goodness of relations.</i> What one must understand by this expression, the author himself has said more explicitly in chapter <small>XXVII</small>, See the Arabic text, fol. 60 <i>a</i> at the bottom: <span dir=\"rtl\">צלאח אחואל אלנאס בעצׄהם מע בעץׄ וגו׳</span>, and the French translation, p. 213.",
    "fn.3427": "Literally: <i>pour qu’il</i> (the animal) <i>ne soit pas perdu pour lui.</i> Ibn-Tibbon translates inaccurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">שלא תעלם מעיניו</span>, <i>for not losing sight of it;</i> better Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">לבל תאבר לן</span>.",
    "fn.3428": "That is to say, unto him who persecutes a person with intent to commit a crime against him, or, as one shall presently see, unto him who meditates an assault upon the life or modesty of a person. Vide Mishnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Sanhedrin,</i> chap. <small>VIII,</small> § 7. The Talmud connects this recommendation unto the words of Leviticus (chap. <small>XIX,</small> <i>v.</i> 16) <span dir=\"rtl\">לא תעמד על דם רעך,</span> which are taken in this sense: 'Thou shalt not stand idle when it concerns the saving of thy neighbour's life.' These words are combined by the Talmudists with <i>v.</i> 26 of chap. <small>XXII</small> of Deuteronomy, which treats of rape. Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Sanhedrin,</i> fol. 73 <i>a.</i>",
    "fn.3296": "In this passage and in the following ones, the author speaks of certain duties related to the study of the law and the respect due to scholars and the elderly. All these duties have been exposed by the author in his Abbreviation of the Talmud, or <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, treatise <i>Talmud Torâ</i> (on the study of the law).",
    "fn.3297": "On this expression, see the t. II, p. 65, note 3.",
    "fn.3942": "Cf. above, chap. <small>XVIII</small>, p. 137.—The reflections contained in this paragraph are rather religious and edifying than rigorously philosophical, and the author could not seriously have believed that they might serve to resolve, <i>even according to the opinions of the philosophers</i>, all the difficulties presented by the problem of <i>individual Providence</i>. This, being an object of religious faith, is not susceptible of philosophical demonstration. The entire book of Job aims to demonstrate that it were a culpable presumption to endeavour to resolve, by means of the reasonings of the intellect, the difficulties of this problem and to lift the veil which covers for us this profound mystery, before which one has but to bow, acknowledging one's impotence. Thus, the author presents his theory as a mere hypothesis, and one must recall what he has said above (chap. <small>XVII</small>, p. 129): “In this belief, I rely not upon demonstrative proofs, but rather upon that which has appeared to me to be the evident intention of the book of God and of the writings of our prophets, etc.” The translator Ibn-Tibbon, who perceived in the author’s words a more positive assertion (vide the preceding note), drafted upon this matter a lengthy letter to Maimonides to express unto him his doubts. The commencement thereof has been published in the collection of the <i>Letters of Maimonides</i> (Amsterdam edition, in-12, 1712, fol. 12 <i>a</i>); therein one reads these words: <span dir=\"rtl\">רק שאלה אחת היא בלבי כאש בוערת עצורה בעצמותי עוררה לי פרק אחד מן הפרקים האחרונים מן החלק השלישי וכו׳</span>. Ibn-Falaquera has acquainted one with the content of this letter, and he has responded to the doubts expressed by Ibn-Tibbon. Vide the Appendix of the <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, chap. <small>II</small> (pp. 145 et seq.). Moses of Narbonne likewise speaks thereof in his commentary on chap. <small>LI</small>. According to him, the author intended to speak of those who (by speculation or by a species of <i>ecstasy</i>) have known how to identify themselves completely with the active intellect and to become similar to the separate intelligences, so that, free from the bonds of matter, they cannot be afflicted by any of the evils which strike material beings. The entire letter in question is found in a manuscript of the Bodleian Library (cod. Poc. n° 74); it is dated from the beginning of Adar I 4965 (towards the end of January 1205); it was written, consequently, after the death of Maimonides, which occurred on 13 December 1204, when this death was yet unknown in Provence.",
    "fn.3339": "The versions of Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi add the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פלוני</span> here, so the translation should be: 'since <i>one such</i> person inhabits, etc.' The Arabic text, consistent across all manuscripts, does not allow for this translation.",
    "fn.3340": "For example, ornaments of an animal to which it is rendered a cult. See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>VII</small>, <i>v.</i> 25, and Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>Temourâ</i>, fol. 28 <i>b;</i> treatise <i>Aboda Zarâ</i>, fol. 51 <i>b</i>. Cf. Maimonides, <i>Sépher miçwoth</i>, negative precepts, n° 22.",
    "fn.3453": "Word for word: <i>witnesses meditating</i> (<i>the evil</i>). The term <span dir=\"rtl\">זוממין</span> is taken from the words of the Pentateuch: 'and you will do according to what he has <i>meditated</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">זמם</span>) to do to his neighbor' (Deuteronomy, <small>XIX</small>, 19).",
    "fn.3454": "See Deuteronomy, chap. <small>IV</small>, <i>v.</i> 8. One must remember that earlier, chap. <small>XXVI</small>, p. 203, the author interpreted the words of Deuteronomy in a more general sense. That is why he says here: 'in this sense <i>also.</i>'",
    "fn.3338": "Vide supra, p. 240, note 1. Maimonides, as one observes, explains the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כלי</span> in the text of Deuteronomy in the sense of <i>armour</i>, following in this the opinion of Onkelos, whose Chaldaic version bears <span dir=\"rtl\">תקון זין</span>, and which is likewise that of Josephus (<i>Antiquities</i>, book IV, chap. <small>VIII</small>, § 43) and of certain doctors of the Talmud (treatise <i>Nazîr</i>, fol. 59 <i>a</i>); cf. <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, treatise on Idolatry, chap. <small>XII</small>, § 10. Vide also Selden, <i>De Diis Syris</i>, Syntagma II, chap. <small>IV</small>; Spencer, <i>De Leg. ritual. Hebræorum</i>, lib. II, chap. <small>XVII</small> (Cambridge edit., in-fol., p. 406 et seq.). Milius, <i>Dissertationes selectæ</i> (Lugd. Bat., 1743, in-4°), dissert. <i>De Commutatione vestium</i>, p. 202 et seq., takes the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כלי</span> in the sense of <i>garment</i>. Be that as it may, an idolatrous custom is here in question. Vide Macrobius, <i>Saturnalia</i>, l. III, chap. <small>VIII</small>: \"Philochorus quoque in Athide eamdem (venerem) affirmat esse lunam, et ei sacrificium facere viros cum veste muliebri, mulieres cum virili, quod eadem et mas æstimatur et femina.\"",
    "fn.3463": "This third category of sins entails two sorts of penalties, to wit: <i>a</i>) scourging or lashing, decreed for the transgression of certain negative precepts enumerated in the Mishnah, treatise <i>Maccôth</i>, chap. 3; <i>b</i>) premature death, with which, according to tradition, those are smitten who render themselves guilty of certain sins enumerated in the Talmud, treatise <i>Synhédrin</i>, fol. 83 <i>a</i>, and <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book XIV, treatise <i>Synhédrin</i>, chap. <small>XIX</small>, § 2. That which constitutes the difference between this penalty and that of <i>extirpation</i> is that the sins which entail this latter penalty are not expiated by earthly death and are moreover punished beyond the grave.",
    "fn.3465": "For example, when one swears to affirm an undeniable truth, or to undertake a materially impossible action; such an oath is prohibited by the third commandment of the Decalogue: <i>You shall not utter the name of the Lord your God in vain</i> (Exodus, <small>XX</small>, 7). Cf. Maimonides, <i>Sépher miçwôth</i>, negative precepts, n° 62.",
    "fn.3466": "That is, the designation of one animal in place of another animal that has been previously designated as a victim; the one who would offer to substitute a good victim for a bad one already designated would commit a punishable sin. See Leviticus, chapter <small>XXVII</small>, <i>v.</i> 10, and below, chapter <small>XLVI</small> (fol. 103 <i>a</i> of the Ar. text, l. 13-15).",
    "fn.3441": "Consult the Babylonian Talmud, <i>l. c.</i>, fol. 47 <i>b:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">נודע מי הכהו אפילו אחד בסוף העולם לא היו עורפין</span>; <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book XI, treatise <i>Rotze'ah</i> (concerning the murderer), chap. <small>IX</small>, §§ 11 and 12. — In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">שיעמד</span> is superfluous, and, conversely, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">או שפחה</span> are wanting; the reading of the MSS. conforms to the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי אחר שיאמר אדם ואפילו אשה ואפילו שפחה פלוני הרגו</span>.",
    "fn.3451": "See Exodus, chap. XXI, v. 37, and cf. II Samuel, chap. XII, v. 6.",
    "fn.3469": "Verbatim: <i>the warning, for them, cannot be imagined;</i> that is to say, one cannot admit that the guilty party could have received a warning ere committing the sin, which consists merely in words. It is known that, according to traditional law, no criminal may be punished unless he has been warned by witnesses, before committing the crime, of the chastisement which awaited him. Consult Mishnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, chap. <small>V</small>, § 1; Maimonides, <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book XIV, treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, chap. <small>XII</small>, § 2. In the Babylonian Talmud, same treatise, fol. 40 <i>b</i> to 41 <i>a</i>, an endeavour is made to connect this traditional law of warning to certain Biblical texts.",
    "fn.3452": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">שמנהגם</span>, which is simply a printing error; the manuscripts have, in accordance with the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">שגניבתם</span>.",
    "fn.3450": "Ibn-Tibbon translates from the Hebrew and the sin <span dir=\"rtl\"> </span>; the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">al-ḥaʿrī </span> is rendered by two words, and <span dir=\"rtl\">min </span> is a scribal error for <span dir=\"rtl\">mīn </span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">all that is of the kind of harm, etc. </span>.",
    "fn.3493": "That is, if each scholar had been allowed to modify the provisions of the law, according to the circumstances of the moment.",
    "fn.3492": "Literally: <i>the law remained one, and one conducted oneself always and in every circumstance in accordance with this one.</i>",
    "fn.3476": "See Leviticus, chap. <small>VII</small>, <i>v.</i> 16-21; chap. <small>XIX</small>, <i>v.</i> 5-8. By <span dir=\"rtl\">נותר</span> <i>remaining</i>, one understands the flesh which remains from a sacrifice, not having been eaten within the legal time-limit. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">פיגול</span>, <i>abomination, impurity</i>, by which the text of Leviticus does but qualify the <i>remaining</i> of the sacrifice (<small>VII</small>, 18; <small>XIX</small>, 7), denots, according to rabbinical tradition, the sacrifice profaned by thought; that is to say, that which has been offered with a profane intention, as, for example, with the intention of eating the portions destined for the altar, or of reserving the flesh, to eat it after the legal time-limit. See Maimonides, <i>Sépher miçwôth</i>, negative precepts, No. 132, where the Talmudic passages relating to this subject are likewise cited.",
    "fn.3462": "The author means that, whenever the text of the Mosaic law pronounces the penalty of <i>extirpation</i>, the judges, according to tradition, apply only the penalty of scourging, even though the crime might doubtless merit a far more severe punishment. Indeed, all Jewish doctors, both Karaite and Rabbinite, declare, on the authority of ancient traditions, that the penalty of extirpation (<span dir=\"rtl\">כרת</span>) fell not within the purview of human jurisdiction, and that the legislator thereby intended a grievous chastisement from heaven (vide <i>Palestine</i>, p. 215 <i>a</i>). Those who had rendered themselves guilty of a crime against which the law decrees the penalty of extirpation were, according to Rabbinic tradition, to suffer no other earthly punishment than that of scourging. Consult Mishnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Maccôth</i>, chap. <small>III</small>, § 15: <span dir=\"rtl\">כל חייבי כריתות שלקו נפטרו ידי כריתתם</span>. — The words <span dir=\"rtl\">והו אלצׄרב באלסוט</span>, which are found in all Arabic MSS., are absent from the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, and indeed from several MSS. of this selfsame version; unintelligent copyists doubtless deemed them superfluous or misplaced, the <i>penalty of scourging</i> properly so called being mentioned immediately thereafter. Nevertheless, several MSS. of Ibn-Tibbon's version, as also the commentary of Ephodi, expressly state: <span dir=\"rtl\">מעלת חיוב כרת והוא ההכאה ברציעה</span>.",
    "fn.3464": "That is to say, transgressions which consist solely in words and in which no act is committed belong to this 4<sup>th</sup> category and occasion no penalty, with the exception of the three transgressions which the author is about to enumerate and which, whilst consisting merely in words, entail the penalty of <i>scourging.</i> Concerning this 4<sup>th</sup> category and the three exceptions, consult Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Maccôth</i>, fol. 16 <i>a</i>; treatise <i>Shebu'ot</i>, fol. 21 <i>a</i>, and treatise <i>Temurah</i>, fol. 3 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3558": "The author uses the term <i>loulab</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לולב</span>) to refer to the entire bundle composed of four plants. See Leviticus, chapter <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 40; this Chaldean term properly designates: <i>the palm branch</i> that is part of the four plants. See <i>Palestine</i>, p. 188.",
    "fn.3559": "See vol. I, Introduction, p. 15, note 1. In the Midrash, <i>Wayyikra rabba</i>, section 30 (fol. 171, col. 1), several allegorical interpretations of the four plants are given, and it is to these allegories that the author is alluding here. Cf. Isaac Arâma, ’<i>Akédâ</i>, chapter 67 (edit. of Pressburg, in-8°, t. III, fol. 124 <i>b</i> to 126 <i>b</i>).",
    "fn.3491": "See the Arabic text of this Introduction in Pococke's <i>Porta Mosis</i> (edition of 1655), pp. 27-28. Maimonides, after having spoken of the faculty possessed by the true prophet to abolish momentarily certain provisions of the law, adds: <span dir=\"rtl\">לכן בשרט אן לא יאבד דׄלך אלאמר ויקול אן אללה אטד בהדׄא אן יפעל בדׄא אלי גאבר אלדהר ואנטא יאטר בה בחסב חאלה̈ מא פי וקת דון וקת … שבה מא יפעל בית דין פי הוראת שעה</span> “upon the condition, however, that he pretend not to issue a perpetual precept and that he say not that God has ordained to act thus forevermore; on the contrary, (he must declare) that he gives this prescription solely in view of a certain momentary circumstance…. as the tribunal does in the <i>temporary decision.</i>” — Ibn-Tibbon, misled doubtless by an orthographical error which his ms. possessed, has here confounded the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">צדר</span>, <i>Introduction</i>, with the Hebrew word <span dir=\"rtl\">סדר</span>, and has translated: <span dir=\"rtl\">בסדר פירוש המשנה</span>. Al-’Harîzi has more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">בפתיחת</span>.",
    "fn.3449": "Word for word: <i>which shall be heard by word of mouth;</i> that is to say, the exposition of which must be delivered orally, and which one cannot commit unto a book. The author doubtless professed upon this subject an opinion which he dared to make known only unto his friends, fearing lest it should be ill interpreted. Perchance he intended to signify that the Rabbins, moved by humanity, did soften the ancient law of retaliation, and caused their interpretation thereof to pass as a tradition ascending even unto Moses himself. The commentators have endeavoured to explain this passage in a sense less offensive to the orthodox, according to whom the traditional interpretation of the Mosaic laws must be regarded as the sole true one. According to Moses of Narbonne, the author would imply that the law of retaliation is admitted literally by the Talmudists themselves, whenever its execution does not endanger the life of the culprit. According to Schem-Tob, the author intended to say that the Talmudic interpretation applies only to him who should have acted without premeditation or involuntarily, whereas the law of retaliation was to be executed literally when the crime was premeditated. Nevertheless, Schem-Tob does so little approve of this view, that he concluds his gloss with these words: <span dir=\"rtl\">והשם יכפר בעדו ובעדינו</span>, “may God pardon him, and us.” — The words <span dir=\"rtl\">דראי ׳יסמע שפאהא</span>, <i>an opinion which ought to be heard by word of mouth</i>, have been translated by Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">דעת יׅשָמע פנים בפנים</span>; Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, Append., p. 158) translates according to the sense: <span dir=\"rtl\">דעת אומר אותה פה אל פה</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates somewhat differently: <span dir=\"rtl\">סברא אשמיעך אותה פה אל פה</span>, <i>an opinion which I shall cause thee to hear by word of mouth</i>, and it is also in this sense that Buxtorf understood Ibn-Tibbon's version, which he renders thus: “Licet habeam etiam quod dicam de sententiis Talmudis, <i>quod autem coram ex me audies</i>.” If one were to admit this translation, the author would here address himself, as in several other passages, to his disciple Joseph, to whom he dedicated his work; but such a supposition is inadmissible, for Maimonides was then settled in old Cairo, and Joseph had fixed his abode in Aleppo, and they no longer communicated save by correspondence. Cf. vol. II, p. 183, note 5.",
    "fn.3593": "The author intends to speak of the two <i>cherubim</i>, symbolic figures, placed above the holy ark (see <i>Palestine</i>, p. 157), and which, according to him, represented the <i>Separate</i> Intelligences or the <i>angels</i> properly so called. If, in another place (above, chap. <small>III</small>), the author identifies Ezekiel's cherubim with the ’<i>hayyôth</i> or celestial spheres, he means only that these latter also have received the name of <i>cherub</i>, because all beings exercising a certain influence upon the earth and entrusted with a divine mission are denominated <i>angels</i>. See chap. <small>VI</small> of the II<sup>nd</sup> part, p. 68.",
    "fn.3600": "See Mishnah, V<sup>olume </sup>part, tractate <i>Middôth</i>, chapter <small>III</small>, section 4: 'Iron was created to shorten human life, while the altar was created to prolong it; it is not fitting that what shortens life should be brought onto what prolongs it.'",
    "fn.3606": "That is to say, even by priests stained with a legal impurity, or who are in a state of uncleanliness, having their hair or garments dishevelled, or having neglected to wash themselves. Vide Leviticus, chap. <small>X</small>, <i>v.</i> 6; Exodus, chap. <small>XXX</small>, <i>v.</i> 19-21; <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book III, treatise <i>Biath ha-Mikdasch</i>, chap. <small>V</small>. — In Ibn-Tibbon’s version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">בעת האנינות וכל מי שלא רחץ גופן</span> constitute a double translation of the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי חאל שעתׄ</span>; by writing <span dir=\"rtl\">בעת האנינות</span>, <i>in a moment of mourning</i>, Ibn-Tibbon doubtless had in mind Aaron and his sons, who were forbidden during their mourning to have dishevelled hair and torn garments (Levit., <small>X</small>, 6); yet this prohibition is taken by traditional law in a more general sense. Vide Maimonides, <i>Sépher Miçwôth</i>, negative precepts, nos. <sup>os</sup> 163 and 164. Concerning the meaning of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">שעתׄ</span>, cf. above, p. 254, note 1.",
    "fn.3630": "See above, chapter <small>XXX</small>, p. 244, note 4.",
    "fn.3629": "See Spencer, <i>De legibus ritualibus Hebrœorum</i>, book III, Dissert. VIII, chap. <small>VII</small>: <i>Expiatio judaica cur Hircis prœcipue prœstita</i> (Cambridge edition, p. 1015).",
    "fn.3628": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">תועבה</span>, which signifies <i>abomination</i>, must be taken, in this passage, in the sense of <i>divinity, object of worship</i>. The sacred writers, lest they should profane the names of the Divinity, often employ, when speaking of pagan deities, terms of contempt, such as <span dir=\"rtl\">תועבה</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">שקוץ</span>, <i>abomination</i>. Vide, for instance, 1 Kings, chap. <small>XI</small>, <i>v</i>. 5 and 7; 2 Kings, chap. <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 13; Isaiah, chap. <small>XLIV</small>, <i>v</i>. 19. It is in the same sense that Onkelos, in his Chaldaic paraphrase on the passage from Exodus, renders the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תועבה</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">בעירא דמצראי דחלין לה</span>, “the animal to which the Egyptians render worship” (cf. the same paraphrase to Genesis, chap. <small>XLIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 32); but one knows not where Maimonides has found that Onkelos speaks of the <i>constellation of the ram</i>, adored by the Egyptians, unless he has attributed this sense to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בעירא</span>. — The second citation, which is found in all the Arabic MSS., is lacking in the versions of Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.3621": "See Exodus, chap. <small>XXVIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 28; Talmud of Babylon, <i>l. c.</i>; <i>Sépher Miçwôth</i>, <i>ibid.</i>, n° 87; <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, <i>l. c.</i>, chap. <small>IX</small>, § 10. — On the Ephod and the pectoral, see <i>Palestine</i>, p. 176.",
    "fn.3617": "See Exodus, chapter <small>XXX</small>, <i>verse</i> 22-23.",
    "fn.3618": "That is, by the decrees of His Providence, which is from all eternity. The Hebrew words <span dir=\"rtl\">עצות מרחוק</span> are borrowed from Isaiah, chap. <small>XXV</small>, <i>v.</i> 1.",
    "fn.3615": "See Leviticus, chapter I, verses 6 to 9, and passim.",
    "fn.3616": "See Mishnah, Vth part, tractate Tamid, chapter III, § 8.",
    "fn.3603": "Cf. Mishnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, chap. <small>VII</small>, § 6; Babylonian Talmud, same treatise, fol. 106 <i>a</i>. It results from the book of Numbers, chap. <small>XXV</small>, <i>v.</i> 3, that the worship of Baal-Peor or Phegor, god of the Moabites, was celebrated by means of obscenities. Cf. Hosea, chap. <small>IX</small>, <i>v.</i> 10, and the commentary of Saint Jerome upon this verse: \"But they, brought out of Egypt, committed fornication with the Midianites, and went in unto Beelphegor, the idol of the Moabites, whom we may call Priapus.\"<i>",
    "fn.3718": "That is, in the place of pilgrimage, or in Jerusalem. The second tithe could be redeemed; but the money had to be spent in the place where the central sanctuary was located. See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XIV</small>, <i>v</i>. 25 and 26. On tithes, see <i>Palestine</i>, p. 172.",
    "fn.3719": "See above, chapter <small>XXXIX</small>, p. 298.",
    "fn.3720": "See above, chap. <small>XXXVII</small>, p. 291, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1.",
    "fn.3721": "See above, chapter <small>XXXV</small>, p. 272.",
    "fn.3682": "Vide Numbers, chap. <small>XV</small>, <i>v</i>. 27. According to tradition, the entire passage in Numbers treats particularly of the sin of idolatry; vide Talmud, treatise <i>Horayôth</i>, fol. 8 <i>a:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">וכי תשגו ולא תעשו את כל המצות האלה איזו היא מצוה שהיא שקולה ככל המצות הוי אומר זו ע״א</span>. Cf. Maimonides, Commentary on the Mishnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, Introduction to the treatise <i>Horayôth</i>. In this respect, there is no difference between the high priest, the prince, and any private individual. All, according to tradition, offer a she-goat for the sin of idolatry. Vide Mishnah, same treatise, chap. <small>II</small>, §6: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובע״א היחיד והנשיא והמשיח מביאין שעירה</span>.",
    "fn.3780": "See Exodus, chap. <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 17-19; chap. <small>XXXIV</small>, <i>v.</i> 25-26. In these two passages, as well as in a third, Deuter., ch. <small>XIV</small>, <i>v.</i> 21, the law forbids one to <i>boil a kid in its mother’s milk</i>. The oral law discerns therein a more general prohibition against the use of the flesh of quadrupeds cooked with dairy produce; and already the Chaldaic version of Onkelos renders in the three passages: <span dir=\"rtl\">לא תיכלון בשר בחלב</span>, <i>ye shall not eat meat boiled in milk</i>. One perceives that this interpretation ascends to a very early period, and Maimonides places it not in doubt. Philo, however, taking the biblical text literally, discerns therein a precept of humanity, similar to the prohibition against slaying the mother and her young on the selfsame day. See <i>Philonis Opera</i>, Geneva edition, 1613, <i>de Charitate</i>, p. 549. Ibn-Ezra indicates in a doubtful manner the same reason (<span dir=\"rtl\">אולי היה אכזריות לב וגו׳</span>), and he adds that meat boiled in milk may have been prohibited generally, because such milk might perchance be that of the mother of the animal which furnished the flesh.",
    "fn.3783": "Ibn-Tibbon’s version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">and they which thou shalt know from them is the mother of the child</span>, which is a contradiction; Al-’Harîzi translates better: <span dir=\"rtl\">and it will be apparent from them the mother from the womb</span>.",
    "fn.3733": "See Numbers, chap. <small>XIX</small>, and cf. <i>Palestine</i>, p. 162. According to tradition, the ashes of the red heifer were exceeding rare; from Moses until Ezra, but two heifers had been burnt, and from Ezra until the destruction of the Second Temple, five or seven. See Mishnah, Sixth<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Parâ</i>, chap. <small>III</small>, § 5. One could procure only at great expense a heifer entirely suitable for this ceremony. See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Kiddushin</i>, fol. 31 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3809": "The author, it would appear, intends to speak of the case in which the wife might have had an assignation after the jealous husband had lodged his complaint of adultery; for, in this case, says the Talmud, it no longer devolved upon the husband to withdraw his complaint, even should the wife succeed in convincing him of her perfect purity and innocence. Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Sôtâ</i>, fol. 25 <i>a:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">בעל שמחל על קינויו ⋯ שמע מינה לאחר סתירה אינו מחול</span>. Cf. <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book <small>IV</small>, treatise <i>Sôtâ</i>, chap. <small>I</small>, § 7: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל אם מחל אחר שתסתר אינו יבול למחול</span>. According to Ibn-Tibbon's version, our passage would not speak of the husband who repents of his complaint, but rather of the accused wife, who would fain redeem herself, by the greatest sacrifices, from the ignominious act to which she must submit. This explanation, in truth, is more rational, but it does not accord with the construction of the Arabic text. Thus has Al-’Harîzi translated this passage: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי אלו תהיה נקייה ויהיה בוטח בה היו פודים נפשם רוב בני אדם בכל ממונם אבל היו בוחרים המות על זאת החרפה הגדולה</span>. It is in this sense that one has deemed it proper to translate.",
    "fn.3811": "Literally: <i>by this fear, therefore, we have cut off serious illnesses;</i> that is to say, through the terror that the ceremony was meant to inspire in the woman and her husband, a ceremony so humiliating, we have prevented the woman from giving rise to suspicions and the husband from succumbing to fits of jealousy.",
    "fn.3881": "Ibn-Tibbon has the singular: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשר כלל אותם זה הכלל</span>; but all the Arabic mss. have the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">אלגׄמל</span>, and indeed, the author is speaking here of all fourteen classes.",
    "fn.3882": "Ibn-Tibbon omitted the word <span dir=\"rtl\">achad</span> in his version; the author wants to speak of certain commandments that he has entirely passed over in silence and the particularities of certain other commandments.",
    "fn.3944": "The author, through an error of memory, wrote <span dir=\"rtl\">אתך</span>, as the mss. ar. and hébr. of the <i>Guide;</i> the text of Isaiah has <span dir=\"rtl\">עמך</span>.",
    "fn.3943": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ינדאה</span> should be pronounced <span dir=\"rtl\">يُبْدِأُ</span> , from the root <span dir=\"rtl\">نبأ</span> , in the sense of <i>commovit, agitavit, supervenit</i>.",
    "fn.3880": "All manuscripts bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי וצׄעוה אולאיך לעבאדתהא</span>, so that the pronoun <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי</span> and the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">וצׄעוה</span> can grammatically relate only to the single word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלנצב</span>. As for the suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">לעבאדתהא</span>, it can relate only to an implied expression, as, for instance, <span dir=\"rtl\">עבודה זרה</span>; Ibn-Tibbon substituted <span dir=\"rtl\">לעברת אלהיהם</span>. It would have been more correct to say: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתי וצׄועהא אולאיך לעבאדתהם</span>.",
    "fn.3997": "Most mss. have: <span dir=\"rtl\">כיף הו</span>, and similarly Ibn-Falaquéra (<i>l. c.</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">איך הוא</span>. This masculine pronoun refers to each of the two words <span dir=\"rtl\">איגׄאד</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">תרביר</span>. Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi have <span dir=\"rtl\">איך היא</span>, and according to them, the pronoun refers to <span dir=\"rtl\">ענאיתה</span>.",
    "fn.3730": "It is stated, in all passages which treat of these impurities, that the person concerned, even after having purified oneself, shall remain impure until the evening: <span dir=\"rtl\">וטמא עד הערב</span>. Vide especially Leviticus, ch. <small>XXII</small>, <i>v</i>. 6-7, and Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Schabbath</i>, fol. 14 <i>b:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">טבול יום דאוריתא הוא דכתיב ובא השמש וטהר</span>; <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book X, treatise <i>Aboth ha-toumoth</i> (of the principal impurities), chap. X, § 1, and book VIII, treatise <i>Biath ha-mikdasch</i> (of entry into the sanctuary), chap. <small>III</small>, § 14.",
    "fn.3425": "Literally: <i>as the rebels reproach us</i>, as Al-’Harîzi indeed translates, <span dir=\"rtl\">במו הדבה שיוציאו עלינו המורדים</span>. Ibn-Tibbon substitutes <span dir=\"rtl\">הצדוקים</span>, <i>the Sadducees.</i> I believe the author is speaking of certain Karaite commentators.<i>",
    "fn.3927": "Cf. vol. II, chapter <small>12</small> (p. 100 and following), and chapter <small>37</small>.",
    "fn.3429": "On the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄבר צדעהא</span>, see above, p. 276, note 2.",
    "fn.3426": "See Leviticus, chapter XX</small>, <i>v.</i> 15 and 16.",
    "fn.3998": "Literally: <i>The conduct of this man will be… to aim always</i>, <i>etc</i>. Ibn-Tibbon has slightly abridged this phrase by attaching it, as a sequel, to the preceding phrase: <span dir=\"rtl\">וללכת אחרי ההשגה ההיא בדרכים וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.3810": "See Numbers, chapter <small>V</small>, <i>v.</i> 18, and Mishnah, III<sup>e</sup> part, tractate <i>Sôtâ</i>, chapter <small>I</small>, §§ 5-7; <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, <i>l. c.</i>, chapter <small>III</small>, §§ 3-11.",
    "fn.3291": "See the <i>Eight Chapters</i>, serving as an Introduction to the treatise <i>Aboth</i>, chap. <small>IV</small>, towards the end.",
    "fn.3301": "See Deutér., 6, 13; X, 20; Lévit., 19, 12; Exode, 20, 7.",
    "fn.3292": "Cf. above, chapter <small>VIII</small>, p. 51, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 3.",
    "fn.3298": "Word for word: <i>s'il ne leur est pas donné de grandeur dans les âmes</i> (<i>of men</i>). Ibn-Tibbon translates, according to the sense: <span dir=\"rtl\">if they were not to be great in the eyes of men and esteemed</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates more literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">if they were not to have a generation in souls</span>.",
    "fn.3402": "For the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">زماذة</span>, see above, ch. <small>XII</small>, p. 67, note 1.",
    "fn.3928": "Literally: 'thou hast the choice if thou wilt strengthen and <i>thicken</i> this bond, thou canst do so; and if thou wilt weaken and <i>attenuate</i> it little by little until it be broken, thou canst do so.' Ibn-Tibbon has not rendered, in his version, the words <i>'thicken'</i> and <i>'attenuate'</i>, and he has been criticised therefor by Ibn-Falaquéra, who deems these two verbs necessary to complete the image. Vide <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 133, and p. 158, last note.",
    "fn.3293": "More literally: <i>the transgressions of man against his neighbour and the transgressions of man against God</i>. These words are borrowed from the Mishnah, II<sup>nd</sup> part, treatise <i>Yomâ</i>, chap. <small>VIII</small>, § 9. In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מצות</span> has been substituted for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עבירות</span>, and the order of the two classes is inverted therein; the manuscripts of this version, as well as Al-’Harîzi’s version, conform to the Arabic text.",
    "fn.3401": "Vide Exodus, chap. <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 26 and 27, and confer Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Kiddouschin</i>, fol. 24 <i>a.</i> By <i>Canaanite</i> slave, one generally understands the foreign or heathen slave. Concerning the benevolent and humane laws prescribed by the Hebrew legislator regarding slaves, vide <i>Palestine</i>, pp. 208-209; these laws amounted almost to the abolition of slavery.",
    "fn.3409": "See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>IV</small>, <i>v.</i> 8, and cf. above, chapter <small>XXVI</small>, p. 203.",
    "fn.3405": "Word for word: <i>a duty is imposed upon thee on his behalf</i>, or <i>an obligation</i>, <i>namely</i>, <i>etc.</i> All the MSS. read <span dir=\"rtl\">מלומא</span>, in the accusative; but one apprehends that <span dir=\"rtl\">מלזם</span> should be read, in the nominative, as the subject of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ילזמד</span>, which one considers a neutral verb, of the first form <span dir=\"rtl\">(يَلْزَمُكَ)</span>. The version of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">שאתה חייב לעיין בתיקוניו</span>, is somewhat abridged here; Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">תתחייב לו דבר אחר והוא לתקן צרכיו</span>.",
    "fn.3926": "It appears evident unto one that here, as in several other passages of these latter chapters, Maimonides has taken as his model the citizen of the ideal State, of whom Al-Farabi has drawn the portrait in his treatise <i>On the Principles of Beings</i> (vide <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, pp. 344-349), and the philosopher presented by Ibn-Bâdja in his <i>Regimen of the Solitary</i> (<i>ibid.</i>, pp. 388-409). Ibn-Bâdja says, among other things, concerning his philosopher (<i>ibid.</i>, p. 402): 'The solitary shall remain pure from the contact of his fellows; for it is his duty not to associate with the <i>material</i> man, nor even with him whose sole aim is the absolute <i>spiritual</i>, but his duty is, on the contrary, to associate with men of science. Now, as men of science, though numerous in certain places, are few in number in certain others, and sometimes even altogether lacking, it is the duty of the <i>solitary</i>, in certain places, to withdraw himself entirely from men, as much as is possible, and to mingle with them only for necessary matters and in the necessary measure.' In the two works which one has just indicated, several features are borrowed from Plato's <i>Republic</i> and Aristotle's <i>Ethics</i>. Maimonides, according to his custom, applies unto these portraits verses from Holy Scripture and passages borrowed from the ancient Rabbins.",
    "fn.3897": "The verb which one has transcribed <span dir=\"rtl\">(تَجَرَّؤُوا) תגראוא</span> is written in the MSS. <span dir=\"rtl\">תגׄרוא</span>, and in some <span dir=\"rtl\">תגרוא</span> (the <span dir=\"rtl\">ג</span> without a point). One believes that it is the V<sup>th</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">جَرُّو</span>, signifying: strenuum, audacem et animosum se ostendit. As for the second verb, the MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ודלוהם</span>; one believes that one ought to read <span dir=\"rtl\">وذكروهم</span>, 'and they humbled them' or 'debased them,'</i> although the dictionaries do not attribute unto the first form, <span dir=\"rtl\">ذל</span>, the active sense. Perchance one ought to write <span dir=\"rtl\">וֹהלוהם</span>, or <span dir=\"rtl\">ואהלהם</span>, in the II<sup>nd</sup> or IV<sup>th</sup> form. Ibn-Tibbon renders it: <span dir=\"rtl\">ושהם הכניעו בני עשו והשפילום</span>; Al-’Harîzi has: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכי הם גברו בבני עשו והכניעום</span>.",
    "fn.3418": "See the passages cited in note 3.",
    "fn.3419": "That is, one is always responsible for such damage, even if it occurs in a public place.",
    "fn.3408": "One must remember that, in the passage of Exodus that has just been cited, it is a matter of the murderer, who must be handed over to <i>the avenger of blood</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">the redeemer of blood</span>, <i>the one who takes vengeance for blood</i>), that is, the closest relative of the victim charged with avenging his murdered parent by killing the assassin. See <i>Palestine</i>, p. 217-218.",
    "fn.3414": "See Exodus, chapter <small>XXII</small>, <i>v.</i> 5; chapter <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 33.",
    "fn.3347": "See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XVIII</small>, verse <i>10</i>.",
    "fn.3413": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">והפטׄה</span> has not been translated by Ibn-Tibbon or Al-’Harîzi. The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">חראסתה</span> and in <span dir=\"rtl\">חפטׄח</span> refers to the thing causing the damage and should also be understood as the subject of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יאדׄי</span>.",
    "fn.3422": "That is, the price that a master of an animal must pay in general for having killed a slave is equivalent to half the price that would be paid for a human being of any kind if one had vowed to consecrate its value to the sanctuary. See Leviticus, chap. <small>XXVII</small>, <i>v.</i> 2-7.",
    "fn.3410": "Ibn-Tibbon's version has <span dir=\"rtl\">הסכלים</span>, and Al-’Harîzi's version <span dir=\"rtl\">הכסילים</span>, <i>the ignorant</i> or <i>the foolish</i>; but I believe that by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">الجاهلية</span> , one must understand here the ancient Arabs, to whose customs the author is clearly alluding in what follows. Cf. t. II, p. 260, note 2.",
    "fn.3415": "That is to say, they admit of exceptions full of equity, partly inscribed in the Law, and partly traditional. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">וכלל</span>, in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, is a mere misprint, and the MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">וכללוּ</span>; Al-’Harîzi renders it <span dir=\"rtl\">ונכלל באלה הדינים</span>. However, the Arabic verb <span dir=\"rtl\">צִיּוֹן</span> does not appear here to bear the sense of <i>to enclose</i>, <i>to contain</i>, but rather that of <i>to entrust to someone</i>, <i>to impose the duty of performing an action.</i>",
    "fn.3326": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלכׄואץ</span> signifies <i>particularities</i> or <i>properties</i>, and refers here to the supposed secret forces that magicians made act; this word was translated in the Ibn-Tibbon version as <span dir=\"rtl\">הסגולות והכחות המיוחדות</span>.",
    "fn.3327": "In editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">and to exalt</span> must be erased, as it is unnecessary and not found in manuscripts of this version.",
    "fn.3346": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">יחתאלוא</span> means literally: <i>they employ a ruse</i> or <i>a means;</i> Ibn-Tibbon translates it with two verbs: <span dir=\"rtl\">they contrived and did a cunning thing</span>. The particle <span dir=\"rtl\">אן</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(إِنَّ)</span> , which precedes the verb, is explicative, and has here the sense of the Latin adverb <i>utique;</i> Ibn-Tibbon did not render this particle.",
    "fn.3325": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">והמרעה</span> is missing in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, but it is found in the manuscripts and in the princeps edition <i>princeps</i>.",
    "fn.3324": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تخيل</span> (V<sup>th</sup> form), which is not found in the dictionaries, is derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">חילה̈</span>, \"ruse, artifice,\" and signifies \"to invent an artifice.\" Manuscript No. 63 of the Hebrew supplement alone has <span dir=\"rtl\">יכׄילוא</span> (for <span dir=\"rtl\">يخيلون</span>), and likewise Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">כל מה שיחשבו עובדי עבודה זרה</span>, \"all that the worshippers of false gods have imagined.\" In that which follows, the manuscripts of Ibn-Tibbon’s version read, more accurately than the printed editions: <span dir=\"rtl\">לחזק עבודתה ולהעמידה לעד ומביא בני אדם לחשוב וגו׳</span>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">בדפע ··· וגׄלב</span>, certain manuscripts bear: <span dir=\"rtl\">אנהא תדפע</span> ··· <span dir=\"rtl\">והגׄלב</span>; similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">שהם דוחים</span> ··· <span dir=\"rtl\">ומביאים</span>.",
    "fn.3434": "See above, p. 304, note 3.",
    "fn.3337": "See <i>Mischné Torâ</i> or the Abbreviated Talmud, Book I, Treatise <i>Abodâ Zara</i>, Chapter <small>XII</small>, Section 7.",
    "fn.3435": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon is approximately <span dir=\"rtl\">the redeemer</span>; that of Al-’Harîzi reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">for in this you will calm the sorrowful soul</span>.",
    "fn.3430": "According to the author, <i>desire</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">תאוה</span>) consists in directing one's thought upon that which belongs to another, without employing any means whatsoever to possess it; whereas <i>covetousness</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">חמידה</span>) consists in placing oneself in possession of another's property, albeit by employing legal means. Vide <i>Sefer Mitzvot,</i> negative precepts, n<sup>os</sup> 265 and 266, and the passages from the <i>Mekhilta</i> cited therein; <i>Mishneh Torah,</i> Book XI, treatise <i>Gezeilah va-Avedah</i> (concerning rapine and lost things), chap. <small>I,</small> §§ 9-11. — The distinction drawn by the Rabbins between <i>desire</i> and <i>covetousness</i> is founded upon the tenth commandment, which, in Deuteronomy (v, 21), is expressed by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לא תתאוה,</span> and in Exodus (<small>XX,</small> 17) by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לא תחמד.</span>",
    "fn.3304": "On the sense of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">هسرع</span>, see above, p. 258, note 4. The ms. n° 63 of the suppl. hébr. has here <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלתצׄרע</span>, and it is this reading that seems to have been followed by the two Hebrew translators; Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">ולהתחנן לו</span>, and Al-’Harîzi <span dir=\"rtl\">ולשַׁוע</span>.",
    "fn.3433": "That is, to retire to one of the six cities of refuge. See Exodus, chap. <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 13; Numbers, chap. <small>XXXV</small>, <i>v.</i> 11-28; Deuteronomy, chap. <small>IV</small>, <i>v.</i> 41-43, and chap. <small>XIX</small>, <i>v.</i> 2-10; Joshua, chap. <small>XX</small>.",
    "fn.3335": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלותראת</span>, which one renders as <i>tendons</i>, is exceedingly doubtful. Several MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">אלבתׄראת</span> (plural of <span dir=\"rtl\">بئر</span>, pustula parva, tuberculum), and this, doubtless, was the reading adopted by Ibn-Tibbon, who translates this word by <span dir=\"rtl\">המכות</span>, and in some MSS. by <span dir=\"rtl\">הענבות</span>, a word which, like the Arabic <span dir=\"rtl\">عنب</span>, signifies likewise <i>pustule, tumour</i> (cf. Buxtorf, <i>lex. talmud.</i>, s.v. <span dir=\"rtl\">ענבתא</span>). MS. No. 18 of Leyden has <span dir=\"rtl\">אלרכב</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">רُקִבَ</span>, pubes, muliebria, or <span dir=\"rtl\">רُקַבֻּ</span>, plural of <span dir=\"rtl\">רُקְבָּהּ</span>, genu); this was the reading possessed by Al-’Harîzi, who renders thus: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולהקטיר בחומץ ובמרקשיתא לצמח הברכים הקשה</span>.",
    "fn.3436": "On this ceremony that the ancient men of a city were to observe in the vicinity of which a person had been found murdered, see Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 1 to 8; cf. <i>Palestine</i>, p. 161 <i>b.</i>",
    "fn.3440": "On the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קראין</span>, see vol. II, p. 296, note 3.",
    "fn.3443": "That is to say, even if there were not enough evidence for the court to pronounce a sentence of condemnation.",
    "fn.3437": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">סאיר,</span> which is read in most MSS., one of our MSS. has <span dir=\"rtl\">עאבר,</span> and another <span dir=\"rtl\">סאיל.</span> This latter reading has been reproduced by Ibn-Tibbon, who translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולתייר כל שואל דרך,</span> '<i>and to examine all those who inquired the way.</i>' One knows not whence Ibn-Tibbon derived the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ולתייר;</span> all MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">ותגפיר,</span> '<i>and to protect;</i>' moreover, the sense of his translation is obscure. Al-Ḥarīzī translats more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולשמור כל עוברי דרך.</span>",
    "fn.3444": "The text reads: <i>dans lequel on brise la nuque à une jeune vache.</i>",
    "fn.3442": "Word for word: <i>While they would take God to witness that they do not know Him;</i> that is, while the inhabitants of the city, through the mouth of the elders, would protest that the murderer is completely unknown to them.",
    "fn.3336": "More literally: <i>and are connected to the manner of purging through purgatives</i>. Ibn-Tibbon paraphrased these words as follows: <span dir=\"rtl\">ונוהג מנהג שלשול הסמנין המשלשלים</span>; Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ונשאר בכלל הרפואות המשלשלות</span>.",
    "fn.3446": "See above, p. 270, note 1.",
    "fn.3438": "The author seems to allude to the words of the Mishnah, III<sup>rd</sup> part, treatise <i>Sotah,</i> chap. <small>IX,</small> § 6, where the verse of Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXI,</small> <i>v.</i> 7, is thus explained: <span dir=\"rtl\">שלא בא לידינו ופטרנוהו בלא מזון ולא ראינוהו והנחנוהו בלא לויה</span> 'He (the murdered man) came not unto us, and consequently we could not dismiss him without provisions (so that he could not have been in peril upon the highway whilst seeking sustenance); neither did we even see him, and consequently we could not suffer him to depart without accompaniment.'",
    "fn.3447": "For <span dir=\"rtl\">Grihyä</span>, the version of Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">a part</span>, <i>a portion</i>. Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately <span dir=\"rtl\">and the judgment of all wondrous things</span>.",
    "fn.3470": "See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXV</small>, <i>verse</i> 3; Mishnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Maccôth</i>, chapter <small>III</small>, sections 10 and 11.",
    "fn.3445": "Consult Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXI</small>, <i>v</i>. 4: «And the elders of that city shall bring down the heifer unto a rough valley, which is neither eared nor sown.» Traditional law discerns in this passage a prohibition against ever transforming that place into a cultivated field, and it is in this sense that Maimonides here interprets the biblical text. Consult the Mishnah, treatise <i>Sotah</i>, chap. <small>IX</small>, § 5.",
    "fn.3472": "See above, p. 240, note 1, and cf. further on, chap. <small>XLVI</small> (Arabic text, fol. 104 <i>a</i>).",
    "fn.3334": "Even now, in certain regions, medicinal properties are attributed to <i>marcassite</i> or <i>pyrite</i>. See Déterville, <i>Dictionnaire d'Histoire naturelle</i>, article <i>Marcassite</i>. The Arabs also appear to designate by this name <i>bismuth</i>, formerly known as <i>glacial tin</i>.",
    "fn.3477": "See Exodus, chap. <small>XXXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 13-15.",
    "fn.3471": "Voy. Lévitique, chapter <small>VII</small>, <i>v.</i> 26 and 27, <i>and passim.</i>",
    "fn.3479": "See Exodus, chapter <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 15 and 17; Leviticus, chapter <small>XX</small>, <i>v.</i> 9.",
    "fn.3474": "The author wants to say that, in non-holocaust sacrifices and whose meat is eaten, either by the priests or by the owners, the fat has a distinct role, being alone destined to be burned on the altar, as a consecrated offering to God. Cf. my <i>Reflections on the cult of the ancient Hebrews</i> (in the fourth volume of M. Cahen's <i>Bible</i>), pp. 30-32.",
    "fn.3480": "See Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 18-21.",
    "fn.3481": "See above, chapter <small>XXXIII</small>, p. 262, note 2.",
    "fn.3456": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ופי מא אחתיט פי אכׁפאיה וציאנתה</span> are rendered, in the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, by <span dir=\"rtl\">ובדברים המוצנעים</span>; the mss. carry <span dir=\"rtl\">ובדברים המצויינים ונשמרים</span>. For <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא פי מא הו טׄאהר מכשוף</span> Ibn-Tibbon has only <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא בנגלה</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא במה שהוא גלוי ונראה</span>.",
    "fn.3475": "See Exodus, chap. <small>XII</small>, <i>v.</i> 15, and Leviticus, chap. <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 29; in this latter passage the expression <i>to mortify</i> or <i>to afflict one's person</i> signifies, according to the Talmud (treatise <i>Yôma</i>, fol. 74 <i>b</i>), <i>to deprive oneself of food, to fast;</i> cf. Isaiah, chap. <small>LVIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 3 and 5.",
    "fn.3485": "See previous page, note 2.",
    "fn.3439": "Vide Deuteronomy, <i>ibid.,</i> <i>vv.</i> 2 and 3. For <span dir=\"rtl\">תגׄריד,</span> which signifies here '<i>sortie</i>' or '<i>expedition,</i>' Ibn-Tibbon has employed the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ויציאת,</span> conformably to the biblical text <span dir=\"rtl\">ויצאו זקינך;</span> some Arabic MSS. have <span dir=\"rtl\">תחדׄיר,</span> '<i>admonition,</i>' a reading which does not here offer a suitable sense, yet has been reproduced in Al-Ḥarīzī's version: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכשנחקור ונזהיר הזקנים.</span> The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלקיאס,</span> which signifies '<i>the act of measuring,</i>' or '<i>measurement,</i>' relats to the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ומדדו</span> of the biblical text; Ibn-Tibbon's version bears <span dir=\"rtl\">והעומדים,</span> according to a faulty reading of certain Arabic MSS. which have <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלקואם</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(والقوّام)</span> . Al-Ḥarīzī has omitted this word.",
    "fn.3484": "All the Arabic manuscripts bear <span dir=\"rtl\">וישר מנהם</span>, and it appears evident to one that the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">וישר</span> is a neutral or passive verb: <i>to be strong</i> or <i>to be strengthened.</i> Ibn-Tibbon’s version has <span dir=\"rtl\">ויסמך אותם</span>, and that of Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ויעזור אותם</span>; according to these versions, one should translate: <i>and</i> (<i>who may</i>) <i>protect them.</i>",
    "fn.3486": "Ibn-Tibbon omitted from his version the word <span dir=\"rtl\">and from here</span>; Al-’Harîzi's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">At all times and in all places</span>. In one of the Arabic mss., one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">In every time and time</span>.",
    "fn.3482": "See Exodus, chap. <small>XXI</small>, <i>v</i>. 16; Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXIV</small>, <i>v.</i> 7. The reason why the abduction of a man is punished by death, the author does indicate by these words: <span dir=\"rtl\">לאנה יערץׄ בה ללמות</span>, <i>for he presents him</i> or <i>prepars him for death.</i> The author doubtless intends that he who abducts a man to make him a slave does in some sort doom him to death by depriving him of his liberty. It is in the same sense that Abravanel expresses himself in his Commentary on Deuteronomy (<small>XXIV</small>, 7): <span dir=\"rtl\">והטעם במצוה הזאת ירוע כי הגונב נפש הרי הוא כאלו הרגו כיון שהוציאו מרשותו ובחירתו</span>. According to Ibn-Caspi, the author intends that the abductor may be led to slay his victim in order to conceal his crime.",
    "fn.3455": "See Leviticus, chap. <small>V</small>, <i>v.</i> 24, where it is stated that he who, having rendered himself guilty of rapine or breach of trust, shall have aggravated his crime by denial and false oath, shall pay a fifth in excess of the value of the object he shall have misappropriated: <span dir=\"rtl\">וחמשתיו יסף עליו</span>. — Ibn-Tibbon here adds the explanatory words: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל על גזלהו אינו מוסיף כלום</span>, <i>but he adds naught</i> (by way of penalty) <i>for the thing carried off.</i> These words are found neither in the Arabic text, nor in Al-’Harîzi's version.",
    "fn.3487": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">קראין אחואל</span> signify <i>a conjunction of circumstances.</i> Cf. t. II, p. 296, note 3. The translation of Ibn-Tibbon, <span dir=\"rtl\">לפי הנראה מן הענינים</span>, is inaccurate.",
    "fn.3489": "Voy. Mischnâ, IV<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Abôth</i>, chapter <small>I</small>, § 1.",
    "fn.3494": "Literally: <i>with a high hand</i>, that is, publicly, in a manner that defies the gaze. See Numbers, chapter <small>XV</small>, <i>v.</i> 30.",
    "fn.3468": "The text states: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולו כאן דׄלך</span>, <i>if this were so;</i> that is to say, if it were with these transgressions as with those in which an act is committed. The Arabic expression being overly concise and obscure, Ibn-Tibbon rendered it by: <span dir=\"rtl\">מלקות</span> (read <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלו היה בו</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בהם</span>), <i>and if one were to punish them with scourging.</i>",
    "fn.3488": "Literally: <i>by means of things newly imagined by them with the aim of closing</i> (or <i>repairing</i>) <i>a fissure.</i> Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">בענינים שיחדשום לשמור התורה</span>; one perceives that he read <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשריעה̈</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלצדיעה̈</span>. But this latter reading is confirmed by Ibn-Falaquéra, who, in censuring the translation of Ibn-Tibbon, renders the words <span dir=\"rtl\">עלי ג̇ה̈ה̇ מר ט̇עריעה</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">על צד סתימת השבר</span>, and he adds that this Arabic locution possesses the same sense as the Hebrew expressions <span dir=\"rtl\">חִזּוּק הבֶדֶק</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">גדירת הפרץ</span>. See Append. of the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 158. Cf. above, p. 276, note 2.",
    "fn.3490": "Ibn-Tibbon translates <span dir=\"rtl\">ולהתיר קצת הנזהר</span>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">מחטׄוראתהא</span>, he appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">מחדׄוראתהא</span>, a reading which one of the mss. of the Imperial Library (suppl. n° 63) indeed possesses. The reading which one has adopted is that of almost all the mss., save that some have <span dir=\"rtl\">צׄ</span> (with point) instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">טׄ</span>, a very common orthographical error. The translation of Al-’Harîzi, <span dir=\"rtl\">שיתירו קצת האיסורין</span>, is in accord with one's reading. Cf. above, p. 271, note 1.",
    "fn.3497": "That is, although his error was sincere and he only erred due to his imperfect studies.",
    "fn.3500": "See above, p. 321, note 4.",
    "fn.3499": "See Mishnah, IVth part, tractate Abôth, chapter IV, § 13.",
    "fn.3501": "That is to say: one is content with him with the simple expiatory sacrifice, when he limits himself to personally practicing his erroneous doctrine.",
    "fn.3495": "He who, through error or inadvertence, committs a sin the premeditation of which would cause him to incur the penalty of <i>excision</i>, must offer a sacrifice in expiation. In this case, the common man must offer a young ewe or a young she-goat (Leviticus, iv, 27-28); the prince or the king, a he-goat (<i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 22); the high priest, a young bullock (<i>v.</i> 3). By <span dir=\"rtl\">בפח</span>, the author does signify the doctor of the law authorized to furnish legal consultations and whose decisions possess authority. As he falls into none of the categories for which the sacrifice of expiation is prescribed, it follows that he is ever considered as acting with premeditation and is punished with rigour, as, for instance, the rebellious doctor.",
    "fn.3473": "That is to say, concerning certain fats designated for the altar, such as the fat which covers the inwards, that which covers the kidneys and the loins, and the whole fat tail of rams. See Leviticus, chap. <small>III</small>, <i>v.</i> 3 and 4, 9 and 10, 14 and 15; chap. <small>VII</small>, <i>v.</i> 3 and 4. All these fats, proceeding from animals meet for sacrifice, such as the ox, the lamb, and the goat, are forbidden for ordinary use (<i>ibid.</i>, ch. <small>VII</small>, <i>v.</i> 23-25). See Mishnah, V<sup>th</sup> part, treatise ’<i>Hullîn</i>, chap. <small>VIII</small>, § 6, and Babylonian Talmud, same treatise, fol. 117 <i>a</i>. According to rabbinical tradition, the fat of the ram's tail is permitted. See Talmud, loc. cit., and cf. the commentary of Ibn-Ezra on Leviticus, <small>VII</small>, 18, where mention is made of a controversy which Ibn-Ezra had on this subject with a Karaite.",
    "fn.3505": "That is to say, the oath sworn by men called to witness in order to affirm that they know nothing. See Leviticus, chap. <small>V</small>, <i>v.</i> 1, and Mishnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Shebu'oth</i>, chap. <small>IV</small>, §§ 2 and 3; Babylonian Talmud, same treatise, fol. 31 <i>b.</i> Should the oath sworn prove false, the culprits must ever offer an expiatory sacrifice, whether they have acted with premeditation or through inadvertence; and in this instance, inadvertence would consist, according to the Talmud, in the witnesses not having known that the law imposed upon them an expiatory sacrifice for such a false oath.",
    "fn.3506": "That is, if someone affirms with an oath that they have not received a deposit that was entrusted to them. See Lévilique, chap. V.21 and 22; Mischnâ, l.c., chap. V.1. Inadvertance is explained in the same way as in the previous case.",
    "fn.3502": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version have <span dir=\"rtl\">לפי השתכלותן</span>; however, according to the mss., one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">השתדלותו</span>; this translator appears to have read in the Arabic text <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄהדה</span>, instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">גׄהלה</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">כפי סכלותו</span>.",
    "fn.3512": "See Vol. II, p. 352, note 3.",
    "fn.3511": "See above, chapter <small>XXVI</small>, p. 204, note 1.",
    "fn.3467": "In the words of Leviticus (<small>XIX</small>, 14): <i>Thou shalt not curse the deaf</i>, the Rabbins discern the prohibition against cursing anyone in his absence; he who curses by one of the names or attributes of the Divinity renders himself liable to the judicial penalty of scourging. Consult Mishnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Shebu'ot</i>, chap. <small>IV</small>, § 13, and Maimonides, <i>Sefer Mitzvot</i>, negative precepts, no. 317.",
    "fn.3483": "See Exodus, chap. <small>XXII</small>, <i>v</i>. 1. Burglary is punished by death, in this sense that the thief is outlawed and it is permitted to slay him when he is taken in the act, because one may suppose that he himself has the intention of committing murder. See Mishnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, chap. <small>VIII</small>, § 6: <span dir=\"rtl\">הבא במחתרת נידון על שם סופו</span>.",
    "fn.3548": "Notably in the crises of certain diseases, according to the theories of Arab physicians.",
    "fn.3547": "That is, the days correspond to an apparent revolution of the sun, and the period of seven days corresponds to the phases of the moon.—For <span dir=\"rtl\">אליום אלטביעי</span>, Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">היום השמשי</span>. Cf. Ibn-Falaquéra, Appendix to the <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 158.",
    "fn.3546": "See Exodus, chap. <small>XXXIV</small>, <i>v.</i> 27-29. According to tradition, the forty days that Moses spent a second time on Mount Sinai are counted from the first Eloul to the 10 Tischri, the day on which Moses came to announce to the Hebrews that the sin of the golden calf was forgiven. See <i>Pirké R. Éliézer</i>, chap. <small>XLVI</small>.",
    "fn.3510": "See Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>Kerithoth</i>, fol. 7 <i>b:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">R. A. b. Ezra says, in the case of one who works on the Sabbath, the Behemoth speaks </span>.",
    "fn.3553": "Book I, treatise <i>Teschoubà</i>, on penance, chapter <small>III</small>, § 4.",
    "fn.3551": "That is, why do we count the days from the offering of a <i>first </i>fruits offering of barley as first fruits. See Leviticus, chapter <small>XXIII</small>, <i>verse </i>15. According to rabbinic tradition, this offering took place the day after the festival of Passover, or the second of the seven days that this festival lasts.",
    "fn.3478": "See Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XVIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 20, and chap. <small>XVII</small>, <i>v.</i> 12. This latter passage, where one speaks generally of a man who enters into open revolt against the judges, applies, according to rabbinical tradition, only to the scholar, doctor of the law, who revolts against the sentence pronounced by the great Sanhedrin and seeks to refute it by his reasonings; he is called <span dir=\"rtl\">זקן ממרה</span>, <i>elder</i> or <i>rebellious doctor.</i> See Mishnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, ch. <small>XI</small>, §§ 1 and 2; Babylonian Talmud, same treatise, fol. 87 <i>a;</i> Maimonides, <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book XIV, treatise <i>Mamrim</i> (concerning rebels), chap. <small>III</small>, §§ 4 and 5.",
    "fn.3565": "That is, the branches of palm and myrtle.",
    "fn.3552": "That is, the festival celebrated on the first day of the seventh month (Leviticus, chap. XXIII[24]; Numbers, chap. XXIX[1]), and whose tradition has made the first day of the year and the anniversary of creation later. See Palestine, p. 184.",
    "fn.3543": "By the Hebrew words <span dir=\"rtl\">הרעו לנו מצרים</span>, the author alludes to a passage from the Book of Numbers, chapter <small>XX</small>, <i>v.</i> 15.",
    "fn.3537": "Ibn-Tibbon's version diverges slightly from the Arabic text; it reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">for the theft and loss come from the fact that he did not keep excellent preservation </span>. Al-’Harîzi's version, which is corrupted in the ms. here, probably read: <span dir=\"rtl\">for in theft and loss is negligence for the most part of preservation and additional effort </span>.",
    "fn.3534": "That is to say, to the four sorts of depositaries, which are: he who keeps a deposit gratuitously, he who borrows any object, he who takes charge of a deposit for hire, and he who takes an object by way of lease. See Exodus, chap. <small>XXII</small>, <i>v.</i> 6-14, and Mischnâ, <i>l. c.</i>, chap. <small>VII</small>, § 8, and treatise <i>Schebouoth</i>, chap. <small>VIII</small>, § 1.",
    "fn.3566": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">טׄאהרה̈ אלעלה̈</span> were omitted by Ibn-Tibbon. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכולם ידועי הטעם גלויי העלה</span>.",
    "fn.3564": "The suffix <span dir=\"rtl\">הא</span> in <span dir=\"rtl\">תׄמרהא</span> and in the following words referrs to the plural <span dir=\"rtl\">מואצׄע</span>, for which some MSS. incorrectly have the singular <span dir=\"rtl\">מוצׄע</span>. Ibn-Tibbon, having reproduced this latter reading (<span dir=\"rtl\">אל מקום הא׳לנות וגו׳</span>), here replacs the suffix <span dir=\"rtl\">הא</span> by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">האדמה</span>. Al-’Harîzi renders more exactly: <span dir=\"rtl\">פריהם ממבחר לקחת צוה לזה זכרון ולהיות ראות אותה בה אשר מים וגו׳</span> .",
    "fn.3540": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">the son first and then the daughter</span>; all the mss. of the Arabic text have only <span dir=\"rtl\">al-awlad</span>, a word that encompasses both the son and the daughter.",
    "fn.3570": "See Numbers, chapter <small>VI</small>, <i>verse</i> 23-26; <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, treatise on Prayer, chapter <small>XIV</small> and <small>XV</small>.",
    "fn.3498": "That is to say, their very error constituts law; those who have acted according to the erroneous decision of the great tribunal, or Sanhedrin, are not responsible, and the tribunal offers, for its error, a sacrifice of expiation. According to tradition, the passage from Leviticus which is about to be cited, as well as a passage from the book of Numbers, <small>XV</small>, 24, applies to the tribunal, designated by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">העדה</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">הקהל</span>, <i>the community.</i> Concerning the various cases and their consequences, see Mischnâ, treatise <i>Horayoth</i>, chap. <small>I</small>; <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book IX, treatise <i>Schgaghoth</i> (of errors or inadvertences), chap. <small>XII</small> and following.",
    "fn.3557": "The Hebrew phrase used here by the author is taken from Genesis, chapter <small>XVIII</small>, <i>verse </i>19; cf. Deuteronomy, chapter <small>VII</small>, <i>verse </i>8, chapter <small>IX</small>, <i>verse </i>5, and chapter <small>X</small>, <i>verse </i>15.",
    "fn.3560": "That is to say, the interpretations given by the rabbis in the <i>draschôth</i> are considered by themselves, not as the real meaning of the biblical texts, but as allegories and moral and poetic considerations that can be attached to these texts. The suffix in the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אנהא</span> (which Ibn-Tibbon renders by <span dir=\"rtl\">שהם</span>) refers to the <i>draschôth</i>.",
    "fn.3496": "That is to say: it results from the silence which the Pentateuch maintains concerning the Doctor of the Law, where the sacrifice of expiation is concerned, that he who acts or renders a decision according to his own erroneous doctrine cannot be regarded as sinning through inadvertence; on the contrary, his sin is ever considered as voluntary and premeditated, and cannot be expiated by a sacrifice. Cf. <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book XIV, treatise <i>Mamrim</i>, chap. iv, § 1: <span dir=\"rtl\">זה שחלק על בית דין הגדול בדבר שחייבין על זדונו כרת ועל שגגתו חטאת</span> ··· <span dir=\"rtl\">הואיל ונשא ונתן עליהן בדבר ועשה או שהורה לעשות הרי זה חייב</span>. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">اجتهاد</span>, which one here renders by <i>personal doctrine</i>, is thus explained in the book <i>Ta’rifât:</i> “this word, in ordinary language, signifies to exert all efforts of which one is capable; as a technical term, it denots, when speaking of a <i>fakîh</i> (jurisconsult, casuist), to employ one's entire capacity to form a personal opinion relative to a legal problem.” See the Extracts from the <i>Ta’rifât</i> by Silvestre de Sacy in the <i>Notices et Extraits des Mss.</i>, vol. X, p. 24.",
    "fn.3561": "It is evident that the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ודׄלך אלקסם</span>, <i>illa pars</i>, can only refer to the first of these two opinions; thus Ibn-Tibbon translated these words as <span dir=\"rtl\">והחלק הראשון</span>.",
    "fn.3563": "See the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Kethoubôth</i>, fol. 15 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3562": "Literally: <i>they fought and obstinately maintained their opinion and defended the draschיth, according to their view, and defended them</i>. On the sense of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אֲאט</span>, cf. vol. I, p. 352, note 2.",
    "fn.3571": "See Exodus, chap. <small>XIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 9 and 16; Deuteronomy, chap. <small>VI</small>, <i>v.</i> 8, chap. <small>XI</small>, <i>v.</i> 18. The Rabbins take these passages in their literal sense and therein discern the prescription to wear upon the left arm and upon the forehead parchments containing certain passages of the Pentateuch. These parchments, called <i>Tephillin</i> or phylacteries (Gospel of Matthew, <small>XXIII</small>, 5), doubtless were intended to replace the superstitious use of amulets. See <i>Palestine</i>, p. 369 <i>a</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.3597": "The author means that, since the cult of sacrifices was admitted, it was necessary in the temple to have all the equipment required by this cult.",
    "fn.3598": "See Exodus, chapter <small>XXV</small>, <i>v.</i> 23-30; <i>Palestine</i>, p. 157 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3602": "Word for word: <i>under the partial form of their cults;</i> that is, we do not worship the universal God under a partial or individual form, by representing Him under the image of any being in nature.",
    "fn.3594": "That is to say: one might also have supposed, by taking the single figure for an angel, that there existed but one angel, or but one <i>Separate</i> Intelligence, beside God, and that there were, in a manner, two gods. — All the Arabic manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">ואן אלמלאך</span>, and likewise Al-Ḥarîzi <span dir=\"rtl\">וכי המלאך</span>, <i>and also that the angel</i>, <i>etc.</i> Ibn-Tibbon's version reads <span dir=\"rtl\">או שהמלאך</span>, <i>or that the angel</i>, which, indeed, is more consonant with the sense.",
    "fn.3599": "Ibn-Tibbon, using the words of the biblical text (Exodus, <small>XX</small>, 22; Deuteronomy, <small>XXVII</small>, 5), thus paraphrased these words: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל האזהרה מהיות אבני המזבח גזית שלא יניף עליהם ברזל</span>.",
    "fn.3595": "The suffix in <span dir=\"rtl\">אמאמה</span> (Ibn-Tibbon <span dir=\"rtl\">לפניו</span>) appears to refer to the <i>ark</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">ארון</span>), mentioned a little earlier, or to the Holy of Holies, to which the author refers in his thought.",
    "fn.3596": "Ibn-Tibbon's rendering <span dir=\"rtl\">הנסתר בפרוכת</span>, <i>hidden by a veil</i>, is not exact. Al-Ḥarîzi translats more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומסד מבדיל תלוי</span>, <i>and where a separating veil was suspended</i>. But one must understand the name of the place from which the temple was separated by a veil; it is manifestly the mysterious Holy of Holies, which was accessible only to the high priest, on the Day of Atonement. — The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מעלה</span>, by which Ibn-Tibbon renders the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">מוֹקֵעַ</span>, appears to one likewise not well chosen; Al-Ḥarîzi's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">יש בו אימה גדולה</span>. The author intends to convey that these ever-burning lamps and the mystery concealed by that great veil possessed something exceeding imposing, which must needs deeply impress the soul and inspire it with a holy dread.",
    "fn.3507": "See Leviticus, chap. <small>XIX</small>, <i>v.</i> 20 and 21. This sin must likewise be expiated by a sacrifice, whether it has been committed with premeditation or through inadvertence. See Mishnah, V<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Kerithoth,</i> chap. <small>II</small>, § 2: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלו מביאין על הזדון כשוגג הבא על השפחה וגו׳</span>. Cf. Talmud, same treatise, fol. 9 <i>a</i>. Concerning the meaning Maimonides assigns to the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הרופה</span>, see vol. I, chap. <small>XXXIX</small>, p. 143.",
    "fn.3601": "See Leviticus, chapter xxvi, verse 1, and Deuteronomy, chapter xvi, <i>v.</i> 21. According to <span dir=\"rtl\">ר אבן משכית</span>, <i>figurative stone</i> or <i>ornamented with figures</i>, it is probable that one should understand obelisks and other monuments bearing hieroglyphic inscriptions.",
    "fn.3556": "This passage is drawn almost textually, not from the ninth (as the author says), but from the eighth book of the <i>Nicomachean Ethics</i>, chap. 11; here is the Greek text: <i>αἱ γàρ ἀρχαῖαι θυσίαι ϰαί σύνοδοι φαίνονται γίνεσθαι μετὰ τὰς τῶν ϰαρπῶν συγϰομιδὰς οἷον ἀπαρχαί · μαλιστα γàρ ἐν τοὑτοις ἐσχὀλαζον τοῖς ϰαιροῖς</i>.—One might suppose that the order of the books of the <i>Ethics</i>, in the Arabic version, differed from that of our editions; but in the commentary of Ibn-Roschd on the <i>Ethics</i>, of which one possesses the Hebrew version, the order of the books is the same as in that of the Greek text, and the passage indicated by Maimonides is found in the VIII<sup>th</sup> book. Cf. hereinafter, at the commencement of chap. <small>XLIX</small>.",
    "fn.3569": "See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>VIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 10; <i>Sépher Miçwoth</i>, <i>ibid.</i>, n° 19.",
    "fn.3631": "All the MSS. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתי תדׄבח סאיר וגו׳</span>, so that <span dir=\"rtl\">הדׄבח</span> can be naught but an active verb whose subject is <span dir=\"rtl\">אלבלאד</span>, literally: <i>in the countries which immolate;</i> likewise Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">בארצות אשר ישחטו שאר וגו׳</span>. Al-’Harîzi translats: <span dir=\"rtl\">בארצות אשר ישחטו שם שאר מיני החיות</span>, which is less literal, but more rational. — What the author here says of the Hindus is true, in the absolute sense, only concerning the cow, for which the Hindus professed a great veneration, and which was inviolable. But they also shared with other peoples of antiquity a respect for the bovine species in general. Vide Bohlen, <i>Das alte Indien</i>, vol. I, p. 253 et seq. Concerning the analogous beliefs of the Egyptians, vide Spencer, <i>l. c.</i>, book II, chap. <small>XV</small>, sect. 2 (Cambridge ed., p. 372 et seq.).",
    "fn.3632": "The text reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">al-dī ḥaṭṭanūnah </span> , and similarly Ibn-Tibbon's version: <span dir=\"rtl\">which you consider </span>, <i>that you consider </i>. On this irregular use of direct discourse, see t. I, p. 283, note 4, and above, p. 289, note 1.",
    "fn.3633": "More literally: <i>and causs to cease what the idolaters absurdly did or believed.</i> The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">استشنع</span> must be taken here in the sense of <i>to do</i> or <i>to believe absurdities</i>, and not, as is ordinary, in that of <i>to repute absurd</i>. Ibn-Tibbon translats according to the sense: <span dir=\"rtl\">בעשות כל מה שהיו מרחיקין עובדי ע״ז</span>. Al-’Harîzi translats more literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובהסיר מה שהיו נזהרין בו</span>; he has exactly rendered the sense of the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">אזאלה̈</span>, but he has not fully apprehended the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">אסתשנע</span>.",
    "fn.3634": "Ibn-Tibbon omitted the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אהליה̈</span>, <i>domestics:</i> Al-’Harîzi translated: <span dir=\"rtl\">they are found in the settlement and in every place</span>.",
    "fn.3635": "Word for word: <i>and not as the idolaters practiced, etc.</i> — On the book Tomtom, see above, p. 240, note 1.",
    "fn.3636": "See Leviticus, chap. <small>V</small>, <i>v</i>. 7 and <i>passim</i>.",
    "fn.3637": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>II</small>, <i>verse</i>. 1-11.",
    "fn.3638": "The text is expressed in a very concise manner: <i>all this for him who wanted</i>. The author means that the legislator, by all these prescriptions, wanted to regulate the sacrifices only for those who practiced this type of cult voluntarily; for, as the author has developed further above (chap. <small>XXXII</small>), the cult of sacrifices was only an accommodation to the customs of the time and was rather tolerated than ordained.",
    "fn.3951": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">حبيب</span> signifies <i>to separate one from a thing, to hinder one from attaining it;</i> here one must understand <i>the intellect</i>. Ibn-Tibbon renders it: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי כבר הוסר המונע אשר היה מבדיל בינו ובין מושכלו</span>; likewise Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי הסתלקו המונעים אשר היו מבדילים בינו ובין הבורא לפעמים</span>. Cf. in the Arabic text, fol. 128 <i>a</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלדׄי הו חינידׄ מחגׄוב ען אללה</span>, and vide the seventh of the <i>Eight Chapters</i>, where Maimonides expounds what is to be understood by the term <span dir=\"rtl\">حجاب</span>, which Ibn-Tibbon has there rendered by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מחיצה</span>.",
    "fn.3952": "Literally: <i>this direction;</i> that is, these counsels to guide you.",
    "fn.3953": "Literally: <i>will awaken and know.</i>",
    "fn.3954": "Several mss. read: <span dir=\"rtl\">אעטׄם מן כל שכׄץ אנסאן הו מלך</span>, \"is greater than all human beings <i>who would be a king.</i>\" The two Hebrew translators reproduced this reading; Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">הוא גדול מכל מלך בשר ודם</span>; Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">הוא יותר נכבד מכל מלך</span>.",
    "fn.3955": "That is, by a calm and clear reasoning emanating from intelligence, and not by the exaggerations of an exalted imagination.",
    "fn.3956": "That is, to characterize the modesty that the doctors put in their relationships with their wives, it was said that they dared to barely lift the woman's clothing and that they only uncovered her body in small spaces the width of a palm. See Talmud of Babylon, tractate Nedarim, fol. 20a, b.",
    "fn.3957": "That is, the one who places there during the night the same decency that he would place there in a moment when he could be seen by everyone. See Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Berakhôth</i>, fol. 62 <i>a.</i>.",
    "fn.3958": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Kiddouschin</i>, fol. 31 <i>a. Marching </i>with one's right foot <i> signifies advancing with a proud and majestic air.",
    "fn.3959": "See the treatise <i>Kiddushin, l.c.</i>, where it is said that R. Houna did not walk with his head uncovered, because, he said, ‘the divine majesty hovers above my head.’",
    "fn.3960": "See the Commentary of Maimonides, on the treatise <i>Abôth</i>, chap. <small>I</small>, § 17.",
    "fn.3961": "That is, this purpose, the fear of God, is the result of practices. In editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">תגיע</span> has been added for greater clarity; manuscripts read: <span dir=\"rtl\">מן</span> (or <span dir=\"rtl\">שהלא</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שהיא המעשים</span>.",
    "fn.3962": "Word for word: <i>by the ideas of the Law which contain the perception of his being, such as God is truly.</i>",
    "fn.3936": "Literally: <i>that it was through them that His name was known in the world</i>. The manner in which the author expresses himself is somewhat inexact; for he means that the appellation by which God was known in the world was connected with the name of the patriarchs, and that He was called <i>God of Abraham, etc.</i> In the passage of Exodus which the author here cites, he takes the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">לעולם</span>, <i>for ever, perpetually</i>, in the sense of <i>for</i> or <i>in the world</i> (cf. vol. I, p. 3, note 2, and above, p. 226, note 4); this explanation is evidently inadmissible.",
    "fn.3937": "See, for example, above, chap. <small>XII</small>, p. 74 et suiv.",
    "fn.3938": "See above, ch. XVII (p. 135), and ch. XVIII (p. 137 et suiv.).",
    "fn.3939": "On the expression <i>puissance prochaine</i>, cf. above, p. 424, note 1.",
    "fn.3940": "See above, chapter <small>XVIII</small>, p. 139.",
    "fn.3945": "This explanation of the sense (<span dir=\"rtl\">אלתקדיר כי תעבר וגו׳</span>) is lacking in most of the Arabic mss. and in the version of Al-’Harîzi; it is also lacking in the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, but we found it in one of the best mss. of this version (Bibliothèque impériale, fonds of the Oratory, n° 46).",
    "fn.3946": "Literally: <i>whoever has prepared himself in such a way that this intelligence</i> (that is, the <i>intellect actif</i>) <i>may overflow upon him</i>. In Ibn-Tibbon's version, one should read <span dir=\"rtl\">שהכין עצמו</span>, and not <span dir=\"rtl\">שהביא</span>, as some editions have. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">כל מי שראוי לזה השכל</span>.",
    "fn.3947": "Or: <i>of ill encounters;</i> it is thus that Psalm XCI, which tradition attributes unto Moses, is designated in the Talmud. Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Schebou’ôth</i>, fol. 19 <i>b;</i> Jerusalem Talmud, treatise <i>Schabbâth</i>, chap. <small>VI</small> (fol. 8, col. 2), and treatise ’<i>Eroubîn</i>, chap. <small>X</small>, at the close (fol. 26, col. 3).",
    "fn.3948": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">הפוגעות</span>; the mss. have, in accordance with the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">הגופניות</span>.",
    "fn.3949": "See vol. I, chap. <small>XXXIV</small>, 4<sup>e</sup> cause, p. 125 and following.",
    "fn.3950": "See the Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Baba bathra</i>, fol. 17 <i>a</i>, where not only Moses, Aaron, and Miriam are mentioned, but also the patriarchs Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.",
    "fn.3773": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">טריפה</span> signifies a <i>torn beast</i> (Exodus, <small>XXII</small>, 30); but the Rabbins do likewise designate by this name an animal that is wounded, or that possesses an organic defect; and they enumerate a series of cases which, according to tradition, render the animal unfit for consumption even though it may have been slaughtered according to the prescribed rites. See Mischnâ, V<sup>th</sup> part, treatise ’<i>Hullin</i>, chap. <small>III</small>; Maimonides here adduces as a reason that which the Talmudists assert, namely, that certain maladies or organic lesions of an animal are a progression towards its demise: <span dir=\"rtl\">טריפה אינה חייה</span>; see Babylonian Talmud, treatise ’<i>Hullin</i>, fol. 42 <i>a</i>, and cf. <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, treatise <i>Maakhalôth Assourôth</i>, chap. <small>IV</small>, § 17, end.",
    "fn.3774": "See Leviticus, ch. <small>11</small>, <i>v.</i> 3, 9, 10; Deuteronomy, ch. <small>14</small>, <i>v.</i> 6 and 9.",
    "fn.3775": "Cf. my <i>Reflections, etc.</i>, p. 60.",
    "fn.3776": "See Genesis, chapter <small>XXXII</small>, <i>verse</i>. 33.",
    "fn.3777": "The rabbis find this defense in the Genesis, chap. <small>IX</small>, <i>v.</i> 4, and in the Deuteronome, chap. <small>XII</small>, <i>v.</i> 23. See Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>Synhedrin</i>, fol. 57 <i>a</i>; treatise <i>’Hullin</i>, fol. 101 <i>b</i>, and Maimonides, <i>Sépher miçwôth</i>, negative precepts, n° 182.",
    "fn.3778": "The author likely found this practice in the <i>Agriculture Nabatéenne</i>, or in some other book of the Sabians.",
    "fn.3779": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">כון תחרים בשר בחלב</span> have been omitted in Ibn-Tibbon's version. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומה שיחזק זה הדבר באיסור בשר בחלב וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.3781": "On the meaning of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">نحر</span>, see above, p. 208, note 1.",
    "fn.3782": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>XXII</small>, <i>verse</i>. 28.",
    "fn.3784": "See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXII</small>, <i>v.</i> 6 and 7, where it is prescribed, when encountering a bird's nest on the path, not to take the mother and the young at the same time, but to send the mother away and take only the young.",
    "fn.3785": "According to the author, this law possesses two motives: 1° to spare the mother the pain of witnessing her young removed; 2° to ensure, for the most part, that birds' nests in the countryside remain undisturbed, inasmuch as the fledglings, which alone may be taken, are not palatable and offer no advantage. Cf. Michaelis, <i>Syntagma Commentationum</i>, t. II, n° 4: Lex mosaica Deut. XXII, 6, 7, ex historia naturali et moribus Ægyptiorum illustrata, and <i>Mosaisches Recht</i>, t. III, § 171. This author discerns in the law in question a hunting regulation, designed to prevent the destruction of certain birds which the agriculturist may at first view as perilous foes to his seeds, and yet which are highly beneficial in Palestine for the extirpation of serpents, as well as the swarms of flies and locusts.",
    "fn.3542": "Literally: <i>one must regard the person of close kinship with benevolent eyes</i>. Ibn-Tibbon translates somewhat freely: <span dir=\"rtl\">it is impossible for him without showing a friendly face to his relative</span>. Al-’Harîzi's version is closer to the Arabic text; it reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">one must look at him with a near and merciful eye</span>.",
    "fn.3544": "See vol. II, chap. <small>XXXI</small>, where the author also discusses the double motive for the institution of the sabbath.",
    "fn.3545": "See Leviticus, chap. <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 27 et seq.; Numbers, chap. <small>XXIX</small>, <i>v.</i> 7 et seq.",
    "fn.3549": "Pentecost, known in the Old Testament as <i>the Feast of Weeks</i>, is, according to tradition, the anniversary of the Revelation on Mount Sinai.",
    "fn.3550": "Instead of the action noun <span dir=\"rtl\">עדה̈</span>, some mss. have the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">עדת</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(عُدَّتُ)</span> ; similarly Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">we counted the days</span>, and Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">they recounted the days</span>. But for this lesson to be admissible, it would have been necessary to say at the beginning of the sentence <span dir=\"rtl\">and they prolonged</span>, or <span dir=\"rtl\">and from the day they prolonged</span>.",
    "fn.3554": "That is to say, in the same season as the festival of <i>commencement of the year</i>, or in the autumn.",
    "fn.3503": "See above, p. 319, note 1, and p. 321, note 1.",
    "fn.3504": "See <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book XIV, treatise <i>Synhédrin</i>, chapter <small>XVI</small>, § 3, and chapter <small>XVIII</small>, § 5. This punishment, which is not written in the Law, can, according to the rabbis, be inflicted even for infractions to the traditional law.",
    "fn.3508": "On the meaning of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تسيب</span>, see above, p. 261, note 1.",
    "fn.3509": "See Mischnâ, <i>loc. cit.</i>, § 5, and Talmud, <i>loc. cit.</i>., fol. 11 <i>a</i>, where it is said that it is here a matter of a pagan slave destined for marriage to a Hebrew slave.",
    "fn.3513": "Generally, the possessions of those condemned to death pass to their heirs, and consequently also to those of the <i>individuals</i> condemned for idolatry; the seduced populace thus suffers a more grievous punishment than those who have individually rendered themselves culpable of idolatry. See Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 13-18; Mishnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, chap. <small>X</small>, § 4; <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book I, treatise on Idolatry, ch. <small>IV</small>, §§ 2 and 5.",
    "fn.3672": "Amongst diverse peoples of antiquity, blood served as an offering, notably in honour of demons and of the Manes; at times it was offered alone, at other times it was mingled with libations. Vide Spencer, l. c., book II, cap. <small>XI</small> (p. 32 et seq.); Saubert, De sacrificiis veterum, cap. <small>XXV</small>, p. 658 et seq. Allusion is made to this custom in a passage of the Psalms, <small>XVI</small>, 4, when speaking of those who hasten to follow foreign customs.",
    "fn.3673": "Instead of the words <span dir=\"rtl\">בנפש ההיא</span>, most of the Ar. mss., as well as the versions of Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi, read <span dir=\"rtl\">באיש ההוא</span>. See above, p. 132, note 1, and cf. p. 289, note 2.",
    "fn.3674": "One has here yet another case in which Maimonides is in disagreement with the Rabbinic tradition and with that which he himself says in his Talmudic works. Cf. above, p. 313, note 1. According to the Talmud (treatise <i>Synhedrin</i>, fol. 63 <i>a</i>), the prohibition expressed by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לא תאכלו על הדם</span> applies to different heterogeneous cases and is a vague and general prohibition (<span dir=\"rtl\">לאו שבכללות</span>); Maimonides himself applies it to the <i>rebellious son</i>, stating that it is forbidden to indulge in youth in feasting and drinking, which may lead to the shedding of blood. Vide <i>Sépher Miçwoth</i>, negative precepts, n° 195; <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book XIV, treatise <i>Mamrîm</i>, chap. <small>VII</small>, §1.",
    "fn.3675": "That is, of non-consecrated meat and what was eaten as one pleased. The talmudic expression <span dir=\"rtl\">בשר תאוה</span>, <i>meat of desire</i>, is borrowed from Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XII</small>, <i>v</i>. 20. See the Babylonian Talmud, tractate ’<i>Hullîn</i>, fol. 17 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3664": "In the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">Ken hetseli mepani hachpazon</span> are missing here, which are found in the mss. of this version.",
    "fn.3665": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">מלאך אלאמר</span> signify <i>the essential part</i> or <i>the foundation of the thing, the summary</i>. Cf. volume II, chapter <small>XVII</small> (Arabic text, fol. 36 <i>b</i>, French translation, p. 136).",
    "fn.3666": "See Mischnâ, V<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Zeba’him</i>, chap. <small>V</small>, §8, and cf. Exodus, chap. <small>XII</small>, <i>v</i>. 4.",
    "fn.3667": "See Exodus, chap. <small>XII</small>, <i>v</i>. 48.",
    "fn.3668": "See the Midrash <i>Schemôth rabbâ</i>, section 19 (folio 104, column 4).",
    "fn.3669": "The author appears to share the opinion of the Talmudists, according to which the Israelites would have been the only ones to practice circumcision. See further the Notes to chap. <small>XLIX</small>, towards the end.",
    "fn.3670": "Cf. the Chaldaic paraphrase of Jonathan and the commentary of Rashi on the passage from Ezekiel.",
    "fn.3671": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon renders <span dir=\"rtl\">the future things</span>, <i>the things to come</i>; Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">and they will reveal to them mysteries of wisdom</span>.",
    "fn.3680": "In our editions of the <i>Siphri</i>, as also in the <i>Yalkout</i> (vol. I, n° 945), this passage is differently worded, and therein one does not find the word <span dir=\"rtl\">בבואה</span>. For this word, cf. Babylonian Talmud, <i>Guittin</i>, fol. 66 <i>a; Nedarim</i>, fol. 9 <i>b; ’Abôdâ Zara</i>, fol. 47 <i>a</i>, and passim.",
    "fn.3681": "That is to say, with regard to the species of animals or vegetable matter used in it.",
    "fn.3683": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>IV</small>, <i>v</i>. 28 and 32.",
    "fn.3684": "Ibn-Tibbon has here <span dir=\"rtl\">a part of a kind</span>, <i>a part of a species</i>; the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">tsenaf</span> indicates here the <i>sex</i>, as later <span dir=\"rtl\">moznua and tsenapha</span>.",
    "fn.3823": "Ibn-Tibbon did not translate the words <span dir=\"rtl\">לו טלק</span>, which are found in all the mss. of the Arabic text; Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל אם היה מגרש היה מתחייב וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.3824": "Here and in the ensuing reasoning, the author does identify the <i>mohar</i> of Biblical times with the dowry of traditional law, which, as has been observed in a preceding note, was fixed at a minimum of two hundred shekels for a virgin. Maimonides does here appear to one to be in error; for the <i>mohar</i> was not a dowry, but was paid to the father at the moment of betrothal, as the price of acquisition for the young woman. One may conclude from a verse in Deuteronomy (<small>XXII</small>, 29) that this price was fifty shekels (approximately 150 francs). Vide <i>Palestine</i>, p. 203 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.3825": "After the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מאה כסף</span>, the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version repeat the words: <span dir=\"rtl\">כי זהו מוהר הבתולות</span>, which are not found in the mss. of this version and are here devoid of sense.",
    "fn.3826": "See above, chap. <small>XLI</small>, p. 316, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1.",
    "fn.3827": "Literally: <i>fixed </i> or <i>destined from his side </i>. The mss. of Ibn-Tibbon's version read <span dir=\"rtl\">worthy of him </span>; in the editions, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">to him </span> have been substituted for the words <span dir=\"rtl\">to give to him </span>.",
    "fn.3828": "Imitation of a passage from Numbers, chapter <small>XV</small>, <i>v.</i> 16.",
    "fn.3702": "That is to say, sometimes man will recognize in advance that he will be incapable of performing the expiation.",
    "fn.3703": "See, for example, Numbers, chapter <small>XXVIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 19; chapter <small>XXIX</small>, <i>v</i>. 13 and 36.",
    "fn.3704": "See, for example, Leviticus, chapter <small>IV</small>, <i>v</i>. 12 and 21.",
    "fn.3705": "On the celebration of new moons among ancient peoples, see Spencer, book III, Dissert. IV (Cambridge edition, 1685, p. 715 and following); on the Egyptians, cf. Lepsius, <i>Chronology of the Egyptians</i>, vol. I, p. 157, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 3.",
    "fn.3829": "Literally: <i>as to the prohibition of</i> ’<small>ARAYÔTH</small> or <i>nakedness</i>. Incestuous and other illicit unions are designated by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עריות</span>, <i>nakedness</i>, because in the chapter of the Pentateuch which treats of these unions (Leviticus, chap. <small>XVIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 9 et seq.), the phrase used is: «thou shalt not uncover the nakedness (<span dir=\"rtl\">ערוה</span>), etc.», and almost all the verses commence with the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ערות</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ערוה</span> is therefore employed by Jewish doctors, both Rabbinite and Karaite, to designate in general a woman with whom it is forbidden to ally oneself in marriage, a kinswoman.",
    "fn.3830": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">מע אלשכׄץ</span>, <i>with the individual</i> or <i>the person</i>, have been, for greater clarity, thus paraphrased by Ibn-Tibbon <span dir=\"rtl\">עם האיש שנאסרה עליו</span>.",
    "fn.3831": "See above, chap <small>XLI</small>, p. 318, note 2.",
    "fn.3832": "Ibn-Tibbon diverges slightly from the literal translation, which he probably did not find clear enough. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">But the reality of this is a light in all nakedness, etc.</span>.",
    "fn.3833": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">פאעתברהן</span>, <i>considère-les</i>, found in all the Arabic manuscripts, has been rendered neither by Ibn-Tibbon nor by Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.3834": "That is, between descendants and ascendants.",
    "fn.3886": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version read <span dir=\"rtl\">ואני אסיר לך הספק</span>; the manuscripts have: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואני אסדר לך זה</span>. Al-’Harîzi's version reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואני אסדר לר הכל</span>.",
    "fn.3887": "See Gen. chap. <small>XIV</small>, <i>v.</i> 1-16.",
    "fn.3888": "Ibn-Tibbon has: <span dir=\"rtl\">his heart was hot</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">for he was a help to his relative</span>.",
    "fn.3963": "Vide Commentary on the Mishnah, treatise <i>Abôth</i>, chap. <small>V</small>, § 7, where it is stated that the term ’H<small>ÉSED</small> denotes exaggeration or excess, of both good and evil. As an instance where the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חסד</span> signifies <i>excess of evil</i>, the commentators Ephodi and Shem-Tob cite the passage from Leviticus (<small>XX</small>, 17): <span dir=\"rtl\">חסד הוא</span>, where the gloss of Ibn-Ezra bears: <span dir=\"rtl\">תוספת בזנות</span>. Cf. the Dictionary of David Kim’hi, under the root <span dir=\"rtl\">חסד</span>.",
    "fn.3964": "The author takes the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עולם</span>, as the Chaldean version does, in the sense of <i>’universe.</i> Cf. t. I, p. 3, note 2, and above, p. 226, note 4, and p. 443, note 2.",
    "fn.3965": "On the expression גבר צדע כל מצדע, cf. above, p. 172, note 4, p. 276, note 2, and passim.",
    "fn.3966": "Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומפני זה תקרא וגו׳</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולהיות כל מדה נכבדת נקראת צדקה על כן אמר וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.3967": "See vol. I, chap. <small>LIII</small> and <small>LIV</small>.",
    "fn.3968": "That is to say, one attributes to him the quality of ’H<small>ESSED</small>, <i>benevolence</i> or <i>liberality.</i>",
    "fn.3969": "The editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version generally carry <span dir=\"rtl\">על העניינים</span>, which is merely a printing error for <span dir=\"rtl\">העניים</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולפי חמלתו על החלושים</span>.",
    "fn.3983": "That is, the one who possesses them in common at least shares the benefit with other people. Ibn-Tibbon, perhaps to make the phrase clearer, added the words <span dir=\"rtl\">גם לך</span>. According to the Arabic text, it should be <span dir=\"rtl\">מבלעדי היותם לך ולזולתך</span>.",
    "fn.3984": "This passage from Proverbs bears no relation to the subject matter being discussed here; however, it is essential to remember that the author has a tendency to apply biblical passages to his ideas and that he allegorizes a significant portion of Solomon's proverbs, viewing them as allegories, sometimes of the material substance, and sometimes of various moral and intellectual qualities. Cf. Vol. I, p. 19 and following, and above, Chap. <small>VIII</small>, p. 49.",
    "fn.3985": "Literally: <i>O you, who neglects your soul, in such a way that its whiteness is darkened, the bodily faculties taking precedence over it</i>.",
    "fn.3986": "That is to say, starting with what, in the eyes of the common people, has the least importance, and passing successively from the quality that is less appreciated to that which is more appreciated.",
    "fn.3987": "We had to deviate a little from the literal translation of this passage, where the author employs the expressions of Jeremiah in an irregular manner.",
    "fn.3731": "See Mishnah, Fifth<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Middoth</i>, chap. <small>I</small>, §§ 1 and 8, and treatise <i>Tamid</i>, chap. <small>I</small>, § 1. It results from these passages that the priests who were on guard at the Temple remained at night in three places which were outside the court, and that the chiefs of the section, lying in one of these places, held the keys of the court, so that no one might penetrate therein during the night. It is observed that the author intends to speak of the <i>impossibility</i>, for all persons, of entering during the night, and that the matter here in question is by no means an <i>illegality;</i> Ibn-Tibbon and Al-’Harîzi have therefore rendered ill the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יגׄוז</span>, the one by <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא הותר</span>, the other by <span dir=\"rtl\">ואסור</span>, and it was requisite to say <span dir=\"rtl\">ולא יתכן</span>, or <span dir=\"rtl\">ואי אפשר</span>. In Ibn-Tibbon’s version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">פירוש אפילו משיעריב שמשו</span> have been added by certain copyists, or by the editors; they are found neither in the manuscripts of this version which one has been able to consult, nor in the commentary of Schem-Tob.",
    "fn.3732": "See Mishnah, II<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Yômâ</i>, chapter <small>III</small>, § 3.",
    "fn.3734": "That is, the people cured of these diseases still had to count seven days to be completely pure. See Leviticus, chap. <small>XV</small>, <i>v</i>. 13, 19, and 28.",
    "fn.3735": "See ibid.<i>, </i>v<i>. 5-9, 21-23 et 27.",
    "fn.3736": "Herein has one perceived a contradiction with the principle which the author has just laid down, namely, that the more frequent a case of impurity, the more difficult and prolonged is the act of purification, whereas herein he does appear to state that it is the rarest cases which require a sacrifice. But, in our passage, the author does merely seek to justify the employment of sacrifice in the purification of the man afflicted with gonorrhoea, of the woman suffering from a flux of blood, and of the woman in childbed, whereas the woman purifying herself from her menses has no need of sacrifices; and, as he does intimate, the reason for this is that the menses occurring more frequently than the three former cases, it was not deemed meet to impose upon the woman the obligation of offering a sacrifice for her purification on each occasion. See the commentary of Schem-Tob. According to Ephodi, the translator Ibn-Tibbon would subsequently have corrected his translation by substituting, higher up, for the words <span dir=\"rtl\">יותר נמצאת</span>, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">יותר מעטה המציאות</span>; in the manuscripts which one has been able to consult, this correction does not exist, and the reading is conformable to that of the editions.",
    "fn.3612": "By the <i>true form</i> of man, the author means the rational soul or intelligence.",
    "fn.3613": "Voy. Talmud de Babylone, traité '<i>Hullîn</i>, fol. 24 <i>a:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">בשילה ובבית עולמים אין נפסלים אלא בקול</span> Cf. <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, <i>l. c.</i>, chap. <small>III</small>, § 8.",
    "fn.3614": "Vide Leviticus, chap. <small>XVI</small>, <i>v.</i> 2; Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Mena’hôth</i>, fol. 27 <i>b;</i> Maimonides, <i>Sépher Miçwôth</i>, negative precepts, No. 68; <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, treatise <i>Biath ha-Mikdasch</i>, chap. <small>II</small>, §§ 1 to 6. — By <span dir=\"rtl\">מקדש</span>, <i>sanctuary</i>, one must here understand the entire enclosure, including the ’<i>azarôth</i> (<i>courts</i> or <i>courtyards</i>). There, says the author, it was not permitted to sit, as the traditional law maintains (vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Sôtâ</i>, fol. 40 <i>b; Synhedrin</i>, fol. 101 <i>b; Mischné Torâ</i>, book VIII, treatise <i>Bêth ha-Be’hîrâ</i>, chap. <small>VII</small>, § 6). By <i>Hekhal</i>, one understands the temple properly so called, where priests might only enter when their functions summoned them thither; but never might they enter into the Holy of Holies, where the high priest alone was permitted to penetrate on the Day of Atonement.",
    "fn.3644": "See Leviticus, chapter II</small>, <i>v</i>. 1 and 4.",
    "fn.3645": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>I</small>, <i>verse</i>. 6-9.",
    "fn.3639": "One cannot assert whether the author has drawn this information from one of the Sabian or pagan books which he has mentioned in chap. <small>XXIX</small>; but, if he intended to speak of the ancient pagans in general, he was not well-informed, for it is certain that the use of salt was most common in the sacrifices of the Greeks and Romans. Pliny says, speaking of salt: “Maxime autem in sacris intelligitur ejus auctoritas quando nulla conficiuntur sine mola salsa.” <i>Hist. nat.</i>, book XXXI, chap. 41. Cf. Spencer, <i>l. c.</i>, book III, Dissert. II, chap. 2, sect. 2 (Cambridge ed., p. 662).",
    "fn.3640": "That is, in order not to expose sacred things to contempt.",
    "fn.3925": "Literally: <i>the intelligible thought</i>; that is, the thought that has as its object intelligible things and not sensible things.",
    "fn.3929": "Ibn-Tibbon omitted the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מן אללה ותשתגל בכליתך</span>; Ibn-Falaquéra (p. 133) translated exactly: <span dir=\"rtl\">כשתפנה מחשבתך מהאלה ית׳ ותתעסק בכללך במזון הכרחי וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.3930": "Literally: <i>the superior men were stingy with the moments in which they allowed themselves to be distracted from Him</i> (<i>by other occupations</i>). The expression <span dir=\"rtl\">to be occupied with something else</span> is elliptical and means: <i>to be occupied in such a way that one cannot think of a certain thing</i>.",
    "fn.3931": "Vide the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Schabbath</i>, fol. 149 <i>a</i>, where the words cited by the author serve to explain the scriptural words <span dir=\"rtl\">אל תפנו אל האלילים</span>, <i>turn ye not unto the idols</i> (Leviticus, <small>XIX</small>, 4). One has translated the Talmudic words <span dir=\"rtl\">אֶל מדעתכם</span> in the sense attributed to them by Rashi's gloss; but perhaps Maimonides, to apply the Talmudic phrase to the subject with which he is here concerned, wished it to be pronounced: <span dir=\"rtl\">אל תְּפַנּוּ אֵל מדעתכם</span>, <i>remove not God from your thought</i>.",
    "fn.3573": "The duty incumbent upon every Israelite to possess a copy of the Law-book is linked by rabbinic tradition to a passage in Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 19. See <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book II, treatise <i>Sépher Torâ</i>, chapter 7, § 1.",
    "fn.3604": "See Numbers, chapter <small>XVIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 2 to 7; Mishnah, V<sup>th</sup> part, tractate <i>Tamid</i>, chapter <small>I</small>, and tractate <i>Middôth</i>, chapter <small>I</small>.",
    "fn.3622": "Literally: <i>the manufacture of all the garments ought to be firmly completed in the fabric</i>. Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Yômâ</i>, fol. 72 <i>b:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">בגדי כהונה אין עושין אותן מעשה מחט אלא מעשה אורנ</span>. Cf. Josephus, <i>Antiquities</i>, book III, chap. <small>VII</small>, § 2. Nevertheless, the sleeves of the tunic, say the Talmudists (<i>ibid.</i>), were woven separately and sewn upon the garment. Cf. <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, loco citato, chap. <small>VIII</small>, § 16.",
    "fn.3623": "See Numbers, chap. <small>IV</small>, <i>v.</i> 19 and 49; Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Arakhîn</i>, fol. 11 <i>b; Mishnah Torah, </i>l. c.<i>, chap. <small>III</small>, § 10.",
    "fn.3988": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">... אלקני̈ה הדה אלדי ... מן אלד֗כּאיר אלתי יתנאפס פיהא</span> are rendered in Ibn-Tibbon’s version by <span dir=\"rtl\">ושאלו הקנינים … שישימם סגולתו</span>. Ibn-Falaquera (<i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 137) translates literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">ושזה הקנין … מהמטמונים שיתפארו בהם</span>; only the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تنافس</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(في شيء)</span> signifies: <i>to ardently desire a thing</i>, and not: <i>to boast of a thing</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שיתפארו בהם</span>).",
    "fn.3989": "Voy. <i>Bereschith rabba</i>, sect. 35 fin (fol. 31, col. 2).",
    "fn.3990": "Voy. the book I, chap. <small>LIV</small>, p. 217 et suiv.",
    "fn.3991": "Properly: <i>the demolishers</i>, or <i>the destroyers</i>, those who overturn religious opinions.",
    "fn.3676": "See my <i>Réflexions sur le culte des anciens Hébreux</i> (Bible de M. Cahen, t. IV), p. 36; <i>Palestine</i>, p. 161.",
    "fn.3677": "One could not offer, in fact, any poultry other than doves and pigeons; but the latter could not be offered as peaceful sacrifices: <span dir=\"rtl\">אין העוף בא שלמים</span>. See <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book VIII, treatise <i>Ma’asé ha-Korbanôth</i>, chap. <small>I</small>, §11.",
    "fn.3678": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>XVII</small>, <i>v</i>. 13; Mishnah, V<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>’Hullîn</i>, chapter <small>VI</small>.",
    "fn.3679": "The Hebrew words <span dir=\"rtl\">להפר האחוה</span> are taken from the book of Zechariah, chap. <small>XI</small>, <i>v</i>. 14.—By <i>those who were truly possessed</i>, the author means those who believed in the existence of demons and thought that one could establish a relationship with them.",
    "fn.3514": "The MSS. generally have <span dir=\"rtl\">תמאלוא</span>; one ought to read <span dir=\"rtl\">תמאלאוא</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">תמאלווא</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(تَمَالُؤُوا)</span> , the VI<sup>th</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">مثلاً</span> , which signifies Invicem juverunt, concordarunt ac unanimes fuerunt. Likewise, a little further on, one ought to read <span dir=\"rtl\">חמאלוהם</span> , instead of the form <span dir=\"rtl\">תמאילהם</span> which most MSS. present.",
    "fn.3535": "Literally: …. <i>est dans la bourse du maître du bien;</i> that is to say, the damage only affects the owner.",
    "fn.3536": "Literally: <i>and all damages are in the purse of the borrower</i>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">the borrower</span>, some mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">the custodian</span>, <i>of the deposit</i>. Similarly, the two Hebrew versions have <span dir=\"rtl\">the custodian</span>.",
    "fn.3605": "By <span dir=\"rtl\">אלגׄהאל</span>, <i>the ignorant</i>, the author likely means here the <i>profanum vulgus</i>, those who are not part of the order of professional scholars; that is, those who are neither priests nor Levites, and who were forbidden from entering the inner temple. See Numbers, <i>the same chapter</i>, <i>v.</i> 4.",
    "fn.3607": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>X</small>, <i>v.</i> 9; <i>Sépher Miçwôth</i>, negative precepts, n° 73; <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, <i>l. c.</i>, chapter <small>I</small>.",
    "fn.3608": "That is, by washing them according to the prescription of the law (Exodus, chapter <small>XXX</small>, <i>v.</i> 19-21); <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, <i>l. c.</i>, chapter <small>V</small>.",
    "fn.3555": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ענדהם</span>, which we translate by <i>à ce qu’il paraît</i>, is found in all the Ar. mss., but was omitted by the two Heb. translators. It means: <i>selon eux</i>, i.e., according to the opinion of the Greeks, and appears to correspond to the word <i>φαίνονται</i> in the passage from Aristotle cited below.",
    "fn.3567": "The rabbis attribute the duty of prayer to several passages of the Pentateuch, where it is prescribed to serve or worship God. See Exodus, chap. XXIII, v. 25; Deuteronomy, chap. XIII, v. 5; Maimonides, Sépher Miçwoth, affirmative precepts, n° 5; Mischné Torâ, book II, treatise on Prayer, chap. I, § 1.",
    "fn.3412": "Hospitality was one of the principal virtues of the ancient Arabs: the person who had been received under their roof was sacred, regardless of whether they deserved or not the protection that was accorded to them. The author makes reference here to the praises by which the ancient Arabic poets extol this virtue.",
    "fn.3416": "That is to say: If an animal causes damage upon the public highway, whether with its tooth or with its foot, the owner of the said animal is not held responsible for it. Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Baba Kamma,</i> fol. 14 <i>a</i> and fol. 19 <i>b;</i> Maimonides, <i>Mishneh Torah,</i> Book XI, treatise <i>Nizké Mammon</i> (concerning pecuniary damages), chap. I, §§ 8-10.",
    "fn.3568": "That is to say, the duty to read in the morning and in the evening the passage of Deuteronomy (VI, 4 et seq.) which beginns with the words S<small>chéma</small>’ Y<small>ISRAEL</small>, 'Hear, O Israel'. See Mischnâ, First part, treatise <i>Berakhôth</i>, chap. <small>I</small>, § 1; Talmud, same treatise, fol. 2 <i>a</i> et seq.; <i>Sépher Miçwoth</i>, <i>ibid.</i>, No. 10.",
    "fn.3572": "See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>VI</small>, <i>v.</i> 9, and chapter <small>XI</small>, <i>v.</i> 20. This usage is analogous to that of phylacteries. See <i>Palestine</i>, p. 364 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.3619": "Vide Numbers, chap. <small>IV</small>, <i>v.</i> 1 to 15; I Chronicles, chap. <small>XV</small>, <i>v.</i> 15; Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Sôtâ</i>, fol. 35 <i>a;</i> Maimonides, <i>Sépher Miçwôth</i>, affirmative precepts, No. 34; <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, treatise on the Sanctuary’s Vessels, chap. <small>II</small>, § 12. In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מפני</span> is a printer’s error; one must read <span dir=\"rtl\">אופני ההגדלה</span>, as the manuscripts possess.",
    "fn.3620": "That is to say, one ought never to change aught concerning the form in which the ark had been constructed, nor even to alter its arrangement by removing the staves, which served to bear it, from the rings in which they were engaged. Vide Exodus, chap. <small>XXV</small>, <i>v.</i> 15; Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Yômâ</i>, fol. 72 <i>a; Sépher Miçwôth</i>, negative precepts, No. 86; <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, loco citato, § 13.",
    "fn.3901": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">או תכריר</span> have been omitted by Ibn-Tibbon.—According to Ibn-Tibbon's version, one should translate: <i>'and when it seems unto thee that such or such a relation, &c., this is merely because thou hast not been a witness…'</i> He appears to have read <span dir=\"rtl\">ותטׄן</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">תטׄן</span>, and <span dir=\"rtl\">עלה̈</span> instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">ועלה̈</span>, so that, according to him, the complement of the phrase commencs only with the word <span dir=\"rtl\">עלה̈</span>.",
    "fn.3902": "That is to say, the stations of the Hebrews during the forty years they spent in the desert (Numbers, chapter <small>XXXIII</small>).",
    "fn.3903": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ויתטרק אליה אלתכדׄיב</span> appear to signify, word for word: 'the denial finds access therein.'</i> The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">تطرّق</span> (V<sup>th</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">طرق</span>) is not to be found in the dictionaries. Ibn-Tibbon renders it: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואפשר שיכזיבם השומע</span>. Further on, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אנה מיחטרק להדה אלמעגזאת...</span> (that in the future there would <i>befall</i> these miracles that which <i>befalls</i> traditions) are thus rendered by Ibn-Tibbon: <span dir=\"rtl\">שאפשר לפקפק באלו המופתים בעתיד כמו שמפקפקין בשאר הספורים</span>.",
    "fn.3904": "See, for example, the rationalist opinion of 'Hiwwi-Balkhi cited by Ibn-Ezra in the Commentary on Exodus, chap. <small>XVI</small>, <i>v.</i> 13.",
    "fn.3905": "See Joshua, chap. <small>VI</small>, <i>v.</i> 26.",
    "fn.3406": "Ibn-Tibbon renders the words <span dir=\"rtl\">product</span> imprecisely as <span dir=\"rtl\">what is fitting for him</span>. Al-’Harîzi renders this phrase more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">how much are you worthy to do in his honor</span>.",
    "fn.3407": "Ibn-Tibbon's translation, <span dir=\"rtl\">בנכבד שבדברים</span>, is inaccurate; Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלו נעזר בגדול מכל בני אדם במעלתו</span>.",
    "fn.3411": "The Arabic phrase is very irregularly constructed; it literally says this: <i>Elles ne sont pas comme les mœurs des païens, qu'ils croient être des vertus par lesquelles on loue l'homme pour son orgueil et pour sa partialité à l'égard du premier venu.</i>",
    "fn.3649": "On this provision and the following ones, see Leviticus, <i>ibid</i>., <i>v</i>. 6 and following; Mischnâ, V<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Zeba’hîm</i>, chapter <small>V</small>, §§ 3, 5 and following.",
    "fn.3650": "See below, p. 376, note 3.",
    "fn.3651": "That is, in addition to the value of the sacred object from which he has profited. See Leviticus, chapter <small>V</small>, <i>v</i>. 15 and 16.",
    "fn.3652": "See Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XV</small>, <i>v</i>. 19.",
    "fn.3294": "Ibn-Tibbon put the plural: שהם מיוחדים וגו׳, and according to him the feminine verb חבץ would relate to the three terms: כלק מא או ראי או צל אח אעמאל; it seems to me more natural to relate the verb תכׄץ to כל מצוה.",
    "fn.3295": "The infinitive <span dir=\"rtl\">חחבע</span> is not expressed in Ibn-Tibbon's version, which adds to its place the months <span dir=\"rtl\">מצוה מצוה</span>; Al-’Harîzi translates more literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">אשוב לדקדק מצות כל חלק מהם</span>.",
    "fn.3757": "See Exodus, chapter <small>XII</small>, <i>verse</i>. 22.",
    "fn.3758": "See Numbers, chapter <small>XIX</small>, <i>v</i>. 9 and 17.",
    "fn.3759": "Ibn-Tibbon adds the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ולאכול בקדשים</span>, which are not expressed in any of our Ar. mss., nor in Al-’Harîzi's version.",
    "fn.3760": "On the analogous customs of the Indous and other peoples, see my Reflections, etc., p. 71.",
    "fn.3687": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>IV</small>, <i>v</i>. 4 and 14.",
    "fn.3688": "Vide Numbers, chap. <small>XV</small>, <i>v</i>. 24: <span dir=\"rtl\">ושעיר עזים אחד לחטאת</span>, <i>and one he-goat</i> (i.e., a male) <i>for a sin offering</i>, besides the bullock which is a burnt offering. The author, contrary to the tradition of which one has spoken in the preceding notes, appears to admit that the high priest also offers an <i>he-goat</i> (a male), and not a <i>she-goat</i> (a female). — Having established as a principle that the gravest sin is expiated by a sacrifice of lesser value, the author deems it necessary to indicate a reason why the king, the high priest, and the great tribunal form an exception to the rule and offer a male of the small cattle, or even a bullock.",
    "fn.3697": "Ibn-Tibbon did not render the words <span dir=\"rtl\">וּכְפָהָא עַן אָלְפַכְרָה</span> ; Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">he took it in abrogation of his thoughts and to put an end to his designs of thought about a matter of interest in the world</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ויחשוב</span> that follows in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version is superfluous and does not appear in the mss. of this version.",
    "fn.3685": "That is to say, the king, by reason of his distinguished rank, offers a male when he has committed any sin of inadvertence (vide Leviticus, chap. <small>IV</small>, <i>v</i>. 22); tradition excepts the sin of idolatry, for which, as has been seen in the preceding note, he offers a she-goat as a mere private individual.—One has adopted the reading <span dir=\"rtl\">ולמויה אלמלך</span>, ob prœstantiam regis, which appears preferable, albeit it is found in but a single one of our MSS. (suppl. hébr. n° 63); all others have <span dir=\"rtl\">ולמיזה̈</span>, but the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ميزة</span> is not found in the dictionaries.",
    "fn.3686": "That is, the sin attributed to them by the Law consists of a legally erroneous decision that serves as a rule of conduct for each individual. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">צבור</span>, <i>community</i>, has here the sense of the biblical word <span dir=\"rtl\">עדה</span>, by which, in the biblical passages related to these sacrifices, the rabbis understand the great tribunal or Synhedrin. See above, p. 328, note 2.",
    "fn.3713": "The version of Ibn-Tibbon renders <span dir=\"rtl\">burdens</span>, <i>loads</i>; that of Al-’Harîzi has, in accordance with the Arabic text, <span dir=\"rtl\">corpses</span>.",
    "fn.3714": "Literally: to leave an image in the soul <i>. Ibn-Tibbon substituted the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">צורה̈</span>, <i>image</i>, for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מורא</span>, <i>crainte</i>.",
    "fn.3715": "On the libations of the Hebrews and pagans, see my <i>Reflections, etc.</i>, p. 39; <i>Palestine</i>, p. 163 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3706": "That is to say, to the three great festivals of the Passover, of Pentecost, and of Tabernacles, and to the other solemn days, such as the Day of Atonement and the first day of the <i>seventh</i> month, upon which a goat was offered as a sin-offering, beyond that of the ordinary new moons. Vide Numbers, chap. <small>XXVIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 21, 30; chap. <small>XXIX</small>, <i>v</i>. 5-6, 11, 16, 19, 22, 25, 28, 31, 34, 37.",
    "fn.3707": "Several mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">the city</span> (with <i>daleth</i>) instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">the plain</span> (with <i>resch</i>); Ibn-Tibbon's version, which has <span dir=\"rtl\">new language</span>, is in favor of the reading we have adopted.",
    "fn.3761": "See Mischnâ, II<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Pesa’him</i>, chap. <small>VII</small>, § 7; Talmud of Babylon, same treatise, fol. 80 <i>b; Yoma</i>, fol. 7 <i>b</i>. Cf. Exodus, chap. <small>XXVIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 38.",
    "fn.3762": "That is to say, the scapegoat of the day of atonements (Leviticus, XVI, 27), and the goat offered for the sin of idolatry (Numbers, XV, 24, and above, p. 375, note 2), and which, according to tradition, is burned. See Maimonides, Mischné Torâ, book VIII, treatise Ma’asé ha-korbanoth, chap. I, § 16. These goats also symbolically take away sins.",
    "fn.3763": "See Numbers, chap. <small>XIX </small>, <i>v. </i> 8, 10, 21, and 22; Leviticus, chap. <small>XVI </small>, <i>v. </i> 28; Mishnah, V <sup>e </sup> part, tractate <i>Zeba’him </i>, chap. <small>XII </small>, § 5.",
    "fn.3764": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>XVI</small>, <i>verse</i>. 26.",
    "fn.3700": "See Leviticus, chap. <small>IX</small>, <i>v</i>. 2, and chap. <small>XVI</small>, <i>v</i>. 3. The words <i>and to all those of his race who were to replace him</i> refer only to the bull, of which it is a question in the second passage; for the sacrifice of a calf, of which the first passage speaks, was imposed only on Aaron alone.",
    "fn.3701": "See previously, p. 378, note 1.",
    "fn.3765": "See above, chap. <small>XXXV</small>, p. 272, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 3.",
    "fn.3766": "Thus, for example, the Jewish physician Isaac Israïli (of the X<sup>e</sup> century) extols pork as a very healthy food. See Sprengel, <i>History of Medicine</i>, French translation by A. J. L. Jourdan, vol. II, p. 323.",
    "fn.3767": "That is, a food that is too substantial. On the unhealthy nature of pork meat, cf. Michaelis, <i>Mosaisches Recht</i>, t. IV, § 203 (edition of 1774, p. 190). On impure animals in general, and on dietary laws among different peoples of the Orient, see my <i>Réflexions, etc.</i>, p. 60, and <i>Palestine</i>, p. 166-168.",
    "fn.3768": "See Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 13-15, and above, ch. <small>XLI</small>, p. 333.",
    "fn.3716": "On these faculties and their respective seats, cf. t. I, p. 355, note 1, and above, p. 80, note 4. The physical faculty is called here the <i>appétition</i>; it is that which Plato calls <i>ἐπιθυμία</i>. Cf. my <i>Notice sur R. Saadia Gaon</i>, p. 9 and 10 (<i>Bible</i>, by M. Cahen, t. IX, p. 81 and 82).",
    "fn.3717": "The author is referring to the commandment to assemble the entire people, men, women, and children, every seven years, to hear the reading of the Law. See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXXI</small>, <i>v</i>. 10-13. Verse <i>12 begins with the word <span dir=\"rtl\">הַקְהֵל</span>, <i>assemble</i>.",
    "fn.3814": "See Exodus, chapter <small>XXII</small>, <i>verse</i> 16. The biblical text only speaks of the father's refusal; but the Talmud explicitly states, as reason also dictates, that the young woman can refuse the marriage. See <i>Kethouboth</i>, fol. 39 <i>b:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">She herself is the one who determines the number of years she will abstain from marriage</span>.",
    "fn.3815": "According to tradition, the punishment for rape also involves paying a stronger indemnity than that paid by the seducer. See Mishnah, III<sup>rd</sup> part, tractate <i>Kethouboth</i>, chapter <small>III</small>, § 4.",
    "fn.3816": "See Genesis, chapter <small>XXXVIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 8, and <i>Palestine</i>, p. 204 <i>b</i>. This custom also existed among the Indians. See <i>Lois de Manou</i>, <small>III</small>, 17-39; <small>IX</small>, 97.",
    "fn.3742": "Word for word: <i>in regard to the mothers and roots of the commandments;</i> that is, the Scripture, when speaking of the transgression of fundamental laws, calls this transgression <i>defilement</i> or <i>impurity</i>, an expression used in relation to fundamental commandments that concern idolatry, incest, and murder.",
    "fn.3743": "Ibn-Tibbon's version has here <span dir=\"rtl\">either to bear a certain word on one's shoulders</span>. The Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">distance</span> , infinitive of the III<sup>rd</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">roof</span>  (to cover with a <i>roof</i>), means <i>to be under the same roof with something</i> ; earlier, Ibn-Tibbon rendered this word better by <span dir=\"rtl\">to dwell</span>.",
    "fn.3744": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Berakhôth</i>, fol. 22 <i>a</i>, where it is said that it is permitted for people afflicted with gonorrhea, leprosy, etc., to read the Law, the prophets, and the other sacred books, because the words of the Law are not susceptible to defilement.",
    "fn.3745": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>XXI</small>, <i>v</i>. 2. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מבאשרה̈</span>, <i>contact</i>, was rendered in the version of Ibn-Tibbon by <span dir=\"rtl\">להמנע מקרוב … ולעמוד עמהם ולנגוע בהם</span>.",
    "fn.3746": "See Leviticus, chap. <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 10-12. This law concerning the High Priest is sufficiently motivated in the biblical text, and the reason which the author seeks for it is scarcely plausible; it is nowhere stated that the High Priest ought never to quit the sanctuary, and above all, such a prohibition is nowhere attached to the passage <span dir=\"rtl\">והיה על מצחו תמיד</span>. Cf., however, Mishnah, Fourth<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, chap. <small>II</small>, § 1, the words of Rabbi Yehudah, and Maimonides, <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book VIII, treatise <i>Klei ha-Mikdash</i>, chap. <small>V</small>, § 5.",
    "fn.3725": "See the Babylonian Talmud, treatise ’<i>Haghîgâ</i>, fol. 7 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3726": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>XI</small>, <i>v</i>. 29-30, which lists eight species of reptiles, mostly <i>saurians</i>, whose contact renders impure. Cf. <i>Palestine</i>, p. 27 <i>a</i>. On the contact of a corpse, see the same chapter of Leviticus, <i>v</i>. 39-40.",
    "fn.3737": "That is to say, of the Guebres, adherents of the Zoroastrian religion. According to the Zend-Avesta, the menses originate from Ahriman, and a great chastisement is reserved for him who approaches a woman during her critical period. See <i>Zend-Avesta</i> by Anquetil-Duperron, vol. I, 2nd part, Vendidad, farg. <small>XVI</small>, p. 397 et seq.; farg. <small>XVIII</small>, p. 411 et seq. — In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">המגורתי</span> is a misprint; the manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">המגוסי</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">שארית עובדי אש</span>.",
    "fn.3738": "That is, no doubt: one purifies by fire.",
    "fn.3739": "See Talmud of Babylone, treatise Kethoubôth, fol. 4b and 61a.",
    "fn.3740": "Cf. Zend-Avesta<i>, </i>ibid.<i>, p. 400 and following.",
    "fn.3741": "That is, moral sanctification through the observance of commandments or moral precepts. Cf. t. I, chap. <small>LIV</small>, p. 224, and above, chap. <small>XXXIII</small>, p. 263, note 4.",
    "fn.3884": "The expression <span dir=\"rtl\">عَمَرَ مَجْلِسَهُ</span> properly signifies <i>he frequented his place of assembly</i> or <i>his council chamber</i>. Cf. the <i>Assemblies of Hariri</i> (ed. of Silvestre de Sacy), p. 199: <span dir=\"rtl\">واتّخذنا ناديا نعتمره</span>, where, according to the commentary, the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">اعتمر</span> is taken in the sense of <i>to visit an assembly</i>. The meaning, therefore, is that King Manasseh incessantly caused the criticism of these passages to resound in his council chamber.",
    "fn.3885": "Literally: <i>interpreting</i> (the Scripture) <i>by means of Haggadoth of blasphemy</i>, that is to say, by making ill-conceived jests upon the sacred text. See Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Sanhedrin</i>, fol. 99 <i>b</i>, in which several of these criticisms by King Manasseh, son of Hezekiah, are cited.—In our editions of the Talmud, one reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא ואחות וגו׳</span>; likewise in most of the Arabic and Hebrew MSS. of the <i>Guide</i>. But the conjunction <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא</span>, which here appears out of place, has been omitted in some ancient Arabic MSS. and in the version of Al-’Harîzi.",
    "fn.3299": "According to rabbinic tradition, the word <i>vieillesse</i> here designates \"science\" or \"learning,\" and by this passage of Leviticus, one is enjoined to respect and honour men of learning. Already in Onkelos’s Chaldaic version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מפני שיבה תקום</span> are rendered in the same sense: <span dir=\"rtl\">מן קדם דסבר באוריתא תקום</span>, \"rise before him who is versed in the law.\" Cf. Maimonides, <i>Sépher ha-miçwôth</i>, affirmative commandments, No. 209.",
    "fn.3300": "That is to say, to be modest and humble before aged men; according to the Talmud, every old man, even if a pagan, ought to be an object of respect, even for the learned. Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Kiddouschîn</i>, fol. 32 <i>b;</i> Maimonides, treatise <i>Talmud Torâ</i>, chap. <small>VI</small>, § 9.—The Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">חיא</span> is rendered in the two Hebrew versions by two words: <span dir=\"rtl\">בשת וענוה</span>.",
    "fn.3302": "Cf. <i>Sֹוphיr miוטיט</i>, affirmative precepts, no. 59, at the end, and <i>Mischnי Torב</i>, or Talmudic digest, book III, tractate <i>Taֹיטיth</i> (fasting), chap. I, ± 1.",
    "fn.3303": "The author takes the word <span dir=\"rtl\">קרי</span> here in the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">case, </span>chance, accident;<i> similarly Ibn-Ezra and David Kim’hi (Rad. </i>קרה<span dir=\"rtl\">), according to the ancient rabbis: </span>meaning that they would say that the sufferings I will bring upon you are by chance and not a punishment for your sins<span dir=\"rtl\">. Cf. </span>Mischné Torâ, l. c.<i>, § 3.",
    "fn.3305": "Word for word: <i>there is no connection to this fracture</i>. The expression <span dir=\"rtl\">repairing a fissure</span> means <i>to reunite</i> or <i>to rejoin a fractured bone</i>, and figuratively <i>to repair a wrong</i>. The author uses the same locution several times; see chap. <small>XXIII</small>, p. 172, note 4, and the other passages indicated in this note.",
    "fn.3306": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>V</small>, <i>v.</i> 5; chapter <small>XVI</small>, <i>v.</i> 21.",
    "fn.3417": "All our manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">קלילה̈</span>, in the feminine; we have written more correctly <span dir=\"rtl\">קליל</span>, because this adjective refers to the masculine word <span dir=\"rtl\">וקוע</span>.",
    "fn.3423": "The author has here committed a most grave error, and his reasoning does err at its very base; for the text of Leviticus (chap. <small>XXVII,</small> <i>v.</i> 3) states expressly that whosoever has vowed to pay the valuation of a man aged from twenty to sixty years does pay <i>fifty shekels,</i> as Maimonides himself likewise repeats in his <i>Mishneh Torah</i> (treatise ’<i>Arakhin,</i> ch. <small>I,</small> § 3). It is to be remarked that Menahem, king of Israel, at the time of the invasion by Pul, king of Assyria, likewise ransomed his army at <i>fifty shekels of silver per man</i> (II Kings, <small>XV,</small> 20). It is truly incredible that Maimonides could have committed so egregious an error here, by simple inadvertence.",
    "fn.3424": "See Exodus, chapter <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 28 and 29.",
    "fn.3538": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>XIX</small>, v. 13; Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXIV</small>, <i>v.</i> 14-15.",
    "fn.3539": "The author refers to the law of Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 25, which permitts him who enters a vineyard to eat of the grapes according to his appetite; according to tradition, this referrs to the hireling employed in the labours of the vineyard. See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Baba Meci’a</i>, fol. 87 <i>b</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">בפועל הכתוב מדבר</span>, and cf. the Chaldaic version of Onkelos, which renders the words <span dir=\"rtl\">כי תבא</span>, 'when thou shalt enter', by <span dir=\"rtl\">ארי תתגר</span>, 'when thou shalt labour as a hireling'. By the words <i>ni même la bête</i>, the author alludes to the passage of Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXV</small>, <i>v.</i> 4, which forbidds to muzzle the ox whilst it treads out the corn.",
    "fn.3541": "See the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Yebamôth</i>, fol. 62 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.3609": "These latter words are drawn from Exodus, chap. <small>XXVIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 2, where in truth they relate solely to the costume of the high priest, which, indeed, might be termed splendid and precious, whereas that of the ordinary priests was exceedingly simple. Concerning the vestments of the priests, vide <i>Michné Torâ</i>, book VIII, treatise on the Sanctuary’s Vessels, chap. <small>VIII</small> to <small>X</small>; cf. <i>Palestine</i>, p. 174 et seq.",
    "fn.3610": "Vide Leviticus, chap. <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 16-21. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">תפסול</span>, which is Hebrew and pertains to the Talmudic idiom, has here been rendered in the third person singular feminine, in conformity with the rules of Arabic grammar, and refers to the feminine plural <span dir=\"rtl\">אלסמאגׄאת</span>; Ibn-Tibbon’s version gives <span dir=\"rtl\">פוסלים בכהנים</span>. The singularity of this Arabic construction of a Hebrew verb has given rise to a variant, <span dir=\"rtl\">חפצי לֹא לכהנים</span>, which one discovers in several manuscripts. It is this variant which Al-’Harîzi reproduced, who translates thus: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלא הכעורים כשנוי הבריה כדי לכבד הבהנים</span>.",
    "fn.3611": "See Mishnah, V<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Bekhôrôth</i>, chapter <small>VII</small>.",
    "fn.3624": "All our MSS. bear <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמכאלה̈</span> (the <span dir=\"rtl\">כ</span> without a dot; it is perhaps necessary to write <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמחבאלה (المخالة)</span>, which would here have the sense of <span dir=\"rtl\">(المخוّلة) אלמכו̇לה̇</span>. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">خمول</span> signifies <i>to place someone in possession of a thing.</i> Vide the Commentary upon the <i>Maqāmāt of Hariri</i>, p. 246: <span dir=\"rtl\">ملكه إياه خوله الله الشيء إذا</span>. But it may also be that the author has incorrectly written <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמכאלה̈</span> for <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמולה</span> from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">וכל</span>, <i>to charge one with a thing</i>, <i>to entrust it unto him.</i>",
    "fn.3625": "See Mischnâ, VIth part, treatise Kelîm, chap. I, §§ 8 and 9.",
    "fn.3626": "This was a large space of ten cubits situated between the outer balustrade and the wall of the sacred enclosure. See on this space, Mischnâ, V<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Middôth</i>, chap. <small>II</small>, § 3; cf. in general <i>Palestine</i>, p. 552 and following.",
    "fn.3627": "See above, chapter <small>XXXII</small>.",
    "fn.3641": "See Leviticus, chap. <small>XXII</small>, <i>v</i>. 27.",
    "fn.3642": "That is to say, an animal which has been presented as a gift to a harlot or which has been given in exchange for a dog. Vide Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 19; Mishnah, V<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Temurah</i>, chap. <small>IV</small>, §3; <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, treatise <i>Issurei Mizbe'ach</i>, chap. <small>IV</small>, §16. According to some commentators, the word <span dir=\"rtl\">כלב</span>, <i>dog</i>, in the passage from Deuteronomy, might bear the sense of <i>cinædus;</i> but the rabbis take it literally, understanding the price of the <i>cinædus</i> to be included in <span dir=\"rtl\">אתנן זונה</span>. Vide Talmud, treatise <i>Temurah</i>, fol. 29 <i>b</i>. It is known that dogs are, in the Orient, the object of profound contempt. Vide Jahn, <i>Biblische Archäologie</i>, vol. I, 1<sup>st</sup> volume, § 60, p. 325 et seq.",
    "fn.3643": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>I</small>, <i>v</i>. 14, and chapter <small>V</small>, <i>v</i>. 7; Mischnâ, V<sup>e</sup> part, treatise ’<i>Hullîn</i>, chapter <small>I</small>, § 5; <i>Mischné Torâ, l. c.</i>, chapter <small>III</small>, § 2.",
    "fn.3646": "With Rashi and Kimchi, we take the word <span dir=\"rtl\">וניבו</span> in the sense of <i>sa parole</i>, that is, the word by which the impious priest insults the altar, which is: <i>sa nourriture est méprisable</i>.",
    "fn.3647": "That is to say, even the priest who, by whatsoever circumstance, has not been circumcised, or who is defiled by a legal impurity. Vide Mischnâ, III<sup>rd</sup> part, treatise <i>Yebamôth</i>, chap. <small>VIII</small>, § 1, and the commentaries of Maimonides and Raschi; <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book VIII, treatise <i>Ma’asé ha-Korbanoth</i>, chap. <small>X</small>, § 9. Concerning the impure, vide Levit., chap. <small>VII</small>, <i>v</i>. 20-21.",
    "fn.3648": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>VII</small>, <i>v</i>. 16-21, and above, p. 322, note 3.",
    "fn.3653": "See above, p. 320, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 1.",
    "fn.3661": "The matter here concerns the offering, whether obligatory or voluntary, presented by a priest, and that which each priest was bound to present on the day of his installation. This latter, the high priest was required to repeat daily throughout the period of his functions, and this is what is understood in the Mischnâ by the words <span dir=\"rtl\">חביתי כהן גדול</span>; the same is confirmed by Josephus. Vide Levit., chap. <small>VI</small>, <i>v</i>. 13-16; Mischnâ, V<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Mena’hoth</i>, chap. <small>IV</small>, §5, and chap. <small>V</small>, §§3 and 5; Maimonides, Commentary on the <i>Mischnâ</i>, Introd. to the treatise <i>Mena’hoth</i> (Pococke, Porta Mosis, Notae miscellaneae, p. 431-432); Josephus, <i>Antiquities</i>, book III, chap. <small>X</small>, §7.",
    "fn.3662": "Cf. <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book VIII, treatise <i>Ma’asé ha-Korbanoth</i>, ch. <small>XII</small>, §4: <span dir=\"rtl\">מנחה שמקריב כל כהן תחלה כשיכנס לעבורה שמקריב אותה בידו.</span>",
    "fn.3663": "See Leviticus, chap. <small>II</small>, <i>v</i>. 2.",
    "fn.3724": "Cf. on this passage from Jeremiah, volume 2, chapter <small>XXXIX</small>, p. 305, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 2.",
    "fn.3727": "Ibn-Tibbon translates according to the sense: <span dir=\"rtl\">the man crushes them through his walking</span>, <i>l’homme les écrase en marchant;</i> Al-’Harîzi translates literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">and the man will stumble on them all day</span>.",
    "fn.3728": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>XV</small>, and chapter <small>XIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 45-46.",
    "fn.3729": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>XV</small>, <i>v</i>. 16-18, and Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 11-12.",
    "fn.3747": "See <i>ibid.</i>, <i>v</i>. 2, and Talmud of Babylon, treatise <i>Sôtâ</i>, fol. 23 <i>b; Mischné Torâ</i>, book XIV, treatise <i>Ebel</i> (of mourning), chap. <small>III</small>, § 11, according to the <i>Torath Kohanîm</i>.",
    "fn.3748": "See above, p. 381, note 2, and Leviticus, chapter <small>XVI</small>, <i>v</i>. 16; Mishnah, IV<sup>th</sup> part, tractate <i>Schebou’ôth</i>, chapter <small>I</small>, § 4.",
    "fn.3749": "See Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Pesa’him</i>, fol. 16 <i>b</i>; <i>Yômâ</i>, fol. 7 <i>a</i>; <i>Mena’hoth</i>, fol. 15 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3750": "See the moral considerations of the author in his <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book X, treatise <i>Toumath çara’ath</i>, chapter 5, § 16, and his Commentary on the Mischnâ, VI<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Negha’îm</i>, chapter <small>XII</small>, § 5.",
    "fn.3751": "See, amongst others, Babylonian Talmud, treatise ’<i>Arakhin</i>, fol. 16 <i>b:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">כל המספר לשון הרע נגעין באין עליו</span>. Cf. <i>Yalkut</i>, First<sup>re</sup> part, No. 937, according to the <i>Siphri</i>. Several peoples consider leprosy as a consequence of great sins committed against the Divinity. Cf. Herodotus, book I, chap. 138, and one’s Notes to the Fifth<sup>e</sup> book of the <i>Laws of Manu</i> following one’s <i>Reflections on the Cult of the Ancient Hebrews</i>, p. 77.",
    "fn.3752": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>XIV</small>, <i>v</i>. 34-48. Cf. <i>Palestine</i>, p. 213 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3753": "See <i>ibid.</i>, chapter <small>XIII</small>, <i>v</i>. 47-59, and <i>Palestine</i>, <i>l. c.</i>. For <span dir=\"rtl\">לפראשה</span>, the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon have <span dir=\"rtl\">לכלי משרתיו</span>, which appears to be a printing error; the manuscripts have <span dir=\"rtl\">לכלי מטתו</span>.",
    "fn.3754": "See Numbers, chap. <small>V</small>, <i>v</i>. 11-31, and <i>Palestine</i>, p. 205 <i>a</i>. The author intends to convey that the leprosy of houses and of garments, and consequently that of persons, was somewhat miraculous and providential, even as was the effect produced by the water administered to the woman suspected of adultery. Indeed, the biblical text itself indicates the direct intervention of Providence, saying, concerning leprosy: <span dir=\"rtl\">ונתתי נגע צרעת</span> (Levit., chap. <small>XIV</small>, <i>v</i>. 34), and concerning the adulterous woman: <span dir=\"rtl\">בתת י״י את ירכך נופלת</span> (Numbers, chap. <small>V</small>, <i>v</i>. 21), as is observed by the commentators of the Pentateuch.",
    "fn.3755": "The author means that the belief that leprosy comes as a result of certain sins is very useful for morality, especially when one considers the disgust it inspires.",
    "fn.3756": "Voy. Lévitique, chap. <small>XIV</small>, <i>v</i>. 4 and 51; <i>Wayyikra rabba</i>, sect. <small>XVI</small> (fol. 158, col. 4), and the <i>Yalkout</i>, I<sup>re</sup> part.",
    "fn.3769": "It is known that by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">فرنج</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">أفرنج</span>, the Arabs designate not only the French, but, since the Crusades, Christians in general from Europe.",
    "fn.3770": "See the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Berakhôth</i>, fol. 25 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3771": "See above, p. 321, note 4.",
    "fn.3772": "That is, to burn them on the altar or to use them for lighting. — Ibn-Tibbon added the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מאכילתו</span>, which is not expressed in any of our Arabic manuscripts; similarly Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">ועל כן היה ראוי לשריפה יותר מן האכילה</span>.",
    "fn.3812": "That is to say, as the wrong which the seducer has wrought her does consist chiefly in preventing her from finding a husband, it has been imposed upon him, as a reparation, the duty of espousing her. Vide Exodus, chap. <small>XXII</small>, <i>v.</i> 16. Here again, the author does have regard solely to the Biblical text; for, according to the Talmudic tradition, the seducer may refuse the marriage upon payment of the <i>mohar</i> (vide <i>Palestine</i>, p. 203 <i>b</i>), by way of a fine. Vide Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Kethouboth</i>, fol. 40 <i>a</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">מהר ימהרנה לו לאשה לו מדעתו</span>; Maimonides, <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, book IV, treatise <i>Na’arâ bethoulâ</i> (of the virgin maiden), chap. <small>I</small>, § 3.",
    "fn.3813": "On the expression <span dir=\"rtl\">אגׄבר לצרעהא</span>, cf. above, p. 276, note 2, and p. 325, note 1. In the editions of the version of Ibn-Tibbon, this locution is translated doubly: <span dir=\"rtl\">and yet more healer than half and yet more restorer of the matter</span>; the mss. have only the words <span dir=\"rtl\">and yet more healer than half</span>.",
    "fn.3817": "See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXV</small>, <i>v.</i> 5 and 6.",
    "fn.3818": "See <i>ibid.</i>, <i>v.</i> 7-10.",
    "fn.3819": "The author is referring to the event of Judah and his daughter-in-law Tamar (Genesis, chapter <small>XXXVIII</small>).",
    "fn.3820": "<i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book IV, treatise <i>Hilchot Ishuth</i>, chapter <small>1</small>, section 4.",
    "fn.3821": "According to traditional law, the husband, at the moment of marriage, must secure unto the wife, by means of a written document (<span dir=\"rtl\">כתובה</span>), a dowry, the minimum of which is fixed for a virgin at two hundred shekels, and for a widow at one-half thereof. Vide Mishnah, III<sup>rd</sup> part, treatise <i>Kethouboth</i>, chap. <small>I</small>, § 2; <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, <i>loc. cit.</i>, chap. <small>X</small>, § 7. It is upon the demise of the husband, or in case of divorce, that the wife is put into possession of this dowry. Here, as further on, the author does appear to confound the <i>Kethoubâ</i> with the Biblical <i>mohar</i>.",
    "fn.3822": "That is to say: against him who accuses his young wife of not having found her a virgin. See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXII</small>, <i>v.</i> 13-19.",
    "fn.3835": "This latter case is not provided for by the Law of Moses, any more than parricide; the enormity of these crimes doubtless absolved the legislator from prohibiting them by specific laws. Moreover, the Law forbidding the union of a man with his granddaughter (Levit., <small>XVIII</small>, 10), it follows a fortiori that it prohibits his union with his daughter. See Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Sanhedrin</i>, fol. 76 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3836": "The second case is omitted in most of the Arabic MSS., as well as in Ibn-Tibbon’s version, which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואין הפרש בין שיבעול השרש או הענף או שיתקבצו וגו׳</span>. One of the Oxford MSS. (Pococke, 234) reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">בין אן ינכח אצל אלפרע או פרע אלאצל</span>; others have merely <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאצל ללפרע</span>, and yet others <span dir=\"rtl\">אלפרע ללאצל</span>. The reading which one has adopted is a combination of these various readings; it is confirmed by the version of Al-’Harîzi, which reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">ואין הפרש בין שיבעל העקו לענף והענף לעיקר או שיתקבץ וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.3837": "That is, for example, that the father and son marry the same woman in succession, or that a man marries both the mother and daughter at the same time.",
    "fn.3838": "The duel <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאכׄואן</span> signifies here the <i>frère et la sœur</i>. The mss. have the oblique case: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאכׄוין</span> or <span dir=\"rtl\">אלאכׄין</span>. In Ibn-Tibbon's version, this duel was omitted; it reads: <span dir=\"rtl\">וגם כגוף אחד</span>. Al-’Harîzi translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל שני האחים הם כאיש אח</span>.",
    "fn.3839": "The text states: the two defenses are equal; that is, it is forbidden for the father to marry the widow of his son, just as it is forbidden for the son to marry the widow of his father.",
    "fn.3840": "That is, stoning. See Mishnah, IVth part, tractate Synhedrin, chap. VII, § 4.",
    "fn.3841": "See <i>Mischné Torâ</i>, V<sup>e</sup> book, treatise <i>Issouré Biâ</i>, chapter <small>XXI</small>, §§ 1 and 2.",
    "fn.3842": "See the same chapter, § 19.",
    "fn.3843": "That is, Satan or evil passion.",
    "fn.3844": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Kiddouschin</i>, fol. 30 <i>b</i>.",
    "fn.3845": "See vol. II, p. 285, and <i>ibid.</i>, note 3, and cf. above, p. 47.",
    "fn.3846": "See, for example, <i>Éthique à Nicomaque</i>, book III, chapter 13; <i>Rhétorique</i>, book I, chapter 2: <i>τῶν δ’ἐπιθυμιῶν αἱ μὲν ἄλογοί εἰσιν ϰ. τ. λ.</i>",
    "fn.3847": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Abodâ Zarâ</i>, fol. 20 <i>b; Mishnah Torah, tractate </i>Issouré Biâ<i>, chapter </i>XXI<small>, § 20.",
    "fn.3848": "See Leviticus, chap. <small>XIX</small>, <i>v.</i> 19.",
    "fn.3849": "In the version of Ibn-Tibbon, read: <span dir=\"rtl\">ושיתעסק</span>, with the <span dir=\"rtl\">ו</span> copulative conjunction, as in the manuscripts of this version.",
    "fn.3850": "Voy. Mischnâ, IVe partie, traité Baba Kamma, chap. V, § 7.",
    "fn.3852": "The author may be alluding to R. Saadia, who, in the <i>Treatise on Beliefs and Opinions</i>, book III, chapter <small>X</small>, professes this opinion: <span dir=\"rtl\">And I have explained that the complete thing is that which has neither addition nor subtraction, and the Creator created this limb with additions, and as it was necessary, the additions were removed and the remainder was complete·</span>",
    "fn.3853": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלללב</span> , which properly means <i>frenzy, avidity, voracity</i>, was not translated by Ibn-Tibbon.",
    "fn.3854": "Philo supposes the same reason for the precept of circumcision; see his work <i>de Circumcisione</i>, at the end.",
    "fn.3855": "See <i>Bereschith rabba</i>, sect. 80, fol. 70, col. 3.",
    "fn.3856": "See the Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Baba bathra</i>, where it is said, regarding Job's words boasting that he has never looked at a young girl (Job, <small>XXXI</small>, 1): <span dir=\"rtl\">איהו באחרניתא אברהם אפילו בדידיה לא אסתכל</span> 'he had not looked at a foreign woman; Abraham had not even looked at his own.' Cf. the commentary of Raschi on the passage of Genesis.",
    "fn.3857": "It might here be objected that circumcision was practised by several peoples of antiquity, notably by the Egyptians, as stated by Herodotus (Lib. II, c. 36 and 104), and as confirmed by two Jewish authors, Philo (<i>de Circumcisione</i>) and Josephus (<i>Contra Apionem</i>, Book II, Chap. 13). But, the circumcision of the Jews differed essentially from that of other ancient peoples, as well as from that of the Christians of Ethiopia and of the Muslims. This distinction is so significant that the circumcision of the Jews alone could be regarded as a distinctive mark, which caused Tacitus (<i>Histories</i>, Book V, Chap. 5) to remark: circumcidere genitalia instituere <i>ut diversitate noscantur</i>. On this grave historical question, see, among others, the authors cited by Milius, <i>Dissert. de Mohammedismo ante Mohamedem</i>, § 16, and Louis Marcus, <i>Mémoire sur l’époque de l’établissement des Juifs dans l’Abyssinie</i>, in the <i>Journal Asiatique</i>, June 1829, p. 419 et seq.",
    "fn.3858": "Ibn-Tibbon adds these explanatory words, <span dir=\"rtl\">meaning the word</span>, <i>I mean the circumcision</i>.",
    "fn.3859": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">ואלתזאם עהדה</span> are omitted in Ibn-Tibbon's version.",
    "fn.3860": "Ibn-Tibbon incorrectly rendered this phrase, unless he had another reading; it appears he read: <span dir=\"rtl\">אנמא תם באלכׄתאן וכונה פי סן אלצגר וגו׳</span>.",
    "fn.3861": "By the imaginative form, the author here means the affection or instinctive sensibility that, according to the classification of Arab authors, is related to the imaginative faculty. Cf. Kazwini, in the Chrestomathie arabe of Silvestre de Sacy, vol. III, p. 488.",
    "fn.3862": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">briah</span>, <i>by the sight</i>, which the version of Ibn-Tibbon has, does not render the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">bilmashra‘a</span> accurately enough.",
    "fn.3863": "See the Babylonian Talmud, treatise Kiddushin, fol. 29a; Tosiphta, same treatise, chap. I: What is the commandment of a father towards his son, etc.",
    "fn.3864": "That is, the time of circumcision is fixed and well determined, and it is not made to depend on the more or less development of the child or other circumstances. On the talmudic expression <span dir=\"rtl\">נתת דבריך לשיעורין</span>, see above, p. 267, note 6.",
    "fn.3865": "See Leviticus, chap. <small>XXII</small>, <i>v.</i> 24.",
    "fn.3866": "Cf. above, chapter <small>XXVI</small>, p. 203.",
    "fn.3867": "See Deuteronomy, chap. <small>XXIII</small>, <i>v.</i> 2.",
    "fn.3868": "That is, he would compromise the woman's conduct and the husband's honor.",
    "fn.3869": "See Deuteronomy, chapter <small>XXIII</small>, <i>verse</i> 3; Mishnah, III<sup>rd</sup> part, tractate <i>Yebamoth</i>, chapter <small>IV</small>, section 13; <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book V, tractate <i>Issurei Biah</i>, chapter <small>XV</small>, sections 1 and 2.",
    "fn.3870": "On the word صنع, see above, p. 407, note 1, and the other passages indicated, p. 172, note 2.",
    "fn.3871": "Lit.: <i>in each custom and in each nation, or religion</i>.",
    "fn.3872": "Literally: <i>a profaned woman</i> or <i>dishonored woman</i>; that is, born of the marriage of a priest with a woman whom he is forbidden to marry. See Leviticus, chapter <small>XXI</small>, <i>v.</i> 7, and the Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Kiddouschin</i>, fol.77 <i>a</i>.",
    "fn.3873": "See Leviticus, chapter <small>XXI</small>, <i>verse</i> 13 and 14.",
    "fn.3874": "See Mischnâ, III<sup>e</sup> part, treatise <i>Kiddouschin</i>, chap. <small>III</small>, § 12; Talmud, <i>ibid.</i>, fol. 68. Cf. Targoum d’Onkelos, Deutér., <small>XXIII</small>, 18.",
    "fn.3875": "See above, chapter <small>XXVI</small>, p. 204.",
    "fn.3876": "The Arabs have several proverbs that express this idea; they say, among other things, <span dir=\"rtl\">ليس الخبر كالعيان</span>. See my <i>Notice sur Abou’l Walid</i>, in the <i>Journal asiatique</i>, September 1850, p. 223, note 3 (separate printing, p. 107, note 3).",
    "fn.3877": "On the meaning of the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">استقلّ</span> , cf. vol. II, p. 270, note 2.",
    "fn.3878": "See above, p. 230, note 2. We have reproduced the citation here as it appears in the oldest manuscripts of the Arabic text and Ibn-Tibbon's translation; in several manuscripts, the passage from the first book of Samuel, XII, 21, or that of Jeremiah, II, 8, or even both passages together, have been substituted.",
    "fn.3879": "Literally: <i>qu'il fallait user</i> (to spend) <i>tous ses efforts to make cease</i>. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">אבלא אלמגׄהוד</span> are rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version as <span dir=\"rtl\">להשתדל האדם בכל יכלתו</span>.",
    "fn.3883": "In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, these latter words are thus paraphrased: <span dir=\"rtl\">טעם יקל לאיש תבונה להוציאו מבח דברינו</span>, a reading which, in some MSS. of this version, is indicated as a <i>variant</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">לשון אחר</span>); and the MSS. bear, in conformity with the Arabic text: <span dir=\"rtl\">בבר נתתי גם בהם טעם בכח קרוב לכל בעל תבונה</span>. In a gloss from MS. No. 47 of the Oratory, the term <span dir=\"rtl\">בח רחוק</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(قوة بعيدة)</span>, <i>remote power</i>, is lengthily explained, e.g., the faculty an infant possesses of learning to write; and that of <span dir=\"rtl\">כח קרוב</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(قوة قريبة)</span>, <i>proximate power</i>, e.g., the faculty of writing possessed by an adult who knows how to write. Then it is added: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכן כתב הרב שאע״פ שלא זכר בפועל טעמי קצת פרטי מצות זכרם בכח ולא בכח רחוק אלא בכח קרוב יוציאם מדבריו המבין בקרוב</span> “Likewise, our master here writes that, although he has not <i>in act</i> expounded the reasons for certain details of the commandments, he has expounded them <i>in potency</i> (virtually), and not in a <i>remote</i> manner, but in a <i>proximate</i> manner, which the intelligent man may promptly deduce from his words.” Cf. further on, Chap. <small>LI</small>, p. 444, the words <i>proximate power</i>, and the example by which the author elucidates these words.",
    "fn.3889": "The participle <span dir=\"rtl\">מחתפל</span>, which all the arabic mss. have here, has been poorly rendered in Ibn-Tibbon's version as <span dir=\"rtl\">מתפאר</span>. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">احتفل</span> signifies <i>animum advertit, curavit, studuit</i>.",
    "fn.3890": "See Gen. chap. <small>XXXVI</small>, <i>v.</i> 20-30.",
    "fn.3891": "See <i>ibid., vv.</i> 12 and 22. It is observed that, according to the author's opinion, the Amalekites descended from Eliphaz, son of Esau, which is also the opinion of Josephus (<i>Antiquities</i>, Book II, Chap. <small>I</small>, §2); if, therefore, in the history of Abraham, mention is already made of the territory of the <i>Amalekites</i> (Genesis, <small>XIV</small>, 7), one must admit that the author of Genesis employed this name by anticipation, as Rashi already observes in his Commentary upon that passage.",
    "fn.3892": "According to a passage in the book of Genesis, chapter <small>XXXVI</small>, <i>v.</i> 25, Oholibama, one of Esau's wives, descended from the Seirites, although, in another passage (<i>ibid., v.</i> 2), she is called 'Hiwite.'",
    "fn.3893": "That is, because Timna’, mother of Amalek, belonged to the race of the Séirites. Ibn-Tibbon made a mistake in translating: <span dir=\"rtl\">להיות אמם מעמלק</span>; Al-’Harîzi translated: <span dir=\"rtl\">מפני שהיתה אמו מהם</span>.",
    "fn.3894": "On the meaning of the word גמאר (غبار), see the t. I, p. 223, note 3.",
    "fn.3895": "See above, chapter <small>XLI</small>, p. 332.",
    "fn.3896": "Maimonides may have followed here the <i>Sépher hayaschar</i>; cf. the <i>Biour</i>, or Hebrew Commentary by Mendelssohn on this passage. It does not positively follow from the biblical text that these kings of the Idumeans were all foreigners; on the contrary, Yobab, from <i>Bosra</i>, and 'Houscham, from the land of the <i>Témanites</i> (<i>v.</i> 33 and 34), were very probably Idumeans.",
    "fn.3898": "I believe that the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">פדׄכרהם</span> should be pronounced in the II<sup>e</sup> form <span dir=\"rtl\">(فذكرهم)</span> ; the suffix of this verb appears to relate to the <i>Israelites</i> implied, and the word-for-word translation would be: <i>we warn them through them;</i> that is, we give the Israelites a warning by reminding them of the conduct of the kings of Edom.",
    "fn.3899": "See the preceding chapter, towards the end, p. 422.",
    "fn.3900": "The author, as we shall see, wants to speak of written relationships that sometimes seem prolix to the reader, but which the reader would well understand the lengths of if he had been a witness to the events and grasped their necessary sequence. In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מפני האריכות</span>, at the end of the sentence, are a nonsensical; the mss. of this version have: <span dir=\"rtl\">only because of the prolixity</span>.",
    "fn.3906": "It is known that the Arabs call the desert that the Israelites traversed the name of <span dir=\"rtl\">al-nīyah</span> , or of <span dir=\"rtl\">tīh b. Isrā'īl</span>  (the error of the Israelites); the name of <span dir=\"rtl\">'ayyad</span>  comes from the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">tāra</span> , <i>he wandered over the land, was bewildered and strayed</i>.",
    "fn.3907": "See <i>Séder Olam rabba</i>, chapter <small>VIII</small>, at the end, where it is said that the Israelites camped at Kadesch for nineteen years; cf. Deuteronomy, chapter <small>I</small>, <i>v.</i> 46, and the Commentary of Raschi on this verse.",
    "fn.3908": "Voy. Numbers, chap. <small>IX</small>, <i>v.</i> 21.",
    "fn.3909": "Properly: <small>those who demolish</small>, or <i>those who destroy</i>, those who overturn religious opinions.",
    "fn.3910": "Literally: <i>who comprehends the particular truths pertaining to him</i>, that is, the true duties to be exercised towards God. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">בה</span>, <i>towards him</i>, refers to <i>God</i>, and one must understand <span dir=\"rtl\">the Exalted</span>. Ibn-Tibbon translates, according to the sense: <span dir=\"rtl\">those which are particularized in the name of the Most High</span>. Al-’Harîzi has the literal translation: <span dir=\"rtl\">those which are particularized in Him</span>.",
    "fn.3911": "The words לצרור החיים, literally: in the bundle of life, are taken from the First book of Samuel, chap. XXV, v. 29, where, according to commentators, they designate the future life. See my Réflexions sur le culte des anciens Hébreux (t. IV of the Bible of M. Cahen), p. 7, note.",
    "fn.3912": "Cf. above, chap. <small>XXIX</small>, p. 221.—The words <span dir=\"rtl\">טאמחונגלין פי טאשמאל</span> signify literally: 'who penetrate' or 'who delve into the North.'</i> Ibn-Tibbon renders it <span dir=\"rtl\">המשוטטים בצפון</span>, 'who <i>wander</i> in the North'; doubtless he had the reading <span dir=\"rtl\">אלמתגוליק</span>, which one also finds in MS. No. 63 of the Hebrew Supplement of the Imperial Library.",
    "fn.3913": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">reason</span>, <i>opinion</i>, the version of Ibn-Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">faith</span>, <i>faith</i>; Al-’Harîzi translates more accurately: <span dir=\"rtl\">men of reason and understanding</span>.",
    "fn.3914": "Cf. vol. I, p. 7, note 1, on the meaning of the word <span dir=\"rtl\">פקה</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">فقه</span> . The adjective <span dir=\"rtl\">فتية</span>  (pl. <span dir=\"rtl\">فقهاء</span> ) refers to one who deals with <i>fiqh</i> or canon law, and who accepts <i>by tradition</i> the fundamental principles of the religion; in particular, among Jews, it is the talmudist.",
    "fn.3915": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate ’<i>Haghigâ</i>, fol. 15 <i>a</i>, where it is a question of a question related to creation and of which Ben-Zôma did not know how to give an exact account. Cf. <i>Beréschith rabbâ</i>, section 2 (fol. 2, col. 4); Talmud of Jerusalem, ’<i>Haghigâ</i>, chapter <small>II</small> (<i>Yephé Mareh, ibid.</i>, § 5).",
    "fn.3916": "Ibn-Tibbon expresses himself more briefly: <span dir=\"rtl\">כבר נכנסת בפרוזדור הבית</span>; similarly Ibn-Falaquéra, <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, p. 132. Al-’Harîzi translates literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">אז אתה נכנס בבית והולך במבואות החצר</span>.",
    "fn.3917": "See vol. II, chap. <small>XLV</small>.",
    "fn.3918": "The words מע כונה do not mean 'wherever they are found' here, but should be translated as 'at the same time as they are found' or 'in addition to where they are found.' Ibn-Falaquéra, l.c., p. 133, translates: הוא אצלי כמי שהוא מחוץ הבית.",
    "fn.3919": "See vol. I, chap. <small>L</small>.",
    "fn.3920": "Ibn-Tibbon has: <span dir=\"rtl\">כפי מה שישכלהו השכל</span>; instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">שישכלהו</span>, the mss. have <span dir=\"rtl\">שיחבלהו</span>. Ibn-Falaquéra, <i>l. c.</i>, translates more literally: <span dir=\"rtl\">כפי שיחייב אותו השכל</span>.",
    "fn.3921": "See the <i>Annotation</i> of the author below.",
    "fn.3922": "See vol. I, p. 144, and <i>ibid.</i>, notes 2 and 3, and above, p. 215.",
    "fn.3923": "Vide the <i>Siphri</i>, at the passage of Deuteronomy just cited, Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Taanîth</i>, fol. 2 <i>a;</i> Jerusalem Talmud, treatise <i>Berakhôth</i>, chap. <small>IV</small>, at the commencement. The Rabbins, 'tis true, speak only of <i>prayer;</i> but our author appears to suppose that they generally allude to a state of the soul similar to that which he has just depicted.",
    "fn.3924": "The words <span dir=\"rtl\">מעקול אול</span>, which generally signify <i>notion première</i>, designate here the being that human intelligence has primarily for the object of understanding, that is, <i>God</i>.",
    "fn.3932": "That is to say, the verse <i>Hear, O Israel, etc.</i> (Deuteronomy <small>VI</small>, 4), and the first of the <i>eighteen benedictions</i> contained in the prayer called <i>Shemoneh Esreh</i>, or ’<i>Amidah</i>. Legally, attention is required only for the first verse of the <i>Shema‘</i> and for the first benediction of the prayer. Vide the Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Berakhoth</i>, fol. 13 <i>b</i> and fol. 34 <i>b;</i> Maimonides, <i>Mishneh Torah</i>, book II, treatise <i>Kerîath Shema‘</i>, chap. <small>II</small>, § 1; treatise <i>Tephillah</i>, chap. <small>X</small>, § 1. Here Maimonides speaks of those men of excellence who aspire to a higher aim.",
    "fn.3933": "The Chaldaic words <span dir=\"rtl\">מילי דעלמא</span>, <i>things of the world</i>, are a very common rabbinic expression, for which the author has just employed the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">אמור אלדניא</span>.",
    "fn.3934": "Literally: <i>not through the path of imaginary impressions</i>. The author means that one must approach God through true philosophical meditation, with all the clarity of intelligence, and not under the influence of an exalted imagination like that of the Pietists.",
    "fn.3935": "See vol. I, p. 11, 63, and 71; vol. II, p. 267 (where the verb in the preterite is a printing error), and above, p. 436.",
    "fn.3970": "The author calls <i>relatives</i> the good and evil that occur in the world, because they exist only relatively to creatures and accidentally (see above, chapter <small>XI</small> and <small>XII</small>); in relation to God, there is neither good nor evil, in the absolute sense.",
    "fn.3971": "The author intends to discuss not only the noun <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמה</span>, but also, as will be seen in the examples, the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">חכם</span> and the adjectives derived from it. Cf. t. I, p. 6, note 1.",
    "fn.3972": "Cf. t. I, ch. <small>XXXIV</small>, fourth cause, p. 125 and following, and above, p. 449. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">wisdom</span>, in the verse cited from Job, could also be taken in the first sense.",
    "fn.3973": "See the Babylonian Talmud, tractate <i>Meghillâ</i>, fol. 13 <i>a.</i>. It follows from this passage that the doctors attribute to Moses not only the Law, but also knowledge, and that knowledge or philosophy is a separate thing.",
    "fn.3974": "Cf. Babylonian Talmud, treatise <i>Rosch haschana</i>, fol. 21 <i>b:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">ביקש קהלת להיות כמשה יצתה בת קול ואמרה לו ולא קם נביא עוד בישראל כמשה</span>. Elsewhere, however, it is stated that Heman, one of the personages whom Solomon surpassed in wisdom, is Moses: <span dir=\"rtl\">הימן זה משה</span>. Vide <i>Tan’houma</i>, section <span dir=\"rtl\">חקת</span> (Verona edition, fol. 77, col. 1).",
    "fn.3975": "Literally: <i>to make him bring out what he should do.</i>",
    "fn.3976": "Cf. Vol. I<sup>er</sup>, Introduction, p. 13.",
    "fn.3977": "See Talmud of Babylon, tractate <i>Schabbath</i>, fol. 31 <i>a.</i>",
    "fn.3978": "See <i>Nicomachean Ethics</i>, book I, chapter 8 and following, where Aristotle discusses three types of goods, one of which, encompassing the goods of the soul, is counted by Maimonides as the 3<sup>rd</sup> and 4<sup>th</sup> types: the former encompassing moral qualities, the latter, intellectual qualities.",
    "fn.3979": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">أصبح</span>, which properly signifies <i>to be early</i>, has here the sense of factus est, evasit; Ibn-Tibbon renders it: <span dir=\"rtl\">ישכים האיש ההוא</span>. The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">השכים</span>, in Biblical idiom, is sometimes employed in the same sense; a construction altogether analogous to our passage is found in II Kings, 19, 35, and Isaiah, 37, 36: <span dir=\"rtl\">וישכימו בבקר והנה כלם פגרים מתים</span>.",
    "fn.3980": "Word for word: as we have mentioned or said; but, the author having nowhere spoken, as far as we know, of this supreme degree of human bodily forces, we believe he wants to speak of what immediately precedes, and we have slightly modified the translation of the words כמא דׄכרנא[5].",
    "fn.3981": "Ibn-Tibbon renders the words <span dir=\"rtl\">באעתבאר אלגיר</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">עם זולתו</span>, which lacks clarity; he should have said: <span dir=\"rtl\">בבחינת זולתו</span>.",
    "fn.3982": "That is to say: they benefit others, but not the one who possesses them, since they do not contribute to perfecting his true essence.",
    "fn.3992": "See above, chapter <small>XVII</small> (2<sup>nd</sup> opinion), pp. 116-119.",
    "fn.3993": "See vol. I, p. 216, note 2.",
    "fn.3994": "More literally: <i>that His Providence extends also unto the earth in so far as it concerns it</i>, <i>as He</i> (<i>God</i>) <i>watches over the heavens in so far as they concern them</i>. To fully apprehend the term <span dir=\"rtl\">בחסבהא</span>, <i>in so far as it</i> (<i>it</i>) <i>concerns it</i>, one must recall what the author has stated above concerning Aristotle’s opinion: «He believs that <i>Providence corresponds to the nature of beings;</i> consequently, the celestial spheres and the bodies they contain being permanent, that which constituts Providence in their regard is their remaining ever invariably in the same state, etc. (p. 116-117).» Aristotle’s opinion is shared to a certain extent by the author (<i>ibid.</i>, p. 130).",
    "fn.3995": "See Vol. I, chapter <small>LIV</small>, p. 218.",
    "fn.3699": "According to Ephôdi, the author would here allude to that which he has stated in chapter <small>V</small> of the I<sup>st</sup> part (p. 47): “He should decide naught according to a first idea that might occur to him, nor suffer his thoughts to run freely at the outset, directing them resolutely towards the knowledge of God; but he should exercise modesty and reserve, and pause betimes, in order to advance by degrees.” Ibn-Caspi believs that the author has in view a passage from the Introduction (p. 10): “Truth does at times appear unto us in such wise as to seem clear as day, etc.” It appears to one that both commentators have been led into error by Ibn-Tibbon's version, which renders the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">פי אול הדׄה אלמקאלה̈</span> by <span dir=\"rtl\">בתחלת זה המאמר</span> “at the <i>commencement</i> of this treatise”; likewise Al-’Harîzi: <span dir=\"rtl\">בתחלת זה הספר</span>. But one believs that these words signify: “in the <i>first</i> (book) of this treatise,” and that the author intends to refer to what he has stated in ch. <small>XXXII</small>, p. 110: “If thou dost pause before that which is obscure, if thou dost not deceive thyself by believing to have found demonstration for that which is not demonstrable, if thou dost not hasten to reject and declare false whatsoever the contrary of which is not demonstrated, and if, finally, thou dost not aspire to the perception of that which thou canst not perceive, then hast thou attained unto human perception, etc.” One would observe that at the commencement of this passage, the author does state in the Arabic original: <span dir=\"rtl\">אן וקפת ענד אלשבהה̈</span>, an expression which corresponds to that which one has here: <span dir=\"rtl\">אלוקוף ענד אלשבהה̈</span>.",
    "fn.3708": "That is to say, for the sin committed through ignorance by the community of Israel, or by the great tribunal, which erred in its decision; this sacrifice derivs its name <span dir=\"rtl\">חטאת העלם דבר</span> from the words <span dir=\"rtl\">ונעלם דבר</span> (Leviticus, <small>IV</small>, 13). Vide Mischnâ, IV<sup>th</sup> part, treatise <i>Horayôth</i>, chap. <small>I</small>, §2, and Maimonides, Commentary on the Mischnâ, preface to the same treatise.",
    "fn.3709": "For example, the sacrifice of the high priest who had sinned (Leviticus, <small>IV</small>, 12), and that which the high priest offered on the day of atonements (<i>ibid.</i>, <small>XVI</small>, 27).",
    "fn.3710": "For example, the <i>offering</i> of the priest (see above, p. 369, note 1), as well as certain parts of the sacrifices and a portion of the offerings called <span dir=\"rtl\">אזכרה</span>, <i>remembrance</i> (Leviticus, <small>II</small>, 9, and <i>passim</i>).",
    "fn.3711": "As we mentioned in the previous note, this term referred to the portion of the offering that was burned on the altar.",
    "fn.3712": "Literally: <i>it was not to be treated either by the act of slaughtering, etc.</i>; that is to say, none of these three modes was to be applied unto it. — The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">مارس</span> (the III<sup>rd</sup> form of <span dir=\"rtl\">مرس</span>) signifies <i>to treat, to handle;</i> Ibn-Tibbon's translation, <span dir=\"rtl\">לא נרצה</span>, and that of Al-’Harîzi, <span dir=\"rtl\">לא נצטוה</span>, do not render this verb precisely. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">להקבר</span>, in the editions of Ibn-Tibbon's version, is an error; the manuscripts possess <span dir=\"rtl\">להקרבה</span>, and the edition <i>princeps</i> the abbreviation <span dir=\"rtl\">להקר׳</span>.",
    "fn.3722": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">thakaph</span> has been rendered neither by Ibn-Tibbon nor by Al-’Harîzi; the latter translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">to lighten the labors and the toils of the journey</span>.",
    "fn.3723": "Literally: <i>it is needful that thou compare</i> (<i>these two things</i>): <i>that man burn his child to celebrate his worship, or that he burn a young dove</i>. For the words <i>it is needful that thou compare</i>, Ibn-Tibbon has rendered <span dir=\"rtl\">ראה ההפרש</span>, <i>behold the difference</i>, and, at the close of the sentence, he has added the words: <span dir=\"rtl\">לעבודת אלהינו</span>.",
    "fn.3698": "We translate this passage according to the sense that Maimonides seems to give it. According to him, the expression <i>ma main a baisé ma bouche</i> means: I have placed my hand on my mouth as a sign of silence. Job wants to say that he has kept silence and stifled the secret sentiments of his heart that the brilliance of the sun and the moon awakened in him.",
    "fn.464": "This proposition pertaineth unto the atomistic <i>Motécallemîn</i>, whose doctrine shall be expounded hereafter (chap. LXXIII). By <span dir=\"rtl\">מכאן</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(سكان)</span>, they seem to understand space in relation to its extension or its dimensions; by <span dir=\"rtl\">חיז (حيّز)</span>, space circumscribed within limits. Albeit the atom, they say, hath no extension, it is nevertheless circumscribed and separated, by limits, from that which is adjacent to it; it is, as it were, the body without dimensions, the <i>point</i> considered as the first element of extension, yet which possesseth none itself. Lo, thus doth Al-Djordjâni, in his <i>Kitâb al-Ta’rifât</i> or Book of Definitions, explain the words <span dir=\"rtl\">مكان</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">حيز</span>, as they are employed by the <i>Motécallemîn:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">المكان عند</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">المتكلمين هو الفراغ المتوهم الذي يشغله الجسم وينفذ فيه ابعاده</span> «<i>Macân</i>, among the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, is the ideal void which the body occupieth and into which it causeth its dimensions to penetrate.» <span dir=\"rtl\">الحيز عند المتكلمين هو الفراغ المتوهم</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">الذي يشغله شيء ممتد كالجسم أو غير ممتد كالجوهر الفرد</span> «’<i>Hayyiz</i>, among the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, is the ideal void which is occupied by a thing whether extended like the body, or non-extended like the simple substance (or the <i>atome</i>).» It is perceived that <i>macân</i> is attributed only to the body having dimensions, whilst <i>’hayyiz</i> is attributed simultaneously to the extended body and to the atom. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">الجوهر الفرد</span> signify, according to M. Silv. de Sacy, <i>substance isolated from form</i> (see Notices et extraits des mss., vol. X, p. 65); but this explanation is incorrect. These words, which, in truth, signify literally <i>isolated substance</i>, were employed by the <i>Motécallemîn</i> to designate the <i>atome</i> which they also call <span dir=\"rtl\">الجزء الذي</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">لا يتجزأ</span> or simply <span dir=\"rtl\">الجزء</span>, as might have been seen somewhat further back. Thus expresseth himself Ibn-Roschd, in his Epitome of the <i>Metaphysics</i>, Book I, when speaking of the <i>substance</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">الجوهر</span>; we cite the Hebrew version: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכן מי שראה כי העצמות הרמוז אליו יתחבר מחלקים אשד לא יתחלקו קרא אותם עצמים כמו שנשמע המדברים מאנשי זמננו יקראו החלק אשר לא יתחלק העצם הפדדי</span> «In like manner, those who think that the indicated substance is composed of <i>atoms</i> give to these latter the name of <i>substances</i>, even as we hear the <i>Motécallemîn</i> of our day call the atom the <i>isolated</i> or <i>simple</i> substance <span dir=\"rtl\">(الجوهر الفرد)</span>.» The term <span dir=\"rtl\">الجوهر الفرد</span> resembleth that of the <i>monad</i>, used by Leibnitz, albeit it doth not designate exactly the same thing; the proposition of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, quoted here by Maïmonides, offereth a striking analogy with what Leibnitz saith, when speaking of monads: <i>Substantia nempe simplex, etsi non habeat in se extensionem, habet tamen positionem, quæ est fundamentum extensionis</i>. See the Works of Leibnitz, Dutens edition, vol. II, part I (<i>Epistolæ ad P. des Bosses</i>), p. 280.",
    "fn.481": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתלאזם</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(التلازم)</span> betokeneth the close and necessary connection between two things. In the <i>Kitâb al-Ta’rifât</i>, at the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ملازمة</span>, we read: that this word expresseth the impossibility of separating two things from one another, and that the words <span dir=\"rtl\">لزوم</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">تلازم</span> are employed in the same sense. Cf. Freytag's Dictionary, at the root <span dir=\"rtl\">لزوم</span>. The enunciation of this <i>connection</i> or <i>inherence</i> formeth a <i>necessary</i> proposition or judgment, such as, for example, the proposition cited here by our author: <i>every man is rational</i>. — The author appeareth to have in view that which Aristotle saith regarding demonstration, in explaining the three terms whereby he designeth the different degrees of the <i>necessary</i>. See <i>Posterior Analytics</i>, book I, chap. 4. He seemeth to allude especially to the terms <i>ϰατά παντός</i> and <i>ϰαθ’ αὑτό</i>. The <i>ϰατά παντός</i> designeth an attribution which pertaineth <i>unto every individual</i>, and not merely to such or such an one, and which is at all times and not merely at such or such a time. Thus, <i>animal being predicated of every man</i> (<i>ϰατά παντός άνθρώπου</i>), <i>it will suffice that it be true that such an individual be a man for it to be true to say of him that he is an animal</i>. The term <i>ϰαθ’ αὑτό</i>, <i>in itself</i> (which hath four principal senses), designeth first the attribution which expresseth that a thing is essential or inherent unto another, and wherein the attribute entereth into the essential definition of its subject; e.g., the line in the triangle, the point in the line. Secondly, the <i>ϰαθ’ αὑτό</i> designeth the attribution wherein the subject entereth into the essential definition of the attribute, or, in other words, wherein the attribute is so essential to the subject that the latter containeth the definition thereof (<i>ὅσοις τῶν ἐνυπαρχόντων αὐτοῖς αὐτὰ ἐν τῷ λόγῳ ένυπάρχουσι τῷ, τί ἐστι, δηλοῦντι</i>). Thus, e.g., the line necessarily entereth into the definition of the <i>straight</i> and of the <i>curved</i>, and number into that of the <i>even</i> and of the <i>odd</i>. Ibn Rushd observeth that, in the first case, that is to say, when the attribute entereth into the definition of the subject, it concerneth either the complete definition or a part of the definition, as, per- adventure, when one defineth the triangle by saying that it is a figure bounded by three straight lines; in the second case, that is to say, when the subject entereth into the definition of the attribute, it concerneth the partial definition, as, for example, when one taketh the line as the definition of the <i>straight</i> and of the <i>curved</i>, which exist in the line, or else number as the definition of the <i>even</i> and of the <i>odd</i>, which exist in number. That which, saith he, entereth especially into the propositions subserving demonstration, are these first two kinds of <i>ϰαθ’ αὑτό</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אשר בעצם</span>), wherein there is a necessary and essential attribution; for, he addeth, <i>the partial definition findeth itself in a necessary relation with the thing defined</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כי יחם חלק הגדר אל הנגדר יהיה הכרחי</span>). See the Middle Commentary on the Posterior Analytics, Hebrew version, at the passage in question. One will now understand what our author had in view in saying that <i>there is here the idea of inherence etc</i>.",
    "fn.594": "From the manner in which the author here expresseth himself, one might believe that it concerneth a well-known proposition belonging to Aristotle; but, as several commentators observe, this subtle distinction between the <i>quiddity</i> of a thing and its existence belongeth to Ibn-Sînâ, and it hath been impugned by other Arabian philosophers, and notably by Ibn-Roschd, who informeth us that those who have seen in existence an <i>accident</i> have based their view, among other things, upon the fact that the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">موجود</span>, which the philosophers employ in the sense of <i>existent</i> or <i>that which is</i> (<i>τὸ ὄν</i>), is primarily a passive participle signifying <i>found</i>, and indicateth, by its etymology, something <i>accidental.</i> At the commencement of the 1<sup>st</sup> book of his <i>Epitome of Metaphysics</i>, Ibn-Roschd, after having set forth the divers meanings of the philosophical term <span dir=\"rtl\">درو جود</span> (<i>that which is, τὸ ὄν</i>), thus summarizeth (Hebr. vers.): <span dir=\"rtl\">ולכן היה שם הנמצא שב אל שני אלו הענינים לבד ר״ל אל הצודק ואל מה שהוא נמצא חוץ לשכל</span>, that is to say, that in summary, the said term is used either for that which the mind judgeth <i>to be true</i> (ἔ<i>τι τὸ εἶναι σημαίνει ϰαί τὸ ἔστιν ὅτι ἀληθές, ϰ. τ. λ.</i> Arist., <i>Metaph</i>., book V, chap. 7), or of being <i>in itself</i>, of that which hath an objective reality outside the mind, and, in this sense, it applieth to all the categories (ϰαθ’ αὑτὸ δέ εἶναι λέγεται ὅσαπερ σημαίνει τὰ σχήματα τῆς ϰατη-γορίας, ϰ. τ. λ. Ibidem</i>). Accidental existence, addeth Ibn-Roschd, cannot be conceived in the isolated being or in being <i>in itself</i> (<i>ϰαθ</i>’ <i>αὑτό</i>), for the substance of the thing and its quiddity cannot be accidental; it can only be conceived in the mutual relation of beings, that is to say, when the <i>ὄν</i> expresseth an accidental relation of one thing to another, or when it is said, as Aristotle expresseth it, <i>ϰατὰ συαβεβηϰός</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">אולם הנמצא במקרה הוא לא יצויר במציאות הנפרד כי עצמות הדבר ומהותו אי אפשר ש׳היה במקרה ואמנם יצויר מיחם הנמצאות קצתם <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> קצת</span>. He then refuteth the opinion of Ibn-Sînâ, according to whom <i>existence</i> is an <i>accident</i> supervening upon the essence or the quiddity of the thing which existeth, and, after having shown all that is false in this way of viewing the matter, he addeth: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל זה דרך האיש הזה ברוב מה שיביאהו מצד עצמו</span> «But such is the manner of this man in almost <i>all that he allegeth of his own accord.</i>» Ibn-Roschd looked with disfavour upon a proposition which appeared to him to be in opposition to the Aristotelian doctrines. In truth, this ideal separation between <i>what the thing is</i> (<i>τὸ τί ἦν εἶνο ι</i>) and its <i>existence</i> is contrary to the spirit of Aristotle’s doctrine; it would be at most in <i>artificial</i> objects that one might separate the <i>what</i> from the <i>existence</i>, the idea of the thing being in the mind of the artist before <i>existing</i> (cf. Aristotle, <i>Metaph</i>., book VII, chap. 7: <i>ἀπὸ τέχνης δὲ γίγνεται ὅσων τὸ εἶδος ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ, εἶδος δὲ λέγω τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑϰάסטου ϰαί τὴν πρώτην οὐσίαν</i>’). According to the partisans of Ibn-Sînâ, the same would hold true of all that is produced by <i>nature;</i> for, say they, all things being in the first cause, or in God, who knoweth them beforehand, the quiddity of things is prior to their existence, which, consequently, is an accident of the quiddity (vide the commentary of Shem-Tob, on our passage). But this reasoning leadeth to the admission, with Plato, of <i>pre-existing</i> ideas or forms, and is little in accordance with the Peripatetic doctrines. —Vide also note 1 of the following page.",
    "fn.597": "As the commentator Shem-Tob observeth, that which the author saith here is nowhere to be found in the <i>Metaphysics</i> of Aristotle, but rather the contrary. Indeed, the <i>one</i> (<i>τὸ ὄν</i>) <i>per se</i>, unto which Aristotle assigneth four principal significations, which are: the <i>continuous</i>, the <i>whole</i>, the <i>individual</i>, and the <i>universal</i> (according as one considereth therein the indivisibility of motion or that of the idea), is inseparable from the very nature and quiddity of the thing which is <i>one</i>, and cannot be considered as an <i>accident</i> of the quiddity. The <i>one</i> is not in itself an essence, and it can exist only in a subject. Its proper character is to be indivisible and the first measure in every genus, and notably the measure of quantity; but, even as <i>being</i> (<i>τὸ ὄν</i>), with which it is confounded and identified, it applyeth to all the <i>categories</i>, and is not found particularly in one alone. The <i>manifold</i> is opposed to the <i>one</i>, as the divisible to the indivisible, and not as non-being to being; for the <i>manifold</i> is in <i>being</i>, as well as the <i>one</i>, and is attributed, like the latter, to all the categories. The <i>one</i> and the <i>manifold</i> in number, designating quantity alone, are abstractions and entirely distinct from <i>being.</i> See the details in the <i>Metaphysics</i> of Aristotle, book V, chap. 6, and book X, chap. 1 et seq. — Maimonides, who seeth in the <i>one</i> and the <i>manifold</i> naught but <i>accidents</i> distinct from <i>being</i>, hath further followed the opinion of Ibn-Sīnā, who, as Ibn-Roschd observeth, hath confounded the <i>one</i> of number with the absolute <i>one</i>: “A difficulty which may be found here,” saith Ibn-Roschd (loc. cit., book III), “is this: how, whilst admitting that the <i>one</i> of number belongeth to the category of <i>quantity</i>, can we subsequently admit that it is found in all the categories, and moreover that it is in the categories themselves, and not something which is accessory thereto? Wherefore Ibn-Sīnā hath thought that its substratum must be an <i>accident</i> which is found in all the categories.” “But it is not so; for the <i>one</i> in number is of another nature than other unities. Indeed, the numerical <i>one</i> is individuality, abstracted from that which is quantity and quality—I mean from that whereby the individual is an individual; for it is an individual only in the sense of the <i>indivisible</i>, and the mind abstracteth it from material things, limiteth it, and taketh it for something separate. The <i>one</i> in number, the numerical unity, is but a thing which the <i>soul</i> effecteth in individual beings, and without the soul there would be neither numerical unity nor number in general, contrary to that which obtaineth for the line and the surface, and, in general, for continuous quantity; wherefore number is, more than any other thing, pure from matter. Ibn-Sīnā hath confounded the nature of the <i>one</i>, the principle of number, with the absolute <i>one</i>, which embraceth all the categories, and since the <i>one</i>, the principle of number, is an <i>accident</i>, he hath admitted that the absolute <i>one</i>, which is the same thing as <i>being</i>, is also an accident.” Cf. R. Levi ben-Gerson, <i>Mil’hamôth</i>, book V, 3, chap. 12.",
    "fn.607": "Literally: <i>know that to describe</i> (or <i>designate</i>) <i>God</i> — <i>may He be glorified and exalted!</i> — <i>by means of negations, is the true description</i> (or <i>manner of assigning attributes unto Him</i>), <i>which is affected by no improper expression</i> (on <span dir=\"rtl\">تسامح</span> , vide supra, page 235, note 1), <i>and in which there is in no wise, nor in any manner whatsoever, a defect in respect of God;</i> that is to say: the attributes expressed by negations are alone true, containing, within their terms, nothing which one need regard as an improper expression, and saying naught, in respect of God, which implieth an imperfection. — Already before Maimonides, the Jewish theologians, and notably those of Spain, had insisted upon this point and had established that one cannot express what God is, but only what He is not, and that the attributes which are commonly ascribed unto Him, far from having an affirmative sense, indicate nothing else than the exclusion of their contrary; by <i>one</i>, it is meant only that God <i>is not manifold;</i> by <i>eternal</i>, that He <i>is not created</i>, and so forth. Vide Bahya, <i>Duties of the Hearts</i>, bk. I, chap. 10; Judah ha-Levi, <i>Khozari</i>, bk. II, § 2; Abraham ben David, <i>Emunah Ramah</i> (The Sublime Faith), bk. II, 3<sup>rd</sup> foundation (p. 51 of the text, and p. 65 of the German translation by M. Simon Weil, Frankfort, 1852). The Arabian philosophers pronounce themselves in the same sense, and certain Muslim sects had made of <i>negative attributes</i> an essential point of their doctrine, as, e.g., the <i>Dhirâriyya</i> (vide Shahrastâni, Germ. transl., vol. I, p. 94). The Karaite Ahron ben Elijah, in his <span dir=\"rtl\">עץ חיים</span> or <i>Tree of Life</i> (chap. LXXI), refutes the partisans of <i>negative attributes.</i> These latter believed they could support themselves by the opinion of Aristotle, whom Bahya cites expressly in this regard. Here follow the terms of the Arabic original of the treatise on the <i>Duties of the Hearts</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">ולהדֹא קאל ארמטוטאלים אלמואלב מן צפאת אלכֹאלק תעאלי אצדק מן אלמוגֹכאת לאן כל מא יוגֹב לה מן אלצפאת לא תכֹלו פן צפאת אלגֹוהר או אלערץֹ וכֹאלק אלגֹוהר ואלערץֹ לא ילחקה פי דֹאתה וצף מן אוצאפהמא וכל מא ינפי ענה מן אלצפאת פהי צאדקה̈ לא מהאלה̈</span> “Wherefore Aristotle saith: The negative attributes of the Creator are more true than the affirmative; for all the attributes that one might assign Him affirmatively could only be attributes of substance or of accident, and the Creator of substance and of accident cannot have in His essence any of their attributes. But the attributes which are removed from Him (that is to say, the attributes expressed negatively) are indubitably true.” — This passage, which is cited also by Ibn-Falaquera (<i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, p. 29), is evidently apocryphal. It is true that Aristotle, in speaking of the <i>first mover</i>, very often expresseth himself by negative terms, such as: <i>being immutable, separate from all that is sensible</i>, which <i>can have no extension</i>, which is <i>indivisible, etc.</i> (Metaph., XII, 7 and <i>passim</i>); but he in no wise excludeth affirmative terms, and that which the doctrine of the Arabian philosophers possesseth of the absolute in this regard appeareth to be drawn from the Neoplatonic commentators and to be connected with the doctrines of Plotinus (cf. Ritter, <i>Geschichte der Philosophie</i>, vol. IV, p. 573 et seq.). St. Thomas in his turn, in saying that we cannot express ourselves concerning God save by the <i>negative way</i>, followed the Arabian philosophers, from whom he doubtless borrowed the term <i>via remotionis</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(طريق النفي)</span> . Vide <i>Summa C. Gent</i>., bk. I, chap. 14.",
    "fn.637": "This somewhat obscure passage hath been diversely interpreted, and the commentators appear greatly perplexed to indicate its precise meaning, as may be recognized in the varied explanations given by Shem-Tob and by Profiat Duran or Ephodi. The simplest sense, it seemeth to me, is this: One could not attribute to God that which, from our point of view, would be a perfection; for perfections, being ever certain <i>capacities</i> (<i>ἕξεις</i>), belong to one of the genera of the category of <i>quality</i>, and could not, consequently, be attributed to God, as resulteth from what the author hath said in chap. LII (p. 195), touching <i>qualities.</i> For a more ample explanation, the author addeth: <i>and every capacity existeth not in everything</i> (<i>being</i>) <i>endowed with capacity;</i> that is to say, that in created beings, capacities are somewhat <i>accidental</i>, being found not equally in all, and not being always <i>in act</i> where they are found. <i>Capacities</i> (<i>ἕξεις</i>), albeit more durable and more solid than <i>dispositions</i> (<i>διαθέσεις</i>), are nevertheless naught else than consolidated <i>dispositions</i>, and designate somewhat which hath been <i>acquired</i> and which hath not always existed in the being endowed therewith (cf. p. 195, note 2). As Aristotle himself saith, capacities are also dispositions; for those who are endowed with capacities are in some wise only <i>disposed</i> for these selfsame capacities (<i>οί μὲν γὰρ ἕξεις ἔχοντες ϰαί διάϰεινταί γέ πως ϰατ’ αὐτά. Categ</i>., chap. 8). Let us add thereto that capacities are often <i>opposed</i> the one to the other, and cannot exist together in the same subject. It resulteth from all this that, if one attributed to God all that which, in relation to us, is a perfection, one would attribute unto Him <i>acquired</i> capacities and even <i>opposing</i> capacities, two things equally inadmissible. — Moses of Narbonne, the most profound commentator of Maimonides, confessing the embarrassment which he had long experienced in explaining the words of the author, supposeth that this entire passage, beginning from these words: <i>Thus it is clear unto thee, etc.</i>, relateth to a passage of the <i>Metaphysics</i> (book XII, chap. 9), where Aristotle saith that the intelligence perceiveth naught else than itself, being unable to be affected by anything outside of itself. Maimonides, in speaking here of all that must be <i>denied</i> of the supreme intelligence, or of God, would allude to this absolute negation, in God, of every perception which should not have for its object the divine intelligence itself. The latter hath its perfection in itself, and cannot be affected by anything. In denying that it perceiveth aught else than itself, one doth not attribute unto it any imperfection; for all perfections are capacities, but these capacities do not constitute perfection for every being endowed therewith. There be many <i>privations</i> which are preferable to <i>capacities;</i> for, as Aristotle saith in the passage indicated, <i>to not see certain things is better than to see them</i> (<i>ϰαί γὰρ μὴ ὁρᾶν ἔνια ϰρεῖττον ἢ ὁρᾶν</i>). Such is, in substance, the interpretation given to our passage by Moses of Narbonne; but we greatly doubt whether it be the true one and whether there be a real relation between the words of Maimonides and the said passage of Aristotle.",
    "fn.743": "The visible manifestation of the Divinity consisteth, according to the theologians, in a light which God causeth to appear in a place and which is a miraculous creation; it is this light which is called <i>Schekhînâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שכינה</span>, majesty) or <i>cabôd</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כבוד</span>, <i>glory</i>), and which appeareth in the place that God chooseth particularly to make His presence known. Saadia likewise speaketh of this <i>created light</i> which God caused to appear in the sanctuary to <i>glorify that place</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">להגדיל המקום ההוא</span>); vide <i>Beliefs and Opinions</i>, bk. II, chap. 8 (Berlin edit., fol. 33 <i>a</i>). Cf. Ahron ben-Eli, <i>Tree of Life</i>, ch. LXIII (Leipzig edit., p. 72). Speculations of this kind go back in general to the ancient Karaites, who took for guides the Muslim theologians of the sect of the Motazalites, and it was through the impulse of the Karaites that Saadia and other Rabbanites approached this manner of questions (vide infra, ch. LXXI). The Karaite Abu-’Ali (Jepheth), in his Arabic commentary upon Exodus (chap. 19, <small>v</small>. 20), speaketh at length of the manner in which the manifestation of God upon Sinai must be understood; one readeth there the following passage relative to the <i>cabôd</i> or the <i>created light</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">וד׳לף אנה אמתחאל אן יבון אלכ׳אלק ג׳ל ועלא אלר׳י לא יוצף ולא <span dir=\"rtl\">חי</span> ולא תחויה אלאמבנה̈ בל לא יג׳וז אן יקאל פיה אנה פי אלעאלם ולא כ׳ארג׳א ענה אד׳ הד׳א מן אוצאף אלמכ׳לוקין אלד׳ין תחתוי עליהם אלאמכגה̈ ותלזמגם אלאעראצ׳ ואד׳א כאן הד׳א הכד׳א פלא שך פי אן וירד יי ישיר בה אלי מכ׳לוק מחדוד וחצ׳רה̈ מוצ׳ע פי ראשׁ ההר</span> “In truth, it is impossible to admit that it is here a question of the Creator, who can be neither qualified nor defined, and who could not be enclosed within space. Nay, more, one could not say of Him that He is in the world, nor that He is outside the world; for such qualifications would suit created things, which are enclosed in space and subject to accidents. This being so, there is no doubt that, by the words: <i>And the Lord came down, etc.</i>, there is indicated a thing <i>created</i> and limited, and its local presence upon the summit of the mountain.” Cf. infra, p. 290, note 2. According to the opinion which Maimonides setteth forth elsewhere concerning miracles in general, one must see in the said <i>created light</i>, as well as in all that occurreth in a miraculous manner, the effect of the primitive will of God, who, from the moment of Creation, hath disposed things in such wise that, under certain given circumstances, there might be produced extraordinary phenomena which seem to us to depart from the general laws of nature. Thus, therefore, miracles are the results of certain physical dispositions established by God at the epoch of Creation, and not the effect of a momentary will; for there can arise no change in the divine will, which is eternal and immutable. Vide what the author saith in this regard in the last of the <i>Eight Chapters</i> serving as an introduction to the treatise <i>Aboth</i> (<i>Porta Mosis</i> of Pococke, p. 240), and cf. the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, towards the end of chap. XXIX. Vide also infra, chap. LXVI, p. 296, note 1.",
    "fn.793": "The author here addresses a subject which, as he himself states at the end of this chapter, can only be well understood by those familiar with Peripatetic philosophy, and particularly the part relating to the soul and its faculties. Without entering here into developments that would exceed the limits of a note, it will be necessary, for a better understanding of the author's reasoning, to briefly recall the Aristotelian theory of intelligence and the developments this theory received among the Arab philosophers whom our author took as guides. — In considering the various faculties of the soul enumerated by Aristotle, we notice a successive progression from the less perfect to the more perfect, such that each higher faculty supposes the lower faculty, whose action it renders more complete, and that one is to the other what <i>energy</i> or <i>act</i> is to <i>potency</i>, or what form is to matter. One can distinguish three main faculties of the soul: <i>nutrition</i> (<i>τὸ θρεπτιϰόν</i> sc. <i>μόριον</i>), to which the life of plants is limited; <i>sensation</i> (<i>τὸ αἰσθητιϰόν</i>), which belongs to all animals, and <i>reason</i> (<i>τὸ διανοητιϰόν</i>), which belongs to man alone (Treatise <i>on the Soul</i>, book II, chap. 2); the third faculty cannot exist without the second, nor the latter without the first. <i>Nutrition</i> has material things as its object; through this faculty, the animal assimilates external things themselves, whereas through <i>sensation</i> it receives only the forms of things, just as wax receives only the form of the seal, and not its matter. <i>Reason</i>, or <i>thought</i>, is to intelligible things what sensation is to sensible things; but through the senses we know only individuals or individual forms; we know that things are, but we do not know why they are; it is reason that recognizes general forms and causes. The soul, says Aristotle, that is to say, the intelligent part of the soul, is <i>the place of forms</i> (<i>τόπος εἰδῶν</i>). Required to dominate everything, reason has no particular bodily organ; it must be <i>impassible</i>, that is to say, not affected by external things, but <i>capable</i> of receiving the form of objects. These forms it does not possess at first <i>in act</i>, that is, in full reality, but only <i>in potency</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, book 3, chap. 4). Just as in all beings one distinguishes on the one hand matter, or what is in potency, and on the other hand form, or the cause that makes what is <i>in potency</i> pass into <i>act</i>, so it is necessary that these differences be found in the rational part of the soul or in the intelligence. Aristotle thus distinguishes two <i>intellects</i>, one <i>passive</i> (<i>νοῦς παθητιϰός</i>), the other <i>active</i> (<i>νοῦς ποιητιϰός</i>). The passive intellect holds the middle ground between sensation and imagination, on one hand, and the active intellect, on the other; it is the latter especially that is separate (from the lower faculties of the soul), impassible, unmixed with anything whatsoever, being in <i>act</i> by its very essence (<i>ϰαὶ οὗτος ὁ νοῦς χωριστὸς ϰαὶ ἀπαθὴς ϰαὶ ἀμιγής, τῇ οὐσίᾳ ὢν ἐνεργείᾳ</i>. <i>Ibid.</i>, chap. 5). Aristotle does not tell us clearly where this active intellect comes from, nor how it is perceived by the passive intellect; but he seems to see in it something divine, which comes from without through the first mover. See the treatise on the <i>Generation of Animals</i>, book II, chap. 3: <i>λείπεται δὲ τὸν νοῦν μόνον θύραθεν ἐπεισιέναι ϰαὶ θεῖον εἶναι ϰ. τ. λ.</i>; cf. Trendelenburg, <i>Arist. de Anima</i>, page 496. It is by the aid of this active intellect that every individual intelligence accomplishes the work of thought, and, passing from <i>potency</i> to <i>act</i>, it appropriates the forms of things and identifies itself with them. These intelligible forms, the object of thought, are themselves thought; for the intellect in act cannot be affected by something outside of itself, and it must draw from within itself what it has as its object. The intellect, therefore, in its <i>entelechy</i> or in its full reality, thinks itself. See Aristotle, Metaphysics, book XII, chap. 7: <i>αὑτὸν δὲ νοεῖ ὁ νοῦς ϰατὰ μετάληψιν τοῦ νοητοῦ νοητὸς γὰρ γίγνεται θιγγάνων ϰαὶ νοῶν ὥστε ταὐτὸν νοῦς ϰαὶ νοητόν</i>. Cf. Treatise <i>on the Soul</i>, book III, at the end of chap. 4. Such is, in substance, Aristotle's theory; the obscurity that prevails in this theory, notably regarding the nature of the active intellect and its union with the passive intellect, has given rise, among commentators, to long discussions and the most diverse opinions, and the Arab philosophers, confounding here as elsewhere the theories of Aristotle with those of his Neoplatonic commentators, have elaborated, on the subject that concerns us, a new doctrine which subsequently spread among the scholastics. Here, in summary, is the theory of the Arab philosophers, who differ among themselves on several essential points. The <i>speculative</i> faculty or <i>theoretical reason</i>, the highest among the faculties of the rational soul (see above, page 210, note 1), receives the impression of forms isolated from matter, whether they be general forms that it abstracts from material and individual things, or immaterial and imperishable beings, which are in themselves abstract forms (as, for example, the <i>separate intelligences</i>). This faculty, called <i>intellect</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(عقل)</span> par excellence, develops gradually, passing through various degrees where the lower is to the higher what matter is to form, or what potency is to act. The Arab philosophers generally distinguish three degrees of development, or three intellects: 1st <i>The hylic intellect</i> or <i>material intellect</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(العقل الهيولانى)</span>, which is the simple <i>disposition</i> that the intellect has to abstract forms; it is a simple <i>potency</i> that has not yet begun to pass into <i>act</i>, and which is called <i>hylic</i> because it can be compared to <i>matter</i> (<i>ὕλη</i>) disposed to receive form. This <i>hylic intellect</i> is the <i>νοῦς παθητιϰός</i> of Aristotle <span dir=\"rtl\">(العقل المنفعل)</span> in all its abstraction. The opinions of the Arab philosophers are divided on the nature of the <i>hylic intellect:</i> some, invoking the authority of Alexander of Aphrodisias, see in it only a pure <i>disposition</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(استعداد)</span> attached to the other faculties of the soul, and which is perishable like them, and this opinion is adopted by Maimonides (see below, chap. LXX); others, taking Themistius as their guide, maintain that, since the <i>passive</i> or <i>hylic intellect</i> must, according to Aristotle, be <i>unmixed</i> (<i>ἀμιγής</i>) with the other faculties of the soul, it must be a disposition having for its <i>substratum</i> a substance separate from the other faculties; for, they say, if the <i>intellect in potency</i> were only a simple disposition attached to the other faculties of the soul, it would have for its substratum something belonging to another <i>genus</i> than itself; but what is disposed to perceive intelligible things can itself only be an <i>intellect.</i> Ibn-Roschd, recognizing on the one hand what is inadmissible in Alexander's opinion, also sees difficulties in admitting the other opinion, according to which there would be a separate and eternal substance whose being would consist only in a <i>disposition</i> and a simple <i>potency</i> (<i>δύναμις</i>); he therefore chooses a middle term, by combining the two opinions, as we have explained elsewhere (see the article <i>Ibn-Roschd</i> in the <i>Dictionnaire des sciences philosophiques</i>, Vol. III, pages 166 and 167). 2nd <i>The intellect in act</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(العقل بالفعل)</span>; this is the intellect that has succeeded in abstracting form from matter, that is to say, which has succeeded in distinguishing, in individual things, that which constitutes their general form, or that by which a thing is what it is. 3rd <i>The acquired intellect</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(العقل المستفاد)</span>; this is nothing other than the intellect in act having become in a sense the property of man, when <i>intelligible forms</i> are always present in his intelligence, and he can identify himself with them at any moment without making new efforts. Having arrived at this level, human intelligence always has as its object pure intelligible forms; its object is the knowledge of its own being and that of the immaterial beings outside of it, and it rises to the knowledge of the <i>separate intelligences</i> and of God. In this state, it becomes in a sense a substance entirely separate from the body (see below, at the end of chap. LXXII). Between the <i>intellect in act</i> and the <i>acquired intellect</i>, some Arab philosophers also place the <i>intellect in capacity</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(العقل بالملكة)</span>; they seem to mean by this the intellect in act having become a <i>capacity</i> (<i>ἕξις</i>, cf. above, page 195, notes 1 and 2), and before it has actually reached the level of the <i>acquired intellect</i> (cf. the book <i>Ta’rifât</i> cited in Freytag's Dictionary, under the word <span dir=\"rtl\">عقل</span>, and the Analysis of the philosophy of Ibn-Sînâ, in Shahrastânî, p. 418 of the Arabic text, and Vol. II, page 317, of the German translation). This is a subtle distinction, whose scope is not well understood, and which, it appears, has not been generally admitted; Ibn Falaquera, in enumerating the different <i>intellects</i> (see the Appendix to the <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, at the beginning of chap. I, page 141), does not speak of the <i>intellect in capacity</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">השכל בקנין</span>). — Studies and philosophical speculation, although they are necessary for the development of the <i>hylic intellect</i>, would not be sufficient on their own to bring it to the levels of the <i>intellect in act</i> and the <i>acquired intellect;</i> like everything that is only <i>in potency</i>, the intellect needs, in order to pass into <i>act</i> or <i>entelechy</i>, a <i>cause</i> that is outside of itself, and this cause is the universal <i>active intellect</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(العقل الفعال)</span>, the last among the intelligences of the spheres (see Part II of this work, chap. IV, and cf. Ibn-Sînâ, in Shahrastânî's work, page 426; German trans., Vol. II, p. 328). It is through the influence of this active intellect that the <i>acquired intellect</i> is formed, considered as its <i>emanation</i> (hence, in the Hebrew versions, <span dir=\"rtl\">השכל הנאצל</span>, the emanated intellect), and which succeeds, in certain chosen men, in identifying itself completely with the universal active intellect (cf. above, page 277, note 3, and my article <i>Ibn-Roschd</i>, loc. cit., pages 167 and 169). — Various details of this theory, of which we thought it necessary to present the whole here, will be discussed in other places. It is understandable that its obscurity has given rise to numerous divergences of opinion; the hypotheses, sought as far as regions inaccessible to the human mind, left a free career for the imagination, and the most enlightened Arab philosophers could not fail to abandon here the true ground of philosophical speculation, to lose themselves in reveries. 'Not a single one,' says Ibn Falaquera, 'has a well-fixed opinion on this subject, and we often see the same author change his opinion and say in one work something entirely different from what he said in another.' See <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, book II, chap. 25 (page 111).",
    "fn.864": "The Arabic text of this passage is quite obscure; here is an approximate word-for-word translation: <i>And likewise they have proved, for these</i> (<i>other</i>) <i>things they have enumerated, that, being</i> (<i>in</i>) <i>a relation with God, they are with Him</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ענדה</span> (<i>at his place</i> or <i>with him</i>) does not refer to God, but to the heaven <i>’Arabôth</i>, and it would have been more regular to construct the Arabic sentence thus: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכדֹלך אמתדלוא עלי אנ תלכ אלתי עדוהא הי ענדה ללה תעאלי</span> (i.e., <span dir=\"rtl\">באנהא נסבֹה</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בכונהא מנסובֹה</span>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">נסבֹה</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(نسبة)</span> which most manuscripts contain, some have <span dir=\"rtl\">נסבת</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(נُסִבَتْ)</span>, and one of the Leiden manuscripts has <span dir=\"rtl\">מנסובֹה</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(منسوبة)</span>; these last two readings appear to have been substituted for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">נסבֹה</span> to facilitate the understanding of the passage. The meaning is: that the doctors, to prove that the other things they have enumerated also exist in <i>’Arabôth</i>, limit themselves to citing biblical passages where these things are directly attributed to God; for nothing emanates from God except through the mediation of <i>’Arabôth</i>, which, as has been seen, is the instrument by which God governs the universe. Thus, for example, to prove that <i>’Arabôth</i> contains <i>treasures of life</i>, these words of the Psalmist are cited: <i>For with you is the source of life</i> (Ps. 36:10); for <i>the treasures of peace</i>, this passage is cited: <i>And he called it</i> (the altar) <i>The Eternal God of peace</i> (Judges 6:24); for <i>the treasures of blessing: He shall receive the blessing from the Lord</i> (Ps. 24:5), and so on. See <i>’Haghîghâ</i>, l. c. — In the editions of Ibn Tibbon's version (with the exception of the <i>editio princeps</i>), our passage is translated twice, first literally, then in a manner that conveys the meaning more clearly; the second translation is expressed in these terms: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובן הביאו ראיה על השאר שהם בערבות מהיותם מיוחסים לשם יתעלה</span> (the words <span dir=\"rtl\">שהם אצלו</span>, which the editions add, are repeated from the first translation and must be deleted). Most of the manuscripts and the <i>editio princeps</i> have only this second translation, which Ibn Tibbon had placed in the margin, adding the following note found in some manuscripts: <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר שמואל בן חבון הלשון המוגה מחוץ אינו יוצא מלשון הערכי אך הוא יוצא מגוף הענין מלשון חגיגה והוא האמת בעצמו והוא שהעיר עליו באמרו והבן זה</span> “Samuel Ibn Tibbon says: The rectified wording found on the outside does not correspond to the Arabic text, but arises from the subject itself contained in the text of <i>’Haghîghâ;</i> it is the true meaning, and it is what the author indicated by saying: <i>Understand this well</i>.”",
    "fn.880": "The word <i>kalam</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(كلام)</span>, which means <i>word</i>, <i>discourse</i>, also designates a science that formed among the Muslims as early as the 2nd century of the Hegira, and whose goal was to combat the doctrines of heterodox sects and later those of the philosophers, by using against its adversaries the weapons of dialectic and reasoning borrowed from philosophical schools. This science can be called <i>dogmatics</i>, or rational theology, and it has much analogy with Christian <i>scholasticism</i>. <i>Kalam</i> developed especially since the introduction of Peripatetic philosophy among the Arabs, and it will be seen further on (ch. LXXIII et seq.) what doctrines were established by this science and how they proceeded to demonstrate the truth of the principal religious dogmas, particularly those establishing the unity and immateriality of God and Creation. As for the name <i>kalam</i>, the Arabs themselves do not agree on its origin; according to some, this science was so named because it primarily discussed what was meant by the <i>divine word</i> or by the <i>word</i> (<i>kalam</i>) attributed to God (cf. below, p. 343, note 3); according to others, the word <i>kalam</i> is merely an imitation of the word <i>mantiq</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(منطق)</span>, which also means <i>speech</i>, or <i>discourse</i>, and by which philosophers designated one of the principal branches of their doctrine, namely, <i>logic</i>. See Shahrastani, <i>History of Religious and Philosophical Sects</i>, Arabic text, p. 18 (German trans., vol. I, p. 26); cf. Pococke, <i>Specimen hist. ar.</i>, p. 195. From the word <i>kalam</i> comes the verb <i>takallam</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(تكلم)</span>, having the sense of <i>professing kalam</i>, and whose participle <i>mutakallim</i>, in the plural <i>mutakallimun</i>, designates the partisans of <i>kalam</i>. Having regard for the meaning of <i>word</i> that the word <i>kalam</i> possesses, the Hebrew translators called this science <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמת הדבר</span> and designated the Mutakallimun under the name <i>Medabberim</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מדברים</span>), which the Latin translators, in turn, rendered as <i>loquentes</i>. One may also see what I have said on <i>kalam</i> in the <i>Dictionnaire des sciences philosophiques</i>, vol. I, art. <i>Arabes</i> (pp. 169 and 174), and in my <i>Notice on R. Saadia Gaon</i>, p. 16 et seq. (Bible by M. Cahen, vol. IX, p. 88 et seq.). — The two Hebrew translators did not fully grasp the meaning that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלכלאם</span> has in our passage, and they took it in its primitive sense of <i>word</i> or <i>discourse;</i> Ibn Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">מדברי ענין היחוד</span>, and Al-Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">מדבריהם בענין היחוד</span>. Following the words we have just cited, one should read in Ibn Tibbon's version: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומה שנתלה בזה הענין</span>, as found in the manuscripts and the <i>editio princeps;</i> in the other editions, it was erroneously put as: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומה שנתגלה מזה הענין</span>.",
    "fn.924": "On the <i>humors</i>, see below, p. 366, note 4. — The <i>spirits</i> are subtle fluids which, according to the hypotheses of ancient naturalists, have their seat in various organs of the animal body and contribute to the manifestations of the soul's different faculties. The three types of <i>spirit</i> correspond to the three faculties of the soul admitted by the ancient physicians, which are the <i>natural</i> faculty, the <i>vital</i> faculty, and the <i>psychic</i> (or animal) faculty. See the <i>Canon</i> of Ibn Sina, book I, <i>fen</i>. 1, ch. 6 (Arabic text, p. 38; Latin version, vol. I, p. 76); cf. Maimonides, <i>Eight Chapters</i>, at the beginning of chapter I, and the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, toward the end of chapters XII and XLVI, where the author alludes to these three faculties. Here is how Maimonides himself, in one of his medical treatises, explains the theory of the <i>spirits</i> and their different types, according to the ancient physicians: <span dir=\"rtl\">הד׳ה אלתי תסמיהא אלאטבא ארואחא הי אבכ׳רה̈ לטיפה̈ מוז׳ודה פי ז׳סם אלחיואן מבדאהא ומעט׳ם מאדתהא מן אלהוא אלמסתנשק מן כ׳ארז׳ פבכ׳אר אלדם אלמוז׳וד פי אלכבד ופי אלאורדה̈ <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> נאבתה̈ מנהא יסמי אלוח אלטביעיה̈ ובכ׳אר אלדם <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> מוז׳וד פי אלקלב ואלשריאן יסמי אלוח אלחיואניה̈ ואלבכ׳אר אלמוז׳וד פי בטון <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> דטאג ומא ינבעת׳ מנה פי מסאם <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> אעצאב יסמי רוחא נפסאניה̈ ומבדא אלז׳מיע ואכת׳ר מאדתה מן אלהוא אלמסתנשק מן כ׳ארז׳ פאד׳א כאן הד׳א <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> הוא עפנא או מנתנא או כדרא תגירת תלך <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> ארואח כלהא וז׳רי אמרהא עלי כ׳לאף מא ינבגי ֗֗֗֗ וכלמא כאנת אלוח אלטף כאן תגירהא בתגיר אלהוא אכת׳ר פאלוח <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> טביעי אגלט׳ מן אלוח <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> חיואני ואלחיואני אגלט׳ מן אלנפסאני פענד תגיר אלהוא איסר תגיר יתגיר חאל אלנפסאני תגירא ישער בה ולד׳לך תז׳ד כת׳ירא מן אלנאס קד ישערו בנקץ אפעאלהם אלנפסאניה̈ ענד פסאד אלהוא אעני אן יחדת׳ להם בלאדה̈ פהם וקצור אדראך ונקצאן חפט׳ ואן כאנת אפעאלהם אלחיואניה̈ ואלטביעיה̈ לא ישער פיהא בתגיר</span> 'What physicians call <i>spirits</i> are subtle vapors that exist in the body of the animal and which derive their origin and their principal matter from the air drawn from outside by respiration. The vapor of the blood existing in the liver and in the veins that originate from it is called the <i>natural spirit</i> or <i>physical</i> (<i>φυσικὸν πνεῦμα</i>); the vapor of the blood existing in the heart and in the arteries is called the <i>vital spirit</i> (<i>ζωτικὸν πν</i>.); finally, the vapor found in the cavities of the brain and that which spreads from it into the channels of the nerves is called <i>animal spirit</i> (<i>ψυχικὸν πν</i>.). The origin of all (these <i>spirits</i>) and their principal matter come (as we have said) from the air drawn in by respiration; if, therefore, this air is corrupted, or foul-smelling, or murky, all these <i>spirits</i> are altered and are no longer what they ought to be... The more subtle the spirit is, the more it is altered by the alteration of the air. Now, as the <i>natural</i> spirit is thicker than the <i>vital</i> spirit, and the latter thicker than the <i>animal</i> spirit, it follows that at the slightest alteration of the air, the <i>animal</i> spirit will undergo a perceptible alteration. This is why you will find many men in whom a weakening of the <i>animal</i> or <i>psychic</i> functions (that is, the functions depending on the <i>animal</i> spirits) is observed when the air is corrupted; I mean that it happens to them to have dull intelligence, difficult understanding, and a weakened memory, although no alteration is noticed in their <i>vital</i> and <i>natural</i> functions.' Cf. Galen, <i>De Hippocr. et Plat. placitis</i>, book VII, chapter 3, and above, p. 111, note 2. — The passage we have just cited is found at the beginning of book IV of the treatise titled <span dir=\"rtl\">في تدبير الصحة</span> (On the Regimen of Health), which Maimonides composed at the request of Al-Malik al-Afdhal, son of Saladin, and whose Arabic original, copied in Hebrew characters, is in the Imperial Library (see Hebrew mss. of the old collection no. 412, fol. 107 <i>a.b</i>, and no. 411, fol. 57 <i>b</i> and 46 <i>a)</i>. The Hebrew version of this treatise, by R. Moses Ibn Tibbon, was published in the collection titled <span dir=\"rtl\">כרם חמד</span>, vol. III (Prague, 1838, in-8vo), p. 9 et seq. There also exists a Latin version of it which has been printed several times.",
    "fn.1061": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">جوّز</span> signifies <i>to allow to pass</i>, and, figuratively, <i>to judge admissible</i> or <i>permitted</i>, and the word <span dir=\"rtl\">تجهيز</span>, which is its <i>verbal noun</i>, is used by the <i>Mutakallimun</i> as a technical term to designate the famous proposition stating that everything the imagination can picture is also <i>admissible</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(جائز)</span> to the reason, that everything in the universe could be other than it is, and that nothing is impossible, provided that an idea of it can be formed. Cf. Maimonides, <i>Eight Chapters</i>, or Introduction to the treatise <i>Aboth</i>, ch. I<sup>st</sup> (<i>Porta Mosis</i> of Pococke, p. 188).—One must truly be surprised to see an author of our day assert with confidence that not only are there very essential errors in the <i>alleged</i> enumeration that Maimonides provides here of the principles of the <i>Mutakallimun</i>, but that <i>even what he calls their primary foundation—namely, that there is no certain knowledge of things, since the contrary can always exist and be thought in our understanding—is entirely erroneous and diametrically opposed to dogmatic doctrine</i> (Schmölders, <i>Essays</i>, p. 135). The same author insinuates that Maimonides only knew the doctrines of the <i>Mutakallimun</i> through the false reports of their adversaries; but it follows from Maimonides' own declaration that he perfectly knew the doctrines in question, and here, as everywhere else, his assertions are based on a serious and thorough study of the most authentic sources; see the beginning of chapter LXXIV. It is sufficient, moreover, to read the <i>Destruction of the Destruction</i> of Ibn Rushd, which contains numerous details on the <i>Mutakallimun</i>, and where one finds all the propositions enumerated by Maimonides. These two illustrious men must have been at least as well informed as the more recent authors that Mr. Schmölders may have consulted. As for this X<sup>th</sup> proposition, which Maimonides calls <i>the basis of the science of Kalam</i>, it is so essential to the system of the <i>Mutakallimun</i> that it is impossible to separate it from it. Ibn Rushd also returns to it on several occasions. We content ourselves with citing here a single passage, where Ibn Rushd (speaking of the principle of <i>causality</i> denied by the <i>Mutakallimun</i>, and notably by the <i>Ash'arites</i>, who see in all phenomena and in all actions that occur in the world the immediate intervention of the Creator) expresses himself thus: <span dir=\"rtl\">והניחו עם זה כל הנמצאות פעולות עוברות ולא יראו שיש בם סדר ולא ערך ולא חכמה גזר אותם טבע הנמצאות אבל האמינו שכל נמצא אפשר שיהיה בחלוף מה שהוא</span> \"They have, with this, posited as a principle that all existing things are <i>admissible</i> facts (and not <i>necessary</i>), and they do not admit that there is in them either order, or proportion, or wisdom, resulting from the <i>nature</i> of things. They believe, on the contrary, that everything that exists could be other than it is.\" See <i>Destr. of the Destruction</i>, III<sup>rd</sup> question (Hebrew trans., MS of the Oratory collection, no. 93, fol. 243 <i>a</i>). Cf. Moses of Narbonne, Commentary on the <i>Moreh Nevukhim</i>, I<sup>st</sup> part, at the end of ch. LIII; Ibn Falaquera, <i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, I, chs. LXXIII and LXXIV (pp. 58 and 63); Ritter, <i>Geschichte der Philosophie</i>, vol. VII, p. 737.",
    "fn.1269": "On infinity in general, see Aristotle, <i>Physics</i>, book III, ch. 4-8; <i>Metaph</i>., book II, ch. 2; book XI, ch. 10. Aristotle shows in these various passages that, in nature, the infinitely great <i>in actuality</i>, that is to say infinite extension, is inadmissible, and he admits as infinite only the infinitely small or the infinite divisibility of space, which he designates (as well as the infinity of abstract number) as <i>potential infinity</i>. Consequently, the universe itself, which is the most extended body, is limited in space (see the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, book I, ch. 7, where Aristotle says in conclusion: <i>ὅτι μὲν τοίνυν οὐκ ἐστὶ τὸ σῶμα τὸ τοῦ παντὸς ἄπειρον ἐκ τούτων φανερόν</i>). The most general demonstration of this first proposition is given in the very definition of body; Aristotle observes that from a <i>logical</i> point of view (<i>λογικῶς</i>), the existence of an infinite body is inadmissible: for, if the idea one has of a body is to be limited by surfaces, there can be no unlimited body, whether intelligible or sensible (<i>εἰ γάρ ἐστι σώματος λόγος τὸ ἐπιπέδῳ ὡρισμένον, οὐκ ἂν εἴη σῶμα ἄπειρον, οὔτε νοητὸν οὔτε αἰσθητόν</i>. <i>Physics</i>, III, 5, <i>Metaph</i>., XI, 10). From a <i>physical</i> point of view, Aristotle shows that an infinite body could be neither composite nor simple: 1. If it were composite, the parts of the composition would be either infinite or finite; now it is evident that they could not be infinite, for the infinity of each necessarily excludes that of the others; but neither could they be finite, for they would be consumed by the infinite and would disappear completely before it. 2. Neither could an infinite body be simple; for none of the elements, each of which has its determined region, is infinite, and there exists no sensible body outside the elements that unites them all, as several physicists have believed.— Another physical proof (which is linked in a way to the logical proof) is this: \"Every sensible body is in space. The species and differences of space are: up and down, front and back, right and left. These distinctions do not exist solely in relation to us and by position, but are grounded in the whole itself.\" However, they could not exist in the infinite.\" (See <i>Physics, loc. cit.</i> at the end of ch. 5). Ibn Sina and other authors, Arabic and Jewish, multiplied the demonstrations of the propositions enumerated by Maimonides. Abu Bakr Muhammad al-Tabrizi, who wrote a commentary on the twenty-five propositions, went into great detail to demonstrate their truth. This 1<sup>st</sup> proposition was demonstrated by the Arabs in several different ways. We shall cite here a demonstration borrowed from Ibn Sina: Let the magnitude supposed to be infinite be a line AB <span dir=\"rtl\">أ ج ب</span>; we may suppose that this line extends to infinity on both sides or only on one side B. In the latter case, let us imagine that from the finite side a part AC is cut; we will then have two lines, AB and CB, each of which will be infinite on side B and finite on the other side. Now, if we apply point C to point A, one of two things will happen: either line CB will extend to infinity like line AB, and then we will have AB—AC=AB, which is impossible, for the part cannot be equal to the whole; or line CB will extend less than line AB, and then it will be finite on side B, which is contrary to the hypothesis. If line AB is supposed to be infinite on both sides, it can be cut at any point so as to make two lines, each of which will be infinite on one side and finite on the other, and the demonstration will be the same as the one just given. Cf. Shahrastani, p. 403 (German trans., vol. <small>II</small>, pp. 295 and 296). — We cannot reproduce the proofs alleged for each of the propositions enumerated here by Maimonides, and we must limit ourselves to indicating the places from which our author drew these various propositions. As will be seen, they are not all borrowed from Aristotle, and several are taken from the works of Ibn Sina, which, as we have already stated elsewhere, are the principal source from which Maimonides drew his knowledge of Peripatetic doctrines. On this first proposition and the following two, see also the 1<sup>st</sup> part of this work, ch. LXXIII, 11<sup>th</sup> proposition, and the notes we have appended there (vol. I, pp. 413 and 414).",
    "fn.1281": "This proposition, stated in too concise a manner, has been found obscure, and when taken in an absolute sense, has met with objections (see the commentary of Moses of Narbonne). This is how it should be understood: Anything that does not have <i>within itself</i> the principle of its motion, but to which an accidental motion is imparted by an external cause that can cease to exist, will necessarily be at rest when that cause ceases, as, for example, a passenger on a ship, who is moved only because he is accidentally in a moving thing that can stop moving (cf. Aristotle, <i>On the Soul,</i> Book I, chap. 3, § 2). What proves that Maimonides understands this proposition in this way is that further on, at the beginning of chap. I, in demonstrating that the first mover cannot be considered the soul of the celestial sphere, he applies this VIII<sup>th</sup> proposition to the human soul, which is moved <i>accidentally</i> with the body by an external cause, either by seeking what is suitable for it or by fleeing what is contrary to it. Therefore, the Arab commentator Al-Tabrizi is wrong to object to this proposition of Maimonides that there are certain motions which, although accidental, are nonetheless perpetual, such as, for example, the diurnal motion from east to west imparted by the ninth sphere to the eight lower spheres, which is contrary to their own <i>essential</i> motion from west to east; or as the circular motion of the sphere of fire and the other elements, whose <i>essential</i> motion is in a straight line (cf. Vol. I of this work, pp. 357-359). It is evident that these accidental motions, having as their cause an essential and perpetual motion, must themselves be perpetual. The proposition in question here appears to relate to a passage from the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i> (Book I, end of chap. 2), where Aristotle establishes that above the four elements which, by their nature, have a straight-line motion, there is a simple substance of another nature that has circular motion. Now, he observes, this motion must be inherent to the nature of this substance; for it would be astonishing and entirely unreasonable if this motion, which alone is continuous and eternal, could be <i>contrary to nature</i>, since generally what is contrary to nature is quickly destroyed: E<i>ἰ δὲ παρὰ φύσιν φέρεται τά φερόμενα ϰύϰλῳ τὴν περὶξ φοράν, θαυμαστὸν ϰαὶ παντελῶς ἀλογον τὸ μόνέν εἶναι συνεχῆ τάυτην τὴν ϰὶνησιν ϰαι ἀίδιον, οὖσαν παρὰ, φύσιν · φαίνεται γὰρ ἔν γε τοῖς ἀλλοις τάχιστα φθειρόμενα τὰ παρὰ φύσιν</i>. The Arabic version, which rendered the words <i>contrary to nature</i> (<i>παρὰ φύσιν</i>) as <i>accidental</i>, better explains the terms of Maimonides' proposition. Here is how the passage we have just cited was paraphrased in Ibn Rushd's Middle Commentary on <i>De Caelo et Mundo,</i> Book I, <i>Summa</i> IV, <i>Demonstration</i> 5 (Hebrew version): <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא שזאת התנועה הסבובית שהיא סביב האמצע לא המלט משתהיה לזה הגשם טבעית או מקרית ושקר הוא שתהיה מקרית בי התנועה המקרית אי אפשר שתמצא תמידית אין תכלית לה ושהצעת זה יוצאה מכל הקש כי אԃנחנו רואים הדברים המקריים כלים אובדים</span>.",
    "fn.1293": "In this proposition, the author establishes that local motion (certainly that which, in the preceding proposition, was designated as the only one that is continuous) is prior <i>according to nature</i> to all other motions and changes; and by <i>prior according to nature</i> one must understand, in accordance with Aristotle's definition (<i>Metaph</i>., book V, chap. 11), that which can be without other things being, but without which other things cannot be. The terms of this proposition are drawn from Aristotle's <i>Physics</i>, book VIII, chap. 7 (Cf. book VII, chap. 2), although the author, it seems to me, does not strictly follow the reasoning of the Stagirite. It was seen above, p. 6, note 2, that according to Aristotle, the idea of motion applies to the categories of quantity, quality, and place. Now, he says, of these three kinds of motion, that of place is necessarily the first: for it is impossible for there to be <i>growth</i> without there having first been <i>transformation.</i> Transformation is change into what is opposite; but when there is transformation, there must be something that transforms and which causes, for example, that which is hot in potentiality to become hot in act. Now it is evident that the mobile [agent] of this transformation is sometimes closer and sometimes further from the thing to be transformed, and that the transformation cannot take place without local motion; the latter, consequently, is the first among the motions. Further on, Aristotle establishes by other proofs that local motion, although it is the last to develop in the individual beings of this world, is the first in the universe and even precedes the birth (<i>γένεσις</i>) of all things, which is followed by transformation and growth (<i>μετὰ γὰρ τὸ γενέσθαι πρῶτον ἀλλοίωσις ϰαὶ αὔξησɩς</i>; <i>Phys</i>., VIII, 7, page 260 <i>b</i>, line 32). One might initially find it surprising that Maimonides places transformation before generation and corruption; however, it appears that our author considers transformation from a more general perspective—that is, not only in relation to the category of quality, as in the IV<sup>th</sup> proposition, but also in relation to <i>generation</i>, which is itself in some sense a transformation of matter by form, a transformation effected by an agent more or less remote, which needs to draw closer to the matter to be transformed. It is also in this sense that Averroes explains the passage in the <i>Physics</i>. See the Works of Aristotle with the Commentaries of Averroes, folio ed., vol. IV, fol. 180 <i>c</i>: «Deinde dicit: <i>Et manifestum est quod dispositio motoris tunc non currit eodem modo</i>, <i>sed forte quandoque erit propinquior alterato et quandoque remotior</i>, etc. Id est, et quia primum alterans, quod non alteratur, non alterat semper, sed quandoque, necesse est ut non habeat se cum alterato in eadem dispositione, sed quandoque appropinquetur ei, et alteret, et quandoque removeatur, et non alteret: et propinquitas, et distantia non est, nisi per translationem: ergo translatio præcedit naturaliter alterationem, scilicet quod, cum utraque fuerit in actu: deinde alterans alteravit, postquam non alterabat: necesse est ut alterum moveatur in loco aut alterans, aut alteratum, aut utrumque. Si autem alterum fuerit <i>generatum</i>, aut utrumque, et posuerimus hoc esse causam ejus, quod quandoque alterat, et quandoque non, <i>manifestabitur quod translatio debet prœcedere eodem modo, cum alteratio etiam prœcedat generationem; generatio enim aut est alteratio aut sequitur alterationem</i>.»",
    "fn.1374": "This demonstration appears to be based on a passage from Aristotle's <i>Physics</i>, which may be summarized as follows: Three things may be considered in motion: the thing moved, the mover, and that by which the latter moves. That which is moved does not necessarily communicate motion; that which serves as an instrument or intermediary communicates motion at the same time as it receives it; finally, that which moves without being an instrument or intermediary is itself immobile. Now, since we see (in the universe), on the one hand, that which is moved without having within itself the principle of motion—that is, without moving anything else—and on the other hand, that which is simultaneously moved by another and the mover of another, it is reasonable, if not necessary, to admit a third thing which moves without being moved. See <i>Phys.</i>, Book VIII, ch. 5 (Bekker ed., p. 256 <i>b</i>): <i>τρία γὰρ ἀνάγκη εἶναι, τό τε κινουμένον, καὶ τὸ κινοῦν, καὶ τὸ ᾧ κινεῖ. κ. τ. λ.</i> In the <i>Metaphysics</i>, Book XII, chap. VII, Aristotle summarizes himself in these terms: <i>ἔστι τοίνυν τι καὶ ὃ κινεῖ, ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ κινούμενον καὶ κινοῦν, καὶ μέσοντοίνυν ἐστί τι ὃ οὐ κινούμενον κινεῖ, ἀΐδιον καὶ οὐσία καὶ ἐνέργεια οὖσα</i>. Alexander of Aphrodisias explained this passage in nearly the same terms used by Maimonides in this second demonstration; and it is evidently from Alexander that our author borrowed his argumentation, as well as the proposition he places at the head as having been stated by Aristotle himself. Alexander's explanation was cited by Averroes in his large commentary on the <i>Metaphysics</i>. We reproduce it from the Latin version of this commentary (folio ed., f. 149 <i>verso</i>): \"Alexander said: This is the reason why [there is] some mover [which] is not moved, and it is stated briefly and as a reminder of what was said in the last [book] of the Physics. And it is founded upon two propositions, one of which is that every composite of two things, of which one can exist by itself, the other will also possibly exist by itself, unless the composition be of substance and accident; for example, because hydromel is composed of water and honey, and honey is found by itself, it is therefore necessary that water be found by itself. And, because we find something moved and moving as if composed of a mover and a moved, and we find something moved by itself and not moving, it is manifest that it is necessary for there to be some mover and not moved.\" \"This mover, therefore, is immune from potentiality, and exists in no matter.\" As for the proposition attributed by Maimonides to Aristotle, some commentators have objected that, in what is composed of <i>substance</i> and <i>accident</i>, one cannot imagine the existence of the accident alone, although evidently the substance can exist alone. This objection fails due to the express condition set by Alexander: <i>nisi compositio sit substantiæ et accidentis</i>. Maimonides, in copying Alexander, perhaps omitted this condition through inadvertence.",
    "fn.1429": "As we have said, the author, in this enumeration of the Intelligences, followed Ibn Sina, who, besides the supreme Intelligence, or God, admits ten Intelligences, the first of which, emanating directly from God (cf. below, ch. <small>XXII</small>), is that of the sphere of diurnal motion, which surrounds the entire universe, and the last of which, emanating from the Intelligence of the lunar sphere, is the <i>active intellect</i>. See <i>Avicennæ opera</i>, <i>Metaph</i>., IX, <i>loc. cit.</i>: «Si autem circuli planetarum fuerint sic quod principium motus circulorum uniuscujus-que planetarum sit virtus fluens a planeta, tunc non erit longe quin <i>separata</i> sint secundum numerum planetarum, non secundum numerum circulorum; et tune eorum numerus est decem post primum (that is to say, God). Primum autem eorum est Intelligentia quæ non movetur, cujus est movere sphæram corporis ultimi (that is to say, the <i>diurnal</i> sphere). Deinde id quod sequitur est quod movet sphæram fixarum. Deinde sequitur quod movet sphæram Saturni. Simililer est quousque pervenitur ad Intelligentiam a qua fluit super nostras animas; et hæc est Intelligentia mundi terreni, et vocamus eam <i>Intelligentiam agentem</i>.» Ibn Rushd, identifying the sphere of diurnal motion with that of the fixed stars, admits only eight spheres. See <i>Epitome in libros metaph</i>., tract. <small>IV</small> (fol. 182, col. <i>a</i>): «Tandem apud me est remotum quod inveniatur orbis nonus sine stellis; nam orbis est stellarum gratia, quæ sunt nobilior pars ejus…. Orbis autem qui movet motum magnum est nobilior cæteris or-bibus; quapropter non videtur nobis quod sit sine stellis, immo apud me est impossibile.» According to this, there would be in total only <i>nine separate Intelligences</i>, the last of which, as in Ibn Sina's theory, is the <i>active intellect</i>. This, therefore, according to the theory of the Arabs, is in a close relationship with the Intelligence of the lunar sphere, from which it emanates directly, but with which it must not be identified. — [In my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, at the beginning of page 332 (cf. also pp. 165 and 448, and Vol. I of this work, p. 277, and note 3), I expressed myself on this matter in an inaccurate manner, and I should have said: «From the last of these <i>separate Intelligences</i>, which presides over the movement of the sphere closest to us (that of the moon), the active intellect <i>emanates</i>, etc.»] — The passage from Ibn Rushd (<i>loc. cit</i>., fol. 184, col. <i>d</i>), which has often been cited to show that, according to this author, the active intellect is itself the mover of the lunar sphere (see Ritter, <i>Geschichte der philosophie</i>, Vol. VIII, p. 148), is thus phrased in the Hebrew version: <span dir=\"rtl\">והשכל הפועל הוא יםודר מםוף אלו המניעים במדרגה ונניחו מניע גלגל הירח</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מםוף</span> seems to me to be an inaccurate translation of the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">من آخر</span> , and it should be translated as follows: «The active intellect emanates from the last of these movers in rank (i.e., from that one of these movers which by its rank is the last), which we assume to be the mover of the sphere of the moon.» To leave no doubt in this regard, I shall cite here a passage from Moses of Narbonne's commentary on Al-Ghazali's <i>Maqāṣid</i>, towards the end of the II<sup>nd</sup> part, or of the <i>Metaph</i>., where the theory of the spheres and their intelligences is set forth (according to Ibn Sina) (see Hebrew MS. of the Oratory collection, no. 93, fol. 179 <i>a</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">ואמנם בן רשד ובל הפילוםופים מודים שהשבל הפועל בלתי מניע לגלגל רק שהוא עשירי לשבלים הנפרדים לפי בן רשד והשבל הראשון בבלל והוא עשירי לשבלים העלולים מבלתי מנוה במחוייב המציאות לפי אבוחמד</span>. «Ibn Rushd and all the philosophers agree that the <i>active intellect</i> is not the mover of any celestial sphere. But, according to Ibn Rushd, it is the tenth of the separate Intelligences, if the first Intelligence (or God) is also included; whereas, according to Abu Hamid (al-Ghazali), it is the tenth of the <i>caused</i> Intelligences, without counting the <i>Necessary Being</i> (i.e., the first absolute cause, or God).»—Cf. Albert the Great, <i>De Causis et processu universitatis</i>, bk. I, tract <small>IV</small>, ch. 8 (Opp. Vol. V, p. 562 <i>a</i>): “Moreover, after the Intelligence of the lunar sphere and the lunar sphere itself, which (as Aristotle says) is in some way terrestrial, there is an Intelligence that illuminates above the sphere of the active and passive [elements], whose light it diffuses in the active and passive, which illuminates above the souls of men, and whose power is conceived in the seeds of generated and passive things.”",
    "fn.1527": "The four astronomical terms used here by the author are borrowed from Ptolemy's theories on the motion of the planets. By <span dir=\"rtl\">اختلاف</span>, one designates the <i>anomaly</i> or the inequality of a celestial body; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">رجوع</span> designates the apparent <i>retrogradation</i> (<i>προνήγησις</i>) of the five planets, as opposed to their <i>direct motion</i> (<i>ὑπόλειψις</i>) designated in Arabic by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">استقامة</span>, <i>rectitude;</i> finally, by <span dir=\"rtl\">إقامة</span>, is meant what Ptolemy calls the <i>station</i> (<i>στηριγμός</i>) of these same planets, that is, the position where the motion of the celestial body appears to stop. Cf. <i>Almagest</i>, book XII, chap. I. In the Arabic version of the <i>Almagest</i> (Hebrew ms. of the Imperial Library, old collection, no. 441), the terms <i>προνήγησις</i> and <i>ὑπόλειψις</i> are rendered more accurately, the first by <span dir=\"rtl\">تقدّم</span>, <i>moving forward,</i> the second by <span dir=\"rtl\">تأخر</span>, <i>remaining behind.</i> Later, the Arabs substituted the first of these two terms with <span dir=\"rtl\">رجوع</span>, <i>retrogradation</i>, and the second with <span dir=\"rtl\">استقامة</span>, <i>rectitude.</i> Indeed, since the periodic motion of the planets occurs from west to east, the planet, when it appears to <i>retrograde</i>, i.e., when it appears less advanced in longitude, is forward in relation to the diurnal motion from east to west; the more advanced it is in longitude in relation to the periodic motion, the further behind it is in relation to the diurnal motion. Delambre, to explain the two terms used by Ptolemy, expresses himself thus (<i>Notes on the Almagest</i>, following the edition by Abbé Halma, vol. II, p. 16): \"One says of a star that precedes another at the meridian, which passes it before him, which moves at its head, that it is <i>προηγούμενος</i>; of a star that passes after him, that it is <i>ἑπόμενος</i>.\" \"But the one that passes first at the meridian is less advanced in longitude; the one following it, <i>ἑπόμενος</i>, which remains behind, <i>ὑπολειπόμενος</i>, is on the contrary more advanced in longitude; thus a star is <i>προηγούμενος</i> when its longitude decreases and it retrogrades; <i>προνήγησις</i> in the language of the Greeks, therefore, corresponds to <i>retrogradation</i>; it is the same motion considered in relation to two different points, just as, in the theory of curves, one may at will place negative abscissas either to the right or left of the center indifferently.\" — The terms <i>retrogradation</i> and <i>rectitude</i> introduced by the Arabs are also found in the <i>Epitome of the Almagest</i> by Ibn Aflah, book VIII, where the Hebrew version, which alone we can consult, uses for the former the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חזרה</span>, and for the latter the word <span dir=\"rtl\">יושר</span>. It is probably in the work of Ibn Aflah that Maimonides took these terms (cf. above, p. 81).",
    "fn.1598": "It can be seen that, according to Maimonides, the difference between Plato and Aristotle is this: that the latter admits not only the eternity of prime matter, but also that of motion and time; whereas Plato, while admitting the eternity of matter and chaos, nevertheless believes that the world, as it is, had a beginning, that the heavens were, like sublunary things, produced from chaos, and that consequently motion and time had a beginning. It is in this sense that Plato's opinion was generally interpreted by the Arabs and the Scholastics, and it is in this sense also that one of the most learned adherents of the new Platonic school of Italy expresses himself. This is how Leone Ebreo speaks on this question (<i>Dialoghi di amore</i>, III, Venice edition, 1572, fol. 145 et seq.): «Concedendo tutti gli huomini che’l sommo Dio genitore et opifice del mondo sia eterno, senza alcun principio temporale, son divisi nella produttion del mondo, se è ab eterno, o da qualche tempo in quà. Molti dei filosofi tengono essere prodotto ab eterno da Dio, e non havere mai havuto principio temporale, così come esso Dio non l’ha mai havuto, et di questa opinione è il grande Aristolile, et tutti i peripatetici… Ma gli fideli, et tutti quelli che credono la sacra legge di Moise, tengono che’l mondo fusse non ab eterno produtto, anzi di nulla creato in principio temporale, et ancora alcuni dei filosofi par que sentino questo; per quali è il divino Platone, che nel Timeo pone il mondo essere fatto et genito da Dio, produtto del chaos, che è la materia confusa, del quale le cose sono generate… È ben vero che lui fa il chaos, di che le cose sono fatte, eterno, cioè eternalmente produtto da Dio, laquai cosa non tengono gli fideli; perche loro tengono che fino all’ hora della creatione solo Dio fusse in essere senza mondo, et senza chaos, et che l’omnipotentia di Dio di nulla tutte le cose in principio di tempo habbia produtto, che in effetto non par già chiaramente in Moise, che’l ponga materia coeterna a Dio.— Sono adunque tre opinioni nella produttione del mondo da Dio: la prima d’Aristotile, che tutto il mondo fue produtto ab eterno; la seconda di Platone, che solamente la materia, o chaos, fue produtto ab eterno, ma il mondo in principio di tempo; et la terza delli fideli, che tutto sia produtto di nulla in principio di tempo.» — But one recognizes from our passage that in the time of Maimonides, as in all eras, opinions were divided on the true sense of Plato's doctrine; and, indeed, Plato's poetic language and the mythical veil under which he often presents his doctrines only too well justify this divergence of opinions. While several of the oldest Platonists, and later the Neoplatonists, claimed that Plato had admitted, like Aristotle, the eternity of the world, others on the contrary (notably some Christians, such as Clement of Alexandria) went so far as to maintain that Plato had professed the doctrine of creation <i>ex nihilo</i>, considering God to be the author not only of the order of the world, but of matter itself. The same divergence is still observed among the scholars of our day. On this subject, see Zeller, <i>Die Philosophie der Griechen</i>, vol. II, p. 508 et seq. (2<sup>nd</sup> ed. Tübingen, 1859), and Henri Martin, <i>Études sur le Timée de Platon</i>, vol. II, p. 179 et seq. This latter scholar, after a thorough examination of the texts, arrives at conclusions that agree with the opinion of Maimonides and Leone Ebreo, namely that, according to the <i>Timaeus</i>, 1. God did not create the prime matter of bodies, that is, the indeterminate substance; 2. he did not even create secondary matter, that is, eternal chaos; 3. he produced the order of the world, but not from all eternity. — The distinction between incorporeal prime matter and corporeal chaos is of no interest to us here, any more than the question of how Plato understood prime matter and in what way his doctrine, in this regard, differs from that of Aristotle. Suffice it to say that Plato admits an eternal principle opposed to the <i>idea,</i> as blind necessity is to reason, non-being to being, the variable and the multiple to the permanent and the absolute <i>one</i>. But this is not the <i>ὕλη</i> of Aristotle, that <i>being in potentiality</i> which necessarily calls for form to become <i>being in actuality;</i> for, according to Plato, it is with consciousness and freedom, and not by necessity, that the idea realizes itself in the indeterminate substratum, which it causes to pass from confusion to order. The term <i>ὕλη</i> is itself unknown to Plato, although Aristotle uses this term when speaking of his master's doctrine. This confused and indeterminate substance that the idea <i>orders</i> and in which it manifests itself is called by Plato: <i>that which receives the imprint</i> (<i>τὸ ἐϰμαγεῖον</i>), <i>that in which things are born, the place</i>, <i>space,</i> etc.; it is also presented as <i>the mother</i> or <i>the receptacle and the nurse of all generation;</i> and Plato himself says (<i>Timaeus</i>, p. 49 <i>a</i>) that it is a species quite obscure and very difficult to understand. Cf. Ritter, <i>Geschichte der Philosophie</i>, vol. II, p. 347 et seq.; Brandis, <i>Handbuch der Geschichte der griechisch-römischen Philosophie</i>, vol. II, A, p. 297 et seq.; Zeller, <i>l. c.</i>, p. 457 et seq.; Henri Martin, <i>l. c</i>., vol. I, p. 16 et seq.",
    "fn.2473": "Maimonides, in approaching his oblique explanation of Ezekiel's vision, in which he seems to find the principal cosmological doctrines of the Arab Peripatetics, deems it necessary to indicate first what is to be understood by the four faces attributed to each of the four <i>'hayyôth</i>, or animal figures (Ezekiel 1:10), in which he sees the celestial spheres, reduced to four: that of the moon, that of the sun, that of the five other planets, and that of the fixed stars. See Part II<sup>e</sup> of this work at the end of Chap. <small>IX</small>, and Chap. <small>X</small>, p. 82 ff. The prophet attributes to the <i>'hayyôth</i> a human face, a lion's face, an ox's face, and an eagle's face, making them the representatives of what is most noble among the different species of animals. According to our author, the three faces of irrational animals are also human faces; but he does not tell us why he insists on this, and he leaves us to guess what he means by the symbol of the four faces. We believe that the author is alluding to the four causes of the movement of the spheres, indicated according to him by the four wings (<i>v.</i> 6), and of which he spoke in Chaps. <small>IV</small> and <small>X</small> of Part II<sup>e</sup>; these are: the shape of the sphere, i.e., its sphericity, its soul, its intellect by which it conceives, and the separate Intelligence, the object of its desire (see Vol. II, p. 88). In the sublunary world, man is the only being in whom these four causes of movement are reflected; man alone possesses a soul endowed with conception and an intellect, which are set in motion by the desire to unite with the separate intelligence, i.e., with the universal <i>active intellect</i>; and from this point of view, man's movement has nothing in common with that of irrational animals and can only be compared to the circular movement of the spheres. Cf. Vol. II, Chap. <small>IV</small>, p. 52. The explanation we have just given is indicated by Samuel ibn Tibbon in his treatise <i>Yikkawou ha-maïm</i>, Chap. XI, p. 47: <span dir=\"rtl\">וענין הנפש והשכל רמז אליו בשרמז שכלם יש להם פני אדם וכל הפנים פני אדם</span>. We omit other explanations given by Moses of Narbonne and Ephodi, which relate to what the author said about the four forces emanating from the four spheres and acting on the four elements (Vol. II, Chap. <small>X</small>, p. 89); these explanations offer no interest for science or its history, and we shall limit ourselves to what is strictly necessary for the understanding of Maimonides' obscure words. — Furthermore, the entire interpretation that Maimonides gives here, in veiled terms, of Ezekiel's vision, is based, as we have said, on Peripatetic cosmology; but if the prophet, as is probable, used astronomical symbols, he could only have borrowed them from the astronomy of his time, and one would have to look to Chaldean science for an explanation. Isaac Abravanel completely overturned Maimonides' interpretation by rightly observing that, if this were the meaning of Ezekiel's vision, there would be no reason to make such a mystery of it, since it would only concern a science taught in all schools of philosophy and familiar to scholars of all faiths. See Abravanel's second Preface to the book of Ezekiel and his commentary on Chap. I of the same book; cf. also Abravanel's commentary on various parts of the <i>Moreh Nevukhim</i>, Book II<sup>e</sup>, fol. 48 ff.",
    "fn.3123": "As I have already noted in my <i>Réflexions sur le culte des anciens Hébreux</i>, p. 2 (published in Vol. IV of Mr. Cahen's <i>Bible</i>), Maimonides, like many other Arabic authors of his time, understands the word <span dir=\"rtl\">צאבה̈</span> or <i>Sabians</i> to refer to all pagan peoples in general. The books from which he drew his knowledge of pagan cults, and which he will discuss further on, led him to believe that these cults were generally based on astrolatry. Consequently, in Maimonides' language, <i>religion of the Sabians</i> signifies the same thing as <i>paganism.</i> In the Quran (<small>II</small>, 59; v, 73; <small>XXII</small>, 17), the Sabians <span dir=\"rtl\">(الصابيون)</span> are mentioned alongside Jews and Christians as a religious community possessing revealed books and having a share in the afterlife. It is generally agreed that the Sabians of the Quran are the Mandaeans or Christians of Saint John, who, because of their frequent ablutions, are called in Syriac <span dir=\"rtl\">צביעיין</span> (or <span dir=\"rtl\">צביין</span>, according to the pronunciation of the Mandaeans, who omit the <span dir=\"rtl\">ע</span>), that is to say, the <i>Baptists.</i> See <i>E. Castelli Lexicon-syriacum seorsim typis describi curavit atque sua adnotata adjecit</i>, J. D. Michaëlis, p. 749. Arabic authors, starting from the time of Caliph Al-Ma'mun, also give the name Sabians to the pagans of Harran and some other cities of Mesopotamia, who are completely different from the Sabians of the Quran, which has given rise to great confusion. Vain efforts have been made to find the etymology of the name <i>Sabians</i> applied to pagans. The most successful conjecture is that of Pococke (<i>Specimen hist. ar</i>., p. 139), who derives this name from the Hebrew word <span dir=\"rtl\">צבא</span>, <i>army</i>, and who sees in it an allusion to the worship of the stars called <span dir=\"rtl\">צבא השמים</span>, <i>host of heaven;</i> but this etymology is no more plausible than any of the others. We now know that in reality there is only one kind of <i>Sabians</i>, namely the Mandaeans, but that the pagans of Harran, threatened with extermination by Caliph Al-Ma'mun, usurped around the year 830 of the Christian era, on the advice of a Muslim doctor, the name of Sabians, and claimed to be the sect mentioned under this name in the Quran and recommended by the prophet to the protection of Muslims. This fact is reported in the <i>Kitab Al-Fihrist</i> by Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Nadim (see the extract from this book given by Mr. de Hammer in the Journal Asiatique, September-October 1841, p. 254 et seq.). It had already been published by Hottinger, <i>Historia Orientalis</i>, p. 169, and, following him, by Spencer, <i>De legibus Hebræorum ritualibus</i>, Bk. II, ch. <small>I</small>, sect. 2 (p. 241 of the Cambridge edition, 1685, folio). It is especially in the work published on the Sabians by Mr. Chwolson that the fact in question was brought to light and supported by numerous proofs. The author shows how the name Sabians, applied in the Quran only to the Mandaeans and used in this sense by Arabic authors until the time of Al-Ma'mun, later served to designate the Harranians as well and ended, in the 6<sup>th</sup> century of the Hijra, by being used in the general sense of Pagans. See <i>Die Ssabier und der Ssabismus</i> (2 vols. large 8vo, St. Petersburg, 1856), Vol. I, ch. <small>VI</small>, p. 139 et seq., and the entire ch. <small>VIII</small>. This excellent work contains numerous details on the religion of the Harranians, which can clarify several facts reported by Maimonides in this chapter and in those following, where our author aims to explain a large part of the ceremonial practices prescribed by Moses by means of the superstitious customs of the Sabians or pagans that the Mosaic laws tended to abolish.",
    "fn.3146": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשמס</span>, one of the two Leiden MSS, No. 18, reads <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשאם</span>; similarly, the version of Ibn Tibbon has <span dir=\"rtl\">השאם</span> (in the editions <span dir=\"rtl\">תשאם</span>), which, as is known, is the name of Syria, including Palestine. Al-Harizi had the same reading, for he translates the word in question as <span dir=\"rtl\">ארץ הצבי</span>, <i>land of beauty</i>, an expression which among the rabbis designates Palestine. However, it would be absurd to think here of Palestine or Syria, since it is stated immediately thereafter that the country in question is near India. The reading <span dir=\"rtl\">אקלים אלשמם</span>, found in nearly all the Arabic MSS, is moreover confirmed by other passages of the <i>Nabatean Agriculture</i>, where there is also mention of plants brought by Adam from southern regions to Babylon. See, for example, the passage cited by Mr. Chwolson in his Memoir on Tammuz: <i>Ueber Tammuz und die Menschenverehrung bei den alten Babyloniern</i>, in the Russian collection entitled \"Acts of the Imperial University of St. Petersburg\" for the year 1859 (St. Petersburg, 8vo, 1860), p. 167. Mr. Chwolson translates the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אקלים אלשמם</span> as <i>Sonnenland</i> or \"land of the sun\" (<i>ibid.</i>, p. 175), and he says in note 2 that this country, according to other passages, was situated to the south of India proper (that is, the Punjab), from which it was separated by a desert. The <i>land of the sun</i> would therefore be, according to Mr. Chwolson, the <i>Deccan.</i> It is true that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">إقليم</span>, <i>clima</i>, is often used by the Arabs in the sense of <i>region</i> or <i>country;</i> but no ancient or modern geographer knows the designation \"land of the sun.\" I believe, therefore, that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אקלים</span> has here its ordinary sense of <i>clima</i>, and that the 2nd <sup> </sup>clima is meant, which contains a large part of India. It is known that Ptolemy and the Arabic geographers divide the inhabited part of the earth, from South to North, into seven zones called <i>climata.</i> According to the Sabians, as will be seen below, each clima is under the influence and direction of one of the seven planets. Cf. the Hebrew work <i>Shevile Emunah</i> by R. Meir al-Dabi, II, 2 (Amsterdam edition, fol. 19 <i>a</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">כשהבורא ברא העולם נתן כח בכוכבים למשול בארץ ולהנהיגה וחלק הארצות לשבעה כוכבי לכת וגו׳</span> \"When the Creator created the world, he placed in the stars a force by which they were to dominate the earth and govern it, and he distributed the countries among the seven planets, etc.\" Yaqut, in the Introduction to his great geographical Dictionary entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">معجم البلدان</span>, speaking of the <i>seven climata</i>, indicates the respective planet that dominates each clima, according to the opinion of the Persians and the Greeks, and he says that the 2nd <sup> </sup>clima is, according to the Persians, under the influence of Jupiter <span dir=\"rtl\">(المشترى)</span>, and according to the Greeks <span dir=\"rtl\">(الروم)</span>, under that of the sun <span dir=\"rtl\">(الشمس)</span>; the 4th <sup> </sup>clima, on the contrary, is, according to the Persians, under the influence of the sun, and according to the Greeks, under that of Jupiter (Arabic MSS of the Imperial Library, supplement, No. 886, at the beginning). I believe, therefore, that the words of our passage <span dir=\"rtl\">למא כׄרגׄ מן אקלים אלשמס אלמגׄאור ללהנד</span> must be understood thus: \"when he emerged from the region of the clima of the sun (that is, the 2nd <sup> </sup>clima) which is near India.\" One cannot think here of the 4th <sup> </sup>clima, no region of which is near India. We should recall that India is also, according to Muslim tradition, the place of Adam's first residence after his fall. Driven from paradise, according to this tradition, he fell upon the mountain of Serandib, which is the island of Ceylon. See d’Herbelot, <i>Bibliothèque orientale</i>, p. 55 <i>b.</i>",
