    "fn.1429.sub_2": "562 <i>a</i>): 'After intelligence, however, of the moon and the moon itself, which (as Aristotle says) is in some terrestrial place, is the intelligence that illuminates above the sphere of active and passive things, whose light spreads in active and passive things, which illuminate human souls, and whose virtue is conceived in the seeds of generators and passives.'",
    "fn.464.sub_0": "This proposition appertains to the atomist <i>Motécallemîn</i>, whose doctrine shall be expounded further on (chap. LXXIII). By <span dir=\"rtl\">מכאן</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(سكان)</span>, they appear to understand space with respect to its extent or its dimensions; by <span dir=\"rtl\">חיז (حيّز)</span>, space circumscribed within limits. Although the atom, say they, possesses no extension, it is nevertheless circumscribed and separated, by limits, from that which adjoins it; it is, so to speak, the body without dimensions, the <i>point</i> considered as the prime element of extension, yet possessing none itself. Here is how Al-Djordjâni, in his <i>Kitâb al-Ta’rifât</i> or Book of Definitions, explains the words <span dir=\"rtl\">مكان</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">حيز</span>, as they are employed by the <i>Motécallemîn:</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">المكان عند</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">المتكلمين هو الفراغ المتوهم الذي يشغله الجسم وينفذ فيه ابعاده</span> “<i>Macân</i>, amongst the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, is the ideal void which the body occupies and into which it causes its dimensions to penetrate.” <span dir=\"rtl\">الحيز عند المتكلمين هو الفراغ المتوهم</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">الذي يشغله شيء ممتد كالجسم أو غير ممتد كالجوهر الفرد</span> “‘<i>Hayyiz</i>, amongst the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, is the ideal void which a thing occupies, whether extended like the body, or unextended like the simple substance (or the <i>atom</i>).” One perceives that <i>macân</i> is attributed solely to the body possessing dimensions, whilst <i>’hayyiz</i> is attributed simultaneously to the extended body and to the atom. The words <span dir=\"rtl\">الجوهر الفرد</span> would signify, according to M. Silv. de Sacy, <i>substance isolated from form</i> (see Notices et extraits des mss., t. X, pag. 65); but this explanation is inexact. These words, which, indeed, signify literally <i>isolated substance</i>, were employed by the <i>Motécallemîn</i> to designate the <i>atom</i> which they likewise term <span dir=\"rtl\">الجزء الذي</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">لا يتجزأ</span> or simply <span dir=\"rtl\">الجزء</span>, as one may have perceived a little higher up.",
    "fn.464.sub_1": "Here is how Ibn-Roschd expresses himself, in his Abridgment of <i>Metaphysics</i>, Book I, when speaking of <i>substance</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">الجوهر</span>; one cites the Hebrew version: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכן מי שראה כי העצמות הרמוז אליו יתחבר מחלקים אשד לא יתחלקו קרא אותם עצמים כמו שנשמע המדברים מאנשי זמננו יקראו החלק אשר לא יתחלק העצם הפדדי</span> “Likewise, those who opine that the indicated substance is composed of <i>atoms</i> bestow upon these the name of <i>substances</i>, just as one hears the <i>Motécallemîn</i> of our own day call the atom the <i>isolated substance</i> or <i>simple</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(الجوهر الفرد)</span>.” The term <span dir=\"rtl\">الجوهر الفرد</span> resembles that of <i>monad</i>, employed by Leibnitz, albeit it does not precisely designate the same thing; the proposition of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, here cited by Maimonides, offers a striking analogy with that which Leibnitz states when speaking of monads: Substantia nempe simplex, etsi non habeat in se extensionem, habet tamen positionem, quœ est fundamentum extensionis</i>. See the Works of Leibnitz, Dutens’ edition, vol. II, part I (<i>Epistolœ ad P. des Bosses</i>), p. 280.",
    "fn.481.sub_0": "The word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלתלאזם</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(التلازم)</span> signifies the close and necessary bond between two things. In the <i>Kitâb al-Ta’rifât</i>, under the word <span dir=\"rtl\">ملازمة</span>, one reads that this word expresses the impossibility of separating two things one from the other, and that in the same sense one employs the words <span dir=\"rtl\">لزوم</span> and <span dir=\"rtl\">تلازم</span>. Cf. Freytag’s Dictionary, under the root <span dir=\"rtl\">لزم</span>. The enunciation of this <i>bond</i> or <i>inherence</i> forms a proposition or a <i>necessary</i> judgment, as, for example, the proposition here cited by our author: <i>every man is rational</i>. — The author doubtless has in view that which Aristotle says concerning demonstration, in explaining the three terms by which he designats the diverse degrees of the <i>necessary</i>. Vide <i>Posterior Analytics</i>, book I, chapter 4. He seems to allude notably to the terms <i>ϰατά παντός</i> and <i>ϰαθ’ αὑτό</i>. The <i>ϰατά παντός</i> designats an attribution which is <i>to every individual</i>, and not merely to such or such, that which is always and not only at such or such a time. Thus, <i>animal being predicated of every man</i> (<i>ϰατά παντός άνθρώπου</i>), <i>it shall suffice that it be true that such an individual is man for it to be true to say of him that he is animal</i>. The term <i>ϰαθ’ αὑτό</i>, <i>in itself</i> (which has four principal senses), designats firstly the attribution which expresses that a thing is essential or inherent in another, and in which the attribute enters into the essential definition of its subject; for example, the line in the triangle, the point in the line. Secondly, the <i>ϰαθ’ αὑτό</i> designats the attribution in which the subject enters into the essential definition of the attribute, or, in other words, in which the attribute is so essential to the subject that the latter comprehends its definition (<i>ὅσοις τῶν ἐνυπαρχόντων αὐτοῖς αὐτὰ ἐν τῷ λόγῳ ένυπάρχουσι τῷ, τί ἐστι, δηλοῦντι</i>). Thus, for example, the line enters necessarily into the definition of the <i>straight</i> and the <i>curved</i>, the number into that of the <i>even</i> and the <i>odd</i>. Ibn-Roschd observs that, in the first case, that is to say, when the attribute enters into the definition of the subject, it concerns either the complete definition or a part of the definition, as, for example,",
    "fn.481.sub_1": "when one defins the triangle by saying that it is a figure bounded by three straight lines; in the second case, that is to say, when the subject enters into the definition of the attribute, it concerns the partial definition, as, for example, when one takes the line as the definition of the <i>straight</i> and the <i>curved</i>, which exist in the line, or else the number as the definition of the <i>even</i> and the <i>odd</i>, which exist in the number. That which, he says, enters principally into propositions serving for demonstration, are these first two species of <i>ϰαθ’ αὑτό</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">אשר בעצם</span>), in which there is a necessary and essential attribution; for, he adds, <i>the partial definition is found with the thing defined in a necessary relation</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כי יחם חלק הגדר אל הנגדר יהיה הכרחי</span>). Vide the Middle Commentary on the Posterior Analytics, Hebrew version, at the passage in question. One will now comprehend that which our author had in view in saying that <i>here there is the idea of inherence etc</i>.",
    "fn.594.sub_0": "From the manner in which the author here expresses himself, one might believe that this concerns a well-known proposition belonging to Aristotle; yet, as several commentators observe, this subtle distinction between the <i>quiddity</i> of a thing and its existence pertains to Ibn-Sînâ, and it has been contested by other Arab philosophers, notably by Ibn-Roschd, who informs us that those who deemed existence an <i>accident</i> founded their view, among other reasons, upon the fact that the Arabic word <span dir=\"rtl\">موجود</span>, which philosophers employ in the sense of <i>existent</i> or of <i>that which is</i> (<i>τὸ ὄν</i>), is originally a passive participle signifying <i>found</i>, and does indicate, by its etymology, somewhat <i>accidental.</i> At the commencement of the I<sup>st</sup> book of his <i>Epitome of Metaphysics</i>, Ibn-Roschd, having expounded the diverse significations of the philosophical term <span dir=\"rtl\">دروجود</span> (<i>that which is, τὸ ὄν</i>), does summarise thus (verse, Hebr.): <span dir=\"rtl\">ולכן היה שם הנמצא שב אל שני אלו הענינים לבד ר״ל אל הצודק ואל מה שהוא נמצא חוץ לשכל</span>, that is to say, in summation, the aforesaid term is predicated either of that which the mind judges <i>to be true</i> (Ἔ<i>τι τὸ εἶναι σημαίνει ϰαί τὸ ἔστιν ὅτι ὰληθἐς, ϰ. τ. λ.</i> Arist., <i>Metaph</i>., book V, chap. 7), or else of being <i>in itself</i>, of that which possesses an objective reality external to the mind, and, in this sense, it is applied to all categories (<i>ϰαθ’ αὑτὸ δέ εἶναι λέγεται ὅσαπερ σημαίνει τὰ σχήματα τῆς ϰατη-γορίας, ϰ. τ. λ. Ibidem</i> ). Accidental existence, adds Ibn-Roschd, cannot be conceived in isolated being or in being <i>in itself</i> (<i>ϰαθ</i>’ <i>αὑτό</i>), for the substance of the thing and its quiddity cannot be accidental; it can only be conceived in the mutual relation of beings, that is to say, when the <i>ὄν</i> expresses an accidental relation of one thing to another, or when it is predicated, as Aristotle expresses it, <i>ϰατὰ συαβεβηϰός</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">אולם הנמצא במקרה הוא לא יצויר במציאות הנפרד כי עצמות הדבר ומהותו אי אפשר ש׳היה במקרה ואמנם יצויר מיחם הנמצאות קצתם <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> קצת</span>.",
    "fn.594.sub_1": "He then refuts the opinion of Ibn-Sînâ, according to whom <i>existence</i> is an <i>accident</i> supervening upon the essence or quiddity of the existing thing, and, after having demonstrated all that is false in this viewpoint, he adds: <span dir=\"rtl\">אבל זה דרך האיש הזה ברוב מה שיביאהו מצד עצמו</span> «But such is the manner of this man in almost <i>all that he allegth of himself.</i>» Ibn-Roschd regarded with disfavour a proposition which appeared to him to be in opposition to Aristotelian doctrines. Indeed, this ideal separation between <i>that which a thing is</i> (<i>τὸ τί ἦν εἶνο ι</i>) and its <i>existence</i> is contrary to the spirit of Aristotle’s doctrine; it would be at most in <i>artificial</i> objects that one might separate the <i>what</i> from <i>existence</i>, the idea of the thing being in the mind of the artist ere it <i>exist</i> (cf. Aristotle, <i>Metaph</i>., book VII, chap. 7: <i>ἀπὸ τέχνης δὲ γίγνεται ὅσων τὸ εἶδος ὲν τῇ ψυχῇ, εἶδος δὲ λέγω τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι ἑϰάστου ϰαί τὴν πρώτην ουσίαν</i>’). According to the adherents of Ibn-Sînâ, the same would hold true for all that comes to be by <i>nature;</i> for, say they, all things being found in the first cause, or in God, who knows them beforehand, the quiddity of things is anterior to their existence, which, consequently, is an accident of quiddity (vide the commentary of Schem-Tob, at our passage). But this reasoning tends to admit, with Plato, <i>pre-existent</i> ideas or forms, and is little conformable to Peripatetic doctrines. —Vide also note 1 of the ensuing page.",
    "fn.597.sub_0": "As the commentator Shem-Tov observes, what the author states here is found nowhere in the <i>Metaphysics</i> of Aristotle, but rather the contrary. Indeed, the <i>one</i> (<i>τὸ ὄν</i>) <i>in itself</i>, to which Aristotle assigns four principal meanings, namely: the <i>continuous</i>, the <i>whole</i>, the <i>individual</i>, and the <i>universal</i> (according as one considers therein the indivisibility of movement or that of the idea), is inseparable from the very nature and quiddity of the thing which is <i>one</i>, and cannot be regarded as an <i>accident</i> of quiddity. The <i>one</i> is not in itself an essence, and it can only subsist in a subject. Its proper character is to be indivisible and the first measure in each genus, and notably the measure of quantity; but, just as <i>being</i> (<i>τὸ ὄν</i>), with which it is confounded and identified, it applies to all the <i>categories</i>, and is not found particularly in a single one. The <i>multiple</i> is opposed to the <i>one</i>, as the divisible to the indivisible, and not as non-being to being; for the <i>multiple</i> is in <i>being</i>, as well as the <i>one</i>, and is attributed, like the latter, to all categories. The <i>one</i> and the <i>multiple</i> in number, designating quantity alone, are abstractions and entirely distinct from <i>being.</i> See details in Aristotle's <i>Metaphysics</i>, book V, chap. 6, and book X, chap. 1 et seq. — Maimonides, who perceives in the <i>one</i> and the <i>multiple</i> naught but <i>accidents</i> distinct from <i>being</i>, has furthermore followed the opinion of Ibn-Sīnā, who, as Ibn-Rushd observes, confounded the <i>one</i> of number with the <i>absolute one</i>: «A difficulty one may encounter here,» says Ibn-Rushd (l. c., book III), «is this: how, admitting that the <i>one</i> of number pertains to the category of <i>quantity</i>, can one subsequently admit that it is found in all categories, and moreover that it is in the categories themselves, and not something merely accessory thereto? It is for this reason that Ibn-Sīnā opined that its substratum must be an <i>accident</i> found in all categories.",
    "fn.597.sub_1": "Yet it is not thus; for the <i>one</i> in number is of a different nature from the other units. Indeed, the <i>numerical one</i> is individuality, abstracted from that which is quantity and quality—one means, from that by which the individual is individual; for it is individual only in the sense of <i>indivisible</i>, and the mind abstracts it from material things, limits it, and takes it for something separate. The <i>one</i> in number, the numerical unity, is naught but a thing that the <i>soul</i> creates in individual beings, and without the soul there would be neither numerical unity nor number in general, contrary to what obtains for the line and the surface, and, in general, for continuous quantity; it is for this reason that number is, more than anything else, pure of matter. Ibn-Sīnā confounded the nature of the <i>one</i>, principle of number, with the <i>absolute one</i>, which embraces all categories, and as the <i>one</i>, principle of number, is an <i>accident</i>, he admitted that the <i>absolute one</i>, which is the same as <i>being</i>, is also an accident.» Cf. R. Levi ben-Gerson, <i>Mil’hamôth</i>, book V, 3, chap. 12.",
    "fn.607.sub_0": "Literally: <i>know that to describe</i> (or <i>to designate</i>) <i>God</i> — <i>may He be glorified and exalted!</i> — <i>by means of negations, this is the true description</i> (or <i>manner of attributing to Him</i>), <i>which is affected by no improper expression</i> (on <span dir=\"rtl\">تسامح</span> , see above, page 235, note 1), <i>and in which there is in no wise, nor in any manner whatsoever, a defect in respect of God;</i> that is to say: attributes expressed by negations are the only true ones, containing, in their terms, nothing which one needs to consider as an improper expression, and stating, in respect of God, nothing which implies an imperfection. — Even before Maimonides, Jewish theologians, and particularly those of Spain, had insisted upon this point and had established that one cannot express what God is, but only what He is not, and that the attributes commonly ascribed to Him, far from possessing an affirmative sense, indicate naught save the exclusion of their contrary; by <i>one</i>, one means only that God <i>is not multiple;</i> by <i>eternal</i>, that He <i>is not created</i>, and so forth. See Ba’hya, <i>Duties of the Hearts</i>, book I, chap. 10; Iehouda ha-Lévi, <i>Khozari</i>, book II, § 2; Abraham ben-David, <i>Emunah Ramah</i> (the Sublime Faith), bk. II, 3<sup>rd</sup> foundation (page 51 of the text, and page 65 of the German translation by M. Simon Weil, Frankfort, 1852). The Arabian philosophers pronounce themselves in the same sense, and certain Muslim sects had made <i>negative attributes</i> an essential point of their doctrine, as, for example, the <i>Dhirâriyya</i> (see Schahrestâni, German trans., vol. I, page 94). The Karaite Ahron ben-Élie, in his <span dir=\"rtl\">עץ חיים</span> or <i>Tree of Life</i> (chap. LXXI), refutes the proponents of <i>negative attributes.</i> These believed themselves able to rely upon the opinion of Aristotle, whom Ba’hya expressly cites in this regard.",
    "fn.607.sub_1": "Here are the terms of the Arabic original of the treatise on the <i>Duties of the Hearts</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">ولهדׄא קאל ארםטוטאלים אלםואלב מן צפאת אלכׄאלק תעאלי אצדק מן אלמוגׄכאת לאן כל מא יוגׄב לה מן אלצפאת לא תכׄלו פן צפאת אלגׄוהר או אלערץׄ וכׄאלק אלגׄוהר ואלערץׄ לא ילחقه פי דׄאתה וצף מן אוצאפהמא וכל מא ינפי ענה מן אלצפאת פהי צאדקה̈ לא מהאלה̈</span> \"it is wherefore Aristotle says: The negative attributes of the Creator are truer than the affirmative; for all attributes which one would give Him affirmatively could only be attributes of substance or accident, and the Creator of substance and accident cannot have in His essence any of their attributes. But the attributes which one removes from Him (that is to say, the attributes expressed negatively) are doubtless true.\" — This passage, which is cited also by Ibn-Falaquera (<i>Moreh ha-Moreh</i>, page 29), is evidently apocryphal. It is true that Aristotle, in speaking of the <i>First Mover</i>, very often expresses himself by negative terms, such as: <i>to be immutable, separated from all that is sensible</i>, which <i>cannot have extension</i>, which is <i>indivisible, etc.</i> (Metaph., XII, 7 and passim); but he by no means excludes affirmative terms, and that which the doctrine of the Arabian philosophers possesses of absolute truth in this regard appears to be drawn from the Neoplatonic commentators and to connect with the doctrines of Plotinus (cf. Ritter, <i>Geschichte der Philosophie</i>, vol. IV, page 573 et seq.). St. Thomas, in his turn, by stating that one can express oneself concerning God only by the <i>negative way</i>, followed the Arabian philosophers, from whom he doubtless borrowed the term via remotionis <span dir=\"rtl\">(طريق النفي)</span>. See <i>Summa C. Gent</i>., book I, chap. 14.",
    "fn.637.sub_0": "This somewhat obscure passage has been diversely interpreted, and commentators appear greatly perplexed in indicating its precise meaning, as one may discern from the varied explanations afforded by Schem-Tob and by Profiat Duran or Ephodi. The simplest meaning, it appears unto me, is this: One could not attribute unto God that which, from our point of view, would be a perfection; for perfections, being ever any <i>capacities</i> (<i>ἕξεις</i>) whatsoever, belong to one of the genera of the category of <i>quality</i>, and consequently could not be attributed unto God, as results from that which the author has stated in Chapter LII (p. 195), concerning <i>qualities.</i> For further explanation, the author adds: <i>and every capacity exists not in every</i> (<i>being</i>) <i>endowed with capacity;</i> that is to say, that in created beings, capacities are somewhat <i>accidental</i>, not being found equally in all, and not being always <i>in act</i> where they are found. <i>Capacities</i> (<i>ἕξεις</i>), though more durable and more solid than <i>dispositions</i> (<i>διαθέσεις</i>), are nevertheless naught else but consolidated <i>dispositions</i>, and designate somewhat which has been <i>acquired</i> and which has not always existed in the being endowed therewith (cf. p. 195, note 2). As Aristotle himself affirms, capacities are likewise dispositions; for those who are endowed with capacities are in a manner but <i>disposed</i> for these same capacities (<i>οί μὲν γὰρ ἕξεις ἔχοντες ϰαί διάϰεινταί γέ πως ϰατ’ αὐτἀς. Catég</i>., chap. 8). Let one add unto this that capacities are oftentimes <i>opposed</i> one to another, and cannot coexist in the same subject. It results from all this that, were one to attribute unto God all that which, in respect of us, is a perfection, one would attribute unto Him <i>acquired</i> capacities and even <i>opposed</i> capacities, one to another, two things equally inadmissible. — Moses of Narbonne, the most profound commentator upon Maimonides, confessing the perplexity which he had long experienced in explaining the author's words, supposs that this entire passage, from these words: <i>Thus it is clear unto thee, etc.</i>, relats to a passage in the <i>Metaphysics</i> (Book.",
    "fn.637.sub_1": "XII, chap. 9), where Aristotle states that the intellect perceives nothing other than itself, being incapable of being affected by aught external to it. Maimonides, when speaking here of all that one must <i>deny</i> concerning the supreme intellect, or God, would, one presumes, allude to this absolute negation, in God, of any perception which should not have for its object the divine intellect itself. This intellect possesses its perfection within itself, and cannot be affected by aught. By denying that it perceives anything other than itself, one does not attribute imperfection to it; for all perfections are capacities, yet these capacities do not constitute perfection for every being endowed with them. There are many <i>privations</i> which are preferable to <i>capacities;</i> for, as Aristotle states in the cited passage, <i>not to see certain things is better than to see them</i> (<i>ϰαί γὰρ μὴ ὁρᾶν ἔνιϰ ϰρεῖττον ἢ ὁρᾶν</i>). Such, in substance, is the interpretation given to our passage by Moses of Narbonne; but one doubts greatly whether it be the true one, and whether there exist a real connection between the words of Maimonides and the aforementioned passage of Aristotle.",
    "fn.743.sub_0": "The visible manifestation of the divinity consists, according to the theologians, in a light which God causs to appear in a place and which is a miraculous creation; it is this light which is called <i>Schekhînâ</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">שכינה</span>, majesty) or <i>cabôd</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">כבוד</span>, <i>glory</i>), and which appears in the place God particularly chooss to make His presence known therein. Saadia likewise speaks of this <i>created light</i> which God caused to appear in the sanctuary in order to <i>magnify that place</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">להגדיל המקום ההוא</span>); vide <i>Beliefs and Opinions</i>, book II, chap. 8 (Berlin edit., fol. 33 <i>a</i>). Cf. Ahron ben-Élie, <i>Tree of Life</i>, chap. LXIII (Leipzig edit., pag. 72). Speculations of this nature generally hark back to the ancient Karaïtes, who took as their guides the Muslim theologians of the Mo’tazale sect, and it was through the impetus of the Karaïtes that Saadia and other Rabbanites approached such questions (vide hereafter, chap. LXXI). The Karaïte Abou-’Ali (Jéphs), in his Arabic commentary on Exodus (chap. 19, <small>V</small>. 20), speaks at length concerning the manner in which one ought to understand God’s manifestation upon Sinai; therein one reads the following passage relative to the <i>cabôd</i> or the <i>created light</i>: <span dir=\"rtl\">ודׄלף אנה אםתחאל אן יבון אלכׄאלק גׄל ועלא אלרׄי לא יוצף ולא <span dir=\"rtl\">חי</span>  ולא תחוيه אלאמבנה̈ בל לא יגׄוז אן יקאל פיה אנה פי אלעאלם ולא כׄארגׄא ענה אדׄ הדׄא מן אוצאף אלמכׄלוקין אלדׄין תחתוי עליהם אלאמכגה̈ ותלזמהם אלאעראץׄ ואדׄא כאן הדׄא הכדׄא פלא שך פי אן וירד י״י ישיר בה אלי מכׄלוק מחדוד וחצׄרה̈ מוצׄע פי ראשּ ההר</span> «Indeed, it is impossible to admit that the reference here is to the Creator, who can be neither qualified nor defined, and who cannot be contained in space. Furthermore, one cannot say of Him that He is in the world, nor that He is outside the world; for such qualifications would apply to creatures, who are contained in space and accessible to accidents. This being so, there is doubtless no doubt that, by the words: <i>And the Lord descended, etc.</i>, one indicats a thing <i>created</i> and limited, and its local presence upon the summit of the mountain.» Cf. hereafter, p. 290, note 2.",
    "fn.743.sub_1": "According to the opinion which Maimonides expounds elsewhere concerning miracles in general, one must discern in the aforementioned <i>created light</i>, as well as in all that occurrs in a miraculous manner, the effect of God’s primordial will, which, from the moment of Creation, so disposed matters that, in certain given circumstances, extraordinary phenomena might come to pass, which to us appear to deviate from the general laws of nature. Thus, then, miracles are the results of certain physical dispositions established by God at the epoch of Creation, and not the effect of a momentary will; for no change can occur in the divine will, which is eternal and immutable. Vide what the author states in this regard in the last of the <i>Eight Chapters</i> serving as an introduction to the treatise <i>Abôth</i> (Pococke’s Porta Mosis, pag. 240), and cf. the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, towards the end of chap. XXIX. Vide also hereafter, chap. LXVI, pag. 296, note 1.",
    "fn.793.sub_0": "The author here approaches a subject which, as he himself states at the close of this chapter, can be properly comprehended only by those familiar with Peripatetic philosophy, and especially that portion pertaining to the soul and its faculties. Without entering here into disquisitions which the confines of a footnote would not admit, it shall be necessary, for the better apprehension of the author's reasoning, to briefly recall the Aristotelian theory of intelligence and the developments this theory has received amongst the Arab philosophers whom our author has taken for his guides. — In considering the diverse faculties of the soul enumerated by Aristotle, one observs therein a successive progression from the less perfect to the more perfect, in such wise that each superior faculty presupposs the inferior faculty, whose action it renders more complete, and that the one is to the other what <i>energy</i> or <i>act</i> is to <i>potency</i>, or what form is to matter. One may distinguish three principal faculties of the soul: <i>nutrition</i> (<i>τὸ θρεπτιϰόν</i> sc. <i>μόριον</i>), to which the life of plants is confined; <i>sensibility</i> (<i>τὸ αἰσθητιϰόν</i>), which pertains to all animals; and <i>reason</i> (<i>τὸ διανοητιϰόν</i>), which belongs to man alone (Treatise <i>on the Soul</i>, Book II, chap. 2); the third faculty could not subsist without the second, nor this latter without the first. <i>Nutrition</i> has for its object material things; by this faculty the animal does assimilate external things themselves, whereas by <i>sensibility</i> it receivs only the forms of things, even as wax receivs only the form of the seal, and not its matter. <i>Reason</i>, or <i>thought</i>, is to intelligible things what sensibility is to sensible things; but through the senses one knows only individuals or individual forms; one knows that things exist, but one knows not why they exist; it is reason which discerns general forms and causes. The soul, says Aristotle, that is to say, the intelligent part of the soul, is <i>the place of forms</i> (<i>τόπος εὶδῶν</i>).",
    "fn.793.sub_1": "As it is to govern all, reason possesses no peculiar bodily organ; it must needs be <i>impassible</i>, that is to say, it must not be affected by external things, but must be <i>capable</i> of receiving the form of objects. These forms it does not possess at the outset <i>in act</i>, that is to say, in full reality, but only <i>in potency</i> (<i>Ibid.</i>, book 3, chapter 4). Just as in all beings one distinguishes, on the one hand, matter, or that which is in potency, and, on the other hand, form, or the cause which brings that which is <i>in potency</i> to pass into <i>act</i>, so also must these differences necessarily be found within the rational part of the soul, or in the intellect. Aristotle, then, distinguishes two <i>intellects</i>, the one <i>passive</i> (<i>νοῦς παθητιϰός</i>), the other <i>active</i> (<i>νοῦς ποιητιϰός</i>). The passive intellect occupies a middle ground between sensibility and imagination, on the one hand, and the active intellect, on the other; it is this latter, above all, which is separate (from the lower faculties of the soul), impassible, unmixed with aught whatsoever, being <i>in act</i> by its very essence (<i>ϰαὶ οὗτος ὁ νοῦς χωριστὸς ϰαὶ ἀπαθὴς ϰαὶ ἀμιγής, τῇ ονσίᾳ ὢν ἐνεργείᾳ</i>. <i>Ibid.</i>, chapter 5). Aristotle does not clearly inform us whence this active intellect proceeds, nor how it is perceived by the passive intellect; yet he seems to discern therein something divine, which comes from without by means of the first mover. Vide the treatise on the <i>Generation of Animals</i>, book II, chapter 3: <i>λείπεται δὲ τὸν νοῦν μóνον θύραθεν ἐπεισιέναι ϰαὶ θει̃ον εἶναι ϰ. τ. λ.</i>; cf. Trendelenburg, Arist. de Animá, page 496. It is by the aid of this active intellect that every individual intelligence accomplishes the labour of thought, and, passing from <i>potency</i> to <i>act</i>, it appropriates to itself the forms of things and identifies itself therewith. These intelligible forms, the object of thought, are themselves thought; for the intellect in act cannot be affected by aught external to itself, and it must needs draw from within itself that which it holds as its object. The intellect, therefore, in its <i>entelechy</i>, or in its complete reality, thinks itself. Vide Aristotle, Metaphysics, book XII, chap.",
    "fn.793.sub_2": "7: <i>αὑτὺν δἐ νοει̃ ὁ νοῦς ϰατὰ μετάληψιν τοῦ νοητοῦ νοητὸς γὰρ γίγνεται θιγγάνων ϰαὶ νοῶν ὣστε ταὐτὸν νοῦς ϰαἰ νοητὀν</i>. Cf. Treatise <i>On the Soul</i>, book III, at the close of chapter 4. Such, in substance, is the theory of Aristotle; the obscurity which reigns within this theory, particularly concerning the nature of the active intellect and its union with the passive intellect, has given rise, among commentators, to protracted discussions and the most diverse opinions; and the Arabic philosophers, confounding herein, as elsewhere, the theories of Aristotle with those of his Neoplatonic commentators, have elaborated, upon the subject which now engages our attention, a new doctrine which subsequently spread amongst the Scholastics. Here follows, in summary, the theory of the Arabic philosophers, who differ amongst themselves on several essential points. The <i>speculative</i> faculty, or the <i>theoretic reason</i>, being the highest among the faculties of the rational soul (vide above, page 210, note 1), receivs the impression of forms separated from matter, whether these be general forms which it abstracts from material and individual things, or immaterial and imperishable beings, which are in themselves abstract forms (as, for instance, the <i>separated intelligences</i>). This faculty, denominated the <i>intellect</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(عقل)</span> by pre-eminence, develops gradually, traversing diverse degrees in which the inferior stands to that which is superior to it as matter stands to form, or as potency stands to act. The Arabic philosophers generally distinguish three degrees of development, or three intellects: 1st, The <i>hylic intellect</i> or <i>material intellect</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(العقل الهيولانى)</span>, which is the simple <i>disposition</i> possessed by the intellect for abstracting forms; it is a mere <i>potency</i> which has not yet begun to pass into <i>act</i>, and which is termed <i>hylic</i> because it may be compared to <i>matter</i> (<i>ὕλη</i>) disposed to receive form. This <i>hylic intellect</i> is the <i>νοῦς παθητιϰός</i> of Aristotle <span dir=\"rtl\">(العقل المنفعل)</span> in its entire abstraction.",
    "fn.793.sub_3": "The opinions of the Arabian philosophers are divided concerning the nature of the <i>hyliac intellect:</i> some, invoking the authority of Alexander of Aphrodisias, perceive therein naught but a pure <i>disposition</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(استعداد)</span> attaching to the other faculties of the soul, and which is perishable even as they; and this opinion is adopted by Maimonides (vide infra, chap. LXX); others, taking Themistius as their guide, maintain that, since the <i>passive</i> or <i>hyliac intellect</i> must, according to Aristotle, be <i>unmixed</i> (<i>ἀμιγής</i>) with the other faculties of the soul, it must needs be a disposition having for its <i>substratum</i> a substance separate from the other faculties; for, they contend, were the <i>intellect in potency</i> naught but a simple disposition attaching to the other faculties of the soul, its substratum would be somewhat appertaining to a <i>genus</i> other than itself; yet that which is disposed to perceive intelligible things can be naught but an <i>intellect.</i> Ibn-Roschd, acknowledging on the one hand the inadmissibility of Alexander’s opinion, likewise discerns difficulties in admitting the other opinion, according to which there would exist a separate and eternal substance whose being would consist solely in a <i>disposition</i> and in a simple <i>potency</i> (<i>δύναμ·ς</i>); he therefore chooses a middle course, by uniting the two opinions, as one has set forth elsewhere (vide the article <i>Ibn-Roschd</i>, in the <i>Dictionnaire des sciences philosophiques</i>, t. III, pp. 166 et 167). 2° The <i>intellect in act</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(العقل بالفعل)</span> ; this is the intellect which has succeeded in abstracting form from matter, that is to say, which has succeeded in distinguishing, in individual things, that which constitutes their general form, or that by which a thing is what it is. 3° The <i>acquired intellect</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(العقل المستفاد)</span> ; this is none other than the intellect in act having become, as it were, the property of man, when the <i>intelligible forms</i> are ever present in his intelligence, and when he is able to identify himself with them at any moment without fresh effort.",
    "fn.793.sub_4": "Having attained unto this degree, human intelligence has ever for its object the pure intelligible forms; it has for its object the knowledge of its own being and that of immaterial beings external to itself, and it does ascend to the knowledge of the <i>separate intelligences</i> and of God. In this state, it becoms, as it were, a substance entirely separated from the body (vide infra, at the close of chap. LXXII). Between the <i>intellect in act</i> and the <i>acquired intellect</i> some Arabian philosophers do furthermore place the <i>intellect in capacity</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(العقل بالملكة)</span> ; by this they appear to understand the intellect in act having become a <i>capacity</i> (<i>ἓξις</i>, cf. supra, p. 195, notes 1 and 2), and ere it has in reality attained unto the degree of the <i>acquired intellect</i> (cf. the work <i>Ta’rifât</i> cited in Freytag’s Dictionary, sub voce under the word <span dir=\"rtl\">عقل</span> , and the Analysis of the philosophy of Ibn-Sinâ, in Schahrestâni, p. 418 of the Arabic text, and t. II, p. 317, of the German translation). This is a subtle distinction, the import of which one does not fully comprehend, and which, it would appear, has not been generally admitted; Ibn-Falaquéra, in enumerating the various <i>intellects</i> (vide the Appendix to the <i>Moré-ha-Moré</i>, at the commencement of chap. I, p. 141), makes no mention of the <i>intellect in capacity</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">השכל בקנין</span>).—Philosophical studies and speculation, albeit necessary for the development of the <i>hyliac intellect</i>, would not suffice of themselves to bring it to the degrees of the <i>intellect in act</i> and the <i>acquired intellect;</i> for, like unto every thing which exists but <i>in potency</i>, the intellect requirs, in order to pass to <i>act</i> or to <i>entelechy</i>, a <i>cause</i> external to itself, and this cause is the universal <i>active intellect</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(العقل الفعال)</span> , the last among the intelligences of the spheres (vide the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work, chap. IV, and cf. Ibn-Sinâ, in the work of Schahrestâni, p. 426; Germ. trans., t. II, p. 328).",
    "fn.793.sub_5": "It is by the influence of this active intellect that the <i>acquired intellect</i> is formed, considered as its <i>emanation</i> (whence, in the Hebrew versions, <span dir=\"rtl\">השכל הנאצל</span>, the emanated intellect), and which in certain elect men attains to a complete identification with the universal active intellect (cf. above, page 277, note 3, and one's article <i>Ibn-Roschd</i>, loco citato, pages 167 and 169). — One shall, in other places, discourse upon divers details of this theory, the whole of which one has deemed it necessary to present here. It is understood that its obscurity has given rise to numerous divergences of opinion; the hypotheses, which one had sought even in regions inaccessible to the human mind, left a free career to the imagination, and the most enlightened Arab philosophers could not but abandon here the true ground of philosophical speculation, to lose themselves in reveries. \"Not a single one,\" says Ibn-Falaquéra, \"has a well-settled opinion upon this subject, and one often observs the same author to change his opinion and to state in one work something altogether different from that which he has stated in another.\" See <i>Moré-ha-Moré</i>, book II, chapter 25 (page 111).",
    "fn.864.sub_0": "The Arabic text of this passage is somewhat obscure; here is an approximately literal rendering thereof: <i>And in like manner have they proved, concerning these</i> (<i>other</i>) <i>things which they have enumerated, that, being</i> (<i>in</i>) <i>a relationship with God, they exist with Him</i>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">ענדה</span> (<i>with Him</i> or <i>beside Him</i>) does not refer to God, but rather to the heaven <i>’Arabôth</i>, and it would have been more regular to construct the Arabic phrase thus: <span dir=\"rtl\">וכדׄלך אםתדלוא עלי אן תלך אלתי עדוהא הי ענדה ללה תעאלי</span> (i.e., <span dir=\"rtl\">באנהא נםבה̈</span> (<span dir=\"rtl\">בכונהא מנםובה̈</span>. Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">נםבה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(نسبة)</span>, which most manuscripts exhibit, some possess <span dir=\"rtl\">נםבת</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(نُسِبَتْ)</span>, and one of the Leyden manuscripts has <span dir=\"rtl\">מנםובה̈</span> <span dir=\"rtl\">(منسوبة)</span>; these latter two readings appear to have been substituted for the word <span dir=\"rtl\">נםבה̈</span> to facilitate the apprehension of the passage. The import is this: that the doctors, to prove that the other things they have enumerated likewise exist in <i>’Arabôth</i>, restrict themselves to citing Scriptural passages where these things are directly attributed to God; for naught emanates from God save through the intermediation of <i>’Arabôth</i>, which, as has been observed, is the instrument by which God governs the universe. Thus, for example, to demonstrate that <i>’Arabôth</i> contains <i>treasures of life</i>, one cits these words of the Psalmist: <i>For with Thee is the fountain of life</i> (Ps. 36, 10); for <i>the treasures of peace</i>, one cits this passage: <i>And he called it</i> (the altar) <i>the Eternal God of peace</i> (Judges, 6, 24); for <i>the treasures of blessing: He shall receive the blessing from the Lord</i> (Ps. 24, 5), and so forth. Vide <i>’Haghîgâ</i>, loc. cit.. — In the editions of Ibn-Tibbon’s version (with the exception of the editio princeps </i>), our passage is found translated twice, firstly literally, then in a manner that more clearly conveys its sense; the second translation is expressed in these terms: <span dir=\"rtl\">ובן הביאו ראיה על השאר שהם בערבות מהיותם מיוחסים לשם יתעלה</span> (the words <span dir=\"rtl\">שהם אצלו</span>, which the editions add, are repeated from the first translation and ought to be effaced).",
    "fn.864.sub_1": "Most manuscripts and the editio princeps </i> contain only this second translation, which Ibn-Tibbon had placed in the margin, adding thereto the following note found in some manuscripts: <span dir=\"rtl\">אמר שמואל בן חבון הלשון המוגה מחוץ אינו יוצא מלשון הערכי אך הוא יוצא מגוף הענין מלשון חגיגה והוא האמת בעצמו והוא שהעיר עליו באמרו והבן זה</span> «Samuel Ibn-Tibbon says: The rectified reading which is found without does not correspond to the Arabic text, but rather proceeds from the very subject contained within the text of <i>’Haghîgâ;</i> this is the veritable sense, and this is what the author has indicated by saying: <i>One must understand this</i>.»",
    "fn.880.sub_0": "The word <i>calâm</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(كلام)</span>, which signifies <i>speech</i> or <i>discourse</i>, designates also a science which arose among the Muslims from the second century of the Hegira, and which aimed to combat the doctrines of heterodox sects, and later those of the philosophers, by employing against its adversaries the weapons of dialectic and arguments borrowed from the philosophical schools. One may give to this science the name of <i>dogmatics</i>, or of rational theology, and it bears much analogy to Christian <i>scholasticism</i>. The <i>calâm</i> developed especially since the introduction, among the Arabs, of Peripatetic philosophy, and one will see further on (chap. LXXIII et seq.) what doctrines were established by this science and how they proceeded to demonstrate the truth of the principal religious dogmas, notably those which establish the unity and immateriality of God and Creation. As for the name <i>calâm</i>, the Arabs themselves are not agreed upon its origin; according to some, the said science was thus called because in it one had at first discussed principally what was to be understood by <i>divine speech</i> or by the <i>speech</i> (<i>calâm</i>) attributed to God (cf. infra, p. 343, note 3); according to others, the word <i>calâm</i> would be but an imitation of the word <i>mantik</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(منطق)</span>, which likewise signifies <i>speech</i>, or <i>discourse</i>, and by which the philosophers designated one of the principal branches of their doctrine, namely, <i>logic</i>. See Schahrestâni, <i>History of Religious and Philosophical Sects</i>, Arab. text, p. 18 (Germ. transl., vol. I, p. 26); cf. Pococke, <i>Specimen hist. ar</i>., p. 195. From the word <i>calâm</i> comes the verb <i>técallam</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(تكلم)</span>, bearing the sense of <i>to profess the calâm</i>, and of which the participle <i>motécallem</i>, in the plural <i>motécallémîn</i>, designates the adherents of <i>calâm</i>.",
    "fn.880.sub_1": "Having regard to the sense of <i>speech</i> that the word <i>calâm</i> possesses, the Hebrew translators denominated this science <span dir=\"rtl\">חכמת הדבר</span> and designated the Motécallémîn by the name of <i>Medabberim</i> (<span dir=\"rtl\">מדברים</span>), which the Latin translators, in their turn, rendered by loquentes. One may also see what one has said concerning the <i>calâm</i> in the <i>Dictionary of Philosophical Sciences</i>, vol. I, art. <i>Arabs</i> (pp. 169 and 174), and in one’s <i>Notice sur R. Saadia Gaôn</i>, p. 16 et seq. (M. Cahen’s Bible, vol. IX, pp. 88 et seq.). — The two Hebrew translators have not well apprehended the sense that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אלכלאם</span> bears in our passage, and they have taken it in its primitive sense of <i>speech</i> or <i>discourse;</i> Ibn-Tibbon translates: <span dir=\"rtl\">מדברי ענין היחוד</span>, and Al-’Harizi: <span dir=\"rtl\">מדבריהם בענין היחוד</span>. Following the words which one has just cited, one must read, in Ibn-Tibbon’s version: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומה שנתלה בזה הענין</span>, as the manuscripts and the editio princeps have it; in the other editions, there has been erroneously inserted: <span dir=\"rtl\">ומה שנתגלה מזה הענין</span>.",
    "fn.924.sub_0": "Regarding the <i>humours</i>, see hereafter, page 366, note 4. — The <i>spirits</i> are subtle fluids which, according to the hypotheses of ancient naturalists, have their seat in different organs of the animal body and contribute to the manifestations of the diverse faculties of the soul. The three species of <i>spirit</i> correspond to the three faculties of the soul admitted by ancient physicians, namely, the <i>natural</i> faculty, the <i>vital</i> faculty, and the <i>psychic</i> (or animal) faculty. See Ibn-Sina’s <i>Canon</i>, Book I, <i>fen</i>. 1, chap. 6 (Arabic text, page 38; Latin version, vol. I, page 76); cf. Maimonides, <i>Eight Chapters</i>, at the commencement of Chapter I, and the III<sup>rd</sup> part of this work, towards the end of Chapters XII and XLVI, where the author alludes to these three faculties. Here is how Maimonides himself, in one of his medical treatises, expounds the theory of the <i>spirits</i> and their different species, according to the ancient physicians: <span dir=\"rtl\">הדׄה אלתי תםמיהא אלאטבא ארואחא הי אבכׄרה̈ לטיפה̈ מוגׄודה̈ פי גׄםם אלחיואן מבדאהא ומעטׄם מאדתהא מן אלהוא אלמםתנשק מן כׄארגׄ פבכׄאר אלדם אלמוגׄוד פי אלכבד ופי אלאורדה̈ <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> נאבתה̈ מנהא יםמי אלרוח אלטביעיה̈ ובכׄאר אלדם <span dir=\"rtl\">لا</span> מוגׄוד פי אלקלב ואלשריאן יםמי אלרוח אלחיואניה̈ ואלבכׄאר אלמוגׄוד פי בטון <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span> דטאג ומא ינבעתׄ מנה פי מםאם <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span> אעצאב יםמי רוחא נפםאניה̈ ומבדא אלגׄמיע ואכתׄר מאדתה מן אלהוא אלמםתנשק מן כׄארגׄ פאדׄא כאן הדׄא <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span> הוא עפנא או מנתנא או כדרא תגירת תלך <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span> ארואח כלהא וגׄרי אמרהא עלי כׄלאף מא ינבגי ˙˙˙˙˙ וכלמא כאנת אלרוח אלטף כאן תגירהא בתגיר אלהוא אכתׄר פאלרוח <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span> טביעי אגלטׄ מן אלרוח <span dir=\"rtl\">לא</span> חיואני ואלחיואני אגלטׄ מן אלנפםאני פענד תגיר אלהוא איםר תגיר יתגיר חאל אלנפםאני תגירא ישער בה ולדׄלך תגׄד כתׄירא מן אלנאם קד ישער בנקץ אפעאלהם אלנפםאניה̈ ענד פםאד אלהוא אעני אן יחדתׄ להם בלאדה̈ פהם וקצור אדראך ונקצאן חפטׄ ואן כאנת אפעאלהם אלחיואניה̈ ואלטביעיה̈ לא ישער פיהא בתגיר</span> \"What physicians call <i>spirits</i> are subtle vapours which exist in the body of the animal and which derive their origin and their principal matter from the air drawn from without by respiration.",
    "fn.924.sub_1": "The vapour of the blood existing in the liver and in the veins departing thence is denominated the <i>natural spirit</i> or <i>physical spirit</i> (<i>φυσιϰὸν πνεῦμα</i>); the vapour of the blood existing in the heart and in the arteries is denominated the <i>vital spirit</i> (<i>ζωτιϰὸν πν</i>.); lastly, the vapour found in the cavities of the brain and that which is diffused thence into the channels of the nerves is denominated the <i>animal spirit</i> (<i>ψυχιϰὸν πν</i>.). The origin of all (these <i>spirits</i>) and their principal matter comes (as one has stated) from the air drawn in by respiration; if, therefore, this air be corrupted, or fetid, or turbid, all these <i>spirits</i> are altered and no longer remain as they ought to be….. The more subtle the spirit, the more is it altered by the alteration of the air. Now, as the <i>natural spirit</i> is denser than the <i>vital spirit</i>, and the latter denser than the <i>animal spirit</i>, it follows that, upon the slightest alteration of the air, the <i>animal spirit</i> will undergo a perceptible alteration. Hence one will discover many individuals in whom an enfeeblement of the <i>animal</i> or <i>psychical</i> functions (that is to say, functions dependent upon the <i>animal</i> spirits) is observable when the air is corrupted; one means, that it does befall them to possess obtuse intelligence, difficult comprehension, and enfeebled memory, albeit no alteration is remarked in their <i>vital</i> and <i>natural</i> functions.» Cf. Galen, De Hippocr. et Plat. placitis, book VII, chap. 3, and above, p. 111, note 2. — The passage one has just cited is to be found at the commencement of book IV of the treatise entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">في تدبير الصحة</span> (On the Regimen of Health), which Maimonides composed at the request of Al-Mâlic al-Afdhal, son of Saladin, and of which the Arabic original, copied in Hebrew characters, exists in the Imperial Library (vid. Heb. mss. of the ancient collection no. 412, fol. 107 <i>a.b</i>, and no. 411, fol. 57 <i>b</i> and 46 <i>a)</i>. The Hebrew version of this treatise, due to R. Moses Ibn-Tibbon, has been published in the collection entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">כרם חמד</span>, vol. III (Prague, 1838, in-8°), p. 9 et seqq. There also exists a Latin version thereof which has been printed several times.",
    "fn.1061.sub_0": "The verb <span dir=\"rtl\">جوزّ</span> signifies <i>to allow to pass</i>, and, figuratively, <i>to deem admissible</i> or <i>permissible</i>, and the word <span dir=\"rtl\">تجويز</span>, which is its <i>nomen actionis</i>, is employed by the <i>Motécallemîn</i> as a technical term to designate the celebrated proposition which asserts that whatsoever the imagination can conceive is likewise <i>admissible</i> <span dir=\"rtl\">(جائز)</span> to reason; that everything in the universe might be otherwise than it is, and that naught is impossible, provided one can form an idea thereof. Cf. Maimonides, <i>Eight Chapters</i>, or Introduction to the treatise <i>Abôth</i>, chap. I<sup>st</sup> (Porta Mosis of Pococke, p. 188).—One must truly marvel to observe an author of our own day affirm with assurance that not only are there most essential errors in Maimonides’s <i>purported</i> enumeration of the principles of the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, but that <i>even that which he terms their principal foundation, namely, that there is no certain knowledge of things, inasmuch as the contrary can ever exist and be conceived in our understanding, is altogether erroneous and diametrically opposed to dogmatic doctrine</i> (Schmœlders, <i>Essays</i>, p. 135). The selfsame author insinuates that Maimonides knew the doctrines of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> solely through the false reports of their adversaries; yet it results from Maimonides’s own declaration that he was perfectly acquainted with the doctrines in question, and here, as everywhere else, his assertions are based upon a serious and profound study of the most authentic sources; vide the commencement of chapter LXXIV. It suffices, moreover, to peruse Ibn-Roschd’s <i>Destruction of the Destruction</i>, which contains numerous particulars concerning the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, and in which one rediscovers all the propositions enumerated by Maimonides. These two illustrious men must assuredly have been at least as well informed as the more recent authors whom M. Schmœlders may have consulted. As for this X<sup>th</sup> proposition, which Maimonides denominates <i>the base of the science of the Calâm</i>, it is so essential in the system of the <i>Motécallemîn</i> that it is impossible to separate it therefrom. Ibn-Roschd likewise reverts to it on various occasions.",
    "fn.1061.sub_1": "One contents oneself with citing here but a single passage, in which Ibn-Roschd (speaking of the principle of <i>causality</i> denied by the <i>Motécallemîn</i>, and notably by the <i>Ascharites</i>, who perceive in all phenomena and in all actions occurring in the world the immediate intervention of the Creator) expresses himself thus: <span dir=\"rtl\">והניחו עם זה כל הנמצאות פעולות עוברות ולא יראו שיש בם סדר ולא ערך ולא חכמה גזר אותם טבע הנמצאות אבל האמינו שכל נמצא אפשר שיהיה בחלוף מה שהוא</span> «They have, withal, laid down as a principle that all existing things are <i>admissible</i> (and not <i>necessary</i>) facts, and they admit not that there is in them either order, or proportion, or wisdom, resulting from the <i>nature</i> of things. They believe, on the contrary, that everything which exists might be otherwise than it is.» Vide <i>Destr. of the Destruction</i>, III<sup>rd</sup> question (Hebr. version, ms. of the Oratory collection, no. 93, fol. 243 <i>a</i> ). Cf. Moses of Narbonne, Commentary on the <i>Moré Neboukhîm</i>, Part I<sup>st</sup>, at the end of chap. LIII; Ibn-Falaquéra, <i>Moré ha-Moré</i>, I, chaps. LXXIII and LXXIV (pp. 58 and 63); Ritter, <i>Geschichte der Philosophie</i>, vol. VII, p. 737.",
    "fn.1269.sub_0": "Regarding infinity in general, vide Aristotle, <i>Physics</i>, Book III, chapters 4-8; <i>Metaph.</i>., Book II, chapter 2; Book XI, chapter 10. Aristotle demonstrates in these various passages that, in nature, the infinitely great <i>in actuality</i>, that is to say, infinite extension, is inadmissible, and he admits no other infinity than the infinitely small or the infinite divisibility of space, which he designates (as well as the infinitude of abstract number) as the <i>infinite in potentiality</i>. Consequently, the universe itself, being the most extended body, is limited in space (vide the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i>, Book I, chapter 7, where Aristotle concludes by saying: Ὅ<i>τι μὲν τοίνυν οὐϰ ἐστὶ τὸ σῶμα τὸ τοῦ παντὸς ἄπειρον ἐϰ τούτων φανερόν</i>). The most general demonstration of this first proposition is given in the very definition of body. Aristotle observes that from a <i>logical</i> point of view (<i>λογιϰῶς</i>), the existence of an infinite body is inadmissible: for, if one's conception of body is that of being limited by surfaces, there can be no unlimited body, whether intelligible or sensible (<i>εἰ γάρ έστι σώματος λόγος τὸ ἐπιπέδῳ ὡρισμένον, οὐϰ ἂν εἴη σῶμα ἄπειρον, οὔτε νοητὸν οὔτε αἰσθητόν</i>. <i>Physics</i>, III, 5, <i>Metaph.</i>., XI, 10). From a <i>physical</i> point of view, Aristotle demonstrates that an infinite body could be neither composite nor simple: 1° Were it composite, the parts of the composition would be either infinite or finite; now it is evident that they could not be infinite, for the infinitude of each necessarily excludes that of the others; but neither could they be finite, for they would be consumed by the infinite, and would entirely disappear before it. 2° Neither could the infinite body be simple; for none of the elements, each of which has its determined region, is infinite, and there exists no sensible body, outside of the elements, which unites them all, as several physicists have believed.— Another physical proof (which in some sort relates to the logical proof) is this: «Every sensible body is in space. The species and differences of space are: up and down, front and back, right and left. These distinctions exist not merely in relation to us and by position, but are founded in the whole itself.",
    "fn.1269.sub_1": "Yet they could not exist in the infinite.» (Vide <i>Physics, l. c.</i> at the end of chapter 5). Ibn-Sînâ and other authors, both Arabic and Jewish, have multiplied the demonstrations of the propositions enumerated by Maimonides. Abou-becr-Mo’hammed al-Tebrizi, who composed a commentary upon the twenty-five propositions, entered into lengthy details to demonstrate their truth. This 1<sup>st</sup> proposition has been demonstrated by the Arabs in several different ways. One shall here cite a demonstration borrowed from Ibn-Sînâ: Let the magnitude supposed infinite be a line AB <span dir=\"rtl\">أ ج ب</span>; one may suppose this line to be prolonged to infinity on both sides or only on one side B. In this latter case, let one imagine that from the finite side a part AC is cut; one shall then have two lines AB and CB, each of which will be infinite on side B and finite on the other side. Now, if one superimposes point C upon point A, one of two things must follow: either the line CB will be prolonged to infinity like the line AB, and then one shall have AB—AC=AB, which is impossible, for the part cannot be equal to the whole; or else the line CB will be prolonged less than the line AB, and then it will be finite on side B, which is contrary to the hypothesis. If the line AB is supposed infinite on both sides, it may be cut at any point whatsoever, so as to form two lines, each of which will be infinite on one side and finite on the other, and the demonstration will be the same as that just given. Cf. Schahrestâni, p. 403 (German transl., vol. <small>II</small>, pp. 295 and 296). — One shall not be able to reproduce the proofs adduced for each of the propositions here enumerated by Maimonides, and one must confine oneself to indicating the passages whence our author has drawn these different propositions. As shall be seen, they are not all borrowed from Aristotle, and several are taken from the works of Ibn-Sînâ, who, as one has already stated elsewhere, is the principal source from which Maimonides drew his knowledge of Peripatetic doctrines. Regarding this first proposition and the two succeeding ones, vide also the 1<sup>st</sup> part of this work, chapter LXXIII, 11<sup>th</sup> proposition, and the notes appended thereto (Vol. I, pp. 413 and 414).",
    "fn.1281.sub_0": "This proposition, enunciated in too concise a manner, has been deemed obscure, and, taken in an absolute sense, it has encountered objections (vide the commentary of Moses of Narbonne). The following is how it ought to be understood: Everything which has not <i>within itself</i> the principle of its motion, but upon which an accidental motion is impressed by an external cause that may cease to exist, will necessarily be at rest when that cause ceass, as, for instance, the passenger of a ship, who is moved only because he is accidentally within a thing in motion, which may cease to move (Cf. Aristotle, <i>De Anima,</i> book I, chap. 3, § 2). That which proves Maimonides to apprehend this proposition thus, is that further on, at the commencement of chapter I, in demonstrating that the prime mover cannot be considered as the soul of the celestial sphere, he applies this VIII<sup>th</sup> proposition to the human soul, which is moved <i>accidentally</i> with the body by an external cause, whether in seeking that which is agreeable unto it, or in fleeing that which is contrary unto it. It is therefore erroneously that the Arabic commentator Al-Tebrîzi objects to this proposition of Maimonides, averring that there are certain motions which, albeit accidental, are nonetheless perpetual, as, for example, the diurnal motion from East to West, impressed by the ninth sphere upon the eight inferior spheres, and which is contrary to their proper and <i>essential</i> motion from West to East; or as the circular motion of the sphere of fire and of the other elements, the <i>essential</i> motion of which is in a straight line (cf. vol. I of this work, pp. 357-359). It is evident that these accidental motions, having for their cause an essential and perpetual motion, must themselves be perpetual. The proposition here in question appears to relate to a passage in the treatise <i>On the Heavens</i> (book I, end of chap. 2), in which Aristotle establishes that above the four elements which, by their nature, possess a rectilinear motion, there exists a simple substance of another nature which possesses circular motion.",
    "fn.1281.sub_1": "Now, he observs, this motion must be inherent in the nature of this substance; for it would be astonishing and altogether unreasonable that this motion, which alone is continuous and eternal, could be <i>contrary to nature</i>, inasmuch as, in general, that which is contrary to nature is swiftly destroyed: E<i>ἰ δὲ παρὰ φύσιν φέρεται τά φερόμενα ϰύϰλῳ τὴν περὶξ φοράν, θαυμαστὸν ϰαὶ παντελῶς ἀλογον τὸ μόνην εἶναι συνεχῆ τάυτην τὴν ϰὶνησιν ϰαι ἀίδιον, οὖσαν παρὰ, φύσιν · φαίνεται γὰρ ἔν γε τοῖς ἀλλοις τάχιστα φθειρόμενα τὰ παρὰ φύσιν</i>. The Arabic version, which rendered the words <i>contrary to nature</i> (<i>παρὰ φύσιν</i>) by <i>accidental</i>, does better explicate the terms of Maimonides' proposition. Behold how the passage we have just cited has been paraphrased in the middle commentary of Ibn-Roschd, <i>De Cœlo et Mundo,</i> book I, <i>summa</i> IV, <i>demonstrat.</i> 5 (Hebrew version): <span dir=\"rtl\">והוא שזאת התנועה הסבובית שהיא סביב האמצע לא המלט משתהיה לזה הגשם טבעית או מקרית ושקר הוא שתהיה מקרית בי התנועה המקרית אי אפשר שתמצא תמידית אין תכלית לה ושהצעת זה יוצאה מכל הקש כי אנחנו רואים הדברים המקריים כלים אובדים</span>.",
    "fn.1293.sub_0": "In this proposition, the author establishes that local motion (that, of course, which, in the preceding proposition, was designated as the sole continuous one) is prior <i>in nature</i> to all other motions and changes; and by <i>prior in nature</i> one must understand, conformably to Aristotle's definition (<i>Metaph</i>., book V, chap. 11), that which can exist without other things existing, but without which other things cannot exist. The terms of this proposition are drawn from Aristotle's <i>Physics</i>, book VIII, chap. 7 (Cf. book VII, chap. 2), although the author, it would appear to one, does not strictly follow the Stagirite's reasoning. It has been observed above, p. 6, note 2, that according to Aristotle, the idea of motion applies to the categories of quantity, quality, and place. Now, he says, of these three species of motion, that of place is necessarily the first: for it is impossible that there should be <i>growth</i> without there having first been <i>transformation.</i> Transformation is the change into that which is opposite; but when there is transformation, it is necessary that there be something which transforms and which causes, for example, that which is potentially hot to become actually hot. Now it is evident that the mobile of this transformation is sometimes nearer and sometimes further from the thing to be transformed, and that transformation could not occur without local motion; this, consequently, is the first among motions. Further on, Aristotle establishes by other proofs that local motion, although it be the last to develop in the individual beings of this world, is the first in the universe and even precedes the generation (<i>γένεσις</i>) of all things, which is followed by transformation and growth (<i>μετὰ γὰρ τὸ γενέσθαι πρῶτον ἀλλοίωσις ϰαὶ αὔξησɩς</i>; <i>Phys</i>., VIII, 7, pag. 260 <i>b</i>, line 32).",
    "fn.1293.sub_1": "One might at first be surprised that Maimonides places transformation before generation and corruption; but it appears that our author considers transformation from a more general standpoint, that is to say, not only in relation to the category of quality, as in the IV<sup>th</sup> proposition, but also in relation to <i>generation</i>, which is itself in some sort a transformation of matter by form, a transformation wrought by an agent more or less remote, which needs to approach the matter to be transformed. It is also in this sense that Ibn-Roschd explains the passage from the <i>Physics</i>. See Aristotle's Works with Averroës' Commentaries, folio edition, t. IV, fol. 180 <i>c</i>: «Then he says: <i>And it is manifest that the disposition of the mover then does not run in the same way</i>, <i>but perhaps sometimes it will be nearer to the altered and sometimes further away</i>, etc. That is, and because the first altering thing, which is not altered, does not always alter, but sometimes, it is necessary that it not hold itself with the altered in the same disposition, but sometimes it approaches it, and alters, and sometimes it withdraws, and does not alter: and proximity, and distance is not, except by translation: therefore translation naturally precedes alteration, namely that, when both have been in act: then the altering thing altered, after it was not altering: it is necessary that one move in place either the altering, or the altered, or both. If, however, one has been <i>generated</i>, or both, and we have posited this to be the cause of that, which sometimes alters, and sometimes does not, <i>it will be manifest that translation ought to precede in the same way, since alteration also precedes generation; for generation is either alteration or follows alteration</i>.»",
    "fn.1374.sub_0": "This demonstration appears to be founded upon a passage in Aristotle's <i>Physics</i>, which may be summarised thus: In motion, three things may be considered: that which is moved, that which moves, and that by which the latter moves. That which is moved does not necessarily impart motion; that which serves as an instrument or intermediary imparts motion whilst simultaneously receiving it; lastly, that which moves without being an instrument or intermediary is itself immobile. Now, as one observes (in the universe), on the one hand, that which is moved without possessing within itself the principle of motion, that is to say, without moving aught else, and on the other hand, that which is at once moved by another and is itself the mover of another, it is reasonable, if not necessary, to admit of a third entity which moves without being moved. See <i>Phys.</i>, Book VIII, Ch. 5 (Bekker's edit., p. 256 <i>b</i>): <i>τρία γὰρ ἀνάγϰη εἶναι, τό τε ϰινούμενον, ϰαὶ τὸ ϰινοῦν, ϰαὶ τὸ ᾧ ϰινεῖ. ϰ. τ. λ.</i> In the <i>Metaphysics</i>, Book XII, Chapter VII, Aristotle himself summarises thus: <i>ἔστι τοίνυν τι ϰαὶ ὅ ϰινεῖ, ἐπεὶ δὲ τὸ ϰινούμενον ϰαὶ ϰινούν, ϰαὶ μέσοντοίνυν ἐστί τι ὃ οὐ ϰινούμενον ϰινεῖ, ἀΐδιον ϰαὶ οὐσία ϰαὶ ἐνέργεια οὖσα</i>. Alexander of Aphrodisias explained this passage in terms nigh identical to those employed by Maimonides in this second demonstration; and it is doubtless from Alexander that our author borrowed his argumentation, as well as the proposition which he places at the head as having been enunciated by Aristotle himself. Alexander’s explanation was cited by Averroes in his grand commentary upon the <i>Metaphysics</i>. One reproduces it from the Latin version of this commentary (edit. in-folio, f. 149 <i>verso</i>): «Dixit Alexander: Ista est ratio quod [est aliquod movens [quod] non movetur, et est dicta breviter et rememoratio ejus quod dictum est in ultimo Physicorum. Et est fundata super duas propositiones, quarum una est quod omne compositum ex duobus quorum alterum potest esse per se, possibile erit etiam alterum esse per se, nisi compositio sit substantiæ et accidentis; verbi gratiâ quod hydromel, quia componitur ex aqua et melle, et mel invenitur per se, necesse est ergo ut aqua inveniatur per se. Et, quia invenimus aliquod motum et movens quasi compositum ex movente et moto, et invenimus aliquod motum per se et non movens, manifestum est quod est necesse aliquod movens esse et non motum.]",
    "fn.1374.sub_1": "Hoc igitur movens immune est a potentia, et in nulla materia existit.» As for the proposition attributed by Maimonides to Aristotle, some commentators have objected that, in that which is composed of <i>substance</i> and <i>accident</i>, one cannot conceive of the existence of the accident alone, albeit the substance may evidently exist alone. This objection falls away by virtue of the express condition posited by Alexander: nisi compositio sit substantiœ et accidentis</i>. Maimonides, in copying Alexander, may perchance have omitted this condition through inadvertence.",
    "fn.1429.sub_0": "As one has observed, the author, in this enumeration of the Intelligences, has followed Ibn-Sinâ, who, besides the supreme Intelligence, or God, admitts ten Intelligences, of which the first, emanating directly from God (Cf. hereinafter, chap. <small>XXII</small>), is that of the sphere of diurnal motion, which encompasses the entire universe, and of which the last, having emanated from the Intelligence of the lunar sphere, is the <i>active intellect</i>. Vide <i>Avicennœ opera</i>, <i>Metaph</i>., IX, <i>l: c.</i>: «Si autem circuli planetarum fuerint sic quod principium motus circulorum uniuscujus-que planetarum sit virtus fluens a planeta, tunc non erit longe quin <i>separata</i> sint secundum numerum planetarum, non secundum numerum circulorum; et tune eorum numerus est decem post primum (that is to say, God). Primum autem eorum est Intelligentia quæ non movetur, cujus est movere sphæram corporis ultimi (that is to say, the <i>diurnal</i> sphere). Deinde id quod sequitur est quod movet sphæram fixarum. Deinde sequitur quod movet sphæram Saturni. Similiter est quousque pervenitur ad Intelligentiam a qua fluit super nostras animas; et hæc est Intelligentia mundi terreni, et vocamus eam <i>Intelligentiam agentem</i>.» Ibn-Roschd, identifying the sphere of diurnal motion with that of the fixed stars, admitts but eight spheres. Vide <i>Epitome in libros metaph</i>., tract. <small>IV</small> (fol. 182, col. <i>a</i>): «Tandem apud me est remotum quod inveniatur orbis nonus sine stellis; nam orbis est stellarum gratia, quæ sunt nobilior pars ejus…. Orbis autem qui movet motum magnum est nobilior cæteris or-bibus; quapropter non videtur nobis quod sit sine stellis, immo apud me est impossibile.» According to this, there would be in all but <i>nine separate Intelligences</i>, of which the last, as in the theory of Ibn-Sinâ, is the <i>active intellect</i>. This latter, therefore, according to the theory of the Arabs, stands in an intimate relation with the Intelligence of the lunar sphere, from which it emanats directly, but with which it must not be identified. — [In my <i>Mélanges de philosophie juive et arabe</i>, at the commencement of page 332 (Cf. also pages 165 and 448, and Vol. I of this work, page",
    "fn.1429.sub_1": "277, and note 3), one has expressed oneself inaccurately in this regard, and ought to have stated: «From the last of these <i>separate Intelligences</i>, which presids over the motion of the sphere nearest unto us (that of the moon), <i>emanats</i> the active intellect, etc.»] — The passage from Ibn-Roschd (<i>l. c</i>., fol. 184, col. <i>d</i>), which has often been cited to demonstrate that, according to this author, the active intellect is itself the mover of the lunar sphere (vide Ritter, <i>Geschichte der philosophie</i>, Vol. VIII, p. 148), is thus rendered in the Hebrew version: <span dir=\"rtl\">והשכל הפועל הוא יםודר מםוף אלו המניעים במדרגה ונניחו מניע גלגל הירח</span>. The word <span dir=\"rtl\">מםוף</span> appears to one to be an inexact translation of the Arabic words <span dir=\"rtl\">من آخر</span>, and one must render it thus: «The active intellect emanats from the last of these movers in rank (that is to say, from that one of these movers which by its rank is the last), which we suppose to be the mover of the sphere of the moon.» Lest any doubt remain in this regard, one shall here cite a passage from the commentary of Moses of Narbonne upon the <i>Makâcid</i> of Al-Gazâli, towards the end of the II<sup>nd</sup> part, or of the <i>Metaph</i>., in which is expounded (according to Ibn-Sîna) the theory of the spheres and of their intelligences (vide Heb. ms. from the Oratory collection, n° 93, fol. 179 <i>a</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">ואמנם בן רשד ובל הפילוםופים מודים שהשבל הפועל בלתי מניע לגלגל רק שהוא עשירי לשבלים הנפרדים לפי בן רשד והשבל הראשון בבלל והוא עשירי לשבלים העלולים מבלתי מנוה במחוייב המציאות לפי אבוחמד</span>. «Ibn-Roschd and all philosophers agree that the <i>active intellect</i> is not the mover of any celestial sphere. But, according to Ibn-Roschd, it is the tenth of the separate Intelligences, if one does include therein the first Intelligence (or God); whilst, according to Abou-’Hamed (al-Gazâli), it is the tenth of the <i>caused</i> Intelligences, without reckoning the <i>Necessary Being</i> (i.e., the first absolute cause, or God).»—Cf. Albert the Great, <i>De Causis et processu universitatis</i>, Book I, tract. <small>IV</small>, chap. 8 (opp. Vol. V, p.",
    "fn.1527.sub_0": "The four astronomical terms here employed by the author are borrowed from the theories of Ptolemy concerning the motion of the planets. By <span dir=\"rtl\">اختلاف</span> is designated <i>anomaly,</i> or the inequality of a celestial body; the word <span dir=\"rtl\">رجوع</span> designates the apparent <i>retrogradation</i> (<i>προήγησις</i>) of the five planets, opposed to their <i>direct motion</i> (<i>ὑπόλειψις</i>) designated in Arabic by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">استقامة</span>, <i>rectitude;</i> finally, by <span dir=\"rtl\">إقامة</span>, one understands what Ptolemy terms the <i>station</i> (<i>στηριγμός</i>) of these same planets, that is to say, the position in which the motion of the celestial body appears to cease. Cf. <i>Almagest</i>, Book XII, chap. I. In the Arabic version of the <i>Almagest</i> (Hebrew MS. of the Imperial Library, ancient collection, no. 441), the terms <i>προήγησις</i> and <i>ὑπόλειψις</i> are rendered more accurately, the former by <span dir=\"rtl\">تقدّم</span> <i>to go forward,</i> the latter by <span dir=\"rtl\">تأخر</span> <i>to remain behind.</i> Later, the Arabs substituted for the former of these two terms that of <span dir=\"rtl\">رجوع</span> <i>retrogradation,</i> and for the latter that of <span dir=\"rtl\">استقامة</span> <i>rectitude.</i> Indeed, the periodic motion of the planets occurring from west to east, the planet, when it appears to <i>retrograde,</i> i.e., when it appears less advanced in longitude, is found to be forward with respect to the diurnal motion from east to west; the more advanced it is in longitude with respect to the periodic motion, the more it is behind with respect to the diurnal motion. Delambre, to account for the two terms employed by Ptolemy, expresses himself thus (<i>Notes on the Almagest</i>, appended to the edition of Abbé Halma, Vol. II, p. 16): “One says of a star which preceds another at the meridian, which passes there before it, which marches at its head, that it is <i>προηγοὐμενος</i>; of a star which passes there after it, that it is <i>ἑπόμενος</i>.",
    "fn.1527.sub_1": "But that which passes first at the meridian is less advanced in longitude; that which follows it, <i>ἑπόμενος</i>, which remains behind, <i>ὑπολειπόμενος</i>, is on the contrary more advanced in longitude; thus a star is <i>προηγοὐμενος</i> when its longitude diminishes and it retrograds; <i>προήγησις,</i> in the language of the Greeks, therefore corresponds to <i>retrogradation;</i> it is the same motion considered with respect to two different points, as, in the theory of curves, one may at will place negative abscissae to the right or to the left of the centre indifferently.” — The terms <i>retrogradation</i> and <i>rectitude</i> introduced by the Arabs, one also finds them in the <i>Abridgement of the Almagest</i> by Ibn-Afla’h, Book VIII, in which the Hebrew version, which alone one is able to consult, has for the former the word <span dir=\"rtl\">חזרה</span>, and for the latter the word <span dir=\"rtl\">יושר</span>. It is probably from the work of Ibn-Afla’h that Maimonides adopted these terms (cf. above, p. 81).",
    "fn.1598.sub_0": "One perceives that, according to Maimonides, the difference between Plato and Aristotle is this: that the latter admits not merely the eternity of prime matter, but also that of motion and of time, whereas Plato, whilst admitting the eternity of matter and of chaos, yet believes that the world, as it now exists, had a beginning, that the heavens were, like sublunary things, produced from chaos, and that consequently both motion and time had a commencement. It is in this sense that Plato's opinion has generally been interpreted by the Arabs and by the Scholastics, and it is yet in this sense that one of the most learned adherents of the new Platonic school of Italy does express himself. Herein does Leo Hebraeus express himself on this question (<i>Dialoghi di amore</i>, III, edit. of Venice, 1572, fol. 145 et seq.): «Concedendo tutti gli huomini che’l sommo Dio genitore et opifice del mondo sia eterno, senza alcun principio temporale, son divisi nella produttion del mondo, se è ab eterno, o da qualche tempo in quà.",
    "fn.1598.sub_1": "Molti dei filosofi tengono essere prodotto ab eterno da Dio, e non havere mai havuto principio temporale, così come esso Dio non l’ha mai havuto, et di questa opinione è il grande Aristolile, et tutti i peripatetici… Ma gli fideli, et tutti quelli che credono la sacra legge di Moise, tengono che’l mondo fusse non ab eterno produtto, anzi di nulla creato in principio temporale, et ancora alcuni dei filosofi par che sentino questo; per quali è il divino Platone, che nel Timeo pone il mondo essere fatto et genito da Dio, produtto del chaos, che è la materia confusa, del quale le cose sono generate… È ben vero che lui fa il chaos, di che le cose sono fatte, eterno, cioè eternalmente produtto da Dio, laquai cosa non tengono gli fideli; perche loro tengono che fino all’ hora della creatione solo Dio fusse in essere senza mondo, et senza chaos, et che l’omnipotentia di Dio di nulla tutte le cose in principio di tempo habbia produtto, che in effetto non par già chiaramente in Moise, che’l ponga materia coeterna a Dio.— Sono adunque tre opinioni nella produttione del mondo da Dio: la prima d’Aristotile, che tutto il mondo fu produtto ab eterno; la seconda di Platone, che solamente la materia, o chaos, fu produtto ab eterno, ma il mondo in principio di tempo; et la terza delli fideli, che tutto sia produtto di nulla in principio di tempo.» — But one does recognise by our passage that in Maimonides' time, as in all epochs, opinions were divided concerning the true import of Plato's doctrine; and, indeed, Plato's poetic language and the mythical garb in which he frequently presents his doctrines do but too well justify this divergence of opinions. Whilst several of the most ancient Platonists, and later the Neoplatonists, maintained that Plato had admitted, like Aristotle, the eternity of the world, others, on the contrary (notably certain Christians, as for instance Clement of Alexandria), went so far as to contend that Plato had professed the doctrine of creation <i>ex nihilo</i>, considering God as the author not merely of the order of the world, but of matter itself. The same divergence is yet to be observed amongst the scholars of our own day. One may consult, in this regard, Zeller, <i>Die Philosophie der Griechen</i>, vol. II, p. 508 et seq. (2<sup>nd</sup> edit. Tübingen, 1859), and Henri Martin, <i>Études sur le Timée de Platon</i>, vol. II, p. 179 et seq..",
    "fn.1598.sub_2": "This latter scholar, after a profound examination of the texts, arrives at conclusions which accord with the opinion of Maimonides and Leo Hebraeus, namely, that according to the <i>Timaeus</i>, 1° God did not create the prime matter of bodies, that is to say, indeterminate substance; 2° He did not even create second matter, that is to say, eternal chaos; 3° He produced the order of the world, but not from all eternity. — The distinction between incorporeal prime matter and corporeal chaos is of no concern to one here, no more than the question of how Plato understood prime matter and in which his doctrine, in this regard, differs from that of Aristotle. Let it suffice to state that Plato admits an eternal principle opposed to the <i>Idea,</i> as blind necessity is to reason, non-being to being, the variable and the multiple to the permanent and the absolute <i>One.</i> Yet this is not Aristotle’s <i>ὕλη</i>, that <i>being in potency</i> which necessarily calls for form to become <i>being in actuality;</i> for, according to Plato, it is with consciousness and freedom, and not by necessity, that the Idea realises itself in the indeterminate substratum, which it causes to pass from confusion to order. The term <i>ὕλη</i> is itself unknown to Plato, although Aristotle employs this term when speaking of his master’s doctrine. This confused and indeterminate substance, which the Idea <i>orders</i> and in which it manifests itself, is termed by Plato: <i>that which receives the impression</i> (<i>τὸ ἐϰμαγεῖον</i>), <i>that in which things are born, the place</i>, <i>space,</i> etc.; it is also presented as <i>the mother</i> or <i>the receptacle and nurse of all generation;</i> and Plato himself states (<i>Timaeus</i>, page 49 <i>a</i>) that it is a species indeed obscure and difficult to comprehend. Cf. Ritter, <i>Geschichte der Philosophie</i>, vol. II, pages 347 et seq.; Brandis, <i>Handbuch der Geschichte der griechisch-römischen Philosophie</i>, vol. II, A, pages 297 et seq.; Zeller, <i>l. c.</i>, pages 457 et seq.; Henri Martin, <i>l. c</i>., vol. I, pages 16 et seq.",
    "fn.2473.sub_0": "Maimonides, in approaching his half-spoken explanation of Ezekiel's vision, in which he seems to rediscover the principal cosmological doctrines of the Arab Peripatetics, deems it necessary to indicate in the first place what one must understand by the four faces attributed to each of the four <i>’hayyôth</i>, or figures of animals (Ezekiel, I, 10), in which he discerns the celestial spheres, reduced to four: that of the moon, that of the sun, that of the five other planets, and that of the fixed stars. See the II<sup>nd</sup> part of this work at the close of chap. <small>IX</small>, and chap. <small>X</small>, p. 82 et seq. The prophet ascribes to the <i>’hayyôth</i> a face of man, a face of lion, a face of ox, and a face of eagle, and thus makes them the representatives of that which is most noble among the diverse species of animals. According to our author, the three faces of unreasoning animals are likewise faces of man; but he tells us not why he does insist thereupon, and he leavs us to conjecture what he intends by the symbol of the four faces. One believs that the author alludes to the four causes of the spheres' motion, indicated according to him by the four wings (<i>v</i>. 6), and of which he has spoken in chaps. <small>IV</small> and <small>X</small> of the II<sup>nd</sup> part; these are: the figure of the sphere, that is to say its sphericity, its soul, its intellect by which it conceivs, and the separated Intelligence, the object of its desire (see vol. II, p. 88). In the sublunar world, man is the sole being in whom these four causes of motion are retraced; man alone possesses a soul endowed with conception and an intellect, which are set in motion by the desire to unite with the separated intelligence, that is to say with the universal <i>active intellect</i>; and from this point of view, the motion of man has naught in common with that of unreasoning animals and can only be compared to the circular motion of the spheres. Cf. vol. II, chap. <small>IV</small>, p. 52. The explanation one has just given is indicated by Samuel ibn-Tibbon in his treatise <i>Yikkawou ha-maïm</i>, chap. XI, p. 47: <span dir=\"rtl\">וענין הנפש והשכל רמז אליו בשרמז שכלם יש להם פני אדם וכל הפנים פני אדם</span>.",
    "fn.2473.sub_1": "One omits other explanations furnished by Moses of Narbonne and Ephodi, which pertain to that which the author has stated concerning the four forces emanating from the four spheres and acting upon the four elements (Vol. II, Chap. <small>X</small>, p. 89); these explanations offer no interest, neither for science nor for its history, and one shall confine oneself to that which is strictly needful for the comprehension of Maimonides’ recondite words. — Moreover, the entire interpretation which Maimonides here offers, in veiled terms, of Ezekiel’s vision, is based, as one has observed, upon Peripatetic cosmology; but if the prophet, as is doubtless probable, availed himself of astronomical symbols, he could have borrowed them only from the astronomy of his own era, and one would needs address Chaldean science to discover their elucidation. Isaac Abravanel has utterly overthrown Maimonides’ interpretation by justly observing that, were such the import of Ezekiel’s vision, there would be no occasion to shroud it in such mystery, since it would merely concern a science taught in all schools of philosophy and familiar to the learned of all communions. See Abravanel’s second Preface to the book of Ezekiel and his commentary on Chap. I of the same book; cf. also Abravanel’s commentary on various parts of the <i>Moreh Nevuchim</i>, Book <sup>II</sup>, fol. 48 et seq.",
    "fn.3123.sub_0": "As one has already observed in one's <i>Réflexions sur le culte des anciens Hébreux</i> [Reflections upon the Worship of the Ancient Hebrews], p. 2 (published in vol. IV of M. Cahen’s <i>Bible</i>), Maimonides, like many other Arabian authors of his time, does understand by the word <span dir=\"rtl\">צאבה̈</span> or <i>Sabians</i> all pagan peoples in general. The books from which he had drawn his knowledge of pagan cults, and of which he will speak further on, gave him occasion to believe that these cults were generally based upon astrolatry. Consequently, in the parlance of Maimonides, <i>religion des Sabiens</i> [religion of the Sabians] signifies the same as <i>paganisme.</i> [paganism]. In the Qur'an (<small>II</small>, 59; V, 73; <small>XXII</small>, 17), the Sabians <span dir=\"rtl\">(الصابيون)</span> are mentioned alongside the Jews and Christians, as a religious community possessing revealed books and having a share in the life to come. It is generally agreed that the Sabians of the Qur'an are the Mandæans or Christians of Saint John, who, owing to their frequent ablutions, are termed in Syriac <span dir=\"rtl\">צביעין</span> (or <span dir=\"rtl\">צביין</span>, according to the pronunciation of the Mandæans, who suppress the <span dir=\"rtl\">ע</span>), that is to say, the <i>Baptists.</i> See <i>E. Castelli Lexicon-syriacum seorsim typis describi curavit atque sua adnotata adjecit</i> E. Castelli oversaw the separate printing of the Syriac Lexicon and added his own annotations, J. D. Michaëlis, p. 749. Arabian authors, from the epoch of the Caliph Al-Mamoun, did also bestow the name of Sabians upon the pagans of Harran and of certain other cities in Mesopotamia, who were entirely distinct from the Sabians of the Qur'an, a circumstance which has occasioned much confusion. Vain efforts have been made to discover the etymology of the name <i>Sabians</i> as applied to the pagans. The conjecture which has met with the most favour is that of Pococke (<i>Specimen hist. ar</i>. Specimen of Arabian History, p. 139), who derivs this name from the Hebrew word <span dir=\"rtl\">צבא</span>, <i>army</i>, and who discerns therein an allusion to the worship of the stars termed <span dir=\"rtl\">צבא השמים</span>, <i>army of heaven;</i> but this etymology is no more plausible than all the others.",
    "fn.3123.sub_1": "One does now know that there is in reality but a single species of <i>Sabians</i>, namely the Mandæans; but that the pagans of Harran, menaced with extermination by the Caliph Al-Mamoun, did usurp, circa the year 830 of the Christian era, upon the counsel of a Muslim doctor, the name of Sabians, and did pretend to be the sect mentioned under this name in the Qur'an and recommended by the prophet to the protection of the Muslims. This fact is reported in the <i>Kitab Al-Fihrist</i> by Mohammed ben Is’hak al-Nedîm (see the extract from this book furnished by M. de Hammer in the Journal Asiatique, September–October 1841, p. 254 et seq.). It had already been published by Hottinger, <i>Historia Orientalis</i> Oriental History, p. 169, and, following him, by Spencer, <i>De legibus Hebrœorum ritualibus</i> On the Ritual Laws of the Hebrews, book II, chap. <small>I</small>, sect. 2 (p. 241 of the Cambridge edition, 1685, folio). It is above all in the work published on the Sabians by M. Chwolson that the fact in question has been brought to light and supported by numerous proofs. The author shows how the name of Sabians, applied in the Qur'an solely to the Mandæans and employed in this sense by Arabian authors, up to the epoch of Al-Mamoun, did subsequently serve to designate likewise the Harranians, and finally, in the VI<sup>th</sup> century of the Hegira, came to be used in the general sense of Pagans. See <i>Die Ssabier und der Ssabismus</i> [Ger.: The Sabians and Sabianism] (2 vols. large octavo, St. Petersburg, 1856), vol. I, chap. <small>VI</small>, p. 139 et seq., and the entirety of chap. <small>VIII</small>. This excellent work contains numerous details concerning the religion of the Harranians, which may elucidate several facts reported by Maimonides in this chapter and in the ensuing ones, in which our author aims largely to explain the ceremonial practices prescribed by Moses, by means of the superstitious customs of the Sabians or pagans which the Mosaic laws sought to efface.",
    "fn.3146.sub_0": "Instead of <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשמס</span>, one of the two Leyden manuscripts, no. 18, bears <span dir=\"rtl\">אלשאם</span>; similarly, Ibn-Tibbon's version has <span dir=\"rtl\">השאם</span> (in the editions <span dir=\"rtl\">תשאם</span>), which, as is known, is the name of Syria, including Palestine. Al-’Harîzi possessed the same reading, for he renders the word in question as <span dir=\"rtl\">ארץ הצבי</span>, <i>land of beauty</i>, an expression which among the Rabbins denotes Palestine. But it were absurd to conceive of Palestine or Syria in this context, since it is stated immediately thereafter that the country in question is near India. The reading <span dir=\"rtl\">אקלים אלשמם</span>, found in almost all Arabic manuscripts, is doubtless confirmed by other passages of the <i>Nabataean Agriculture</i>, where likewise mention is made of plants brought by Adam from the southern regions to Babylon. Vid., for instance, the passage cited by M. Chwolson in his Memoir on Tammuz: <i>Ueber Tammuz und die Menschenverehrung bei den alten Babyloniern</i>, in the Russian Collection entitled «Acta Universitatis Imperialis Petropolitanae» for the year 1859 (St. Petersburg, in-8°, 1860), p. 167. M. Chwolson translates the words <span dir=\"rtl\">אקלים אלשמם</span> as <i>Sonnenland</i> or «country of the sun» (<i>ibid.</i>, p. 175), and he states in note 2 that this country, according to other passages, was situated to the south of India proper (that is to say, of the Punjab), from which it was separated by a desert. The <i>country of the sun</i> would therefore be, according to M. Chwolson, the <i>Dekhan.</i> It is true that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">إقليم</span> , <i>climat</i>, is often employed among the Arabs in the sense of <i>region</i>, <i>country;</i> but no geographer, ancient or modern, is acquainted with the denomination «country of the sun». One therefore apprehends that the word <span dir=\"rtl\">אקלים</span> here bears its ordinary sense of <i>climat</i>, and that the reference is to the 2<sup>nd</sup> climat, which encompasses a large portion of India. It is known that Ptolemy and the Arab geographers divide the inhabited part of the earth, from South to North, into seven zones designated <i>climats.</i> According to the Sabaeans, as shall be seen further on, each climat is subject to the influence and direction of one of the seven planets. Cf. the Hebrew work <i>Schebilé Emounâ</i> by R. Meir al-Dabi, II, 2 (Amsterdam edition, fol.",
    "fn.3146.sub_1": "19 <i>a</i>): <span dir=\"rtl\">כשהבורא ברא העולם נתן כח בכוכבים למשול בארץ ולהנהיגה וחלק הארצות לשבעה כוכבי לכת וגו׳</span> «When the Creator created the world, He placed within the stars a force by means of which they were to dominate the earth and govern it, and He distributed the countries among the seven planets, etc.» Yakout, in the Introduction to his great Geographical Dictionary entitled <span dir=\"rtl\">معجم البلدان</span> , when speaking of the <i>seven climates</i>, indicates the respective planet dominating over each climat, according to the opinion of the Persians and the Greeks, and he states that the 2<sup>nd</sup> climat is found, according to the Persians, under the influence of Jupiter <span dir=\"rtl\">(المشترى)</span> , and according to the Greeks <span dir=\"rtl\">(الרوم)</span> , under that of the sun <span dir=\"rtl\">(الشمس)</span> ; the 4<sup>th</sup> climat, on the contrary, is found, according to the Persians, under the influence of the sun, and according to the Greeks, under that of Jupiter (Arabic mss. of the Imperial Library, supplement, no. 886, at the commencement). One therefore apprehends that the words of our passage <span dir=\"rtl\">למא כׄרגׄ מן אקלים אלשמס אלמגׄאור ללהנד</span> ought to be understood thus: «when he departed from the region of the climat of the sun (that is to say, of the 2<sup>nd</sup> climat) which is contiguous to India.» One cannot conceive here of the 4<sup>th</sup> climat, no region of which is contiguous to India. It is to be recalled that India is also, according to Muslim tradition, the place of Adam's first sojourn after his fall. Expelled from Paradise, states this tradition, he fell upon the mountain of Serandib, which is the island of Ceylon. Vid. d’Herbelot, <i>Bibliothèque orientale</i>, p. 55 <i>b.</i>",
    "fn.3851.sub_0": "In this phrase, Ibn-Tibbon's version exhibits certain inaccuracies: the word <span dir=\"rtl\">מטעמים</span> is merely a typographical error for <span dir=\"rtl\">מטעמיה</span>; furthermore, this version does not adequately convey the verb <span dir=\"rtl\">ויגׄם</span> (derived from <span dir=\"rtl\">جم</span>, quievit post coitum, or the passive of the fourth form, <span dir=\"rtl\">أجمّ</span>, quietem concessit), and the words <span dir=\"rtl\">מא אמכן</span>, <i>as much as possible</i>, are found transposed."
